window.CS168_TRANSLATIONS = {"nav_titles":{"CS 168 Textbook":"CS 168 教材","Home":"首页","Introduction":"引言","Introduction to the Internet":"互联网(Internet)简介","Layers of the Internet":"互联网分层","Headers":"首部(headers)","Network Architecture":"网络体系结构","Designing Resource Sharing":"资源共享设计","Links":"链路(links)","Routing":"路由(Routing)","Introduction to Routing":"路由简介","Model for Intra-Domain Routing":"域内路由模型","Routing States":"路由状态","Distance-Vector Protocols":"距离向量协议","Link-State Protocols":"链路状态协议","Addressing":"寻址(Addressing)","Router Hardware":"路由器硬件","Model for Inter-Domain Routing":"域间路由模型","Border Gateway Protocol (BGP)":"边界网关协议(BGP)","BGP Implementation and Issues":"BGP实现与问题","IP Header":"IP首部","Transport":"传输层(Transport)","Applications":"应用层(Applications)","End-to-End":"端到端(End-to-End)","Datacenters":"数据中心(Datacenters)","Beyond Client-Server":"超越客户端-服务器(Beyond Client-Server)","Wireless":"无线网络(Wireless)","Glossary":"术语表","Transport Layer Principles":"传输层原理(Transport Layer Principles)","TCP Design":"TCP设计(TCP Design)","TCP Implementation":"TCP实现(TCP Implementation)","Congestion Control Principles":"拥塞控制原理(Congestion Control Principles)","Congestion Control Design":"拥塞控制设计(Congestion Control Design)","Congestion Control Implementation":"拥塞控制实现(Congestion Control Implementation)","TCP Throughput Model":"TCP吞吐量模型(TCP Throughput Model)","Congestion Control Issues":"拥塞控制问题(Congestion Control Issues)","Router-Assisted Congestion Control":"路由器辅助拥塞控制(Router-Assisted Congestion Control)","DNS":"DNS（域名系统）","HTTP":"HTTP（超文本传输协议）","Ethernet":"以太网(Ethernet)","Layer 2 Routing (STP)":"二层路由/STP(Layer 2 Routing, STP)","ARP: Connecting Layers 2 and 3":"ARP：连接第二层与第三层(ARP: Connecting Layers 2 and 3)","DHCP: Joining Networks":"DHCP：加入网络(DHCP: Joining Networks)","NAT: Network Address Translation":"NAT：网络地址转换(NAT: Network Address Translation)","TLS: Secure Bytestreams":"TLS：安全字节流(TLS: Secure Bytestreams)","End-to-End Connectivity":"端到端连接(End-to-End Connectivity)","Topologies":"拓扑(Topologies)","Congestion Control":"拥塞控制(Congestion Control)","Virtualization":"虚拟化(Virtualization)","Software-Defined Networking":"软件定义网络(Software-Defined Networking)","Host Networking":"主机网络(Host Networking)","Multicast":"多播(Multicast)","IP Multicast":"IP多播(IP Multicast)","DVMRP":"DVMRP（距离向量多播路由协议）","Core-Based Trees":"核心树(Core-Based Trees)","IP Multicast Challenges":"IP多播面临的挑战(IP Multicast Challenges)","Overlay Multicast":"覆盖多播(Overlay Multicast)","Collective Operations":"集合操作(Collective Operations)","Collective Implementations":"集合操作的实现(Collective Implementations)","Wireless Links":"无线链路(Wireless Links)","Cellular":"蜂窝网络(Cellular)"},"pages":{"/applications/dns.html":{"title":"dns","blocks":[{"en":"DNS","en_html":"<a href=\"#dns\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dns\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DNS","zh_html":"域名系统(DNS)"},{"en":"What is DNS For?","en_html":"<a href=\"#what-is-dns-for\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"what-is-dns-for\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> What is DNS For?","zh_html":"DNS 有什么用？"},{"en":"In this section, we’ll be looking at a few common applications (Layer 7) that operate on top of the layers we’ve built so far.","en_html":"In this section, we’ll be looking at a few common applications (Layer 7) that operate on top of the layers we’ve built so far.","zh_html":"在本节中，我们将介绍一些运行在已构建的各层之上的常见应用(applications)，即第7层(Layer 7)。"},{"en":"The first application we’ll look at is DNS, which is a protocol that operates on top of the layers (1-4) to provide the important network functionality of name resolution.","en_html":"The first application we’ll look at is DNS, which is a protocol that operates on top of the layers (1-4) to provide the important network functionality of name resolution.","zh_html":"我们首先介绍 DNS，它是在第1到4层之上运行的协议(protocol)，提供重要的网络功能：名称解析(name resolution)。"},{"en":"The Internet is commonly indexed in two different ways. Humans refer to websites using human-readable names such as google.com and eecs.berkeley.edu, while computers refer to websites using IP addresses such as 172.217.4.174 and 23.195.69.108. DNS, or the Domain Name System, is the protocol that translates between the two.","en_html":"The Internet is commonly indexed in two different ways. Humans refer to websites using human-readable names such as <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">google.com</code> and <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>, while computers refer to websites using IP addresses such as <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">172.217.4.174</code> and <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">23.195.69.108</code>. <strong>DNS</strong>, or the <strong>Domain Name System</strong>, is the protocol that translates between the two.","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)通常有两种索引方式。人类使用可读的名称，例如<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">google.com</code>和<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>来访问网站；而计算机使用IP地址(IP addresses)，例如<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">172.217.4.174</code>和<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">23.195.69.108</code>来访问网站。<strong>DNS</strong>，即<strong>域名系统(Domain Name System)</strong>，就是在两者之间进行翻译的协议。"},{"en":"Brief History of DNS","en_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-dns\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-dns\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Brief History of DNS","zh_html":"DNS 简史"},{"en":"To understand why DNS was designed the way it was, it helps to go back and look at the history of its development.","en_html":"To understand why DNS was designed the way it was, it helps to go back and look at the history of its development.","zh_html":"要理解 DNS 为何这样设计，回顾其发展历史会很有帮助。"},{"en":"On the original Internet and its predecessor (ARPANET), there were three key applications. This is before the World Wide Web and web browsers, back when most applications ran on the command line.","en_html":"On the original Internet and its predecessor (ARPANET), there were three key applications. This is before the World Wide Web and web browsers, back when most applications ran on the command line.","zh_html":"在最初的互联网及其前身 ARPANET 上，有三个关键应用。那是在万维网(World Wide Web)和网页浏览器出现之前，大多数应用还在命令行上运行的年代。"},{"en":"Remote terminal (telnet) allowed users to connect to another machine remotely, and run commands on that remote computer. You might have heard of SSH, which is a modern, secure version of this protocol.","en_html":"Remote terminal (telnet) allowed users to connect to another machine remotely, and run commands on that remote computer. You might have heard of SSH, which is a modern, secure version of this protocol.","zh_html":"远程终端(remote terminal, telnet)允许用户远程连接到另一台机器，并在那台远程计算机上运行命令。你可能听说过 SSH，它是这个协议的现代安全版本。"},{"en":"File transfer allowed users to transfer files between their local computer and a remote computer. You might have heard of FTP, which is the application-level protocol for file transfers.","en_html":"File transfer allowed users to transfer files between their local computer and a remote computer. You might have heard of FTP, which is the application-level protocol for file transfers.","zh_html":"文件传输(file transfer)允许用户在本地计算机和远程计算机之间传输文件。你可能听说过 FTP，它是用于文件传输的应用层协议(application-level protocol)。"},{"en":"Email allowed users to exchange messages to each other. Modern email comes with a web client in your browser, but originally, users had to type a command in the terminal like mail alice@46.0.1.2, where 46.0.1.2 is the IP address of the recipient, and alice is the recipient user on that machine.","en_html":"Email allowed users to exchange messages to each other. Modern email comes with a web client in your browser, but originally, users had to type a command in the terminal like <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">mail alice@46.0.1.2</code>, where 46.0.1.2 is the IP address of the recipient, and alice is the recipient user on that machine.","zh_html":"电子邮件(email)允许用户之间交换消息。现代电子邮件可以通过浏览器中的网页客户端访问，但最初用户必须在终端中输入命令，例如<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">mail alice@46.0.1.2</code>，其中 46.0.1.2 是收件人的IP地址，alice 是该机器上的收件人用户名。"},{"en":"In all three cases, performing an operation requires specifying a remote host. But, as we mentioned, remembering the IP addresses of remote hosts is hard and not user-friendly.","en_html":"In all three cases, performing an operation requires specifying a remote host. But, as we mentioned, remembering the IP addresses of remote hosts is hard and not user-friendly.","zh_html":"在这三种场景中，执行操作都需要指定一台远程主机(remote host)。但正如我们提到的，记住远程主机的 IP 地址既困难又不友好。"},{"en":"The first attempt to fix this problem was to assign a hostname to every IP address. Every computer would have a file called hosts.txt mapping every hostname to its IP address. For example, we can map hostname ucb-arpa to 10.0.0.78, and rewrite mail mosher@10.0.0.78 as mail mosher@ucb-arpa.","en_html":"The first attempt to fix this problem was to assign a <strong>hostname</strong> to every IP address. Every computer would have a file called <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">hosts.txt</code> mapping every hostname to its IP address. For example, we can map hostname <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">ucb-arpa</code> to 10.0.0.78, and rewrite <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">mail mosher@10.0.0.78</code> as <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">mail mosher@ucb-arpa</code>.","zh_html":"解决这个问题的首次尝试是为每个IP地址分配一个<strong>主机名(hostname)</strong>。每台计算机都会有一个名为<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">hosts.txt</code>的文件，将每个主机名映射到其IP地址。例如，我们可以将主机名<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">ucb-arpa</code>映射到 10.0.0.78，然后把<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">mail mosher@10.0.0.78</code>重写为<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">mail mosher@ucb-arpa</code>。"},{"en":"This concept actually still exists today. If you’ve ever started a server on your own computer, it probably has the IP address 127.0.0.1 and the hostname localhost. Typing either one in your browser gives you the same result.","en_html":"This concept actually still exists today. If you’ve ever started a server on your own computer, it probably has the IP address 127.0.0.1 and the hostname <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">localhost</code>. Typing either one in your browser gives you the same result.","zh_html":"这个概念实际上至今仍然存在。如果你在自己的计算机上启动过服务器(server)，它很可能有 IP 地址 127.0.0.1 和主机名<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">localhost</code>。在浏览器中输入两者中任何一个都会得到相同的结果。"},{"en":"We have to ensure that hosts.txt is the same across different computers. If you went to a different computer and typed mail mosher@ucb-arpa, you should probably be sending mail to the same person. The hosts file was originally maintained by a single person (Elizabeth Feinler), and passed between users by physically copying a paper document.","en_html":"We have to ensure that <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">hosts.txt</code> is the same across different computers. If you went to a different computer and typed <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">mail mosher@ucb-arpa</code>, you should probably be sending mail to the same person. The hosts file was originally maintained by a single person (Elizabeth Feinler), and passed between users by physically copying a paper document.","zh_html":"我们必须确保<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">hosts.txt</code>在不同计算机上保持一致。如果你到另一台计算机上输入<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">mail mosher@ucb-arpa</code>，你发送邮件的应该是同一个人。该 hosts 文件最初由一个人(Elizabeth Feinler)维护，并通过物理复制纸质文档的方式在用户之间传递。"},{"en":"The original paper hosts file was human-readable. It mapped hostnames to addresses, but also included information like the user’s full name, the protocols they ran (e.g. TCP, FTP), and even their phone number.","en_html":"The original paper hosts file was human-readable. It mapped hostnames to addresses, but also included information like the user’s full name, the protocols they ran (e.g. TCP, FTP), and even their phone number.","zh_html":"最初的纸质 hosts 文件是人类可读的。它不仅将主机名映射到地址，还包含了用户的完整姓名、他们运行的协议(例如 TCP、FTP)，甚至电话号码。"},{"en":"Everybody agreed (as mentioned in RFC606 in 1973) that this was an absurd situation. If you get a paper copy of the file, you’d have to manually input it into your computer. Also, the file was passed around on paper, so you might have an outdated file.","en_html":"Everybody agreed (as mentioned in RFC606 in 1973) that this was an absurd situation. If you get a paper copy of the file, you’d have to manually input it into your computer. Also, the file was passed around on paper, so you might have an outdated file.","zh_html":"所有人都认为(如 1973 年 RFC 606 所述)这种情况非常荒谬。如果你拿到文件的纸质副本，必须手动输入到计算机中。而且文件以纸质形式传递，你拿到的很可能是过时的版本。"},{"en":"The first improvement was to make the list machine-readable. Now, instead of paper copies, we could at least use protocols like FTP to share the file. But this still isn’t scalable. We can’t ask a single person to maintain this file forever. Also, as the file grew big, downloading the file could get very slow, and if the network connection broke, you might end up with a partial file.","en_html":"The first improvement was to make the list machine-readable. Now, instead of paper copies, we could at least use protocols like FTP to share the file. But this still isn’t scalable. We can’t ask a single person to maintain this file forever. Also, as the file grew big, downloading the file could get very slow, and if the network connection broke, you might end up with a partial file.","zh_html":"第一个改进是让列表变成机器可读的(machine-readable)。这样一来，至少可以使用 FTP 等协议来共享文件，而不再需要纸质副本。但这仍然不可扩展(scalable)。我们不可能让一个人永远维护这个文件。而且，随着文件变大，下载速度会变得非常慢；如果网络连接中断，还可能会得到不完整的文件。"},{"en":"DNS was first proposed in 1983 (RFC882) as a solution to these problems. There have been some modifications since, but the fundamental system is still in use to this day.","en_html":"DNS was first proposed in 1983 (RFC882) as a solution to these problems. There have been some modifications since, but the fundamental system is still in use to this day.","zh_html":"DNS 于 1983 年(RFC 882)首次提出，作为这些问题的解决方案。此后经历了一些修改，但基本系统至今仍在使用。"},{"en":"Fun fact: The first software written for running DNS servers on Unix was BIND (1984, UC Berkeley), and it’s still pretty common today.","en_html":"Fun fact: The first software written for running DNS servers on Unix was BIND (1984, UC Berkeley), and it’s still pretty common today.","zh_html":"趣闻：第一个在 Unix 上运行 DNS 服务器的软件是 BIND (1984 年，加州大学伯克利分校)，至今仍相当普遍。"},{"en":"DNS Design Goals","en_html":"<a href=\"#dns-design-goals\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dns-design-goals\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DNS Design Goals","zh_html":"DNS 设计目标"},{"en":"This brief history informs some of the design goals of DNS that we should keep in mind.","en_html":"This brief history informs some of the design goals of DNS that we should keep in mind.","zh_html":"这段简史揭示了我们应注意的 DNS 设计目标。"},{"en":"DNS must be scalable. The Internet has a huge number of hosts, and a huge number of lookups are performed every second. Hosts can also be added and removed frequently.","en_html":"DNS must be scalable. The Internet has a huge number of hosts, and a huge number of lookups are performed every second. Hosts can also be added and removed frequently.","zh_html":"DNS 必须是可扩展的(scalable)。互联网上有海量的主机(hosts)，每秒执行海量的查询(lookups)。主机也会频繁地添加和移除。"},{"en":"DNS must be highly-available, lightweight, and fast. Almost every Internet connection starts with a DNS lookup to translate host name to IP address. Therefore, DNS should be extremely fast, or else every connection would be slowed down. Also, there shouldn’t be a single point of failure, or else the Internet would stop working on a failure.","en_html":"DNS must be highly-available, lightweight, and fast. Almost every Internet connection starts with a DNS lookup to translate host name to IP address. Therefore, DNS should be extremely fast, or else every connection would be slowed down. Also, there shouldn’t be a single point of failure, or else the Internet would stop working on a failure.","zh_html":"DNS 必须高可用(highly-available)、轻量级(lightweight)且快速(fast)。几乎每次互联网连接都以 DNS 查询开始，将主机名翻译为 IP 地址。因此，DNS 必须极其快速，否则每次连接都会被拖慢。同时，不应存在单点故障(single point of failure)，否则一次故障就会导致整个互联网瘫痪。"},{"en":"Name servers","en_html":"<a href=\"#name-servers\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"name-servers\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Name servers","zh_html":"名称服务器(name servers)"},{"en":"It would be great if there was single centralized server that stored a mapping from every domain to every IP address that everyone could query, but unfortunately, there is no server big enough to store the IP address of every domain on the Internet and fast enough to handle the volume of DNS requests generated by the entire world. Instead, DNS uses a collection of many name servers, which are servers dedicated to replying to DNS requests.","en_html":"It would be great if there was single centralized server that stored a mapping from every domain to every IP address that everyone could query, but unfortunately, there is no server big enough to store the IP address of every domain on the Internet and fast enough to handle the volume of DNS requests generated by the entire world. Instead, DNS uses a collection of many <strong>name servers</strong>, which are servers dedicated to replying to DNS requests.","zh_html":"如果能有一个单一的集中式服务器(centralized server)存储从每个域名(domain)到 IP 地址的映射，让所有人都可以查询，那就太好了。但不幸的是，没有一台服务器足够大，能存下互联网上每个域名的 IP 地址，也没有一台服务器足够快，能处理全世界产生的 DNS 请求量。因此，DNS 使用一组<strong>名称服务器(name servers)</strong>，它们是专门回复 DNS 请求的服务器。"},{"en":"Each name server is responsible for a specific zone of domains, so that no single server needs to store every domain on the Internet. For example, a name server responsible for the .com zone only needs to answer queries for domains that end in .com. This name server doesn’t need to store any DNS information related to wikipedia.org. Likewise, a name server responsible for the berkeley.edu zone doesn’t need to store any DNS information related to stanford.edu.","en_html":"Each name server is responsible for a specific zone of domains, so that no single server needs to store every domain on the Internet. For example, a name server responsible for the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.com</code> zone only needs to answer queries for domains that end in <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.com</code>. This name server doesn’t need to store any DNS information related to <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">wikipedia.org</code>. Likewise, a name server responsible for the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code> zone doesn’t need to store any DNS information related to <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">stanford.edu</code>.","zh_html":"每个名称服务器负责一个特定的域名区域(zone)，因此没有单台服务器需要存储互联网上的每个域名。例如，负责<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.com</code>区域的名称服务器只需要回答以<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.com</code>结尾的域名查询。这台名称服务器不需要存储任何与<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">wikipedia.org</code>相关的 DNS 信息。同样，负责<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code>区域的名称服务器不需要存储任何与<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">stanford.edu</code>相关的 DNS 信息。"},{"en":"Even though it has a special purpose (responding to DNS requests), a name server is just like any other server you can contact on the Internet–each one has a human-readable domain name (e.g. a.edu-servers.net) and a computer-readable IP address (e.g. 192.5.6.30). Be careful not to confuse the domain name with the zone. For example, this name server has .net in its domain, but it responds to DNS requests for .edu domains.","en_html":"Even though it has a special purpose (responding to DNS requests), a name server is just like any other server you can contact on the Internet–each one has a human-readable domain name (e.g. <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a.edu-servers.net</code>) and a computer-readable IP address (e.g. <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">192.5.6.30</code>). Be careful not to confuse the domain name with the zone. For example, this name server has <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.net</code> in its domain, but it responds to DNS requests for <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code> domains.","zh_html":"尽管名称服务器有特殊用途(响应 DNS 请求)，但它就像你在互联网上可以联系的其他任何服务器一样——每台服务器都有一个人类可读的域名(domain name)，例如<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a.edu-servers.net</code>，以及一个计算机可读的 IP 地址，例如<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">192.5.6.30</code>。注意不要将域名与区域(zone)混淆。例如，这台名称服务器的域名中含有<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.net</code>，但它响应的是针对<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>域名的 DNS 请求。"},{"en":"Name server hierarchy","en_html":"<a href=\"#name-server-hierarchy\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"name-server-hierarchy\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Name server hierarchy","zh_html":"名称服务器层次结构(name server hierarchy)"},{"en":"You might notice two problems with this design. First, the .com zone may be smaller than the entire Internet, but it is still impractical for one name server to store all domains ending in .com. Second, if there are many name servers, how does your computer know which one to contact?","en_html":"You might notice two problems with this design. First, the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.com</code> zone may be smaller than the entire Internet, but it is still impractical for one name server to store all domains ending in <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.com</code>. Second, if there are many name servers, how does your computer know which one to contact?","zh_html":"你可能会注意到这种设计有两个问题。第一，<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.com</code>区域虽然比整个互联网小，但让一台名称服务器存储所有以<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.com</code>结尾的域名仍然不现实。第二，如果有这么多名称服务器，你的计算机如何知道该联系哪一台？"},{"en":"DNS solves both of these problems by introducing a new idea: when you query a name server, instead of always returning the IP address of the domain you queried, the name server can also direct you to another name server for the answer. This allows name servers with large zones such as .edu to redirect your query to other name servers with smaller zones such as berkeley.edu. Now, the name server for the .edu zone doesn’t need to store any information about eecs.berkeley.edu, math.berkeley.edu, etc. Instead, the .edu name server stores information about the berkeley.edu name server and redirects requests for eecs.berkeley.edu, math.berkeley.edu, etc. to a berkeley.edu name server.","en_html":"DNS solves both of these problems by introducing a new idea: when you query a name server, instead of always returning the IP address of the domain you queried, the name server can also direct you to another name server for the answer. This allows name servers with large zones such as <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code> to redirect your query to other name servers with smaller zones such as <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code>. Now, the name server for the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code> zone doesn’t need to store any information about <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>, <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">math.berkeley.edu</code>, etc. Instead, the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code> name server stores information about the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code> name server and redirects requests for <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>, <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">math.berkeley.edu</code>, etc. to a <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code> name server.","zh_html":"DNS 通过引入一个新思路解决了这两个问题：当你查询一个名称服务器时，它不一定会直接返回你查询的域名的 IP 地址，而是可以将你指引到另一个名称服务器去获取答案。这让拥有较大区域(如<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>)的名称服务器可以将你的查询重定向到拥有更小区域(如<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code>)的其他名称服务器。这样，<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>区域的名称服务器不需要存储<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>、<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">math.berkeley.edu</code>等的任何信息。相反，<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>名称服务器只存储关于<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code>名称服务器的信息，并将针对<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>、<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">math.berkeley.edu</code>等的请求重定向到<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code>名称服务器。"},{"en":"DNS arranges all the name servers in a tree hierarchy based on their zones:","en_html":"DNS arranges all the name servers in a tree hierarchy based on their zones:","zh_html":"DNS 将所有名称服务器按照区域组织成一个树形层次结构(tree hierarchy)："},{"en":"The root server at the top level of the tree has all domains in its zone (this zone is usually written as .). Name servers at lower levels of the tree have smaller, more specific zones.","en_html":"The <strong>root server</strong> at the top level of the tree has all domains in its zone (this zone is usually written as <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.</code>). Name servers at lower levels of the tree have smaller, more specific zones.","zh_html":"位于树顶层的<strong>根服务器(root server)</strong>的区域包含所有域名(该区域通常写作<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.</code>)。树中较低层级的名称服务器拥有更小、更具体的区域。"},{"en":"DNS Lookup (Conceptual)","en_html":"<a href=\"#dns-lookup-conceptual\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dns-lookup-conceptual\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DNS Lookup (Conceptual)","zh_html":"DNS 查询(概念篇)(DNS Lookup)"},{"en":"DNS queries always start at the root. The root will direct your query to one of its children name servers. Then you make a query to the child name server, and that name server redirects you to one of its children. The process repeats until you make a query to a name server that knows the answer, which will return the IP address corresponding to your domain.","en_html":"DNS queries always start at the root. The root will direct your query to one of its children name servers. Then you make a query to the child name server, and that name server redirects you to one of its children. The process repeats until you make a query to a name server that knows the answer, which will return the IP address corresponding to your domain.","zh_html":"DNS 查询总是从根服务器开始。根服务器会将你的查询引导至其某个子名称服务器。然后你向该子名称服务器发起查询，它再将你重定向到它的某个子服务器。这个过程反复进行，直到你向某个知道答案的名称服务器发起查询，该服务器会返回你的域名对应的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"To redirect you to a child name server, the parent name server must provide the child’s zone, human-readable domain name, and IP address, so that you can contact that child name server for more information.","en_html":"To redirect you to a child name server, the parent name server must provide the child’s zone, human-readable domain name, and IP address, so that you can contact that child name server for more information.","zh_html":"为了将你重定向到一个子名称服务器，父名称服务器必须提供子服务器的区域、人类可读的域名和 IP 地址，以便你可以联系该子名称服务器获取更多信息。"},{"en":"As an example, a DNS query for eecs.berkeley.edu might have the following steps. (A comic version of this query is available at howdns.works.)","en_html":"As an example, a DNS query for <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code> might have the following steps. (A comic version of this query is available at <a href=\"https://howdns.works/\">howdns.works</a>.)","zh_html":"以查询<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>的 DNS 为例，可能包含以下步骤(该查询的漫画版可在 <a href=\"https://howdns.works/\">howdns.works</a> 查看)。"},{"en":"You to the root name server: Please tell me the IP address of eecs.berkeley.edu.","en_html":"You to the root name server: Please tell me the IP address of <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>.","zh_html":"你问根名称服务器：请告诉我<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"Root server to you: I don’t know, but I can redirect you to another name server with more information. This name server is responsible for the .edu zone. It has human-readable domain name a.edu-servers.net and IP address 192.5.6.30.","en_html":"Root server to you: I don’t know, but I can redirect you to another name server with more information. This name server is responsible for the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code> zone. It has human-readable domain name <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a.edu-servers.net</code> and IP address <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">192.5.6.30</code>.","zh_html":"根服务器回复你：我不知道，但我可以把你重定向到另一个有更多信息的名称服务器。这个名称服务器负责<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>区域。它的人类可读域名是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a.edu-servers.net</code>，IP 地址是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">192.5.6.30</code>。"},{"en":"You to the .edu name server: Please tell me the IP address of eecs.berkeley.edu.","en_html":"You to the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code> name server: Please tell me the IP address of <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>.","zh_html":"你问<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>名称服务器：请告诉我<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"The .edu name server to you: I don’t know, but I can redirect you to another name server with more information. This name server is responsible for the berkeley.edu zone. It has human-readable domain name adns1.berkeley.edu and IP address 128.32.136.3.","en_html":"The <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code> name server to you: I don’t know, but I can redirect you to another name server with more information. This name server is responsible for the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code> zone. It has human-readable domain name <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">adns1.berkeley.edu</code> and IP address <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">128.32.136.3</code>.","zh_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>名称服务器回复你：我不知道，但我可以把你重定向到另一个有更多信息的名称服务器。这个名称服务器负责<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code>区域。它的人类可读域名是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">adns1.berkeley.edu</code>，IP 地址是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">128.32.136.3</code>。"},{"en":"You to the berkeley.edu name server: Please tell me the IP address of eecs.berkeley.edu.","en_html":"You to the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code> name server: Please tell me the IP address of <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>.","zh_html":"你问<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code>名称服务器：请告诉我<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"The berkeley.edu name server to you: OK, the IP address of eecs.berkeley.edu is 23.185.0.1.","en_html":"The <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code> name server to you: OK, the IP address of <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code> is <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">23.185.0.1</code>.","zh_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code>名称服务器回复你：好的，<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>的 IP 地址是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">23.185.0.1</code>。"},{"en":"Once we get the answer, we can store it in our cache so that we don’t have to ask again if we need this record again later.","en_html":"Once we get the answer, we can store it in our cache so that we don’t have to ask again if we need this record again later.","zh_html":"一旦我们得到答案，就可以将其存储在缓存(cache)中，这样以后再次需要这条记录时就不必重新查询了。"},{"en":"Stub Resolvers and Recursive Resolvers","en_html":"<a href=\"#stub-resolvers-and-recursive-resolvers\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"stub-resolvers-and-recursive-resolvers\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Stub Resolvers and Recursive Resolvers","zh_html":"Stub 解析器(Stub Resolvers)与递归解析器(Recursive Resolvers)"},{"en":"Originally, the end host (e.g. your computer) would perform the DNS lookups, contacting each name server.","en_html":"Originally, the end host (e.g. your computer) would perform the DNS lookups, contacting each name server.","zh_html":"最初，终端主机(end host)，例如你的计算机，会自行执行 DNS 查询，依次联系各个名称服务器。"},{"en":"Today, your local computer usually delegates the task of DNS lookups to a DNS Recursive Resolver, which queries the name servers for you. When performing a lookup, the DNS Stub Resolver on your computer sends a query to the recursive resolver, lets the resolver do all the work, and receives the response back from the resolver.","en_html":"Today, your local computer usually delegates the task of DNS lookups to a <strong>DNS Recursive Resolver</strong>, which queries the name servers for you. When performing a lookup, the <strong>DNS Stub Resolver</strong> on your computer sends a query to the recursive resolver, lets the resolver do all the work, and receives the response back from the resolver.","zh_html":"如今，你的本地计算机通常将 DNS 查询的任务委托给<strong>DNS 递归解析器(DNS Recursive Resolver)</strong>，由它为你查询名称服务器。执行查询时，你计算机上的<strong>DNS Stub 解析器(DNS Stub Resolver)</strong>向递归解析器发送一条查询，让解析器完成所有后续工作，然后从解析器接收响应。"},{"en":"How do we learn the IP address of the resolver? When you first connect to the Internet, somebody can tell you the resolver address. You can also manually enter the address of a resolver you want to use.","en_html":"How do we learn the IP address of the resolver? When you first connect to the Internet, somebody can tell you the resolver address. You can also manually enter the address of a resolver you want to use.","zh_html":"我们如何获知解析器的 IP 地址？当你首次连接到互联网时，有人会告诉你解析器的地址。你也可以手动输入你希望使用的解析器地址。"},{"en":"Some well-known resolvers on the Internet include 1.1.1.1 (run by Cloudflare) and 8.8.8.8 (run by Google). They often have memorable IP addresses so we don’t have to refer to them by name. Otherwise, we’d have to do a DNS lookup to find their IP address, and the whole point of these servers is to do DNS lookups for us.","en_html":"Some well-known resolvers on the Internet include 1.1.1.1 (run by Cloudflare) and 8.8.8.8 (run by Google). They often have memorable IP addresses so we don’t have to refer to them by name. Otherwise, we’d have to do a DNS lookup to find their IP address, and the whole point of these servers is to do DNS lookups for us.","zh_html":"互联网上一些知名的解析器包括 1.1.1.1(由 Cloudflare 运营)和 8.8.8.8(由 Google 运营)。它们通常拥有易于记忆的 IP 地址，这样我们就不必用名称来查找它们。否则，我们还得先做一次 DNS 查询才能找到它们的 IP 地址，而这些服务器存在的意义恰恰就是为我们做 DNS 查询。"},{"en":"In addition to tech companies, ISPs also operate resolvers, as part of the Internet service they sell to customers. (It would be pretty bad if you paid for Internet service, but had to type IP addresses in your web browser.)","en_html":"In addition to tech companies, ISPs also operate resolvers, as part of the Internet service they sell to customers. (It would be pretty bad if you paid for Internet service, but had to type IP addresses in your web browser.)","zh_html":"除科技公司外，互联网服务提供商(ISPs)也运营解析器，作为他们向客户销售的互联网服务的一部分。(如果你支付了互联网服务费用，却不得不在浏览器中输入 IP 地址上网，那就太糟糕了。)"},{"en":"The router in your home can also act as a resolver. DNS queries can be faster if you use a router that’s physically close to you (less delay between you and the router).","en_html":"The router in your home can also act as a resolver. DNS queries can be faster if you use a router that’s physically close to you (less delay between you and the router).","zh_html":"你家里的路由器(router)也可以充当解析器。如果你使用物理上离你较近的路由器(你和路由器之间延迟更小)，DNS 查询可能会更快。"},{"en":"One major benefit of a resolver is better caching. The resolver processes queries from lots of different end hosts (not just your own computer), so it builds a much larger cache. If you ask the resolver about eecs.berkeley.edu, and somebody before you asked the resolver that same question recently, the resolver can give you the answer without contacting any additional name servers.","en_html":"One major benefit of a resolver is better caching. The resolver processes queries from lots of different end hosts (not just your own computer), so it builds a much larger cache. If you ask the resolver about <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>, and somebody before you asked the resolver that same question recently, the resolver can give you the answer without contacting any additional name servers.","zh_html":"解析器的一个主要优势在于更好的缓存(caching)。解析器处理来自许多不同终端主机(不仅仅是你的计算机)的查询，因此它构建了更大的缓存。如果你向解析器查询<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>，而之前已经有人问过相同的问题，解析器就可以直接返回答案，无需再联系其他名称服务器。"},{"en":"Note that even though recursive resolvers store larger caches, the stub resolver can still maintain its own separate cache. Some queries can get answered by the stub resolver’s cache, without even asking the recursive resolver.","en_html":"Note that even though recursive resolvers store larger caches, the stub resolver can still maintain its own separate cache. Some queries can get answered by the stub resolver’s cache, without even asking the recursive resolver.","zh_html":"注意，即使递归解析器存储了更大的缓存，stub 解析器仍然可以维护自己的独立缓存。一些查询可以直接由 stub 解析器的缓存回答，甚至不需要询问递归解析器。"},{"en":"Redundancy","en_html":"<a href=\"#redundancy\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"redundancy\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Redundancy","zh_html":"冗余(Redundancy)"},{"en":"So far, we’ve been talking about “the berkeley.edu name server,” but in reality, each zone has multiple name servers. All the name servers for a zone are functionally identical, and each name server can answer any query in the zone.","en_html":"So far, we’ve been talking about “the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code> name server,” but in reality, each zone has multiple name servers. All the name servers for a zone are functionally identical, and each name server can answer any query in the zone.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们一直在谈论\"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code>名称服务器\"，但现实中，每个区域都有多个名称服务器。同一区域的所有名称服务器在功能上是相同的，每台名称服务器都可以回答该区域内的任何查询。"},{"en":"This ensures that we have availability for that zone, and if one name server goes down, the others can keep answering queries for that zone. By convention, zones are required to have two name servers, though in practice, most zones will have at least three.","en_html":"This ensures that we have availability for that zone, and if one name server goes down, the others can keep answering queries for that zone. By convention, zones are required to have two name servers, though in practice, most zones will have at least three.","zh_html":"这确保了该区域的高可用性(availability)：如果一台名称服务器宕机，其他服务器可以继续回答该区域的查询。按照惯例，每个区域至少需要两台名称服务器，不过实践中大多数区域至少有三台。"},{"en":"Usually, one of the name servers is designated as the primary server that actually manages the zone. The rest of the servers are secondary mirror servers that just store and serve a copy of the information on the primary server.","en_html":"Usually, one of the name servers is designated as the primary server that actually manages the zone. The rest of the servers are secondary mirror servers that just store and serve a copy of the information on the primary server.","zh_html":"通常，其中一台名称服务器被指定为主服务器(primary server)，实际管理该区域。其余服务器是辅助镜像服务器(secondary mirror servers)，仅存储和提供主服务器上信息的副本。"},{"en":"Now, in the DNS lookup, name servers might reply to you with: “I don’t know, but you should ask the .edu zone. This zone has 13 name servers. Here are the domains and IP addresses for each of them.” Then, you can pick which of the name servers to contact next.","en_html":"Now, in the DNS lookup, name servers might reply to you with: “I don’t know, but you should ask the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code> zone. This zone has 13 name servers. Here are the domains and IP addresses for each of them.” Then, you can pick which of the name servers to contact next.","zh_html":"现在，在 DNS 查询中，名称服务器可能会回复你：\"我不知道，但你应该询问<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>区域。该区域有 13 台名称服务器，以下是它们的域名和 IP 地址。\"然后，你可以选择下一步联系哪台名称服务器。"},{"en":"DNS APIs","en_html":"<a href=\"#dns-apis\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dns-apis\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DNS APIs","zh_html":"<a href=\"#dns-apis\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dns-apis\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DNS API（DNS编程接口）"},{"en":"Now that we have a conceptual picture of DNS, let’s see how it’s actually implemented.","en_html":"Now that we have a conceptual picture of DNS, let’s see how it’s actually implemented.","zh_html":"现在我们对 DNS 有了概念性的了解，接下来看看它是如何实际实现的。"},{"en":"For starters, how do programmers use DNS to perform lookups?","en_html":"For starters, how do programmers use DNS to perform lookups?","zh_html":"首先，程序员如何使用 DNS 执行查询？"},{"en":"There are relatively simple, common, and stable stable APIs to perform DNS lookups. The API is fairly similar across different languages.","en_html":"There are relatively simple, common, and stable stable APIs to perform DNS lookups. The API is fairly similar across different languages.","zh_html":"有相对简单、通用且稳定的 API 来执行 DNS 查询。这些 API 在不同编程语言之间相当相似。"},{"en":"In the standard C library, gethostbyname(\"foo.com\") can be used to look up the IP address corresponding to foo.com. This function is limited to IPv4, though, so it’s now deprecated (though you’ll still see it used).","en_html":"In the standard C library, <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">gethostbyname(\"foo.com\")</code> can be used to look up the IP address corresponding to <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">foo.com</code>. This function is limited to IPv4, though, so it’s now deprecated (though you’ll still see it used).","zh_html":"在标准 C 库中，<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">gethostbyname(\"foo.com\")</code>可用于查找<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">foo.com</code>对应的 IP 地址。不过，该函数仅限于 IPv4，因此现已弃用(但你仍会看到有人使用它)。"},{"en":"The updated version, also in the standard C library, is getaddrinfo(\"example.com\", NULL, NULL, &amp;result), which looks up the IP address of example.com and stores the answer in the result struct. Don’t worry too much about the specifics or the extra arguments (set to null here). This replacement API supports more than IPv4 (e.g. IPv6).","en_html":"The updated version, also in the standard C library, is <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">getaddrinfo(\"example.com\", NULL, NULL, &amp;result)</code>, which looks up the IP address of <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">example.com</code> and stores the answer in the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">result</code> struct. Don’t worry too much about the specifics or the extra arguments (set to null here). This replacement API supports more than IPv4 (e.g. IPv6).","zh_html":"更新的版本也在标准 C 库中，即<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">getaddrinfo(\"example.com\", NULL, NULL, &amp;result)</code>，它查找<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">example.com</code>的 IP 地址并将答案存储在<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">result</code>结构体中。不必过于关心具体细节或额外参数(这里设为 null)。这个替代 API 支持 IPv4 之外的其他协议(例如 IPv6)。"},{"en":"As a programmer, you don’t need to worry about DNS complexities like recursive resolvers or specific name servers. You can just call a standard library function. These functions usually make requests to the stub resolver in your operating system.","en_html":"As a programmer, you don’t need to worry about DNS complexities like recursive resolvers or specific name servers. You can just call a standard library function. These functions usually make requests to the stub resolver in your operating system.","zh_html":"作为程序员，你不需要担心 DNS 的复杂性，如递归解析器或特定的名称服务器。你只需调用一个标准库函数即可。这些函数通常向操作系统中的 stub 解析器发起请求。"},{"en":"DNS uses UDP, Not TCP","en_html":"<a href=\"#dns-uses-udp-not-tcp\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dns-uses-udp-not-tcp\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DNS uses UDP, Not TCP","zh_html":"DNS 使用 UDP，而非 TCP"},{"en":"Fundamentally, DNS is a client-server protocol. One person (the client) sends a question, and the other person (the server) sends the answer. The client is usually a user host or recursive resolver, and the server is usually a name server. How should the client and server format their messages?","en_html":"Fundamentally, DNS is a client-server protocol. One person (the client) sends a question, and the other person (the server) sends the answer. The client is usually a user host or recursive resolver, and the server is usually a name server. How should the client and server format their messages?","zh_html":"从根本上说，DNS 是一个客户端-服务器(client-server)协议。一方(客户端)发送问题，另一方(服务器)发送答案。客户端通常是用户主机或递归解析器，服务器通常是名称服务器。客户端和服务器应如何格式化它们的消息？"},{"en":"DNS uses UDP (best-effort packets, no TCP handshakes) to make DNS lightweight and fast. We don’t have to wait for a 3-way TCP handshake to complete. We don’t care about packets arriving in order, because queries and responses often fit into a single UDP packet.","en_html":"DNS uses UDP (best-effort packets, no TCP handshakes) to make DNS lightweight and fast. We don’t have to wait for a 3-way TCP handshake to complete. We don’t care about packets arriving in order, because queries and responses often fit into a single UDP packet.","zh_html":"DNS 使用 UDP(尽力而为的数据包，无需 TCP 握手)来保持轻量和快速。我们不必等待三次 TCP 握手完成。我们也不关心数据包是否按顺序到达，因为查询和响应通常都适合放在单个 UDP 数据包中。"},{"en":"With UDP, servers don’t need to keep state per connection (contrast with TCP, where the server has to maintain a buffer). Every packet can be processed independently. This also helps keep DNS lightweight, since name servers receive lots of requests, and opening a new connection for each one would be expensive.","en_html":"With UDP, servers don’t need to keep state per connection (contrast with TCP, where the server has to maintain a buffer). Every packet can be processed independently. This also helps keep DNS lightweight, since name servers receive lots of requests, and opening a new connection for each one would be expensive.","zh_html":"使用 UDP 时，服务器不需要维护每连接的状态(这与 TCP 不同，TCP 中服务器必须维护缓冲区)。每个数据包可以独立处理。这也有助于保持 DNS 轻量，因为名称服务器会收到大量请求，为每个请求都建立新连接的开销太大。"},{"en":"How do we deal with UDP being unreliable and packets being dropped? We can solve this with a simple retry mechanism. If you don’t get a reply within some time limit, send the query again. The timeout varies between operating systems, though it can be fairly slow.","en_html":"How do we deal with UDP being unreliable and packets being dropped? We can solve this with a simple retry mechanism. If you don’t get a reply within some time limit, send the query again. The timeout varies between operating systems, though it can be fairly slow.","zh_html":"如何处理 UDP 不可靠以及数据包丢失的问题？我们可以用简单的重试机制来解决。如果在某个时间限制内没有收到回复，就重新发送查询。超时时间因操作系统而异，但可能相当慢。"},{"en":"UDP being unreliable and timeouts being slow is why it’s important to have a resolver that’s nearby and can be contacted reliably. For example, a resolver in your home router is close to you, and probably pretty reliable (not too much congestion in your home network). You can also improve reliability by having multiple backup resolvers (e.g. home router and 8.8.8.8).","en_html":"UDP being unreliable and timeouts being slow is why it’s important to have a resolver that’s nearby and can be contacted reliably. For example, a resolver in your home router is close to you, and probably pretty reliable (not too much congestion in your home network). You can also improve reliability by having multiple backup resolvers (e.g. home router and 8.8.8.8).","zh_html":"UDP 的不可靠性和超时的缓慢正是为什么拥有一个附近且可以可靠联系的解析器很重要。例如，你家路由器中的解析器离你很近，而且可能相当可靠(家庭网络中的拥塞程度不高)。你还可以通过设置多个备用解析器(例如家庭路由器和 8.8.8.8)来提高可靠性。"},{"en":"As we mentioned, DNS queries and responses usually fit into a single packet. One notable exception is when we transfer a zone between a primary name server and secondary name servers. The secondary name server has to say: Give me all your records, so that I can help you serve them. The response is probably going to be very big, so these transfers are often done over TCP.","en_html":"As we mentioned, DNS queries and responses usually fit into a single packet. One notable exception is when we transfer a zone between a primary name server and secondary name servers. The secondary name server has to say: Give me all your records, so that I can help you serve them. The response is probably going to be very big, so these transfers are often done over TCP.","zh_html":"正如我们提到的，DNS 查询和响应通常适合单个数据包。一个显著的例外是在主名称服务器和辅助名称服务器之间传输区域(zone transfer)时。辅助名称服务器需要说：把你所有的记录都给我，这样我就可以帮你提供服务。响应可能会非常大，因此这些传输通常通过 TCP 完成。"},{"en":"Recent advances in DNS have added security features (e.g. stop an attacker from changing the records in flight), which might also require switching from UDP to TCP.","en_html":"Recent advances in DNS have added security features (e.g. stop an attacker from changing the records in flight), which might also require switching from UDP to TCP.","zh_html":"DNS 的最新进展增加了安全功能(例如阻止攻击者在传输中篡改记录)，这也可能需要从 UDP 切换到 TCP。"},{"en":"Recall that UDP implements ports to support multiple applications on a single server. By convention, all name servers listen for DNS queries on UDP port 53. This port number is well-known and constant so that everybody can contact the correct port on name servers.","en_html":"Recall that UDP implements ports to support multiple applications on a single server. By convention, all name servers listen for DNS queries on UDP port 53. This port number is well-known and constant so that everybody can contact the correct port on name servers.","zh_html":"回顾一下，UDP 通过端口(ports)来支持单台服务器上的多个应用。按照惯例，所有名称服务器都在 UDP 端口 53 监听 DNS 查询。这个端口号是广为人知且固定不变的，这样所有人都能联系到名称服务器上的正确端口。"},{"en":"DNS Message Format","en_html":"<a href=\"#dns-message-format\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dns-message-format\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DNS Message Format","zh_html":"DNS 消息格式(DNS Message Format)"},{"en":"The first field is a 16 bit identification field that is randomly selected per query and used to match requests to responses. When a DNS query is sent, the ID field is filled with random bits. Since UDP is stateless, the DNS response must send back the same bits in the ID field so that the original query sender knows which DNS query the response corresponds to.","en_html":"The first field is a 16 bit <strong>identification field</strong> that is randomly selected per query and used to match requests to responses. When a DNS query is sent, the ID field is filled with random bits. Since UDP is stateless, the DNS response must send back the same bits in the ID field so that the original query sender knows which DNS query the response corresponds to.","zh_html":"第一个字段是 16 位的<strong>标识字段(identification field)</strong>，每个查询随机选择，用于匹配请求和响应。发送 DNS 查询时，ID 字段填入随机位。由于 UDP 是无状态的，DNS 响应必须在 ID 字段中发回相同的位，这样原始查询的发送者才知道该响应对应哪个 DNS 查询。"},{"en":"The next 16 bits are reserved for flags. The QR bit specifies whether the message is a query (bit is 0) or a response (bit is 1). The RD bit indicates whether you want the resolver to perform a recursive lookup, or just return whatever the name server says (even if it’s “I don’t know”).","en_html":"The next 16 bits are reserved for flags. The QR bit specifies whether the message is a query (bit is 0) or a response (bit is 1). The RD bit indicates whether you want the resolver to perform a recursive lookup, or just return whatever the name server says (even if it’s “I don’t know”).","zh_html":"接下来的 16 位保留给标志(flags)。QR 位指定消息是查询(位为 0)还是响应(位为 1)。RD 位指示你是否希望解析器执行递归查询(recursive lookup)，还是只返回名称服务器的回答(即使回答是\"我不知道\")。"},{"en":"Theoretically, you can specify a query type in the flags, though the IQUERY type is obsolete, and the STATUS type is not really defined, so the QUERY type is used for basically everything.","en_html":"Theoretically, you can specify a query type in the flags, though the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">IQUERY</code> type is obsolete, and the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">STATUS</code> type is not really defined, so the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">QUERY</code> type is used for basically everything.","zh_html":"理论上，你可以在标志中指定查询类型，不过<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">IQUERY</code>类型已废弃，<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">STATUS</code>类型没有得到真正定义，因此基本上所有情况都使用<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">QUERY</code>类型。"},{"en":"The flags can also specify whether the query was successful (e.g. the NOERROR flag is set in the reply if the query succeeded, the NXDOMAIN flag is set in the reply if the query asked about a non-existent name).","en_html":"The flags can also specify whether the query was successful (e.g. the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">NOERROR</code> flag is set in the reply if the query succeeded, the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">NXDOMAIN</code> flag is set in the reply if the query asked about a non-existent name).","zh_html":"标志还可以指定查询是否成功(例如，查询成功时回复中设置<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">NOERROR</code>标志，查询的名称不存在时回复中设置<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">NXDOMAIN</code>标志)。"},{"en":"The next field specifies the number of questions asked (in practice, this is always 1). The three fields after that are used in response messages and specify the number of resource records (RRs) contained in the message. We’ll describe each of these categories of RRs in depth later.","en_html":"The next field specifies the number of questions asked (in practice, this is always 1). The three fields after that are used in response messages and specify the number of <strong>resource records</strong> (RRs) contained in the message. We’ll describe each of these categories of RRs in depth later.","zh_html":"下一个字段指定提出的问题数量(实践中总是 1)。后面的三个字段在响应消息中使用，指定消息中包含的资源记录(resource records, RRs)数量。我们稍后将深入介绍每类 RR。"},{"en":"The rest of the message contains the actual content of the DNS query/response. This content is always structured as a set of RRs, where each RR is a key-value pair with an associated type.","en_html":"The rest of the message contains the actual content of the DNS query/response. This content is always structured as a set of RRs, where each RR is a key-value pair with an associated type.","zh_html":"消息的其余部分包含 DNS 查询/响应的实际内容。这些内容始终以一组 RR 的形式组织，每个 RR 是一个带有类型(type)的键值对(key-value pair)。"},{"en":"For completeness, a DNS record key is formally defined as a 3-tuple &lt;Name, Class, Type&gt;, where Name is the actual key data, Class is always IN for Internet (except for special queries used to get information about DNS itself), and Type specifies the record type. A DNS record value contains &lt;TTL, Value&gt;, where TTL is the time-to-live (how long, in seconds, the record can be cached), and Value is the actual value data.","en_html":"For completeness, a DNS record key is formally defined as a 3-tuple <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">&lt;Name, Class, Type&gt;</code>, where <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">Name</code> is the actual key data, <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">Class</code> is always <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">IN</code> for Internet (except for special queries used to get information about DNS itself), and <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">Type</code> specifies the record type. A DNS record value contains <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">&lt;TTL, Value&gt;</code>, where <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">TTL</code> is the time-to-live (how long, in seconds, the record can be cached), and <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">Value</code> is the actual value data.","zh_html":"完整来说，DNS 记录键(key)正式定义为三元组<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">&lt;Name, Class, Type&gt;</code>，其中<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">Name</code>是实际的键数据，<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">Class</code>对于互联网总是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">IN</code>(除了用于获取 DNS 自身信息的特殊查询)，<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">Type</code>指定记录类型。DNS 记录的值(value)包含<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">&lt;TTL, Value&gt;</code>，其中<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">TTL</code>是生存时间(time-to-live)，以秒为单位指定记录可被缓存的时间，<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">Value</code>是实际的值数据。"},{"en":"There are three main types of records in DNS. A type records map domains to IPv4 addresses. The key is a domain, and the value is an IP address. Similarly, AAAA type records map domains to IPv6 addresses. NS type records map zones to domains. The key is a zone, and the value is a domain.","en_html":"There are three main types of records in DNS. <strong>A type records</strong> map domains to IPv4 addresses. The key is a domain, and the value is an IP address. Similarly, <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">AAAA type records</code> map domains to IPv6 addresses. <strong>NS type records</strong> map zones to domains. The key is a zone, and the value is a domain.","zh_html":"DNS 中有三种主要的记录类型。<strong>A 类型记录(A type records)</strong>将域名映射到 IPv4 地址。键是域名，值是 IP 地址。类似地，<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">AAAA 类型记录</code>将域名映射到 IPv6 地址。<strong>NS 类型记录(NS type records)</strong>将区域映射到域名。键是区域，值是域名。"},{"en":"You might sometimes encounter a CNAME type record, which is used for aliasing or redirecting. The name and value are both domains, and the record indicates that both domains have the same IP address.","en_html":"You might sometimes encounter a <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">CNAME</code> type record, which is used for aliasing or redirecting. The name and value are both domains, and the record indicates that both domains have the same IP address.","zh_html":"你有时可能遇到<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">CNAME</code>类型记录，用于别名(aliasing)或重定向。名称和值都是域名，该记录表明两个域名具有相同的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"Important takeaways from this section: Each DNS packet has a 16-bit random ID field, some metadata, and a set of resource records. Each record falls into one of four categories (question, answer, authority, additional), and each record contains a type, a key, and a value. There are A type records and NS type records.","en_html":"Important takeaways from this section: Each DNS packet has a 16-bit random ID field, some metadata, and a set of resource records. Each record falls into one of four categories (question, answer, authority, additional), and each record contains a type, a key, and a value. There are A type records and NS type records.","zh_html":"本节的重要要点：每个 DNS 数据包都有一个 16 位随机 ID 字段、一些元数据(metadata)和一组资源记录。每条记录属于四个类别之一(问题、回答、授权、附加)，每条记录包含类型、键和值。存在 A 类型记录和 NS 类型记录。"},{"en":"DNS Lookup (Implementation)","en_html":"<a href=\"#dns-lookup-implementation\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dns-lookup-implementation\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DNS Lookup (Implementation)","zh_html":"DNS 查询(实现篇)(DNS Lookup)"},{"en":"Now, let’s walk through a real DNS query for the IP address of eecs.berkeley.edu. You can try this at home with the dig utility – remember to set the +norecurse flag so you can unravel the recursion yourself.","en_html":"Now, let’s walk through a real DNS query for the IP address of <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>. You can try this at home with the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dig_(command)\"><code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">dig</code> utility</a> – remember to set the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">+norecurse</code> flag so you can unravel the recursion yourself.","zh_html":"现在，我们逐步走一遍真实的 DNS 查询过程，以查询<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>的 IP 地址为例。你可以在家使用 <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dig_(command)\"><code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">dig</code> 工具</a> 自行尝试——记得设置<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">+norecurse</code>标志，这样你可以自己展开递归过程。"},{"en":"Every DNS query begins with the root server. The names and IP addresses of the root servers are usually hardcoded into resolvers, in the form of a root hints file. Here’s a root hints file if you’re curious: https://www.internic.net/domain/named.root.","en_html":"Every DNS query begins with the root server. The names and IP addresses of the root servers are usually hardcoded into resolvers, in the form of a root hints file. Here’s a root hints file if you’re curious: <a href=\"https://www.internic.net/domain/named.root\">https://www.internic.net/domain/named.root</a>.","zh_html":"每次 DNS 查询都从根服务器开始。根服务器的名称和 IP 地址通常以根提示文件(root hints file)的形式硬编码在解析器中。如果你感兴趣，可以查看根提示文件：<a href=\"https://www.internic.net/domain/named.root\">https://www.internic.net/domain/named.root</a>。"},{"en":"The first root server has domain a.root-servers.net and IP address 198.41.0.4. We can use dig to send a DNS request to this address, asking for the IP address of eecs.berkeley.edu.","en_html":"The first root server has domain <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a.root-servers.net</code> and IP address <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">198.41.0.4</code>. We can use <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">dig</code> to send a DNS request to this address, asking for the IP address of <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>.","zh_html":"第一个根服务器域名为<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a.root-servers.net</code>，IP 地址为<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">198.41.0.4</code>。我们可以使用<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">dig</code>向该地址发送 DNS 请求，询问<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"In the first section of the answer, we can see the header information, including the ID field (26114), the return flags (NOERROR), and the number of records returned in each section.","en_html":"In the first section of the answer, we can see the header information, including the ID field (<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">26114</code>), the return flags (<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">NOERROR</code>), and the number of records returned in each section.","zh_html":"在答案的第一部分，我们可以看到头部(header)信息，包括 ID 字段(<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">26114</code>)、返回标志(<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">NOERROR</code>)以及每个部分返回的记录数。"},{"en":"The question section contains 1 record (you can verify by seeing QUERY: 1 in the header). It has key eecs.berkeley.edu, type A, and a blank value. This represents the domain we queried for (the value is blank because we don’t know the corresponding IP address).","en_html":"The <strong>question section</strong> contains 1 record (you can verify by seeing <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">QUERY: 1</code> in the header). It has key <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>, type <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code>, and a blank value. This represents the domain we queried for (the value is blank because we don’t know the corresponding IP address).","zh_html":"<strong>问题部分(question section)</strong>包含 1 条记录(你可以通过头部中的<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">QUERY: 1</code>确认)。它包含键<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>、类型<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code>和一个空白值。这代表我们查询的域名(值为空是因为我们不知道对应的 IP 地址)。"},{"en":"The answer section is blank (ANSWER: 0 in the header), because the root server didn’t provide a direct answer to our query.","en_html":"The <strong>answer section</strong> is blank (<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">ANSWER: 0</code> in the header), because the root server didn’t provide a direct answer to our query.","zh_html":"<strong>回答部分(answer section)</strong>是空白的(头部显示<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">ANSWER: 0</code>)，因为根服务器没有对我们的查询提供直接答案。"},{"en":"The authority section contains 13 records. The first one has key .edu, type NS, and value a.edu-servers.net. This is the root server giving us the zone and the domain name of the next name servers we could contact. Each record in this section corresponds to a potential name server we could ask next.","en_html":"The <strong>authority section</strong> contains 13 records. The first one has key <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>, type <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">NS</code>, and value <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a.edu-servers.net</code>. This is the root server giving us the zone and the domain name of the next name servers we could contact. Each record in this section corresponds to a potential name server we could ask next.","zh_html":"<strong>授权部分(authority section)</strong>包含 13 条记录。第一条记录的键是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>，类型是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">NS</code>，值是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a.edu-servers.net</code>。这是根服务器在给我们提供下一个名称服务器的区域和域名。该部分的每条记录都对应一个我们可以下一步询问的名称服务器。"},{"en":"Notes: Usually, the server with the earliest name (alphabetically or numerically) is the primary server (here, a.edu-servers.net), and the rest are mirrors. Also, note that it’s okay that there are multiple records with the same name (.edu). This just tells us there are multiple name servers that can all answer queries for this zone.","en_html":"Notes: Usually, the server with the earliest name (alphabetically or numerically) is the primary server (here, <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a.edu-servers.net</code>), and the rest are mirrors. Also, note that it’s okay that there are multiple records with the same name (<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>). This just tells us there are multiple name servers that can all answer queries for this zone.","zh_html":"注：通常，名称最早(按字母或数字顺序)的服务器是主服务器(这里是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a.edu-servers.net</code>)，其余的是镜像。另外请注意，多条记录具有相同的名称(<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>)是正常的。这只是告诉我们有多个名称服务器都可以回答该区域的查询。"},{"en":"The additional section contains 27 records. The first one has key a.edu-servers.net, type A, and value 192.5.6.30. This is the root server giving us the IP address of the next name server by mapping a domain from the authority section to an IP address.","en_html":"The <strong>additional section</strong> contains 27 records. The first one has key <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a.edu-servers.net</code>, type <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code>, and value <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">192.5.6.30</code>. This is the root server giving us the IP address of the next name server by mapping a domain from the authority section to an IP address.","zh_html":"<strong>附加部分(additional section)</strong>包含 27 条记录。第一条记录的键是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a.edu-servers.net</code>，类型是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code>，值是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">192.5.6.30</code>。这是根服务器通过将授权部分的域名映射到 IP 地址，为我们提供下一个名称服务器的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"Together, the authority section and additional section combined give us the zone, domain name, and IP address of the next name server. This information is spread across two sections to maintain the key-value structure of the DNS message.","en_html":"Together, the authority section and additional section combined give us the zone, domain name, and IP address of the next name server. This information is spread across two sections to maintain the key-value structure of the DNS message.","zh_html":"授权部分和附加部分一起，为我们提供了下一个名称服务器的区域、域名和 IP 地址。这些信息分布在两个部分中，是为了保持 DNS 消息的键值结构。"},{"en":"For completeness: 172800 is the TTL (time-to-live) for each record, set at 172,800 seconds = 48 hours here. The IN is the Internet class and can basically be ignored. Sometimes you will see records of type AAAA, which correspond to IPv6 addresses (the usual A type records correspond to IPv4 addresses).","en_html":"For completeness: <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">172800</code> is the TTL (time-to-live) for each record, set at 172,800 seconds = 48 hours here. The <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">IN</code> is the Internet class and can basically be ignored. Sometimes you will see records of type <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">AAAA</code>, which correspond to IPv6 addresses (the usual <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code> type records correspond to IPv4 addresses).","zh_html":"完整起见：<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">172800</code>是每条记录的 TTL(生存时间)，这里设为 172,800 秒 = 48 小时。<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">IN</code>是互联网类(Internet class)，基本上可以忽略。有时你会看到类型为<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">AAAA</code>的记录，对应 IPv6 地址(通常的<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code>类型记录对应 IPv4 地址)。"},{"en":"Sanity check: What name server do we query next? How do we know where that name server is located? What do we query that name server for?\\footnote{Query a.edu-servers.net, whose location we know because of the records in the additional section. Query for the IP address of eecs.berkeley.edu just like before.}","en_html":"Sanity check: What name server do we query next? How do we know where that name server is located? What do we query that name server for?\\footnote{Query <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a.edu-servers.net</code>, whose location we know because of the records in the additional section. Query for the IP address of <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code> just like before.}","zh_html":"思考题：接下来我们应该查询哪个名称服务器？如何知道该名称服务器的位置？我们应该向该名称服务器查询什么？\\footnote{查询<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a.edu-servers.net</code>，它的位置由附加部分的记录给出。和之前一样，查询<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>的 IP 地址。}"},{"en":"The next query also has an empty answer section, with NS records in the authority section and A records in the additional section which give us the domains and IP addresses of name servers responsible for the berkeley.edu zone.","en_html":"The next query also has an empty answer section, with <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">NS</code> records in the authority section and <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code> records in the additional section which give us the domains and IP addresses of name servers responsible for the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code> zone.","zh_html":"下一次查询的回答部分也是空的，授权部分有<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">NS</code>记录，附加部分有<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code>记录，它们给出了负责<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code>区域的名称服务器的域名和 IP 地址。"},{"en":"Finally, the last query gives us the IP address corresponding to eecs.berkeley.edu in the form of a single A type record in the answer section.","en_html":"Finally, the last query gives us the IP address corresponding to <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code> in the form of a single <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code> type record in the answer section.","zh_html":"最后一次查询在回答部分以单个<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code>类型记录的形式，给出了<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>对应的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"In practice, because the recursive resolver caches as many answers as possible, most queries can skip the first few steps and used cached records instead of asking root servers and high-level name servers like .edu every time. Caching helps speed up DNS, because fewer packets need to be sent across the network to translate a domain name to an IP address. Caching also helps reduce request load on the highest-level name servers.","en_html":"In practice, because the recursive resolver caches as many answers as possible, most queries can skip the first few steps and used cached records instead of asking root servers and high-level name servers like <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code> every time. Caching helps speed up DNS, because fewer packets need to be sent across the network to translate a domain name to an IP address. Caching also helps reduce request load on the highest-level name servers.","zh_html":"在实践中，由于递归解析器会尽可能多地缓存答案，大多数查询可以跳过前几步，使用缓存的记录，而不必每次都询问根服务器和像<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>这样的高层级名称服务器。缓存有助于加速 DNS，因为将域名翻译为 IP 地址需要在网络上发送的数据包更少。缓存还有助于减少最高层级名称服务器上的请求负载。"},{"en":"Now that we know how DNS is implemented to support queries, we can look at how DNS actually works in practice, and explore the real-world business side of DNS.","en_html":"Now that we know how DNS is implemented to support queries, we can look at how DNS actually works in practice, and explore the real-world business side of DNS.","zh_html":"现在我们知道了 DNS 如何实现查询功能，接下来可以看看 DNS 在实践中是如何工作的，并探索 DNS 在现实世界中的商业层面。"},{"en":"DNS Authority Hierarchy","en_html":"<a href=\"#dns-authority-hierarchy\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dns-authority-hierarchy\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DNS Authority Hierarchy","zh_html":"DNS 授权层次结构(DNS Authority Hierarchy)"},{"en":"The tree hierarchy that we’ve drawn actually represents three different ways in which DNS is hierarchical.","en_html":"The tree hierarchy that we’ve drawn actually represents three different ways in which DNS is hierarchical.","zh_html":"我们绘制的树形层次结构实际上代表了 DNS 在三个不同方面的层次性。"},{"en":"As we’ve already seen, DNS names are hierarchical. This is why our domain names, like eecs.berkeley.edu, are multiple words separated by dots.","en_html":"As we’ve already seen, DNS names are hierarchical. This is why our domain names, like <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>, are multiple words separated by dots.","zh_html":"正如我们已经看到的，DNS 名称是层次化的。这就是我们的域名(如<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>)是由点分隔的多个单词组成的原因。"},{"en":"We’ve also seen that the infrastructure of DNS is hierarchical. We can organize name servers into the tree hierarchy, where each name server only knows about its own part of the tree.","en_html":"We’ve also seen that the infrastructure of DNS is hierarchical. We can organize name servers into the tree hierarchy, where each name server only knows about its own part of the tree.","zh_html":"我们还看到 DNS 的基础设施是层次化的。我们可以将名称服务器组织成树形层次结构，每个名称服务器只了解树中自己所属的部分。"},{"en":"The third hierarchy that we’ll introduce is authority. This tells us who defines the names that exist in the tree. For example, the organization that operates the .edu name server is responsible for all the domains in the .edu zone. The .edu organization can then delegate authority for parts of its zone to subordinates in the tree.","en_html":"The third hierarchy that we’ll introduce is authority. This tells us who defines the names that exist in the tree. For example, the organization that operates the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code> name server is responsible for all the domains in the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code> zone. The <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code> organization can then delegate authority for parts of its zone to subordinates in the tree.","zh_html":"我们将介绍的第三个层次是授权(authority)。它告诉我们谁定义了树中存在的名称。例如，运营<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>名称服务器的组织负责<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>区域中的所有域名。<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>组织可以将其区域的一部分授权委托给树中的下级。"},{"en":"For example, the .edu organization can say: berkeley.edu (and all subdomains) is in my zone, and I will transfer control of this part of my zone to the UC Berkeley organization. Now, we’ve created a new zone owned by UC Berkeley, and the .edu organization doesn’t need to be aware of updates in this new berkeley.edu zone. UC Berkeley has the authority to create new domains in its zone, or perhaps delegate some parts of its zone to further sub-organizations.","en_html":"For example, the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code> organization can say: <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code> (and all subdomains) is in my zone, and I will transfer control of this part of my zone to the UC Berkeley organization. Now, we’ve created a new zone owned by UC Berkeley, and the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code> organization doesn’t need to be aware of updates in this new <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code> zone. UC Berkeley has the authority to create new domains in its zone, or perhaps delegate some parts of its zone to further sub-organizations.","zh_html":"例如，<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>组织可以说：<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code>(及其所有子域名)在我的区域中，我将把该部分区域的控制权移交给 UC Berkeley 组织。现在，我们创建了一个由 UC Berkeley 拥有的新区域，<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">.edu</code>组织不需要了解这个新的<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code>区域中的更新。UC Berkeley 有权在其区域中创建新的域名，或者将其区域的某些部分进一步委托给下级子组织。"},{"en":"When we draw this tree with all three hierarchies in mind, we can be more precise and say that each node represents a zone. A zone is formally defined as an administrative authority responsible for some part of the hierarchy.","en_html":"When we draw this tree with all three hierarchies in mind, we can be more precise and say that each node represents a zone. A zone is formally defined as an administrative authority responsible for some part of the hierarchy.","zh_html":"当我们把三个层次放在一起考虑来绘制这棵树时，可以更精确地说每个节点代表一个区域(zone)。一个区域正式定义为一个负责层次结构中某部分的行政授权实体(administrative authority)。"},{"en":"From the naming perspective, each zone contains one or more name-IP mappings, where the names are subdomains of that zone. The eecs.berkeley.edu zone can contain the name eecs.berkeley.edu or the name iris.eecs.berkeley.edu, but cannot contain the name math.berkeley.edu.","en_html":"From the naming perspective, each zone contains one or more name-IP mappings, where the names are subdomains of that zone. The <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code> zone can contain the name <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code> or the name <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">iris.eecs.berkeley.edu</code>, but cannot contain the name <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">math.berkeley.edu</code>.","zh_html":"从名称角度来看，每个区域包含一个或多个名称到 IP 的映射，其中名称是该区域的子域名。<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>区域可以包含名称<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>或<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">iris.eecs.berkeley.edu</code>，但不能包含名称<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">math.berkeley.edu</code>。"},{"en":"From the infrastructure perspective, that zone is supported by one or more name servers answering queries about the domains in that zone.","en_html":"From the infrastructure perspective, that zone is supported by one or more name servers answering queries about the domains in that zone.","zh_html":"从基础设施角度来看，该区域由一台或多台名称服务器支持，这些服务器回答关于该区域中域名的查询。"},{"en":"From the authority perspective, the zone is controlled by some organization, who was given authority by its parent to manage the names in that zone. For example, UC Berkeley, who controls the berkeley.edu zone, can delegate the eecs.berkeley.edu zone to the EECS department.","en_html":"From the authority perspective, the zone is controlled by some organization, who was given authority by its parent to manage the names in that zone. For example, UC Berkeley, who controls the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code> zone, can delegate the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code> zone to the EECS department.","zh_html":"从授权角度来看，该区域由某个组织控制，该组织从其父级获得授权来管理该区域中的名称。例如，控制<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code>区域的 UC Berkeley，可以将<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">eecs.berkeley.edu</code>区域委托给 EECS 系。"},{"en":"DNS Zones in Practice","en_html":"<a href=\"#dns-zones-in-practice\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dns-zones-in-practice\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DNS Zones in Practice","zh_html":"现实中的 DNS 区域(DNS Zones in Practice)"},{"en":"Who are these organizations in real life?","en_html":"Who are these organizations in real life?","zh_html":"这些组织在现实中是谁？"},{"en":"The root zone is controlled by ICANN (Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers). They are responsible for delegating parts of the root zone (which represents the entire Internet) to specific top-level domains.","en_html":"The root zone is controlled by ICANN (Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers). They are responsible for delegating parts of the root zone (which represents the entire Internet) to specific top-level domains.","zh_html":"根区域由 ICANN(互联网名称与数字地址分配机构，Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers)控制。他们负责将根区域(代表整个互联网)的部分授权给特定的顶级域名(top-level domains)。"},{"en":"All of the zones directly beneath the root in the tree are top-level domains (TLDs). The Internet was originally developed in the US, so the earliest TLDs split the Internet into zones based on purpose, such as .com (commercial), .gov (government), .edu (education), and .mil (military). Eventually, TLDs for countries were created, such as .fr (France) and .jp (Japan).","en_html":"All of the zones directly beneath the root in the tree are <strong>top-level domains (TLDs)</strong>. The Internet was originally developed in the US, so the earliest TLDs split the Internet into zones based on purpose, such as .com (commercial), .gov (government), .edu (education), and .mil (military). Eventually, TLDs for countries were created, such as .fr (France) and .jp (Japan).","zh_html":"根之下直接位于树中的区域都是<strong>顶级域名(top-level domains, TLDs)</strong>。互联网最初在美国开发，因此最早的 TLD 按用途划分互联网区域，例如 .com(商业)、.gov(政府)、.edu(教育)和 .mil(军事)。后来，国家 TLD 被创建出来，例如 .fr(法国)和 .jp(日本)。"},{"en":"Historically, there were relatively few TLDs. More recently, ICANN started selling new TLDs for a registration price of $150,000 (plus ongoing maintenance costs), which led to an explosion of new TLDs. As of 2024, there are over 1,500 TLDs, including company-specific TLDs like .google, and more exotic TLDs like .travel or .pizza.","en_html":"Historically, there were relatively few TLDs. More recently, ICANN started selling new TLDs for a registration price of $150,000 (plus ongoing maintenance costs), which led to an explosion of new TLDs. As of 2024, there are over 1,500 TLDs, including company-specific TLDs like .google, and more exotic TLDs like .travel or .pizza.","zh_html":"历史上 TLD 相对较少。最近，ICANN 开始以 15 万美元的注册价格(加上持续维护费用)销售新的 TLD，这导致新 TLD 数量激增。截至 2024 年，已有超过 1,500 个 TLD，包括公司特定的 TLD，如 .google，以及更独特的 TLD，如 .travel 或 .pizza。"},{"en":"Each TLD is run by some organization, and that organization can decide its own structure for how to further divide up that TLD. For example, the .uk TLD is managed by Nominet, and they decided to split up the .uk zone by purpose, creating zones such as .co.uk (commercial), .ac.uk (academia).","en_html":"Each TLD is run by some organization, and that organization can decide its own structure for how to further divide up that TLD. For example, the .uk TLD is managed by Nominet, and they decided to split up the .uk zone by purpose, creating zones such as .co.uk (commercial), .ac.uk (academia).","zh_html":"每个 TLD 由某个组织运营，该组织可以决定如何进一步细分该 TLD。例如，.uk TLD 由 Nominet 管理，他们决定按用途拆分 .uk 区域，创建了 .co.uk(商业)、.ac.uk(学术)等区域。"},{"en":"Zones further down in the tree can be referred to by their depth. example.com is a second-level domain, and blog.example.com is a third-level domain. These zones are usually controlled by various organizations and companies, who buy those zones from the parent zone. When you buy a zone, you also need to tell the parent zone about the name servers you’re using. This allows the parent zone to redirect users to your name servers.","en_html":"Zones further down in the tree can be referred to by their depth. example.com is a second-level domain, and blog.example.com is a third-level domain. These zones are usually controlled by various organizations and companies, who buy those zones from the parent zone. When you buy a zone, you also need to tell the parent zone about the name servers you’re using. This allows the parent zone to redirect users to your name servers.","zh_html":"树中更下层的区域可以按深度来称呼。example.com 是二级域名(second-level domain)，blog.example.com 是三级域名(third-level domain)。这些区域通常由各种组织和公司控制，他们从父区域购买这些区域。购买区域时，你还需要告诉父区域你正在使用的名称服务器。这样父区域才能将用户重定向到你的名称服务器。"},{"en":"The name servers further down the tree are usually often run by domain name registrars. Registrars are companies that own specific zones, and allow users to host their services on specific domains in that zone. For example, I might pay a monthly fee to host my website on blog.foo.com. The registrar will usually offer to add the corresponding domain-to-IP mapping to its name servers.","en_html":"The name servers further down the tree are usually often run by domain name registrars. Registrars are companies that own specific zones, and allow users to host their services on specific domains in that zone. For example, I might pay a monthly fee to host my website on <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">blog.foo.com</code>. The registrar will usually offer to add the corresponding domain-to-IP mapping to its name servers.","zh_html":"树中更下层的名称服务器通常由域名注册商(domain name registrars)运营。注册商是拥有特定区域的公司，允许用户在该区域中的特定域名上托管服务。例如，我可能每月支付费用来将我的网站托管在<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">blog.foo.com</code>上。注册商通常会提供将相应的域名到 IP 映射添加到其名称服务器的服务。"},{"en":"In addition to registrars, companies like Google might also run their own name servers for their own applications (e.g. provide the record for maps.google.com). Amazon Web Services also has a name server called Route 53, where users can add records to be served.","en_html":"In addition to registrars, companies like Google might also run their own name servers for their own applications (e.g. provide the record for <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">maps.google.com</code>). Amazon Web Services also has a name server called Route 53, where users can add records to be served.","zh_html":"除了注册商，像 Google 这样的公司也可能运行自己的名称服务器来服务于自己的应用(例如提供<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">maps.google.com</code>的记录)。Amazon Web Services 也有一个名为 Route 53 的名称服务器，用户可以添加记录供其服务。"},{"en":"Root Server Availability with Anycast","en_html":"<a href=\"#root-server-availability-with-anycast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"root-server-availability-with-anycast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Root Server Availability with Anycast","zh_html":"利用任播(Anycast)实现根服务器高可用性(Root Server Availability with Anycast)"},{"en":"It would be really bad if the root servers were unavailable. Someone with an empty cache would be unable to make any DNS requests. Eventually, everyone’s caches would empty out as TTLs expire, and the Internet would stop working.","en_html":"It would be really bad if the root servers were unavailable. Someone with an empty cache would be unable to make any DNS requests. Eventually, everyone’s caches would empty out as TTLs expire, and the Internet would stop working.","zh_html":"如果根服务器不可用，后果将非常严重。缓存为空的人将无法进行任何 DNS 请求。最终，随着 TTL 过期，所有人的缓存都会清空，互联网将停止工作。"},{"en":"For redundancy, there are 13 root servers located around the world. We can look up the IP addresses of the root servers, which are public and well-known.","en_html":"For redundancy, there are 13 root servers located around the world. We can look up the <a href=\"https://www.iana.org/domains/root/servers\">IP addresses</a> of the root servers, which are public and well-known.","zh_html":"为了实现冗余，全球有 13 台根服务器。我们可以查找根服务器的 <a href=\"https://www.iana.org/domains/root/servers\">IP 地址</a>，这些地址是公开且广为人知的。"},{"en":"13 still seems like kind of a low number, considering the entire Internet relies on them. To provide extremely high availability, there are actually way more than 13 root servers, but the list only contains 13 IP addresses, because of a clever trick called anycast.","en_html":"13 still seems like kind of a low number, considering the entire Internet relies on them. To provide extremely high availability, there are actually way more than 13 root servers, but the list only contains 13 IP addresses, because of a clever trick called anycast.","zh_html":"考虑到整个互联网都依赖它们，13 台似乎仍然有点少。为了实现极高的可用性，实际的根服务器远远超过 13 台，但列表中只有 13 个 IP 地址，这是使用了一种称为任播(anycast)的巧妙技术。"},{"en":"In the anycast trick, we deploy many mirrors of a root server, and use the same IP address for all of them. If you contact the domain k.root-servers.net or its corresponding IP address, you could be talking to any one of its mirrors.","en_html":"In the <strong>anycast</strong> trick, we deploy many mirrors of a root server, and use the same IP address for all of them. If you contact the domain <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">k.root-servers.net</code> or its corresponding IP address, you could be talking to any one of its mirrors.","zh_html":"在任播(anycast)技巧中，我们部署许多根服务器的镜像(mirrors)，并为它们全部使用相同的 IP 地址。如果你联系域名<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">k.root-servers.net</code>或其对应的 IP 地址，你实际上可能与任何一个镜像进行通信。"},{"en":"Each of the 13 root servers is actually made up of a bunch of mirrors. For example, f.root-servers.net has over 3,000 instances. The mirrors could be run by different network operators (e.g. Google and Cloudflare might help run root mirrors).","en_html":"Each of the 13 root servers is actually made up of a bunch of mirrors. For example, <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">f.root-servers.net</code> has over 3,000 instances. The mirrors could be run by different network operators (e.g. Google and Cloudflare might help run root mirrors).","zh_html":"13 台根服务器中的每一台实际上都由一批镜像组成。例如，<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">f.root-servers.net</code>有超过 3,000 个实例。这些镜像可能由不同的网络运营商运营(例如 Google 和 Cloudflare 可能帮助运行根镜像)。"},{"en":"To implement anycast, during the routing protocol, every mirror announces the same IP address. The rest of the routing protocol still works the same. You might hear many announcements about routes to k.root-servers.net, and you can accept any of them, and you’ll end up routing packets to one of the mirrors. If one of the mirrors goes down, the rest of the mirrors are still sending announcements, and you can accept a different route to maintain availability.","en_html":"To implement anycast, during the routing protocol, every mirror announces the same IP address. The rest of the routing protocol still works the same. You might hear many announcements about routes to <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">k.root-servers.net</code>, and you can accept any of them, and you’ll end up routing packets to one of the mirrors. If one of the mirrors goes down, the rest of the mirrors are still sending announcements, and you can accept a different route to maintain availability.","zh_html":"为了实现任播，在路由协议(routing protocol)中，每个镜像都通告相同的 IP 地址。路由协议的其他部分仍然照常工作。你可能会听到许多关于<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">k.root-servers.net</code>的路由通告，你可以接受其中任何一个，最终数据包会被路由到其中一个镜像。如果某个镜像宕机，其他镜像仍在发送通告，你可以接受不同的路由以保持可用性。"},{"en":"Anycast also means that root IPs very rarely change, even as mirrors are added and removed. As a result, the root hints file contains records for the root name servers with very long TTLs (42 days).","en_html":"Anycast also means that root IPs very rarely change, even as mirrors are added and removed. As a result, the root hints file contains records for the root name servers with very long TTLs (42 days).","zh_html":"任播还意味着即使镜像被添加或移除，根 IP 也很少改变。因此，根提示文件中根名称服务器的记录具有非常长的 TTL(42 天)。"},{"en":"Here’s a map of all the mirrors for k.root-servers.net. All of them advertise the same IP address, and your router probably chooses to talk to the closest mirror. k.root-servers.net is operated by RIPE (in Europe), which might explain why there are so many mirrors in Europe.","en_html":"Here’s a map of all the mirrors for <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">k.root-servers.net</code>. All of them advertise the same IP address, and your router probably chooses to talk to the closest mirror. <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">k.root-servers.net</code> is operated by RIPE (in Europe), which might explain why there are so many mirrors in Europe.","zh_html":"以下是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">k.root-servers.net</code>所有镜像的地图。它们都通告相同的 IP 地址，你的路由器可能会选择与最近的镜像通信。<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">k.root-servers.net</code>由 RIPE(位于欧洲)运营，这可能解释了为什么欧洲有这么多镜像。"},{"en":"Public resolvers like Google’s 8.8.8.8 can also be anycast for high availability.","en_html":"Public resolvers like Google’s 8.8.8.8 can also be anycast for high availability.","zh_html":"公共解析器，如 Google 的 8.8.8.8，也可以使用任播实现高可用性。"},{"en":"DNS for Email","en_html":"<a href=\"#dns-for-email\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dns-for-email\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DNS for Email","zh_html":"用于电子邮件的 DNS(DNS for Email)"},{"en":"DNS can be used to store and serve more than domain-to-IP mappings. For example, if you want to send email to an address like evanbot@berkeley.edu, your computer still needs to know where to send packets.","en_html":"DNS can be used to store and serve more than domain-to-IP mappings. For example, if you want to send email to an address like <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">evanbot@berkeley.edu</code>, your computer still needs to know where to send packets.","zh_html":"DNS 不仅可以存储和提供域名到 IP 的映射。例如，如果你想发送电子邮件到<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">evanbot@berkeley.edu</code>，你的计算机仍然需要知道将数据包发送到哪里。"},{"en":"To translate email addresses to mail servers, we can use MX type records. These records map domains like berkeley.edu to mail servers like aspmx.l.google.com. Note that it’s okay if the mail server is in a different zone (e.g. here, the mail servers for UC Berkeley are operated by Google).","en_html":"To translate email addresses to mail servers, we can use <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">MX</code> type records. These records map domains like <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code> to mail servers like <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">aspmx.l.google.com</code>. Note that it’s okay if the mail server is in a different zone (e.g. here, the mail servers for UC Berkeley are operated by Google).","zh_html":"要将电子邮件地址翻译为邮件服务器，我们可以使用<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">MX</code>类型记录。这些记录将<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">berkeley.edu</code>等域名映射到<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">aspmx.l.google.com</code>等邮件服务器。注意，邮件服务器位于不同的区域也是可以的(例如，这里 UC Berkeley 的邮件服务器由 Google 运营)。"},{"en":"Historically, an email address corresponded to a user on a specific machine, so the mail server would be that machine. Nowadays, you probably want to be able to access your email from anywhere. That’s why the MX records map to mail servers like aspmx.l.google.com, which receive your mail and let you access your mail from any computer.","en_html":"Historically, an email address corresponded to a user on a specific machine, so the mail server would be that machine. Nowadays, you probably want to be able to access your email from anywhere. That’s why the MX records map to mail servers like <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">aspmx.l.google.com</code>, which receive your mail and let you access your mail from any computer.","zh_html":"历史上，电子邮件地址对应特定机器上的某个用户，因此邮件服务器就是那台机器。如今，你可能希望随时随地访问你的电子邮件。这就是 MX 记录映射到<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">aspmx.l.google.com</code>这样的邮件服务器的原因，这些服务器接收你的邮件并让你从任何计算机访问你的邮件。"},{"en":"One key difference in MX records is that the values also contain a priority. If you receive multiple MX records, all mapping the same domain to different mail servers, you should try the mail server with the highest priority (lowest number) first.","en_html":"One key difference in MX records is that the values also contain a priority. If you receive multiple MX records, all mapping the same domain to different mail servers, you should try the mail server with the highest priority (lowest number) first.","zh_html":"MX 记录的一个关键区别是值中还包含优先级(priority)。如果你收到多个 MX 记录，都将同一个域名映射到不同的邮件服务器，你应该首先尝试优先级最高(数值最小)的邮件服务器。"},{"en":"DNS for Load Balancing","en_html":"<a href=\"#dns-for-load-balancing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dns-for-load-balancing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DNS for Load Balancing","zh_html":"用于负载均衡的 DNS(DNS for Load Balancing)"},{"en":"We saw in the demo that the final name server returned a single A type record, but it’s actually possible to receive multiple A type records, mapping a single domain to multiple IP addresses. There’s no order to these records, and the server might shuffle the order before returning them. Any of them are valid, and the client can pick any of them (usually the first one). Providing multiple IP addresses is useful for load-balancing and redundancy.","en_html":"We saw in the demo that the final name server returned a single <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code> type record, but it’s actually possible to receive multiple <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code> type records, mapping a single domain to multiple IP addresses. There’s no order to these records, and the server might shuffle the order before returning them. Any of them are valid, and the client can pick any of them (usually the first one). Providing multiple IP addresses is useful for load-balancing and redundancy.","zh_html":"我们在演示中看到最后的名称服务器返回了一个单个的<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code>类型记录，但实际上可以返回多个<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code>类型记录，将单个域名映射到多个 IP 地址。这些记录没有顺序，服务器可能在返回前打乱顺序。任何一个都是有效的，客户端可以选择其中任何一个(通常是第一个)。提供多个 IP 地址对于负载均衡(load-balancing)和冗余很有用。"},{"en":"It’s also possible for the name server to send back different A type records, depending on who sent the query, and where they sent the query from. This can be useful if we want to balance the load for a popular domain like www.google.com across multiple servers. For example, we can try to send the user to the nearest server.","en_html":"It’s also possible for the name server to send back different <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code> type records, depending on who sent the query, and where they sent the query from. This can be useful if we want to balance the load for a popular domain like <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">www.google.com</code> across multiple servers. For example, we can try to send the user to the nearest server.","zh_html":"名称服务器也可以根据谁发送了查询以及从何处发送，返回不同的<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code>类型记录。这对于将<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">www.google.com</code>这样的热门域名的负载均衡到多台服务器非常有用。例如，我们可以尝试将用户发送到最近的服务器。"},{"en":"The name server now needs additional (possibly proprietary) logic to decide which record(s) to send in the reply. The name server could check who the recursive resolver is. It could also check who the end client is, though this requires an extension to DNS so that the resolver’s query includes the client address. It could even check the geographic location of the end user, though this requires some mapping of IP address to physical location. Commerical databases like MaxMind exist for mapping IP addresses to physical locations.","en_html":"The name server now needs additional (possibly proprietary) logic to decide which record(s) to send in the reply. The name server could check who the recursive resolver is. It could also check who the end client is, though this requires an extension to DNS so that the resolver’s query includes the client address. It could even check the geographic location of the end user, though this requires some mapping of IP address to physical location. Commerical databases like MaxMind exist for mapping IP addresses to physical locations.","zh_html":"名称服务器现在需要额外的(可能是专有的)逻辑来决定在回复中发送哪些记录。名称服务器可以检查递归解析器是谁。它还可以检查最终客户端是谁，尽管这需要 DNS 的扩展，以便解析器的查询包含客户端地址。它甚至可以检查最终用户的地理位置，尽管这需要某种将 IP 地址映射到物理位置的方案。存在像 MaxMind 这样的商业数据库用于将 IP 地址映射到物理位置。"},{"en":"Load balancing based on the user’s geographical location isn’t perfect. Even if we knew our servers’ physical locations and the user’s physical location, we have to do some guesswork about which server is closest from a network perspective. We also don’t know about the performance (e.g. network bandwidth) between the user and the different servers.","en_html":"Load balancing based on the user’s geographical location isn’t perfect. Even if we knew our servers’ physical locations and the user’s physical location, we have to do some guesswork about which server is closest from a network perspective. We also don’t know about the performance (e.g. network bandwidth) between the user and the different servers.","zh_html":"基于用户地理位置的负载均衡并不完美。即使我们知道服务器的物理位置和用户的物理位置，我们也需要做一些关于从网络角度看哪台服务器最近的猜测。我们也不知道用户和不同服务器之间的性能(例如网络带宽)。"},{"en":"Here’s an experiment to see how well Google’s geographic load balancing performs. We looked up www.google.com in San Francisco and Oregon and got two different IP addresses.","en_html":"Here’s an experiment to see how well Google’s geographic load balancing performs. We looked up <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">www.google.com</code> in San Francisco and Oregon and got two different IP addresses.","zh_html":"下面是一个实验，看看 Google 的地理负载均衡效果如何。我们在旧金山和俄勒冈分别查询了<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">www.google.com</code>，得到了两个不同的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"We then did a reverse lookup to match each IP address to a specific server name (different for each server, not www.google.com). The result is a PTR type record, which maps IP address to name (reverse of A type record). We can see that the IP address that Google gave us in San Francisco is mapped to some server with the name sfo03s25. Assuming sfo stands for San Francisco, that’s pretty good!","en_html":"We then did a reverse lookup to match each IP address to a specific server name (different for each server, not <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">www.google.com</code>). The result is a <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">PTR</code> type record, which maps IP address to name (reverse of <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code> type record). We can see that the IP address that Google gave us in San Francisco is mapped to some server with the name <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">sfo03s25</code>. Assuming <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">sfo</code> stands for San Francisco, that’s pretty good!","zh_html":"然后我们做了一个反向查找(reverse lookup)，将每个 IP 地址匹配到特定的服务器名称(与 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">www.google.com</code> 不同)。结果是<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">PTR</code>类型记录，它将 IP 地址映射到名称(<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">A</code>类型记录的反向操作)。我们可以看到，Google 在旧金山给我们的 IP 地址映射到名称为<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">sfo03s25</code>的服务器。假设<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">sfo</code>代表旧金山(San Francisco)，那效果相当不错！"},{"en":"If we look at the latency, connecting from the San Francisco computer to the IP address served to San Francisco takes 20 ms, and connecting from the San Francisco computer to the IP address served to Oregon takes 35 seconds. Google did a good job giving the San Francisco computer a closer server!","en_html":"If we look at the latency, connecting from the San Francisco computer to the IP address served to San Francisco takes 20 ms, and connecting from the San Francisco computer to the IP address served to Oregon takes 35 seconds. Google did a good job giving the San Francisco computer a closer server!","zh_html":"我们来看延迟：从旧金山的计算机连接到分配给旧金山的 IP 地址需要 20 毫秒，而从旧金山的计算机连接到分配给俄勒冈的 IP 地址需要 35 秒。Google 在给旧金山的计算机分配更近的服务器方面做得很好！"}]},"/applications/http.html":{"title":"http","blocks":[{"en":"HTTP","en_html":"<a href=\"#http\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"http\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> HTTP","zh_html":"超文本传输协议(HTTP)"},{"en":"Brief History of HTTP","en_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-http\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-http\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Brief History of HTTP","zh_html":"HTTP 简史"},{"en":"In 1989, Tim Berners-Lee was working at CERN (Switzerland research lab) and needed to exchange information between scientists. At the time, protocols like FTP existed for transferring files over the Internet. However, a file will often have links to other resources on the Internet. His goal was to create a protocol and file format that would allow linking pages to each other and fetching those pages.","en_html":"In 1989, Tim Berners-Lee was working at CERN (Switzerland research lab) and needed to exchange information between scientists. At the time, protocols like FTP existed for transferring files over the Internet. However, a file will often have links to other resources on the Internet. His goal was to create a protocol and file format that would allow linking pages to each other and fetching those pages.","zh_html":"1989 年，Tim Berners-Lee 在 CERN(瑞士研究实验室)工作，需要在科学家之间交换信息。当时，已有 FTP 等协议用于通过互联网传输文件。然而，文件通常包含指向互联网上其他资源的链接。他的目标是创建一种协议和文件格式，允许页面之间相互链接并获取这些页面。"},{"en":"The original HTTP specification was given version number HTTP/0.9 and released in 1991. HTTP/1.0 was standardized in 1996, and HTTP/1.1 was standardized in 1997. Unless otherwise specified, this section is referring to HTTP/1.1, since this is the most common version in use today. More recent versions do exist (see end of this section), but the fundamentals of the protocol have stayed the same for over 20 years.","en_html":"The original HTTP specification was given version number HTTP/0.9 and released in 1991. HTTP/1.0 was standardized in 1996, and HTTP/1.1 was standardized in 1997. Unless otherwise specified, this section is referring to HTTP/1.1, since this is the most common version in use today. More recent versions do exist (see end of this section), but the fundamentals of the protocol have stayed the same for over 20 years.","zh_html":"最初的 HTTP 规范版本号为 HTTP/0.9，于 1991 年发布。HTTP/1.0 于 1996 年标准化，HTTP/1.1 于 1997 年标准化。除非另有说明，本节指的是 HTTP/1.1，因为它是当今使用最广泛的版本。确实存在更新的版本(见本节末尾)，但该协议的基础已经保持了 20 多年不变。"},{"en":"HTTP Basics","en_html":"<a href=\"#http-basics\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"http-basics\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> HTTP Basics","zh_html":"HTTP 基础(HTTP Basics)"},{"en":"HTTP runs over TCP. Two people who want to send data over HTTP will first start a TCP connection. Then, they can use its bytestream abstraction to reliably exchange arbitrary-length data. Hosts running HTTP don’t have to worry about packets being reordered, dropped, and so on.","en_html":"HTTP runs over TCP. Two people who want to send data over HTTP will first start a TCP connection. Then, they can use its bytestream abstraction to reliably exchange arbitrary-length data. Hosts running HTTP don’t have to worry about packets being reordered, dropped, and so on.","zh_html":"HTTP 运行在 TCP 之上。希望通过 HTTP 发送数据的双方首先建立 TCP 连接，然后可以利用其字节流抽象(bytestream abstraction)可靠地交换任意长度的数据。运行 HTTP 的主机不必担心数据包被重新排序、丢弃等问题。"},{"en":"HTTP is a client-server protocol. We designate one person as the client (e.g. you, the end user), and one person as the server (e.g. Google, a bank website, etc.). The client is almost always running HTTP in a web browser (e.g. Firefox or Chrome), though HTTP can also be run in other ways (e.g. directly on the terminal).","en_html":"HTTP is a <strong>client-server</strong> protocol. We designate one person as the client (e.g. you, the end user), and one person as the server (e.g. Google, a bank website, etc.). The client is almost always running HTTP in a web browser (e.g. Firefox or Chrome), though HTTP can also be run in other ways (e.g. directly on the terminal).","zh_html":"HTTP 是一个<strong>客户端-服务器(client-server)</strong>协议。我们将一方指定为客户端(例如你，最终用户)，另一方指定为服务器(例如 Google、银行网站等)。客户端几乎总是在网页浏览器(如 Firefox 或 Chrome)中运行 HTTP，尽管 HTTP 也可以通过其他方式运行(例如直接在终端中)。"},{"en":"When forming an HTTP connection, the server must listen for connection requests on the well-known, constant port number 80. (HTTPS, a more recent secure version, uses port 443). The client can choose any random ephemeral port number to start the connection, and the server can send replies to that port number.","en_html":"When forming an HTTP connection, the server must listen for connection requests on the well-known, constant port number 80. (HTTPS, a more recent secure version, uses port 443). The client can choose any random ephemeral port number to start the connection, and the server can send replies to that port number.","zh_html":"建立 HTTP 连接时，服务器必须在众所周知的固定端口 80 上监听连接请求。(HTTPS 是一个更新的安全版本，使用端口 443)。客户端可以选择任何随机的临时端口(ephemeral port number)来建立连接，服务器可以向该端口发送回复。"},{"en":"HTTP is a request-response protocol. For each request that the client sends, the server sends exactly one corresponding response.","en_html":"HTTP is a <strong>request-response</strong> protocol. For each request that the client sends, the server sends exactly one corresponding response.","zh_html":"HTTP 是一个<strong>请求-响应(request-response)</strong>协议。对于客户端发送的每个请求，服务器只发送一个对应的响应。"},{"en":"HTTP Requests","en_html":"<a href=\"#http-requests\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"http-requests\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> HTTP Requests","zh_html":"HTTP 请求(HTTP Requests)"},{"en":"The HTTP request message is formatted in human-readable plaintext, which means you can type raw HTTP requests into the terminal. The request contains three parts: method, URL, version, and optional content.","en_html":"The HTTP request message is formatted in human-readable plaintext, which means you can type raw HTTP requests into the terminal. The request contains three parts: method, URL, version, and optional content.","zh_html":"HTTP 请求消息以人类可读的纯文本(plaintext)格式组成，这意味着你可以在终端中输入原始的 HTTP 请求。请求包含三个部分：方法(method)、URL、版本(version)，以及可选的内容(content)。"},{"en":"The message ends with a newline (technically, CRLF, look it up if curious), which you can think of as the user pressing the Enter key after typing in the HTTP request in their terminal.","en_html":"The message ends with a newline (technically, CRLF, look it up if curious), which you can think of as the user pressing the Enter key after typing in the HTTP request in their terminal.","zh_html":"消息以换行符结束(严格来说，是 CRLF，好奇的话可以自行查阅)，你可以把它想象成用户在终端中输入 HTTP 请求后按下回车键。"},{"en":"The version number specifies what version of HTTP you’re using, e.g. HTTP/0.9, HTTP/1.0, HTTP/1.1, etc.","en_html":"The version number specifies what version of HTTP you’re using, e.g. HTTP/0.9, HTTP/1.0, HTTP/1.1, etc.","zh_html":"版本号指定你使用的 HTTP 版本，例如 HTTP/0.9、HTTP/1.0、HTTP/1.1 等。"},{"en":"The requested URL identifies a resource on the server. You can think of the URL as the filepath of what you’re trying to retrieve from the remote server. For example, in the URL http://cs168.io/assets/lectures/lecture1.pdf, we’re trying to retrieve a file in the assets/lectures folder, named lecture1.pdf, on the cs168.io remote server. (Servers aren’t required to work this way, but it’s a useful intuition to have.)","en_html":"The requested URL identifies a resource on the server. You can think of the URL as the filepath of what you’re trying to retrieve from the remote server. For example, in the URL http://cs168.io/assets/lectures/lecture1.pdf, we’re trying to retrieve a file in the assets/lectures folder, named lecture1.pdf, on the cs168.io remote server. (Servers aren’t required to work this way, but it’s a useful intuition to have.)","zh_html":"请求的 URL 标识服务器上的一个资源。你可以把 URL 理解为你想从远程服务器获取内容的文件路径。例如，在 URL http://cs168.io/assets/lectures/lecture1.pdf 中，我们试图从 cs168.io 远程服务器获取 assets/lectures 文件夹中名为 lecture1.pdf 的文件。(服务器不一定要这样工作，但这是一种有用的直觉。)"},{"en":"The method identifies what action the user wants to perform. Initially, HTTP only had one method, GET, which allows a client to retrieve a specific page (indicated by the URL) from the server.","en_html":"The method identifies what action the user wants to perform. Initially, HTTP only had one method, GET, which allows a client to retrieve a specific page (indicated by the URL) from the server.","zh_html":"方法(method)标识用户想要执行的操作。最初，HTTP 只有一种方法，即 GET，它允许客户端从服务器获取特定页面(由 URL 指示)。"},{"en":"Later, HTTP was extended to add other methods. Notably, the POST method was added, which allows the client to supply information to the server as well. For example, if the user fills out a form and clicks Submit, that data is sent to the server in a POST request.","en_html":"Later, HTTP was extended to add other methods. Notably, the POST method was added, which allows the client to supply information to the server as well. For example, if the user fills out a form and clicks Submit, that data is sent to the server in a POST request.","zh_html":"后来，HTTP 被扩展以增加其他方法。值得注意的是，添加了 POST 方法，它允许客户端也向服务器提供信息。例如，如果用户填写表单并点击提交，该数据会通过 POST 请求发送到服务器。"},{"en":"Some less-used methods exist as well. HEAD retrieves the headers (metadata) of the response, but not the actual content of the response. Other methods like PUT, CONNECT, DELETE, OPTIONS, PATCH, and TRACE extend HTTP into a protocol that lets the user interact with content on the server. The user can now make changes to the content, as opposed to the original design, where the user could only retrieve content. These extra methods make HTTP very flexible for all sorts of different application.","en_html":"Some less-used methods exist as well. HEAD retrieves the headers (metadata) of the response, but not the actual content of the response. Other methods like PUT, CONNECT, DELETE, OPTIONS, PATCH, and TRACE extend HTTP into a protocol that lets the user interact with content on the server. The user can now make changes to the content, as opposed to the original design, where the user could only retrieve content. These extra methods make HTTP very flexible for all sorts of different application.","zh_html":"还存在一些较少使用的方法。HEAD 获取响应的头部(元数据)，但不获取响应的实际内容。其他方法如 PUT、CONNECT、DELETE、OPTIONS、PATCH 和 TRACE 将 HTTP 扩展为一个允许用户与服务器上的内容进行交互的协议。用户现在可以对内容进行修改，而最初的设计中用户只能获取内容。这些额外的方法使 HTTP 对于各种不同的应用都非常灵活。"},{"en":"Note that with other methods like POST, we still have to provide a URL to indicate how to interpret the data we’re sending. On a bank website, sending a name to the /send-money URL will probably do something different from sending that same name to the /request-money URL.","en_html":"Note that with other methods like POST, we still have to provide a URL to indicate how to interpret the data we’re sending. On a bank website, sending a name to the /send-money URL will probably do something different from sending that same name to the /request-money URL.","zh_html":"请注意，即使使用像 POST 这样的其他方法，我们仍然需要提供 URL 来指示如何解释我们发送的数据。在银行网站上，将姓名发送到 /send-money URL 可能与将同一个姓名发送到 /request-money URL 的效果完全不同。"},{"en":"For GET requests, the content of the request is usually empty, since we’re asking for a page from the server and not sending any of our own information. By contrast, for a POST request, the content of the request contains the data we want to send to the server.","en_html":"For GET requests, the content of the request is usually empty, since we’re asking for a page from the server and not sending any of our own information. By contrast, for a POST request, the content of the request contains the data we want to send to the server.","zh_html":"对于 GET 请求，请求的内容通常是空的，因为我们是在向服务器请求一个页面，而不是发送我们自己的信息。相比之下，对于 POST 请求，请求的内容包含我们要发送给服务器的数据。"},{"en":"HTTP Responses","en_html":"<a href=\"#http-responses\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"http-responses\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> HTTP Responses","zh_html":"HTTP 响应(HTTP Responses)"},{"en":"Each HTTP request corresponds to one HTTP response. The response is also in human-readable plaintext, which means you can read raw HTTP responses in the terminal. The response contains four parts: version, status code, optional message, and content.","en_html":"Each HTTP request corresponds to one HTTP response. The response is also in human-readable plaintext, which means you can read raw HTTP responses in the terminal. The response contains four parts: version, status code, optional message, and content.","zh_html":"每个 HTTP 请求对应一个 HTTP 响应。响应也是人类可读的纯文本，这意味着你可以在终端中阅读原始的 HTTP 响应。响应包含四个部分：版本(version)、状态码(status code)、可选消息(message)和内容(content)。"},{"en":"As before, the version specifies the version of HTTP being used.","en_html":"As before, the version specifies the version of HTTP being used.","zh_html":"和之前一样，版本指定所使用的 HTTP 版本。"},{"en":"The content is where the server would put, for example, the page that the user requested in a GET request.","en_html":"The content is where the server would put, for example, the page that the user requested in a GET request.","zh_html":"内容是服务器放置例如用户在 GET 请求中请求的页面的地方。"},{"en":"The status code is a number that allows the server to indicate the result of the client’s request. Each status code has a corresponding human-readable message.","en_html":"The status code is a number that allows the server to indicate the result of the client’s request. Each status code has a corresponding human-readable message.","zh_html":"状态码(status code)是一个数字，允许服务器指示客户端请求的结果。每个状态码都有对应的人类可读消息。"},{"en":"The status codes are classified into various categories according to numeric values:","en_html":"The status codes are classified into various categories according to numeric values:","zh_html":"状态码根据数值分为不同的类别："},{"en":"100 = Informational responses.","en_html":"100 = Informational responses.","zh_html":"100 = 信息响应(Informational responses)。"},{"en":"200 = Successful responses. 200 OK indicates a successful request, where the definition of success depends on the method of the request and the application using HTTP (remember, status codes are in every response, regardless of what method, GET/POST/etc., the request was). 201 Created indicates that the request succeeded and some new resource was created. This is usually seen in POST or PUT requests.","en_html":"200 = Successful responses. 200 OK indicates a successful request, where the definition of success depends on the method of the request and the application using HTTP (remember, status codes are in every response, regardless of what method, GET/POST/etc., the request was). 201 Created indicates that the request succeeded and some new resource was created. This is usually seen in POST or PUT requests.","zh_html":"200 = 成功响应(Successful responses)。200 OK 表示请求成功，成功的定义取决于请求的方法和使用 HTTP 的应用程序(记住，状态码在任何响应中都存在，无论请求的方法是 GET、POST 等)。201 Created 表示请求成功并创建了某些新资源。这通常出现在 POST 或 PUT 请求中。"},{"en":"300 = Redirection messages. These allow the server to tell the client that they should go look for the resource (specified by the URL) somewhere else. Two common ones are 301 Moved Permanently and 302 Found (a weird name for moved temporarily). Sometimes, the status code itself doesn’t provide enough context (as seen with these redirects). Therefore, the response will also contain additional information about where the resource has moved (e.g. another URL).","en_html":"300 = Redirection messages. These allow the server to tell the client that they should go look for the resource (specified by the URL) somewhere else. Two common ones are 301 Moved Permanently and 302 Found (a weird name for moved temporarily). Sometimes, the status code itself doesn’t provide enough context (as seen with these redirects). Therefore, the response will also contain additional information about where the resource has moved (e.g. another URL).","zh_html":"300 = 重定向消息(Redirection messages)。这些状态码允许服务器告诉客户端，他们应该去其他地方查找资源(由 URL 指定)。两个常见的是 301 Moved Permanently 和 302 Found(一个表示临时移动的怪异名称)。有时，状态码本身不提供足够的上下文(正如这些重定向所示)。因此，响应还将包含关于资源移动到了哪里的额外信息(例如另一个 URL)。"},{"en":"The use of more specific status codes allows the client to determine its future behavior based on the code. For example, 301 Moved Permanently tells the client to stop looking in the original location, while 302 Found (moved temporarily) might tell the client to come back and check again later.","en_html":"The use of more specific status codes allows the client to determine its future behavior based on the code. For example, 301 Moved Permanently tells the client to stop looking in the original location, while 302 Found (moved temporarily) might tell the client to come back and check again later.","zh_html":"使用更具体的状态码允许客户端根据代码确定其后续行为。例如，301 Moved Permanently 告诉客户端停止在原始位置查找，而 302 Found(临时移动)可能告诉客户端稍后再回来检查。"},{"en":"400 = An error attributable to client action. 401 Unauthorized says that the client is not allowed to access this content, but if they authenticate their identity (e.g. log in), then they might be able to access the content. 403 Forbidden says that the client is authenticated, and the server knows their identity, but they’re still not allowed to access the content.","en_html":"400 = An error attributable to client action. 401 Unauthorized says that the client is not allowed to access this content, but if they authenticate their identity (e.g. log in), then they might be able to access the content. 403 Forbidden says that the client is authenticated, and the server knows their identity, but they’re still not allowed to access the content.","zh_html":"400 = 客户端操作导致的错误。401 Unauthorized 表示客户端不允许访问此内容，但如果他们认证身份(例如登录)，则可能能够访问该内容。403 Forbidden 表示客户端已通过认证，服务器知道他们的身份，但他们仍然不允许访问该内容。"},{"en":"Again, using more specific codes lets the client determine future behavior. 401 Unauthorized might cause the client browser to show a login window, while 403 Forbidden might cause the client browser to show an error message (since the user has already logged in).","en_html":"Again, using more specific codes lets the client determine future behavior. 401 Unauthorized might cause the client browser to show a login window, while 403 Forbidden might cause the client browser to show an error message (since the user has already logged in).","zh_html":"同样，使用更具体的代码让客户端确定后续行为。401 Unauthorized 可能导致客户端浏览器显示登录窗口，而 403 Forbidden 可能导致客户端浏览器显示错误消息(因为用户已经登录)。"},{"en":"500 = An error attributable to server action. 500 Internal Server Error and 503 Service Unavailable are common. There’s not much the client can do about these errors, except maybe try again later.","en_html":"500 = An error attributable to server action. 500 Internal Server Error and 503 Service Unavailable are common. There’s not much the client can do about these errors, except maybe try again later.","zh_html":"500 = 服务器操作导致的错误。500 Internal Server Error 和 503 Service Unavailable 很常见。对于这些错误，客户端能做的并不多，除了可能稍后重试。"},{"en":"Some error codes like 404 (File Not Found) and 503 (Service Unavailable) are very recognizable.","en_html":"Some error codes like 404 (File Not Found) and 503 (Service Unavailable) are very recognizable.","zh_html":"一些错误代码如 404(File Not Found)和 503(Service Unavailable)非常易于识别。"},{"en":"Sometimes, the appropriate status code to use can be ambiguous. For example, if we sent an HTTP request to Google using version 0.9, the appropriate request might be 505 (HTTP Version Not Supported). Instead, Google responds with 400 (Bad Request). Usually, the goal is to provide an error from the correct category (e.g. 400 and 500 indicate errors) that elicits the correct behavior from the client.","en_html":"Sometimes, the appropriate status code to use can be ambiguous. For example, if we sent an HTTP request to Google using version 0.9, the appropriate request might be 505 (HTTP Version Not Supported). Instead, Google responds with 400 (Bad Request). Usually, the goal is to provide an error from the correct category (e.g. 400 and 500 indicate errors) that elicits the correct behavior from the client.","zh_html":"有时，使用哪个状态码可能比较模糊。例如，如果我们使用版本 0.9 向 Google 发送 HTTP 请求，合适的响应可能是 505(HTTP Version Not Supported)。然而，Google 回复的是 400(Bad Request)。通常，目标是提供一个来自正确类别(例如 400 和 500 表示错误)的错误代码，以引导客户端产生正确的行为。"},{"en":"HTTP Headers","en_html":"<a href=\"#http-headers\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"http-headers\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> HTTP Headers","zh_html":"HTTP 头部(HTTP Headers)"},{"en":"If the client has additional information they’d like to send to the server, they can include additional metadata called headers. In HTTP/1.1, no headers are mandatory, so it’s legal to not include any (though the server/client might expect a header and error).","en_html":"If the client has additional information they’d like to send to the server, they can include additional metadata called <strong>headers</strong>. In HTTP/1.1, no headers are mandatory, so it’s legal to not include any (though the server/client might expect a header and error).","zh_html":"如果客户端有额外的信息想要发送给服务器，他们可以包含称为头部(headers)的额外元数据。在 HTTP/1.1 中，没有头部是强制性的，因此不包含任何头部是合法的(尽管服务器/客户端可能期望某个头部并报错)。"},{"en":"For example, the Location header can be used in HTTP 300 responses to indicate where the resource has moved.","en_html":"For example, the Location header can be used in HTTP 300 responses to indicate where the resource has moved.","zh_html":"例如，Location 头部可以在 HTTP 300 响应中使用，以指示资源已经移动到了哪里。"},{"en":"Sometimes, the header information is optional. For example, the User-Agent header in the request lets the client tell the server about the client browser or program (e.g. Firefox or Chrome). This could allow the request to be processed differently depending on the header field (e.g. whether the user is on Chrome or the terminal).","en_html":"Sometimes, the header information is optional. For example, the User-Agent header in the request lets the client tell the server about the client browser or program (e.g. Firefox or Chrome). This could allow the request to be processed differently depending on the header field (e.g. whether the user is on Chrome or the terminal).","zh_html":"有时，头部信息是可选的。例如，请求中的 User-Agent 头部允许客户端告诉服务器有关客户端浏览器或程序的信息(例如 Firefox 或 Chrome)。这可以根据头部字段(例如用户使用的是 Chrome 还是终端)来不同地处理请求。"},{"en":"Other times, header information is more critical. For example, Content-Type tells us whether the payload is an HTML page, image, video, etc. This tells the browser how to display the HTTP response. If a server is hosting multiple websites, the Host header can be used in requests to specify what website to request.","en_html":"Other times, header information is more critical. For example, Content-Type tells us whether the payload is an HTML page, image, video, etc. This tells the browser how to display the HTTP response. If a server is hosting multiple websites, the Host header can be used in requests to specify what website to request.","zh_html":"其他时候，头部信息更为关键。例如，Content-Type 告诉我们载荷(payload)是 HTML 页面、图片、视频等。这告诉浏览器如何显示 HTTP 响应。如果服务器托管了多个网站，可以使用请求中的 Host 头部来指定要请求哪个网站。"},{"en":"Some headers are relevant in requests. These allow the client to pass information to the server. For example, the Accept header lets the client tell the server which content type the client is expecting (e.g. HTML for human-readable pages, JSON for machine-parsable data). The User-Agent header indicates the type of client being used, and the Host header indicates the specific host being accessed (in case a server is hosting multiple websites). The Referer header indicates how the client made the request (e.g. if they clicked on a link from Facebook to make this request).","en_html":"Some headers are relevant in requests. These allow the client to pass information to the server. For example, the Accept header lets the client tell the server which content type the client is expecting (e.g. HTML for human-readable pages, JSON for machine-parsable data). The User-Agent header indicates the type of client being used, and the Host header indicates the specific host being accessed (in case a server is hosting multiple websites). The Referer header indicates how the client made the request (e.g. if they clicked on a link from Facebook to make this request).","zh_html":"一些头部与请求相关。它们允许客户端向服务器传递信息。例如，Accept 头部允许客户端告诉服务器它期望的内容类型(例如 HTML 用于人类可读的页面，JSON 用于机器可解析的数据)。User-Agent 头部指示使用的客户端类型，Host 头部指示正在访问的特定主机(以防服务器托管了多个网站)。Referer 头部指示客户端如何发起请求(例如，如果用户通过点击 Facebook 上的链接来发起这个请求)。"},{"en":"Other headers are relevant in responses. Remember, headers are metadata about the content, not the content itself. For example, Content-Encoding tells us how the bits of the response should be interpreted (e.g. Unicode/ASCII for human-readable text, or gzip for a compressed file). The Date header tells us when the server generated the response.","en_html":"Other headers are relevant in responses. Remember, headers are metadata about the content, not the content itself. For example, Content-Encoding tells us how the bits of the response should be interpreted (e.g. Unicode/ASCII for human-readable text, or gzip for a compressed file). The Date header tells us when the server generated the response.","zh_html":"其他头部与响应相关。请记住，头部是关于内容的元数据，而不是内容本身。例如，Content-Encoding 告诉我们应如何解释响应的位(例如 Unicode/ASCII 用于人类可读的文本，或 gzip 用于压缩文件)。Date 头部告诉我们服务器生成响应的时间。"},{"en":"Some headers are representation headers, which are used in both requests and responses to describe how the content is represented. For example, the Content-Type header specifies the type of the document (e.g. text, image) and can be in POST requests, or GET responses. Representation headers let us carry different types of content over HTTP, which allows the protocol to be generalized and usable by all sorts of applications.","en_html":"Some headers are representation headers, which are used in both requests and responses to describe how the content is represented. For example, the Content-Type header specifies the type of the document (e.g. text, image) and can be in POST requests, or GET responses. Representation headers let us carry different types of content over HTTP, which allows the protocol to be generalized and usable by all sorts of applications.","zh_html":"一些头部是表示头部(representation headers)，在请求和响应中都使用，用于描述内容的表示方式。例如，Content-Type 头部指定文档的类型(例如文本、图像)，可以出现在 POST 请求或 GET 响应中。表示头部允许我们通过 HTTP 传输不同类型的内容，这使得该协议具有通用性，可以被各种应用使用。"},{"en":"HTTP Examples","en_html":"<a href=\"#http-examples\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"http-examples\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> HTTP Examples","zh_html":"HTTP 示例(HTTP Examples)"},{"en":"In your terminal, you can type telnet google.com 80 to connect to Port 80 (HTTP) on Google’s server. The terminal will then allow you to type a raw HTTP request, with headers, like:","en_html":"In your terminal, you can type <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">telnet google.com 80</code> to connect to Port 80 (HTTP) on Google’s server. The terminal will then allow you to type a raw HTTP request, with headers, like:","zh_html":"在你的终端中，你可以输入<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">telnet google.com 80</code>连接到 Google 服务器的 80 端口(HTTP)。终端将允许你输入原始的 HTTP 请求，包括头部，例如："},{"en":"GET / HTTP/1.1","en_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">GET / HTTP/1.1</code>","zh_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">GET / HTTP/1.1</code>"},{"en":"User-Agent: robjs","en_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">User-Agent: robjs</code>","zh_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">User-Agent: robjs</code>"},{"en":"This is a GET request for the root page on the server, running on HTTP version 1.1. The User-Agent header indicates the type of client we’re using.","en_html":"This is a GET request for the root page on the server, running on HTTP version 1.1. The User-Agent header indicates the type of client we’re using.","zh_html":"这是一个 GET 请求，获取服务器上的根页面，使用 HTTP 版本 1.1。User-Agent 头部指示我们使用的客户端类型。"},{"en":"Likewise, the response is also human-readable.","en_html":"Likewise, the response is also human-readable.","zh_html":"同样，响应也是人类可读的。"},{"en":"HTTP/1.1 200 OK","en_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">HTTP/1.1 200 OK</code>","zh_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">HTTP/1.1 200 OK</code>"},{"en":"Date: Sat, 16 Mar 2024 18:33:08 GMT","en_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">Date: Sat, 16 Mar 2024 18:33:08 GMT</code>","zh_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">Date: Sat, 16 Mar 2024 18:33:08 GMT</code>"},{"en":"Content-Type: text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1","en_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">Content-Type: text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1</code>","zh_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">Content-Type: text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1</code>"},{"en":"&lt;!doctype html&gt;&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;&lt;head&gt;&lt;meta content=\"Search the world's information, including webpages, images, videos and more. Google has many special features to help you find exactly what you're looking for.\" name=\"description\"&gt;...","en_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">&lt;!doctype html&gt;&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;&lt;head&gt;&lt;meta content=\"Search the world's information, including webpages, images, videos and more. Google has many special features to help you find exactly what you're looking for.\" name=\"description\"&gt;...</code>","zh_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">&lt;!doctype html&gt;&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;&lt;head&gt;&lt;meta content=\"Search the world's information, including webpages, images, videos and more. Google has many special features to help you find exactly what you're looking for.\" name=\"description\"&gt;...</code>"},{"en":"The HTTP/1.1 200 OK tells us the version, and the status code (200) with its corresponding message (OK). There are two headers attached, the date of the response, and the content type. Then, the content contains the raw HTML of the webpage. If we opened this HTML in a web browser, it would look like an actual webpage.","en_html":"The <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">HTTP/1.1 200 OK</code> tells us the version, and the status code (200) with its corresponding message (OK). There are two headers attached, the date of the response, and the content type. Then, the content contains the raw HTML of the webpage. If we opened this HTML in a web browser, it would look like an actual webpage.","zh_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">HTTP/1.1 200 OK</code>告诉我们版本、状态码 (200) 及其对应消息 (OK)。附带两个头部：响应的日期和内容类型。然后，内容包含网页的原始 HTML。如果我们在网页浏览器中打开这个 HTML，它会看起来像一个实际的网页。"},{"en":"Here are some other examples. Notice that the content section is blank in the GET request, but contains data in the POST and PUT request. Conversely, the POST and PUT responses have no contents, but the GET response does.","en_html":"Here are some other examples. Notice that the content section is blank in the GET request, but contains data in the POST and PUT request. Conversely, the POST and PUT responses have no contents, but the GET response does.","zh_html":"这里还有一些其他例子。注意，GET 请求的内容部分为空，但 POST 和 PUT 请求中包含数据。反之，POST 和 PUT 响应的内容为空，但 GET 响应中有内容。"},{"en":"The status code and header tells us useful metadata about the request. For example, status 201 Created tells us that the file we send was successfully stored on the server. The header tells us where on the server the file was stored (and we might use that location to retrieve the file later).","en_html":"The status code and header tells us useful metadata about the request. For example, status 201 Created tells us that the file we send was successfully stored on the server. The header tells us where on the server the file was stored (and we might use that location to retrieve the file later).","zh_html":"状态码和头部告诉我们关于请求的有用元数据。例如，状态 201 Created 告诉我们发送的文件已成功存储在服务器上。头部告诉我们在服务器上文件存储的位置(我们以后可能使用该位置来检索文件)。"},{"en":"Speeding Up HTTP with Pipelining","en_html":"<a href=\"#speeding-up-http-with-pipelining\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"speeding-up-http-with-pipelining\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Speeding Up HTTP with Pipelining","zh_html":"使用流水线(Pipelining)加速 HTTP"},{"en":"Loading a single page in your web browser can require several HTTP requests. When you make a request for a YouTube video, your browser has to make separate requests for the video itself, the HTML with the other text on the webpage (e.g. video title, comments), thumbnails of related videos, and so on. Many of these requests probably go to the same server (e.g. YouTube’s server in this case).","en_html":"Loading a single page in your web browser can require several HTTP requests. When you make a request for a YouTube video, your browser has to make separate requests for the video itself, the HTML with the other text on the webpage (e.g. video title, comments), thumbnails of related videos, and so on. Many of these requests probably go to the same server (e.g. YouTube’s server in this case).","zh_html":"在网页浏览器中加载单个页面可能需要多个 HTTP 请求。当你请求一个 YouTube 视频时，你的浏览器需要分别请求视频本身、网页上的其他文本(如视频标题、评论)的 HTML、相关视频的缩略图等等。其中许多请求可能都发往同一台服务器(例如这里的 YouTube 服务器)。"},{"en":"Recall that HTTP runs over TCP. In the naive case, every separate request would require starting a new TCP connection with a 3-way handshake. After the request, we close the connection and then immediately re-do a handshake for the next request.","en_html":"Recall that HTTP runs over TCP. In the naive case, every separate request would require starting a new TCP connection with a 3-way handshake. After the request, we close the connection and then immediately re-do a handshake for the next request.","zh_html":"回顾一下，HTTP 运行在 TCP 之上。在简单的情况下，每个单独的请求都需要通过三次握手建立新的 TCP 连接。请求之后，我们关闭连接，然后立即为下一个请求重新进行握手。"},{"en":"HTTP 1.1 optimized this by allowing multiple HTTP requests and responses to be pipelined over the same connection. Now, we no longer need a separate TCP connection (with a separate handshake) for every request.","en_html":"HTTP 1.1 optimized this by allowing multiple HTTP requests and responses to be pipelined over the same connection. Now, we no longer need a separate TCP connection (with a separate handshake) for every request.","zh_html":"HTTP 1.1 对此进行了优化，允许在同一个连接上流水线传输(pipelined)多个 HTTP 请求和响应。现在，我们不再需要为每个请求都建立单独的 TCP 连接(以及单独的握手)。"},{"en":"One downside to this optimization is, the server now has to keep more simultaneous open connections. The server needs to have some way to time out connections. If the server gets overloaded with open connections, the client might get an error like 503 Service Unavailable. Attackers could exploit this in a denial-of-service attack.","en_html":"One downside to this optimization is, the server now has to keep more simultaneous open connections. The server needs to have some way to time out connections. If the server gets overloaded with open connections, the client might get an error like 503 Service Unavailable. Attackers could exploit this in a denial-of-service attack.","zh_html":"这种优化的一个缺点是，服务器现在需要保持更多的同时打开连接。服务器需要有某种方式来进行连接超时。如果服务器因打开连接过多而过载，客户端可能会收到 503 Service Unavailable 错误。攻击者可以利用这一点进行拒绝服务攻击(denial-of-service attack)。"},{"en":"Speeding Up HTTP with Caching: Types","en_html":"<a href=\"#speeding-up-http-with-caching-types\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"speeding-up-http-with-caching-types\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Speeding Up HTTP with Caching: Types","zh_html":"使用缓存加速 HTTP：类型(Speeding Up HTTP with Caching: Types)"},{"en":"Another strategy for speeding up HTTP is caching responses to avoid making duplicate requests for the same data.","en_html":"Another strategy for speeding up HTTP is caching responses to avoid making duplicate requests for the same data.","zh_html":"加速 HTTP 的另一种策略是缓存(caching)响应，以避免对相同数据进行重复请求。"},{"en":"If we don’t cache, every request must reach the server.","en_html":"If we don’t cache, every request must reach the server.","zh_html":"如果我们不缓存，每个请求都必须到达服务器。"},{"en":"There are three types of HTTP caches:","en_html":"There are three types of HTTP caches:","zh_html":"HTTP 缓存有三种类型："},{"en":"Private caches are associated with a specific end client connecting to the server (e.g. the cache in your own browser). Now, if the same user requests the same resource a second time, they can fetch the resource from their local cache. However, private caches are not shared between users.","en_html":"<strong>Private caches</strong> are associated with a specific end client connecting to the server (e.g. the cache in your own browser). Now, if the same user requests the same resource a second time, they can fetch the resource from their local cache. However, private caches are not shared between users.","zh_html":"<strong>私有缓存(Private caches)</strong>与连接服务器的特定最终客户端相关联(例如你自己浏览器中的缓存)。这样，如果同一个用户第二次请求相同的资源，他们可以从本地缓存中获取。但是，私有缓存在用户之间不共享。"},{"en":"Proxy caches are in the network (not on the end host), and are controlled by the network operator, not the application provider. These caches can be shared between lots of users, so a user requesting a resource for the first time might get the data from the proxy cache instead of the origin server.","en_html":"<strong>Proxy caches</strong> are in the network (not on the end host), and are controlled by the network operator, not the application provider. These caches can be shared between lots of users, so a user requesting a resource for the first time might get the data from the proxy cache instead of the origin server.","zh_html":"<strong>代理缓存(Proxy caches)</strong>位于网络中(不在终端主机上)，由网络运营商(而非应用提供商)控制。这些缓存在大量用户之间共享，因此首次请求某个资源的用户可能会从代理缓存而不是源服务器(origin server)获取数据。"},{"en":"One problem with proxy caches is, the clients need some way to be redirected to the proxy cache. The application isn’t running the proxy cache, so the origin server doesn’t necessarily know about the proxy cache. The network operator needs some way to control the end client to inform them about the proxy cache.","en_html":"One problem with proxy caches is, the clients need some way to be redirected to the proxy cache. The application isn’t running the proxy cache, so the origin server doesn’t necessarily know about the proxy cache. The network operator needs some way to control the end client to inform them about the proxy cache.","zh_html":"代理缓存的一个问题是，客户端需要某种方式被重定向到代理缓存。应用并不运行代理缓存，因此源服务器不一定知道代理缓存的存在。网络运营商需要某种方式来控制最终客户端，告知他们代理缓存的存在。"},{"en":"One common approach is lying in DNS responses, which is possible if the network operator controls both the proxy cache and the recursive resolver. When the client makes a request to the origin server, it has to look up the origin server’s IP address. The recursive resolver can lie and say, “The IP address of the origin server is, 1.2.3.4 (proxy cache’s IP address).” Now, requests to the origin server go to the proxy cache instead, who can serve cached responses. Or, if the requested resource isn’t in the proxy cache, the proxy cache can make a request to the origin server, and then the cache can serve the request back to the user.","en_html":"One common approach is lying in DNS responses, which is possible if the network operator controls both the proxy cache and the recursive resolver. When the client makes a request to the origin server, it has to look up the origin server’s IP address. The recursive resolver can lie and say, “The IP address of the origin server is, 1.2.3.4 (proxy cache’s IP address).” Now, requests to the origin server go to the proxy cache instead, who can serve cached responses. Or, if the requested resource isn’t in the proxy cache, the proxy cache can make a request to the origin server, and then the cache can serve the request back to the user.","zh_html":"一种常见的方法是在 DNS 响应中\"说谎\"，这要求网络运营商同时控制代理缓存和递归解析器。当客户端向源服务器发出请求时，它必须查找源服务器的 IP 地址。递归解析器可以撒谎说：\"源服务器的 IP 地址是 1.2.3.4(代理缓存的 IP 地址)。\"现在，发往源服务器的请求实际上发到了代理缓存，代理缓存可以提供缓存的响应。或者，如果请求的资源不在代理缓存中，代理缓存可以向源服务器发出请求，然后将响应返回给用户。"},{"en":"Another problem with proxy caches is, the application isn’t managing the proxy cache. The origin server has to trust that the proxy cache is doing the right thing (e.g. respecting cache expiry dates, serving the correct data).","en_html":"Another problem with proxy caches is, the application isn’t managing the proxy cache. The origin server has to trust that the proxy cache is doing the right thing (e.g. respecting cache expiry dates, serving the correct data).","zh_html":"代理缓存的另一个问题是，应用并不管理代理缓存。源服务器必须信任代理缓存正在做正确的事情(例如尊重缓存过期日期，提供正确的数据)。"},{"en":"Managed caches are in the network, and are controlled by the application provider. Note that managed cache servers are deployed separately, and are not the original server that generated the content. Because these caches are controlled by the application provider, this gives the application more control.","en_html":"<strong>Managed caches</strong> are in the network, and are controlled by the application provider. Note that managed cache servers are deployed separately, and are not the original server that generated the content. Because these caches are controlled by the application provider, this gives the application more control.","zh_html":"<strong>托管缓存(Managed caches)</strong>位于网络中，由应用提供商控制。请注意，托管缓存服务器是单独部署的，而不是生成内容的原始服务器。由于这些缓存由应用提供商控制，这给了应用更多的控制权。"},{"en":"Because applications control both the origin server and the cache, they can redirect users to the caches themselves. For example, if you request a YouTube video page from the origin server, the reply might contain the HTML (video title, comments). The HTML might then include links to specifically fetch the video and images from the proxy caches (e.g. load from static.youtube.com instead of www.youtube.com).","en_html":"Because applications control both the origin server and the cache, they can redirect users to the caches themselves. For example, if you request a YouTube video page from the origin server, the reply might contain the HTML (video title, comments). The HTML might then include links to specifically fetch the video and images from the proxy caches (e.g. load from static.youtube.com instead of www.youtube.com).","zh_html":"因为应用同时控制源服务器和缓存，他们可以自己将用户重定向到缓存。例如，如果你向源服务器请求一个 YouTube 视频页面，回复可能包含 HTML(视频标题、评论)。然后，HTML 可能包含链接，专门从代理缓存获取视频和图片(例如从 static.youtube.com 而非 www.youtube.com 加载)。"},{"en":"Speeding Up HTTP with Caching: Benefits and Drawbacks","en_html":"<a href=\"#speeding-up-http-with-caching-benefits-and-drawbacks\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"speeding-up-http-with-caching-benefits-and-drawbacks\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Speeding Up HTTP with Caching: Benefits and Drawbacks","zh_html":"使用缓存加速 HTTP：好处与弊端(Speeding Up HTTP with Caching: Benefits and Drawbacks)"},{"en":"Caching benefits everybody. The client gets to load pages faster, because they can avoid making duplicate requests, and use nearby proxies. The ISPs benefit because there are fewer HTTP requests/responses being sent over the network, so they can build less bandwidth. Servers benefit because users make fewer requests, and they don’t need to process as many requests.","en_html":"Caching benefits everybody. The client gets to load pages faster, because they can avoid making duplicate requests, and use nearby proxies. The ISPs benefit because there are fewer HTTP requests/responses being sent over the network, so they can build less bandwidth. Servers benefit because users make fewer requests, and they don’t need to process as many requests.","zh_html":"缓存对每个人都有好处。客户端可以更快地加载页面，因为他们可以避免重复请求并使用附近的代理。ISP 受益，因为网络上传送的 HTTP 请求/响应更少，因此他们可以建设更少的带宽。服务器受益，因为用户发起的请求更少，不需要处理那么多请求。"},{"en":"Clients, ISPs, and servers all care about giving good performance to the client. The client wants to watch videos in high quality, and ISPs and applications will get more customers by delivering good performance. Caching helps everybody achieve this, because the client can get their request served more efficiently from a closer cache (local, or in-network), with less latency. Also, recall that TCP throughput and RTT are inversely proportional, so a shorter RTT to a closer server means that we get higher throughput. This is especially helpful for large content like videos.","en_html":"Clients, ISPs, and servers all care about giving good performance to the client. The client wants to watch videos in high quality, and ISPs and applications will get more customers by delivering good performance. Caching helps everybody achieve this, because the client can get their request served more efficiently from a closer cache (local, or in-network), with less latency. Also, recall that TCP throughput and RTT are inversely proportional, so a shorter RTT to a closer server means that we get higher throughput. This is especially helpful for large content like videos.","zh_html":"客户端、ISP 和服务器都关心为客户端提供良好的性能。客户端希望观看高质量视频，ISP 和应用通过提供良好的性能来获得更多客户。缓存帮助所有人实现这一目标，因为客户端可以从更近的缓存(本地或在网络中)以更低的延迟更高效地获取服务。另外，回顾一下 TCP 吞吐量与 RTT 成反比，因此到更近服务器的更短 RTT 意味着更高的吞吐量。这对于视频等大内容尤其有帮助。"},{"en":"When thinking about caching, we have to consider whether the content will change on future requests. Some HTTP resources are static. If you make a request for the Google logo, it stays the same across multiple requests.","en_html":"When thinking about caching, we have to consider whether the content will change on future requests. Some HTTP resources are static. If you make a request for the Google logo, it stays the same across multiple requests.","zh_html":"在考虑缓存时，我们必须考虑内容在未来的请求中是否会改变。一些 HTTP 资源是静态的(static)。如果你请求 Google 的 logo，它在多次请求中保持不变。"},{"en":"Other HTTP resources are dynamic. If you make a Google search request, the response might change depending on who asks and when they ask. The server needs to dynamically generate a different response for every request.","en_html":"Other HTTP resources are dynamic. If you make a Google search request, the response might change depending on who asks and when they ask. The server needs to dynamically generate a different response for every request.","zh_html":"其他 HTTP 资源是动态的(dynamic)。如果你发起 Google 搜索请求，响应可能会根据谁在何时询问而改变。服务器需要为每个请求动态生成不同的响应。"},{"en":"Some resources are static and can be cached and served from proxy or managed caches, while other resources must be dynamically generated. For example, if you make a Google search, the HTML response probably needs to be dynamically generated by the origin server. However, the HTML can include a link to fetch the Google logo, a static resource, from one of the managed cache servers.","en_html":"Some resources are static and can be cached and served from proxy or managed caches, while other resources must be dynamically generated. For example, if you make a Google search, the HTML response probably needs to be dynamically generated by the origin server. However, the HTML can include a link to fetch the Google logo, a static resource, from one of the managed cache servers.","zh_html":"一些资源是静态的，可以从代理或托管缓存中提供；而其他资源必须动态生成。例如，如果你进行 Google 搜索，HTML 响应可能需要由源服务器动态生成。然而，HTML 可以包含一个链接，从某个托管缓存服务器获取 Google logo 这个静态资源。"},{"en":"Conveniently, larger resources like images and videos are static, and can be cached aggressively. Dynamic content, like customized HTML pages, tend to be smaller. Clients can request the dynamic content from the origin server (far away), and use caches and proxies (closer) for all the static content.","en_html":"Conveniently, larger resources like images and videos are static, and can be cached aggressively. Dynamic content, like customized HTML pages, tend to be smaller. Clients can request the dynamic content from the origin server (far away), and use caches and proxies (closer) for all the static content.","zh_html":"方便的是，较大的资源如图片和视频是静态的，可以被积极缓存。动态内容(如定制的 HTML 页面)往往较小。客户端可以从源服务器(距离较远)请求动态内容，而对所有静态内容使用缓存和代理(距离较近)。"},{"en":"Speeding Up HTTP with Caching: Implementation","en_html":"<a href=\"#speeding-up-http-with-caching-implementation\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"speeding-up-http-with-caching-implementation\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Speeding Up HTTP with Caching: Implementation","zh_html":"使用缓存加速 HTTP：实现(Speeding Up HTTP with Caching: Implementation)"},{"en":"To implement caching, we’ll need to use headers to carry some metadata about caching (e.g. how long to cache the data). This is another example of headers allowing extensibility of the original protocol (which did not support caching).","en_html":"To implement caching, we’ll need to use headers to carry some metadata about caching (e.g. how long to cache the data). This is another example of headers allowing extensibility of the original protocol (which did not support caching).","zh_html":"为了实现缓存，我们需要使用头部来携带关于缓存的元数据(例如数据可以缓存多久)。这是头部使原始协议(原本不支持缓存)具有可扩展性的另一个例子。"},{"en":"The original legacy caching functionality in HTTP/1.0 used the Expires header, which just specified how long the data can be cached. In HTTP/1.1, a more sophisticated Cache-Control header was introduced. To support compatibility, some web servers will return data with both headers. HTTP/1.0 clients won’t understand the newer Cache-Control header and will ignore it. HTTP/1.1 clients will prioritize the newer Cache-Control header over the older Expires header.","en_html":"The original legacy caching functionality in HTTP/1.0 used the Expires header, which just specified how long the data can be cached. In HTTP/1.1, a more sophisticated Cache-Control header was introduced. To support compatibility, some web servers will return data with both headers. HTTP/1.0 clients won’t understand the newer Cache-Control header and will ignore it. HTTP/1.1 clients will prioritize the newer Cache-Control header over the older Expires header.","zh_html":"HTTP/1.0 中的原始传统缓存功能使用 Expires 头部，它只指定数据可以缓存多久。在 HTTP/1.1 中，引入了更复杂的 Cache-Control 头部。为了支持兼容性，一些 Web 服务器会同时返回包含这两个头部的数据。HTTP/1.0 的客户端不理解新的 Cache-Control 头部，会忽略它。HTTP/1.1 的客户端会优先考虑新的 Cache-Control 头部而不是旧的 Expires 头部。"},{"en":"The Cache-Control header specifies what types of caches can cache the data, and how long the data can be cached. For example, if the resource is dynamic and changes per user, but stays the same across time for a specific user, then the server could reply with: Cache-Control: private, max-age:86400. This says that this content should only be stored in a user’s local cache (not in shared proxy/managed caches), and can be stored for one day (86400 seconds).","en_html":"The Cache-Control header specifies what types of caches can cache the data, and how long the data can be cached. For example, if the resource is dynamic and changes per user, but stays the same across time for a specific user, then the server could reply with: Cache-Control: private, max-age:86400. This says that this content should only be stored in a user’s local cache (not in shared proxy/managed caches), and can be stored for one day (86400 seconds).","zh_html":"Cache-Control 头部指定哪些类型的缓存可以缓存数据，以及数据可以缓存多久。例如，如果资源是动态的，因用户而异，但对于特定用户来说随时间保持不变，那么服务器可以回复：Cache-Control: private, max-age:86400。这表示该内容只应存储在用户的本地缓存中(不能存储在共享的代理/托管缓存中)，并且可以存储一天(86400 秒)。"},{"en":"Some data cannot be cached (e.g. dynamic content that changes frequently). In this case, the server can set Cache-Control: no-store to indicate that the client and proxy cannot cache the content.","en_html":"Some data cannot be cached (e.g. dynamic content that changes frequently). In this case, the server can set Cache-Control: no-store to indicate that the client and proxy cannot cache the content.","zh_html":"有些数据不能被缓存(例如频繁变化的动态内容)。在这种情况下，服务器可以设置 Cache-Control: no-store 以指示客户端和代理不能缓存此内容。"},{"en":"The Cache-Control header is optional, so there’s no guarantee that the client will read or respect the header. You can think of this header as a request from the server cache something. This is especially a concern for proxy caches, which are not operated by the application provider. By contrast, a private cache is run by the client (i.e. their browser) and breaking rules only affects the client themselves. A managed cache is run by the same application provider, so they can enforce that rules from the origin server are obeyed by the managed caches.","en_html":"The Cache-Control header is optional, so there’s no guarantee that the client will read or respect the header. You can think of this header as a request from the server cache something. This is especially a concern for proxy caches, which are not operated by the application provider. By contrast, a private cache is run by the client (i.e. their browser) and breaking rules only affects the client themselves. A managed cache is run by the same application provider, so they can enforce that rules from the origin server are obeyed by the managed caches.","zh_html":"Cache-Control 头部是可选的，因此不能保证客户端会读取或尊重该头部。你可以将这个头部视为来自服务器的缓存请求。对于代理缓存来说，这一点尤其值得关注，因为它们不是由应用提供商运营的。相比之下，私有缓存由客户端(即他们的浏览器)运行，违反规则只会影响客户端自己。托管缓存由同一个应用提供商运行，因此他们可以确保源服务器的规则被托管缓存遵守。"},{"en":"This header can be used for more complex policies as well. For example, the server might say, you can cache this data, but before you use the cached data, please make an HTTP HEAD request to re-request the header and re-validate the data. If the header indicates that the data has changed, invalidate the cache.","en_html":"This header can be used for more complex policies as well. For example, the server might say, you can cache this data, but before you use the cached data, please make an HTTP HEAD request to re-request the header and re-validate the data. If the header indicates that the data has changed, invalidate the cache.","zh_html":"这个头部还可以用于更复杂的策略。例如，服务器可以说，你可以缓存这些数据，但在使用缓存数据之前，请先发出 HTTP HEAD 请求重新请求头部并重新验证数据。如果头部指示数据已更改，则使缓存失效。"},{"en":"Content Delivery Networks (CDNs)","en_html":"<a href=\"#content-delivery-networks-cdns\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"content-delivery-networks-cdns\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Content Delivery Networks (CDNs)","zh_html":"内容分发网络(Content Delivery Networks, CDNs)"},{"en":"Earlier, we saw that managed caches are a good strategy for caching and improving user performance. Unlike private caches, they’re shared between users (e.g. a user requesting something for the first time can be served by the cache). Also, unlike proxy caches, they’re controlled by the application provider, which gives the application more control. The application can ensure that the caches follow rules set by the origin server, and the origin server can control which caches the user is redirected to.","en_html":"Earlier, we saw that managed caches are a good strategy for caching and improving user performance. Unlike private caches, they’re shared between users (e.g. a user requesting something for the first time can be served by the cache). Also, unlike proxy caches, they’re controlled by the application provider, which gives the application more control. The application can ensure that the caches follow rules set by the origin server, and the origin server can control which caches the user is redirected to.","zh_html":"之前，我们看到托管缓存是缓存和改善用户体验的好策略。与私有缓存不同，它们在用户之间共享(例如，首次请求某内容的用户可以由缓存提供服务)。此外，与代理缓存不同，它们由应用提供商控制，这给了应用更多的控制权。应用可以确保缓存遵守源服务器设定的规则，并且源服务器可以控制用户被重定向到哪些缓存。"},{"en":"Deploying managed caches across the network leads us to the idea of content delivery networks (CDNs), which are sets of servers in the network serving content (e.g. HTTP resources).","en_html":"Deploying managed caches across the network leads us to the idea of <strong>content delivery networks (CDNs)</strong>, which are sets of servers in the network serving content (e.g. HTTP resources).","zh_html":"在网络中部署托管缓存引出了<strong>内容分发网络(content delivery networks, CDNs)</strong>的概念，它是在网络中提供服务内容(例如 HTTP 资源)的一组服务器。"},{"en":"For good performance, we try to put CDNs close to end users. Here, close means geographically close, but also close from a network perspective (fewer hops).","en_html":"For good performance, we try to put CDNs close to end users. Here, close means geographically close, but also close from a network perspective (fewer hops).","zh_html":"为了获得良好的性能，我们尝试将 CDN 放置得离最终用户近一些。这里的近既指地理上的近，也指网络角度的近(更少的跳数，hops)。"},{"en":"CDNs give us all the benefits of caching. Users get higher-performance delivery of content, since servers are closer. We can reduce the amount of network bandwidth and infrastructure needed, since users are making most requests to nearby servers instead of a single origin server (possibly far away).","en_html":"CDNs give us all the benefits of caching. Users get higher-performance delivery of content, since servers are closer. We can reduce the amount of network bandwidth and infrastructure needed, since users are making most requests to nearby servers instead of a single origin server (possibly far away).","zh_html":"CDN 给我们带来了缓存的所有好处。用户获得更高性能的内容交付，因为服务器更近了。我们可以减少所需的网络带宽和基础设施量，因为用户大部分请求都发往附近的服务器，而不是单一的(可能很远的)源服务器。"},{"en":"CDNs allow providers to scale their server infrastructure more easily. With a single origin server, we’d have to scale that server by making it incredibly powerful and giving it incredibly high bandwidth. By contrast, with CDNs, we can scale just by adding more small servers throughout the Internet.","en_html":"CDNs allow providers to scale their server infrastructure more easily. With a single origin server, we’d have to scale that server by making it incredibly powerful and giving it incredibly high bandwidth. By contrast, with CDNs, we can scale just by adding more small servers throughout the Internet.","zh_html":"CDN 使提供商能够更轻松地扩展其服务器基础设施。对于单一的源服务器，我们必须通过让服务器变得极其强大并拥有极高的带宽来扩展它。相比之下，使用 CDN，我们只需通过在整个互联网中增加更多的小型服务器来扩展。"},{"en":"CDNs also provide better redundancy for providers. If a single origin server goes down, the service might become unavailable. By contrast, with CDNs, if one server goes down, users can still be redirected to other servers.","en_html":"CDNs also provide better redundancy for providers. If a single origin server goes down, the service might become unavailable. By contrast, with CDNs, if one server goes down, users can still be redirected to other servers.","zh_html":"CDN 还为提供商提供了更好的冗余。如果单一的源服务器宕机，服务可能变得不可用。相比之下，使用 CDN，如果一台服务器宕机，用户仍然可以被重定向到其他服务器。"},{"en":"CDN Deployment","en_html":"<a href=\"#cdn-deployment\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"cdn-deployment\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> CDN Deployment","zh_html":"CDN 部署(CDN Deployment)"},{"en":"Recall our model of the Internet: The client’s request is forwarded through WAN routers (owned by the ISP) until it reaches a peering location. Then, the request goes to a peering location in the application provider’s network. The request goes through the application’s WAN networks until it reaches a datacenter network, where the origin server lives.","en_html":"Recall our model of the Internet: The client’s request is forwarded through WAN routers (owned by the ISP) until it reaches a peering location. Then, the request goes to a peering location in the application provider’s network. The request goes through the application’s WAN networks until it reaches a datacenter network, where the origin server lives.","zh_html":"回顾我们的互联网模型：客户端的请求通过 WAN 路由器(由 ISP 拥有)转发，直到到达一个对等互连点(peering location)。然后，请求进入应用提供商网络中的对等互连点。请求通过应用的 WAN 网络，直到到达源服务器所在的数据中心网络。"},{"en":"If we don’t deploy any CDN, every request has to reach the origin server. This has the maximum latency (compared to later options with CDNs), resulting in the lowest performance. Also, this requires the most bandwidth to be traversed, which means we have to build more bandwidth. Finally, this requires the origin server to scale to handle every request.","en_html":"If we don’t deploy any CDN, every request has to reach the origin server. This has the maximum latency (compared to later options with CDNs), resulting in the lowest performance. Also, this requires the most bandwidth to be traversed, which means we have to build more bandwidth. Finally, this requires the origin server to scale to handle every request.","zh_html":"如果我们不部署任何 CDN，每个请求都必须到达源服务器。这具有最大的延迟(与后面使用 CDN 的选项相比)，导致最低的性能。同时，这也需要最大的带宽传输量，意味着我们必须建设更多的带宽。最后，这要求源服务器扩展以处理每个请求。"},{"en":"A better option would be to deploy some CDN servers at the edge of the application provider network. For example, if Google’s network peers with ISP networks in New York, we could put some CDNs there.","en_html":"A better option would be to deploy some CDN servers at the edge of the application provider network. For example, if Google’s network peers with ISP networks in New York, we could put some CDNs there.","zh_html":"更好的选择是在应用提供商网络的边缘部署一些 CDN 服务器。例如，如果 Google 的网络在纽约与 ISP 网络对等互连，我们可以将一些 CDN 部署在那里。"},{"en":"Now, the amount of bandwidth sent over the application provider’s network is much lower. The origin server sends the video to the CDN once, and the CDN can serve that video to many users. The application network no longer needs to scale its WAN network.","en_html":"Now, the amount of bandwidth sent over the application provider’s network is much lower. The origin server sends the video to the CDN once, and the CDN can serve that video to many users. The application network no longer needs to scale its WAN network.","zh_html":"现在，通过应用提供商网络传输的带宽量要低得多。源服务器将视频发送到 CDN 一次，CDN 就可以将该视频提供给许多用户。应用网络不再需要扩展其 WAN 网络。"},{"en":"Also, as we saw earlier, we can now scale by adding more CDNs, instead of upgrading a single origin server. We also have more redundancy.","en_html":"Also, as we saw earlier, we can now scale by adding more CDNs, instead of upgrading a single origin server. We also have more redundancy.","zh_html":"而且，正如我们之前看到的，我们现在可以通过添加更多 CDN 来扩展，而不是升级单个源服务器。我们还获得了更多的冗余。"},{"en":"We can do even better and push caching deeper into the network. Now, the application is deploying servers inside the ISP’s network.","en_html":"We can do even better and push caching deeper into the network. Now, the application is deploying servers inside the ISP’s network.","zh_html":"我们可以做得更好，将缓存推得更深，进入网络内部。现在，应用将服务器部署在 ISP 的网络内部。"},{"en":"Why would an ISP agree to let the application deploy a CDN in their network? It turns out this is mutually beneficial for everybody. The ISP’s customers will get better performance because they can use this new closer CDN. Also, the heavy traffic between users and the CDN is now all contained within the ISP’s network. This means that ISP needs less bandwidth in the peering connection between the ISP and the application (since the content is sent only once across that peering connection).","en_html":"Why would an ISP agree to let the application deploy a CDN in their network? It turns out this is mutually beneficial for everybody. The ISP’s customers will get better performance because they can use this new closer CDN. Also, the heavy traffic between users and the CDN is now all contained within the ISP’s network. This means that ISP needs less bandwidth in the peering connection between the ISP and the application (since the content is sent only once across that peering connection).","zh_html":"为什么 ISP 会同意让应用在其网络中部署 CDN？事实证明这对双方都有利。ISP 的客户将获得更好的性能，因为他们可以使用这个更近的新 CDN。同时，用户和 CDN 之间的重流量现在完全包含在 ISP 网络内部。这意味着 ISP 在 ISP 和应用之间的对等连接中需要的带宽更少(因为内容只通过该对等连接传输一次)。"},{"en":"In practice, the ISP and CDN often cooperate to deploy servers. For example, the application provides the servers for free, and the ISP connects the server to the network for free. In some cases, the ISP and CDN need to negotiate on some payment (CDN to ISP, or ISP to CDN), depending on where in the network the server is deployed, and the costs of the server and the connectivity. Still, both parties have an interest in deploying these servers.","en_html":"In practice, the ISP and CDN often cooperate to deploy servers. For example, the application provides the servers for free, and the ISP connects the server to the network for free. In some cases, the ISP and CDN need to negotiate on some payment (CDN to ISP, or ISP to CDN), depending on where in the network the server is deployed, and the costs of the server and the connectivity. Still, both parties have an interest in deploying these servers.","zh_html":"在实践中，ISP 和 CDN 经常合作部署服务器。例如，应用免费提供服务器，ISP 免费将服务器连接到网络。在某些情况下，ISP 和 CDN 需要协商支付费用(CDN 向 ISP 支付，或 ISP 向 CDN 支付)，取决于服务器在网络中的部署位置，以及服务器和连接的成本。尽管如此，双方都有兴趣部署这些服务器。"},{"en":"We could try to go even further, but eventually, we encounter cost-benefit trade-offs. In the most extreme case, we could deploy a CDN in everybody’s home, but the cost probably outweighs the benefit. In particular, CDNs work best when multiple users are using it. The collective cache is larger, and one deployment can reach many users.","en_html":"We could try to go even further, but eventually, we encounter cost-benefit trade-offs. In the most extreme case, we could deploy a CDN in everybody’s home, but the cost probably outweighs the benefit. In particular, CDNs work best when multiple users are using it. The collective cache is larger, and one deployment can reach many users.","zh_html":"我们可以尝试走得更远，但最终会遇到成本效益的权衡。在最极端的情况下，我们可以在每个人的家中部署 CDN，但成本可能超过收益。特别是，CDN 在多个用户使用时效果最好。集体缓存更大，一个部署可以覆盖许多用户。"},{"en":"More generally, there’s a trade-off between the cost of adding new CDNs, and the money you save from building less bandwidth. In practice, CDNs do exist in ISP networks because they’re still profitable to install there.","en_html":"More generally, there’s a trade-off between the cost of adding new CDNs, and the money you save from building less bandwidth. In practice, CDNs do exist in ISP networks because they’re still profitable to install there.","zh_html":"更一般地说，在添加新 CDN 的成本和因建设更少带宽而节省的资金之间存在权衡。在实践中，CDN 确实存在于 ISP 网络中，因为它们在那里部署仍然有利可图。"},{"en":"A 2023 Sandvine (packet inspection company) report showed that 15% of all Internet traffic is from Netflix, 11.4% of traffic is from YouTube, and 4.5% is from Disney+. If an ISP installs servers for just these three applications in their network, they could potentially build 25% less network capacity.","en_html":"A 2023 Sandvine (packet inspection company) report showed that 15% of all Internet traffic is from Netflix, 11.4% of traffic is from YouTube, and 4.5% is from Disney+. If an ISP installs servers for just these three applications in their network, they could potentially build 25% less network capacity.","zh_html":"2023 年 Sandvine(一家数据包检查公司)的报告显示，所有互联网流量的 15% 来自 Netflix，11.4% 来自 YouTube，4.5% 来自 Disney+。如果一个 ISP 在其网络中仅为这三个应用安装服务器，他们可能可以减少 25% 的网络容量建设。"},{"en":"Major application providers like Google, Netflix, Amazon, and Facebook deploy CDNs, both in their own networks, and in ISP networks.","en_html":"Major application providers like Google, Netflix, Amazon, and Facebook deploy CDNs, both in their own networks, and in ISP networks.","zh_html":"主要的应用提供商如 Google、Netflix、Amazon 和 Facebook 都部署了 CDN，既在自己的网络中，也在 ISP 网络中。"},{"en":"If you’re an application provider, you might not be a tech giant like Google or Amazon, but you still want your content to be served through a CDN for good performance. These smaller applications probably can’t afford to install their own CDNs. However, companies like Cloudflare, Akamai, and Edgio have deployed CDNs, and you can pay those companies to deploy your content on their existing CDN. These CDN providers also deploy infrastructure in both their own networks and in ISP networks.","en_html":"If you’re an application provider, you might not be a tech giant like Google or Amazon, but you still want your content to be served through a CDN for good performance. These smaller applications probably can’t afford to install their own CDNs. However, companies like Cloudflare, Akamai, and Edgio have deployed CDNs, and you can pay those companies to deploy your content on their existing CDN. These CDN providers also deploy infrastructure in both their own networks and in ISP networks.","zh_html":"如果你是一个应用提供商，你可能不是 Google 或 Amazon 这样的科技巨头，但你仍然希望你的内容通过 CDN 提供以获得良好的性能。这些较小的应用可能负担不起安装自己的 CDN。然而，像 Cloudflare、Akamai 和 Edgio 这样的公司已经部署了 CDN，你可以付费让这些公司将你的内容部署在他们现有的 CDN 上。这些 CDN 提供商也在自己的网络和 ISP 网络中部署基础设施。"},{"en":"CDNs can also be used by ISPs, because ISPs themselves can also have applications serving content. When you pay for Internet service, the ISP might also offer TV service (live TV, or video on demand). These ISPs install their own CDNs to efficiently serve that TV content to you.","en_html":"CDNs can also be used by ISPs, because ISPs themselves can also have applications serving content. When you pay for Internet service, the ISP might also offer TV service (live TV, or video on demand). These ISPs install their own CDNs to efficiently serve that TV content to you.","zh_html":"ISP 也可以使用 CDN，因为 ISP 本身也可能有提供服务内容的应用。当你支付互联网服务费用时，ISP 可能还提供电视服务(直播电视或视频点播)。这些 ISP 安装自己的 CDN 以高效地向你提供电视内容。"},{"en":"Fundamentally, the servers in CDNs are the same as any other servers on the Internet providing content, though they are often highly optimized for storing and delivering large amounts of content. Some servers might be better at storing and serving large amounts of content, while others might be better at rapidly serving smaller pieces of content to a large number of customers.","en_html":"Fundamentally, the servers in CDNs are the same as any other servers on the Internet providing content, though they are often highly optimized for storing and delivering large amounts of content. Some servers might be better at storing and serving large amounts of content, while others might be better at rapidly serving smaller pieces of content to a large number of customers.","zh_html":"从根本上说，CDN 中的服务器与互联网上提供内容的其他任何服务器是一样的，不过它们通常针对存储和交付大量内容进行了高度优化。有些服务器可能更擅长存储和提供大量内容，而另一些则更擅长快速向大量客户提供较小的内容片段。"},{"en":"Directing Clients to Caches","en_html":"<a href=\"#directing-clients-to-caches\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"directing-clients-to-caches\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Directing Clients to Caches","zh_html":"将客户端引导到缓存(Directing Clients to Caches)"},{"en":"In a CDN, many different servers throughout the Internet are providing the same content. How does the client know which server to contact?","en_html":"In a CDN, many different servers throughout the Internet are providing the same content. How does the client know which server to contact?","zh_html":"在 CDN 中，互联网上许多不同的服务器都在提供相同的内容。客户端如何知道该联系哪台服务器？"},{"en":"Some of the tricks from DNS can also apply to CDNs. We could use anycast, where multiple servers advertise the same IP prefix. This allows the routing algorithm to find the best path to any one of the servers.","en_html":"Some of the tricks from DNS can also apply to CDNs. We could use anycast, where multiple servers advertise the same IP prefix. This allows the routing algorithm to find the best path to any one of the servers.","zh_html":"DNS 中的一些技巧也可以应用于 CDN。我们可以使用任播(anycast)，让多台服务器通告相同的 IP 前缀。这允许路由算法找到通往任何一台服务器的最佳路径。"},{"en":"One problem with anycast is with long-running connections. Suppose the client has an ongoing TCP connection with one of the servers. During the connection, some intermediate link in the network fails. Since all the servers have the same IP address, from an intermediate router’s perspective, forwarding to any of the servers is valid. The intermediate router may now start forwarding packets to a different server (with the same IP address). However, the TCP connection was with the original server, and this new server has no way to continue the original connection.","en_html":"One problem with anycast is with long-running connections. Suppose the client has an ongoing TCP connection with one of the servers. During the connection, some intermediate link in the network fails. Since all the servers have the same IP address, from an intermediate router’s perspective, forwarding to any of the servers is valid. The intermediate router may now start forwarding packets to a different server (with the same IP address). However, the TCP connection was with the original server, and this new server has no way to continue the original connection.","zh_html":"任播的一个问题是长连接(long-running connections)。假设客户端与其中一台服务器建立了正在进行的 TCP 连接。在连接期间，网络中某个中间链路发生故障。由于所有服务器都有相同的 IP 地址，从中间路由器的角度来看，转发到任何一台服务器都是有效的。中间路由器现在可能开始将数据包转发到不同的服务器(具有相同的 IP 地址)。然而，TCP 连接是与原始服务器建立的，而这台新服务器没有办法继续原始连接。"},{"en":"Note that this problem didn’t apply when we used anycast in DNS, because DNS connections are very short (usually just one UDP packet).","en_html":"Note that this problem didn’t apply when we used anycast in DNS, because DNS connections are very short (usually just one UDP packet).","zh_html":"请注意，这个问题在 DNS 中使用任播时并不存在，因为 DNS 连接非常短(通常只有一个 UDP 数据包)。"},{"en":"We could also use DNS to load-balance. Unlike in anycast, the servers now have different IP addresses, though they still all have the same domain. When the client queries for the domain-to-IP mapping, the DNS name server can provide a different IP address depending on the client’s location.","en_html":"We could also use DNS to load-balance. Unlike in anycast, the servers now have different IP addresses, though they still all have the same domain. When the client queries for the domain-to-IP mapping, the DNS name server can provide a different IP address depending on the client’s location.","zh_html":"我们也可以使用 DNS 进行负载均衡。与任播不同，服务器现在有不同的 IP 地址，尽管它们仍然拥有相同的域名。当客户端查询域名到 IP 的映射时，DNS 名称服务器可以根据客户端的位置提供不同的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"This DNS-based approach doesn’t have the same problem with long-lived connections that anycast did, because the servers now have different addresses. The router won’t suddenly start forwarding packets to a different server.","en_html":"This DNS-based approach doesn’t have the same problem with long-lived connections that anycast did, because the servers now have different addresses. The router won’t suddenly start forwarding packets to a different server.","zh_html":"这种基于 DNS 的方法没有任播存在的长连接问题，因为服务器现在有不同的地址。路由器不会突然开始将数据包转发到不同的服务器。"},{"en":"One problem with the DNS-based approach is lack of granularity. As an extreme example, suppose everybody in Comcast’s ISP used the same recursive resolver. This means that everybody sends their DNS queries to the resolver, who then makes the query to the application name server. The application name server can only see that the DNS request came from Comcast, and has to give a single IP address back to Comcast. Now, every user in Comcast’s network is using the same server, even if the users are all over the world.","en_html":"One problem with the DNS-based approach is lack of granularity. As an extreme example, suppose everybody in Comcast’s ISP used the same recursive resolver. This means that everybody sends their DNS queries to the resolver, who then makes the query to the application name server. The application name server can only see that the DNS request came from Comcast, and has to give a single IP address back to Comcast. Now, every user in Comcast’s network is using the same server, even if the users are all over the world.","zh_html":"基于 DNS 的方法的一个问题是缺乏细粒度(granularity)。一个极端的例子是，假设 Comcast 的 ISP 中每个人都使用同一个递归解析器。这意味着每个人都把 DNS 查询发送到该解析器，然后解析器再向应用名称服务器发起查询。应用名称服务器只能看到 DNS 请求来自 Comcast，因此必须向 Comcast 返回一个单一的 IP 地址。现在，即使这些用户分布在世界各地，Comcast 网络中的每个用户都使用同一台服务器。"},{"en":"A more robust approach than anycast or DNS is application-level mapping. When the origin server receives an HTTP request, the links in the response can point to different servers (e.g. static1.google.com or static2.google.com, two servers in different places), depending on where the request came from. Or, the origin server can reply with an HTTP 300-level status code to redirect the user to the appropriate server.","en_html":"A more robust approach than anycast or DNS is application-level mapping. When the origin server receives an HTTP request, the links in the response can point to different servers (e.g. static1.google.com or static2.google.com, two servers in different places), depending on where the request came from. Or, the origin server can reply with an HTTP 300-level status code to redirect the user to the appropriate server.","zh_html":"比任播或 DNS 更健壮的方法是应用层映射(application-level mapping)。当源服务器收到 HTTP 请求时，响应中的链接可以指向不同的服务器(例如 static1.google.com 或 static2.google.com，位于不同地方的两台服务器)，具体取决于请求的来源。或者，源服务器可以用 HTTP 300 级别的状态码回复，将用户重定向到适当的服务器。"},{"en":"This application-level approach doesn’t have the granularity problem of DNS, because the application can see the client’s address in the HTTP request. This also doesn’t have the anycast problem, since different servers can have different IP addresses.","en_html":"This application-level approach doesn’t have the granularity problem of DNS, because the application can see the client’s address in the HTTP request. This also doesn’t have the anycast problem, since different servers can have different IP addresses.","zh_html":"这种应用层方法没有 DNS 的细粒度问题，因为应用可以在 HTTP 请求中看到客户端的地址。也没有任播的问题，因为不同的服务器可以有不同的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"However, just like in DNS load-balancing, the application still needs some way to guess the closest server to the client (where close might be geographic or based on network topology).","en_html":"However, just like in DNS load-balancing, the application still needs some way to guess the closest server to the client (where close might be geographic or based on network topology).","zh_html":"然而，就像在 DNS 负载均衡中一样，应用仍然需要某种方式来猜测离客户端最近的服务器(近的定义可以是地理上的，也可以基于网络拓扑)。"},{"en":"One benefit of application-level mapping is additional granularity depending on the content. For example, popular videos can be deployed to lots of servers, allowing every client to get the video from a nearby server. By contrast, unpopular videos that are rarely accessed can be deployed to fewer servers, and require users to go further for the content.","en_html":"One benefit of application-level mapping is additional granularity depending on the content. For example, popular videos can be deployed to lots of servers, allowing every client to get the video from a nearby server. By contrast, unpopular videos that are rarely accessed can be deployed to fewer servers, and require users to go further for the content.","zh_html":"应用层映射的一个好处是可以根据内容提供额外的细粒度。例如，热门视频可以部署到大量服务器上，让每个客户端都能从附近的服务器获取视频。相比之下，很少被访问的非热门视频可以部署到较少的服务器上，要求用户走更远的路来获取内容。"},{"en":"Newer HTTP Versions","en_html":"<a href=\"#newer-http-versions\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"newer-http-versions\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Newer HTTP Versions","zh_html":"更新的 HTTP 版本(Newer HTTP Versions)"},{"en":"As the Internet grew, HTTP started to be used by more and more applications, because it’s a very generalizable protocol.","en_html":"As the Internet grew, HTTP started to be used by more and more applications, because it’s a very generalizable protocol.","zh_html":"随着互联网的发展，HTTP 开始被越来越多的应用使用，因为它是一个高度通用化的协议。"},{"en":"Eventually, HTTP security became an increasing concern. A bank server running HTTP probably doesn’t want information to be sent in human-readable plaintext over the network, where intermediate routers or malicious attackers can read it.","en_html":"Eventually, HTTP security became an increasing concern. A bank server running HTTP probably doesn’t want information to be sent in human-readable plaintext over the network, where intermediate routers or malicious attackers can read it.","zh_html":"最终，HTTP 的安全性成为越来越受关注的问题。运行 HTTP 的银行服务器可能不希望信息以人类可读的纯文本形式在网络中传输，因为中间路由器或恶意攻击者可以读取它。"},{"en":"HTTPS is an extension to HTTP that introduces extra security. A protocol called TLS (Transport Layer Security) is built on top of TCP, where users exchange secret keys and encrypt messages before sending them through the bytestream. HTTPS has the same fundamental protocol, but now runs on top of TLS (which itself is over TCP), instead of directly over raw insecure TCP. In recent years, there’s been a push for websites to upgrade to HTTPS, and as of 2024, over 85% of websites now default to using HTTPS.","en_html":"HTTPS is an extension to HTTP that introduces extra security. A protocol called TLS (Transport Layer Security) is built on top of TCP, where users exchange secret keys and encrypt messages before sending them through the bytestream. HTTPS has the same fundamental protocol, but now runs on top of TLS (which itself is over TCP), instead of directly over raw insecure TCP. In recent years, there’s been a push for websites to upgrade to HTTPS, and as of 2024, over 85% of websites now default to using HTTPS.","zh_html":"HTTPS 是引入额外安全性的 HTTP 扩展。一种称为 TLS(传输层安全，Transport Layer Security)的协议构建在 TCP 之上，用户在字节流上交换密钥并在发送消息之前对其进行加密。HTTPS 具有相同的基本协议，但现在运行在 TLS 之上(TLS 本身在 TCP 之上)，而不是直接运行在原始的不安全 TCP 之上。近年来，推动网站升级到 HTTPS 的努力一直在进行，截至 2024 年，超过 85% 的网站默认使用 HTTPS。"},{"en":"HTTP/2.0 was introduced in 2015, and was the first major revision to the protocol since 1997. The main goal of the revision was to improve performance by reducing latency and improving page load speed.","en_html":"HTTP/2.0 was introduced in 2015, and was the first major revision to the protocol since 1997. The main goal of the revision was to improve performance by reducing latency and improving page load speed.","zh_html":"HTTP/2.0 于 2015 年推出，是自 1997 年以来协议的首次重大修订。该修订的主要目标是通过减少延迟和提高页面加载速度来提升性能。"},{"en":"HTTP/2.0 introduced server-side pushing, where the server can send a response even if the client doesn’t make a request. This allows the server to predict and preemptively serve something the user might need, without waiting for the user to make a request. For example, if we make a Google search, the HTML of the results comes back. Then, the user’s browser parses the HTML, realizes it needs the Google logo, and makes another HTTP request. With HTTP/2.0, the server can preemptively give the Google logo to the user, without waiting for the user request.","en_html":"HTTP/2.0 introduced server-side pushing, where the server can send a response even if the client doesn’t make a request. This allows the server to predict and preemptively serve something the user might need, without waiting for the user to make a request. For example, if we make a Google search, the HTML of the results comes back. Then, the user’s browser parses the HTML, realizes it needs the Google logo, and makes another HTTP request. With HTTP/2.0, the server can preemptively give the Google logo to the user, without waiting for the user request.","zh_html":"HTTP/2.0 引入了服务器端推送(server-side pushing)，即服务器可以在客户端没有发出请求的情况下发送响应。这允许服务器预测并抢先提供用户可能需要的内容，而无需等待用户发出请求。例如，如果我们进行 Google 搜索，结果的 HTML 会返回。然后，用户的浏览器解析 HTML，意识到需要 Google logo，并发出另一个 HTTP 请求。使用 HTTP/2.0，服务器可以抢先向用户提供 Google logo，而无需等待用户请求。"},{"en":"HTTP/2.0 had other performance improvements. Headers can be compressed to save space. Requests and responses can have priorities set, so that high-priority content (e.g. text of the search results) is delivered before low-priority content (e.g. the Google logo). Simultaneous requests can be multiplexed more efficiently. If the first request has a 4 GB response and the second request has a 1 KB response, a naive implementation might cause the second response to be stuck waiting for the first one to finish. HTTP/2.0 allows for smarter management of these responses.","en_html":"HTTP/2.0 had other performance improvements. Headers can be compressed to save space. Requests and responses can have priorities set, so that high-priority content (e.g. text of the search results) is delivered before low-priority content (e.g. the Google logo). Simultaneous requests can be multiplexed more efficiently. If the first request has a 4 GB response and the second request has a 1 KB response, a naive implementation might cause the second response to be stuck waiting for the first one to finish. HTTP/2.0 allows for smarter management of these responses.","zh_html":"HTTP/2.0 还有其他性能改进。头部可以被压缩以节省空间。请求和响应可以设置优先级，使得高优先级的内容(例如搜索结果文本)在低优先级的内容(例如 Google logo)之前交付。同时请求可以被更有效地多路复用(multiplexed)。如果第一个请求有 4 GB 的响应而第二个请求有 1 KB 的响应，简单的实现可能会导致第二个响应被卡住，等待第一个完成。HTTP/2.0 允许对这些响应进行更智能的管理。"},{"en":"HTTP/2.0 is widely adopted by both clients (e.g. modern browsers) and servers (e.g. CDNs).","en_html":"HTTP/2.0 is widely adopted by both clients (e.g. modern browsers) and servers (e.g. CDNs).","zh_html":"HTTP/2.0 已被客户端(例如现代浏览器)和服务器(例如 CDN)广泛采用。"},{"en":"HTTP/3.0 was introduced in 2022 (not long after HTTP/2.0, compared to the gap between 1.1 and 2.0). The semantics are the same as HTTP/2.0, but it replaces the underlying transport layer protocol. Instead of running over the TCP bytestream, HTTP/3.0 runs over a new transport protocol called QUIC, which is custom-built to work well with HTTP/3.0. QUIC = Quick UDP Connections, was designed at Google, and standardized in the IETF.","en_html":"HTTP/3.0 was introduced in 2022 (not long after HTTP/2.0, compared to the gap between 1.1 and 2.0). The semantics are the same as HTTP/2.0, but it replaces the underlying transport layer protocol. Instead of running over the TCP bytestream, HTTP/3.0 runs over a new transport protocol called QUIC, which is custom-built to work well with HTTP/3.0. QUIC = Quick UDP Connections, was designed at Google, and standardized in the IETF.","zh_html":"HTTP/3.0 于 2022 年推出(与 1.1 到 2.0 之间的差距相比，距 HTTP/2.0 并不算太久)。语义与 HTTP/2.0 相同，但它替换了底层传输层协议。HTTP/3.0 不再运行在 TCP 字节流上，而是运行在一种称为 QUIC 的新传输协议上，该协议是为与 HTTP/3.0 良好配合而定制构建的。QUIC = Quick UDP Connections，由 Google 设计，并在 IETF 中标准化。"},{"en":"HTTP/3.0 is an example where we intentionally abandon one of the core networking paradigms (layering) in exchange for better efficiency. By giving designers the freedom to customize both the transport layer (QUIC) and application layer (HTTP/3.0) protocols, we can design both protocols to work well together.","en_html":"HTTP/3.0 is an example where we intentionally abandon one of the core networking paradigms (layering) in exchange for better efficiency. By giving designers the freedom to customize both the transport layer (QUIC) and application layer (HTTP/3.0) protocols, we can design both protocols to work well together.","zh_html":"HTTP/3.0 是一个我们有意放弃核心网络范式之一(分层，layering)以换取更高效率的例子。通过给予设计者自定义传输层(QUIC)和应用层(HTTP/3.0)协议的自由，我们可以使两个协议设计得更好地协同工作。"}]},"/applications/index.html":{"title":"index","blocks":[{"en":"DNS","en_html":"<a href=\"dns.html\">DNS</a>","zh_html":"域名系统(DNS)"},{"en":"HTTP","en_html":"<a href=\"http.html\">HTTP</a>","zh_html":"HTTP"}]},"/beyond-client-server/cbt.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Core-Based Trees (CBT)","en_html":"<a href=\"#core-based-trees-cbt\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"core-based-trees-cbt\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Core-Based Trees (CBT)","zh_html":"<a href=\"#core-based-trees-cbt\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"core-based-trees-cbt\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 核心基干树(Core-Based Trees, CBT)"},{"en":"CBT Definition","en_html":"<a href=\"#cbt-definition\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"cbt-definition\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> CBT Definition","zh_html":"<a href=\"#cbt-definition\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"cbt-definition\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> CBT 定义"},{"en":"The goal of multicast routing is still the same: We have a packet whose destination is a group, and the routers need to work together to forward this packet to all members of the group.","en_html":"The goal of multicast routing is still the same: We have a packet whose destination is a group, and the routers need to work together to forward this packet to all members of the group.","zh_html":"多播路由选择(multicast routing)的目标仍然不变：我们有一个目的地址是某个组(group)的分组(packet)，路由器(routers)需要协同工作，把这个分组转发(forward)给该组的所有成员。"},{"en":"However, we will now try a different approach, completely different from DVMRP.","en_html":"However, we will now try a different approach, completely different from DVMRP.","zh_html":"不过，我们现在将尝试一种与 DVMRP 完全不同的方法。"},{"en":"In the Core-Based Tree (CBT) approach, each destination group has its own tree. The CBT for a destination group is simply a tree that touches every member of that group.","en_html":"In the <strong>Core-Based Tree (CBT)</strong> approach, each destination group has its own tree. The CBT for a destination group is simply a tree that touches every member of that group.","zh_html":"在<strong>核心基干树(Core-Based Tree, CBT)</strong>方法中，每个目的组(destination group)都有自己的树。某个目的组的 CBT 就是一棵连接该组所有成员的树。"},{"en":"It can be confusing to think about CBT trees and DVMRP trees at the same time. For now, you can think of them as totally different trees with nothing in common.","en_html":"It can be confusing to think about CBT trees and DVMRP trees at the same time. For now, you can think of them as totally different trees with nothing in common.","zh_html":"同时思考 CBT 树和 DVMRP 树可能会让人困惑。目前，你可以把它们看作完全不同的树，没有任何共同之处。"},{"en":"Building CBTs","en_html":"<a href=\"#building-cbts\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"building-cbts\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Building CBTs","zh_html":"<a href=\"#building-cbts\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"building-cbts\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 构建 CBT"},{"en":"To build a core-based tree, the tree needs a root, which we’ll call the core. The core is some arbitrary router in the network, chosen ahead of time.","en_html":"To build a core-based tree, the tree needs a root, which we’ll call the core. The core is some arbitrary router in the network, chosen ahead of time.","zh_html":"要构建核心基干树，树需要一个根(root)，我们称之为核心(core)。核心是网络中预先选定的某个路由器(router)。"},{"en":"Now, we’ll build a tree that touches every group member, with the core as the root.","en_html":"Now, we’ll build a tree that touches every group member, with the core as the root.","zh_html":"现在，以核心为根，构建一棵连接每个组成员的树。"},{"en":"If a member wants to join a group, the member unicasts a join message to the core. This packet travels through several routers to reach the core. All of these routers join the tree as well, so that the tree now has a path from the core to the new member.","en_html":"If a member wants to join a group, the member unicasts a join message to the core. This packet travels through several routers to reach the core. All of these routers join the tree as well, so that the tree now has a path from the core to the new member.","zh_html":"如果某个成员想加入一个组，该成员会向核心单播(unicast)一条加入消息(join message)。这个分组经过若干路由器到达核心。所有这些路由器也都加入这棵树，于是树就有一条从核心到新成员的路径。"},{"en":"More formally, if you’re a router and you receive a join message for a specific group, you know that you are now part of this group’s tree. The join message’s incoming link is your child (link pointing away from the root). The join message’s outgoing link (next-hop to root) is your parent (link pointing toward the root). You can write down your parent and your children to remember where you are in the tree. There’s no global mastermind remembering the tree; each router on the tree is responsible for remembering its own parent and children.","en_html":"More formally, if you’re a router and you receive a join message for a specific group, you know that you are now part of this group’s tree. The join message’s incoming link is your child (link pointing away from the root). The join message’s outgoing link (next-hop to root) is your parent (link pointing toward the root). You can write down your parent and your children to remember where you are in the tree. There’s no global mastermind remembering the tree; each router on the tree is responsible for remembering its own parent and children.","zh_html":"更正式地说，如果你是一个路由器并收到了某个特定组的加入消息，你就知道自己现在是该组树的一部分。加入消息的入向链路是你的子节点(child，指向远离根的方向)。加入消息的出向链路（下一跳到根）是你的父节点(parent，指向根的方向)。你可以记录下自己的父节点和子节点，以便记住自己在树中的位置。没有全局的主控者来记住整棵树；树上的每个路由器负责记住自己的父节点和子节点。"},{"en":"If a member wants to leave a group, the member can unicast a quit message to its direct parent on the tree. If all of your children on the tree have sent a quit message, that means that you can also leave the tree, so you can send a quit message to your direct parent. Quit messages are sent to your direct parent, and are not forwarded any further than that.","en_html":"If a member wants to leave a group, the member can unicast a quit message to its direct parent on the tree. If all of your children on the tree have sent a quit message, that means that you can also leave the tree, so you can send a quit message to your direct parent. Quit messages are sent to your direct parent, and are not forwarded any further than that.","zh_html":"如果一个成员想离开某个组，它可以向树上的直接父节点单播一条退出消息(quit message)。如果树上的所有子节点都发送了退出消息，就意味着你也可以离开这棵树，于是你可以向直接父节点发送退出消息。退出消息只发送给直接父节点，不会转发到更远的地方。"},{"en":"Remember that we are building one tree per group. This means that routers must remember their parent and children for each tree that they belong to. Also, join and leave messages must be associated with specific group, e.g. “I want to join group G2.”","en_html":"Remember that we are building one tree per group. This means that routers must remember their parent and children for each tree that they belong to. Also, join and leave messages must be associated with specific group, e.g. “I want to join group G2.”","zh_html":"记住，我们是为每个组构建一棵树。这意味着路由器必须记住它们所属的每棵树上的父节点和子节点。另外，加入和离开消息必须关联到具体的组，例如“我想加入组 G2。”。"},{"en":"Here’s some fine print about the core, though it’s not the main intuition behind the protocol.","en_html":"Here’s some fine print about the core, though it’s not the main intuition behind the protocol.","zh_html":"以下是一些关于核心(core)的细节说明，虽然不是该协议的主要直觉。"},{"en":"Since the core is a router, it has a unicast IP address, and everyone can send unicast packets to the core.","en_html":"Since the core is a router, it has a unicast IP address, and everyone can send unicast packets to the core.","zh_html":"由于核心是一个路由器，它有一个单播 IP 地址(unicast IP address)，所有人都可以向核心发送单播分组(unicast packets)。"},{"en":"We’re building one tree per group. Different groups can use different cores.","en_html":"We’re building one tree per group. Different groups can use different cores.","zh_html":"我们为每个组构建一棵树。不同的组可以使用不同的核心。"},{"en":"We’ll assume that everyone knows the mapping from groups to cores, e.g. “Group G1 is using R2 as its core.” This mapping could be published using something like DNS (recall: DNS is useful for distributing key-value pairs).","en_html":"We’ll assume that everyone knows the mapping from groups to cores, e.g. “Group G1 is using R2 as its core.” This mapping could be published using something like DNS (recall: DNS is useful for distributing key-value pairs).","zh_html":"我们假设所有人都知道从组到核心的映射关系，例如“组 G1 使用 R2 作为其核心。”这种映射可以通过 DNS 之类的方式发布（回顾：DNS 擅长分发键值对）。"},{"en":"The core isn’t a group member. In our model, we’ve assumed that hosts can join/leave groups, not routers. The core is a router, so it isn’t joining multicast groups.","en_html":"The core isn’t a group member. In our model, we’ve assumed that hosts can join/leave groups, not routers. The core is a router, so it isn’t joining multicast groups.","zh_html":"核心不是组成员。在我们的模型中，我们假设主机(hosts)可以加入/离开组，而路由器不可以。核心是一个路由器，所以它不会加入多播组(multicast groups)。"},{"en":"Here’s some fine print about the join and quit messages, though it’s not the main intuition behind the protocol.","en_html":"Here’s some fine print about the join and quit messages, though it’s not the main intuition behind the protocol.","zh_html":"以下是一些关于加入和退出消息的细节说明，虽然不是该协议的主要直觉。"},{"en":"The join and quit messages are technically sent by the first-hop router. The router uses IGMP to detect that one of its directly-connected hosts has joined or left the group, and the first-hop router sends out the join or quit message.","en_html":"The join and quit messages are technically sent by the first-hop router. The router uses IGMP to detect that one of its directly-connected hosts has joined or left the group, and the first-hop router sends out the join or quit message.","zh_html":"加入和退出消息在技术层面上是由第一跳路由器(first-hop router)发送的。路由器使用 IGMP 检测到其直连主机(directly-connected hosts)之一加入或离开了组，然后第一跳路由器发出加入或退出消息。"},{"en":"In reality, a JOIN-ACK is sent in response to join messages, and routers note their parent and children when the JOIN-ACK is sent. Likewise, a QUIT-ACK message is sent in response to quit messages. For this class, we’ll ignore this feature.","en_html":"In reality, a JOIN-ACK is sent in response to join messages, and routers note their parent and children when the JOIN-ACK is sent. Likewise, a QUIT-ACK message is sent in response to quit messages. For this class, we’ll ignore this feature.","zh_html":"在现实中，JOIN-ACK 会作为加入消息的响应被发送，路由器在发送 JOIN-ACK 时记录自己的父节点和子节点。同样，QUIT-ACK 消息会作为退出消息的响应被发送。在本课程中，我们忽略这个细节。"},{"en":"Using CBTs","en_html":"<a href=\"#using-cbts\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"using-cbts\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Using CBTs","zh_html":"<a href=\"#using-cbts\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"using-cbts\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 使用 CBT"},{"en":"Now that we’ve built a CBT for a group, how do we use them to send messages to that group?","en_html":"Now that we’ve built a CBT for a group, how do we use them to send messages to that group?","zh_html":"现在我们已经为一个组构建了 CBT，如何用它向该组发送消息呢？"},{"en":"Case 1: If you are a group member, that means you’re already touching the tree. Therefore, all you need to do is broadcast the message to everybody on the tree.","en_html":"Case 1: If you are a group member, that means you’re already touching the tree. Therefore, all you need to do is broadcast the message to everybody on the tree.","zh_html":"情况 1：如果你是组成员，说明你已经连接到了树上。因此，只需向树上的所有人广播(broadcast)消息即可。"},{"en":"More specifically, you start by forwarding the packet to your parent on the tree. Then, every router on the tree receives the packet and floods the packet to all of its tree links (both parent links and child links).","en_html":"More specifically, you start by forwarding the packet to your parent on the tree. Then, every router on the tree receives the packet and floods the packet to all of its tree links (both parent links and child links).","zh_html":"更具体地说，你先把分组转发给树上的父节点。然后，树上的每个路由器收到该分组后，会把它泛洪(flood)到所有树链路（包括父节点链路和子节点链路）。"},{"en":"Case 2: If you’re not a group member, you aren’t touching the tree, so the Case 1 strategy won’t work. Instead, you can unicast the packet to the core. Then, the core can broadcast the message to everybody on the tree.","en_html":"Case 2: If you’re not a group member, you aren’t touching the tree, so the Case 1 strategy won’t work. Instead, you can unicast the packet to the core. Then, the core can broadcast the message to everybody on the tree.","zh_html":"情况 2：如果你不是组成员，你并不在树上，所以情况 1 的策略行不通。相反，你可以向核心单播(unicast)该分组。然后，核心就可以向树上的所有人广播消息。"},{"en":"More specifically, when you unicast the packet to the core, you need to encapsulate the packet. The outer header has unicast information to reach the core. The inner header has the multicast information.","en_html":"More specifically, when you unicast the packet to the core, you need to encapsulate the packet. The outer header has unicast information to reach the core. The inner header has the multicast information.","zh_html":"更具体地说，当你向核心单播分组时，需要封装(encapsulate)该分组。外层头部包含到达核心的单播信息。内层头部包含多播信息。"},{"en":"When the core receives the packet, it unwraps the outer header and sees the inner multicast packet. The core is then able to broadcast this packet along the tree. As in Case 1, every router on the tree receives the packet and floods the packet to all of its tree links (both parent and child links).","en_html":"When the core receives the packet, it unwraps the outer header and sees the inner multicast packet. The core is then able to broadcast this packet along the tree. As in Case 1, every router on the tree receives the packet and floods the packet to all of its tree links (both parent and child links).","zh_html":"当核心收到分组时，它会解封(unwrap)外层头部，看到内层的多播分组。然后核心就可以沿着树广播这个分组。与情况 1 一样，树上的每个路由器收到分组后，会把它泛洪到所有树链路（包括父节点链路和子节点链路）。"},{"en":"Benefit: Better Scaling","en_html":"<a href=\"#benefit-better-scaling\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"benefit-better-scaling\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Benefit: Better Scaling","zh_html":"<a href=\"#benefit-better-scaling\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"benefit-better-scaling\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 优点：更好的扩展性"},{"en":"Recall that DVMRP scales poorly because the routers must keep track of one tree per source, per destination group. Each tree shows the shortest paths from one source, to all members of one destination group.","en_html":"Recall that DVMRP scales poorly because the routers must keep track of one tree per source, per destination group. Each tree shows the shortest paths from one source, to all members of one destination group.","zh_html":"回顾一下，DVMRP 的扩展性很差，因为路由器必须追踪每个源(source)、每个目的组(destination group)各一棵树。每棵树展示了从一个源到一个目的组所有成员的最短路径(shortest paths)。"},{"en":"In the CBT approach, a CBT for a destination group is a simply a tree that touches every member of that group.","en_html":"In the CBT approach, a CBT for a destination group is a simply a tree that touches every member of that group.","zh_html":"在 CBT 方法中，某个目的组的 CBT 只是一棵连接该组所有成员的树。"},{"en":"Notice that the CBT is the same for all sources. Unlike DVMRP (one tree per source, per destination group), we now only have one tree per destination group.","en_html":"Notice that the CBT is the same for all sources. Unlike DVMRP (one tree per source, per destination group), we now only have one tree per destination group.","zh_html":"注意，CBT 对所有源(source)都是相同的。不同于 DVMRP（每个源、每个目的组各一棵树），我们现在每个目的组只有一棵树。"},{"en":"It’s useful to compare DVMRP trees and CBT trees to see how the protocols scale, but beyond that, the trees we build in each protocol have totally different semantics. If you’re confused, it might be easier to think of the trees as completely separate conceptual topics.","en_html":"It’s useful to compare DVMRP trees and CBT trees to see how the protocols scale, but beyond that, the trees we build in each protocol have totally different semantics. If you’re confused, it might be easier to think of the trees as completely separate conceptual topics.","zh_html":"比较 DVMRP 树和 CBT 树有助于理解这两种协议的扩展性，但除此之外，每种协议构建的树具有完全不同的语义。如果你感到困惑，不妨把它们看作完全不同的概念主题。"},{"en":"Recall that another scaling problem with DVMRP is the fact that pruning states are periodically cleared, and when that happens, packets get broadcast to everybody on the network (including non-group members). CBT also solves this problem, because there’s no point in CBT operation where a packet needs to get broadcast to everybody. The tree itself tells us where the group members are, and therefore ensures that non-group members will never receive the packet.","en_html":"Recall that another scaling problem with DVMRP is the fact that pruning states are periodically cleared, and when that happens, packets get broadcast to everybody on the network (including non-group members). CBT also solves this problem, because there’s no point in CBT operation where a packet needs to get broadcast to everybody. The tree itself tells us where the group members are, and therefore ensures that non-group members will never receive the packet.","zh_html":"回顾一下，DVMRP 的另一个扩展性问题是剪枝状态(pruning states)会定期清除，此时分组会被广播给网络上的所有人（包括非组成员）。CBT 也解决了这个问题，因为 CBT 运行过程中没有任何一步需要把分组广播给所有人。树本身告诉了我们组成员在哪里，从而确保非组成员永远不会收到该分组。"},{"en":"Efficiency Analysis","en_html":"<a href=\"#efficiency-analysis\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"efficiency-analysis\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Efficiency Analysis","zh_html":"<a href=\"#efficiency-analysis\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"efficiency-analysis\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 效率分析"},{"en":"Recall that DVMRP built least-cost trees from the sender to all the group members. By forwarding packets along these trees, we ensured that packets would be forwarded along the least-cost paths to all group members.","en_html":"Recall that DVMRP built least-cost trees from the sender to all the group members. By forwarding packets along these trees, we ensured that packets would be forwarded along the least-cost paths to all group members.","zh_html":"回顾一下，DVMRP 构建了从发送方(sender)到所有组成员的最近成本树(least-cost trees)。通过沿着这些树转发分组，我们确保分组沿着最近成本路径到达所有组成员。"},{"en":"By contrast, CBT trees don’t involve the sender at all, so there is no more guarantee of optimality. The paths from the sender to all group members are not necessarily the least-cost paths.","en_html":"By contrast, CBT trees don’t involve the sender at all, so there is no more guarantee of optimality. The paths from the sender to all group members are not necessarily the least-cost paths.","zh_html":"相比之下，CBT 树完全不涉及发送方，因此不再保证最优性。从发送方到所有组成员的路径不一定是最短路径(least-cost paths)。"},{"en":"CBT trades scalability for efficiency. CBT is more scalable because fewer trees need to be built (i.e. routers store less state), but in exchange, packets may be forwarded along suboptimal paths.","en_html":"CBT trades scalability for efficiency. CBT is more scalable because fewer trees need to be built (i.e. routers store less state), but in exchange, packets may be forwarded along suboptimal paths.","zh_html":"CBT 用效率换取扩展性。CBT 的扩展性更好，因为需要构建的树更少（即路由器存储的状态更少），但代价是分组可能沿着次优路径转发。"},{"en":"The efficiency of CBT is highly dependent on which router is chosen to be the core. For example, consider the topology below, with various choices of core.","en_html":"The efficiency of CBT is highly dependent on which router is chosen to be the core. For example, consider the topology below, with various choices of core.","zh_html":"CBT 的效率高度依赖于选择哪个路由器作为核心。例如，考虑下面的拓扑结构和不同的核心选择。"},{"en":"In every choice of core, at least one pair of routers are connected by a suboptimal path. We no longer have a guaranteed shortest paths tree from one source to all group members.","en_html":"In every choice of core, at least one pair of routers are connected by a suboptimal path. We no longer have a guaranteed shortest paths tree from one source to all group members.","zh_html":"无论选择哪个核心，至少有一对路由器通过次优路径相连。我们不再保证从某个源到所有组成员的最短路径树(shortest paths tree)。"},{"en":"For example, if A plans on sending a lot of packets to the group, R2 might be a good choice of core, since it just happens to connect A to B and C along the shortest paths. However, if B wanted to send packets to the group, the packets would travel along a suboptimal path to C.","en_html":"For example, if A plans on sending a lot of packets to the group, R2 might be a good choice of core, since it just happens to connect A to B and C along the shortest paths. However, if B wanted to send packets to the group, the packets would travel along a suboptimal path to C.","zh_html":"例如，如果 A 计划向该组发送大量分组，R2 可能是一个很好的核心选择，因为它恰好将 A 与 B 和 C 沿着最短路径连接起来。但是，如果 B 想向该组发送分组，分组将沿着次优路径到达 C。"},{"en":"Finding the optimal core is infeasible, especially since members can join and leave the group at any time. In practice, operators often manually select the core.","en_html":"Finding the optimal core is infeasible, especially since members can join and leave the group at any time. In practice, operators often manually select the core.","zh_html":"找到最优核心是不可行的，尤其是因为成员可以随时加入和离开组。在实践中，运维人员(operators)通常手动选择核心。"},{"en":"Other CBT Pros and Cons","en_html":"<a href=\"#other-cbt-pros-and-cons\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"other-cbt-pros-and-cons\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Other CBT Pros and Cons","zh_html":"<a href=\"#other-cbt-pros-and-cons\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"other-cbt-pros-and-cons\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> CBT 的其他优缺点"},{"en":"CBT creates a single point of failure at the root. To introduce fault-tolerance, we would need the tree to have multiple cores. This can be done, though it introduces more complexity. We won’t discuss multi-core trees any further, but see the linked paper below if you’re curious.","en_html":"CBT creates a single point of failure at the root. To introduce fault-tolerance, we would need the tree to have multiple cores. This can be done, though it introduces more complexity. We won’t discuss multi-core trees any further, but see the linked paper below if you’re curious.","zh_html":"CBT 在根处引入了单点故障(single point of failure)。为了实现容错(fault-tolerance)，我们需要树有多个核心。这是可以做到的，虽然会增加更多复杂性。我们不再进一步讨论多核树，但如果你感兴趣，可以参考下面链接的论文。"},{"en":"Recall that DVMRP was built as an extension to distance-vector, which results in the multicast protocol (DVMRP) and the unicast protocol (distance-vector) being tightly-coupled. Changing one protocol requires also updating the other protocol. By contrast, CBT is decoupled from the unicast routing protocol. CBT does use the unicast forwarding tables (e.g. to forward join messages to the root), but it doesn’t matter how those forwarding tables were generated (distance-vector, link-state, hard-coded, etc.). As a result, CBT does not rely on any particular unicast protocol being used, and CBT works with any unicast protocol.","en_html":"Recall that DVMRP was built as an extension to distance-vector, which results in the multicast protocol (DVMRP) and the unicast protocol (distance-vector) being tightly-coupled. Changing one protocol requires also updating the other protocol. By contrast, CBT is decoupled from the unicast routing protocol. CBT does use the unicast forwarding tables (e.g. to forward join messages to the root), but it doesn’t matter how those forwarding tables were generated (distance-vector, link-state, hard-coded, etc.). As a result, CBT does not rely on any particular unicast protocol being used, and CBT works with any unicast protocol.","zh_html":"回顾一下，DVMRP 是作为距离向量(distance-vector)协议的一个扩展构建的，这导致多播协议(DVMRP)和单播协议(距离向量)紧密耦合。改变一个协议需要同时更新另一个协议。相比之下，CBT 与单播路由协议(unicast routing protocol)是解耦的。CBT 确实会使用单播转发表(unicast forwarding tables)，例如将加入消息转发到根，但不管这些转发表是如何生成的（距离向量、链路状态、硬编码等），都没有关系。因此，CBT 不依赖于任何特定的单播协议，可以与任何单播协议配合使用。"},{"en":"Further reading on CBT: https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~sylvia/cs268-2019/papers/cbt.pdf","en_html":"Further reading on CBT: <a href=\"https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~sylvia/cs268-2019/papers/cbt.pdf\">https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~sylvia/cs268-2019/papers/cbt.pdf</a>","zh_html":"关于 CBT 的扩展阅读：<a href=\"https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~sylvia/cs268-2019/papers/cbt.pdf\">https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~sylvia/cs268-2019/papers/cbt.pdf</a>"},{"en":"Is DVMRP or CBT better? As we’ve seen, there are trade-offs between the two protocols.","en_html":"Is DVMRP or CBT better? As we’ve seen, there are trade-offs between the two protocols.","zh_html":"DVMRP 和 CBT 哪个更好？正如我们所见，两种协议之间各有取舍(trade-offs)。"},{"en":"If you have one source sending data to a large group, then DVMRP might be the better solution, since it will ensure that all this data travels along optimal paths through the network. Lots of data is being sent (to lots of group members), so using optimal paths results in significant bandwidth savings. Also, if the group is large (e.g. includes almost everyone on the network), then DVMRP’s occasional flooding may not be a big problem.","en_html":"If you have one source sending data to a large group, then DVMRP might be the better solution, since it will ensure that all this data travels along optimal paths through the network. Lots of data is being sent (to lots of group members), so using optimal paths results in significant bandwidth savings. Also, if the group is large (e.g. includes almost everyone on the network), then DVMRP’s occasional flooding may not be a big problem.","zh_html":"如果你有一个源向一个大组发送数据，那么 DVMRP 可能是更好的解决方案，因为它能确保所有数据沿着最优路径在网络中传输。大量数据被发送给众多组成员，因此使用最优路径可以显著节省带宽。此外，如果组很大（例如包括网络上几乎所有人），那么 DVMRP 偶尔发生的泛洪(flooding)可能不是大问题。"},{"en":"By contrast, if you have a small group whose members are scattered across a large network, then CBT might be the better solution. CBT will avoid flooding packets to non-members, which would waste a lot of bandwidth (since most members are not in the group).","en_html":"By contrast, if you have a small group whose members are scattered across a large network, then CBT might be the better solution. CBT will avoid flooding packets to non-members, which would waste a lot of bandwidth (since most members are not in the group).","zh_html":"相比之下，如果你有一个小规模的组，成员散布在大型网络中，那么 CBT 可能是更好的解决方案。CBT 会避免向非组成员泛洪分组，泛洪会浪费大量带宽（因为大多数节点不在组中）。"},{"en":"In practice, both DVMRP and CBT are used today. DVMRP is sometimes named PIM-DM (Protocol Independent Multicast - Dense Mode), which reflects the fact that DVMRP is good for large groups. CBT is sometimes called PIM-SM (Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode), which reflects the fact that CBT is good for smaller groups.","en_html":"In practice, both DVMRP and CBT are used today. DVMRP is sometimes named PIM-DM (Protocol Independent Multicast - Dense Mode), which reflects the fact that DVMRP is good for large groups. CBT is sometimes called PIM-SM (Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode), which reflects the fact that CBT is good for smaller groups.","zh_html":"在实践中，DVMRP 和 CBT 目前都有人使用。DVMRP 有时被称为 PIM-DM（协议无关多播-密集模式，Protocol Independent Multicast - Dense Mode），这反映了 DVMRP 适用于大型组的事实。CBT 有时被称为 PIM-SM（协议无关多播-稀疏模式，Protocol Independent Multicast - Sparse Mode），这反映了 CBT 适用于较小规模组的事实。"}]},"/beyond-client-server/collective-implementations.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Collective Implementations","en_html":"<a href=\"#collective-implementations\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"collective-implementations\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Collective Implementations","zh_html":"<a href=\"#collective-implementations\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"collective-implementations\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 集合操作的实现(Collective Implementations)"},{"en":"Motivation: Implementing AllReduce","en_html":"<a href=\"#motivation-implementing-allreduce\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"motivation-implementing-allreduce\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Motivation: Implementing AllReduce","zh_html":"<a href=\"#motivation-implementing-allreduce\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"motivation-implementing-allreduce\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 动机：实现 AllReduce"},{"en":"Now that we have definitions of the 7 collectives, we can start thinking about how to implement them in a network. To implement a collective, there are two questions we need to answer: What topology do we use to connect the nodes? What data has to be exchanged between the nodes in order to efficiently complete the operation?","en_html":"Now that we have definitions of the 7 collectives, we can start thinking about how to implement them in a network. To implement a collective, there are two questions we need to answer: What topology do we use to connect the nodes? What data has to be exchanged between the nodes in order to efficiently complete the operation?","zh_html":"现在我们有了 7 种集合操作(collectives)的定义，可以开始思考如何在网络中实现它们。要实现一个集合操作，我们需要回答两个问题：我们用什么拓扑(topology)来连接节点(node)？节点之间需要交换什么数据才能高效地完成操作？"},{"en":"Once we’ve decided on what topology to use and what data to exchange, we can then analyze the performance of our design. What was the total amount of network bandwidth we used? How long did it take for the operation to complete? Other performance metrics can also be focused, but we’ll focus on these two for these notes.","en_html":"Once we’ve decided on what topology to use and what data to exchange, we can then analyze the performance of our design. What was the total amount of network bandwidth we used? How long did it take for the operation to complete? Other performance metrics can also be focused, but we’ll focus on these two for these notes.","zh_html":"一旦我们决定了使用什么拓扑和交换什么数据，就可以分析设计的性能。我们总共使用了多少网络带宽？操作完成需要多长时间？也可以关注其他性能指标，但在这份笔记中我们将重点关注这两个。"},{"en":"To measure performance, we’ll define some variables. There are \\(p\\) nodes in total. Each vector is \\(D\\) bytes in total. This means that each vector element (i.e. each box in the diagram) is \\(D/p\\) bytes.","en_html":"To measure performance, we’ll define some variables. There are \\(p\\) nodes in total. Each vector is \\(D\\) bytes in total. This means that each vector element (i.e. each box in the diagram) is \\(D/p\\) bytes.","zh_html":"为了衡量性能，我们定义一些变量。总共有 \\(p\\) 个节点。每个向量(vector)总共是 \\(D\\) 字节。这意味着每个向量元素（即图中的每个框）是 \\(D/p\\) 字节。"},{"en":"In this section, we’ll set \\(p=5\\) to make some of the demos more illustrative. Note that this also means that each vector is now 5 elements instead of 4 elements. (Side note: Remember that the vector represents arbitrary data, and we divide each vector into \\(p\\) equally-sized sub-vectors, where \\(p\\) is the total number of nodes. Increasing \\(p\\) from 4 to 5 doesn’t necessarily mean we have more data. It could just mean we split the same data into 5 chunks instead of 4 chunks.)","en_html":"In this section, we’ll set \\(p=5\\) to make some of the demos more illustrative. Note that this also means that each vector is now 5 elements instead of 4 elements. (Side note: Remember that the vector represents arbitrary data, and we divide each vector into \\(p\\) equally-sized sub-vectors, where \\(p\\) is the total number of nodes. Increasing \\(p\\) from 4 to 5 doesn’t necessarily mean we have more data. It could just mean we split the same data into 5 chunks instead of 4 chunks.)","zh_html":"在本节中，我们设 \\(p=5\\) 以使一些演示更具说明性。注意，这也意味着每个向量现在是 5 个元素而不是 4 个元素。（旁注：记住向量代表任意数据，我们将每个向量分成 \\(p\\) 个大小相等的子向量，其中 \\(p\\) 是节点总数。将 \\(p\\) 从 4 增加到 5 并不一定意味着我们有更多数据。它可能只是意味着我们将相同的数据分成 5 块而不是 4 块。）"},{"en":"In this section, we’ll focus on implementing the AllReduce collective, although the ideas can be applied to the other collectives as well. Recall that AllReduce computes an element-wise sum of the vectors, and then sends the sum vector to all nodes.","en_html":"In this section, we’ll focus on implementing the AllReduce collective, although the ideas can be applied to the other collectives as well. Recall that AllReduce computes an element-wise sum of the vectors, and then sends the sum vector to all nodes.","zh_html":"在本节中，我们将专注于实现 AllReduce 集合操作，尽管这些思路也可以应用于其他集合操作。回顾一下，AllReduce 计算向量的逐元素和(element-wise sum)，然后将结果向量发送给所有节点。"},{"en":"Approach 1: Full Mesh","en_html":"<a href=\"#approach-1-full-mesh\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"approach-1-full-mesh\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Approach 1: Full Mesh","zh_html":"<a href=\"#approach-1-full-mesh\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"approach-1-full-mesh\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 方法 1：全网状(Full Mesh)"},{"en":"The first topology we’ll consider is a full-mesh, where every node has a direct link to every other node.","en_html":"The first topology we’ll consider is a full-mesh, where every node has a direct link to every other node.","zh_html":"我们考虑的第一个拓扑是全网状(full-mesh)，其中每个节点与其他每个节点都有直接链接。"},{"en":"With this topology, we can implement AllReduce with these steps: First, everyone sends their entire vector directly to every other node.","en_html":"With this topology, we can implement AllReduce with these steps: First, everyone sends their entire vector directly to every other node.","zh_html":"使用这种拓扑，我们可以通过以下步骤实现 AllReduce：首先，每个人将其整个向量直接发送给所有其他节点。"},{"en":"Then, each node sums all the vectors it receives.","en_html":"Then, each node sums all the vectors it receives.","zh_html":"然后，每个节点对其收到的所有向量求和。"},{"en":"How much bandwidth does this approach use? Each node needs to send its entire vector (\\(D\\) bytes) to all \\(p-1\\) other nodes, so each node sends \\(D(p-1)\\) bytes. There are \\(p\\) nodes in total, so the total data sent is \\(Dp(p-1) = O(D \\cdot p^2)\\) bytes.","en_html":"How much bandwidth does this approach use? Each node needs to send its entire vector (\\(D\\) bytes) to all \\(p-1\\) other nodes, so each node sends \\(D(p-1)\\) bytes. There are \\(p\\) nodes in total, so the total data sent is \\(Dp(p-1) = O(D \\cdot p^2)\\) bytes.","zh_html":"这种方法使用了多少带宽？每个节点需要将其整个向量（\\(D\\) 字节）发送给其他 \\(p-1\\) 个节点，因此每个节点发送 \\(D(p-1)\\) 字节。总共有 \\(p\\) 个节点，所以发送的总数据是 \\(Dp(p-1) = O(D \\cdot p^2)\\) 字节。"},{"en":"How much time does this approach take? It depends on the exact resource limits of the nodes and the links, but assuming no resource limits, all of the vector sending can happen at the same time, completing in a single time step. In other words, Node 1 sends data to all other nodes, using all 3 of its outgoing links simultaneously. At the same time, Node 2 can also send data to all other nodes, using all 3 of its outgoing links simultaneously. Assuming no resource limits, this approach takes a single time step to complete, where each node needs to send and receive \\(2 \\cdot D \\cdot (p-1)\\) bytes per time step. (Each node sends \\(D \\cdot (p-1)\\) bytes and receives \\(D \\cdot (p-1)\\) bytes, and summing those up gives us the extra factor of 2.)","en_html":"How much time does this approach take? It depends on the exact resource limits of the nodes and the links, but assuming no resource limits, all of the vector sending can happen at the same time, completing in a single time step. In other words, Node 1 sends data to all other nodes, using all 3 of its outgoing links simultaneously. At the same time, Node 2 can also send data to all other nodes, using all 3 of its outgoing links simultaneously. Assuming no resource limits, this approach takes a single time step to complete, where each node needs to send and receive \\(2 \\cdot D \\cdot (p-1)\\) bytes per time step. (Each node sends \\(D \\cdot (p-1)\\) bytes and receives \\(D \\cdot (p-1)\\) bytes, and summing those up gives us the extra factor of 2.)","zh_html":"这种方法需要多长时间？这取决于节点和链路的确切资源限制，但假设没有资源限制，所有的向量发送可以同时发生，在一个时间步(time step)内完成。换句话说，节点 1 同时使用其所有 3 条出向链接向所有其他节点发送数据。同时，节点 2 也可以同时使用其所有 3 条出向链接向所有其他节点发送数据。假设没有资源限制，这种方法在一个时间步内完成，每个节点需要在该时间步内发送和接收 \\(2 \\cdot D \\cdot (p-1)\\) 字节。（每个节点发送 \\(D \\cdot (p-1)\\) 字节并接收 \\(D \\cdot (p-1)\\) 字节，求和后得到额外的因子 2。）"},{"en":"Approach 2: Reduce at One Node","en_html":"<a href=\"#approach-2-reduce-at-one-node\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"approach-2-reduce-at-one-node\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Approach 2: Reduce at One Node","zh_html":"<a href=\"#approach-2-reduce-at-one-node\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"approach-2-reduce-at-one-node\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 方法 2：单节点归约(Reduce at One Node)"},{"en":"In the next topology, let’s have a single topology do all the computation work:","en_html":"In the next topology, let’s have a single topology do all the computation work:","zh_html":"在下一个拓扑中，让一个拓扑节点完成所有计算工作："},{"en":"To run AllReduce: First, everybody (except Node 1) sends their vector to Node 1.","en_html":"To run AllReduce: First, everybody (except Node 1) sends their vector to Node 1.","zh_html":"运行 AllReduce：首先，每个人（除了节点 1）将其向量发送给节点 1。"},{"en":"Then, Node 1 computes the sum, and sends the sum back to everybody.","en_html":"Then, Node 1 computes the sum, and sends the sum back to everybody.","zh_html":"然后，节点 1 计算总和，并将总和发送回每个人。"},{"en":"How much bandwidth does this approach use? Each node (except Node 1) needs to send its entire vector to Node 1, which means \\(D\\) bytes are sent. There are \\(p-1\\) nodes that need to send data, so the total data sent in the first step is \\(D(p-1)\\) bytes.","en_html":"How much bandwidth does this approach use? Each node (except Node 1) needs to send its entire vector to Node 1, which means \\(D\\) bytes are sent. There are \\(p-1\\) nodes that need to send data, so the total data sent in the first step is \\(D(p-1)\\) bytes.","zh_html":"这种方法使用了多少带宽？每个节点（除了节点 1）需要将其整个向量发送给节点 1，即发送 \\(D\\) 字节。需要发送数据的节点有 \\(p-1\\) 个，因此第一步发送的总数据是 \\(D(p-1)\\) 字节。"},{"en":"Then, in the second step, Node 1 has to send the sum vector to everybody else. The sum vector is \\(D\\) bytes, and it has to be sent to \\(p-1\\) other nodes, so the total data sent in the second step is also \\(D(p-1)\\) bytes.","en_html":"Then, in the second step, Node 1 has to send the sum vector to everybody else. The sum vector is \\(D\\) bytes, and it has to be sent to \\(p-1\\) other nodes, so the total data sent in the second step is also \\(D(p-1)\\) bytes.","zh_html":"然后，在第二步中，节点 1 需要将总和向量发送给所有其他节点。总和向量是 \\(D\\) 字节，需要发送给 \\(p-1\\) 个其他节点，所以第二步发送的总数据也是 \\(D(p-1)\\) 字节。"},{"en":"In total, across the two steps, we sent \\(2 \\cdot D \\cdot (p-1) = O(D \\cdot p)\\) bytes. Notice that this is a factor of \\(p\\) better than the \\(O(D \\cdot p^2)\\) bytes sent in the full-mesh approach.","en_html":"In total, across the two steps, we sent \\(2 \\cdot D \\cdot (p-1) = O(D \\cdot p)\\) bytes. Notice that this is a factor of \\(p\\) better than the \\(O(D \\cdot p^2)\\) bytes sent in the full-mesh approach.","zh_html":"总的来说，跨两步我们发送了 \\(2 \\cdot D \\cdot (p-1) = O(D \\cdot p)\\) 字节。注意，这比全网状方法的 \\(O(D \\cdot p^2)\\) 字节好了一个 \\(p\\) 因子。"},{"en":"How much time does this approach take? Again, it depends on the exact resource limits, but assuming no resource limits, everyone can send their vector to Node 1 at the same time. Then, we have to wait for Node 1 to compute the sum. After the sum is computed, Node 1 can send the sum back to everybody else at the same time. In total, this approach takes 2 time steps to complete, where Node 1 has to send or receive \\(D \\cdot (p-1)\\) bytes per time step.","en_html":"How much time does this approach take? Again, it depends on the exact resource limits, but assuming no resource limits, everyone can send their vector to Node 1 at the same time. Then, we have to wait for Node 1 to compute the sum. After the sum is computed, Node 1 can send the sum back to everybody else at the same time. In total, this approach takes 2 time steps to complete, where Node 1 has to send or receive \\(D \\cdot (p-1)\\) bytes per time step.","zh_html":"这种方法需要多长时间？同样，这取决于确切资源限制，但假设没有资源限制，所有人可以同时向节点 1 发送向量。然后，我们必须等待节点 1 计算总和。计算完总和后，节点 1 可以同时将总和发送回所有其他人。总的来说，这种方法需要 2 个时间步完成，节点 1 在每个时间步需要发送或接收 \\(D \\cdot (p-1)\\) 字节。"},{"en":"We aren’t precisely measuring how long a “time step” is here, but the main point of comparison here is that with this approach, all the sending in the first step has to finish before sending in the second step can start. By contrast, in the first approach, all of the data sending could happen at the same time.","en_html":"We aren’t precisely measuring how long a “time step” is here, but the main point of comparison here is that with this approach, all the sending in the first step has to finish before sending in the second step can start. By contrast, in the first approach, all of the data sending could happen at the same time.","zh_html":"我们并没有精确地衡量这里的一个\"时间步\"有多长，但主要的比较点是：在这种方法中，第一步的所有发送必须在第二步的发送开始之前完成。相比之下，在第一种方法中，所有的数据发送可以同时发生。"},{"en":"One downside of this approach is that we have a single point of failure at Node 1. This approach is not commonly used in practice.","en_html":"One downside of this approach is that we have a single point of failure at Node 1. This approach is not commonly used in practice.","zh_html":"这种方法的一个缺点是节点 1 存在单点故障。这种方法在实践中不常用。"},{"en":"Approach 3: Tree-Based","en_html":"<a href=\"#approach-3-tree-based\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"approach-3-tree-based\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Approach 3: Tree-Based","zh_html":"<a href=\"#approach-3-tree-based\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"approach-3-tree-based\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 方法 3：基于树(Tree-Based)"},{"en":"In the next topology, we’ll build a binary tree. Remember that binary here means that each node has at most 2 children.","en_html":"In the next topology, we’ll build a binary tree. Remember that binary here means that each node has at most 2 children.","zh_html":"在下一个拓扑中，我们将构建一棵二叉树。记住，这里的二叉(binary)意味着每个节点最多有 2 个子节点。"},{"en":"To run AllReduce: Starting from the leaf nodes at the bottom, each node sends its vector to its parent.","en_html":"To run AllReduce: Starting from the leaf nodes at the bottom, each node sends its vector to its parent.","zh_html":"运行 AllReduce：从底部的叶子节点开始，每个节点将其向量发送给父节点。"},{"en":"When you receive all of your children’s vectors, you should sum them with your vector.","en_html":"When you receive all of your children’s vectors, you should sum them with your vector.","zh_html":"当你收到所有子节点的向量后，将它们与自己的向量求和。"},{"en":"Then, you should send this resulting sum vector to your parent.","en_html":"Then, you should send this resulting sum vector to your parent.","zh_html":"然后，将得到的和向量发送给你的父节点。"},{"en":"After repeating this step up all the layers of the tree, the root should have computed the overall sum.","en_html":"After repeating this step up all the layers of the tree, the root should have computed the overall sum.","zh_html":"在树的所有层重复这一步之后，根节点应该已经计算出了总和。"},{"en":"Then, in the second step, the root sends the overall sum vector down the tree, to its children. When you receive the sum vector from your parent, you should send a copy of that sum vector to all your children.","en_html":"Then, in the second step, the root sends the overall sum vector down the tree, to its children. When you receive the sum vector from your parent, you should send a copy of that sum vector to all your children.","zh_html":"然后，在第二步中，根节点将总和向量沿树向下发送给其子节点。当你从父节点收到总和向量时，你应该将该总和向量的副本发送给你所有的子节点。"},{"en":"How much bandwidth does this approach use? In Step 1, each node receives up to 2 vectors from its children (recall: the tree is binary), and each node sends 1 vector to its parent. This gives us an upper-bound of \\(3D\\) bytes per node, for a total of \\(3D \\cdot p\\) bytes in Step 1.","en_html":"How much bandwidth does this approach use? In Step 1, each node receives up to 2 vectors from its children (recall: the tree is binary), and each node sends 1 vector to its parent. This gives us an upper-bound of \\(3D\\) bytes per node, for a total of \\(3D \\cdot p\\) bytes in Step 1.","zh_html":"这种方法使用了多少带宽？在第一步中，每个节点从其子节点接收最多 2 个向量（回顾：树是二叉的），每个节点向其父节点发送 1 个向量。这给出了每个节点最多 \\(3D\\) 字节的上界，第一步总共 \\(3D \\cdot p\\) 字节。"},{"en":"Then, in the second step, each each node receives 1 vector from its parent, and sends up to 2 vectors to its children. Again, we get an upper-bound of \\(3D\\) bytes per node, for a total of \\(3D \\cdot p\\) bytes in Step 2.","en_html":"Then, in the second step, each each node receives 1 vector from its parent, and sends up to 2 vectors to its children. Again, we get an upper-bound of \\(3D\\) bytes per node, for a total of \\(3D \\cdot p\\) bytes in Step 2.","zh_html":"然后，在第二步中，每个节点从其父节点接收 1 个向量，并向其子节点发送最多 2 个向量。同样，每个节点最多 \\(3D\\) 字节，第二步总共 \\(3D \\cdot p\\) 字节。"},{"en":"In total, across the two steps, we sent \\(6 \\cdot D \\cdot p = O(D \\cdot p)\\) bytes. This is a factor of \\(p\\) better than the full-mesh, and the same as the reduce-at-one-node approach.","en_html":"In total, across the two steps, we sent \\(6 \\cdot D \\cdot p = O(D \\cdot p)\\) bytes. This is a factor of \\(p\\) better than the full-mesh, and the same as the reduce-at-one-node approach.","zh_html":"总的来说，跨两步我们发送了 \\(6 \\cdot D \\cdot p = O(D \\cdot p)\\) 字节。这比全网状方法好了一个 \\(p\\) 因子，与单节点归约方法相同。"},{"en":"How much time does this approach take? You have to wait to receive vectors from your children before you can send the sum (i.e. sum of your vector and your children’s vectors) to your parent. In total, this approach takes \\(O(\\log p)\\) time steps to send vectors up the tree, and another \\(O(\\log p)\\) time steps to send the overall sum down the tree, for a total of \\(O(\\log p)\\) time steps. Each node has to send or receive \\(3D\\) bytes per time step (note that this is fewer bytes per time step than the other approaches). An exact time comparison would require plugging in values for \\(D\\) and the resource limits in the network, but roughly speaking, this approach requires more time steps, but each time step can probably complete faster since there’s less data to transmit per time step.","en_html":"How much time does this approach take? You have to wait to receive vectors from your children before you can send the sum (i.e. sum of your vector and your children’s vectors) to your parent. In total, this approach takes \\(O(\\log p)\\) time steps to send vectors up the tree, and another \\(O(\\log p)\\) time steps to send the overall sum down the tree, for a total of \\(O(\\log p)\\) time steps. Each node has to send or receive \\(3D\\) bytes per time step (note that this is fewer bytes per time step than the other approaches). An exact time comparison would require plugging in values for \\(D\\) and the resource limits in the network, but roughly speaking, this approach requires more time steps, but each time step can probably complete faster since there’s less data to transmit per time step.","zh_html":"这种方法需要多长时间？你必须等待收到子节点的向量后才能将总和（即你的向量和子节点的向量之和）发送给父节点。总的来说，这种方法需要 \\(O(\\log p)\\) 个时间步将向量沿树向上发送，另外 \\(O(\\log p)\\) 个时间步将总和沿树向下发送，总共 \\(O(\\log p)\\) 个时间步。每个节点每个时间步需要发送或接收 \\(3D\\) 字节（注意，这比其他方法每个时间步的字节数更少）。精确的时间比较需要代入 \\(D\\) 和网络资源限制的具体值，但粗略地说，这种方法需要更多的时间步，但每个时间步可能完成得更快，因为每个时间步传输的数据更少。"},{"en":"Notice that we took advantage of the reduction operation in this implementation. Each node sums up its vector and its children’s vectors, so that it only has to send up a single sum vector to its parent. In a more naive approach, each node would have sent up 3 vectors to its parent (its own vector, and both of its children’s vectors), but we took advantage of the reduction to save bandwidth.","en_html":"Notice that we took advantage of the reduction operation in this implementation. Each node sums up its vector and its children’s vectors, so that it only has to send up a single sum vector to its parent. In a more naive approach, each node would have sent up 3 vectors to its parent (its own vector, and both of its children’s vectors), but we took advantage of the reduction to save bandwidth.","zh_html":"注意，我们在这个实现中利用了归约操作(reduction operation)。每个节点将其向量和子节点的向量求和，因此只需要向父节点发送一个和向量。在更朴素的方法中，每个节点需要向其父节点发送 3 个向量（自己的向量和两个子节点的向量），但我们利用了归约来节省带宽。"},{"en":"More generally, the consolidation collectives (Reduce, ReduceScatter, AllReduce) give us an opportunity to optimize their implementation. In Reduce and ReduceScatter, the total amount of data received is actually less than the amount of data sent, and we can take advantage of that in our implementations. For example, if we know that the output is a sum of all vectors, and we receive two vectors, we can sum up the vectors and forward a single, summed vector, instead of forwarding the two vectors separately.","en_html":"More generally, the consolidation collectives (Reduce, ReduceScatter, AllReduce) give us an opportunity to optimize their implementation. In Reduce and ReduceScatter, the total amount of data received is actually less than the amount of data sent, and we can take advantage of that in our implementations. For example, if we know that the output is a sum of all vectors, and we receive two vectors, we can sum up the vectors and forward a single, summed vector, instead of forwarding the two vectors separately.","zh_html":"更一般地说，合并类集合操作(consolidation collectives)（Reduce、ReduceScatter、AllReduce）为我们提供了优化实现的机会。在 Reduce 和 ReduceScatter 中，实际接收的数据总量少于发送的数据量，我们可以在实现中利用这一点。例如，如果我们知道输出是所有向量的和，并且我们收到两个向量，我们可以将向量求和并转发一个求和后的向量，而不是分别转发这两个向量。"},{"en":"Approach 4: Ring-Based (Naive)","en_html":"<a href=\"#approach-4-ring-based-naive\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"approach-4-ring-based-naive\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Approach 4: Ring-Based (Naive)","zh_html":"<a href=\"#approach-4-ring-based-naive\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"approach-4-ring-based-naive\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 方法 4：基于环-朴素(Ring-Based, Naive)"},{"en":"In the last two approaches, we’ll build a ring-shaped topology. Note that there’s nothing special about the wrap-around link from Node 1 to Node 5, compared to the other links (i.e. the link being longer doesn’t mean anything).","en_html":"In the last two approaches, we’ll build a ring-shaped topology. Note that there’s nothing special about the wrap-around link from Node 1 to Node 5, compared to the other links (i.e. the link being longer doesn’t mean anything).","zh_html":"在最后两种方法中，我们将构建一个环形拓扑。注意，从节点 1 到节点 5 的回环链接与其他链接相比没有什么特别之处（即链接更长并不意味着什么）。"},{"en":"To run AllReduce naively: Node 5 starts by sending its vector left.","en_html":"To run AllReduce naively: Node 5 starts by sending its vector left.","zh_html":"朴素地运行 AllReduce：节点 5 开始将其向量向左发送。"},{"en":"When you receive a vector from your neighbor to the right, you should sum it with your vector.","en_html":"When you receive a vector from your neighbor to the right, you should sum it with your vector.","zh_html":"当你从右侧邻居收到一个向量时，将其与你的向量求和。"},{"en":"Then, you should send this resulting sum vector to your left neighbor.","en_html":"Then, you should send this resulting sum vector to your left neighbor.","zh_html":"然后，将得到的和向量发送给你的左侧邻居。"},{"en":"Eventually, this process will work around the loop.","en_html":"Eventually, this process will work around the loop.","zh_html":"最终，这个过程会在环上绕一圈。"},{"en":"To finish up, Node 1 will compute the overall sum.","en_html":"To finish up, Node 1 will compute the overall sum.","zh_html":"最后，节点 1 计算出总和。"},{"en":"Then, in the second step, we will send the overall sum around the loop so that everyone has a copy. Node 5 starts by sending the overall sum left. When you receive the overall sum vector from your neighbor to the right, you should send a copy of the sum vector to your left neighbor. Eventually, this process works around the loop, and everyone receives a copy of the overall sum.","en_html":"Then, in the second step, we will send the overall sum around the loop so that everyone has a copy. Node 5 starts by sending the overall sum left. When you receive the overall sum vector from your neighbor to the right, you should send a copy of the sum vector to your left neighbor. Eventually, this process works around the loop, and everyone receives a copy of the overall sum.","zh_html":"然后，在第二步中，我们将总和沿环发送，以便每个人都有副本。节点 5 开始将总和向左发送。当你从右侧邻居收到总和向量时，你应该将该总和向量的副本发送给你的左侧邻居。最终，这个过程在环上绕一圈，每个人都收到总和向量的副本。"},{"en":"How much bandwidth does this approach use? In Step 1, each node receives a vector from its right neighbor, and sends a vector to its left neighbor. This gives us an upper-bound of \\(2D\\) bytes per node, for a total of \\(2D \\cdot p\\) bytes in Step 1.","en_html":"How much bandwidth does this approach use? In Step 1, each node receives a vector from its right neighbor, and sends a vector to its left neighbor. This gives us an upper-bound of \\(2D\\) bytes per node, for a total of \\(2D \\cdot p\\) bytes in Step 1.","zh_html":"这种方法使用了多少带宽？在第一步中，每个节点从其右侧邻居接收一个向量，并向其左侧邻居发送一个向量。这给出了每个节点最多 \\(2D\\) 字节的上界，第一步总共 \\(2D \\cdot p\\) 字节。"},{"en":"In the second step, each node again receives 1 vector and sends 1 vector. Again, we get an upper-bound of \\(2D\\) bytes per node, for a total of \\(2D \\cdot p\\) bytes in Step 2.","en_html":"In the second step, each node again receives 1 vector and sends 1 vector. Again, we get an upper-bound of \\(2D\\) bytes per node, for a total of \\(2D \\cdot p\\) bytes in Step 2.","zh_html":"在第二步中，每个节点再次接收 1 个向量并发送 1 个向量。同样，每个节点最多 \\(2D\\) 字节，第二步总共 \\(2D \\cdot p\\) 字节。"},{"en":"In total, across the two steps, we sent \\(4 \\cdot D \\cdot p = O(D \\cdot p)\\) bytes.","en_html":"In total, across the two steps, we sent \\(4 \\cdot D \\cdot p = O(D \\cdot p)\\) bytes.","zh_html":"总的来说，跨两步我们发送了 \\(4 \\cdot D \\cdot p = O(D \\cdot p)\\) 字节。"},{"en":"How much time does this approach take? You have to wait to receive a vector (from your left) before you can send a vector (to your right). In total, this approach takes \\(p\\) time steps to circle the loop in the first step, and another \\(p\\) time steps to send the overall sum in a loop in the second loop, for a total of \\(2p = O(p)\\) time steps. Each node has to send or receive up to \\(2D\\) bytes per time step.","en_html":"How much time does this approach take? You have to wait to receive a vector (from your left) before you can send a vector (to your right). In total, this approach takes \\(p\\) time steps to circle the loop in the first step, and another \\(p\\) time steps to send the overall sum in a loop in the second loop, for a total of \\(2p = O(p)\\) time steps. Each node has to send or receive up to \\(2D\\) bytes per time step.","zh_html":"这种方法需要多长时间？你必须等待收到向量（从左侧）之后才能发送向量（到右侧）。总的来说，这种方法需要 \\(p\\) 个时间步在第一步绕环一圈，另外 \\(p\\) 个时间步在第二步发送总和绕环一圈，总共 \\(2p = O(p)\\) 个时间步。每个节点每个时间步需要发送或接收最多 \\(2D\\) 字节。"},{"en":"As in the tree-based topology, an exact time comparison would require plugging in values for \\(D\\) and the resource limits in the network. Roughly speaking, compared to the first 2 approaches, this approach requires more time steps, but each time step can probably complete faster since there’s less data to transmit per time step.","en_html":"As in the tree-based topology, an exact time comparison would require plugging in values for \\(D\\) and the resource limits in the network. Roughly speaking, compared to the first 2 approaches, this approach requires more time steps, but each time step can probably complete faster since there’s less data to transmit per time step.","zh_html":"与基于树的方法一样，精确的时间比较需要代入 \\(D\\) 和网络资源限制的具体值。粗略地说，与前两种方法相比，这种方法需要更多的时间步，但每个时间步可能完成得更快，因为每个时间步传输的数据更少。"},{"en":"Note: We chose Node 5 as the starting point, but other starting points would have also worked. Likewise, we could have also moved left-to-right in the loop, instead of right-to-left.","en_html":"Note: We chose Node 5 as the starting point, but other starting points would have also worked. Likewise, we could have also moved left-to-right in the loop, instead of right-to-left.","zh_html":"注意：我们选择了节点 5 作为起点，但其他起点也同样可行。同样，我们也可以选择在环中从左向右移动，而不是从右向左。"},{"en":"Approach 5: Ring-Based (Optimized)","en_html":"<a href=\"#approach-5-ring-based-optimized\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"approach-5-ring-based-optimized\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Approach 5: Ring-Based (Optimized)","zh_html":"<a href=\"#approach-5-ring-based-optimized\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"approach-5-ring-based-optimized\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 方法 5：基于环-优化(Ring-Based, Optimized)"},{"en":"The approaches we’ve seen so far will give us the right answer, but they create bursty workloads. In the naive ring-based approach, each node spends most of its time idling and doing nothing. At one point, you suddenly receive an entire vector, and you have to immediately add that vector to your own vector, and send the result to your left. Everyone else has to wait for you to finish this operation.","en_html":"The approaches we’ve seen so far will give us the right answer, but they create bursty workloads. In the naive ring-based approach, each node spends most of its time idling and doing nothing. At one point, you suddenly receive an entire vector, and you have to immediately add that vector to your own vector, and send the result to your left. Everyone else has to wait for you to finish this operation.","zh_html":"我们到目前为止看到的方法都能给出正确答案，但它们会产生突发性工作负载(bursty workloads)。在朴素的基于环方法中，每个节点大部分时间都在空闲，什么也不做。在某个时刻，你突然收到整个向量，必须立即将该向量与自己的向量相加，并将结果发送到左侧。其他所有人都必须等待你完成这个操作。"},{"en":"To create a less bursty, more balanced workload, we can stagger the steps of the naive ring-based AllReduce. Sending your entire vector to the left at once creates a burst of work for your left neighbor. Instead, you can send your vector to the left incrementally, by sending one element per time step.","en_html":"To create a less bursty, more balanced workload, we can stagger the steps of the naive ring-based AllReduce. Sending your entire vector to the left at once creates a burst of work for your left neighbor. Instead, you can send your vector to the left incrementally, by sending one element per time step.","zh_html":"为了创建更少突发、更均衡的工作负载，我们可以错开(stagger)朴素基于环 AllReduce 的步骤。一次性将整个向量发送到左侧会给左侧邻居带来突发的工作。相反，你可以逐渐地将向量发送到左侧，每个时间步发送一个元素。"},{"en":"When you receive a single element (from your left), you can add that element to your own corresponding element. You can then send out that resulting sum (still a single element) to your left.","en_html":"When you receive a single element (from your left), you can add that element to your own corresponding element. You can then send out that resulting sum (still a single element) to your left.","zh_html":"当你收到一个元素（从左侧）时，你可以将该元素添加到你自己对应的元素中。然后你可以将得到的和（仍然是一个元素）发送到左侧。"},{"en":"In addition to staggering the sending of each vector, notice that the starting points were also staggered. Instead of the starting point being Node 5 sending all of its elements, we now start by having the \\(i\\)th node send its \\(i\\)th element.","en_html":"In addition to staggering the sending of each vector, notice that the starting points were also staggered. Instead of the starting point being Node 5 sending all of its elements, we now start by having the \\(i\\)th node send its \\(i\\)th element.","zh_html":"除了错开每个向量的发送外，注意起点也被错开了。不再是节点 5 开始发送其所有元素，而是第 \\(i\\) 个节点开始发送其第 \\(i\\) 个元素。"},{"en":"By staggering the operation along both of these dimensions (each node sends one element at a time, and each node starts at a different element), we can create a more balanced workload. At every time step, each node receives exactly one element from its right, computes one sum, and sends exactly one element to its left.","en_html":"By staggering the operation along both of these dimensions (each node sends one element at a time, and each node starts at a different element), we can create a more balanced workload. At every time step, each node receives exactly one element from its right, computes one sum, and sends exactly one element to its left.","zh_html":"通过在两个维度上错开操作（每个节点一次发送一个元素，且每个节点从不同的元素开始），我们可以创建更均衡的工作负载。在每个时间步，每个节点从其右侧恰好接收一个元素，计算一个和，并向其左侧恰好发送一个元素。"},{"en":"If we repeat this \\(p\\) times, then each element will have cycled all the way around the ring.","en_html":"If we repeat this \\(p\\) times, then each element will have cycled all the way around the ring.","zh_html":"如果我们重复这个过程 \\(p\\) 次，每个元素将绕环完整地循环一圈。"},{"en":"However, not everyone knows all the elements of the sum vector, so we have to cycle around the ring one more time. Just like in the naive approach, in this second cycle, when you receive an element of the overall sum, you simply send a copy to your right.","en_html":"However, not everyone knows all the elements of the sum vector, so we have to cycle around the ring one more time. Just like in the naive approach, in this second cycle, when you receive an element of the overall sum, you simply send a copy to your right.","zh_html":"然而，不是每个人都知道总和向量的所有元素，所以我们必须再绕环一圈。就像在朴素方法中一样，在这第二圈中，当你收到总和的一个元素时，你只需向右发送一个副本。"},{"en":"When watching this animated demo, try to focus on the two dimensions in which we are staggering the operations. If you focus on a single column, you’ll notice that we send the elements one at a time, and we receive elements one at a time.","en_html":"When watching this animated demo, try to focus on the two dimensions in which we are staggering the operations. If you focus on a single column, you’ll notice that we send the elements one at a time, and we receive elements one at a time.","zh_html":"在观看这个动画演示时，试着专注于我们错开操作的两个维度。如果你专注于单一列，你会注意到我们一次发送一个元素，一次接收一个元素。"},{"en":"Also, if you focus on a single row, you’ll notice that every node receives the sum of all the \\(i\\)th elements so far, adds its own \\(i\\)th element, and sends the new sum left. Since this operation cycles through all the nodes, we know that we’ll end up adding all the \\(i\\)th elements together.","en_html":"Also, if you focus on a single row, you’ll notice that every node receives the sum of all the \\(i\\)th elements so far, adds its own \\(i\\)th element, and sends the new sum left. Since this operation cycles through all the nodes, we know that we’ll end up adding all the \\(i\\)th elements together.","zh_html":"另外，如果你专注于单一行，你会注意到每个节点收到到目前为止所有第 \\(i\\) 个元素的和，添加自己的第 \\(i\\) 个元素，并将新的和向左发送。因为这个操作循环经过所有节点，我们知道我们最终会将所有第 \\(i\\) 个元素相加起来。"},{"en":"In summary, the optimized ring-based AllReduce does exactly the same operations as the naive ring-based AllReduce. The only difference is we have staggered the sending and receiving of vectors, to reduce the burstiness of the workload at each node.","en_html":"In summary, the optimized ring-based AllReduce does exactly the same operations as the naive ring-based AllReduce. The only difference is we have staggered the sending and receiving of vectors, to reduce the burstiness of the workload at each node.","zh_html":"总结一下，优化的基于环的 AllReduce 与朴素的基于环的 AllReduce 执行完全相同的操作。唯一的区别是我们错开了向量的发送和接收，以减少每个节点工作负载的突发性。"},{"en":"The bandwidth and time analysis of the optimized ring-based AllReduce is the same as the naive ring-based AllReduce. Each node receives/sends \\(2D\\) bytes in the first step, and another \\(2D\\) bytes in the second step, for a total of \\(4 \\cdot D \\cdot p = O(D \\cdot p)\\) bytes. We still need \\(O(p)\\) time steps to make two cycles around the ring.","en_html":"The bandwidth and time analysis of the optimized ring-based AllReduce is the same as the naive ring-based AllReduce. Each node receives/sends \\(2D\\) bytes in the first step, and another \\(2D\\) bytes in the second step, for a total of \\(4 \\cdot D \\cdot p = O(D \\cdot p)\\) bytes. We still need \\(O(p)\\) time steps to make two cycles around the ring.","zh_html":"优化的基于环的 AllReduce 的带宽和时间分析与朴素方法相同。每个节点在第一步中接收/发送 \\(2D\\) 字节，在第二步中再接收/发送 \\(2D\\) 字节，总共 \\(4 \\cdot D \\cdot p = O(D \\cdot p)\\) 字节。我们仍然需要 \\(O(p)\\) 个时间步来绕环两圈。"},{"en":"However, the bandwidth per time step is has been improved in the optimized approach. In the naive approach, each node had to receive and send an entire vector of in a single time step, for a total of \\(2D\\) bytes transmitted in a single time step. In the optimized approach, each node only has to receive and send a single element at each time step, for a total of \\(2D/p\\) bytes transmitted in a single time step","en_html":"However, the bandwidth per time step is has been improved in the optimized approach. In the naive approach, each node had to receive and send an entire vector of in a single time step, for a total of \\(2D\\) bytes transmitted in a single time step. In the optimized approach, each node only has to receive and send a single element at each time step, for a total of \\(2D/p\\) bytes transmitted in a single time step","zh_html":"然而，每个时间步的带宽在优化方法中得到了改善。在朴素方法中，每个节点在一个时间步内必须接收和发送整个向量，总计在一个时间步内传输 \\(2D\\) 字节。在优化方法中，每个节点在每个时间步只需接收和发送单个元素，总计在一个时间步内传输 \\(2D/p\\) 字节。"},{"en":"Overlay and Underlay Topologies","en_html":"<a href=\"#overlay-and-underlay-topologies\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"overlay-and-underlay-topologies\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Overlay and Underlay Topologies","zh_html":"<a href=\"#overlay-and-underlay-topologies\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"overlay-and-underlay-topologies\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 覆盖拓扑与底层拓扑(Overlay and Underlay Topologies)"},{"en":"Recall that these collective operations are defined such that the user (i.e. AI training program) can select any set of \\(p\\) hosts, and ask them to run an AllReduce operation. When the user selects \\(p\\) hosts, it’s unlikely that they are already nicely connected in a ring topology. How can we implement the ring-based AllReduce, even if the hosts themselves aren’t physically connected in the ring topology?","en_html":"Recall that these collective operations are defined such that the user (i.e. AI training program) can select any set of \\(p\\) hosts, and ask them to run an AllReduce operation. When the user selects \\(p\\) hosts, it’s unlikely that they are already nicely connected in a ring topology. How can we implement the ring-based AllReduce, even if the hosts themselves aren’t physically connected in the ring topology?","zh_html":"回顾一下，这些集合操作的定义使得用户（即 AI 训练程序）可以选择任意一组 \\(p\\) 个主机，并要求它们运行 AllReduce 操作。当用户选择 \\(p\\) 个主机时，它们不太可能已经以环形拓扑整齐地连接在一起。我们如何实现基于环的 AllReduce，即使主机本身在物理上并没有以环形拓扑连接？"},{"en":"The answer is to use overlays. We can draw virtual links to connect the hosts in a ring topology:","en_html":"The answer is to use overlays. We can draw virtual links to connect the hosts in a ring topology:","zh_html":"答案是使用覆盖(overlays)。我们可以绘制虚拟链接来将主机连接成环形拓扑："},{"en":"When Node D sends its vector to Node B, in the overlay perspective, Node D is sending the vector along a single (virtual) link to its direct neighbor. In the underlay perspective, this vector actually has to travel several hops before reaching its destination of Node B.","en_html":"When Node D sends its vector to Node B, in the overlay perspective, Node D is sending the vector along a single (virtual) link to its direct neighbor. In the underlay perspective, this vector actually has to travel several hops before reaching its destination of Node B.","zh_html":"当节点 D 将其向量发送给节点 B 时，从覆盖层的角度看，节点 D 是沿着一条单一的（虚拟）链接发送向量给其直接邻居。从底层网络的角度看，这个向量实际上需要经过多跳才能到达其目的地节点 B。"},{"en":"As we saw when we discussed overlay-based multicast, overlay performance depends on how well the overlay topology matches the underlay network. In the context of AI training, performance is especially important because we’re transmitting huge amounts of data.","en_html":"As we saw when we discussed overlay-based multicast, overlay performance depends on how well the overlay topology matches the underlay network. In the context of AI training, performance is especially important because we’re transmitting huge amounts of data.","zh_html":"正如我们在讨论基于覆盖的多播时所见，覆盖的性能取决于覆盖拓扑与底层网络的匹配程度。在 AI 训练的背景下，性能尤其重要，因为我们传输的是海量数据。"},{"en":"To demonstrate why overlay topology matters, suppose that 4 nodes want to run an AllReduce operation. How do we number the nodes to achieve the best performance?","en_html":"To demonstrate why overlay topology matters, suppose that 4 nodes want to run an AllReduce operation. How do we number the nodes to achieve the best performance?","zh_html":"为了演示为什么覆盖拓扑很重要，假设 4 个节点想要运行 AllReduce 操作。我们如何对节点进行编号以获得最佳性能？"},{"en":"First, note that any numbering of nodes will produce the correct AllReduce result. In other words, any of the nodes could be Node 1, and any of the nodes could be Node 2, and so on. (This is not true for all collective operations, but it is true for AllReduce.)","en_html":"First, note that any numbering of nodes will produce the correct AllReduce result. In other words, any of the nodes could be Node 1, and any of the nodes could be Node 2, and so on. (This is not true for all collective operations, but it is true for AllReduce.)","zh_html":"首先，注意任何节点编号都会产生正确的 AllReduce 结果。换句话说，任何节点可以是节点 1，任何节点可以是节点 2，以此类推。（这对所有集合操作并不成立，但对 AllReduce 成立。）"},{"en":"Here are two possible numberings of the nodes:","en_html":"Here are two possible numberings of the nodes:","zh_html":"这里是两种可能的节点编号方式："},{"en":"The first approach results in an average stretch of 3.5. In particular, notice that the C-to-D and B-to-A virtual links require traversing lots of links through the underlay network.","en_html":"The first approach results in an average stretch of 3.5. In particular, notice that the C-to-D and B-to-A virtual links require traversing lots of links through the underlay network.","zh_html":"第一种方法产生的平均伸展因子(stretch)为 3.5。特别是，C 到 D 和 B 到 A 的虚拟链接需要通过底层网络遍历大量链接。"},{"en":"By contrast, the second approach results in an average stretch of 2.5. This set of virtual links puts neighboring links in the ring closer to each other.","en_html":"By contrast, the second approach results in an average stretch of 2.5. This set of virtual links puts neighboring links in the ring closer to each other.","zh_html":"相比之下，第二种方法产生的平均伸展因子为 2.5。这组虚拟链接将环中相邻的链接放置得更近。"},{"en":"More generally, to optimize the performance of ring-based AllReduce, we would like adjacent nodes (e.g. Node \\(i\\) and Node \\(i+1\\)) to be near each other in the network.","en_html":"More generally, to optimize the performance of ring-based AllReduce, we would like adjacent nodes (e.g. Node \\(i\\) and Node \\(i+1\\)) to be near each other in the network.","zh_html":"更一般地说，为了优化基于环的 AllReduce 的性能，我们希望相邻节点（例如节点 \\(i\\) 和节点 \\(i+1\\)）在网络上彼此靠近。"},{"en":"This diagram shows an arbitrary underlay network topology, but the same idea holds for the highly-structured datacenter-like topologies we use for AI training. Recall that in these datacenter-like topologies, some nodes have very high-performance connections (e.g. two GPUs on the same machine), while other nodes have worse-performing connections (e.g. two GPUs on different racks).","en_html":"This diagram shows an arbitrary underlay network topology, but the same idea holds for the highly-structured datacenter-like topologies we use for AI training. Recall that in these datacenter-like topologies, some nodes have very high-performance connections (e.g. two GPUs on the same machine), while other nodes have worse-performing connections (e.g. two GPUs on different racks).","zh_html":"这张图展示了一个任意的底层网络拓扑，但同样的想法也适用于我们用于 AI 训练的高度结构化的数据中心类拓扑。回顾一下，在这些数据中心类拓扑中，有些节点具有非常高性能的连接（例如同一台机器上的两个 GPU），而其他节点具有较低性能的连接（例如不同机架上的两个 GPU）。"},{"en":"AI training jobs are predictable, and the underlying topology is fixed and regular. This means that we have many opportunities to optimize the performance of our training job. For example, we can assign specific jobs to specific nodes, so that collective operations are performed on nearby nodes (e.g. all the nodes in the same rack). Finding ways to optimize AI training jobs is an active area of research.","en_html":"AI training jobs are predictable, and the underlying topology is fixed and regular. This means that we have many opportunities to optimize the performance of our training job. For example, we can assign specific jobs to specific nodes, so that collective operations are performed on nearby nodes (e.g. all the nodes in the same rack). Finding ways to optimize AI training jobs is an active area of research.","zh_html":"AI 训练作业是可预测的，底层拓扑是固定且规则的。这意味着我们有很多机会来优化训练作业的性能。例如，我们可以将特定作业分配给特定节点，以便集合操作在附近的节点上执行（例如同一个机架上的所有节点）。寻找优化 AI 训练作业的方法是一个活跃的研究领域。"},{"en":"Layers of Abstraction","en_html":"<a href=\"#layers-of-abstraction\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layers-of-abstraction\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Layers of Abstraction","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layers-of-abstraction\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layers-of-abstraction\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 抽象层(Layers of Abstraction)"},{"en":"In summary, you can think about collective operations at three layer of operations:","en_html":"In summary, you can think about collective operations at three layer of operations:","zh_html":"总结一下，你可以从三个操作层面来思考集合操作："},{"en":"Definitions. At the highest layer of abstraction, we defined the operations by specifying the input and the expected output. The user only needs to understand these definitions to use the collectives. The user does not need to know how the operation is implemented.","en_html":"<p>Definitions. At the highest layer of abstraction, we defined the operations by specifying the input and the expected output. The user only needs to understand these definitions to use the collectives. The user does not need to know how the operation is implemented.</p>","zh_html":"<p>定义(Definitions)。在最高的抽象层，我们通过指定输入和预期输出来定义操作。用户只需要理解这些定义即可使用集合操作。用户不需要知道操作是如何实现的。</p>"},{"en":"Overlay. Going down one layer of abstraction, we can think about what data gets exchanged in the overlay topology. At this level, you can assume that the nodes are organized in a useful topology (e.g. tree or ring), and can send data along virtual links in that topology.","en_html":"<p>Overlay. Going down one layer of abstraction, we can think about what data gets exchanged in the overlay topology. At this level, you can assume that the nodes are organized in a useful topology (e.g. tree or ring), and can send data along virtual links in that topology.</p>","zh_html":"<p>覆盖(Overlay)。向下一层抽象，我们可以思考覆盖拓扑中交换了哪些数据。在这个层面上，你可以假设节点按某种有用的拓扑（例如树或环）组织，并且可以沿着该拓扑中的虚拟链接发送数据。</p>"},{"en":"Underlay. At the lowest level of abstraction, we think about how the virtual links (overlay) correspond to actual physical links in the underlay. When Node 5 sends a vector to Node 4, that vector actually has to be forwarded across several physical routers and links.","en_html":"<p>Underlay. At the lowest level of abstraction, we think about how the virtual links (overlay) correspond to actual physical links in the underlay. When Node 5 sends a vector to Node 4, that vector actually has to be forwarded across several physical routers and links.</p>","zh_html":"<p>底层(Underlay)。在最低的抽象层，我们思考虚拟链接（覆盖）如何对应于底层中的实际物理链接。当节点 5 向节点 4 发送向量时，该向量实际上必须经过多个物理路由器和链路转发。</p>"}]},"/beyond-client-server/collective-operations.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Collective Operations","en_html":"<a href=\"#collective-operations\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"collective-operations\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Collective Operations","zh_html":"<a href=\"#collective-operations\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"collective-operations\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 集合操作(Collective Operations)"},{"en":"Motivation: AI Training","en_html":"<a href=\"#motivation-ai-training\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"motivation-ai-training\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Motivation: AI Training","zh_html":"<a href=\"#motivation-ai-training\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"motivation-ai-training\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 动机：AI 训练"},{"en":"As you’ve probably read in the news, AI (artificial intelligence) is a very active area of research. Modern AI systems require training models on huge amounts of data.","en_html":"As you’ve probably read in the news, AI (artificial intelligence) is a very active area of research. Modern AI systems require training models on huge amounts of data.","zh_html":"你可能已经从新闻中了解到，AI（人工智能）是一个非常活跃的研究领域。现代 AI 系统需要在海量数据上训练模型。"},{"en":"For these notes, we will completely ignore the details of how these models work. All you need to know is that we start with some untrained model: think of this as a big matrix filled with random numbers. Then, we use train the model using a huge amount of training data: think of this as running many matrix multiplication operations (i.e. multiplications and additions) with the training data and the model. Eventually, the output is a trained model: think of this like the big matrix from before, but now filled with useful numbers.","en_html":"For these notes, we will completely ignore the details of how these models work. All you need to know is that we start with some untrained model: think of this as a big matrix filled with random numbers. Then, we use train the model using a huge amount of training data: think of this as running many matrix multiplication operations (i.e. multiplications and additions) with the training data and the model. Eventually, the output is a trained model: think of this like the big matrix from before, but now filled with useful numbers.","zh_html":"在这份笔记中，我们将完全忽略这些模型如何工作的细节。你只需要知道我们从一些未训练的模型开始：把它想象成一个填满随机数的大型矩阵。然后，我们使用大量的训练数据来训练模型：把它想象成用训练数据和模型运行许多矩阵乘法操作（即乘法和加法）。最终，输出是一个训练好的模型：可以想象成之前的大矩阵，但现在填满了有用的数字。"},{"en":"In reality, AI training is far more complicated than this. For example, the training process is iterative: you’ll run the model on some training data, and see how well you did. Then, you’ll compute an error term based on the mistakes you made, and use that to update your model. We will not care about any of this. We’ll just think of training as a black box that runs lots of matrix multiplications on very very big datasets.","en_html":"In reality, AI training is far more complicated than this. For example, the training process is iterative: you’ll run the model on some training data, and see how well you did. Then, you’ll compute an error term based on the mistakes you made, and use that to update your model. We will not care about any of this. We’ll just think of training as a black box that runs lots of matrix multiplications on very very big datasets.","zh_html":"在现实中，AI 训练远比这复杂。例如，训练过程是迭代的：你会在一些训练数据上运行模型，看看表现得如何。然后，你根据所犯的错误计算误差项，并用它来更新模型。我们不会关心这些。我们只把训练看作一个黑盒，它在非常大的数据集上运行大量矩阵乘法。"},{"en":"Distributed Training","en_html":"<a href=\"#distributed-training\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"distributed-training\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Distributed Training","zh_html":"<a href=\"#distributed-training\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"distributed-training\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 分布式训练(Distributed Training)"},{"en":"AI training jobs are far too large to be run serially on a single computer. If you ran the matrix multiplication by multiplying numbers together one at a time, your training job would never finish. Instead, we need to parallelize these jobs so that many operations (e.g. multiplications) are being run at the same time. There are many approaches to distributed computing, each of which parallelizes the job along a different dimension:","en_html":"AI training jobs are far too large to be run serially on a single computer. If you ran the matrix multiplication by multiplying numbers together one at a time, your training job would never finish. Instead, we need to parallelize these jobs so that many operations (e.g. multiplications) are being run at the same time. There are many approaches to <strong>distributed computing</strong>, each of which parallelizes the job along a different dimension:","zh_html":"AI 训练任务太大了，无法在单台计算机上串行运行。如果你通过一次一个地相乘数字来运行矩阵乘法，你的训练任务将永远无法完成。相反，我们需要并行化这些任务，使得许多操作（例如乘法）同时运行。有许多分布式计算(distributed computing)的方法，每种方法沿着不同的维度对任务进行并行化："},{"en":"We could split up the training data so that each node is training on a different subset of the data.","en_html":"We could split up the training data so that each node is training on a different subset of the data.","zh_html":"我们可以拆分训练数据，使每个节点在数据的不同子集上进行训练。"},{"en":"We could split up the model itself, so that each node is training a different subset of the model.","en_html":"We could split up the model itself, so that each node is training a different subset of the model.","zh_html":"我们可以拆分模型本身，使每个节点训练模型的不同子集。"},{"en":"We could pipeline the operations, so that each node is running a different subset of the operations. For example, if the desired operation is “add 5” followed by “square the number,” we could split this up so that your node do the addition, and then pass the result to me so that my node can do the squaring. Then, every piece of data passes through your node first, and then my node, to complete the overall operation.","en_html":"We could pipeline the operations, so that each node is running a different subset of the operations. For example, if the desired operation is “add 5” followed by “square the number,” we could split this up so that your node do the addition, and then pass the result to me so that my node can do the squaring. Then, every piece of data passes through your node first, and then my node, to complete the overall operation.","zh_html":"我们可以将操作流水线化(pipeline)，使每个节点运行操作的不同子集。例如，如果期望的操作是\"加 5\"后跟\"计算平方\"，我们可以将其拆分，使得你的节点做加法，然后将结果传给我，让我的节点做平方。然后，每条数据先经过你的节点，再经过我的节点，完成整体操作。"},{"en":"Again, we will completely ignore the details of how the work is distributed. We have some large task, and it has been split up into smaller sub-tasks.","en_html":"Again, we will completely ignore the details of how the work is distributed. We have some large task, and it has been split up into smaller sub-tasks.","zh_html":"同样，我们将完全忽略工作是如何分布的细节。我们有一些大型任务，它已经被拆分成较小的子任务。"},{"en":"One important thing that we do care about is how these nodes synchronize with each other. These nodes will often need to communicate with one another to ensure that their states are consistent. Also, after running some operation, it could be the case that each node has a piece of the output, and everyone needs to coordinate to combine those pieces into the full output.","en_html":"One important thing that we do care about is how these nodes synchronize with each other. These nodes will often need to communicate with one another to ensure that their states are consistent. Also, after running some operation, it could be the case that each node has a piece of the output, and everyone needs to coordinate to combine those pieces into the full output.","zh_html":"我们确实关心的一个问题是这些节点如何彼此同步。这些节点经常需要相互通信，以确保它们的状态是一致的。此外，在运行某个操作之后，可能出现每个节点都拥有输出的一部分的情况，每个人需要协调以将这些部分组合成完整的输出。"},{"en":"By combining our picture of the training model with our picture of distributed computing, we have a very high-level overview of what distributed training looks like:","en_html":"By combining our picture of the training model with our picture of distributed computing, we have a very high-level overview of what distributed training looks like:","zh_html":"通过结合我们关于训练模型和分布式计算的图景，我们对分布式训练有了一个非常高层级的概览："},{"en":"Split the task up into sub-tasks. Each node runs a sub-task.","en_html":"<p>Split the task up into sub-tasks. Each node runs a sub-task.</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/beyond-client-server/7-063-distributed-1.png\" /></img></p>","zh_html":"<p>将任务拆分成子任务。每个节点运行一个子任务。</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/beyond-client-server/7-063-distributed-1.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"After every node finishes their sub-task, everyone exchanges a large amount of state.","en_html":"<p>After every node finishes their sub-task, everyone exchanges a large amount of state.</p> <p><img width=\"800px\" src=\"../assets/beyond-client-server/7-064-distributed-2.png\" /></img></p>","zh_html":"<p>在每个节点完成子任务后，每个人都交换大量状态。</p> <p><img width=\"800px\" src=\"../assets/beyond-client-server/7-064-distributed-2.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"Proceed to the next task, and repeat steps 1-2 for the next task.","en_html":"<p>Proceed to the next task, and repeat steps 1-2 for the next task.</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/beyond-client-server/7-065-distributed-3.png\" /></img></p>","zh_html":"<p>继续下一个任务，并重复步骤 1-2。</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/beyond-client-server/7-065-distributed-3.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"Our focus is the data exchange in the second step, and how to make this data exchange efficient.","en_html":"Our focus is the data exchange in the second step, and how to make this data exchange efficient.","zh_html":"我们的重点是第二步中的数据交换，以及如何使这种数据交换高效。"},{"en":"Again, we don’t care about exactly what data is being exchanged. Depending on how we distribute the work, and depending on the specific AI model we’re building, the nature of the data we’re exchanging can be slightly different. Our focus is on how that data is exchanged.","en_html":"Again, we don’t care about exactly what data is being exchanged. Depending on how we distribute the work, and depending on the specific AI model we’re building, the nature of the data we’re exchanging can be slightly different. Our focus is on how that data is exchanged.","zh_html":"同样，我们不关心具体交换了什么数据。根据我们如何分布工作，以及根据我们正在构建的具体 AI 模型，我们正在交换的数据的性质可能略有不同。我们的重点是如何交换这些数据。"},{"en":"Distributed Training Infrastructure","en_html":"<a href=\"#distributed-training-infrastructure\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"distributed-training-infrastructure\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Distributed Training Infrastructure","zh_html":"<a href=\"#distributed-training-infrastructure\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"distributed-training-infrastructure\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 分布式训练基础设施"},{"en":"When we split up a training job amongst many nodes, what exactly is each node?","en_html":"When we split up a training job amongst many nodes, what exactly is each node?","zh_html":"当我们将训练任务分配到多个节点时，每个节点到底是什么？"},{"en":"Each node could be a computer running a standard CPU, but in reality, nodes are specialized GPUs (Graphics Processing Units). These are processing chips that are specially designed to run AI operations (e.g. matrix multiplication) very efficiently. Instead of GPUs, nodes could also be TPUs (Tensor Processing Units), which are AI-optimized chips developed by Google.","en_html":"Each node could be a computer running a standard CPU, but in reality, nodes are specialized GPUs (Graphics Processing Units). These are processing chips that are specially designed to run AI operations (e.g. matrix multiplication) very efficiently. Instead of GPUs, nodes could also be TPUs (Tensor Processing Units), which are AI-optimized chips developed by Google.","zh_html":"每个节点可以是一台运行标准 CPU 的计算机，但实际上，节点是专门的 GPU（图形处理单元，Graphics Processing Units）。这些是专门设计用于非常高效地运行 AI 操作（例如矩阵乘法）的处理芯片。除了 GPU，节点也可以是 TPU（张量处理单元，Tensor Processing Units），这是谷歌开发的 AI 优化芯片。"},{"en":"A single training job could be run on a few hundred nodes, or even tens of thousands of nodes, depending on the size and context of the job, and how powerful each node is.","en_html":"A single training job could be run on a few hundred nodes, or even tens of thousands of nodes, depending on the size and context of the job, and how powerful each node is.","zh_html":"一个训练任务可以在几百个节点上运行，甚至数万个节点，具体取决于任务的大小和上下文，以及每个节点的能力。"},{"en":"The GPUs are inter-connected in a datacenter-like network, which gives us the datacenter benefits that we’ve previously seen: The nodes are physically close to each other (e.g. in the same building). The nodes are organized in a structured topology (e.g. Clos network). The nodes are homogenous (all built the same), and the links have very high bandwidth.","en_html":"The GPUs are inter-connected in a datacenter-like network, which gives us the datacenter benefits that we’ve previously seen: The nodes are physically close to each other (e.g. in the same building). The nodes are organized in a structured topology (e.g. Clos network). The nodes are homogenous (all built the same), and the links have very high bandwidth.","zh_html":"GPU 在类似数据中心的网络中互连，这给我们提供了之前见过的数据中心优势：节点在物理上彼此靠近（例如在同一栋建筑中）。节点以结构化拓扑组织（例如 Clos 网络）。节点是同质的（都是相同构造），链路具有非常高的带宽。"},{"en":"If you look inside an AI training datacenter, you’ll see servers organized into racks, just like in any other datacenter. However, unlike other datacenters we’ve seen so far, each server contains of one or more GPUs for AI computation. The server can also have a regular general-purpose CPU for miscellaneous operations, although the CPU is typically not that strong and is not doing the bulk of the computation work. All the GPUs on the server use the same NIC to exchange data with other servers.","en_html":"If you look inside an AI training datacenter, you’ll see servers organized into racks, just like in any other datacenter. However, unlike other datacenters we’ve seen so far, each server contains of one or more GPUs for AI computation. The server can also have a regular general-purpose CPU for miscellaneous operations, although the CPU is typically not that strong and is not doing the bulk of the computation work. All the GPUs on the server use the same NIC to exchange data with other servers.","zh_html":"如果你查看一个 AI 训练数据中心的内部，你会看到服务器像任何其他数据中心一样组织在机架中。然而，与我们之前见过的其他数据中心不同，每个服务器包含一个或多个用于 AI 计算的 GPU。服务器还可以有一个常规的通用 CPU 用于杂项操作，尽管 CPU 通常不那么强大，且不承担主要的计算工作。服务器上的所有 GPU 使用同一个 NIC 与其他服务器交换数据。"},{"en":"Because each server has multiple GPUs, we have to slightly modify our network topology abstraction. As before, the servers are connected with switches and high-bandwidth links. However, we now also have to consider the possibility of two nodes on the same server talking to each other. Communication within the same server is extremely efficient compared to communication across servers, so we can model intra-server communication as a link with infinite bandwidth and zero latency.","en_html":"Because each server has multiple GPUs, we have to slightly modify our network topology abstraction. As before, the servers are connected with switches and high-bandwidth links. However, we now also have to consider the possibility of two nodes on the same server talking to each other. Communication within the same server is extremely efficient compared to communication across servers, so we can model intra-server communication as a link with infinite bandwidth and zero latency.","zh_html":"由于每个服务器有多个 GPU，我们必须稍微修改我们的网络拓扑抽象。和之前一样，服务器通过交换机和高带宽链路连接。然而，我们现在还必须考虑同一台服务器上的两个节点相互通信的可能性。同一台服务器内的通信与跨服务器通信相比极其高效，因此我们可以将服务器内通信建模为具有无限带宽和零延迟的链路。"},{"en":"Each GPU could have its own dedicated memory, and we can use techniques like RDMA to speed up transferring data between one GPU’s memory and another GPU’s memory.","en_html":"Each GPU could have its own dedicated memory, and we can use techniques like RDMA to speed up transferring data between one GPU’s memory and another GPU’s memory.","zh_html":"每个 GPU 可以有自己的专用内存，我们可以使用 RDMA 等技术来加速数据从一个 GPU 的内存到另一个 GPU 的内存的传输。"},{"en":"There are many different topologies for inter-connection between racks, though for our purposes, we’ll use a fat-tree Clos topology to connect the racks. Regardless of which topology you use, some pairs of GPUs will be closer (e.g. GPUs in the same server can communicate without using the network at all), other pairs of GPUs will be further away (e.g. GPUs on different servers, but in the same pod/rack, connected via a single switch), and other pairs of GPUs will be the furthest away (e.g. GPUs on different racks, connected via multiple hops). The closer pairs of GPUs can communicate with higher bandwidth and less latency, compared to the further pairs of GPUs. In summary, if you pick any pair of nodes, some pairs are better-connected than others.","en_html":"There are many different topologies for inter-connection between racks, though for our purposes, we’ll use a fat-tree Clos topology to connect the racks. Regardless of which topology you use, some pairs of GPUs will be closer (e.g. GPUs in the same server can communicate without using the network at all), other pairs of GPUs will be further away (e.g. GPUs on different servers, but in the same pod/rack, connected via a single switch), and other pairs of GPUs will be the furthest away (e.g. GPUs on different racks, connected via multiple hops). The closer pairs of GPUs can communicate with higher bandwidth and less latency, compared to the further pairs of GPUs. In summary, if you pick any pair of nodes, some pairs are better-connected than others.","zh_html":"机架间互连有许多不同的拓扑结构，但出于我们的目的，我们将使用胖树(fat-tree)Clos 拓扑来连接机架。无论你使用哪种拓扑，有些 GPU 对会更近（例如同一台服务器内的 GPU，完全不需要使用网络即可通信），其他 GPU 对会更远（例如不同服务器但在同一个 pod/机架中的 GPU，通过单个交换机连接），还有一些 GPU 对是最远的（例如不同机架上的 GPU，通过多跳连接）。较近的 GPU 对可以比较远的 GPU 对以更高的带宽和更低的延迟进行通信。总之，如果你选择任意一对节点，有些对之间的连接比其他对更好。"},{"en":"Other topologies exist as well. TPUs are built with a router directly on them, so it’s possible to directly connect TPUs into a network without any switches at all. One common topology with TPUs is to inter-connect them in a 3D torus, which looks like a cube with the edges wrapping around. For example, if you reach the top of the cube and traverse an upwards link, you end up at the bottom of the cube. Or, if you reach the front of the cube and traverse a front-facing link, you end up at the back of the cube. Just like in the Clos topology, some pairs of nodes are closer (e.g. direct neighbors), while other pairs of nodes are further (e.g. multiple hops away).","en_html":"Other topologies exist as well. TPUs are built with a router directly on them, so it’s possible to directly connect TPUs into a network without any switches at all. One common topology with TPUs is to inter-connect them in a 3D torus, which looks like a cube with the edges wrapping around. For example, if you reach the top of the cube and traverse an upwards link, you end up at the bottom of the cube. Or, if you reach the front of the cube and traverse a front-facing link, you end up at the back of the cube. Just like in the Clos topology, some pairs of nodes are closer (e.g. direct neighbors), while other pairs of nodes are further (e.g. multiple hops away).","zh_html":"还存在其他拓扑。TPU 上直接集成了路由器，因此可以将 TPU 直接连接成网络而不需要任何交换机。TPU 的一种常见拓扑是将其互连成 3D 环面(3D torus)，它看起来像一个边缘环绕的立方体。例如，如果你到达立方体的顶部并穿过向上的链接，你会到达立方体的底部。或者，如果你到达立方体的前面并穿过面向前方的链接，你会到达立方体的背面。就像在 Clos 拓扑中一样，有些节点对更近（例如直接邻居），而其他节点对更远（例如多跳的距离）。"},{"en":"Collective Communication: Definition","en_html":"<a href=\"#collective-communication-definition\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"collective-communication-definition\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Collective Communication: Definition","zh_html":"<a href=\"#collective-communication-definition\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"collective-communication-definition\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 集合通信(Collective Communication)：定义"},{"en":"Now that we know the task (distributed computing) and the infrastructure we’re running the task on (datacenter-like network of GPUs/TPUs), we can formally define the problem we want to solve.","en_html":"Now that we know the task (distributed computing) and the infrastructure we’re running the task on (datacenter-like network of GPUs/TPUs), we can formally define the problem we want to solve.","zh_html":"现在我们知道了任务（分布式计算）和运行任务的设施（类似数据中心的 GPU/TPU 网络），我们可以正式定义我们想要解决的问题了。"},{"en":"The textbook definition of collective communication is: A group of nodes that exchange data in a coordinated manner as part of a group computation. Informally, the idea is that many nodes work together to achieve a common goal, and the nodes have to exchange data during that process.","en_html":"The textbook definition of collective communication is: A group of nodes that exchange data in a coordinated manner as part of a group computation. Informally, the idea is that many nodes work together to achieve a common goal, and the nodes have to exchange data during that process.","zh_html":"集合通信(collective communication)的教科书定义是：一组节点以协调方式交换数据，作为组计算的一部分。非正式地说，其思想是许多节点协同工作以实现共同目标，节点在此过程中必须交换数据。"},{"en":"The ideas and terms behind collective communication were originally developed many decades ago in the context of supercomputers. The topic has once again become an active area of research thanks to recent advances in AI. Modern implementations of Collectives Communication Libraries include NCCL (Nvidia), MSCCL (Microsoft), TCCL (Thunder Research Group), and so on. The code for NCCL is available online if you’re interested.","en_html":"The ideas and terms behind collective communication were originally developed many decades ago in the context of supercomputers. The topic has once again become an active area of research thanks to recent advances in AI. Modern implementations of Collectives Communication Libraries include NCCL (Nvidia), MSCCL (Microsoft), TCCL (Thunder Research Group), and so on. The code for NCCL is available online if you’re interested.","zh_html":"集合通信背后的思想和术语最初是在几十年前超级计算机的背景下发展起来的。由于 AI 的最新进展，这个主题再次成为一个活跃的研究领域。现代集合通信库的实现包括 NCCL（Nvidia）、MSCCL（Microsoft）、TCCL（Thunder Research Group）等等。NCCL 的代码可以在线获取，如果你感兴趣的话。"},{"en":"What makes collective communication different from everything we’ve seen so far? There are 3 main differences we’ll look at.","en_html":"What makes collective communication different from everything we’ve seen so far? There are 3 main differences we’ll look at.","zh_html":"集合通信与我们目前所见的一切有什么不同？我们来看 3 个主要区别。"},{"en":"Highly structured communication: So far, when we think about the network, we’ve abstracted away the data being exchanged. We don’t know ahead of time who wants to communicate, and we build our networks so that any pair of hosts can communicate, at any time they want.","en_html":"<strong>Highly structured communication:</strong> So far, when we think about the network, we’ve abstracted away the data being exchanged. We don’t know ahead of time who wants to communicate, and we build our networks so that any pair of hosts can communicate, at any time they want.","zh_html":"<strong>高度结构化的通信：</strong>到目前为止，当考虑网络时，我们抽象掉了正在交换的数据。我们不知道谁想提前通信，我们构建网络使得任何一对主机可以在任何他们想要的时候通信。"},{"en":"By contrast, in collective communication, there is a very specific goal that the nodes want to achieve, and we know this goal ahead of time. This means that unlike in the general Internet, we have a very well-defined structure of what data is being exchanged through the network, and when that data needs to be exchanged. In other words, we have a tightly-scripted set of data exchanges and computations that all the nodes will work together to achieve.","en_html":"By contrast, in collective communication, there is a very specific goal that the nodes want to achieve, and we know this goal ahead of time. This means that unlike in the general Internet, we have a very well-defined structure of what data is being exchanged through the network, and when that data needs to be exchanged. In other words, we have a tightly-scripted set of data exchanges and computations that all the nodes will work together to achieve.","zh_html":"相比之下，在集合通信中，节点想要达到一个非常具体的目标，而且我们提前知道这个目标。这意味着与通用互联网不同，我们对通过网络交换了什么数据以及何时需要交换这些数据有非常明确的结构。换句话说，我们有一套紧密编排的数据交换和计算，所有节点将协同工作以实现它们。"},{"en":"Dedicated network infrastructure: So far, we’ve built networks that can support multiple connections happening at the same time. Even within a datacenter network, multiple tenants could be sending data over the datacenter network at the same time.","en_html":"<strong>Dedicated network infrastructure:</strong> So far, we’ve built networks that can support multiple connections happening at the same time. Even within a datacenter network, multiple tenants could be sending data over the datacenter network at the same time.","zh_html":"<strong>专用网络基础设施：</strong>到目前为止，我们构建的网络可以同时支持多个连接。即使在数据中心网络内，也可能有多个租户同时在数据中心网络上发送数据。"},{"en":"By contrast, AI training jobs are so large that they are often run on dedicated infrastructure. The training job is the only job running on the network, and no other data is being sent over the network. This means that we can predict exactly how much bandwidth is being used at any given time.","en_html":"By contrast, AI training jobs are so large that they are often run on dedicated infrastructure. The training job is the only job running on the network, and no other data is being sent over the network. This means that we can predict exactly how much bandwidth is being used at any given time.","zh_html":"相比之下，AI 训练任务非常庞大，它们通常在专用基础设施上运行。训练任务是网络上唯一运行的任务，没有其他数据在网络上传送。这意味着我们可以精确预测在任何给定时间使用了多少带宽。"},{"en":"Data is transformed as it’s exchanged: So far, when we think about sending data through the Internet (e.g. the HTTP/TCP/IP stack), our mental model is that the server has some data (e.g. a file), and they want to send a copy of that data to the user.","en_html":"<strong>Data is transformed as it’s exchanged:</strong> So far, when we think about sending data through the Internet (e.g. the HTTP/TCP/IP stack), our mental model is that the server has some data (e.g. a file), and they want to send a copy of that data to the user.","zh_html":"<strong>数据在交换过程中被转换：</strong>到目前为止，当考虑通过互联网发送数据时（例如 HTTP/TCP/IP 栈），我们的思维模型是服务器有一些数据（例如一个文件），并且它想将该数据的副本发送给用户。"},{"en":"By contrast, when running a collective operation, the data can be transformed as it is sent through the network. This is different from anything we’ve seen so far. The operations are usually fairly simple (e.g. computing sums), but it does mean the data sent by the sender(s) is not necessarily the same as the data received at the other end.","en_html":"By contrast, when running a collective operation, the data can be transformed as it is sent through the network. This is different from anything we’ve seen so far. The operations are usually fairly simple (e.g. computing sums), but it does mean the data sent by the sender(s) is not necessarily the same as the data received at the other end.","zh_html":"相比之下，在运行集合操作时，数据可以在通过网络发送的过程中被转换。这与我们目前所见的一切都不同。这些操作通常相当简单（例如计算和），但它确实意味着发送方发送的数据不一定与另一端接收的数据相同。"},{"en":"We could design a coordinated communication schemes from scratch for every AI model we build, but this would be tedious and result in lots of repeated work. Instead, we’ll define a set of basic communication patterns called collectives. Then, we can use these collectives as basic building blocks to design coordinated communication schemes for specific jobs. You can think of the basic collective operations as the API for distributed communication, e.g. the library functions available to the users. Then, the users can call these collective functions in various ways to achieve their specific goals.","en_html":"We could design a coordinated communication schemes from scratch for every AI model we build, but this would be tedious and result in lots of repeated work. Instead, we’ll define a set of basic communication patterns called <strong>collectives</strong>. Then, we can use these collectives as basic building blocks to design coordinated communication schemes for specific jobs. You can think of the basic collective operations as the API for distributed communication, e.g. the library functions available to the users. Then, the users can call these collective functions in various ways to achieve their specific goals.","zh_html":"我们可以为我们构建的每个 AI 模型从头设计协调通信方案，但这会很繁琐，并且导致大量重复工作。相反，我们将定义一组基本的通信模式，称为集合操作(collectives)。然后，我们可以将这些集合操作作为基本构建块，为特定任务设计协调通信方案。你可以将基本集合操作视为分布式通信的 API，例如库函数可供用户使用。然后，用户可以以各种方式调用这些集合函数来实现他们的特定目标。"},{"en":"It turns out that we only need a relatively small number of primitive collective operations, and most of the tasks in AI training can be broken down into these operations, and represented as various combinations of these operations.","en_html":"It turns out that we only need a relatively small number of primitive collective operations, and most of the tasks in AI training can be broken down into these operations, and represented as various combinations of these operations.","zh_html":"事实证明，我们只需要相对较少的基本集合操作，AI 训练中的大多数任务都可以分解为这些操作，并用这些操作的各种组合来表示。"},{"en":"Our focus will be on what these collectives are, and how they are implemented in the network. We won’t discuss why AI training leads to these particular collective operations. The reason why we picked these particular operations as our basic building blocks has more to do with the nature of AI computation, which is beyond our scope.","en_html":"Our focus will be on what these collectives are, and how they are implemented in the network. We won’t discuss why AI training leads to these particular collective operations. The reason why we picked these particular operations as our basic building blocks has more to do with the nature of AI computation, which is beyond our scope.","zh_html":"我们的重点将是这些集合操作是什么，以及它们如何在网络中实现。我们不会讨论为什么 AI 训练会导致这些特定的集合操作。我们选择这些特定操作作为基本构建块的原因更多与 AI 计算的性质有关，这超出了我们的范围。"},{"en":"Collective Operations: Setup","en_html":"<strong>Collective Operations: Setup</strong>","zh_html":"<strong>集合操作：设置</strong>"},{"en":"We’ll now define the 7 basic collective operations. We will define what the operations should do, by specifying an input (the data each node is holding before the operation), and a corresponding output (the data each node is holding after the operation). We are not specifying how the operation is implemented in the network (that will come later).","en_html":"We’ll now define the 7 basic collective operations. We will define what the operations should do, by specifying an input (the data each node is holding before the operation), and a corresponding output (the data each node is holding after the operation). We are not specifying how the operation is implemented in the network (that will come later).","zh_html":"我们现在将定义 7 种基本集合操作。我们将通过指定输入（操作前每个节点持有的数据）和相应的输出（操作后每个节点持有的数据）来定义操作应该做什么。我们不指定操作如何在网络中实现（这将在后面进行）。"},{"en":"Input: There are \\(p\\) nodes. In our examples, we’ll set \\(p=4\\) but other values are fine too.","en_html":"<strong>Input:</strong> There are \\(p\\) nodes. In our examples, we’ll set \\(p=4\\) but other values are fine too.","zh_html":"<strong>输入：</strong>有 \\(p\\) 个节点。在我们的例子中，我们设 \\(p=4\\)，但其他值也可以。"},{"en":"Each node has a \\(p\\)-element vector of data. For these examples, you can think of the data as an array of 4 integers. In practice, this data could be higher-dimensional as well, e.g. 4 rows of a matrix, or 4 equally-sized chunks of training data.","en_html":"Each node has a \\(p\\)-element vector of data. For these examples, you can think of the data as an array of 4 integers. In practice, this data could be higher-dimensional as well, e.g. 4 rows of a matrix, or 4 equally-sized chunks of training data.","zh_html":"每个节点有一个 \\(p\\) 元素的向量(vector)。在这些例子中，你可以把数据看作一个包含 4 个整数的数组。在实践中，这些数据也可以是更高维的，例如一个矩阵的 4 行，或 4 个大小相等的训练数据块。"},{"en":"Output: The elements are moved around between nodes in some specified way. The output specifies what values go in which boxes as a result of this operation.","en_html":"<strong>Output:</strong> The elements are moved around between nodes in some specified way. The output specifies what values go in which boxes as a result of this operation.","zh_html":"<strong>输出：</strong>元素以某种指定的方式在节点之间移动。输出指定了操作结果中哪些值放在哪些框中。"},{"en":"Also, sometimes the elements can be aggregated (e.g. summed together). Again, the output specifies what computation(s) this specific operation does, if any, and which box(es) to put the computation result(s) in.","en_html":"Also, sometimes the elements can be aggregated (e.g. summed together). Again, the output specifies what computation(s) this specific operation does, if any, and which box(es) to put the computation result(s) in.","zh_html":"此外，有时元素可以被聚合（例如求和）。同样，输出指定了这个特定操作要做什么计算（如果有的话），以及将计算结果放在哪个（些）框中。"},{"en":"Before the collective operation occurs, some additional coordination needs to take place so that each node knows its own number and the total number of nodes (e.g. “You am node 1, and there are 4 nodes in total”). This additional coordination is beyond our scope, but you can imagine that some centralized scheduler or controller will distribute this information to the nodes and set up the job.","en_html":"Before the collective operation occurs, some additional coordination needs to take place so that each node knows its own number and the total number of nodes (e.g. “You am node 1, and there are 4 nodes in total”). This additional coordination is beyond our scope, but you can imagine that some centralized scheduler or controller will distribute this information to the nodes and set up the job.","zh_html":"在集合操作发生之前，需要进行一些额外的协调，以便每个节点知道自己的编号和节点总数（例如“你是节点 1，总共有 4 个节点”）。这种额外的协调超出了我们的范围，但你可以想象某个集中式的调度器(scheduler)或控制器(controller)会将这些信息分发给节点并设置好任务。"},{"en":"To execute a collective operation, each node runs the exact same code, in parallel, at the same time. Everyone independently calls the same collective operation to start the operation, and when the operation completes, the output should match the operation definition. Ideally, the nodes have identical hardware resources, so that they all finish at the same time. If some nodes are slower than others, the operation is blocking, which means that we have to wait for everybody to finish the operation before we can proceed to the next task.","en_html":"To execute a collective operation, each node runs the exact same code, in parallel, at the same time. Everyone independently calls the same collective operation to start the operation, and when the operation completes, the output should match the operation definition. Ideally, the nodes have identical hardware resources, so that they all finish at the same time. If some nodes are slower than others, the operation is blocking, which means that we have to wait for everybody to finish the operation before we can proceed to the next task.","zh_html":"为了执行集合操作，每个节点同时并行运行完全相同的代码。每个人独立调用相同的集合操作来启动操作，当操作完成时，输出应该符合操作定义。理想情况下，节点具有相同的硬件资源，因此它们都在同一时间完成。如果某些节点比其他节点慢，该操作是阻塞的(blocking)，这意味着我们必须等待每个人都完成操作，然后才能继续下一个任务。"},{"en":"In summary, collective operations are orchestrated by a controller that sets up the job. The operation is synchronized (everyone starts at the same time), homogenous (ideally everyone finishes at the same time), and blocking (must wait for everyone to finish before moving on).","en_html":"In summary, collective operations are orchestrated by a controller that sets up the job. The operation is synchronized (everyone starts at the same time), homogenous (ideally everyone finishes at the same time), and blocking (must wait for everyone to finish before moving on).","zh_html":"总结一下，集合操作由设置任务的控制器编排。操作是同步的(synchronized，所有人同时开始)，同质的(homogenous，理想情况下所有人同时完成)，并且是阻塞的(blocking，必须等待所有人完成才能继续）。"},{"en":"With the setup complete, we’re now ready to see how the 7 collective operations are defined. The operations can be roughly separated into two categories: 4 of the operations are about redistribution (moving data around without transforming it), and 3 of the operations are about consolidation (aggregating many pieces of data into a single output).","en_html":"With the setup complete, we’re now ready to see how the 7 collective operations are defined. The operations can be roughly separated into two categories: 4 of the operations are about redistribution (moving data around without transforming it), and 3 of the operations are about consolidation (aggregating many pieces of data into a single output).","zh_html":"设置完成后，我们现在可以看看 7 种集合操作是如何定义的。这些操作大致可以分为两类：4 种操作是关于重新分配(redistribution)的（在不转换数据的情况下移动数据），3 种操作是关于合并(consolidation)的（将多个数据聚合到单个输出中）。"},{"en":"Operation: Broadcast","en_html":"<strong>Operation: Broadcast</strong>","zh_html":"<strong>操作：广播(Broadcast)</strong>"},{"en":"English description: Take the entire vector in a specified root node, and send a copy of that entire vector to every node.","en_html":"English description: Take the entire vector in a specified root node, and send a copy of that entire vector to every node.","zh_html":"中文描述：获取指定根节点(root node)中的整个向量，并将该整个向量的副本发送给每个节点。"},{"en":"Note: This diagram is showing a Broadcast operation with Node 1 as the root node, but we could also do the operation with a different root node. The user running the Broadcast operation must specify the root node as one of the “arguments” to the operation.","en_html":"Note: This diagram is showing a Broadcast operation with Node 1 as the root node, but we could also do the operation with a different root node. The user running the Broadcast operation must specify the root node as one of the “arguments” to the operation.","zh_html":"注意：这张图展示了以节点 1 为根节点的广播操作，但我们也可以使用不同的根节点。运行广播操作的用户必须将根节点指定为操作的\"参数\"之一。"},{"en":"Note: The input vectors in the non-root nodes are not used to create the output. You can think of them as arguments to a function that don’t actually get used.","en_html":"Note: The input vectors in the non-root nodes are not used to create the output. You can think of them as arguments to a function that don’t actually get used.","zh_html":"注意：非根节点中的输入向量不用于创建输出。你可以把它们看作函数中实际未被使用的参数。"},{"en":"Note: Each node’s input and output vector don’t necessarily have to be stored in the same location. If you used the same memory address to hold both the input and output vector, then some operations (like Broadcast) would overwrite the input data with the output data. Alternatively, you could use a different memory address to hold the output vector.","en_html":"Note: Each node’s input and output vector don’t necessarily have to be stored in the same location. If you used the same memory address to hold both the input and output vector, then some operations (like Broadcast) would overwrite the input data with the output data. Alternatively, you could use a different memory address to hold the output vector.","zh_html":"注意：每个节点的输入和输出向量不一定存储在同一位置。如果你使用相同的内存地址来保存输入和输出向量，那么某些操作（如广播）会用输出数据覆盖输入数据。或者，你可以使用不同的内存地址来保存输出向量。"},{"en":"Operation: Scatter","en_html":"<strong>Operation: Scatter</strong>","zh_html":"<strong>操作：分散(Scatter)</strong>"},{"en":"English description: Take the entire vector in a specified root node. Send the \\(i\\)th element of this vector to the \\(i\\)th node.","en_html":"English description: Take the entire vector in a specified root node. Send the \\(i\\)th element of this vector to the \\(i\\)th node.","zh_html":"中文描述：获取指定根节点中的整个向量。将该向量的第 \\(i\\) 个元素发送给第 \\(i\\) 个节点。"},{"en":"Note: Just like Broadcast operation, you can specify any of the nodes to be the root node. Also, just like the Broadcast operation, the input vectors for the non-root nodes are not used to create the output (think: unused arguments to the function).","en_html":"Note: Just like Broadcast operation, you can specify any of the nodes to be the root node. Also, just like the Broadcast operation, the input vectors for the non-root nodes are not used to create the output (think: unused arguments to the function).","zh_html":"注意：就像广播操作一样，你可以指定任何节点为根节点。另外，与广播操作一样，非根节点的输入向量不用于创建输出（可以当作函数中未使用的参数）。"},{"en":"Operation: Gather","en_html":"<strong>Operation: Gather</strong>","zh_html":"<strong>操作：收集(Gather)</strong>"},{"en":"English description: Build a new vector, where the \\(i\\)th element is defined as the \\(i\\)th element from the \\(i\\)th node. Send this vector to a specified root node.","en_html":"English description: Build a new vector, where the \\(i\\)th element is defined as the \\(i\\)th element from the \\(i\\)th node. Send this vector to a specified root node.","zh_html":"中文描述：构建一个新向量，其中第 \\(i\\) 个元素定义为来自第 \\(i\\) 个节点的第 \\(i\\) 个元素。将这个向量发送给指定的根节点。"},{"en":"Note: In this operation, nothing is stored in the receive buffers of the non-root nodes.","en_html":"Note: In this operation, nothing is stored in the receive buffers of the non-root nodes.","zh_html":"注意：在此操作中，非根节点的接收缓冲区中不存储任何内容。"},{"en":"Operation: AllGather","en_html":"<strong>Operation: AllGather</strong>","zh_html":"<strong>操作：全收集(AllGather)</strong>"},{"en":"English description: Build a new vector, where the \\(i\\)th element is defined as the \\(i\\)th element from the \\(i\\)th node. Send a copy of this new vector to every node.","en_html":"English description: Build a new vector, where the \\(i\\)th element is defined as the \\(i\\)th element from the \\(i\\)th node. Send a copy of this new vector to every node.","zh_html":"中文描述：构建一个新向量，其中第 \\(i\\) 个元素定义为来自第 \\(i\\) 个节点的第 \\(i\\) 个元素。将这个新向量的副本发送给每个节点。"},{"en":"Alternative description (equivalent to above): Node \\(i\\) broadcasts its \\(i\\)th element, so that it becomes the \\(i\\)th element of every node’s output vector.","en_html":"Alternative description (equivalent to above): Node \\(i\\) broadcasts its \\(i\\)th element, so that it becomes the \\(i\\)th element of every node’s output vector.","zh_html":"替代描述（等价于上面）：节点 \\(i\\) 广播其第 \\(i\\) 个元素，使其成为每个节点输出向量的第 \\(i\\) 个元素。"},{"en":"Operation: Reduce","en_html":"<strong>Operation: Reduce</strong>","zh_html":"<strong>操作：归约(Reduce)</strong>"},{"en":"English description: Compute the element-wise sum of all the vectors, and send the resulting sum vector to a specified root node.","en_html":"English description: Compute the element-wise sum of all the vectors, and send the resulting sum vector to a specified root node.","zh_html":"中文描述：计算所有向量的逐元素和，并将得到的和向量发送给指定的根节点。"},{"en":"For these notes, we’ll use summation as our reduction operation, but other reduction operations could exist. For example, we could swap out addition for multiplication in the Reduce operation (or ReduceScatter or AllReduce). Reduction operations are typically associative and commutative, which roughly means that you can do them in any order and still get the same result (e.g. addition is associative and commutative).","en_html":"For these notes, we’ll use summation as our reduction operation, but other reduction operations could exist. For example, we could swap out addition for multiplication in the Reduce operation (or ReduceScatter or AllReduce). Reduction operations are typically associative and commutative, which roughly means that you can do them in any order and still get the same result (e.g. addition is associative and commutative).","zh_html":"在这份笔记中，我们将使用求和作为归约操作，但也可能存在其他归约操作。例如，我们可以在 Reduce 操作（或 ReduceScatter 或 AllReduce）中用乘法替换加法。归约操作通常是可结合的(associative)和可交换的(commutative)，这大致意味着你可以按任何顺序执行它们，仍然得到相同的结果（例如加法是可结合和可交换的）。"},{"en":"Operation: AllReduce","en_html":"<strong>Operation: AllReduce</strong>","zh_html":"<strong>操作：全归约(AllReduce)</strong>"},{"en":"English description: Compute the element-wise sum of all the vectors, and send a copy of the resulting sum vector to all nodes.","en_html":"English description: Compute the element-wise sum of all the vectors, and send a copy of the resulting sum vector to all nodes.","zh_html":"中文描述：计算所有向量的逐元素和，并将得到的和向量的副本发送给所有节点。"},{"en":"Operation: ReduceScatter","en_html":"<strong>Operation: ReduceScatter</strong>","zh_html":"<strong>操作：归约分散(ReduceScatter)</strong>"},{"en":"English description: Compute the element-wise sum of all the vectors. Send the \\(i\\)th element of the sum vector to the \\(i\\)th node.","en_html":"English description: Compute the element-wise sum of all the vectors. Send the \\(i\\)th element of the sum vector to the \\(i\\)th node.","zh_html":"中文描述：计算所有向量的逐元素和。将和向量的第 \\(i\\) 个元素发送给第 \\(i\\) 个节点。"},{"en":"Alternative description (equivalent to above): The \\(i\\)th element of each node is summed, and the resulting sum (scalar) is sent to node \\(i\\).","en_html":"Alternative description (equivalent to above): The \\(i\\)th element of each node is summed, and the resulting sum (scalar) is sent to node \\(i\\).","zh_html":"替代描述（等价于上面）：每个节点的第 \\(i\\) 个元素被求和，得到的和（标量）被发送给节点 \\(i\\)。"},{"en":"Duals","en_html":"<a href=\"#duals\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"duals\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Duals","zh_html":"<a href=\"#duals\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"duals\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 对偶(Duals)"},{"en":"Some pairs of operations are duals of each other. Roughly speaking, this means that one operation is the reverse of the other operation. For example, in math, you could say that squaring and square root are duals of each other.","en_html":"Some pairs of operations are <strong>duals</strong> of each other. Roughly speaking, this means that one operation is the reverse of the other operation. For example, in math, you could say that squaring and square root are duals of each other.","zh_html":"有些操作对是彼此的对偶(duals)。粗略地说，这意味着一个操作是另一个操作的逆操作。例如，在数学中，你可以说平方和平方根互为对偶。"},{"en":"When checking if a pair of operations form a dual pair, we ignore any reduction computations. We only care about which boxes get written to in the output, regardless of what value is written to those boxes.","en_html":"When checking if a pair of operations form a dual pair, we ignore any reduction computations. We only care about which boxes get written to in the output, regardless of what value is written to those boxes.","zh_html":"在检查一对操作是否构成对偶对时，我们忽略任何归约计算。我们只关心输出中哪些框被写入，而不管这些框中写入了什么值。"},{"en":"Broadcast and Reduce are duals of each other. Broadcast reads from the 4 boxes in the root node, and writes to all 16 boxes in all nodes. Reduce does the reverse: It reads from all 16 boxes in all nodes, and writes to the 4 boxes in the root node.","en_html":"Broadcast and Reduce are duals of each other. Broadcast reads from the 4 boxes in the root node, and writes to all 16 boxes in all nodes. Reduce does the reverse: It reads from all 16 boxes in all nodes, and writes to the 4 boxes in the root node.","zh_html":"广播(Broadcast)和归约(Reduce)互为对偶。广播从根节点中的 4 个框读取，并写入所有节点中的所有 16 个框。归约做相反的操作：它从所有节点中的所有 16 个框读取，并写入根节点中的 4 个框。"},{"en":"Scatter and Gather are duals of each other. Scatter reads from the 4 boxes in the root node, and writes to to the \\(i\\)th box of the \\(i\\)th node (4 boxes in total). Gather does the reverse: It reads from the \\(i\\)th box in the \\(i\\)th node (4 boxes in total), and writes to the 4 boxes in the root node.","en_html":"Scatter and Gather are duals of each other. Scatter reads from the 4 boxes in the root node, and writes to to the \\(i\\)th box of the \\(i\\)th node (4 boxes in total). Gather does the reverse: It reads from the \\(i\\)th box in the \\(i\\)th node (4 boxes in total), and writes to the 4 boxes in the root node.","zh_html":"分散(Scatter)和收集(Gather)互为对偶。分散从根节点中的 4 个框读取，并写入第 \\(i\\) 个节点的第 \\(i\\) 个框（总共 4 个框）。收集做相反的操作：它从第 \\(i\\) 个节点中的第 \\(i\\) 个框（总共 4 个框）读取，并写入根节点中的 4 个框。"},{"en":"AllGather and ReduceScatter are duals of each other. AllGather reads from the \\(i\\)th box in the \\(i\\)th node (4 boxes in total), and writes to all 16 boxes in all nodes. ReduceScatter does the reverse: It reads from all 16 boxes in all nodes, and writes to the \\(i\\)th box of the \\(i\\)th node (4 boxes in total).","en_html":"AllGather and ReduceScatter are duals of each other. AllGather reads from the \\(i\\)th box in the \\(i\\)th node (4 boxes in total), and writes to all 16 boxes in all nodes. ReduceScatter does the reverse: It reads from all 16 boxes in all nodes, and writes to the \\(i\\)th box of the \\(i\\)th node (4 boxes in total).","zh_html":"全收集(AllGather)和归约分散(ReduceScatter)互为对偶。全收集从第 \\(i\\) 个节点中的第 \\(i\\) 个框（总共 4 个框）读取，并写入所有节点中的所有 16 个框。归约分散做相反的操作：它从所有节点中的所有 16 个框读取，并写入第 \\(i\\) 个节点的第 \\(i\\) 个框（总共 4 个框）。"},{"en":"AllReduce does not have a dual. Alternatively, you could view AllReduce as its own dual, since it reads from all 16 boxes and writes to all 16 boxes.","en_html":"AllReduce does not have a dual. Alternatively, you could view AllReduce as its own dual, since it reads from all 16 boxes and writes to all 16 boxes.","zh_html":"AllReduce 没有对偶。或者，你可以将 AllReduce 视为自身的对偶，因为它从所有 16 个框读取并写入所有 16 个框。"},{"en":"The idea of duals is useful when we start thinking about the implementations of these collectives. For a specific topology and routing scheme, a collective and its dual will have the same performance (e.g. same total bandwidth usage), since the total amount of data sent and received is the same in both the collective and its dual.","en_html":"The idea of duals is useful when we start thinking about the implementations of these collectives. For a specific topology and routing scheme, a collective and its dual will have the same performance (e.g. same total bandwidth usage), since the total amount of data sent and received is the same in both the collective and its dual.","zh_html":"对偶的思想在我们开始思考这些集合操作的实现时很有用。对于特定的拓扑和路由方案，一个集合操作及其对偶将具有相同的性能（例如相同的总带宽使用量），因为在该集合操作及其对偶中发送和接收的数据总量是相同的。"},{"en":"Compositing Operations","en_html":"<a href=\"#compositing-operations\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"compositing-operations\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Compositing Operations","zh_html":"<a href=\"#compositing-operations\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"compositing-operations\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 操作组合(Compositing Operations)"},{"en":"Users can combine multiple operations to get their desired operation.","en_html":"Users can combine multiple operations to get their desired operation.","zh_html":"用户可以组合多个操作来得到他们想要的操作。"},{"en":"For example, AllReduce could equivalently be expressed as a ReduceScatter, followed by an AllGather.","en_html":"For example, AllReduce could equivalently be expressed as a ReduceScatter, followed by an AllGather.","zh_html":"例如，AllReduce 可以等价地表示为 ReduceScatter 后跟 AllGather。"}]},"/beyond-client-server/dvmrp.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"DVMRP","en_html":"<a href=\"#dvmrp\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dvmrp\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DVMRP","zh_html":"<a href=\"#dvmrp\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dvmrp\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DVMRP（距离向量多播路由协议，Distance Vector Multicast Routing Protocol）"},{"en":"Naive Algorithm: Flooding","en_html":"<a href=\"#naive-algorithm-flooding\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"naive-algorithm-flooding\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Naive Algorithm: Flooding","zh_html":"<a href=\"#naive-algorithm-flooding\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"naive-algorithm-flooding\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 朴素算法：泛洪(flooding)"},{"en":"Recall that the goal of multicast routing: We have a packet whose destination is a group, and the routers need to work together to forward this packet to all members of the group.","en_html":"Recall that the goal of multicast routing: We have a packet whose destination is a group, and the routers need to work together to forward this packet to all members of the group.","zh_html":"回顾一下多播路由选择(multicast routing)的目标：我们有一个目的地址是某个组(group)的分组(packet)，路由器(routers)需要协同工作，把这个分组转发给该组的所有成员。"},{"en":"The most naive way to implement this is flooding. When a router receives a packet, it simply forwards the packet out of every port (except the incoming port).","en_html":"The most naive way to implement this is flooding. When a router receives a packet, it simply forwards the packet out of every port (except the incoming port).","zh_html":"最朴素的实现方式是泛洪(flooding)。当路由器收到一个分组时，它简单地将该分组转发到除了入端口之外的所有端口。"},{"en":"Why does flooding work? It ensures that every host on the network receives the packet, and that will include all members of the desired group.","en_html":"Why does flooding work? It ensures that every host on the network receives the packet, and that will include all members of the desired group.","zh_html":"为什么泛洪能工作？它确保网络上的每个主机(host)都收到分组，其中自然包括目标组的所有成员。"},{"en":"What’s good about flooding? It’s conceptually simple, and doesn’t require running any routing protocols.","en_html":"What’s good about flooding? It’s conceptually simple, and doesn’t require running any routing protocols.","zh_html":"泛洪有什么优点？它在概念上很简单，而且不需要运行任何路由协议(routing protocols)。"},{"en":"What are some problems with flooding? There are two major problems, which we’ll solve one at a time:","en_html":"What are some problems with flooding? There are two major problems, which we’ll solve one at a time:","zh_html":"泛洪存在哪些问题？主要有两个问题，我们将逐一解决："},{"en":"Flooding wastes bandwidth sending the same data along multiple paths, when that data only needed to be sent along one path.","en_html":"<p>Flooding wastes bandwidth sending the same data along multiple paths, when that data only needed to be sent along one path.</p>","zh_html":"<p>泛洪会浪费带宽，数据本只需沿一条路径发送，却被重复地沿多条路径发送。</p>"},{"en":"Flooding wastes bandwidth sending the packet to non-members.","en_html":"<p>Flooding wastes bandwidth sending the packet to non-members.</p>","zh_html":"<p>泛洪将分组发送给非组成员，浪费了带宽。</p>"},{"en":"Also, loops can cause broadcast storms where the same packet is forwarded infinitely in a loop, though this can be solved by having routers discard a packet if they’ve seen it before.","en_html":"Also, loops can cause broadcast storms where the same packet is forwarded infinitely in a loop, though this can be solved by having routers discard a packet if they’ve seen it before.","zh_html":"此外，环路可能引发广播风暴(broadcast storms)，导致同一分组在环路中无限转发，不过可以让路由器丢弃已经见过的分组来解决这个问题。"},{"en":"Reverse Path Broadcasting (RPB)","en_html":"<a href=\"#reverse-path-broadcasting-rpb\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"reverse-path-broadcasting-rpb\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Reverse Path Broadcasting (RPB)","zh_html":"<a href=\"#reverse-path-broadcasting-rpb\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"reverse-path-broadcasting-rpb\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 反向路径广播(Reverse Path Broadcasting, RPB)"},{"en":"Let’s focus on the first problem for now. (Note: This means that for now, we’ll still be sending the multicast packet to everybody, including non-members. We’ll solve that problem later.)","en_html":"Let’s focus on the first problem for now. (Note: This means that for now, we’ll still be sending the multicast packet to everybody, including non-members. We’ll solve that problem later.)","zh_html":"我们先关注第一个问题。（注意：这意味着现在我们仍然会把多播分组发送给所有人，包括非组成员。稍后再解决那个问题。）"},{"en":"Flooding correctly sends the packet to everybody, but it wastes data sending data along redundant links. For example, if there are many paths between R1 and R4, flooding will cause copies of the packet to travel along every path from R1 to R4. Then, R4 will discard all the duplicate copies of the packet.","en_html":"Flooding correctly sends the packet to everybody, but it wastes data sending data along redundant links. For example, if there are many paths between R1 and R4, flooding will cause copies of the packet to travel along every path from R1 to R4. Then, R4 will discard all the duplicate copies of the packet.","zh_html":"泛洪确实能把分组发送给所有人，但它会沿着冗余链路发送数据，浪费带宽。例如，如果 R1 和 R4 之间有多条路径，泛洪会导致分组副本沿着每条路径从 R1 传送到 R4。然后 R4 会丢弃所有重复的副本。"},{"en":"Ideally, we’d like the packet to travel along a single path from R1 to R4, and likewise between any other pair of routers.","en_html":"Ideally, we’d like the packet to travel along a single path from R1 to R4, and likewise between any other pair of routers.","zh_html":"理想情况下，我们希望分组从 R1 到 R4 只沿着一条路径传输，其他任意一对路由器之间也是如此。"},{"en":"We’d like packets to take only a single path between any pair of nodes. What data structure does this remind you of? Trees have a single path between any pair of nodes!","en_html":"We’d like packets to take only a single path between any pair of nodes. What data structure does this remind you of? Trees have a single path between any pair of nodes!","zh_html":"我们希望任何一对节点之间的分组只走一条路径。这让你想起了什么数据结构？树(tree)恰好满足任意一对节点之间只有一条路径！"},{"en":"Specifically, we want to build a spanning tree, so that everyone receives the packet along a single path only.","en_html":"Specifically, we want to build a <strong>spanning tree</strong>, so that everyone receives the packet along a single path only.","zh_html":"具体来说，我们想要构建一棵生成树(spanning tree)，这样每个人都只沿着一条路径收到分组。"},{"en":"We could build a spanning tree from scratch, but we could be more clever and reuse some work that we’ve already done. Where have we already seen spanning trees?","en_html":"We could build a spanning tree from scratch, but we could be more clever and reuse some work that we’ve already done. Where have we already seen spanning trees?","zh_html":"我们可以从头构建一棵生成树，但更聪明的办法是复用我们已经做过的工作。我们在哪里见过生成树？"},{"en":"When we ran distance-vector routing for unicast packets, we built a spanning tree pointing toward the destination. This allowed all packets to flow “upwards” in the network graph, toward the single destination (the root of the tree).","en_html":"When we ran distance-vector routing for unicast packets, we built a spanning tree pointing toward the destination. This allowed all packets to flow “upwards” in the network graph, toward the single destination (the root of the tree).","zh_html":"当我们运行距离向量(distance-vector)路由进行单播(unicast)时，我们构建了一棵指向目的地的生成树。这使得所有分组能在网络图中向\"上\"流动，朝向单一目的地（树的根）。"},{"en":"If we took this graph and just reversed all the arrows, we now have a suitable spanning tree for multicast packets. The root of the tree is now the sender, and copies of the packet flow “downwards” in the network graph, away from the sender and through the network to reach every destination.","en_html":"If we took this graph and just reversed all the arrows, we now have a suitable spanning tree for multicast packets. The root of the tree is now the sender, and copies of the packet flow “downwards” in the network graph, away from the sender and through the network to reach every destination.","zh_html":"如果我们把这幅图的所有箭头反转，就得到了一个适用于多播分组的生成树。树的根现在是发送方(sender)，分组的副本在网络图中向\"下\"流动，远离发送方，经过网络到达每一个目的地。"},{"en":"At this point, thinking about reversed arrows can be confusing, so let’s switch to using some less confusing terminology. In the tree of routers, every router has exactly one parent, and zero or more children. The router at the “top” of the tree is the root, and routers at the “bottom” of the tree with no children are called leaves. (These are the same definitions that you’re probably used to from any data structures course. Nothing special about them.)","en_html":"At this point, thinking about reversed arrows can be confusing, so let’s switch to using some less confusing terminology. In the tree of routers, every router has exactly one parent, and zero or more children. The router at the “top” of the tree is the root, and routers at the “bottom” of the tree with no children are called leaves. (These are the same definitions that you’re probably used to from any data structures course. Nothing special about them.)","zh_html":"到此为止，考虑反转箭头可能会让人困惑，所以让我们改用更清晰的术语。在路由器树中，每个路由器恰好有一个父节点(parent)和零个或多个子节点(children)。树\"顶部\"的路由器是根(root)，树\"底部\"没有子节点的路由器称为叶子(leaves)。（这些定义跟你在任何数据结构课程中学到的相同，没什么特别之处。）"},{"en":"When we thought about unicast routing, the root was the destination. Everyone receives packets from their children, and forwards their packets to their parents, “upwards” toward the destination.","en_html":"When we thought about unicast routing, the root was the destination. Everyone receives packets from their children, and forwards their packets to their parents, “upwards” toward the destination.","zh_html":"在考虑单播路由时，根是目的地。每个人都从其子节点接收分组，并向其父节点转发分组，沿\"上\"行方向到达目的地。"},{"en":"By contrast, when we think about multicast routing, the root is the source. Everyone receives packets from their parent, and forwards their packets to their children, “downwards” through the network to reach every destination.","en_html":"By contrast, when we think about multicast routing, the root is the source. Everyone receives packets from their parent, and forwards their packets to their children, “downwards” through the network to reach every destination.","zh_html":"相比之下，在考虑多播路由时，根是源(source)。每个人都从其父节点接收分组，并向其子节点转发分组，沿\"下\"行方向经过网络到达每个目的地。"},{"en":"In summary, the forwarding rule for multicast routing is: If you get a packet from your parent, send it to all your children. Otherwise, if you get a packet from someone else (not your parent), drop the packet.","en_html":"In summary, the forwarding rule for multicast routing is: If you get a packet from your parent, send it to all your children. Otherwise, if you get a packet from someone else (not your parent), drop the packet.","zh_html":"总结一下，多播路由的转发规则是：如果你从父节点收到分组，就把它发送给你所有的子节点。否则，如果你从其他人（不是你的父节点）那里收到分组，就丢弃该分组。"},{"en":"This rule helps us avoid packets getting sent along multiple paths. Even if there are multiple paths to you, you will only receive the packet from your parent (and forward it to your children) a single time. If you receive another copy of the packet from someone else (not your parent), you’ll drop the packet.","en_html":"This rule helps us avoid packets getting sent along multiple paths. Even if there are multiple paths to you, you will only receive the packet from your parent (and forward it to your children) a single time. If you receive another copy of the packet from someone else (not your parent), you’ll drop the packet.","zh_html":"这条规则帮助我们避免分组沿多条路径发送。即使存在多条路径可以到达你，你也只会从父节点收到一次分组（然后转发给子节点）。如果你从其他人那里收到另一个副本，你会将其丢弃。"},{"en":"RPM: Learning Your Parent and Children","en_html":"<a href=\"#rpm-learning-your-parent-and-children\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rpm-learning-your-parent-and-children\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> RPM: Learning Your Parent and Children","zh_html":"<a href=\"#rpm-learning-your-parent-and-children\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rpm-learning-your-parent-and-children\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> RPM：了解你的父节点和子节点"},{"en":"How do we actually implement this rule? Each router needs to know about its parent, and all of its children.","en_html":"How do we actually implement this rule? Each router needs to know about its parent, and all of its children.","zh_html":"我们如何实际实现这条规则？每个路由器需要知道它的父节点和所有子节点。"},{"en":"Figuring out your parent is easy. Remember that this tree is exactly the same as the tree from distance-vector for unicast routing. In your unicast forwarding table, your next-hop to the root is your parent! To determine your parent, you can just reuse the forwarding table entry you computed for unicast routing.","en_html":"Figuring out your parent is easy. Remember that this tree is exactly the same as the tree from distance-vector for unicast routing. In your unicast forwarding table, your next-hop to the root is your parent! To determine your parent, you can just reuse the forwarding table entry you computed for unicast routing.","zh_html":"找出父节点很容易。记住，这棵树与单播路由的距离向量树完全相同。在你的单播转发表(unicast forwarding table)中，到根的下一跳(next-hop)就是你的父节点！要确定父节点，你可以直接复用已经为单播路由计算好的转发表项。"},{"en":"Figuring out your children takes a bit more work. The forwarding table only tells you about your parent (next-hop, toward the root), but the forwarding table has no information about your children (previous-hop, away from the root).","en_html":"Figuring out your children takes a bit more work. The forwarding table only tells you about your parent (next-hop, toward the root), but the forwarding table has no information about your children (previous-hop, away from the root).","zh_html":"找出子节点则需要更多工作。转发表只告诉你父节点（下一跳，朝向根），但转发表没有关于子节点（上一跳，远离根）的信息。"},{"en":"Since you don’t know about your children, you need your children to tell you who they are. Specifically, everybody sends multicast routing advertisements to their parents saying: “I am your child (in the tree rooted at A).” (Remember that everybody knows their parents from their unicast forwarding table.)","en_html":"Since you don’t know about your children, you need your children to tell you who they are. Specifically, everybody sends multicast routing advertisements to their parents saying: “I am your child (in the tree rooted at A).” (Remember that everybody knows their parents from their unicast forwarding table.)","zh_html":"既然你不知道自己的子节点，就需要你的子节点来告诉你它们是谁。具体来说，每个人都会向其父节点发送多播路由通告(multicast routing advertisements)，说“我是你的子节点（在以 A 为根的树中）。”（记住，每个人都知道自己的父节点，来自单播转发表。）"},{"en":"Then, every router receives these advertisements and stores additional information about who their children are. This is new information that we’ve added specifically for multicast routing. This new multicast forwarding table is separate from the forwarding table we used in unicast routing (and for determining parents).","en_html":"Then, every router receives these advertisements and stores additional information about who their children are. This is new information that we’ve added specifically for multicast routing. This new multicast forwarding table is separate from the forwarding table we used in unicast routing (and for determining parents).","zh_html":"然后，每个路由器收到这些通告后，会额外存储关于其子节点的信息。这是我们专门为多播路由新增的信息。这个新的多播转发表(multicast forwarding table)与我们在单播路由（以及确定父节点时）使用的转发表是分开的。"},{"en":"In summary, the forwarding rule for multicast is implemented like this. When you receive a packet, use the unicast forwarding table (which lists your parent) to check if the packet is from your parent. If the packet is from your parent, use the new multicast forwarding table (containing advertisements from your children) to forward it to your children.","en_html":"In summary, the forwarding rule for multicast is implemented like this. When you receive a packet, use the unicast forwarding table (which lists your parent) to check if the packet is from your parent. If the packet is from your parent, use the new multicast forwarding table (containing advertisements from your children) to forward it to your children.","zh_html":"总结一下，多播的转发规则是这样实现的：当你收到一个分组时，使用单播转发表（其中列出了你的父节点）来检查分组是否来自你的父节点。如果分组来自父节点，就使用新的多播转发表（包含从子节点收到的通告）将其转发给你的子节点。"},{"en":"Now that we have two forwarding tables, let’s stop and think about how each one is used. The unicast forwarding table lists your parents. This table is used for unicasting packets toward their destinations, as in standard distance-vector routing. This table is also used for checking if a multicast packet came from your parent. Finally, this table is used to send multicast routing advertisements to tell your parents “I am your child.”","en_html":"Now that we have two forwarding tables, let’s stop and think about how each one is used. The unicast forwarding table lists your parents. This table is used for unicasting packets toward their destinations, as in standard distance-vector routing. This table is also used for checking if a multicast packet came from your parent. Finally, this table is used to send multicast routing advertisements to tell your parents “I am your child.”","zh_html":"现在我们有了两个转发表，停下来想一想各自的用途。单播转发表列出你的父节点。该表用于像标准距离向量路由那样向目的地单播分组。它也用于检查多播分组是否来自你的父节点。最后，它还用于发送多播路由通告，告诉你的父节点“我是你的子节点。”"},{"en":"The multicast forwarding table lists your children. This table is constructed by receiving advertisements from your children. This table is used to forward multicast packets to all of your children.","en_html":"The multicast forwarding table lists your children. This table is constructed by receiving advertisements from your children. This table is used to forward multicast packets to all of your children.","zh_html":"多播转发表列出你的子节点。该表通过接收子节点的通告来构建。该表用于将多播分组转发给你所有的子节点。"},{"en":"One last, but important, observation: In distance-vector unicast routing, we built one spanning tree for every destination. As a result, our unicast forwarding table has one next-hop for every destination. In other words, for each destination, you have a parent for that particular tree.","en_html":"One last, but important, observation: In distance-vector unicast routing, we built one spanning tree for every destination. As a result, our unicast forwarding table has one next-hop for every destination. In other words, for each destination, you have a parent for that particular tree.","zh_html":"最后还有一个重要的观察：在距离向量单播路由中，我们为每个目的地构建了一棵生成树。因此，我们的单播转发表中每个目的地都有一个下一跳。换句话说，对于每个目的地，你在那棵特定的树上有一个父节点。"},{"en":"When we reverse the arrows, we now end up with one spanning tree for every source. Our multicast forwarding table has a list of children for each different source. In other words, a multicast forwarding table entry can be interpreted as: “If you receive a packet from source A, forward it to children R6, R7.”","en_html":"When we reverse the arrows, we now end up with one spanning tree for every source. Our multicast forwarding table has a list of children for each different source. In other words, a multicast forwarding table entry can be interpreted as: “If you receive a packet from source A, forward it to children R6, R7.”","zh_html":"当我们反转箭头后，现在每个源都有一棵生成树。我们的多播转发表中每个源都有一个子节点列表。换句话说，多播转发表项可以解读为：“如果你收到来自源 A 的分组，就转发给子节点 R6、R7。”"},{"en":"Reverse Path Multicasting (RPM): Pruning","en_html":"<a href=\"#reverse-path-multicasting-rpm-pruning\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"reverse-path-multicasting-rpm-pruning\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Reverse Path Multicasting (RPM): Pruning","zh_html":"<a href=\"#reverse-path-multicasting-rpm-pruning\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"reverse-path-multicasting-rpm-pruning\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 反向路径多播(Reverse Path Multicasting, RPM)：剪枝(pruning)"},{"en":"Our Reverse Path Broadcasting rule ensured that packets travel along a spanning tree, starting at the source (the root) and traveling “downwards” through the network to all destinations. Using a tree solved our first problem (packets taking multiple paths and wasting bandwidth).","en_html":"Our Reverse Path Broadcasting rule ensured that packets travel along a spanning tree, starting at the source (the root) and traveling “downwards” through the network to all destinations. Using a tree solved our first problem (packets taking multiple paths and wasting bandwidth).","zh_html":"我们的反向路径广播规则确保分组沿生成树传输，从源（根）开始，沿\"下\"行方向经过网络到达所有目的地。使用树解决了我们的第一个问题（分组走多条路径浪费带宽）。"},{"en":"However, we still have the second problem to solve. So far, our packets are still being broadcast to everybody, including hosts who are not in the group. This wastes bandwidth.","en_html":"However, we still have the second problem to solve. So far, our packets are still being broadcast to everybody, including hosts who are not in the group. This wastes bandwidth.","zh_html":"然而，我们还需要解决第二个问题。到目前为止，我们的分组仍然广播给所有人，包括不在组中的主机。这浪费了带宽。"},{"en":"To solve this, we will prune the tree by cutting off any branches where there are no group members.","en_html":"To solve this, we will <strong>prune</strong> the tree by cutting off any branches where there are no group members.","zh_html":"为了解决这个问题，我们将通过剪掉任何没有组成员的树枝来修剪(prune)这棵树。"},{"en":"Pruning propagates from children to their parents. Suppose you are R5, and you are directly connected to 3 hosts. Using IGMP (i.e. talking to those hosts), you learn that none of them are in the group. This means that there’s no reason for you to be part of this tree.","en_html":"Pruning propagates from children to their parents. Suppose you are R5, and you are directly connected to 3 hosts. Using IGMP (i.e. talking to those hosts), you learn that none of them are in the group. This means that there’s no reason for you to be part of this tree.","zh_html":"剪枝从子节点向其父节点传播。假设你是 R5，直接连接了 3 个主机。通过使用 IGMP（即与这些主机通信），你得知它们都不在该组中。这意味着你没有理由成为这棵树的一部分。"},{"en":"You can send an advertisement to your parent: “I am your child, but none of my descendants are involved in this group, so don’t send me data packets.” Your parent can then update their multicast forwarding table entry accordingly, so that you are no longer one of the children. Note that pruning messages are only sent to your direct parent (they’re not forwarded any further).","en_html":"You can send an advertisement to your parent: “I am your child, but none of my descendants are involved in this group, so don’t send me data packets.” Your parent can then update their multicast forwarding table entry accordingly, so that you are no longer one of the children. Note that pruning messages are only sent to your direct parent (they’re not forwarded any further).","zh_html":"你可以向你的父节点发送通告：“我是你的子节点，但我的所有后代都不参与这个组，所以不要给我发送数据分组。”然后你的父节点可以相应地更新其多播转发表项，这样你就不再是子节点之一了。注意，剪枝消息只发送给你的直接父节点（不会进一步转发）。"},{"en":"Pruning can happen at higher levels of the tree as well. Consider R3, a router with 2 children. Suppose both children send pruning advertisements, saying that they’re not involved in this group. If none of your children are involved in this group, then there’s no reason for you to be involved in this group either. Therefore, you can remove yourself from this tree as well. You can do this by sending a pruning advertisement to your parent, so that your parent stops sending you data packets.","en_html":"Pruning can happen at higher levels of the tree as well. Consider R3, a router with 2 children. Suppose both children send pruning advertisements, saying that they’re not involved in this group. If none of your children are involved in this group, then there’s no reason for you to be involved in this group either. Therefore, you can remove yourself from this tree as well. You can do this by sending a pruning advertisement to your parent, so that your parent stops sending you data packets.","zh_html":"剪枝也可能发生在树的更高层。考虑 R3，一个有两个子节点的路由器。假设两个子节点都发送剪枝通告，说它们不参与这个组。如果你的所有子节点都不参与这个组，那么你也没有理由参与这棵树。因此，你也可以从这棵树中移除自己。你可以通过向父节点发送剪枝通告来实现这一点，这样你的父节点就会停止向你发送数据分组。"},{"en":"Note: Routers at higher levels could have both children routers and directly-connected hosts. In this case, the router can only remove themselves from the tree if all their children send pruning advertisements, and all their directly-connected hosts are not part of this group.","en_html":"Note: Routers at higher levels could have both children routers <em>and</em> directly-connected hosts. In this case, the router can only remove themselves from the tree if all their children send pruning advertisements, <em>and</em> all their directly-connected hosts are not part of this group.","zh_html":"注意：高层路由器可能既有子节点路由器，也有直连主机(directly-connected hosts)。在这种情况下，路由器只有在其所有子节点都发送了剪枝通告，并且其所有直连主机都不属于该组时，才能从树中移除自己。"},{"en":"Pruning makes our multicast forwarding tables a little more complicated. So far, each entry maps a source to a list of children: “If you receive a packet from source A, forward it to children R11, R12.” However, the list of children now also depends on the destination group. For example, maybe R11 and R2 both have descendants belonging to group G1. But, only R11 has descendants belonging to group G2 (i.e. R12 has sent you a prune message).","en_html":"Pruning makes our multicast forwarding tables a little more complicated. So far, each entry maps a source to a list of children: “If you receive a packet from source A, forward it to children R11, R12.” However, the list of children now also depends on the destination group. For example, maybe R11 and R2 both have descendants belonging to group G1. But, only R11 has descendants belonging to group G2 (i.e. R12 has sent you a prune message).","zh_html":"剪枝让我们的多播转发表变得稍微复杂了一些。到目前为止，每个表项将一个源映射到一个子节点列表：“如果你收到来自源 A 的分组，就转发给子节点 R11、R12。”然而，子节点列表现在也取决于目的组。例如，也许 R11 和 R2 都有属于组 G1 的后代。但是，只有 R11 有属于组 G2 的后代（即 R12 已向你发送了剪枝消息）。"},{"en":"To fix this, our multicast forwarding table must have one entry per source, per group. For example: “If you receive a packet from source A to group G1, forward it to children R11, R12.”","en_html":"To fix this, our multicast forwarding table must have one entry per source, per group. For example: “If you receive a packet from source A to group G1, forward it to children R11, R12.”","zh_html":"为了解决这个问题，我们的多播转发表必须为每个源、每个组各有一个表项。例如：“如果你收到来自源 A、目的组 G1 的分组，就转发给子节点 R11、R12。”"},{"en":"Another separate entry would be: “If you receive a packet from source A to group G2, forward it to child R11.”","en_html":"Another separate entry would be: “If you receive a packet from source A to group G2, forward it to child R11.”","zh_html":"另一个单独的表项是：“如果你收到来自源 A、目的组 G2 的分组，就转发给子节点 R11。”"},{"en":"Another way to think about this modification: Previously, we had one tree per source, showing how that source sends multicast packets to everyone else. However, we are now cutting off tree branches depending on the destination group. Therefore, we need one tree per source, per destination group.","en_html":"Another way to think about this modification: Previously, we had one tree per source, showing how that source sends multicast packets to everyone else. However, we are now cutting off tree branches depending on the destination group. Therefore, we need one tree per source, per destination group.","zh_html":"另一种理解这种修改的方式：之前，每个源有一棵树，展示该源如何向其他人发送多播分组。但现在我们根据目的组来剪断树枝。因此，我们需要每个源、每个目的组各有一棵树。"},{"en":"One final note: It’s possible that none of your children currently belong to a group, but some time later, one of your descendants decides to join the group. To fix this problem, every router will periodically clear all of its pruning information, so that nobody is pruned anymore. This causes everyone to revert to the original RPB behavior, where you always forward to all your children.","en_html":"One final note: It’s possible that none of your children currently belong to a group, but some time later, one of your descendants decides to join the group. To fix this problem, every router will periodically clear all of its pruning information, so that nobody is pruned anymore. This causes everyone to revert to the original RPB behavior, where you always forward to all your children.","zh_html":"最后一点：可能当前你的所有子节点都不属于某个组，但后来你的某个后代决定加入该组。为了解决这个问题，每个路由器会定期清除所有剪枝信息，这样就没有人再被剪枝了。这会使所有人恢复到原始的 RPB 行为，即始终向所有子节点转发。"},{"en":"This way, if one of your descendants has joined a group, then after the timer expires, you are no longer pruned and you have re-joined the tree. On the other hand, if it’s still the case that none of your descendants belong to the group, you can just send another pruning message to your parent, so that you are removed from the tree again.","en_html":"This way, if one of your descendants has joined a group, then after the timer expires, you are no longer pruned and you have re-joined the tree. On the other hand, if it’s still the case that none of your descendants belong to the group, you can just send another pruning message to your parent, so that you are removed from the tree again.","zh_html":"这样，如果你的某个后代加入了组，那么在计时器过期后，你就不再被剪枝，重新加入树。另一方面，如果仍然没有后代属于该组，你可以再次向父节点发送剪枝消息，从而再次从树中移除自己。"},{"en":"Summary of DVMRP Rules","en_html":"<a href=\"#summary-of-dvmrp-rules\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"summary-of-dvmrp-rules\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Summary of DVMRP Rules","zh_html":"<a href=\"#summary-of-dvmrp-rules\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"summary-of-dvmrp-rules\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DVMRP 规则总结"},{"en":"Routing Rules:","en_html":"<strong>Routing Rules:</strong>","zh_html":"<strong>路由规则：</strong>"},{"en":"For each source’s spanning tree, you need to learn your parents and your children.","en_html":"For each source’s spanning tree, you need to learn your parents and your children.","zh_html":"对于每个源的生成树，你需要了解你的父节点和子节点。"},{"en":"Learning your parents: No action needed. Your unicast forwarding table already identifies your parent.","en_html":"<p>Learning your parents: No action needed. Your unicast forwarding table already identifies your parent.</p>","zh_html":"<p>了解父节点：不需要额外操作。你的单播转发表已经标识了你的父节点。</p>"},{"en":"Learning your children: Everyone sends an advertisement to their parent. When you receive these advertisements, you learn who your children are.","en_html":"<p>Learning your children: Everyone sends an advertisement to their parent. When you receive these advertisements, you learn who your children are.</p>","zh_html":"<p>了解子节点：每个人向其父节点发送一个通告。当你收到这些通告时，你就知道谁是自己的子节点了。</p>"},{"en":"Forwarding Rules:","en_html":"<strong>Forwarding Rules:</strong>","zh_html":"<strong>转发规则：</strong>"},{"en":"When you receive a packet, use the unicast forwarding table for the given source to check if the packet is from your parent.","en_html":"<p>When you receive a packet, use the unicast forwarding table for the given source to check if the packet is from your parent.</p>","zh_html":"<p>当你收到一个分组时，使用针对该源的单播转发表检查分组是否来自你的父节点。</p>"},{"en":"If the packet is from your parent, use the new multicast forwarding table to forward it to your children. Only forward to the non-pruned children for the given destination.","en_html":"<p>If the packet is from your parent, use the new multicast forwarding table to forward it to your children. Only forward to the non-pruned children for the given destination.</p>","zh_html":"<p>如果分组来自父节点，使用新的多播转发表将其转发给你的子节点。只转发给给定目的下未被剪枝的子节点。</p>"},{"en":"Otherwise, if the packet is not from your parent, then just drop the packet.","en_html":"<p>Otherwise, if the packet is not from your parent, then just drop the packet.</p>","zh_html":"<p>否则，如果分组不是来自父节点，就直接丢弃。</p>"},{"en":"Pruning Rules:","en_html":"<strong>Pruning Rules:</strong>","zh_html":"<strong>剪枝规则：</strong>"},{"en":"For each (destination group, source) pair:","en_html":"For each (destination group, source) pair:","zh_html":"对于每个（目的组，源）对："},{"en":"If you receive a pruning message from a child, remove that child from your multicast forwarding table entry for this destination group.","en_html":"<p>If you receive a pruning message from a child, remove that child from your multicast forwarding table entry for this destination group.</p>","zh_html":"<p>如果你从某个子节点收到剪枝消息，从该目的组的多播转发表项中移除该子节点。</p>"},{"en":"If none of your descendants (directly-connected hosts or children) belong to this group, send a pruning message to your parent.","en_html":"<p>If none of your descendants (directly-connected hosts or children) belong to this group, send a pruning message to your parent.</p>","zh_html":"<p>如果你的所有后代（直连主机或子节点）都不属于该组，向你的父节点发送一条剪枝消息。</p>"},{"en":"Periodically clear all pruning information (revert to forwarding to all children).","en_html":"<p>Periodically clear all pruning information (revert to forwarding to all children).</p>","zh_html":"<p>定期清除所有剪枝信息（恢复为向所有子节点转发）。</p>"},{"en":"DVMRP Pros and Cons","en_html":"<a href=\"#dvmrp-pros-and-cons\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dvmrp-pros-and-cons\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DVMRP Pros and Cons","zh_html":"<a href=\"#dvmrp-pros-and-cons\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"dvmrp-pros-and-cons\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> DVMRP 的优缺点"},{"en":"What’s bad about this routing protocol?","en_html":"What’s bad about this routing protocol?","zh_html":"这个路由协议有什么缺点？"},{"en":"Pruning information is periodically cleared. When that happens, packets end up getting broadcast to everybody again, until pruning converges again (Recall that without pruning, packets were getting sent to everybody.)","en_html":"Pruning information is periodically cleared. When that happens, packets end up getting broadcast to everybody again, until pruning converges again (Recall that without pruning, packets were getting sent to everybody.)","zh_html":"剪枝信息会定期清除。发生这种情况时，分组会再次被广播给所有人，直到剪枝重新收敛（回顾一下：没有剪枝时，分组会被发送给所有人。）"},{"en":"Forwarding tables scale poorly. The multicast forwarding table needs one entry per source, per destination group.","en_html":"Forwarding tables scale poorly. The multicast forwarding table needs one entry per source, per destination group.","zh_html":"转发表扩展性差。多播转发表需要每个源、每个目的组各一个表项。"},{"en":"What’s good about this routing protocol?","en_html":"What’s good about this routing protocol?","zh_html":"这个路由协议有什么优点？"},{"en":"DVMRP is a simple, elegant extension to an existing routing protocol (distance-vector). We were able to elegantly reuse the unicast forwarding table to help us implement DVMRP. For example, we didn’t have to think hard about how to identify our parent, because it was already done for us.","en_html":"DVMRP is a simple, elegant extension to an existing routing protocol (distance-vector). We were able to elegantly reuse the unicast forwarding table to help us implement DVMRP. For example, we didn’t have to think hard about how to identify our parent, because it was already done for us.","zh_html":"DVMRP 是对现有路由协议（距离向量）的一个简单、优雅的扩展。我们巧妙地复用了单播转发表来帮助实现 DVMRP。例如，我们不需要费心思考如何识别父节点，因为它已经为我们做好了。"},{"en":"Because we reused the delivery trees from the distance-vector protocol, the trees we produced are also least-cost trees. In other words, they give us the best path from the sender to all group members. This property is why we say that IP multicasting is optimal: in other words, DVMRP achieves the best possible performance, in terms of the costs in the network topology.","en_html":"Because we reused the delivery trees from the distance-vector protocol, the trees we produced are also least-cost trees. In other words, they give us the best path from the sender to all group members. This property is why we say that IP multicasting is optimal: in other words, DVMRP achieves the best possible performance, in terms of the costs in the network topology.","zh_html":"因为我们复用了距离向量协议中的交付树，生成的树也是最近成本树(least-cost trees)。换句话说，它们提供了从发送方到所有组成员的最近路径。正是这个性质让我们说 IP 多播是最优的：也就是说，DVMRP 在网络拓扑的成本方面实现了可能的最佳性能。"},{"en":"One downside of coupling multicast and unicast routing is that switching protocols is harder. For example, if we switched our unicast routing protocol from distance-vector to link-state, we would have to rethink our multicast routing protocol as well.","en_html":"One downside of coupling multicast and unicast routing is that switching protocols is harder. For example, if we switched our unicast routing protocol from distance-vector to link-state, we would have to rethink our multicast routing protocol as well.","zh_html":"将多播和单播路由耦合的一个缺点是更换协议更加困难。例如，如果我们将单播路由协议从距离向量切换到链路状态(link-state)，我们也必须重新考虑多播路由协议。"}]},"/beyond-client-server/index.html":{"title":"index","blocks":[{"en":"Multicast","en_html":"<a href=\"intro.html\">Multicast</a>","zh_html":"多播(Multicast)"},{"en":"IP Multicast","en_html":"<a href=\"ip-multicast-service-model.html\">IP Multicast</a>","zh_html":"IP多播(IP Multicast)"},{"en":"DVMRP","en_html":"<a href=\"dvmrp.html\">DVMRP</a>","zh_html":"DVMRP"},{"en":"Core-Based Trees","en_html":"<a href=\"cbt.html\">Core-Based Trees</a>","zh_html":"基于核心的树(Core-Based Trees)"},{"en":"IP Multicast Challenges","en_html":"<a href=\"ip-multicast-challenges.html\">IP Multicast Challenges</a>","zh_html":"IP多播挑战(IP Multicast Challenges)"},{"en":"Overlay Multicast","en_html":"<a href=\"overlay-multicast.html\">Overlay Multicast</a>","zh_html":"覆盖多播(Overlay Multicast)"},{"en":"Collective Operations","en_html":"<a href=\"collective-operations.html\">Collective Operations</a>","zh_html":"集体操作(Collective Operations)"},{"en":"Collective Implementations","en_html":"<a href=\"collective-implementations.html\">Collective Implementations</a>","zh_html":"集体操作实现(Collective Implementations)"}]},"/beyond-client-server/intro.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Multicast","zh_html":"多播(multicast)"},{"en":"Motivation: Multicast","zh_html":"动机：多播(multicast)"},{"en":"In every topic we’ve seen so far, we’ve said that the goal of the Internet is to deliver data between hosts. In particular, we’ve assumed unicast delivery, which means that there is a single source, sending data to a single destination.","zh_html":"在目前已经讨论过的每个主题中，我们都说互联网(Internet)的目标是在主机(hosts)之间传递数据。更具体地说，我们一直假设的是单播(unicast)交付，也就是一个源(source)向一个目的地(destination)发送数据。"},{"en":"Many protocols we’ve seen (e.g. HTTP, DNS, TCP, TLS) rely on a client-server model, which relies on the unicast delivery model. In the client-server model, there is one client and one server exchanging data, which implies that they are sending unicast data between each other.","zh_html":"我们见过的许多协议(protocols)，例如 HTTP、DNS、TCP、TLS，都依赖客户端-服务器模型(client-server model)，而这个模型依赖单播交付模型(unicast delivery model)。在客户端-服务器模型中，一个客户端(client)和一个服务器(server)交换数据，这意味着它们彼此之间发送的是单播数据(unicast data)。"},{"en":"Most of the traffic on the Internet is indeed unicast, but there are some exceptions. In particular, some applications involve groups of hosts communicating. For example, consider a multi-player game, or a live content delivery app (e.g. Zoom meeting, live-streaming a sports game), or a collaborative document (e.g. Google Docs). More exotic uses of group communication also exist, such as discovery (e.g. send a message to all Apple devices so that you can find the nearest speaker), or AI training (we’ll study this later in these notes).","zh_html":"互联网上的大多数流量(traffic)确实是单播(unicast)，但也有例外。尤其是，有些应用(applications)涉及一组主机(hosts)之间的通信。例如多人游戏、实时内容分发应用(live content delivery app)，如 Zoom 会议或体育比赛直播，或者协作文档(collaborative document)，如 Google Docs。组通信(group communication)还有一些更特殊的用途，例如发现(discovery)，也就是向所有 Apple 设备发送消息以找到最近的音箱；或者 AI 训练(AI training)，这会在后面的笔记中学习。"},{"en":"The client-server paradigm is not the most natural way to think about these situations. In a multi-player game or a video-conferencing app, there isn’t a single client or a single server. How should the network support these applications, to make it easier for developers to write these types of applications?","zh_html":"客户端-服务器范式(client-server paradigm)并不是理解这些场景最自然的方式。在多人游戏或视频会议应用(video-conferencing app)中，并不存在单一客户端或单一服务器。网络(network)应该如何支持这些应用，才能让开发者更容易编写这类应用？"},{"en":"One possible answer to this question is: The network should offer no support at all. Group communication can be implemented with unicast. For example, when you make an update to the collaborative document, you can send a separate unicast packet to everyone else in the group, so that they all know about your update.","zh_html":"这个问题的一种可能答案是：网络完全不提供额外支持。组通信(group communication)可以用单播(unicast)实现。例如，当你更新协作文档(collaborative document)时，可以向组内其他每个人分别发送一个单播分组(unicast packet)，让他们都知道你的更新。"},{"en":"However, this unicast-only approach can be inefficient. Consider this network topology, where you are in the USA and all other group members are in Europe. If you send separate unicast packets to each group member, you’re sending duplicate copies of the data across the expensive undersea cable. Also, this forces the sender to send many duplicate unicast packets, which scales poorly (e.g. imagine a single server streaming a sports game to millions of users).","zh_html":"不过，这种只使用单播(unicast-only)的方法可能效率很低。考虑这样一个网络拓扑(network topology)：你在美国，而组内其他成员都在欧洲。如果你分别向每个组成员发送单播分组(unicast packets)，就会把同一份数据的多个副本重复送过昂贵的海底光缆(undersea cable)。此外，这会迫使发送方(sender)发送大量重复的单播分组，扩展性很差；例如想象一台服务器向数百万用户直播一场体育比赛。"},{"en":"Intuitively, a more natural approach would be to send just a single packet across the undersea cable, and then let someone in Europe (e.g. a router or a host) distribute copies of the packet to the group members. Ideally, we would like to avoid sending duplicate copies of a packet along a link. In other words, each link should only carry the packet once (or possibly zero times, if there are no group members along that link).","zh_html":"直觉上，更自然的做法是只让一个分组(packet)穿过海底光缆(undersea cable)，然后让欧洲的某个节点，例如路由器(router)或主机(host)，把该分组的副本分发给组成员。理想情况下，我们希望避免在同一条链路(link)上发送某个分组的重复副本。换句话说，每条链路最多只应承载该分组一次；如果那条链路方向上没有组成员，也可以是零次。"},{"en":"This approach requires extra support from the network, and requires developing some new protocols.","zh_html":"这种方法需要网络(network)提供额外支持，也需要开发一些新的协议(protocols)。"},{"en":"Multicast Definitions","zh_html":"多播(multicast)定义"},{"en":"Recall that we have seen four packet delivery models so far:","zh_html":"回顾一下，到目前为止我们已经见过四种分组交付模型(packet delivery models)："},{"en":"Unicast: Send a packet to exactly one destination.","zh_html":"单播(unicast)：把一个分组(packet)发送给恰好一个目的地(destination)。"},{"en":"Anycast: Send a packet to anyone in a set of possible destinations. Only one member of the set needs to receive the packet.","zh_html":"任播(anycast)：把一个分组(packet)发送给一组可能目的地中的任意一个。集合中只需要有一个成员接收到该分组。"},{"en":"Broadcast: Send a packet to all destinations. The definition of “all” depends on the context of the problem, but you can think of it as all hosts in a local network.","zh_html":"广播(broadcast)：把一个分组(packet)发送给所有目的地(destinations)。“所有”的定义取决于问题上下文，但可以先把它理解为本地网络(local network)中的所有主机(hosts)。"},{"en":"Multicast: Send a packet to all members in a group. Hosts can choose to join/leave groups at any time. Note that you can send a packet to a group, even if you yourself are not a member of that group.","zh_html":"多播(multicast)：把一个分组(packet)发送给某个组(group)中的所有成员。主机(hosts)可以随时选择加入或离开组。注意，即使你自己不是该组成员，也可以向这个组发送分组。"},{"en":"The multicast paradigm can be used to think about the group communication problems from earlier. For example, all hosts interested in receiving the live-streamed sports game can join a multicast group. Then, the streaming service can multicast packets to the entire group.","zh_html":"多播范式(multicast paradigm)可以用来思考前面的组通信(group communication)问题。例如，所有想接收体育比赛直播的主机(hosts)都可以加入一个多播组(multicast group)。随后，流媒体服务(streaming service)可以向整个组多播分组(multicast packets)。"},{"en":"As another example, if we wanted to use multicast for discovery, we could have all printers in the building join a multicast group. Then, users can multicast packets to the entire group to find the printers that they can use.","zh_html":"再举一个例子，如果我们想用多播(multicast)做发现(discovery)，可以让建筑物里的所有打印机加入一个多播组(multicast group)。然后，用户可以向整个组多播分组，以找到自己可以使用的打印机。"},{"en":"IP vs. Overlay Multicast","zh_html":"IP 多播(IP multicast)与覆盖多播(overlay multicast)"},{"en":"A perennial debate throughout the history of multicast is an architectural question: At what layer should we implement multicast?","zh_html":"贯穿多播(multicast)历史的一个长期争论，是一个体系结构(architecture)问题：我们应该在哪一层(layer)实现多播？"},{"en":"One option is to implement multicast in Layer 3, sometimes called IP multicast. In this approach, we add specialized support to routers so that they understand how to multicast packets. This option gives better performance, but is harder to implement.","zh_html":"一种选择是在第 3 层(Layer 3)实现多播(multicast)，这有时称为 IP 多播(IP multicast)。在这种方法中，我们给路由器(routers)加入专门支持，让它们理解如何多播分组(multicast packets)。这种选择性能更好，但实现更困难。"},{"en":"The other option is implement multicast in Layer 7, sometimes called overlay multicast. In this approach, applications handle any multicast functionality. This approach leaves Layer 3 untouched, so routers only need to understand unicast. This option gives worse performance, but is simpler to implement.","zh_html":"另一种选择是在第 7 层(Layer 7)实现多播(multicast)，这有时称为覆盖多播(overlay multicast)。在这种方法中，应用(applications)处理所有多播功能(multicast functionality)。这种方法不改动第 3 层，因此路由器(routers)只需要理解单播(unicast)。这种选择性能较差，但实现更简单。"},{"en":"Neither option is strictly better. We’ll study both options and analyze the trade-offs between them.","zh_html":"这两种选择没有哪一种绝对更好。我们会学习两种方案，并分析它们之间的权衡(trade-offs)。"}]},"/beyond-client-server/ip-multicast-challenges.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"IP Multicast Challenges","en_html":"<a href=\"#ip-multicast-challenges\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-multicast-challenges\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP Multicast Challenges","zh_html":"<a href=\"#ip-multicast-challenges\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-multicast-challenges\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP 多播的挑战"},{"en":"Inter-Domain Routing","en_html":"<a href=\"#inter-domain-routing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"inter-domain-routing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Inter-Domain Routing","zh_html":"<a href=\"#inter-domain-routing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"inter-domain-routing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 域间路由(Inter-Domain Routing)"},{"en":"The protocols we’ve described so far (IGMP, DVMRP, CBT) can be used for intra-domain multicast routing. However, they cannot easily be extended to inter-domain multicast routing.","en_html":"The protocols we’ve described so far (IGMP, DVMRP, CBT) can be used for intra-domain multicast routing. However, they cannot easily be extended to inter-domain multicast routing.","zh_html":"我们目前描述的协议（IGMP、DVMRP、CBT）可以用于域内多播路由(intra-domain multicast routing)。然而，它们不容易扩展到域间多播路由(inter-domain multicast routing)。"},{"en":"One major problem here is scalability. For example, if we used DVMRP at a global scale, then periodically, when the pruning state is deleted, packets will get flooded to the entire Internet, which isn’t practical.","en_html":"One major problem here is scalability. For example, if we used DVMRP at a global scale, then periodically, when the pruning state is deleted, packets will get flooded to the entire Internet, which isn’t practical.","zh_html":"一个主要问题是扩展性。例如，如果我们在全球范围内使用 DVMRP，那么当剪枝状态被定期删除时，分组会被泛洪到整个互联网，这是不现实的。"},{"en":"Also, recall that inter-domain routing introduces the additional challenge of AS autonomy and privacy. For example, if we used CBT at a global scale, then the core router might be in a different network, and this requires you to trust somebody else to control the core router.","en_html":"Also, recall that inter-domain routing introduces the additional challenge of AS autonomy and privacy. For example, if we used CBT at a global scale, then the core router might be in a different network, and this requires you to trust somebody else to control the core router.","zh_html":"此外，回顾一下，域间路由还引入了 AS 自治性和隐私性的额外挑战。例如，如果我们在全球范围内使用 CBT，核心路由器可能在不同的网络中，这要求你信任其他人来控制核心路由器。"},{"en":"Inter-domain multicast routing is a hard problem, and much work has been done to develop solutions. For example, the CBT core selection problem could be solved by having multiple cores (one per network) that communicate. However, in practice, there has been very little adoption of inter-domain multicast routing.","en_html":"Inter-domain multicast routing is a hard problem, and much work has been done to develop solutions. For example, the CBT core selection problem could be solved by having multiple cores (one per network) that communicate. However, in practice, there has been very little adoption of inter-domain multicast routing.","zh_html":"域间多播路由是一个难题，已经有很多工作致力于开发解决方案。例如，CBT 核心选择问题可以通过使用多个核心（每个网络一个）进行通信来解决。然而，在实践中，域间多播路由的采用非常少。"},{"en":"Charging","en_html":"<a href=\"#charging\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"charging\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Charging","zh_html":"<a href=\"#charging\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"charging\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 计费(Charging)"},{"en":"The IP multicast service model is fundamentally at odds with the business model that modern ISPs use. For example, consider this AS graph, where AS A and AS B are peers:","en_html":"The IP multicast service model is fundamentally at odds with the business model that modern ISPs use. For example, consider this AS graph, where AS A and AS B are peers:","zh_html":"IP 多播服务模型与现代 ISP 使用的商业模式从根本上存在冲突。例如，考虑这个 AS 图，其中 AS A 和 AS B 是对等体(peers)："},{"en":"As peers, AS A and AS B should be able to exchange equivalent amounts of traffic, but multicast makes it difficult to define what counts as equivalent traffic. As an example, suppose AS A sends a multicast packet to AS B. It’s possible that AS B has many children who are part of the group. This means that AS B received one packet, but had to send out many packets. AS B used much more bandwidth here than AS A did. Does AS A need to pay some extra to AS B because of this? (It’s an open question, with no clear answer.)","en_html":"As peers, AS A and AS B should be able to exchange equivalent amounts of traffic, but multicast makes it difficult to define what counts as equivalent traffic. As an example, suppose AS A sends a multicast packet to AS B. It’s possible that AS B has many children who are part of the group. This means that AS B received one packet, but had to send out many packets. AS B used much more bandwidth here than AS A did. Does AS A need to pay some extra to AS B because of this? (It’s an open question, with no clear answer.)","zh_html":"作为对等体，AS A 和 AS B 应该能够交换等量的流量，但多播使得定义什么算作等量流量变得困难。举个例子，假设 AS A 向 AS B 发送一个多播分组。AS B 可能有很多属于该组的子节点，这意味着 AS B 收到一个分组，但需要发出多个分组。AS B 在此消耗的带宽远多于 AS A。AS A 是否需要为此向 AS B 额外付费？（这是一个开放性问题，没有明确的答案。）"},{"en":"As another example, consider this AS graph, where AS A is the provider and AS B is the customer:","en_html":"As another example, consider this AS graph, where AS A is the provider and AS B is the customer:","zh_html":"另一个例子，考虑这个 AS 图，其中 AS A 是提供商(provider)，AS B 是客户(customer)："},{"en":"AS B is paying AS A for service. What if AS B sends a multicast packet, and AS A has to forward copies of that packet to many other destinations? Should AS A charge more for this packet compared to a unicast packet, and if so, how much more should AS A charge? (It’s an open question, with no clear answer.)","en_html":"AS B is paying AS A for service. What if AS B sends a multicast packet, and AS A has to forward copies of that packet to many other destinations? Should AS A charge more for this packet compared to a unicast packet, and if so, how much more should AS A charge? (It’s an open question, with no clear answer.)","zh_html":"AS B 向 AS A 支付服务费用。如果 AS B 发送一个多播分组，而 AS A 需要将该分组的副本转发给许多其他目的地，应该怎么办？AS A 是否应该为此分组收取比单播分组更高的费用？如果是，应该多收多少？（这是一个开放性问题，没有明确的答案。）"},{"en":"Designing a business model is made more difficult by the fact that the IP multicast model does not explicitly keep track of group size. If you wanted to charge users based on the size of the destination group, there’s no clear way to determine the size of any given destination group. Your forwarding tables tell you about your parent and your children on different delivery trees, but the tables do not tell you how many end hosts will be receiving this packet in total.","en_html":"Designing a business model is made more difficult by the fact that the IP multicast model does not explicitly keep track of group size. If you wanted to charge users based on the size of the destination group, there’s no clear way to determine the size of any given destination group. Your forwarding tables tell you about your parent and your children on different delivery trees, but the tables do not tell you how many end hosts will be receiving this packet in total.","zh_html":"设计商业模式还因为 IP 多播模型没有明确追踪组大小而变得更加困难。如果你想根据目的组的大小向用户收费，没有明确的方法来确定任何给定目的组的大小。你的转发表告诉你不同交付树上的父节点和子节点，但这些表不会告诉你总共有多少终端主机会收到这个分组。"},{"en":"Congestion Control","en_html":"<a href=\"#congestion-control\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"congestion-control\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Congestion Control","zh_html":"<a href=\"#congestion-control\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"congestion-control\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 拥塞控制(Congestion Control)"},{"en":"Consider a source sending a multicast packet down the delivery tree, to many recipients. The source needs to pick a good sending rate to avoid overloading the network. What rate should the source pick?","en_html":"Consider a source sending a multicast packet down the delivery tree, to many recipients. The source needs to pick a good sending rate to avoid overloading the network. What rate should the source pick?","zh_html":"考虑一个源沿着交付树向下发送多播分组给许多接收方。源需要选择一个好的发送速率，以避免使网络过载。源应该选择什么速率？"},{"en":"The traffic will travel along many different paths, and each path could have a different capacity. The source could send at 1 Mbps to avoid overloading any of the links, but this leave unused capacity along the other paths. On the other hand, the source could send at 100 Mbps to maximize performance, but this causes some links to be overloaded. There’s no clear answer for what rate the source should pick.","en_html":"The traffic will travel along many different paths, and each path could have a different capacity. The source could send at 1 Mbps to avoid overloading any of the links, but this leave unused capacity along the other paths. On the other hand, the source could send at 100 Mbps to maximize performance, but this causes some links to be overloaded. There’s no clear answer for what rate the source should pick.","zh_html":"流量将沿着许多不同的路径传输，每条路径可能有不同的容量。源可以以 1 Mbps 的速率发送，以避免使任何链路过载，但这会浪费其他路径上的未用容量。另一方面，源可以以 100 Mbps 的速率发送以最大化性能，但这会导致某些链路过载。源应该选择什么速率，没有明确的答案。"},{"en":"In practice, one possible solution is to define different groups depending on performance. For example, we could define four different multicast groups, where each group receives the same video feed, but with different video qualities. Then, any interested recipients can try joining different groups to see which one gives them the best performance.","en_html":"In practice, one possible solution is to define different groups depending on performance. For example, we could define four different multicast groups, where each group receives the same video feed, but with different video qualities. Then, any interested recipients can try joining different groups to see which one gives them the best performance.","zh_html":"在实践中，一种可能的解决方案是根据性能定义不同的组。例如，我们可以定义四个不同的多播组，每个组接收相同的视频流，但具有不同的视频质量。然后，感兴趣的接收方可以尝试加入不同的组，看看哪一个能给他们最好的性能。"},{"en":"Reliability","en_html":"<a href=\"#reliability\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"reliability\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Reliability","zh_html":"<a href=\"#reliability\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"reliability\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 可靠性(Reliability)"},{"en":"Just like IP unicast, IP multicast is best-effort, which introduces some additional complexity. For example, you might send a packet, and it might reach some, but not all, group members.","en_html":"Just like IP unicast, IP multicast is best-effort, which introduces some additional complexity. For example, you might send a packet, and it might reach some, but not all, group members.","zh_html":"与 IP 单播一样，IP 多播也是尽力而为(best-effort)的，这引入了一些额外的复杂性。例如，你可能发送一个分组，它可能到达部分组成员，但并非全部。"},{"en":"We could try to add acks to solve this, but this is also potentially problematic. If the group has millions of members, a single sender won’t be able to process millions of acks for every packet.","en_html":"We could try to add acks to solve this, but this is also potentially problematic. If the group has millions of members, a single sender won’t be able to process millions of acks for every packet.","zh_html":"我们可以尝试添加确认(acks)来解决这个问题，但这也可能存在问题。如果组有数百万成员，一个单一的发送方无法处理每个分组的数百万个确认。"},{"en":"Another possible approach is to use negative acknowledgements (nacks), where a group member sends nothing if they receive a packet, and sends a nack if they don’t receive a packet (e.g. their timer expires). Again, if the group has millions of members, a single sender could get overwhelmed.","en_html":"Another possible approach is to use negative acknowledgements (nacks), where a group member sends nothing if they receive a packet, and sends a nack if they don’t receive a packet (e.g. their timer expires). Again, if the group has millions of members, a single sender could get overwhelmed.","zh_html":"另一种可能的方法是使用否定确认(negative acknowledgements, nacks)，即组成员在收到分组时不发送任何内容，而在未收到分组时发送 nack（例如其计时器超时）。同样，如果组有数百万成员，一个单一的发送方仍可能被淹没。"},{"en":"In the nack approach, it’s also not clear how to recover from failure. If someone didn’t receive the packet, do we multicast the packet to the entire group again? This wastes bandwidth because some group members already received the packet and will be receiving a duplicate copy.","en_html":"In the nack approach, it’s also not clear how to recover from failure. If someone didn’t receive the packet, do we multicast the packet to the entire group again? This wastes bandwidth because some group members already received the packet and will be receiving a duplicate copy.","zh_html":"在 nack 方法中，如何从故障中恢复也不清楚。如果有人没有收到分组，我们是否应该向整个组再次多播该分组？这会浪费带宽，因为有些组成员已经收到了分组，会收到一个重复的副本。"},{"en":"Another approach is to unicast the packet to just the group members who sent nacks. If many group members didn’t receive the packet, this could be wasteful because we have to unicast many copies of the same packet. For example, consider the case where the very first link drops the packet, which means that none of the group members received the packet.","en_html":"Another approach is to unicast the packet to just the group members who sent nacks. If many group members didn’t receive the packet, this could be wasteful because we have to unicast many copies of the same packet. For example, consider the case where the very first link drops the packet, which means that none of the group members received the packet.","zh_html":"另一种方法是只向发送了 nack 的组成员单播该分组。如果许多组成员没有收到分组，这可能很浪费，因为我们必须单播该分组的许多副本。例如，考虑第一个链路就丢弃了分组的情况，这意味着没有任何组成员收到该分组。"},{"en":"Which retransmission approach is better? It’s not immediately clear, and it can depend on how many group members received the packet.","en_html":"Which retransmission approach is better? It’s not immediately clear, and it can depend on how many group members received the packet.","zh_html":"哪种重传方法更好？这并不明确，可能取决于有多少组成员收到了分组。"},{"en":"In practice, some modern IP multicast applications don’t implement reliability at all. Or, they implement reliability by encoding some redundancy into the data stream (think: error-correcting codes) so that losses and corruptions can be corrected from the data itself, without the network’s help.","en_html":"In practice, some modern IP multicast applications don’t implement reliability at all. Or, they implement reliability by encoding some redundancy into the data stream (think: error-correcting codes) so that losses and corruptions can be corrected from the data itself, without the network’s help.","zh_html":"在实践中，一些现代 IP 多播应用根本不实现可靠性。或者，它们通过在数据流中编码一些冗余（想想：纠错码）来实现可靠性，使得丢失和损坏可以从数据本身得到纠正，而无需网络的帮助。"},{"en":"Encoding redundancy does mean you need more bits to encode the same data. For example, if you want to send 5 packets’ worth of data, you might send 10 packets, and encode the bits in such a way that any 5 of the packets can be used to reconstruct the original data.","en_html":"Encoding redundancy does mean you need more bits to encode the same data. For example, if you want to send 5 packets’ worth of data, you might send 10 packets, and encode the bits in such a way that any 5 of the packets can be used to reconstruct the original data.","zh_html":"编码冗余确实意味着你需要更多的比特来编码同样的数据。例如，如果你想发送 5 个分组的数据，你可能发送 10 个分组，并以某种方式编码这些比特，使得任何 5 个分组都可以用来重建原始数据。"},{"en":"Security","en_html":"<a href=\"#security\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"security\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Security","zh_html":"<a href=\"#security\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"security\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 安全性(Security)"},{"en":"Another limitation of IP multicast is the lack of access control. Anybody can join a group, and anybody can send messages to any group. If you want to enforce access control (e.g. only paid users can watch the sports game), you have to build that functionality separately.","en_html":"Another limitation of IP multicast is the lack of access control. Anybody can join a group, and anybody can send messages to any group. If you want to enforce access control (e.g. only paid users can watch the sports game), you have to build that functionality separately.","zh_html":"IP 多播的另一个限制是缺乏访问控制(access control)。任何人都可以加入一个组，任何人都可以向任何组发送消息。如果你想实施访问控制（例如只有付费用户才能观看体育比赛），你必须单独构建该功能。"},{"en":"The lack of access control leads to security vulnerabilities. A malicious sender could flood packets to a specific multicast group, causing all members of that group to be overwhelmed. Note that this is more effective than the unicast alternative, where the malicious sender would have to flood packets to every member separately.","en_html":"The lack of access control leads to security vulnerabilities. A malicious sender could flood packets to a specific multicast group, causing all members of that group to be overwhelmed. Note that this is more effective than the unicast alternative, where the malicious sender would have to flood packets to every member separately.","zh_html":"缺乏访问控制会导致安全漏洞。恶意发送方可以向特定多播组泛洪分组，导致该组的所有成员被淹没。注意，这比单播替代方案更有效——在单播方案中，恶意发送方必须分别向每个成员泛洪分组。"},{"en":"Additional security measures like encryption are also difficult to implement. Suppose you encrypt multicast messages by giving every group member a shared secret key. What if someone leaves the group? If you keep using the same key, that user still knows the secret key and can read your messages. One approach is to switch to using a new key, but now you need a way to securely distribute this new key to the remaining group members.","en_html":"Additional security measures like encryption are also difficult to implement. Suppose you encrypt multicast messages by giving every group member a shared secret key. What if someone leaves the group? If you keep using the same key, that user still knows the secret key and can read your messages. One approach is to switch to using a new key, but now you need a way to securely distribute this new key to the remaining group members.","zh_html":"额外的安全措施如加密也很难实现。假设你通过给每个组成员一个共享密钥来加密多播消息。如果有人离开组会怎样？如果你继续使用同一个密钥，那个用户仍然知道密钥，可以阅读你的消息。一种方法是切换到使用新密钥，但现在你需要一种安全地将这个新密钥分发给剩余组成员的方式。"},{"en":"IP Multicast in Practice","en_html":"<a href=\"#ip-multicast-in-practice\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-multicast-in-practice\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP Multicast in Practice","zh_html":"<a href=\"#ip-multicast-in-practice\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-multicast-in-practice\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP 多播的实践现状"},{"en":"Because of all these challenges, IP multicast is mostly used today within a single domain, and not across different domains.","en_html":"Because of all these challenges, IP multicast is mostly used today within a single domain, and not across different domains.","zh_html":"由于所有这些挑战，IP 多播目前主要在单个域内使用，而不是跨不同域使用。"},{"en":"Some applications might still want group communication across multiple networks (e.g. multi-player gaming, video-conferencing). Instead of relying on IP multicasting, which doesn’t support inter-network communication, many applications have implemented their own custom solutions for group communication.","en_html":"Some applications might still want group communication across multiple networks (e.g. multi-player gaming, video-conferencing). Instead of relying on IP multicasting, which doesn’t support inter-network communication, many applications have implemented their own custom solutions for group communication.","zh_html":"有些应用可能仍然需要跨多个网络的组通信（例如多人游戏、视频会议）。由于 IP 多播不支持网络间通信，许多应用已经实现了自己的自定义组通信解决方案。"},{"en":"For example, if the group is small enough, the application could implement a central relay server. Group communications are unicast to the central relay server, which then unicasts the message to the other group members.","en_html":"For example, if the group is small enough, the application could implement a central relay server. Group communications are unicast to the central relay server, which then unicasts the message to the other group members.","zh_html":"例如，如果组足够小，应用可以实现一个中央中继服务器(central relay server)。组通信被单播到中央中继服务器，然后该服务器再将消息单播给其他组成员。"},{"en":"Or, if the group is small enough, the naive unicast-based solution (send separate unicast packets to each group member) might work just fine.","en_html":"Or, if the group is small enough, the naive unicast-based solution (send separate unicast packets to each group member) might work just fine.","zh_html":"或者，如果组足够小，朴素的基于单播的解决方案（向每个组成员分别发送单播分组）可能就很好用。"},{"en":"If IP multicasting doesn’t work across domains, and custom solutions require extra work to implement and scale up, how do modern applications handle group communications? One solution is to use overlay multicast, which is an alternative to IP multicast that implements network functionality at Layer 7 instead of Layer 3. We’ll look at overlay multicasts next.","en_html":"If IP multicasting doesn’t work across domains, and custom solutions require extra work to implement and scale up, how do modern applications handle group communications? One solution is to use overlay multicast, which is an alternative to IP multicast that implements network functionality at Layer 7 instead of Layer 3. We’ll look at overlay multicasts next.","zh_html":"如果 IP 多播不能跨域工作，而自定义解决方案需要额外的工作来实现和扩展，那么现代应用如何处理组通信？一种解决方案是使用覆盖多播(overlay multicast)，它是 IP 多播的替代方案，在第 7 层而不是第 3 层实现网络功能。我们接下来会学习覆盖多播。"}]},"/beyond-client-server/ip-multicast-service-model.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"IP Multicast","en_html":"<a href=\"#ip-multicast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-multicast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP Multicast","zh_html":"<a href=\"#ip-multicast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-multicast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP 多播(IP multicast)"},{"en":"Brief History of IP Multicast","en_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-ip-multicast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-ip-multicast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Brief History of IP Multicast","zh_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-ip-multicast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-ip-multicast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP 多播简史"},{"en":"IP multicast was actively researched and developed in the 1990s and 2000s. The development was motivated by the expectation that the killer application for the Internet would be live-streamed TV or radio. (Fun fact: One of the earliest live-streamed concerts was the Rolling Stones in 1994.)","en_html":"IP multicast was actively researched and developed in the 1990s and 2000s. The development was motivated by the expectation that the killer application for the Internet would be live-streamed TV or radio. (Fun fact: One of the earliest live-streamed concerts was the Rolling Stones in 1994.)","zh_html":"IP 多播(IP multicast)在 1990 年代和 2000 年代得到了积极的研究和开发。其发展动力源于一种预期：互联网的杀手级应用将是实时流媒体电视或广播。（趣味事实：最早的流媒体直播音乐会之一是 1994 年的滚石乐队演唱会。）"},{"en":"Looking back, the IP multicast protocols developed in the 1990s and 2000s had mixed success in terms of adoption. Modern routers do offer support for the IP multicast protocols we’ll see, but network operators don’t always enable these protocols on the routers. (Disabling the protocol on the router essentially means that the router doesn’t understand or support that protocol.)","en_html":"Looking back, the IP multicast protocols developed in the 1990s and 2000s had mixed success in terms of adoption. Modern routers do offer support for the IP multicast protocols we’ll see, but network operators don’t always enable these protocols on the routers. (Disabling the protocol on the router essentially means that the router doesn’t understand or support that protocol.)","zh_html":"回顾来看，1990 年代和 2000 年代开发的 IP 多播协议在采用方面取得了混合的成功。现代路由器确实支持我们将要看到的 IP 多播协议，但网络运维人员并不总是在路由器上启用这些协议。（在路由器上禁用协议实质上意味着路由器不理解或不支持该协议。）"},{"en":"IP multicast protocols are sometimes used within individual domains (e.g. inside a datacenter network). However, IP multicast protocols are rarely/never deployed across different domains. This means that users cannot expect to use IP multicast at the global Internet level, e.g. if a group of users around the world joined a multicast group, the modern Internet would not automatically support multicasting packets to that group.","en_html":"IP multicast protocols are sometimes used within individual domains (e.g. inside a datacenter network). However, IP multicast protocols are rarely/never deployed across different domains. This means that users cannot expect to use IP multicast at the global Internet level, e.g. if a group of users around the world joined a multicast group, the modern Internet would not automatically support multicasting packets to that group.","zh_html":"IP 多播协议有时在单个域内使用（例如在数据中心网络内部）。然而，IP 多播协议很少/从未跨不同域部署。这意味着用户不能指望在全局互联网级别使用 IP 多播，例如，如果世界各地的用户加入了一个多播组，现代互联网不会自动支持向该组多播分组。"},{"en":"Although these protocols were not globally deployed, the techniques used in these protocols can be applied to solve different networking problems. In particular, these techniques have become relevant again for solving problems related to AI training (we’ll study this when we discuss collectives).","en_html":"Although these protocols were not globally deployed, the techniques used in these protocols can be applied to solve different networking problems. In particular, these techniques have become relevant again for solving problems related to AI training (we’ll study this when we discuss collectives).","zh_html":"尽管这些协议没有在全局范围内部署，但其中使用的技术可以应用于解决不同的网络问题。特别是，这些技术再次变得相关，用于解决与 AI 训练相关的问题（我们将在讨论集合操作(collectives)时研究这一点）。"},{"en":"IP Multicast Service Model","en_html":"<a href=\"#ip-multicast-service-model\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-multicast-service-model\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP Multicast Service Model","zh_html":"<a href=\"#ip-multicast-service-model\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-multicast-service-model\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP 多播服务模型(IP Multicast Service Model)"},{"en":"How do we define a group? Each multicast group is defined by an IP address. The addresses from 224.0.0.0 to 239.255.255.255 are multicast addresses, and everyone knows that addresses in this hard-coded range are multicast addresses.","en_html":"How do we define a group? Each multicast group is defined by an IP address. The addresses from 224.0.0.0 to 239.255.255.255 are multicast addresses, and everyone knows that addresses in this hard-coded range are multicast addresses.","zh_html":"我们如何定义一个组？每个多播组由一个 IP 地址定义。从 224.0.0.0 到 239.255.255.255 的地址是多播地址(multicast addresses)，每个人都知道这个硬编码范围内的地址是多播地址。"},{"en":"To join a group, you will announce the multicast address of the group you want to join. At least one router should hear your message (e.g. your home router), and then the routers will coordinate amongst themselves to spread this information (e.g. with a routing protocol). Eventually, all the routers will have learned that you are part of that group.","en_html":"To join a group, you will announce the multicast address of the group you want to join. At least one router should hear your message (e.g. your home router), and then the routers will coordinate amongst themselves to spread this information (e.g. with a routing protocol). Eventually, all the routers will have learned that you are part of that group.","zh_html":"要加入一个组，你需要宣告你想加入的组的多播地址。至少有一个路由器应该听到你的消息（例如你的家庭路由器），然后路由器之间会协调以传播这个信息（例如通过路由协议）。最终，所有路由器都会了解到你是该组的一部分。"},{"en":"Similarly, you can announce that you are leaving a group, and you again use the multicast address to identify which group you are talking about.","en_html":"Similarly, you can announce that you are leaving a group, and you again use the multicast address to identify which group you are talking about.","zh_html":"类似地，你可以宣告你正在离开一个组，也是用多播地址来标识你谈论的是哪个组。"},{"en":"To send a packet to a group, all you need to do is fill in the multicast group address as the IP destination field. Then, the routers will use that group address to forward the packet to all group members. Notice that as the sender, you don’t need to worry about who belongs to the group, because the routers will figure that out for you.","en_html":"To send a packet to a group, all you need to do is fill in the multicast group address as the IP destination field. Then, the routers will use that group address to forward the packet to all group members. Notice that as the sender, you don’t need to worry about who belongs to the group, because the routers will figure that out for you.","zh_html":"要向一个组发送分组，你只需要在 IP 目的字段中填入多播组地址即可。然后，路由器会使用该组地址将分组转发给所有组成员。注意，作为发送方，你不需要担心谁属于该组，因为路由器会为你处理好。"},{"en":"In summary, the IP multicast service model defines three operations for end hosts: You can send packets to a group (even if you are not a part of that group yourself). You can announce that you are joining a group. You can announce that you are leaving a group. In all three operations, your job is just to send out packets. The routers will process those packets, coordinate with each other (e.g. run a routing protocol), and decide how to route multicast packets accordingly.","en_html":"In summary, the IP multicast service model defines three operations for end hosts: You can send packets to a group (even if you are not a part of that group yourself). You can announce that you are joining a group. You can announce that you are leaving a group. In all three operations, your job is just to send out packets. The routers will process those packets, coordinate with each other (e.g. run a routing protocol), and decide how to route multicast packets accordingly.","zh_html":"总结一下，IP 多播服务模型为终端主机(end hosts)定义了三种操作：你可以向一个组发送分组（即使你自己不是该组的成员）；你可以宣告你正在加入一个组；你可以宣告你正在离开一个组。在所有三种操作中，你的工作只是发出分组。路由器会处理这些分组，相互协调（例如运行路由协议），并据此决定如何路由多播分组。"},{"en":"Now that we know how hosts interact with IP multicasting (sending, joining, and leaving), we can think about how routers deliver multicast packets.","en_html":"Now that we know how hosts interact with IP multicasting (sending, joining, and leaving), we can think about how routers deliver multicast packets.","zh_html":"现在我们知道主机如何与 IP 多播交互（发送、加入和离开），我们可以考虑路由器如何交付多播分组。"},{"en":"In the unicast model, a router receives a packet and forwards the packet along a single next-hop. Now, in the IP multicast model, when a router receives a multicast packet (i.e. destination is a multicast group address), the router will forward the packet along zero, one, or multiple outgoing links, so that the packet reaches all group members.","en_html":"In the unicast model, a router receives a packet and forwards the packet along a single next-hop. Now, in the IP multicast model, when a router receives a multicast packet (i.e. destination is a multicast group address), the router will forward the packet along zero, one, or multiple outgoing links, so that the packet reaches all group members.","zh_html":"在单播(unicast)模型中，路由器收到一个分组并将其转发到单一的下一跳(next-hop)。现在，在 IP 多播模型中，当路由器收到一个多播分组（即目的地址是多播组地址）时，路由器将沿着零条、一条或多条出向链路转发该分组，以便分组到达所有组成员。"},{"en":"To implement multicast, the router needs some additional state to keep track of group membership, so that the router can forward the packet to only the next-hops that lead toward the group members. If a next-hop doesn’t lead to any group members, there’s no need to send the packet along that next-hop. As users join and leave the group, a router’s next-hops for that group might change.","en_html":"To implement multicast, the router needs some additional state to keep track of group membership, so that the router can forward the packet to only the next-hops that lead toward the group members. If a next-hop doesn’t lead to any group members, there’s no need to send the packet along that next-hop. As users join and leave the group, a router’s next-hops for that group might change.","zh_html":"为了实现多播，路由器需要一些额外的状态来追踪组成员关系，以便路由器只将分组转发给那些通向组成员的下一跳。如果某个下一跳不通往任何组成员，就没有必要沿着那个下一跳发送分组。随着用户加入和离开组，路由器对该组的下一跳可能会改变。"},{"en":"Implementing Multicast","en_html":"<a href=\"#implementing-multicast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"implementing-multicast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Implementing Multicast","zh_html":"<a href=\"#implementing-multicast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"implementing-multicast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 实现多播"},{"en":"With our service model defined, we are now ready to implement IP multicasting in routers. Remember our end goal here: Users interact with the network by sending packets, announcing joins, and announcing leaves. The routers must take this information and use it to correctly forward multicast packets to all members of that group (as defined by the multicast address).","en_html":"With our service model defined, we are now ready to implement IP multicasting in routers. Remember our end goal here: Users interact with the network by sending packets, announcing joins, and announcing leaves. The routers must take this information and use it to correctly forward multicast packets to all members of that group (as defined by the multicast address).","zh_html":"服务模型定义好了，现在我们准备在路由器中实现 IP 多播。请记住我们的最终目标：用户通过网络发送分组、宣告加入和宣告离开来与网络交互。路由器必须获取这些信息，并用它来正确地将多播分组转发给该组的所有成员（由多播地址定义）。"},{"en":"We can divide this problem into two parts:","en_html":"We can divide this problem into two parts:","zh_html":"我们可以把这个问题分成两部分："},{"en":"How do routers know what groups their directly-connected hosts belong to? We’ll use a protocol called IGMP to solve this.","en_html":"<p>How do routers know what groups their directly-connected hosts belong to? We’ll use a protocol called IGMP to solve this.</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/beyond-client-server/7-009-igmp-taxonomy.png\" /></img></p>","zh_html":"<p>路由器如何知道其直连主机属于哪些组？我们将使用一个叫做 IGMP 的协议来解决这个问题。</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/beyond-client-server/7-009-igmp-taxonomy.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"How do routers forward packets through the network to reach the destination group members? We’ll look at two protocols for solving this: DVMRP and CBT. Both protocols achieve the same goal, so you can pick either one for your implementation (the same way you can pick either distance-vector or link-state, but not both).","en_html":"<p>How do routers forward packets through the network to reach the destination group members? We’ll look at two protocols for solving this: DVMRP and CBT. Both protocols achieve the same goal, so you can pick either one for your implementation (the same way you can pick either distance-vector or link-state, but not both).</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/beyond-client-server/7-010-dvmrp-cbt-taxonomy.png\" /></img></p>","zh_html":"<p>路由器如何通过网络转发分组以到达目的组成员？我们将研究两种解决这个问题的协议：DVMRP 和 CBT。两种协议都能达到相同的目标，所以你可以选择其中任何一种来实现（就像你可以选择距离向量或链路状态，但不需要同时选择两者）。</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/beyond-client-server/7-010-dvmrp-cbt-taxonomy.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"IGMP: Directly-Connected Hosts","en_html":"<a href=\"#igmp-directly-connected-hosts\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"igmp-directly-connected-hosts\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IGMP: Directly-Connected Hosts","zh_html":"<a href=\"#igmp-directly-connected-hosts\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"igmp-directly-connected-hosts\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IGMP：直连主机"},{"en":"Before we solve the larger problem of multicast routing, let’s start with a smaller problem. Suppose a router is directly connected to many hosts. The router needs some way to know which group(s) each host belongs to. We’ll use a protocol called IGMP (Internet Group Management Protocol) to achieve this.","en_html":"Before we solve the larger problem of multicast routing, let’s start with a smaller problem. Suppose a router is directly connected to many hosts. The router needs some way to know which group(s) each host belongs to. We’ll use a protocol called IGMP (Internet Group Management Protocol) to achieve this.","zh_html":"在我们解决更大的多播路由问题之前，先从一个较小的问题开始。假设一个路由器直连了许多主机。路由器需要某种方式来知道每个主机属于哪个（哪些）组。我们将使用一个叫做 IGMP（互联网组管理协议，Internet Group Management Protocol）的协议来实现这一点。"},{"en":"At a high level, the router and the hosts exchange messages so that the router is informed about everybody’s group membership(s). Some types of messages that can be exchanged:","en_html":"At a high level, the router and the hosts exchange messages so that the router is informed about everybody’s group membership(s). Some types of messages that can be exchanged:","zh_html":"在高层面上，路由器和主机之间交换消息，以便路由器了解每个人的组成员关系。可以交换的消息类型包括："},{"en":"Queries: The router periodically sends Queries to the hosts. These messages ask: What group(s) do you belong to?","en_html":"<strong>Queries:</strong> The router periodically sends Queries to the hosts. These messages ask: What group(s) do you belong to?","zh_html":"<strong>查询(Queries)：</strong>路由器定期向主机发送查询。这些消息问的是：你属于哪个（哪些）组？"},{"en":"Reports: In response, hosts send Reports back to the router. Reports answer the question: These are the group(s) I belong to. Hosts can also send unsolicited Reports (i.e. without waiting for a Query).","en_html":"<strong>Reports:</strong> In response, hosts send Reports back to the router. Reports answer the question: These are the group(s) I belong to. Hosts can also send unsolicited Reports (i.e. without waiting for a Query).","zh_html":"<strong>报告(Reports)：</strong>作为响应，主机向路由器发送报告。报告回答了问题：这是我所属的组。主机也可以发送未经请求的报告（即不等待查询）。"},{"en":"By periodically exchanging Queries and Reports, the router stays informed about the latest group membership(s). If the router doesn’t receive a Report about a membership for a long time, the router will assume that membership has expired and invalidate it.","en_html":"By periodically exchanging Queries and Reports, the router stays informed about the latest group membership(s). If the router doesn’t receive a Report about a membership for a long time, the router will assume that membership has expired and invalidate it.","zh_html":"通过定期交换查询和报告，路由器始终了解最新的组成员关系。如果路由器长时间没有收到关于某个成员关系的报告，就会假定该成员关系已过期并将其失效。"},{"en":"IGMP helps routers learn about directly-connected hosts. However, routers still don’t know anything about other hosts elsewhere in the network, so we’ll need routing algorithms for those.","en_html":"IGMP helps routers learn about directly-connected hosts. However, routers still don’t know anything about other hosts elsewhere in the network, so we’ll need routing algorithms for those.","zh_html":"IGMP 帮助路由器了解直连主机。然而，路由器仍然不知道网络中其他位置的其他主机，因此我们还需要路由算法来处理那些。"},{"en":"To draw a comparison to distance-vector routing, you can think of IGMP as the multicast version of static routing, where a router learns about its directly-connected hosts (but not other hosts elsewhere in the network).","en_html":"To draw a comparison to distance-vector routing, you can think of IGMP as the multicast version of static routing, where a router learns about its directly-connected hosts (but not other hosts elsewhere in the network).","zh_html":"与距离向量路由进行对比，你可以将 IGMP 看作多播版本的静态路由，即路由器了解其直连主机（但不了解网络中其他位置的主机）。"}]},"/beyond-client-server/overlay-multicast.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Overlay Multicast","en_html":"<a href=\"#overlay-multicast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"overlay-multicast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Overlay Multicast","zh_html":"<a href=\"#overlay-multicast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"overlay-multicast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 覆盖多播(Overlay Multicast)"},{"en":"Brief History of Overlay Multicast","en_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-overlay-multicast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-overlay-multicast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Brief History of Overlay Multicast","zh_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-overlay-multicast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-overlay-multicast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 覆盖多播简史"},{"en":"Recall that IP multicast was developed in the 1990s and 2000s. In the early 2000s, deployment of IP multicast was slow, in part because of the problems we discussed earlier. As a result, many startups emerged, including FastForward Networks (Berkeley), ProxyNet (Berkeley), Sightpath (MIT), and Akamai (MIT). Their work was largely independent, but their solutions all used the same fundamental idea of overlay-based multicast.","en_html":"Recall that IP multicast was developed in the 1990s and 2000s. In the early 2000s, deployment of IP multicast was slow, in part because of the problems we discussed earlier. As a result, many startups emerged, including FastForward Networks (Berkeley), ProxyNet (Berkeley), Sightpath (MIT), and Akamai (MIT). Their work was largely independent, but their solutions all used the same fundamental idea of overlay-based multicast.","zh_html":"回顾一下，IP 多播是在 1990 年代和 2000 年代开发的。在 2000 年代初期，IP 多播的部署进展缓慢，部分原因是我们之前讨论过的问题。因此，涌现了许多初创公司，包括 FastForward Networks（伯克利）、ProxyNet（伯克利）、Sightpath（MIT）和 Akamai（MIT）。它们的工作很大程度上是独立的，但它们的解决方案都使用了相同的基本思想——基于覆盖的多播(overlay-based multicast)。"},{"en":"Overlay Multicast: Definition","en_html":"<a href=\"#overlay-multicast-definition\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"overlay-multicast-definition\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Overlay Multicast: Definition","zh_html":"<a href=\"#overlay-multicast-definition\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"overlay-multicast-definition\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 覆盖多播：定义"},{"en":"Recall one of our major problems with IP multicast: It’s difficult to implement multicast across different networks. In this diagram, if all the hosts are members of the same group, it’s difficult for routers across different networks to coordinate and send packets to the entire group.","en_html":"Recall one of our major problems with IP multicast: It’s difficult to implement multicast across different networks. In this diagram, if all the hosts are members of the same group, it’s difficult for routers across different networks to coordinate and send packets to the entire group.","zh_html":"回顾一下 IP 多播的一个主要问题：跨不同网络实现多播很困难。在这张图中，如果所有主机都是同一个组的成员，不同网络中的路由器很难协调并向整个组发送分组。"},{"en":"Our solution here is to build a virtual network topology that directly connects the hosts to each other:","en_html":"Our solution here is to build a <em>virtual network topology</em> that directly connects the hosts to each other:","zh_html":"我们的解决方案是构建一个虚拟网络拓扑(virtual network topology)，直接将主机彼此连接起来："},{"en":"The virtual links we’ve drawn here are a fiction, and they don’t actually correspond to physical links in real life. For example, if A wanted to send a packet to D along that virtual link, the packet would still have to travel across several real routers and links.","en_html":"The virtual links we’ve drawn here are a fiction, and they don’t actually correspond to physical links in real life. For example, if A wanted to send a packet to D along that virtual link, the packet would still have to travel across several real routers and links.","zh_html":"我们在这里画的虚拟链接(virtual links)是一种抽象，它们在现实生活中并不对应实际的物理链接。例如，如果 A 想沿着虚拟链接向 D 发送一个分组，该分组仍然需要经过多个真实的路由器和链路。"},{"en":"However, by drawing these virtual links, we can now pretend that all the hosts are connected to each other in a small local network. These hosts can then run multicast routing algorithms to forward packets between each other.","en_html":"However, by drawing these virtual links, we can now pretend that all the hosts are connected to each other in a small local network. These hosts can then run multicast routing algorithms to forward packets between each other.","zh_html":"然而，通过绘制这些虚拟链接，我们现在可以假装所有主机都连接在一个小型本地网络中。这些主机可以运行多播路由算法来在彼此之间转发分组。"},{"en":"For example, we could build a core-based tree rooted at D, using the virtual links. Then, everyone can multicast packets by broadcasting packets along the virtual links of the tree.","en_html":"For example, we could build a core-based tree rooted at D, using the virtual links. Then, everyone can multicast packets by broadcasting packets along the virtual links of the tree.","zh_html":"例如，我们可以使用虚拟链接构建一棵以 D 为根的核心基干树。然后，每个人都可以通过沿着树的虚拟链接广播分组来进行多播。"},{"en":"Remember that when a packet is sent along a virtual link, it still has to travel across several real routers and links. For example, if A wants to forward a packet to both D and B, it must send two unicast packets: “From A, To D,” and “From A, To B.” Both of these unicast packets will travel along several real routers and links to reach their destination.","en_html":"Remember that when a packet is sent along a virtual link, it still has to travel across several real routers and links. For example, if A wants to forward a packet to both D and B, it must send two unicast packets: “From A, To D,” and “From A, To B.” Both of these unicast packets will travel along several real routers and links to reach their destination.","zh_html":"记住，当分组沿着虚拟链接发送时，它仍然需要经过许多真实的路由器和链路。例如，如果 A 想向 D 和 B 都转发一个分组，它必须发送两个单播分组：“从 A 到 D”和“从 A 到 B。”这两个单播分组都会经过多个真实的路由器和链路才能到达目的地。"},{"en":"In the example below, A is sending a unicast packet to G. The packet travels along several real routers and links to reach G. Along the way, intermediate hosts C and D receive and forward the packet.","en_html":"In the example below, A is sending a unicast packet to G. The packet travels along several real routers and links to reach G. Along the way, intermediate hosts C and D receive and forward the packet.","zh_html":"在下面的例子中，A 向 G 发送一个单播分组。该分组经过多个真实的路由器和链路到达 G。沿途，中间主机 C 和 D 接收并转发该分组。"},{"en":"Intuitively, the virtual network gives us the illusion that all the hosts are connected in a small local network, even though they are scattered all around the world in real life.","en_html":"Intuitively, the virtual network gives us the illusion that all the hosts are connected in a small local network, even though they are scattered all around the world in real life.","zh_html":"直观地说，虚拟网络给我们一种错觉，好像所有主机都连接在一个小型本地网络中，尽管它们在现实世界中分散在世界各地。"},{"en":"From a network architecture standpoint, the end hosts (at Layer 7) are now responsible for running multicast protocols. The end hosts are now acting as virtual routers. This means that the end hosts have to build multicast forwarding tables, know about their outgoing virtual links (e.g. in static table entries), and forward packets along virtual links. Routers don’t need to think about multicasting at all (they can just run standard unicast protocols).","en_html":"From a network architecture standpoint, the end hosts (at Layer 7) are now responsible for running multicast protocols. The end hosts are now acting as <strong>virtual routers</strong>. This means that the end hosts have to build multicast forwarding tables, know about their outgoing virtual links (e.g. in static table entries), and forward packets along virtual links. Routers don’t need to think about multicasting at all (they can just run standard unicast protocols).","zh_html":"从网络架构的角度看，终端主机（第 7 层）现在负责运行多播协议。终端主机现在充当虚拟路由器(virtual routers)。这意味着终端主机必须构建多播转发表，了解它们的出向虚拟链接（例如在静态表项中），并沿着虚拟链接转发分组。路由器完全不需要考虑多播（它们只需运行标准的单播协议）。"},{"en":"This is different from IP multicasting, where the routers were responsible for running multicast protocols, and the end hosts didn’t need to think about the protocols (they could just send packets to a group address).","en_html":"This is different from IP multicasting, where the routers were responsible for running multicast protocols, and the end hosts didn’t need to think about the protocols (they could just send packets to a group address).","zh_html":"这与 IP 多播不同，在 IP 多播中，路由器负责运行多播协议，终端主机不需要考虑这些协议（它们只需向组地址发送分组）。"},{"en":"The virtual links we’ve drawn form the overlay network. The end hosts (virtual routers) in the overlay network talk to each other to run a multicast routing algorithm. The overlay routing tables are based on the virtual links (e.g. B’s table might say, if I receive packets from A, forward them to C and D).","en_html":"The virtual links we’ve drawn form the <strong>overlay network</strong>. The end hosts (virtual routers) in the overlay network talk to each other to run a multicast routing algorithm. The overlay routing tables are based on the virtual links (e.g. B’s table might say, if I receive packets from A, forward them to C and D).","zh_html":"我们画的虚拟链接构成了覆盖网络(overlay network)。覆盖网络中的终端主机（虚拟路由器）彼此通信以运行多播路由算法。覆盖路由表基于虚拟链接（例如 B 的路由表可能说：如果我从 A 收到分组，就转发给 C 和 D）。"},{"en":"The real links and routers responsible for sending packets along virtual links form the underlay network. The underlay network routers talk to each other to run the standard unicast routing algorithms (e.g. distance-vector, BGP). The underlay routing tables are based on the physical links (e.g. R1’s table might say, the next-hop to G is R2).","en_html":"The real links and routers responsible for sending packets along virtual links form the <strong>underlay network</strong>. The underlay network routers talk to each other to run the standard unicast routing algorithms (e.g. distance-vector, BGP). The underlay routing tables are based on the physical links (e.g. R1’s table might say, the next-hop to G is R2).","zh_html":"负责沿着虚拟链接发送分组的真实链路和路由器构成了底层网络(underlay network)。底层网络中的路由器彼此通信以运行标准的单播路由算法（例如距离向量、BGP）。底层路由表基于物理链接（例如 R1 的路由表可能说：到 G 的下一跳是 R2）。"},{"en":"To implement the overlay and underlay networks, we’ll use encapsulation. Suppose we want to multicast a packet to the group address. Then the inner header (overlay) would say “From A, To G1,” and the hosts would read this overlay packet to decide how to forward packets.","en_html":"To implement the overlay and underlay networks, we’ll use encapsulation. Suppose we want to multicast a packet to the group address. Then the inner header (overlay) would say “From A, To G1,” and the hosts would read this overlay packet to decide how to forward packets.","zh_html":"为了实现覆盖网络和底层网络，我们将使用封装(encapsulation)。假设我们要向组地址多播一个分组。那么内层头部（覆盖层）会说“从 A 到 G1，”主机会读取这个覆盖层分组来决定如何转发。"},{"en":"Suppose Host A decides that this packet needs to be forwarded along the virtual link C. Then Host A will encapsulate this packet with an outer header “From A, To C” and unicast this packet to C. The underlay is responsible for forwarding the unicast packet from A to C, using only the outer header.","en_html":"Suppose Host A decides that this packet needs to be forwarded along the virtual link C. Then Host A will encapsulate this packet with an outer header “From A, To C” and unicast this packet to C. The underlay is responsible for forwarding the unicast packet from A to C, using only the outer header.","zh_html":"假设主机 A 决定该分组需要沿着到 C 的虚拟链接转发。那么主机 A 会用外层头部“从 A 到 C”封装这个分组，并将这个单播分组发送给 C。底层网络负责将单播分组从 A 转发到 C，只使用外层头部。"},{"en":"Implementing Overlay Networks","en_html":"<a href=\"#implementing-overlay-networks\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"implementing-overlay-networks\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Implementing Overlay Networks","zh_html":"<a href=\"#implementing-overlay-networks\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"implementing-overlay-networks\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 实现覆盖网络"},{"en":"In the most basic model, the nodes in the overlay network are the end hosts (e.g. your personal laptop). This means that the end hosts need to understand the multicast routing protocol, build their own forwarding tables, and forward packets.","en_html":"In the most basic model, the nodes in the overlay network are the end hosts (e.g. your personal laptop). This means that the end hosts need to understand the multicast routing protocol, build their own forwarding tables, and forward packets.","zh_html":"在最基本的模型中，覆盖网络中的节点就是终端主机（例如你的个人笔记本电脑）。这意味着终端主机需要理解多播路由协议，构建自己的转发表，并转发分组。"},{"en":"The end hosts could also be proxy servers installed by some company (similar to CDN servers). These machines are still end hosts running multicast routing protocols, but instead of being actual user machines (e.g. your personal laptop), they are deployed solely to help support multicast routing. Note that these proxy servers are still end hosts running multicast routing in the overlay. These servers still need to encapsulate packets and unicast them through the underlay network, so these servers are not Layer 3 routers.","en_html":"The end hosts could also be proxy servers installed by some company (similar to CDN servers). These machines are still end hosts running multicast routing protocols, but instead of being actual user machines (e.g. your personal laptop), they are deployed solely to help support multicast routing. Note that these proxy servers are still end hosts running multicast routing in the overlay. These servers still need to encapsulate packets and unicast them through the underlay network, so these servers are not Layer 3 routers.","zh_html":"终端主机也可以是某个公司安装的代理服务器(proxy servers)（类似于 CDN 服务器）。这些机器仍然是运行多播路由协议的终端主机，但它们不是实际的用户机器（例如你的个人笔记本电脑），而是专门部署来帮助支持多播路由的。注意，这些代理服务器仍然是在覆盖网络中运行多播路由的终端主机。这些服务器仍然需要封装分组并通过底层网络单播它们，因此这些服务器不是第 3 层路由器。"},{"en":"The general idea of overlay networks can be also used for other purposes besides multicast. For example, packets could be unicast across the overlay network as well. You could build a peer-to-peer file sharing service using an overlay topology. (A peer-to-peer service is one where any user in the group can share a file with any other user, without relying on a central server storing all the files.)","en_html":"The general idea of overlay networks can be also used for other purposes besides multicast. For example, packets could be unicast across the overlay network as well. You could build a peer-to-peer file sharing service using an overlay topology. (A peer-to-peer service is one where any user in the group can share a file with any other user, without relying on a central server storing all the files.)","zh_html":"覆盖网络的总体思路也可以用于多播之外的其他目的。例如，分组也可以在覆盖网络中单播。你可以使用覆盖拓扑构建一个对等网络文件共享服务(peer-to-peer file sharing service)。（对等网络服务是指组中的任何用户都可以与任何其他用户共享文件，而不依赖于存储所有文件的中央服务器。）"},{"en":"Many overlay networks can co-exist at the same time, over the same underlay network. From the end host’s perspective, the end host would be running two separate applications. Each application has its own separate forwarding table, list of neighboring links, and so on. Each application could be offering a different service.","en_html":"Many overlay networks can co-exist at the same time, over the same underlay network. From the end host’s perspective, the end host would be running two separate applications. Each application has its own separate forwarding table, list of neighboring links, and so on. Each application could be offering a different service.","zh_html":"多个覆盖网络可以在同一底层网络之上同时共存。从终端主机的角度来看，该终端主机会运行两个独立的应用程序。每个应用程序有自己的独立转发表、邻居链接列表等等。每个应用程序可以提供不同的服务。"},{"en":"Benefits of Overlay Multicast","en_html":"<a href=\"#benefits-of-overlay-multicast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"benefits-of-overlay-multicast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Benefits of Overlay Multicast","zh_html":"<a href=\"#benefits-of-overlay-multicast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"benefits-of-overlay-multicast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 覆盖多播的优势"},{"en":"What’s good about the overlay multicast approach?","en_html":"What’s good about the overlay multicast approach?","zh_html":"覆盖多播方法有什么优点？"},{"en":"The biggest benefit is ease of deployment. From the perspective of the underlay routers, the overlay network is just another application sending and receiving unicast packets. The underlay routers and protocols don’t need any modifications.","en_html":"The biggest benefit is ease of deployment. From the perspective of the underlay routers, the overlay network is just another application sending and receiving unicast packets. The underlay routers and protocols don’t need any modifications.","zh_html":"最大的优势是易于部署。从底层路由器的角度来看，覆盖网络只是另一个发送和接收单播分组的应用程序。底层路由器和协议不需要任何修改。"},{"en":"IP multicast required most or all of the routers to understand multicast protocols. By contrast, in overlay multicast, only certain participating nodes (e.g. the users in the group) need to understand the protocol. All of the other end hosts don’t need any modifications.","en_html":"IP multicast required most or all of the routers to understand multicast protocols. By contrast, in overlay multicast, only certain participating nodes (e.g. the users in the group) need to understand the protocol. All of the other end hosts don’t need any modifications.","zh_html":"IP 多播要求大部分或所有路由器理解多播协议。相比之下，在覆盖多播中，只有某些参与的节点（例如组中的用户）需要理解该协议。所有其他终端主机不需要任何修改。"},{"en":"Each overlay multicast application can use its own implementation or protocol, so there’s no need for standardization between different applications (e.g. different groups). Contrast this with IP multicast, where all routers need to speak the same protocol so that they can coordinate with each other.","en_html":"Each overlay multicast application can use its own implementation or protocol, so there’s no need for standardization between different applications (e.g. different groups). Contrast this with IP multicast, where all routers need to speak the same protocol so that they can coordinate with each other.","zh_html":"每个覆盖多播应用可以使用自己的实现或协议，因此不同应用（例如不同组）之间不需要标准化。相比之下，IP 多播要求所有路由器使用相同的协议，以便它们能够相互协调。"},{"en":"Because each overlay multicast application can make its own implementation decisions, this approach also gives applications the freedom to define their own goals.","en_html":"Because each overlay multicast application can make its own implementation decisions, this approach also gives applications the freedom to define their own goals.","zh_html":"因为每个覆盖多播应用可以自己做实现决策，这种方法也给了应用定义自己目标的自由。"},{"en":"Each application can decide how to draw their virtual topology, how to set their link costs, and how to compute paths through the network. Some groups might care more about latency, while other groups might care more about throughput.","en_html":"Each application can decide how to draw their virtual topology, how to set their link costs, and how to compute paths through the network. Some groups might care more about latency, while other groups might care more about throughput.","zh_html":"每个应用可以决定如何绘制它们的虚拟拓扑，如何设置链路成本，以及如何计算通过网络的路由。有些组可能更关心延迟(latency)，而其他组可能更关心吞吐量(throughput)。"},{"en":"Access control is also easier in overlay multicast (compared to IP multicast). The routing protocol implementation can be customized to only allow authorized users to participate in the protocol. Each application can make their own decision about what it means to authorize a user.","en_html":"Access control is also easier in overlay multicast (compared to IP multicast). The routing protocol implementation can be customized to only allow authorized users to participate in the protocol. Each application can make their own decision about what it means to authorize a user.","zh_html":"访问控制(access control)在覆盖多播中也更容易（与 IP 多播相比）。路由协议实现可以被定制为只允许授权用户参与协议。每个应用可以自己决定什么是授权用户。"},{"en":"Each application can also decide its own business model. The routing protocol implementation can be customized to track usage and charge users accordingly, and each application can make their own decision about what it means to track usage. For example, an overlay network of CDN servers might be used to stream a sports game to millions of users. The application itself can track which users are watching the sports game, and charge them accordingly.","en_html":"Each application can also decide its own business model. The routing protocol implementation can be customized to track usage and charge users accordingly, and each application can make their own decision about what it means to track usage. For example, an overlay network of CDN servers might be used to stream a sports game to millions of users. The application itself can track which users are watching the sports game, and charge them accordingly.","zh_html":"每个应用还可以决定自己的商业模式。路由协议实现可以被定制为跟踪使用情况并相应地向用户收费，每个应用可以自己决定什么是使用情况跟踪。例如，一个 CDN 服务器的覆盖网络可能被用来向数百万用户流式传输体育比赛。应用本身可以跟踪哪些用户在观看体育比赛，并相应地向他们收费。"},{"en":"More unusual business models also exist. For example, a peer-to-peer file sharing system might be used to illegally stream copyrighted material. This system might want to avoid tracking users to avoid getting users in trouble.","en_html":"More unusual business models also exist. For example, a peer-to-peer file sharing system might be used to illegally stream copyrighted material. This system might want to avoid tracking users to avoid getting users in trouble.","zh_html":"也存在更不寻常的商业模式。例如，一个对等文件共享系统可能被用来非法流式传输受版权保护的内容。这个系统可能希望避免跟踪用户，以防止用户惹上麻烦。"},{"en":"Overlay Multicast Performance","en_html":"<a href=\"#overlay-multicast-performance\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"overlay-multicast-performance\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Overlay Multicast Performance","zh_html":"<a href=\"#overlay-multicast-performance\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"overlay-multicast-performance\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 覆盖多播的性能"},{"en":"The performance of an overlay network is highly dependent on the virtual topology that you draw between end hosts. In particular, the links and costs in the virtual topology should accurately reflect the corresponding underlay topology.","en_html":"The performance of an overlay network is highly dependent on the virtual topology that you draw between end hosts. In particular, the links and costs in the virtual topology should accurately reflect the corresponding underlay topology.","zh_html":"覆盖网络的性能高度依赖于你在终端主机之间绘制的虚拟拓扑。特别是，虚拟拓扑中的链路和成本应该准确反映相应的底层拓扑。"},{"en":"For example, this overlay network topology closely matches the corresponding underlay topology.","en_html":"For example, this overlay network topology closely matches the corresponding underlay topology.","zh_html":"例如，这个覆盖网络拓扑与相应的底层拓扑紧密匹配。"},{"en":"The virtual link from A to C could be assigned a low cost because in reality, A and C are close to each other (the underlay path passes through 3 routers). The virtual link from D to G could be assigned a high cost because in reality, D and G are further away (the underlay path passes through 5 routers). If we compute shortest paths in the overlay topology, the resulting paths should be pretty similar to the shortest paths in the underlay topology. Having short paths through the underlay is desirable, since the packets are ultimately being forwarded through the underlay network.","en_html":"The virtual link from A to C could be assigned a low cost because in reality, A and C are close to each other (the underlay path passes through 3 routers). The virtual link from D to G could be assigned a high cost because in reality, D and G are further away (the underlay path passes through 5 routers). If we compute shortest paths in the overlay topology, the resulting paths should be pretty similar to the shortest paths in the underlay topology. Having short paths through the underlay is desirable, since the packets are ultimately being forwarded through the underlay network.","zh_html":"从 A 到 C 的虚拟链接可以被分配一个低成本，因为实际上 A 和 C 彼此靠近（底层路径经过 3 个路由器）。从 D 到 G 的虚拟链接可以被分配一个高成本，因为实际上 D 和 G 相距较远（底层路径经过 5 个路由器）。如果我们在覆盖拓扑中计算最短路径，得到的路径应该与底层拓扑中的最短路径非常相似。通过底层网络的短路径是理想的，因为分组最终是通过底层网络转发的。"},{"en":"In this particular overlay topology, packets from A to G end up getting forwarded along a path that is pretty close to the shortest path.","en_html":"In this particular overlay topology, packets from A to G end up getting forwarded along a path that is pretty close to the shortest path.","zh_html":"在这个特定的覆盖拓扑中，从 A 到 G 的分组最终沿着一条非常接近最短路径的路径传输。"},{"en":"Here is an example of an overlay network that poorly models the corresponding underlay topology.","en_html":"Here is an example of an overlay network that poorly models the corresponding underlay topology.","zh_html":"这是一个覆盖网络未能良好模拟相应底层拓扑的例子。"},{"en":"Notice that we didn’t change anything about the underlay topology. We only changed the placements of the virtual links.","en_html":"Notice that we didn’t change anything about the underlay topology. We only changed the placements of the virtual links.","zh_html":"注意，我们没有对底层拓扑做任何改变。我们只是改变了虚拟链接的放置。"},{"en":"In this particular overlay topology, if we tried to compute the shortest path from A to G, we would get the path from A to C to B to E to F to G. If we then sent packets along this path, packets from A to G end up getting forwarded along a much worse path in the underlay network.","en_html":"In this particular overlay topology, if we tried to compute the shortest path from A to G, we would get the path from A to C to B to E to F to G. If we then sent packets along this path, packets from A to G end up getting forwarded along a much worse path in the underlay network.","zh_html":"在这个特定的覆盖拓扑中，如果我们尝试计算从 A 到 G 的最短路径，我们会得到从 A 到 C 到 B 到 E 到 F 到 G 的路径。如果我们随后沿着这条路径发送分组，从 A 到 G 的分组最终会在底层网络中沿着一条更糟糕的路径传输。"},{"en":"To measure the performance of an overlay network, we can define the stretch factor. This is the ratio of the underlay path cost to the overlay path cost.","en_html":"To measure the performance of an overlay network, we can define the <strong>stretch factor</strong>. This is the ratio of the underlay path cost to the overlay path cost.","zh_html":"为了衡量覆盖网络的性能，我们可以定义伸展因子(stretch factor)。这是底层路径成本与覆盖路径成本的比值。"},{"en":"In the example above, the underlay cost is 4, and the overlay cost is 1, which gives us a stretch factor of 4. To better model the underlay network, it might make more sense to assign the virtual link a cost of 4.","en_html":"In the example above, the underlay cost is 4, and the overlay cost is 1, which gives us a stretch factor of 4. To better model the underlay network, it might make more sense to assign the virtual link a cost of 4.","zh_html":"在上面的例子中，底层成本是 4，覆盖成本是 1，得出的伸展因子是 4。为了更好地模拟底层网络，将虚拟链接分配为成本 4 可能更有意义。"},{"en":"High stretch values are bad, because it means that the underlay path is many times longer than the corresponding overlay path. Ideally, we’d like to have stretch values that are lower (closer to 1), which means that our underlay path cost is roughly the same as the overlay path cost.","en_html":"High stretch values are bad, because it means that the underlay path is many times longer than the corresponding overlay path. Ideally, we’d like to have stretch values that are lower (closer to 1), which means that our underlay path cost is roughly the same as the overlay path cost.","zh_html":"高伸展值是不好的，因为它意味着底层路径比相应的覆盖路径长很多倍。理想情况下，我们希望伸展值较低（接近 1），这意味着我们的底层路径成本与覆盖路径成本大致相同。"},{"en":"How do we build low-stretch overlay topologies? Sometimes, operators manually design the topologies.","en_html":"How do we build low-stretch overlay topologies? Sometimes, operators manually design the topologies.","zh_html":"我们如何构建低伸展的覆盖拓扑？有时，运维人员手动设计拓扑。"},{"en":"Self-organizing protocols also exist for automatically discovering a good overlay topology. At a high level, a self-organizing protocol might work like this: Initially, your neighbors are selected at random (i.e. draw virtual links from you to random neighbors). Periodically, you search for new candidate neighbors, and measure your distance to those new candidate neighbors (e.g. send a packet and measure the round-trip time). If the best candidate neighbor outperforms your worst current neighbor, then abandon your worst current neighbor (delete the virtual link) and add the best candidate neighbor (add a new virtual link).","en_html":"Self-organizing protocols also exist for automatically discovering a good overlay topology. At a high level, a self-organizing protocol might work like this: Initially, your neighbors are selected at random (i.e. draw virtual links from you to random neighbors). Periodically, you search for new candidate neighbors, and measure your distance to those new candidate neighbors (e.g. send a packet and measure the round-trip time). If the best candidate neighbor outperforms your worst current neighbor, then abandon your worst current neighbor (delete the virtual link) and add the best candidate neighbor (add a new virtual link).","zh_html":"也存在自组织(self-organizing)协议，用于自动发现良好的覆盖拓扑。在高层面上，自组织协议可能这样工作：最初，你的邻居是随机选择的（即绘制从你到随机邻居的虚拟链接）。定期地，你搜索新的候选邻居，并测量你到这些候选邻居的距离（例如发送一个分组并测量往返时间）。如果最佳的候选邻居比你当前最差的邻居表现更好，那么放弃你最差的当前邻居（删除虚拟链接）并添加最佳的候选邻居（添加新的虚拟链接）。"},{"en":"Drawbacks of Overlay Multicast","en_html":"<a href=\"#drawbacks-of-overlay-multicast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"drawbacks-of-overlay-multicast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Drawbacks of Overlay Multicast","zh_html":"<a href=\"#drawbacks-of-overlay-multicast\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"drawbacks-of-overlay-multicast\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 覆盖多播的缺点"},{"en":"Overlay multicast introduces additional overhead, which affects performance. For example, extra time and processing power is needed to encapsulate and decapsulate packets.","en_html":"Overlay multicast introduces additional overhead, which affects performance. For example, extra time and processing power is needed to encapsulate and decapsulate packets.","zh_html":"覆盖多播引入了额外的开销，这会影响性能。例如，封装和解封分组需要额外的时间和计算能力。"},{"en":"Overlay multicast is not built into the Internet, which means that application developers must implement overlay multicast themselves. Contrast this with IP multicast, where the application developer can just send packets to group addresses, without having to build their own forwarding tables and so on.","en_html":"Overlay multicast is not built into the Internet, which means that application developers must implement overlay multicast themselves. Contrast this with IP multicast, where the application developer can just send packets to group addresses, without having to build their own forwarding tables and so on.","zh_html":"覆盖多播没有被内置于互联网中，这意味着应用程序开发人员必须自己实现覆盖多播。相比之下，在 IP 多播中，应用程序开发人员只需向组地址发送分组，无需自行构建转发表等。"},{"en":"Despite these drawbacks, overlay multicast has good enough performance that it is commonly deployed in the Internet today.","en_html":"Despite these drawbacks, overlay multicast has good enough performance that it is commonly deployed in the Internet today.","zh_html":"尽管存在这些缺点，覆盖多播的性能足够好，以至于它目前已经广泛部署在互联网上。"}]},"/datacenter/datacenter-addressing.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Datacenter Addressing","zh_html":"数据中心寻址(datacenter addressing)"},{"en":"Why are Datacenters Different?","zh_html":"为什么数据中心(datacenters)不同？"},{"en":"In the previous section, we saw that we can modify distance-vector and link-state routing protocols to compute all paths through the datacenter network.","zh_html":"在上一节中，我们看到可以修改距离向量(distance-vector)和链路状态(link-state)路由协议(routing protocols)，让它们计算穿过数据中心网络(datacenter network)的所有路径(paths)。"},{"en":"However, these protocols might scale poorly in datacenters. In distance-vector protocols, we have to make an announcement for every destination, which means that 100,000+ destinations have to be advertised. In link-state protocols, we have to flood advertisements along every link, which scales poorly in Clos networks with a huge number of links. Also, recall that datacenter topologies often use cheap commodity switches, which have limited memory and CPU resources (e.g. the forwarding table can’t be too large).","zh_html":"不过，这些协议(protocols)在数据中心(datacenters)里可能扩展性很差。在距离向量协议(distance-vector protocols)中，我们必须为每个目的地(destination)发布通告(announcement)，这意味着 100,000 个以上的目的地都需要被通告。在链路状态协议(link-state protocols)中，我们必须沿每条链路(link)泛洪(flood)通告；而在拥有大量链路的 Clos 网络(Clos networks)中，这种方式扩展性也不好。另外，回想一下，数据中心拓扑(datacenter topologies)通常使用便宜的商用交换机(commodity switches)，它们的内存(memory)和 CPU 资源有限，例如转发表(forwarding table)不能太大。"},{"en":"In general-purpose networks, we solved these scaling problems by introducing hierarchical IP addressing. Higher-level organizations (e.g. country-level) could allocate ranges of addresses to smaller organizations (e.g. universities). Datacenters don’t have geographic and organizational hierarchies that we can use to organize addresses.","zh_html":"在通用网络(general-purpose networks)中，我们通过引入层次化 IP 寻址(hierarchical IP addressing)解决这些扩展性问题。较高层级的组织，例如国家级组织，可以把地址范围(address ranges)分配给较小的组织，例如大学。数据中心(datacenters)没有可用来组织地址的地理层次(geographic hierarchies)或组织层次(organizational hierarchies)。"},{"en":"However, in datacenters, we can exploit the fact that the operator controls the physical topology of the network, and assign addresses to servers based on where they’re located in the building. We can also exploit the fact that the topology has some regular structure (e.g. we’re probably organizing servers in rows, instead of randomly stuffing them in the building).","zh_html":"不过在数据中心(datacenters)中，我们可以利用一个事实：运营方(operator)控制着网络的物理拓扑(physical topology)，因此可以根据服务器(server)在建筑中的位置为它们分配地址。我们还可以利用拓扑(topology)具有某种规则结构这一点，例如服务器通常按行排列，而不是随机塞进建筑里。"},{"en":"Topology-Aware Addressing","zh_html":"拓扑感知寻址(topology-aware addressing)"},{"en":"In this particular topology, the racks are physically organized into separate pods in the building. One natural approach would be to allocate a range of addresses to each pod. Then, each pod can allocate sub-ranges to each rack in the pod. Finally, each rack can allocate an individual IP address to each server.","zh_html":"在这个特定拓扑(topology)中，机架(racks)在建筑内被物理组织成不同的 pod。一种自然做法是给每个 pod 分配一个地址范围(address range)。然后，每个 pod 可以把子范围(sub-ranges)分配给其中的每个机架。最后，每个机架再为其中的每台服务器(server)分配一个单独的 IP 地址(IP address)。"},{"en":"The operator knows how many servers are in each rack, and how many racks are in each pod, so we can use that information to allocate ranges of the appropriate size. For example, a rack could receive a /24 range, which gives that rack 256 addresses for its servers.","zh_html":"运营方(operator)知道每个机架(rack)里有多少服务器(server)，也知道每个 pod 里有多少机架，因此可以用这些信息分配大小合适的地址范围(address ranges)。例如，一个机架可以获得一个 /24 范围，这会给该机架中的服务器提供 256 个地址。"},{"en":"This allocation approach lets aggregate routes and store fewer entries in our forwarding table. For example, consider one of the spine routers at the top of the diagram. This router doesn’t need to remember a path for every single server. Instead, the forwarding table only needs four entries, one for each pod. When a packet arrives, the router checks the first 16 bits to forward the packet to the appropriate pod.","zh_html":"这种分配方法使我们能够聚合路由(aggregate routes)，并在转发表(forwarding table)中存储更少的条目(entries)。例如，考虑图中顶部的某个 spine 路由器(spine router)。这个路由器不需要为每一台服务器记住一条路径(path)。相反，转发表只需要四个条目，每个 pod 一个。当分组(packet)到达时，路由器检查前 16 位(bits)，把分组转发到合适的 pod。"},{"en":"Route aggregation also results in more stability. If a host is added or removed inside a specific rack, the spine router doesn’t need to know. As long as we maintain the same addressing scheme, the existing forwarding table is still correct without any changes. As a result, routing updates usually occur when links and switches fail, but not when hosts fail.","zh_html":"路由聚合(route aggregation)还会带来更好的稳定性(stability)。如果某个机架(rack)内部新增或移除一台主机(host)，spine 路由器(spine router)不需要知道。只要我们保持同样的寻址方案(addressing scheme)，现有转发表(forwarding table)无需修改仍然是正确的。因此，路由更新(routing updates)通常发生在链路(links)或交换机(switches)故障时，而不是主机故障时。"},{"en":"Assigning addresses based on datacenter topology is good for scaling, but there are some limitations. In particular, if we move a server to a different location, we’d have to change its address.","zh_html":"基于数据中心拓扑(datacenter topology)分配地址有利于扩展(scaling)，但也有一些限制。尤其是，如果我们把一台服务器(server)移动到不同位置，就必须改变它的地址(address)。"}]},"/datacenter/datacenter-cc.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Congestion Control in Datacenters","zh_html":"数据中心(datacenters)中的拥塞控制(congestion control)"},{"en":"Why are Datacenters Different?","zh_html":"为什么数据中心(datacenters)不同？"},{"en":"We’ve seen that datacenter networks have additional constraints (e.g. physically in one building, owned by one operator) compared to general-purpose networks. This can lead to special protocols that exploit these special characteristics of the network. In this section, we’ll explore TCP congestion control algorithms that may not work on the general Internet, but are effective in datacenter contexts. This is an active area of research and development.","zh_html":"我们已经看到，相比通用网络(general-purpose networks)，数据中心网络(datacenter networks)有额外约束，例如物理上位于同一栋建筑中、由同一个运营方(operator)拥有。这些约束会催生一些专门利用网络特性的特殊协议(protocols)。本节会探讨一些 TCP 拥塞控制(congestion control)算法：它们在通用互联网(Internet)上未必适用，但在数据中心环境(datacenter contexts)中很有效。这也是一个活跃的研究和开发方向。"},{"en":"First, we should answer: What makes the congestion control different in a datacenter?","zh_html":"首先，我们需要回答：数据中心(datacenter)里的拥塞控制(congestion control)为什么不同？"},{"en":"Recall that packet delay consists of transmission delay (time to signal the bits on the wire, determined by bandwidth), propagation delay (time for bits to travel across wire), and queuing delay.","zh_html":"回想一下，分组延迟(packet delay)由传输延迟(transmission delay，即把比特(bits)发送到链路上的时间，由带宽(bandwidth)决定)、传播延迟(propagation delay，即比特沿链路传播所需时间)以及排队延迟(queuing delay)组成。"},{"en":"In datacenters, transmission delay is usually relatively small (remember, we have high-capacity 10 Gbps links). Propagation delay is also relatively small in datacenters (remember, all the servers are in the same building). As a result, in datacenters, queuing delay is often the dominant source of delay. By contrast, in the wide-area Internet, the propagation delay could be orders of magnitude larger (e.g. packets could have to travel across the country), and is a more common dominating source of delay.","zh_html":"在数据中心(datacenters)中，传输延迟(transmission delay)通常较小；别忘了这里有高容量的 10 Gbps 链路。传播延迟(propagation delay)也相对较小，因为所有服务器(server)都在同一栋建筑里。因此，在数据中心中，排队延迟(queuing delay)往往是主要的延迟来源。相比之下，在广域互联网(wide-area Internet)中，传播延迟可能大几个数量级，例如分组(packet)可能要跨越整个国家传输，因此传播延迟更常成为主导因素。"},{"en":"Recall that TCP congestion control deliberately fills up queues until packets get lost (we detect congestion by checking for loss). The TCP designers had not considered datacenter contexts, where queuing delay can have a much larger impact on performance.","zh_html":"再回想一下，TCP 拥塞控制(TCP congestion control)会有意把队列(queue)填满，直到出现分组丢失(packet loss)；也就是说，它通过检查丢包来检测拥塞(congestion)。TCP 的设计者当时并没有考虑数据中心环境(datacenter contexts)，而在这种环境中，排队延迟(queuing delay)对性能(performance)的影响可能大得多。"},{"en":"The problem of large queues is exacerbated in datacenters, because unlike in the wide-area Internet, most datacenter connections fall into one of two categories. Most connections are mice, which are short and latency-sensitive. For example, a web search query and the results page contain a very small amount of data, but we want to return the results to the user very quickly. By contrast, some connections are elephants, which are large and throughput-sensitive. For example, backing up data from one server to another server requires a long-running connection that sends a lot of data at high throughput.","zh_html":"大队列(large queues)的问题在数据中心(datacenters)中会被放大，因为不同于广域互联网(wide-area Internet)，大多数数据中心连接(connections)会落入两类之一。多数连接是<strong>小流(mice)</strong>：它们很短，并且对延迟(latency)敏感。例如，一次 Web 搜索查询和结果页只包含很少的数据，但我们希望很快把结果返回给用户。相反，有些连接是<strong>大流(elephants)</strong>：它们规模大，并且对吞吐量(throughput)敏感。例如，把数据从一台服务器备份到另一台服务器，需要一个长时间运行的连接，以高吞吐量发送大量数据。"},{"en":"If we run TCP congestion control with these two types of connections, the elephants will increase their rates until the queues are all filled up. Now, any subsequent mice will be stuck in the queues, causing the mice to be delayed.","zh_html":"如果对这两类连接运行 TCP 拥塞控制(TCP congestion control)，大流(elephants)会不断提高发送速率，直到队列(queue)被填满。此后到来的任何小流(mice)都会被卡在队列中，从而产生延迟。"},{"en":"In order to maximize performance for these specific types of connections, datacenter congestion control algorithms must avoid filling up queues. Many datacenter-specific solutions have been developed in recent years.","zh_html":"为了让这些特定类型的连接获得更好性能(performance)，数据中心拥塞控制算法(datacenter congestion control algorithms)必须避免把队列(queue)填满。近年来已经出现了许多面向数据中心的专门方案。"},{"en":"For example, BBR was released by Google in 2016. In this approach, instead of detecting congestion by checking for loss (which requires queues to be full), we instead detect congestion by checking for packet delay.","zh_html":"例如，Google 在 2016 年发布了 BBR。在这种方法中，我们不再通过检查丢包(loss)来检测拥塞(congestion)，因为那要求队列(queue)已经被填满；相反，我们通过检查分组延迟(packet delay)来检测拥塞。"},{"en":"DCTCP: Feedback from Routers","zh_html":"DCTCP：来自路由器(routers)的反馈(feedback)"},{"en":"DCTCP (Datacenter TCP) was released in 2010 by Microsoft, and is now widely used (e.g. implemented in the Linux kernel).","zh_html":"DCTCP(Datacenter TCP，数据中心 TCP)由 Microsoft 在 2010 年发布，现在已经被广泛使用，例如已经在 Linux 内核(kernel)中实现。"},{"en":"Recall that the IP header has an ECN bit, and the router can enable this bit to indicate that it’s congested. When the packet reaches the destination, the ack will also have the ECN bit set, and this tells the sender to slow down.","zh_html":"回想一下，IP 首部(IP header)中有一个 ECN 位(ECN bit)，路由器(router)可以置位这个比特，表示自己发生了拥塞(congested)。当分组(packet)到达目的地(destination)后，确认(ack)中也会带上这个 ECN 位，从而通知发送方(sender)降低速率。"},{"en":"In DCTCP, routers will enable the ECN bit when the queue length exceeds some threshold. This allows senders to detect and adapt to congestion earlier (as the queue is filling up, before the queue gets totally full).","zh_html":"在 DCTCP 中，当队列长度(queue length)超过某个阈值(threshold)时，路由器(routers)会设置 ECN 位。这样发送方(senders)可以更早检测并适应拥塞(congestion)：也就是在队列正在变长、尚未完全填满时就作出反应。"},{"en":"In response to congestion, the sender reduces the rate in proportion to the number of packets with ECN markings. This allows the sender to adapt to congestion more gently. Instead of binary decision (congestion or no congestion), the sender can detect that some congestion might be happening, and slightly decrease the rate to compensate.","zh_html":"作为对拥塞(congestion)的响应，发送方(sender)会按带有 ECN 标记(ECN markings)的分组数量成比例地降低速率。这让发送方可以更温和地适应拥塞。它不再只做二元决策(binary decision)，也就是拥塞或不拥塞；发送方可以察觉到可能正在出现某种程度的拥塞，并略微降低速率作为补偿。"},{"en":"The ECN bit is not very effective in the wide-area Internet because not all routers support it. However, in a datacenter, the operator controls all the switches and can have them all toggle the ECN in a consistent way. In practice, implementing DCTCP at hosts and routers is a relatively small change.","zh_html":"ECN 位(ECN bit)在广域互联网(wide-area Internet)中效果并不很好，因为不是所有路由器(routers)都支持它。不过在数据中心(datacenter)中，运营方(operator)控制所有交换机(switches)，可以让它们以一致的方式设置 ECN。实践中，在主机(hosts)和路由器(routers)上实现 DCTCP 是相对较小的改动。"},{"en":"To measure how DCTCP performs, we can measure flow completion time (FCT), which measures the time between the first byte being sent and the last byte being received. As a benchmark, the ideal FCT is the completion time if we used an omniscient scheduler that had global knowledge of the entire network and all connections. The scheduler could then use that knowledge to optimally schedule flows and allocate bandwidth to flows.","zh_html":"为了衡量 DCTCP 的表现，我们可以测量<strong>流完成时间(flow completion time, FCT)</strong>，也就是从第一个字节(byte)发出到最后一个字节被接收之间的时间。作为基准(benchmark)，理想 FCT 是这样一种完成时间：假设我们使用一个全知调度器(omniscient scheduler)，它拥有整个网络和所有连接(connections)的全局知识，就能利用这些信息最优地调度流(flows)并为流分配带宽(bandwidth)。"},{"en":"This graph shows the normalized FCT, which is a ratio of actual FCT to ideal FCT. This tells us how much worse we’re doing, compared to the ideal congestion control algorithm. We can see that standard TCP congestion control performs 3x worse than ideal, and up to 10x worse than ideal if the load on the network is higher. By contrast, DCTCP performs significantly better than standard TCP congestion control. DCTCP connections are finishing much faster, with less queuing delay.","zh_html":"这张图展示的是归一化 FCT(normalized FCT)，即实际 FCT 与理想 FCT 的比值。它告诉我们，相比理想的拥塞控制算法(congestion control algorithm)，实际表现差了多少。可以看到，标准 TCP 拥塞控制(standard TCP congestion control)比理想情况差 3 倍；如果网络负载(load)更高，最多会差 10 倍。相比之下，DCTCP 明显优于标准 TCP 拥塞控制。DCTCP 连接完成得更快，排队延迟(queuing delay)也更小。"},{"en":"pFabric: Packet Priorities","zh_html":"pFabric：分组优先级(packet priorities)"},{"en":"We saw that the issue in datacenters is that mice can be trapped in queues behind elephants. What if we gave the mice some way to skip to the front of the queue so they could complete faster?","zh_html":"我们看到，数据中心(datacenters)中的问题在于小流(mice)可能被困在大流(elephants)后面的队列(queue)里。如果我们给小流一种跳到队列前面的方式，让它们更快完成，会怎样？"},{"en":"To prioritize mice, we will assign a priority number to every packet. The priority is computed as the remaining flow size (number of unacknowledged bytes). Lower numbers have higher priority.","zh_html":"为了优先处理小流(mice)，我们会给每个分组(packet)分配一个优先级编号(priority number)。优先级按照剩余流大小(remaining flow size，即尚未确认的字节数)计算。数值越小，优先级越高。"},{"en":"With this system, mice packets will be high-priority (flow sizes are very low). Elephants will be low-priority, though the last few bytes in an elephant connection will be higher-priority. This has the effect of prioritizing connections that are almost-done (even if they’re larger elephant connections).","zh_html":"在这个系统中，小流分组(mice packets)会有高优先级，因为流大小(flow size)很小。大流(elephants)通常是低优先级，不过大流连接最后几个字节的优先级会变高。这会产生一种效果：优先处理那些快要完成的连接，即使它们原本是更大的大流连接(elephant connections)。"},{"en":"To implement this idea, recall that IP packet headers have fields to indicate the priority of a packet. In pFabric, each packet carries a single priority number, and switches are modified so that they send the highest-priority packet. If the queue is full, the switch will drop the lowest-priority packet.","zh_html":"为了实现这个想法，回想一下 IP 分组首部(IP packet headers)中有字段可以表示分组(packet)的优先级(priority)。在 pFabric 中，每个分组携带一个单独的优先级编号，交换机(switches)也被修改为发送优先级最高的分组。如果队列(queue)已满，交换机会丢弃优先级最低的分组。"},{"en":"With the priority system in place, senders can now safely transmit and retransmit packets at full line rate, without needing to adjust their rate for congestion control. Senders only need to reduce their rate in cases of extreme loss (e.g. timeout).","zh_html":"有了这套优先级系统(priority system)，发送方(senders)就可以安全地以满线速(full line rate)发送和重传分组(packets)，无需为了拥塞控制(congestion control)而调整速率。只有在极端丢包(loss)的情况下，例如超时(timeout)，发送方才需要降低速率。"},{"en":"If we look at the graph of FCTs again, we see that pFabric performance is even better than DCTCP, and is very close to ideal.","zh_html":"如果再看 FCT 图，我们会发现 pFabric 的性能(performance)甚至比 DCTCP 更好，并且非常接近理想情况。"},{"en":"Why does pFabric work so well? Elephants and mice travel together, and everybody is sending at full line rate, which ensures full utilization of the available bandwidth. We don’t have to waste time on slow start. Also, we can avoid collapses because most of the packets in large elephants are low-priority. The priority system ensures that mice packets still get through the queue with low latency.","zh_html":"为什么 pFabric 效果这么好？大流(elephants)和小流(mice)一起传输，而且所有发送方都以满线速(full line rate)发送，这保证了可用带宽(available bandwidth)被充分利用。我们不必在慢启动(slow start)上浪费时间。同时，由于大流中的大部分分组都是低优先级，我们也可以避免崩溃(collapse)。优先级系统(priority system)保证小流分组仍能以低延迟(low latency)通过队列(queue)。"},{"en":"Implementing this system requires non-trivial changes at both switches and end hosts, and requires full control of both switches and end hosts. Switches need to implement a priority system, and senders need to replace their TCP implementation to send at full line rate. Still, pFabric is a good example of networks (switches) and end hosts cooperating to achieve good performance.","zh_html":"实现这个系统需要对交换机(switches)和端主机(end hosts)都做不小的修改，并且要求完全控制交换机和端主机。交换机需要实现优先级系统(priority system)，发送方需要替换 TCP 实现，以便按满线速(full line rate)发送。即便如此，pFabric 仍是一个很好的例子，说明网络(networks，尤其是交换机)和端主机如何协作以获得良好性能(performance)。"}]},"/datacenter/datacenter-routing.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Datacenter Routing","zh_html":"数据中心路由(datacenter routing)"},{"en":"Why are Datacenters Different?","zh_html":"为什么数据中心(datacenters)不同？"},{"en":"In the previous section, we designed Clos networks, which created many paths between servers. Servers can communicate simultaneously at high bandwidth by using different paths through the network.","zh_html":"在上一节中，我们设计了 Clos 网络(Clos networks)，它在服务器(servers)之间创建了多条路径(paths)。服务器可以使用网络中的不同路径，同时以高带宽(high bandwidth)通信。"},{"en":"What problems occur if we apply our standard routing algorithms to these network topologies?","zh_html":"如果把标准路由算法(standard routing algorithms)应用到这些网络拓扑(network topologies)上，会出现什么问题？"},{"en":"So far, our routing protocols pick a single path between a source and destination. If all our traffic uses the same path, we aren’t taking advantage of all the extra links in the Clos network. Ideally, we’d like to modify our routing protocols so that a packet can use multiple paths between the same endpoints.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们的路由协议(routing protocols)会在源(source)和目的地(destination)之间选择一条路径。如果所有流量(traffic)都使用同一条路径，我们就没有利用 Clos 网络(Clos network)中的额外链路(links)。理想情况下，我们希望修改路由协议，让一个分组(packet)可以在同一组端点(endpoints)之间使用多条路径。"},{"en":"Suppose that A and B have 200 Gbps uplink bandwidth, and the switch-to-switch links have 100 Gbps bandwidth. If all traffic between A and B is forced to take the green path, we’re leaving the red path unused. We could have sent data at full rate, if we allowed packets to take different paths.","zh_html":"假设 A 和 B 的上行带宽(uplink bandwidth)为 200 Gbps，而交换机到交换机(switch-to-switch)链路的带宽为 100 Gbps。如果 A 和 B 之间的所有流量都被迫走绿色路径，我们就把红色路径闲置了。只要允许分组(packets)走不同路径，就本可以按满速率(full rate)发送数据。"},{"en":"Also, if there are multiple simultaneous connections, we’d like those connections to use different paths in order to maximize bandwidth.","zh_html":"此外，如果同时存在多个连接(connections)，我们也希望这些连接使用不同路径(paths)，从而最大化带宽(bandwidth)。"},{"en":"Suppose that all links have 100 Gbps bandwidth. In this example, multiple connections are competing for bandwidth. If the A-B and C-D connections both pick the same path, the R1-R2 and R2-R4 links are overused (200 Gbps on 100 Gbps capacity). We could have sent data at full rate, if A-B and C-D used different paths.","zh_html":"假设所有链路(links)的带宽都是 100 Gbps。在这个例子中，多个连接(connections)正在竞争带宽。如果 A-B 和 C-D 这两个连接都选择同一条路径，R1-R2 和 R2-R4 链路就会被过度使用：100 Gbps 的容量上承载了 200 Gbps。若 A-B 和 C-D 使用不同路径，本可以按满速率(full rate)发送数据。"},{"en":"Equal Cost Multi-Path (ECMP) Routing","zh_html":"等价多路径(ECMP)路由"},{"en":"In equal cost multi-path routing, our goal is to find all of the shortest paths (with equal cost), and load-balance packets across those paths.","zh_html":"在<strong>等价多路径(equal cost multi-path, ECMP)</strong>路由中，我们的目标是找出所有最短路径(shortest paths，且代价相同)，并在这些路径之间对分组(packets)做负载均衡(load-balance)。"},{"en":"If a packet arrives at a router, but there are multiple outgoing links that are all valid shortest paths, which link should the router choose? The router needs some function (think of it like a piece of code) that takes a packet, and outputs a choice of link. The function should properly load-balance traffic across the equal-cost paths.","zh_html":"如果一个分组(packet)到达路由器(router)，但有多条出链路(outgoing links)都是有效的最短路径(shortest paths)，路由器应该选择哪一条？路由器需要某个函数(function，可以把它看作一段代码)，它接收一个分组并输出一个链路选择。这个函数应当在等价路径(equal-cost paths)之间正确地均衡流量(traffic)。"},{"en":"One possible strategy is round-robin. If there are two shortest-path outgoing links, our function could say: send all odd packets along Link 1 and all even packets along Link 2.","zh_html":"一种可能策略是轮转(round-robin)。如果有两条最短路径的出链路，我们的函数可以规定：所有奇数分组(odd packets)走链路 1，所有偶数分组(even packets)走链路 2。"},{"en":"What are some problems with this approach? Equal-cost paths doesn’t necessarily mean all paths have the same latency. (Remember, the costs are defined by the operator using whatever metric they like.) If we send all odd packets along a slow link, and all even packets along a fast link, then the TCP recipient might end up receiving all the even packets before the odd packets. TCP cares about reordering packets, so the recipient would be forced to buffer the even packets until the missing odd packets arrive, resulting in poor performance.","zh_html":"这种方法有什么问题？等价路径(equal-cost paths)并不一定意味着所有路径具有相同延迟(latency)。（记住，代价(costs)由运营方(operator)按照自己选择的度量(metric)定义。）如果我们把所有奇数分组发到慢链路，把所有偶数分组发到快链路，那么 TCP 接收方(recipient)可能会先收到所有偶数分组，再收到奇数分组。TCP 关心分组重排(packet reordering)，所以接收方会被迫缓存偶数分组，直到缺失的奇数分组到达，这会导致性能(performance)很差。"},{"en":"A smarter strategy would involve looking at some of the packet header fields, and using those fields to make some deterministic choice of link. What fields could we look at?","zh_html":"更聪明的策略是查看分组首部(packet header)中的某些字段(fields)，并用这些字段对链路作出确定性的(deterministic)选择。我们可以查看哪些字段？"},{"en":"We could use the destination IP to select between shortest links. (We’re already using the destination IP in routing anyway.) But, what if lots of sources send packets to the same destination? All the packets have the same destination IP, so they all get mapped to the same shortest link. We aren’t load-balancing packets across the various shortest links.","zh_html":"我们可以用目的 IP(destination IP)在最短链路(shortest links)之间选择。（反正路由时已经会使用目的 IP。）但是，如果很多源(source)都向同一个目的地(destination)发送分组，会怎样？所有分组都有相同的目的 IP，因此都会映射到同一条最短链路。这样就没有在多条最短链路之间做负载均衡(load-balancing)。"},{"en":"What if we used the source IP to select between shortest links? We have a similar problem, if one source is sending packets to lost of destinations. All the packets have the same source, so they all get mapped to the same shortest link.","zh_html":"如果改用源 IP(source IP)在最短链路之间选择呢？如果一个源正在向很多目的地发送分组，我们会遇到类似问题。所有分组都有同一个源，因此都会被映射到同一条最短链路。"},{"en":"Instead of looking at only one field, we could look at both the source and destination IP. To load-balance between shortest links, we could hash the source and destination IP and map the resulting hash to a link (similar to how hash tables work). The source and destination IP together contain enough entropy to avoid our problems from earlier, where many connections with the same source or the same destination get mapped to the same link.","zh_html":"与其只看一个字段，我们可以同时查看源 IP(source IP)和目的 IP(destination IP)。为了在最短链路之间做负载均衡(load-balance)，可以对源 IP 和目的 IP 做哈希(hash)，再把哈希结果映射到某条链路，类似哈希表(hash tables)的工作方式。源 IP 和目的 IP 合在一起包含足够的熵(entropy)，可以避免前面的问题：大量具有相同源或相同目的地的连接都被映射到同一条链路。"},{"en":"We still have one more problem: What if there are multiple large connections between the same source and destination? We don’t want all these connections to map to the same link. To solve this, we can additionally look at the source and destination ports in the TCP or UDP header.","zh_html":"还有一个问题：如果同一个源和目的地之间有多个大型连接(large connections)，会怎样？我们不希望这些连接全部映射到同一条链路。为了解决这个问题，还可以查看 TCP 或 UDP 首部(header)中的源端口(source port)和目的端口(destination port)。"},{"en":"More generally, all of the problems we’ve described (reordering in a TCP connection, too many connections on one link) can be solved if we place each connection on a separate link. To uniquely identify a connection, we need a 5-tuple of: (source IP, destination IP, protocol, source port, destination port). Note that we need the protocol to distinguish between TCP and UDP connections using the same IPs/ports. Two packets are part of the same connection if and only if they have the same 5-tuple.","zh_html":"更一般地说，如果把每个连接(connection)放到单独链路上，就能解决我们描述的所有问题，包括 TCP 连接中的重排(reordering)，以及一条链路上连接过多。为了唯一标识一个连接，我们需要一个五元组(5-tuple)：源 IP、目的 IP、协议(protocol)、源端口、目的端口。注意，我们需要协议字段来区分使用相同 IP 和端口的 TCP 与 UDP 连接。当且仅当两个分组具有相同五元组时，它们才属于同一个连接。"},{"en":"By hashing all 5 values, we can ensure packets in the same connection use the same path (avoiding reordering problems), and we can load-balance connections across different paths. This approach is sometimes called per-flow load balancing. Modern commodity routers usually have built-in support to read these 5 values.","zh_html":"通过对这 5 个值全部做哈希(hash)，可以确保同一连接(connection)中的分组(packets)使用同一条路径，从而避免重排问题(reordering problems)，同时又能把连接负载均衡(load-balance)到不同路径上。这种方法有时称为<strong>按流负载均衡(per-flow load balancing)</strong>。现代商用路由器(commodity routers)通常内置支持读取这 5 个值。"},{"en":"Per-flow load balancing ensures that each link is being used by roughly the same number of connections, though it doesn’t account for connections being different sizes. Accounting for connection size is technically possible, though it’s more expensive (routers would have to do more work) without a lot of benefit (per-flow does a pretty good job balancing different-sized connections), so this is not done in practice.","zh_html":"按流负载均衡(per-flow load balancing)可以保证每条链路大致承载相同数量的连接，但它不会考虑连接大小是否不同。技术上可以把连接大小(connection size)也纳入考虑，不过代价更高，因为路由器(routers)必须做更多工作，而收益不大；按流方法已经能相当好地均衡不同大小的连接。因此实践中通常不会这么做。"},{"en":"Multi-Path Distance-Vector Protocols","zh_html":"多路径距离向量协议(multi-path distance-vector protocols)"},{"en":"To maximize bandwidth, we should send packets along different paths, even if they’re going to the same destination (e.g. if the packets are part of different connections). This means we have to modify our routing protocols so that routers learn about all the shortest paths, not just one.","zh_html":"为了最大化带宽(bandwidth)，即使分组(packets)前往同一个目的地(destination)，我们也应该沿不同路径发送它们，例如这些分组属于不同连接(connections)时。这意味着必须修改路由协议(routing protocols)，让路由器(routers)学习所有最短路径(shortest paths)，而不只是其中一条。"},{"en":"In standard distance-vector protocols, if we receive an advertisement for a new path with cost equal to the best-known cost, we don’t accept that new path. But, in order to remember all least-cost paths, we should actually accept that equal-cost path, and store both paths in the forwarding table. In the forwarding table, a destination can now be mapped to multiple next hops, as long as they all have the same minimal cost.","zh_html":"在标准距离向量协议(distance-vector protocols)中，如果收到一条新路径(path)的通告(advertisement)，且其代价(cost)等于当前已知的最优代价，我们不会接受这条新路径。但是，为了记住所有最低代价路径(least-cost paths)，我们实际上应当接受这条等价路径(equal-cost path)，并把两条路径都存入转发表(forwarding table)。在转发表中，只要多个下一跳(next hops)都具有相同的最小代价(minimal cost)，一个目的地现在就可以映射到多个下一跳。"},{"en":"In this example, R1 receives advertisements from both R4 and R3, both advertising that they can reach B in 2 hops. Our forwarding table stores both R4 and R3 as possible next hops, both with equal minimal cost of 3.","zh_html":"在这个例子中，R1 同时收到来自 R4 和 R3 的通告(advertisements)，它们都声明自己可以用 2 跳(hops)到达 B。我们的转发表(forwarding table)会把 R4 和 R3 都存为可能的下一跳(next hops)，二者具有相同的最小代价 3。"},{"en":"When forwarding packets, the router hashes the 5-tuple to forward roughly half the connections to R3, and the other half to R2.","zh_html":"转发分组(packets)时，路由器(router)会对五元组(5-tuple)做哈希(hash)，将大约一半连接(connections)转发到 R3，另一半转发到 R2。"},{"en":"Multi-Path Link-State Protocols","zh_html":"多路径链路状态协议(multi-path link-state protocols)"},{"en":"In link-state protocols, we flood advertisements so that everybody has a full picture of the network. Normally, each node calculates a shortest path to each destination to populate the forwarding table. To support multiple paths, we need each node to instead compute all of the shortest paths for each destination.","zh_html":"在链路状态协议(link-state protocols)中，我们会泛洪(flood)通告(advertisements)，让每个节点(node)都掌握网络的完整图景。通常，每个节点会为每个目的地(destination)计算一条最短路径(shortest path)，并用它填充转发表(forwarding table)。为了支持多路径(multiple paths)，每个节点需要改为为每个目的地计算所有最短路径。"},{"en":"As in the modified distance-vector protocol, the forwarding table can now contain multiple next-hops for a given destination.","zh_html":"和修改后的距离向量协议(distance-vector protocol)一样，转发表(forwarding table)现在可以为某个给定目的地(destination)包含多个下一跳(next-hops)。"}]},"/datacenter/host-networking.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Host Networking","zh_html":"<a href=\"#host-networking\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"host-networking\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 主机网络(host networking)"},{"en":"What is Host Networking?","zh_html":"<a href=\"#what-is-host-networking\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"what-is-host-networking\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 什么是主机网络(host networking)？"},{"en":"Traditionally, the bottleneck of the network is inside the network infrastructure, not at the end hosts. However, in modern high-performance datacenters, as network performance demand continues to increase, the end hosts are struggling to keep up with the demand.","zh_html":"传统上，网络的瓶颈在网络基础设施内部，而不是在端主机(end hosts)。然而，在现代高性能数据中心(high-performance datacenters)中，随着网络性能需求持续增长，端主机越来越难以跟上需求。"},{"en":"In particular, the CPU running network protocols like TCP is no longer able to deliver the high performance that the datacenter needs. CPUs are expensive, and delivering high performance means that the CPU is spending all its time running network protocols, with fewer resources allocated toward running the actual applications.","zh_html":"具体来说，运行网络协议(network protocols)如 TCP 的 CPU 已经无法提供数据中心(datacenter)所需的高性能。CPU 成本很高，而提供高性能意味着 CPU 把所有时间都花在运行网络协议上，分配给实际应用(applications)的资源就更少了。"},{"en":"Also, the actual protocols that we’ve been running, like IP and TCP, are no longer able to meet modern high performance demands.","zh_html":"此外，我们一直在运行的实际协议，如 IP 和 TCP，也再也无法满足现代高性能需求。"},{"en":"To solve these two problems, we turn to host networking, which involves optimizations at the end hosts (as opposed to inside the network).","zh_html":"为了解决这两个问题，我们转向<strong>主机网络(host networking)</strong>，它涉及端主机(end hosts)上的优化，而不是网络内部的优化。"},{"en":"Optimization: Shared Memory in User Space","zh_html":"<a href=\"#optimization-shared-memory-in-user-space\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"optimization-shared-memory-in-user-space\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 优化：用户空间(user space)的共享内存(shared memory)"},{"en":"Recall that at the end host, Layers 1 and 2 are implemented in hardware at the network interface card (NIC). Layers 3 and 4 are implemented in software in the operating system (on the CPU). Layer 7 is the application itself.","zh_html":"回想一下，在端主机(end host)上，第 1 层和第 2 层由网卡(NIC，network interface card)以硬件方式实现。第 3 层和第 4 层由操作系统(operating system)以软件方式在 CPU 上实现。第 7 层是应用(application)本身。"},{"en":"Recall from a prerequisite class (e.g. CS 61C at UC Berkeley) that modern computers are designed with virtual memory, so that each application gets its own dedicated address space, isolated from other applications. In particular, each Layer 7 application gets its own dedicated address space in user space. By contrast, the operating system itself runs in kernel space, which is a special part of memory that applications in user space cannot access.","zh_html":"从先修课程中我们知道，现代计算机采用虚拟内存(virtual memory)设计，每个应用(application)拥有自己专用的地址空间(address space)，与其他应用隔离。具体来说，每个第 7 层应用在<strong>用户空间(user space)</strong>中拥有自己专用的地址空间。相反，操作系统本身运行在<strong>内核空间(kernel space)</strong>中，这是用户空间中的应用无法访问的一块特殊内存区域。"},{"en":"This memory management model means that when we pass packets down the stack to send data, we are constantly copying data from user space to kernel space. Also, when we pass packets up the stack to receive data, we are constantly copying data from kernel space to user space. Copying bits between kernel space and user space is expensive and kind of pointless.","zh_html":"这种内存管理模型意味着，当我们在协议栈中向下传递分组(packets)以发送数据时，需要不断把数据从用户空间(user space)复制到内核空间(kernel space)。同样，当我们在协议栈中向上传递分组以接收数据时，也需要不断把数据从内核空间复制到用户空间。在内核空间和用户空间之间复制比特既昂贵又没有太大意义。"},{"en":"Another problem with this memory management model is, programming in kernel space is difficult. If we wanted to modify TCP and optimize it for our purposes, we would have to reach into the operating system and program at a very low level. Deployment and testing is harder and slower in the kernel space than in the user space.","zh_html":"这种内存管理模型的另一个问题是，在内核空间(kernel space)中编程很困难。如果我们想修改 TCP 并针对自己的需求进行优化，就必须深入操作系统(operating system)，在非常低的层次上编程。在内核空间中部署和测试比在用户空间(user space)中更难、更慢。"},{"en":"To solve these two problems, we can move the networking stack (e.g. Layer 3 and 4 protocols) out of kernel space, and into user space. Now, Layers 3, 4, and 7 can all access a shared address space, and no copying back-and-forth is needed. Also, iterating and innovating in user space is now easier.","zh_html":"为了解决这两个问题，我们可以把网络协议栈(networking stack，如第 3 层和第 4 层协议)移出内核空间(kernel space)，移入用户空间(user space)。现在，第 3、4、7 层都可以访问同一块共享地址空间(shared address space)，不再需要来回复制。而且，在用户空间中迭代和创新也更容易。"},{"en":"Using shared memory in user space helps us eliminate some extraneous work like copying back-and-forth, but it still isn’t enough to make our hosts meet modern performance requirements.","zh_html":"在用户空间(user space)中使用共享内存(shared memory)有助于消除一些额外工作，比如来回复制，但这仍然不足以让我们的主机(hosts)满足现代性能要求。"},{"en":"Optimization: Offloading to NIC","zh_html":"<a href=\"#optimization-offloading-to-nic\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"optimization-offloading-to-nic\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 优化：卸载(offloading)到 NIC"},{"en":"CPUs are not fast enough to run network protocols (e.g. IP, TCP) at modern performance speeds. Also, using CPUs to run network protocols leaves less CPU resources for the applications themselves to use.","zh_html":"CPU 的速度不足以在现代性能水平上运行网络协议(network protocols，如 IP、TCP)。另外，用 CPU 运行网络协议也意味着应用(applications)可用的 CPU 资源变少了。"},{"en":"To solve this problem, we can offload the networking stack out of the CPU (software), and into the NIC (hardware).","zh_html":"为了解决这个问题，我们可以把网络协议栈(networking stack)从 CPU(软件)卸载(offload)到 NIC(硬件)。"},{"en":"The NIC is a natural place for offloading operations. Every packet has to pass through the NIC, so the NIC can do some extra processing and save the CPU from doing that work.","zh_html":"NIC 是卸载(offloading)操作的自然选择。每个分组(packet)都必须经过 NIC，因此 NIC 可以做一些额外处理，从而让 CPU 省去这部分工作。"},{"en":"The network driver is a piece of software in the OS that programs and manages the NIC. The driver provides an API that allows higher-level programs in the OS to interact with the NIC. You can think of the driver as the bridge between hardware and software.","zh_html":"<strong>网络驱动程序(network driver)</strong>是操作系统(OS)中一段用于编程和管理 NIC 的软件。驱动程序提供了一个 API，让操作系统中更高级的程序可以与 NIC 交互。可以把驱动程序看作是硬件和软件之间的桥梁。"},{"en":"What are the benefits of offloading? It frees up CPU resources for the application to use. Also, specialized processing in hardware can be more efficient than processing on general-purpose CPUs. Here, efficiency refers to both speed and power consumption. Finally, running operations in hardware gives us not just lower latency, but also more predictable and consistent latencies. When running applications in software, the CPU has to schedule different processes, which can add unpredictable delay. (For example, if I have a packet to process, the CPU might have to finish its current job before switching over to processing my packet.)","zh_html":"卸载(offloading)的好处是什么？它释放了 CPU 资源供应用(application)使用。此外，硬件中的专用处理可能比通用 CPU 上的处理更高效。这里的效率同时指速度(speed)和功耗(power consumption)。最后，在硬件中运行操作不仅能降低延迟(latency)，还能提供更可预测且一致的延迟。当应用在软件中运行时，CPU 需要调度不同进程(processes)，这可能会引入不可预测的延迟。例如，如果有一个分组需要处理，CPU 可能必须先完成当前任务，然后才能切换到处理我的分组。"},{"en":"Brief History of Offloading: Epoch 0","zh_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-offloading-epoch-0\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-offloading-epoch-0\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 卸载(offloading)简史：时代 0"},{"en":"Offloading operations from the OS (software) to the NIC (hardware) is an active, ongoing area of research. There have been three epochs of development, where increasingly complicated operations have been offloaded to the NIC.","zh_html":"把操作从 OS(软件)卸载(offload)到 NIC(硬件)是一个活跃且持续的研究方向。经历了三个发展阶段，越来越复杂的操作被卸载到 NIC 上。"},{"en":"Epoch 0: Before any offloading, let’s see what the NIC does in the standard networking stack we’ve seen so far.","zh_html":"<strong>时代 0(Epoch 0)</strong>：在卸载之前，让我们看看 NIC 在我们迄今看到的标准网络协议栈中做了什么。"},{"en":"The NIC has a central controller processor that manages operation on the card.","zh_html":"NIC 有一个中央控制器处理器(central controller processor)，负责管理网卡上的操作。"},{"en":"For incoming packets, the transceiver converts electrical signals into digital signals (1s and 0s) and puts those bits in a buffer. Then, the NIC reads bits from the buffer, parses them as Ethernet frames, processes the frame (e.g. verifies checksum), and removes the Layer 2 header. Finally, the NIC generates an interrupt to tell the CPU to stop what it’s doing and collect the resulting Layer 3 packet for further processing.","zh_html":"对于入站分组(incoming packets)，收发器(transceiver)把电信号转换为数字信号(0 和 1)，并将这些比特放入缓冲区(buffer)。然后，NIC 从缓冲区读取比特，解析为以太网帧(Ethernet frames)，处理帧(例如验证校验和)，并移除第 2 层首部(Layer 2 header)。最后，NIC 产生一个中断(interrupt)，告诉 CPU 停下手头工作，来收取生成的第 3 层分组(Layer 3 packet)做进一步处理。"},{"en":"For outgoing packets, packets from the network driver are placed in a buffer. The NIC reads bits from the buffer and processes them to construct Ethernet frames. Then, the frame is passed to a transceiver, which converts the digital bits to electrical signals.","zh_html":"对于出站分组(outgoing packets)，来自网络驱动程序的被放入缓冲区。NIC 从缓冲区读取比特并进行处理，以构建以太网帧(Ethernet frames)。然后，帧被传递给收发器(transceiver)，后者把数字比特转换为电信号。"},{"en":"In the standard networking stack, you can think of the NIC as a doormat that passes incoming packets to the OS, and sends outgoing packets for the OS, but performs very minimal processing on those packets.","zh_html":"在标准网络协议栈中，可以把 NIC 看作一个门垫(doormat)：它把入站分组传递给 OS，把出站分组从 OS 发出，但对这些分组只做非常少的处理。"},{"en":"Brief History of Offloading: Epoch 1","zh_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-offloading-epoch-1\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-offloading-epoch-1\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 卸载(offloading)简史：时代 1"},{"en":"The first operations that we tried to offload to the NIC are simple, stateless operations. These stateless operations can be done independently per packet, and the NIC doesn’t have to remember any state across multiple packets.","zh_html":"我们最早尝试卸载(offload)到 NIC 的操作是简单、无状态(stateless)的操作。这些无状态操作可以独立地按每个分组(packet)完成，NIC 不需要跨多个分组记住任何状态(state)。"},{"en":"One stateless operation we can offload is checksum computations, not just at Layer 2, but also at Layers 3 and 4. The NIC can validate these checksums (for incoming packets) and compute these checksums (for outgoing packets), so that the CPU doesn’t have to.","zh_html":"一个可以卸载的无状态操作是校验和(checksum)计算，不仅在第 2 层，也包括第 3 层和第 4 层。NIC 可以验证(对入站分组)和计算(对出站分组)这些校验和，这样 CPU 就不必做了。"},{"en":"Another stateless operation we can offload is segmentation. In our standard model, if the application has a huge file to send, then the OS is responsible for splitting up the file into smaller packets. Then, at the recipient, the OS is responsible for reassembling those packets. As an optimization, we can make the NIC deal with splitting up and reassembling packets. Now, the OS no longer has to deal with a ton of small packets, and can instead deal with a few large packets, which is more efficient (e.g. fewer headers to process).","zh_html":"另一个可以卸载的无状态操作是分段(segmentation)。在标准模型中，如果应用(application)有一个大文件要发送，OS 负责把这个大文件拆分成更小的分组(packets)。在接收端，OS 负责重组这些分组。作为优化，我们可以让 NIC 来处理分组的分段和重组。现在，OS 不再需要处理大量小分组，而是可以处理少量大分组，这样更高效(例如，需要处理的首部更少)。"},{"en":"With segmentation, there’s a trade-off between smooth connections and CPU efficiency. If the application hands large packets to the NIC, the CPU has less work to do. However, the NIC now gets large bursts of data, and the connection is more bursty. By contrast, if the application hands smaller packets to the NIC, the CPU has more work to do, but the NIC gets a steadier stream of data, and the resulting connection is smoother.","zh_html":"分段(segmentation)在连接流畅度和 CPU 效率之间存在权衡。如果应用(application)把大分组交给 NIC，CPU 的工作量更小。然而，NIC 会收到大的数据突发(bursts)，连接变得更加突发。相反，如果应用把更小的分组交给 NIC，CPU 工作量更大，但 NIC 会得到更稳定的数据流，连接也更平滑。"},{"en":"There are some challenges associated with aggregating small packets. What if an intermediate packet is lost? Then the NIC might have to pass up a bunch of small packets, and is unable to combine them into one big packet. What if some packets have a flag (e.g. ECN for congestion) set, and others don’t? Should the resulting aggregated packet have the flag set or not?","zh_html":"聚合小分组(aggregating small packets)存在一些挑战。如果中间某个分组丢失了怎么办？那么 NIC 可能不得不向上传递一堆小分组，而无法把它们组合成一个大分组。如果一些分组设置了某个标志(flag)，例如 ECN 拥塞标志，而其他分组没有，那么聚合后的分组应该设置还是清除这个标志？"},{"en":"The third stateless operation we’ll look at offloading is multi-queue support. In our standard model, the NIC has one queue for outgoing packets, and one queue for incoming packets, and all applications share these queues. The network driver (in software) was responsible for load balancing, in case multiple applications or multiple CPUs were sending and receiving data.","zh_html":"我们要看的第三个可以卸载的无状态操作是多队列(multi-queue)支持。在标准模型中，NIC 有一个出站队列和一个入站队列，所有应用共享这些队列。网络驱动程序(软件)负责负载均衡(load balancing)，以应对多个应用或多个 CPU 同时发送和接收数据的情况。"},{"en":"We can instead offload this load balancing job to the NIC. Now, the NIC has multiple transmit queues, and multiple receive queues. For example, in a multi-processor system, each CPU can have its own dedicated transmit/receive queues. The NIC maintains all the queues in parallel, ensuring isolation and load-balancing between the different CPUs. The NIC can also prioritize certain queues over others.","zh_html":"我们可以把这个负载均衡(load balancing)任务卸载(offload)到 NIC。现在，NIC 有多个发送队列(transmit queues)和多个接收队列(receive queues)。例如，在多处理器系统中，每个 CPU 可以有自己的专用发送/接收队列。NIC 并行维护所有队列，确保不同 CPU 之间的隔离(isolation)和负载均衡。NIC 还可以对某些队列设置优先级(prioritize)。"},{"en":"Even though the NIC has multiple queues, it ultimately still has to send out all the packets along one wire. Therefore, the NIC needs some packet scheduler to decide which queue to send from next. The scheduler can be programmed to achieve the desired load-balancing behavior (e.g. if we want to prioritize one queue over another).","zh_html":"即使 NIC 有多个队列，它最终仍然需要通过一条线路发送所有分组(packets)。因此，NIC 需要某种分组调度器(packet scheduler)来决定接下来从哪个队列发送。调度器可以被编程以实现期望的负载均衡行为，例如如果我们想优先处理某个队列。"},{"en":"One challenge with multiple queues is mapping packets to queues. When a CPU has some data to send, which queue does it use? In particular, we want to make sure that all the packets within a single flow end up in the same queue (and not spread out across many queues). This helps us ensure that packets in a flow are sent in-order. Recall that in TCP, sending packets out-of-order works, but is bad for performance (e.g. receiver has to buffer out-of-order packets).","zh_html":"多队列(multiple queues)的一个挑战是如何把分组(packets)映射到队列。当 CPU 有数据要发送时，它该使用哪个队列？特别地，我们希望确保同一个流(flow)中的所有分组最终都在同一个队列中，而不是分散到多个队列。这有助于保证流中的分组按序(in-order)发送。回想一下，在 TCP 中，乱序发送也是可行的，但对性能不利，因为接收方需要缓冲乱序分组。"},{"en":"When processing incoming packets from the various receive queues, the NIC can hash the packet to decide which CPU will handle that incoming packet. Then, the NIC interrupts that CPU and tells it to process the packet. The hash-based behavior is similar to ECMP (Equal-Cost Multi-Path Routing), and helps us ensure that all packets in the same flow are processed in order by the same CPU.","zh_html":"当处理来自各个接收队列(receive queues)的入站分组(incoming packets)时，NIC 可以对分组做哈希(hash)，以决定由哪个 CPU 来处理这个入站分组。然后，NIC 中断(interrupt)那个 CPU，告诉它处理这个分组。这种基于哈希的行为类似于 ECMP(等成本多路径路由，Equal-Cost Multi-Path Routing)，有助于确保同一个流中的所有分组由同一个 CPU 按序处理。"},{"en":"Brief History of Offloading: Epoch 2","zh_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-offloading-epoch-2\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-offloading-epoch-2\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 卸载(offloading)简史：时代 2"},{"en":"Later, we started to offload more complicated, stateful operations to the NIC.","zh_html":"后来，我们开始把更复杂的、有状态(stateful)的操作卸载(offload)到 NIC。"},{"en":"The development of Epoch 2 has been driven by virtualization in datacenters, where multiple virtual machines run on the same physical server. For example, in virtualization, we needed a virtual switch to forward incoming packets to the appropriate VM. We showed the virtual switch running in software, but the virtual switch could also be implemented in hardware.","zh_html":"时代 2 的发展由数据中心(datacenters)中的虚拟化(virtualization)驱动，多台虚拟机(virtual machines)在同一台物理服务器(physical server)上运行。例如，在虚拟化中，我们需要一个虚拟交换机(virtual switch)来把入站分组(incoming packets)转发到相应的 VM。我们之前展示的虚拟交换机运行在软件中，但虚拟交换机也可以在硬件中实现。"},{"en":"Firewalls and bandwidth management are another example of a stateful offload. In software, we can implement a firewall that enforces security policies (e.g. drop all incoming packets from this malicious IP). We can also enforce policies to manage bandwidth between users (e.g. User A can only send 100 packets per minute, any excess is dropped). These security policies could be checked by hardware instead.","zh_html":"防火墙(firewalls)和带宽管理(bandwidth management)是有状态卸载(stateful offload)的另一个例子。在软件中，我们可以实现防火墙来执行安全策略(security policies)，例如丢弃来自某个恶意 IP 的所有入站分组。我们还可以实施策略来管理用户之间的带宽，例如用户 A 每分钟只能发送 100 个分组，超出部分被丢弃。这些安全策略也可以由硬件来检查。"},{"en":"To implement these stateful operations, we can use a match-action pair table, similar to the OpenFlow tables (from the SDN section). This API allows the software to program different policies onto the hardware, so that the hardware can process packets according to those policies. As we saw earlier, the match could be against a 5-tuple or some other header fields. The actions could be dropping packets, forwarding packets to a specific next-hop, or modifying headers.","zh_html":"为了实现这些有状态操作(stateful operations)，我们可以使用匹配-动作对表(match-action pair table)，类似于(SDN 章节中的)OpenFlow 表。这个 API 允许软件在硬件上编程不同的策略(policies)，从而让硬件根据这些策略处理分组。正如我们之前看到的，匹配可以针对五元组(5-tuple)或其他首部字段。动作可以是丢弃分组、把分组转发到特定下一跳(next-hop)，或修改首部。"},{"en":"Brief History of Offloading: Epoch 3","zh_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-offloading-epoch-3\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-offloading-epoch-3\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 卸载(offloading)简史：时代 3"},{"en":"This is the current era of offloading. There are ongoing efforts to offload entire protocols, like TCP, out of the OS and onto the NIC. This epoch is being driven by even higher performance demands, especially with applications like AI/ML (artificial intelligence, machine learning) with high performance requirements.","zh_html":"这是当前卸载(offloading)的时代。人们正在努力把整个协议(如 TCP)从 OS 中卸载到 NIC 上。这个时代由更高的性能需求驱动，特别是 AI/ML(人工智能、机器学习)等高性能要求的应用。"},{"en":"Ideally, we’d like to let the application directly hand data to hardware, and let the hardware perform all the necessary network processing at Layers 4, 3, 2, and 1. The OS is entirely out of the picture, and all the network protocols are implemented directly in hardware.","zh_html":"理想情况下，我们希望让应用(application)直接把数据交给硬件，让硬件在第 4、3、2、1 层执行所有必要的网络处理。OS 完全退出，所有网络协议(network protocols)直接在硬件中实现。"},{"en":"While there’s been some experimentation with offloading standard networking protocols like TCP onto the NIC, they haven’t been deployed at scale. Instead, we’ve designed new networking protocols like RDMA, which are specially designed to allow implementation directly in hardware.","zh_html":"虽然已经有一些将标准网络协议(如 TCP)卸载到 NIC 的实验，但它们还没有大规模部署。相反，我们设计了像 RDMA 这样专门为直接在硬件中实现而设计的新网络协议(networking protocols)。"},{"en":"RDMA: Remote Direct Memory Access","zh_html":"<a href=\"#rdma-remote-direct-memory-access\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rdma-remote-direct-memory-access\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> RDMA：远程直接内存访问(Remote Direct Memory Access)"},{"en":"RDMA offers an abstraction where Server A can directly access the memory in Server B, without the involvement of the OS or the CPU in either server. RDMA can be implemented directly in hardware, replacing the standard TCP/IP software networking stack.","zh_html":"RDMA 提供了一种抽象(abstraction)：服务器 A 可以直接访问服务器 B 的内存(memory)，而无需任何一台服务器上的 OS 或 CPU 参与。RDMA 可以直接在硬件中实现，取代标准的 TCP/IP 软件网络协议栈。"},{"en":"Suppose that Server A wants to send a 10 GB file to Server B. In the standard networking stack, the CPU reads the file from memory, processes it (e.g. TCP/IP), and passes the resulting packets to the NIC. At the recipient, the NIC passes the packets to the CPU, which processes the packets, and writes the resulting file payload into memory. Notice that the CPU is involved in processing every single packet of the 10 GB file.","zh_html":"假设服务器 A 想要向服务器 B 发送一个 10 GB 的文件。在标准网络协议栈中，CPU 从内存中读取文件，进行处理(如 TCP/IP)，并将生成的分组(packets)传递给 NIC。在接收端，NIC 把分组传递给 CPU，CPU 处理这些分组，然后把生成的文件内容写入内存。注意，CPU 参与了 10 GB 文件中每个分组的处理。"},{"en":"In the RDMA abstraction, the NIC reads the file from memory and sends it out, with no CPU involvement. At the recipient, the NIC processes the incoming bytes and writes them to memory, again with no CPU involvement. Note that the CPU is still needed at the beginning to set up the transfer, and at the end to complete the transfer. But the bulk of the 10 GB file transfer is done without the CPU.","zh_html":"在 RDMA 抽象中，NIC 从内存中读取文件并发送出去，无需 CPU 参与。在接收端，NIC 处理传入的字节并写入内存，同样无需 CPU 参与。注意，在传输开始时仍然需要 CPU 来设置传输，在传输结束时也需要 CPU 来完成传输。但 10 GB 文件传输的主体是在没有 CPU 参与的情况下完成的。"},{"en":"To use RDMA, programmers no longer use the socket abstraction. Instead, the main abstraction we use is the queue pair. The send work queue has all of the pending jobs where data needs to be transferred from me to somebody else. The receive work queue has all of the pending jobs where I need to receive data from somebody else. A single NIC can have multiple queue pairs, where each offers different service to the programmer. For example, one pair might offer reliable, in-order delivery, while another pair might offer unreliable delivery. A queue pair configured to be reliable and in-order is the closest to a traditional TCP connection.","zh_html":"要使用 RDMA，程序员不再使用套接字抽象(socket abstraction)。相反，我们使用的主要抽象是<strong>队列对(queue pair)</strong>。发送工作队列(send work queue)包含所有待处理的作业，这些作业需要把数据从我这里传输到其他人。接收工作队列(receive work queue)包含所有待处理的作业，我需要从其他人那里接收数据。单个 NIC 可以有多个队列对，每个队列对为程序员提供不同的服务。例如，一个队列对可能提供可靠、按序的交付，而另一个可能提供不可靠的交付。配置为可靠且按序的队列对最接近传统的 TCP 连接。"},{"en":"Each element in the queue is called a work queue element (WQE). A WQE lets the application describe what work needs to be done. In English, the WQE in the receive queue might say, “Take 100 MB starting from address 0xffff1234 on the remote server, and write them to address 0xffff7890 in my local memory.” In code, the WQE is a struct that contains these instructions, e.g. a pointer to where we’re writing the received data.","zh_html":"队列中的每个元素称为<strong>工作队列元素(WQE，work queue element)</strong>。WQE 让应用(application)描述需要完成什么工作。用通俗的话说，接收队列中的 WQE 可能说：\"从远程服务器地址 0xffff1234 开始取 100 MB，写入我本地内存的地址 0xffff7890。\"在代码中，WQE 是一个包含这些指令的结构体(struct)，例如指向接收数据写入位置的指针。"},{"en":"Notice that the WQE abstraction gives the RDMA protocol a higher-level view of the application. In the TCP/IP stack, the network just sees a bytestream, but in RDMA, the WQE allows the application to describe the job in more detail (e.g. specifying the start and end of a block of data being transferred).","zh_html":"注意，WQE 抽象(abstraction)为 RDMA 协议提供了更高层次的应用视图。在 TCP/IP 协议栈中，网络只看到字节流(bytestream)，但在 RDMA 中，WQE 允许应用更详细地描述任务，例如指定正在传输的数据块的开始和结束。"},{"en":"When a job is finished, the WQE is removed from the queue, and the NIC creates a new struct called a Completion Queue Element (CQE), describing what happened to the job (e.g. success or failure). This CQE is stored in the Completion Queue, and sits there waiting until the application is ready to read the CQE and understand what happened to the job.","zh_html":"当一个作业完成时，WQE 从队列中被移除，NIC 创建一个新的结构体，称为<strong>完成队列元素(CQE，Completion Queue Element)</strong>，描述作业的结果，例如成功或失败。这个 CQE 被存储在完成队列(Completion Queue)中，等待应用准备好读取 CQE 以了解作业的状态。"},{"en":"Notice that RDMA is asynchronous. Applications can add jobs (WQEs) to the queue pairs whenever they want, and the NIC will process the jobs in order. Similarly, when the job is done, a CQE is placed in the completion queue, and the application can read the CQE whenever it wants. (Contrast this with the TCP/IP stack, where incoming data triggers an interrupt for the CPU to handle that data.)","zh_html":"注意 RDMA 是异步(asynchronous)的。应用(applications)可以随时向队列对(queue pairs)添加作业(WQEs)，NIC 会按序处理这些作业。类似地，当作业完成时，CQE 被放入完成队列(completion queue)，应用可以随时读取 CQE。这与 TCP/IP 协议栈形成对比，后者中传入数据会触发中断(interrupt)让 CPU 处理该数据。"},{"en":"RDMA Example","zh_html":"<a href=\"#rdma-example\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rdma-example\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> RDMA 示例"},{"en":"RDMA can be used for several different operations between servers. Each operation has its own performance specifications (e.g. different latencies), and different semantics (e.g. different error messages). As an example, let’s look at an RDMA send operation, where Server A reads a file from its memory, transfers that data, and Server B writes that file into its memory.","zh_html":"RDMA 可用于服务器之间的多种不同操作。每个操作有自己的性能规格(如不同的延迟)和不同的语义(如不同的错误消息)。作为示例，让我们看一个 RDMA 发送操作：服务器 A 从自己的内存中读取一个文件，传输这些数据，服务器 B 把该文件写入自己的内存。"},{"en":"Each server designates some section of its memory to be accessible by the NIC for RDMA transfers. Server A designates the memory corresponding to the file as NIC-readable. Server B designates a blank buffer where it will receive the file as NIC-readable.","zh_html":"<p>每个服务器指定部分内存供 NIC 用于 RDMA 传输。服务器 A 把文件对应的内存标记为 NIC 可读。服务器 B 指定一个空白缓冲区(将接收文件的位置)为 NIC 可写。</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/datacenter/6-096-rdma1.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"Each server sets up queues. Both NICs now have a send queue, a receive queue, and a completion queue. Note that this step can be done out-of-band, using a traditional protocol like TCP to coordinate between the two servers.","zh_html":"<p>每个服务器设置队列。两个 NIC 现在都有一个发送队列、一个接收队列和一个完成队列。注意，这一步可以带外(out-of-band)完成，使用传统协议如 TCP 来协调两台服务器。</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/datacenter/6-097-rdma2.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"Server A creates a WQE in the send queue. This WQE contains a pointer to the file, indicating the data to be sent. On the other side, Server B creates a WQE in the receive queue. This WQE contains a pointer to the blank buffer, indicating where the received data should be written.","zh_html":"<p>服务器 A 在发送队列中创建一个 WQE。这个 WQE 包含一个指向该文件的指针，指示要发送的数据。在另一端，服务器 B 在接收队列中创建一个 WQE。这个 WQE 包含一个指向空白缓冲区的指针，指示接收数据应写入的位置。</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/datacenter/6-098-rdma3.png\" /></img></p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/datacenter/6-099-rdma4.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"Once the transfer is queued on both sides, the data transfer can occur, with no involvement from software. The NIC handles everything, including reliability, congestion control, and so on.","zh_html":"<p>一旦传输在两侧都排队完成，数据传输就可以进行，无需软件参与。NIC 处理一切，包括可靠性(reliability)、拥塞控制(congestion control)等。</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/datacenter/6-100-rdma5.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"When the transfer is done, the WQEs are removed from the queues. Both NICs generate a CQE, indicating that the transfer is done, and including any relevant status messages (e.g. error messages). Server A’s CQE indicates that the data was successfully sent, and Server B’s CQE indicates that the data was successfully received.","zh_html":"<p>当传输完成时，WQEs 从队列中被移除。两个 NIC 都生成一个 CQE，指示传输已完成，并包含任何相关的状态消息(例如错误消息)。服务器 A 的 CQE 指示数据成功发送，服务器 B 的 CQE 指示数据成功接收。</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/datacenter/6-101-rdma6.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"Eventually, the applications read the CQE to understand what happened to the transfer.","zh_html":"<p>最终，应用(applications)读取 CQE 以了解传输的结果。</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/datacenter/6-102-rdma7.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"RDMA Pros, Cons, Applications","zh_html":"<a href=\"#rdma-pros-cons-applications\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rdma-pros-cons-applications\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> RDMA 优缺点与应用"},{"en":"RDMA gives us high-performance data transfer (low latency, high bandwidth), and frees up the CPU for applications. However, RDMA doesn’t come for free. RDMA requires specialized hardware and software, and is generally more complex than the traditional networking stack. Remember, RDMA is replacing the TCP/IP stack, so it has to implement all the TCP/IP functionality like reliabilty and congestion control, all directly in hardware.","zh_html":"RDMA 提供了高性能数据传输(低延迟、高带宽)，并为应用释放了 CPU。然而，RDMA 并非没有代价。RDMA 需要专门的硬件和软件，通常比传统的网络协议栈更复杂。记住，RDMA 取代了 TCP/IP 协议栈，因此它必须实现所有 TCP/IP 功能，如可靠性和拥塞控制，并且全部直接在硬件中实现。"},{"en":"RDMA also has some limitations, and usually works best in datacenters where the two servers are physically near each other. If the two servers are far away, the dominant delay comes from sending data across the network, and the time savings from RDMA are negligible. By contrast, if the two servers are nearby, the host processing packets could be the dominant delay, so RDMA gives significant time savings.","zh_html":"RDMA 也有一些限制，通常在最适用于两台服务器物理位置相近的数据中心。如果两台服务器距离很远，主要延迟来自跨网络传输数据，RDMA 节省的时间可忽略不计。相反，如果两台服务器很近，主机处理分组可能是主要延迟来源，因此 RDMA 能显著节省时间。"},{"en":"RDMA has been applied in many different settings that require high-performance, low-latency computing. Examples include scientific research, financial modeling, weather forecasting, machine learning, and search queries. In cloud computing, RDMA can be used to migrate a large VM from one physical server to another, freeing up the CPU for customers to use. In AI/ML training, RDMA not only frees up the CPU and gives us low latency, but it also gives us predictable latency, which is important when different servers need to coordinate to train AI/ML models.","zh_html":"RDMA 已经应用于许多需要高性能、低延迟计算的不同场景。例子包括科学研究、金融建模、天气预报、机器学习(ML)和搜索查询。在云计算(cloud computing)中，RDMA 可用于将大型 VM 从一台物理服务器迁移到另一台，释放 CPU 给客户使用。在 AI/ML 训练中，RDMA 不仅释放了 CPU 并提供低延迟，还提供了可预测的延迟，这在不同服务器需要协调训练 AI/ML 模型时非常重要。"},{"en":"Implementing RDMA","zh_html":"<a href=\"#implementing-rdma\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"implementing-rdma\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 实现 RDMA"},{"en":"Remember, RDMA replaces the TCP/IP networking stack, so RDMA is responsible for reliability, congestion control, and so on. There are two broad philosophies for how to implement this.","zh_html":"记住，RDMA 取代了 TCP/IP 网络协议栈，因此 RDMA 负责可靠性(reliability)、拥塞控制(congestion control)等。实现方式大致有两种哲学。"},{"en":"One option is to implement these features in the network itself, e.g. reliability at the switches. This is the idea behind Nvidia’s InfiniBand.","zh_html":"一种选择是在网络本身中实现这些功能，例如在交换机(switches)处实现可靠性(reliability)。这就是 Nvidia 的 InfiniBand 背后的思想。"},{"en":"Another option is to implement these features in the NIC, underneath the queue-pair abstraction. This is the idea currently being pursued at Google.","zh_html":"另一种选择是在 NIC 中、在队列对抽象(queue-pair abstraction)之下实现这些功能。这是 Google 目前正在追求的思路。"},{"en":"In both cases, the application and the OS in software gets the illusion of reliable, in-order delivery via the queue pair abstraction. The difference here is how RDMA actually implements those service guarantees.","zh_html":"在这两种情况下，软件中的应用(application)和 OS 都通过队列对抽象(queue pair abstraction)获得了可靠、按序交付的假象。区别在于 RDMA 如何实际实现这些服务保证(service guarantees)。"}]},"/datacenter/index.html":{"title":"index","blocks":[{"en":"Topologies","en_html":"<a href=\"topology.html\">Topologies</a>","zh_html":"拓扑(Topologies)"},{"en":"Congestion Control","en_html":"<a href=\"datacenter-cc.html\">Congestion Control</a>","zh_html":"拥塞控制(Congestion Control)"},{"en":"Routing","en_html":"<a href=\"datacenter-routing.html\">Routing</a>","zh_html":"路由(Routing)"},{"en":"Addressing","en_html":"<a href=\"datacenter-addressing.html\">Addressing</a>","zh_html":"寻址(Addressing)"},{"en":"Virtualization","en_html":"<a href=\"virtualization.html\">Virtualization</a>","zh_html":"虚拟化(Virtualization)"},{"en":"Software-Defined Networking","en_html":"<a href=\"sdn.html\">Software-Defined Networking</a>","zh_html":"软件定义网络(Software-Defined Networking)"},{"en":"Host Networking","en_html":"<a href=\"host-networking.html\">Host Networking</a>","zh_html":"主机网络(Host Networking)"}]},"/datacenter/sdn.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Software-Defined Networking","zh_html":"<a href=\"#software-defined-networking\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"software-defined-networking\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 软件定义网络(software-defined networking, SDN)"},{"en":"Why Software-Defined Networking?","zh_html":"<a href=\"#why-software-defined-networking\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"why-software-defined-networking\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 为什么需要软件定义网络(software-defined networking)？"},{"en":"Previously, we saw how routing protocols can be adapted to work in datacenter contexts (e.g. equal-cost multi-path). What if we want to optimize our routing protocols even further for our specific network’s constraints and use cases? The standard routing protocols might no longer work.","zh_html":"之前，我们看到了路由协议(routing protocols)如何被适配到数据中心环境(datacenter contexts)，例如等成本多路径(equal-cost multi-path)。如果我们想针对自己网络的特定约束和用例进一步优化路由协议呢？标准路由协议可能不再适用。"},{"en":"In this section, we’ll explore software-defined networking, a totally new paradigm for thinking about routing and network management. In the context of routing, the SDN architecture involves having a centralized control center compute routes and distribute them to individual routers. We’ll see how SDN works in the context of datacenters and the wide-area network, and discuss benefits and drawbacks of this new approach.","zh_html":"在本节中，我们将探索<strong>软件定义网络(software-defined networking, SDN)</strong>，这是一种思考路由(routing)和网络管理(network management)的完全新范式。在路由的背景下，SDN 架构涉及一个集中式控制中心(centralized control center)来计算路由并分发给各个路由器。我们将看到 SDN 如何工作在数据中心和广域网的背景下，并讨论这种新方法的优缺点。"},{"en":"Brief History of Software-Defined Networking","zh_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-software-defined-networking\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-software-defined-networking\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 软件定义网络(software-defined networking)简史"},{"en":"Although we’ll be looking at SDN as a new approach for specialized routing protocols, the SDN paradigm was originally designed in response to headaches at the management plane.","zh_html":"虽然我们将把 SDN 看作一种用于专用路由协议的新方法，但 SDN 范式最初是为了响应管理平面(management plane)的痛点而设计的。"},{"en":"Recall that the management plane is critical for network operation. Routers can’t do anything unless someone configures them (e.g. assigns costs to links) and tells them what to do (e.g. what routing protocol to run). Also, we need routers to report errors to keep the network up and running. A lot of this management work has historically been done manually.","zh_html":"回想一下，管理平面(management plane)对网络运行至关重要。除非有人配置路由器(configures)，例如分配链路成本(costs)，并告诉它们该做什么，例如运行什么路由协议，否则路由器什么也做不了。此外，我们需要路由器报告错误，以保持网络的正常运行。历史上，这些管理工作很多是手动完成的。"},{"en":"Even though the management plane is so important, there’s been relatively little focus on innovating it. At the control plane, we’ve see lots of different routing protocols, but the way we configure and control routers has evolved more slowly.","zh_html":"尽管管理平面(management plane)如此重要，但对其创新的关注却相对较少。在控制平面(control plane)上，我们看到了许多不同的路由协议，但配置和控制路由器的方式演进得更慢。"},{"en":"Over the Internet’s history, there’s been a slow evolution toward using scripts to programatically interact with the network. These scripts take jobs that the operator would manually do, and implement them in code (without much intelligence). For example, scripts allow automating the process of adding routers and links to the network. A script for repairing the network might say: if a router fails, check that it’s actually failed, reboot it, and if it’s still not fixed, report to the operator.","zh_html":"在互联网的发展历程中，有一个缓慢的演进趋势：使用脚本(scripts)以编程方式与网络交互。这些脚本把操作员手动完成的任务用代码实现，但不需要太多智能。例如，脚本可以自动化添加路由器和链路到网络的过程。一个用于修复网络的脚本可能是：如果一台路由器发生故障，检查它是否真的故障了，重启它，如果仍然没有修复，则报告给操作员。"},{"en":"Despite the progress, these management systems have been the bottleneck for network operations for a long time. We still might have to wait for human intervention every time a new router is added.","zh_html":"尽管取得了进展，这些管理系统长期以来一直是网络运营的瓶颈。每次添加新路由器时，我们可能仍然需要等待人工干预。"},{"en":"In 2005, a paper by Albert Greenberg et. al. described the problem by saying: “Today’s data networks are surprisingly fragile and difficult to manage. We argue that the root of these problems lies in the complexity of the control and management planes.”","zh_html":"在 2005 年，Albert Greenberg 等人的一篇论文描述了这个问题：\"当今的数据网络出奇地脆弱且难以管理。我们认为这些问题的根源在于控制平面(control plane)和管理平面(management plane)的复杂性。\""},{"en":"In response to these problems, researchers began thinking about different ways to run a network system. This led to more radical proposals that reimagined the fundamental design of routers.","zh_html":"为了应对这些问题，研究人员开始思考运行网络系统的不同方式。这引出了更激进的方案，重新构想路由器的基本设计。"},{"en":"The concepts we’ll see were first considered in 2003, though they didn’t gain much momentum at the time. Frustrations with network management accelerated the development of new management paradigms. By 2008, there was more momentum, leading to the OpenFlow switch interface (which we’ll see soon).","zh_html":"我们将看到的概念最早在 2003 年被提出，尽管当时没有获得太多关注。对网络管理的不满加速了新管理范式的发展。到 2008 年，势头更强，催生了 OpenFlow 交换机接口(我们很快就会看到)。"},{"en":"By 2011, it was evident the industry was moving in this new direction, and the Open Networking Foundation (ONF) was established by major network operators (Google, Yahoo, Verizon, Microsoft, Facebook) and vendors (Cisco, Juniper, HP, Dell). Nicira, the SDN-focused startup that developed the OpenFlow interface, was a $40 million startup in 2012.","zh_html":"到 2011 年，行业显然正在朝着这个新方向发展，由主要网络运营商(Google、Yahoo、Verizon、Microsoft、Facebook)和设备商(Cisco、Juniper、HP、Dell)成立了开放网络基金会(ONF，Open Networking Foundation)。专注于 SDN 并开发了 OpenFlow 接口的初创公司 Nicira 在 2012 年估值 4000 万美元。"},{"en":"Routers are Vertically Integrated and Standardized","zh_html":"<a href=\"#routers-are-vertically-integrated-and-standardized\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"routers-are-vertically-integrated-and-standardized\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 路由器是垂直集成(vertically integrated)且标准化(standardized)的"},{"en":"If we wanted to reimagine the design of routers, how would that be implemented in practice? How do technologies on routers change over time?","zh_html":"如果我们想重新构想路由器的设计，在实践中应如何实现？路由器上的技术如何随时间变化？"},{"en":"If your network needs a router, you’d probably purchase one from a major equipment vendor like Cisco or Juniper. In order to ensure that routers are compatible with each other, all the major equipment vendors build their routers according to some pre-defined standards.","zh_html":"如果你的网络需要一台路由器，你可能会从 Cisco 或 Juniper 等主要设备商那里购买。为了确保路由器彼此兼容，所有主要设备商都按照一些预定义的标准来构建他们的路由器。"},{"en":"This business model can make innovation and experimentation with new approaches difficult. Suppose you had a new idea for a routing protocol. You would need to get the protocol approved by a standards body, which could take years. Then, you’d have to wait for the vendors to upgrade their manufacturing to conform to the new standard.","zh_html":"这种商业模式可能使新方法的创新和实验变得困难。假设你有一个新的路由协议想法。你需要让标准组织批准该协议，这可能要花上几年。然后，你还需要等待设备商升级他们的制造工艺以符合新标准。"},{"en":"Standardization also makes routers less flexible for users implementing custom solutions. If you have a problem specific to your network, but no one else has this problem, your solution probably won’t be adopted by the standards body. Vendors want to make routers that satisfy everybody’s needs, and they won’t necessarily implement a solution that’s perfect for you, if nobody else wants it.","zh_html":"标准化(standardization)也使路由器对实施定制解决方案的用户来说不够灵活。如果你有一个针对自己网络的特定问题，但没有其他人有这个问题，你的解决方案很可能不会被标准组织采纳。设备商想制造能满足所有人需求的路由器，如果没有人想要，他们不一定会实现一个对你来说完美的解决方案。"},{"en":"On the other hand, standardization also means that if others have a problem that you don’t, the router might come with a solution to their problem, even if you don’t need it. This can make routers unnecessarily complex for the purposes of your specific network.","zh_html":"另一方面，标准化也意味着如果别人有你没有的问题，路由器可能会附带解决他们问题的方案，即使你并不需要。这可能会使路由器对你特定网络的需求来说不必要地复杂。"},{"en":"Standardization also makes experimentation and research difficult. If you want to try a new idea to see if it works, you might not be able to buy routers that can implement your new idea. Vendors don’t want to build experimental products, intended for one specific customer, that might not even work.","zh_html":"标准化也使实验和研究变得困难。如果你想尝试一个新想法看看是否有效，你可能买不到能实现你这个新想法的路由器。设备商不想制造实验性的产品，只为一个特定客户，而且还可能不成功。"},{"en":"Another major obstacle to innovation and experimentation is routers being vertically integrated. The router you buy already has the functionality for all three planes wired on the chips. There’s no modularity that would let you swap out just the control plane by itself.","zh_html":"创新和实验的另一个主要障碍是路由器是<strong>垂直集成(vertically integrated)</strong>的。你买的路由器三个平面的功能已经全部集成在芯片上。没有模块化(modularity)可以让你单独更换控制平面(control plane)。"},{"en":"Innovating Routers","zh_html":"<a href=\"#innovating-routers\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"innovating-routers\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 路由器的创新"},{"en":"If we did want to innovate routers, what could we innovate at each plane, and what kinds of pre-existing standards would we be working with?","zh_html":"如果我们确实想对路由器进行创新，在每个平面我们可以创新什么？我们需要面对哪些现有标准？"},{"en":"The data plane is standardized by IEEE (electrical engineering group) and requires everyone to strictly follow the standards. If two routers from different vendors are connected, we have to make sure both sides are sending bits along the physical wire in the same consistent format.","zh_html":"数据平面(data plane)由 IEEE(电子工程组织)标准化，要求每个人都严格遵守标准。如果来自不同设备商的两台路由器相连，我们必须确保双方以相同一致的格式沿着物理线路发送比特。"},{"en":"Data plane innovation is usually driven by the demand for higher-bandwidth routers, and new features are not often introduced. This development happens quite slowly, in 2-3 year increments, because we have to solve physical hardware problems and design chips for increased bandwidth. Since the core data plane features are relatively stable, router innovation is not really focused on the data plane, and it’s okay that the development cycle is slow.","zh_html":"数据平面(data plane)的创新通常由对更高带宽路由器的需求驱动，新功能不常引入。这种发展相当缓慢，以 2 到 3 年为周期，因为我们必须解决物理硬件问题并设计芯片以提高带宽。由于核心数据平面功能相对稳定，路由器的创新并不真正集中在数据平面上，并且开发周期慢也是可以接受的。"},{"en":"The control plane is standardized by the IETF (the network group behind RFCs). Vendors sometimes add their own extensions, though the core features are mostly standardized. For example, we assume that every router (even if they’re from different routers) are following the same routing protocol.","zh_html":"控制平面(control plane)由 IETF(负责 RFC 的网络组织)标准化。设备商有时会添加自己的扩展，但核心功能大多是标准化的。例如，我们假设每台路由器，即使来自不同设备商，都遵循相同的路由协议。"},{"en":"Control plane innovation (e.g. new routing protocols) can take several years to be adopted. You might have to submit an RFC draft proposal, and the community may spend some time discussing the proposal before agreeing on its terms.","zh_html":"控制平面(control plane)的创新(例如新的路由协议)可能需要数年才能被采纳。你可能需要提交一份 RFC 草案，社区需要花一些时间讨论该提案，然后才能就条款达成一致。"},{"en":"The management plane is also standardized by the IETF, though it’s much less standardized. Different operators can use different software to configure their routers, and we don’t really need different vendors to agree on some standardized software. Because this plane is only loosely standardized, many different approaches with different features exist.","zh_html":"管理平面(management plane)也由 IETF 标准化，但标准化程度低得多。不同运营方可以使用不同软件来配置他们的路由器，我们并不真的需要不同设备商就某种标准化软件达成一致。由于这个平面只是松散地标准化，存在许多具有不同功能的方法。"},{"en":"In summary: The data plane is standardized (but we don’t really have new features in mind), the control plane is standardized (but we want to try new solutions), and the management plane is not really standardized.","zh_html":"总结一下：数据平面(data plane)是标准化的(但我们并没有真正想要的新功能)，控制平面(control plane)是标准化的(但我们想尝试新方案)，而管理平面(management plane)并没有真正标准化。"},{"en":"Radical Idea: Disaggregating Routers","zh_html":"<a href=\"#radical-idea-disaggregating-routers\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"radical-idea-disaggregating-routers\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 激进想法：解耦路由器(disaggregating routers)"},{"en":"Standardization and vertical integration were making it difficult to innovate and experiment. This led to the radical idea of disaggregating routers by splitting the planes into different layers of abstraction. Instead of buying a single router with all three planes, we could now buy data and control plane functionality separately. This allows us to change layers independently from each other.","zh_html":"标准化(standardization)和垂直集成(vertical integration)使创新和实验变得困难。这引出了解耦路由器(disaggregating routers)的激进想法：把平面拆分为不同的抽象层(abstraction layers)。与其购买一台包含所有三个平面的路由器，我们可以分别购买数据平面和控制平面功能。这允许我们独立地更改各层。"},{"en":"In order to connect the three layers, we need an API between the layers of abstraction. In a vertically-coupled router, we don’t care how the data plane and control plane talk to each other. However, if we buy the data plane separately, and we want to design our own custom control plane on top, we need an interface to interact with the data plane.","zh_html":"为了连接这三层，我们需要在抽象层(abstraction layers)之间有一个 API。在垂直耦合(vertically-coupled)的路由器中，我们不关心数据平面(data plane)和控制平面(control plane)如何交互。但是，如果我们分开购买数据平面，并想在上面设计自己的自定义控制平面，就需要一个接口来与数据平面交互。"},{"en":"An even more radical idea is to stop thinking about the three planes in terms of only the router, and instead design a new system architecture that naturally splits up the data plane and control plane.","zh_html":"一个更激进的想法是，不再仅仅从路由器的角度思考这三个平面，而是设计一种新的系统架构，自然地分离数据平面(data plane)和控制平面(control plane)。"},{"en":"At the bottom, we have commodity network devices. You can think of these as buying just the data plane by itself. These routers receive instructions from the control program via the network OS, and simply forward packets according to those instructions. These routers don’t need to think about routing protocols at all, so they can be cheaper.","zh_html":"在底层，我们有通用网络设备(commodity network devices)。你可以把这看作只购买数据平面(data plane)本身。这些路由器通过网络 OS 从控制程序接收指令，并根据这些指令简单地转发分组(forward packets)。这些路由器完全不需要考虑路由协议，因此可以更便宜。"},{"en":"In the middle, we have the network OS. You can think of this as the API connecting the data plane routers and the control plane program. The network OS provides an abstraction of the routers (e.g. as a graph) that can be passed up to the control program. Then, the control program can send routing instructions to the network OS, without worrying about how to program specific routers. The network OS can take those instructions and program them onto individual routers.","zh_html":"在中间层，我们有网络 OS(network OS)。你可以把这看作连接数据平面路由器(控制平面程序)的 API。网络 OS 提供路由器的抽象(abstraction)，例如以图(graph)的形式，可以向上传递给控制程序。然后，控制程序可以向网络 OS 发送路由指令，而无需担心如何编程特定路由器。网络 OS 可以接收这些指令并将它们编程到各个路由器上。"},{"en":"At the top, we have the control program. You can think of this as buying or implementing the control plane by itself. Here, the operator receives an abstraction of the network (e.g. graph) from the network OS, and can use that to write their own custom routing protocol. Then, the resulting routes can be passed to the network OS, which will program them onto routers.","zh_html":"在最上层，我们有控制程序(control program)。你可以把这看作单独购买或实现控制平面(control plane)。在这里，操作员从网络 OS 接收网络的抽象(如图)，并可以用它来编写自己的自定义路由协议。然后，生成的路由可以传递给网络 OS，网络 OS 再将它们编程到路由器上。"},{"en":"OpenFlow API Format","zh_html":"<a href=\"#openflow-api-format\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"openflow-api-format\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> OpenFlow API 格式"},{"en":"OpenFlow is an API for interacting with the data plane of a router. The operator writes their own fancy code, separate from the router, that computes routes through the network. Then, those routes can be programmed onto the forwarding chip.","zh_html":"<strong>OpenFlow</strong> 是一个用于与路由器数据平面(data plane)交互的 API。操作员编写自己的高级代码，独立于路由器，计算通过网络的路径。然后，这些路径可以被编程到转发芯片(forwarding chip)上。"},{"en":"The OpenFlow paradigm is different from traditional routers, where the control plane is implemented in the router, and there’s no clear API for programming custom routes onto the forwarding chip.","zh_html":"OpenFlow 范式不同于传统路由器，后者控制平面(control plane)在路由器内部实现，没有清晰的 API 用于在转发芯片上编程自定义路由。"},{"en":"The OpenFlow API defines a flow table abstraction to describe routes and forwarding rules. The operator code can output any rules and routes it wants, and install them on the router, as long as they’re in the flow table format.","zh_html":"OpenFlow API 定义了一个<strong>流表(flow table)</strong>抽象(abstraction)来描述路由和转发规则。操作员代码可以输出任何想要的规则和路由，并将它们安装到路由器上，只要它们符合流表格式。"},{"en":"The basic building block of the API is a flow table, which you can think of as a generalized version of a forwarding table. Each flow table consists of key-value pairs, just like a forwarding table. The key specifies what to match the packet against. This could be a destination prefix, an exact destination, a 5-tuple, or other relatively simple matches. The corresponding value specifies what action to set when a packet matches. The action could be sending the packet to a next hop (like a forwarding table), but could also specify fancier actions like adding an extra header.","zh_html":"API 的基本构建模块是流表(flow table)，你可以把它看作是转发表(forwarding table)的通用版本。每个流表由键值对(key-value pairs)组成，就像转发表一样。键指定要对分组进行什么<strong>匹配(match)</strong>。这可以是目的前缀(destination prefix)、确切目的地(exact destination)、五元组(5-tuple)或其他相对简单的匹配。对应的值指定当分组匹配时要执行什么<strong>动作(action)</strong>。动作可以是把分组发送到下一跳(next hop)，就像转发表那样，但也可以指定更复杂的动作，比如添加额外首部(extra header)。"},{"en":"The output format is a sequence of one or more numbered flow tables, where each table has its own different match-action entries. These flow tables can then be programmed onto the forwarding chip.","zh_html":"输出格式是一个由一个或多个编号流表组成的序列，每个表有自己的不同匹配-动作条目。这些流表然后可以被编程到转发芯片(forwarding chip)上。"},{"en":"When a packet arrives at a router, it is checked against each table in order (e.g. Table 0, Table 1, Table 2, etc.), and when there’s a match, we write down the corresponding action (without executing it yet). Eventually, once the packet is checked against the final table, any action(s) we wrote down are applied to the packet.","zh_html":"当一个分组(packet)到达路由器时，会按顺序与每个表进行比对，例如表 0、表 1、表 2 等。当匹配时，我们记录下相应的动作，但先不执行。最终，在分组与最后一个表比对完毕后，之前记录的任何动作都会应用到该分组上。"},{"en":"There are also special actions for skipping to later tables, which we can use in rules like: If the source port matches this number, skip to table 5 to set additional actions.","zh_html":"还有一些特殊动作可以跳过到后面的表，我们可以用在这样的规则中：如果源端口匹配这个数字，跳到表 5 来设置额外的动作。"},{"en":"The operator can run any code they want to generate flow tables, and the flow tables can be more general than a destination/next-hop forwarding table. However, the rules (match/action pairs) that we generate are still constrained by the specialized forwarding chip hardware. The forwarding chip is optimized for speed, and probably can’t handle complex match rules like “if the TCP payload is in English, set this action.”","zh_html":"操作员可以运行任何他们想要的代码来生成流表，而流表可以比目的/下一跳转发表更通用。然而，我们生成的规则(匹配/动作对)仍然受限于专门的转发芯片硬件。转发芯片针对速度进行了优化，很可能无法处理像\"如果 TCP 负载是英文，设置这个动作\"这样的复杂匹配规则。"},{"en":"As a result, the flow tables we see in practice end up looking pretty similar to the tables we’ve already seen. Common match rules include longest prefix matching on IP destinations, 5-tuples to identify flows, and exact matches on encapsulation headers (e.g. MPLS).","zh_html":"因此，我们在实践中看到的流表最终看起来与我们已经见过的表非常相似。常见的匹配规则包括 IP 目的地的 最长前缀匹配(longest prefix matching)、用于标识流的五元组(5-tuples)，以及封装首部(encapsulation headers)的精确匹配(例如 MPLS)。"},{"en":"If the forwarding rules aren’t so different, why use OpenFlow at all? Remember, the main advantage is that it gives the operator total freedom at the control plane. We’re not limited to distance-vector or link-state protocols anymore.","zh_html":"如果转发规则没有太大区别，那为什么还要使用 OpenFlow 呢？记住，主要优势是它给了操作员在控制平面(control plane)上的完全自由。我们不再局限于距离向量(distance-vector)或链路状态(link-state)协议。"},{"en":"Benefits of a Flexible Control Plane","zh_html":"<a href=\"#benefits-of-a-flexible-control-plane\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"benefits-of-a-flexible-control-plane\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 灵活控制平面(flexible control plane)的好处"},{"en":"Our new architecture gives the operator flexibility to implement their new routing protocol at the control plane. What are some benefits of this approach?","zh_html":"我们的新架构让操作员可以在控制平面(control plane)上灵活地实现他们自己的路由协议。这种方法有什么好处？"},{"en":"The operator can implement custom routing protocols best-suited for the operator’s specific needs. The operator is no longer constrained by standards bodies and vendors.","zh_html":"操作员可以实现最适合自己特定需求的自定义路由协议(custom routing protocols)。操作员不再受标准组织和设备商的约束。"},{"en":"Flexibility also gives us an opportunity to simplify. For example, if the standardized protocol includes features we don’t need, we don’t have to implement them in our custom solution. Simpler protocols can have less code and simpler code, which might allow for easier development and maintenance of that protocol.","zh_html":"灵活性也给了我们简化的机会。例如，如果标准化协议包含我们不需要的功能，我们就不必在自定义方案中实现它们。更简单的协议可以有更少的代码和更简单的代码，这可能使该协议的开发和维护更容易。"},{"en":"Finally, a flexible control plane enables centralized computation of routes at the control program, instead of distributed across multiple routers. Centralization comes with several benefits as well.","zh_html":"最后，灵活的控制平面(control plane)可以在控制程序(control program)处实现集中式路由计算，而不是分布在多个路由器上。集中化(centralization)也带来了几个好处。"},{"en":"Centralization can result in more intelligent routing decisions that lead to excellent performance. In a 2013 report from Google, engineers who deployed an SDN architecture noted that “centralized traffic engineering service drives links to near 100% utilization, while splitting application flows among multiple paths to balance capacity against application priority/demands.” A 2013 paper from Microsoft describes using an OpenFlow controller to “achieve high utilization with software-driven WAN.”","zh_html":"集中化(centralization)可以带来更智能的路由决策，实现卓越的性能。在 Google 工程师的一份 2013 年报告中，部署了 SDN 架构的工程师指出：\"集中式流量工程服务将链路利用率驱动到接近 100%，同时将应用流(application flows)分配到多条路径上，以平衡容量与应用优先级/需求。\"Microsoft 在 2013 年的一篇论文中描述了使用 OpenFlow 控制器\"通过软件驱动的 WAN 实现高利用率\"。"},{"en":"More intelligent routing decisions can help optimize other criteria besides performance, that a standard routing protocol can’t easily optimize. For example, a US government network might implement a geofencing rule that says, don’t send traffic via links that are in Canada. Or, a broadcast TV network might want to optimize for path diversity to increase reliability. We can enforce that two flows travel via paths that don’t share any links, so that if a link goes down, only one of the flows is affected. The two paths can serve as backups for each other.","zh_html":"更智能的路由决策可以帮助优化除了性能之外的其他指标，而标准路由协议不容易优化这些指标。例如，美国政府网络可能实现一个地理围栏(geofencing)规则：不通过位于加拿大的链路发送流量。或者，广播电视网络可能想要优化路径多样性(path diversity)以提高可靠性。我们可以强制两个流经由不共享任何链路的路径传输，这样如果一条链路中断，只有一个流受到影响。这两条路径可以互为备份。"},{"en":"Centralization can also make it easier for routing protocols to converge. In a distributed protocol, if the network changed, the routers have to coordinate to converge on a new routing state. In this centralized model, if a link fails, that router could tell the boss, and the boss could recompute routes and install the new routes on the routers.","zh_html":"集中化(centralization)还可以使路由协议更容易收敛(converge)。在分布式协议中，如果网络发生变化，路由器必须协调以收敛到新的路由状态。在这种集中式模型中，如果一条链路发生故障，该路由器可以告诉\"老板\"，老板可以重新计算路由并在路由器上安装新路由。"},{"en":"Traffic Engineering","zh_html":"<a href=\"#traffic-engineering\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"traffic-engineering\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 流量工程(traffic engineering)"},{"en":"A flexible control plane allows us to perform traffic engineering, which means we can route traffic in a more intelligent and efficient way than a standard distributed routing protocol could.","zh_html":"灵活的控制平面(control plane)允许我们执行<strong>流量工程(traffic engineering)</strong>，这意味着我们可以以比标准分布式路由协议更智能、更高效的方式路由流量。"},{"en":"Suppose there are two connections, S1-D at 10 Gbps and S2-D at 10 Gbps. If we just ran standard least-cost routing, both flows would send traffic along the bottom path. The bottom path would be congested (20 Gbps on 10 Gbps link), while the top path’s bandwidth is sitting there unused.","zh_html":"假设有两个连接，S1-D 为 10 Gbps，S2-D 为 10 Gbps。如果我们只运行标准的最少成本路由(least-cost routing)，两个流都会沿着底部路径发送流量。底部路径会拥塞(congested)，10 Gbps 链路上有 20 Gbps，而顶部路径的带宽则闲置不用。"},{"en":"With a more intelligent routing scheme, we could send S1-D traffic along the top path, and S2-D traffic along the bottom path. Using traffic engineering, we’ve forced some packets to take a longer route, in order to better utilize the bandwidth in the network.","zh_html":"通过更智能的路由方案，我们可以把 S1-D 的流量沿顶部路径发送，S2-D 的流量沿底部路径发送。通过流量工程(traffic engineering)，我们强制一些分组走更长的路径，以更好地利用网络中的带宽。"},{"en":"To compute these routes, we can modify least-cost routing, and instead enforce that traffic should be on the shortest path that has sufficient capacity. We can also enforce other constraints instead of capacity, such as latency. The resulting algorithm is called constrained Shortest Path First (cSPF).","zh_html":"为了计算这些路由，我们可以修改最少成本路由(least-cost routing)，改为强制要求流量应该走有足够容量的最短路径。我们还可以施加其他约束，比如延迟(latency)，而不是容量。由此产生的算法称为<strong>约束最短路径优先(cSPF，constrained Shortest Path First)</strong>。"},{"en":"Now, suppose that S1-D needs 12 Gbps, and S2-D needs 8 Gbps. cSPF will send the flows along different paths to maximize bandwidth, but S1-D is sending 12 Gbps over a 10 Gbps link.","zh_html":"现在，假设 S1-D 需要 12 Gbps，S2-D 需要 8 Gbps。cSPF 会沿不同路径发送这些流以最大化带宽，但 S1-D 在 10 Gbps 链路上发送 12 Gbps。"},{"en":"To fix this, our traffic engineering can be even more intelligent, and split traffic in a flow across different paths. S1-D can send 10 Gbps of its traffic along the top path, and the remaining 2 Gbps along the bottom path.","zh_html":"为了解决这个问题，我们的流量工程(traffic engineering)可以更加智能，把一个流中的流量分摊到不同路径上。S1-D 可以沿顶部路径发送 10 Gbps 的流量，剩余 2 Gbps 沿底部路径发送。"},{"en":"Again, our traffic engineering allowed us to implement custom logic that resulted in better utilization of the network capacity.","zh_html":"再次，我们的流量工程(traffic engineering)允许我们实现自定义逻辑，从而更好地利用网络容量。"},{"en":"How do we actually implement split paths through the network, using the OpenFlow API from earlier? Remember, our routing decisions should still follow simple rules that forwarding tables can understand.","zh_html":"我们如何实际利用前面的 OpenFlow API 来实现网络中的拆分路径(split paths)？记住，我们的路由决策仍然应该遵循转发表(forwarding tables)能理解的简单规则。"},{"en":"One approach is to use encapsulation. At the sender, we can add rules to add an extra header, where some packets get label 0, and the rest get label 1. This label tells us which path to send the traffic along.","zh_html":"一种方法是使用封装(encapsulation)。在发送端，我们可以添加规则来增加一个额外首部(extra header)，其中一些分组获得标签 0，其余获得标签 1。这个标签告诉我们应该沿哪条路径发送流量。"},{"en":"Now, at R1, we can add simple rules to route label 0 packets upwards to R2, and label 1 packets downwards to R3. This idea can be applied in addition to the other rules we had for constrained least-cost routing (e.g. the flow tables might have other entries for other destinations or other flows).","zh_html":"现在，在 R1 上，我们可以添加简单规则：标签 0 的分组向上路由到 R2，标签 1 的分组向下路由到 R3。这个想法可以与我们已有的约束最少成本路由(constrained least-cost routing)的其他规则一起应用，例如流表中可能还有其他目的地或其他流的条目。"},{"en":"Centralized Traffic Engineering and Globally Optimal Decisions","zh_html":"<a href=\"#centralized-traffic-engineering-and-globally-optimal-decisions\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"centralized-traffic-engineering-and-globally-optimal-decisions\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 集中式流量工程(centralized traffic engineering)与全局最优决策(globally optimal decisions)"},{"en":"One major difference in the SDN model of custom routing protocols is centralization. In the original model, every router was running its own routing protocol. Now, we can have a single computer outside of the routers compute all the routes, and then use the flow table API to install those routes on the routers.","zh_html":"SDN 自定义路由协议模型的一个主要区别是集中化(centralization)。在原始模型中，每台路由器都运行自己的路由协议。现在，我们可以有一台独立于路由器的计算机来计算所有路由，然后使用流表 API 将这些路由安装到路由器上。"},{"en":"Centralization allows us to make globally optimal decisions. In a distributed protocol, each router is making the best decision for itself, but that might not be the best decision for other routers. In the centralized model, the boss can use its global view of the network to decide what’s best for everybody, and tell the routers to follow that decision.","zh_html":"集中化(centralization)允许我们做出<strong>全局最优决策(globally optimal decisions)</strong>。在分布式协议中，每台路由器都在为自己做出最佳决策，但这可能不是对其他路由器的最佳决策。在集中式模型中，\"老板\"可以利用其全局网络视图来决定对所有人来说什么是最优的，并告诉路由器遵循该决策。"},{"en":"Consider this network with two flows, S1-D at 20 Gbps, and S2-D at 100 Gbps. Assume we haven’t implemented support for splitting a flow onto multiple paths.","zh_html":"考虑这个有两个流的网络：S1-D 为 20 Gbps，S2-D 为 100 Gbps。假设我们还没有实现把流拆分到多条路径的支持。"},{"en":"Suppose the 20 Gbps S1-D flow starts first. Using constrained shortest path first, S1 could choose to use the bottom path. From the perspective of S1, this is a locally optimal decision (top and bottom paths both equally good).","zh_html":"假设 20 Gbps 的 S1-D 流先开始。使用约束最短路径优先(constrained shortest path first)，S1 可以选择使用底部路径。从 S1 的角度来看，这是一个局部最优决策(顶部和底部路径同样好)。"},{"en":"Later, the 100 Gbps S2-D flow starts. Now, using constrained shortest path first, S2-D doesn’t have any single path that meets its demands. The top path (20 Gbps) and bottom path (80 Gbps) both have insufficient capacity.","zh_html":"后来，100 Gbps 的 S2-D 流开始。现在使用约束最短路径优先，S2-D 没有一条能单独满足其需求的路径。顶部路径(20 Gbps)和底部路径(80 Gbps)的容量都不够。"},{"en":"The key problem here is, each individual router made its own decision independently, without coordination.","zh_html":"这里的关键问题是：每台路由器独立做出自己的决策，没有相互协调。"},{"en":"By introducing a centralized controller, the controller can look at the overall network structure and the demands of each flow, and assign paths to each flow more intelligently. The resulting decision is globally optimal, and increases network efficiency.","zh_html":"通过引入集中式控制器(centralized controller)，控制器可以查看整体网络结构和每个流的需求，更智能地为每个流分配路径。由此产生的决策是全局最优的，提高了网络效率。"},{"en":"Centralized traffic engineering can make even more intelligent routing decisions, depending on what the operator wants to optimize. For example, we could classify flows as high-priority or low-priority, and make decisions that optimize both network utilization and the needs of different applications.","zh_html":"集中式流量工程(centralized traffic engineering)可以根据操作员想要优化的目标做出更智能的路由决策。例如，我们可以把流分类为高优先级或低优先级，并做出同时优化网络利用率和不同应用需求的决策。"},{"en":"SDN in Datacenter Overlay","zh_html":"<a href=\"#sdn-in-datacenter-overlay\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"sdn-in-datacenter-overlay\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 数据中心覆盖网络(datacenter overlay)中的 SDN"},{"en":"In the previous section, we saw that virtual switches can apply encapsulation to connect the overlay and underlay networks. Given a virtual address, we can add a header with the corresponding physical address, which allows the packet to be sent along the underlay network. But, how do we know the mapping between virtual addresses and physical addresses?","zh_html":"在上一节中，我们看到虚拟交换机(virtual switches)可以应用封装(encapsulation)来连接覆盖网络(overlay)和底层网络(underlay)。给定一个虚拟地址(virtual address)，我们可以添加一个包含对应物理地址的首部(header)，使分组可以沿底层网络发送。但是，我们如何知道虚拟地址和物理地址之间的映射？"},{"en":"We also saw that encapsulation can be used to to support multiple tenants in a single datacenter, each running their own private network. Switches can add headers with a virtual network ID. But, how do we know which virtual network ID to use?","zh_html":"我们还看到封装(encapsulation)可用于支持单个数据中心中的多个租户(multiple tenants)，每个租户运行自己的私有网络(private network)。交换机(switches)可以添加带有虚拟网络 ID(virtual network ID)的首部。但是，我们如何知道使用哪个虚拟网络 ID？"},{"en":"A centralized SDN controller can be used in the datacenter to solve these problems. Each tenant can operate its own controller. When a new VM is created, the SDN learns about its virtual and physical addresses. Then, the SDN can update the forwarding tables in the other virtual switches, adding encapsulation rules with the new virtual/physical address mapping.","zh_html":"集中式 SDN 控制器(centralized SDN controller)可以在数据中心中用来解决这些问题。每个租户可以运行自己的控制器。当创建新的 VM 时，SDN 会了解其虚拟地址和物理地址。然后，SDN 可以更新其他虚拟交换机中的转发表，添加带有新虚拟/物理地址映射的封装规则。"},{"en":"For example, suppose Coke VM 2 is created with virtual IP 192.0.2.1 and physical IP 2.2.2.2. The SDN knows Coke VM 1 lives on physical server 1.1.1.1, so it can go to the virtual switch on 1.1.1.1 and add an encapsulation rule for the new Coke VM 2.","zh_html":"例如，假设 Coke VM 2 被创建，虚拟 IP 为 192.0.2.1，物理 IP 为 2.2.2.2。SDN 知道 Coke VM 1 在物理服务器 1.1.1.1 上，因此它可以去 1.1.1.1 上的虚拟交换机，为新的 Coke VM 2 添加封装规则。"},{"en":"The flow table at 1.1.1.1 might say: If you receive a packet with destination 192.0.2.1, add a header with Coke’s virtual network ID of 42. Also, add a header with the corresponding physical address 2.2.2.2. Then, send the packet along the underlay network.","zh_html":"1.1.1.1 上的流表可能是：如果你收到一个目的地址为 192.0.2.1 的分组，添加一个带有 Coke 的虚拟网络 ID 42 的首部。同时，添加一个带有对应物理地址 2.2.2.2 的首部。然后，沿底层网络发送该分组。"},{"en":"Benefits of SDN in Datacenter Overlay","zh_html":"<a href=\"#benefits-of-sdn-in-datacenter-overlay\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"benefits-of-sdn-in-datacenter-overlay\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 数据中心覆盖网络(datacenter overlay)中 SDN 的好处"},{"en":"Why might we use a centralized SDN architecture to support virtualization and multi-tenancy in datacenters, instead of a more standard routing protocol?","zh_html":"为什么我们要使用集中式 SDN 架构来支持数据中心中的虚拟化(virtualization)和多租户(multi-tenancy)，而不是使用更标准的路由协议？"},{"en":"The centralized SDN architecture allows us to cleanly split the overlay and underlay networks into two scalable layers. In a traditional architecture, the routers in the underlay network would have to process the custom encapsulation headers (e.g. virtual network IDs). SDN allows the underlay network to remain simple, without thinking about virtualization or multi-tenancy.","zh_html":"集中式 SDN 架构允许我们把覆盖网络(overlay)和底层网络(underlay)干净地拆分为两个可扩展的层。在传统架构中，底层网络中的路由器必须处理自定义封装首部(如虚拟网络 ID)。SDN 允许底层网络保持简单，不需要考虑虚拟化(virtualization)或多租户(multi-tenancy)。"},{"en":"Centralization gives us a simple way to implement the control plane at end hosts, without any complicated routing protocols. The controller learns about a new host and updates the other hosts accordingly. Without a centralized controller, we might need some complex distributed scheme to figure out which encapsulation headers to add.","zh_html":"集中化(centralization)为我们提供了一种在端主机(end hosts)上实现控制平面(control plane)的简单方法，无需复杂路由协议。控制器了解新主机，并相应地更新其他主机。如果没有集中式控制器，我们可能需要某种复杂的分布式方案来确定要添加哪些封装首部。"},{"en":"This SDN architecture also shows us why overlay networks can scale well. The SDN controller for a tenant only needs to know about the VMs belonging to that specific tenant. By contrast, if we used a traditional architecture, a new Coke VM might have to be advertised to all the other VMs, even Pepsi VMs.","zh_html":"这种 SDN 架构也展示了覆盖网络(overlay networks)为什么可以很好地扩展。租户的 SDN 控制器只需要知道属于该特定租户的 VM。相比之下，如果我们使用传统架构，一个新的 Coke VM 可能不得不向所有其他 VM 广播，甚至包括 Pepsi 的 VM。"},{"en":"SDN in Datacenter Underlay","zh_html":"<a href=\"#sdn-in-datacenter-underlay\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"sdn-in-datacenter-underlay\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 数据中心底层网络(datacenter underlay)中的 SDN"},{"en":"The datacenter underlay is a physical network, just like any other network, although with a special topology. Many general-purpose network challenges, like achieving high utilization of links, also apply to datacenter underlay networks. That means we can apply SDN to the underlay network as well.","zh_html":"数据中心底层网络(datacenter underlay)是一个物理网络(physical network)，就像任何其他网络一样，尽管具有特殊的拓扑结构。许多通用网络挑战，比如实现链路的高利用率，也适用于数据中心底层网络。这意味着我们也可以将 SDN 应用于底层网络。"},{"en":"SDN at the underlay network can help us efficiently route packets through the datacenter. For example, the operator might want to send mice flows along links with small delay, and elephant flows along links with high bandwidth.","zh_html":"底层网络(underlay network)上的 SDN 可以帮助我们高效地通过数据中心路由分组(packets)。例如，操作员可能希望把小鼠流(mice flows)沿延迟小的链路发送，把大象流(elephant flows)沿带宽高的链路发送。"},{"en":"In our underlay Clos network, per-flow load balancing (hash 5-tuple to choose a path) could still send multiple elephant flows along the same path. Even if two elephant flows used different paths, the paths might share links, and those links might become congested. An SDN controller could solve this problem by coordinating the flows and placing them onto non-overlapping paths.","zh_html":"在我们底层 Clos 网络中，每流负载均衡(per-flow load balancing，对五元组哈希以选择路径)仍可能把多个大象流发送到同一条路径。即使两个大象流使用了不同路径，这些路径也可能共享链路，而这些链路可能会变得拥塞。SDN 控制器可以通过协调这些流并将它们放置到不重叠的路径上来解决这个问题。"},{"en":"This 2022 Google paper describes eliminating layers in the Clos network (fewer links, cheaper datacenter) by using SDN to route traffic more intelligently.","zh_html":"这篇 2022 年的 Google 论文描述了通过使用 SDN 更智能地路由流量，来消除 Clos 网络中的层(更少的链路、更便宜的数据中心)。"},{"en":"Hyperscale datacenters often use SDN in both the overlay and underlay networks. These are usually implemented as decoupled systems. There’s one SDN thinking about the underlay, and a separate SDN thinking about the overlay.","zh_html":"超大规模数据中心(hyperscale datacenters)通常在覆盖网络(overlay)和底层网络(underlay)中都使用 SDN。这些通常作为解耦的系统实现。有一个 SDN 考虑底层网络，另一个独立的 SDN 考虑覆盖网络。"},{"en":"SDN in Wide Area Networks","zh_html":"<a href=\"#sdn-in-wide-area-networks\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"sdn-in-wide-area-networks\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 广域网(WAN)中的 SDN"},{"en":"In addition to datacenters, SDN can be useful in general wide-area networks, especially when efficient utilization of bandwidth is critical. For example, in the traffic engineering example from earlier, imagine if our 10 Gbps links were undersea cables. There’s no cheap way to add additional bandwidth, so optimizations have to instead focus on efficient utilization of the bandwidth we do have.","zh_html":"除了数据中心，SDN 在通用广域网(wide-area networks)中也很有用，特别是当带宽的高效利用至关重要时。例如，在之前的流量工程例子中，想象一下，如果我们的 10 Gbps 链路是海底电缆。没有廉价的方法来增加额外带宽，因此优化必须集中在高效利用我们已有的带宽上。"},{"en":"Drawbacks of Centralized Control","zh_html":"<a href=\"#drawbacks-of-centralized-control\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"drawbacks-of-centralized-control\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 集中式控制(centralized control)的缺点"},{"en":"Centralization doesn’t come for free, and has some drawbacks.","zh_html":"集中化(centralization)并非没有代价，它有一些缺点。"},{"en":"One drawback is reliability. In a traditional network, if a router fails, the routing protocol converges around the failure. The other routers can reroute traffic along other paths. By contrast, if the central controller fails, we don’t have a way to update the network anymore, and the routers don’t know how to adjust to changes.","zh_html":"一个缺点是可靠性(reliability)。在传统网络中，如果一台路由器发生故障，路由协议会围绕故障收敛(converge)。其他路由器可以沿其他路径重新路由流量。相反，如果中央控制器发生故障，我们就没有办法再更新网络了，路由器不知道如何适应变化。"},{"en":"Note: We’ve drawn the centralized controller as a single entity, but it doesn’t need to be run on a single server. The control plane computation could happen across multiple servers, where those servers coordinate to operate in a logically centralized way. This is different from the original model, where routers coordinated but still made their own distributed decisions. This helps to avoid having a single point of failure in hardware, though the controller as a logical unit could still fail (e.g. bug in the code).","zh_html":"注意：我们把集中式控制器画成了一个单一实体，但它并不需要运行在单台服务器上。控制平面(control plane)的计算可以跨多台服务器进行，这些服务器协调起来以逻辑集中的方式运作。这与原始模型不同，在原始模型中路由器相互协调但仍然做出自己的分布式决策。这有助于避免硬件层面的单点故障，但控制器作为逻辑单元仍可能失败(例如代码中的 bug)。"},{"en":"Centralization also introduces scalability problems. The controller has to make decisions for everybody, which can get expensive for large networks. By contrast, in a traditional network, each router only has to perform computations for itself.","zh_html":"集中化(centralization)也引入了可扩展性问题(scalability problems)。控制器必须为所有人做出决策，这对于大型网络来说可能会变得昂贵。相反，在传统网络中，每台路由器只需要为自己执行计算。"},{"en":"Centralization could also introduce different types of complexity. In a traditional network, we could buy a router and connect it, and it more or less starts working right away. With a central controller, we have additional infrastructure challenges. Where do we put this controller? How do we connect it to the individual routers in a reliable way?","zh_html":"集中化(centralization)还可能引入不同类型的复杂性。在传统网络中，我们可以买一台路由器并连接它，它或多或少可以直接开始工作。有了中央控制器，我们面临额外的基础设施挑战。我们把控制器放在哪里？如何以可靠的方式将它连接到各个路由器？"},{"en":"This is an active area of research, including a project by Sylvia Ratnasamy and Rob Shakir (Berkeley CS 168 instructors).","zh_html":"这是一个活跃的研究领域，包括 Sylvia Ratnasamy 和 Rob Shakir(Berkeley CS 168 讲师)的一个项目。"},{"en":"SDN in the Management and Data Plane","zh_html":"<a href=\"#sdn-in-the-management-and-data-plane\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"sdn-in-the-management-and-data-plane\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 管理平面(management plane)和数据平面(data plane)中的 SDN"},{"en":"We’ve seen SDN as a new way to implement the control plane. But, the initial frustration that led to the development of SDN was at the management plane.","zh_html":"我们已经看到 SDN 是实现控制平面(control plane)的一种新方式。但最初导致 SDN 发展的挫折来自管理平面(management plane)。"},{"en":"It turns out, many of the design paradigms that SDN used at the control plane can also apply to the management plane. For example, we saw that SDN relies on well-defined, programmatic APIs (e.g. OpenFlow).","zh_html":"事实证明，SDN 在控制平面上使用的许多设计范式也可以应用于管理平面(management plane)。例如，我们看到 SDN 依赖于定义良好的编程 API(如 OpenFlow)。"},{"en":"TODO ran out of time in SP24.","zh_html":"TODO ran out of time in SP24."}]},"/datacenter/topology.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Datacenter Topology","zh_html":"<a href=\"#datacenter-topology\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"datacenter-topology\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 数据中心拓扑(datacenter topology)"},{"en":"What is a Datacenter?","zh_html":"<a href=\"#what-is-a-datacenter\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"what-is-a-datacenter\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 什么是数据中心(datacenter)？"},{"en":"So far, in our model of the Internet, we’ve shown end hosts sending packets to each other. The end host might be a client machine (e.g. your local computer), or a server (e.g. YouTube). But, is YouTube really a single machine on the Internet serving videos to the entire world?","zh_html":"到目前为止，在我们的互联网模型中，我们看到端主机(end hosts)相互发送分组。端主机可能是客户端机器(如你的本地计算机)，也可能是服务器(如 YouTube)。但是，YouTube 真的只是互联网上一台为全世界提供视频服务的单台机器吗？"},{"en":"In reality, YouTube is an entire building of interconnected machines, working together to serve videos to clients. All these machines are in the same local network, and can communicate with each other to fulfill requests (e.g. if the video you requested is stored across different machines).","zh_html":"实际上，YouTube 是一整栋建筑中相互连接的机器，它们协同工作为客户提供视频服务。所有这些机器都在同一个本地网络中，可以相互通信以完成请求，例如，如果你请求的视频存储在不同的机器上。"},{"en":"Recall that in the network-of-network model of the Internet, each operator is free to manage their local network however they want. In this section, we’ll focus on local networks dedicated to connecting servers inside a datacenter (as opposed to users like your personal computer). We’ll talk about challenges unique to these local networks, and specialized solutions to networking problems (e.g. congestion control and routing) that are specifically designed to work well in datacenter contexts.","zh_html":"回想一下，在互联网的网络之网络模型中，每个运营方可以自由管理自己的本地网络。在本节中，我们将专注于连接数据中心内部服务器的本地网络，而不是像你的个人电脑这样的用户。我们将讨论这些本地网络特有的挑战，以及专门设计用于数据中心环境的网络问题(如拥塞控制和路由)的专门解决方案。"},{"en":"In real life, a datacenter is housed in one physical location, often on dedicated properties. In addition to computing infrastructure (e.g. servers), datacenters also need supporting infrastructure like cooling systems and power supplies, though we’ll be focusing on the local network that connects the servers.","zh_html":"在现实中，数据中心位于一个物理位置，通常在专用场地上。除了计算基础设施(如服务器)，数据中心还需要冷却系统和电源等配套基础设施，不过我们将重点关注连接服务器的本地网络。"},{"en":"Datacenters serve applications (e.g. YouTube videos, Google search results, etc.). This is the infrastructure for the end hosts that you might want to talk to. Note that this is different from Internet infrastructure we’ve seen so far. Previously, we saw carrier hotels, buildings where lots of networks (owned by different companies) connect to each other with heavy-duty routers. This is the infrastructure for routers forwarding your packets to various destinations, but applications are usually not hosted in carrier hotels.","zh_html":"数据中心为应用(applications)提供服务，例如 YouTube 视频、Google 搜索结果等。这是你可能想要通信的端主机的基础设施。注意，这与我们迄今看到的互联网基础设施不同。之前，我们看到了运营商旅馆(carrier hotels)，即许多不同公司拥有的网络使用重型路由器相互连接的建筑物。这是路由器把你的分组转发到各个目的地的基础设施，但应用通常不托管在运营商旅馆中。"},{"en":"A datacenter is usually owned by a single organization (e.g. Google, Amazon), and that organization could host many different applications (e.g. Gmail, YouTube, etc.) in a single datacenter. This means that the organization has control over all the network infrastructure inside the datacenter’s local network.","zh_html":"数据中心通常由单个组织(如 Google、Amazon)拥有，该组织可以在一个数据中心内托管许多不同的应用(如 Gmail、YouTube 等)。这意味着该组织可以控制数据中心本地网络内的所有网络基础设施。"},{"en":"Our focus is on modern hyperscale datacenters, operated by tech giants like Google and Amazon. The large scale introduces some unique challenges, but the concepts we’ll see also work at smaller scales.","zh_html":"我们的重点是现代超大规模数据中心(hyperscale datacenters)，由 Google 和 Amazon 等科技巨头运营。这种大规模引入了一些独特的挑战，但我们看到的概念在较小规模上也适用。"},{"en":"This map shows the wide area network (WAN) of all the networks owned by a tech giant like Google.","zh_html":"这张地图显示了像 Google 这样的科技巨头拥有的所有网络的广域网(WAN，wide area network)。"},{"en":"The peering locations connect Google to the rest of the Internet. These mainly consist of Google-operated routers that connect to other autonomous systems.","zh_html":"互联点(peering locations)把 Google 连接到互联网的其他部分。这些主要由 Google 运营的路由器组成，连接到其他自治系统(autonomous systems)。"},{"en":"In addition to peering locations, Google also operates many datacenters. Applications in datacenters can communicate with the rest of the Internet via the peering locations. The datacenters and peering locations are all connected through Google-managed routers and links in Google’s wide area network.","zh_html":"除了互联点，Google 还运营着许多数据中心。数据中心中的应用可以通过互联点与互联网的其他部分通信。这些数据中心和互联点都通过 Google 管理下的路由器和链路在 Google 的广域网(WAN)中相互连接。"},{"en":"Datacenters and peering locations optimize for different performance goals, so they’re often physically located in different places.","zh_html":"数据中心(datacenters)和互联点(peering locations)针对不同的性能目标进行优化，因此它们通常位于不同的物理位置。"},{"en":"Peering locations care about being physically close to other companies and networks. As a result, carrier hotels are often located in cities to be physically closer to customers and other companies.","zh_html":"互联点(peering locations)关心的是物理上靠近其他公司和网络。因此，运营商旅馆(carrier hotels)通常位于城市中，以在物理上更接近客户和其他公司。"},{"en":"By contrast, datacenters care less about being close to other companies, and instead prioritize requirements like physical space, power, and cooling. As a result, datacenters are often located in less-populated areas, sometimes with a nearby river (for cooling) or power station (datacenters might need hundreds of times more power than peering locations).","zh_html":"相反，数据中心(datacenters)不那么关心靠近其他公司，而是优先考虑物理空间、电力和冷却等需求。因此，数据中心通常位于人口较少的地区，有时靠近河流(用于冷却)或电站(数据中心可能需要比互联点多几百倍的电力)。"},{"en":"Why is the Datacenter Different?","zh_html":"<a href=\"#why-is-the-datacenter-different\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"why-is-the-datacenter-different\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 为什么数据中心(datacenter)与众不同？"},{"en":"What makes a datacenter’s local network different from general-purpose (wide area) networks on the rest of the Internet?","zh_html":"是什么让数据中心的本地网络与互联网其他部分的通用(广域)网络不同？"},{"en":"The datacenter network is run by a single organization, which gives us more control over the network and hosts. Unlike in the general-purpose Internet, we can run our own custom hardware or software, and we can enforce that every machine follows the same custom protocol.","zh_html":"数据中心网络由单个组织运营，这给了我们对网络和主机的更多控制。与通用互联网不同，我们可以运行自己的自定义硬件或软件，并且可以强制每台机器遵循相同的自定义协议。"},{"en":"Datacenters are often homogeneous, where every server and switch is built and operated exactly the same. Unlike in the general-purpose Internet, we don’t have to consider some links being wireless, and others being wired. In the general-purpose Internet, some computers might be newer than others, but in a datacenter, every computer is usually part of the same generation, and the entire datacenter is upgraded at the same time.","zh_html":"数据中心通常是同构(homogeneous)的，每台服务器和交换机构造和运行方式完全相同。与通用互联网不同，我们不必考虑有些链路是无线、有些是有线的。在通用互联网中，有些计算机可能比其他新，但在数据中心中，每台计算机通常属于同一代，整个数据中心同时升级。"},{"en":"The datacenter network exists in a single physical location, so we don’t have to think about long-distance links like undersea cables. Within that single location, we have to support extremely high bandwidth.","zh_html":"数据中心网络存在于单个物理位置，所以我们不必考虑像海底电缆这样的长距离链路。在那个单一位置内，我们必须支持极高的带宽。"},{"en":"Datacenter Traffic Patterns","zh_html":"<a href=\"#datacenter-traffic-patterns\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"datacenter-traffic-patterns\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 数据中心流量模式(datacenter traffic patterns)"},{"en":"When you make a request to a datacenter application, your packet travels across routers in the general-purpose Internet, eventually reaching Google-operated router. That router forwards your packet to one of the datacenter’s edge routers, which then forwards your packet to some individual server in the datacenter.","zh_html":"当你向数据中心应用发出请求时，你的分组穿越通用互联网中的路由器，最终到达 Google 运营的路由器。该路由器把你的分组转发到数据中心的边缘路由器之一，然后该边缘路由器把你的分组转发到数据中心中的某台服务器。"},{"en":"This one server probably doesn’t have all the information to process your request. For example, if you requested a Facebook feed, different servers might need to work together to combine ads, photos, posts, etc. It wouldn’t be practical if every server had to know everything about Facebook to process your request by itself.","zh_html":"这台服务器可能没有处理你请求所需的全部信息。例如，如果你请求一个 Facebook 信息流，不同的服务器可能需要协同工作来组合广告、照片、帖子等。如果每台服务器都必须知道关于 Facebook 的一切才能独立处理你的请求，那是不切实际的。"},{"en":"In order for the different servers to coordinate, the first server triggers many backend requests to collect all the information needed in your request. A single user request could trigger hundreds of backend requests (521 on average, per a 2013 Facebook paper) before the response can be sent back to the user. In general, there’s significantly more backend traffic between servers, and the external traffic with the user is very small in comparison.","zh_html":"为了让不同的服务器协调工作，第一台服务器会触发许多后端请求来收集你请求所需的所有信息。一个用户请求可能触发数百个后端请求(根据 2013 年 Facebook 的一篇论文，平均 521 个)，然后才能将响应发送回用户。通常，服务器之间的后端流量要多得多，与外部的用户流量相比非常小。"},{"en":"Most modern applications are dominated by internal traffic between machines. For example, if you run a distributed program like mapreduce, the different servers need to communicate to each other to collectively solve your large query. Some applications might even have no user-facing network traffic at all. For example, Google might run periodic backups, which requires servers communicating, but produces no visible result for the end user.","zh_html":"大多数现代应用以机器间的内部流量为主。例如，如果你运行像 mapreduce 这样的分布式程序，不同的服务器需要相互通信以共同解决你的大型查询。有些应用甚至可能完全没有面向用户的网络流量。例如，Google 可能会运行定期备份，这需要服务器之间通信，但对最终用户没有可见的结果。"},{"en":"Connections that go outside the network (e.g. to end users or other datacenters) are described as north-south traffic. By contrast, connections between machines inside the network are described as east-west traffic. East-west traffic is several orders of magnitude larger than north-south traffic, and the volume of east-west traffic is increasing in recent years (e.g. with the growth of machine learning).","zh_html":"前往网络外部的连接，例如到最终用户或其他数据中心，被描述为<strong>南北向(north-south)</strong>流量。相反，网络内部机器之间的连接被描述为<strong>东西向(east-west)</strong>流量。东西向流量比南北向流量大几个数量级，并且近年来东西向流量的规模还在增长，例如随着机器学习的发展。"},{"en":"Racks","zh_html":"<a href=\"#racks\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"racks\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 机架(racks)"},{"en":"A datacenter fundamentally consists of many servers. The servers are organized in physical racks, where each rack has 40-48 rack units (slots), and each rack unit can fit 1-2 servers.","zh_html":"数据中心从根本上由许多服务器组成。这些服务器被组织在物理机架(physical racks)中，每个机架有 40-48 个机架单位(插槽)，每个机架单位可容纳 1-2 台服务器。"},{"en":"We’d like all the servers in the datacenter to be able to communicate with each other, so we need to build a network to connect them all. What does this network look like? How do we efficiently install links and switches to meet our requirements?","zh_html":"我们希望数据中心中的所有服务器都能相互通信，因此我们需要构建一个网络来连接它们。这个网络是什么样的？我们如何高效地安装链路和交换机来满足需求？"},{"en":"First, we can connect all the servers within a single rack. Each rack has a single switch called a top-of-rack (TOR) switch, and every server in the rack has a link (called an access link or uplink) connecting to that switch. The TOR is a relatively small router, with a single forwarding chip, and physical ports connecting to all the servers on the rack. Each server uplink typically has a capacity of around 100 Gbps.","zh_html":"首先，我们可以连接单个机架内的所有服务器。每个机架有一个交换机，称为<strong>机架顶端交换机(TOR，top-of-rack switch)</strong>，机架中的每台服务器都有一条链路(称为<strong>接入链路(access link)</strong>或<strong>上行链路(uplink)</strong>)连接到该交换机。TOR 是一个相对较小的路由器，具有单个转发芯片和连接到机架上所有服务器的物理端口。每个服务器上行链路通常具有大约 100 Gbps 的容量。"},{"en":"Next, we have to think about how to connect the racks together. Ideally, we’d like every server to talk to every other server at their full line rate (i.e. using the entire uplink bandwidth).","zh_html":"接下来，我们必须考虑如何连接各个机架。理想情况下，我们希望每台服务器都能以其全线速(full line rate)，即使用全部上行链路带宽，与每台其他服务器通信。"},{"en":"Bisection Bandwidth","zh_html":"<a href=\"#bisection-bandwidth\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"bisection-bandwidth\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 对分带宽(bisection bandwidth)"},{"en":"Before thinking about how to connect racks, let’s develop a metric for how connected a set of computers are.","zh_html":"在考虑如何连接机架之前，让我们先定义一个指标来衡量一组计算机的连接程度。"},{"en":"Intuitively, even though all three networks are fully connected, the left network is the most connected, the middle network is less connected, and the right network is the least connected. For example, the left and middle networks could support 1-4 and 3-6 simultaneously communicating at full line rate, while the right network cannot.","zh_html":"直观上，即使所有三个网络都是全连接的，左图网络连接度最高，中间网络连接度较低，右图网络连接度最低。例如，左图和中间图网络可以同时支持 1-4 和 3-6 以全线速通信，而右图网络则不能。"},{"en":"One way to argue that the left network is more connected is to say: We have to cut more links to disconnect the network. This indicates that there are lots of redundant links, which allows us to run many simultaneous high-bandwidth connections. Similarly, one way to argue that the right network is less connected is to say: We only have to cut the 2-5 link to connect the network, which indicates the existence of a bottleneck that prevents simultaneous high-bandwidth connections.","zh_html":"论证左图网络更连接的一种方式是：我们需要切断更多链路才能使网络断开。这表明存在大量冗余链路(redundant links)，允许我们同时运行许多高带宽连接。类似地，论证右图网络连接度较低的一种方式是：我们只需要切断 2-5 链路就能使网络断开，这表明存在一个瓶颈(bottleneck)，阻止同时的高带宽连接。"},{"en":"Bisection bandwidth is a way to quantify how connected a network is. To compute bisection bandwidth, we compute the number of links we need to remove in order to partition the network into two disconnected halves of equal size. The bisection bandwidth is the sum of the bandwidths on the links that we cut.","zh_html":"<strong>对分带宽(bisection bandwidth)</strong>是一种量化网络连接程度的方法。要计算对分带宽，我们计算需要移除多少条链路才能将网络划分为两个大小相等的、互不相连的半区。对分带宽就是被切断链路的带宽之和。"},{"en":"In the rightmost structure, we only need to remove one link to partition the network, so the bisection bandwidth is just that one link. By contrast, in the leftmost structure, we need to remove 9 links to partition the network, so the bisection bandwidth is the combined bandwidth of all 9 links.","zh_html":"在最右边的结构中，我们只需要移除一条链路就能分割网络，所以对分带宽(bisection bandwidth)就是那一条链路的带宽。相反，在最左边的结构中，我们需要移除 9 条链路才能分割网络，所以对分带宽是所有 9 条链路的带宽之和。"},{"en":"An equivalent way of defining bisection bandwidth is: We divide the network into two halves, and each node in one half wants to simultaneously send data to a corresponding node in the other half. Among all possible partitions of nodes, what is the minimum bandwidth that the nodes can collectively send at? Considering the worst case (minimum bandwidth) forces us to think about bottlenecks.","zh_html":"定义对分带宽(bisection bandwidth)的另一种等价方式是：我们把网络分成两半，一半中的每个节点想同时向另一半中的对应节点发送数据。在所有可能的节点划分中，所有节点可以同时发送的最小带宽(minimum bandwidth)是多少？考虑最坏情况(最小带宽)迫使我们思考瓶颈(bottlenecks)。"},{"en":"The most-connected network has full bisection bandwidth. This means that there are no bottlenecks, and no matter how you assign nodes to partitions, all nodes in one partition can communicate simultaneously with all nodes in the other partition at full rate. If there are N nodes, and all N/2 nodes in the left partition are sending data at full rate R, then the full bisection bandwidth is N/2 times R.","zh_html":"连接度最高的网络具有全对分带宽(full bisection bandwidth)。这意味着没有瓶颈，无论你如何将节点分配到两个分区，一个分区中的所有节点都可以与另一个分区中的所有节点以全速率同时通信。如果有 N 个节点，左分区中的所有 N/2 个节点都以全速率 R 发送数据，那么全对分带宽就是 N/2 乘以 R。"},{"en":"Oversubscription is a measure of how far from the full bisection bandwidth we are, or equivalently, how overloaded the bottleneck part of the network is. It’s a ratio of the bisection bandwidth to the full bisection bandwidth (the bandwidth if all hosts sent at full rate).","zh_html":"<strong>超额订阅(oversubscription)</strong>是衡量我们距离全对分带宽有多远的指标，或者等价地，网络瓶颈部分的过载程度。它是实际对分带宽与全对分带宽(所有主机以全速率发送时的带宽)的比率。"},{"en":"In the rightmost example, assuming all links are 1 Gbps, then the bisection bandwidth is 2 Gbps (to split the left four hosts with the right four hosts). The full bisection bandwidth, achieved when all four left hosts were simultaneously sending data, is 4 Gbps. Therefore, the ratio 2/4 tells us that the hosts can only send at 50% of their full rate. In other words, our network is 2x oversubscribed, because if the hosts all sent at full rate, the bottleneck links would be 2x overloaded (4 Gbps on 2 Gbps of links).","zh_html":"在最右边的例子中，假设所有链路都是 1 Gbps，那么对分带宽(bisection bandwidth)是 2 Gbps(分割左边的四个主机和右边的四个主机)。当左边四个主机同时发送数据时实现的全对分带宽是 4 Gbps。因此，比率 2/4 告诉我们主机只能以其全速率的 50% 发送。换句话说，我们的网络是 2 倍超额订阅的，因为如果所有主机都以全速率发送，瓶颈链路将过载 2 倍(2 Gbps 链路上有 4 Gbps)。"},{"en":"Datacenter Topology","zh_html":"<a href=\"#datacenter-topology-1\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"datacenter-topology-1\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 数据中心拓扑(datacenter topology)"},{"en":"We’ve now defined bisection bandwidth, a measure of connectedness that’s a function of the network topology. In a datacenter, we can choose our topology (e.g. choose where to install cables). What topology should we build to maximize bisection bandwidth?","zh_html":"我们现在定义了<strong>对分带宽(bisection bandwidth)</strong>，这是一种依赖于网络拓扑(network topology)的连接度度量。在数据中心中，我们可以选择我们的拓扑，例如选择在哪里安装线缆。我们应该构建什么拓扑来最大化对分带宽？"},{"en":"One possible approach is to connect every rack to a giant cross-bar switch. All the racks on the left side can simultaneously send data at full rate into the switch, which forwards all that data to the right side at full rate. This would allow us to achieve full bisection bandwidth.","zh_html":"一种可能的方法是把每个机架连接到一个巨大的纵横式交换机(cross-bar switch)。左侧的所有机架可以同时以全速率向交换机发送数据，然后交换机会以全速率把所有数据转发到右侧。这将使我们能够实现全对分带宽(full bisection bandwidth)。"},{"en":"What are some problems with this approach? The switch will need one physical port for every rack (potentially up to 2500 ports). We sometimes refer to the number of external ports as the radix of the switch, so this switch would need a large radix. Also, this switch would need to have enormous capacity (potentially petabits per second) to support all the racks. Unsurprisingly, this switch is impractical to build (even if we could, it would be prohibitively expensive).","zh_html":"这种方法有什么问题？交换机需要为每个机架提供一个物理端口，可能多达 2500 个端口。我们有时把外部端口的数量称为交换机的<strong>基数(radix)</strong>，因此这个交换机需要很大的基数。此外，这个交换机需要巨大的容量，可能达到每秒数 Pb，来支持所有机架。不出所料，这种交换机是不切实际的，即使能造出来，也会贵得令人望而却步。"},{"en":"Fun fact: In the 2000s, Google tried asking switch vendors to build a 10,000-port switch. The vendors declined, saying it’s not possible to build this, and even if we could, nobody is asking for this except you (so there’s no profit to be made in building it).","zh_html":"趣闻：在 2000 年代，Google 曾试图让交换机厂商制造一个 10000 端口的交换机。厂商拒绝了，说这是不可能制造的，即使能造出来，除了你们也没人需要，所以造了也没有利润。"},{"en":"Another problem is that this switch is a single point of failure, and the entire datacenter network stops working if this switch breaks.","zh_html":"另一个问题是这个交换机是一个单点故障(single point of failure)，如果这个交换机坏了，整个数据中心网络都会停止工作。"},{"en":"Another possible approach is to arrange switches in a tree topology. This can help us reduce the radix and the bandwidth of each link.","zh_html":"另一种可能的方法是把交换机排列成树形拓扑(tree topology)。这有助于降低基数和每条链路的带宽。"},{"en":"What are some problems with this approach? The bisection bandwidth is lower. A single link is the bottleneck between the two halves of the tree.","zh_html":"这种方法有什么问题？对分带宽(bisection bandwidth)较低。一条单独的链路是树的两半之间的瓶颈。"},{"en":"To increase bisection bandwidth, we could install higher-bandwidth links at higher layers.","zh_html":"为了提高对分带宽(bisection bandwidth)，我们可以在较高层安装更高带宽的链路。"},{"en":"In this case, if the four lower links are 100 Gbps, and the two higher links are 300 Gbps, then we’ve removed the bottleneck and restored full bisection bandwidth.","zh_html":"在这种情况下，如果四条较低层的链路是 100 Gbps，两条较高层的链路是 300 Gbps，那么我们就消除了瓶颈，恢复了全对分带宽。"},{"en":"This topology can be used, although we still haven’t solved the problem where the top switch is expensive and scales poorly.","zh_html":"这种拓扑可以使用，尽管我们仍然没有解决顶部交换机昂贵且可扩展性差的问题。"},{"en":"Clos Networks","zh_html":"<a href=\"#clos-networks\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"clos-networks\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Clos 网络(Clos networks)"},{"en":"So far, we’ve tried building networks using custom-built switches, potentially with very high bandwidth or radix. These switches are still expensive to build. Could we instead design a topology that gives high bisection bandwidth, using cheap commodity elements? In particular, we’d like to use a large number of cheap off-the-shelf switches, where all the switches have the same number of ports, each switch has a low number of ports, and all link speeds are the same.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们尝试了使用定制构建的交换机来构建网络，这些交换机可能具有非常高的带宽或基数(base radix)。这些交换机仍然很昂贵。我们能不能设计一种使用廉价通用元件来提供高对分带宽的拓扑？具体来说，我们希望使用大量廉价的现成交换机，所有交换机具有相同的端口数，每个交换机的端口数较少，并且所有链路速率相同。"},{"en":"A Clos network achieves high bandwidth with commodity parts by introducing a huge number of paths between nodes in the network. Because there are so many links and paths through the network, we can achieve high bisection bandwidth by having each node send data along a different path.","zh_html":"<strong>Clos 网络(Clos network)</strong>通过在网络中的节点之间引入大量路径(paths)来用通用部件实现高带宽。因为有如此多的链路和路径穿越网络，我们可以让每个节点沿不同路径发送数据，从而实现高对分带宽。"},{"en":"Unlike custom-built switches, where we scaled the network by building a bigger switch, we can scale Clos networks by simply adding more of the same switches. This solution is cost-effective and scalable!","zh_html":"与定制构建的交换机不同，我们通过构建更大的交换机来扩展网络，而 Clos 网络可以通过简单地添加更多相同的交换机来扩展。这种解决方案既经济高效又可扩展(cost-effective and scalable)！"},{"en":"Clos networks have been used in other applications too, and are named for their inventor (Charles Clos, 1952).","zh_html":"Clos 网络也被用于其他应用，以其发明者(Charles Clos，1952 年)命名。"},{"en":"In a classic Clos network, we’d have all the racks on the left send data to the racks on the right. In datacenters, racks can both send and receive data, so instead of having a separate layer of senders and recipients, we can have a single layer with all the racks (acting as either sender or recipient). Then, data travels along one of the many paths deeper into the network, and then back out to reach the recipient. This result is called a folded Clos network, because we’ve “folded” the sender and recipient layers into one.","zh_html":"在经典的 Clos 网络中，左侧所有机架向右侧机架发送数据。在数据中心中，机架既可以发送也可以接收数据，因此不必有独立的发送层和接收层，我们可以有一个包含所有机架的单一层，它们既可以作为发送方也可以作为接收方。然后，数据沿着众多路径之一深入到网络中，再折返回来到达接收方。这种结果称为<strong>折叠式 Clos 网络(folded Clos network)</strong>，因为我们把发送方和接收方层\"折叠\"在了一起。"},{"en":"Fat-Tree Clos Topology","zh_html":"<a href=\"#fat-tree-clos-topology\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"fat-tree-clos-topology\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 胖树(fat-tree)Clos 拓扑"},{"en":"The fat-tree topology has low radix per switch, and achieves full bisection bandwidth. However, the switch at the top of the tree is expensive, scales poorly, and still represents a single point of failure.","zh_html":"胖树(fat-tree)拓扑每个交换机的基数(radix)较低，并能实现全对分带宽(full bisection bandwidth)。然而，树顶端的交换机很昂贵，可扩展性差，并且仍然是一个单点故障(single point of failure)。"},{"en":"The Clos topology allows us to use commodity switches to scale up our network. If we combine the Clos topology with the fat-tree topology, we can build a scalable topology out of commodity switches!","zh_html":"Clos 拓扑允许我们使用通用交换机(commodity switches)来扩展网络。如果把 Clos 拓扑与胖树拓扑结合起来，我们就可以用通用交换机构建一个可扩展的拓扑！"},{"en":"The topology presented here was introduced in a 2008 SIGCOMM paper titled “A Scalable, Commodity Data Center Network Architecture” (Mohammad Al-Fares, Alexander Loukissas, Amin Vahdat).","zh_html":"这里展示的拓扑由 2008 年 SIGCOMM 论文\"A Scalable, Commodity Data Center Network Architecture\"(Mohammad Al-Fares, Alexander Loukissas, Amin Vahdat)提出。"},{"en":"In a k-ary fat tree, we create k pods. Each pod has k switches.","zh_html":"在一个 k 元胖树(k-ary fat tree)中，我们创建 k 个 pod。每个 pod 有 k 台交换机。"},{"en":"Within a pod, k/2 switches are in the upper aggregation layer, and the other k/2 switches are in the lower edge layer.","zh_html":"在一个 pod 内，k/2 台交换机位于上层的聚合层(aggregation layer)，另外 k/2 台交换机位于下层的边缘层(edge layer)。"},{"en":"(Note: This topology is defined for even k, so that we can split up the switches evenly between the aggregation layer and edge layer).","zh_html":"(注意：这个拓扑是为偶数 k 定义的，这样我们可以把交换机均匀分配到聚合层和边缘层。)"},{"en":"Each switch in the pod has k links. Half of the links (k/2) connect upwards, and the other half (k/2) connect downwards.","zh_html":"pod 中的每台交换机有 k 条链路。一半的链路(k/2)向上连接，另一半(k/2)向下连接。"},{"en":"Consider a switch in the upper aggregation layer. Half (k/2) of its links connect up to the core layer (which connects the pods, discussed more below). The other half (k/2) of its links connect downwards to the k/2 switches in the edge layer.","zh_html":"考虑一台位于上聚合层(aggregation layer)的交换机。它的一半链路(k/2)向上连接到核心层(core layer，连接各个 pod，详见下文)。另一半链路(k/2)向下连接到边缘层中的 k/2 台交换机。"},{"en":"Similarly, consider a switch in the lower edge layer. Half (k/2) of its links connect upwards to the k/2 switches in the aggregation layer. The other half (k/2) of its links connect downwards to k/2 hosts in this pod.","zh_html":"类似地，考虑一台位于下边缘层(edge layer)的交换机。它的一半链路(k/2)向上连接到聚合层中的 k/2 台交换机。另一半链路(k/2)向下连接到该 pod 中的 k/2 个主机(hosts)。"},{"en":"Next, let’s look at the core layer, which connects the pods together. Each core switch has k links, connecting to each of the k pods.","zh_html":"接下来，让我们看看连接各个 pod 的核心层(core layer)。每台核心交换机有 k 条链路，分别连接到 k 个 pod。"},{"en":"There are \\((k/2)^2\\) core switches. How did we derive this number? There are k pods, and each pod has k/2 switches in the upper aggregation layer, for a total of \\(k^2/2\\) switches in the aggregation layer. Each aggregation-layer switch has k/2 links pointing upwards, for a total of \\(k^2/2 \\times k/2 = k^3/4\\) links pointing upwards. This means that the core layer will need to have a total of \\(k^3/4\\) links pointing downwards, to match the number of upwards links from the aggregation layer.","zh_html":"有 \\((k/2)^2\\) 台核心交换机(core switches)。我们是如何推导出这个数字的？有 k 个 pod，每个 pod 在上聚合层有 k/2 台交换机，总共 \\(k^2/2\\) 台聚合层交换机。每台聚合层交换机有 k/2 条向上链路，总共 \\(k^2/2 \times k/2 = k^3/4\\) 条向上链路。这意味着核心层需要有总共 \\(k^3/4\\) 条向下链路，以匹配来自聚合层的向上链路数量。"},{"en":"Each core layer switch has k links pointing downwards, so we need \\(k^2/4\\) core layer swiches (each with k links) to create \\(k^3/4\\) links pointing towards. This allows the number of links up from the aggregation layer to match the number of links down from the core layer.","zh_html":"每台核心层交换机有 k 条向下链路，因此我们需要 \\(k^2/4\\) 台核心层交换机(每台有 k 条链路)来创建 \\(k^3/4\\) 条向下链路。这样可以使来自聚合层的向上链路数量与来自核心层的向下链路数量相匹配。"},{"en":"We can also compute that there are \\((k/2)^2\\) hosts per pod in this topology. How did we derive this number? There are k/2 switches at the edge layer of each pod. Each edge-layer switch has k/2 downwards links to hosts, for a total of \\(k/2 \\times k/2 = (k/2)^2\\) hosts per pod. Note that each host is only connected to one edge-layer switch (a host is not connected to multiple switches in this topology). Since there are k pods in total, we can also deduce that there are \\((k/2)^2 \\times k\\) hosts in total in this topology.","zh_html":"我们还可以计算出在这种拓扑中，每个 pod 有 \\((k/2)^2\\) 个主机(hosts)。我们是如何推导出这个数字的？每个 pod 的边缘层有 k/2 台交换机。每台边缘层交换机有 k/2 条向下链路连接到主机，因此每个 pod 总共有 \\(k/2 \times k/2 = (k/2)^2\\) 个主机。注意，每个主机只连接到一台边缘层交换机，在这种拓扑中主机不连接到多台交换机。由于总共有 k 个 pod，我们还可以推导出在这种拓扑中总共有 \\((k/2)^2 \times k\\) 个主机。"},{"en":"k = 4, the smallest example, is unfortunately a little confusing because some of the numbers coincidentally end up the same (e.g. \\((k/2)^2 = k = 4\\)). For a clearer example, we can look at k = 6.","zh_html":"最小的例子 k = 4 不幸地有点令人困惑，因为一些数字巧合地相等，例如 \\((k/2)^2 = k = 4\\)。为了更清晰的例子，我们可以看 k = 6。"},{"en":"Each pod has k = 6 switches. k/2 = 3 switches are in the upper aggregation layer, and k/2 = 3 switches are in the lower edge layer.","zh_html":"每个 pod 有 k = 6 台交换机。k/2 = 3 台交换机位于上聚合层，k/2 = 3 台交换机位于下边缘层。"},{"en":"An edge layer switch has k/2 = 3 links downwards to 3 hosts, and k/2 = 3 links upwards to the 3 aggregation switches in the same pod.","zh_html":"边缘层交换机有 k/2 = 3 条向下链路连接到 3 个主机，以及 k/2 = 3 条向上链路连接到同一个 pod 中的 3 台聚合交换机。"},{"en":"An aggregation layer switch has k/2 = 3 links upwards to the core layer (specifically, to 3 different core layer switches), and k/2 = 3 links downwards to the 3 edge layer switches in the same pod.","zh_html":"聚合层交换机有 k/2 = 3 条向上链路连接到核心层(具体来说，连接到 3 台不同的核心层交换机)，以及 k/2 = 3 条向下链路连接到同一个 pod 中的 3 台边缘层交换机。"},{"en":"Each pod has k/2 = 3 edge switches, each connected to k/2 = 3 hosts, so each pod has a total of \\((k/2)^2 = 9\\) hosts. The topology has k pods in total, for a total of \\(k \\times (k/2)^2 = 54\\) hosts.","zh_html":"每个 pod 有 k/2 = 3 台边缘交换机，每台连接到 k/2 = 3 个主机，因此每个 pod 总共有 \\((k/2)^2 = 9\\) 个主机。拓扑总共有 k 个 pod，因此总共有 \\(k \times (k/2)^2 = 54\\) 个主机。"},{"en":"At the core layer, we have \\((k/2)^2 = 9\\) core switches. Each switch has k = 6 links, connecting downwards to each of the k = 6 pods.","zh_html":"在核心层，我们有 \\((k/2)^2 = 9\\) 台核心交换机。每台交换机有 k = 6 条链路，向下连接到 k = 6 个 pod。"},{"en":"In total, the core layer has \\((k/2)^2 \\times k\\) links pointing downwards (number of core switches, times number of links per switch). The aggregation layer has \\(k \\times (k/2) \\times (k/2)\\) links pointing upwards (number of pods, times number of aggregation switches per pod, times number of upwards links per aggregation switch). These two expressions match (and evaluate to 54 for k = 6), allowing the core layer to be fully-connected to the aggregation layer.","zh_html":"总的来说，核心层有 \\((k/2)^2 \times k\\) 条向下链路(核心交换机数量乘以每台交换机的链路数)。聚合层有 \\(k \times (k/2) \times (k/2)\\) 条向上链路(pod 数乘以每个 pod 的聚合交换机数乘以每台聚合交换机的向上链路数)。这两个表达式相等(对于 k = 6，计算结果为 54)，使得核心层能够与聚合层全连接。"},{"en":"This topology achieves full bisection bandwidth. If you split the pods into two halves (e.g. left half and right half), then every host in the left half has a dedicated path to a corresponding host in the right half. This allows all the hosts to pair up (one in left half, one in right half), and for each pair to communicate along a dedicated path, with no bottlenecks.","zh_html":"这种拓扑实现了全对分带宽(full bisection bandwidth)。如果你把 pod 分成两半(例如左半和右半)，那么左半中的每个主机都有一条专用路径连接到右半中的对应主机。这允许所有主机配对(一个在左半，一个在右半)，每对沿着专用路径通信，没有任何瓶颈。"},{"en":"Also, notice that this topology can be built out of commodity switches. Every switch has a radix of k links, regardless of which layer the switch is in. Also, every link can have the same bandwidth (e.g. 1 Gbps), and the scalability comes from the fact that we’ve created a dedicated path between any pair of hosts.","zh_html":"此外，请注意这种拓扑可以用通用交换机(commodity switches)构建。每台交换机的基数(radix)都是 k 条链路，无论交换机在哪一层。而且，每条链路可以有相同的带宽(例如 1 Gbps)，可扩展性来自于我们在任意一对主机之间创建了一条专用路径这个事实。"},{"en":"Another way to see the full bisection bandwidth is to delete links until the network is partitioned into two halves (pods in the left half, and pods in the right half).","zh_html":"另一种理解全对分带宽(full bisection bandwidth)的方法是删除链路直到网络被划分为两半：左半的 pod 和右半的 pod。"},{"en":"Each core layer switch has k links, one to each of the pods. This also means that each core layer switch has k/2 links to the left side, and k/2 links to the right side.","zh_html":"每台核心层交换机有 k 条链路，每个 pod 一条。这也意味着每台核心层交换机有 k/2 条链路到左侧，k/2 条链路到右侧。"},{"en":"In order to fully isolate one side (e.g. fully isolate the left side), then for each core switch, we’d have to cut k/2 links to the left side. There are \\((k/2)^2\\) core switches, and we have to cut k/2 links per switch, for a total of \\((k/2)^3\\) links cut. This means our bisection bandwidth is \\((k/2)^3\\) links (assuming every link has identical bandwidth).","zh_html":"为了完全隔离一侧(例如完全隔离左侧)，对于每台核心交换机，我们必须切断到左侧的 k/2 条链路。有 \\((k/2)^2\\) 台核心交换机，每台需要切断 k/2 条链路，总共切断 \\((k/2)^3\\) 条链路。这意味着我们的对分带宽就是 \\((k/2)^3\\) 条链路(假设每条链路带宽相同)。"},{"en":"There are \\((k/2)^2\\) hosts per pod, and k/2 pods in the left side, for a total of \\((k/2)^3\\) links in the left side. Similarly, there are \\((k/2)^3\\) links in the right side. If every host in the left side wanted to communicate with every host in the right side, then \\((k/2)^3\\) links’ worth of bandwidth would be needed. Our bisection bandwidth matches this number, which means that full bisection bandwidth is achieved.","zh_html":"每个 pod 有 \\((k/2)^2\\) 个主机，左侧有 k/2 个 pod，因此左侧共有 \\((k/2)^3\\) 条链路。类似地，右侧也有 \\((k/2)^3\\) 条链路。如果左侧每个主机都想与右侧每个主机通信，那么需要 \\((k/2)^3\\) 条链路的带宽。我们的对分带宽匹配这个数字，这意味着实现了全对分带宽。"},{"en":"How does this Clos fat-tree topology relate to the idea of racks and top-of-rack switches from earlier?","zh_html":"这种 Clos 胖树拓扑如何与之前提到的机架(racks)和机架顶端交换机(TOR，top-of-rack switches)的概念联系起来？"},{"en":"For specific nice values of k, we can arrange the hosts and switches inside a pod into separate racks, and connect the racks to to each other.","zh_html":"对于特定的 k 值，我们可以把 pod 内的主机和交换机安排到不同的机架中，并将这些机架相互连接。"},{"en":"For example, consider k = 48, the example value used in the original paper. This means that inside a pod, there are k/2 = 24 aggregation layer switches, k/2 = 24 edge layer switches, and \\((k/2)^2\\) = 576 hosts per pod.","zh_html":"例如，考虑原始论文中使用的示例值 k = 48。这意味着在一个 pod 内，有 k/2 = 24 台聚合层交换机，k/2 = 24 台边缘层交换机，以及 \\((k/2)^2 = 576\\) 个主机。"},{"en":"We can arrange the switches and hosts such that all 48 switches live in a rack that we place in the middle. Then, we can surround that rack of switches with 12 racks, each holding 48 hosts. This helps us fit all switches and hosts into identically-sized racks (48 machines per rack). Placing the switches in the middle rack also reduces the amount of physical wiring needed to build this topology.","zh_html":"我们可以安排交换机和主机，使所有 48 台交换机位于一个放在中间的机架中。然后，我们可以用 12 个机架包围那个交换机机架，每个机架容纳 48 个主机。这有助于我们把所有交换机和主机装入相同尺寸的机架(每个机架 48 台设备)。把交换机放在中间的机架也减少了构建此拓扑所需的物理布线量。"},{"en":"The middle rack has k = 48 switches. Each switch has k = 48 ports, for a total of \\(48^2 = 2304\\) ports in this rack.","zh_html":"中间的机架有 k = 48 台交换机。每台交换机有 k = 48 个端口，因此这个机架总共有 \\(48^2 = 2304\\) 个端口。"},{"en":"Of these \\(k^2 = 2304\\) ports, half of them (\\(k^2/2 = 1152\\)) connect switches inside the rack to each other. How did we derive \\(k^2/2\\)? It might help to look at some of the conceptual diagrams from earlier. Each of the k/2 aggregation layer switches has k/2 downward links, for a total of \\((k/2)^2\\) ports used. Similarly, each of the k/2 edge layer switches has k/2 upward links, for a total of \\((k/2)^2\\) ports used. This gives a total of \\(2 \\times (k/2)^2 = k^2/2\\) ports used.","zh_html":"在这 \\(k^2 = 2304\\) 个端口中，一半(\\(k^2/2 = 1152\\))用于机架内部的交换机相互连接。我们如何推导出 \\(k^2/2\\)？看看之前的一些概念图可能会有帮助。k/2 台聚合层交换机每台有 k/2 条向下链路，总共使用 \\((k/2)^2\\) 个端口。类似地，k/2 台边缘层交换机每台有 k/2 条向上链路，总共使用 \\((k/2)^2\\) 个端口。这总共是 \\(2 \times (k/2)^2 = k^2/2\\) 个端口。"},{"en":"Note that the links between aggregation and edge switches are connecting switches inside the same rack. Therefore, two ports are needed for each link (one from an aggregation switch, and one from an edge switch), and that’s why we doubled the \\((k/2)^2\\) value (or equivalently, accounted for that value twice at both the aggregation and edge layers).","zh_html":"注意，聚合层和边缘层交换机之间的链路连接的是同一机架内的交换机。因此，每条链路需要两个端口，一个来自聚合交换机，一个来自边缘交换机，这就是为什么我们把 \\((k/2)^2\\) 值翻倍，或者等价地，在聚合层和边缘层分别计算了该值。"},{"en":"Of the \\(k^2 = 2304\\) ports, another quarter of them (\\(k^2/4 = 576\\)) connect switches to hosts inside the same pod. How did we derive this number? Remember that there are \\((k/2)^2\\) hosts within a pod, and each host is connected to exactly one switch. Therefore, we need \\((k/2)^2 = k^2/4\\) ports on the switches to connect to hosts.","zh_html":"在 \\(k^2 = 2304\\) 个端口中，另外四分之一(\\(k^2/4 = 576\\))用于连接同一 pod 内的交换机到主机。我们如何推导出这个数字？记住每个 pod 有 \\((k/2)^2\\) 个主机，每个主机恰好连接到一台交换机。因此，交换机上需要 \\((k/2)^2 = k^2/4\\) 个端口来连接到主机。"},{"en":"Finally, of the \\(k^2 = 2304\\) ports, the remaining quarter (\\(k^2/4 = 576\\)) connect the pod to the core layer. How did we derive this number? Remember that there are \\((k/2)^2\\) core switches, and each core switch has a link to each pod. In other words, a pod has a single link to each of the \\((k/2)^2\\) core switches. Therefore, we need \\((k/2)^2 = k^2/4\\) ports on the switches to connect to the core switches.","zh_html":"最后，在 \\(k^2 = 2304\\) 个端口中，剩余的四分之一(\\(k^2/4 = 576\\))用于将 pod 连接到核心层(core layer)。我们如何推导出这个数字？记住有 \\((k/2)^2\\) 台核心交换机，每台核心交换机有一条链路连接到每个 pod。换句话说，一个 pod 有单条链路连接到每台 \\((k/2)^2\\) 个核心交换机。因此，交换机上需要 \\((k/2)^2 = k^2/4\\) 个端口来连接到核心交换机。"},{"en":"In summary: Out of \\(k^2\\) total ports, half of them are used to interconnect aggregation/edge switches in the same layer (connections happen entirely within the middle rack). Another quarter of them are used to connect edge switches to hosts in the pod (connections between the middle rack and the 12 surrounding racks with hosts). The last quarter of them are used to connect aggregation switches to the core layer (connections between the middle rack and other core-layer racks).","zh_html":"总结一下：在总共 \\(k^2\\) 个端口中，一半用于同一层内聚合/边缘交换机之间的互连(连接完全在中间机架内发生)。另外四分之一用于边缘层交换机连接到 pod 中的主机(中间机架与 12 个容纳主机的周围机架之间的连接)。最后四分之一用于聚合层交换机连接到核心层(中间机架与其他核心层机架之间的连接)。"},{"en":"Real-World Topologies","zh_html":"<a href=\"#real-world-topologies\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"real-world-topologies\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 实际拓扑(real-world topologies)"},{"en":"In this example (2008), there are many different paths between any two end hosts.","zh_html":"在这个例子(2008)中，任意两个端主机之间存在许多不同的路径。"},{"en":"In this paper (2015), various topologies were explored.","zh_html":"在这篇论文(2015)中，探索了各种拓扑结构。"},{"en":"Many specifics variants exist (2009, 2015), but they all share the same goal of achieving high bandwidth between any two servers.","zh_html":"存在许多具体的变体(2009, 2015)，但它们都有共同的目标：在任意两台服务器之间实现高带宽。"}]},"/datacenter/virtualization.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Virtualization and Encapsulation","zh_html":"虚拟化(virtualization)与封装(encapsulation)"},{"en":"Physical Datacenter Limitations","zh_html":"物理数据中心(physical datacenter)的限制"},{"en":"Datacenters are organized in a fixed and structured way. Identical servers are organized into racks, and racks arranged in some fixed topology. This approach has some benefits. For example, it gives us a natural way to assign hierarchical addresses.","zh_html":"数据中心(datacenters)以固定且结构化的方式组织。相同的服务器(servers)被放入机架(racks)，机架再按某种固定拓扑(topology)排列。这种做法有一些好处。例如，它为分配层次化地址(hierarchical addresses)提供了一种自然方式。"},{"en":"However, when we consider how applications are hosted on datacenters, the fixed organization of datacenters has some downsides. Suppose Google introduced a new service that they want to host in an existing datacenter. If we placed that application directly on a physical server, someone would have to physically install a new server, with its own IP address, for this application. If the service expands, more servers might need to be installed. If the server goes down, we’d have to wait for somebody to fix it. The key problem here is that changing physical infrastructure is hard, but we often want to add new hosts, scale up existing hosts, and move hosts quickly and frequently.","zh_html":"但是，当我们考虑应用(application)如何托管(host)在数据中心中时，数据中心这种固定组织方式也有缺点。假设 Google 推出一个新服务，希望把它托管在已有数据中心里。如果我们把这个应用直接放在一台物理服务器(physical server)上，就需要有人为这个应用实际安装一台新服务器，并给它自己的 IP 地址。如果服务扩张，可能还要安装更多服务器。如果服务器宕机，我们还得等人去修。这里的关键问题是：改变物理基础设施(physical infrastructure)很难，但我们又经常希望快速且频繁地添加新主机(hosts)、扩展已有主机，以及迁移主机。"},{"en":"Placing applications on physical servers also introduces scaling issues. Suppose Google’s new service is very lightweight, but needs a dedicated server (e.g. for security reasons). We’d have to assign an entire physical server to this lightweight service, and most of the server’s computing capacity would be unused.","zh_html":"把应用放在物理服务器上也会带来扩展性问题(scaling issues)。假设 Google 的新服务非常轻量，但出于安全(security)等原因需要独占服务器(dedicated server)。我们就必须把一整台物理服务器分配给这个轻量服务，而服务器的大部分计算能力(computing capacity)都会闲置。"},{"en":"This approach also has routing issues. Suppose we wanted to move the service to a different part of the datacenter building (e.g. because part of the building is undergoing maintenance). First, someone would have to physically move the server in the building. Also, in our hierarchical address model, we would need to assign this service a new IP address corresponding to its new physical location. Ideally, the application would prefer to keep the same address, regardless of its datacenter location.","zh_html":"这种方法还会带来路由(routing)问题。假设我们想把服务移动到数据中心建筑的另一个区域，例如因为建筑某部分正在维护。首先，有人必须在建筑内实际搬动这台服务器。此外，在我们的层次化地址模型(hierarchical address model)中，还需要给这个服务分配一个与新物理位置对应的新 IP 地址。理想情况下，应用更希望无论位于数据中心(datacenter)的哪里，都保持同一个地址。"},{"en":"Virtualization","zh_html":"虚拟化(virtualization)"},{"en":"We can use virtualization to solve these problems and give applications more flexibility, while maintaining the rigid physical structure of the datacenter. Virtualization allows us to run one or more virtual servers inside a physical server.","zh_html":"我们可以用虚拟化(virtualization)解决这些问题，在保持数据中心(datacenter)刚性物理结构的同时，为应用(applications)提供更大的灵活性(flexibility)。<strong>虚拟化(virtualization)</strong>允许我们在一台物理服务器(physical server)内部运行一个或多个虚拟服务器(virtual servers)。"},{"en":"The virtual server gives applications the illusion that they are running on a dedicated physical machine. However, in reality, multiple virtual servers might be running on the same machine. When the application tries to interact with hardware (e.g. disk, network card), it is actually interacting with a hypervisor in software. The hypervisor presents each virtual application with the same interface that real hardware would. The hypervisor itself runs on actual physical hardware, and can forward application requests (e.g. disk write, network packet send) to the hardware level.","zh_html":"虚拟服务器(virtual server)让应用产生一种错觉：自己正在一台专用物理机器(dedicated physical machine)上运行。但实际上，多台虚拟服务器可能运行在同一台机器上。当应用试图与硬件(hardware)交互，例如磁盘(disk)或网卡(network card)时，它实际是在与软件中的<strong>虚拟机监控器(hypervisor)</strong>交互。虚拟机监控器会向每个虚拟应用呈现真实硬件会提供的同样接口(interface)。虚拟机监控器本身运行在实际物理硬件上，并可以把应用请求，例如磁盘写入或网络分组发送，转发到硬件层。"},{"en":"With virtualization, if we have a new application, we can ask a hypervisor to start up a new virtual machine for this application. The hypervisor runs in software, so there’s no need to install any new server in the physical datacenter. Similarly, we can move hosts to a different physical machine, entirely in software.","zh_html":"有了虚拟化(virtualization)，如果有一个新应用，我们可以让虚拟机监控器(hypervisor)为这个应用启动一台新的虚拟机(virtual machine)。虚拟机监控器运行在软件中，所以不需要在物理数据中心(physical datacenter)里安装任何新服务器。同样，我们也可以完全通过软件把主机(hosts)移动到另一台物理机器上。"},{"en":"Virtualization allows multiple applications to share a physical server. The applications can be separated from each other, and can be managed by different people. This lets us use the compute resources in the datacenter more efficiently. This also allows us to have more hosts in the datacenter. For example, a single rack with 40 servers could have more than 40 end hosts.","zh_html":"虚拟化(virtualization)允许多个应用(applications)共享一台物理服务器(physical server)。这些应用可以彼此隔离，也可以由不同的人管理。这样能更高效地使用数据中心(datacenter)中的计算资源(compute resources)。它还允许数据中心拥有更多主机(hosts)。例如，一个有 40 台服务器的机架(rack)可以拥有超过 40 个端主机(end hosts)。"},{"en":"Virtual Switches","zh_html":"虚拟交换机(virtual switches)"},{"en":"The physical server has a single network card and a single IP address, but we need to give each virtual machine the illusion that it has its own dedicated network card and address. Also, switches might now have multiple virtual machines connected to a single physical port.","zh_html":"物理服务器(physical server)只有一块网卡(network card)和一个 IP 地址，但我们需要让每台虚拟机(virtual machine)看起来都有自己的专用网卡和地址。此外，交换机(switches)现在可能会看到多个虚拟机连接到同一个物理端口(physical port)。"},{"en":"In order to manage multiple network connections on the same physical machine, the server needs a virtual switch. This virtual switch runs in software on the server (it’s not a physical router), and performs the same operations as a real switch (e.g. forwarding packets). Each virtual machine is connected to the virtual switch, and the virtual switch is connected to the rest of the network.","zh_html":"为了在同一台物理机器上管理多个网络连接(network connections)，服务器需要一个<strong>虚拟交换机(virtual switch)</strong>。这个虚拟交换机以软件形式运行在服务器上，它不是物理路由器(physical router)，但执行与真实交换机相同的操作，例如转发分组(forwarding packets)。每台虚拟机都连接到虚拟交换机，而虚拟交换机再连接到网络的其他部分。"},{"en":"Note: Switches usually run on dedicated hardware to maximize efficiency. Virtual switches can be run in software on a general-purpose CPU because they only need to support a few virtual machines (lower capacity than what switches usually handle).","zh_html":"注意：交换机(switches)通常运行在专用硬件(dedicated hardware)上，以最大化效率。虚拟交换机(virtual switches)可以在通用 CPU(general-purpose CPU)上以软件方式运行，因为它们只需要支持少量虚拟机，容量要求低于普通交换机通常处理的规模。"},{"en":"Underlay and Overlay Network","zh_html":"底层网络(underlay network)与覆盖网络(overlay network)"},{"en":"With virtualization, we now have virtual hosts running on top of physical servers. Unlike physical servers, virtual hosts can be created, shut down, and changed rapidly.","zh_html":"有了虚拟化(virtualization)，我们现在可以在物理服务器(physical servers)之上运行虚拟主机(virtual hosts)。不同于物理服务器，虚拟主机可以快速创建、关闭和修改。"},{"en":"Virtual machines don’t necessarily use the same addressing scheme as the physical servers. Physical server IP addresses are defined by the physical datacenter topology (e.g. pods, racks). By contrast, virtual machine IP addresses are usually defined by some real-life hierarchy (e.g. countries, organizations). In particular, the virtual hosts on a single physical server don’t necessarily all have the same IP prefixes, so we can’t use the same aggregation tricks to scale up.","zh_html":"虚拟机(virtual machines)不一定使用与物理服务器(physical servers)相同的寻址方案(addressing scheme)。物理服务器 IP 地址通常由物理数据中心拓扑(physical datacenter topology)定义，例如 pod 和机架(racks)。相反，虚拟机 IP 地址通常由现实世界中的某种层次结构(hierarchy)定义，例如国家或组织。尤其是，同一台物理服务器上的虚拟主机(virtual hosts)不一定都有相同的 IP 前缀(IP prefixes)，因此我们不能使用同样的聚合技巧(aggregation tricks)来扩展。"},{"en":"If we tried to naively extend our routing schemes to support virtual machines, our forwarding tables would become very large, very quickly. Previously, we could aggregate by saying: “all servers in the blue pod have the same IP prefix, and they all have a next hop of R2.” Now, the servers in that blue pod could contain hundreds of virtual hosts, all with different IP addresses (no common prefix). We would need a separate forwarding entry for every virtual host. Also, if a virtual host moves to a different physical machine (keeping the same IP address), the routing protocol would have to re-discover paths to this virtual host. Can we find a way to avoid scaling the datacenter to support every VM address?","zh_html":"如果我们天真地扩展现有路由方案(routing schemes)来支持虚拟机(virtual machines)，转发表(forwarding tables)会很快变得非常大。以前，我们可以通过聚合(aggregate)来表达：“蓝色 pod 中的所有服务器都有相同 IP 前缀，并且下一跳(next hop)都是 R2。”现在，蓝色 pod 中的服务器可能包含数百个虚拟主机(virtual hosts)，它们都有不同 IP 地址，且没有共同前缀(common prefix)。我们需要为每个虚拟主机单独设置一条转发表项(forwarding entry)。另外，如果一个虚拟主机移动到另一台物理机器上，同时保持相同 IP 地址，路由协议(routing protocol)还必须重新发现到这个虚拟主机的路径(paths)。有没有办法避免让数据中心(datacenter)为了支持每个 VM 地址而膨胀？"},{"en":"The key problem here is that we now have two different addressing systems, one for virtual hosts, and one for physical hosts. Both addressing schemes work at the IP layer, but within the IP layer, there are now two sub-layers of abstraction that we need to think about.","zh_html":"这里的关键问题是：现在有两个不同的寻址系统(addressing systems)，一个用于虚拟主机(virtual hosts)，一个用于物理主机(physical hosts)。两种寻址方案都工作在 IP 层(IP layer)，但在 IP 层内部，现在又出现了两个需要考虑的抽象子层(sub-layers of abstraction)。"},{"en":"The underlay network handles routing between physical machines. The underlay network contains datacenter infrastructure like top-of-rack switches and spine switches. The underlay network scales well because we define hierarchical addresses using the physical datacenter topology.","zh_html":"<strong>底层网络(underlay network)</strong>负责物理机器(physical machines)之间的路由(routing)。底层网络包含数据中心基础设施(datacenter infrastructure)，例如机架顶端交换机(top-of-rack switches)和 spine 交换机(spine switches)。底层网络扩展性很好，因为我们可以利用物理数据中心拓扑(physical datacenter topology)定义层次化地址(hierarchical addresses)。"},{"en":"The overlay network exists on top of the physical topology (underlay), and it only thinks about routing between virtual machines. In practice, each virtual machine usually only needs to communicate with a few other virtual machines in the network. As a result, the overlay network scales well because a virtual machine does not need to know about every single other virtual machine.","zh_html":"<strong>覆盖网络(overlay network)</strong>存在于物理拓扑(physical topology，也就是 underlay)之上，并且只关心虚拟机(virtual machines)之间的路由(routing)。实践中，每台虚拟机通常只需要与网络中的少数其他虚拟机通信。因此，覆盖网络扩展性很好，因为一台虚拟机不需要知道每一台其他虚拟机。"},{"en":"Ideally, we’d like the two layers to think about addressing separately. The underlay network should not need to know about virtual host addresses (otherwise, it would scale poorly). Similarly, the overlay network should not need to know about every physical server in the datacenter (each VM only needs to know about a few other VMs).","zh_html":"理想情况下，我们希望这两层分别处理寻址(addressing)。底层网络(underlay network)不应该需要知道虚拟主机地址(virtual host addresses)，否则扩展性会很差。类似地，覆盖网络(overlay network)也不应该需要知道数据中心(datacenter)里的每台物理服务器；每台 VM 通常只需要知道少数其他 VM。"},{"en":"If we didn’t tell the underlay network about virtual host addresses, then if a datacenter switch gets a packet with a virtual IP as the destination, it would look in its forwarding table, not find any virtual IPs, and drop this packet. We need some way to bridge the gap between the overlay (thinking virtually) and the underlay (thinking physically).","zh_html":"如果不把虚拟主机地址(virtual host addresses)告诉底层网络(underlay network)，那么当数据中心交换机(datacenter switch)收到一个以虚拟 IP(virtual IP)为目的地的分组(packet)时，它会查自己的转发表(forwarding table)，找不到任何虚拟 IP，然后丢弃这个分组。我们需要某种方式弥合覆盖网络(overlay，按虚拟视角思考)和底层网络(underlay，按物理视角思考)之间的差距。"},{"en":"Encapsulation","zh_html":"封装(encapsulation)"},{"en":"To unify the overlay and underlay layers, we can use the same strategies with layering and headers that we used when we designed the Internet!","zh_html":"为了统一覆盖层(overlay layer)和底层(underlay layer)，我们可以使用设计互联网(Internet)时采用过的同样策略：分层(layering)和首部(headers)。"},{"en":"So far, we’ve treated IP as a single layer, and every packet has a single IP header, which understands the IP addressing system.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们一直把 IP 当作单独一层来看待，每个分组(packet)只有一个 IP 首部(IP header)，这个首部理解 IP 寻址系统(IP addressing system)。"},{"en":"Now that we have two IP sub-layers with two different IP addressing systems, we could introduce an additional header into the packet. For example, we could have two IP headers, where one header understands the overlay network, and the other header understands the underlay network. Or, we could use the original IP header for the underlay network, and introduce a new type of header (different from IP) for the overlay network.","zh_html":"现在我们有了两个 IP 子层(IP sub-layers)，它们使用两个不同的 IP 寻址系统(IP addressing systems)，因此可以在分组(packet)中引入额外首部(header)。例如，可以有两个 IP 首部：一个首部理解覆盖网络(overlay network)，另一个首部理解底层网络(underlay network)。或者，也可以把原来的 IP 首部用于底层网络，再为覆盖网络引入一种新的首部类型，它不同于 IP。"},{"en":"Now, our strategy for routing packets can combine the overlay and underlay networks. Suppose VM A wants to send a packet to VM B.","zh_html":"现在，我们的分组路由策略(routing packets)就可以结合覆盖网络(overlay network)和底层网络(underlay network)。假设 VM A 想向 VM B 发送一个分组(packet)。"},{"en":"VM A creates a packet with a single IP header, which contains the virtual IP address of B. (Remember, A is thinking in terms of overlay, and does not know about underlay physical IP addresses.) VM A forwards this packet to the virtual switch (on A’s physical server).","zh_html":"VM A 创建一个只有单个 IP 首部(IP header)的分组(packet)，其中包含 B 的虚拟 IP 地址(virtual IP address)。（记住，A 是按覆盖网络(overlay)来思考的，并不知道底层网络(underlay)中的物理 IP 地址。）VM A 将这个分组转发给虚拟交换机(virtual switch)，也就是 A 所在物理服务器上的虚拟交换机。"},{"en":"The virtual switch reads the header to learn B’s virtual IP address. Then, the virtual switch looks up the physical server address corresponding to B’s virtual IP address. (We haven’t described how to do this yet.) The virtual switch adds an additional outer header containing B’s physical server address. Adding the header is sometimes called encapsulation. At this point, the packet has two headers. The inner header (higher layer, overlay, added by VM A) contains B’s virtual IP address, and the outer header (lower layer, underlay, added by virtual switch) contains B’s physical server address. The virtual switch forwards this packet to the next hop switch, based on the physical server address.","zh_html":"虚拟交换机(virtual switch)读取首部(header)，得知 B 的虚拟 IP 地址(virtual IP address)。然后，虚拟交换机会查找与 B 的虚拟 IP 地址对应的物理服务器地址(physical server address)；我们还没有说明这个查找如何完成。虚拟交换机会添加一个额外的外层首部(outer header)，其中包含 B 的物理服务器地址。添加这个首部有时称为<strong>封装(encapsulation)</strong>。此时，分组(packet)有两个首部。内层首部(inner header，高层、overlay、由 VM A 添加)包含 B 的虚拟 IP 地址；外层首部(outer header，低层、underlay、由虚拟交换机添加)包含 B 的物理服务器地址。虚拟交换机根据物理服务器地址，把这个分组转发到下一跳交换机(next hop switch)。"},{"en":"The packet is sent through the underlay network. Each switch in the datacenter only looks at the outer header (underlay, physical server address) to decide how to forward the packet. (Remember, the datacenter switches think in terms of underlay, and do not know about the overlay virtual IP address.)","zh_html":"分组(packet)会穿过底层网络(underlay network)。数据中心(datacenter)中的每台交换机(switch)只查看外层首部(outer header，也就是 underlay 中的物理服务器地址)来决定如何转发分组。（记住，数据中心交换机按底层网络(underlay)来思考，并不知道覆盖网络(overlay)中的虚拟 IP 地址。）"},{"en":"Eventually, the packet reaches the destination physical server’s virtual switch. The virtual switch looks at the outer header (underlay) and notices that the destination physical server address is itself. The virtual switch removes the outer header, exposing the inner header inside. Removing the outer header is sometimes called decapsulation.","zh_html":"最终，分组(packet)到达目的物理服务器(destination physical server)上的虚拟交换机(virtual switch)。虚拟交换机查看外层首部(outer header，也就是 underlay)，发现目的物理服务器地址就是自己。虚拟交换机移除外层首部，露出里面的内层首部(inner header)。移除外层首部有时称为<strong>解封装(decapsulation)</strong>。"},{"en":"Finally, the virtual switch reads the inner header (overlay). This tells the virtual switch which of the VMs on the physical server the packet should be forwarded to.","zh_html":"最后，虚拟交换机(virtual switch)读取内层首部(inner header，也就是 overlay)。这会告诉虚拟交换机，应该把分组(packet)转发给物理服务器上的哪一台 VM。"},{"en":"In this process, encapsulation allowed us to think about routing at two different layers. The underlay was able to route packets using physical server addresses, without thinking about the overlay. Similarly, the VM in the overlay was able to send and receive packets without thinking about how to forward packets in the underlay. The virtual switches bridged the two layers by translating the virtual machine address into a physical server address, and adding and removing the extra underlay header.","zh_html":"在这个过程中，<strong>封装(encapsulation)</strong>让我们能够在两个不同层次上思考路由(routing)。底层网络(underlay)可以使用物理服务器地址(physical server addresses)路由分组，而不需要考虑覆盖网络(overlay)。类似地，覆盖网络中的 VM 可以收发分组，而不必考虑底层网络中如何转发分组。虚拟交换机(virtual switches)通过把虚拟机地址(virtual machine address)转换为物理服务器地址，并添加、移除额外的底层首部(underlay header)，把这两层连接起来。"},{"en":"Forwarding Tables with Encapsulation","zh_html":"带封装(encapsulation)的转发表(forwarding tables)"},{"en":"What entries should we install in the forwarding tables to support routing with encapsulation?","zh_html":"为了支持带封装(encapsulation)的路由(routing)，我们应该在转发表(forwarding tables)中安装哪些条目(entries)？"},{"en":"The virtual machines should install a default route that forwards every packet to the virtual switch on the physical machine.","zh_html":"虚拟机(virtual machines)应该安装一条默认路由(default route)，把每个分组(packet)都转发给物理机器上的虚拟交换机(virtual switch)。"},{"en":"The virtual switches need to implement some extra functionality to bridge the two layers. In particular, when you see a virtual address, you should apply encapsulation (add an outer layer) with the corresponding physical address. The forwarding table has entries for every destination VM that any of the VMs on this server might want to talk to. We can support this scale because we assume the VMs won’t need to talk to every other VM in the datacenter. Unlike standard routing algorithms, we don’t need any-to-any routing (we don’t need paths to every other VM).","zh_html":"虚拟交换机(virtual switches)需要实现一些额外功能来连接这两层。具体来说，当看到虚拟地址(virtual address)时，应当用对应的物理地址(physical address)进行封装(encapsulation)，也就是添加外层。转发表(forwarding table)中需要为本服务器上的任意 VM 可能想通信的每个目的 VM 设置条目。这个规模是可支持的，因为我们假设这些 VM 不需要与数据中心(datacenter)里的每个其他 VM 通信。不同于标准路由算法(standard routing algorithms)，我们不需要任意到任意(any-to-any)路由，也就是不需要到每个其他 VM 的路径。"},{"en":"Virtual switches also need an extra rule for decapsulating packets. If the outer (underlay) packet destination is the switch itself, you should decapsulate (remove the outer header) and pass the packet to the VM address in the inner header. This rule scales with the number of VMs on the server, which is usually small enough to be manageable.","zh_html":"虚拟交换机(virtual switches)还需要一条额外规则来对分组(packets)进行解封装(decapsulating)。如果外层(underlay)分组目的地是交换机自己，就应当解封装，也就是移除外层首部(outer header)，并把分组交给内层首部(inner header)中的 VM 地址。这条规则的规模随服务器上的 VM 数量增长，而这个数量通常足够小，可以管理。"},{"en":"Is it hard to add this functionality? Fortunately, virtual switches are implemented in software, so adding this functionality just requires writing code (no extra hardware needed). In practice, though, encapsulation is so common that it’s sometimes implemented in hardware anyway.","zh_html":"添加这种功能难吗？幸运的是，虚拟交换机(virtual switches)由软件实现，因此添加这种功能只需要写代码，不需要额外硬件(hardware)。不过在实践中，封装(encapsulation)非常常见，所以有时也会直接在硬件中实现。"},{"en":"The switches in the datacenter work exactly the same as they did before we introduced virtualization. The forwarding tables only contain physical server addresses, and we know that these can be scaled with aggregation tricks based on physical topology.","zh_html":"数据中心(datacenter)中的交换机(switches)工作方式与引入虚拟化(virtualization)之前完全相同。它们的转发表(forwarding tables)只包含物理服务器地址(physical server addresses)，而我们知道这些地址可以通过基于物理拓扑(physical topology)的聚合技巧(aggregation tricks)扩展。"},{"en":"Multi-Tenancy and Private Networks","zh_html":"多租户(multi-tenancy)与私有网络(private networks)"},{"en":"Datacenters are managed by a single operator, but different organizations might be running applications inside that datacenter. For example, a datacenter run by Google might have some virtual servers run by Gmail, and others run by Google Maps. This approach of hosting multiple services in one datacenter is called multi-tenancy.","zh_html":"数据中心(datacenters)由单个运营方(operator)管理，但不同组织(organizations)可能会在同一个数据中心内部运行应用(applications)。例如，Google 运营的数据中心里，可能有一些虚拟服务器(virtual servers)由 Gmail 使用，另一些由 Google Maps 使用。这种在一个数据中心中托管多个服务(services)的方法称为<strong>多租户(multi-tenancy)</strong>。"},{"en":"Cloud providers also use datacenters to supply virtual machines for customers. For example, Amazon Web Services (AWS) and Google Cloud Platform (GCP) allow users to start up a virtual machine in a datacenter, do whatever they want, and destroy the virtual machine when they’re done.","zh_html":"云服务提供商(cloud providers)也使用数据中心为客户提供虚拟机(virtual machines)。例如，Amazon Web Services(AWS) 和 Google Cloud Platform(GCP) 允许用户在数据中心中启动一台虚拟机，做自己需要的事情，完成后再销毁这台虚拟机。"},{"en":"One problem with multi-tenancy is, we don’t always want the different tenants to be able to communicate with each other. For example, if a customer requests a VM, they probably shouldn’t be able to connect to every other VM in the datacenter.","zh_html":"多租户(multi-tenancy)的一个问题是，我们并不总是希望不同租户(tenants)能够彼此通信。例如，如果某个客户申请了一台 VM，它大概不应该能够连接到数据中心(datacenter)中的每一台其他 VM。"},{"en":"Another problem is, tenants in a datacenter don’t coordinate with each other when choosing addresses. For example, suppose our datacenter had two tenants, Pepsi and Coke. Each tenant creates their own private network, where they assign internal IP addresses to virtual machines. The private network is only for hosts inside the datacenter to communicate with each other, and these hosts will never be contacted from the public Internet. Because the networks are private, the two tenants can both use addresses in the same specially-allocated private ranges (RFC 1918 addresses). Pepsi’s private network might have a VM with IP address 192.0.2.2, and Coke’s private network might have a different VM with IP address 192.0.2.2. (In practice, we use private ranges in order to reuse IPv4 addresses, since we’re running out of them.)","zh_html":"另一个问题是，数据中心(datacenter)中的租户(tenants)在选择地址时不会彼此协调。例如，假设我们的数据中心有两个租户 Pepsi 和 Coke。每个租户都会创建自己的私有网络(private network)，并在其中为虚拟机(virtual machines)分配内部 IP 地址(internal IP addresses)。私有网络只供数据中心内部主机(hosts)相互通信，这些主机永远不会从公共互联网(public Internet)直接访问。由于这些网络是私有的，两个租户都可以使用同一组专门分配的私有地址范围(private ranges，即 RFC 1918 地址)。Pepsi 的私有网络中可能有一台 IP 地址为 192.0.2.2 的 VM，Coke 的私有网络中也可能有另一台 IP 地址为 192.0.2.2 的 VM。（实践中，我们使用私有地址范围是为了复用 IPv4 地址，因为 IPv4 地址正在耗尽。）"},{"en":"From the perspective of each tenant, this is not a problem. Pepsi’s 192.0.2.2 will never communicate with Coke’s 192.0.2.2, and neither host is accessible to the global Internet. However, this is a problem for the datacenter. If we use destination-based forwarding, and we see a packet with destination 192.0.2.2, we have no idea which VM this address is referring to.","zh_html":"从每个租户(tenant)的角度看，这不是问题。Pepsi 的 192.0.2.2 永远不会与 Coke 的 192.0.2.2 通信，而且两台主机(hosts)都不能被全球互联网(global Internet)访问。然而，这对数据中心(datacenter)来说是个问题。如果我们使用基于目的地的转发(destination-based forwarding)，并看到一个目的地为 192.0.2.2 的分组(packet)，就不知道这个地址到底指的是哪一台 VM。"},{"en":"Duplicate IP addresses occur in practice for two reasons. First, datacenters usually don’t have control over what addresses the tenants are assigning to their VMs. Second, in IP, it’s standard practice to use specific ranges for private networks, which often leads to duplicate addresses.","zh_html":"实践中出现重复 IP 地址(duplicate IP addresses)主要有两个原因。第一，数据中心(datacenters)通常无法控制租户(tenants)给自己的 VM 分配哪些地址。第二，在 IP 中，为私有网络(private networks)使用特定地址范围是一种标准做法，而这经常会导致地址重复。"},{"en":"Encapsulation For Multi-Tenancy","zh_html":"用于多租户(multi-tenancy)的封装(encapsulation)"},{"en":"We can use the idea of encapsulation again to solve this problem. We can add a new header that contains a virtual network ID for identifying a specific tenant (e.g. Pepsi has ID 1, Coke has ID 2). This new header doesn’t contain information for forwarding and routing, but it provides additional context. Now, if a physical server has VMs for multiple tenants, it can pass the packet up to the correct virtual network.","zh_html":"我们可以再次使用封装(encapsulation)的思想来解决这个问题。可以添加一个新首部(header)，其中包含<strong>虚拟网络 ID(virtual network ID)</strong>，用于标识特定租户(tenant)，例如 Pepsi 的 ID 是 1，Coke 的 ID 是 2。这个新首部不包含用于转发(forwarding)和路由(routing)的信息，但它提供了额外上下文(context)。现在，如果一台物理服务器(physical server)上有多个租户的 VM，它就可以把分组(packet)交给正确的虚拟网络(virtual network)。"},{"en":"When a virtual switch receives a packet and unwraps the outer (underlay) header, it looks at our new header to decide which tenant the packet is meant for. Then, it looks at the overlay header to forward the packet to a specific VM belonging to the correct tenant.","zh_html":"当虚拟交换机(virtual switch)收到一个分组(packet)并拆开外层(underlay)首部后，它会查看这个新首部来判断该分组属于哪个租户(tenant)。然后，它再查看覆盖层首部(overlay header)，把分组转发给正确租户下的某台具体 VM。"},{"en":"Stacking Encapsulations","zh_html":"叠加封装(stacking encapsulations)"},{"en":"We can use the idea of encapsulation multiple times, adding multiple new headers to support both virtualization and multi-tenancy.","zh_html":"我们可以多次使用封装(encapsulation)的思想，添加多个新首部(headers)，同时支持虚拟化(virtualization)和多租户(multi-tenancy)。"},{"en":"To start, the virtual machine creates a standard TCP/IP packet, with a virtual IP destination.","zh_html":"首先，虚拟机(virtual machine)创建一个标准 TCP/IP 分组(packet)，其中目的地是虚拟 IP(virtual IP)。"},{"en":"In the first encapsulation step, we add a virtual network header, which tells us which tenant sent this packet. This helps us disambiguate two tenants using the same address, and also prevents packets from being sent to a different tenant.","zh_html":"在第一步封装(encapsulation)中，我们添加一个虚拟网络首部(virtual network header)，它告诉我们这个分组(packet)由哪个租户(tenant)发送。这有助于区分两个使用相同地址的租户，也能防止分组被发送到另一个租户。"},{"en":"In the second encapsulation step, we add an underlay network header, which tells us the physical server address corresponding to the virtual IP destination.","zh_html":"在第二步封装(encapsulation)中，我们添加一个底层网络首部(underlay network header)，它告诉我们与虚拟 IP 目的地(virtual IP destination)对应的物理服务器地址(physical server address)。"},{"en":"The layers of abstraction still hold when we stack encapsulations. The underlay network doesn’t need to know that multiple tenants are in the same datacenter. The underlay network just looks at the outermost header for a physical server address, and forwards the packet accordingly.","zh_html":"当我们叠加封装(stacking encapsulations)时，抽象层次(layers of abstraction)仍然成立。底层网络(underlay network)不需要知道同一个数据中心(datacenter)里存在多个租户(tenants)。底层网络只查看最外层首部(outermost header)中的物理服务器地址，然后相应地转发分组(packet)。"},{"en":"The decapsulation step works in reverse order. The virtual switch on the destination server receives a packet with two extra headers.","zh_html":"解封装(decapsulation)步骤按相反顺序进行。目的服务器(destination server)上的虚拟交换机(virtual switch)会收到一个带有两个额外首部(headers)的分组(packet)。"},{"en":"In the first decapsulation step, we remove the outer underlay header. This is no longer necessary since the packet has reached the destination physical server.","zh_html":"在第一步解封装(decapsulation)中，我们移除外层底层首部(outer underlay header)。因为分组(packet)已经到达目的物理服务器(destination physical server)，这个首部不再需要。"},{"en":"In the second decapsulation step, we use the virtual network header to decide which set of VMs we should think about. The physical server might have VMs for multiple tenants, and this helps narrows down to a single tenant.","zh_html":"在第二步解封装(decapsulation)中，我们使用虚拟网络首部(virtual network header)来决定应该考虑哪一组 VM。物理服务器(physical server)上可能有多个租户(tenants)的 VM，而这个首部帮助我们缩小到单个租户。"},{"en":"Finally, we use the innermost IP header to send the packet to the correct VM in the correct virtual network.","zh_html":"最后，我们使用最内层 IP 首部(innermost IP header)，把分组(packet)发送到正确虚拟网络(virtual network)中的正确 VM。"},{"en":"Note: With encapsulation, we have to be careful when reading the 5-tuple (IPs, ports, and protocol) for load-balancing packets across multiple paths. Fortunately, modern router hardware is good at parsing packets to understand where the relevant headers are located in the packet, even if additional headers are inserted.","zh_html":"注意：使用封装(encapsulation)时，如果为了在多条路径(multiple paths)之间对分组(packets)做负载均衡(load-balancing)而读取五元组(5-tuple，即 IP、端口和协议)，就必须小心。幸运的是，现代路由器硬件(router hardware)很擅长解析分组，即使插入了额外首部(headers)，也能理解相关首部位于分组中的哪里。"},{"en":"In practice, many different protocols exist for encapsulation. We could use IP-in-IP to support two IP headers (one for overlay, one for underlay).","zh_html":"实践中，存在许多不同的封装协议(encapsulation protocols)。我们可以使用 IP-in-IP 来支持两个 IP 首部(IP headers)：一个用于覆盖网络(overlay)，一个用于底层网络(underlay)。"},{"en":"MPLS is a simple header for adding a label that identifies a service (e.g. a virtual network, a tenant). This can be used to add encapsulation for multi-tenancy.","zh_html":"MPLS 是一种简单首部(header)，可添加一个标签(label)来标识某个服务(service)，例如虚拟网络(virtual network)或租户(tenant)。它可以用于为多租户(multi-tenancy)添加封装(encapsulation)。"},{"en":"As datacenters have become more popular, many other protocols like GRE, VXLAN, and GENEVE have been developed. Most of these work over IP, so these custom protocols are the inner overlay header, and regular IP is the outer underlay header.","zh_html":"随着数据中心(datacenters)越来越普及，GRE、VXLAN 和 GENEVE 等许多其他协议(protocols)也被开发出来。它们大多运行在 IP 之上，因此这些自定义协议(custom protocols)充当内层覆盖首部(inner overlay header)，而普通 IP 则充当外层底层首部(outer underlay header)。"}]},"/end-to-end/arp.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"ARP: Connecting Layers 2 and 3","zh_html":"ARP：连接第 2 层和第 3 层"},{"en":"Connecting Layers 2 and 3","zh_html":"连接第 2 层和第 3 层"},{"en":"Recall that packets get additional headers wrapped around them as they move down the stack, to lower layers. To send an IP packet, we first fill in its destination IP at Layer 3. Then, we pass that packet down to Layer 2, where we have to add a MAC address to send the packet along the link. What MAC address do we add?","zh_html":"回想一下，分组(packets)沿协议栈向下移动到更低层时，会被额外的首部(headers)包裹。为了发送一个 IP 分组，我们首先在第 3 层填入它的目的 IP。然后，我们把这个分组向下交给第 2 层，在那里必须添加一个 MAC 地址，才能沿链路(link)发送这个分组。那么我们应该添加哪个 MAC 地址？"},{"en":"First, we need to check if the destination IP is somebody in our own local network, or somebody in a different local network. To determine this, the sender’s forwarding table will have an entry indicating the range of local IP addresses, sometimes called our subnet. For example, the entry might say that 192.0.2.0/24 is direct, which means all addresses between 192.0.2.0 and 192.0.2.255 are on the same local network. The table also has a default route, saying that all other non-local destinations should be forwarded to the router.","zh_html":"首先，我们需要检查目的 IP 是位于本地网络(local network)中的某台机器，还是位于另一个本地网络中的某台机器。为了判断这一点，发送方的转发表(forwarding table)会有一个条目，表示本地 IP 地址的范围，有时称为我们的<strong>子网(subnet)</strong>。例如，该条目可能写着 192.0.2.0/24 是 direct，这意味着 192.0.2.0 到 192.0.2.255 之间的所有地址都在同一个本地网络中。该表还会有一条默认路由(default route)，表示所有其他非本地目的地都应被转发给路由器(router)。"},{"en":"If the destination IP is in our subnet, we need some way to translate between the destination IP address and that machine’s corresponding MAC address. If the destination is outside our subnet, we need some way to translate the router’s IP address (from the forwarding table) to its corresponding MAC address, so we can send the packet to the router.","zh_html":"如果目的 IP 位于我们的子网(subnet)中，我们需要某种方法把目的 IP 地址转换为那台机器对应的 MAC 地址。如果目的地在我们的子网之外，我们需要某种方法把路由器的 IP 地址(来自转发表)转换为对应的 MAC 地址，这样才能把分组发送给路由器。"},{"en":"One naive solution is to broadcast every packet, so that the destination or router will definitely receive and process it. However, this is inefficient. It forces everyone to parse every packet (e.g. read the Layer 3 headers) to check if the packet is meant for them. Also, if the Layer 2 network has more than one link, the switches at Layer 2 have to flood the packet across all the links.","zh_html":"一种朴素的解决方案是广播(broadcast)每个分组，这样目的主机或路由器一定会收到并处理它。然而，这种方法效率很低。它迫使每台机器解析每个分组(例如读取第 3 层首部)，以检查该分组是否发给自己。此外，如果第 2 层网络有多条链路，第 2 层的交换机(switches)就必须把该分组泛洪(flood)到所有链路上。"},{"en":"A better approach would be to translate the destination IP address to its corresponding MAC address (if local) or the router’s MAC address (if non-local), and unicast the packet at Layer 2.","zh_html":"更好的方法是把目的 IP 地址转换为对应的 MAC 地址(如果目的地是本地的)，或者转换为路由器的 MAC 地址(如果目的地不是本地的)，然后在第 2 层单播(unicast)该分组。"},{"en":"ARP: Address Resolution Protocol","zh_html":"ARP：地址解析协议(Address Resolution Protocol)"},{"en":"ARP (Address Resolution Protocol) allows machines to translate an IP address into its corresponding MAC address.","zh_html":"<strong>地址解析协议(ARP, Address Resolution Protocol)</strong>允许机器把 IP 地址转换为对应的 MAC 地址。"},{"en":"To request a translation, a machine can broadcast a soliciation message: “I have MAC address f8:ff:c2:2b:36:16. What is the MAC address of the machine with IP 192.0.2.1?”","zh_html":"为了请求一次转换，机器可以广播一条请求消息：“我的 MAC 地址是 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">f8:ff:c2:2b:36:16</code>。IP 为 192.0.2.1 的机器的 MAC 地址是什么？”"},{"en":"All machines who are not this IP address ignore the message. The user who has this IP address unicasts a reply to the sender’s MAC address, saying ``I am 192.0.2.1, and my MAC address is a2:ff:28:02:f2:10.","zh_html":"所有不拥有这个 IP 地址的机器都会忽略该消息。拥有这个 IP 地址的用户会向发送方的 MAC 地址单播一条回复，说明“我是 192.0.2.1，我的 MAC 地址是 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">a2:ff:28:02:f2:10</code>。”"},{"en":"Machines can also broadcast their own IP-to-MAC mapping to everybody, even if nobody asks.","zh_html":"机器也可以把自己的 IP 到 MAC 映射(IP-to-MAC mapping)广播给所有人，即使没有人请求。"},{"en":"When you receive an IP-to-MAC mapping, you can add it to your local ARP Table, which caches these mappings for the future. The table also includes an expiry date for each entry, since IP addresses aren’t permanently assigned to a computer. A different computer could get assigned the same IP address, or the same computer could change IP addresses. (TODO: interfaces?)","zh_html":"当你收到一个 IP 到 MAC 映射时，可以把它加入本地<strong>ARP 表(ARP Table)</strong>，该表会缓存这些映射以供将来使用。表中还会为每个条目包含一个过期时间，因为 IP 地址并不是永久分配给某台计算机的。另一台计算机可能被分配到同一个 IP 地址，或者同一台计算机也可能更换 IP 地址。(TODO: interfaces?)"},{"en":"Step 1:","zh_html":"第 1 步："},{"en":"Step 2:","zh_html":"第 2 步："},{"en":"Step 3:","zh_html":"第 3 步："},{"en":"Step 4:","zh_html":"第 4 步："},{"en":"Note that ARP runs directly on Layer 2, so all packets are sent and received over Ethernet, not IP.","zh_html":"注意，ARP 直接运行在第 2 层，因此所有分组都是通过以太网(Ethernet)发送和接收的，而不是通过 IP。"},{"en":"Connecting ARP and Forwarding Tables","zh_html":"连接 ARP 与转发表"},{"en":"Recall that in a router’s forwarding table, we would sometimes include an entry indicating that a host is directly connected to the router.","zh_html":"回想一下，在路由器的转发表(forwarding table)中，我们有时会包含一个条目，表示某台主机(host)直接连接到该路由器。"},{"en":"In reality, the router’s forwarding table contains a single entry, mapping the entire subnet’s range of IP addresses to be direct. If the router receives a packet whose destination is in this local range, the router runs ARP to find the corresponding MAC address, and uses Layer 2 to send the packet to the correct host on the link.","zh_html":"实际上，路由器的转发表包含一个单独的条目，把整个子网(subnet)的 IP 地址范围映射为 direct。如果路由器收到一个目的地位于这个本地范围内的分组，路由器就运行 ARP 来找到对应的 MAC 地址，并使用第 2 层把分组发送到链路上的正确主机。"},{"en":"This also helps us in the case where multiple hosts are connected on the same link. In our conceptual picture, we’d say that Host A is directly connected on Port 1. Multiple hosts might be on that link, so by using ARP, we can create a Layer 2 packet that gets unicast to only Host A, and not other computers on the link.","zh_html":"当多台主机连接在同一条链路上时，这也很有帮助。在我们的概念图中，我们会说主机 A 直接连接在端口 1 上。但这条链路上可能有多台主机，因此通过使用 ARP，我们可以构造一个第 2 层分组，只单播给主机 A，而不是发给链路上的其他计算机。"},{"en":"Given a forwarding entry that maps a subnet like 192.0.2.0/24 as direct, how can we determine if a given IP address falls in that range? This is where it’s useful to write ranges using a netmask instead of slash notation. Recall that to write this range as a netmask, we set all fixed bits to 1 and all unfixed bits to 0, to get 255.255.255.0. Then the range is expressed as 192.0.2.0 with netmask 255.255.255.0.","zh_html":"给定一个转发条目，把 192.0.2.0/24 这样的子网映射为 direct，我们如何判断某个给定 IP 地址是否落在这个范围内？这时，用网络掩码(netmask)而不是斜线表示法(slash notation)来写范围会很有用。回想一下，要把这个范围写成网络掩码，我们把所有固定比特设为 1，把所有非固定比特设为 0，从而得到 255.255.255.0。于是，这个范围可以表示为 192.0.2.0，网络掩码为 255.255.255.0。"},{"en":"Now, to check if an address is in the range, we perform a bitwise AND of the address and the netmask. This causes all unfixed lower bits to get zeroed out, retaining only the fixed upper bits. Then, we check if the result matches 192.0.2.0 (the first address in the range, where all unfixed bits are 0).","zh_html":"现在，为了检查某个地址是否在该范围内，我们对该地址和网络掩码执行按位与(bitwise AND)。这会把所有非固定的低位比特清零，只保留固定的高位比特。然后，我们检查结果是否等于 192.0.2.0(该范围中的第一个地址，其中所有非固定比特都是 0)。"},{"en":"Note that as packets get forwarded across hops, the Layer 2 destination will change to the MAC address of the next hop, so that packets can travel across links. However, the Layer 3 destination stays the same across each hop.","zh_html":"注意，当分组跨越多跳(hops)转发时，第 2 层目的地会变成下一跳(next hop)的 MAC 地址，这样分组才能跨链路传输。然而，第 3 层目的地在每一跳上都保持不变。"},{"en":"Neighbor Discovery in IPv6","zh_html":"IPv6 中的邻居发现"},{"en":"ARP translates IPv4 addresses to MAC addresses. To translate IPv6 addresses to MAC addresses, we use a similar protocol called neighbor discovery.","zh_html":"ARP 把 IPv4 地址转换为 MAC 地址。为了把 IPv6 地址转换为 MAC 地址，我们使用一种类似的协议，称为<strong>邻居发现(neighbor discovery)</strong>。"},{"en":"Instead of broadcasting the request for an IP-to-MAC translation, neighbor discovery instead multicasts the request to a specific group, and each computer listens on a specific group based on its IP address. For example, everyone with an IP address ending in 12:3456 might listen on the group MAC address 33:33:FF:12:34:56, while everyone with an IP address ending in 78:90AB might listen on the group MAC address 33:33:FF:78:90:AB.","zh_html":"邻居发现不会广播 IP 到 MAC 转换请求，而是把请求多播(multicast)到某个特定组；每台计算机根据自己的 IP 地址监听某个特定组。例如，所有 IP 地址以 12:3456 结尾的机器可能监听组 MAC 地址 33:33:FF:12:34:56，而所有 IP 地址以 78:90AB 结尾的机器可能监听组 MAC 地址 33:33:FF:78:90:AB。"},{"en":"If I want the MAC address corresponding to the user with an IPv6 address ending in 12:3456, I can plug those IPv6 bits into the group MAC address to get 33:33:FF:12:34:56, and I know that the user with that IP address must be listening to this group MAC address.","zh_html":"如果我想得到某个 IPv6 地址以 12:3456 结尾的用户对应的 MAC 地址，我可以把这些 IPv6 比特填入组 MAC 地址，得到 33:33:FF:12:34:56，并且我知道拥有该 IP 地址的用户一定正在监听这个组 MAC 地址。"},{"en":"Some terminology: In the neighbor discovery protocol, the request for a mapping is called Neighbor Solicitation, and the reply containing the mapping is called Neighbor Advertisement.","zh_html":"一些术语：在邻居发现协议(neighbor discovery protocol)中，请求某个映射的消息称为 Neighbor Solicitation，包含该映射的回复称为 Neighbor Advertisement。"}]},"/end-to-end/dhcp.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"DHCP: Joining Networks","zh_html":"DHCP：加入网络"},{"en":"Joining Networks","zh_html":"加入网络"},{"en":"When a computer first joins the network, what information does it need to connect to the Internet?","zh_html":"当一台计算机第一次加入网络时，它需要哪些信息才能连接到互联网(Internet)？"},{"en":"We always know our own MAC address, because it’s burned into the hardware.","zh_html":"我们总是知道自己的 MAC 地址，因为它被烧录在硬件中。"},{"en":"We need to be allocated an IP address so we can send and receive packets. Recall, IP addresses are allocated geographically, so when we connect to a new network, someone has to give us an IP address to use.","zh_html":"我们需要被分配一个 IP 地址，这样才能发送和接收分组。回想一下，IP 地址是按地理位置分配的，因此当我们连接到一个新网络时，必须有人给我们一个可用的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"We need to learn the subnet mask so that we can learn the range of local IP addresses. Given the mask (fixed bits all ones, unfixed bits all zeros), we can bitwise AND the mask with our own IP address to learn the local IP prefix.","zh_html":"我们需要获知子网掩码(subnet mask)，这样才能得知本地 IP 地址的范围。给定掩码(固定比特全为 1，非固定比特全为 0)，我们可以把该掩码与自己的 IP 地址执行按位与(bitwise AND)，从而得知本地 IP 前缀(local IP prefix)。"},{"en":"We need to learn who the router on the local network is, so that we can send any non-local packets to the router. Sometimes we call this router the default gateway.","zh_html":"我们需要知道本地网络中的路由器是谁，这样才能把所有非本地分组发送给该路由器。有时我们把这个路由器称为<strong>默认网关(default gateway)</strong>。"},{"en":"We also might need to learn where the DNS recursive resolver is located for this network.","zh_html":"我们还可能需要知道该网络的 DNS 递归解析器(DNS recursive resolver)位于哪里。"},{"en":"The user could manually configure these values when they first join the network. This is time-consuming, especially since we have to re-configure these values every time we join a different network. Also, the average Internet user probably has no idea how to configure these values manually. That said, manual configuration does sometimes work for machines like routers, which don’t move around often.","zh_html":"用户第一次加入网络时，可以手动配置这些值。这很耗时，尤其是每次加入不同网络时都必须重新配置这些值。此外，普通互联网用户很可能不知道如何手动配置这些值。话虽如此，手动配置有时适用于路由器这类不经常移动的机器。"},{"en":"We need an protocol that allows new hosts to automatically learn these values (and possibly other useful information).","zh_html":"我们需要一种协议，让新主机(hosts)能够自动获知这些值(以及可能的其他有用信息)。"},{"en":"DHCP: Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol","zh_html":"DHCP：动态主机配置协议(Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol)"},{"en":"DHCP has four steps:","zh_html":"动态主机配置协议(DHCP)包含四个步骤："},{"en":"The new client broadcasts a Discover message, asking for configuration information.","zh_html":"<p>新客户端(client)广播(broadcast)一条 <strong>Discover</strong> 消息，请求配置信息。</p> <p><img width=\"800px\" src=\"../assets/end-to-end/5-050-dhcp1.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"Any DHCP server who can help will unicast an Offer to the client, with a configuration that the client can use (e.g. IP address, gateway address, DNS address).","zh_html":"<p>任何能够提供帮助的 <strong>DHCP 服务器(DHCP server)</strong>都会向客户端单播(unicast)一个 <strong>Offer</strong>，其中包含客户端可使用的配置(例如 IP 地址、网关地址、DNS 地址)。</p> <p><img width=\"800px\" src=\"../assets/end-to-end/5-051-dhcp2.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"The client will broadcast a Request message, indicating which offer they accepted. This message is broadcast because the client might get multiple offers. By telling everybody which offer it’s accepting, the client allows the rejected offers to be freed up for future clients.","zh_html":"<p>客户端会广播一条 <strong>Request</strong> 消息，表示它接受了哪个 offer。之所以广播这条消息，是因为客户端可能收到多个 offer。通过告诉所有人自己接受了哪个 offer，客户端可以让被拒绝的 offer 释放出来，供未来的客户端使用。</p> <p><img width=\"800px\" src=\"../assets/end-to-end/5-052-dhcp3.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"The server sends an acknowledgement to confirm that the request was granted.","zh_html":"<p>服务器发送确认(acknowledgement)，确认该请求已被批准。</p> <p><img width=\"800px\" src=\"../assets/end-to-end/5-053-dhcp4.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"DHCP Servers","zh_html":"DHCP 服务器"},{"en":"In step 2, who is able to offer configurations? DHCP servers are added to the network, and their goal is to offer this information to new hosts. On smaller networks like your home network, the home router itself often acts as the DHCP server. In larger networks, there could be a separate machine that acts as the DHCP server.","zh_html":"在第 2 步中，谁能够提供配置？网络中会加入 DHCP 服务器(DHCP servers)，它们的目标是向新主机提供这些信息。在较小的网络中，例如你的家庭网络，家用路由器本身通常充当 DHCP 服务器。在较大的网络中，可能会有一台单独的机器充当 DHCP 服务器。"},{"en":"DHCP servers need to be in the same local network as the client, since the protocol operates inside the local network. In larger networks, we might not want to run DHCP server code inside every router, so local routers can relay requests to a central remote DHCP server that actually runs the protocol.","zh_html":"DHCP 服务器需要与客户端位于同一个本地网络中，因为该协议在本地网络内部运行。在较大的网络中，我们可能不希望在每台路由器内运行 DHCP 服务器代码，因此本地路由器可以把请求中继(relay)到真正运行该协议的中央远程 DHCP 服务器。"},{"en":"DHCP servers listen on a fixed port, UDP port 67, for requests from new machines. The servers are configured with all the necessary information: They know about the gateway and DNS servers, and they have a pool of usable IP addresses that they can allocate to new users.","zh_html":"DHCP 服务器监听固定端口 UDP 端口 67，用于接收新机器的请求。服务器会配置好所有必要信息：它们知道网关(gateway)和 DNS 服务器，并且拥有一个可用 IP 地址池(pool)，可以分配给新用户。"},{"en":"Note that IP addresses are only temporarily leased to hosts. The lease is only valid for a limited amount of time (e.g. order of hours or days). If the host wants to keep using the address, it must renew the lease. If an IP address is currently leased to a host, the DHCP server cannot offer the same address to other clients.","zh_html":"注意，IP 地址只是临时租给主机。租约(lease)只在有限时间内有效(例如数小时或数天的量级)。如果主机想继续使用该地址，就必须续租(renew the lease)。如果某个 IP 地址当前已租给一台主机，DHCP 服务器就不能把同一个地址提供给其他客户端。"},{"en":"DHCP Implementation","zh_html":"DHCP 实现"},{"en":"Note that DHCP is a Layer 7 application protocol, and it runs on top of UDP, which itself runs on top of IP.","zh_html":"注意，DHCP 是第 7 层应用协议(application protocol)，它运行在 UDP 之上，而 UDP 本身运行在 IP 之上。"},{"en":"In step 1, how does the client broadcast a message over IP? It sends a packet with destination IP of 255.255.255.255 (all ones), which is the IPv4 broadcast address. When this packet is passed down to Layer 2, instead of translating this IP address using ARP, the IPv4 broadcast address is mapped to the Ethernet broadcast address of FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF (all ones). Then, the packet can get broadcast across the network at Layer 2.","zh_html":"在第 1 步中，客户端如何通过 IP 广播消息？它发送一个目的 IP 为 255.255.255.255(全 1)的分组，这是 IPv4 广播地址(IPv4 broadcast address)。当这个分组向下传递到第 2 层时，不会使用 ARP 转换这个 IP 地址，而是把 IPv4 广播地址映射到以太网广播地址(Ethernet broadcast address) FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF(全 1)。然后，该分组就可以在第 2 层跨网络广播。"},{"en":"What about the source IP? The client doesn’t have one at the start of the protocol, so it sets the source IP to be 0.0.0.0.","zh_html":"源 IP 怎么办？客户端在协议开始时还没有源 IP，因此它把源 IP 设为 0.0.0.0。"},{"en":"With the hard-coded source IP of 0.0.0.0 and destination IP of 255.255.255.255, the client doesn’t need to know anything about the local network to start running this protocol.","zh_html":"借助硬编码的源 IP 0.0.0.0 和目的 IP 255.255.255.255，客户端不需要知道本地网络的任何信息，就可以开始运行这个协议。"},{"en":"If there’s no source IP, how do the DHCP servers know how to unicast the offers? The DHCP servers could either broadcast the offers, or use the client’s MAC address to unicast the offers.","zh_html":"如果没有源 IP，DHCP 服务器怎么知道如何单播这些 offer？DHCP 服务器可以广播这些 offer，也可以使用客户端的 MAC 地址来单播这些 offer。"},{"en":"Autoconfiguration in IPv6","zh_html":"IPv6 中的自动配置"},{"en":"DHCP also exists in IPv6 networks. However, because IPv6 addresses are longer, it turns out that we can give ourselves a guaranteed unique IPv6 address without anybody else managing a pool of addresses and leasing them. This protocol is called Stateless Address Autoconfiguration (SLAAC).","zh_html":"DHCP 也存在于 IPv6 网络中。不过，由于 IPv6 地址更长，事实证明，即使没有其他人管理地址池并租出地址，我们也可以给自己一个保证唯一的 IPv6 地址。这个协议称为<strong>无状态地址自动配置(Stateless Address Autoconfiguration, SLAAC)</strong>。"},{"en":"The trick is to use the MAC address, which we know is unique to each machine. As before, we ask for the local network information, which includes the gateway address, DNS address, and notably, the prefix for the local network. This prefix is usually 64 bits long. Then, we copy our own MAC address bits into the host bits of the IPv6 address. We can be confident that nobody else has this IPv6 address: users in other networks will have a different prefix, and no one else in the network (or anywhere else) will have the same MAC address bits.","zh_html":"关键技巧是使用 MAC 地址，因为我们知道每台机器的 MAC 地址都是唯一的。和之前一样，我们请求本地网络信息，其中包括网关地址、DNS 地址，尤其包括本地网络的前缀(prefix)。这个前缀通常是 64 位长。然后，我们把自己的 MAC 地址比特复制到 IPv6 地址的主机比特(host bits)中。我们可以确信没有其他人拥有这个 IPv6 地址：其他网络中的用户会有不同的前缀，而本网络中(或任何其他地方)也不会有人拥有相同的 MAC 地址比特。"},{"en":"To get the local network information, we can extend the Neighbor Discovery protocol (IPv6 version of ARP). The Router Solicitation message lets the user broadcast a request for the local network information, and the Router Advertisement message lets routers reply with that information.","zh_html":"为了获得本地网络信息，我们可以扩展邻居发现协议(Neighbor Discovery protocol，即 IPv6 版本的 ARP)。Router Solicitation 消息允许用户广播一个本地网络信息请求，而 Router Advertisement 消息允许路由器回复这些信息。"},{"en":"SLAAC has additional mechanisms to detect duplicate addresses, just in case.","zh_html":"SLAAC 还包含额外机制，用来检测重复地址，以防万一。"}]},"/end-to-end/end-to-end.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"End-to-End Connectivity","zh_html":"<a href=\"#end-to-end-connectivity\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"end-to-end-connectivity\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 端到端连接(End-to-End Connectivity)"},{"en":"Motivation","zh_html":"<a href=\"#motivation\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"motivation\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 动机(Motivation)"},{"en":"In this section, we’ll do a step-by-step walkthrough of what happens when we turn on our computer, plug it into an Ethernet network, and type www.berkeley.edu in our web browser. In the process of doing so, we’ll see how all the different pieces of the network work together to process the user’s request.","zh_html":"在本节中，我们将逐步演练当我们打开计算机、将其接入以太网(Ethernet)并在网络浏览器中输入 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">www.berkeley.edu</code> 时所发生的事情。在此过程中，我们将看到我们所讨论的所有网络协议如何协同工作，从 DHCP 到 DNS 再到 TCP/IP。"},{"en":"We’ll assume that we don’t need to turn on the Internet from scratch. For example, routers are already actively running routing protocols and have populated their forwarding tables accordingly.","zh_html":"我们将假设不需要从头启动整个互联网。例如，路由器已经在运行路由协议(routing protocols)并已填充了各自的转发表(forwarding tables)。"},{"en":"Step 1: DHCP","zh_html":"<a href=\"#step-1-dhcp\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"step-1-dhcp\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 第 1 步：DHCP"},{"en":"We turn on our computer and plug it into an Ethernet network. We don’t have any information about the network yet, so we broadcast a DHCP request.","zh_html":"我们打开计算机并将其接入以太网网络。我们还不知道关于该网络的任何信息，因此我们广播(broadcast)一条 DHCP 请求。"},{"en":"We’ll assume the home router is the DHCP server, which is common in home networks. The router/server unicasts an offer back to us. The offer contains information about the network: the subnet mask, the IP address of the default gateway, and the IP address of the DNS server. The offer also gives us an IP address we can use.","zh_html":"我们假设家用路由器是 DHCP 服务器(DHCP server)，这在家庭网络中很常见。路由器/服务器单播(unicast)一个提供(offer)消息作为回复。该提供消息包含关于网络的信息：子网掩码(subnet mask)、我们可以使用的 IP 地址、DNS 服务器的 IP 地址以及路由器的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"To complete the DHCP protocol, we send the request message confirming we’d like to use the offered configuration, and the router/server responds with an acknowledgement.","zh_html":"为了完成 DHCP 协议，我们发送请求(request)消息以确认我们想要使用所提供的配置，然后路由器/服务器回复一个确认(acknowledgement)。"},{"en":"Step 2: Find Router at Layer 2","zh_html":"<a href=\"#step-2-find-router-at-layer-2\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"step-2-find-router-at-layer-2\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 第 2 步：在第 2 层找到路由器"},{"en":"From DHCP, we learned about the IP address of the router, and our forwarding table now says that all non-local packets should be forwarded to this router. We’re about to send some packets to the DNS server (to look up the IP address of www.berkeley.edu), and to the Berkeley server itself, both of which may be non-local.","zh_html":"从 DHCP 中，我们知道了路由器的 IP 地址，并且我们的转发表现在显示所有非本地(non-local)分组都应被转发到该路由器。我们即将向 DNS 服务器发送一些分组，而 DNS 服务器在本地网络之外，因此这些分组需要被转发到路由器。"},{"en":"Before we can forward IP packets to the router, though, we need to figure out the router’s Layer 2 MAC address, so that we can send the packet to the router inside the local network.","zh_html":"不过，在我们能够将 IP 分组转发到路由器之前，我们需要找出路由器的第 2 层 MAC 地址，这样才能在本地网络内部将分组发送到路由器。"},{"en":"First, we can verify that the router’s IP address, 192.168.1.1, belongs to the local subnet, 192.168.1.2/24. This tells us that the router is in the local network, and by sending an Ethernet packet to the router’s MAC address, we’ll reach the router.","zh_html":"首先，我们可以验证路由器的 IP 地址 192.168.1.1 是否属于本地子网 192.168.1.2/24。这告诉我们路由器在本地网络中，并且通过向路由器发送以太网帧(ethernet frame)，该帧将到达路由器而无需经过任何中间路由器。"},{"en":"To find the router’s MAC address, we broadcast an ARP request, asking for the MAC address of 192.168.1.1 (router’s IP address). The router hears this request and replies, saying, “I’m 192.168.1.1, and my MAC address is 01:ab:cd:ef:42:01.”","zh_html":"为了找到路由器的 MAC 地址，我们广播(broadcast)一条 ARP 请求(ARP request)，询问 192.168.1.1(路由器的 IP 地址)的 MAC 地址。路由器听到该请求并回复：\"我是一台 MAC 地址为 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">f8:ff:c2:2b:36:16</code> 的 192.168.1.1。\""},{"en":"We can now cache this IP-to-MAC mapping, and we now know the router’s MAC address. As long as this entry stays in the cache, we won’t have to make the same ARP request again. All future requests to the outside Internet can be forwarded to the router’s MAC address.","zh_html":"我们现在可以缓存这个 IP 到 MAC 映射(IP-to-MAC mapping)，并且我们知道了路由器的 MAC 地址。只要该条目保留在缓存中，我们就不必再次发出同样的 ARP 请求。未来对该路由器发出的所有请求都将具有相同的目标 MAC 地址。"},{"en":"Step 3: DNS Lookup","zh_html":"<a href=\"#step-3-dns-lookup\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"step-3-dns-lookup\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 第 3 步：DNS 查询(DNS Lookup)"},{"en":"Next, we need to look up IP address of www.berkeley.edu. This is all done in the operating system, after the browser code calls something like getaddrinfo to trigger the DNS lookup.","zh_html":"接下来，我们需要查找 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">www.berkeley.edu</code> 的 IP 地址。这一切都在操作系统中完成，浏览器代码调用类似 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">getaddrinfo</code> 的函数来触发 DNS 查询。"},{"en":"From DHCP, we learned the IP address of the DNS server, 8.8.8.8. We also learned that we’re in the subnet 192.168.1.2/24. The DNS server isn’t in our local network, so we need to forward the DNS packet to the router.","zh_html":"从 DHCP 中，我们知道了 DNS 服务器的 IP 地址 8.8.8.8。我们还知道自己位于子网 192.168.1.2/24 中。DNS 服务器不在我们的本地网络中，因此我们需要将 DNS 分组转发到路由器，路由器再将其转发到互联网上。"},{"en":"We can now build up our DNS request packet, from the top down.","zh_html":"现在我们可以自上而下地构建 DNS 请求分组。"},{"en":"Layer 7: In the Question section, we add a DNS record requesting the A record with www.berkeley.edu’s IP address. We add the DNS header with the ID, number of records, and so on.","zh_html":"第 7 层：在问题(Question)部分，我们添加一条 DNS 记录，请求 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">www.berkeley.edu</code> 的 IP 地址的 A 记录(A record)。我们添加带有 ID、记录数量等信息的 DNS 首部(DNS header)。"},{"en":"Layer 4: DNS runs on top of UDP. We pick any random source port, since we’re the client. We pick Port 53 for the destination port, since this is where resolvers and name servers listen for DNS queries.","zh_html":"第 4 层：DNS 运行在 UDP 之上。我们随机选择一个源端口(source port)，因为我们是客户端。我们选择端口 53 作为目的端口(destination port)，因为解析器(resolvers)和名称服务器(name servers)在此监听 DNS 查询。"},{"en":"Layer 3: The source IP is our own IP, as assigned by DHCP. The destination IP is 8.8.8.8, the IP address of the DNS server, which we learned from DHCP.","zh_html":"第 3 层：源 IP 是我们自己的 IP(由 DHCP 分配)。目的 IP 是 8.8.8.8，即我们从 DHCP 获知的 DNS 服务器的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"Layer 2: The source MAC is our own MAC address, which is burned into our hardware. The destination MAC is the MAC address of the router (the next hop), which we learned from ARP.","zh_html":"第 2 层：源 MAC 是我们自己的 MAC 地址(固化在硬件中)。目的 MAC 是路由器(下一跳)的 MAC 地址，我们通过 ARP 获知。"},{"en":"With the packet fully built, we can send the bits along the wire (Layer 1).","zh_html":"分组完全构建好之后，我们就可以沿着网线发送比特(第 1 层)。"},{"en":"When the packet reaches the router, if the network is using NAT, the router might rewrite the UDP/IP headers to translate our private IP address into a public IP address. However, as the end host, we don’t have to worry about NAT. The router should be doing all the translation for us, giving us the illusion that we can use our own IP address (from DHCP).","zh_html":"当分组到达路由器时，如果网络使用了 NAT，路由器可能会重写 UDP/IP 首部，把我们的专用 IP 地址( private IP address)转换成一个公共 IP 地址(public IP address)。不过，作为端主机(end host)，我们不知道也不关心这些细节。"},{"en":"When our packet reaches the recursive resolver at 8.8.8.8, if the resolver doesn’t have our answer cached already, it might need to perform some additional lookups and ask the authoritative name servers for the records. Eventually, the recursive resolver finds the answer, and sends the A record back to us. We now have www.berkeley.edu’s IP address.","zh_html":"当我们的分组到达 8.8.8.8 的递归解析器(recursive resolver)时，如果该解析器的缓存中还没有我们的答案，它可能需要进行一些额外的查询，并询问权威名称服务器(authoritative name servers)以找到正确的 IP 地址。最终，解析器将 IP 地址 141.193.213.21 作为回复发送回来。"},{"en":"Step 4: Connect to Website","zh_html":"<a href=\"#step-4-connect-to-website\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"step-4-connect-to-website\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 第 4 步：连接网站(Connect to Website)"},{"en":"Now that we have www.berkeley.edu’s IP address, we can send packets to Berkeley. We’re using a web browser, so our goal is to make an HTTP request to this server.","zh_html":"现在我们有了 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">www.berkeley.edu</code> 的 IP 地址，我们可以向 Berkeley 发送分组了。我们正在使用网络浏览器，因此我们的目标是向该服务器发出 HTTP 请求。"},{"en":"HTTP runs on top of TCP, so we first have to make a TCP handshake to open a connection with the Berkeley server. The browser will call something like connect on a particular socket to open this connection, and the operating system (where TCP is running) will perform the handshake and pass packets to and from the browser.","zh_html":"HTTP 运行在 TCP 之上，因此我们首先需要进行 TCP 握手(TCP handshake)来与 Berkeley 服务器建立连接。浏览器会在特定的套接字(socket)上调用类似 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">connect</code> 的函数来打开此连接。"},{"en":"The TCP handshake is performed: We send a SYN, Berkeley sends a SYN-ACK, and we send an ACK. We now have a bytestream between our computer and the Berkeley server.","zh_html":"TCP 握手执行如下：我们发送 SYN，Berkeley 发送 SYN-ACK，然后我们发送 ACK。现在我们的计算机和 Berkeley 服务器之间建立了一个字节流(bytestream)。"},{"en":"Now, we can build up or HTTP packet, from the top down.","zh_html":"现在我们可以自上而下地构建 HTTP 分组。"},{"en":"Layer 7: The HTTP method is GET. The resource we want is / (the homepage). The version is HTTP/1.1.","zh_html":"第 7 层：HTTP 方法是 GET。我们请求的资源是 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">/</code>(首页)。版本是 HTTP/1.1。"},{"en":"Layer 4: HTTP runs on top of TCP. The browser can pick any source port, since it’s the client. In general, this port could be manually specified by the application, or the application could specify “Port 0,” which is shorthand for asking the operating system to pick a random ephemeral port that’s currently unused. (As an aside, thinking back to NAT, allowing applications to manually specify ports is why two users might choose the same source port.) The destination port is 80, the fixed port number for HTTP.","zh_html":"第 4 层：HTTP 运行在 TCP 之上。浏览器可以选择任意源端口(source port)，因为它是客户端。通常，这个端口可以由应用程序手动指定，或者应用程序可以指定\"让操作系统选择一个端口\"(端口 0)。对于 HTTP，目的端口是 80。"},{"en":"Layer 3: The source IP is our own IP, as assigned by DHCP. The destination IP is 141.193.213.21, the IP address of www.berkeley.edu that was returned from our DNS query earlier.","zh_html":"第 3 层：源 IP 是我们自己的 IP(由 DHCP 分配)。目的 IP 是 141.193.213.21，即之前 DNS 查询返回的 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">www.berkeley.edu</code> 的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"Layer 2: This is the same as our DNS packet earlier. The source MAC is our own (burned into hardware), and the destination MAC is the router’s (discovered and cached from ARP).","zh_html":"第 2 层：这与我们之前的 DNS 分组相同。源 MAC 是我们自己的(固化在硬件中)，目的 MAC 是路由器的(通过 ARP 发现并缓存)。"},{"en":"The HTTP response comes back with status code 200 OK, and the content of the response has the HTML code of the website. The browser calls read on the socket to fetch the bytes of the HTTP payload, with the status code and the response, and processes them accordingly.","zh_html":"HTTP 响应返回状态码 200 OK，响应的内容包含网站的 HTML 代码。浏览器在套接字上调用 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">read</code> 来获取 HTTP 负载的字节，并解析/渲染 HTML。"},{"en":"Within the bytestream, HTTP can add some delimiter like a newline character to denote the end of a request or response. Also, HTTP headers like Content-Length can specify the length of the payload. This also allows the browser to allocate enough memory to receive the response.","zh_html":"在字节流中，HTTP 可以添加一些定界符(delimiter)，比如换行符，来标识请求或响应的结束。此外，像 Content-Length 这样的 HTTP 首部可以指定负载的长度。这样，接收方就能知道何时停止读取。"},{"en":"The HTTP response that comes back might trigger further requests. If the HTML in the response has some syntax like &lt;img src=\"../logo.png\"&gt;, this tells the browser to make another HTTP request to fetch the /logo.png resource. Or, the user might click a link on the website like www.berkeley.edu/about.html, which would also trigger another HTTP request to the same server.","zh_html":"返回的 HTTP 响应可能会触发进一步的请求。如果响应中的 HTML 包含类似 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">&lt;img src=\"../logo.png\"&gt;</code> 的语法，这告诉浏览器还要为这个图片文件再向服务器发出一个 HTTP 请求。浏览器在同一个 TCP 连接上发出另一个 HTTP 请求。"},{"en":"Recall that multiple HTTP requests to the same server can be pipelined across the same TCP connection for efficiency, so we can keep the TCP connection open and keep using it for subsequent HTTP requests and responses.","zh_html":"回想一下，对同一台服务器的多个 HTTP 请求为了提高效率可以在同一个 TCP 连接上流水线化(pipelined)，因此我们可以保持 TCP 连接开放并持续用于后续的 HTTP 请求。"},{"en":"Eventually, after some pipelining, the client or server chooses to close the connection. The normal teardown handshake occurs, where each side sends a FIN, and both FIN packets are acked. We’re all done!","zh_html":"最终，经过一些流水线操作后，客户端或服务器选择关闭连接。执行正常的拆除握手(teardown handshake)，其中每一方发送一个 FIN，并且两个 FIN 分组都被确认(acked)。至此结束。"},{"en":"Note that the HTTP requests/responses are not necessarily contained in a single packet. HTTP is built on top of the TCP bytestream, so a single HTTP request or response could get split up across multiple TCP/IP packets, where each packet has the same headers at Layers 1-3, and the Layer 4 headers differ in sequence number. There’s only a single header for the entire HTTP request/response, even if the request/response is split across packets. With HTTP, there’s no longer a one-to-one correlation from one request/response to one packet.","zh_html":"注意，HTTP 请求/响应不一定包含在单个分组中。HTTP 构建在 TCP 字节流之上，因此单个 HTTP 请求或响应可能被分割到多个 TCP 分组中。接收方在读取字节流时，必须知道何时停止(通过 Content-Length 或定界符)。"},{"en":"Sockets","zh_html":"<a href=\"#sockets\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"sockets\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 套接字(Sockets)"},{"en":"If you’re a user visiting a website in your browser, you don’t need to write any code to run the application (HTTP) over the Internet. However, if you were a programmer writing your own application, you probably need to write some code to interact with the network.","zh_html":"如果你是使用浏览器访问网站的用户，你不需要编写任何代码来通过互联网运行应用(HTTP)。但是，如果你是正在编写自己的应用程序的程序员，你将需要编写一些代码来通过网络发送数据。套接字抽象(socket abstraction)就是为了让这变得更容易。"},{"en":"The socket abstraction gives programmers a convenient way to interact with the network. The socket abstraction exists entirely in software, and there are five basic operations that programmers can run:","zh_html":"<strong>套接字(socket)</strong>抽象为程序员提供了一种与网络交互的便捷方式。套接字抽象完全存在于软件中，程序员可以对套接字运行五种基本操作。"},{"en":"We can create a new socket, corresponding to a new connection. In an object-oriented language like Java, this could be a constructor call.","zh_html":"我们可以<strong>创建(create)</strong>一个新套接字，对应于一个新连接。在像 Java 这样的面向对象语言中，这可能是一个构造函数调用。"},{"en":"We can call connect, which initiates a TCP connection to some remote machine. This is useful if we’re the client in a client-server connection.","zh_html":"我们可以调用 <strong>connect</strong>，它会向某台远程机器发起一个 TCP 连接。如果我们充当客户端-服务器连接中的客户端，这很有用。"},{"en":"We can call listen on a specific port. This does not start a connection, but allows others to initiate a connection with us on the specified port.","zh_html":"我们可以在特定端口上调用 <strong>listen</strong>。这不会启动连接，但允许其他人在指定端口上与我们发起连接。"},{"en":"Once the connection is open, we can call write to send some bytes on the connection. We can also call read, which takes one argument N, to read N bytes from the connection.","zh_html":"一旦连接建立，我们可以调用 <strong>write</strong> 在连接上发送一些字节。我们还可以调用 <strong>read</strong>，它接受一个参数 N，表示从连接中读取 N 个字节。"},{"en":"This socket abstraction gives programmers a way to write applications without thinking about lower-level abstractions like TCP, IP, or Ethernet.","zh_html":"这种套接字抽象让程序员能够编写应用程序，而无需考虑像 TCP、IP 或以太网这样的底层抽象。"},{"en":"From the operating system perspective, each socket is associated with a Layer 4 port number. All packets to and from a single socket have the same port number, and the operating system can use the port number to de-multiplex and send packets to the correct socket.","zh_html":"从操作系统的角度来看，每个套接字都与一个第 4 层端口号相关联。进出单个套接字的所有分组都具有相同的端口号，操作系统可以使用该端口号来正确地将分组路由到正确的套接字。"},{"en":"Layers in the OS","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layers-in-the-os\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layers-in-the-os\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 操作系统中的各层(Layers in the OS)"},{"en":"In hardware, Layers 1 and 2 are implemented on your computer’s hardware Network Interface Card (NIC). Layers 3 and 4 are implemented in the networking stack in the operating system. The Layer 7 applications are implemented in software. The benefit of putting Layers 3 and 4 in the OS is, the applications don’t have to worry about re-implementing them every time.","zh_html":"在硬件中，第 1 层和第 2 层在你的计算机硬件网络接口卡(NIC, Network Interface Card)上实现。第 3 层和第 4 层在操作系统的网络协议栈(networking stack)中实现。第 7 层应用(application)在用户空间的进程中运行。"},{"en":"With this division of labor, the application just needs to think about data. The NIC just needs to think about packets. The network stack in the OS translates between connections and packets.","zh_html":"通过这种分工，应用程序只需要考虑数据(data)。NIC 只需要考虑分组(packets)。操作系统中的网络协议栈在连接(connections)和分组(packets)之间进行转换。"},{"en":"Viewing Packets","zh_html":"<a href=\"#viewing-packets\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"viewing-packets\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 查看分组(Viewing Packets)"},{"en":"Tools like tshark and wireshark exist if you want to look at packets being sent across the network. These tools are useful when debugging the networking part of your code.","zh_html":"如果你想要查看网络上传送的分组，可以使用像 tshark 和 wireshark 这样的工具。这些工具在调试代码的网络部分时非常有用。"},{"en":"In your browser, you can also use the Network tab of the inspect element console to view data being sent and received.","zh_html":"在浏览器中，你也可以使用检查元素控制台的 Network 选项卡来查看发送和接收的数据。"},{"en":"If you actually looked the raw packets being sent across the network, you’ll see some real-world complexities that we didn’t cover in our end-to-end walkthrough. For example, packets might be encrypted and sent over TLS. Also, if we’re using HTTP/3.0, packets might be sent over QUIC (the UDP variant optimized for HTTP) instead of TCP.","zh_html":"如果你实际查看网络上传送的原始分组，你会发现一些我们的端到端演练中没有涵盖的现实世界复杂性。例如，分组可能被加密(encrypted)、分片(fragmented)或使用我们没有讨论的首部选项。"},{"en":"Revisiting Layering","zh_html":"<a href=\"#revisiting-layering\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"revisiting-layering\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 重温分层(Revisiting Layering)"},{"en":"The full end-to-end picture lets us see why layering is a useful principle for building the network. We were able to solve specific problems at a single layer, without thinking about all the layers at the same time.","zh_html":"完整的端到端图景让我们看到了为什么分层(layering)是构建网络的一个有用原则。我们能够在单个层解决特定问题，而无需同时考虑所有层。作为网络设计者，我们不需要在实现 IP 路由的同时考虑 802.11 无线网络(Wi-Fi)的物理层细节。"},{"en":"In fact, we haven’t discussed Layer 1 at all in this class. We didn’t talk about the electrical engineering or physics required to send signals across a wire. However, we were still able to build the other layers on top of Layer 1, without knowing exactly how Layer 1 works.","zh_html":"事实上，我们在本课程中完全没有讨论第 1 层。我们没有讨论通过网线发送信号所需的电气工程或物理知识。然而，我们仍然能够构建出在第 2 层到第 7 层运行的良好工作协议。这就是分层的强大之处。"},{"en":"In this class, we’ve discussed HTTP as the predominant Layer 7 protocol, but HTTP is a relatively simple protocol. It’s possible that multiple applications want to build the same complicated functionality on top of HTTP, but they don’t want to each write the code for that functionality independently. To support this, we can actually build further protocols on top of HTTP, so that programmers don’t always have to start from scratch with HTTP.","zh_html":"在本课程中，我们将 HTTP 作为主要的第 7 层协议进行了讨论，但 HTTP 是一个相对简单的协议。多个不同的应用程序可能想要构建相同的复杂功能，而无需各自独立实现。因此，有时会构建位于第 7 层之上的额外抽象层。"},{"en":"One example of a protocol above Layer 7 is a remote procedure call (RPC) library. This allows a programmer to write some code, where some of the functions actually execute on a different computer elsewhere in the network. It would be annoying if everyone had to write RPC on top of HTTP from scratch, so instead, libraries like Apache Thrift and gRPC exist to abstract even more details away from the programmer.","zh_html":"一个位于第 7 层之上的协议的例子是远程过程调用(RPC, remote procedure call)库。这允许程序员编写一些代码，其中某些函数实际上在另一台远程计算机上执行。RPC 运行在 HTTP/TCP 之上。"},{"en":"Here’s an example of some network code that a programmer might write. It programs a client to say hello to some remote server.","zh_html":"以下是一个程序员可能编写的网络代码示例。它让一个客户端向某个远程服务器发送问候(hello)。"},{"en":"Notice that all of the network protocols we discussed are completely hidden behind the two lines of calls to networking libraries. The programmer didn’t have to think about HTTP, TCP, IP, Ethernet, ARP, DHCP, or any other lower-level protocol. It’s still useful to know about these protocols if they go wrong, and understanding the protocols can help you optimize your code for specific protocols, but ultimately, layering is a very powerful tool for abstraction.","zh_html":"请注意，我们讨论过的所有网络协议完全隐藏在两行网络库调用之后。程序员不需要考虑 HTTP、TCP、IP、以太网、ARP 或任何其他协议。分层使得构建复杂的网络应用程序变得容易。"}]},"/end-to-end/ethernet.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Ethernet","zh_html":"<a href=\"#ethernet\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ethernet\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 以太网(Ethernet)"},{"en":"Local Networks","zh_html":"<a href=\"#local-networks\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"local-networks\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 局域网(Local Networks)"},{"en":"In this section, we’ll focus on what happens inside a local area network, such as the network in your home with your computer and your home router. This is in contrast with the wide-area networks we’ve been seeing so far, which span longer distances.","zh_html":"在本节中，我们将重点关注局域网(local area network)内部发生的事情，比如你家中连接计算机和家庭路由器的网络。这与我们之前讨论的广域网(wide-area networks)形成对比，广域网涉及连接世界各地的机器。"},{"en":"In particular, we’ll look at forwarding and addressing at Layer 2. We’ll have to define how packets are forwarded from a local host to a router. We’ll also see how hosts in the same local network can exchange messages at Layer 2, without a need to contact routers at all. The predominant protocol at Layer 2 is Ethernet.","zh_html":"具体来说，我们将研究第 2 层的转发和寻址(addressing)。我们需要定义分组如何从本地主机(host)转发到路由器。我们还将看到同一本地网络中的主机如何在不经过路由器的情况下相互直接发送消息。"},{"en":"Connecting Local Hosts","zh_html":"<a href=\"#connecting-local-hosts\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"connecting-local-hosts\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 连接本地主机(Connecting Local Hosts)"},{"en":"So far, we’ve drawn links connecting exactly two machines. In the local network, we drew a line connecting each host to the router.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们画的链路(links)都刚好连接两台机器。在局域网中，我们画了一条线将每台主机连接到路由器。"},{"en":"In reality, a single wire might be used to connect multiple machines. In the local network, the hosts and the router can all be on the same wire. We can abstract even further and note that at Layer 2, the router is really just a machine like any other (that happens to run routing protocols at higher layers). Ultimately, the wire doesn’t really care what the connected machines are doing with the data they exchange.","zh_html":"在现实中，一根导线可能用于连接多台机器。在局域网中，主机和路由器都可以在同一条导线上。我们甚至可以进一步抽象，注意到在第 2 层，我们不关心物理介质——只需要知道连接机器的是什么，以及分组如何在机器之间传送。"},{"en":"What is the best way to wire up computers in a local network? Earlier, when we first introduced routing, we thought about using a mesh topology to connect all pairs of computers in the world. We also considered using a single wire to connect up all the computers. Ultimately, we decided that for a global network, neither approach was practical, and we needed to introduce routers.","zh_html":"在局域网中连接计算机的最佳方式是什么？之前，当我们首次引入路由时，我们考虑了使用网状拓扑(mesh topology)来连接世界上所有计算机对。我们还考虑了总线拓扑(bus topology)，其中所有计算机共享同一条导线。"},{"en":"We can consider these topologies again in the local network. A mesh topology is still pretty impractical. If a new host joins, we’d have to add a wire connecting it to every other host. However, a bus topology, where we connect all the computers along a single wire, is pretty common and practical in a local network.","zh_html":"我们可以在局域网中再次考虑这些拓扑。网状拓扑仍然相当不实用。如果新主机加入，我们必须添加一条导线将其连接到每一台其他主机。然而，<strong>总线(bus)</strong>拓扑更实用——只需添加一条到共享导线(bus)的连接。"},{"en":"The single-wire bus topology introduces the notion of a shared media. When we drew links connecting two machines, only those two computers used that link to communicate. Now, a packet from A to C, and a packet from B to D, might be on the wire at the same time, and the electrical signal on that wire cannot hold both packets simultaneously.","zh_html":"单线总线拓扑引入了<strong>共享介质(shared media)</strong>的概念。当我们画出连接两台机器的链路时，只有那两台计算机使用该链路进行通信。现在，从 A 到 C 和从 B 到 D 的分组不能同时发送，因为它们使用相同的导线进行通信。"},{"en":"As an analogy, consider multiple people on a group call, sharing a single phone line: Any two people can talk to each other, but you can’t have two simultaneous conversations, or else nobody understands what’s being said.","zh_html":"打个比方，想象多人在一个群组通话中共享一条电话线：任何两个人都可以互相交谈，但不能同时进行两个对话，否则没人能听清。你可能需要协调谁在什么时候发言。"},{"en":"We’ve drawn links as wires with electrical signals on them for simplicity, but in reality, the link technology could use other shared media. For example, in a wireless link technology, all hosts connected by the link share the same part of the electromagnetic spectrum.","zh_html":"为了简单起见，我们把链路画成带有电信号的导线，但现实中，链路技术可能使用其他共享介质。例如，在无线链路技术(wireless link technology)中，连接到同一接入点(access point)的所有主机共享无线电波这一介质。"},{"en":"Communicating over Shared Media: Coordinated Approaches","zh_html":"<a href=\"#communicating-over-shared-media-coordinated-approaches\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"communicating-over-shared-media-coordinated-approaches\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 在共享介质上通信：协调式方法(Coordinated Approaches)"},{"en":"In a network with a shared medium, there’s a risk that transmissions from different nodes may interfere or collide with each other. If two computers try to transmit data simultaneously, their signals will overlap and interfere. The recipients may be unable to decode the signal, and they can’t tell who sent the signal. To solve this problem, we need a multiple access protocol that ensures that multiple computers can share the link and transmit over it.","zh_html":"在具有共享介质的网络中，存在来自不同节点的传输可能相互干扰或碰撞(collide)的风险。如果两台计算机同时尝试传输数据，它们的信号会混在一起，无法被理解，因此数据丢失。"},{"en":"One possible category of approaches is to allocate a fixed portion of resources to each node on the link. There are two ways we could consider dividing up the resources. In frequency-division multiplexing, we allocate a different slice of frequencies to each computer. (Consider AM/FM radio or broadcast TV, which divide up frequencies into channels.) In time-division multiplexing, we divide time into fixed slots and allocate a slot to every connected node.","zh_html":"一类可能的方法是为链路上的每个节点分配固定的资源份额。我们可以考虑两种划分资源的方式。在<strong>频分多址(FDMA, frequency-division multiple access)</strong>中，每个节点被分配一个专用的频率子带。在<strong>时分多址(TDMA, time-division multiple access)</strong>中，每个节点被分配一个专用的时隙(time slot)。"},{"en":"Fixed allocation of resources has some downsides. There’s only a limited amount of frequency/time to distribute. Also, not everyone has something to say all the time, so the frequency/time we allocate might go unused most of the time. This approach is wasteful, because it confines computers to their specific allocated slice, even while other slices might be unused.","zh_html":"固定资源分配有一些缺点。可分配的频率/时间总量是有限的。而且，并非每个人一直都有话要说，因此我们分配给人人但未被使用的频率/时间就被浪费了。"},{"en":"Instead of fixed allocation, another category of approaches are based on the nodes taking turns, without any fixed allocations. In this category, we’re dynamically partitioning by time, so that nodes use only the time they need during their turn, with no wasted time. There are two ways we could consider having nodes take turns.","zh_html":"除了固定分配，另一类方法基于节点轮流(taking turns)，没有任何固定分配。在这类方法中，我们按时间动态划分，使节点得到公平的发送机会。"},{"en":"In a polling protocol, a centralized coordinator decides when each connected node gets to speak. The coordinator goes to each node one by one and asks if the node has something to say. If the node says yes, the coordinator lets the node speak for some time. If the node says no, the coordinator immediately moves on to the next node, and the node doesn’t waste any resources. Bluetooth is a real-world protocol using this idea.","zh_html":"在<strong>轮询协议(polling protocol)</strong>中，一个集中的协调器决定每个连接节点何时可以发言。协调器逐个询问每个节点是否有话要说。如果节点说有，协调器就为该节点分配一个时隙。如果节点说没有，协调器就继续询问下一个节点。"},{"en":"The other way to let nodes take turns is token passing. Instead of having a centralized coordinator, we have a virtual token that can be passed between nodes, and only the node with the token is allowed to speak. If a node has something to say, it holds onto the token while transmitting, then passes it to the next node. If a node doesn’t have anything to say at the moment, it immediately passes the token to the next node. IBM Token Ring and FDDI are real-world examples of protocols that use this idea.","zh_html":"让节点轮流发言的另一种方式是<strong>令牌传递(token passing)</strong>。我们不需要集中式协调器，而是在节点之间传递一个虚拟令牌(token)，只有拥有令牌的节点才被允许发送。"},{"en":"One downside to these turn-based approaches is complexity. We have to implement some form of inter-node communication, which could get complicated. In token passing, we might need some dedicated frequency channel for nodes to reliably pass the token between each other. We might also have to deal with complications like two nodes both thinking they have the token and causing a collision. In a polling protocol, we need to designate a central coordinator to communicate with nodes, and implement a way for the coordinator to talk to the nodes. In Bluetooth, your smartphone can be the central coordinator talking to auxiliary devices, but in other networks, it might not be obvious who the coordinator is.","zh_html":"这些轮流方法的一个缺点是复杂性。我们必须实现某种形式的节点间通信，这可能变得复杂。在令牌传递中，我们可能需要一些专用的频率或时间来传递令牌。同时，轮询需要额外的消息来询问和响应。"},{"en":"Communicating over Shared Media: Random Access Approaches","zh_html":"<a href=\"#communicating-over-shared-media-random-access-approaches\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"communicating-over-shared-media-random-access-approaches\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 在共享介质上通信：随机接入方法(Random Access Approaches)"},{"en":"A third category of approaches, besides fixed allocation or taking turns, is random access. In this approach, we just allow nodes to talk whenever they have something to say, and deal with collisions when they occur. The nodes don’t coordinate between each other, and just send data whenever they have something to send.","zh_html":"除了固定分配或轮流之外，第三类方法是<strong>随机接入(random access)</strong>。在这种方法中，我们允许节点在有话要说时随时发言，如果发生碰撞则稍后处理。"},{"en":"One major benefit of random access protocols is simplicity. Unlike the turn-based approaches, we don’t need to implement inter-node communication.","zh_html":"随机接入协议的一个主要好处是简单性。与轮流方法不同，我们不需要实现节点间通信。"},{"en":"When the recipient gets a packet, it replies with an ack. If two nodes send data simultaneously, the collision causes their packets to be corrupted, so no ack is sent. If the sender doesn’t see an ack, it waits some random amount of time and re-sends. Waiting some random amount of time, instead of re-sending immediately, helps us avoid collisions when the packets are resent.","zh_html":"当接收方收到一个分组时，它回复一个确认(ack)。如果两个节点同时发送数据，碰撞会导致它们的分组损坏，因此不会发送确认。如果发送方没有在超时时间内看到确认，它就知道必须重传。"},{"en":"The naive random access protocol is “rude” because nodes start talking whenever they want, and deal with collisions afterwards. A more “polite” variant of this protocol is called Carrier Sense Multiple Access (CSMA). Nodes listen to the shared medium first to see if anybody is speaking, and only start talking when it is quiet. Here, “listen” refers to sensing a signal on the wire.","zh_html":"朴素的随机接入协议是\"粗鲁的\"，因为节点在任何想说话的时候就开始说话，之后才处理碰撞。该协议的一个更\"礼貌\"的变种称为<strong>载波侦听多路访问(CSMA, Carrier Sense Multiple Access)</strong>。在 CSMA 中，节点在说话之前先\"倾听\"：如果介质空闲，你就说话；如果介质忙，你就等待。"},{"en":"Note that CSMA does not help us avoid all collisions. If signals instantaneously propagated along the entire length of the wire, there would be no collisions in CSMA. However, propagation delay can introduce issues. Suppose node A on one end of the wire hears silence and starts transmitting. The signal might not have propagated to node B yet, on the other end of the wire. Node B hears silence and also starts transmitting, causing a collision.","zh_html":"注意 CSMA 并不能帮助我们避免所有碰撞。如果信号瞬间沿着整个导线传播，CSMA 就不会产生碰撞。然而，传播延迟(propagation delay)会干扰这个过程：一个节点可能开始发送，而另一个节点的信号尚未到达。"},{"en":"This 2D diagram demonstrates how propagation delay can cause conflicts. A horizontal cross-section shows the wire at an instant in time, and lets us see how far the signal has propagated across the wire at that instant. A vertical cross-section shows a single location on the wire across time, and lets us see when that location sees the first and last bits of the transmission. Both H2 and H4 hear silence before they start transmitting, but their signals still collide.","zh_html":"这个 2D 图演示了传播延迟如何导致冲突。水平截面显示了导线在某一时刻的状态，让我们看到信号已经沿导线传播了多远。"},{"en":"To mitigate this problem, we can use CSMA/CD (Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Detection), which extends the idea of CSMA. In addition to listening before speaking, we also listen while we speak. If you start hearing something while you’re transmitting, you stop immediately. Note that CSMA/CD still doesn’t fix the problem of collisions, but it allows us to detect collisions sooner.","zh_html":"为了缓解这个问题，我们可以使用 <strong>CSMA/CD</strong>(载波侦听多路访问/碰撞检测, Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Detection)，它扩展了 CSMA 的思想。除了在说话之前侦听，我们在说话的同时也侦听：在发送数据的同时监听碰撞。如果检测到碰撞，包括发送方在内的所有人立即停止发送。"},{"en":"If there’s only one speaker, there won’t be any collisions, and all of our random access schemes should work fine. If there are only a few speakers, there might be occasional collisions, but all of our schemes can deal with them. However, if many senders want to talk simultaneously, we may have problems with repeated collisions, and waiting a random amount of time to re-send won’t help.","zh_html":"如果只有一个发言者，就不会有任何碰撞，我们所有的随机接入方案都应该正常工作。如果只有少量发言者，可能会出现偶发碰撞，但我们所有的方案在经过几次重传后应该都能成功。如果有许多发言者，就会有很多碰撞，我们需要某种方式来应对。"},{"en":"To deal with repeated collisions, CSMA/CD uses binary exponential backoff. Each time we detect a collision on a retransmission attempt, we wait up to twice as long before the next retransmission. Note that we still randomly choose the retransimssion time, but each time we detect a collision, we choose the random number from a range with a limit that’s twice as high. For example, if we chose a random time in the range [0, 4] and detected a collision, the next random time we choose is in the range [0, 8].","zh_html":"为了处理重复碰撞，CSMA/CD 使用<strong>二进制指数退避(binary exponential backoff)</strong>。每次我们在一次重传尝试中检测到碰撞，我们就等待最多两倍的时间再进行下一次重传。注意退避只适用于发生碰撞的分组——新分组不受限制。"},{"en":"Binary exponential backoff works well in both scenarios. When there are a few nodes speaking, repeated collisions are uncommon, so we can retransmit after a short wait time. When there are many nodes speaking, there are many repeated collisions, so the delay increases exponentially until there are no collisions (e.g. enough nodes have been delayed far into the future, and there are fewer nodes competing right now). This approach ensures we only slow down when many nodes want to speak, and maintains fast transmission when few nodes want to speak.","zh_html":"二进制指数退避在两种情况下都表现良好。当只有少量节点在说话时，重复碰撞并不常见，因此我们可以在短暂的等待后重传。当有很多节点在说话时，冲突很常见，因此我们等待更长时间，多次重试，最终成功发送。"},{"en":"Brief History of Layer 2: ALOHANet","zh_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-layer-2-alohanet\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-layer-2-alohanet\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 第 2 层简史：ALOHANet"},{"en":"In 1968, Norman Abramson had a problem at the University of Hawaii. There was a central computer at the University of Hawaii, and he needed a way for computers on other islands to access this central computer. The resulting design was very influential to modern Layer 2 protocol designs.","zh_html":"1968 年，Norman Abramson 在夏威夷大学遇到了一个问题。夏威夷大学有一台中央计算机，他需要一种方法让其他岛屿上的计算机访问这台中央计算机，但在水下铺设电缆成本高昂。"},{"en":"The resulting protocol was called ALOHANet (Additive Links On-line Hawaii Area), which allowed wireless communication from other islands to the central computer. ALOHANet was wireless and used a shared medium, where everybody is sending data over the same link.","zh_html":"由此产生的协议被称为 ALOHANet(Additive Links On-line Hawaii Area)，它允许从其他岛屿到中央计算机的无线通信。ALOHANet 使用无线方式，使用共享介质(无线电频率)。它是首批使用随机接入(random access)来处理碰撞的系统之一。"},{"en":"ALOHANet used a combination of fixed allocation and random access, because of its asymmetric setup. The central computer (hub) used its own dedicated frequency to transmit outgoing messages, and all remote nodes listened on this frequency to receive messages. With only one sender on a dedicated frequency, there’s no risk of collisions.","zh_html":"ALOHANet 由于其非对称的设计，结合了固定分配和随机接入。中央计算机(集线器, hub)使用自己专用的频率发送出站消息，所有远程节点都监听该频率。这意味着远程节点之间的传输不会相互干扰，因为它们在不同的频率上传输。"},{"en":"By contrast, all the remote nodes transmit on a separate shared frequency, and the hub listened to this frequency. The hub won’t collide with the remote nodes, because they use different frequencies, but the remote nodes could collide with each other.","zh_html":"相比之下，所有远程节点在另一个单独的共享频率上传输，集线器监听这个频率。集线器不会与远程节点碰撞，因为它们使用不同的频率，但两个远程节点可能使用相同的共享频率相互碰撞。"},{"en":"This asymmetric design worked well for ALOHANet because the hub probably has more to send than the remote nodes.","zh_html":"这种非对称设计对 ALOHANet 很有效，因为集线器可能比远程节点有更多的数据要发送。"},{"en":"ALOHANet was one of the first systems to use a random access protocol to handle collisions, and this approach would later be used in Ethernet. ALOHANet used the naive rude approach to random access. Later protocols like Ethernet used the more polite approach of CSMA/CD, where we listen for collisions before and during transimssion, and we back off exponentially when there are collisions.","zh_html":"ALOHANet 是最早使用随机接入协议处理碰撞的系统之一，这种方法后来被用于以太网。ALOHANet 使用了朴素的粗鲁随机接入方法。后来，以太网添加了载波侦听(carrier sensing)和碰撞检测(collision detection)。"},{"en":"LAN Communication: MAC Addresses","zh_html":"<a href=\"#lan-communication-mac-addresses\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"lan-communication-mac-addresses\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 局域网通信：MAC 地址(MAC Addresses)"},{"en":"Because multiple computers can be connected along the same Ethernet link, we can actually use Layer 2 protocols to send messages between local computers on the same link, without using any Layer 3 protocols at all (e.g. no routers forwarding packets). In the postal system analogy, two people in the same room can pass letters between each other, without sending the letter to the post office.","zh_html":"由于多台计算机可以连接到同一条以太网链路上，我们实际上可以使用第 2 层协议在同一链路上的本地计算机之间发送消息，而无需使用任何第 3 层协议。"},{"en":"One problem with sending messages over a shared media is: When we transmit the message, everybody on the link gets the message, not just the intended recipient. To send a message to just one person, we need an addressing system at Layer 2 so that we can identify which machine the message is intended for. In the postal system analogy, if I speak in a room, everyone gets the message. To talk to one specific person, I need to refer to them using their name.","zh_html":"通过共享介质发送消息的一个问题是：当我们传输消息时，链路上的每个人都会收到该消息，而不仅仅是预期的接收者。为了只向一个人发送消息，我们需要一种寻址方式，让每台计算机将消息只传递给特定的预期接收者。"},{"en":"At Layer 2, every computer has a MAC address (Media Access Control). MAC addresses are 48 bits long, and are usually written in hexadecimal with colons separating every 2 hex digits (8 bits), e.g. f8:ff:c2:2b:36:16. MAC addresses are sometimes called ether addresses or link addresses.","zh_html":"在第 2 层，每台计算机都有一个 <strong>MAC 地址(MAC address)</strong>(介质访问控制, Media Access Control)。MAC 地址是 48 位长，通常用十六进制书写，每两个十六进制数字(8 位)之间用冒号分隔，例如 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">f8:ff:c2:2b:36:16</code>。MAC 地址通常被称为\"物理地址(physical address)\"。"},{"en":"MAC addresses are usually permanently hard-coded (“burned in”) on a device (e.g. the NIC in your computer). Most OSes will let you override the MAC address in software, but every device already comes with a MAC address installed. MAC addresses are allocated according to the manufacturer that creates the hardware. The first two bits are flags, then the next 22 bits identify the manufacturer, then the last 24 bits identify the specific machine within that manufacturer’s address space.","zh_html":"MAC 地址通常在设备上永久硬编码(\"烧录\", burned in)(例如你计算机中的 NIC)。大多数操作系统允许你在软件中覆盖 MAC 地址，但每台设备出厂时已经有一个默认的 MAC 地址。"},{"en":"Why not just use IP addressing? Hosts on a link might want to exchange messages, without ever being connected to the Internet (i.e. they don’t have an IP address at all).","zh_html":"为什么不直接使用 IP 寻址？链路上的主机可能想要交换消息，而根本不连接到互联网(即它们根本没有 IP 地址)。即使它们有 IP 地址，使用第 2 层地址也允许我们绕过 IP 路由的开销。"},{"en":"This permanent addressing scheme is different from IP, where you receive an address when you first join a network, and the address depends on your geographic location. MAC addresses are usually supposed to be globally unique, because you might plug your computer into any local network, and it’d be bad if two computers on a link had the same MAC address.","zh_html":"这种永久性寻址方案与 IP 不同，IP 地址在你首次加入网络时获得，并且取决于你的地理位置。MAC 地址通常应该保持不变，与位置无关，尽管你可以在软件中覆盖它。IP 地址是分层分配的(locational)，而 MAC 地址是扁平的(flat)——它们不包含任何关于设备在网络中位置的信息。"},{"en":"LAN Communication Types, Ethernet Packet Structure","zh_html":"<a href=\"#lan-communication-types-ethernet-packet-structure\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"lan-communication-types-ethernet-packet-structure\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 局域网通信类型、以太网分组结构"},{"en":"There are different possible destinations in a Layer 2 packet. In unicast, the packet is intended to a single recipient. In broadcast, the packet is intended for all machines on the local network. In multicast, the packet is intended for all machines in the local network that belong to a particular group. Machines can choose to join certain groups to receive packets meant for that group. Ethernet supports unicast, multicast, and broadcast.","zh_html":"第 2 层分组中有不同的可能目的地。在<strong>单播(unicast)</strong>中，分组是发给单个接收者的。在<strong>广播(broadcast)</strong>中，分组是发给局域网上所有机器的。在<strong>多播(multicast)</strong>中，分组只发给特定多播组的成员。"},{"en":"Note that broadcast is sometimes thought of as a special case of multicast, where everybody is automatically part of the broadcast group.","zh_html":"注意，广播有时被视为多播的一种特殊情况，其中每个人自动成为广播组的成员。"},{"en":"This unicast/broadcast/multicast model extends to other layers too. For example, we saw anycast at Layer 3, where the goal was to send to any one member of a group (any of the servers with the same IP address).","zh_html":"这种单播/广播/多播模型也扩展到其他层。例如，我们在第 3 层看到了任播(anycast)，其目标是发送给组中的任意一个成员(具有相同 IP 的服务器中的任意一个)。"},{"en":"Ethernet Packet Structure","zh_html":"<a href=\"#ethernet-packet-structure\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ethernet-packet-structure\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 以太网分组结构(Ethernet Packet Structure)"},{"en":"A data packet in Ethernet is called a frame. Many fields look similar to the IP header fields, though there are some differences.","zh_html":"以太网中的数据分组称为<strong>帧(frame)</strong>。许多字段看起来与 IP 首部字段相似，但也有一些差异。"},{"en":"The Ethernet packet starts with a 7-byte preamble, which indicates the start of a packet. This helps separate packets as they’re signalled across the wire.","zh_html":"以太网分组以 7 字节的前导码(preamble)开始，它标识分组的开始。这有助于在信号通过导线传输时区分不同的分组。"},{"en":"Then, we have the destination and source MAC addresses, similar to the destination and source fields in the IP header. We have a 2-byte type field, which allows us to demultiplex between IPv4 or IPv6, and pass the packet payload to the correct next protocol. This is similar to the protocol field in the IP header, or the port field in the TCP/UDP headers. We also have a checksum, though unlike IP, the checksum is over the entire packet, so that we don’t have to rely on higher layers (e.g. the packet might not be TCP/IP at all).","zh_html":"然后，我们有目的 MAC 地址和源 MAC 地址，类似于 IP 首部中的目的和源字段。我们有一个 2 字节的类型字段(type field)，它允许我们在 IPv4 或 IPv6 之间进行多路分解(demultiplex)，类似于 IP 首部中的协议字段(protocol field)。我们还有一个 4 字节的帧校验序列(FCS, frame check sequence)，类似于 IP 首部中的校验和(checksum)。"},{"en":"To unicast a message, we set the destination MAC address to a specific machine’s MAC address. Everybody on the shared medium receives the packet, so everybody needs to check the destination MAC to see if the packet is meant for them. If the destination MAC address doesn’t match your address, you should ignore the packet.","zh_html":"要单播一条消息，我们将目的 MAC 地址设置为某台特定机器的 MAC 地址。共享介质上的每个人都会收到该分组，因此每个人都需要检查目的 MAC 以查看该分组是否发给自己。如果不是，就丢弃该分组。"},{"en":"To broadcast a message, we set the destination MAC to the special address FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF (all ones). Just like in unicast, everybody on the shared medium receives the packet, but this time, because the destination MAC address is the broadcast address, everybody knows to reads the packet. Note that this all-ones broadcast address is the same in every Ethernet network.","zh_html":"要广播一条消息，我们将目的 MAC 设置为特殊地址 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF</code>(全 1)。与单播一样，共享介质上的每个人都会收到该分组，但这一次，因为目的 MAC 是广播地址，每个人都知道该分组是发给所有人的。"},{"en":"To multicast a message, we set the destination MAC to the address of that group. Recall that the first two bits of the MAC addresses are flags. Normal addresses allocated to machines always set the first bit to 0, and addresses for groups always set the first bit to 1. Just like in unicast and broadcast, everybody still gets the message. Anybody who’s part of a group needs to make sure they’re listening on that group’s address in order to receive packets multicast to that group. Additional protocols are necessary to control who belongs to which groups, and we won’t discuss them further.","zh_html":"要多播一条消息，我们将目的 MAC 设置为该组的地址。回想一下，MAC 地址的前两位是标志位。分配给机器的正常地址总是将第一位设置为 0。多播地址将第一位设置为 1，第二位设置为 1(表示多播组地址)。"},{"en":"Layer 2 Networks with Ethernet","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layer-2-networks-with-ethernet\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layer-2-networks-with-ethernet\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 具有以太网的第 2 层网络(Layer 2 Networks with Ethernet)"},{"en":"So far, we’ve shown Layer 2 protocols as operating on a single link with multiple computers attached to it, but we could introduce multiple links and build a network entirely using Layer 2. Packets could be forwarded, and machines could even run routing protocols, all exclusively using Layer 2 MAC addresses.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们展示了第 2 层协议在一条连接多台计算机的单一链路上运行，但我们可以引入多条链路并完全使用第 2 层构建一个网络。分组可以经过一跳或多跳(通过交换机, switches)到达目的地。"},{"en":"The routing protocols we ran at the IP layer could also work at Layer 2, though one downside is that we can’t aggregate MAC addresses. IP addresses are allocated based on geography, but MAC addresses are allocated based on manufacturer, so there’s no clear way to aggregate them. This downside is why we can’t build the global Internet out of only Layer 2.","zh_html":"我们在 IP 层运行的路由协议也可以在第 2 层工作，但一个缺点是 MAC 地址无法聚合(aggregate)。IP 地址基于地理分配，但 MAC 地址是扁平的——它们不给出关于设备位置的信息。因此，我们需要比 IP 路由更简单的第 2 层路由协议。"},{"en":"If there are multiple links in a single local network, we’d have to ensure that if someone broadcasts a message, any switches at Layer 2 forward the packet out of all outgoing ports.","zh_html":"如果单个局域网中有多条链路，我们必须确保如果有人广播一条消息，第 2 层的交换机(switches)将该分组从所有出站端口转发出去。"},{"en":"Multicast gets more complicated in a Layer 2 network with multiple links. Additional protocols are needed, though we won’t discuss further.","zh_html":"在具有多条链路的第 2 层网络中，多播变得更加复杂。需要额外的协议，我们不再进一步讨论。"},{"en":"One example of multicast being useful on a LAN is Bonjour/mDNS, a protocol developed by Apple. In this protocol, all Apple devices (e.g. iPhone, iPad, Apple TV) are hard-coded to join a special group on the local network. If your iPhone wants to find nearby devices to play music (e.g. Apple TV, Apple speaker or HomePod or whatever they call it), the iPhone can multicast a message to the group, asking if anybody can play music. Devices in the group can also multicast responses, saying “I am an Apple TV and I can play music.” Interestingly, this protocol actually also uses DNS in the multicast group to send SRV records, which maps each machine to its capabilities.","zh_html":"多播在局域网上有用的一个例子是 Bonjour/mDNS，这是 Apple 开发的一个协议。在这个协议中，所有 Apple 设备(例如 iPhone、iPad、Apple TV)被硬编码加入一个特殊的多播组。当某设备加入网络时，它可以发送一条多播消息寻找打印机或其他服务。"},{"en":"Historical note: In the modern Internet, we’ve said that the terms “router” and “switch” are interchangeable. Now that we have the notion of a Layer 2 network, we could say that a switch only operates at Layers 1 and 2, while a router operates at Layers 1, 2, and 3.","zh_html":"历史注记：在现代互联网中，我们说\"路由器(router)\"和\"交换机(switch)\"这两个术语可以互换。现在我们有了第 2 层网络的概念，我们可以说交换机只操作到第 2 层，而路由器(第 3 层设备)也解析 IP 首部。然而，在实践中，这两个术语仍然几乎可以互换使用。"},{"en":"If you go back to our picture of wrapping and unwrapping headers, we’ve assumed that every router parses the packet up to Layer 3, and forwards the packet to the next router over IP. However, if we had a Layer 2 network with multiple links, a switch only needs to pass the packet up to Layer 2 and forward the packet to the next switch over Ethernet.","zh_html":"如果你回到我们包装和解包首部的图中，我们假设每台路由器解析分组直到第 3 层，并通过 IP 将分组转发到下一台路由器。然而，如果我们有一条连接两台路由器的单一链路，我们仍然需要在底层使用第 2 层以太网相关技术(ethernet)进行通信。"},{"en":"Today, pretty much all switches also implement Layer 3, which is why we use the terms interchangeably. Historically, Ethernet predates the Internet, which is why there was a distinction between switches and routers.","zh_html":"今天，几乎所有交换机也实现了第 3 层，这就是为什么我们可以互换使用这两个术语。历史上，以太网(Ethernet)早于互联网，因此过去交换机和路由器之间是有区别的：交换机只解析到第 2 层，而路由器解析到第 3 层。"}]},"/end-to-end/index.html":{"title":"index","blocks":[{"en":"Ethernet","en_html":"<a href=\"ethernet.html\">Ethernet</a>","zh_html":"以太网(Ethernet)"},{"en":"Layer 2 Routing (STP)","en_html":"<a href=\"l2-routing.html\">Layer 2 Routing (STP)</a>","zh_html":"第二层路由(Layer 2 Routing, STP)"},{"en":"ARP: Connecting Layers 2 and 3","en_html":"<a href=\"arp.html\">ARP: Connecting Layers 2 and 3</a>","zh_html":"ARP：连接第二层与第三层(ARP: Connecting Layers 2 and 3)"},{"en":"DHCP: Joining Networks","en_html":"<a href=\"dhcp.html\">DHCP: Joining Networks</a>","zh_html":"DHCP：接入网络(DHCP: Joining Networks)"},{"en":"NAT: Network Address Translation","en_html":"<a href=\"nat.html\">NAT: Network Address Translation</a>","zh_html":"NAT：网络地址转换(NAT: Network Address Translation)"},{"en":"TLS: Secure Bytestreams","en_html":"<a href=\"tls.html\">TLS: Secure Bytestreams</a>","zh_html":"TLS：安全字节流(TLS: Secure Bytestreams)"},{"en":"End-to-End Connectivity","en_html":"<a href=\"end-to-end.html\">End-to-End Connectivity</a>","zh_html":"端到端连接(End-to-End Connectivity)"}]},"/end-to-end/l2-routing.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Layer 2 Routing (STP)","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layer-2-routing-stp\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layer-2-routing-stp\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 第 2 层路由(STP, Layer 2 Routing)"},{"en":"Layer 2 Networks with Ethernet","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layer-2-networks-with-ethernet\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layer-2-networks-with-ethernet\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 具有以太网的第 2 层网络(Layer 2 Networks with Ethernet)"},{"en":"So far, we’ve shown Layer 2 protocols as operating on a single link with multiple computers attached to it, but we could introduce multiple links and build a network entirely using Layer 2. Packets could be forwarded, and machines could even run routing protocols, all exclusively using Layer 2 MAC addresses.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们展示了第 2 层协议在一条连接多台计算机的单一链路上运行，但我们可以引入多条链路并完全使用第 2 层构建一个网络。分组可以经过一跳或多跳(通过交换机, switches)到达目的地。"},{"en":"The routing protocols we ran at the IP layer could also work at Layer 2, though one downside is that we can’t aggregate MAC addresses. IP addresses are allocated based on geography, but MAC addresses are allocated based on manufacturer, so there’s no clear way to aggregate them. This downside is why we can’t build the global Internet out of only Layer 2.","zh_html":"我们在 IP 层运行的路由协议也可以在第 2 层工作，但一个缺点是 MAC 地址无法聚合(aggregate)。IP 地址基于地理分配，但 MAC 地址是扁平的——它们不给出关于设备位置的信息。"},{"en":"If there are multiple links in a single local network, we’d have to ensure that if someone broadcasts a message, any switches at Layer 2 forward the packet out of all outgoing ports.","zh_html":"如果单个局域网中有多条链路，我们必须确保如果有人广播一条消息，第 2 层的交换机(switches)将该分组从所有出站端口转发出去。"},{"en":"Multicast gets more complicated in a Layer 2 network with multiple links. Additional protocols are needed, discussed later (in the Special Topics section).","zh_html":"在具有多条链路的第 2 层网络中，多播变得更加复杂。需要额外的协议，稍后讨论(在专题部分)。"},{"en":"One example of multicast being useful on a LAN is Bonjour/mDNS, a protocol developed by Apple. In this protocol, all Apple devices (e.g. iPhone, iPad, Apple TV) are hard-coded to join a special group on the local network. If your iPhone wants to find nearby devices to play music (e.g. Apple TV, Apple speaker or HomePod or whatever they call it), the iPhone can multicast a message to the group, asking if anybody can play music. Devices in the group can also multicast responses, saying “I am an Apple TV and I can play music.” Interestingly, this protocol actually also uses DNS in the multicast group to send SRV records, which maps each machine to its capabilities.","zh_html":"多播在局域网上有用的一个例子是 Bonjour/mDNS，这是 Apple 开发的一个协议。在这个协议中，所有 Apple 设备(例如 iPhone、iPad、Apple TV)被硬编码加入一个特殊的多播组。当某设备加入网络时，它可以发送一条多播消息寻找打印机或其他服务。"},{"en":"Historical note: In the modern Internet, we’ve said that the terms “router” and “switch” are interchangeable. Now that we have the notion of a Layer 2 network, we could say that a switch only operates at Layers 1 and 2, while a router operates at Layers 1, 2, and 3.","zh_html":"历史注记：在现代互联网中，我们说\"路由器(router)\"和\"交换机(switch)\"这两个术语可以互换。现在我们有了第 2 层网络的概念，我们可以说交换机只操作到第 2 层，而路由器(第 3 层设备)也解析 IP 首部。"},{"en":"If you go back to our picture of wrapping and unwrapping headers, we’ve assumed that every router parses the packet up to Layer 3, and forwards the packet to the next router over IP. However, if we had a Layer 2 network with multiple links, a switch only needs to pass the packet up to Layer 2 and forward the packet to the next switch over Ethernet.","zh_html":"如果你回到我们包装和解包首部的图中，我们假设每台路由器解析分组直到第 3 层，并通过 IP 将分组转发到下一台路由器。然而，如果我们有一条连接两台路由器的单一链路，我们仍然需要在底层使用第 2 层以太网相关技术进行通信。"},{"en":"Today, pretty much all switches also implement Layer 3, which is why we use the terms interchangeably. Historically, Ethernet predates the Internet, which is why there was a distinction between switches and routers.","zh_html":"今天，几乎所有交换机也实现了第 3 层，这就是为什么我们可以互换使用这两个术语。历史上，以太网早于互联网，因此过去交换机和路由器之间是有区别的：交换机只解析到第 2 层，而路由器解析到第 3 层。"},{"en":"Layer 2 Network Topology","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layer-2-network-topology\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layer-2-network-topology\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 第 2 层网络拓扑(Layer 2 Network Topology)"},{"en":"Just like we saw in the routing unit, there are many different topologies we can use to connect up computers in a local network.","zh_html":"就像我们在路由单元中看到的，有许多不同的拓扑(topologies)可用于连接局域网中的计算机。"},{"en":"We could use a single link to connect all the computers, but this is inefficient. We only have a single link’s worth of bandwidth to use. Also, everyone needs to wait their turn to send messages, and if two computers send messages simultaneously, there could be collisions.","zh_html":"我们可以使用一条单一的链路连接所有计算机，但这效率很低。我们只能使用一条链路的带宽。此外，每个人都需要轮流等待发送消息，因此随着更多主机加入网络，情况会变慢。"},{"en":"We could also use a full mesh, which gives every pair of hosts a dedicated link, but is difficult to scale up.","zh_html":"我们也可以使用全网状拓扑(full mesh)，它为每对主机提供了一条专用链路，但难以扩展。"},{"en":"Just like at Layer 3, we could introduce switches that forward packets through a topology, toward their final destination. But, just like at Layer 3, this introduces the routing problem, where the switches need to decide where to forward packets.","zh_html":"就像在第 3 层一样，我们可以引入交换机(switches)，通过拓扑结构将分组转发至最终目的地。但是，就像在第 3 层一样，这引入了路由问题(routing problem)：交换机需要知道将每个分组发送到哪里。"},{"en":"In this section, we’ll explore some routing protocols that are specifically designed for local Layer 2 networks. We’ll also see some challenges that prevent these protocols from being scaled up and used for the global Layer 3 network.","zh_html":"在本节中，我们将探讨一些专门为本地第 2 层网络设计的路由协议。我们还将看到一些阻止这些协议被扩展到并用于广域网(wide-area networks)的挑战。"},{"en":"Forwarding with Flooding","zh_html":"<a href=\"#forwarding-with-flooding\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"forwarding-with-flooding\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 通过泛洪转发(Forwarding with Flooding)"},{"en":"The most naive approach to forwarding is to flood every packet you receive. When a switch receives a packet, it sends the packet out of every port.","zh_html":"最朴素的转发方法是对你收到的每个分组进行泛洪(flood)。当交换机收到一个分组时，它将该分组从每个端口发送出去。"},{"en":"As a slight optimization, we don’t need to send the packet back out of the port we received the packet from.","zh_html":"作为一个小的优化，我们不需要将分组从收到它的那个端口再发送回去。"},{"en":"This naive approach has two major problems:","zh_html":"这种朴素的方法有两个主要问题："},{"en":"It wastes bandwidth. Copies of the packet get unnecessarily sent toward switches and hosts that don’t need that packet.","zh_html":"<p>浪费带宽。分组副本被不必要地发送到不需要该分组的交换机和主机。</p>"},{"en":"Flooding can cause packets to loop and overwhelm the network.","zh_html":"<p>泛洪可能导致分组循环(loop)并使网络不堪重负。</p>"},{"en":"Learning Switches","zh_html":"<a href=\"#learning-switches\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"learning-switches\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 自学习交换机(Learning Switches)"},{"en":"Let’s start with the first problem: Flooding packets wastes bandwidth.","zh_html":"让我们从第一个问题开始：泛洪分组浪费带宽。"},{"en":"To solve this problem, we’d like to populate the forwarding tables for switches, so that they are able to forward packets directly toward their destination, instead of flooding copies of the packets in all directions.","zh_html":"为了解决这个问题，我们需要填充交换机的转发表(forwarding tables)，使它们能够将分组直接转发到目的地，而不是在网络中泛洪分组副本。"},{"en":"We could run a routing algorithm to populate the forwarding tables, but an even simpler approach is use learning switches.","zh_html":"我们可以运行一个路由算法来填充转发表，但更简单的方法是使用<strong>自学习交换机(learning switches)</strong>。"},{"en":"Suppose you are router R2. You don’t have any information about the full network topology, and your forwarding table is empty. You have ports to the north, south, east, and west.","zh_html":"假设你是路由器 R2。你没有任何关于完整网络拓扑的信息，你的转发表是空的。你有分别通向北方、南方、东方和西方的端口。"},{"en":"You see a packet coming from the port to your west. The packet says: “From A, To B.” From this packet, you can deduce that A must be to your west.","zh_html":"你看到一个分组从你的西边端口到达。分组说：\"From A, To B。\"从这个分组中，你可以推断出 A 一定在你的西边。"},{"en":"You can now add an entry to your forwarding table: Packets for A should be forwarded to the west.","zh_html":"你现在可以在你的转发表中添加一个条目：发往 A 的分组应该转发到西边。"},{"en":"This is the key idea behind learning switches. When you receive an incoming packet, you get a clue about where the sender is. You can use that information to populate the forwarding entry for the sender.","zh_html":"这就是自学习交换机的核心思想。当你收到一个入站分组时，你会获得关于<em>发送方</em>所在位置的线索。你可以利用这些信息来填充发送方的转发条目。"},{"en":"Note that the incoming packet doesn’t tell you anything about where the recipient is. In the example above, when you receive “From A, To B” from the west port, that doesn’t tell you anything about where B (recipient) is. Instead, you populate the forwarding table for A, so that future packets for A can be forwarded to the west.","zh_html":"注意，入站分组不会告诉你关于接收方位置的任何信息。在上面的例子中，当你从西边端口收到\"From A, To B\"时，这不会告诉你任何关于 B 位置的信息。"},{"en":"As you receive more incoming packets, you are able to start filling in your forwarding table with more entries. If you receive a packet whose destination is not in your forwarding table, you can still forward the packet by flooding it out of all ports (except the incoming port).","zh_html":"随着你收到更多的入站分组，你开始用更多的条目填充转发表。如果你收到的分组的目的地不在你的转发表中，你仍然可以泛洪该分组。"},{"en":"For example, when you receive “From A, To B” from the west port, you don’t have a forwarding table for B yet. Therefore, you should forward this packet out of all ports (except the west port).","zh_html":"例如，当你从西边端口收到\"From A, To B\"时，你还没有 B 的转发表条目。因此，你应该将该分组转发到所有端口(除了西边端口)。"},{"en":"Note: There’s no need to send the packet back out of the incoming port (e.g. west), because the previous switch/host (e.g. to your west) already had a copy of the packet and forwarded it (that’s how it reached you). If you send the packet back again, the previous switch/host would just make the same forwarding decision again (either flooding again, or forwarding back to you again), and this repeated forwarding doesn’t help the packet reach its destination.","zh_html":"注意：不需要将分组从入站端口(例如西边)发回，因为上一台交换机/主机(例如你西边的那个)已经有一份分组副本并已将其转发(这就是它到达这里的方式)。"},{"en":"In summary, learning switches have two rules to follow:","zh_html":"总之，自学习交换机要遵循两条规则："},{"en":"When you receive an incoming packet, update the forwarding table to associate the sender with the incoming port.","zh_html":"<p>当你收到一个入站分组时，更新转发表，将发送方与入站端口关联起来。</p>"},{"en":"If the destination is in your forwarding table, then forward the packet to the correct next-hop. Otherwise, flood the packet out of all ports except the incoming port.","zh_html":"<p>如果目的地在你的转发表中，则将分组转发到正确的下一跳。否则，将分组泛洪到除入站端口外的所有端口。</p>"},{"en":"Here’s an example of learning switches in action. Consider this network topology. All switches are learning switches, and their forwarding tables start empty.","zh_html":"以下是一个自学习交换机运行中的示例。考虑这个网络拓扑。所有交换机都是自学习交换机，它们的转发表开始时都是空的。"},{"en":"A sends a packet to B. A forwards the packet to R1.","zh_html":"A 向 B 发送一个分组。A 将分组转发到 R1。"},{"en":"R1 sees the packet “From A, To B” incoming from Port 1. Therefore, A must be toward Port 1. R1 adds this mapping to its forwarding table.","zh_html":"R1 看到从端口 1 进入的分组\"From A, To B\"。因此，A 一定在端口 1 方向。R1 将此映射添加到其转发表中。"},{"en":"R1 does not know where B is, so R1 floods this packet out of all ports (except the incoming port).","zh_html":"R1 不知道 B 在哪里，因此 R1 将该分组泛洪到所有端口(除了入站端口)。"},{"en":"R2 and R4 both receive the “From A, to B” packet. Both of them now have a clue about where A is, and add a mapping for A to their forwarding tables. Both of them do not know where B is, so they flood the packet out of all ports (except the incoming port).","zh_html":"R2 和 R4 都收到\"From A, to B\"分组。它们现在都有了关于 A 位置的线索，并将 A 的映射添加到它们的转发表中。它们都不知道 B 在哪里，因此它们将分组泛洪到所有端口(除了入站端口)。"},{"en":"R3 and R5 both receive the “From A, to B” packet. Both of them now have a clue about where A is, and add a mapping for A to their forwarding tables. Both of them do not know where B is, so they flood the packet out of all ports (except the incoming port).","zh_html":"R3 和 R5 都收到\"From A, to B\"分组。它们现在都有了关于 A 位置的线索，并将 A 的映射添加到它们的转发表中。它们都不知道 B 在哪里，因此它们将分组泛洪到所有端口(除了入站端口)。"},{"en":"C receives the “From A, to B” packet. C checks the header and realizes that it is not the intended recipient of this packet, so C drops the packet.","zh_html":"C 收到\"From A, to B\"分组。C 检查首部并意识到它不是该分组的预期接收者，因此 C 丢弃该分组。"},{"en":"B receives the “From A, to B” packet. B checks the header and realizes that it is the recipient, so B successfully receives and processes this packet.","zh_html":"B 收到\"From A, to B\"分组。B 检查首部并意识到它是接收者，因此 B 成功接收并处理该分组。"},{"en":"Next, suppose B sends a packet to A. First, B forwards the packet to R3.","zh_html":"接下来，假设 B 向 A 发送一个分组。首先，B 将分组转发到 R3。"},{"en":"R3 receives the “From B, to A” packet. This gives R3 a clue about where B is, so R3 adds a mapping for B to its forwarding table. Also, R3 notices that A is in its forwarding table, so R3 can forward the packet along the next-hop to A (instead of flooding the packet).","zh_html":"R3 收到\"From B, to A\"分组。这给了 R3 关于 B 位置的线索，因此 R3 将 B 的映射添加到其转发表中。此外，R3 注意到 A 在其转发表中，因此 R3 可以直接将分组转发到通往 R1 的下一跳。"},{"en":"R2 receives the “From B, to A” packet. This allows R2 to add a mapping for B to its forwarding table. R2 looks in its forwarding table and sees an entry for A, so it forwards the packet along the next-hop to A.","zh_html":"R2 收到\"From B, to A\"分组。这使得 R2 可以将 B 的映射添加到其转发表中。R2 查看其转发表，发现 A 的条目，因此它将分组沿通往 R1 的下一跳转发。"},{"en":"R1 receives the “From B, to A” packet. This allows R1 to add a mapping for B to its forwarding table. R1 looks in its forwarding table and sees an entry for A, so it forwards the packet along the next-hop to A.","zh_html":"R1 收到\"From B, to A\"分组。这使得 R1 可以将 B 的映射添加到其转发表中。R1 查看其转发表，发现 A 的条目，因此它将分组沿通往 A 的下一跳转发。"},{"en":"As more packets get sent, more entries get added to forwarding tables, and less flooding takes place.","zh_html":"随着更多分组的发送，越来越多的条目被添加到转发表中，泛洪就越来越少。"},{"en":"One last feature we need to add: When a forwarding table entry is installed, we assign it a TTL. If the TTL expires, the entry is deleted. This allows routes that are busted (e.g. because a link, host, or switch went down) to expire. For example, if B leaves the network in the example above, the TTL will ensure that all forwarding tables for B will eventually expire.","zh_html":"我们需要添加的最后一个特性：当安装一个转发表条目时，我们为其分配一个 TTL。如果 TTL 到期，该条目被删除。这允许失效的路由(例如由于链路、主机或交换机断开连接)被清理，并在必要时通过泛洪重新发现。"},{"en":"STP Motivation: Loops","zh_html":"<a href=\"#stp-motivation-loops\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"stp-motivation-loops\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> STP 动机：循环(STP Motivation: Loops)"},{"en":"Recall that flooding has two problems: It wastes bandwidth, and loops can overwhelm the network. Learning switches solved the first problem, but they do not solve the problem of loops.","zh_html":"回想一下，泛洪有两个问题：浪费带宽，以及循环可能使网络不堪重负。自学习交换机解决了第一个问题，但它们没有解决循环问题。"},{"en":"To see why, consider this topology with loops. Suppose all switches are learning switches, and all forwarding tables start empty. A tries to send a packet to B, and forwards the packet to R1.","zh_html":"为了理解原因，考虑这个具有循环的拓扑。假设所有交换机都是自学习交换机，所有转发表开始时都是空的。A 试图向 B 发送一个分组，并将该分组转发到 R1。"},{"en":"R1 has no entry for B, so it floods the packet to R2 (and R3).","zh_html":"R1 没有 B 的条目，因此它将分组泛洪到 R2(和 R3)。"},{"en":"R2 has no entry for B, so it floods the packet to R4.","zh_html":"R2 没有 B 的条目，因此它将分组泛洪到 R4。"},{"en":"R4 has no entry for B, so it floods the packet to R3.","zh_html":"R4 没有 B 的条目，因此它将分组泛洪到 R3。"},{"en":"R3 has no entry for B, so it floods the packet to R1.","zh_html":"R3 没有 B 的条目，因此它将分组泛洪到 R1。"},{"en":"R1 has no entry for B, so it floods the packet to R2, and the cycle continues.","zh_html":"R1 没有 B 的条目，因此它将分组泛洪到 R2，循环继续。"},{"en":"At the same time, a copy of the packet is also traveling in a loop in the other direction: R1 flooded to R3 initially, which then flooded to R4, which then flooded to R2, which then flooded to R1, which then floods to R3, continuing the cycle.","zh_html":"同时，分组副本也在另一个方向上循环：R1 最初泛洪到 R3，R3 又泛洪到 R4，R4 又泛洪到 R2，R2 又泛洪到 R1，无限继续。"},{"en":"During this entire process, the switches install forwarding entries for A, but they never get any entries for B, so the infinite loop is never resolved. Nobody has a forwarding entry for B, so everybody floods the packet when they receive it.","zh_html":"在整个过程中，交换机会安装 A 的转发条目，但它们永远无法获得 B 的任何条目，因此无限循环永远不会被解决。没有人有 B 的转发条目，因此每个人继续将分组泛洪给其他人，循环继续。"},{"en":"This problem is sometimes called a broadcast storm, since the network is getting overwhelmed with broadcast traffic.","zh_html":"这个问题有时被称为广播风暴(broadcast storm)，因为网络被广播流量(broadcast traffic)淹没。"},{"en":"How do we solve this problem? Ideally, we’d like to “delete” redundant links, so that the topology has no loops. Then, the learning switch approach will work just fine, with no broadcast storms.","zh_html":"我们如何解决这个问题？理想情况下，我们希望\"删除\"冗余链路(redundant links)，使得拓扑结构没有循环。这样，自学习交换机方法就能正常工作，没有广播风暴。"},{"en":"Note: Another solution might be to add a TTL field to each packet, so that the packet expires after being forwarded too many times. Unfortunately, the Ethernet header does not have a TTL field, so this solution can’t be implemented.","zh_html":"注：另一种解决方案可能是为每个分组添加一个 TTL 字段，使得分组在被转发太多次后过期。不幸的是，以太网首部(Ethernet header)没有 TTL 字段，因此这个解决方案不可行。"},{"en":"Note: Another solution might be to drop packets if you’ve seen them before. This would require attaching some sort of timestamp or unique ID to each packet. Again, the Ethernet header doesn’t have a header field for this, so this solution can’t be implemented either.","zh_html":"注：另一种解决方案可能是丢弃以前见过的分组。这需要为每个分组附加某种时间戳或唯一 ID。同样，以太网首部没有用于此目的的首部字段。"},{"en":"STP: Electing a Root","zh_html":"<a href=\"#stp-electing-a-root\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"stp-electing-a-root\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> STP：选举根桥(STP: Electing a Root)"},{"en":"The Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) helps us disable links, so that the resulting topology has no loops. This will help us avoid broadcast storms.","zh_html":"<strong>生成树协议(STP, Spanning Tree Protocol)</strong>帮助我们禁用链路，使得生成的拓扑结构没有循环。这将帮助我们避免广播风暴。"},{"en":"Note that hosts don’t participate in this protocol. Routers will work together to disable links and reomve loops from the topology. As a result, we’ll ignore hosts when describing this protocol.","zh_html":"注意，主机不参与此协议。路由器将协同工作来禁用链路并从拓扑中移除循环。因此，在描述该协议时，我们将忽略主机。"},{"en":"How does STP decide which links to disable? Let’s start by solving this problem with a global view of the network. Then, we’ll think about how switches exchange messages to achieve this, without a global view of the network.","zh_html":"STP 如何决定禁用哪些链路？让我们从全局视角来看待这个问题。然后，我们将思考交换机如何在无需全局视图的情况下交换消息来实现这一点。"},{"en":"The first step in STP is to elect a root switch, as follows:","zh_html":"STP 的第一步是选举一个<strong>根桥(root switch)</strong>，如下所示："},{"en":"Each switch is assigned an ID, consisting of a priority value (manually set by the network operator), and the MAC address of the switch.","zh_html":"每台交换机被分配一个 ID，由一个优先级值(priority value，由网络管理员手动设置)和交换机的 MAC 地址组成。"},{"en":"When comparing two switches, the switch with the lower priority has the lower ID. If the priorities are tied, then the switch with the lower MAC address has the lower ID.","zh_html":"在比较两台交换机时，优先级较低的交换机具有较低的 ID。如果优先级相同，则 MAC 地址较低的交换机具有较低的 ID。"},{"en":"The root switch is the switch with the lowest ID.","zh_html":"根桥就是 ID 最低的交换机。"},{"en":"If the network operator wants to pick a specific root, they can do so by manually setting the priorities of various switches. Or, the operator could leave all the switch priorities at their default value, which would cause the switch with the lowest MAC address to be elected as the root. For these notes, we won’t discuss which root is best; the important thing is just that one of the routers is unambiguously chosen as the root.","zh_html":"如果网络管理员想选择特定的根桥，他们可以通过手动设置各交换机的优先级来实现。或者，管理员可以保持所有交换机的优先级为默认值，让 MAC 地址决定哪个交换机成为根桥。"},{"en":"STP: Port States","zh_html":"<a href=\"#stp-port-states\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"stp-port-states\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> STP：端口状态(STP: Port States)"},{"en":"Now that we have a root switch, we will classify every port on every switch into one of three states:","zh_html":"现在我们有了根桥，我们将每台交换机上的每个端口分为以下三种状态之一："},{"en":"Designated Port: These are ports pointing away from the root (i.e. they lead somewhere further from the root).","zh_html":"<p><strong>指定端口(Designated Port)：</strong>这些是指向远离根桥方向的端口(即它们通往离根桥更远的地方)。</p>"},{"en":"Root Port: There are one or more ports pointing toward the root (i.e. they lead somewhere closer to the root). Of these ports, the one along the least-cost path to the root is the root port.","zh_html":"<p><strong>根端口(Root Port)：</strong>有一个或多个端口指向根桥方向(即它们通往离根桥更近的地方)。在这些端口中，通往根桥的 least-cost 路径上的那个端口就是根端口。</p>"},{"en":"Blocked Port: All ports pointing toward the root, that are not the root port (best way to reach the root), are blocked ports.","zh_html":"<p><strong>阻塞端口(Blocked Port)：</strong>所有指向根桥方向但不是根端口(到达根桥的最佳方式)的端口都是阻塞端口。</p>"},{"en":"Here are some examples of the port states in action. Assume that IDs are ordered according to the router labels. This means that R1 has the lowest ID, so it is elected as the root switch.","zh_html":"以下是一些端口状态的实际示例。假设 ID 按照路由器标签的顺序排列。这意味着 R1 具有最低的 ID，因此它被选为根桥。"},{"en":"All the ports on the root switch (R1) point away from the root, so they are all designated ports.","zh_html":"根桥(R1)上的所有端口都指向远离根桥的方向，因此它们都是指定端口。"},{"en":"R2 has two ports. Only one of them points toward the root, so that must be the best path toward the root. Therefore, R2’s upward-facing port is labeled as a root port.","zh_html":"R2 有两个端口。其中只有一个指向根桥方向，因此那一定是最好的通往根桥的路径。因此，R2 的向上端口被标记为根端口。"},{"en":"The other port at R2 points away from the root, so R2’s downward-facing port is labeled as a designated port.","zh_html":"R2 的另一个端口指向远离根桥的方向，因此 R2 的向下端口被标记为指定端口。"},{"en":"R6 has three ports. The downward-facing port points away from the root, so it is a designated port.","zh_html":"R6 有三个端口。向下端口指向远离根桥的方向，因此它是一个指定端口。"},{"en":"At R6, the ports to R4 and R3 both point toward the root. However, the port to R3 provides the least-cost path to the root (cost 2), while the port to R4 provides a worse path to the root (cost 3). Therefore, we label the port to R3 as the root port (best way to reach root), and the port to R4 as a blocked port (points toward root, but not the best path).","zh_html":"在 R6，通往 R4 和 R3 的端口都指向根桥方向。然而，通往 R3 的端口提供了到根桥的最小成本路径(cost 2)，而通往 R4 的端口提供了较差的到根桥的路径(cost 3)。因此，通往 R3 的端口被标记为根端口，而通往 R4 的端口被标记为阻塞端口。"},{"en":"Sometimes, we have a tie, and there are two best ways to reach the root.","zh_html":"有时我们会遇到平局(tie)，有两个到达根桥的最佳路径。"},{"en":"For example, at R4, both the port to R2 and the port to R3 point toward the root, and both of them provide a cost-2 path to the root. In case of a tie, we will say that the next-hop with the lower ID is the better path to the root. This makes the port to R2 the root port, and the port to R3 a blocked port.","zh_html":"例如，在 R4，通往 R2 的端口和通往 R3 的端口都指向根桥方向，并且都提供了到根桥的成本为 2 的路径。如果出现平局，我们将选择下一跳 ID 较低的端口作为根端口。这里，R2 的 ID 低于 R3，因此 R4 通往 R2 的端口成为根端口，而通往 R3 的端口成为阻塞端口。"},{"en":"Sometimes, we’ll have a link that leads somewhere equally-far from the root.","zh_html":"有时，我们会有一条链路通向距离根桥同样远的地方。"},{"en":"For example, R4 is distance 2 from the root, and it has a link to R5, which is also distance 2 from the root. Again, we’ll use router IDs as a tiebreaker. If the link leads to a higher-ID router, we’ll say that the link points away from the root. If the link leads to a lower-ID router, we’ll say that the link points toward the root. In this example, R4’s right-facing port points away from the root (leads to somewhere same-distance, but higher-ID), so it is a designated port. On the other hand, R5’s left-facing port points toward the root (leads to somewhere same-distance, but lower-ID), so it is either a root port or a blocked port.","zh_html":"例如，R4 距离根桥为 2，它有一条通往 R5 的链路，R5 距离根桥也是 2。同样，我们使用路由器 ID 作为平局决胜。如果链路通向 ID 较高的路由器，我们会将该端口标记为阻塞端口。(R5 看到通向 R4(R4 的 ID 较低)的链路，并将其标记为根端口或指定端口。)"},{"en":"STP: Disabling Links","zh_html":"<a href=\"#stp-disabling-links\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"stp-disabling-links\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> STP：禁用链路(STP: Disabling Links)"},{"en":"Now that every port has been assigned a state (designated port, root port, or blocked port), we are ready to remove loops from the network topology.","zh_html":"现在每个端口都被分配了一个状态(指定端口、根端口或阻塞端口)，我们准备从网络拓扑中移除循环。"},{"en":"To remove loops, each switch simply needs to pretend like its blocked ports don’t exist. In other words, do not send any user data out of that port, and do not receive any user data from that port.","zh_html":"为了移除循环，每台交换机只需要假装它的阻塞端口不存在。换句话说，不要从该端口发送任何用户数据，也不要从该端口接收任何用户数据。"},{"en":"(Note: We specify user data here because STP packets could still be sent and received from the blocked port. This will allow STP to re-enable the blocked port if the topology changes.)","zh_html":"(注：我们在这里指定用户数据，因为 STP 分组仍然可以从阻塞端口发送和接收。这将允许 STP 在拓扑结构发生变化时重新启用该阻塞端口。)"},{"en":"If we stop sending user data along blocked ports, then any link with a blocked port will end up being disabled.","zh_html":"如果我们停止沿阻塞端口发送用户数据，那么任何具有阻塞端口的链路最终都将被禁用。"},{"en":"Why does this work? Let’s think about it from the perspective of a specific switch. Your root port is the best way for you to reach the root. Your blocked ports also point toward the root, but they are not the best path to the root. This means that the blocked port actually creates a redundant (but worse) path to the root, so we should disable that link.","zh_html":"为什么这样做？让我们从特定交换机的角度来思考。你的根端口是你到达根桥的最佳方式。你的阻塞端口也指向根桥方向，但它们不是最优的。因此，你不应该需要它们来向根桥发送数据。"},{"en":"One concern you might have is: What you block a port, but somebody else needs to use that disabled link to forward packets to you, on their way to the root? Luckily, this will never happen. Remember that your blocked port points toward the root (i.e. you are further, and the other side is closer to the root than you). Therefore, if the switch on the other side (closer) forwards packets to you (further), they’ll be forwarding packets away from the root. This means we can safely block this port and disable this link without worrying about other switches trying to use that link as part of their path to root.","zh_html":"你可能有的一个担忧是：如果你阻塞了一个端口，但其他人需要使用那条被禁用的链路来向你转发分组(在他们前往根桥的路上)，该怎么办？幸运的是，这永远不会发生。请记住，你的阻塞端口指向根桥方向——因此彼端的交换机比<em>你</em>离根桥更远。更远的交换机不应该通过你向根桥转发——它应该直接使用自己的根端口。"},{"en":"By contrast, designated links cannot be safely disabled, because they lead away from the root (i.e. the switch on the other side is further from the root than you). The switch on the other side might actually want to forward packets to you, because you are along their best path to root. Luckily, this is also not a problem. Although you cannot safely disable this link, you can rely on the switch on the other side to disable the link if they don’t need it. The switch on the other side is further than you, so they will either keep this link if it’s their best path to root (i.e. root port), or they will disable this link if it’s not their best path to root (i.e. blocked port).","zh_html":"相比之下，指定链路(designated links)不能被安全地禁用，因为它们指向远离根桥的方向(即另一端的交换机离根桥比你更远)。另一端的交换机可能需要使用这条链路向你发送数据，以便到达根桥。"},{"en":"With this strategy, every link is disabled by only one side. The side that’s further away asks the question: Am I using this link as my best path to root? If yes, this link’s port is a root port. If no, this link’s port is a blocked port.","zh_html":"通过这种策略，每条链路只被一侧禁用。距离更远的那一侧会问自己：我是否使用这条链路作为到根桥的最佳路径？如果是，这条链路的端口就是根端口。如果不是，这条链路的端口就是阻塞端口(因此链路被禁用)。"},{"en":"The side that’s closer always makes this link’s port a designated port. This has the effect of leaving the decision to disable up to the further side. This is good, because the closer side has no idea if the further side will be using this link as their best path to root.","zh_html":"距离较近的那一侧总是将该链路的端口设为指定端口。这实际上是将禁用的决定留给距离更远的一侧。这样做很好，因为距离较近的一侧不知道另一侧是否将此链路用作根端口。"},{"en":"STP: Designated Ports","zh_html":"<a href=\"#stp-designated-ports\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"stp-designated-ports\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> STP：指定端口(STP: Designated Ports)"},{"en":"Side note: Why did we call them designated ports? So far, we’ve been drawing networks where every link connects two machines, but remember that sometimes we can have links that connect multiple computers.","zh_html":"补充说明：为什么我们称它们为指定端口？到目前为止，我们画的网络中每条链路连接两台机器，但请记住，有时我们可以有连接多台计算机的链路(想想无线或有线共享介质)。"},{"en":"Suppose that a link connecting two switches also has lots of hosts connected to it. If these hosts want to send or receive data, they will send data to the designated port, but not the blocked port. (The blocked port won’t receive any user data.) This ensures that their data takes exactly one path to the destination. If the data was sent to both the designated port and blocked port, the data could take two paths to the destination, creating a loop.","zh_html":"假设一条连接两台交换机的链路还连接了许多主机。如果这些主机想要发送或接收数据，它们会将数据发送到指定端口(designated port)，而不是阻塞端口(blocked port)。(在无线网络中，如果 AP 是连接到有线网络的设备，它通常是该链路上的指定端口。)"},{"en":"With this in mind, another equivalent interpretation of a designated port is: Hosts on a link should send data toward the designated port to reach the root (or anywhere else on the spanning tree). From the switch’s perspective, the designated port points away from the root. From the hosts’ perspective, sending to the designated port takes them closer to the root (or anywhere else on the spanning tree).","zh_html":"记住这一点，指定端口的另一个等价解释是：链路上的主机应向指定端口发送数据以到达根桥(或生成树上的任何其他地方)。从该主机的角度来看，指定端口就像是通往网络其余部分的上行链路(uplink)。"},{"en":"STP: BPDU Exchanges","zh_html":"<a href=\"#stp-bpdu-exchanges\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"stp-bpdu-exchanges\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> STP：BPDU 交换(STP: BPDU Exchanges)"},{"en":"We now know how to use STP to disable links and remove loops from a network topology. However, our protocol so far assumes global knowledge of the network. You would need a global view to identify the root, and to decide if ports point toward or away from the root.","zh_html":"我们现在知道如何使用 STP 禁用链路并从网络拓扑中移除循环。然而，我们目前的协议假设对网络有全局认知。你需要全局视图才能识别根桥并标记端口。"},{"en":"In order for switches to learn the information they need to label their ports, the switches exchange messages called Bridge Protocol Data Units (BPDUs). These are pretty much the same thing as the control-plane routing messages we exchanged in other routing protocols, but with a fancy name. Note that these control-plane messages are distinct from data-plane user packets (the actual data we’re forwarding).","zh_html":"为了使交换机能够获知标记端口所需的信息，交换机之间交换称为<strong>网桥协议数据单元(BPDU, Bridge Protocol Data Unit)</strong>的消息。这些与我们在距离向量协议(distance-vector protocol)中使用的控制消息几乎相同。"},{"en":"When the protocol begins, every switch thinks that the root is itself, and the cost to the root (itself) is 0.","zh_html":"当协议开始时，每台交换机都认为根桥就是自己，到根桥(自身)的成本为 0。"},{"en":"As the protocol runs, every switch keeps track of what it thinks the root is, and the best-known path to that root (and the cost of that path).","zh_html":"随着协议运行，每台交换机跟踪它认为的根桥是谁，以及到该根桥的最佳已知路径(以及该路径的成本)。"},{"en":"When you send a BPDU, you include two pieces of information: Who you think the root is, and how far away you are from the root. For example, a BPDU might say: “The root is R2, and I can reach R2 with cost 7.”","zh_html":"当你发送 BPDU 时，你需要包含两条信息：你认为的根桥是谁，以及你离根桥有多远。例如，一条 BPDU 可能说：\"根桥是 R2，我可以用成本 7 到达 R2。\""},{"en":"When you receive a BPDU, you check if it has any “better” information. The BPDU could be better for two reasons:","zh_html":"当你收到一条 BPDU 时，你检查它是否包含任何\"更好\"的信息。BPDU 可能更好有两个原因："},{"en":"The root in the BPDU has a lower ID. This means that you have discovered a better root. You should abandon your current root and cost, and instead adopt the new root and the path to the new root.","zh_html":"<p>BPDU 中的根桥具有更低的 ID。这意味着你发现了一个更好的根桥。你应该放弃当前的根桥和成本，转而采用新的根桥和通往新根桥的路径。</p>"},{"en":"The root in the BPDU is the same, but the BPDU is offering a better path to the root. You should adopt the new path to the root.","zh_html":"<p>BPDU 中的根桥相同，但 BPDU 提供了通往根桥的更好路径。你应该采用通往根桥的新路径。</p>"},{"en":"Costs to root are computed just like we did in the distance-vector protocol. For example, suppose your neighbor tells you “The root is R2, and I can reach R2 with cost 7.” Then your cost to the root is your direct link cost to your neighbor, plus your neighbor’s cost to root (as specified in the advertisement).","zh_html":"到根桥的成本计算方式与我们之前在距离向量协议中的做法相同。例如，假设你的邻居告诉你\"根桥是 R2，我可以用成本 7 到达 R2。\"那么你到达根桥的成本就是 7 + 该链路的成本。"},{"en":"When you update your state (who you think the root is, or your best-known cost to root), you should send a BPDU to your neighbors to inform them about your new state.","zh_html":"当你更新你的状态(你认为的根桥是谁，或你到根桥的最佳已知成本)时，你应该向邻居发送一条 BPDU 来通知他们你的新状态。"},{"en":"Once the protocol converges, the state gives each switch enough information to label all of its ports. You know the best path to the root, so you can label the corresponding port as the root port.","zh_html":"一旦协议收敛，状态就为每台交换机提供了足够的标记信息来标记其所有端口。你知道到根桥的最佳路径，因此你可以将相应的端口标记为根端口。"},{"en":"Your neighbors have also all told you how far away they are from the root. If a neighbor says they are further, then you can label the corresponding port as a designated port. If a neighbor says they are closer (but they are not on your best path to the root), then you can label the corresponding port as a blocked port.","zh_html":"你的邻居也告诉你他们离根桥有多远。如果一个邻居说他们更远，你就可以将相应端口标记为指定端口。如果一个邻居说他们更近，你就知道该端口指向根桥方向。"},{"en":"BPDUs are exchanged regularly, so that if the network topology changes, the protocol can adapt and find a spanning tree (i.e. disable links) for the new topology.","zh_html":"BPDU 定期交换，以便如果网络拓扑发生变化，协议可以适应并为新拓扑找到一棵生成树(即禁用链路)。"},{"en":"STP: BPDU Exchanges Example","zh_html":"<a href=\"#stp-bpdu-exchanges-example\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"stp-bpdu-exchanges-example\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> STP：BPDU 交换示例(STP: BPDU Exchanges Example)"},{"en":"Routers send and receive BPDU exchanges in parallel, so there isn’t a specific router that sends the first BPDU. In this example, we’ll show a subset of the BPDUs that get sent.","zh_html":"路由器并行发送和接收 BPDU 交换，因此没有特定的路由器发送第一个 BPDU。在这个例子中，我们将展示所发送 BPDU 的一个子集。"},{"en":"R3’s initial state says: Root R3 is 0 away. R3’s first advertisement sends this state to its neighbors.","zh_html":"R3 的初始状态说：根桥 R3 距离为 0。R3 的第一个通告将此状态发送给它的邻居。"},{"en":"R1 hears this advertisement. R1 currently thinks the root is R1, and the advertisement offers a root of R3. The advertised root is worse (higher ID), so R1 rejects this advertisement.","zh_html":"R1 听到这个通告。R1 当前认为根桥是 R1，而该通告提供了一个根桥 R3。通告的根桥更差(更高的 ID)，因此 R1 拒绝此通告。"},{"en":"R6 hears this advertisement. R6 currently thinks the root is R6, and this advertisement offers a root of R3. The advertised root is better, so R6 accepts this advertisement. R6’s updated state says: Root R3 is 1 away. Note: The cost is calculated from 0, the cost in the advertisement from R3, plus 1, the cost of the link to R3.","zh_html":"R6 听到这个通告。R6 当前认为根桥是 R6，而该通告提供了一个根桥 R3。通告的根桥更好，因此 R6 接受此通告。R6 的更新状态说：根桥 R3 距离为 1。"},{"en":"At this point, R6 has updated its state, so it will send an advertisement to its neighbors (not shown in this demo).","zh_html":"此时，R6 已更新其状态，因此它将向其邻居发送一个通告(此演示中未显示)。"},{"en":"Some time later, R1 sends an advertisement to its neighbors with its state: Root R1 is 0 away.","zh_html":"一段时间后，R1 向其邻居发送一个通告，其中包含其状态：根桥 R1 距离为 0。"},{"en":"R2 hears this advertisement. The advertised root (R1) is better than the currently best-known root (R2), so R2 accepts this advertisement. R2’s updated state says: Root R1 is 1 away.","zh_html":"R2 听到这个通告。通告的根桥(R1)优于当前最佳已知根桥(R2)，因此 R2 接受此通告。R2 的更新状态说：根桥 R1 距离为 1。"},{"en":"Likewise, R3 hears this advertisement, and accepts it because the advertised root (R1) is better than the currently best-known root (R3). R3’s updated state says: Root R1 is 1 away.","zh_html":"同样，R3 听到这个通告，并接受它，因为通告的根桥(R1)优于当前最佳已知根桥(R3)。R3 的更新状态说：根桥 R1 距离为 1。"},{"en":"R2 and R3 have updated their states, so they will each send an advertisement to their neighbors.","zh_html":"R2 和 R3 已经更新了它们的状态，因此它们将各自向邻居发送一个通告。"},{"en":"R4 hears the advertisement from R2. The advertised root (R1) is better than the currently best-known root (R4), so R4 accepts this advertisement. R4’s updated state says: Root R1 is 2 away. Note: This cost is computed by summing 1 (cost in advertisement from R2), plus 1 (link cost to R2).","zh_html":"R4 听到来自 R2 的通告。通告的根桥(R1)优于当前最佳已知根桥(R4)，因此 R4 接受此通告。R4 的更新状态说：根桥 R1 距离为 2。注：R4 可能也听到了来自 R3 的通告(下一跳 R3)，这将提供到 R1 的相同成本。"},{"en":"R6 hears the advertisement from R3. The advertised root (R1) is better than the currently best-known root (R3), so R6 accepts this advertisement. R6’s updated state says: Root R1 is 2 away. Note: R6’s old state said R3 was 1 away, and the new state says R1 is 2 away. Even though the new state has a higher distance, it’s still better because the new state has a better root.","zh_html":"R6 听到来自 R3 的通告。通告的根桥(R1)优于当前最佳已知根桥(R3)，因此 R6 接受此通告。R6 的更新状态说：根桥 R1 距离为 2。注：R6 的下一跳现在是 R3(成本从 R6 到 R3 为 1，从 R3 到 R1 为 1)。"},{"en":"R4 and R6 have updated its state, so they will send advertisements to their neighbors with its updated state. We’ll first show R4’s advertisement, then come back to R6 later (again, remember that all of these are happening in parallel in reality).","zh_html":"R4 和 R6 已更新它们的根桥，因此它们将向邻居发送包含更新状态的通告。我们将首先展示 R4 的通告，然后稍后再回到 R6(再次记住，所有通告都是并行发生的)。"},{"en":"R5 hears the advertisement from R4. The advertised root (R1) is better than the currently best-known root (R5), so R5 accepts this advertisement. R5’s updated state says: Root R1 is 3 away (2 from the advertisement, plus 1 from the link cost to R4).","zh_html":"R5 听到来自 R4 的通告。通告的根桥(R1)优于当前最佳已知根桥(R5)，因此 R5 接受此通告。R5 的更新状态说：根桥 R1 距离为 3(距离 R4 为 2 + 链路的成本 1)。"},{"en":"R6 also hears the advertisement from R4. The advertised root (R1) is the same than the currently best-known root (R1), so we need to check the cost. Accepting the advertisement would give a cost of 2 (from the advertisement), plus 1 (from the link cost to R4), for a total of 3. The currently best-known cost is 2. Therefore, R6 rejects the advertisement (3 is worse than 2).","zh_html":"R6 也听到了来自 R4 的通告。通告的根桥(R1)与当前最佳已知根桥(R1)相同，因此我们需要检查成本。接受该通告将得到成本 2 + 1 = 3，这比当前成本(2)更差，因此 R6 拒绝此通告。"},{"en":"R5 has updated its state, so it will send an advertisement to its neighbors with its updated state.","zh_html":"R5 已更新其状态，因此它将向其邻居发送一个包含更新状态的通告。"},{"en":"R7 hears the advertisement from R5. The advertised root (R1) is better than the currently best-known root (R7), so R7 accepts this advertisement.","zh_html":"R7 听到来自 R5 的通告。通告的根桥(R1)优于当前最佳已知根桥(R7)，因此 R7 接受此通告。"},{"en":"R7 has updated its state, and will send an advertisement to its neighbors, though that advertisement is not shown here (R6 would reject it for having a worse cost to root).","zh_html":"R7 已更新其状态，并将向其邻居发送一个通告，尽管该通告未在此处显示(R6 将因到根桥的成本更差而拒绝它)。"},{"en":"Following up from earlier, R6 sends an advertisement to its neighbors, and R7 receives this advertisement. The advertised root (R1) is the same than the currently best-known root (R1), so we need to check the cost. Accepting the advertisement would give a cost of 2 (from the advertisement), plus 1 (from the link cost to R6), for a total of 3. The currently best-known cost is 4. Therefore, R7 rejects the advertisement (3 is better than 4), and updates its state to have a cost-to-root of 3 (instead of 4).","zh_html":"接着前面的内容，R6 向其邻居发送一个通告，R7 收到这个通告。通告的根桥(R1)与当前最佳已知根桥(R1)相同，因此我们需要检查成本。接受该通告将得到成本 1(R6 到 R7 为 1) + 2(R6 到根桥的成本) = 3，这与 R7 当前的成本(3)相同。既然平局，我们使用路由器 ID 作为平局决胜。R6 的 ID 低于 R5，因此 R7 将 R6 作为到根桥的下一跳，并更新其状态。"},{"en":"The routers continue to periodically exchange advertisements with each other. Not all advertisements were shown in this demo, but eventually, the protocol will converge, and all routers will know that the root is R1. Also, all routers will know about their cost to the root.","zh_html":"路由器继续定期相互交换通告。并非所有通告都在此演示中显示，但最终协议将收敛，并且所有路由器都将知道 R1 是根桥，以及它们各自到 R1 的成本。"},{"en":"Once all the routers know their cost to the agreed-upon root, they can exchange periodic advertisements. This allows routers to learn about their neighbors’ cost-to-root values, which in turn allows the routers to assign ports as DP, RP, or BP.","zh_html":"一旦所有路由器知道它们到商定根桥的成本，它们就可以交换定期通告。这使得路由器能够了解邻居的到根桥成本值，从而允许路由器正确地标记其端口(指定端口、根端口或阻塞端口)。"}]},"/end-to-end/nat.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"NAT: Network Address Translation","zh_html":"NAT：网络地址转换(Network Address Translation)"},{"en":"Motivation: IPv4 Address Exhaustion","zh_html":"动机：IPv4 地址耗尽"},{"en":"Recall that we only have \\(2^{32}\\) different IPv4 addresses, which is not enough to address every host on the Internet. We’ve already seen that IPv6 is a robust solution to IPv4 address exhaustion, but IPv6 adoption has been fairly slow.","zh_html":"回想一下，我们只有 \\(2^{32}\\) 个不同的 IPv4 地址，这不足以为互联网上的每台主机分配地址。我们已经看到，IPv6 是解决 IPv4 地址耗尽(IPv4 address exhaustion)的稳健方案，但 IPv6 的采用一直相当缓慢。"},{"en":"In the meantime, to conserve addresses, recall that IANA allocated special RFC 1918 ranges of private IP addresses, which can be used by any networks that don’t require Internet addresses: 192.168.0.0/16, 10.0.0.0/8, and 172.16.0.0/12. It turns out that these addresses are often used in your home network as well, so that your own personal device doesn’t need a unique IP address. But, you do need Internet access, so how can you use a private IP address?","zh_html":"与此同时，为了节省地址，回想一下，IANA 分配了特殊的 RFC 1918 专用 IP 地址(private IP addresses)范围，任何不需要互联网地址的网络都可以使用它们：192.168.0.0/16、10.0.0.0/8 和 172.16.0.0/12。事实证明，这些地址也经常用于你的家庭网络，这样你自己的个人设备就不需要唯一的 IP 地址。但是，你确实需要访问互联网，那么如何使用专用 IP 地址呢？"},{"en":"NAT: Conceptual","zh_html":"NAT：概念"},{"en":"In NAT, the goal is to use a single public IP address to represent many hosts in the local network. The trick is to have the gateway router convert private IP addresses into the single public address before sending out messages. Then, the router converts the public address back into a private address for incoming replies.","zh_html":"在网络地址转换(NAT)中，目标是用一个单一的公共 IP 地址(public IP address)代表本地网络中的许多主机。技巧是让网关路由器(gateway router)在发送消息之前，把专用 IP 地址转换成这个单一的公共地址。随后，对于收到的回复，路由器再把公共地址转换回专用地址。"},{"en":"Alice, Bob, and Chuck are all working for Joe’s Tire Shop. They have private IP addresses A, B, and C, which cannot be used on the wide Internet, because they’re not unique. Instead, everyone in Joe’s Tire Shop must share a single public IP address, which is the only unique, publicly-understandable IP address they have.","zh_html":"Alice、Bob 和 Chuck 都在 Joe 的轮胎店工作。他们拥有专用 IP 地址 A、B 和 C，这些地址不能在广域互联网中使用，因为它们并不唯一。因此，Joe 的轮胎店里的所有人都必须共享一个单一的公共 IP 地址，这是他们唯一拥有的、全局唯一且公网可理解的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"Alice wants to send a message to an external public server with public IP address S. She sends a packet that says “From: A, To: S.” If we sent this packet naively, S would be unable to send replies, because A is a private IP address.","zh_html":"Alice 想向一个公共 IP 地址为 S 的外部公共服务器发送消息。她发送一个分组，其中写着“From: A, To: S”。如果我们天真地发送这个分组，S 将无法发送回复，因为 A 是一个专用 IP 地址。"},{"en":"Instead, when the packet reaches the gateway router, it rewrites the header to say “From: R1, To: S.” The router also makes a note: If I get any replies from S, they should go to A.","zh_html":"相反，当该分组到达网关路由器时，路由器会重写首部(header)，让它写成“From: R1, To: S”。路由器还会做一条记录：如果我收到任何来自 S 的回复，它们应该发往 A。"},{"en":"Now, when S gets a packet, it can send replies to the public address R1: “From: S, To: R1.” When R1, the gateway router, receives the reply, it checks its note, and rewrites the header to say “From: S, To: A.” Then, the packet gets sent back to A.","zh_html":"现在，当 S 收到分组时，它可以把回复发送到公共地址 R1：“From: S, To: R1”。当网关路由器 R1 收到回复时，它检查自己的记录，并把首部重写为“From: S, To: A”。然后，该分组被发回 A。"},{"en":"Now, Alice, Bob, and Chuck can all send outgoing packets. When the router receives a packet, it must remember a mapping between the external destination and the internal sender. (“B just sent a packet to N, so any replies from N should be sent back to B.”)","zh_html":"现在，Alice、Bob 和 Chuck 都可以发送出站分组(outgoing packets)。当路由器收到一个分组时，它必须记住外部目的地与内部发送方之间的映射。(“B 刚刚向 N 发送了一个分组，所以来自 N 的任何回复都应该发回 B。”)"},{"en":"One problem arises if Alice and Bob both want to talk to S.","zh_html":"如果 Alice 和 Bob 都想与 S 通信，就会出现一个问题。"},{"en":"We now have ambiguity if a reply arrives from S. Should the router send this reply to A or B?","zh_html":"现在，如果来自 S 的回复到达，就会产生歧义。路由器应该把这个回复发送给 A 还是 B？"},{"en":"We can solve this problem by using logical ports, from Layer 4. Alice’s connection says: “From: A, Port 50000, To: S, Port 80.” The router rewrites this to say “From: R1, Port 50000, To: S, Port 80,” just like before. The note now says, if I get any replies from S, Port 80, to R1, Port 50000, it should go to A.","zh_html":"我们可以使用第 4 层的逻辑端口(logical ports)来解决这个问题。Alice 的连接写着：“From: A, Port 50000, To: S, Port 80。”路由器像之前一样把它重写为“From: R1, Port 50000, To: S, Port 80”。现在记录中写着：如果我收到任何来自 S、Port 80，发往 R1、Port 50000 的回复，它应该发往 A。"},{"en":"Bob could create a separate connection that says: “From B, Port 60000, To: S, Port 80.” The router rewrites this to say “From: R1, Port 60000, To: S, Port 80,” just like before. The note for this connection says, if I get any replies from S, Port 80 to R1, Port 60000, it should to go B.","zh_html":"Bob 可以创建一个单独的连接，写着：“From B, Port 60000, To: S, Port 80。”路由器像之前一样把它重写为“From: R1, Port 60000, To: S, Port 80”。这个连接的记录写着：如果我收到任何来自 S、Port 80，发往 R1、Port 60000 的回复，它应该发往 B。"},{"en":"More generally, the router is now keeping track of connections using the 5-tuple of source IP, destination IP, protocol, source port, and destination port. When the router receives an outgoing packet, it changes the private source IP to the public source IP, and makes a note of the 5-tuple. Then, when the router receives an incoming packet, it looks up which connection the packet belongs to, and sends the packet to the appropriate client (with their private IP).","zh_html":"更一般地说，路由器现在使用由源 IP、目的 IP、协议(protocol)、源端口和目的端口组成的五元组(5-tuple)来跟踪连接。当路由器收到一个出站分组时，它把专用源 IP 改为公共源 IP，并记录这个五元组。然后，当路由器收到一个入站分组(incoming packet)时，它查找该分组属于哪个连接，并把分组发送给相应的客户端(使用其专用 IP)。"},{"en":"Rewriting Client Port Numbers","zh_html":"重写客户端端口号"},{"en":"We have one last issue: What if, instead of Port 50000 and Port 60000, Alice and Bob both chose the same port number (e.g. Port 50000)?","zh_html":"还有最后一个问题：如果 Alice 和 Bob 没有分别选择 Port 50000 和 Port 60000，而是都选择了同一个端口号(例如 Port 50000)，会怎样？"},{"en":"Now, the router remembers two connections: (A Port 50000 to S Port 80), and (B Port 50000 to S Port 80). If the router receives an incoming packet “From: S, Port 80, To: R1 Port 50000,” it’s ambiguous whether this packet was from A or B’s connection.","zh_html":"现在，路由器记住了两个连接：(A Port 50000 to S Port 80) 和 (B Port 50000 to S Port 80)。如果路由器收到一个入站分组“From: S, Port 80, To: R1 Port 50000”，就无法判断这个分组来自 A 的连接还是 B 的连接。"},{"en":"The last fix we have to make is to also allow the router to rewrite the port number. When Bob sends “From: B, Port 50000, To: S, Port 80,” the router realizes that someone else already has a connection using Port 50000, to S Port 80. Therefore, the router makes up a “fake” port number for Bob (let’s use 60000) and rewrites both the source IP and source port to get: “From: R1, Port 60000, To: S, Port 80.”","zh_html":"我们必须做的最后一个修正是，也允许路由器重写端口号。当 Bob 发送“From: B, Port 50000, To: S, Port 80”时，路由器会发现已经有人使用 Port 50000 到 S Port 80 建立了连接。因此，路由器为 Bob 编造一个“假”的端口号(这里用 60000)，并同时重写源 IP 和源端口，得到：“From: R1, Port 60000, To: S, Port 80”。"},{"en":"As before, the router remembers the active connection (A Port 50000 to S Port 80), but for Bob, the router additionally notes the fake port number: (B Port 50000, faked as 60000, to S Port 80).","zh_html":"和之前一样，路由器记住活跃连接 (A Port 50000 to S Port 80)，但对于 Bob，路由器还额外记录这个假端口号：(B Port 50000, faked as 60000, to S Port 80)。"},{"en":"Now, if the router receives an incoming packet “From: S, Port 80, To: R1, Port 50000,” this must be for Alice. By contrast, an incoming packet like “From: S, Port 80, To: R1, Port 60000,” with the fake port number, this must be for Bob.","zh_html":"现在，如果路由器收到一个入站分组“From: S, Port 80, To: R1, Port 50000”，它一定是给 Alice 的。相反，像“From: S, Port 80, To: R1, Port 60000”这样带有假端口号的入站分组，一定是给 Bob 的。"},{"en":"Note that Bob has no idea that the router is changing his port number. When the router forwards this packet back to Bob, the fake port number must be changed back to the original port number. “From: S, Port 80, To: R1, Port 60000” must be rewritten as “From: S, Port 80, To: R1, Port 60000.” More generally, none of the private clients should need to know or care about their packets getting rewritten. The router should be giving all of them the illusion that they’re sending and receiving packets from their private IP address and whatever ports they choose.","zh_html":"注意，Bob 并不知道路由器正在更改他的端口号。当路由器把这个分组转发回 Bob 时，假端口号必须改回原始端口号。“From: S, Port 80, To: R1, Port 60000”必须被重写为“From: S, Port 80, To: R1, Port 60000”。更一般地说，任何专用客户端都不应该需要知道或关心自己的分组被重写。路由器应该让它们都产生一种错觉：它们正在使用自己的专用 IP 地址和自己选择的任意端口发送、接收分组。"},{"en":"NAT: Implementation","zh_html":"NAT：实现"},{"en":"When a home router connects to the ISP for the first time, it can run DHCP to receive an IP address. (Earlier, we talked about hosts running DHCP, but routers can also run DHCP.) The ISP’s DHCP server replies and allocates a single IP address to the home router. This is the single public address that all the hosts in this router’s home network will be sharing.","zh_html":"当家用路由器第一次连接到互联网服务提供商(ISP)时，它可以运行 DHCP 来获得一个 IP 地址。(前面我们讨论的是主机运行 DHCP，但路由器也可以运行 DHCP。)ISP 的 DHCP 服务器会回复，并向家用路由器分配一个单一的 IP 地址。这就是该路由器家庭网络中所有主机将要共享的单一公共地址。"},{"en":"There are several different modes of NAT. The one we just saw is called Port Address Translation (PAT), and it gives us the ability to introduce the fake port numbers that we saw. The PAT mode requires routers to be aware of Layer 4 protocols, so that they can parse the packets, keep track of connections, and rewrite headers.","zh_html":"NAT 有几种不同模式。我们刚才看到的模式称为<strong>端口地址转换(Port Address Translation, PAT)</strong>，它让我们能够引入前面看到的假端口号。PAT 模式要求路由器理解第 4 层协议，这样它们才能解析分组、跟踪连接并重写首部。"},{"en":"PAT is the most complex and widely-used mode of NAT, but simpler modes of NAT also exist for one-to-one address translation. If every host actually had their own IP address, but they sent packets from private addresses, the router could just do a one-to-one translation, mapping 10.0.0.1 (private) to 42.0.2.1 (public), and 10.0.0.2 (private) to 42.0.2.2 (public), and so on. This simpler mode wouldn’t let us conserve IP addresses by hiding multiple hosts behind a single public address, but it can still be useful in other situations.","zh_html":"PAT 是最复杂、也最广泛使用的 NAT 模式，但也存在更简单的 NAT 模式，用于一对一地址转换(one-to-one address translation)。如果每台主机实际上都有自己的 IP 地址，但它们从专用地址发送分组，那么路由器可以只做一对一转换，把 10.0.0.1(private) 映射到 42.0.2.1(public)，把 10.0.0.2(private) 映射到 42.0.2.2(public)，依此类推。这种更简单的模式不能通过把多台主机隐藏在单一公共地址后面来节省 IP 地址，但在其他场景中仍然可能有用。"},{"en":"Where is NAT Used?","zh_html":"NAT 用在哪里？"},{"en":"NAT increases the complexity of packet forwarding for the router. The router must now be able to parse the Layer 4 header, in addition to the Layer 3 header. Also, the router must be able to rewrite the Layer 3 and Layer 4 headers. Finally, the router must maintain a connection state table to keep track of all the flows passing through the router. All this functionality increases the number of CPU cycles needed to forward each packet, and also increases the amount of memory needed on the router per flow.","zh_html":"NAT 增加了路由器进行分组转发(packet forwarding)的复杂性。除了第 3 层首部之外，路由器现在还必须能够解析第 4 层首部。此外，路由器必须能够重写第 3 层和第 4 层首部。最后，路由器还必须维护一个连接状态表(connection state table)，以跟踪所有经过路由器的流(flows)。所有这些功能都会增加转发每个分组所需的 CPU 周期数，也会增加路由器为每条流所需的内存量。"},{"en":"Because NAT increases router complexity, it is performed as close to the edge of the network as possible, in order to limit the number of flows passing through the router. Running NAT on your home router is a good idea, since there aren’t too many devices in your home that will send connections through the home router. By contrast, running NAT on a high-performance datacenter router would be a bad idea.","zh_html":"由于 NAT 增加了路由器复杂性，它会尽可能靠近网络边缘(edge of the network)执行，以限制经过该路由器的流数量。在你的家用路由器上运行 NAT 是合理的，因为家里不会有太多设备通过家用路由器发送连接。相比之下，在高性能数据中心路由器(datacenter router)上运行 NAT 就不是好主意。"},{"en":"In practice, small-scale NAT is used in almost every personal (home/office) network for IPv4, even today. As IPv4 addresses ran out, ISPs were unable to give one public address to each customer (i.e. each home router). As a result, the ISP network itself also had to run a more complex version of NAT called Carrier Grade NAT (CGNAT). This version of NAT is deployed deeper in the network, and requires routers to keep track of many more connections.","zh_html":"在实践中，即使到今天，几乎每个个人(家庭/办公室)IPv4 网络都会使用小规模 NAT。随着 IPv4 地址耗尽，ISP 无法为每个客户(即每台家用路由器)提供一个公共地址。因此，ISP 网络本身也必须运行一种更复杂的 NAT 版本，称为运营商级 NAT(Carrier Grade NAT, CGNAT)。这个版本的 NAT 部署在网络更深处，并要求路由器跟踪更多连接。"},{"en":"Note that we generally don’t use NAT for IPv6, because there are enough IPv6 addresses to assign a unique public one to every computer in the world.","zh_html":"注意，我们通常不对 IPv6 使用 NAT，因为 IPv6 地址足够多，可以为世界上的每台计算机分配一个唯一的公共地址。"},{"en":"Inbound Connections","zh_html":"入站连接"},{"en":"So far, we’ve assumed that connections are always initiated by the client with the private IP address. In other words, the first packet is always outgoing, from client to server. This is consistent with how most home networks operate. When you load a website in your browser, you’re the client initiating the connection. It’s generally not the case that others are trying to connect to you.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们一直假设连接总是由拥有专用 IP 地址的客户端发起。换句话说，第一个分组总是出站的，从客户端发往服务器。这与大多数家庭网络的运行方式一致。当你在浏览器中加载网站时，你就是发起连接的客户端。通常并不是其他人在试图连接到你。"},{"en":"But, what if you were running a server, and you did want people from the outside world to be able to initiate connections to this server? Users from the outside can’t send packets to a private IP address. They could try to send packets to the router’s IP address, but if the router gets a packet like “From: outside user, To: R1, Port 28,” the router has no idea which of the private clients to forward this packet to. This is the very first packet of a new connection, so the router’s table has no information about this connection yet.","zh_html":"但是，如果你正在运行一个服务器，并且确实希望外部世界的人能够主动连接到这个服务器，该怎么办？外部用户无法向专用 IP 地址发送分组。他们可以尝试向路由器的 IP 地址发送分组，但如果路由器收到类似“From: outside user, To: R1, Port 28”的分组，路由器并不知道应该把这个分组转发给哪个专用客户端。这是一个新连接的第一个分组，因此路由器的表中还没有关于该连接的信息。"},{"en":"To allow inbound connections, routers performing NAT need a port mapping table. Alice, who is inside the network and only has a private IP address, tells the router: I’m going to run a new server, and listen for requests on Port 28. Now, if the router sees some packet from an outside user to R1, Port 28, the router knows to forward this packet to Alice.","zh_html":"为了允许入站连接，执行 NAT 的路由器需要一张<strong>端口映射表(port mapping table)</strong>。Alice 位于网络内部，并且只有专用 IP 地址，她告诉路由器：我要运行一个新服务器，并在 Port 28 上监听请求。现在，如果路由器看到某个来自外部用户、发往 R1、Port 28 的分组，路由器就知道要把这个分组转发给 Alice。"},{"en":"Entries in this port mapping table may need to be specified manually (e.g. Alice manually configuring the router). Dynamic protocols such as UPnP (Universal Plug-n-Play) and NAT-PMP (NAT Port Mapping Protocol) allow for dynamic configuration of open ports. These protocols are sometimes used by applications like online gaming, where inbound connections are needed.","zh_html":"这张端口映射表中的条目可能需要手动指定(例如 Alice 手动配置路由器)。UPnP(Universal Plug-n-Play) 和 NAT-PMP(NAT Port Mapping Protocol) 这样的动态协议允许动态配置开放端口(open ports)。这些协议有时会被网络游戏等需要入站连接的应用使用。"},{"en":"Security Implications of NAT","zh_html":"NAT 的安全影响"},{"en":"NAT breaks the end-to-end principle. So far, we’ve said that with Layer 3, anybody on the Internet can reach anyone else. However, because NAT doesn’t allow inbound connections by default, users in a home network, who only have a private IP address and are sharing a public IP address, cannot be reached automatically. They’d need to configure the router before they can accept inbound packets.","zh_html":"NAT 破坏了端到端原则(end-to-end principle)。到目前为止，我们说过，在第 3 层，互联网上的任何人都可以到达其他任何人。然而，由于 NAT 默认不允许入站连接，家庭网络中的用户如果只有专用 IP 地址并共享一个公共 IP 地址，就无法被自动访问。他们必须先配置路由器，才能接受入站分组。"},{"en":"NAT has the property that it doesn’t allow inbound connections by default. This could be viewed as a security feature, though it’s more of a side effect than an intentional design feature. NAT causes inbound connections to be blocked by default, which might be useful for stopping attackers from trying to connect to hosts inside the network. This behavior is actually pretty similar to firewalls (see UC Berkeley CS 161 notes for more information), which also often block inbound connections by default. That said, this is mostly a coincidence, so NAT isn’t really implementing a principled security policy, and shouldn’t be thought of as a bulletproof defense.","zh_html":"NAT 具有默认不允许入站连接的性质。这可以被看作一种安全特性，尽管它更像是副作用，而不是有意设计的特性。NAT 会使入站连接默认被阻止，这可能有助于阻止攻击者尝试连接网络内部的主机。这种行为实际上与防火墙(firewalls)相当类似(更多信息见 UC Berkeley CS 161 笔记)，防火墙也常常默认阻止入站连接。话虽如此，这主要只是巧合，因此 NAT 并没有真正实现一套有原则的安全策略，也不应被视为牢不可破的防御。"},{"en":"NAT also has the side effect that it can help preserve client privacy. Again, this isn’t really an intentional security feature. Because the router rewrites the client’s IP address, when the server receives a packet, it doesn’t know the original sender’s identity (could be Alice, Bob, or Chuck).","zh_html":"NAT 还有一个副作用，即它可以帮助保护客户端隐私(client privacy)。同样，这并不是真正有意设计的安全特性。由于路由器会重写客户端的 IP 地址，当服务器收到分组时，它不知道原始发送方的身份(可能是 Alice、Bob 或 Chuck)。"},{"en":"By contrast, if we didn’t use NAT, the server can learn the exact identity of the sender. Also, if we didn’t use NAT and we used IPv6, the server might be able to learn the exact computer the sender is using, since IPv6 addresses are sometimes auto-configured using the MAC address (copying MAC address bits into the IP address). If we were using IPv6 and still wanted client privacy, some alternate solutions like IPv6 temporary/privacy addresses do exist.","zh_html":"相比之下，如果我们不使用 NAT，服务器就可以获知发送方的精确身份。此外，如果我们不使用 NAT 并使用 IPv6，服务器也许能够获知发送方正在使用的具体计算机，因为 IPv6 地址有时会使用 MAC 地址自动配置(把 MAC 地址比特复制到 IP 地址中)。如果我们使用 IPv6 但仍希望保护客户端隐私，也确实存在一些替代方案，例如 IPv6 临时/隐私地址(IPv6 temporary/privacy addresses)。"}]},"/end-to-end/tls.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"TLS: Secure Bytestreams","zh_html":"TLS：安全字节流(secure bytestreams)"},{"en":"Secure Bytestreams","zh_html":"安全字节流(secure bytestreams)"},{"en":"TCP by itself is insecure against network attackers. Someone on the network (e.g. a malicious router, an attacker sniffing packets on a wire) could read or even modify your TCP packets while they’re in transit.","zh_html":"单独使用 TCP 时，它无法抵御网络攻击者(network attackers)。网络中的某个人，例如恶意路由器(malicious router)，或在线路上嗅探分组(sniffing packets)的攻击者，可能在 TCP 分组(TCP packets)传输途中读取甚至修改它们。"},{"en":"Also, with TCP, you might connect to an attacker instead of the real server. Suppose you want to connect to a bank website, and you do a DNS lookup for www.bank.com. The attacker (e.g. someone who hacked into the resolver or a router) changes the DNS response so that it maps www.bank.com to the attacker’s IP address, 6.6.6.6. Now, when you form a TCP connection to the bank website, you’re talking to the attacker. You might end up sending your bank password to the attacker!","zh_html":"此外，使用 TCP 时，你可能连接到攻击者，而不是真正的服务器(server)。假设你想访问一家银行网站，并对 www.bank.com 进行 DNS 查询(DNS lookup)。攻击者，例如入侵了解析器(resolver)或路由器(router)的人，篡改 DNS 响应(DNS response)，让 www.bank.com 映射到攻击者的 IP 地址 6.6.6.6。现在，当你与银行网站建立 TCP 连接(TCP connection)时，实际上是在和攻击者通信。你最终可能把银行密码发给攻击者。"},{"en":"To address these security issues, we add a new protocol, Transport Layer Security (TLS), on top of TCP.","zh_html":"为了解决这些安全问题，我们在 TCP 之上加入一种新协议：传输层安全协议(Transport Layer Security, TLS)。"},{"en":"TLS can be thought of as a Layer 4.5 protocol, sitting in between TCP and application protocols like HTTP. (We use a weird number like 4.5 because the obsolete Layers 5 and 6 have nothing to do with security.) TLS relies on the bytestream abstraction of TCP, so it doesn’t think about individual packets or packet loss/reordering. TLS provides the exact same bytestream abstraction to applications as TCP does, but the bytestream is now secure against network attackers. This is why HTTP and HTTPS are semantically identical protocols. The only difference is that HTTPS runs over the secure bytestream of TLS-over-TCP, while HTTP runs over raw TCP with no TLS.","zh_html":"可以把 TLS 看作第 4.5 层协议(Layer 4.5 protocol)，位于 TCP 和 HTTP 这类应用协议(application protocols)之间。我们使用 4.5 这样有点奇怪的编号，是因为已经过时的第 5 层和第 6 层与安全性无关。TLS 依赖 TCP 的字节流抽象(bytestream abstraction)，因此它不关心单个分组(packet)，也不直接处理丢包(packet loss)或重排序(reordering)。TLS 向应用提供与 TCP 完全相同的字节流抽象，但这个字节流现在能够抵御网络攻击者(network attackers)。这就是为什么 HTTP 和 HTTPS 在语义上是相同协议；区别只在于 HTTPS 运行在 TLS-over-TCP 的安全字节流(secure bytestream)之上，而 HTTP 直接运行在没有 TLS 的原始 TCP(raw TCP)之上。"},{"en":"To distinguish between HTTPS and HTTP, we use Port 80 for HTTP connections, and Port 443 for HTTPS connections. Servers can force users to use HTTPS by replying to all Port 80 requests with a redirect to use Port 443 instead.","zh_html":"为了区分 HTTPS 和 HTTP，我们使用端口 80(Port 80)承载 HTTP 连接，使用端口 443(Port 443)承载 HTTPS 连接。服务器(server)可以通过把所有端口 80 请求都重定向(redirect)到端口 443，强制用户使用 HTTPS。"},{"en":"TLS Handshake","zh_html":"TLS 握手(TLS handshake)"},{"en":"At a high level, TLS uses cryptography to encrypt messages sent over the bytestream. TLS also uses other cryptographic protocols (message authentication codes) to prevent attackers from changing messages as they’re sent over the network.","zh_html":"从高层看，TLS 使用密码学(cryptography)对通过字节流(bytestream)发送的消息进行加密(encrypt)。TLS 还使用其他密码学协议(cryptographic protocols)，例如消息认证码(message authentication codes)，防止攻击者在消息通过网络传输时篡改它们。"},{"en":"In order to encrypt traffic, TLS must start with an additional handshake to exchange keys and verify the identity of the server (e.g. real bank, not someone impersonating the bank).","zh_html":"为了加密流量(traffic)，TLS 必须先执行一个额外的握手(handshake)，用于交换密钥(keys)并验证服务器身份(identity)，例如确认对方是真正的银行，而不是冒充银行的人。"},{"en":"Because TLS is built on top of TCP, the TCP three-way handshake first proceeds as normal. This creates an (insecure) bytestream, allowing all future messages, including the TLS handshake, to proceed without thinking about individual packets.","zh_html":"由于 TLS 构建在 TCP 之上，TCP 三次握手(three-way handshake)会先按正常流程进行。这会创建一个不安全的字节流(bytestream)，使后续所有消息，包括 TLS 握手(TLS handshake)，都可以在不考虑单个分组(packet)的情况下继续进行。"},{"en":"The TLS handshake can now proceed:","zh_html":"接下来，TLS 握手(TLS handshake)可以开始："},{"en":"The client and server exchange hellos. The hellos contain random numbers, which ensures that every handshake results in different secret keys. (It would be bad if we used the same key every time, and attackers hacked us and learned that key.) The hellos also allow the client and server to agree on specific cryptographic protocols to use. The client’s hello lists all cryptographic schemes the client supports, and the server’s hello picks one to use.","zh_html":"客户端(client)和服务器(server)交换 hello 消息。hello 中包含随机数(random numbers)，确保每次握手都会产生不同的秘密密钥(secret keys)。如果每次都使用同一个密钥，而攻击者入侵后学到了这个密钥，那会很糟。hello 还允许客户端和服务器协商要使用的具体密码学协议(cryptographic protocols)。客户端的 hello 会列出客户端支持的所有密码学方案(cryptographic schemes)，服务器的 hello 则从中选择一个。"},{"en":"The server sends a certificate of authenticity. This will allow the client to verify that it’s talking to the real server, and not an impersonator. There’s a bit of complexity in how the client actually verifies this certificate, which we won’t discuss here.","zh_html":"服务器(server)发送一个真实性证书(certificate of authenticity)。这使客户端(client)能够验证自己正在与真正的服务器通信，而不是与冒充者(impersonator)通信。客户端实际如何验证这个证书(certificate)有一些复杂细节，这里不展开讨论。"},{"en":"The client and server derive a secret that only the two of them know. Since the bytestream is still insecure at this point, they’ll need a cryptographic protocol that enables sharing a secret over an insecure channel. We won’t discuss the details here, but if you’re familiar with RSA public-key encryption (e.g. from CS 70 at UC Berkeley), that’s one possible cryptographic scheme to use here. The client encrypts a secret with the server’s public key and sends it to the server. Only the server knows the corresponding private key and is able to decrypt the message and learn the secret.","zh_html":"客户端(client)和服务器(server)推导出一个只有双方知道的秘密(secret)。由于此时字节流(bytestream)仍然不安全，它们需要一种密码学协议(cryptographic protocol)，能够在不安全信道(insecure channel)上共享秘密。这里不讨论细节；如果你熟悉 RSA 公钥加密(public-key encryption)，例如在 UC Berkeley 的 CS 70 中学过，那么它就是这里可用的一种密码学方案(cryptographic scheme)。客户端用服务器的公钥(public key)加密一个秘密并发送给服务器。只有服务器知道对应的私钥(private key)，因此只有服务器能够解密消息并获得这个秘密。"},{"en":"The client and server derive secret keys based on the shared secret and the random values from the hellos. Using the secret ensures that attackers can’t learn the secret keys. Using the random values ensures that we derive a different key every time. This derivation is done locally and independently by both the client and server. The secret keys are never actually sent across the network, so the attacker has no chance to learn them.","zh_html":"客户端(client)和服务器(server)基于共享秘密(shared secret)以及 hello 中的随机值(random values)推导秘密密钥(secret keys)。使用这个秘密可以确保攻击者无法学到秘密密钥；使用随机值则确保每次都会推导出不同的密钥(key)。这个推导过程由客户端和服务器在本地独立完成。秘密密钥实际上从不会通过网络发送，因此攻击者没有机会学到它们。"},{"en":"The client and server exchange some acknowledgements to confirm that they derived the same secrets, and nobody tampered with the messages sent over the network so far (since the bytestream is still insecure).","zh_html":"客户端(client)和服务器(server)交换一些确认(acknowledgements)，以确认双方推导出了相同的秘密(secrets)，并确认到目前为止通过网络发送的消息没有被篡改(tampered with)，因为此时字节流(bytestream)仍然是不安全的。"},{"en":"At this point, the handshake is complete, and all future messages are encrypted with the secret key (message authentication codes are also used to prevent tampering). We have now established a secure bytestream on top of the TCP connection, and applications can exchange data on top of our secure bytestream.","zh_html":"到此为止，握手(handshake)完成，之后所有消息都会用秘密密钥(secret key)加密，同时也会使用消息认证码(message authentication codes)防止篡改。我们已经在 TCP 连接(TCP connection)之上建立了一个安全字节流(secure bytestream)，应用(applications)可以在这个安全字节流之上交换数据。"}]},"/glossary.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Glossary","zh_html":"术语表(Glossary)"},{"en":"This glossary is adapted from past offerings of CS 168.","zh_html":"本术语表改编自<a href=\"https://sp24.cs168.io/glossary\">CS 168 往期课程</a>。"},{"en":"TCP packet with ACK flag set, that indicates data has been received.","zh_html":"设置了 ACK（确认，acknowledgment）标志的 TCP 报文段，表示数据已被接收。"},{"en":"Address Resolution Protocol. The protocol that allows devices to map MAC addresses to IP addresses. A device will send out (broadcast) an ARP Request message, to find out the MAC address corresponding to the IP Address. The device that is being queried will respond (unicast) with an ARP Response message. Mappings between MAC addresses and IP addresses are stored in the ARP table, which serves as a cache. Entries in the ARP table will time out (soft state).","zh_html":"地址解析协议(Address Resolution Protocol, ARP)。该协议允许设备将 MAC 地址映射到 IP 地址。设备会发送（广播(broadcast)）ARP 请求(Request)消息，以查找与 IP 地址对应的 MAC 地址。被查询的设备会以 ARP 响应(Response)消息进行回复（单播(unicast)）。MAC 地址与 IP 地址之间的映射关系存储在 ARP 表(ARP table)中，该表用作缓存(cache)。ARP 表中的条目会超时（软状态(soft state)）。"},{"en":"A network or set of networks that are all managed and supervised by a single entity or organization. A single ISP is often a single AS; however, some ISPs partition their network into multiple ASes. Each AS is assigned a number, which is used in BGP to identify paths.","zh_html":"自治系统(Autonomous System, AS)。由一个单一实体或组织管理和监督的一个或一组网络。单个 ISP（互联网服务提供商）通常是一个 AS，但有些 ISP 会将其网络划分为多个 AS。每个 AS 被分配一个编号，在 BGP 中用于标识路径。"},{"en":"A proposed alternative to STP, where loops are prevented without the need for a spanning tree (by using duplicate suppression instead). Source of some of the finest poetry the world has ever seen.","zh_html":"一种提出的 STP 替代方案，无需生成树即可防止环路（通过使用重复抑制(duplicate suppression)）。也是世界上一些最优秀诗歌的出处。"},{"en":"Lost, corrupted, reordered, delayed, duplicated.","zh_html":"丢失(lost)、损坏(corrupted)、乱序(reordered)、延迟(delayed)、重复(duplicated)。"},{"en":"This is the quantity (bandwidth)*(propagation delay) which represents the number of bits needed to “fill the pipe” (i.e., the number of bits that would have been sent but not yet received if the sender was sending at the bandwidth of the link).","zh_html":"带宽-延迟积(bandwidth-delay product)。即（带宽(bandwidth)）×（传播延迟(propagation delay)），表示“填满管道”所需的比特数（即如果发送方以链路带宽发送，已发送但尚未被接收的比特数）。"},{"en":"Equation that says your shortest distance to a destination is the minimum of your cost to a neighbour plus that neighbour’s distance to the link, for all neighbours. Or, more concretely, Node u’s cost to a given destination v is then: d(u,v) = min(nbrs w) [c(u,w) + d(w,v)]","zh_html":"贝尔曼-福特方程(Bellman-Ford equation)。该方程表明到目的地的最短距离等于到邻居的成本加上该邻居到目的地的距离，对所有邻居取最小值。更具体地说，节点 u 到给定目的地 v 的成本为：d(u,v) = min(邻居 w) [c(u,w) + d(w,v)]"},{"en":"On-demand delivery where the system provides no performance guarantees other than the system will try its best.","zh_html":"尽力而为交付(best-effort delivery)。按需交付，系统除了“尽力而为”外不提供任何性能保证。"},{"en":"Routers that are connected to routers in another network.","zh_html":"边界路由器(border routers)。连接到另一个网络中路由器的路由器。"},{"en":"This is used to detect corruption, and is a number computed over some portion of the packet (which depends on which protocol).","zh_html":"校验和(checksum)。用于检测损坏(corruption)，是根据数据包的某一部分（具体取决于协议）计算出的数值。"},{"en":"Short for Classless Interdomain Routing. Couples IP address with a network mask to determine which bits are the network bits. Far more flexible than the original IP addressing scheme, or classful addressing.","zh_html":"无类别域间路由(Classless Interdomain Routing, CIDR)。将 IP 地址与网络掩码(network mask)结合，以确定哪些位是网络位。比原始的 IP 地址方案（即分类寻址(classful addressing)）灵活得多。"},{"en":"Method of data transfer in which an end system reserves BW along a path - sets up a circuit - for communication. No need for packets.","zh_html":"电路交换(circuit switching)。一种数据传输方法，端系统在路径上预留带宽(bandwidth)——即建立一条电路(circuit)——用于通信。无需使用数据包。"},{"en":"A scheme for determining the network and host bits of an IP address. There are three classes (that we deal with): Class A addresses start with 0, and use the first 8 bits to identify the network. The last 24 bits identify the host. Class B addresses start with 10, and use the first 16 bits to identify the network. The last 16 bits identify the host. Class C addresses start with 110, and use the first 24 bits to identify the network. The last 8 bits identify the host.”","zh_html":"分类寻址(classful addressing)。一种确定 IP 地址中网络位和主机位的方案。共有三类（我们关心的）：<strong>A 类(Class A)</strong>地址以 0 开头，前 8 位标识网络，后 24 位标识主机。<strong>B 类(Class B)</strong>地址以 10 开头，前 16 位标识网络，后 16 位标识主机。<strong>C 类(Class C)</strong>地址以 110 开头，前 24 位标识网络，后 8 位标识主机。"},{"en":"This refers to the network mechanisms used to compute routing tables and other forwarding information.","zh_html":"控制平面(control plane)。指用于计算路由表(routing tables)和其他转发(forwarding)信息的网络机制。"},{"en":"We say that an algorithm has converged once all parties have up-to-date information and, barring a change in the topology of the network, all subsequent “updates” sent and received don’t affect the routing state.","zh_html":"收敛(convergence)。当所有参与方都拥有最新信息，并且在网络拓扑没有变化的情况下，所有后续发送和接收的“更新”都不会影响路由状态(routing state)时，我们称算法已收敛。"},{"en":"Routers that are connected to other internal routers.","zh_html":"核心路由器(core routers)。连接到其他内部路由器的路由器。"},{"en":"Data structure on routers that contains the set of costs to all neighbors.","zh_html":"成本表(cost table)。路由器上的数据结构，包含到所有邻居的成本集合。"},{"en":"The name for a certain type of routing loop that can occur due to the asynchronous nature of information propogation using Distance Vector. Usually caused by a link going down, a router, originally using a broken path to a certain destination, believes their neighbour has a valid path to said destination and adopts this path without knowing that it contains as subset of its original broken path. Both neighbours repeatedly receive updates from each other, adopting this broken path.","zh_html":"计数到无穷(count to infinity)。一种因距离向量(Distance Vector)信息传播的异步特性而可能发生的路由环路(routing loop)。通常由链路断开引起：原本使用某条已断开路径到达目的地的路由器，认为其邻居拥有一条有效路径，便在不知该路径包含其原断开路径的子集的情况下采纳了它。两个邻居反复接收对方的更新，不断采纳这条已断开的路径。"},{"en":"ACK means “I have received all packets (or bytes) up until this one”.","zh_html":"累积确认(cumulative ACK)。ACK（确认）表示“我已经收到了截至此数据包的所有数据包（或字节）”。"},{"en":"This refers to the network mechanisms used to forward data.","zh_html":"数据平面(data plane)。指用于转发数据(forwarding data)的网络机制。"},{"en":"Massive collections of machines.","zh_html":"数据中心(datacenters)。大规模机器集合。"},{"en":"The unsung hero of the Internet. He was the chief architect and authored the end-to-end principle.","zh_html":"David Clark。互联网的无名英雄。他是首席架构师，提出了端到端原则(end-to-end principle)。"},{"en":"When a packet arrives at a router or switch but the forwarding decision does not yield an outgoing port, forcing the packet to be dropped.","zh_html":"目的地不可达(destination unreachable) / 黑洞(black hole)。当数据包到达路由器或交换机，但转发决策未能产生出端口，导致数据包被丢弃。"},{"en":"Routing that only depends on the destination. Paths from two difference sources to same destination must coincide once they overlap.","zh_html":"目的地驱动路由(destination-driven routing)。仅依赖于目的地的路由。从两个不同源发往同一目的地的路径，一旦重叠就必须重合。"},{"en":"Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol. The protocol that provides a host with its IP address upon connecting to a network. When a host connects to a new network, it sends a DHCP Discovery message to notify the DHCP server(s) that it needs an IP address. The server sends an Offer message, containing an offered IP address, a subnet mask, the IP address of the first-hop router, and a lease time. The host will send a Request, corresponding to the offer it would like to accept. The server responds with an Acknowledgement/Acceptance message. All DHCP messages are broadcasted.","zh_html":"动态主机配置协议(Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol, DHCP)。该协议在主机连接到网络时为其提供 IP 地址。当主机连接到新网络时，它会发送 DHCP 发现(Discovery)消息以通知 DHCP 服务器它需要一个 IP 地址。服务器发送提供(Offer)消息，包含提供的 IP 地址、子网掩码(subnet mask)、第一跳路由器的 IP 地址以及租期(lease time)。主机发送请求(Request)消息，选择它要接受的提供。服务器以确认/接受(Acknowledgement/Acceptance)消息响应。所有 DHCP 消息均为广播(broadcast)。"},{"en":"Distance vector routing is a scalable and distributed routing algorithm in which each router keeps a “vector” of distances as well the next-hop router to each destination. Each node floods its vector shortest distances to its neighbors and upon receiving a vector each router uses Bellman-Ford to update its own vector.","zh_html":"距离向量路由(distance vector routing)。一种可扩展的分布式路由算法，每个路由器维护一个到每个目的地的“距离向量(vector)”以及下一跳路由器。每个节点将其最短距离向量泛洪(flood)给邻居，收到向量后，每个路由器使用贝尔曼-福特(Bellman-Ford)算法更新自己的向量。"},{"en":"Domain Name Service, a system which associates names with addresses and is commonly used to look up the address of a host given a name.","zh_html":"域名系统(Domain Name Service, DNS)。一种将名称与地址关联的系统，常用于根据名称查找主机的地址。"},{"en":"A notation that writes down IPv4 addresses as 4 numbers, one number per byte. For example, 12.34.158.5","zh_html":"点分十进制表示法(dotted decimal notation)。将 IPv4 地址写为 4 个数字，每字节一个数字。例如：12.34.158.5"},{"en":"A stream of cumulative ACKs that acknowledge the same received data multiple times: a sign of an isolated packet loss, because these additional ACKs show that data is still being received.","zh_html":"重复确认(duplicate ACK)。一系列累积确认(cumulative ACK)，多次确认同一已接收数据：这是孤立丢包的信号，因为这些额外的 ACK 表明数据仍在被接收。"},{"en":"Routers to which end hosts are attached.","zh_html":"边缘路由器(edge routers)。端主机(end hosts)所连接的路由器。"},{"en":"Helps determine whether or not something should be implemented in the network, or only in the end hosts. This class presented three interpretations: Only-if-necessary: If a function can be implemented by the hosts, don’t implement it in the network. Only-if-sufficient: Only implement a function at this level if it can be completely implemented at this level and you can relieve burden from the hosts. Only-if-useful: Implement a function in the network if it can improve the performance of the funciton without burdening applications that don’t require it.","zh_html":"端到端原则(end-to-end principle)。帮助确定某功能应在网络中实现还是仅在端主机中实现。本课程给出了三种解释：<strong>仅在必要时(Only-if-necessary)</strong>：如果主机可以实现该功能，就不要在网络中实现。<strong>仅在充分时(Only-if-sufficient)</strong>：仅当该功能能在本层完全实现并能减轻主机负担时，才在本层实现。<strong>仅在有帮助时(Only-if-useful)</strong>：如果该功能能在不增加不需要该功能的应用的负担的情况下改善性能，则在网络中实现。"},{"en":"Companies and universities.","zh_html":"企业(enterprises)。公司及大学。"},{"en":"Store state in the entities that rely on that state, such that that entity will not be affected by other failures.","zh_html":"容错(fault tolerance)。将状态存储在使用该状态的实体中，使得该实体不会受到其他故障的影响。"},{"en":"The router that a host sends a packet to when it wants to send that packet to a destination outside its L2 network.","zh_html":"第一跳路由器(first-hop router) / 默认网关(default gateway)。当主机想要将数据包发送到其 L2 网络之外的目的地时，发送数据包所指向的路由器。"},{"en":"In this class, we use flooding to refer to the act of sending a packet out all ports (excepting the incoming port) in a single switch.","zh_html":"泛洪(flooding)。在本课程中，泛洪指在单个交换机上向所有端口（除了接收端口）发送数据包的行为。"},{"en":"A stream of packets between two processes.","zh_html":"流(flow)。两个进程之间的数据包流。"},{"en":"Sending a packet towards its destination. This is done by reading the address from the packet’s header, searching the routing state for the correct output port, and sending the packet out that port. This is a local process within a router, done in the data plane, and it must be done quickly.","zh_html":"转发(forwarding)。将数据包向目的地发送。通过读取数据包首部(header)中的地址，在路由状态中查找正确的出端口，然后将数据包从该端口发出。这是路由器内部的本地过程，在数据平面(data plane)中完成，必须快速执行。"},{"en":"An entry in the forwarding table that maps an address or set of addresses to an outgoing port.","zh_html":"转发表项(forwarding entry)。转发表中的条目，将一个地址或一组地址映射到一个出端口。"},{"en":"A table the router computes for itself to guide its forwarding decisions. The forwarding table is computer using the information in the peer and cost tables.","zh_html":"转发表(forwarding table)。路由器自行计算以指导其转发决策的表。转发表使用对等表(peer table)和成本表(cost table)中的信息进行计算。"},{"en":"Dividing a packet into smaller packets to fit the maximum transmission unit (MTU) of a link.","zh_html":"分片(fragmentation)。将数据包分割成更小的数据包，以适配链路的最大传输单元(Maximum Transmission Unit, MTU)。"},{"en":"An ACK that describes all data received so far, and can take the form “I have received all packets up until this one, plus these additional ones”.","zh_html":"全确认(general cumulative ACK)。一种描述迄今为止所有已接收数据的 ACK，形式为“我已收到截至此数据包的所有数据包，再加上以下这些”。"},{"en":"Systems in “hard state” do not time out their information – they assume once they have been given some knowledge, it remains true and valid until explicitly told otherwise.","zh_html":"硬状态(hard state)。处于“硬状态”的系统不会使信息超时——它们假设一旦获得某些知识，这些知识将持续有效，直到被明确告知改变。"},{"en":"The part of the IP address that identifies the host inside its network.","zh_html":"主机位(host bits)。IP 地址中标识网络内部主机的部分。"},{"en":"End-points of a network. These entities are responsible for the generation of data packets that are then routed across the network.","zh_html":"主机(hosts) / 端系统(end systems)。网络的端点。这些实体负责生成随后通过网络路由的数据包。"},{"en":"An ACK that means “I received this single, specific packet”.","zh_html":"独立确认(individual ACK)。表示“我收到了这个单独的特定数据包”的 ACK。"},{"en":"The core network infrastructure that links all connected computing devices.","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)。连接所有计算设备的核心网络基础设施。"},{"en":"The addressing scheme used in layer 3.","zh_html":"IP 寻址(IP addressing)。第 3 层(Network Layer)使用的寻址方案。"},{"en":"Version 4 of the IP protocol.","zh_html":"IPv4。IP 协议的第 4 版。"},{"en":"A network of packet switches and communication links providing network access to end systems.","zh_html":"ISP（互联网服务提供商）网络(ISP network)。由分组交换机和通信链路组成的网络，为端系统提供网络接入。"},{"en":"Local area network, an L2 network that spans a small geographical area, for example a house.","zh_html":"局域网(Local Area Network, LAN)。覆盖较小地理区域（例如一栋房屋）的 L2 网络。"},{"en":"Generally, layering is splitting a complex system into separate levels that build upon/depend on each other. In the Internet context, this refers to a specific set of layers (physical = L1, datalink = L2, internetworking = L3, transport = L4) that only interact with the layers directly above or below.","zh_html":"分层(layering)。通常，分层是将复杂系统拆分为相互构建/依赖的不同层次。在互联网语境下，特指一组特定层次（物理层(physical, L1)、数据链路层(datalink, L2)、网际层(internetworking, L3)、传输层(transport, L4)），各层仅与直接上层或下层交互。"},{"en":"Application (Network support for apps). 4: Transport (Reliable/Unreliable end-to-end delivery). 3: Network (Global best-effort delivery). 2: Datalink (local best-effort delivery). 1: Physical (bits being transmitted over some medium).","zh_html":"分层(Layering)——五层模型。<strong>应用层(Application)</strong>（为应用提供网络支持）。4：<strong>传输层(Transport)</strong>（可靠的/不可靠的端到端交付）。3：<strong>网络层(Network)</strong>（全局尽力而为交付）。2：<strong>数据链路层(Datalink)</strong>（本地尽力而为交付）。1：<strong>物理层(Physical)</strong>（通过某种介质传输比特）。"},{"en":"Typically used at L2 in combination with the spanning tree protocol. Learning switches maintain a forwarding table mapping destination to output link. They learn from the “source” field of a packet. When a packet comes in, the switch checks if the destination is in it’s table. If it is, it forwards the packet down that link. If it isn’t, it floods the packet.","zh_html":"自学习交换机(learning switch)。通常与生成树协议(Spanning Tree Protocol, STP)结合使用于 L2。自学习交换机维护一个将目的地映射到出链路的转发表。它们从数据包的“源(source)”字段学习。当数据包到达时，交换机检查目的地是否在表中。如果是，则从该链路转发数据包；否则泛洪(flood)该数据包。"},{"en":"Piece of hardware (in a router) that receives/sends packets. They update various fields (checksum, TTL, etc.) and select outgoing port.","zh_html":"线卡(line card)。路由器中接收/发送数据包的硬件。它们更新各种字段（校验和(checksum)、生存时间(TTL)等）并选择出端口。"},{"en":"The physical pieces of infrastructure that connects routers.","zh_html":"链路(links)。连接路由器的物理基础设施。"},{"en":"In Link-State routing, each router sends (using a protocol-specific broadcast mechanism) its link state to all other routers in the network. This way, every router learns the entire network graph. Then, every router computes the least-cost paths from themselves to all other nodes using any valid algorithm (for example, Dijkstra’s).","zh_html":"链路状态路由(link-state routing)。在链路状态路由中，每个路由器（使用协议特定的广播机制）将其链路状态(link state)发送给网络中的所有其他路由器。这样，每个路由器都能学到完整的网络图。然后，每个路由器使用任意有效算法（例如 Dijkstra 算法）计算自己到所有其他节点的最低成本路径。"},{"en":"When a packet cycles around the same set of nodes forever.","zh_html":"路由环路(routing loop)。数据包永远在同一组节点之间循环。"},{"en":"Longest-prefix-match: When an IP Address matches multiple prefixes, select the longest match (Think of traversing the prefix tree until the address ‘falls off’).","zh_html":"最长前缀匹配(Longest-Prefix-Match, LPM)。当 IP 地址匹配多个前缀时，选择最长匹配（想象沿着前缀树(prefix tree)遍历，直到地址“掉落”）。"},{"en":"Used for L2 routing, a MAC Address is a 48 bit number burned into the network interface of hosts and routers. The MAC address is encoded in the physical hardware stored in Read-Only memory, making it a permanent identifier.","zh_html":"MAC 地址(MAC Address)。用于 L2 路由，MAC 地址是一个 48 位的编号，固化在主机和路由器的网络接口中。MAC 地址编码在只读存储器(Read-Only Memory)的物理硬件中，是一个永久标识符。"},{"en":"The largest number of bits a link can transfer as a single unit, the largest packet size that can be sent across a link.","zh_html":"最大传输单元(Maximum Transmission Unit, MTU)。链路作为单个单元所能传输的最大比特数，即可以跨链路发送的最大数据包大小。"},{"en":"Decomposing a problem into tasks or abstractions. Leads to the design principles of layering.","zh_html":"模块化(modularity)。将问题分解为任务或抽象，导出分层(layering)的设计原则。"},{"en":"Connecting one host to multiple, disparate networks, so that if one parent network goes offline, the host is still accessible. Prevents aggregation.","zh_html":"多宿主(multi-homing)。将一个主机连接到多个不同的网络，使得如果一个父网络离线，该主机仍然可访问。这会阻止聚合(aggregation)。"},{"en":"“Non-acknowledgement” message – “I did not receive this data [that I was expecting to]”.","zh_html":"NAK（否定确认，Negative Acknowledgment）。“未确认”消息——“我没有收到[我期望的]这个数据”。"},{"en":"When used informally, this refers to a system composing of end systems, routers/switches, and links that is able to transfer data between hosts (e.g. Berkeley’s campus network). When used formally, it refers to a set of network elements that share the same network address in IPv4, and is often used synonymously with subnet.","zh_html":"网络(network)。非正式使用时，指由端系统、路由器/交换机以及链路组成的系统，能够在主机之间传输数据（例如伯克利的校园网）。正式使用时，指在 IPv4 中共享相同网络地址的一组网络元素，常与子网(subnet)同义使用。"},{"en":"The component of an IP address that refers to the network (or subnet), rather than the host.","zh_html":"网络地址(network address)。IP 地址中指向网络（或子网）而非主机的部分。"},{"en":"The part of the IP address that identifies the network the host is on.","zh_html":"网络位(network bits)。IP 地址中标识主机所在网络的部分。"},{"en":"An IP-address-like string of bits used to identify the network portion of an IP Address. Made up of some set number of 1s (one per network address bit), followed by all 0s.","zh_html":"网络掩码(network mask)。一种类似 IP 地址的比特串，用于标识 IP 地址的网络部分。由若干连续的 1（每个网络地址位对应一个 1）后跟连续的 0 组成。"},{"en":"The name of a host (Something human-friendly).","zh_html":"主机名(hostname)。主机的名称（便于人类记忆）。"},{"en":"The networking software on the host, it replicates some functionality found at the routers and also adds additional functionality (e.g. Sockets, TCP header, etc).","zh_html":"网络协议栈(network stack)。主机上的网络软件，它复制了路由器上的部分功能，并增加了额外功能（例如套接字(Sockets)、TCP 首部等）。"},{"en":"Bags of bits. Consists of: Header with meaningful information for network and network stack to make decisions. Body containing a payload. Ex. A file, imagine, an application header, etc.","zh_html":"数据包(packet)。比特包。由以下部分组成：<strong>首部(Header)</strong>包含供网络和网络协议栈做出决策的有意义信息。<strong>主体(Body)</strong>包含有效载荷(payload)。例如文件、图像、应用首部等。"},{"en":"Method of data transfer in which data is segmented into packets and routers/switches service each packet they receive independently by inspecting its header.","zh_html":"分组交换(packet switching)。一种数据传输方法，数据被分割成数据包(packets)，路由器/交换机通过检查每个数据包的首部来独立处理它们。"},{"en":"Similar to distance vector routing, but when advertising to neighbors, instead of sending them your shortest distance, you send them your paths to destinations.","zh_html":"路径向量路由(path vector routing)。类似于距离向量路由，但在向邻居通告时，不是发送最短距离，而是发送到达目的地的路径。"},{"en":"Data carried in packet.","zh_html":"有效载荷(payload)。数据包中携带的数据。"},{"en":"Data structure on routers that contains copies of the information each of the router’s “peers” or “neighbours” sent them.","zh_html":"对等表(peer table)。路由器上的数据结构，包含该路由器的每个“对等方(peers)”或“邻居”发送给它的信息的副本。"},{"en":"Method attempting to mitigate the count to infinity problem by not advertising the ability to reach a destination (i.e. advertising a distance of infinity) to a neighbour you use on the path to said destination. For example, router A creates a temporary copy of its vector to send to router C that advertises a distance of infinity for all destinations in which router A uses link AC.","zh_html":"毒性反转(poison reverse)。一种尝试缓解计数到无穷(count to infinity)问题的方法，通过不向用于到达某目的地的邻居通告到达该目的地的能力（即通告距离为无穷大）。例如，路由器 A 创建其向路由器 C 发送的向量的临时副本，将所有通过链路 AC 到达的目的地的距离设置为无穷大。"},{"en":"A number that an OS assigns to a socket that is used to identify the socket.","zh_html":"端口号(port number)。操作系统分配给套接字(socket)的数字，用于标识该套接字。"},{"en":"The physical port which connects a router to another router through a link.","zh_html":"端口(port)。通过链路将路由器连接到另一个路由器的物理端口。"},{"en":"Combining routing table entries into one entry by using a common prefix (i.e. combining 101 and 100 to 10*).","zh_html":"路由聚合(route aggregation)。通过使用公共前缀将多个路由表条目合并为一个条目（例如将 101 和 100 合并为 10*）。"},{"en":"Binary tree that represents matching bits in IP address lookup (how the lookup table is traversed).","zh_html":"前缀树(prefix tree / trie)。表示 IP 地址查找中匹配位的二叉树（查找表的遍历方式）。"},{"en":"Two interpretations: 1) The network recovers from failures quickly, allowing two, non-partitioned endpoints to communicate. 2) Network failures do not interfere with endpoint semantics.","zh_html":"鲁棒性(robustness)。两种解释：1）网络快速从故障中恢复，使两个非分区的端点能够通信。2）网络故障不干扰端点语义(endpoint semantics)。"},{"en":"Building a reliable transport service on top of best-effort delivery.","zh_html":"可靠传输(reliable transport)。在尽力而为交付(best-effort delivery)之上构建可靠的传输服务。"},{"en":"A transport mechanism is “reliable” if and only if (a) it resends all dropped or corrupted packets, and (b) it attempts to make progress.","zh_html":"可靠性(reliability)。当且仅当传输机制：(a) 重传所有丢失或损坏的数据包，并且 (b) 尝试继续推进，才被称为“可靠(reliable)”。"},{"en":"The ability to know who is using what resources (bandwidth) so that you can hold them accountable to it. A failure in Internet architecture.","zh_html":"问责(accountability)。能够知道谁在使用什么资源（带宽），以便追究其责任。这是互联网体系结构中的一个失败之处。"},{"en":"As long as the network is not partitioned, two hosts should be able to communicate enventually, AND failures should never interfere with application semantics.","zh_html":"可生存性(survivability)。只要网络没有被分割，两个主机最终应该能够通信，并且故障不应干扰应用语义(application semantics)。"},{"en":"Instead of having one forwarding entry per host, have one entry per set of hosts with the same prefix that all go out the same port.","zh_html":"可扩展性(scalability) / 聚合(aggregation)。不是每个主机一个转发表项，而是每组合适的具有相同前缀且走同一出端口的主机一个表项。"},{"en":"Procedure to mitigate network inconsistencies that says when a link goes down between A and B, router B should advertise to all its neighbours that it no longer has a link to router A (i.e. B advertises a distance of infinity), in order to signify that it can no longer reach A.","zh_html":"触发更新(triggered update)。一种缓解网络不一致性的过程：当 A 和 B 之间的链路断开时，路由器 B 应向所有邻居通告它不再有到路由器 A 的链路（即 B 通告距离为无穷大），以表明它无法再到达 A。"},{"en":"Guiding packets from source to destination (can be done in many ways - see link-state, distance vector, spanning tree, etc). This is inherently a global process, so it must scale. This is done in the control plane, and may be done slowly.","zh_html":"路由(routing)。引导数据包从源到目的地的过程（可通过多种方式实现，参见链路状态(link-state)、距离向量(distance vector)、生成树(spanning tree)等）。这本质上是一个全局过程，因此必须可扩展。在控制平面(control plane)中完成，可以较慢执行。"},{"en":"This is similar to the Forwarding Table, but can refer to all the information a router (including from other peers) rather than just the best forwarding entries.","zh_html":"路由表(routing table / RIB)。类似于转发表(Forwarding Table)，但可以包含路由器的所有信息（包括来自其他对等方的信息），而不仅仅是最佳转发条目。"},{"en":"Notation for taking about a subnet. Looks like 1.2.0.0/10 where the first 10 bits of 1.2.0.0 are the subnet prefix.","zh_html":"子网表示法(subnet notation)。用于描述子网的表示法。例如 1.2.0.0/10，其中 1.2.0.0 的前 10 位是子网前缀。"},{"en":"A finite number of un-acked packets allowed to be in flight (for efficiency purposes) before we stop sending more.","zh_html":"窗口(window)。允许在传输中的未确认数据包的最大数量（出于效率考虑），超过此数量则停止发送更多数据。"},{"en":"An OS mechanism used to connect a process to the networking stack.","zh_html":"套接字(socket)。一种操作系统机制，用于将进程连接到网络协议栈。"},{"en":"The concept of allowing your stored knowledge to “time out”, under the assumption that it may have changed/no longer be valid/etc. Systems that operate under soft state will periodically “forget” what they know and need to “re-learn” it – by requesting the information again, waiting for new messages and information, etc. DHCP offers having a ‘lease time’, cached ARP entries timing out, and the periodic messages in Distance-Vector Routing are all examples of soft-state.","zh_html":"软状态(soft state)。允许存储的知识“超时(time out)”的概念，假设知识可能已更改或不再有效等。运行于软状态下的系统会周期性“忘记”其所知内容，并需要“重新学习”——通过重新请求信息、等待新消息和信息等。DHCP 提供的租期(lease time)、ARP 缓存条目超时以及距离向量路由中的周期性消息都是软状态的例子。"},{"en":"A distributed protocol in which switches send messages of the format (Y, d, X) from node X proposing Y as the root and advertising a distance of d to Y. This protocol identifies the node with the lowest ID and builds a spanning tree with that node as the root.","zh_html":"生成树协议(Spanning Tree Protocol, STP)。一种分布式协议，其中交换机发送格式为(Y, d, X)的消息（节点 X 提议 Y 为根，并通告到 Y 的距离 d）。该协议识别具有最低 ID 的节点，并以该节点为根构建生成树。"},{"en":"Split horizon provides the same functionality as poison reverse, but expresses the information differently. Split Horizon is used in the context of full updates, and the router does not advertise any route to destination X to the neighbor it uses to reach destination X.","zh_html":"水平分割(split horizon)。水平分割提供与毒性反转(poison reverse)相同的功能，但表达信息的方式不同。水平分割在全更新(full updates)的上下文中使用，路由器不会向用于到达目的地 X 的邻居通告任何到目的地 X 的路由。"},{"en":"Summing the maximum rate of flows is greater than combining the flows and finding the maximum.","zh_html":"统计复用(statistical multiplexing)。各流的最大速率之和大于合并流后的最大速率。"},{"en":"In this class we use this term to refer to a portion of a network that is connected by L2 and shares the same network address.","zh_html":"子网(subnet)。本课程中使用此术语指代通过 L2 连接且共享同一网络地址的网络部分。"},{"en":"In IP, this refers to the number of hops a packet can travel before being dropped, which is useful in preventing loops. More generally, TTL refers to the time until something expires (such as a cached entry).","zh_html":"生存时间(Time To Live, TTL)。在 IP 中，指数据包在被丢弃前可以经过的跳数(hops)，有助于防止环路。更一般地，TTL 指某事物的到期时间（例如缓存的条目）。"},{"en":"A routing state is valid if an only if there are no loops and no deadends (assuming no packet replication). If there is packet replication, then this changes to saying at least one replica does not hit a deadend.","zh_html":"有效路由状态(valid routing state)。当且仅当没有环路且没有死路时（假设无数据包复制），路由状态有效。如果有数据包复制，则改为至少有一个副本不会遇到死路。"},{"en":"This can refer to any L3 network (i.e., not just a local area network), or to networks that span large geographic distances (i.e., not a datacenter).","zh_html":"广域网(Wide Area Network, WAN)。可指任何 L3 网络（即不限于局域网），也可指跨越大地理距离的网络（即不是数据中心）。"}]},"/":{"blocks":[{"en":"Introduction to the Internet: Architecture and Protocols","zh_html":"互联网导论：体系结构(Architecture)与协议(Protocols)"},{"en":"By Peyrin Kao, based on lectures by Sylvia Ratnasamy, Rob Shakir, and others.","zh_html":"<em>作者 <a href=\"https://peyrin.github.io\">Peyrin Kao</a>，基于 <a href=\"https://www2.eecs.berkeley.edu/Faculty/Homepages/ratnasamy.html\">Sylvia Ratnasamy</a>、<a href=\"https://rob.sh/\">Rob Shakir</a> 等人的讲义编写。</em>"},{"en":"These are the course notes for CS 168: Introduction to the Internet at UC Berkeley.","zh_html":"这是 <a href=\"https://cs168.io/\">CS 168：互联网导论(Introduction to the Internet)</a> 在 <a href=\"https://eecs.berkeley.edu/\">加州大学伯克利分校（UC Berkeley）</a> 的课程讲义。"},{"en":"Here is the official course description:","zh_html":"以下是官方课程描述："},{"en":"This course is an introduction to the Internet architecture. We will focus on the concepts and fundamental design principles that have contributed to the Internet's scalability and robustness and survey the various protocols and algorithms used within this architecture. Topics include layering, addressing, intradomain routing, interdomain routing, reliable delivery, congestion control, and the core protocols (e.g., TCP, UDP, IP, DNS, and HTTP) and network technologies (e.g., Ethernet, wireless).","zh_html":"本课程介绍互联网体系结构(Internet architecture)。我们将重点介绍推动互联网可扩展性(scalability)和鲁棒性(robustness)的核心概念和基本设计原则，并综述该体系结构中使用的各种协议(protocols)和算法(algorithms)。主题包括分层(layering)、寻址(addressing)、域内路由(intradomain routing)、域间路由(interdomain routing)、可靠交付(reliable delivery)、拥塞控制(congestion control)以及核心协议（如 TCP、UDP、IP、DNS、HTTP）和网络技术（如以太网(Ethernet)、无线网络(wireless)）。"},{"en":"Disclaimer: Beta","zh_html":"免责声明：测试版(Beta)"},{"en":"These notes have not been proofread. They likely contain errors.","zh_html":"这些笔记尚未经过校对，可能包含错误。"},{"en":"If you’re a CS 168 student at Berkeley, in any case of dispute, the official course lectures are the correct source of truth.","zh_html":"如果你是伯克利的 CS 168 学生，在任何有争议的情况下，请以官方课程讲义为准。"},{"en":"Diagrams","zh_html":"图表(Diagrams)"},{"en":"Slideshow versions of these notes with diagrams are available here.","zh_html":"<a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/13RnAGH1OrsOVvXdmQC73WkmoVD9r5lzd\">此处提供包含图表的幻灯片版本。</a>"},{"en":"PDF Version","zh_html":"PDF 版本"},{"en":"These notes are available as a PDF here.","zh_html":"<a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1PPSkHOnFsOI9noWWMuJnaxmsOzM6RIKX/view?usp=sharing\">此处提供 PDF 版本。</a>"},{"en":"The PDF version is not always up-to-date. It was last updated in November 2024.","zh_html":"PDF 版本并非始终最新，最后更新于 2024 年 11 月。"},{"en":"Corrections","zh_html":"更正(Corrections)"},{"en":"As of the Fall 2024 semester, this textbook is still being actively maintained and updated.","zh_html":"截至 2024 年秋季学期，本教材仍在积极维护和更新中。"},{"en":"If you see any parts that needs to be corrected, please open a Github issue here.","zh_html":"如果您发现任何需要更正的地方，请在 Github 上<a href=\"https://github.com/berkeley-cs168/textbook/issues\">提交 Issue</a>。"},{"en":"Source and Changelog","zh_html":"源代码与变更日志(Source and Changelog)"},{"en":"The source for the textbook and a log of all changes is available on Github.","zh_html":"教材的源代码及所有变更记录已<a href=\"https://github.com/berkeley-cs168/textbook\">发布在 Github 上</a>。"},{"en":"License","zh_html":"许可协议(License)"},{"en":"This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License.","zh_html":"<a rel=\"license\" href=\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\"><img alt=\"知识共享许可协议\" style=\"border-width:0\" src=\"https://i.creativecommons.org/l/by-sa/4.0/88x31.png\" /></img></a><br /></br>本<span xmlns:dct=\"http://purl.org/dc/terms/\" href=\"http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/Text\" rel=\"dct:type\">作品</span>采用<a rel=\"license\" href=\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\">知识共享署名-相同方式共享 4.0 国际许可协议（Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License）</a>进行许可。"}]},"/intro/architecture.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Network Architecture","zh_html":"<a href=\"#network-architecture\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"network-architecture\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 网络体系结构(Network Architecture)"},{"en":"Design Paradigms","zh_html":"<a href=\"#design-paradigms\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"design-paradigms\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 设计范式(Design Paradigms)"},{"en":"So far, we’ve seen a bottom-up view of the Internet, starting with fundamental pieces to build up the overall picture. In this section, we’ll take a top-down view of the Internet, and analyze the overarching architectural choices in the design.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们已经从底层到顶层的自底向上(bottom-up)视角了解了互联网(Internet)，从基础组件开始逐步构建整体图景。本节我们将从顶层到底层的自顶向下(top-down)视角来看待互联网，分析其设计中最重要的体系结构选择(architectural choices)。"},{"en":"These Internet design paradigms influence why the Internet works the way it does, and also influences the applications we build on top of the Internet. These paradigms were a radical departure from how systems were historically built.","zh_html":"这些互联网(Internet)设计范式(design paradigms)深刻影响了互联网为何以现在这种方式工作，同时也影响了我们在互联网之上构建的应用(applications)。这些范式与历史上构建系统的方式截然不同，是一次彻底的背离。"},{"en":"These designs are just one of many possible designs, and many design choices were made years ago, before the Internet grew to its current scale. Other designs exist, and debates still exist about what the best design is.","zh_html":"这些设计只是众多可能设计中的一种，许多设计选择是在多年前做出的，早在互联网(Internet)发展到如今规模之前。其他设计方案同样存在，关于什么才是最佳设计的争论至今仍在继续。"},{"en":"For example, the Internet was built to be federated (independent operators cooperating), but in recent years, software-defined networking (SDN) emerged as a more centralized approach to managing a network.","zh_html":"例如，互联网(Internet)最初被构建为联邦式(federated)系统，即独立运营方(operators)相互协作。但近年来，软件定义网络(SDN, software-defined networking)作为一种更加集中化(centralized)的网络管理方式出现了。"},{"en":"In the original Internet, switches were intentionally designed to be dumb and forward data without parsing it. However, in the modern Internet, attackers might try to overwhelm a switch by flooding it with useless data, and switches might need a way to detect this. Early Internet designers who came up with the dumb infrastructure paradigm did not consider this security implication.","zh_html":"在最初的互联网(Internet)中，交换机(switches)被有意设计为“笨”(dumb)的——只转发数据而不解析内容。然而，在现代互联网中，攻击者(attackers)可能会试图通过大量无用数据淹没(flood)交换机来使其不堪重负，交换机因此需要检测这类攻击的能力。提出“笨基础设施”范式(dumb infrastructure paradigm)的早期互联网设计者们当时并没有考虑到这一安全(security)方面的影响。"},{"en":"Narrow Waist","zh_html":"<a href=\"#narrow-waist\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"narrow-waist\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 窄腰(Narrow Waist)"},{"en":"It’s possible to have multiple protocols at a given layer. For example, at Layer 7, we could use HTTP to serve websites, or NTP to sync system clocks, both built on the same Internet infrastructure. Or, at Layer 2, we could use Ethernet for wired networks, or Wi-Fi for wireless networks.","zh_html":"在某一层(layer)上运行多种协议(protocols)是可能的。例如，在第七层(Layer 7)，我们可以使用 HTTP 来提供网站服务，也可以使用 NTP 来同步系统时钟，两者都建立在相同的互联网(Internet)基础设施之上。又或者，在第二层(Layer 2)，我们可以使用以太网(Ethernet)用于有线网络，或使用 Wi-Fi 用于无线网络。"},{"en":"Note that even though there are multiple protocols at a given layer, you can commit to using a specific stack of protocols for your application. For example, you can commit to using HTTP over TCP over IP, and you don’t need to use the other Layer 7 or Layer 4 protocols. Then, everybody using your application uses the same stack.","zh_html":"请注意，即使某一层(layer)上有多种协议(protocols)，你仍然可以为你的应用(application)承诺使用一组特定的协议栈(protocol stack)。例如，你可以承诺使用 HTTP over TCP over IP，而不需要用到其他第七层(Layer 7)或第四层(Layer 4)的协议。这样，每个使用你应用的人就都使用同一套协议栈。"},{"en":"If you look at this diagram, you’ll notice there’s only one protocol at Layer 3. This is the “narrow waist” that enables Internet connectivity. Ultimately, everybody on the Internet must agree to speak IP so that packets can be sent across the Internet.","zh_html":"如果你看一下这张图，你会注意到第三层(Layer 3)只有一种协议(protocol)。这就是使互联网(Internet)连接成为可能的“窄腰”(narrow waist)。归根结底，互联网上的每个人都必须同意使用 IP，这样分组(packets)才能跨越整个互联网传输。"},{"en":"Demultiplexing","zh_html":"<a href=\"#demultiplexing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"demultiplexing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 解复用(Demultiplexing)"},{"en":"TODO write about demultiplexing.","zh_html":"TODO：待补充解复用(demultiplexing)相关内容。"},{"en":"Be careful about naming. In networking, two different things are called ports. A physical port is the actual physical place where you plug a link into a switch. A logical port is a number in the Layer 4 header to disambiguate which application a packet belongs to.","zh_html":"要注意命名的区别。在网络(networking)中，有两种不同的东西都被称为端口(port)。物理端口(physical port)是交换机(switch)上实际插入链路(link)的物理接口。逻辑端口(logical port)是第四层(Layer 4)首部(header)中的一个数字，用于区分一个分组(packet)属于哪个应用(application)。"},{"en":"Note: The term socket refers to an OS mechanism for connecting an application to the networking stack in the OS. When an application opens a socket, that socket is associated with a logical port number. When the OS receives a packet, it uses the port number to direct that packet to the associated socket.","zh_html":"注意：术语<strong>套接字(socket)</strong>指的是操作系统(OS)中将应用(application)连接到网络协议栈(networking stack)的一种机制。当应用打开一个套接字时，该套接字与一个逻辑端口号(logical port number)关联。当操作系统收到一个分组(packet)时，它利用端口号将该分组导向关联的套接字。"},{"en":"End-to-End Principle","zh_html":"<a href=\"#end-to-end-principle\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"end-to-end-principle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 端到端原则(End-to-End Principle)"},{"en":"Why did we design the Internet with the layering structure that we did? Why do only the hosts understand Layers 4 and 7, and not the routers as well?","zh_html":"我们为什么按照现有的分层(layering)结构来设计互联网(Internet)？为什么只有主机(hosts)理解第四层(Layer 4)和第七层(Layer 7)，而路由器(routers)不需要也理解这些层？"},{"en":"The end-to-end principle offers wisdom and guidance for designing the Internet. David D. Clark, a scientist at MIT and a member of the Internet Architecture Board, was a major contributor to this principle. Two of his papers, “End-to-End Arguments in System Design” (1981) and “The Design Philosophy of the DARPA Internet Protocols” (1988), were hugely influential on the philosophy of the Internet design.","zh_html":"<strong>端到端原则(end-to-end principle)</strong>为互联网(Internet)的设计提供了智慧和指导。David D. Clark 是 MIT 的科学家和互联网架构委员会(Internet Architecture Board)的成员，他对这一原则做出了重要贡献。他的两篇论文——“End-to-End Arguments in System Design”（1981）和“The Design Philosophy of the DARPA Internet Protocols”（1988）——对互联网设计哲学产生了巨大影响。"},{"en":"The end-to-end principle guides the debate about what functionality the network does and doesn’t implement. The principle is quite broad and has many applications, but we’ll focus on the question of: Should we implement reliability (Layer 4) in the network, or only at the end hosts?","zh_html":"端到端原则(end-to-end principle)指导着关于网络应该实现哪些功能、不应该实现哪些功能的讨论。该原则相当宽泛，有许多应用场景，但我们将聚焦于一个核心问题：我们是否应该在网络中实现可靠性(reliability, Layer 4)，还是只在终端主机(end hosts)上实现它？"},{"en":"For now, let’s think of a simple protocol for reliability. Host A wants to send 10 packets to Host B, so it sends the packets, numbered 1 through 10, across the network. The goal is for B to either receive all the packets, or realize that some packets got lost and error (we’ll ignore recovering from the error).","zh_html":"现在，让我们设想一个简单的可靠性(reliability)协议(protocol)。主机 A 想向主机 B 发送 10 个分组(packets)，于是它将这些编号为 1 到 10 的分组通过网络发送出去。目标是主机 B 要么收到所有分组，要么意识到有些分组丢失了并报错（我们暂时忽略从错误中恢复的问题）。"},{"en":"What would the Internet look like if we implemented reliability in the network? Unlike our picture from earlier, every router must now understand Layer 4 in addition to Layers 1, 2, and 3.","zh_html":"如果我们在网络中实现可靠性(reliability)，互联网(Internet)会是什么样子？与我们之前的图不同，现在每个路由器(router)除了第一、二、三层(Layers 1, 2, 3)之外，还必须理解第四层(Layer 4)。"},{"en":"With this new picture, an intermediate router must reliably send a packet to its next hop. It must guarantee that the next hop received all the packets, and if not, the router must re-send any lost packets. The hosts don’t check that all packets were received, and instead rely on the network to ensure that all packets were received.","zh_html":"在这张新图景下，中间路由器(intermediate router)必须将分组(packet)可靠地(reliably)发送给它的下一跳(next hop)。它必须保证下一跳收到了所有分组，否则必须重新发送丢失的分组。主机(hosts)不检查是否所有分组都已收到，而是依赖网络来确保所有分组都已接收。"},{"en":"In this approach, the hosts have to trust the network. If one of the routers is buggy, and drops a packet, there’s nothing the hosts can really do about it.","zh_html":"在这种方案中，主机(hosts)必须信任网络。如果某个路由器(router)有缺陷(buggy)，丢弃了一个分组(packet)，主机对此基本上无能为力。"},{"en":"The other approach is the end-to-end approach, where we do not implement reliability in the network, and we instead force the two end hosts to enforce reliability. Routers can drop packets, and it’s up to the end hosts to verify that all packets were received.","zh_html":"另一种方案是端到端(end-to-end)的方式：我们不在网络中实现可靠性(reliability)，而是强制两个终端主机(end hosts)来实施可靠性。路由器(routers)可以丢弃分组(packets)，由终端主机来验证所有分组是否都已收到。"},{"en":"In the end-to-end approach, where the end hosts implemented reliability, the control is with the hosts. The hosts could still be buggy and drop packets, but this time, the hosts have the power to fix the bug themselves. More generally, if you’re writing code, it’s better if you have the control over making the feature correct, instead of relying on other people who might mess up (and you can’t fix their mistakes).","zh_html":"在端到端(end-to-end)方案中，可靠性(reliability)由终端主机(end hosts)来实现，控制权掌握在主机手中。主机本身仍可能有缺陷(buggy)并丢弃分组(packets)，但这次主机有能力自行修复缺陷。更一般地说，如果你在编写代码，由你自己掌控让功能正确实现，比依赖其他可能出错的人（而你无法修复他们的错误）要好得多。"},{"en":"With this comparison in mind, if we used the first approach, where we relied on the network to be correct, we can’t actually guarantee perfect reliability if the network is buggy. The end hosts would probably end up doing an end-to-end check (as in the second solution) anyway.","zh_html":"有了以上比较，我们可以看到：如果我们采用第一种方案，依赖网络来保证正确性，而网络本身有缺陷(buggy)，我们实际上无法保证完美的可靠性(reliability)。终端主机(end hosts)很可能最终还是要做一个端到端(end-to-end)的检查——就像第二种方案那样。"},{"en":"In the old Internet, every link did implement reliability. However, as we saw, the modern Internet only implements best-effort in the network, and forces the end hosts to implement reliability, in line with the end-to-end principle.","zh_html":"在早期互联网(Internet)中，每条链路(link)确实实现了可靠性(reliability)。然而，正如我们所见，现代互联网只在网络中实现尽力而为(best-effort)，而强制终端主机(end hosts)来实现可靠性，这与端到端原则(end-to-end principle)一脉相承。"},{"en":"In summary: Some application requirements must be implemented end-to-end in order to ensure correctness. Also, the end-to-end implementation is sufficient, and no additional support from the network is needed. Because the end-to-end implementation alone is sufficient, adding network functionality would introduce additional unnecessary complexity (and cost), without helping us actually achieve the requirements.","zh_html":"总结来说：某些应用(application)需求必须在端到端(end-to-end)实现，才能确保正确性(correctness)。同时，端到端的实现已经是充分的(sufficient)，不需要网络的额外支持。正因为仅凭端到端实现就足够满足需求，在网络中增加额外功能只会引入不必要(unnecessary)的复杂性(complexity)和成本(cost)，而实际上并不能帮助我们更好地达成这些需求。"},{"en":"Note that the end-to-end principle is not a proof or a theorem that’s always true. It’s a guiding principle and a philosophical argument, and different designers might make different arguments for or against the principle.","zh_html":"需要注意的是，端到端原则(end-to-end principle)并不是一个永远成立的证明或定理。它是一个指导原则和哲学论据，不同的设计者可能给出支持或反对该原则的不同论证。"},{"en":"Here’s an example of the end-to-end principle not being a strict rule. Even though the end-to-end principle says to implement reliability in the end hosts only, we could still add some extra reliability in the network in addition to the end-to-end check. This might be useful if we have highly unreliable links. Suppose there are 10 links between A and B, and each one fails 10% of the time. Then, each time we send the packet, it has a 65% chance of getting dropped. However, if each router was modified to send two copies of the packet for reliability purposes, each link only fails 0.1% of the time, and packets now only have a 1% chance of getting dropped. Wireless links will sometimes implement reliability to reduce error rates and improve performance for the end hosts.","zh_html":"下面这个例子说明端到端原则(end-to-end principle)并非一条严格的规则。虽然端到端原则说只在终端主机(end hosts)中实现可靠性(reliability)，但我们仍然可以在端到端检查之外，再在网络中增加一些额外的可靠性。这在链路(links)非常不可靠(unreliable)的情况下可能是有用的。假设 A 和 B 之间有 10 条链路，每条链路有 10% 的概率失败。那么，每次发送分组(packet)时，它有 65% 的概率被丢弃。然而，如果每个路由器(router)都被修改为发送两份分组副本以提高可靠性，那么每条链路的失败率降为 0.1%，分组便只有 1% 的概率被丢弃。无线链路(wireless links)有时会实现可靠性以降低错误率(error rates)，从而改善终端主机的性能(performance)。"},{"en":"The end-to-end principle extends to other fields as well. For example, in security, the end-to-end principle might say that two end hosts communicating should encrypt their messages at the end hosts, instead of at intermediate points in the network.","zh_html":"端到端原则(end-to-end principle)也延伸到其他领域。例如，在安全(security)领域，端到端原则主张：两台通信中的终端主机(end hosts)应该在终端主机处对消息进行加密(encrypt)，而不是在网络中间的某个节点进行加密。"},{"en":"The end-to-end argument in Clark’s words: “The function in question can completely and correctly be implemented only with the knowledge and help of the application at the end points. Therefore, providing that function as a feature of the communication system itself is not possible. Sometimes an incomplete version of the function provided by the communication system may be useful as a performance enhancement.”","zh_html":"用 David D. Clark 的话来总结端到端论证(end-to-end argument)：“所讨论的功能(function)只有借助端点(end points)处应用(application)的知识和协助，才能完整且正确地实现。因此，将该功能作为通信系统本身的一个特性来提供是不可能的。有时，由通信系统提供该功能的一个不完整版本，可能作为性能增强(performance enhancement)而有所助益。”"}]},"/intro/headers.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Headers","zh_html":"<a href=\"#headers\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"headers\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 首部(Headers)"},{"en":"Why Do We Need Headers?","zh_html":"<a href=\"#why-do-we-need-headers\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"why-do-we-need-headers\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 为什么需要首部(Headers)？"},{"en":"In the previous section, we saw that at Layer 3, data travels across the Internet in packets. Suppose an application wants to send a file over the Internet. We can take some bits of the image, put them in a packet, and send them over the Internet. When a switch receives this sequence of 1s and 0s, it has no idea what to do with these bits.","zh_html":"在上一节中，我们看到在第三层(Layer 3)，数据以分组(packets)的形式在互联网(Internet)上传输。假设某个应用(application)想通过互联网发送一个文件。我们可以把图片的一些比特(bits)放入一个分组中，然后通过互联网发送出去。当交换机(switch)收到这串 0 和 1 的序列时，它完全不知道该拿这些比特做什么。"},{"en":"In the analogy, if I write a letter to my friend, and hand it to the post office, the post office has no idea what to do with it. Instead, we should put the letter inside an envelope, and write some information on the envelope (e.g. my friend’s address) that tells the post office what to do with the letter.","zh_html":"打个比方：如果我写了一封信给我的朋友，然后直接把它交给邮局，邮局完全不知道该怎么处理它。正确的做法是，我们应该把信放入信封(envelope)中，并在信封上写一些信息（例如朋友的地址），告诉邮局该怎么处理这封信。"},{"en":"Just like the envelope, when we send a packet, we need to attach additional metadata that tells the network infrastructure what to do with that packet. This additional metadata is called a header. The rest of the bits (e.g. the file being sent, the letter inside the envelope) is called the payload.","zh_html":"就像信封一样，当我们发送一个分组(packet)时，我们需要附加额外的元数据(metadata)，告诉网络基础设施(network infrastructure)该对这个分组做什么。这些额外的元数据称为<strong>首部(header)</strong>。其余的比特（例如正在发送的文件，就像信封里的信）称为<strong>载荷(payload)</strong>。"},{"en":"In the analogy, the post office shouldn’t be reading the contents of my letter. It should only read what’s on the envelope to decide how to send my letter. Similarly, the network infrastructure should only read the header to decide how to deliver the data.","zh_html":"在上面的比喻中，邮局不应该阅读我信的内容，只需要读信封上的信息来决定如何投递。同样地，网络基础设施(network infrastructure)应该只读首部(header)来决定如何交付数据。"},{"en":"The recipient cares about the inside of the letter, not the envelope. Similarly, the application at the end host cares about the payload, not the header. That said, the end hosts still need to know about headers, in order to add headers to packets before sending them.","zh_html":"收信人关心的是信的内容，而不是信封。同样地，终端主机(end host)上的应用(application)关心的是载荷(payload)，而不是首部(header)。不过，终端主机仍然需要了解首部，因为在发送分组(packets)之前，它们需要给分组附加上首部。"},{"en":"Headers are Standardized","zh_html":"<a href=\"#headers-are-standardized\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"headers-are-standardized\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 首部是标准化的"},{"en":"You can also think of headers as the API between the end hosts sending/receiving data, and the network infrastructure carrying the data. When we write software, we need to decide on the interface that users will use to interact with our code (e.g. what functions users can call, the parameters to those functions). Similarly, the information in the header is how users access functions and pass parameters to the network.","zh_html":"你也可以把首部(headers)理解为发送/接收数据的终端主机(end hosts)与承载数据的网络基础设施(network infrastructure)之间的 API。当我们编写软件时，需要决定用户通过怎样的接口(interface)与我们的代码交互（例如用户可以调用哪些函数，以及这些函数的参数）。类似地，首部中的信息就是用户访问网络功能、向其传递参数的方式。"},{"en":"Everybody on the Internet (every end host, every switch) needs to agree on the format of a header. If Microsoft Windows changes the code in its operating system to send packets with a different header structure, nobody else will understand the packets being sent.","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)上的每一个参与者（每一台终端主机(end host)、每一台交换机(switch)）都必须对首部(header)的格式达成一致。如果 Microsoft Windows 修改了其操作系统中的代码，用不同的首部结构(header structure)发送分组(packets)，那么其他所有人都无法理解这些被发送的分组。"},{"en":"This also means we need to be careful about designing headers. Once we design a header and deploy it on the Internet, it’s very hard to change the design (we’d have to get everybody to agree to change it). This is why standards bodies can spend years designing and standardizing headers.","zh_html":"这也意味着我们在设计首部(header)时必须非常谨慎。一旦我们设计好一个首部并在互联网(Internet)上部署，后续要更改设计就非常困难——我们得让所有人都同意去改。这就是为什么标准组织(standards bodies)可能要花数年时间来设计和标准化首部。"},{"en":"What Should a Header Contain?","zh_html":"<a href=\"#what-should-a-header-contain\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"what-should-a-header-contain\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 首部应该包含什么？"},{"en":"What information should we put in the header?","zh_html":"我们应该在首部(header)中放什么信息？"},{"en":"The header should definitely contain the destination address, which tells us where to send the packet.","zh_html":"首部(header)中必须包含目的地址(destination address)，它告诉我们分组(packet)应该发往哪里。"},{"en":"Headers could also contain other information that’s not required, but is useful to have. Technically, the source address is not required to deliver the packet, but in practice, we almost always include the source address in the header. This allows the recipient to send replies back to the sender.","zh_html":"首部(header)还可以包含其他并非必需、但有则更好的信息。从技术上讲，源地址(source address)对投递分组(packet)并不是必需的，但在实践中，我们几乎总是会在首部中包含源地址。这让接收方能够向发送方回送响应(replies)。"},{"en":"The header could also include a checksum, to ensure that packet is not corrupted while in transit.","zh_html":"首部(header)还可以包含校验和(checksum)，以确保分组(packet)在传输过程中没有被损坏(corrupted)。"},{"en":"The header could also contain other metadata like the length of the packet. Note that packets can vary in size (e.g. the user might only need to send a few bytes).","zh_html":"首部(header)还可以包含其他元数据(metadata)，例如分组的长度(length)。注意分组(packet)的大小可以变化——用户可能只需要发送几个字节。"},{"en":"Multiple Headers","zh_html":"<a href=\"#multiple-headers\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"multiple-headers\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 多层首部(Multiple Headers)"},{"en":"Let’s go back to the postal analogy briefly. Suppose the boss of Company A wants to write a letter to the boss of Company B. How does the message get sent?","zh_html":"让我们暂时回到邮政的比喻。假设公司 A 的老板想给公司 B 的老板写一封信。这条消息(message)是如何发送的呢？"},{"en":"Company A’s boss folds the letter and hands it to their secretary. Then, the secretary puts the letter in an envelope with Company B’s boss’s full name.","zh_html":"公司 A 的老板把信折好交给秘书。然后，秘书把信放入信封(envelope)，在信封上写上公司 B 老板的全名。"},{"en":"The secretary passes this letter to the mailroom. The postal worker puts the letter in a box with Company B’s street address on it, and puts the package in a delivery truck.","zh_html":"秘书把这封信传递给收发室(mailroom)。邮局工作人员把信放进一个盒子，盒子上写着公司 B 的街道地址，然后把包裹放上送货卡车。"},{"en":"At this point, the letter itself is wrapped in multiple layers of identifying information (envelope, box). The delivery company sends the letter to Company B (possibly across several trucks, planes, mailmen, etc.).","zh_html":"此时，信本身被包裹在多层标识信息中（信封、盒子）。快递公司将信送到公司 B——途中可能经过多次卡车、飞机、邮递员等转运。"},{"en":"When the letter reaches Company B, the mailroom removes the box and passes the envelope to the secretary.","zh_html":"当信到达公司 B 时，收发室(mailroom)拆掉盒子，把信封(envelope)递给秘书。"},{"en":"Then, the secretary sees the boss’s name on the envelope, removes the envelope, and passes the letter up to the Company B boss.","zh_html":"然后，秘书看到信封上老板的名字，拆掉信封，把信递交给公司 B 的老板。"},{"en":"Notice that as we moved to lower abstraction layers, we wrapped more headers around the data. Then, as we moved to higher abstraction layers, we peeled layers off the data.","zh_html":"注意，当我们向更低的抽象层(abstraction layers)移动时，我们在数据外面包裹了更多的首部(header)。然后，当我们向更高的抽象层移动时，我们从数据外面一层层剥离首部。"},{"en":"Each layer only has to understand its own header, and is “communicating” (in some sense) with its peers at the same layer. When Secretary A writes the name on the envelope, that’s meant for Secretary B to read (not the mailmen, or the boss).","zh_html":"每一层(layer)只需要理解它自己的首部(header)，并在某种意义上与同一层的对等实体(peers)进行“通信”。秘书 A 在信封上写名字，是写给秘书 B 看的（不是写给邮递员或老板的）。"},{"en":"More formally, on the Internet, peers at the same layer communicate by establishing a protocol at that layer. The protocol only makes sense to entities at that specific layer.","zh_html":"更正式地说，在互联网(Internet)上，同一层的对等实体(peers)通过建立该层的协议(protocol)来通信。该协议只对该特定层(layer)的实体有意义。"},{"en":"Note that some layers offer multiple choices of protocol (e.g. wireless or wired protocols at Layer 2). In these cases, the two people communicating need to use the same choice of protocol. A wired sender can’t talk to a wireless recipient.","zh_html":"注意，某些层(layers)提供了多种协议(protocol)可供选择（例如第二层(Layer 2)的无线协议或有线协议）。在这些情况下，通信双方必须使用相同的协议选择。有线发送方无法与无线接收方通信。"},{"en":"Addressing and Naming","zh_html":"<a href=\"#addressing-and-naming\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"addressing-and-naming\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 寻址与命名(Addressing and Naming)"},{"en":"Earlier, we said that our headers need to contain the address of the recipient. What actually is that address? Formally, a network address is some value that tells us where a host is located in the network.","zh_html":"之前我们说过，首部(header)需要包含接收方的地址(address)。那么地址到底是什么？从形式上讲，网络地址(network address)是某种告诉我们主机(host)在网络中所处位置的值。"},{"en":"As we look at the different layers in more detail, we’ll see that different layers have different addressing schemes. If you want to send a letter inside Soda Hall, you could write the destination address as 413 Soda Hall, and the people in the building know where to deliver the letter. By contrast, if you want to send a letter to New York, you’d have to write a full street address like 123 Main Street, New York, NY.","zh_html":"随着我们深入研究不同层(layers)，我们会看到不同层有不同的寻址方案(addressing schemes)。如果你想在 Soda Hall 楼内送一封信，可以写目标地址为 413 Soda Hall，楼里的人就知道送到哪里。相比之下，如果你想寄信到纽约(New York)，就必须写完整的街道地址，如 123 Main Street, New York, NY。"},{"en":"Similarly, different layers in the Internet have different addressing schemes that work best for that particular layer. For example, sometimes a host is referred to by its human-readable name (e.g. www.google.com). Other times, that same host is referred to by a machine-readable IP address (e.g. 74.124.56.2), where this number somehow encodes something about the server’s location (and could change if the server moves). Other times, that same host could be referred to by its hardware MAC address, which never changes.","zh_html":"同样地，互联网(Internet)中不同层(layers)有不同的寻址方案(addressing schemes)，各自最适合该层的需求。例如，有时一台主机(host)用人类可读的名称(human-readable name)来指代，如 www.google.com。另一时候，同一台主机用机器可读的 IP 地址(IP address)来指代，如 74.124.56.2，这个数字以某种方式编码了服务器(server)的位置信息（如果服务器迁移，这个地址也可能改变）。还有些时候，同一台主机可以用其硬件 MAC 地址(MAC address)来指代，这个地址永远不会改变。"},{"en":"Layers at Hosts and Routers","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layers-at-hosts-and-routers\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layers-at-hosts-and-routers\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 主机与路由器上的分层(Layers at Hosts and Routers)"},{"en":"The Internet is more than just a sender and a recipient. In addition to the two end hosts, there are routers forwarding the packet across multiple hops toward the destination. How do our ideas of layering and headers interact across all these machines?","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)不仅仅是一个发送方和一个接收方。除了两台终端主机(end hosts)之外，还有路由器(routers)通过多跳(multiple hops)将分组(packet)转发到目的地。我们的分层(layering)和首部(header)概念在这些机器之间是如何互动的呢？"},{"en":"The end hosts need to implement all the layers. Your computer needs to know about Layer 7 to run a web browser. Your computer also needs to know about Layer 1 to send the bits out along the wire. You’ll also need all the layers in between in order for application-level data (the boss’s letter) to be passed all the way down to the physical layer.","zh_html":"终端主机(end hosts)需要实现所有层(layers)。你的计算机需要理解第七层(Layer 7)才能运行网页浏览器(web browser)，也需要理解第一层(Layer 1)才能沿着线路把比特(bits)发送出去。你还需要中间的所有层，以便应用层数据(application-level data)——就像老板的信——能够一直向下传递到物理层(physical layer)。"},{"en":"What about routers? The router does need Layer 1 for receiving bits on a wire, Layer 2 for sending packets along the wire, and Layer 3 for forwarding packets in the global network. However, the routers don’t really need to think about Layer 4 and Layer 7. The router isn’t running a web browser to display webpages, and the router doesn’t need to think about reliability (recall, best-effort service model).","zh_html":"路由器(routers)呢？路由器确实需要第一层(Layer 1)来从线路上接收比特(bits)，需要第二层(Layer 2)来沿线路发送分组(packets)，需要第三层(Layer 3)来在全局网络中转发分组。但是，路由器并不真的需要考虑第四层(Layer 4)和第七层(Layer 7)。路由器并不运行网页浏览器来显示网页，也不需要关心可靠性(reliability)——回想一下，网络只提供尽力而为(best-effort)的服务模型(service model)。"},{"en":"In summary: The lower 3 layers are implemented everywhere, but the top 2 layers are only implemented at the end hosts.","zh_html":"总结：较低的 3 层在所有设备上都有实现，但最高的 2 层只在终端主机(end hosts)上实现。"},{"en":"Multiple Headers at Hosts and Routers: Analogy","zh_html":"<a href=\"#multiple-headers-at-hosts-and-routers-analogy\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"multiple-headers-at-hosts-and-routers-analogy\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 主机与路由器上的多层首部：比喻"},{"en":"Let’s think about sending mail again. Company A wrapped the letter in an envelope, which was then put in a box. The box doesn’t magically travel to Company B. In fact, it might travel through several post offices.","zh_html":"让我们再次思考邮寄信件的例子。公司 A 把信封装进信封(envelope)，再把信封放入一个盒子。盒子并不会魔法般地直接飞到公司 B。实际上，它可能要经过多个邮局(post offices)。"},{"en":"At each post office, the mailman opens the box and sorts through the mail. The mailman looks at the envelope (the next header revealed after opening the box), and sees that the envelope is meant for Company B.","zh_html":"在每个邮局，邮递员打开盒子，对邮件进行分类。邮递员查看信封(envelope)——这是打开盒子后露出的下一层首部(header)——发现信封是寄往公司 B 的。"},{"en":"The mailman then puts the envelope in another box, possibly different, so that the letter can reach the next post office on the way to Company B.","zh_html":"然后，邮递员把信封放入另一个盒子——盒子可能和之前的不一样——以便信件能够去往前往公司 B 途中的下一个邮局。"},{"en":"This process repeats at every post office. The box is opened, revealing the envelope inside. Then, the envelope goes in a new box, destined for the next post office. Notice that none of the post offices open the envelope to reveal the letter inside, because they don’t need to read it.","zh_html":"这个过程在每个邮局不断重复：打开盒子，露出里面的信封(envelope)；然后把信封放入一个新的盒子，发往下一个邮局。注意，这些邮局中没有一个会打开信封去看里面的信，因为它们不需要读信的内容。"},{"en":"Eventually, the letter reaches Company B in a box, and this time, Company B opens the box, and the envelope, to reveal the letter inside.","zh_html":"最终，信件装在盒子里到达了公司 B。这一次，公司 B 打开了盒子，又打开了信封(envelope)，看到了里面的信。"},{"en":"Multiple Headers at Hosts and Routers","zh_html":"<a href=\"#multiple-headers-at-hosts-and-routers\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"multiple-headers-at-hosts-and-routers\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 主机与路由器上的多层首部"},{"en":"Now that we have the full picture with hosts and routers, let’s revisit the demo of wrapping and unwrapping headers, as the packet takes multiple hops across the network.","zh_html":"现在我们已经有了包含主机(hosts)和路由器(routers)的完整图景，让我们重新回顾首部(headers)的包裹(wrapping)与解包(unwrapping)演示——分组(packet)将在网络中经过多跳(multiple hops)传输。"},{"en":"First, Host A takes the message and works its way down the stack, adding headers for Layer 7, 4, 3, 2, and 1. We now have a packet wrapped with headers for every layer.","zh_html":"首先，主机 A (Host A)拿到消息(message)，然后沿着协议栈(protocol stack)向下处理，依次加上第七层(Layer 7)、第四层(Layer 4)、第三层(Layer 3)、第二层(Layer 2)和第一层(Layer 1)的首部(headers)。现在我们得到了一个被各层首部包裹的分组(packet)。"},{"en":"The Layer 1 protocol sends the bits of this packet along the wire, to the first router on the way to the destination.","zh_html":"第一层(Layer 1)协议将这些比特(bits)沿着线路发送到通往目的地的第一个路由器(router)。"},{"en":"This router must forward the packet to the next hop, so that the packet eventually reaches Host B. We know that forwarding packets in the global network is a Layer 3 job. Therefore, the router must parse this packet up to Layer 3.","zh_html":"这个路由器(router)必须将分组(packet)转发给下一跳(next hop)，以便分组最终到达主机 B (Host B)。我们知道，在全局网络中转发分组是第三层(Layer 3)的职责。因此，路由器必须解析这个分组直到第三层。"},{"en":"The router reads and unwraps the Layer 1 and Layer 2 headers, revealing the Layer 3 header underneath. The router reads this header to decide where to forward the packet next.","zh_html":"路由器(router)读取并解包(unwraps)第一层(Layer 1)和第二层(Layer 2)的首部(headers)，露出下面第三层(Layer 3)的首部。路由器读取这个首部来决定下一步将分组(packet)转发到哪里。"},{"en":"Now, to pass the packet along to the next hop, the router must go down the stack again, wrapping new Layer 2 and Layer 1 headers, and then sending the bits along the wire to the next hop.","zh_html":"现在，为了将分组(packet)传递给下一跳(next hop)，路由器(router)必须再次沿协议栈(protocol stack)向下，包裹上新的第二层(Layer 2)和第一层(Layer 1)首部(headers)，然后将比特(bits)沿线路发送到下一跳。"},{"en":"This pattern repeats at every router: Layers 1 and 2 are unwrapped to reveal the Layer 3 header, and then new Layer 2 and Layer 1 headers are wrapped before sending the packet to the next hop. Notice that none of the routers look beyond the Layer 3 protocol, because the upper layers are only parsed by the end hosts.","zh_html":"这一模式在每个路由器(router)处重复：解包(unwrap)第一层(Layer 1)和第二层(Layer 2)以露出第三层(Layer 3)首部(header)，然后包裹上新的第二层和第一层首部，再将分组(packet)发往下一跳(next hop)。注意，没有一个路由器会查看第三层协议(protocol)以上的内容，因为更高层(upper layers)只由终端主机(end hosts)来解析。"},{"en":"Eventually, the packet reaches Host B, who unwraps every layer, one by one: Layer 1, 2, 3, 4, 7. Host B has successfully received the message!","zh_html":"最终，分组(packet)到达主机 B (Host B)。主机 B 逐层解包(unwraps)：第一层(Layer 1)、第二层(Layer 2)、第三层(Layer 3)、第四层(Layer 4)、第七层(Layer 7)。主机 B 成功收到了消息(message)！"},{"en":"One consequence of this layering scheme is that each hop can use different protocols at Layer 2 and 1. For example, the first hop could get sent along a wire, and the initial Layer 2 and 1 headers used by Host A and the first router can be for a wired protocol. By contrast, a later hop could get sent along a wireless link, and the Layer 2 and 1 headers used by the routers on either end of that hop can be for a wireless protocol.","zh_html":"这种分层方案(layering scheme)的一个结果是：每一跳(hop)可以在第二层(Layer 2)和第一层(Layer 1)使用不同的协议(protocols)。例如，第一跳可能沿着有线线路发送，主机 A(Host A)和第一个路由器(router)使用的第二层和第一层首部(headers)属于有线协议(wired protocol)。相比之下，后面的某一跳可能沿着无线链路(wireless link)发送，该跳两端路由器使用的第二层和第一层首部则属于无线协议(wireless protocol)。"},{"en":"More generally, we said that each layer only needs to communicate with its peers at the same layer. We can now see this at play across all the layers. At Layers 4 and 7, the two hosts must speak the same protocols to send and receive packets. The host’s peer is the other host.","zh_html":"更一般地说，我们之前讲到每一层(layer)只需要与同一层的对等实体(peers)通信。现在我们可以看到这在所有层中是如何体现的。在第四层(Layer 4)和第七层(Layer 7)，两台主机(hosts)必须使用相同的协议(protocols)来发送和接收分组(packets)。主机的对等实体就是另一台主机。"},{"en":"By contrast, at Layers 1 and 2, the router must speak the same protocol as the previous-hop and the next-hop router, so that the router can receive packets from the previous hop and send packets to the next hop. The router’s peers are its neighboring routers along the path.","zh_html":"相比之下，在第一层(Layer 1)和第二层(Layer 2)，路由器(router)必须与前一跳和下一跳的路由器使用相同的协议(protocol)，这样才能从前一跳接收分组(packets)并向下一跳发送分组。路由器的对等实体(peers)是它沿路径上的相邻路由器。"},{"en":"In summary: Each router parses Layers 1 through 3, while the end hosts parse Layers 1 through 7.","zh_html":"总结：每个路由器(router)解析第一层到第三层(Layers 1-3)，而终端主机(end hosts)解析第一层到第七层(Layers 1-7)。"}]},"/intro/index.html":{"title":"index","blocks":[{"en":"Introduction to the Internet","en_html":"<a href=\"intro.html\">Introduction to the Internet</a>","zh_html":"互联网简介(Introduction to the Internet)"},{"en":"Layers of the Internet","en_html":"<a href=\"layers.html\">Layers of the Internet</a>","zh_html":"互联网分层(Layers of the Internet)"},{"en":"Headers","en_html":"<a href=\"headers.html\">Headers</a>","zh_html":"首部(Headers)"},{"en":"Network Architecture","en_html":"<a href=\"architecture.html\">Network Architecture</a>","zh_html":"网络体系结构(Network Architecture)"},{"en":"Designing Resource Sharing","en_html":"<a href=\"sharing-resources.html\">Designing Resource Sharing</a>","zh_html":"资源共享设计(Designing Resource Sharing)"},{"en":"Links","en_html":"<a href=\"links.html\">Links</a>","zh_html":"链路(Links)"}]},"/intro/intro.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Introduction to the Internet","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)简介"},{"en":"What is the Internet?","zh_html":"什么是互联网(Internet)？"},{"en":"The Internet is ubiquitous as a tool for transferring data between devices around the world. In this class, we’ll be focusing on the infrastructure (both hardware and software) that supports this.","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)已经无处不在，它是世界各地设备之间传输数据的工具。本课程关注支撑这些数据传输的基础设施(infrastructure)，包括硬件(hardware)和软件(software)。"},{"en":"The Internet and the World Wide Web are not the same thing. You can think of the web as applications built on top of the Internet (e.g. Facebook, Twitter) that you can access through a web browser (e.g. Firefox, Chrome). Other applications besides the web can also use Internet infrastructure, too. Examples of non-web applications are Zoom or online games, or even Internet-of-things (IoT) devices like a sensor in your refrigerator or car.","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)和万维网(World Wide Web)不是同一个概念。你可以把 Web 理解为构建在互联网之上的应用(applications)，例如 Facebook、Twitter；用户通过浏览器(web browser)，例如 Firefox、Chrome，访问这些应用。除 Web 之外，其他应用也可以使用互联网基础设施，例如 Zoom、在线游戏，甚至冰箱或汽车里的传感器这类物联网(Internet of Things, IoT)设备。"},{"en":"Why is the Internet Interesting?","zh_html":"为什么互联网(Internet)值得研究？"},{"en":"The Internet is not a new type of network technology (e.g. electrical wires already existed), but is instead about a completely new problem of tying together different, existing networks. Solving this problem required a new design paradigm that influenced other computer science fields.","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)并不是一种全新的网络技术(network technology)，例如电线早已存在；它真正面对的是一个全新的问题：如何把不同的、已经存在的网络连接成一个整体。解决这个问题需要新的设计范式(design paradigm)，而这种范式后来也影响了计算机科学的其他领域。"},{"en":"Networking is a relatively new addition to the field of computer science. The Internet introduced many new challenges that are different from many traditional computer science fields. For example, unlike theory fields, we don’t have a formal model of the Internet. Unlike hardware fields, we don’t have a measurable benchmark for performance.","zh_html":"网络(networking)是计算机科学中相对较新的方向。互联网(Internet)带来了许多不同于传统计算机科学领域的新挑战。例如，不同于理论领域，我们没有一个形式化模型(formal model)可以完整描述互联网；不同于硬件领域，我们也没有一个统一且可度量的性能基准(benchmark)。"},{"en":"Unlike previous classes you might have taken, it’s no longer enough to write code that simply works. The code you write has to scale to billions of users. The code you write also has to align with the business relationships of different operators (otherwise, they might not agree to run your code).","zh_html":"这也不同于你过去可能上过的一些课程：仅仅写出“能跑”的代码已经不够了。你的代码必须能够扩展(scale)到数十亿用户；它还必须符合不同运营方(operators)之间的商业关系，否则这些运营方可能根本不会愿意运行你的代码。"},{"en":"Code that works for a lightweight application (e.g. your home computer) might not work for a heavy-duty server. Code that works today might not work tomorrow, when different computers join and leave the network.","zh_html":"适用于轻量级应用(lightweight application)，例如家用电脑的代码，未必适用于高负载服务器(heavy-duty server)。今天能正常工作的代码，明天也可能因为不同计算机加入或离开网络而失效。"},{"en":"The design of the Internet has influenced the way in which we architect modern systems (e.g. reasoning about goals, constraints, and trade-offs in the design). Network architecture is more about thinking about designs, and less about proving theorems or writing code. It’s more about considering tradeoffs, and less about meeting specific benchmarks. It’s more about designing systems that are practical, and less about finding the optimal design. The Internet is not optimal, but has successfully balanced a wide range of goals.","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)的设计影响了我们构建现代系统的方式，例如如何围绕目标(goals)、约束(constraints)和权衡(trade-offs)进行推理。网络体系结构(network architecture)更强调设计思考，而不是证明定理或编写代码；它更强调权衡(tradeoffs)，而不是满足某个单一性能指标；它更强调设计实用的系统，而不是寻找理论上的最优设计。互联网并不是最优的，但它成功地在许多目标之间取得了平衡。"},{"en":"The Internet is Federated","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)是联邦式系统(federated system)"},{"en":"The Internet is a federated system, and requires interoperability between operators. In other words, each operator (ISP) acts independently, but every operator has to cooperate in order to connect the entire world. In other words, all the ISPs in the world need to agree on some common protocol(s) in order to achieve global connectivity.","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)是一个联邦式系统(federated system)，它要求不同运营方(operators)之间能够互操作(interoperability)。换句话说，每个运营方，也就是互联网服务提供商(Internet Service Provider, ISP)，都独立行动；但为了把整个世界连接起来，所有运营方又必须合作。也就是说，世界上的 ISP 需要就某些共同协议(protocols)达成一致，才能实现全球连通性(global connectivity)。"},{"en":"Federation introduces several challenges. Competing entities (e.g. rival companies) are forced to cooperate, even though competitors might not want to share confidential information with each other. When designing protocols, we have to consider real-life business incentives in addition to technical considerations.","zh_html":"联邦(federation)带来了若干挑战。彼此竞争的实体，例如竞争公司，被迫进行合作，尽管竞争者之间通常并不愿意共享机密信息。设计协议(protocols)时，除了技术因素，我们还必须考虑现实中的商业激励(business incentives)。"},{"en":"Federation also complicates innovation. In other fields, companies can innovate by developing a new feature that no one else has. But on the Internet, if you have a feature that nobody else has, you can’t use it. Everybody has to speak a common language (protocol), so any upgrades to the Internet have to be made with interoperability in mind.","zh_html":"联邦(federation)也让创新变得更复杂。在其他领域，公司可以通过开发别人没有的新功能来创新。但在互联网(Internet)上，如果某个功能只有你有，别人都没有，那么你反而无法真正使用它。所有参与者都必须说同一种“语言”，也就是协议(protocol)，因此互联网的任何升级都必须把互操作性(interoperability)放在设计中心。"},{"en":"The Internet is Scalable","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)是可扩展的(scalable)"},{"en":"Federation enables the tremendous scale of the Internet. Instead of a single operator managing billions of users and trillions of services, we only need to focus on interconnecting all the different operators. Federation also allows us to build the Internet out of a huge diversity of technologies (e.g. wireless, optical), with a huge range of capabilities (e.g. home links with tiny capacity, or undersea cables with huge capacity). These technologies are also constantly evolving, which means we can’t aim for a fixed target (e.g. capacity and demand is constantly increasing by orders of magnitude).","zh_html":"联邦(federation)使互联网(Internet)能够达到巨大的规模。我们不需要让单一运营方管理数十亿用户和数万亿服务，只需要关注如何把不同运营方连接起来。联邦也允许互联网由极其多样的技术组成，例如无线(wireless)、光通信(optical)；这些技术的能力范围也很广，例如容量很小的家庭链路(home links)，或容量巨大的海底光缆(undersea cables)。这些技术还在不断演进，因此我们无法瞄准一个固定目标，例如容量(capacity)和需求(demand)都会持续以数量级增长。"},{"en":"The massive scale of the Internet also means that any system we design has to support the massive range of users and applications on the Internet (e.g. some need more capacity than others, some may be malicious).","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)的巨大规模还意味着，我们设计的任何系统都必须支持范围极广的用户和应用(applications)。有些应用需要更多容量(capacity)，有些参与者甚至可能是恶意的(malicious)。"},{"en":"The worldwide scale of the Internet means that our systems and protocols need to operate asynchronously. Data can’t move faster than the speed of light (and often moves much slower than that). Suppose you send a message to a server on the other side of the world. By the time your message arrives, your CPU might have executed millions of additional instructions, and the message you sent might already be outdated.","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)的全球规模意味着，我们的系统和协议(protocols)必须能够异步(asynchronously)运行。数据无法比光速更快传播，而且通常远慢于光速。假设你向地球另一端的一台服务器(server)发送消息；当消息抵达时，你的 CPU 可能已经额外执行了数百万条指令，而你发送的消息也可能已经过时。"},{"en":"The scale of the Internet means that even sending a single message can require interacting with many components (e.g. software, switches, links). Any of the components could fail, and we might not even know if they fail. If something does fail, it could take a long time to hear the bad news. The Internet was the first system that had to be designed for failure at scale. Many of these ideas have since been adopted in other fields.","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)的规模还意味着，即使只发送一条消息，也可能需要与许多组件(component)交互，例如软件(software)、交换机(switches)和链路(links)。任何组件都有可能失败(fail)，而我们甚至未必知道它是否失败了。即使确实发生故障，坏消息也可能很久之后才传回来。互联网是第一个必须按大规模故障(failure at scale)来设计的系统。此后，许多相关思想被其他领域吸收采用。"},{"en":"Protocols","zh_html":"协议(protocols)"},{"en":"In this class, much of our focus will be on protocols that specify how entities exchange in communication. What is the format of the messages they exchange, and how do they respond to those messages?","zh_html":"本课程很大一部分内容会关注<strong>协议(protocols)</strong>。协议规定实体(entities)之间如何通信：它们交换的消息(message)采用什么格式，以及收到这些消息后应如何响应。"},{"en":"For example, imagine you’re writing an application that needs to send and receive data over the Internet. The code at the sender machine and the code at the recipient machine need to both agree on how the data is formatted, and what they should do in response to different messages.","zh_html":"例如，假设你正在编写一个需要通过互联网(Internet)发送和接收数据的应用(application)。发送方机器(sender machine)上的代码和接收方机器(recipient machine)上的代码，必须共同约定数据如何格式化(format)，以及面对不同消息(message)时应采取什么动作。"},{"en":"Here’s an example of a protocol. Alice and Bob both say hello, then Alice requests a file, and Bob replies with the file. To define this protocol, we need to define syntax (e.g. how to write “give me this file” in 1s and 0s), and semantics (e.g. Alice must receive a hello from Bob before requesting a file).","zh_html":"下面是一个协议(protocol)示例。Alice 和 Bob 先互相打招呼，然后 Alice 请求一个文件，Bob 再用该文件进行响应。要定义这个协议，我们需要定义语法(syntax)，例如如何用 0 和 1 表示“把这个文件给我”；还要定义语义(semantics)，例如 Alice 必须先收到 Bob 的 hello，才能请求文件。"},{"en":"Different protocols are designed for different needs. For example, if Alice needs to get the file as quickly as possible, we might design a protocol without the initial hello messages. Designing a good protocol can be harder than it seems! We might also need to account for edge cases, bugs, and malicious behavior. For example, what if Alice requests a file, and Bob replies with hello? How should Alice respond?","zh_html":"不同协议(protocols)服务于不同需求。例如，如果 Alice 需要尽快拿到文件，我们可能会设计一个省略初始 hello 消息的协议。设计一个好的协议可能比看起来更难；我们还需要考虑边界情况(edge cases)、缺陷(bugs)和恶意行为(malicious behavior)。例如，如果 Alice 请求文件，而 Bob 回复 hello，Alice 应该如何处理？"},{"en":"Throughout this class, we’ll see many protocols that have been standardized across the Internet. You’ll sometimes see the acronym RFC (Request For Comments) when we mention a protocol. Many standards are published as RFC documents that are eventually widely accepted, though not all RFCs end up adopted. RFC documents are numbered, and sometimes protocols are referred to by their RFC number. For example, “RFC 1918 addresses” refers to addresses defined by that particular document.","zh_html":"在整个课程中，我们会看到许多已经在互联网(Internet)上标准化的协议(protocols)。提到协议时，你有时会看到 RFC(Request For Comments) 这个缩写。许多标准会以 RFC 文档的形式发布，并最终被广泛接受；但并不是所有 RFC 最后都会被采用。RFC 文档有编号，有时协议也会用对应的 RFC 编号来指代。例如，“RFC 1918 地址”指的就是那份特定文档中定义的地址(addresses)。"},{"en":"There are different standards bodies responsible for standardizing protocols. The IEEE focuses on the lower-layer electrical engineering side. The IETF focuses on the Internet and is responsible for RFCs.","zh_html":"不同的标准组织(standards bodies)负责标准化不同协议(protocols)。IEEE 关注较低层、偏电气工程(electrical engineering)的一侧；IETF 关注互联网(Internet)，并负责 RFC。"}]},"/intro/layers.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Layers of the Internet","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layers-of-the-internet\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layers-of-the-internet\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 互联网分层(Layers of the Internet)"},{"en":"Layer 1: Physical Layer","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layer-1-physical-layer\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layer-1-physical-layer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 第一层：物理层(Physical Layer)"},{"en":"In this section, we’ll build the Internet from the bottom-up, starting with basic building blocks and combining them to form the Internet infrastructure. We’ll use the postal system as a running analogy, since it shares many design choices with the Internet.","zh_html":"在本节中，我们将从底层到顶层——自底向上(bottom-up)——构建互联网(Internet)，从基本组件开始，逐步组合成互联网基础设施(infrastructure)。我们将用邮政系统(postal system)作为贯穿全书的类比，因为它与互联网有许多相似的设计选择。"},{"en":"First, we need some way to send a signal across space. In the postal system, this could be a mailman, the Pony Express, a truck, a carrier pigeon, etc.","zh_html":"首先，我们需要某种方式在空间中传递信号。在邮政系统中，这可以是邮递员、驿马快信(Pony Express)、卡车、信鸽等等。"},{"en":"In the Internet, we’re looking for a way to signal bits (1s and 0s) across space. The technology could be voltages on an electrical wire, wireless radio waves, light pulses along optical fiber cables, among others. There are entire fields of electrical engineering dedicated to sending signals across space, but we won’t go into detail in this class.","zh_html":"在互联网(Internet)中，我们需要的是在空间中传输比特(bits, 1和0)的方式。可以采用的技术包括电线上的电压、无线(radio)电磁波、光纤(optical fiber)中的光脉冲等。电气工程(electrical engineering)中有专门研究如何将信号传过空间的完整领域，但本课程不会深入这些细节。"},{"en":"Layer 2: Link Layer","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layer-2-link-layer\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layer-2-link-layer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 第二层：链路层(Link Layer)"},{"en":"In the analogy, now that we have a way to send data across space, we can use that building block to connect up two homes. We could even try to connect up all the homes in the local town.","zh_html":"在这个类比中，既然我们有了在空间中传输数据的方式，就可以用这个基础模块来连接两户人家。我们甚至可以尝试将本地小镇的所有人家都连接起来。"},{"en":"In the Internet, a link connects two machines. That link could be using any sort of technology (wired, wireless, optical fiber, etc.). If we use links to connect up a bunch of nearby computers (e.g. all the computers in UC Berkeley), we get a local area network (LAN).","zh_html":"在互联网(Internet)中，<strong>链路(link)</strong>连接两台机器。该链路可以使用任何一种技术（有线、无线、光纤等）。如果我们用链路连接一批附近的计算机，例如 UC Berkeley 的所有计算机，就得到了一个<strong>局域网(LAN, local area network)</strong>。"},{"en":"At Layer 2, we can also group bits into units of data called packets (sometimes called frames at this layer), and define where a packet starts and ends in the physical signal. We can also handle problems like multiple people simultaneously using the same wire to send data.","zh_html":"在第二层(Layer 2)，我们还可以将比特(bits)分组为<strong>分组(packets)</strong>（在这一层有时称为帧(frame)），并定义分组在物理信号中的起始和结束位置。我们还可以处理多人同时使用同一根电线发送数据之类的问题。"},{"en":"Layer 3: Internet Layer","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layer-3-internet-layer\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layer-3-internet-layer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 第三层：互联网层(Internet Layer)"},{"en":"We now have a way to connect everybody in a local area, but what if two people in different areas wanted to communicate? One possible approach is to add a bunch of links between different local networks, but this doesn’t seem very efficient. (What if the two local networks were in different continents?)","zh_html":"我们现在有了连接本地所有人的方式，但如果两个不同区域的人想要通信呢？一种可能的方案是在不同本地网络之间添加大量链路(links)，但这似乎不够高效。（如果两个本地网络位于不同大洲呢？）"},{"en":"Instead, a smarter approach would be to introduce a post office in each network, and just connect the two post offices together. Now, if someone in network A wants to communicate with someone in network B, they can send the mail to the post office in network A. This post office forwards the mail to the post office in network B, which delivers the mail to the destination.","zh_html":"更聪明的做法是：在每个网络中引入一个邮局(post office)，然后将两个邮局连接起来。这样，如果网络 A 中的某人想与网络 B 中的某人通信，他们可以把信件寄到网络 A 的邮局；该邮局将信件转发给网络 B 的邮局，再由后者将信件投递给收件人。"},{"en":"In the Internet, the post office receiving and redirecting mail is called a switch or router.","zh_html":"在互联网(Internet)中，负责接收和转发信件的邮局被称为<strong>交换机(switch)</strong>或<strong>路由器(router)</strong>。"},{"en":"If we build additional links between switches, we can connect up local networks. With enough links and local networks, we can connect everybody in the world, resulting in the Internet.","zh_html":"如果在交换机(switches)之间额外添加链路(links)，我们就可以把多个本地网络连接起来。有了足够的链路和本地网络，我们就能将全世界的人连接起来，从而形成互联网(Internet)。"},{"en":"One question we’ll need to answer is how to find paths across a network. When a switch receives a packet, how does it know where to forward the packet, so that it gets closer to its final destination? This will be the focus of our routing unit.","zh_html":"我们需要回答的一个问题是如何在网络中找到路径(paths)。当交换机(switch)收到一个分组(packet)时，它如何知道应该把分组转发到哪里，才能使其更接近最终目的地？这将是我们路由(routing)章节的重点。"},{"en":"We’ll also need to make sure that there’s enough capacity on these links to carry our data. This will be the focus of our congestion control unit.","zh_html":"我们还需要确保这些链路(links)有足够的容量(capacity)来承载数据。这将是我们拥塞控制(congestion control)章节的重点。"},{"en":"This picture now shows the infrastructure of the Internet, but in this class, we’ll also study the operators managing the infrastructure. In the analogy, these are the people who build and manage the post office. On the Internet, the operators are Internet service providers like AT&amp;T, Amazon Web Services, or even UC Berkeley, who own and operate Internet structure. In addition to the hardware and software infrastructure, we’ll need to think about these entities as real-life businesses and organizations, and consider their economic and political incentives. For example, if AT&amp;T builds an undersea cable, they might charge a fee for other ISPs to send data through that cable.","zh_html":"这幅图景展示了互联网(Internet)的基础设施，但本课程还会研究管理这些基础设施的运营方(operators)。在类比中，他们是建造和管理邮局的人。在互联网上，运营方是<strong>互联网服务提供商(ISPs, Internet service providers)</strong>，例如 AT&amp;T、Amazon Web Services，甚至 UC Berkeley，他们拥有和运营互联网基础设施。除了硬件和软件基础设施，我们还需要把这些实体当作现实中的商业机构和组织来思考，考虑他们的经济(economic)和政治(political)激励。例如，如果 AT&amp;T 铺设了一条海底光缆，他们可能会向其他 ISP 收取费用，允许其数据通过该光缆传输。"},{"en":"Network of Networks","zh_html":"<a href=\"#network-of-networks\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"network-of-networks\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 网络之网络(Network of Networks)"},{"en":"The Internet is often described as a network of networks. There are lots of small local networks, and what happens inside that local network can be managed locally (e.g. by UC Berkeley). Then, all the local networks can connect to each other to form the Internet.","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)常被描述为一个<strong>网络之网络(network of networks)</strong>。有许多小型本地网络，每个本地网络内部的事务可以本地管理（例如由 UC Berkeley 管理）。然后，所有本地网络相互连接，构成互联网。"},{"en":"In the network, different links might be using different Layer 2 technology. Some links might use wired Ethernet, and other links might use optical fiber or wireless cellular technology. At Layer 2, we figure out how to send a packet inside a local network, across the link(s) in that network, using the specific technology in that network. Then, at Layer 3, we use the ability to send packets along links as a building block to send packets anywhere in the Internet. As the packet hops across different networks, it may be transmitted across lots of different types of links.","zh_html":"在网络中，不同链路(links)可能使用不同的第二层(Layer 2)技术。有些链路可能使用有线以太网(Ethernet)，另一些可能使用光纤(optical fiber)或无线蜂窝网络(wireless cellular)技术。在第二层，我们考虑如何利用该网络的特定技术，在本地网络内部通过链路发送分组(packet)。然后，在第三层(Layer 3)，我们将沿链路发送分组的能力作为基础模块，将分组发送到互联网(Internet)的任何地方。当分组在不同网络之间跳转(hop)时，可能会穿越许多不同类型的链路。"},{"en":"In our analogy, we can see a distinction between homes and post offices. The homes are sending and receiving letters to each other. The post offices aren’t sending or receiving their own mail, but they’re helping to connect up the other homes.","zh_html":"在我们的类比中，可以看到房屋(homes)和邮局(post offices)之间的区别。房屋之间相互发送和接收信件。邮局并不发送或接收自己的信件，而是帮助连接各个房屋。"},{"en":"In the Internet, end hosts are machines (e.g. servers, laptops, phones) communicating over the Internet. By contrast, a switch (also called a router) is a machine that isn’t sending or receiving its own data, but exists to help the end hosts communicate with each other. Examples of switches are the router in your home, or larger routers deployed by Internet service providers (e.g. AT&amp;T).","zh_html":"在互联网(Internet)中，<strong>终端主机(end hosts)</strong>是通过互联网通信的机器，例如服务器(servers)、笔记本电脑(laptops)、手机(phones)。相比之下，<strong>交换机(switch)</strong>（也称为<strong>路由器(router)</strong>）是不发送或接收自己数据的机器，它的存在是为了帮助终端主机之间相互通信。交换机的例子包括你家里的路由器，或者互联网服务提供商(ISPs)部署的大型路由器（如 AT&amp;T）。"},{"en":"In these notes, we’ll typically draw end hosts as circles, and routers as squares.","zh_html":"在本教材中，我们通常用圆圈表示终端主机(end hosts)，用方块表示路由器(routers)。"},{"en":"Layers of Abstraction","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layers-of-abstraction\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layers-of-abstraction\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 抽象层(Layers of Abstraction)"},{"en":"As we’ve built up the Internet, you might have noticed that we’ve been decomposing the problem into smaller tasks and abstractions.","zh_html":"在构建互联网(Internet)的过程中，你可能已经注意到我们一直在将问题分解为更小的任务和抽象(abstractions)。"},{"en":"“Modularity based on abstraction is the way things are done.” (Barbara Liskov, Turing lecture). This is how we build and maintain large computer systems. Modularity is especially important for the Internet because the Internet consists of many devices (hosts, routers) and many real-world entities (users, tech companies, ISPs), and having everybody agree on the breakdown of tasks is what enables the Internet to work at scale.","zh_html":"“基于抽象的模块化是做事的方式。”（Barbara Liskov，图灵奖演讲）。这就是我们构建和维护大型计算机系统的方式。模块化(modularity)对互联网(Internet)尤为重要，因为互联网由大量设备（主机(hosts)、路由器(routers)）和许多现实实体（用户、科技公司、ISPs）组成，让所有人都认同任务的分解方式，是互联网能够大规模工作的关键。"},{"en":"One major advantage of this layered, network-of-network approaches is, each network can make its own decisions about how to move data. For example, as your packet travels across Internet hops, some links might use wireless technology, and other links might use wired technology. The lower-layer protocols can change across different hops, and the Layer 3 protocol still works.","zh_html":"这种分层(layered)、网络之网络(network-of-network)方法的一大优势是：每个网络可以自行决定如何移动数据。例如，当你的分组(packet)在互联网(Internet)上逐跳(hop)传输时，某些链路(links)可能使用无线技术，另一些可能使用有线技术。底层协议(protocols)在不同跳之间可以变化，而第三层(Layer 3)协议仍然正常工作。"},{"en":"Layering also allows innovation to proceed in parallel. Different communities (e.g. hardware chip designers, software developers) can pursue innovation at different layers.","zh_html":"分层(layering)还允许创新并行进行。不同的群体，例如硬件芯片设计者和软件开发人员，可以在不同层(layers)上各自推进创新。"},{"en":"Layer 3: Best-Effort Service Model","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layer-3-best-effort-service-model\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layer-3-best-effort-service-model\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 第三层：尽力而为服务模型(Best-Effort Service Model)"},{"en":"It seems like we’ve built something that can send data anywhere in the world, so why not stop here? There are two issues with Layer 3 that we still need to solve.","zh_html":"我们似乎已经构建了一个可以将数据发送到世界任何地方的系统，那么为什么不就此打住呢？第三层(Layer 3)还有两个问题需要解决。"},{"en":"The first issue involves the Layer 3 service model. If you use the Layer 3 infrastructure to send messages across the Internet, what service model does the network offer to you as a user? You can think of the service model as a contract between the network and users, describing what the network does and doesn’t support.","zh_html":"第一个问题涉及第三层(Layer 3)的服务模型(service model)。如果你使用第三层的基础设施通过互联网(Internet)发送消息，网络向你提供的服务模型是什么？你可以把服务模型理解为网络与用户之间的契约(contract)，描述网络支持和不支持的功能。"},{"en":"Examples of practical service models might include: The network guarantees that data is delivered. Or, the network guarantees that data is delivered within some time limit. Or, the network doesn’t guarantee delivery, but promises to report an error on failure.","zh_html":"实际的服务模型可能包括：网络保证数据送达；或者网络保证在某个时间限制内送达；或者网络不保证送达，但承诺在失败时报告错误。"},{"en":"The designers of the Internet didn’t support any of those models. Instead, the Internet only supports best effort delivery of data. If you send data over Layer 3, the Internet will try its best to deliver it, but there is no guarantee that the data will be delivered. The Internet also won’t tell you whether or not the delivery succeeded.","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)的设计者们没有选择上述任何一种模型。相反，互联网只支持<strong>尽力而为(best effort)</strong>的数据交付。如果你通过第三层(Layer 3)发送数据，互联网会尽力尝试送达，但并不保证数据一定能送达。互联网也不会告诉你是否送达成功。"},{"en":"Why did the designers choose such a weak service model? One major reason is, it is much easier to build networks that satisfy these weaker demands.","zh_html":"为什么设计者们选择了这样一个弱服务模型？一个主要原因是：满足这些较弱需求的网络更容易构建。"},{"en":"Layer 3: Packets Abstraction","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layer-3-packets-abstraction\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layer-3-packets-abstraction\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 第三层：分组抽象(Packets Abstraction)"},{"en":"So far, up to Layer 3, we’ve been thinking about sending each message through the Internet independently. More formally, the primary unit of data transfer at Layer 3 is a packet, which is some small chunk of bytes that travel through the Internet, bouncing between routers, as a single unit.","zh_html":"到目前为止，到第三层(Layer 3)为止，我们一直认为每条消息(message)是独立地通过互联网(Internet)发送的。更正式地说，第三层数据传输的基本单位是<strong>分组(packet)</strong>，它是一小段字节(bytes)，作为一个整体在互联网中传输并在路由器(routers)之间跳转。"},{"en":"The second issue at Layer 3 is: Packets are limited in size. If the application has some large data to send (e.g. a video), we need to somehow split up that data into packets, and send each packet through the network independently.","zh_html":"第三层(Layer 3)的第二个问题是：分组(packets)的大小是有限的。如果应用(application)要发送较大的数据（例如视频），我们需要以某种方式将该数据拆分为多个分组，然后通过网络独立地发送每个分组。"},{"en":"With this packet abstraction, we can now look at the life of a packet as it travels across the network. The sender breaks up data into individual packets. The packet travels along a link and arrives at a switch. The switch forwards the packet either to the destination, or to another switch that’s closer to the destination. The packet hops between one or more switches, each one forwarding the packet closer, until it eventually reaches its destination. Note that because of the best-effort model, any of the switches might drop the packet, and there’s no guarantee the packet actually reaches the destination.","zh_html":"有了分组(packet)抽象，我们现在可以观察一个分组在网络中的一生。发送方将数据拆分成一个个分组。分组沿链路(link)传输并到达交换机(switch)。交换机将分组转发给目的地，或转发给另一个更接近目的地的交换机。分组在一个或多个交换机之间跳转(hop)，每一跳都使分组更接近目的地，直到最终到达。注意，由于尽力而为(best-effort)模型，任何交换机都可能丢弃分组，因此不能保证分组一定到达目的地。"},{"en":"Layer 4: Transport","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layer-4-transport\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layer-4-transport\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 第四层：传输层(Transport)"},{"en":"We’ve identified two issues at Layer 3 so far. Large data has to be split into packets, and IP is only best-effort.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们已经发现了第三层(Layer 3)的两个问题：大数据必须拆分为分组(packets)，而 IP 只是尽力而为(best-effort)的。"},{"en":"To solve both of these problems, we’ll introduce a new layer, the transport layer. This layer uses Layer 3 as a building block, and implements an additional protocol for re-sending lost packets, splitting data into packets, and reordering packets that arrive out-of-order (among other features).","zh_html":"为了解决这两个问题，我们将引入一个新层——<strong>传输层(transport layer)</strong>。该层以第三层(Layer 3)为基础模块，提供额外的协议(protocol)功能，包括重发丢失的分组、将数据拆分为分组，以及对乱序到达的分组进行重排序等。"},{"en":"The transport layer protocol allows us to stop thinking in terms of packets, and start thinking in terms of flows, streams of packets that are exchanged between two endpoints.","zh_html":"传输层协议(transport layer protocol)让我们不再从分组(packets)的角度思考，而是开始从<strong>流(flows)</strong>的角度思考——流是在两个端点之间交换的分组流。"},{"en":"Layer 7: Application","zh_html":"<a href=\"#layer-7-application\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"layer-7-application\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 第七层：应用层(Application)"},{"en":"The application layer being built on top of the Internet is a powerful design choice. If, at lower layers, we built infrastructure for specifically transferring videos between end hosts, then email clients would have to build their own separate infrastructure for transferring emails. The Internet’s design allows it to be a general-purpose communication network for any type of application data.","zh_html":"将应用层(application layer)构建在互联网(Internet)之上是一个强大的设计选择。如果在较低层，我们专门构建了用于在终端主机(end hosts)之间传输视频的基础设施，那么电子邮件客户端就必须构建自己的独立基础设施来传输电子邮件。互联网的设计使其成为一个通用(general-purpose)通信网络，可以承载任何类型的应用数据。"},{"en":"In this class, we’ll focus more on the infrastructure supporting the application layer (e.g. the mailman, the post offices) and less on the applications themselves (e.g. the contents of the mail). We’ll see some common application protocols near the end of the class, though.","zh_html":"本课程将更关注支撑应用层(application layer)的基础设施，例如邮递员和邮局，而不是应用本身，例如信件内容。不过，我们会在课程接近尾声时了解一些常见的应用协议(application protocols)。"},{"en":"Now that we’ve seen all the layers, notice that each layer relies on services from the layer directly below, and provides services to the layer directly above. For example, someone writing a Layer 7 (application) protocol can assume that they have reliable data delivery from Layer 4. They don’t have to worry about individual packets being lost, since that’s what Layer 4 already dealt with.","zh_html":"现在我们已经了解了所有各层，请注意每一层都依赖其正下方的层提供的服务，并向其正上方的层提供服务。例如，编写第七层(Layer 7, 应用层)协议(protocol)的人可以假设他们已经从第四层(Layer 4)获得了可靠的数据交付。他们不需要担心单个分组(packets)丢失的问题，因为第四层已经处理了这个问题。"},{"en":"Two layers interact directly through the interface between them. There’s no practical way to skip layers and build Layer 7 on top of Layer 3, for example.","zh_html":"相邻两层通过它们之间的接口(interface)直接交互。实际上无法跳过某一层，例如在第三层(Layer 3)之上直接构建第七层(Layer 7)。"},{"en":"Note: You might have noticed we skipped Layers 5 and 6. In the 1970s, when the layers were first standardized, the designers thought that these layers were needed, but they’re obsolete in the modern Internet. If you’re curious, the session layer (5) was supposed to assemble different flows into a session (e.g. loading various images and ads to form a webpage), and the presentation layer (6) was supposed to help the user visualize the data. Today, the functionality of these layers is mostly implemented in Layer 7.","zh_html":"注意：你可能已经注意到我们跳过了第五层和第六层(Layers 5 and 6)。在 1970 年代首次标准化各层时，设计者们认为这些层是必要的，但在现代互联网(Internet)中它们已经过时了。如果你好奇的话：会话层(session layer, Layer 5)原本用于将不同流(flow)组合成一个会话(session)，例如加载各种图片和广告以形成网页；表示层(presentation layer, Layer 6)原本用于帮助用户可视化数据。如今，这些层的功能大多在第七层(Layer 7)中实现。"}]},"/intro/links.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Links","zh_html":"<a href=\"#links\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"links\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 链路(Links)"},{"en":"Properties of Links","zh_html":"<a href=\"#properties-of-links\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"properties-of-links\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 链路的性质(Properties of Links)"},{"en":"Now that we have a picture of how the layers of the Internet are built, let’s focus on how a packet is sent across a link.","zh_html":"既然我们已经了解了互联网(Internet)各层的结构，现在让我们聚焦于分组(packet)是如何通过链路(link)发送的。"},{"en":"There are three properties we can use to measure the performance of a link.","zh_html":"有三个性质(properties)可以用来衡量链路(link)的性能(performance)。"},{"en":"The bandwidth of a link tells us how many bits we can send on the link per unit time. Intuitively, this is the speed of the link. If you think of a link as a pipe carrying water, the bandwidth is the width of the pipe. A wider pipe lets us feed more water into the pipe per second. We usually measure bandwidth in bits per second (e.g. 5 Gbps = 5 billion bits per second).","zh_html":"链路的<strong>带宽(bandwidth)</strong>告诉我们每单位时间可以在该链路上发送多少比特(bits)。直观地说，这就是链路的速度。如果你把链路想象成一根输水管，带宽就是管子的宽度。更宽的管子允许我们每秒灌入更多的水。我们通常用比特每秒(bps, bits per second)来衡量带宽，例如 5 Gbps = 50 亿比特每秒。"},{"en":"The propagation delay of a link tells us how long it takes for a bit to travel along the link. In the pipe analogy, this is the length of the link. A shorter pipe means that water spends less time in the pipe before arriving at the other end. Propagation delay is measured in time (e.g. nanoseconds, milliseconds).","zh_html":"链路的<strong>传播时延(propagation delay)</strong>告诉我们一个比特(bits)在链路上传输需要多长时间。在管道类比中，这就是管子的长度。较短的管子意味着水在管中到达另一端所花的时间更少。传播时延以时间单位衡量，例如纳秒(nanoseconds)、毫秒(milliseconds)。"},{"en":"If we multiply the bandwidth and the propagation delay, we get the bandwidth-delay product (BDP). Intuitively, this is the capacity of the link, or the number of bits that exist on the link at any given instant. In the pipe analogy, if we fill up the pipe and freeze time, the capacity of the pipe is how much water is in the pipe in that instant.","zh_html":"将带宽(bandwidth)和传播时延(propagation delay)相乘，就得到<strong>带宽时延乘积(BDP, bandwidth-delay product)</strong>。直观地说，这就是链路的容量(capacity)，即任意时刻存在于链路上的比特(bits)数量。在管道类比中，如果我们灌满水管然后冻结时间，管道的容量就是那一刻管中的水量。"},{"en":"Note: You might sometimes see the term latency. In the context of a link, the latency is its propagation delay, though this word can also be used in other contexts (e.g. the latency from end host to end host, across multiple links). Latency by itself is not formally defined, and is context-dependent.","zh_html":"注意：你可能有时会看到术语<strong>延迟(latency)</strong>。在链路(link)的上下文中，延迟就是传播时延(propagation delay)，不过这个词也可以用于其他上下文，例如从终端主机(end host)到终端主机、跨多条链路的延迟。延迟本身没有正式定义，而是依赖上下文(context-dependent)的。"},{"en":"Timing Diagram","zh_html":"<a href=\"#timing-diagram\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"timing-diagram\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 时序图(Timing Diagram)"},{"en":"Suppose we have a link with bandwidth 1 Mbps = 1 million bits per second, and propagation delay of 1 ms = 0.001 seconds.","zh_html":"假设我们有一条带宽(bandwidth)为 1 Mbps = 每秒 100 万比特，传播时延(propagation delay)为 1 ms = 0.001 秒的链路(link)。"},{"en":"We want to send a 100 byte = 800 bit packet along this link. How long does it take to send this packet, from the time the first bit is sent, to the time the last bit is received?","zh_html":"我们想沿着这条链路发送一个 100 字节 = 800 比特的分组(packet)。从发送第一个比特到接收最后一个比特，总共需要多长时间？"},{"en":"To answer this question, we can draw a timing diagram. The left bar is the sender, and the right bar is the recipient. Time starts at 0 and increases as we move down the diagram.","zh_html":"为了回答这个问题，我们可以画一个时序图(timing diagram)。左侧竖条代表发送方(sender)，右侧竖条代表接收方(recipient)。时间从 0 开始，沿图向下递增。"},{"en":"Let’s focus on the first bit. We can put 1,000,000 bits on the link per second (bandwidth), so it takes 1/1,000,000 = 0.000001 seconds to put a single bit on the link. At time 0.000001 seconds, the link has a single bit on it, at the sender end.","zh_html":"让我们先关注第一个比特。我们每秒可以在链路上放入 1,000,000 个比特（带宽(bandwidth)），因此将单个比特放到链路上需要 1/1,000,000 = 0.000001 秒。在 0.000001 秒时，链路上有一个比特，位于发送方(sender)一端。"},{"en":"It then takes 0.001 seconds for this bit to travel across the link (propagation delay), so at time 0.000001 + 0.001 seconds, the very first bit arrives at the recipient.","zh_html":"然后这个比特需要 0.001 秒穿越链路（传播时延(propagation delay)），因此在 0.000001 + 0.001 秒时，第一个比特到达接收方(recipient)。"},{"en":"Now let’s think about the last bit. From before, it takes 0.000001 to put a bit on the link. We have 800 bits to send, so the last bit is placed on the link at time \\(800 \\cdot 0.000001 = 0.0008\\) seconds.","zh_html":"现在考虑最后一个比特。如前所述，将单个比特放到链路上需要 0.000001 秒。我们要发送 800 个比特，因此最后一个比特在 \\(800 \\cdot 0.000001 = 0.0008\\) 秒时被放到链路上。"},{"en":"It then takes 0.001 seconds for the last bit to travel across the link, so at time 0.0008 + 0.001 seconds, the very last bit arrives at the recipient. This is the time when we can say the packet has arrived at the recipient.","zh_html":"然后最后一个比特需要 0.001 秒穿越链路，因此在 0.0008 + 0.001 秒时，最后一个比特到达接收方(recipient)。这就是我们可以说分组(packet)已到达接收方的时间。"},{"en":"Packet Delay","zh_html":"<a href=\"#packet-delay\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"packet-delay\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 分组时延(Packet Delay)"},{"en":"More generally, the packet delay is the time it takes for an entire packet to be sent, starting from the time the first bit is put on the wire, to the time the last bit is received at the other end. This delay is the sum of the transmission delay and the propagation delay.","zh_html":"更一般地说，<strong>分组时延(packet delay)</strong>是发送整个分组(packet)所需的时间，从第一个比特被放到线缆(wire)上开始，到最后一个比特在另一端被接收为止。该时延是传输时延(transmission delay)和传播时延(propagation delay)之和。"},{"en":"The transmission delay tells us how long it takes to put the bits on the wire. In the example, this was \\(800 \\cdot (1/1{,}000{,}000)\\). In general, this is the packet size divided by the link bandwidth.","zh_html":"传输时延(transmission delay)告诉我们把比特(bits)放到线缆(wire)上需要多长时间。在例子中，这是 \\(800 \\cdot (1/1{,}000{,}000)\\)。一般来说，这是分组大小(packet size)除以链路带宽(link bandwidth)。"},{"en":"Since the transmission delay is a function of bandwidth, we can calculate packet delay in terms of the two link properties of bandwidth and propagation delay.","zh_html":"由于传输时延(transmission delay)是带宽(bandwidth)的函数，我们可以用带宽和传播时延(propagation delay)这两个链路性质来计算分组时延(packet delay)。"},{"en":"Bandwidth and Propagation Delay Tradeoffs","zh_html":"<a href=\"#bandwidth-and-propagation-delay-tradeoffs\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"bandwidth-and-propagation-delay-tradeoffs\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 带宽与传播时延的权衡(Bandwidth and Propagation Delay Tradeoffs)"},{"en":"Consider two links:","zh_html":"考虑两条链路(links)："},{"en":"Link 1 has bandwidth 10 Mbps and propagation delay 10 ms.","zh_html":"链路 1 的带宽(bandwidth)为 10 Mbps，传播时延(propagation delay)为 10 ms。"},{"en":"Link 2 has bandwidth 1 Mbps and propagation delay 1 ms.","zh_html":"链路 2 的带宽(bandwidth)为 1 Mbps，传播时延(propagation delay)为 1 ms。"},{"en":"Which link is better? It depends on the packets you’re sending.","zh_html":"哪条链路更好？这取决于你要发送的分组(packets)。"},{"en":"Suppose we wanted to send a single 10-byte packet. For both links, the time it takes to put one packet on the wire is negligible, and the propagation delay is the dominant source of delay. Link 2 has the shorter propagation delay, so it’s the better choice.","zh_html":"假设我们要发送一个 10 字节的分组(packet)。对两条链路而言，将一个分组放到线缆上的时间都可以忽略不计，传播时延(propagation delay)是主要时延来源。链路 2 的传播时延更短，因此它是更好的选择。"},{"en":"Suppose we instead wanted to send a single 10,000-byte packet. Now, the transmission delay is the dominant source of delay, and we prefer Link 1, which allows us to put the bytes on the wire faster (higher bandwidth). You could validate this intuition with formal packet delay calculations: Link 1 takes roughly 18 ms to send this packet, while Link 2 takes roughly 81 ms.","zh_html":"假设我们改为发送一个 10,000 字节的分组(packet)。现在，传输时延(transmission delay)成为主要时延来源，我们更偏好链路 1，因为它允许我们更快地将字节放到线缆上（更高的带宽(bandwidth)）。你可以通过正式的分组时延计算来验证这一直觉：链路 1 发送这个分组大约需要 18 ms，而链路 2 需要大约 81 ms。"},{"en":"For a real-world example, consider a video call. If the video quality is poor, you probably have insufficient bandwidth (and shortening propagation delay won’t help). By contrast, if there’s a delay between the time you speak and the time the other person answers, the propagation delay is probably too long (and more bandwidth won’t help).","zh_html":"举一个现实世界的例子：视频通话(video call)。如果视频质量很差，很可能是带宽(bandwidth)不足（缩短传播时延(propagation delay)也无济于事）。相反，如果你说话和对方回应之间存在明显延迟，那很可能是传播时延太长（增加带宽也没有帮助）。"},{"en":"Pipe Diagram","zh_html":"<a href=\"#pipe-diagram\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"pipe-diagram\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 管道图(Pipe Diagram)"},{"en":"So far, we’ve been drawing timing diagrams to denote when network events happen (e.g. when the recipient gets the packet).","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们一直在用时序图(timing diagrams)来表示网络事件发生的时间，例如接收方(recipient)何时收到分组(packet)。"},{"en":"Another way to view packets being sent across the network is to draw the bits on the link at a frozen moment in time. Both views convey the same information, but depending on the context, one view might be more useful than the other.","zh_html":"另一种观察分组(packets)在网络上发送的方式是：在某个冻结的时间点，绘制链路上的比特(bits)。两种视角传达的信息相同，但根据上下文，一种视角可能比另一种更有用。"},{"en":"To draw the link, we can imagine the link is a pipe (similar to the water analogy) and draw the pipe as a rectangle, where the width is the propagation delay, and the height is the bandwidth. The area of the pipe is the capacity of the link.","zh_html":"要绘制链路(link)，我们可以把链路想象成一根管子（类似于水的类比），然后将管子画成一个矩形：宽度是传播时延(propagation delay)，高度是带宽(bandwidth)。管子的面积就是链路的容量(capacity)。"},{"en":"Suppose we want to send a 50-byte packet across the link. In the pipe view, we can show a frozen moment in time with the packet being sent along the link.","zh_html":"假设我们要通过链路发送一个 50 字节的分组(packet)。在管道视图中，我们可以展示一个冻结的时间点，显示分组正在链路上传输。"},{"en":"The packet is arranged in a rectangle, where the height of the rectangle tells us how many bytes were placed on the wire in a single time step. At every time step, the packet slides right in the pipe. Eventually, the packet starts to exit the pipe, and at each time step, one column of the rectangle exits the pipe.","zh_html":"分组被表示为一个矩形，矩形的高度告诉我们每个时间步(time step)放到线缆上的字节数。在每个时间步，分组在管道中向右滑动。最终，分组开始离开管道，每个时间步有一列矩形从管道中退出。"},{"en":"Non-obvious fact: The packet transmission delay in the timing diagram corresponds to the width of the rectangle.","zh_html":"不那么明显的事实：时序图(timing diagram)中的分组传输时延(transmission delay)对应管道图中矩形的宽度。"},{"en":"To see why, suppose we have a link that can send 5 bits per second, and we have a 20-bit packet. In the timing diagram, there are 11 seconds between the time of the first and last bit being sent.","zh_html":"为了理解原因，假设我们有一条每秒发送 5 个比特(bits)的链路(link)，要发送一个 20 比特的分组(packet)。在时序图(timing diagram)中，第一个比特和最后一个比特发送的时间间隔是 11 秒。"},{"en":"In the pipe diagram, every second, a column of 5 bits marches into the pipe. We need 4 columns to enter the pipe, which takes 4 seconds. This means the width of the packet in the pipe is 4 columns of packets = 4 seconds.","zh_html":"在管道图(pipe diagram)中，每秒有一列 5 个比特进入管道。我们需要 4 列才能全部进入管道，这需要 4 秒。这意味着分组在管道中的宽度是 4 列 = 4 秒。"},{"en":"The pipe diagram lets us view the packet transmission time on the same axis as the propagation delay, and compare the two terms.","zh_html":"管道图让我们在同一个轴上看到分组传输时间(packet transmission time)和传播时延(propagation delay)，便于比较这两项。"},{"en":"Pipe diagrams can be useful for comparing different links. Let’s look at the exact same packets traveling through three different links.","zh_html":"管道图(pipe diagrams)对于比较不同链路(links)很有用。让我们看看完全相同的分组(packets)在三条不同链路中传输的情况。"},{"en":"If we shorten the propagation delay, the pipe width gets shorter. The pipe height stays the same, and the shape of each rectangular packet is the same. (Remember, you can think of the packet height as the number of bits marching into the pipe at each time step, and the packet width as the time it takes to march all bits into the pipe.)","zh_html":"如果缩短传播时延(propagation delay)，管道宽度变窄。管道高度保持不变，每个矩形分组(packet)的形状也相同。（记住，你可以把分组高度理解为每个时间步进入管道的比特(bits)数，把分组宽度理解为将所有比特送入管道所需的时间。）"},{"en":"Other observations here: The packet width staying the same means the transmission delay didn’t change. Also, the area of the link decreased, which tells us that the link has less capacity.","zh_html":"其他观察：分组宽度保持不变意味着传输时延(transmission delay)没有改变。此外，链路的面积减小了，说明链路的容量(capacity)变小了。"},{"en":"When we increase the bandwidth, the pipe height gets taller, indicating that we can march more bits into the pipe per unit time.","zh_html":"增加带宽(bandwidth)时，管道高度变高，表示每单位时间可以往管道中送入更多比特(bits)。"},{"en":"Notice that the shape of the packets also changed. The packets are now taller, because we can march more bits into the pipe per unit time. As a result, we finish feeding the packet into the pipe much faster, so the width of the packets (transmission delay) decreases.","zh_html":"注意分组(packets)的形状也发生了变化。分组现在更高了，因为我们每单位时间可以向管道中送入更多比特(bits)。结果就是，我们可以更快地把分组送入管道，因此分组的宽度（传输时延(transmission delay)）减小了。"},{"en":"Overloaded Links","zh_html":"<a href=\"#overloaded-links\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"overloaded-links\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 过载链路(Overloaded Links)"},{"en":"Consider this picture of packets arriving at a switch. The switch needs to forward all the packets along the outgoing link. In this case, there’s no problem, because the switch has enough capacity to process every packet as it arrives.","zh_html":"考虑这幅图：分组(packets)到达交换机(switch)。交换机需要将所有分组从出站链路(outgoing link)转发出去。这种情况下没有问题，因为交换机有足够的容量(capacity)来处理每个到达的分组。"},{"en":"What about in this picture?","zh_html":"那这幅图呢？"},{"en":"In the long term, we have enough capacity to send all the outgoing packets, but at this very instant in time, we have two packets arriving simultaneously, and we can only send out one. This is called transient overload, and it’s extremely common at switches in the Internet.","zh_html":"从长期来看，我们有足够的容量(capacity)发送所有出站分组(packets)，但在这一瞬间，有两个分组同时到达，而我们一次只能发送一个。这称为<strong>瞬态过载(transient overload)</strong>，在互联网(Internet)的交换机(switches)中极其常见。"},{"en":"To cope with transient overload, the switch maintains a queue of packets. If two packets arrive simultaneously, the switch queues one of them and sends out the other one.","zh_html":"为了应对瞬态过载(transient overload)，交换机(switch)维护一个分组队列(queue)。如果两个分组同时到达，交换机将一个放入队列，发送另一个。"},{"en":"At any given time, the switch could choose to send a packet from one of the incoming links, or send a packet from the queue. This choice is determined by a packet scheduling algorithm, and there are lots of different designs that we’ll look at.","zh_html":"在任何时刻，交换机(switch)可以选择发送来自某条入站链路(incoming link)的分组，或者发送队列(queue)中的分组。这个选择由<strong>分组调度(packet scheduling)</strong>算法决定，我们将会看到许多不同的设计。"},{"en":"When there are no incoming packets, the switch can drain the queue and send out any queued packets.","zh_html":"如果没有入站分组(packets)，交换机(switch)可以清空队列(queue)，发送所有排队的分组。"},{"en":"This allows queues to help us absorb transient bursts.","zh_html":"这使得队列(queues)可以帮助我们吸收瞬态突发(transient bursts)。"},{"en":"What if the incoming links looked like this?","zh_html":"如果入站链路(incoming links)是这种情况呢？"},{"en":"Now we have persistent overload. There just isn’t enough capacity on the outgoing link to support the level of incoming traffic.","zh_html":"现在我们有了<strong>持续过载(persistent overload)</strong>。出站链路(outgoing link)根本没有足够的容量(capacity)来支撑入站流量(incoming traffic)的水平。"},{"en":"We could fill the queue up, but that still isn’t enough to support the incoming load. One way or another, the switch will drop packets.","zh_html":"我们可以填满队列(queue)，但这仍然不足以支撑入站负载(incoming load)。无论如何，交换机(switch)都会丢弃分组(packets)。"},{"en":"How do we account for persistent overload? Operators need to properly provision their links and switches. If they notice that a switch is frequently overloaded, they might decide to upgrade the link (which may require manual work).","zh_html":"如何应对持续过载(persistent overload)？运营方(operators)需要合理规划(provision)他们的链路(links)和交换机(switches)。如果他们发现某个交换机频繁过载，可能会决定升级链路（这可能需要人工操作）。"},{"en":"One possible solution to overload is to have the router tell the senders to slow down. (We’ll study this later when we look at congestion control.) Ultimately, there’s not much we can do to solve overload, though, which is why the Internet is designed to only offer best-effort service.","zh_html":"过载(overload)的一个可能解决方案是让路由器(router)通知发送方放慢速度。（我们将在拥塞控制(congestion control)章节研究这个问题。）但归根结底，我们对解决过载能做的不多，这也是为什么互联网(Internet)被设计为只提供尽力而为(best-effort)服务。"},{"en":"Now that we have a notion of queuing, we need to go back and update our packet delay formula. Now, packet delay is the sum of transmission delay, propagation delay, and queuing delay.","zh_html":"既然我们有了排队(queuing)的概念，就需要回过头来更新我们的分组时延(packet delay)公式。现在，分组时延是传输时延(transmission delay)、传播时延(propagation delay)和排队时延(queuing delay)之和。"}]},"/intro/sharing-resources.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Designing Resource Sharing","zh_html":"<a href=\"#designing-resource-sharing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"designing-resource-sharing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 资源共享设计(Designing Resource Sharing)"},{"en":"Sharing Resources: Statistical Multiplexing","zh_html":"<a href=\"#sharing-resources-statistical-multiplexing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"sharing-resources-statistical-multiplexing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 资源共享：统计复用(Statistical Multiplexing)"},{"en":"Links and switches on the Internet have finite capacity. One key design problem we need to solve is: How do we share these resources between different Internet users?","zh_html":"互联网(Internet)上的链路(links)和交换机(switches)容量(capacity)有限。我们需要解决的一个关键设计问题是：如何在不同的互联网用户之间共享这些资源？"},{"en":"Let’s formalize the problem a bit more. Recall that a flow is a stream of packets exchanged between two end hosts (e.g. a video call between you and a friend). The Internet needs to support many simultaneous flows at the same time, despite limited capacity.","zh_html":"让我们把问题再形式化一点。回忆一下，流(flow)是在两个终端主机(end hosts)之间交换的分组流，例如你和朋友之间的视频通话。互联网(Internet)需要在容量(capacity)有限的情况下同时支持许多并发的流(flows)。"},{"en":"We often say that the network resources are statistically multiplexed, which means that we’ll dynamically allocate resources to users based on their demand, instead of partitioning a fixed share of resources to users.","zh_html":"我们常说网络资源是<strong>统计复用(statistically multiplexed)</strong>的，这意味着我们会根据用户的需求动态地分配资源，而不是将固定的资源份额预先划分给用户。"},{"en":"As an analogy, consider your personal computer. It’s not the case that your computer preemptively allocates half its CPU to Firefox, and half its CPU to Zoom, and only allows each application to use its half of the CPU. Instead, your computer dynamically allocates resources to different applications depending on their needs.","zh_html":"打个比方，想象你的个人电脑。你的电脑不会预先将一半的 CPU 分配给 Firefox，另一半分配给 Zoom，且只允许每个应用使用分配给它的那一半。相反，你的计算机会根据不同应用的需求，动态地分配资源。"},{"en":"Statistical multiplexing is now everywhere in computer science. For example, in cloud computing, different companies might dynamically share resources in a datacenter.","zh_html":"统计复用(statistical multiplexing)如今在计算机科学中无处不在。例如，在云计算(cloud computing)中，不同公司可能会动态地共享数据中心(datacenter)中的资源。"},{"en":"Statistical multiplexing is a great way to efficiently share network resources, because user demand changes over time. You probably aren’t using a constant 10 Mbps of bandwidth every second, 24 hours a day. You probably have more demand while you’re awake, and less while you’re sleeping.","zh_html":"统计复用(statistical multiplexing)是高效共享网络资源的好方法，因为用户的需求随时间变化。你可能不会 24 小时每秒都使用恒定的 10 Mbps 带宽(bandwidth)。你在清醒时需求可能更高，而睡眠时需求更低。"},{"en":"The premise that makes statistical multiplexing work is: In practice, the peak of aggregate demand is much less than the aggregate of peak demands.","zh_html":"使统计复用(statistical multiplexing)有效的前提是：在实践中，总需求的峰值(peak of aggregate demand)远小于各峰值需求之和(aggregate of peak demands)。"},{"en":"Let’s unpack what this means. Suppose we have two users, A and B. We can plot each user’s demand over time.","zh_html":"让我们解释一下这意味着什么。假设有两个用户 A 和 B。我们可以绘制每个用户随时间变化的需求曲线。"},{"en":"How much capacity do we need to allocate in order to fully meet both users’ demands?","zh_html":"我们需要分配多少容量(capacity)才能完全满足两个用户的需求？"},{"en":"The bad strategy (no statistical multiplexing) is to compute the aggregate of peak demands. We find A’s peak demand and B’s peak demand, and add them together.","zh_html":"糟糕的策略（无统计复用(statistical multiplexing)）是计算各峰值需求之和(aggregate of peak demands)。我们找到 A 的峰值需求和 B 的峰值需求，然后将它们相加。"},{"en":"If we allocate this much capacity, we can definitely meet their demands. A’s peak demand is X, so we allocate X to A, and likewise, we allocate Y to B. However, this approach is wasteful, because A’s peak and B’s peak didn’t happen at the same time.","zh_html":"如果分配这么多容量(capacity)，我们肯定能满足他们的需求。A 的峰值需求是 X，所以我们给 A 分配 X；同样，给 B 分配 Y。但这种方法是浪费的，因为 A 的峰值和 B 的峰值并没有在同一时间发生。"},{"en":"The better strategy (statistical multiplexing) is to first compute the aggregate demand by graphing their combined demand over time. For example, the 10am demand in the new graph is the A’s 10am demand, plus B’s 10am demand. Then, we compute the peak of the aggregate demand.","zh_html":"更好的策略（统计复用(statistical multiplexing)）是首先绘制合并需求随时间变化的总需求曲线。例如，新图中上午 10 点的需求是 A 在上午 10 点的需求加上 B 在上午 10 点的需求。然后，我们计算总需求的峰值(peak of the aggregate demand)。"},{"en":"If we allocate this much capacity, we can no longer statically allocate a portion to each user. However, by dynamically changing the amount we allocate to each user over time, we can still successfully meet their demands, even while having less capacity.","zh_html":"如果分配这么多容量(capacity)，我们就不能再静态地为每个用户分配固定份额。然而，通过随时间动态调整分配给每个用户的资源量，即使使用更少的容量，我们仍然可以满足他们的需求。"},{"en":"The statistical multiplexing approach allows us to support the same users with less capacity (cheaper for us, more efficient use of resources). For many distributions, we can show that the peak of the aggregate is actually closer to the sum of the average demands, which is much less than the sum of the peak demands.","zh_html":"统计复用(statistical multiplexing)方法让我们用更少的容量(capacity)支持同样的用户（更便宜、更高效地使用资源）。对于许多分布，可以证明总需求的峰值实际上更接近平均需求之和(sum of average demands)，这远小于峰值需求之和(sum of peak demands)。"},{"en":"In practice, in the network, we don’t provision for the absolute worst case, when everything peaks at the same time. Instead, we share resources dynamically and hope that peaks don’t occur at the same time. Peaks could still happen at the same time, which would cause packets to be delayed or dropped (recall the link queue). Nevertheless, we made the design choice to statistically multiplex and use resources more efficiently, while dealing with the consequences (occasional simultaneous peaks).","zh_html":"在实践中，在网络中，我们不会按绝对最坏情况（所有峰值同时出现）来规划容量(capacity)。相反，我们动态共享资源，并希望峰值不会同时发生。峰值仍然可能同时出现，这会导致分组(packets)被延迟或丢弃（回想一下链路队列(link queue)）。尽管如此，我们仍然选择统计复用(statistical multiplexing)以更高效地利用资源，同时承担偶尔出现并发峰值的后果。"},{"en":"At the end of the day, statistical multiplexing is a design choice with trade-offs, and different users might make different choices. For example, financial exchanges sometimes decide to build their own dedicated networks to support peak demand, because they care more about ensuring network connectivity during peak periods, and they can afford the extra cost.","zh_html":"归根结底，统计复用(statistical multiplexing)是一个充满权衡(trade-offs)的设计选择，不同的用户可能会做出不同的决定。例如，金融交易所有时会决定构建自己的专用网络(dedicated networks)以支持峰值需求，因为他们更关心峰值期间的网络连通性，并且能够承担额外成本。"},{"en":"Sharing Resources: Circuit Switching vs. Packet Switching","zh_html":"<a href=\"#sharing-resources-circuit-switching-vs-packet-switching\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"sharing-resources-circuit-switching-vs-packet-switching\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 资源共享：电路交换 vs. 分组交换(Circuit Switching vs. Packet Switching)"},{"en":"We now know that we can use statistical multiplexing to decide how much capacity to build. Our next question is: How do we actually dynamically allocate resources between users?","zh_html":"我们现在知道可以用统计复用(statistical multiplexing)来决定建造多少容量(capacity)。接下来的问题是：我们实际上如何动态地在用户之间分配资源？"},{"en":"As an analogy, consider a popular restaurant with many customers and a limited supply of tables. There are two ways we could imagine allocating tables to customers. We could have customers make reservations, or we could seat customers first-come first-serve.","zh_html":"打个比方，想象一家热门餐厅，顾客很多而餐位有限。有两种分配餐位的方式：让顾客预约(reservations)，或者先到先服务(first-come first-serve)。"},{"en":"The two approaches to sharing resources in the network are similar. One approach is best-effort. Everybody sends their data into the network, without making any reservations, and hopes for the best. There’s no guarantee that there will be enough bandwidth to meet your demand.","zh_html":"网络中资源共享的两种方式与此类似。一种方式是<strong>尽力而为(best-effort)</strong>。每个人都将数据发送到网络中，不做任何预约(reservations)，然后期待最好的结果。不能保证有足够的带宽(bandwidth)来满足你的需求。"},{"en":"The canonical design for best-effort is called packet switching. The switch looks at each packet independently and forwards the packet closer to its destination. The switches don’t think about flows or reservations.","zh_html":"尽力而为(best-effort)的经典设计称为<strong>分组交换(packet switching)</strong>。交换机(switch)独立地查看每个分组(packet)，将其转发到更接近目的地的方向。交换机不关心流(flows)或预约(reservations)。"},{"en":"In addition to packets being independent from each other, the switches are also independent from each other. As a packet hops across switches, every switch considers the packet independently (the switches don’t coordinate).","zh_html":"除了分组(packets)之间相互独立，交换机(switches)之间也是相互独立的。当分组在交换机之间跳转(hop)时，每个交换机都独立地处理该分组（交换机之间不协调(coordinate)）。"},{"en":"The other approach is based on reservations. At the start of a flow, users explicitly request and reserve the bandwidth they need. After the data is sent, the resources can be released for others to reserve.","zh_html":"另一种方式基于<strong>预约(reservations)</strong>。在流(flow)开始时，用户显式地请求并预约(reserve)他们需要的带宽(bandwidth)。数据发送完毕后，资源可以被释放供他人预约。"},{"en":"The canonical design for reservations, explored in both research and industry, is called circuit switching.","zh_html":"在研究和工业界都探索过的预约(reservations)经典设计称为<strong>电路交换(circuit switching)</strong>。"},{"en":"At the start of a flow, the end hosts identify a path (sequence of switches and links) through the network, using some routing algorithm. (We haven’t discussed routing algorithms to find this path yet, so you can assume it happens by magic for now.)","zh_html":"在流(flow)开始时，终端主机(end hosts)使用某种路由算法(routing algorithm)在网络中确定一条路径(path)，即交换机(switches)和链路(links)的序列。（我们还没有讨论寻找路径的路由算法，所以暂时可以假设这是通过魔法实现的。）"},{"en":"Then, the source sends a special reservation request message to the destination. Along the way, every switch hears about this request as well. If every switch accepts the request, then the reservation is made, and a circuit of switches has been established between the source and destination.","zh_html":"然后，源端(source)向目的地发送一条特殊的预约请求(reservation request)消息。沿途的每个交换机(switch)都会收到这个请求。如果每个交换机都接受该请求，则预约成功，源端和目的地之间就建立了一条由交换机组成的电路(circuit)。"},{"en":"Once the reservation is confirmed by every switch, data can be sent. Eventually, when the flow ends, the source sends a teardown message to the recipient. Along the way, every switch sees this message and releases its capacity.","zh_html":"一旦所有交换机(switch)确认预约，数据就可以开始发送。最终，当流(flow)结束时，源端(source)向接收方发送一条拆除(teardown)消息。沿途每个交换机都会看到这条消息并释放其容量(capacity)。"},{"en":"Note: We use the term circuit here because this idea came from the phone network, which uses this same idea to allow two people to call each other.","zh_html":"注意：我们在这里使用术语电路(circuit)，是因为这个想法来自电话网络(phone network)，它使用相同的思路让两个人互相通话。"},{"en":"Remember, both circuit switching and packet switching are embodying statistical multiplexing. The main difference is the granularity at which we’re allocating resources: per-flow with reservations, or per-packet with best-effort. Even in circuit switching, we’re dynamically allocating resources based on reservations. We are not preemptively reserving for all flows that might ever exist.","zh_html":"请记住，电路交换(circuit switching)和分组交换(packet switching)都体现了统计复用(statistical multiplexing)。主要区别在于资源分配的粒度：是基于预约(reservations)的按流(per-flow)分配，还是基于尽力而为(best-effort)的按分组(per-packet)分配。即使在电路交换中，我们也是基于预约动态分配资源，而不是为所有可能存在的流预先保留资源。"},{"en":"Circuit Switching vs. Packet Switching Trade-offs","zh_html":"<a href=\"#circuit-switching-vs-packet-switching-trade-offs\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"circuit-switching-vs-packet-switching-trade-offs\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 电路交换 vs. 分组交换的权衡(Circuit Switching vs. Packet Switching Trade-offs)"},{"en":"We now have two approaches to sharing resources on the Internet. Which is better? It depends on the criteria we’re using to evaluate each approach.","zh_html":"我们现在有两种在互联网(Internet)上共享资源的方式。哪种更好？这取决于我们用来评估每种方式的标准(criteria)。"},{"en":"There are four dimensions we can use to compare the two approaches.","zh_html":"我们可以从四个维度(dimensions)来比较这两种方式。"},{"en":"Is this a good abstraction (or API) for the network to offer to an application developer?","zh_html":"这是网络向应用开发者提供的好的抽象(abstraction)或 API 吗？"},{"en":"Circuit switching offers a more useful abstraction to developers, because there’s a guarantee of reserved bandwidth. This gives the developer more predictable and understandable behavior (assuming all goes well). As an analogy, consider reserving a machine in the cloud to run some task. It’s easier for the developer to reason about performance if they know the specs of the machine they’re getting. If the developer had no idea what machine they were using, the task could still run, but the performance is less predictable.","zh_html":"电路交换(circuit switching)为开发者提供了更有用的抽象，因为它保证了预约的带宽(bandwidth)。这给了开发者更可预测(predictable)和更易理解的行为（假设一切顺利）。打个比方，想象在云中预约一台机器来运行某个任务。如果开发者知道他们得到的机器规格(specs)，就更容易推理其性能(performance)。如果开发者完全不知道他们在用什么机器，任务可能仍然可以运行，但性能就较难预测了。"},{"en":"Circuit switching is also a useful abstraction if you’re a network operator who has to distribute resources to users. You know exactly how much bandwidth each user is requesting, and you can charge them the appropriate amount of money. It’s a little harder to implement an intuitive business model if there are no guarantees about what you’re offering to a client.","zh_html":"如果你是需要向用户分配资源的网络运营者(network operator)，电路交换(circuit switching)也是一个有用的抽象。你确切地知道每个用户请求了多少带宽(bandwidth)，并可以向他们收取相应费用。如果对你向客户提供的东西没有任何保证，实现一个直观的商业模式(business model)就会困难一些。"},{"en":"Is the approach efficient at scale? Does the approach use all the available bandwidth on the network, or is some bandwidth wasted?","zh_html":"这种方案在大规模下是否高效？它能充分利用网络中所有可用的带宽(bandwidth)，还是会浪费一些带宽？"},{"en":"Packet switching is typically more efficient. Exactly how much better depends on the burstiness of the traffic sources.","zh_html":"分组交换(packet switching)通常更高效。具体好多少取决于流量源(traffic sources)的突发性(burstiness)。"},{"en":"If each sender sends data at a constant rate throughout time, then both circuit switching and packet switching makes full use of the capacity.","zh_html":"如果每个发送方都以恒定速率(constant rate)发送数据，那么电路交换(circuit switching)和分组交换(packet switching)都能充分利用容量(capacity)。"},{"en":"By contrast, if each sender’s rate varies over time, then packet switching gives us a better use of bandwidth.","zh_html":"相比之下，如果每个发送方的速率随时间变化，那么分组交换(packet switching)能更好地利用带宽(bandwidth)。"},{"en":"Here’s an example of demand varying over time. With reservations, the three flows must reserve 12, 11, and 13 Mbps. One of the reservations will be rejected, since we can only distribute 30 Mbps.","zh_html":"这是一个需求随时间变化的例子。使用预约(reservations)，三个流(flows)必须预约 12、11 和 13 Mbps。其中一个预约将被拒绝，因为我们只能分配 30 Mbps。"},{"en":"This approach is wasting bandwidth in two different ways. The flow reserving 12 Mbps is not actually using its bandwidth for most of its time. Also, if the 12 Mbps and 11 Mbps flows get reservations, we have 7 Mbps left over that isn’t being reserved by anybody.","zh_html":"这种方法在两个方面浪费了带宽(bandwidth)。预约 12 Mbps 的流(flow)在大部分时间里实际上并没有使用其带宽。此外，如果 12 Mbps 和 11 Mbps 的流获得了预约，我们还有 7 Mbps 没有被任何人预约。"},{"en":"By contrast, in the packet switching approach, where we just send packets as they arrive, the total amount of bandwidth being used at any time never exceeds 30 Mbps. We can support every flow with the bandwidth we have.","zh_html":"相比之下，在分组交换(packet switching)方案中，我们只是按到达顺序发送分组(packets)，任何时候使用的总带宽(bandwidth)都不会超过 30 Mbps。我们可以用现有的带宽支持所有流(flows)。"},{"en":"Formally, the burstiness of a flow is defined by the ratio between its peak rate and its average rate. There’s no clear threshold for when something is smooth or bursty (they’re more descriptive terms).","zh_html":"形式化地说，流(flow)的突发性(burstiness)由其峰值速率(peak rate)与平均速率(average rate)之比定义。对于何时算平滑(smooth)或突发(bursty)，没有明确的阈值——它们更多是描述性术语。"},{"en":"Voice calls usually have smoother ratios like 3:1, while web browsing usually has burstier ratios like 100:1. (Voice calls having a smooth ratio is also why the phone network used reservations!)","zh_html":"语音通话(voice calls)通常有较平滑的比率，例如 3:1；而网页浏览(web browsing)通常有更突发的比率，例如 100:1。（语音通话的平滑比率也正是电话网络使用预约(reservations)的原因！）"},{"en":"Another reason why packet switching is more efficient is: Circuit switching spends additional time setting up and tearing down a circuit. This is especially inefficient for very short flows (e.g. downloading a tiny file).","zh_html":"分组交换(packet switching)更高效的另一个原因是：电路交换(circuit switching)在建立(setting up)和拆除(tearing down)电路时需要额外时间。这对于非常短的流(flows)，例如下载一个小文件，尤其低效。"},{"en":"How well does each approach handle failure at scale?","zh_html":"每种方法在大规模下处理故障(failure)的能力如何？"},{"en":"Packet switching is better at handling failure at scale. If a router fails, we can just send packets along a different path in the network. (We haven’t discussed how yet, but it turns out routing algorithms are good at adjusting to failure.) The end host doesn’t have to do anything different.","zh_html":"分组交换(packet switching)在大规模故障处理方面更好。如果一个路由器(router)发生故障，我们可以直接将分组(packets)沿网络中的另一条路径发送。（我们还没有讨论具体如何实现，但事实证明路由算法(routing algorithms)非常擅长适应故障。）终端主机(end host)不需要做任何额外的操作。"},{"en":"By contrast, in circuit switching, if a router along the path fails, the network still has to find a new path, but there’s more for the end host to do. The host has to somehow detect failure, and it has to resend a reservation request. It also has to free up the reservation along the old path somehow. What if the new reservation request is rejected?","zh_html":"相比之下，在电路交换(circuit switching)中，如果路径上的一个路由器(router)发生故障，网络仍然需要找到新的路径，但终端主机(end host)要做的事情就多得多。主机必须以某种方式检测故障，并重新发送预约请求(reservation request)。它还必须以某种方式释放旧路径上的预约。如果新的预约请求被拒绝怎么办？"},{"en":"This failure mode scales poorly. If a single router goes down, but millions of flows were using that router, then millions of reservation requests have to be simultaneously re-established.","zh_html":"这种故障模式(failure mode)的扩展性很差。如果单个路由器(router)宕机，而有数百万个流(flows)正在使用该路由器，那么数百万个预约请求必须同时重新建立。"},{"en":"We won’t solve these problems in detail, but hopefully you’re getting a sense that handling failures in circuit switching is a pretty hard problem.","zh_html":"我们不会详细解决这些问题，但希望你已经开始感受到：在电路交换(circuit switching)中处理故障是一个相当困难的问题。"},{"en":"How complex is it to implement each approach at scale?","zh_html":"每种方法在大规模下实现的复杂度如何？"},{"en":"If you actually tried to design circuit switching, a lot of additional design questions start to make the protocol really complicated, really quickly.","zh_html":"如果你真的尝试设计电路交换(circuit switching)，大量额外的设计问题会很快使协议(protocol)变得非常复杂。"},{"en":"How do the routers know that the reservation was successful? When 2 sees the request and agrees, how does it know that 3 and 4 also agreed? (Possible approach: We send a confirmation back in the other direction, indicating that the reservation is confirmed.)","zh_html":"路由器(routers)如何知道预约(reservation)成功了？当 2 看到请求并同意后，它如何知道 3 和 4 也同意了？（可能的方案：我们沿另一方向发回一条确认(confirmation)，表示预约已确认。）"},{"en":"What if the reservation request is lost along the way? 1 and 2 agree, but the request packet is dropped before it reaches 3 and 4. (Possible approach: Set a timer, and if the reservation isn’t confirmed in time, delete the reservation. Now the end host has to try again.)","zh_html":"如果预约请求(reservation request)在传输途中丢失了怎么办？1 和 2 同意了，但请求分组(packet)在到达 3 和 4 之前被丢弃了。（可能的方案：设置一个计时器(timer)，如果预约没有及时得到确认，就删除该预约。然后终端主机(end host)必须重试。）"},{"en":"What if the request is sent and everybody agrees, but the confirmation on the way back is dropped? 4 and 3 see the confirmation, but the confirmation packet is dropped before it reaches 2 and 1.","zh_html":"如果请求发送后所有人同意，但返回途中的确认(confirmation)被丢弃了怎么办？4 和 3 看到了确认，但确认分组(packet)在到达 2 和 1 之前被丢弃了。"},{"en":"What if the reservation is declined? Should the end host try again and request less? Should the end host wait a bit and try again with the same request? Should the router say in the rejection, “I can’t do 10 Mbps, but I can give you 8 Mbps?”","zh_html":"如果预约(reservation)被拒绝怎么办？终端主机(end host)应该重新尝试并请求更少的资源吗？还是应该稍等片刻，用同样的请求再试一次？路由器(router)应该在拒绝消息中说“我无法提供 10 Mbps，但我可以给你 8 Mbps”吗？"},{"en":"We won’t solve every design problem, but hopefully you’re noticing that circuit switching is harder to implement than it first seemed.","zh_html":"我们不会解决所有设计问题，但希望你注意到电路交换(circuit switching)实现起来比最初看起来要困难得多。"},{"en":"The fundamental problem that makes circuit switching complicated is state consensus. All the routers have to keep track of extra state, and they all have to agree on what that state is.","zh_html":"使电路交换(circuit switching)复杂的根本问题是状态共识(state consensus)。所有路由器(routers)都必须跟踪额外的状态(state)，并且它们必须就该状态是什么达成一致。"},{"en":"You might have heard of the Paxos protocols, which are extremely complicated protocols for getting multiple processors to agree on state. In practice, people to run these algorithms on a group of 4-5 servers. With circuit switching, we’re basically asking the Internet to run that on Internet scale, with millions of routers and flows.","zh_html":"你可能听说过 Paxos 协议(protocols)，这是一套极其复杂的协议，用于让多个处理器在状态(state)上达成一致。在实践中，人们通常在一组 4-5 台服务器上运行这些算法。而电路交换(circuit switching)本质上是在要求互联网(Internet)在互联网规模上运行这样的系统——面对数百万路由器(routers)和流(flows)。"},{"en":"In summary: Circuit switching gives the application better performance with reserved bandwidth. It also gives the developer more predictable behavior.","zh_html":"总结来说：电路交换(circuit switching)通过预留带宽(bandwidth)为应用(application)提供了更好的性能，也为开发者提供了更可预测(predictable)的行为。"},{"en":"However, packet switching gives us more efficient sharing of bandwidth, and avoids startup time. It also gives us easier recovery from failure, and is generally simpler to implement (less for routers to think about).","zh_html":"然而，分组交换(packet switching)让我们更高效地共享带宽(bandwidth)，避免了启动时间(startup time)，更容易从故障(failure)中恢复，并且通常实现更简单（路由器(routers)需要考虑的事情更少）。"},{"en":"Circuit Switching vs. Packet Switching In Practice","zh_html":"<a href=\"#circuit-switching-vs-packet-switching-in-practice\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"circuit-switching-vs-packet-switching-in-practice\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 电路交换 vs. 分组交换：实际应用"},{"en":"In the modern Internet, packet switching is the default approach.","zh_html":"在现代互联网(Internet)中，分组交换(packet switching)是默认方案。"},{"en":"There are limited cases where circuit switching is used. For example, RSVP (Resource Reservation Protocol) can be used within a small local network, to allow routers (not end hosts) to reserve bandwidth between themselves.","zh_html":"在有限的场景中仍然使用电路交换(circuit switching)。例如，RSVP(Resource Reservation Protocol, 资源预约协议)可以在小型本地网络中使用，允许路由器(routers, 而非终端主机)之间预约带宽(bandwidth)。"},{"en":"Another use of circuit switching in the modern Internet is dedicated circuits (e.g. MPLS circuits, leased lines). As a company, you can specifically buy some Internet bandwidth (possibly including physical infrastructure) dedicated to your business. This is very expensive compared to a standard Internet connection.","zh_html":"电路交换(circuit switching)在现代互联网(Internet)中的另一个用途是专用电路(dedicated circuits)，例如 MPLS 电路、租用线路(leased lines)。作为一家公司，你可以专门购买一些互联网带宽(bandwidth)（可能包括物理基础设施），专用于你的业务。这比标准互联网连接昂贵得多。"},{"en":"Dedicated circuits are deployed at less ambitious scales than hypothetical full-Internet circuit switching. Someone usually manually sets up the reservation. The reservation is long-lived (e.g. years). The reservation is at the granularity of companies, not individual flows.","zh_html":"专用电路(dedicated circuits)在规模上比假想的全互联网电路交换小得多。通常由人工手动设置预约(reservation)。预约是长期有效的（例如按年计）。预约的粒度是公司级别，而不是单个流(flow)级别。"},{"en":"Brief history: When the Internet was first designed in the 1970s-1980s as a smaller-scale, government-funded research project, it was packet switched.","zh_html":"简要历史：互联网(Internet)最初在 1970-1980 年代作为一个小规模、政府资助的研究项目被设计出来时，就是分组交换(packet switched)的。"},{"en":"In the 1990s, when the government stopped funding the Internet and control passed over to commercial enterprises, research and industry thought we would need to change to circuit switching. The designers predicted that voice and live TV would be the main heavy-duty uses of the Internet. Both of these applications have smooth bandwidth demand, well-suited for circuit switching. Also, because ISPs had to make money off the new commercialized Internet, they thought that circuit switching would offer a more intuitive business model.","zh_html":"在 1990 年代，当政府停止资助互联网(Internet)并将控制权移交给商业企业时，研究界和工业界认为我们需要转向电路交换(circuit switching)。设计者预测语音通话和电视直播将是互联网的主要重度用途。这两种应用都有平滑的带宽(bandwidth)需求，非常适合电路交换。同时，由于 ISP 需要从新的商业化互联网中盈利，他们认为电路交换将提供更直观的商业模式(business model)。"},{"en":"There was a lot of work in research and standards bodies to implement circuit switching, but ultimately, this was a failed vision, for many of the reasons we discussed. Also, the main applications driving Internet growth were email and the web, not voice calls and TV, which is another reason why circuit switching vision didn’t work out.","zh_html":"研究机构和标准组织做了大量工作来实现电路交换(circuit switching)，但最终，这是一个失败的愿景——原因有很多，我们已讨论过其中许多。此外，推动互联网(Internet)增长的主要应用是电子邮件(email)和万维网(web)，而不是语音通话和电视，这也是电路交换愿景未能实现的另一个原因。"},{"en":"An interesting consequence of these design choices is, users and developers adapted to the realities of packet switching. If you watch a video and the connection is poor, you’re used to the application adapting and the video quality decreasing. (Contrast with broadcast TV, which wouldn’t do this.) This is a lesson in how technology can transform user behavior!","zh_html":"这些设计选择的一个有趣结果是，用户和开发者已经适应了分组交换(packet switching)的现实。如果你在看视频时网络连接不佳，你已经习惯了应用(application)自适应地降低视频质量。（与不会这样做的广播电视(broadcast TV)形成对比。）这是技术如何改变用户行为的一课！"}]},"/routing/addressing.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Addressing","zh_html":"寻址(addressing)"},{"en":"Scaling Routing","zh_html":"扩展路由(routing)"},{"en":"So far, our forwarding table has one entry per destination. This won’t scale to the entire Internet.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们的转发表(forwarding table)对每个目的地(destination)都有一个条目(entry)。这种做法无法扩展到整个互联网(Internet)。"},{"en":"If we ran distance-vector on the entire Internet, we’d have to send an announcement for every host on the Internet. If we ran link-state on the entire Internet, every router would have to know the full Internet network graph. In both cases, if any host joins or leaves the Internet, we’d have to re-do computations to converge on a new routing state.","zh_html":"如果在整个互联网(Internet)上运行距离向量(distance-vector)，我们就必须为互联网上的每台主机(host)发送通告(announcement)。如果在整个互联网运行链路状态(link-state)，每台路由器(router)都必须知道完整的互联网网络图(network graph)。在这两种情况下，只要有任何主机加入或离开互联网，我们都必须重新计算，才能收敛(converge)到新的路由状态(routing state)。"},{"en":"The trick to scale routing is how we address hosts. So far, we’ve called every host and router by some name (e.g. R1, R2, A, B), but in practice, we’ll use a smarter addressing scheme.","zh_html":"扩展路由(routing)的关键在于如何给主机(host)分配地址(address)。到目前为止，我们一直用某个名字称呼每台主机和路由器(router)，例如 R1、R2、A、B；但在实践中，我们会使用更智能的寻址方案(addressing scheme)。"},{"en":"IP Addressing","zh_html":"IP 寻址(IP addressing)"},{"en":"Recall that in our postal service analogy, we had different addressing schemes for different contexts. The mailman used a street address like 2551 Hearst Ave. The secretary inside the building used a room number like 413 Soda Hall. Addresses are assigned in some structured way. For example, all third-floor room number start with the digit 3, and all fourth-floor rooms number start with the digit 4.","zh_html":"回想邮政服务(postal service)的类比：在不同上下文中，我们会使用不同的寻址方案(addressing schemes)。邮递员使用街道地址(street address)，例如 2551 Hearst Ave；楼内秘书使用房间号(room number)，例如 413 Soda Hall。地址(addresses)会按某种结构化方式分配。例如，所有三楼房间号都以数字 3 开头，所有四楼房间号都以数字 4 开头。"},{"en":"Just like the postal system, the Internet uses different addressing schemes at each layer. In this section, we’ll focus on IP addresses, which can be used for routing at Layer 3.","zh_html":"和邮政系统一样，互联网(Internet)在每一层(layer)都会使用不同的寻址方案(addressing schemes)。本节会关注 IP 地址(IP addresses)，它们可用于第 3 层(Layer 3)的路由(routing)。"},{"en":"Every host on the network (e.g. your computer, Google’s server) is assigned an IP address. For this section, you can assume every host has a unique IP address.","zh_html":"网络中的每台主机(host)，例如你的电脑或 Google 的服务器(server)，都会被分配一个 IP 地址(IP address)。在本节中，你可以假设每台主机都有一个唯一的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"An IP address is a number that uniquely identifies a host. Just like the postal system, the number is chosen to contain some context about where the host is located.","zh_html":"<strong>IP 地址(IP address)</strong>是一个能唯一标识主机(host)的数字。和邮政系统一样，这个数字的选择会包含一些关于主机所在位置的上下文信息。"},{"en":"Note that IP addresses are not necessarily static. In the analogy, if you move to a different house, your address changes. Similarly, if your computer moves to a different location, it may be assigned a different IP address when it joins the network (and your old IP address will eventually expire).","zh_html":"注意，IP 地址(IP addresses)不一定是静态的(static)。在类比中，如果你搬到另一栋房子，你的地址(address)会改变。类似地，如果你的电脑移动到不同位置，它加入网络时可能会被分配不同的 IP 地址，而旧的 IP 地址最终会过期(expire)。"},{"en":"The length of an IP address depends on the version of IP being used. IPv4 addresses are 32 bits, and IPv6 addresses are 128 bits. The routing concepts are similar for both versions, but we’ll use IPv4 when possible, because smaller addresses are easier to read.","zh_html":"IP 地址(IP address)的长度取决于使用的 IP 版本。IPv4 地址是 32 位(bits)，IPv6 地址是 128 位。两个版本中的路由概念相似，但在可能的情况下我们会使用 IPv4，因为较短的地址更容易阅读。"},{"en":"Hierarchical Addressing","zh_html":"层次化寻址(hierarchical addressing)"},{"en":"Recall that the Internet is a network of networks. There are many local networks, and we add links between local networks to form the wider Internet. This gives us a natural hierarchy that we can use to organize our addressing scheme.","zh_html":"回想一下，互联网(Internet)是网络的网络(network of networks)。其中有许多本地网络(local networks)，我们在本地网络之间添加链路(links)，形成更大的互联网。这给了我们一种自然的层次结构(hierarchy)，可用于组织寻址方案(addressing scheme)。"},{"en":"Here’s an intuitive picture of addressing. We could assign a number to every network. Then, within network 3, we could assign host numbers 3.1, 3.2, 3.3, etc., and similar for hosts in the other networks.","zh_html":"下面是寻址(addressing)的一种直观图景。我们可以给每个网络分配一个编号。然后，在网络 3 内部，可以给主机(host)分配 3.1、3.2、3.3 等编号；其他网络中的主机也类似。"},{"en":"Now, consider the forwarding table in router R9. Before, we would have one entry for every host in network 1, and they would all have the same next hop of R6. With our hierarchical addressing, we could instead have a single entry for the entire local network, saying that all 1.* addresses (where the * represents any number) have a next hop of R6. We could also say that all 2.* addresses have a next hop of R8.","zh_html":"现在，考虑路由器(router) R9 中的转发表(forwarding table)。以前，对于网络 1 中的每台主机(host)，我们都会有一个条目(entry)，而它们的下一跳(next hop)都是 R6。使用层次化寻址(hierarchical addressing)后，我们可以为整个本地网络(local network)只保留一个条目，表示所有 1.* 地址(addresses)，其中 * 表示任意数字，其下一跳都是 R6。我们也可以表示所有 2.* 地址的下一跳都是 R8。"},{"en":"This hierarchical model, where we use wildcard matches to summarize routes, makes our forwarding tables smaller.","zh_html":"这种层次化模型(hierarchical model)使用通配符匹配(wildcard matches)来汇总路由(routes)，从而让转发表(forwarding tables)更小。"},{"en":"In addition, this model also makes our tables more stable.","zh_html":"此外，这种模型也让我们的表(table)更加稳定(stable)。"},{"en":"If the topology inside network 1 changes, we don’t need to update R9’s forwarding table (or any other tables in other networks). In practice, changes within a local network (e.g. new host joins the network) happens much more often than changes between networks (e.g. new underground cable installed), so it’s a good thing that local changes only affect local tables.","zh_html":"如果网络 1 内部的拓扑(topology)发生变化，我们不需要更新 R9 的转发表(forwarding table)，也不需要更新其他网络中的任何表。在实践中，本地网络(local network)内部的变化，例如新主机(host)加入网络，远比网络之间的变化，例如安装新的地下光缆，更常发生。因此，本地变化只影响本地表是件好事。"},{"en":"More generally, our addresses have two parts: a network ID,and a host ID. This allows inter-domain routing protocols to focus on the network ID to find routes between networks, and intra-domain routing protocols to focus on the host ID to find routes inside networks. This also makes our routing protocols more stable as the network changes. Inter-domain protocols don’t care about changes inside networks, and intra-domain protocols don’t care about changes in other networks.","zh_html":"更一般地，我们的地址(addresses)由两部分组成：网络 ID(network ID)和主机 ID(host ID)。这使域间路由协议(inter-domain routing protocols)可以专注于网络 ID，以寻找网络之间的路由(routes)；域内路由协议(intra-domain routing protocols)则可以专注于主机 ID，以寻找网络内部的路由。随着网络变化，这也让路由协议(routing protocols)更加稳定。域间协议不关心网络内部的变化，域内协议也不关心其他网络中的变化。"},{"en":"Note that the forwarding table in R9 still needs entries for each individual host inside its own network (i.e. network 2).","zh_html":"注意，R9 的转发表(forwarding table)仍然需要为其自身网络，也就是网络 2，内部的每台单独主机(host)保留条目(entries)。"},{"en":"Similarly, R4, an internal router with no connections to other networks, needs both entries for individual hosts inside network 3, and aggregated entries for other networks (e.g. 2.* has a next hop of R9). The scale of a forwarding table depends on the number of internal hosts in the same network, plus the number of external networks.","zh_html":"类似地，R4 是一台不连接其他网络的内部路由器(internal router)，它既需要网络 3 内部各个主机(host)的条目(entries)，也需要其他网络的聚合条目(aggregated entries)，例如 2.* 的下一跳(next hop)是 R9。转发表(forwarding table)的规模取决于同一网络中的内部主机数量，加上外部网络(external networks)的数量。"},{"en":"Default Routes","zh_html":"默认路由(default routes)"},{"en":"We now know that our entries can represent entire ranges of addresses, instead of always representing a single address. We can extend this idea even further to improve scale.","zh_html":"现在我们知道，条目(entries)可以表示整个地址范围(address ranges)，而不必总是表示单个地址(address)。我们可以进一步扩展这个想法，以提升扩展性(scale)。"},{"en":"Consider R4. It has an entry for every external network (1.*, 3.*, and 4.*), all with the same next hop of R9. We could aggregate every external network into a single entry. We’ll still have entries for every internal host (2.1, 2.2, etc.), but at the end, we’ll say: For all other hosts not in the forwarding table, the next hop is R9.","zh_html":"考虑 R4。它为每个外部网络(external network)，也就是 1.*、3.* 和 4.*，都有一个条目(entry)，并且它们的下一跳(next hop)都是 R9。我们可以把所有外部网络聚合(aggregate)成一个条目。对于每台内部主机(internal host)，例如 2.1、2.2 等，我们仍然会保留条目；但最后我们会说：对于转发表(forwarding table)中没有列出的所有其他主机，下一跳是 R9。"},{"en":"We can use more aggressive aggregation at R2. Again, all external networks have a next hop of R3. But, 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 2.6, and 2.7 also have a next hop of R3. Therefore, the forwarding table only needs static entries for 2.4 and 2.5. Then, we can say, for all other hosts not in the forwarding table (including some internal and some external hosts), the next hop is R3.","zh_html":"在 R2 上，我们可以使用更激进的聚合(aggregation)。同样，所有外部网络(external networks)的下一跳(next hop)都是 R3。但是，2.1、2.2、2.3、2.6 和 2.7 的下一跳也是 R3。因此，转发表(forwarding table)只需要为 2.4 和 2.5 保留静态条目(static entries)。然后我们可以说，对于转发表中没有列出的所有其他主机(hosts)，包括一些内部主机和一些外部主机，下一跳都是 R3。"},{"en":"To represent all hosts not in the table, we can use a wildcard . that matches everything. When forwarding toward a given destination, the router first checks specific hosts (e.g. 3.1) or ranges (e.g. 2.) for matches. If the router can’t find any matches, it will eventually match the *. wildcard. This is called the default route.","zh_html":"为了表示表(table)中没有列出的所有主机(hosts)，我们可以使用能匹配所有内容的通配符(wildcard) <em>.</em>。当向给定目的地(destination)转发时，路由器(router)会先检查具体主机，例如 3.1，或范围(range)，例如 2.<em>，是否匹配。如果路由器找不到任何匹配，最终会匹配 *.</em> 这个通配符。这称为<strong>默认路由(default route)</strong>。"},{"en":"Most hosts only have a single hard-coded default route. For example, host 2.4’s forwarding table has a single entry, saying to send everything to R2. In practice, your home computer has a single entry, saying to send everything to your home router. This is why hosts don’t need to participate in routing protocols.","zh_html":"大多数主机(hosts)只有一条硬编码(hard-coded)的默认路由(default route)。例如，主机 2.4 的转发表(forwarding table)只有一个条目(entry)，表示把所有内容都发送给 R2。在实践中，你的家用电脑只有一个条目，表示把所有内容都发送给家用路由器(home router)。这就是为什么主机不需要参与路由协议(routing protocols)。"},{"en":"Assigning Hierarchical IP Addresses: Early Internet","zh_html":"分配层次化 IP 地址：早期互联网"},{"en":"In order to get more scalable routing, we need to assign addresses in some hierarchical way. The addresses need to contain some information about their location (e.g. nearby hosts need to share some part of their address).","zh_html":"为了获得更可扩展的路由(routing)，我们需要以某种层次化方式分配地址(addresses)。地址需要包含一些关于其位置的信息，例如相邻主机(hosts)需要共享地址中的某一部分。"},{"en":"In the early Internet, IPv4 addresses had an 8-bit network ID and a 24-bit host ID, just like in our intuitive version.","zh_html":"在早期互联网(Internet)中，IPv4 地址(addresses)有 8 位(bits)网络 ID(network ID)和 24 位主机 ID(host ID)，就像我们直观版本中的设计一样。"},{"en":"For example, AT&amp;T has network ID 12, Apple has network ID 17, and the US Department of Defense has 13 different network IDs.","zh_html":"例如，AT&amp;T 的网络 ID(network ID)是 12，Apple 的网络 ID 是 17，美国国防部(US Department of Defense)拥有 13 个不同的网络 ID。"},{"en":"The 8-bit network ID means we can only assign 256 different network IDs, but in real life, there are way more than 256 organizations that might operate their own local network. Also, our 24-bit host ID means that every network gets 2\\^24 = 16,777,216 addresses. A small network (e.g. a company with 10 employees) probably doesn’t need 16 million addresses. As the Internet grew larger, a new approach to addressing was needed.","zh_html":"8 位(bits)网络 ID(network ID)意味着我们最多只能分配 256 个不同的网络 ID；但在现实中，可能运营自己本地网络(local network)的组织远远超过 256 个。另外，24 位主机 ID(host ID)意味着每个网络都会获得 2\\^24 = 16,777,216 个地址(addresses)。一个小型网络，例如只有 10 名员工的公司，可能并不需要 1600 万个地址。随着互联网(Internet)变得更大，需要一种新的寻址(addressing)方法。"},{"en":"Assigning Hierarchical IP Addresses: Classful Addressing","zh_html":"分配层次化 IP 地址：分类寻址(classful addressing)"},{"en":"The first attempt to fix this was classful addressing, which allocates different network sizes based on need. In this approach, there are 3 classes of addresses, each with a different number of bits allocated to the network ID and host ID. The first 1-3 bits identify which class is being used.","zh_html":"解决这个问题的第一次尝试是<strong>分类寻址(classful addressing)</strong>，它会根据需求分配不同大小的网络。在这种方法中，地址(addresses)分为 3 类，每一类分配给网络 ID(network ID)和主机 ID(host ID)的位数(bits)不同。最前面的 1 到 3 位用于标识正在使用哪一类(class)。"},{"en":"Class A addresses start with leading bit 0. The next 7 bits are the network ID (128 networks), and the next 24 bits are the host ID (16 million hosts).","zh_html":"A 类地址(Class A addresses)以前导位(leading bit) 0 开头。接下来的 7 位(bits)是网络 ID(network ID)，可表示 128 个网络；再接下来的 24 位是主机 ID(host ID)，可表示 1600 万台主机(hosts)。"},{"en":"Class B addresses start with leading bits 10. The next 14 bits are the network ID (16,000 networks), and the next 16 bits are the host ID (65,000 hosts).","zh_html":"B 类地址(Class B addresses)以前导位(leading bits) 10 开头。接下来的 14 位(bits)是网络 ID(network ID)，可表示 16,000 个网络；再接下来的 16 位是主机 ID(host ID)，可表示 65,000 台主机(hosts)。"},{"en":"Class C addresses start with leading bits 110. The next 21 bits are the network ID (2 million networks), and the next 8 bits are the host ID (256 hosts).","zh_html":"C 类地址(Class C addresses)以前导位(leading bits) 110 开头。接下来的 21 位(bits)是网络 ID(network ID)，可表示 200 万个网络；再接下来的 8 位是主机 ID(host ID)，可表示 256 台主机(hosts)。"},{"en":"In this approach, we can now have 2 million + 16,000 + 128 different local networks. Larger organizations with more hosts could receive a Class A network, and smaller organizations could receive a Class B or Class C network. As before, within a single network, the leading class bit(s) and network ID bits are the same, and each host gets a different host ID.","zh_html":"在这种方法中，我们现在可以拥有 200 万 + 16,000 + 128 个不同的本地网络(local networks)。拥有更多主机(hosts)的大型组织可以获得一个 A 类网络(Class A network)，较小的组织可以获得 B 类或 C 类网络。和之前一样，在单个网络内部，前导类别位(leading class bits)和网络 ID 位(network ID bits)相同，每台主机获得不同的主机 ID(host ID)。"},{"en":"One major problem with classful addressing is the size of each class. Class A (16 million hosts) is way too big for most organizations, and Class C (256 hosts) is way too small for most organizations. As a result, most networks need to be in Class B.","zh_html":"分类寻址(classful addressing)的一个主要问题是每个类别(class)的大小。A 类(Class A，1600 万台主机)对大多数组织来说太大，而 C 类(Class C，256 台主机)对大多数组织来说又太小。因此，大多数网络都需要落在 B 类(Class B)。"},{"en":"Unfortunately, there are only 16,000 Class B network IDs, and by 1994, we were running out of Class B networks. Again, a new approach to addressing was needed.","zh_html":"不幸的是，B 类网络 ID(Class B network IDs)只有 16,000 个。到 1994 年，B 类网络已经快要用完。因此，又需要一种新的寻址(addressing)方法。"},{"en":"Note: Classful addressing is now obsolete on the modern Internet.","zh_html":"注意：分类寻址(classful addressing)在现代互联网(Internet)上已经废弃(obsolete)。"},{"en":"Note: Technically, the number of hosts per network is off by 2, because the all-zeroes address and all-ones address are reserved for special purposes. For example, in Class C, there are actually 254 hosts per network, not 256.","zh_html":"注意：严格来说，每个网络中的主机(host)数量要少 2 个，因为全 0 地址(all-zeroes address)和全 1 地址(all-ones address)被保留作特殊用途。例如，在 C 类(Class C)中，每个网络实际上有 254 台主机，而不是 256 台。"},{"en":"Assigning Hierarchical IP Addresses: CIDR","zh_html":"分配层次化 IP 地址：CIDR"},{"en":"Our third approach to hierarchical addressing, and the one still used on the modern Internet, is CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing). In CIDR, we still have variable-length network IDs, but instead of only 3 different network ID lengths (Class A, B, C), we make the number of fixed bits arbitrary.","zh_html":"第三种层次化寻址(hierarchical addressing)方法，也是现代互联网(Internet)仍在使用的方法，是 <strong>CIDR</strong>(Classless Inter-Domain Routing，无类别域间路由)。在 CIDR 中，我们仍然使用可变长度(variable-length)的网络 ID(network IDs)，但不再只有 3 种不同的网络 ID 长度，即 A 类、B 类、C 类；固定比特(fixed bits)的数量可以是任意的。"},{"en":"For example, consider the tiny company with 10 employees from earlier. In classful addressing, they would get a Class C network with 256 host addresses. If they only need 10 host addresses, we could allocate fewer addresses by giving them a longer network ID.","zh_html":"例如，考虑前面那家只有 10 名员工的小公司。在分类寻址(classful addressing)中，它会得到一个包含 256 个主机地址(host addresses)的 C 类网络(Class C network)。如果它只需要 10 个主机地址，我们可以给它更长的网络 ID(network ID)，从而分配更少的地址(addresses)。"},{"en":"If we allocated a 28-bit network ID, the host ID would be 4 bits long (16 possible addresses). If we allocated a 29-bit network ID, the host ID would be 3 bits long (8 possible addresses). We can’t allocate exactly 10 addresses, but a 28-bit network ID would be sufficient for this company’s purposes. There’s a little bit of waste (6 unused addresses), but this is still way better than allocating 256 addresses.","zh_html":"如果我们分配 28 位(bits)网络 ID(network ID)，主机 ID(host ID)长度就是 4 位，可提供 16 个可能地址(addresses)。如果分配 29 位网络 ID，主机 ID 长度就是 3 位，可提供 8 个可能地址。我们无法精确分配 10 个地址，但 28 位网络 ID 已经足够满足这家公司的需求。这里会有少量浪费，即 6 个未使用地址，但仍然远好于分配 256 个地址。"},{"en":"As another example, consider an organization that needs 450 host addresses. In classful addressing, Class C (256 addresses) isn’t sufficient, so they would receive a Class B network with 65,000 host addresses, and most of the addresses would go unused. With arbitrary-length network IDs, we can assign a 23-bit network ID, which gives 9 bits for host addressing (512 addresses). This meets the organization’s needs and wastes far fewer addresses.","zh_html":"再举一个例子，考虑一个需要 450 个主机地址(host addresses)的组织。在分类寻址(classful addressing)中，C 类(Class C，256 个地址)不够用，所以它会获得一个有 65,000 个主机地址的 B 类网络(Class B network)，其中大多数地址都会闲置。使用任意长度的网络 ID(network IDs)后，我们可以分配 23 位(bits)网络 ID，从而留出 9 位用于主机寻址(host addressing)，也就是 512 个地址。这既满足组织需求，也浪费少得多的地址。"},{"en":"Multi-Layered Hierarchical Assignment","zh_html":"多层次的层次化分配(multi-layered hierarchical assignment)"},{"en":"In real life, hierarchies can be multi-layered. For example, inside a network, an organization can choose to assign specific ranges of addresses to specific sub-organizations (e.g. departments in a company or university).","zh_html":"在现实中，层次结构(hierarchies)可以是多层的。例如，在一个网络内部，组织可以选择把特定地址范围(address ranges)分配给特定下级组织(sub-organizations)，例如公司或大学里的部门。"},{"en":"In practice, we exploit real-life multi-layered organizational and geographical hierarchies to assign addresses. ICANN (Internet Corporation for Names and Numbers) is the global organization that owns all the IP addresses.","zh_html":"在实践中，我们会利用现实中的多层组织层次(organizational hierarchies)和地理层次(geographical hierarchies)来分配地址(addresses)。ICANN(Internet Corporation for Names and Numbers，互联网名称与数字地址分配机构)是拥有所有 IP 地址(IP addresses)的全球组织。"},{"en":"ICANN gives out blocks of addresses to Regional Internet Registries (RIRs) representing specific countries or continents. For example, RIPE gets all addresses for the European Union, ARIN gets North American addresses, APNIC gets Asia/Pacific addresses, LACNIC gets South American addresses, and AFRINIC gets African addresses. Example: ICANN gives ARIN all addresses starting with 1101.","zh_html":"ICANN 会把地址块(blocks of addresses)分发给代表特定国家或大洲的区域互联网注册机构(Regional Internet Registries, RIRs)。例如，RIPE 获得欧盟(European Union)的所有地址，ARIN 获得北美地址，APNIC 获得亚太地址，LACNIC 获得南美地址，AFRINIC 获得非洲地址。示例：ICANN 把所有以 1101 开头的地址分给 ARIN。"},{"en":"Each RIR then gives out portions of their ranges to large organizations (e.g. companies, universities) or ISPs. These organizations or ISPs are called Local Internet Registries. Example: ARIN controls all addresses starting with 1101, and gives AT&amp;T all addresses starting with 1101 11001.","zh_html":"随后，每个 RIR 会把自己地址范围(ranges)中的一部分分给大型组织，例如公司、大学，或 ISP。这些组织或 ISP 称为本地互联网注册机构(Local Internet Registries)。示例：ARIN 控制所有以 1101 开头的地址，并把所有以 1101 11001 开头的地址分给 AT&amp;T。"},{"en":"Finally, each local Internet registry assigns individual IPs to specific hosts. For additional hierarchy, local registries can also assign IP ranges to small organizations, and the small organizations can in turn assign individual IPs.","zh_html":"最后，每个本地互联网注册机构(local Internet registry)会把单独的 IP 地址(IPs)分配给具体主机(hosts)。为了形成更多层次，本地注册机构也可以把 IP 范围(IP ranges)分配给小型组织，而小型组织再进一步分配单独的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"At each level, the number of additional bits fixed is determined by the number of addresses to be allocated. For example, ARIN might want to give AT&amp;T 8 million addresses, and computes that fixing 9 bits results in 8 million host addresses. ARIN had 4 bits fixed already, so it fixes another 5 bits and assigns AT&amp;T all addresses starting with those 9 bits. AT&amp;T might then give the prefix 1101 11001 110100010 to give 16,000 addresses to UC Berkeley. As we allocate addresses to sub-organizations, more bits are fixed, always keeping the fixed bits from parent organizations.","zh_html":"在每一层，额外固定多少位(bits)由需要分配的地址(addresses)数量决定。例如，ARIN 可能想给 AT&amp;T 800 万个地址，并计算出固定 9 位可以得到 800 万个主机地址(host addresses)。ARIN 已经固定了 4 位，因此再固定 5 位，并把所有以这 9 位开头的地址分配给 AT&amp;T。然后，AT&amp;T 可能把前缀(prefix) 1101 11001 110100010 分配出去，以给 UC Berkeley 提供 16,000 个地址。随着我们向下级组织(sub-organizations)分配地址，会有更多位被固定，并且始终保留父级组织(parent organizations)固定的位。"},{"en":"Writing IP Addresses","zh_html":"书写 IP 地址"},{"en":"We could write IP addresses as a 32-bit sequence of 1s and 0s, or as a single big integer. In practice, for readability, we take each sequence of 8 bits and write it as an integer (between 0 and 255). For example, the IP address 00010001 00100010 10011110 00000101 can be written as 17.34.158.5. This is sometimes called a dotted quad representation.","zh_html":"我们可以把 IP 地址(IP addresses)写成由 1 和 0 组成的 32 位(bits)序列，也可以写成一个大整数。在实践中，为了可读性，我们把每连续 8 位写成一个 0 到 255 之间的整数。例如，IP 地址 00010001 00100010 10011110 00000101 可以写成 17.34.158.5。这有时称为<strong>点分四段(dotted quad)</strong>表示法。"},{"en":"So far, we’ve been writing ranges of addresses as bits (e.g. all IPs starting with 1101). To write a range of addresses, we can use slash notation. We write the fixed prefix, then we write 0s for all remaining unfixed bits, and we convert the resulting 32-bit value into an dotted quad IP address. Then, after the slash, we write the number of fixed bits.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们一直把地址范围(address ranges)写成比特(bits)形式，例如所有以 1101 开头的 IP。要书写一个地址范围，我们可以使用<strong>斜杠表示法(slash notation)</strong>。我们先写固定前缀(prefix)，再为所有剩余的未固定比特(unfixed bits)写 0，并把得到的 32 位值转换成点分四段(dotted quad)IP 地址。然后，在斜杠后写固定比特(fixed bits)的数量。"},{"en":"For example, if the prefix is 11000000, we add zeros for all the unfixed bits to get 11000000 00000000 00000000 00000000. As a 32-bit address, this is 192.0.0.0. Then, because 8 bits were fixed, we write the range as 192.0.0.0/8.","zh_html":"例如，如果前缀(prefix)是 11000000，我们为所有未固定比特(unfixed bits)补 0，得到 11000000 00000000 00000000 00000000。作为一个 32 位(bits)地址(address)，它是 192.0.0.0。然后，由于固定了 8 位，我们把这个范围写成 192.0.0.0/8。"},{"en":"To write an individual address as a range, we could write something like 192.168.1.1/32, which indicates that all 32 bits are fixed. Also, the default route . can be written as 0.0.0.0/0.","zh_html":"要把单个地址(address)写成范围(range)，可以写成类似 192.168.1.1/32 的形式，表示全部 32 位(bits)都被固定。另外，默认路由(default route) <em>.</em> 可以写成 0.0.0.0/0。"},{"en":"Slash notation can sometimes look a little confusing because we’re using arbitrary 8-bit divisions and writing numbers in decimal. For example, the 8-bit prefix 11000000 and the 12-bit prefix 11000000 0000 would be written as 192.0.0.0/8 and 192.0.0.0/12 (same IP address representing different ranges). As another example, if I owned the 4-bit prefix 1100, I could assign the 5-bit prefix 11001. As ranges, these are written as 192.0.0.0/4 and 200.0.0.0/5. At first glance, it’s not clear that the second range is actually a subset of the first one, and we’d have to write out the bits to confirm.","zh_html":"斜杠表示法(slash notation)有时看起来会有点令人困惑，因为我们使用任意的 8 位(bits)分段，并用十进制(decimal)书写数字。例如，8 位前缀(prefix) 11000000 和 12 位前缀 11000000 0000 会分别写成 192.0.0.0/8 和 192.0.0.0/12，即同一个 IP 地址表示不同范围(range)。再举一个例子，如果我拥有 4 位前缀 1100，我可以分配 5 位前缀 11001。作为范围，它们会写成 192.0.0.0/4 和 200.0.0.0/5。乍看之下，不容易看出第二个范围其实是第一个范围的子集(subset)，我们必须写出比特才能确认。"},{"en":"An alternative to the slash (e.g. /16) in the slash notation is a netmask. Just like the number after the slash, the netmask tells us how which bits are fixed. To write a netmask, we write 1s for all fixed bits and 0 for all unfixed bits, and convert the result into a dotted quad. For example, if we had the range 192.168.1.0/29, we could write 29 ones (fixed bits) and 3 zeros (unfixed bits). 11111111 11111111 11111111 11111000 as a dotted quad is 255.255.255.248. The range in netmask notation is 192.168.1.0, with netmask 255.255.255.248 (replaced the slash with a netmask).","zh_html":"斜杠表示法(slash notation)中的斜杠，例如 /16，另一种替代形式是<strong>网络掩码(netmask)</strong>。和斜杠后的数字一样，网络掩码告诉我们哪些位(bits)是固定的。要书写网络掩码，我们为所有固定比特(fixed bits)写 1，为所有未固定比特(unfixed bits)写 0，并把结果转换成点分四段(dotted quad)。例如，如果范围是 192.168.1.0/29，我们可以写 29 个 1，即固定比特，和 3 个 0，即未固定比特。11111111 11111111 11111111 11111000 用点分四段表示就是 255.255.255.248。用网络掩码表示这个范围，就是 192.168.1.0，网络掩码为 255.255.255.248，也就是用网络掩码替代斜杠。"},{"en":"In these notes, we’ll usually use slash notations because they’re more convenient to read. In practice, netmasks can be useful because given a specific IP address, if you perform a bitwise AND between the IP address and the netmask, all the host bits will get zeroed out, and only the network bits will remain.","zh_html":"在这些笔记中，我们通常使用斜杠表示法(slash notation)，因为它更便于阅读。在实践中，网络掩码(netmasks)也很有用：给定一个具体 IP 地址(IP address)时，如果对 IP 地址和网络掩码执行按位与(bitwise AND)，所有主机位(host bits)都会被清零，只留下网络位(network bits)。"},{"en":"Aggregating Routes with CIDR","zh_html":"用 CIDR 聚合路由"},{"en":"In our original model with a network ID and host ID, we could aggregate all hosts inside the same network into a single route in the forwarding table (e.g. 2.* for everything in network 2).","zh_html":"在原先包含网络 ID(network ID)和主机 ID(host ID)的模型中，我们可以把同一网络内部的所有主机(hosts)聚合(aggregate)成转发表(forwarding table)中的一条路由(route)，例如用 2.* 表示网络 2 中的所有对象。"},{"en":"Multi-layered hierarchical addressing means that we can also aggregate multiple networks into a single route.","zh_html":"多层次的层次化寻址(multi-layered hierarchical addressing)意味着，我们也可以把多个网络聚合(aggregate)成一条路由(route)。"},{"en":"Consider this diagram of networks. In our original model, R6 needs a separate forwarding entry for AT&amp;T, UCB, and Stanford.","zh_html":"考虑这张网络图。在原来的模型中，R6 需要分别为 AT&amp;T、UCB 和 Stanford 保留单独的转发条目(forwarding entry)。"},{"en":"However, if we used hierarchical addressing, then UCB’s range (4.12.0.0/16) and Stanford’s range (4.29.0.0/16) are both subsets of AT&amp;T’s range (4.0.0.0/8). This could happen if AT&amp;T allocated those ranges to its subordinate customers UCB and Stanford.","zh_html":"然而，如果使用层次化寻址(hierarchical addressing)，那么 UCB 的范围(range) 4.12.0.0/16 和 Stanford 的范围 4.29.0.0/16 都是 AT&amp;T 范围 4.0.0.0/8 的子集(subsets)。如果 AT&amp;T 把这些范围分配给其下级客户(subordinate customers) UCB 和 Stanford，就会出现这种情况。"},{"en":"Now, R6 only needs a single entry for AT&amp;T, UCB, and Stanford. We’ve aggregated the two smaller ranges into the wider range that they both belong to.","zh_html":"现在，R6 只需要为 AT&amp;T、UCB 和 Stanford 保留一个条目(entry)。我们把两个较小范围(ranges)聚合到了它们共同所属的更大范围中。"},{"en":"Multi-Homing","zh_html":"多宿主(multi-homing)"},{"en":"Aggregating ranges doesn’t always work. Suppose we added a link from R6 directly to Stanford.","zh_html":"聚合范围(aggregating ranges)并不总是可行。假设我们新增一条从 R6 直接连接到 Stanford 的链路(link)。"},{"en":"Our aggregated route says that all packets to AT&amp;T (and its subordinates) have a next hop of R2. We need to add an additional entry saying that Stanford has a next hop of R7.","zh_html":"我们的聚合路由(aggregated route)表示，所有发往 AT&amp;T 及其下级网络的分组(packets)，下一跳(next hop)都是 R2。现在我们需要添加一个额外条目(entry)，表示 Stanford 的下一跳是 R7。"},{"en":"Notice that our forwarding table now has ranges that overlap. A destination could match multiple ranges. To pick a route, we’ll run longest prefix matching, which means we’ll use the most specific range that matches our destination IP address. For example, if we had a packet destined for a UCM host, we would use the UCM-specific entry because it has the longer 19-bit prefix. Even though the 9-bit AT&amp;T entry also matches the destination, its prefix is shorter, so we don’t use this route.","zh_html":"注意，我们的转发表(forwarding table)现在包含相互重叠的范围(ranges)。一个目的地(destination)可能匹配多个范围。为了选择路由(route)，我们会执行<strong>最长前缀匹配(longest prefix matching)</strong>，也就是使用与目的 IP 地址(destination IP address)匹配的最具体范围。例如，如果有一个分组(packet)发往 UCM 主机(host)，我们会使用 UCM 专用条目，因为它有更长的 19 位(bits)前缀(prefix)。虽然 9 位的 AT&amp;T 条目也匹配该目的地，但它的前缀更短，所以我们不使用这条路由。"},{"en":"If instead we had a packet destined for a UCB host, we can’t use the Stanford-specific entry, because the 16-bit prefix won’t match the UCB host. But we can still use the 8-bit AT&amp;T entry, which will match the destination.","zh_html":"如果我们有一个发往 UCB 主机(host)的分组(packet)，就不能使用 Stanford 专用条目，因为这个 16 位(bits)前缀(prefix)不会匹配 UCB 主机。但我们仍然可以使用 8 位的 AT&amp;T 条目，因为它会匹配该目的地(destination)。"},{"en":"Brief History of IPv6","zh_html":"IPv6 简史"},{"en":"IPv4 addresses are 32 bits, which means we have roughly 4 billion addresses available. Is this enough?","zh_html":"IPv4 地址(addresses)是 32 位(bits)，这意味着大约有 40 亿个可用地址。这够用吗？"},{"en":"This graph plots the number of remaining unallocated IP addresses (y-axis) for each regional registry over time.","zh_html":"这张图绘制了每个区域注册机构(regional registry)随时间变化的剩余未分配 IP 地址(unallocated IP addresses)数量，纵轴(y-axis)表示该数量。"},{"en":"By 2017, everybody had less than one /8 block (i.e. less than 2\\^24 = 16 million addresses) available. Each regional registry held a spare /8 block of addresses just in case, but by 2017, everybody had to start using their spare supply of addresses. By 2021, even the spare supply of addresses was running out.","zh_html":"到 2017 年，每个机构可用的地址都少于一个 /8 块(block)，也就是少于 2\\^24 = 1600 万个地址(addresses)。每个区域注册机构(regional registry)都保留了一个备用 /8 地址块以防万一，但到 2017 年，所有机构都不得不开始使用自己的备用地址。到 2021 年，甚至备用地址也快用完了。"},{"en":"Fun fact: In February 2011, there was an in-person ceremony when the final /8 block was allocated. There was even a special paper certificate issued.","zh_html":"趣闻：2011 年 2 月，最后一个 /8 块(block)被分配时，还举行了一场线下仪式。甚至还签发了一张特殊的纸质证书。"},{"en":"As the Internet grew, we started to realize that we would eventually run out of addresses. Luckily, this was realized early on, and IPv6 was developed in 1998 as a response to IP address exhaustion.","zh_html":"随着互联网(Internet)发展，我们开始意识到地址(addresses)最终会被耗尽。幸运的是，人们很早就认识到了这一点，并在 1998 年开发了 IPv6，作为对 IP 地址耗尽(IP address exhaustion)问题的回应。"},{"en":"Fundamentally, IPv6 addressing structure is the same as IPv4. There are some minor implementation changes needed for IPv6, though they aren’t relevant here.","zh_html":"从根本上说，IPv6 的寻址结构(addressing structure)与 IPv4 相同。IPv6 需要一些较小的实现变化(implementation changes)，但这些与这里的讨论无关。"},{"en":"The main new feature in IPv6 is longer addresses. IPv6 addresses are 128 bits long, which means there are roughly \\(3.4 \\times 10^{38}\\) possible addresses. This is an astronomically big number, so we’ll never run out. The universe is \\(10^{21}\\) seconds old, so we could assign an address to every second and still have only used 0.000000000000001% of all available addresses.","zh_html":"IPv6 的主要新特性是地址(addresses)更长。IPv6 地址长度为 128 位(bits)，这意味着大约有 \\(3.4 \\times 10^{38}\\) 个可能地址。这是一个天文数字，所以我们永远不会用完。宇宙已经存在了 \\(10^{21}\\) 秒；即使给每一秒都分配一个地址，也只会用掉全部可用地址的 0.000000000000001%。"},{"en":"IPv6 was developed in the 1990s, but was not immediately adopted by all computers. Even in 2010, basically nobody used IPv6. As of 2024, IPv6 is used by around 45% of end users, and most of these users are located in developed countries with wider Internet adoption. The main reason why IPv6 is becoming more widely-adopted is because we’re running out of IPv4 addresses.","zh_html":"IPv6 在 1990 年代开发出来，但并没有立即被所有计算机采用。即使到了 2010 年，基本上也没有人使用 IPv6。截至 2024 年，约 45% 的终端用户(end users)使用 IPv6，并且这些用户大多位于互联网(Internet)普及率更高的发达国家。IPv6 变得更广泛采用的主要原因，是 IPv4 地址(addresses)正在耗尽。"},{"en":"Why is IPv6 adoption so slow? Users, servers, and Internet operators have to upgrade their software and hardware (e.g. routers, links, device drivers on computers) to support IPv6. Routers now need two forwarding tables, one with IPv4 addresses and one with IPv6 addresses.","zh_html":"为什么 IPv6 的采用这么慢？用户、服务器(servers)和互联网运营方(Internet operators)都必须升级软件和硬件，例如路由器(routers)、链路(links)、计算机上的设备驱动(device drivers)，才能支持 IPv6。路由器现在需要两张转发表(forwarding tables)：一张用于 IPv4 地址，一张用于 IPv6 地址。"},{"en":"IPv6 upgrades have to be backwards-compatible. If a server only had an IPv6 address, users on older computers that only support IPv4 can’t use this server. IPv4 and IPv6 are essentially separate addressing systems, and there’s no way to convert between IPv4 and IPv6 addresses. As of 2024, many computers still don’t support IPv6, so many services need to support both IPv4 and IPv6.","zh_html":"IPv6 升级必须保持向后兼容(backwards-compatible)。如果一台服务器(server)只有 IPv6 地址(address)，只支持 IPv4 的旧电脑用户就无法使用这台服务器。IPv4 和 IPv6 本质上是相互独立的寻址系统(addressing systems)，无法在 IPv4 地址和 IPv6 地址之间直接转换。截至 2024 年，许多计算机仍不支持 IPv6，因此许多服务需要同时支持 IPv4 和 IPv6。"},{"en":"Computers that do support both IPv4 and IPv6 also have to think about which one to use. Is one better than the other? In practice, IPv6 is faster, but many other implementation details could affect your choice.","zh_html":"同时支持 IPv4 和 IPv6 的计算机还必须考虑使用哪一个。一个是否比另一个更好？在实践中，IPv6 更快，但许多其他实现细节(implementation details)也可能影响选择。"},{"en":"IPv6 Address Notation","zh_html":"IPv6 地址表示法(address notation)"},{"en":"IPv6 addresses are usually written in hexadecimal instead of decimal. For example:","zh_html":"IPv6 地址(addresses)通常用十六进制(hexadecimal)而不是十进制(decimal)书写。例如："},{"en":"2001:0D08:CAFE:BEEF:DEAD:1234:5678:9012","zh_html":"2001:0D08:CAFE:BEEF:DEAD:1234:5678:9012"},{"en":"is an IPv6 address (32 hex digits = 128 bits). We add colons in between every 4 hex digits (16 bits) for readability.","zh_html":"是一个 IPv6 地址(address)，32 个十六进制数字(hex digits)等于 128 位(bits)。为了可读性，我们在每 4 个十六进制数字，也就是 16 位，之间添加冒号(colons)。"},{"en":"For readability, we can omit leading zeros within a 4-digit block. For example:","zh_html":"为了可读性，我们可以省略 4 位数字块(block)中的前导零(leading zeros)。例如："},{"en":"2001:0DB8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0001","zh_html":"2001:0DB8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0001"},{"en":"can be shortened to 2001:DB8:0:0:0:0:0:1.","zh_html":"可以缩短为 2001:DB8:0:0:0:0:0:1。"},{"en":"For readability, we can also omit a long string of zeroes, e.g. 2001:DB8::1. The double colon says to fill in all missing 4-digit blocks with 0000. This can only be done once per address. (Omitting two ranges creates ambiguity, because we don’t know how many zeroes go in each range.)","zh_html":"为了可读性，我们也可以省略一长串 0，例如 2001:DB8::1。双冒号(double colon)表示用 0000 填充所有缺失的 4 位数字块(blocks)。每个地址(address)中只能这样做一次。省略两个范围(ranges)会产生歧义，因为我们不知道每个范围中应填入多少个 0。"},{"en":"Slash notation can still be used in IPv6. An individual address has /128 (all bits fixed). A 32-bit prefix might look like 2001:0DB8::/32.","zh_html":"IPv6 中仍然可以使用斜杠表示法(slash notation)。单个地址(address)是 /128，即所有位(bits)都固定。一个 32 位前缀(prefix)可能写成 2001:0DB8::/32。"},{"en":"Because the address space is so large, in IPv6, you could fix the network ID to be 64 bits and the host ID to be 64 bits, and still never run out of network IDs or host IDs. In fact, special protocols exist where networks and hosts can pick their own 64-bit network ID and host ID (and check that no one else is using it), without an organization needing to allocate specific IDs.","zh_html":"由于地址空间(address space)非常大，在 IPv6 中，即使把网络 ID(network ID)固定为 64 位(bits)，把主机 ID(host ID)也设为 64 位，也永远不会用完网络 ID 或主机 ID。事实上，存在一些特殊协议(protocols)，允许网络和主机(hosts)选择自己的 64 位网络 ID 和主机 ID，并检查没有其他人正在使用它，而不需要某个组织分配具体 ID。"},{"en":"In practice, regional registries typically allocate 32-bit prefixes to ISPs, and ISPs typically allocate 48-bit prefixes to organizations. The organization can then allocate 64-bit prefixes to smaller sub-networks. In IPv6, we usually don’t see prefixes longer than /64. Even the smallest sub-networks inside an organization have a 64-bit prefix, and 64 bits for addressing specific hosts. Using these standardized prefix sizes allows prefixes to be more informative. For example, in IPv6, it’s not clear what a /19 prefix represents, but in IPv6, we know a /32 prefix typically represents an ISP. TODO double check this","zh_html":"在实践中，区域注册机构(regional registries)通常向 ISP 分配 32 位(bits)前缀(prefixes)，ISP 通常向组织分配 48 位前缀。随后，组织可以向更小的子网络(sub-networks)分配 64 位前缀。在 IPv6 中，我们通常不会看到长于 /64 的前缀。即使是组织内部最小的子网络，也有 64 位前缀，并用 64 位来为具体主机(hosts)寻址(addressing)。使用这些标准化的前缀大小可以让前缀包含更多信息。例如，在 IPv6 中，/19 前缀表示什么并不清楚；但在 IPv6 中，我们知道 /32 前缀通常表示一个 ISP。TODO double check this"}]},"/routing/autonomous-systems.html":{"title":"autonomous-systems","blocks":[{"en":"Model for Inter-Domain Routing","en_html":"<a href=\"#model-for-inter-domain-routing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"model-for-inter-domain-routing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Model for Inter-Domain Routing","zh_html":"<a href=\"#model-for-inter-domain-routing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"model-for-inter-domain-routing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 域间路由(inter-domain routing)模型"},{"en":"Inter-Domain Routing","en_html":"<a href=\"#inter-domain-routing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"inter-domain-routing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Inter-Domain Routing","zh_html":"<a href=\"#inter-domain-routing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"inter-domain-routing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 域间路由(Inter-Domain Routing)"},{"en":"Recall from earlier that routing is performed in a network of networks. We’ve seen distance-vector and link-state protocols that can be used to implement intra-domain routing, which allows packets to be sent within a local network.","en_html":"Recall from earlier that routing is performed in a network of networks. We’ve seen distance-vector and link-state protocols that can be used to implement intra-domain routing, which allows packets to be sent within a local network.","zh_html":"回想一下，路由(routing)是在网络的网络(network of networks)中进行的。我们已经学习了可用于实现域内路由(intra-domain routing)的距离向量(distance-vector)和链路状态(link-state)协议，它们让分组(packets)可以在本地网络(local network)内部发送。"},{"en":"In this section, we’ll build a model that will allow us to define inter-domain routing protocols, which can send packets between different local networks. We’ll also see how inter-domain and intra-domain routing protocols combine to allow packets to be sent to any host in any network.","en_html":"In this section, we’ll build a model that will allow us to define inter-domain routing protocols, which can send packets between different local networks. We’ll also see how inter-domain and intra-domain routing protocols combine to allow packets to be sent to any host in any network.","zh_html":"在本节中，我们会构造一个模型(model)，用来定义域间路由协议(inter-domain routing protocols)，它让分组可以在不同本地网络(local networks)之间发送。我们还会看到域间路由和域内路由协议如何协同工作，让分组可以到达任何网络中的任何主机(host)。"},{"en":"Defining Autonomous Systems","en_html":"<a href=\"#defining-autonomous-systems\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"defining-autonomous-systems\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Defining Autonomous Systems","zh_html":"<a href=\"#defining-autonomous-systems\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"defining-autonomous-systems\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 定义自治系统(Defining Autonomous Systems)"},{"en":"We can formalize the notion of a local network by defining an autonomous system (AS), which is one or more local network(s) all run by the same operator. For example, within a company like Google, there might be a local network for employee computers, and another local network for data centers, but both networks are controlled by the same company. The operator can deploy a single intra-domain routing protocol to send messages between machines on any of those local networks. Sometimes, the term domain is used to informally refer to an AS, though this term is also used in other protocols, so we will say AS when possible.","en_html":"We can formalize the notion of a local network by defining an <strong>autonomous system (AS)</strong>, which is one or more local network(s) all run by the same operator. For example, within a company like Google, there might be a local network for employee computers, and another local network for data centers, but both networks are controlled by the same company. The operator can deploy a single intra-domain routing protocol to send messages between machines on any of those local networks. Sometimes, the term <strong>domain</strong> is used to informally refer to an AS, though this term is also used in other protocols, so we will say AS when possible.","zh_html":"我们可以通过定义<strong>自治系统(autonomous system, AS)</strong>来形式化(formalize)本地网络(local network)的概念。一个 AS 是由同一个运营方(operator)管理的一个或多个本地网络。例如，在 Google 这样的公司内部，可能有员工电脑的本地网络和数据中心(data centers)的另一个本地网络，但两个网络都由同一家公司控制。运营方可以部署统一的域内路由协议(intra-domain routing protocol)，在这些本地网络中的机器之间发送消息。术语<strong>域(domain)</strong>有时被非正式地用来指代 AS，但这个词也在其他协议中使用，所以我们尽量用 AS。"},{"en":"To think about routing packets between autonomous systems, we can abstract away all of the individual routers and hosts within the AS, and treat the AS as a single entity. Then, we can draw a graph where each node represents an AS, and edges between two ASes represents a connection between them. This graph is sometimes called the inter-domain topology or an AS graph.","en_html":"To think about routing packets between autonomous systems, we can abstract away all of the individual routers and hosts within the AS, and treat the AS as a single entity. Then, we can draw a graph where each node represents an AS, and edges between two ASes represents a connection between them. This graph is sometimes called the <strong>inter-domain topology</strong> or an <strong>AS graph</strong>.","zh_html":"为了思考如何在自治系统(autonomous systems)之间路由分组，我们可以抽象掉 AS 内部的所有单独路由器和主机，把 AS 当作一个整体对待。然后，我们可以画一个图(graph)，其中每个节点代表一个 AS，两个 AS 之间的边(edge)表示它们之间的连接。这个图有时被称为<strong>域间拓扑(inter-domain topology)</strong>或<strong>AS 图(AS graph)</strong>。"},{"en":"Brief History of Autonomous Systems","en_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-autonomous-systems\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-autonomous-systems\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Brief History of Autonomous Systems","zh_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-autonomous-systems\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-autonomous-systems\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 自治系统简史(Brief History of Autonomous Systems)"},{"en":"In real life, an organization called the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) manages a global list of all autonomous systems that exist in the Internet. In order to be an AS, you must register with this organization and receive a unique autonomous system number (ASN).","en_html":"In real life, an organization called the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) manages a global list of all autonomous systems that exist in the Internet. In order to be an AS, you must register with this organization and receive a unique autonomous system number (ASN).","zh_html":"在现实中，一个名为互联网号码分配局(Internet Assigned Numbers Authority, IANA)的组织负责管理互联网上所有自治系统(autonomous systems)的全局列表。要成为一个 AS，你必须向该组织注册，并获得一个唯一的自治系统编号(autonomous system number, ASN)。"},{"en":"Fun fact: In the early days, the IANA was administered manually by a single person, Jon Postel. This meant that anybody in the world who wanted to register a new AS would have to ask for his approval.","en_html":"Fun fact: In the early days, the IANA was administered manually by a single person, Jon Postel. This meant that anybody in the world who wanted to register a new AS would have to ask for his approval.","zh_html":"有趣的事实：在早期，IANA 由一个人，Jon Postel，手动管理。这意味着世界上任何想要注册新 AS 的人都必须征得他的批准。"},{"en":"Today, there are over 90,000 autonomous systems, with the United States having the most ASes of any country.","en_html":"Today, there are over 90,000 autonomous systems, with the United States having the most ASes of any country.","zh_html":"如今，有超过 90,000 个自治系统(autonomous systems)，其中美国拥有的 AS 数量最多。"},{"en":"Fun fact: UC Berkeley has ASN 25, which is a remarkably low number given that there are so many ASNs. This reflects the fact that UC Berkeley received its ASN very early in the Internet’s history (in the 1980s).","en_html":"Fun fact: UC Berkeley has ASN 25, which is a remarkably low number given that there are so many ASNs. This reflects the fact that UC Berkeley received its ASN very early in the Internet’s history (in the 1980s).","zh_html":"有趣的事实：加州大学伯克利分校(UC Berkeley)的 ASN 是 25，考虑到有这么多 ASN，这是一个非常小的数字。这反映了 UC Berkeley 在互联网历史早期（1980 年代）就获得了它的 ASN。"},{"en":"Types of ASes","en_html":"<a href=\"#types-of-ases\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"types-of-ases\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Types of ASes","zh_html":"<a href=\"#types-of-ases\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"types-of-ases\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> AS 的类型(Types of ASes)"},{"en":"Recall that when modeling the network for intra-domain routing, we made a distinction between end hosts and routers. We’ll make a similar distinction for inter-domain routing by defining two types of ASes.","en_html":"Recall that when modeling the network for intra-domain routing, we made a distinction between end hosts and routers. We’ll make a similar distinction for inter-domain routing by defining two types of ASes.","zh_html":"回想一下，在为域内路由(intra-domain routing)建模网络时，我们区分了终端主机(end hosts)和路由器(routers)。对于域间路由(inter-domain routing)，我们通过定义两种类型的 AS 来做类似的区分。"},{"en":"A stub autonomous system only exists to provide Internet connectivity to the hosts in its local networks. A stub AS only sends and receives packets on behalf of hosts that are inside the AS, and does not forward packets between different ASes. These are analogous to the end hosts in our intra-domain routing model, which only sent and received their own packets and did not forward other people’s packets.","en_html":"A <strong>stub autonomous system</strong> only exists to provide Internet connectivity to the hosts in its local networks. A stub AS only sends and receives packets on behalf of hosts that are inside the AS, and does not forward packets between different ASes. These are analogous to the end hosts in our intra-domain routing model, which only sent and received their own packets and did not forward other people’s packets.","zh_html":"一个<strong>末梢自治系统(stub autonomous system)</strong>只为其本地网络(local networks)中的主机(hosts)提供互联网连接(Internet connectivity)。末梢 AS(stub AS)只代表其内部的主机发送和接收分组(packets)，不负责在不同 AS 之间转发分组。这类似于我们域内路由模型中的终端主机(end hosts)，它们只发送和接收自己的分组，不转发其他人的分组。"},{"en":"Real-life examples of stub ASes include non-Internet companies (e.g. a bank offering connectivity to its employees) or universities (e.g. UC Berkeley offering connectivity to its students and employees). These organizations are not responsible for carrying Internet traffic from other organizations. The vast majority of ASes in the world are stub ASes.","en_html":"Real-life examples of stub ASes include non-Internet companies (e.g. a bank offering connectivity to its employees) or universities (e.g. UC Berkeley offering connectivity to its students and employees). These organizations are not responsible for carrying Internet traffic from other organizations. The vast majority of ASes in the world are stub ASes.","zh_html":"现实中的末梢 AS(stub AS)例子包括非互联网公司（例如为员工提供网络连接的银行）或大学（例如为师生提供网络连接的 UC Berkeley）。这些组织不负责承载来自其他组织的互联网流量。世界上绝大多数 AS 都是末梢 AS。"},{"en":"By contrast, a transit autonomous system forwards packets on behalf of other ASes. A transit AS could carry a packet between two different ASes by receiving and forwarding that packet.","en_html":"By contrast, a <strong>transit autonomous system</strong> forwards packets on behalf of other ASes. A transit AS could carry a packet between two different ASes by receiving and forwarding that packet.","zh_html":"相反，一个<strong>中转自治系统(transit autonomous system)</strong>代表其他 AS 转发分组。一个中转 AS(transit AS)可以通过接收并转发分组，在两个不同的 AS 之间传输分组。"},{"en":"Transit ASes correspond to real-life companies whose business includes selling Internet connectivity to other organizations. Real-life examples of transit ASes include AT&amp;T and Verizon, which are companies that you can pay to offer you Internet connectivity. Some transit ASes like AT&amp;T are global, with infrastructure around the world. Others might be specific to a region, like Sonic, an Internet service provider that only forwards traffic to and from California.","en_html":"Transit ASes correspond to real-life companies whose business includes selling Internet connectivity to other organizations. Real-life examples of transit ASes include AT&amp;T and Verizon, which are companies that you can pay to offer you Internet connectivity. Some transit ASes like AT&amp;T are global, with infrastructure around the world. Others might be specific to a region, like Sonic, an Internet service provider that only forwards traffic to and from California.","zh_html":"中转 AS(transit AS)对应于现实中那些业务包括向其他组织出售互联网连接的公司。现实中的例子包括 AT&T 和 Verizon，你可以付费从这些公司获得互联网连接。一些中转 AS，如 AT&T，是全球性的，在世界各地都有基础设施。其他一些可能只针对某个地区，例如 Sonic，一家只转发进出加利福尼亚州流量的互联网服务提供商(Internet service provider)。"},{"en":"Note that a transit AS can still contain end hosts that send and receive packets of their own. Nevertheless, a transit AS is similar to the routers in our intra-domain routing model, which received other users’ packets and forwarded them on behalf of users.","en_html":"Note that a transit AS can still contain end hosts that send and receive packets of their own. Nevertheless, a transit AS is similar to the routers in our intra-domain routing model, which received other users’ packets and forwarded them on behalf of users.","zh_html":"注意，中转 AS(transit AS)仍然可以包含发送和接收自己分组的主机(hosts)。不过，中转 AS 类似于我们域内路由模型中的路由器(routers)，它们接收其他用户的分组并代表用户转发。"},{"en":"This model of stub and transit ASes is what we’ll use in these notes, though sometimes, the classification in real life can be less well-defined. For example, major tech companies like Google, Microsoft, and Amazon control massive ASes that carry as much traffic as transit ASes (and maybe even more). Because their primary role is to carry traffic to and from their services (e.g. receive Google search requests and send search results), they could be classifed as stub ASes. In recent years, though, these companies have also offered to carry traffic between ASes, so they could arguably be classified as transit ASes as well.","en_html":"This model of stub and transit ASes is what we’ll use in these notes, though sometimes, the classification in real life can be less well-defined. For example, major tech companies like Google, Microsoft, and Amazon control massive ASes that carry as much traffic as transit ASes (and maybe even more). Because their primary role is to carry traffic to and from their services (e.g. receive Google search requests and send search results), they could be classifed as stub ASes. In recent years, though, these companies have also offered to carry traffic between ASes, so they could arguably be classified as transit ASes as well.","zh_html":"本笔记将使用这种末梢(stub)和中转(transit)AS 模型，尽管现实中的分类有时不那么明确。例如，Google、Microsoft 和 Amazon 等大型科技公司控制着庞大的 AS，承载的流量不亚于中转 AS（甚至可能更多）。由于它们的主要角色是承载进出其服务的流量（例如接收 Google 搜索请求并发送搜索结果），它们可以被归类为末梢 AS。但近年来，这些公司也开始提供 AS 之间的流量承载服务，因此也可以说它们属于中转 AS。"},{"en":"Inter-Domain Topology Is Defined by Business Relationships","en_html":"<a href=\"#inter-domain-topology-is-defined-by-business-relationships\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"inter-domain-topology-is-defined-by-business-relationships\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Inter-Domain Topology Is Defined by Business Relationships","zh_html":"<a href=\"#inter-domain-topology-is-defined-by-business-relationships\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"inter-domain-topology-is-defined-by-business-relationships\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 域间拓扑由商业关系定义(Inter-Domain Topology Is Defined by Business Relationships)"},{"en":"In our inter-domain topology, we draw an edge between two ASes if they exchange traffic. What causes two real-life organizations, such as a local bank and Verizon, to agree to exchange traffic? The edges in the AS are defined by real-world business relationships between ASes.","en_html":"In our inter-domain topology, we draw an edge between two ASes if they exchange traffic. What causes two real-life organizations, such as a local bank and Verizon, to agree to exchange traffic? The edges in the AS are defined by real-world business relationships between ASes.","zh_html":"在我们的域间拓扑(inter-domain topology)中，如果两个 AS 交换流量，我们就在它们之间画一条边(edge)。什么原因会让两个现实中的组织，比如一家本地银行和 Verizon，同意交换流量？AS 图中的边是由 AS 之间真实的商业关系(business relationships)定义的。"},{"en":"There are two possible ways that a pair of ASes could be related.","en_html":"There are two possible ways that a pair of ASes could be related.","zh_html":"一对 AS 之间可能存在两种关系。"},{"en":"A pair of ASes could be involved in a customer-provider relationship. In real life, the customer is paying for service, and the provider is offering connectivity in exchange for money. For example, the local bank AS could be the customer, paying the provider, Verizon, for Internet services.","en_html":"A pair of ASes could be involved in a customer-provider relationship. In real life, the <strong>customer</strong> is paying for service, and the <strong>provider</strong> is offering connectivity in exchange for money. For example, the local bank AS could be the customer, paying the provider, Verizon, for Internet services.","zh_html":"一对 AS 之间可能存在<strong>客户-提供商关系(customer-provider relationship)</strong>。在现实中，<strong>客户(customer)</strong>为服务付费，<strong>提供商(provider)</strong>提供连接以换取金钱。例如，本地银行 AS 可以是客户，向提供商 Verizon 付费以获取互联网服务。"},{"en":"A pair of ASes could also be involved in a peer relationship. Two peer ASes usually send each other a roughly equal amount of traffic. In real life, two ASes could agree to become peers by signing a legal contract between companies. Usually, the two peers agree to not pay each other for connectivity services, as long as the traffic sent in either direction is roughly equal.","en_html":"A pair of ASes could also be involved in a <strong>peer</strong> relationship. Two peer ASes usually send each other a roughly equal amount of traffic. In real life, two ASes could agree to become peers by signing a legal contract between companies. Usually, the two peers agree to not pay each other for connectivity services, as long as the traffic sent in either direction is roughly equal.","zh_html":"一对 AS 之间也可能存在<strong>对等关系(peer relationship)</strong>。两个对等 AS(peer ASes)通常互相发送大致相等的流量。在现实中，两个 AS 可以通过签署公司间的法律合同来成为对等体。通常情况下，两个对等体同意不互相支付连接费用，只要双向发送的流量大致相等。"},{"en":"AS Graph with Business Relationships","en_html":"<a href=\"#as-graph-with-business-relationships\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"as-graph-with-business-relationships\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> AS Graph with Business Relationships","zh_html":"<a href=\"#as-graph-with-business-relationships\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"as-graph-with-business-relationships\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 带商业关系的 AS 图(AS Graph with Business Relationships)"},{"en":"We can draw these relationships into the AS graph by adding arrows to the graph. A directed edge points from the provider to the customer. An undirected edge connects two peers. Note that the graph can contain both directed and undirected edges (not all edges need to have arrows).","en_html":"We can draw these relationships into the AS graph by adding arrows to the graph. A directed edge points from the provider to the customer. An undirected edge connects two peers. Note that the graph can contain both directed and undirected edges (not all edges need to have arrows).","zh_html":"我们可以通过在图(graph)中添加箭头来将这些关系画入 AS 图。有向边(directed edge)从提供商(provider)指向客户(customer)。无向边(undirected edge)连接两个对等体(peers)。注意，图中可以同时包含有向边和无向边，并非所有边都需要有箭头。"},{"en":"Stub ASes in the graph are only customers. They have incoming edges, showing who provides them with connectivity. However, they don’t have any outgoing edges, because they don’t provide connectivity to others.","en_html":"Stub ASes in the graph are only customers. They have incoming edges, showing who provides them with connectivity. However, they don’t have any outgoing edges, because they don’t provide connectivity to others.","zh_html":"图中的末梢 AS(stub ASes)只是客户。它们有入边(incoming edges)，显示谁为它们提供连接。然而，它们没有出边(outgoing edges)，因为她们不为其他人提供连接。"},{"en":"By contrast, transit ASes in the graph are the providers. Their outgoing arrows show that they are selling connectivity to other organizations.","en_html":"By contrast, transit ASes in the graph are the providers. Their outgoing arrows show that they are selling connectivity to other organizations.","zh_html":"相反，图中的中转 AS(transit ASes)是提供商(providers)。它们的出箭头表示它们正在向其他组织出售连接服务。"},{"en":"Note that the direction of the arrow does not tell us anything about what direction the packets are being sent. In fact, packets can be sent in both directions even along a directed edge. The customer often pays the provider for the ability to send packets to and receive packets from the rest of the Internet.","en_html":"Note that the direction of the arrow does not tell us anything about what direction the packets are being sent. In fact, packets can be sent in both directions even along a directed edge. The customer often pays the provider for the ability to send packets to and receive packets from the rest of the Internet.","zh_html":"注意，箭头的方向并不告诉我们分组(packets)的发送方向。实际上，即使沿着有向边(directed edge)，分组也可以在两个方向上发送。客户通常向提供商付费，以获得向互联网其余部分发送和接收分组的能力。"},{"en":"AS Graphs are Acyclic","en_html":"<a href=\"#as-graphs-are-acyclic\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"as-graphs-are-acyclic\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> AS Graphs are Acyclic","zh_html":"<a href=\"#as-graphs-are-acyclic\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"as-graphs-are-acyclic\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> AS 图是无环的(AS Graphs are Acyclic)"},{"en":"The graph of customer-provider relationships is acyclic. The graph does not contain any cycles consisting of directed edges.","en_html":"The graph of customer-provider relationships is acyclic. The graph does not contain any cycles consisting of directed edges.","zh_html":"客户-提供商关系(customer-provider relationships)的图是无环的(acyclic)。该图不包含由有向边(directed edges)组成的环(cycles)。"},{"en":"This acyclic property exists because of the real-world implications of having a cycle. In real life, a cycle would mean that A pays B, B pays C, and then C pays A, and it doesn’t make sense for money to flow from somebody back to themselves. Also, this cycle would mean that A provides service to C, which provides service to B, which provides service to A. It also doesn’t make sense for somebody to provide connectivity to themselves.","en_html":"This acyclic property exists because of the real-world implications of having a cycle. In real life, a cycle would mean that A pays B, B pays C, and then C pays A, and it doesn’t make sense for money to flow from somebody back to themselves. Also, this cycle would mean that A provides service to C, which provides service to B, which provides service to A. It also doesn’t make sense for somebody to provide connectivity to themselves.","zh_html":"这种无环性质(acyclic property)源于现实中存在环的含义。在现实中，一个环意味着 A 付钱给 B，B 付钱给 C，然后 C 又付钱给 A，钱流回自己身上是不合理的。同样，这个环还意味着 A 为 C 提供服务，C 为 B 提供服务，B 又为 A 提供服务。自己为自己提供连接也是不合理的。"},{"en":"To use an analogy, imagine if you paid UC Berkeley tuition for classes, then UC Berkeley paid the UC system for classes, and then the UC system paid you for classes. This business relationship doesn’t make any sense!","en_html":"To use an analogy, imagine if you paid UC Berkeley tuition for classes, then UC Berkeley paid the UC system for classes, and then the UC system paid you for classes. This business relationship doesn’t make any sense!","zh_html":"打个比方，想象一下你向 UC Berkeley 付学费上课，然后 UC Berkeley 向加州大学系统付学费上课，然后加州大学系统又向你付学费上课。这种商业关系毫无意义！"},{"en":"Note that the acyclic property only applies to customer-provider relationships. It is okay if peering relationships form a cycle. For example, it’s okay if A-B, B-C, and C-A are all peers. None of them are sending each other money, so we don’t have an ill-defined business relationship.","en_html":"Note that the acyclic property only applies to customer-provider relationships. It is okay if peering relationships form a cycle. For example, it’s okay if A-B, B-C, and C-A are all peers. None of them are sending each other money, so we don’t have an ill-defined business relationship.","zh_html":"注意，无环性质只适用于客户-提供商关系(customer-provider relationships)。对等关系(peering relationships)形成环是可以的。例如，A-B、B-C 和 C-A 都是对等体是没问题的。它们之间没有任何金钱往来，所以不存在不明确的商业关系。"},{"en":"Provider Hierarchy and Tier 1 ASes","en_html":"<a href=\"#provider-hierarchy-and-tier-1-ases\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"provider-hierarchy-and-tier-1-ases\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Provider Hierarchy and Tier 1 ASes","zh_html":"<a href=\"#provider-hierarchy-and-tier-1-ases\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"provider-hierarchy-and-tier-1-ases\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 提供商层次结构和 Tier 1 AS(Provider Hierarchy and Tier 1 ASes)"},{"en":"A consequence of the graph being acyclic is, we can form a hierarchy of providers. In other words, we can arrange the nodes such that all the arrows point downward. The stub ASes are at the bottom, the providers are at the top. Service flows from higher to lower nodes. The lower nodes pay money up to higher nodes.","en_html":"A consequence of the graph being acyclic is, we can form a hierarchy of providers. In other words, we can arrange the nodes such that all the arrows point downward. The stub ASes are at the bottom, the providers are at the top. Service flows from higher to lower nodes. The lower nodes pay money up to higher nodes.","zh_html":"图是无环的一个结果是，我们可以形成提供商的层次结构(hierarchy)。换句话说，我们可以排列节点，使得所有箭头都指向下方。末梢 AS(stub ASes)在底部，提供商在顶部。服务从较高节点流向较低节点。较低节点向较高节点付钱。"},{"en":"At the very top of the hierarchy, there are Tier 1 autonomous systems, which have no providers (no incoming edges). Every Tier 1 AS has a peering relationship with every other Tier 1 AS.","en_html":"At the very top of the hierarchy, there are <strong>Tier 1 autonomous systems</strong>, which have no providers (no incoming edges). Every Tier 1 AS has a peering relationship with every other Tier 1 AS.","zh_html":"在层次结构的顶端，有<strong>第一层自治系统(Tier 1 autonomous systems)</strong>，它们没有提供商（没有入边）。每个 Tier 1 AS 与所有其他 Tier 1 AS 都有对等关系(peering relationship)。"},{"en":"A consequence of this hierarchy is: Every non-Tier 1 AS has at least one provider (incoming edge). This makes sense in real life, since you have to pay somebody to offer you connectivity.","en_html":"A consequence of this hierarchy is: Every non-Tier 1 AS has at least one provider (incoming edge). This makes sense in real life, since you have to pay somebody to offer you connectivity.","zh_html":"这个层次结构的一个后果是：每个非 Tier 1 AS 至少有一个提供商（入边）。这在现实中是有意义的，因为你必须付钱给某人来为你提供连接。"},{"en":"In this hierarchy, starting from any AS, and following the uphill chain of providers, always leads to a Tier 1 AS. This also makes sense in real life. The Tier 1 ASes all peering with each other is why the entire Internet is connected (as opposed to, say, two disconnected subgraphs representing two separate Internets where you can only talk to hosts in your own half). In order to guarantee having a path to every other AS in the graph, every AS must have a path upwards that eventually leads to a Tier 1 AS.","en_html":"In this hierarchy, starting from any AS, and following the uphill chain of providers, always leads to a Tier 1 AS. This also makes sense in real life. The Tier 1 ASes all peering with each other is why the entire Internet is connected (as opposed to, say, two disconnected subgraphs representing two separate Internets where you can only talk to hosts in your own half). In order to guarantee having a path to every other AS in the graph, every AS must have a path upwards that eventually leads to a Tier 1 AS.","zh_html":"在这个层次结构中，从任何 AS 出发，沿着提供商的上行链路上行，最终都会到达一个 Tier 1 AS。这在现实中也有意义。所有 Tier 1 AS 互相之间都是对等关系，这就是为什么整个互联网是连通的（而不是两个不连通的子图，代表两个独立的互联网，你只能与自己一半的主机通信）。为了保证图中到每个其他 AS 都有路径，每个 AS 必须有一条上行路径，最终到达一个 Tier 1 AS。"},{"en":"TODO-diagram","en_html":"TODO-diagram","zh_html":"TODO-diagram"},{"en":"Some real-world examples of Tier 1 ASes in the AT&amp;T and Verizon (US-based), France Telecom and Telecom Italia (Europe-based), and NTT Communications (Japan-based). There are around 20 ASes that are Tier 1 or nearly Tier 1 in real life. These Tier 1 ASes usually own infrastructure spanning multiple continents (e.g. undersea cables).","en_html":"Some real-world examples of Tier 1 ASes in the AT&amp;T and Verizon (US-based), France Telecom and Telecom Italia (Europe-based), and NTT Communications (Japan-based). There are around 20 ASes that are Tier 1 or nearly Tier 1 in real life. These Tier 1 ASes usually own infrastructure spanning multiple continents (e.g. undersea cables).","zh_html":"现实中一些 Tier 1 AS 的例子包括 AT&T 和 Verizon（美国）、France Telecom 和 Telecom Italia（欧洲）、以及 NTT Communications（日本）。现实中有大约 20 个 AS 是 Tier 1 或接近 Tier 1。这些 Tier 1 AS 通常拥有横跨多个大洲的基础设施（例如海底光缆）。"},{"en":"The hierarchy structure of the AS graph is defined by real-world business and political motivations. In theory, it would be possible to draw an AS graph that looks like a tree, with a single Tier 1 AS at the root providing services to every stub AS. However, this means that a single real-life entity controls the entire world’s Internet access, which may be undesirable for political reasons.","en_html":"The hierarchy structure of the AS graph is defined by real-world business and political motivations. In theory, it would be possible to draw an AS graph that looks like a tree, with a single Tier 1 AS at the root providing services to every stub AS. However, this means that a single real-life entity controls the entire world’s Internet access, which may be undesirable for political reasons.","zh_html":"AS 图的层次结构由现实的商业和政治动机决定。理论上，我们可以画一个树状的 AS 图，由一个 Tier 1 AS 作为根节点，为所有末梢 AS(stub AS)提供服务。然而，这意味着单一的实体控制着全世界的互联网接入，这可能出于政治原因是不理想的。"},{"en":"Policy-Based Routing","en_html":"<a href=\"#policy-based-routing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"policy-based-routing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Policy-Based Routing","zh_html":"<a href=\"#policy-based-routing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"policy-based-routing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 基于策略的路由(Policy-Based Routing)"},{"en":"Recall that in intra-domain routing, our goal was to find paths that are valid (no loops and no dead-ends) and good (least cost).","en_html":"Recall that in intra-domain routing, our goal was to find paths that are valid (no loops and no dead-ends) and good (least cost).","zh_html":"回想一下，在域内路由(intra-domain routing)中，我们的目标是找到有效(valid)（无环且无死胡同）且好(good)（最小成本）的路径(paths)。"},{"en":"In inter-domain routing, we still want paths to be valid. However, unlike in intra-domain routing, where there was nothing special about one router over another, each autonomous system has its own business goals and relationships with other ASes (e.g. customer, provider, peer). Therefore, we will need to re-define “good” to reflect the real-world business goals and preferences of ASes.","en_html":"In inter-domain routing, we still want paths to be valid. However, unlike in intra-domain routing, where there was nothing special about one router over another, each autonomous system has its own business goals and relationships with other ASes (e.g. customer, provider, peer). Therefore, we will need to re-define “good” to reflect the real-world business goals and preferences of ASes.","zh_html":"在域间路由(inter-domain routing)中，我们仍然希望路径是有效的。然而，与域内路由不同的是，域内路由中一台路由器并不比另一台特殊；而每个自治系统(autonomous system)都有自己的商业目标以及与其他 AS 的关系（如客户、提供商、对等体）。因此，我们需要重新定义\"好\"以反映 AS 在现实中的商业目标和偏好。"},{"en":"In order to allow each AS to carry traffic in a way that’s compatible with its real-world goals, our routing protocol will allow each AS to set its own policy. Then, the paths computed by the protocol should properly respect each AS’s policy.","en_html":"In order to allow each AS to carry traffic in a way that’s compatible with its real-world goals, our routing protocol will allow each AS to set its own policy. Then, the paths computed by the protocol should properly respect each AS’s policy.","zh_html":"为了让每个 AS 以与其现实目标兼容的方式承载流量，我们的路由协议将允许每个 AS 设置自己的策略(policy)。然后，协议计算出的路径应适当尊重每个 AS 的策略。"},{"en":"In theory, ASes can set any sort of policy that they like, although standard conventions do exist (which we’ll discuss next). Here are some examples of policies that an AS could set:","en_html":"In theory, ASes can set any sort of policy that they like, although standard conventions do exist (which we’ll discuss next). Here are some examples of policies that an AS could set:","zh_html":"理论上，AS 可以设置任何它们喜欢的策略，尽管确实存在一些标准惯例（我们接下来会讨论）。以下是 AS 可能设置的一些策略示例："},{"en":"“I don’t want to carry AS#2046’s traffic through my network.” (Defining how I will handle traffic from other ASes.)","en_html":"“I don’t want to carry AS#2046’s traffic through my network.” (Defining how I will handle traffic from other ASes.)","zh_html":"\"我不想让 AS#2046 的流量经过我的网络。\"（定义我将如何处理来自其他 AS 的流量。）"},{"en":"“I prefer if my traffic was carried by AS#10 instead of AS#4.” (Defining how other ASes should handle my traffic.)","en_html":"“I prefer if my traffic was carried by AS#10 instead of AS#4.” (Defining how other ASes should handle my traffic.)","zh_html":"\"我希望我的流量由 AS#10 而非 AS#4 来承载。\"（定义其他 AS 应如何处理我的流量。）"},{"en":"“Don’t send my traffic through AS#54 unless absolutely necessary.”","en_html":"“Don’t send my traffic through AS#54 unless absolutely necessary.”","zh_html":"\"除非绝对必要，否则不要将我的流量发送经过 AS#54。\""},{"en":"“I prefer AS#12 on weekdays, and AS#13 on weekends.” (Policies can change over time!)","en_html":"“I prefer AS#12 on weekdays, and AS#13 on weekends.” (Policies can change over time!)","zh_html":"\"我更喜欢在工作日使用 AS#12，在周末使用 AS#13。\"（策略可以随时间变化！）"},{"en":"The routing protocol doesn’t care why the AS has these preferences. Perhaps I’m refusing to carry traffic from AS#2046 because it’s a rival company, but the protocol doesn’t need to know that.","en_html":"The routing protocol doesn’t care why the AS has these preferences. Perhaps I’m refusing to carry traffic from AS#2046 because it’s a rival company, but the protocol doesn’t need to know that.","zh_html":"路由协议不关心 AS 为什么有这些偏好。也许我拒绝承载来自 AS#2046 的流量是因为它是竞争对手，但协议不需要知道这一点。"},{"en":"Our least-cost routing protocols so far have no way of supporting these policies. Least-cost was a global minimization problem, where every router was trying to solve the same problem. By contrast, in policy-based routing, each AS only cares about its own policy, and there isn’t a global problem that everybody is cooperating to solve.","en_html":"Our least-cost routing protocols so far have no way of supporting these policies. Least-cost was a global minimization problem, where every router was trying to solve the same problem. By contrast, in policy-based routing, each AS only cares about its own policy, and there isn’t a global problem that everybody is cooperating to solve.","zh_html":"我们目前的最小成本路由协议没有办法支持这些策略。最小成本是一个全局最小化问题，每台路由器都在尝试解决同一个问题。相比之下，在基于策略的路由(policy-based routing)中，每个 AS 只关心自己的策略，不存在一个大家合作解决的全局问题。"},{"en":"Gao-Rexford Rules for Routing Policies","en_html":"<a href=\"#gao-rexford-rules-for-routing-policies\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"gao-rexford-rules-for-routing-policies\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Gao-Rexford Rules for Routing Policies","zh_html":"<a href=\"#gao-rexford-rules-for-routing-policies\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"gao-rexford-rules-for-routing-policies\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 路由策略的 Gao-Rexford 规则(Gao-Rexford Rules for Routing Policies)"},{"en":"Although our routing protocol allows each AS to set any arbitrary policy they like, in practice, most ASes set their policies according to some standard conventions, known as the Gao-Rexford rules. These conventions are based in the assumption that real-world organizations like making money, and dislike losing money.","en_html":"Although our routing protocol allows each AS to set any arbitrary policy they like, in practice, most ASes set their policies according to some standard conventions, known as the <strong>Gao-Rexford rules</strong>. These conventions are based in the assumption that real-world organizations like making money, and dislike losing money.","zh_html":"尽管我们的路由协议允许每个 AS 设置任意策略，但在实践中，大多数 AS 根据一些标准惯例设置策略，这些惯例被称为<strong>Gao-Rexford 规则(Gao-Rexford rules)</strong>。这些惯例基于一个假设：现实中的组织喜欢赚钱，不喜欢亏钱。"},{"en":"There are two broad rules that ASes typically follow. First, when an AS has a choice of multiple routes, the AS prefers to forward packets to the most profitable next hop. Specifically, the AS prefers a route with a next hop that is a customer. If there are no such routes, the AS prefers a route with a next hop that is a peer. The AS will only select a route with a next hop that is a provider if it’s forced to do so, because there are no better routes.","en_html":"There are two broad rules that ASes typically follow. First, when an AS has a choice of multiple routes, the AS prefers to forward packets to the most profitable next hop. Specifically, the AS prefers a route with a next hop that is a customer. If there are no such routes, the AS prefers a route with a next hop that is a peer. The AS will only select a route with a next hop that is a provider if it’s forced to do so, because there are no better routes.","zh_html":"AS 通常遵循两个主要规则。第一，当一个 AS 有多条路由可以选择时，它倾向于将分组转发给最有利可图的下一跳(next hop)。具体来说，AS 优先选择下一跳是客户(customer)的路由。如果没有这样的路由，则优先选择下一跳是对等体(peer)的路由。只有在没有更好的路由时，AS 才会选择下一跳是提供商(provider)的路由。"},{"en":"This principle dictates the routes that the AS selects. You can think of this principle as a preference-based version of selecting paths in the distance-vector protocol. Instead of selecting the shortest route I know about, I select the route where the next hop makes me money (customer best), or saves me money (if no customers, then peer), and avoids losing money (if no customers or peers, then provider).","en_html":"This principle dictates the routes that the AS selects. You can think of this principle as a preference-based version of selecting paths in the distance-vector protocol. Instead of selecting the shortest route I know about, I select the route where the next hop makes me money (customer best), or saves me money (if no customers, then peer), and avoids losing money (if no customers or peers, then provider).","zh_html":"这一原则决定了 AS 选择哪条路由。你可以把这个原则理解为距离向量协议(distance-vector protocol)中路径选择的基于偏好的版本。不选择我知道的最短路径，而是选择下一跳能让我赚钱（客户最好），或帮我省钱（没有客户则选对等体），并且避免亏钱（没有客户或对等体时选择提供商）的路径。"},{"en":"Second, ASes only carry traffic if they’re getting paid for it. There’s no incentive for ASes to perform free labor. This principle dictates the paths that the AS is willing to participate in. You can think of this principle as a more restrictive version of announcing paths in the distance-vector protocol. Instead of advertising a route to every neighbor, allowing anybody to forward packets through me, I only advertise routes in which I’m paid to forward packets.","en_html":"Second, ASes only carry traffic if they’re getting paid for it. There’s no incentive for ASes to perform free labor. This principle dictates the paths that the AS is willing to participate in. You can think of this principle as a more restrictive version of announcing paths in the distance-vector protocol. Instead of advertising a route to every neighbor, allowing anybody to forward packets through me, I only advertise routes in which I’m paid to forward packets.","zh_html":"第二，AS 只有在自己能获得报酬时才承载流量。AS 没有动力去提供免费劳动。这一原则决定了 AS 愿意参与哪些路径。你可以把这个原则理解为距离向量协议中路径通告的更具限制性的版本。不向所有邻居通告路由从而允许任何人通过我转发分组，我只通告那些我因转发分组而获得报酬的路由。"},{"en":"A consequence of this second principle is: As an AS, the traffic I carry should come from a customer, or go to a customer. In other words, for any route going through me, one of my neighbors must be a customer.","en_html":"A consequence of this second principle is: As an AS, the traffic I carry should come from a customer, or go to a customer. In other words, for any route going through me, one of my neighbors must be a customer.","zh_html":"第二个原则的一个结果是：作为一个 AS，我承载的流量应该来自一个客户，或者去往一个客户。换句话说，对于任何经过我的路由，我的一个邻居必须是客户。"},{"en":"Let’s go through all the specific cases.","en_html":"Let’s go through all the specific cases.","zh_html":"让我们看看所有具体的情况。"},{"en":"Routes where both of my neighbors are customers are good, because I am getting paid by the two customers to forward packets.","en_html":"Routes where both of my neighbors are customers are good, because I am getting paid by the two customers to forward packets.","zh_html":"我的两个邻居都是客户的路由是好的，因为两个客户都在为我转发分组付费。"},{"en":"Similarly, routes where one of my neighbors is a customer, and one of my neighbors is a peer are good, because even though the peer doesn’t pay me, the customer does.","en_html":"Similarly, routes where one of my neighbors is a customer, and one of my neighbors is a peer are good, because even though the peer doesn’t pay me, the customer does.","zh_html":"类似地，一个邻居是客户，另一个邻居是对等体的路由也是好的，因为即使对等体不付钱给我，客户会付。"},{"en":"Routes where one of my neighbors is a customer, and the other is a provider are good. At first, it might seem like this path is bad, because the customer is paying me, and then I’m paying the provider. Isn’t it possible that I make no money, or lose money from this transaction? That may be true, but if we didn’t participate in these routes, we would be a useless AS with no customers. An AS’s job is to give connectivity to its users, and participating in these customer-AS-provider routes unlocks more routes to the rest of the Internet.","en_html":"Routes where one of my neighbors is a customer, and the other is a provider are good. At first, it might seem like this path is bad, because the customer is paying me, and then I’m paying the provider. Isn’t it possible that I make no money, or lose money from this transaction? That may be true, but if we didn’t participate in these routes, we would be a useless AS with no customers. An AS’s job is to give connectivity to its users, and participating in these customer-AS-provider routes unlocks more routes to the rest of the Internet.","zh_html":"一个邻居是客户，另一个是提供商的路由也是好的。乍一看，这条路径似乎不好，因为客户付钱给我，然后我又付钱给提供商。难道我可能从这笔交易中赚不到钱，甚至亏钱吗？这可能是真的，但如果我们不参与这些路由，我们就会成为一个没有客户的无用 AS。AS 的职责是给它的用户提供连接，参与这些客户-AS-提供商路由可以解锁通往互联网其他部分的更多路由。"},{"en":"Routes where both of my neighbors are peers are bad, because neither side is paying me to forward packets.","en_html":"Routes where both of my neighbors are peers are bad, because neither side is paying me to forward packets.","zh_html":"我的两个邻居都是对等体的路由是坏的，因为两边都不付钱给我转发分组。"},{"en":"More generally, peers do not provide transit between other peers. Thinking in terms of the hierarchy structure, a path should not stay at a given level for multiple hops.","en_html":"More generally, peers do not provide transit between other peers. Thinking in terms of the hierarchy structure, a path should not stay at a given level for multiple hops.","zh_html":"更一般地说，对等体(peers)不在其他对等体之间提供中转(transit)。从层次结构的角度来看，一条路径不应当在同一层级停留多跳。"},{"en":"Routes where one of my neighbors is a peer, and the other is a provider are also bad, because again, neither side is paying me to forward packets.","en_html":"Routes where one of my neighbors is a peer, and the other is a provider are also bad, because again, neither side is paying me to forward packets.","zh_html":"一个邻居是对等体，另一个是提供商的路由也是坏的，因为同样两边都不付钱给我转发分组。"},{"en":"More generally, if an AS has a peering link, that link will only carry traffic to/from its own customers. In other words, when packets arrive at B via that peering link, B’s only profitable option is to forward the packet to a customer (not a provider, and not another peer). Similarly, packets from customers can be forwarded through the peering link (customer pays), but packets from providers and peers cannot be forwarded through the peering link (nobody is paying).","en_html":"More generally, if an AS has a peering link, that link will only carry traffic to/from its own customers. In other words, when packets arrive at B via that peering link, B’s only profitable option is to forward the packet to a customer (not a provider, and not another peer). Similarly, packets from customers can be forwarded through the peering link (customer pays), but packets from providers and peers cannot be forwarded through the peering link (nobody is paying).","zh_html":"更一般地说，如果一个 AS 有一条对等链路(peering link)，该链路只承载来往于其自身客户的流量。换句话说，当分组通过对等链路到达 B 时，B 唯一有利可图的选择是将分组转发给一个客户（而不是提供商或其他对等体）。类似地，来自客户的分组可以通过对等链路转发（客户付费），但来自提供商和对等体的分组不能通过对等链路转发（没人付钱）。"},{"en":"Similarly, routes where both of my neighbors are providers are bad, because I have to pay both sides to forward the packet, and nobody is paying me to do this.","en_html":"Similarly, routes where both of my neighbors are providers are bad, because I have to pay both sides to forward the packet, and nobody is paying me to do this.","zh_html":"类似地，我的两个邻居都是提供商的路由是坏的，因为我必须向两边付钱来转发分组，而没人付钱给我做这件事。"},{"en":"Examples of Gao-Rexford Rules","en_html":"<a href=\"#examples-of-gao-rexford-rules\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"examples-of-gao-rexford-rules\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Examples of Gao-Rexford Rules","zh_html":"<a href=\"#examples-of-gao-rexford-rules\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"examples-of-gao-rexford-rules\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Gao-Rexford 规则示例(Examples of Gao-Rexford Rules)"},{"en":"The policy for selecting routes (customer best, provider worst), and the policy for announcing routes (only announce and participate in routes where one of my neighbors is a customer) will be used in our modified protocol to compute routes that respect each AS’s policy. We haven’t said how to compute routes yet, but given a route, we can check if it satisfies these two policies.","en_html":"The policy for selecting routes (customer best, provider worst), and the policy for announcing routes (only announce and participate in routes where one of my neighbors is a customer) will be used in our modified protocol to compute routes that respect each AS’s policy. We haven’t said how to compute routes yet, but given a route, we can check if it satisfies these two policies.","zh_html":"选择路由的策略（客户最好，提供商最差）和通告路由的策略（只通告和参与至少有一个邻居是客户的路由）将被用于我们修改后的协议中，以计算出尊重每个 AS 策略的路由。我们还没说如何计算路由，但给定一条路由，我们可以检查它是否满足这两个策略。"},{"en":"In this example, suppose that a computer in D (a stub AS) wants to talk to a computer in E (another stub AS). D and E might want to exchange messages (remember, arrows represent customer/provider relationships, not direction of packets).","en_html":"In this example, suppose that a computer in D (a stub AS) wants to talk to a computer in E (another stub AS). D and E might want to exchange messages (remember, arrows represent customer/provider relationships, not direction of packets).","zh_html":"在这个例子中，假设 D（一个末梢 AS）中的一台电脑想与 E（另一个末梢 AS）中的一台电脑通信。D 和 E 可能想要交换消息（记住，箭头表示客户/提供商关系，而不是分组的发送方向）。"},{"en":"One possible path for the traffic is D, B, A, C, E (and reverse for messages from E to D).","en_html":"One possible path for the traffic is D, B, A, C, E (and reverse for messages from E to D).","zh_html":"一条可能的流量路径是 D, B, A, C, E（对于从 E 到 D 的消息，反之亦然）。"},{"en":"Who is paying whom in this path? Since traffic is being sent along the D-B link, the customer (D) must pay the provider (B). Similarly, E must pay C, and B and C must both pay A.","en_html":"Who is paying whom in this path? Since traffic is being sent along the D-B link, the customer (D) must pay the provider (B). Similarly, E must pay C, and B and C must both pay A.","zh_html":"谁在这条路径上付钱给谁？由于流量沿着 D-B 链路发送，客户(D)必须付钱给提供商(B)。类似地，E 必须付钱给 C，B 和 C 都必须付钱给 A。"},{"en":"Will the transit ASes A, B, and C agree to announce and participate in this route? Let’s check each of their neighbors.","en_html":"Will the transit ASes A, B, and C agree to announce and participate in this route? Let’s check each of their neighbors.","zh_html":"中转 AS A、B 和 C 会同意通告并参与这条路由吗？让我们逐一检查它们的邻居。"},{"en":"A’s neighbors along this path are both customers, so A is making money, and thinks this path is good.","en_html":"A’s neighbors along this path are both customers, so A is making money, and thinks this path is good.","zh_html":"A 在这条路径上的邻居都是客户，所以 A 在赚钱，认为这条路径是好的。"},{"en":"B’s neighbors are a customer (D) and a provider (A). B is making money from the customer (D), and thinks this path is good. (Remember, paths with one customer neighbor and one provider neighbor are good, even if the AS has net profit of 0, because they enable greater connectivity.)","en_html":"B’s neighbors are a customer (D) and a provider (A). B is making money from the customer (D), and thinks this path is good. (Remember, paths with one customer neighbor and one provider neighbor are good, even if the AS has net profit of 0, because they enable greater connectivity.)","zh_html":"B 的邻居是一个客户(D)和一个提供商(A)。B 从客户(D)那里赚钱，认为这条路径是好的。（记住，有一个客户邻居和一个提供商邻居的路径是好的，即使 AS 的净利润为 0，因为它们实现了更大的连通性。）"},{"en":"Similarly, C has at least one customer neighbor (E), so it also thinks this route is good.","en_html":"Similarly, C has at least one customer neighbor (E), so it also thinks this route is good.","zh_html":"类似地，C 至少有一个客户邻居(E)，所以它也认为这条路由是好的。"},{"en":"Instead of B and C both paying A, perhaps they choose to establish a peer relationship, which causes the AS graph to change:","en_html":"Instead of B and C both paying A, perhaps they choose to establish a peer relationship, which causes the AS graph to change:","zh_html":"B 和 C 不都付钱给 A，而是可能选择建立对等关系(peer relationship)，这会导致 AS 图发生变化："},{"en":"Now, another possible path for the traffic is D, B, C, E. Now, D still needs to pay B, and E still has to pay C. However, B and C no longer need to pay A, and they don’t pay each other (peering relationship).","en_html":"Now, another possible path for the traffic is D, B, C, E. Now, D still needs to pay B, and E still has to pay C. However, B and C no longer need to pay A, and they don’t pay each other (peering relationship).","zh_html":"现在，另一条可能的流量路径是 D, B, C, E。现在，D 仍然需要付钱给 B，E 仍然需要付钱给 C。然而，B 和 C 不再需要付钱给 A，她们也不付钱给对方（对等关系）。"},{"en":"Again, we can check if the transit ASes on this path, namely B and C, will agree to announce and participate in this route. B’s neighbors are a customer (D) and a provider (C). B is making money from the customer (D), and thinks this path is good. Similarly, C has at least one customer neighbor (E), so C also thinks this path is good.","en_html":"Again, we can check if the transit ASes on this path, namely B and C, will agree to announce and participate in this route. B’s neighbors are a customer (D) and a provider (C). B is making money from the customer (D), and thinks this path is good. Similarly, C has at least one customer neighbor (E), so C also thinks this path is good.","zh_html":"同样，我们可以检查这条路径上的中转 AS，即 B 和 C，是否会同意通告并参与这条路由。B 的邻居是一个客户(D)和一个提供商(C)。B 从客户(D)那里赚钱，认为这条路径是好的。类似地，C 至少有一个客户邻居(E)，所以 C 也认为这条路径是好的。"},{"en":"We’ve just reasoned that there are two good paths that can be used to send messages from D to E. Now, B must decide to forward through either path B-A-C-E, or path B-C-E. Which path should B choose? According to our first principle, B prefers the most profitable path (not the shortest path). In B-A-C-E, the next hop is provider A (who we’d have to pay), and in B-C-E, the next hop is peer C (no payment needed). Therefore, B will select the path through C, giving final path D-B-C-E.","en_html":"We’ve just reasoned that there are two good paths that can be used to send messages from D to E. Now, B must decide to forward through either path B-A-C-E, or path B-C-E. Which path should B choose? According to our first principle, B prefers the most profitable path (not the shortest path). In B-A-C-E, the next hop is provider A (who we’d have to pay), and in B-C-E, the next hop is peer C (no payment needed). Therefore, B will select the path through C, giving final path D-B-C-E.","zh_html":"我们已经推断出有两条好路径可以用来从 D 向 E 发送消息。现在，B 必须决定是通过路径 B-A-C-E 转发，还是通过路径 B-C-E 转发。B 应该选择哪条路径？根据我们的第一个原则，B 偏好最有利可图的路径（而不是最短路径）。在 B-A-C-E 中，下一跳是提供商 A（我们得付钱给他），而在 B-C-E 中，下一跳是对等体 C（无需付款）。因此，B 会选择经过 C 的路径，最终路径为 D-B-C-E。"},{"en":"Note: It seems like B and C are saving money with the additional peering relationship, so why wouldn’t every AS establish peer relationships to save money? In real life, establishing a link also requires installing physical infrastructure (e.g. laying cables underground), so there’s a cost trade-off to establishing new relationships between ASes, in exchange for cheaper routes.","en_html":"Note: It seems like B and C are saving money with the additional peering relationship, so why wouldn’t every AS establish peer relationships to save money? In real life, establishing a link also requires installing physical infrastructure (e.g. laying cables underground), so there’s a cost trade-off to establishing new relationships between ASes, in exchange for cheaper routes.","zh_html":"注意：看起来 B 和 C 通过额外的对等关系(peering relationship)省了钱，那为什么不是每个 AS 都建立对等关系来省钱呢？在现实中，建立链路还需要安装物理基础设施（例如在地下铺设光缆），所以在 AS 之间建立新关系需要权衡成本，以换取更便宜的路由。"},{"en":"Routes are Valley-Free","en_html":"<a href=\"#routes-are-valley-free\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"routes-are-valley-free\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Routes are Valley-Free","zh_html":"<a href=\"#routes-are-valley-free\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"routes-are-valley-free\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 路由是无谷的(Routes are Valley-Free)"},{"en":"More generally, paths in the AS graph are always valley-free.","en_html":"More generally, paths in the AS graph are always <strong>valley-free</strong>.","zh_html":"更一般地说，AS 图(graph)中的路径(paths)总是<strong>无谷的(valley-free)</strong>。"},{"en":"Thinking in terms of the hierarchy structure, if a path includes a lateral hop via a peering link, the immediate next hop needs to go downhill to a customer. The next hop cannot be lateral again (both neighbors peers), and the next hop cannot be uphill to a provider (peer and provider neighbors).","en_html":"Thinking in terms of the hierarchy structure, if a path includes a lateral hop via a peering link, the immediate next hop needs to go downhill to a customer. The next hop cannot be lateral again (both neighbors peers), and the next hop cannot be uphill to a provider (peer and provider neighbors).","zh_html":"从层次结构的角度来看，如果一条路径包含经过对等链路(peering link)的水平跳，紧接着的下一跳需要下行到客户。下一跳不能再次是水平的（两个邻居都是对等体），也不能上行到提供商（对等体和提供商邻居）。"},{"en":"Thinking in terms of the hierarchy structure, if a path includes a downward hop from provider to customer, the immediate next hop must continue to go downhill to one of its customers. The next hop cannot be lateral again (neighbors are provider and peer), and the next hop cannot be uphill (neighbors are both providers).","en_html":"Thinking in terms of the hierarchy structure, if a path includes a downward hop from provider to customer, the immediate next hop must continue to go downhill to one of its customers. The next hop cannot be lateral again (neighbors are provider and peer), and the next hop cannot be uphill (neighbors are both providers).","zh_html":"从层次结构的角度来看，如果一条路径包含从提供商到客户的下行跳，紧接着的下一跳必须继续下行到它的一个客户。下一跳不能再次是水平的（邻居是提供商和对等体），也不能上行（两个邻居都是提供商）。"},{"en":"If a downhill link must be followed by another downhill link, then we can conclude that as soon as you have a downhill link in a path, all subsequent links must also be downhill. A valley is a path that goes downhill, and then turns around to start going uphill. Paths cannot contain valleys, because once you start going downhill, you must continue downhill all the way to the destination.","en_html":"If a downhill link must be followed by another downhill link, then we can conclude that as soon as you have a downhill link in a path, all subsequent links must also be downhill. A valley is a path that goes downhill, and then turns around to start going uphill. Paths cannot contain valleys, because once you start going downhill, you must continue downhill all the way to the destination.","zh_html":"如果一个下行链路必须跟着另一个下行链路，那么我们可以得出结论：一旦路径中有一个下行链路，所有后续链路也必须都是下行链路。谷(valley)是指一条路径先下行，然后掉头开始上行。路径不能包含谷，因为一旦你开始下行，你必须一直下行到目的地。"},{"en":"In summary, here are the rules we’ve derived (though it’s better to understand them in terms of respecting AS money preferences, instead of memorizing them):","en_html":"In summary, here are the rules we’ve derived (though it’s better to understand them in terms of respecting AS money preferences, instead of memorizing them):","zh_html":"总结一下，以下是推导出的规则（不过最好从尊重 AS 金钱偏好的角度去理解，而不是死记硬背）："},{"en":"An uphill link can be followed by peering link, a downhill link, or another uphill link. (If the previous hop pays me money, I’m happy to forward the packet to anybody.)","en_html":"An uphill link can be followed by peering link, a downhill link, or another uphill link. (If the previous hop pays me money, I’m happy to forward the packet to anybody.)","zh_html":"上行链路可以跟着对等链路、下行链路或另一个上行链路。（如果上一跳付钱给我，我很乐意把分组转发给任何人。）"},{"en":"A peering link can only be followed by a downhill link. (If the previous hop isn’t paying me, I need the next hop to be a customer that pays me.)","en_html":"A peering link can only be followed by a downhill link. (If the previous hop isn’t paying me, I need the next hop to be a customer that pays me.)","zh_html":"对等链路只能跟着下行链路。（如果上一跳不付钱给我，我需要下一跳是一个付钱给我的客户。）"},{"en":"A downhill link can only be followed by a downhill link. (If the previous hop is a provider I’m paying, I need the next hop to be a customer that pays me.)","en_html":"A downhill link can only be followed by a downhill link. (If the previous hop is a provider I’m paying, I need the next hop to be a customer that pays me.)","zh_html":"下行链路只能跟着下行链路。（如果上一跳是我付钱给的提供商，我需要下一跳是一个付钱给我的客户。）"},{"en":"These rules mean that routes are always valley-free and single-peaked. A route can start 0 or more climbing uphill links. Eventually, it will reach a single peak, and traverse 0 or 1 peering links. Then, the route must start going downhill all the way to the destination (no more lateral or uphill moves).","en_html":"These rules mean that routes are always valley-free and single-peaked. A route can start 0 or more climbing uphill links. Eventually, it will reach a single peak, and traverse 0 or 1 peering links. Then, the route must start going downhill all the way to the destination (no more lateral or uphill moves).","zh_html":"这些规则意味着路径(paths)总是无谷的(valley-free)且单峰的(single-peaked)。一条路径可以以 0 个或多个上行链路开始。最终，它会到达一个峰顶，并经过 0 或 1 个对等链路。然后，路径必须一路下行到目的地（不再有水平或上行移动）。"},{"en":"Paths cannot have valleys (going downhill and then turning to go back uphill). Also, paths cannot have lateral moves anywhere except the peak. As soon as you make a lateral move, you must turn around and go back down. You cannot continue traveling laterally or uphill.","en_html":"Paths cannot have valleys (going downhill and then turning to go back uphill). Also, paths cannot have lateral moves anywhere except the peak. As soon as you make a lateral move, you must turn around and go back down. You cannot continue traveling laterally or uphill.","zh_html":"路径不能有谷（先下行然后掉头再上行）。此外，除峰顶外，路径不能有任何水平移动。一旦你做了水平移动，你必须掉头回去下行。你不能继续水平或上行。"},{"en":"ASes Want Autonomy and Privacy","en_html":"<a href=\"#ases-want-autonomy-and-privacy\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ases-want-autonomy-and-privacy\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> ASes Want Autonomy and Privacy","zh_html":"<a href=\"#ases-want-autonomy-and-privacy\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ases-want-autonomy-and-privacy\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> AS 需要自治权和隐私(ASes Want Autonomy and Privacy)"},{"en":"When designing a protocol for computing inter-domain routes, our protocol should respect the autonomy and privacy of each AS.","en_html":"When designing a protocol for computing inter-domain routes, our protocol should respect the autonomy and privacy of each AS.","zh_html":"在设计用于计算域间路由(inter-domain routes)的协议时，我们的协议应尊重每个 AS 的自治权(autonomy)和隐私(privacy)。"},{"en":"ASes want autonomy, the freedom to choose their own arbitrary policies, without coordinating with other ASes, or worrying about what policies the protocol allows. In practice, the policies usually follow the money-based principles we described, but the protocol shouldn’t force the AS to follow any specific policy.","en_html":"ASes want <strong>autonomy</strong>, the freedom to choose their own arbitrary policies, without coordinating with other ASes, or worrying about what policies the protocol allows. In practice, the policies usually follow the money-based principles we described, but the protocol shouldn’t force the AS to follow any specific policy.","zh_html":"AS 需要<strong>自治权(autonomy)</strong>，即自由选择任意策略的权利，无需与其他 AS 协调，也无需担心协议允许什么策略。在实践中，策略通常遵循我们描述的基于金钱的原则，但协议不应该强制 AS 遵守任何特定策略。"},{"en":"ASes also want privacy. ASes don’t want to have to explicitly tell others in the network about their preferences and policies. For example, an AS shouldn’t need to explicitly tell everybody about whether its neighbors are peers, customers, or providers. This reflects real-world business strategies. As a company, you might not want to reveal information about your customers and providers to your rivals.","en_html":"ASes also want <strong>privacy</strong>. ASes don’t want to have to explicitly tell others in the network about their preferences and policies. For example, an AS shouldn’t need to explicitly tell everybody about whether its neighbors are peers, customers, or providers. This reflects real-world business strategies. As a company, you might not want to reveal information about your customers and providers to your rivals.","zh_html":"AS 也需要<strong>隐私(privacy)</strong>。AS 不希望必须明确告诉网络中的其他人它们的偏好和策略。例如，一个 AS 不需要明确告诉所有人它的邻居是对等体、客户还是提供商。这反映了现实的商业策略。作为一家公司，你可能不希望向竞争对手透露你的客户和提供商信息。"},{"en":"Note that our definition of privacy says that ASes shouldn’t need to explicitly reveal their policies. In practice, ASes still need to coordinate with the rest of the network to agree on paths through the network, so some amount of information leakage is inevitable. Reverse-engineering techniques exist to trace the routes packets are taking through the network.","en_html":"Note that our definition of privacy says that ASes shouldn’t need to <em>explicitly</em> reveal their policies. In practice, ASes still need to coordinate with the rest of the network to agree on paths through the network, so some amount of information leakage is inevitable. Reverse-engineering techniques exist to trace the routes packets are taking through the network.","zh_html":"注意，我们对隐私的定义说 AS 不需要<em>明确</em>透露其策略。在实践中，AS 仍然需要与网络的其余部分协调以就网络路径达成一致，因此一定程度的的信息泄露是不可避免的。存在逆向工程技术来追踪分组经过网络的路径。"},{"en":"For example, it’s unavoidable that others on the network can discover what route a packet is taking. However, our protocol shouldn’t force an AS to tell the world “I liked this path more than this other path.” We also shouldn’t force an AS to disclose who their providers, peers, and customers are.","en_html":"For example, it’s unavoidable that others on the network can discover what route a packet is taking. However, our protocol shouldn’t force an AS to tell the world “I liked this path more than this other path.” We also shouldn’t force an AS to disclose who their providers, peers, and customers are.","zh_html":"例如，网络中其他人发现分组走的是哪条路径是不可避免的。然而，我们的协议不应该强制 AS 告诉世界\"我更喜欢这条路径而非那条路径。\"我们也不应该强制 AS 披露谁是它的提供商、对等体和客户。"}]},"/routing/bgp.html":{"title":"bgp","blocks":[{"en":"Border Gateway Protocol (BGP)","en_html":"<a href=\"#border-gateway-protocol-bgp\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"border-gateway-protocol-bgp\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Border Gateway Protocol (BGP)","zh_html":"<a href=\"#border-gateway-protocol-bgp\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"border-gateway-protocol-bgp\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 边界网关协议(Border Gateway Protocol, BGP)"},{"en":"Brief History of BGP","en_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-bgp\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-bgp\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Brief History of BGP","zh_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-bgp\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-bgp\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> BGP 简史(Brief History of BGP)"},{"en":"Least-cost routing protocols are closely related to the shortest-paths problem in graph theory, which has been studied in computer science even before the Internet existed. Dijkstra’s algorithm is from 1956, and the Bellman-Ford algorithm is from 1958. When developing early routing protocols, designers could adapt the ideas from these algorithms.","en_html":"Least-cost routing protocols are closely related to the shortest-paths problem in graph theory, which has been studied in computer science even before the Internet existed. Dijkstra’s algorithm is from 1956, and the Bellman-Ford algorithm is from 1958. When developing early routing protocols, designers could adapt the ideas from these algorithms.","zh_html":"最小成本路由协议(least-cost routing protocols)与图论(graph theory)中的最短路径问题(shortest-paths problem)密切相关，这在互联网出现之前就已在计算机科学中研究过。Dijkstra 算法来自 1956 年，Bellman-Ford 算法来自 1958 年。在开发早期路由协议时，设计者可以借鉴这些算法的思想。"},{"en":"In the early days, the Internet was a government-funded project, where the network was centrally controlled by the US Department of Defense. The notion of autonomous systems didn’t exist yet, and least-cost algorithms could be scaled up to the small size of the early Internet. Eventually, as the Internet grew, the government transferred control over to different commercial entities, who had to develop inter-domain routing protocols on the fly.","en_html":"In the early days, the Internet was a government-funded project, where the network was centrally controlled by the US Department of Defense. The notion of autonomous systems didn’t exist yet, and least-cost algorithms could be scaled up to the small size of the early Internet. Eventually, as the Internet grew, the government transferred control over to different commercial entities, who had to develop inter-domain routing protocols on the fly.","zh_html":"在早期，互联网是一个政府资助的项目，网络由美国国防部集中控制。自治系统(autonomous systems)的概念还不存在，最小成本算法可以扩展到早期互联网的小规模。最终，随着互联网的发展，政府将控制权移交给不同的商业实体，这些实体必须临时开发域间路由协议(inter-domain routing protocols)。"},{"en":"Unlike the early least-cost routing protocols, the notion of autonomous systems each having their own private policies had no precedent in computer science. The ideas behind inter-domain routing protocols had to be developed on the fly in response to the needs of these new Internet companies.","en_html":"Unlike the early least-cost routing protocols, the notion of autonomous systems each having their own private policies had no precedent in computer science. The ideas behind inter-domain routing protocols had to be developed on the fly in response to the needs of these new Internet companies.","zh_html":"与早期的最小成本路由协议不同，每个自治系统都有自己的私有策略(private policies)这一概念在计算机科学中没有先例。域间路由协议的思想必须临时开发，以满足这些新的互联网公司的需求。"},{"en":"BGP was created in 1989-1995, and its ad-hoc development process means that the protocol isn’t perfect. If we could rewrite the protocol from scratch today, the result might look different. However, the protocol has proven to be effective and resilient, and is still the inter-domain routing protocol in use today. (Remember, everybody has to agree to use the same inter-domain routing protocol, so there is only one.)","en_html":"BGP was created in 1989-1995, and its ad-hoc development process means that the protocol isn’t perfect. If we could rewrite the protocol from scratch today, the result might look different. However, the protocol has proven to be effective and resilient, and is still the inter-domain routing protocol in use today. (Remember, everybody has to agree to use the same inter-domain routing protocol, so there is only one.)","zh_html":"BGP 创建于 1989-1995 年，其 ad-hoc 的开发过程意味着协议并不完美。如果我们今天从头重写协议，结果可能会有所不同。然而，该协议已被证明是有效且 resilient 的，并且至今仍在使用中的域间路由协议。（记住，每个人都必须同意使用相同的域间路由协议，所以只有一个。）"},{"en":"BGP is based on Distance-Vector","en_html":"<a href=\"#bgp-is-based-on-distance-vector\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"bgp-is-based-on-distance-vector\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> BGP is based on Distance-Vector","zh_html":"<a href=\"#bgp-is-based-on-distance-vector\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"bgp-is-based-on-distance-vector\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> BGP 基于距离向量(BGP is based on Distance-Vector)"},{"en":"Recall that we saw two classes of intra-domain routing algorithms: distance-vector algorithms and link-state algorithms. When designing BGP, which class of algorithm would be a better starting point for our design?","en_html":"Recall that we saw two classes of intra-domain routing algorithms: distance-vector algorithms and link-state algorithms. When designing BGP, which class of algorithm would be a better starting point for our design?","zh_html":"回想一下，我们学习了两类域内路由算法(intra-domain routing algorithms)：距离向量算法(distance-vector algorithms)和链路状态算法(link-state algorithms)。在设计 BGP 时，哪类算法更适合作为设计的起点？"},{"en":"Remember that in BGP, we need to respect the privacy of individual ASes. If we used a link-state protocol, then every AS has to tell the entire network about its policies, so that everybody has the full knowledge to compute routes by themselves.","en_html":"Remember that in BGP, we need to respect the privacy of individual ASes. If we used a link-state protocol, then every AS has to tell the entire network about its policies, so that everybody has the full knowledge to compute routes by themselves.","zh_html":"记住，在 BGP 中，我们需要尊重各个 AS 的隐私(privacy)。如果我们使用链路状态协议(link-state protocol)，那么每个 AS 必须告诉整个网络它的策略，这样每个人才能拥有完整知识来自己计算路由。"},{"en":"Also, in BGP, we need to respect autonomy and allow each AS to make its own policy decisions. However, a link-state protocol requires everybody to compute routes in some consistent way (e.g. everyone agrees to use least-cost paths).","en_html":"Also, in BGP, we need to respect autonomy and allow each AS to make its own policy decisions. However, a link-state protocol requires everybody to compute routes in some consistent way (e.g. everyone agrees to use least-cost paths).","zh_html":"同时，在 BGP 中，我们需要尊重自治权(autonomy)，允许每个 AS 做出自己的策略决策。然而，链路状态协议要求每个人都以某种一致的方式计算路由（例如，每个人都同意使用最小成本路径）。"},{"en":"Link-state algorithm don’t respect the privacy or autonomy of ASes, so link-state would be a poor choice of algorithm to design BGP around. By contrast, distance-vector would allow every individual AS to make its own decisions about what routes to accept/reject, and what routes to announce. Also, because distance-vector is not a global protocol, each AS doesn’t need to know about everyone else’s policies in order to compute valid routes.","en_html":"Link-state algorithm don’t respect the privacy or autonomy of ASes, so link-state would be a poor choice of algorithm to design BGP around. By contrast, distance-vector would allow every individual AS to make its own decisions about what routes to accept/reject, and what routes to announce. Also, because distance-vector is not a global protocol, each AS doesn’t need to know about everyone else’s policies in order to compute valid routes.","zh_html":"链路状态算法不尊重 AS 的隐私或自治权，所以链路状态不适合作为设计 BGP 的算法基础。相比之下，距离向量(distance-vector)会让每个 AS 自行决定接受/拒绝哪些路由，以及通告哪些路由。此外，由于距离向量不是全局协议，每个 AS 不需要知道其他所有人的策略就能计算出有效的路由。"},{"en":"Many core ideas in distance-vector protocols will still apply in BGP. The advertisements that we send and receive will still be specific to one destination. Just like in previous sections, we’ll think about advertisements and routes for a single destination, but know that the protocol is being run for multiple destinations simultaneously.","en_html":"Many core ideas in distance-vector protocols will still apply in BGP. The advertisements that we send and receive will still be specific to one destination. Just like in previous sections, we’ll think about advertisements and routes for a single destination, but know that the protocol is being run for multiple destinations simultaneously.","zh_html":"距离向量协议中的许多核心思想仍然适用于 BGP。我们发送和接收的通告(advertisements)仍然针对单个目的地(destination)。和之前一样，我们会从单个目的地的角度来思考通告和路由，但要知道协议是同时为多个目的地运行的。"},{"en":"In both distance-vector protocols and BGP, each AS computes routes using only information from the advertisements it receives, without seeing a global picture of the network topology. Also, in both types of protocols, the AS will send and receive advertisements indefinitely, until everybody has converged on a set of routes.","en_html":"In both distance-vector protocols and BGP, each AS computes routes using only information from the advertisements it receives, without seeing a global picture of the network topology. Also, in both types of protocols, the AS will send and receive advertisements indefinitely, until everybody has converged on a set of routes.","zh_html":"在距离向量协议和 BGP 中，每个 AS 仅使用接收到的通告中的信息来计算路由，而不需要看到网络拓扑的全貌。同样，在这两种协议中，AS 会无限期地发送和接收通告，直到所有人收敛(converge)到一组路由。"},{"en":"BGP follows the same core idea as distance-vector protocols, but with a slight change in terminology. Instead of saying that each AS announces or advertises routes, we say that the AS is exporting routes. Then, each AS listens to advertisements and selects its preferred route, which we’ll call importing routes.","en_html":"BGP follows the same core idea as distance-vector protocols, but with a slight change in terminology. Instead of saying that each AS announces or advertises routes, we say that the AS is <strong>exporting</strong> routes. Then, each AS listens to advertisements and selects its preferred route, which we’ll call <strong>importing</strong> routes.","zh_html":"BGP 遵循与距离向量协议相同的核心思想，但术语略有变化。我们不称每个 AS 在通告(announces)或广播路由，而说 AS 在<strong>导出(exporting)</strong>路由。然后，每个 AS 监听通告并选择其首选路由，我们称之为<strong>导入(importing)</strong>路由。"},{"en":"Distance-vector is a good starting point, but what’s missing?","en_html":"Distance-vector is a good starting point, but what’s missing?","zh_html":"距离向量(distance-vector)是一个好的起点，但缺少了什么？"},{"en":"Distance-vector protocols are designed to find least-cost routes, but in BGP, we want routes to be decided based on each AS’s individual policies.","en_html":"Distance-vector protocols are designed to find least-cost routes, but in BGP, we want routes to be decided based on each AS’s individual policies.","zh_html":"距离向量协议旨在找到最小成本路由，但在 BGP 中，我们希望路由基于每个 AS 的个体策略(policies)来决定。"},{"en":"Policy-Based Importing and Exporting","en_html":"<a href=\"#policy-based-importing-and-exporting\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"policy-based-importing-and-exporting\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Policy-Based Importing and Exporting","zh_html":"<a href=\"#policy-based-importing-and-exporting\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"policy-based-importing-and-exporting\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 基于策略的导入和导出(Policy-Based Importing and Exporting)"},{"en":"At a high level, in order to support policies, we will change the rules for importing and exporting routes. Each AS will only export (advertise) routes that the AS likes (according to its policy). Also, when importing (selecting) routes, the AS will select the best route according to policy, not distance.","en_html":"At a high level, in order to support policies, we will change the rules for importing and exporting routes. Each AS will only export (advertise) routes that the AS likes (according to its policy). Also, when importing (selecting) routes, the AS will select the best route according to policy, not distance.","zh_html":"在高层面上，为了支持策略，我们将改变导入(importing)和导出(exporting)路由的规则。每个 AS 只导出（通告）它喜欢的路由（根据其策略）。同时，在导入（选择）路由时，AS 会根据策略选择最佳路由，而不是距离。"},{"en":"When an AS receives multiple advertisements for the same destination, instead of picking the shortest route, the AS now selects (imports) a route based on policy.","en_html":"When an AS receives multiple advertisements for the same destination, instead of picking the shortest route, the AS now selects (imports) a route based on policy.","zh_html":"当一个 AS 收到针对同一目的地的多个通告时，不再选择最短的路由，而是基于策略(policy)选择（导入）一条路由。"},{"en":"Remember that advertisements propagate outward from the destination, and messages are forwarded closer to the destination (the opposite direction from advertisements). The import decision dictates where an AS is sending its outbound traffic. For example, if S hears advertisements from A, B, and C about the same destination, S’s import decision (A or B or C) determines where packets for that destination will be forwarded.","en_html":"Remember that advertisements propagate outward from the destination, and messages are forwarded closer to the destination (the opposite direction from advertisements). The import decision dictates where an AS is sending its outbound traffic. For example, if S hears advertisements from A, B, and C about the same destination, S’s import decision (A or B or C) determines where packets for that destination will be forwarded.","zh_html":"记住，通告从目的地向外传播，而消息被转发到更接近目的地（与通告方向相反）。导入决策(import decision)决定了 AS 将其出站流量(outbound traffic)发送到哪里。例如，如果 S 听到来自 A、B 和 C 关于同一目的地的通告，S 的导入决策（A 还是 B 还是 C）决定了该目的地的分组将被转发到哪里。"},{"en":"In the distance-vector protocol, when I receive an announcement and install a new route, I always announce this new route to all my neighbors.","en_html":"In the distance-vector protocol, when I receive an announcement and install a new route, I always announce this new route to all my neighbors.","zh_html":"在距离向量协议中，当我收到一个通告并安装了一条新路由时，我总是向所有邻居通告这条新路由。"},{"en":"Now that ASes have their own policies, they can choose whether or not they want to participate in a route. If an AS has a route it potentially dislikes, it can now choose to not export that route to certain neighbors.","en_html":"Now that ASes have their own policies, they can choose whether or not they want to participate in a route. If an AS has a route it potentially dislikes, it can now choose to not export that route to certain neighbors.","zh_html":"现在 AS 有了自己的策略，它们可以选择是否想参与某条路由。如果一个 AS 有一条可能不喜欢的路由，它可以选择不向某些邻居导出该路由。"},{"en":"For example, suppose my policy is that I don’t want to carry C’s traffic. This could be because of monetary reasons, or it could be some other policy decision by me. When I accept an advertisement and install a route, it’s okay if I don’t advertise that route to C.","en_html":"For example, suppose my policy is that I don’t want to carry C’s traffic. This could be because of monetary reasons, or it could be some other policy decision by me. When I accept an advertisement and install a route, it’s okay if I don’t advertise that route to C.","zh_html":"例如，假设我的策略是不想承载 C 的流量。这可能出于金钱原因，也可能是我做出的其他策略决定。当我接受一个通告并安装一条路由时，我不向 C 通告该路由是可以的。"},{"en":"Again, remember that data flows in the opposite direction from advertisements. The export decision dictates what inbound traffic an AS is willing to carry. If I export a route, I am agreeing to participate in this route and let other people forward packets to me along this route.","en_html":"Again, remember that data flows in the opposite direction from advertisements. The export decision dictates what inbound traffic an AS is willing to carry. If I export a route, I am agreeing to participate in this route and let other people forward packets to me along this route.","zh_html":"再次强调，数据流与通告方向相反。导出决策(export decision)决定了 AS 愿意承载什么样的入站流量(inbound traffic)。如果我导出了一条路由，我就是同意参与这条路由，并允许其他人沿着这条路由向我转发分组。"},{"en":"A consequence of this rule is, even if the underlying graph is connected (a path exists between any two nodes), it is not guaranteed that every AS can reach every other AS. In practice, we’ll be able to guarantee reachability by establishing some conventions about the ASes’ policies and the structure of the AS graph.","en_html":"A consequence of this rule is, even if the underlying graph is connected (a path exists between any two nodes), it is not guaranteed that every AS can reach every other AS. In practice, we’ll be able to guarantee reachability by establishing some conventions about the ASes’ policies and the structure of the AS graph.","zh_html":"这个规则的一个后果是：即使底层图是连通的（任意两个节点之间存在路径），也不能保证每个 AS 都能到达其他每个 AS。在实践中，我们可以通过建立关于 AS 策略和 AS 图结构的一些惯例来保证可达性(reachability)。"},{"en":"Implementing Gao-Rexford Rules","en_html":"<a href=\"#implementing-gao-rexford-rules\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"implementing-gao-rexford-rules\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Implementing Gao-Rexford Rules","zh_html":"<a href=\"#implementing-gao-rexford-rules\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"implementing-gao-rexford-rules\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 实现 Gao-Rexford 规则(Implementing Gao-Rexford Rules)"},{"en":"In general, BGP supports arbitrary policies, but arbitrary policies don’t give us any guarantees that the Internet is fully connected (packets can go from any source to any destination).","en_html":"In general, BGP supports arbitrary policies, but arbitrary policies don’t give us any guarantees that the Internet is fully connected (packets can go from any source to any destination).","zh_html":"一般来说，BGP 支持任意策略，但任意策略不能给我们任何保证，保证互联网是完全连通的（分组可以从任何源发送到任何目的地）。"},{"en":"Recall that the Gao-Rexford rules enforce a more restrictive set of policies, based on common money-based import and export policies. Nobody enforces that an AS must follow these rules. However, if ASes agree to follow these rules, we can make stronger assumptions about Internet connectivity.","en_html":"Recall that the <strong>Gao-Rexford rules</strong> enforce a more restrictive set of policies, based on common money-based import and export policies. Nobody enforces that an AS must follow these rules. However, if ASes agree to follow these rules, we can make stronger assumptions about Internet connectivity.","zh_html":"回想一下，<strong>Gao-Rexford 规则(Gao-Rexford rules)</strong>强制了一套更严格的策略集合，基于常见的基于金钱的导入和导出策略。没有人强制 AS 必须遵循这些规则。然而，如果 AS 同意遵循这些规则，我们可以对互联网连通性做出更强的假设。"},{"en":"Brief history: The rules are named for Lixin Gao and Jennifer Rexford at AT&amp;T in the 1990s. Back then, each AS made up their own policies on the fly. Gao and Rexford surveyed ASes about their policies to come up with these rules, and used them to prove guarantees about the Internet.","en_html":"Brief history: The rules are named for Lixin Gao and Jennifer Rexford at AT&amp;T in the 1990s. Back then, each AS made up their own policies on the fly. Gao and Rexford surveyed ASes about their policies to come up with these rules, and used them to prove guarantees about the Internet.","zh_html":"背景历史：这些规则以 Lixin Gao 和 Jennifer Rexford 命名，她们在 1990 年代任职于 AT&T。当时，每个 AS 临时制定自己的策略。Gao 和 Rexford 调查了 AS 的策略，总结出这些规则，并用它们来证明关于互联网的保证。"},{"en":"When importing routes, the Gao-Rexford rules say that the AS prefers to import a route advertised by a customer, over a route advertised by a peer, over a route advertised by a provider.","en_html":"When importing routes, the Gao-Rexford rules say that the AS prefers to import a route advertised by a customer, over a route advertised by a peer, over a route advertised by a provider.","zh_html":"在导入路由时，Gao-Rexford 规则规定，AS 优先导入由客户(customer)通告的路由，其次是对等体(peer)通告的路由，最后是提供商(provider)通告的路由。"},{"en":"In practice, ASes also implement additional tiebreaking rules in addition to the Gao-Rexford rules. For example, if I receive advertisements from two customers, I need some additional tiebreaker to prefer one of them. Performance is a common tiebreaker, where we pick routes with higher bandwidth or shorter paths.","en_html":"In practice, ASes also implement additional tiebreaking rules in addition to the Gao-Rexford rules. For example, if I receive advertisements from two customers, I need some additional tiebreaker to prefer one of them. Performance is a common tiebreaker, where we pick routes with higher bandwidth or shorter paths.","zh_html":"在实践中，除了 Gao-Rexford 规则之外，AS 还会实现额外的破平局规则(tiebreaking rules)。例如，如果我收到两个客户的通告，我需要额外的规则来优先选择其中一个。性能(performance)是一种常见的破平局规则，我们选择带宽(bandwidth)更高或路径更短的路由。"},{"en":"Based on the Gao-Rexford rules, how should we export paths? Recall that an AS agrees to participate in a route if at least one neighbor is a customer. Therefore, the AS should only advertise routes if the resulting route, if accepted, has a neighbor on one side.","en_html":"Based on the Gao-Rexford rules, how should we export paths? Recall that an AS agrees to participate in a route if at least one neighbor is a customer. Therefore, the AS should only advertise routes if the resulting route, if accepted, has a neighbor on one side.","zh_html":"基于 Gao-Rexford 规则，我们应该如何导出路径？回想一下，如果一个 AS 至少有一个邻居是客户，它就同意参与这条路由。因此，只有当结果路由被接受后会有一侧有客户邻居时，AS 才应该通告该路由。"},{"en":"Let’s go through all the specific cases.","en_html":"Let’s go through all the specific cases.","zh_html":"让我们检查所有具体的情况。"},{"en":"I receive and install a route from a customer. This means that the next hop on this route is that customer. Who should I export this route to? I’ve already guaranteed that there’s a customer on one side paying me, so I can export this route to everybody (customers, providers, and peers).","en_html":"I receive and install a route from a customer. This means that the next hop on this route is that customer. Who should I export this route to? I’ve already guaranteed that there’s a customer on one side paying me, so I can export this route to everybody (customers, providers, and peers).","zh_html":"我从一个客户收到并安装了一条路由。这意味着这条路由的下一跳(next hop)就是那个客户。我应该向谁导出这条路由？我已经保证了有一侧是客户在付钱给我，所以我可以向所有人（客户、提供商和对等体）导出这条路由。"},{"en":"I receive and install a route from a peer (the next hop is a peer). Who should I export this route to? Nobody is paying me yet, so I should only export this route to customers. If I export this route to a peer or provider who accepts, then I’ve created a route where neither side is paying me.","en_html":"I receive and install a route from a peer (the next hop is a peer). Who should I export this route to? Nobody is paying me yet, so I should only export this route to customers. If I export this route to a peer or provider who accepts, then I’ve created a route where neither side is paying me.","zh_html":"我从一个对等体收到并安装了一条路由（下一跳是一个对等体）。我应该向谁导出这条路由？还没有人在付钱给我，所以我只应该向客户导出这条路由。如果我把这条路由导出给一个对等体或提供商并且他们接受了，那么我创造了一条两侧都不付钱给我的路由。"},{"en":"Similarly, if I receive and install a route from a provider, I should only export this route to customers, because I need at least one side to pay me, and the provider isn’t paying.","en_html":"Similarly, if I receive and install a route from a provider, I should only export this route to customers, because I need at least one side to pay me, and the provider isn’t paying.","zh_html":"类似地，如果我从一个提供商收到并安装了一条路由，我应该只向客户导出这条路由，因为我需要至少一侧付钱给我，而提供商不付钱。"},{"en":"The Gao-Rexford rules allow us to provably show that this statement is true: Assuming that the AS graph is hierarchical and acyclic, and all ASes follow the Gao-Rexford rules, then we can guarantee reachability and convergence in steady state.","en_html":"The Gao-Rexford rules allow us to provably show that this statement is true: Assuming that the AS graph is hierarchical and acyclic, and all ASes follow the Gao-Rexford rules, then we can guarantee reachability and convergence in steady state.","zh_html":"Gao-Rexford 规则让我们能够证明以下陈述是正确的：假设 AS 图是层次化的(hierarchical)且无环的(acyclic)，并且所有 AS 都遵循 Gao-Rexford 规则，那么我们可以保证可达性(reachability)和稳态(steady state)下的收敛(convergence)。"},{"en":"Breaking down the specific terms in the statement: Reachability means that any two ASes in the graph can communicate. Convergence means that all ASes will eventually stop updating their paths, and the network will reach a steady state with valid paths between any two ASes. “In steady state” means that if the network topology changes, the paths might take some time to change and reach steady state again.","en_html":"Breaking down the specific terms in the statement: Reachability means that any two ASes in the graph can communicate. Convergence means that all ASes will eventually stop updating their paths, and the network will reach a steady state with valid paths between any two ASes. “In steady state” means that if the network topology changes, the paths might take some time to change and reach steady state again.","zh_html":"分解陈述中的具体术语：可达性(Reachability)意味着图中的任何两个 AS 都能通信。收敛(Convergence)意味着所有 AS 最终会停止更新它们的路径，网络达到稳态，任意两个 AS 之间存在有效路径。\"在稳态下(In steady state)\"意味着如果网络拓扑发生变化，路径可能需要一些时间来改变并再次到达稳态。"},{"en":"Recall that hierarchical means that starting from any AS, moving up the hierarchy (from customers to providers) will lead to a Tier 1 AS. Acyclic means there is no cycle of customer-provider relationships (directed edges).","en_html":"Recall that hierarchical means that starting from any AS, moving up the hierarchy (from customers to providers) will lead to a Tier 1 AS. Acyclic means there is no cycle of customer-provider relationships (directed edges).","zh_html":"回想一下，层次化(hierarchical)意味着从任何 AS 出发，沿层次结构上行（从客户到提供商）将到达一个 Tier 1 AS。无环(acyclic)意味着不存在客户-提供商关系（有向边）的环。"},{"en":"The proof of this statement requires that everybody follows the Gao-Rexford rules. If ASes were running their own arbitrary policies, the guarantees would no longer hold.","en_html":"The proof of this statement requires that everybody follows the Gao-Rexford rules. If ASes were running their own arbitrary policies, the guarantees would no longer hold.","zh_html":"这个陈述的证明要求每个人都遵循 Gao-Rexford 规则。如果 AS 运行自己的任意策略，这些保证将不再成立。"},{"en":"Modification: BGP Aggregates Destinations","en_html":"<a href=\"#modification-bgp-aggregates-destinations\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"modification-bgp-aggregates-destinations\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Modification: BGP Aggregates Destinations","zh_html":"<a href=\"#modification-bgp-aggregates-destinations\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"modification-bgp-aggregates-destinations\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 修改：BGP 聚合目的地(Modification: BGP Aggregates Destinations)"},{"en":"There are two more modifications we need to make to the distance-vector protocol.","en_html":"There are two more modifications we need to make to the distance-vector protocol.","zh_html":"我们需要对距离向量协议再进行两项修改。"},{"en":"In distance-vector protocols, we showed that each destination had a unique address, and the forwarding table mapped each destination to a next hop and distance.","en_html":"In distance-vector protocols, we showed that each destination had a unique address, and the forwarding table mapped each destination to a next hop and distance.","zh_html":"在距离向量协议中，我们看到每个目的地(destination)都有一个唯一的地址(address)，转发表(forwarding table)将每个目的地映射到下一跳(next hop)和距离(distance)。"},{"en":"In BGP, each AS is addressed by a prefix, which indicates that all machines inside that AS share the same prefix.","en_html":"In BGP, each AS is addressed by a prefix, which indicates that all machines inside that AS share the same prefix.","zh_html":"在 BGP 中，每个 AS 通过一个前缀(prefix)来寻址，这表示该 AS 内的所有机器共享相同的前缀。"},{"en":"These forwarding tables could get very large (imagine if a provider had hundreds of customers), and every single destination would need to be described in a separate announcement. Is there any way we can express this forwarding table more concisely?","en_html":"These forwarding tables could get very large (imagine if a provider had hundreds of customers), and every single destination would need to be described in a separate announcement. Is there any way we can express this forwarding table more concisely?","zh_html":"这些转发表(forwarding tables)可能会变得非常大（想象一下如果一个提供商有数百个客户），每个目的地都需要在单独的通告中描述。有没有更简洁的方式来表达这个转发表？"},{"en":"To improve scalability, BGP allows ASes to aggregate multiple destinations into a single forwarding table entry, and announce a more general prefix that includes all of the destinations combined.","en_html":"To improve scalability, BGP allows ASes to <strong>aggregate</strong> multiple destinations into a single forwarding table entry, and announce a more general prefix that includes all of the destinations combined.","zh_html":"为了提高可扩展性(scalability)，BGP 允许 AS 将多个目的地(aggregate)聚合到一个转发表条目中，并通告一个包含所有组合目的的更通用的前缀。"},{"en":"Note that in practice, BGP has conventions on the size of the prefixes being announced. For example, ASes will not make an announcement for an individual IP address. 24-bit prefixes (blocks of 256 addresses) are usually the smallest unit of addresses that are announced.","en_html":"Note that in practice, BGP has conventions on the size of the prefixes being announced. For example, ASes will not make an announcement for an individual IP address. 24-bit prefixes (blocks of 256 addresses) are usually the smallest unit of addresses that are announced.","zh_html":"注意，在实践中，BGP 对所通告的前缀大小有约定。例如，AS 不会为单个 IP 地址发布通告。24 位前缀（256 个地址的块）通常是通告的最小地址单元。"},{"en":"Modification: Path-Vector Protocol","en_html":"<a href=\"#modification-path-vector-protocol\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"modification-path-vector-protocol\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Modification: Path-Vector Protocol","zh_html":"<a href=\"#modification-path-vector-protocol\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"modification-path-vector-protocol\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 修改：路径向量协议(Modification: Path-Vector Protocol)"},{"en":"In least-cost protocols such as distance vector, we didn’t have to worry about loops. Every router was trying to find least-cost routes, and by definition, the least-cost route will not contain a loop.","en_html":"In least-cost protocols such as distance vector, we didn’t have to worry about loops. Every router was trying to find least-cost routes, and by definition, the least-cost route will not contain a loop.","zh_html":"在像距离向量这样的最小成本协议中，我们不必担心环路(loops)。每台路由器都在试图找到最小成本路由，根据定义，最小成本路由不会包含环。"},{"en":"Now that each AS is choosing routes based on its own preferences, we’ve lost the guarantee of no loops. For example, suppose B likes paths through C, and C likes paths through B. We’ve created a routing loop!","en_html":"Now that each AS is choosing routes based on its own preferences, we’ve lost the guarantee of no loops. For example, suppose B likes paths through C, and C likes paths through B. We’ve created a routing loop!","zh_html":"现在每个 AS 都基于自己的偏好选择路由，我们失去了无环的保证。例如，假设 B 喜欢经过 C 的路径，而 C 喜欢经过 B 的路径。我们就创造了一个路由环路(routing loop)！"},{"en":"To fix this problem, instead of the distance to destination, BGP announcements will include the full AS path to the destination. This changes the protocol from a distance-vector to a path-vector protocol.","en_html":"To fix this problem, instead of the distance to destination, BGP announcements will include the full AS path to the destination. This changes the protocol from a distance-vector to a <strong>path-vector</strong> protocol.","zh_html":"为了解决这个问题，BGP 通告中将包含完整的 AS 路径(path)到目的地，而不是到目的地的距离。这把这个协议从距离向量(distance-vector)变成了一个<strong>路径向量(path-vector)</strong>协议。"},{"en":"For example, in a distance-vector protocol, A would announce: “I can reach the destination with cost 1.” Then, B would announce: “I can reach the destination with cost 2.”","en_html":"For example, in a distance-vector protocol, A would announce: “I can reach the destination with cost 1.” Then, B would announce: “I can reach the destination with cost 2.”","zh_html":"例如，在距离向量协议中，A 会通告：\"我可以用成本 1 到达目的地。\"然后，B 会通告：\"我可以用成本 2 到达目的地。\""},{"en":"In a path-vector protocol, A would announce: “I can reach the destination with the path [A].” Then, B would announce: “I can reach the destination with the path [B, A].”","en_html":"In a path-vector protocol, A would announce: “I can reach the destination with the path [A].” Then, B would announce: “I can reach the destination with the path [B, A].”","zh_html":"在路径向量协议中，A 会通告：\"我可以用路径 [A] 到达目的地。\"然后，B 会通告：\"我可以用路径 [B, A] 到达目的地。\""},{"en":"With this modification, ASes can determine whether an advertised path contains a loop by tracing through the path in the advertisement. Specifically, if I receive an advertisement, I just need to check if the path includes myself. That would cause the packet to be sent back to me, creating a loop, so I would ignore that advertisement and not accept or advertise the route with the loop.","en_html":"With this modification, ASes can determine whether an advertised path contains a loop by tracing through the path in the advertisement. Specifically, if I receive an advertisement, I just need to check if the path includes myself. That would cause the packet to be sent back to me, creating a loop, so I would ignore that advertisement and not accept or advertise the route with the loop.","zh_html":"有了这个修改，AS 可以通过追踪通告中的路径来确定一条被通告的路径是否包含环路。具体来说，如果我收到一个通告，我只需要检查路径中是否包含我自己。如果包含，就会导致分组被送回给我，产生环路，所以我会忽略该通告，不接受或通告这条有环的路由。"},{"en":"Note: If everybody agrees to discard routes with loops, this guarantees that advertisements won’t contain loops. The only way that an advertised route would create a loop is if I see a route that already includes myself, and the addition of myself is what creates the loop.","en_html":"Note: If everybody agrees to discard routes with loops, this guarantees that advertisements won’t contain loops. The only way that an advertised route would create a loop is if I see a route that already includes myself, and the addition of myself is what creates the loop.","zh_html":"注意：如果每个人都同意丢弃有环的路由，这就保证了通告不会包含环路。被通告的路由会产生环路的唯一方式是，如果我看到一条已经包含我自己的路由，而我自己的加入正是产生环路的原因。"},{"en":"The change from distance-vector to path-vector also allows ASes to implement arbitrary policies. In a distance-vector protocol, I might have a policy like “avoid AS#2063 when possible.” If I receive an advertisement “I can reach the destination with cost 12,” I have no idea if the path being advertised goes through AS#2063. If instead, the advertisement contained the entire path, I can check if the path goes through AS#2063 before deciding to accept or reject it.","en_html":"The change from distance-vector to path-vector also allows ASes to implement arbitrary policies. In a distance-vector protocol, I might have a policy like “avoid AS#2063 when possible.” If I receive an advertisement “I can reach the destination with cost 12,” I have no idea if the path being advertised goes through AS#2063. If instead, the advertisement contained the entire path, I can check if the path goes through AS#2063 before deciding to accept or reject it.","zh_html":"从距离向量到路径向量的改变也允许 AS 实现任意策略。在距离向量协议中，我可能有\"尽可能避免 AS#2063\"这样的策略。如果我收到一个通告\"我可以用成本 12 到达目的地\"，我完全不知道被通告的路径是否经过 AS#2063。如果通告包含完整路径，我可以在决定接受或拒绝之前检查路径是否经过 AS#2063。"},{"en":"Note: The conventional BGP import policy we saw earlier (prefer selecting routes that go to customers, over peers, over providers) only depends on the next hop, not the entire path. Still, the change to path-vector is useful for loop detection, and lets us generalize the protocol to arbitrary policies.","en_html":"Note: The conventional BGP import policy we saw earlier (prefer selecting routes that go to customers, over peers, over providers) only depends on the next hop, not the entire path. Still, the change to path-vector is useful for loop detection, and lets us generalize the protocol to arbitrary policies.","zh_html":"注意：我们之前看到的常规 BGP 导入策略（优先选择经过客户的路由，然后是对等体，最后是提供商）只依赖于下一跳(next hop)，而不是完整路径。尽管如此，改为路径向量对环路检测很有用，并且让我们能够将协议推广到任意策略。"},{"en":"Stub ASes Use Default Routes","en_html":"<a href=\"#stub-ases-use-default-routes\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"stub-ases-use-default-routes\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Stub ASes Use Default Routes","zh_html":"<a href=\"#stub-ases-use-default-routes\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"stub-ases-use-default-routes\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 末梢 AS 使用默认路由(Stub ASes Use Default Routes)"},{"en":"Some ASes don’t need to run BGP to determine how to forward packets through the network. In particular, if a stub AS is only connected to a single provider, then every packet bound for other ASes should be sent to that one provider. The stub AS can install a single hard-coded default route for all destinations in other ASes.","en_html":"Some ASes don’t need to run BGP to determine how to forward packets through the network. In particular, if a stub AS is only connected to a single provider, then every packet bound for other ASes should be sent to that one provider. The stub AS can install a single hard-coded <strong>default route</strong> for all destinations in other ASes.","zh_html":"一些 AS 不需要运行 BGP 来确定如何通过网络转发分组。特别是，如果一个末梢 AS(stub AS)只连接到一个提供商，那么每个发往其他 AS 的分组都应该发送给那一个提供商。末梢 AS 可以为其他 AS 中的所有目的地安装一个硬编码的<strong>默认路由(default route)</strong>。"},{"en":"What about other ASes trying to send packets to the stub AS? The stub can ask the provider to install a static route, which tells the provider how to send packets to the stub AS. Now, the provider can run BGP and advertise this static route to the rest of the Internet. The stub can ask the provider to hard-code the static route, and the stub never has to run BGP, since the provider is advertising routes to the stub on behalf of the stub.","en_html":"What about other ASes trying to send packets to the stub AS? The stub can ask the provider to install a <strong>static route</strong>, which tells the provider how to send packets to the stub AS. Now, the provider can run BGP and advertise this static route to the rest of the Internet. The stub can ask the provider to hard-code the static route, and the stub never has to run BGP, since the provider is advertising routes to the stub on behalf of the stub.","zh_html":"其他 AS 尝试向该末梢 AS 发送分组怎么办？末梢 AS 可以要求提供商安装一个<strong>静态路由(static route)</strong>，告诉提供商如何将分组发送到该末梢 AS。现在，提供商可以运行 BGP 并将这条静态路由通告给互联网的其余部分。末梢 AS 可以要求提供商硬编码这条静态路由，末梢 AS 永远不必运行 BGP，因为提供商代表末梢 AS 在通告路由。"},{"en":"Most small ASes in the Internet are stub ASes that use default and static routes.","en_html":"Most small ASes in the Internet are stub ASes that use default and static routes.","zh_html":"互联网上的大多数小型 AS 都是使用默认路由和静态路由的末梢 AS。"},{"en":"Stub ASes are similar to end hosts in intra-domain routing. They send and receive packets for their own AS, but do not forward packets of their own and do not participate in the routing process. Just like in intra-domain routing, we will usually ignore stub ASes and only consider transit ASes that actually participate in BGP.","en_html":"Stub ASes are similar to end hosts in intra-domain routing. They send and receive packets for their own AS, but do not forward packets of their own and do not participate in the routing process. Just like in intra-domain routing, we will usually ignore stub ASes and only consider transit ASes that actually participate in BGP.","zh_html":"末梢 AS(stub ASes)类似于域内路由中的终端主机(end hosts)。它们为自己 AS 发送和接收分组，但不转发其他人的分组，也不参与路由过程。就像在域内路由中一样，我们通常会忽略末梢 AS，只考虑实际参与 BGP 的中转 AS(transit ASes)。"}]},"/routing/bgp-implementation.html":{"title":"bgp-implementation","blocks":[{"en":"BGP Implementation and Issues","en_html":"<a href=\"#bgp-implementation-and-issues\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"bgp-implementation-and-issues\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> BGP Implementation and Issues","zh_html":"<a href=\"#bgp-implementation-and-issues\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"bgp-implementation-and-issues\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> BGP 实现与问题(BGP Implementation and Issues)"},{"en":"Border and Interior Routers","en_html":"<a href=\"#border-and-interior-routers\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"border-and-interior-routers\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Border and Interior Routers","zh_html":"<a href=\"#border-and-interior-routers\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"border-and-interior-routers\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 边界路由器和内部路由器(Border and Interior Routers)"},{"en":"At this point, we have an intuitive picture of how BGP works between ASes. In this section, we’ll show how BGP is actually implemented at the router level. In doing so, we will also show how BGP interacts with the intra-domain routing protocols from earlier.","en_html":"At this point, we have an intuitive picture of how BGP works between ASes. In this section, we’ll show how BGP is actually implemented at the router level. In doing so, we will also show how BGP interacts with the intra-domain routing protocols from earlier.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们对 BGP 在 AS 之间如何工作有了一个直观的图景。在本节中，我们将展示 BGP 实际上是如何在路由器级别实现的。在此过程中，我们还将展示 BGP 如何与之前介绍的域内路由协议(intra-domain routing protocols)交互。"},{"en":"So far, our model of inter-domain routing has treated an entire AS as a single entity, importing and exporting paths.","en_html":"So far, our model of inter-domain routing has treated an entire AS as a single entity, importing and exporting paths.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们的域间路由(inter-domain routing)模型将整个 AS 视为一个单一的实体，负责导入和导出路径。"},{"en":"However, in reality, the AS contains many routers (and hosts) connected by links.","en_html":"However, in reality, the AS contains many routers (and hosts) connected by links.","zh_html":"然而，在现实中，AS 包含许多由链路连接的路由器(routers)和主机(hosts)。"},{"en":"In order to actually implement BGP, we need all the routers inside the AS to work cooperatively to act as a single node.","en_html":"In order to actually implement BGP, we need all the routers inside the AS to work cooperatively to act as a single node.","zh_html":"为了实际实现 BGP，我们需要 AS 内部的所有路由器协同工作，扮演一个单一节点。"},{"en":"Within an AS, we will classify all routers into two types. Border routers have at least one link to a router in a different AS. Interior routers only have links to other routers within the same AS.","en_html":"Within an AS, we will classify all routers into two types. <strong>Border routers</strong> have at least one link to a router in a different AS. <strong>Interior routers</strong> only have links to other routers within the same AS.","zh_html":"在一个 AS 内部，我们将所有路由器分为两类。<strong>边界路由器(Border routers)</strong>至少有一条链路连接到不同 AS 的路由器。<strong>内部路由器(Interior routers)</strong>只有到同一 AS 内其他路由器的链路。"},{"en":"Only the border routers need to advertise routes to other ASes. Sometimes, we call the routers advertising BGP routes BGP speakers. The BGP speakers need to understand the semantics and syntax of the BGP protocol (how to read and create a BGP announcement, what to do when receiving an announcement, and so on).","en_html":"Only the border routers need to advertise routes to other ASes. Sometimes, we call the routers advertising BGP routes <strong>BGP speakers</strong>. The BGP speakers need to understand the semantics and syntax of the BGP protocol (how to read and create a BGP announcement, what to do when receiving an announcement, and so on).","zh_html":"只有边界路由器需要向其他 AS 通告路由。有时，我们将通告 BGP 路由的路由器称为<strong>BGP 发言者(BGP speakers)</strong>。BGP 发言者需要理解 BGP 协议的语义和语法（如何读取和创建 BGP 通告、收到通告时该怎么办等）。"},{"en":"External and Internal BGP Sessions","en_html":"<a href=\"#external-and-internal-bgp-sessions\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"external-and-internal-bgp-sessions\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> External and Internal BGP Sessions","zh_html":"<a href=\"#external-and-internal-bgp-sessions\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"external-and-internal-bgp-sessions\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 外部和内部 BGP 会话(External and Internal BGP Sessions)"},{"en":"A BGP session consists of two routers exchanging information between each other.","en_html":"A <strong>BGP session</strong> consists of two routers exchanging information between each other.","zh_html":"一个<strong>BGP 会话(BGP session)</strong>由两台路由器之间相互交换信息组成。"},{"en":"An external BGP (eBGP) session is between two routers from different ASes. eBGP sessions can be used to exchange announcements between different ASes and learn about routes to other ASes. Only border routers participate in eBGP sessions (since eBGP requires talking to a different AS).","en_html":"An <strong>external BGP (eBGP) session</strong> is between two routers from different ASes. eBGP sessions can be used to exchange announcements between different ASes and learn about routes to other ASes. Only border routers participate in eBGP sessions (since eBGP requires talking to a different AS).","zh_html":"一个<strong>外部 BGP(external BGP, eBGP)会话</strong>是在来自不同 AS 的两台路由器之间建立的。eBGP 会话可用于在不同 AS 之间交换通告，并学习通往其他 AS 的路由。只有边界路由器参与 eBGP 会话（因为 eBGP 需要与不同的 AS 通信）。"},{"en":"By contrast, an internal BGP (iBGP) session is between two routers in the same AS (not necessarily directly connected by a link). More specifically, if a border router learns about a new route, it can use iBGP to distribute that new route to the other routers in the AS. This allows all the routers in the AS to coordinate and act together as one entity. Both border and internal routers participate in iBGP sessions.","en_html":"By contrast, an <strong>internal BGP (iBGP) session</strong> is between two routers in the same AS (not necessarily directly connected by a link). More specifically, if a border router learns about a new route, it can use iBGP to distribute that new route to the other routers in the AS. This allows all the routers in the AS to coordinate and act together as one entity. Both border and internal routers participate in iBGP sessions.","zh_html":"相比之下，一个<strong>内部 BGP(internal BGP, iBGP)会话</strong>是在同一 AS 内的两台路由器之间建立的（不一定直接由链路连接）。更具体地说，如果一台边界路由器学到一条新路由，它可以使用 iBGP 将该新路由分发给 AS 内的其他路由器。这使得 AS 内的所有路由器能够协同工作，作为一个整体。边界路由器和内部路由器都参与 iBGP 会话。"},{"en":"eBGP and iBGP sessions are different from interior gateway protocols (IGP). These are the intra-domain routing protocols (e.g. distance-vector, link-state) that are deployed within an AS to route packets inside the AS.","en_html":"eBGP and iBGP sessions are different from <strong>interior gateway protocols (IGP)</strong>. These are the intra-domain routing protocols (e.g. distance-vector, link-state) that are deployed within an AS to route packets inside the AS.","zh_html":"eBGP 和 iBGP 会话与<strong>内部网关协议(interior gateway protocols, IGP)</strong>不同。IGP 是在 AS 内部部署的域内路由协议(intra-domain routing protocols)（例如距离向量、链路状态），用于在 AS 内部路由分组。"},{"en":"It’s easy to confuse iBGP and IGP. Both exchange messages within the same AS. However, iBGP is part of an inter-domain protocol, helping routers learn about paths to other ASes. IGP is an intra-domain protocol, helping routers learn about paths to destinations in the same AS.","en_html":"It’s easy to confuse iBGP and IGP. Both exchange messages within the same AS. However, iBGP is part of an inter-domain protocol, helping routers learn about paths to other ASes. IGP is an intra-domain protocol, helping routers learn about paths to destinations in the same AS.","zh_html":"很容易混淆 iBGP 和 IGP。两者都在同一 AS 内部交换消息。然而，iBGP 是域间协议的一部分，帮助路由器了解通往其他 AS 的路径。IGP 是域内协议，帮助路由器了解通往同一 AS 内目的地的路径。"},{"en":"eBGP, iBGP, and IGP work together to establish routes from any one router to any other router in the Internet (even if the routers are in different ASes).","en_html":"eBGP, iBGP, and IGP work together to establish routes from any one router to any other router in the Internet (even if the routers are in different ASes).","zh_html":"eBGP、iBGP 和 IGP 协同工作，建立从任何一台路由器到互联网中任何其他路由器的路由（即使路由器在不同的 AS 中）。"},{"en":"First, each AS runs IGP to learn least-cost paths between any two routers inside the same AS.","en_html":"First, each AS runs IGP to learn least-cost paths between any two routers inside the same AS.","zh_html":"首先，每个 AS 运行 IGP 来学习同一 AS 内任意两台路由器之间的最小成本路径。"},{"en":"Next, the ASes run eBGP, advertising routes to each other to learn about routes to other ASes.","en_html":"Next, the ASes run eBGP, advertising routes to each other to learn about routes to other ASes.","zh_html":"然后，AS 运行 eBGP，互相通告路由，以了解通往其他 AS 的路由。"},{"en":"Finally, the ASes run iBGP, so that a router that has learned about an external route can distribute that route to all the other routers in the same AS.","en_html":"Finally, the ASes run iBGP, so that a router that has learned about an external route can distribute that route to all the other routers in the same AS.","zh_html":"最后，AS 运行 iBGP，使得已经学到外部路由的路由器能够将该路由分发给同一 AS 内的所有其他路由器。"},{"en":"The routes learned from eBGP, iBGP, and IGP can be used to send packets anywhere in the Internet. If the destination is within the same AS (same IP prefix), we can use the routes learned from IGP to forward the packet. If the destination is in a different AS (different IP prefix), we can think back to iBGP, which told us about any external routes discovered by anybody in my AS. Using the iBGP results, we can figure out which border router is on that external route. Then, we can use IGP to forward the packet to the correct border router (who will then forward the packet to the next AS).","en_html":"The routes learned from eBGP, iBGP, and IGP can be used to send packets anywhere in the Internet. If the destination is within the same AS (same IP prefix), we can use the routes learned from IGP to forward the packet. If the destination is in a different AS (different IP prefix), we can think back to iBGP, which told us about any external routes discovered by anybody in my AS. Using the iBGP results, we can figure out which border router is on that external route. Then, we can use IGP to forward the packet to the correct border router (who will then forward the packet to the next AS).","zh_html":"从 eBGP、iBGP 和 IGP 学到的路由可用于将分组发送到互联网的任何地方。如果目的地(destination)在同一 AS 内（相同 IP 前缀(prefix)），我们可以使用从 IGP 学到的路由来转发分组。如果目的地在不同的 AS 中（不同 IP 前缀），我们可以回忆 iBGP，它告诉我们 AS 中任何人发现的外部路由。使用 iBGP 的结果，我们可以确定哪个边界路由器位于那条外部路由上。然后，我们可以使用 IGP 将分组转发到正确的边界路由器（该路由器然后将分组转发到下一个 AS）。"},{"en":"As a concrete example, let’s say E wants to send packets to Z. First, every router in E’s AS runs IGP, learning all the internal routes. Next, some router in AS#5 advertises a route to Z using eBGP. At this point, only G knows that it can reach Z. Finally, G tells all routers in its own AS that it can reach Z, using iBGP.","en_html":"As a concrete example, let’s say E wants to send packets to Z. First, every router in E’s AS runs IGP, learning all the internal routes. Next, some router in AS#5 advertises a route to Z using eBGP. At this point, only G knows that it can reach Z. Finally, G tells all routers in its own AS that it can reach Z, using iBGP.","zh_html":"作为一个具体例子，假设 E 想要向 Z 发送分组。首先，E 的 AS 中的每台路由器运行 IGP，学习所有内部路由。然后，AS#5 中的某台路由器使用 eBGP 通告一条通往 Z 的路由。此时，只有 G 知道它可以到达 Z。最后，G 使用 iBGP 告诉它自己 AS 中的所有路由器，它可以到达 Z。"},{"en":"E has heard from iBGP that G, a router in the same AS, can reach Z. Using the IGP routes, E can send the packet to G (forwarding to F first). Then, G can use the route learned in eBGP to send the packet to Z.","en_html":"E has heard from iBGP that G, a router in the same AS, can reach Z. Using the IGP routes, E can send the packet to G (forwarding to F first). Then, G can use the route learned in eBGP to send the packet to Z.","zh_html":"E 通过 iBGP 得知，同一 AS 中的路由器 G 可以到达 Z。使用 IGP 路由，E 可以将分组发送到 G（先转发到 F）。然后，G 可以使用在 eBGP 中学到的路由将分组发送到 Z。"},{"en":"The border router who advertises a route to an external destination is sometimes called the egress router for that destination. This is the router who can help your packet exit the local network and move to other networks closer to the destination. In the example above, G is the egress router for destination Z.","en_html":"The border router who advertises a route to an external destination is sometimes called the <strong>egress router</strong> for that destination. This is the router who can help your packet exit the local network and move to other networks closer to the destination. In the example above, G is the egress router for destination Z.","zh_html":"通告通往外部目的地的路由的边界路由器有时被称为该目的地的<strong>出口路由器(egress router)</strong>。这是可以帮助你的分组离开本地网络并移动到更接近目的地的其他网络的路由器。在上面的例子中，G 是通往目的地 Z 的出口路由器。"},{"en":"A consequence of these protocols is that every router has two forwarding tables. One is a table mapping all internal destinations (same AS) to a next hop, populated with information from IGP. The other is a table mapping all external destinations to an egress router (who knows a route to the external destination), populated with information from eBGP.","en_html":"A consequence of these protocols is that every router has two forwarding tables. One is a table mapping all internal destinations (same AS) to a next hop, populated with information from IGP. The other is a table mapping all external destinations to an egress router (who knows a route to the external destination), populated with information from eBGP.","zh_html":"这些协议的一个结果是，每台路由器有两个转发表(forwarding tables)。一个表映射所有内部目的地（同一 AS）到下一跳(next hop)，由 IGP 提供信息。另一个表映射所有外部目的地到出口路由器(egress router)（它知道通往外部目的地的路由），由 eBGP 提供信息。"},{"en":"Note that in the eBGP table, the egress router is not necessarily a next hop. The egress router might be several local hops away, but we use IGP to reach that egress router.","en_html":"Note that in the eBGP table, the egress router is not necessarily a next hop. The egress router might be several local hops away, but we use IGP to reach that egress router.","zh_html":"注意，在 eBGP 表中，出口路由器不一定是下一跳。出口路由器可能相隔几个本地跳，但我们使用 IGP 来到达那个 eBGP 路由器。"},{"en":"We’ve seen how eBGP (path-vector, advertising routes) and IGP (distance-vector or link-state) are implemented as algorithms. How is iBGP implemented? When a border router installs a new route to a destination, it has to inform the other routers in the AS. One simple solution is to have the border router directly tell every other router in the AS.","en_html":"We’ve seen how eBGP (path-vector, advertising routes) and IGP (distance-vector or link-state) are implemented as algorithms. How is iBGP implemented? When a border router installs a new route to a destination, it has to inform the other routers in the AS. One simple solution is to have the border router directly tell every other router in the AS.","zh_html":"我们已经看到了 eBGP（路径向量，通告路由）和 IGP（距离向量或链路状态）是如何作为算法实现的。iBGP 是如何实现的？当一台边界路由器安装了一条到某个目的地的新路由时，它必须通知 AS 中的其他路由器。一个简单的解决方案是让边界路由器直接告诉 AS 中的每台其他路由器。"},{"en":"This solution is relatively simple, though it requires every border router to have an iBGP session with every other router. In a network with B border routers and N routers total, this protocol would require BN iBGP connections, and might scale poorly as local networks get larger.","en_html":"This solution is relatively simple, though it requires every border router to have an iBGP session with every other router. In a network with B border routers and N routers total, this protocol would require BN iBGP connections, and might scale poorly as local networks get larger.","zh_html":"这个解决方案相对简单，尽管它要求每个边界路由器与所有其他路由器都有 iBGP 会话。在拥有 B 个边界路由器和总计 N 个路由器的网络中，这个协议需要 BN 个 iBGP 连接，随着本地网络变得更大，可扩展性可能会变差。"},{"en":"Note: In reality, there are other ways to combine inter-domain and intra-domain routers. You can look up “route reflectors” if you’re interested, though they won’t be covered in this class.","en_html":"Note: In reality, there are other ways to combine inter-domain and intra-domain routers. You can look up “route reflectors” if you’re interested, though they won’t be covered in this class.","zh_html":"注意：在现实中，还有其他组合域间和域内路由器的方法。如果你感兴趣，可以查阅\"路由反射器(route reflectors)\"，但本课程不会涉及。"},{"en":"Multiple Links Between ASes: Hot Potato Routing","en_html":"<a href=\"#multiple-links-between-ases-hot-potato-routing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"multiple-links-between-ases-hot-potato-routing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Multiple Links Between ASes: Hot Potato Routing","zh_html":"<a href=\"#multiple-links-between-ases-hot-potato-routing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"multiple-links-between-ases-hot-potato-routing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> AS 之间的多条链路：热土豆路由(Multiple Links Between ASes: Hot Potato Routing)"},{"en":"So far, in our AS graph, we’ve shown two ASes having a single link (edge) between them if they are connected. In practice, because an AS actually consists of many routers, it’s possible for two ASes to be connected by multiple links.","en_html":"So far, in our AS graph, we’ve shown two ASes having a single link (edge) between them if they are connected. In practice, because an AS actually consists of many routers, it’s possible for two ASes to be connected by multiple links.","zh_html":"到目前为止，在我们的 AS 图(graph)中，如果两个 AS 之间有连接，我们只显示了一条链路（边）。在实践中，由于一个 AS 实际上由许多路由器组成，两个 AS 之间可能存在多条链路。"},{"en":"In practice, it can be useful to have multiple links between large ASes. For example, Verizon and AT&amp;T are very large ASes with infrastructure across the entire United States. Suppose there was only one link between the two ASes on the west coast. If a Verizon router in the east coast and an AT&amp;T router in the east coast wanted to communicate, the packet would have to travel across the country on Verizon’s network, traverse the link into AT&amp;T’s network, and then travel back across the country to the destination.","en_html":"In practice, it can be useful to have multiple links between large ASes. For example, Verizon and AT&amp;T are very large ASes with infrastructure across the entire United States. Suppose there was only one link between the two ASes on the west coast. If a Verizon router in the east coast and an AT&amp;T router in the east coast wanted to communicate, the packet would have to travel across the country on Verizon’s network, traverse the link into AT&amp;T’s network, and then travel back across the country to the destination.","zh_html":"在实践中，大型 AS 之间有多条链路是有用的。例如，Verizon 和 AT&T 是非常大的 AS，基础设施遍布整个美国。假设两者之间在西海岸只有一条链路。如果东海岸的一台 Verizon 路由器和东海岸的一台 AT&T 路由器想要通信，分组将不得不穿越整个美国的 Verizon 网络，穿过链路进入 AT&T 网络，然后再穿越整个美国回到目的地。"},{"en":"Multiple links between two ASes also means that there can be multiple paths between two routers that pass through the same ASes. At the AS level, both of these paths go through the same ASes, and our earlier model made no distinction between them. However, in our more detailed model, both paths need to be exported, and a preferred route has to be imported.","en_html":"Multiple links between two ASes also means that there can be multiple paths between two routers that pass through the same ASes. At the AS level, both of these paths go through the same ASes, and our earlier model made no distinction between them. However, in our more detailed model, both paths need to be exported, and a preferred route has to be imported.","zh_html":"两个 AS 之间的多条链路也意味着，两个路由器之间可能存在多条经过相同 AS 的路径。在 AS 层面上，这两条路径都经过相同的 AS，我们之前的模型没有区分它们。然而，在我们更详细的模型中，两条路径都需要被导出，并且需要导入一条首选路由。"},{"en":"If there are two routes, which route does the importing AS prefer?","en_html":"If there are two routes, which route does the importing AS prefer?","zh_html":"如果有两条路由，导入方 AS 偏好哪一条？"},{"en":"Bandwidth costs money, so I would prefer if this traffic traveled as far as possible on infrastructure owned and paid for by other people, and traveled as little as possible on my own infrastructure. Therefore, the orange path is preferred.","en_html":"Bandwidth costs money, so I would prefer if this traffic traveled as far as possible on infrastructure owned and paid for by other people, and traveled as little as possible on my own infrastructure. Therefore, the orange path is preferred.","zh_html":"带宽(bandwidth)要花钱，所以我更希望流量尽可能多地在自己拥有并付费的基础设施上传输，而尽可能少地在我的基础设施上传输。因此，橙色路径是更优的。"},{"en":"More formally, the importing AS receives two announcements: one from the west router, and one from the east router.","en_html":"More formally, the importing AS receives two announcements: one from the west router, and one from the east router.","zh_html":"更正式地说，导入 AS 收到两个通告：一个来自西部路由器，一个来自东部路由器。"},{"en":"Using iBGP, every router inside the AS sees both announcements. One says, the egress router is the west router, and the other says, the egress router is the east router. Every router has to decide which announcement to import.","en_html":"Using iBGP, every router inside the AS sees both announcements. One says, the egress router is the west router, and the other says, the egress router is the east router. Every router has to decide which announcement to import.","zh_html":"使用 iBGP，AS 内的每台路由器都能看到这两个通告。一个说，出口路由器是西部路由器；另一个说，出口路由器是东部路由器。每台路由器必须决定导入哪个通告。"},{"en":"Let’s focus on router E. Using IGP, this router can figure out the distance to the west egress router (F), and the distance to the east egress router (I). Since the west egress router (F) is closer, routing packets via the west egress router (F) will use up less of this AS’s bandwidth. Therefore, this router will import the path via the west egress router (F). Another router, like one closer to the east egress router (I), might decide to import a different path.","en_html":"Let’s focus on router E. Using IGP, this router can figure out the distance to the west egress router (F), and the distance to the east egress router (I). Since the west egress router (F) is closer, routing packets via the west egress router (F) will use up less of this AS’s bandwidth. Therefore, this router will import the path via the west egress router (F). Another router, like one closer to the east egress router (I), might decide to import a different path.","zh_html":"让我们关注路由器 E。使用 IGP，这台路由器可以计算出到西部出口路由器(F)的距离，以及到东部出口路由器(I)的距离。由于西部出口路由器(F)更近，通过西部出口路由器(F)路由分组将耗费更少的本 AS 带宽。因此，这台路由器会导入经过西部出口路由器(F)的路径。另一台路由器，比如更靠近东部出口路由器(I)的，可能会决定导入不同的路径。"},{"en":"This strategy of selecting the nearest egress router is sometimes called hot potato routing. We want the packet to leave our AS as soon as possible, and start traveling over somebody else’s links as soon as possible.","en_html":"This strategy of selecting the nearest egress router is sometimes called <strong>hot potato routing</strong>. We want the packet to leave our AS as soon as possible, and start traveling over somebody else’s links as soon as possible.","zh_html":"这种选择最近出口路由器的策略有时被称为<strong>热土豆路由(hot potato routing)</strong>。我们希望分组尽可能快地离开我们的 AS，尽快开始经过别人的链路传输。"},{"en":"Multiple Links Between Routers: MED","en_html":"<a href=\"#multiple-links-between-routers-med\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"multiple-links-between-routers-med\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Multiple Links Between Routers: MED","zh_html":"<a href=\"#multiple-links-between-routers-med\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"multiple-links-between-routers-med\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 路由器之间的多条链路：MED(Multiple Links Between Routers: MED)"},{"en":"What if a router is equally close to both possible egress routers?","en_html":"What if a router is equally close to both possible egress routers?","zh_html":"如果一台路由器距离两个可能的出口路由器同样近怎么办？"},{"en":"In order to tiebreak, the exporting AS can announce a preference for one route over the other.","en_html":"In order to tiebreak, the exporting AS can announce a preference for one route over the other.","zh_html":"为了破平局，导出方 AS 可以在通告中声明对某条路由的偏好。"},{"en":"Which route does the exporting AS prefer? Again, since bandwidth costs money, the exporting AS prefers the pink path, which uses less of its bandwidth. In the announcement of the pink path, the exporting AS can additionally say “I prefer if you used this path,” and in the announcement of the orange path, the exporting AS can additionally say “I prefer if you avoided this path.”","en_html":"Which route does the exporting AS prefer? Again, since bandwidth costs money, the exporting AS prefers the pink path, which uses less of its bandwidth. In the announcement of the pink path, the exporting AS can additionally say “I prefer if you used this path,” and in the announcement of the orange path, the exporting AS can additionally say “I prefer if you avoided this path.”","zh_html":"导出方 AS 偏好哪条路由？同样，由于带宽要花钱，导出方 AS 偏好粉色路径，它耗费更少的自身带宽。在粉色路径的通告中，导出方 AS 可以额外声明\"我更希望你使用这条路径\"，而在橙色路径的通告中，导出方 AS 可以额外声明\"我更希望你避免使用这条路径。\""},{"en":"Now, the router that is equally close to both egress routers can see this extra information in the iBGP announcement.","en_html":"Now, the router that is equally close to both egress routers can see this extra information in the iBGP announcement.","zh_html":"现在，距离两个出口路由器同样近的路由器可以在 iBGP 通告中看到这个额外信息。"},{"en":"Using this extra information, the router can select the egress router on the pink path, since the exporting AS preferred this path.","en_html":"Using this extra information, the router can select the egress router on the pink path, since the exporting AS preferred this path.","zh_html":"利用这个额外信息，路由器可以选择粉色路径上的出口路由器，因为导出方 AS 偏好这条路径。"},{"en":"This additional information in the exporting announcement is called the Multi-Exit Discriminator (MED). From the perspective of the exporter, it indicates my preferred router for entering my network. From the perspective of the importer, it indicates the other AS’s preferred router for exiting my network and entering the other AS’s network.","en_html":"This additional information in the exporting announcement is called the <strong>Multi-Exit Discriminator (MED)</strong>. From the perspective of the exporter, it indicates my preferred router for entering my network. From the perspective of the importer, it indicates the other AS’s preferred router for exiting my network and entering the other AS’s network.","zh_html":"这个导出通告中的额外信息被称为<strong>多出口区分符(Multi-Exit Discriminator, MED)</strong>。从导出方的角度来看，它指示了我偏好的进入我的网络的路由器。从导入方的角度来看，它指示了其他 AS 偏好的离开我的网络并进入其他 AS 网络的路由器。"},{"en":"Another way to interpret the MED is, the distance to the destination, via this router. The exporter can say, “the west coast router is 3 hops away from the destination,” and “the east coast router is 12 hops away from the destination.” Lower MED numbers are preferred, since the exporter wants to use as little of its own bandwidth as possible. The exporter would rather use 3 of its own links, instead of 12 of its own links.","en_html":"Another way to interpret the MED is, the distance to the destination, via this router. The exporter can say, “the west coast router is 3 hops away from the destination,” and “the east coast router is 12 hops away from the destination.” Lower MED numbers are preferred, since the exporter wants to use as little of its own bandwidth as possible. The exporter would rather use 3 of its own links, instead of 12 of its own links.","zh_html":"另一种理解 MED 的方式是，到目的地经过这台路由器的距离。导出方可以说：\"西海岸路由器距离目的地 3 跳\"，以及\"东海岸路由器距离目的地 12 跳\"。较低的 MED 数字更优，因为导出方希望尽可能少地使用自己的带宽。导出方宁愿使用自己的 3 条链路，而不是自己的 12 条链路。"},{"en":"Import Policy Priority","en_html":"<a href=\"#import-policy-priority\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"import-policy-priority\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Import Policy Priority","zh_html":"<a href=\"#import-policy-priority\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"import-policy-priority\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 导入策略优先级(Import Policy Priority)"},{"en":"Our more detailed model, where two ASes can be connected with multiple links, means that we now have additional import policy rules, in addition to the Gao-Rexford rules. When you receive multiple announcements for the same destination, select a path based on these tiebreaking rules, in this order:","en_html":"Our more detailed model, where two ASes can be connected with multiple links, means that we now have additional import policy rules, in addition to the Gao-Rexford rules. When you receive multiple announcements for the same destination, select a path based on these tiebreaking rules, in this order:","zh_html":"我们更详细的模型（两台 AS 之间可以有多条链路）意味着，除了 Gao-Rexford 规则之外，我们现在还有额外的导入策略规则。当你收到针对同一目的地的多个通告时，按以下顺序根据这些破平局规则选择一条路径："},{"en":"Use the Gao-Rexford rules. Select the path advertised by a customer, over the path advertised by a peer, over the path advertised by a provider.","en_html":"Use the <strong>Gao-Rexford rules</strong>. Select the path advertised by a customer, over the path advertised by a peer, over the path advertised by a provider.","zh_html":"使用<strong>Gao-Rexford 规则</strong>。优先选择由客户(customer)通告的路径，其次是由对等体(peer)通告的路径，最后是由提供商(provider)通告的路径。"},{"en":"If multiple paths have the same Gao-Rexford priority (e.g. two paths from customers), select the shorter path (the path passing through fewer ASes).","en_html":"If multiple paths have the same Gao-Rexford priority (e.g. two paths from customers), select the <strong>shorter path</strong> (the path passing through fewer ASes).","zh_html":"如果多条路径具有相同的 Gao-Rexford 优先级（例如两条来自客户的路径），选择<strong>较短的路径(shorter path)</strong>（经过的 AS 数量更少的路径）。"},{"en":"If multiple paths have the same length, select the path with the closer egress router (using IGP to find distance to each egress router).","en_html":"If multiple paths have the same length, select the path with the <strong>closer egress router</strong> (using IGP to find distance to each egress router).","zh_html":"如果多条路径长度相同，选择<strong>出口路由器更近(closer egress router)</strong>的路径（使用 IGP 找到每个出口路由器的距离）。"},{"en":"If multiple paths have the same distance to egress router, select the path with the lower MED (where MED is included in the advertisement).","en_html":"If multiple paths have the same distance to egress router, select the path with the <strong>lower MED</strong> (where MED is included in the advertisement).","zh_html":"如果多条路径到出口路由器的距离相同，选择<strong>MED 较低(lower MED)</strong>的路径（MED 包含在通告中）。"},{"en":"If multiple paths have the same MED, tiebreak arbitrarily (e.g. pick the router with the lower IP address).","en_html":"If multiple paths have the same MED, <strong>tiebreak arbitrarily</strong> (e.g. pick the router with the lower IP address).","zh_html":"如果多条路径的 MED 相同，<strong>任意破平局(tiebreak arbitrarily)</strong>（例如选择 IP 地址较低的路由器）。"},{"en":"Notice that closest egress router (hot potato routing) and MED are often contradictory. Every AS prefers to minimize their own bandwidth usage, and wants the packet to be carried on other ASes’ bandwidth.","en_html":"Notice that closest egress router (hot potato routing) and MED are often contradictory. Every AS prefers to minimize their own bandwidth usage, and wants the packet to be carried on other ASes’ bandwidth.","zh_html":"注意，最近出口路由器（热土豆路由）和 MED 通常是相互矛盾的。每个 AS 都倾向于最小化自己的带宽使用，希望分组在其他 AS 的带宽上传输。"},{"en":"As the exporting AS, I want the packet to enter my AS as close to the destination as possible. This means I want the importing AS to carry the packet really far (long path to egress).","en_html":"As the exporting AS, I want the packet to enter my AS as close to the destination as possible. This means I want the importing AS to carry the packet really far (long path to egress).","zh_html":"作为导出方 AS，我希望分组尽可能靠近目的地才进入我的 AS。这意味着我希望导入方 AS 把分组传输得很远（到出口路由器的长路径）。"},{"en":"By contrast, as the importing AS, I want to carry the packet as little as possible (short path to egress). This means I want the packet to enter the other AS as far from the destination as possible (force the other AS to do all the work).","en_html":"By contrast, as the importing AS, I want to carry the packet as little as possible (short path to egress). This means I want the packet to enter the other AS as far from the destination as possible (force the other AS to do all the work).","zh_html":"相反，作为导入方 AS，我想尽可能少地传输分组（到出口路由器的短路径）。这意味着我希望分组尽可能远离目的地才进入另一个 AS（迫使另一个 AS 做所有工作）。"},{"en":"One consequence of this contradiction is that paths through the Internet are often asymmetric. If two hosts are sending packets back and forth, the path in one direction might be different from the path in the other direction.","en_html":"One consequence of this contradiction is that paths through the Internet are often asymmetric. If two hosts are sending packets back and forth, the path in one direction might be different from the path in the other direction.","zh_html":"这种矛盾的一个后果是，互联网上的路径通常是不对称的(asymmetric)。如果两台主机来回发送分组，一个方向上的路径可能与另一个方向上的路径不同。"},{"en":"In this example, for eastbound packets, A picks the west egress router and forces B to carry the traffic most of the way. In the other (westbound) direction, B picks the east egress router, and forces A to carry the traffic most of the way.","en_html":"In this example, for eastbound packets, A picks the west egress router and forces B to carry the traffic most of the way. In the other (westbound) direction, B picks the east egress router, and forces A to carry the traffic most of the way.","zh_html":"在这个例子中，对于向东的分组，A 选择西出口路由器，迫使 B 承载大部分流量。在另一个方向（向西），B 选择东出口路由器，迫使 A 承载大部分流量。"},{"en":"Fundamentally, BGP allows this behavior because every AS is granted the autonomy to set their own policy (here, that policy is hot potato routing).","en_html":"Fundamentally, BGP allows this behavior because every AS is granted the autonomy to set their own policy (here, that policy is hot potato routing).","zh_html":"从根本上说，BGP 允许这种行为，因为每个 AS 被授予设置自己策略的自治权（这里的策略是热土豆路由(hot potato routing)）。"},{"en":"In practice, sometimes ASes will try and implement more clever strategies to trick other ASes into carrying the packet further. Or, an AS with better bandwidth might agree to carry your traffic further for you, if you pay a premium fee.","en_html":"In practice, sometimes ASes will try and implement more clever strategies to trick other ASes into carrying the packet further. Or, an AS with better bandwidth might agree to carry your traffic further for you, if you pay a premium fee.","zh_html":"在实践中，有时 AS 会尝试实施更巧妙的策略来欺骗其他 AS 把分组传输得更远。或者，一个带宽更好的 AS 可能同意为你把流量传输得更远，如果你支付额外费用的话。"},{"en":"BGP Message Types and Route Attributes","en_html":"<a href=\"#bgp-message-types-and-route-attributes\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"bgp-message-types-and-route-attributes\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> BGP Message Types and Route Attributes","zh_html":"<a href=\"#bgp-message-types-and-route-attributes\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"bgp-message-types-and-route-attributes\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> BGP 消息类型和路由属性(BGP Message Types and Route Attributes)"},{"en":"Recall that a protocol must specify syntax and semantics. Specifically, BGP must specify the structure of messages being sent and received. BGP must also specify what a router should do when it receives a message.","en_html":"Recall that a protocol must specify syntax and semantics. Specifically, BGP must specify the structure of messages being sent and received. BGP must also specify what a router should do when it receives a message.","zh_html":"回想一下，一个协议必须规定语法(syntax)和语义(semantics)。具体来说，BGP 必须规定发送和接收的消息的结构。BGP 还必须规定路由器收到消息时应该做什么。"},{"en":"There are four different BGP message types. Open messages can be used to start a session between two routers to communicate with each other. KeepAlive messages can be used to confirm that a session is still open, even if messages haven’t been sent recently. Notification messages can be used to process errors. We won’t describe these first three message types in any further detail.","en_html":"There are four different BGP message types. Open messages can be used to start a session between two routers to communicate with each other. KeepAlive messages can be used to confirm that a session is still open, even if messages haven’t been sent recently. Notification messages can be used to process errors. We won’t describe these first three message types in any further detail.","zh_html":"有四种不同的 BGP 消息类型。Open 消息可用于在两台路由器之间建立会话，使它们能够互相通信。KeepAlive 消息可用于确认会话仍然处于打开状态，即使最近没有发送消息。Notification 消息可用于处理错误。我们不会进一步详细描述前三种消息类型。"},{"en":"We’ll focus on the fourth and most interesting message type, Update. These messages are used to announce new routes, change existing routes, or delete routes that are no longer active.","en_html":"We’ll focus on the fourth and most interesting message type, Update. These messages are used to announce new routes, change existing routes, or delete routes that are no longer active.","zh_html":"我们将重点关注第四种也是最有意思的消息类型：Update。这些消息用于通告新路由、更改现有路由或删除不再活动的路由。"},{"en":"The Update message contains a destination, represented as an IP prefix. The message also contains route attributes, which can be used to encode any useful information corresponding to that IP prefix. The route attributes are a set of name-value pairs, where the name indicates the type of attribute, and the value indicates the value of that attribute. A non-networking example of attributes might be: color=red, shape=triangle. The attribute names are color and shape, and they correspond to values of red and triangle, respectively.","en_html":"The Update message contains a destination, represented as an IP prefix. The message also contains <strong>route attributes</strong>, which can be used to encode any useful information corresponding to that IP prefix. The route attributes are a set of name-value pairs, where the name indicates the type of attribute, and the value indicates the value of that attribute. A non-networking example of attributes might be: color=red, shape=triangle. The attribute names are color and shape, and they correspond to values of red and triangle, respectively.","zh_html":"Update 消息包含一个目的地，表示为 IP 前缀(IP prefix)。消息还包含<strong>路由属性(route attributes)</strong>，可用于编码与该 IP 前缀对应的任何有用信息。路由属性是一组名称-值对，名称表示属性类型，值表示该属性的值。一个非网络的属性示例可能是：color=red, shape=triangle。属性名是 color 和 shape，它们对应的值是 red 和 triangle。"},{"en":"Some attributes are local to an AS, and are only exchanged in iBGP messages. Other attributes are global, and can be sent in eBGP advertisements.","en_html":"Some attributes are local to an AS, and are only exchanged in iBGP messages. Other attributes are global, and can be sent in eBGP advertisements.","zh_html":"一些属性是 AS 本地的，只在 iBGP 消息中交换。其他属性是全局的，可以在 eBGP 通告中发送。"},{"en":"There are many BGP attributes, but we’ll focus on three important ones, which are used to encode the different tiebreakers for importing paths.","en_html":"There are many BGP attributes, but we’ll focus on three important ones, which are used to encode the different tiebreakers for importing paths.","zh_html":"BGP 有许多属性，但我们将关注三个重要的属性，它们用于编码导入路径时的不同破平局规则。"},{"en":"The LOCAL PREFERENCE attribute encodes the Gao-Rexford import rules (top priority tiebreaker) inside a specific AS. An AS can assign a higher value to more preferred routes (e.g. from customers), and a lower value to less preferred routes (e.g. from providers). This attribute is local, and only carried in iBGP messages. This attribute is not sent to other ASes in eBGP announcements, because other ASes don’t need to know about this AS’s preferences.","en_html":"The <strong>LOCAL PREFERENCE</strong> attribute encodes the Gao-Rexford import rules (top priority tiebreaker) inside a specific AS. An AS can assign a higher value to more preferred routes (e.g. from customers), and a lower value to less preferred routes (e.g. from providers). This attribute is local, and only carried in iBGP messages. This attribute is not sent to other ASes in eBGP announcements, because other ASes don’t need to know about this AS’s preferences.","zh_html":"<strong>LOCAL PREFERENCE</strong> 属性在特定 AS 内部编码了 Gao-Rexford 导入规则（最高优先级的破平局规则）。一个 AS 可以为更偏好的路由（例如来自客户的路由）分配更高的值，为不太偏好的路由（例如来自提供商的路由）分配较低的值。此属性是本地的，只在 iBGP 消息中携带。此属性不会在 eBGP 通告中发送给其他 AS，因为其他 AS 不需要知道本 AS 的偏好。"},{"en":"As an example, suppose router E receives an eBGP announcement from AS#7, and router A knows that AS#7 is a customer. Then, in the iBGP message, router E can set a local preference value of 3000 (high number). Now, every other router in the same AS knows that router E can reach the destination it’s announcing, via the path in the ASPATH attribute, with a local preference of 3000.","en_html":"As an example, suppose router E receives an eBGP announcement from AS#7, and router A knows that AS#7 is a customer. Then, in the iBGP message, router E can set a local preference value of 3000 (high number). Now, every other router in the same AS knows that router E can reach the destination it’s announcing, via the path in the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">ASPATH</code> attribute, with a local preference of 3000.","zh_html":"举个例子，假设路由器 E 收到来自 AS#7 的 eBGP 通告，并且路由器 A 知道 AS#7 是一个客户。那么，在 iBGP 消息中，路由器 E 可以设置本地偏好(local preference)值为 3000（高数值）。现在，同一 AS 中的每台其他路由器都知道，路由器 E 可以通过 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">ASPATH</code> 属性中的路径到达它所通告的目的地，本地偏好为 3000。"},{"en":"By contrast, if router D receives an eBGP announcement from AS#79, and this AS is a peer, then in the iBGP message, router D can set a lower local preference value of 1000 and then distribute this path (with lower local preference) to the other routers in the AS.","en_html":"By contrast, if router D receives an eBGP announcement from AS#79, and this AS is a peer, then in the iBGP message, router D can set a lower local preference value of 1000 and then distribute this path (with lower local preference) to the other routers in the AS.","zh_html":"相反，如果路由器 D 收到来自 AS#79 的 eBGP 通告，并且这个 AS 是一个对等体，那么在 iBGP 消息中，路由器 D 可以设置较低的本地偏好值 1000，然后将这条路径（具有较低本地偏好）分发给 AS 内的其他路由器。"},{"en":"The local preference numbers are arbitrary, and only their relative ranking is important. In the example above, the numbers could have been 300 and 100 instead of 3000 and 1000, and the behavior would be the same. The local preference numbers are often set manually by operators.","en_html":"The local preference numbers are arbitrary, and only their relative ranking is important. In the example above, the numbers could have been 300 and 100 instead of 3000 and 1000, and the behavior would be the same. The local preference numbers are often set manually by operators.","zh_html":"本地偏好的数值是任意的，只有它们的相对顺序才是重要的。在上面的例子中，数值可以是 300 和 100，而不是 3000 和 1000，行为是一样的。本地偏好值通常由操作员手动设置。"},{"en":"The ASPATH attribute contains a list of ASes along the route being advertised (in reverse order). This attribute is global, and can be sent in eBGP announcements.","en_html":"The <strong>ASPATH</strong> attribute contains a list of ASes along the route being advertised (in reverse order). This attribute is global, and can be sent in eBGP announcements.","zh_html":"<strong>ASPATH</strong> 属性包含沿被通告路由的 AS 列表（逆序）。此属性是全局的，可以在 eBGP 通告中发送。"},{"en":"As an example, an announcement would have IP prefix of the destination (128.112.0.0/16), and an ASPATH attribute of [3, 72, 25].","en_html":"As an example, an announcement would have IP prefix of the destination (128.112.0.0/16), and an ASPATH attribute of [3, 72, 25].","zh_html":"举个例子，一个通告会包含目的地的 IP 前缀(128.112.0.0/16)和一个 ASPATH 属性 [3, 72, 25]。"},{"en":"The ASPATH is the second priority tiebreaker when importing paths. If two announcements have the same local preference (e.g. both are from customers), then we’ll select the shorter path. ASPATH tells us the length of each path, measured by the number of ASes the path goes through.","en_html":"The <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">ASPATH</code> is the second priority tiebreaker when importing paths. If two announcements have the same local preference (e.g. both are from customers), then we’ll select the shorter path. <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">ASPATH</code> tells us the length of each path, measured by the number of ASes the path goes through.","zh_html":"<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">ASPATH</code> 是导入路径时的第二优先级破平局规则。如果两个通告具有相同的本地偏好（例如都来自客户），那么我们会选择较短的路径。<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">ASPATH</code> 告诉我们每条路径的长度，用路径经过的 AS 数量来衡量。"},{"en":"If the local preference and path length are tied, the third priority tiebreaker is the IGP cost to the egress router. This cost is stored in the router’s local forwarding table (e.g. a local distance-vector protocol would store the cost to every other router in the same AS).","en_html":"If the local preference and path length are tied, the third priority tiebreaker is the IGP cost to the egress router. This cost is stored in the router’s local forwarding table (e.g. a local distance-vector protocol would store the cost to every other router in the same AS).","zh_html":"如果本地偏好和路径长度都相同，第三优先级的破平局规则是到出口路由器(egress router)的 IGP 成本。这个成本存储在路由器的本地转发表中（例如，本地距离向量协议会存储到同一 AS 内所有其他路由器的成本）。"},{"en":"The MED attribute encodes the preferences of the exporting AS. Equivalently, this attribute represents the distance from the exporting router to the destination (lower numbers are preferred).","en_html":"The <strong>MED</strong> attribute encodes the preferences of the exporting AS. Equivalently, this attribute represents the distance from the exporting router to the destination (lower numbers are preferred).","zh_html":"<strong>MED</strong> 属性编码了导出方 AS 的偏好。等价地，该属性表示从导出路由器到目的地的距离（较低的数字更优）。"},{"en":"For example, if there are two links between these two ASes, both border routers from the exporting AS will announce a path. The ASPATH and destination are the same, since the path of ASes to the destination is the same in both cases. However, the west router will include a lower MED attribute number, than the east router. This says: when possible, please route packets for the destination through my west router (lower number), because this router is closer to the destination.","en_html":"For example, if there are two links between these two ASes, both border routers from the exporting AS will announce a path. The <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">ASPATH</code> and destination are the same, since the path of ASes to the destination is the same in both cases. However, the west router will include a lower <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">MED</code> attribute number, than the east router. This says: when possible, please route packets for the destination through my west router (lower number), because this router is closer to the destination.","zh_html":"例如，如果两个 AS 之间有两条链路，导出方 AS 的两个边界路由器都会通告一条路径。由于两种情况下的 AS 路径到目的地是相同的，所以 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">ASPATH</code> 和目的地相同。然而，西部路由器会包含比东部路由器更低的 <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">MED</code> 属性值。这表示：如果可能的话，请通过我的西部路由器路由目的地为...的分组（较低的数字），因为这台路由器更接近目的地。"},{"en":"If the local preference, path length, and distance to egress router are all tied, the fourth priority tiebreaker is the MED number inside each announcement.","en_html":"If the local preference, path length, and distance to egress router are all tied, the fourth priority tiebreaker is the MED number inside each announcement.","zh_html":"如果本地偏好、路径长度和到出口路由器的距离都相同，第四优先级的破平局规则是每个通告中的 MED 数值。"},{"en":"Issues with BGP","en_html":"<a href=\"#issues-with-bgp\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"issues-with-bgp\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Issues with BGP","zh_html":"<a href=\"#issues-with-bgp\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"issues-with-bgp\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> BGP 的问题(Issues with BGP)"},{"en":"BGP has no built-in security guarantees. A malicious AS could lie and advertise a route to a destination, even if the AS cannot reach that destination. A malicious AS could also advertise a very cheap route to a destination, even if that cheap route doesn’t actually exist. This could encourage other ASes to route packets through the malicious AS, where the attacker could delete or modify packets passing through the malicious AS. These attacks are called prefix hijacking. There is active research on using cryptography to secure BGP, though such protocols are not widely deployed.","en_html":"BGP has no built-in security guarantees. A malicious AS could lie and advertise a route to a destination, even if the AS cannot reach that destination. A malicious AS could also advertise a very cheap route to a destination, even if that cheap route doesn’t actually exist. This could encourage other ASes to route packets through the malicious AS, where the attacker could delete or modify packets passing through the malicious AS. These attacks are called <strong>prefix hijacking</strong>. There is active research on using cryptography to secure BGP, though such protocols are not widely deployed.","zh_html":"BGP 没有内建的安全保证。一个恶意的 AS 可能撒谎，通告一条通往某个目的地的路由，即使该 AS 实际上无法到达该目的地。一个恶意的 AS 也可能通告一条通往目的地非常廉价的路由，即使那条廉价路由实际上并不存在。这可能诱使其他 AS 通过该恶意 AS 路由分组，攻击者就可以删除或修改经过该恶意 AS 的分组。这些攻击被称为<strong>前缀劫持(prefix hijacking)</strong>。目前有使用加密技术来保障 BGP 安全的活跃研究，但这类协议尚未广泛部署。"},{"en":"BGP prioritizes policy over least-cost when selecting paths. Also, because BGP measures path length in terms of the number of ASes, the path length can be misleading (e.g. one AS could contain 2 routers or 200 routers along the path being advertised). This can lead to issues where packets don’t always take least-cost paths, and it’s difficult to reason about performance on the Internet. Some might classify these as issues, though they may be more of an intentional design trade-off. The designers of BGP made a conscious design choice to prioritize policy and hide the internal topology of an AS, at the expense of performance.","en_html":"BGP prioritizes policy over least-cost when selecting paths. Also, because BGP measures path length in terms of the number of ASes, the path length can be misleading (e.g. one AS could contain 2 routers or 200 routers along the path being advertised). This can lead to issues where packets don’t always take least-cost paths, and it’s difficult to reason about performance on the Internet. Some might classify these as issues, though they may be more of an intentional design trade-off. The designers of BGP made a conscious design choice to prioritize policy and hide the internal topology of an AS, at the expense of performance.","zh_html":"BGP 在选择路径时优先考虑策略(policy)而非最小成本。此外，由于 BGP 以 AS 数量来衡量路径长度，路径长度可能具有误导性（例如，一个 AS 沿被通告的路径可能包含 2 台路由器或 200 台路由器）。这可能导致分组并不总是走最小成本路径，且难以推断互联网上的性能。有些人可能将这些归类为问题，尽管它们可能更像是有意的设计权衡。BGP 的设计者有意选择了优先考虑策略和隐藏 AS 内部拓扑，代价是牺牲了性能。"},{"en":"BGP is complicated to implement. There are many subtle implementation details that we didn’t cover. Even in the topics we covered, certain configurations like local preference or MED numbers have to be manually set by the operator, and incorrect configurations could lead to incorrect paths spreading through the network. BGP misconfigurations can often lead to Internet outages, and there is active research on tools to verify that BGP is properly configured.","en_html":"BGP is complicated to implement. There are many subtle implementation details that we didn’t cover. Even in the topics we covered, certain configurations like local preference or MED numbers have to be manually set by the operator, and incorrect configurations could lead to incorrect paths spreading through the network. BGP misconfigurations can often lead to Internet outages, and there is active research on tools to verify that BGP is properly configured.","zh_html":"BGP 实现起来很复杂。我们未涉及的微妙实现细节有很多。即使在我们涉及的主题中，某些配置（例如本地偏好或 MED 数值）也必须由操作员手动设置，错误的配置可能导致不正确的路径在网络上传播。BGP 配置错误经常导致互联网中断，目前有关于验证 BGP 配置是否正确工具的活跃研究。"},{"en":"BGP requires certain assumptions (everybody is following the Gao-Rexford rules, AS graph forms a hierarchy, no provider-customer cycles) in order to guarantee reachability and convergence. If these assumptions don’t hold (e.g. an AS chooses its own policy that violates Gao-Rexford), BGP can produce unstable behavior, where routes never converge, or cycles and dead-ends appear.","en_html":"BGP requires certain assumptions (everybody is following the Gao-Rexford rules, AS graph forms a hierarchy, no provider-customer cycles) in order to guarantee reachability and convergence. If these assumptions don’t hold (e.g. an AS chooses its own policy that violates Gao-Rexford), BGP can produce unstable behavior, where routes never converge, or cycles and dead-ends appear.","zh_html":"BGP 需要某些假设（每个人都遵循 Gao-Rexford 规则，AS 图形成层次结构，无提供商-客户环）来保证可达性(reachability)和收敛(convergence)。如果这些假设不成立（例如，一个 AS 选择违反 Gao-Rexford 的自身策略），BGP 可能产生不稳定的行为，路由永不收敛，或者出现环路和死胡同。"}]},"/routing/distance-vector.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Distance-Vector Protocols","en_html":"<a href=\"#distance-vector-protocols\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"distance-vector-protocols\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Distance-Vector Protocols","zh_html":"<a href=\"#distance-vector-protocols\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"distance-vector-protocols\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 距离向量协议(Distance-Vector Protocols)"},{"en":"Algorithm Sketch","en_html":"<a href=\"#algorithm-sketch\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"algorithm-sketch\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Algorithm Sketch","zh_html":"<a href=\"#algorithm-sketch\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"algorithm-sketch\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 算法草图(Algorithm Sketch)"},{"en":"In this section, we’ll design a distance-vector protocol, which is one of three classes of routing algorithms (along with link-state and path-vector).","en_html":"In this section, we’ll design a <strong>distance-vector protocol</strong>, which is one of three classes of routing algorithms (along with link-state and path-vector).","zh_html":"在本节中，我们将设计一个<strong>距离向量协议(distance-vector protocol)</strong>，这是三类路由算法(路由算法)之一（另外两种是链路状态(link-state)和路径向量(path-vector)）。"},{"en":"Distance-vector protocols have a long history on the Internet and ARPANET (the predecessor to the Internet). The prototypical distance-vector protocol is the Routing Information Protocol (RIP), and the D-V protocol we’ll design shares many similarities with RIP.","en_html":"Distance-vector protocols have a long history on the Internet and ARPANET (the predecessor to the Internet). The prototypical distance-vector protocol is the <strong>Routing Information Protocol (RIP)</strong>, and the D-V protocol we’ll design shares many similarities with RIP.","zh_html":"距离向量协议(distance-vector protocols)在互联网(Internet)及其前身 ARPANET 上有着悠久的历史。最具代表性的距离向量协议是<strong>路由信息协议(Routing Information Protocol, RIP)</strong>，我们即将设计的 D-V 协议与 RIP 有许多相似之处。"},{"en":"To gain some intuition for the routing protocol we’ll study in this section, consider the following network.","en_html":"To gain some intuition for the routing protocol we’ll study in this section, consider the following network.","zh_html":"为了获得一些关于本节将要学习的路由协议(routing protocol)的直觉，请考虑下面的网络。"},{"en":"To start out, every router’s forwarding table is empty. Our goal is to fill in the forwarding tables of every router, such that packets can be routed from anywhere to the destination, A.","en_html":"To start out, every router’s forwarding table is empty. Our goal is to fill in the forwarding tables of every router, such that packets can be routed from anywhere to the destination, A.","zh_html":"开始时，每台路由器的转发表(forwarding table)都是空的。我们的目标是填满每台路由器的转发表，使得分组(packets)可以从任何地方路由到目的地(destination) A。"},{"en":"To start, A can tell R1: “I am A.” Now, R1 knows how to forward packets to A.","en_html":"To start, A can tell R1: “I am A.” Now, R1 knows how to forward packets to A.","zh_html":"首先，A 可以告诉 R1：\"我是 A。\"现在，R1 知道如何将分组转发到 A。"},{"en":"Now that R1 has a path to A, it can tell its neighbors, R2 and R3: “I am R1, and I can reach A.”","en_html":"Now that R1 has a path to A, it can tell its neighbors, R2 and R3: “I am R1, and I can reach A.”","zh_html":"既然 R1 有了一条通往 A 的路径，它可以告诉它的邻居 R2 和 R3：\"我是 R1，我能到达 A。\""},{"en":"Now, R2 and R3 know that they can reach A by forwarding packets to R1.","en_html":"Now, R2 and R3 know that they can reach A by forwarding packets to R1.","zh_html":"现在，R2 和 R3 知道它们可以通过将分组转发给 R1 来到达 A。"},{"en":"R2 can now tell its neighbors, R4 and R5: “I am R2, and I can reach A.” Similarly, R3 can tell its neighbors, R6 and R7: “I am R3, and I can reach A.”","en_html":"R2 can now tell its neighbors, R4 and R5: “I am R2, and I can reach A.” Similarly, R3 can tell its neighbors, R6 and R7: “I am R3, and I can reach A.”","zh_html":"R2 现在可以告诉它的邻居 R4 和 R5：\"我是 R2，我能到达 A。\"类似地，R3 可以告诉它的邻居 R6 和 R7：\"我是 R3，我能到达 A。\""},{"en":"Now, R4 and R5 know that packets for A can be forwarded to R2, and R6 and R7 know that packets for A can be forwarded to R3.","en_html":"Now, R4 and R5 know that packets for A can be forwarded to R2, and R6 and R7 know that packets for A can be forwarded to R3.","zh_html":"现在，R4 和 R5 知道发往 A 的分组可以转发给 R2，R6 和 R7 知道发往 A 的分组可以转发给 R3。"},{"en":"The process continues: R4, R5, R6, and R7 each tell their neighbors who they are, and that they can reach A. By the end, everybody’s forwarding table is filled in, and we can route packets from anywhere in the network towards A.","en_html":"The process continues: R4, R5, R6, and R7 each tell their neighbors who they are, and that they can reach A. By the end, everybody’s forwarding table is filled in, and we can route packets from anywhere in the network towards A.","zh_html":"这个过程会继续：R4、R5、R6 和 R7 各自告诉它们的邻居它们是谁，以及它们能到达 A。最终，每台路由器的转发表都被填满了，我们可以将分组从网络中的任何地方路由到 A。"},{"en":"In summary: When you receive an announcement from someone saying they can reach A, you should write down who sent the announcement. Now, you can send messages bound for A through that person.","en_html":"In summary: When you receive an announcement from someone saying they can reach A, you should write down who sent the announcement. Now, you can send messages bound for A through that person.","zh_html":"总结：当你收到某人说他们能到达 A 的宣告(announcement)时，你应该记下是谁发送了宣告。现在，你可以将发往 A 的消息通过那个人发送。"},{"en":"Also, now that you have a way to send messages to A, you should make an announcement to all of your neighbors, so that they can send messages bound for A through you.","en_html":"Also, now that you have a way to send messages to A, you should make an announcement to all of your neighbors, so that they can send messages bound for A through you.","zh_html":"此外，既然你有了向 A 发送消息的方式，你应该向你所有的邻居发送宣告，这样他们就可以通过你发送发往 A 的消息。"},{"en":"What if there were multiple destinations? We could run this same algorithm repeatedly, once per destination. The forwarding table would then contain multiple entries, one per destination.","en_html":"What if there were multiple destinations? We could run this same algorithm repeatedly, once per destination. The forwarding table would then contain multiple entries, one per destination.","zh_html":"如果有多个目的地(destinations)呢？我们可以对每个目的地重复运行同样的算法。那么转发表(forwarding table)中就会包含多个条目，每个目的地一个。"},{"en":"In these notes, we’ll focus on a single section for simplicity, but the protocol we’ll design can extend to multiple destinations.","en_html":"In these notes, we’ll focus on a single section for simplicity, but the protocol we’ll design can extend to multiple destinations.","zh_html":"在这些笔记中，为简单起见我们将专注于一个目的地，但我们设计的协议可以扩展到多个目的地。"},{"en":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","en_html":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","zh_html":"让我们回顾一下目前的协议。"},{"en":"For each destination:","en_html":"For each destination:","zh_html":"对于每个目的地(destination)："},{"en":"If you hear about a path to that destination, update the table.","en_html":"If you hear about a path to that destination, update the table.","zh_html":"如果你听说了一条到达该目的地的路径，就更新转发表(forwarding table)。"},{"en":"Then, tell all your neighbors.","en_html":"Then, tell all your neighbors.","zh_html":"然后，告诉你所有的邻居。"},{"en":"Direction of Announcements and Messages","en_html":"<a href=\"#direction-of-announcements-and-messages\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"direction-of-announcements-and-messages\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Direction of Announcements and Messages","zh_html":"<a href=\"#direction-of-announcements-and-messages\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"direction-of-announcements-and-messages\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 宣告与消息的方向(Direction of Announcements and Messages)"},{"en":"In this protocol, it’s easy to confuse the the direction in which announcements and messages are sent.","en_html":"In this protocol, it’s easy to confuse the the direction in which announcements and messages are sent.","zh_html":"在这个协议中，容易混淆宣告(announcement)和消息(message)的发送方向。"},{"en":"The announcements for how to reach A start at A, and propagate outward. For example, B sent an announcement to D, saying “I am B, and messages for A can be sent through me.”","en_html":"The announcements for how to reach A start at A, and propagate outward. For example, B sent an announcement to D, saying “I am B, and messages for A can be sent through me.”","zh_html":"关于如何到达 A 的宣告从 A 开始，向外传播。例如，B 向 D 发送了一个宣告，说\"我是 B，发往 A 的消息可以经由我发送。\""},{"en":"By contrast, the actual messages being sent to A are sent inward, toward A. For example, a message might start at D and be sent to B on its way to A.","en_html":"By contrast, the actual messages being sent to A are sent inward, toward A. For example, a message might start at D and be sent to B on its way to A.","zh_html":"相比之下，实际发送到 A 的消息是向内、朝向 A 发送的。例如，一条消息可能从 D 出发，在前往 A 的途中经过 B。"},{"en":"The direction of announcements is exactly the opposite of the direction of the messages themselves. Be careful not to confuse announcements with the actual messages!","en_html":"The direction of announcements is exactly the opposite of the direction of the messages themselves. Be careful not to confuse announcements with the actual messages!","zh_html":"宣告的方向与消息本身的方向完全相反。注意不要把宣告和实际消息搞混了！"},{"en":"Rule 1: Bellman-Ford Updates","en_html":"<a href=\"#rule-1-bellman-ford-updates\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-1-bellman-ford-updates\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Rule 1: Bellman-Ford Updates","zh_html":"<a href=\"#rule-1-bellman-ford-updates\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-1-bellman-ford-updates\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 规则 1：Bellman-Ford 更新(Rule 1: Bellman-Ford Updates)"},{"en":"What if there are multiple paths to reach A?","en_html":"What if there are multiple paths to reach A?","zh_html":"如果有多条路径可以到达 A 呢？"},{"en":"In this scenario, both R3 and R4 will announce that they can reach A. Should R5 choose to forward packets to R3 or R4?","en_html":"In this scenario, both R3 and R4 will announce that they can reach A. Should R5 choose to forward packets to R3 or R4?","zh_html":"在这种情况下，R3 和 R4 都会宣告它们能到达 A。R5 应该选择将分组转发给 R3 还是 R4？"},{"en":"Recall that our goal is to find least-cost routes through the network. To allow routers to pick the least-cost path out of multiple being advertised, we’ll need to also include costs in the announcements.","en_html":"Recall that our goal is to find least-cost routes through the network. To allow routers to pick the least-cost path out of multiple being advertised, we’ll need to also include costs in the announcements.","zh_html":"回想一下，我们的目标是找到通过网络的最低成本路径(least-cost routes)。为了让路由器能够在多个被宣告的路径中选择成本最低的那条，我们还需要在宣告中包含成本(cost)信息。"},{"en":"R3’s announcement now says: “I am R3, and I can reach A with cost 3.”","en_html":"R3’s announcement now says: “I am R3, and I can reach A with cost 3.”","zh_html":"R3 的宣告现在说：\"我是 R3，我能以成本 3 到达 A。\""},{"en":"R4’s announcement now says: “I am R4, and I can reach A with cost 2.”","en_html":"R4’s announcement now says: “I am R4, and I can reach A with cost 2.”","zh_html":"R4 的宣告现在说：\"我是 R4，我能以成本 2 到达 A。\""},{"en":"Now, R5 notices that R4 is offering the shorter path, and decides to forward packets via R4.","en_html":"Now, R5 notices that R4 is offering the shorter path, and decides to forward packets via R4.","zh_html":"现在，R5 注意到 R4 提供的路径更短，于是决定通过 R4 转发分组。"},{"en":"We’ll use the forwarding table to remember the best-known cost to the destination (and the corresponding next-hop). Each entry of the forwarding table now tells us: the destination, the next-hop for that destination, and the cost to reach the destination via that next hop.","en_html":"We’ll use the forwarding table to remember the best-known cost to the destination (and the corresponding next-hop). Each entry of the forwarding table now tells us: the destination, the next-hop for that destination, and the cost to reach the destination via that next hop.","zh_html":"我们将使用转发表(forwarding table)来记住到目的地的最佳已知成本(及对应的下一跳)。转发表中的每个条目现在告诉我们：目的地(destination)、该目的地的下一跳(next-hop)、以及经由该下一跳到达目的地的成本。"},{"en":"Note: Formally, the forwarding table stores key-value pairs, mapping each destination to a 2-tuple containing the next hop and the distance. We’ll draw tables with 3 columns for simplicity.","en_html":"Note: Formally, the forwarding table stores key-value pairs, mapping each destination to a 2-tuple containing the next hop and the distance. We’ll draw tables with 3 columns for simplicity.","zh_html":"注：从形式上讲，转发表(forwarding table)存储键值对(key-value pairs)，将每个目的地映射到一个包含下一跳(next hop)和距离(distance)的二元组。为简单起见，我们将绘制三列表格。"},{"en":"R5 might not hear about both paths simultaneously, so we’ll need to be more precise about what happens when we hear about a new path. There are three possibilities when we hear about a path:","en_html":"R5 might not hear about both paths simultaneously, so we’ll need to be more precise about what happens when we hear about a new path. There are three possibilities when we hear about a path:","zh_html":"R5 可能不会同时听到两条路径，所以我们需要更精确地描述当我们听说一条新路径时会发生什么。当我们听说一条路径时，有三种可能："},{"en":"If the table doesn’t have a path to the destination, accept the path. If I don’t have a way to reach A, I should accept any path offered.","en_html":"<p>If the table doesn’t have a path to the destination, accept the path. If I don’t have a way to reach A, I should accept any path offered.</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/routing/2-039-multipath3.png\" /></img></p>","zh_html":"<p>如果表中没有到达该目的地的路径，接受该路径。如果我没有办法到达 A，我应该接受任何提供的路径。</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/routing/2-039-multipath3.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"If the new path (that we hear about) is better than the best-known path (from the forwarding table), we should accept the new path, and replace the old path from the table.","en_html":"<p>If the new path (that we hear about) is better than the best-known path (from the forwarding table), we should accept the new path, and replace the old path from the table.</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/routing/2-040-multipath4.png\" /></img></p>","zh_html":"<p>如果新路径（我们听说的）比最佳已知路径（来自转发表）更好，我们应该接受新路径，并用新路径替换表中的旧路径。</p> <p><img width=\"900px\" src=\"../assets/routing/2-040-multipath4.png\" /></img></p>"},{"en":"If the new path (that we hear about) is worse than the best-known path (from the forwarding table), we should ignore the new path, and keep using the path in the table.","en_html":"<p>If the new path (that we hear about) is worse than the best-known path (from the forwarding table), we should ignore the new path, and keep using the path in the table.</p>","zh_html":"<p>如果新路径（我们听说的）比最佳已知路径（来自转发表）更差，我们应该忽略新路径，继续使用表中的路径。</p>"},{"en":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","en_html":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","zh_html":"让我们回顾一下目前的协议。"},{"en":"For each destination:","en_html":"For each destination:","zh_html":"对于每个目的地(destination)："},{"en":"If you hear about a path to that destination, update the table if: The destination isn’t in the table. The advertised cost is better than the best-known cost.","en_html":"If you hear about a path to that destination, update the table if: <ul> <li><strong>The destination isn’t in the table.</strong></li> <li><strong>The advertised cost is better than the best-known cost.</strong></li> </ul>","zh_html":"如果你听说了一条到达该目的地的路径，在以下情况下更新转发表(forwarding table)： <ul> <li><strong>表中没有该目的地。</strong></li> <li><strong>被宣告的成本(advertised cost)优于最佳已知成本。</strong></li> </ul>"},{"en":"Then, tell all your neighbors.","en_html":"Then, tell all your neighbors.","zh_html":"然后，告诉你所有的邻居。"},{"en":"How do we know if a new path is better or worse? We have to be careful, because not all link costs are the same. When someone advertises a path, the cost via that path is actually the sum of two numbers: The link cost from you to the neighbor, plus the cost from the neighbor to the destination (as advertised by the neighbor).","en_html":"How do we know if a new path is better or worse? We have to be careful, because not all link costs are the same. When someone advertises a path, the cost via that path is actually the sum of two numbers: The link cost from you to the neighbor, plus the cost from the neighbor to the destination (as advertised by the neighbor).","zh_html":"我们怎么知道新路径更好还是更差？我们必须小心，因为并不是所有链路成本(link costs)都相同。当有人宣告一条路径时，经由该路径的成本实际上是两个数字之和：从你到邻居的链路成本(link cost)，加上从邻居到目的地的成本（邻居宣告的）。"},{"en":"As a concrete example, suppose we hear: “I am R1, and A is 5 away from me.” The cost of this new path is actually 1 (the link cost from us to R1), plus 5 (the cost from R1 to A, from the advertisement), which is 6.","en_html":"As a concrete example, suppose we hear: “I am R1, and A is 5 away from me.” The cost of this new path is actually 1 (the link cost from us to R1), plus 5 (the cost from R1 to A, from the advertisement), which is 6.","zh_html":"举一个具体的例子，假设我们听到：\"我是 R1，A 离我有 5。\"这条新路径的成本实际上是 1（从我们到 R1 的链路成本），加上 5（从 R1 到 A 的成本，来自宣告），等于 6。"},{"en":"Later, we might hear: “I am R2, and A is 3 away from me.” It is incorrect to just look at the cost in the advertisement. In this case, the cost of the new path is actually 10 (the link cost from us to R2), plus 3 (the cost from R2 to A, from the advertisement), which is 13. This cost is not better than our best-known cost of 6, so we don’t update the table. Packets still get forwarded to R1.","en_html":"Later, we might hear: “I am R2, and A is 3 away from me.” It is incorrect to just look at the cost in the advertisement. In this case, the cost of the new path is actually 10 (the link cost from us to R2), plus 3 (the cost from R2 to A, from the advertisement), which is 13. This cost is not better than our best-known cost of 6, so we don’t update the table. Packets still get forwarded to R1.","zh_html":"之后，我们可能听到：\"我是 R2，A 离我有 3。\"只看宣告中的成本是不对的。在这种情况下，新路径的成本实际上是 10（从我们到 R2 的链路成本），加上 3（从 R2 到 A 的成本，来自宣告），等于 13。这个成本并不比我们最佳已知成本 6 好，所以我们不更新转发表(forwarding table)。分组(packets)仍然转发给 R1。"},{"en":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","en_html":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","zh_html":"让我们回顾一下目前的协议。"},{"en":"For each destination:","en_html":"For each destination:","zh_html":"对于每个目的地(destination)："},{"en":"If you hear about a path to that destination, update the table if: The destination isn’t in the table. The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost.","en_html":"If you hear about a path to that destination, update the table if: <ul> <li>The destination isn’t in the table.</li> <li>The advertised cost, <strong>plus the link cost to the neighbor</strong>, is better than the best-known cost.</li> </ul>","zh_html":"如果你听说了一条到达该目的地的路径，在以下情况下更新转发表(forwarding table)： <ul> <li>表中没有该目的地。</li> <li>被宣告的成本<strong>加上到达邻居的链路成本</strong>，优于最佳已知成本。</li> </ul>"},{"en":"Then, tell all your neighbors.","en_html":"Then, tell all your neighbors.","zh_html":"然后，告诉你所有的邻居。"},{"en":"For every announcement we hear, we have to compare two numbers. One number is the best-known cost in the table. The other number is the sum of the link cost to the neighbor, plus the advertised cost from neighbor to destination. If the latter number is lower, we use the new path and abandon the old path.","en_html":"For every announcement we hear, we have to compare two numbers. One number is the best-known cost in the table. The other number is the sum of the link cost to the neighbor, plus the advertised cost from neighbor to destination. If the latter number is lower, we use the new path and abandon the old path.","zh_html":"对于每次听到的宣告(announcement)，我们都必须比较两个数字。一个数字是转发表(forwarding table)中的最佳已知成本。另一个数字是到邻居的链路成本(link cost)加上从邻居到目的地的宣告成本之和。如果后一个数字更低，我们就使用新路径并放弃旧路径。"},{"en":"Rule 1: Distributed Bellman-Ford Algorithm","en_html":"<a href=\"#rule-1-distributed-bellman-ford-algorithm\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-1-distributed-bellman-ford-algorithm\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Rule 1: Distributed Bellman-Ford Algorithm","zh_html":"<a href=\"#rule-1-distributed-bellman-ford-algorithm\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-1-distributed-bellman-ford-algorithm\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 规则 1：分布式 Bellman-Ford 算法(Rule 1: Distributed Bellman-Ford Algorithm)"},{"en":"Does this operation look familiar? It turns out, this is exactly the relaxation operation from Dijkstra’s shortest paths algorithm!","en_html":"Does this operation look familiar? It turns out, this is exactly the relaxation operation from Dijkstra’s shortest paths algorithm!","zh_html":"这个操作看起来眼熟吗？事实证明，这正是 Dijkstra 最短路径算法中的松弛操作(relaxation operation)！"},{"en":"Bellman-Ford is another shortest paths algorithm that relies on relaxation as the key operation. Bellman-Ford is even simpler than Dijkstra’s: Cycle through all the edges repeatedly, relaxing every edge, until we get all the shortest paths.","en_html":"<strong>Bellman-Ford</strong> is another shortest paths algorithm that relies on relaxation as the key operation. Bellman-Ford is even simpler than Dijkstra’s: Cycle through all the edges repeatedly, relaxing every edge, until we get all the shortest paths.","zh_html":"<strong>Bellman-Ford</strong> 是另一种依赖于松弛(relaxation)作为关键操作的最短路径算法。Bellman-Ford 甚至比 Dijkstra 更简单：反复遍历所有边，松弛每条边，直到得到所有最短路径(shortest paths)。"},{"en":"You might have implemented Dijkstra’s or Bellman-Ford before in a data structures class, like CS 61B at UC Berkeley. Unfortunately, the code you wrote wouldn’t be very useful for our routing protocol. Remember, the routing protocol must be distributed, because routers don’t have a global view of the network (no central mastermind). Also, the routers are operating asynchronously. There’s nobody enforcing the order in which routers perform relaxation operations, or the order in which routers send out announcements.","en_html":"You might have implemented Dijkstra’s or Bellman-Ford before in a data structures class, like CS 61B at UC Berkeley. Unfortunately, the code you wrote wouldn’t be very useful for our routing protocol. Remember, the routing protocol must be distributed, because routers don’t have a global view of the network (no central mastermind). Also, the routers are operating asynchronously. There’s nobody enforcing the order in which routers perform relaxation operations, or the order in which routers send out announcements.","zh_html":"你可能在数据结构课程（如 UC Berkeley 的 CS 61B）中实现过 Dijkstra 或 Bellman-Ford。可惜你当时写的代码对我们的路由协议(routing protocol)用处不大。记住，路由协议必须是分布式的(distributed)，因为路由器没有网络的全局视图（没有中央主脑）。而且，路由器是异步(asynchronously)运行的。没有人强制路由器执行松弛操作的顺序，也没有人强制路由器发送宣告的顺序。"},{"en":"Instead, the routing protocol that we’ve been designing is a distributed, asynchronous version of the Bellman-Ford algorithm. The protocol is distributed, because we aren’t asking a single computer to run the entire algorithm. Instead, every router is computing its own part of the answer (populating its own forwarding table) without seeing the entire graph. The protocol is asynchronous, because the routers can all run the algorithm at the same time, without needing to control the order of operations.","en_html":"Instead, the routing protocol that we’ve been designing is a distributed, asynchronous version of the Bellman-Ford algorithm. The protocol is distributed, because we aren’t asking a single computer to run the entire algorithm. Instead, every router is computing its own part of the answer (populating its own forwarding table) without seeing the entire graph. The protocol is asynchronous, because the routers can all run the algorithm at the same time, without needing to control the order of operations.","zh_html":"相反，我们一直在设计的路由协议(routing protocol)是 Bellman-Ford 算法的一个分布式、异步的版本。该协议是分布式的(distributed)，因为我们不是让一台计算机运行整个算法。相反，每台路由器都在不看到整张图(graph)的情况下计算自己的那部分答案（填充自己的转发表(forwarding table)）。该协议是异步的(asynchronous)，因为路由器可以同时运行该算法，无需控制操作的顺序。"},{"en":"Note: Although we’re showing a single destination for simplicity, don’t forget that our routing protocol will be able to find shortest paths to all destinations, just like the centralized (single-computer) Dijkstra’s or Bellman-Ford algorithms.","en_html":"Note: Although we’re showing a single destination for simplicity, don’t forget that our routing protocol will be able to find shortest paths to all destinations, just like the centralized (single-computer) Dijkstra’s or Bellman-Ford algorithms.","zh_html":"注：虽然为简单起见我们只展示了一个目的地，但别忘了我们的路由协议(routing protocol)将能够找到到所有目的地的最短路径，就像集中式（单机）的 Dijkstra 或 Bellman-Ford 算法一样。"},{"en":"Bellman-Ford Demo","en_html":"<a href=\"#bellman-ford-demo\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"bellman-ford-demo\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Bellman-Ford Demo","zh_html":"<a href=\"#bellman-ford-demo\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"bellman-ford-demo\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Bellman-Ford 演示(Bellman-Ford Demo)"},{"en":"Terminology note: When we send a message like “I am R1, and I can reach A with cost 5,” to our neighbors, this is often called announcing or advertising a route. Notice that the advertisement contains three values: the destination, your identity (so your neighbors can forward to you), and the total cost from you to the destination.","en_html":"Terminology note: When we send a message like “I am R1, and I can reach A with cost 5,” to our neighbors, this is often called <strong>announcing</strong> or <strong>advertising</strong> a route. Notice that the advertisement contains three values: the destination, your identity (so your neighbors can forward to you), and the total cost from you to the destination.","zh_html":"术语说明：当我们向邻居发送诸如\"我是 R1，我能以成本 5 到达 A\"这样的消息时，这通常被称为<strong>宣告(announcing)</strong>或<strong>通告(advertising)</strong>一条路由。注意宣告包含三个值：目的地(destination)、你的身份（这样你的邻居就可以转发给你）以及从你到目的地的总成本。"},{"en":"To restate the algorithm so far one more time:","en_html":"To restate the algorithm so far one more time:","zh_html":"再重申一下到目前为止的算法："},{"en":"When you receive an announcement from another router, you add the cost from the destination to the other router (this cost is in the announcement), plus the cost of the link from the other router to you. If this sum is less than the best-known distance to destination in your table, you replace your forwarding table entry for this destination with the new next hop (identity of the other router from the announcement) and the new distance (the sum you just computed).","en_html":"When you receive an announcement from another router, you add the cost from the destination to the other router (this cost is in the announcement), plus the cost of the link from the other router to you. If this sum is less than the best-known distance to destination in your table, you replace your forwarding table entry for this destination with the new next hop (identity of the other router from the announcement) and the new distance (the sum you just computed).","zh_html":"当你收到来自另一台路由器的宣告(announcement)时，你将到目的地的成本（宣告中的成本）加上从该路由器到你的链路成本(link cost)。如果这个和小于你转发表(forwarding table)中到目的地的最佳已知距离，你就用新的下一跳(next hop)（宣告中另一台路由器的身份）和新的距离（你刚算出的和）替换该目的地的转发表条目。"},{"en":"What if you receive an announcement from another router, and the destination isn’t in your forwarding table? You don’t have a best-known distance to this destination, because you don’t know how to reach this destination yet. In this case, you can add a new entry to your forwarding table with the new destination, and the next hop and cost from the announcement.","en_html":"What if you receive an announcement from another router, and the destination isn’t in your forwarding table? You don’t have a best-known distance to this destination, because you don’t know how to reach this destination yet. In this case, you can add a new entry to your forwarding table with the new destination, and the next hop and cost from the announcement.","zh_html":"如果你收到来自另一台路由器的宣告，但目的地不在你的转发表(forwarding table)中呢？你没有到该目的地的最佳已知距离，因为你还不知道如何到达该目的地。在这种情况下，你可以在转发表中添加一个新条目，包含新的目的地，以及来自宣告的下一跳(next hop)和成本(cost)。"},{"en":"When you change your forwarding table, that means that you’ve discovered a new path to the destination. In order to propagate this new path to the rest of the network, you will need to announce this new path (destination, your identity, and cost via you) to your adjacent routers.","en_html":"When you change your forwarding table, that means that you’ve discovered a new path to the destination. In order to propagate this new path to the rest of the network, you will need to announce this new path (destination, your identity, and cost via you) to your adjacent routers.","zh_html":"当你更改了转发表(forwarding table)，这意味着你发现了一条到达目的地的新路径。为了将此新路径传播到网络的其余部分，你需要向你的相邻路由器(adjacent routers)宣告此新路径（目的地、你的身份以及经由你的成本）。"},{"en":"With this algorithm in mind, let’s run through an example. In this network, we’ll assume all edges have cost 1 since the edges are unlabeled. We want to populate the forwarding tables with routes to A, the one and only destination.","en_html":"With this algorithm in mind, let’s run through an example. In this network, we’ll assume all edges have cost 1 since the edges are unlabeled. We want to populate the forwarding tables with routes to A, the one and only destination.","zh_html":"带着这个算法，让我们运行一个例子。在这个网络中，由于边没有标记，我们假设所有边的成本都是 1。我们想要用通往 A（唯一的目的地）的路由来填充转发表(forwarding tables)。"},{"en":"First, using static routing, we hard-code an entry in R1’s forwarding table. To reach destination A, the next hop is A itself, and the cost of this path is 1.","en_html":"First, using static routing, we hard-code an entry in R1’s forwarding table. To reach destination A, the next hop is A itself, and the cost of this path is 1.","zh_html":"首先，使用静态路由(static routing)，我们在 R1 的转发表(forwarding table)中硬编码一个条目。要到达目的地 A，下一跳(next hop)就是 A 本身，这条路径的成本是 1。"},{"en":"R1’s forwarding table has changed, so R1 will create a new announcement with 3 values: the destination (A), the router’s identity (R1), and the cost to the destination via this router (1). This announcement is sent to all of R1’s adjacent routers, namely only R2.","en_html":"R1’s forwarding table has changed, so R1 will create a new announcement with 3 values: the destination (A), the router’s identity (R1), and the cost to the destination via this router (1). This announcement is sent to all of R1’s adjacent routers, namely only R2.","zh_html":"R1 的转发表(forwarding table)发生了变化，所以 R1 将创建一个包含 3 个值的新宣告(announcement)：目的地(A)、路由器的身份(R1)、以及经由该路由器到目的地的成本(1)。此宣告被发送给 R1 的所有相邻路由器，即只有 R2。"},{"en":"R2 receives this announcement and looks in its forwarding table for an entry corresponding to destination A. The forwarding table is empty, so no such entry exists. Therefore, R2 will add a new entry with 3 values: the destination (A), the next hop (R1, from the announcement), and the cost to the destination via R1 (2, summing the cost in the announcement and the cost of the link to R1).","en_html":"R2 receives this announcement and looks in its forwarding table for an entry corresponding to destination A. The forwarding table is empty, so no such entry exists. Therefore, R2 will add a new entry with 3 values: the destination (A), the next hop (R1, from the announcement), and the cost to the destination via R1 (2, summing the cost in the announcement and the cost of the link to R1).","zh_html":"R2 收到此宣告并在其转发表(forwarding table)中查找对应于目的地 A 的条目。转发表是空的，所以没有这样的条目。因此，R2 将添加一个包含 3 个值的新条目：目的地(A)、下一跳(R1，来自宣告)、以及经由 R1 到目的地的成本(2，将宣告中的成本与到达 R1 的链路成本相加)。"},{"en":"R2’s forwarding table has changed, so R2 will make an announcement with 3 values: the destination (A), the router’s identity (R2), and the cost to the destination via this router (2). This announcement is sent to all of R2’s adjacent routers, namely R3 and R1.","en_html":"R2’s forwarding table has changed, so R2 will make an announcement with 3 values: the destination (A), the router’s identity (R2), and the cost to the destination via this router (2). This announcement is sent to all of R2’s adjacent routers, namely R3 and R1.","zh_html":"R2 的转发表(forwarding table)发生了变化，所以 R2 将发出一个包含 3 个值的宣告(announcement)：目的地(A)、路由器的身份(R2)、以及经由该路由器到目的地的成本(2)。此宣告被发送给 R2 的所有相邻路由器，即 R3 和 R1。"},{"en":"Note that in our protocol so far, routers send announcements to all of their neighbors. This means that R2’s announcement is sent to R1 as well. If this bothers you, stay tuned, we’ll revisit it later.","en_html":"Note that in our protocol so far, routers send announcements to all of their neighbors. This means that R2’s announcement is sent to R1 as well. If this bothers you, stay tuned, we’ll revisit it later.","zh_html":"注意，在我们目前的协议中，路由器向它们所有的邻居发送宣告(announcements)。这意味着 R2 的宣告也会发送给 R1。如果这让你感到困扰，请继续关注，我们稍后会重新讨论这个问题。"},{"en":"R1 receives this announcement. According to R1’s forwarding table, the best-known way to reach A has cost 1. The path via R2 would instead cost 2 (from R2’s announcement), plus 1 (link to R2), for a total of 3. This is a worse way to reach A, so R1 will ignore this announcement and leave its forwarding table unchanged.","en_html":"R1 receives this announcement. According to R1’s forwarding table, the best-known way to reach A has cost 1. The path via R2 would instead cost 2 (from R2’s announcement), plus 1 (link to R2), for a total of 3. This is a worse way to reach A, so R1 will ignore this announcement and leave its forwarding table unchanged.","zh_html":"R1 收到此宣告。根据 R1 的转发表(forwarding table)，到达 A 的最佳已知方式是成本 1。经由 R2 的路径成本将为 2（来自 R2 的宣告）加上 1（到 R2 的链路成本），总共 3。这是到达 A 的更差方式，所以 R1 将忽略此宣告，不改变其转发表。"},{"en":"R3 also receives the same announcement. R3’s forwarding table is empty, so R3 will install a new entry with 3 values: the destination (A), the next hop (R2, from the announcement), and the cost to the destination via R2 (3 summing the cost from the announcement, and the cost of the R3-R2 link).","en_html":"R3 also receives the same announcement. R3’s forwarding table is empty, so R3 will install a new entry with 3 values: the destination (A), the next hop (R2, from the announcement), and the cost to the destination via R2 (3 summing the cost from the announcement, and the cost of the R3-R2 link).","zh_html":"R3 也收到了同样的宣告。R3 的转发表(forwarding table)是空的，所以 R3 将安装一个包含 3 个值的新条目：目的地(A)、下一跳(R2，来自宣告)、以及经由 R2 到目的地的成本(3，将宣告中的成本与 R3-R2 链路成本相加)。"},{"en":"According to our rules so far, if you update your forwarding table, you need to send an announcement to all your neighbors. Even though we can see that this next announcement won’t change anything, R3 doesn’t have the same global view of the network that we have, so R3 will send an announcement to all of its neighbors, namely R2. The announcement contains: destination (A), next hop (R3), and cost via this next hop (3).","en_html":"According to our rules so far, if you update your forwarding table, you need to send an announcement to all your neighbors. Even though we can see that this next announcement won’t change anything, R3 doesn’t have the same global view of the network that we have, so R3 will send an announcement to all of its neighbors, namely R2. The announcement contains: destination (A), next hop (R3), and cost via this next hop (3).","zh_html":"根据我们目前的规则，如果你更新了转发表(forwarding table)，你需要向你所有的邻居发送一个宣告(announcement)。虽然我们可以看到下一个宣告不会改变任何东西，但 R3 没有我们这样的全局网络视图，所以 R3 会向其所有邻居（即 R2）发送宣告。宣告包含：目的地(A)、下一跳(R3)、以及经由该下一跳的成本(3)。"},{"en":"R2 receives this announcement. R2 knows of a way to reach A with cost 2, from the forwarding table. The announcement offers a path with cost 3 (from the announcement), plus 1 (cost of R2-R3 link), for a total cost of 4. This is worse than the cost in the forwarding table, so R2 ignores the announcement.","en_html":"R2 receives this announcement. R2 knows of a way to reach A with cost 2, from the forwarding table. The announcement offers a path with cost 3 (from the announcement), plus 1 (cost of R2-R3 link), for a total cost of 4. This is worse than the cost in the forwarding table, so R2 ignores the announcement.","zh_html":"R2 收到此宣告。R2 从转发表(forwarding table)中知道一种成本为 2 的到达 A 的方式。该宣告提供的路径成本为 3（来自宣告）加上 1（R2-R3 链路成本），总成本为 4。这比转发表中的成本更差，所以 R2 忽略该宣告。"},{"en":"R2 did not update its forwarding table, so it does not make an announcement. At this point, no further announcements are made, and we can see that every router has populated its forwarding table with information about how to reach A. We can also see that the forwarding tables together form a valid, least-cost delivery tree with the shortest routes for reaching A.","en_html":"R2 did not update its forwarding table, so it does not make an announcement. At this point, no further announcements are made, and we can see that every router has populated its forwarding table with information about how to reach A. We can also see that the forwarding tables together form a valid, least-cost delivery tree with the shortest routes for reaching A.","zh_html":"R2 没有更新其转发表(forwarding table)，所以它不会发出宣告。此时，不再有进一步的宣告，我们可以看到每台路由器都已经填充了其转发表，其中包含了如何到达 A 的信息。我们还可以看到这些转发表共同构成了一棵有效的、最低成本的交付树(delivery tree)，提供了到达 A 的最短路由。"},{"en":"Rule 2: Updates From Next-Hop","en_html":"<a href=\"#rule-2-updates-from-next-hop\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-2-updates-from-next-hop\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Rule 2: Updates From Next-Hop","zh_html":"<a href=\"#rule-2-updates-from-next-hop\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-2-updates-from-next-hop\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 规则 2：来自下一跳的更新(Rule 2: Updates From Next-Hop)"},{"en":"Recall one of our routing challenges from the last section: The network topology can change.","en_html":"Recall one of our routing challenges from the last section: The network topology can change.","zh_html":"回想上一节中我们的路由挑战之一：网络拓扑(network topology)可能会发生变化。"},{"en":"Suppose that we hear an advertisement from R2, saying that A is 3 away from R2. If there’s nothing in our table, we’ll accept this advertisement and record a cost of 1+3=4..","en_html":"Suppose that we hear an advertisement from R2, saying that A is 3 away from R2. If there’s nothing in our table, we’ll accept this advertisement and record a cost of 1+3=4..","zh_html":"假设我们听到来自 R2 的宣告(announcement)，说 A 离 R2 有 3。如果我们的表里没有任何记录，我们将接受这个宣告，并记录成本为 1+3=4。"},{"en":"Later, we might hear a different advertisement from R2, saying that A is 8 away from R2. From the previous rule, we would reject this, because the advertised cost (1+8=9) is worse than our current cost (4).","en_html":"Later, we might hear a different advertisement from R2, saying that A is 8 away from R2. From the previous rule, we would reject this, because the advertised cost (1+8=9) is worse than our current cost (4).","zh_html":"之后，我们可能听到来自 R2 的另一个宣告，说 A 离 R2 有 8。根据之前的规则，我们会拒绝它，因为宣告成本(1+8=9)比我们当前的成本(4)更差。"},{"en":"However, we have to be careful about rejecting this advertisement. The router making the announcement (R2), was the same as the next hop router we were using. R2 is trying to say: “If you’re using me as a next hop, my distance to A is no longer 3, it’s 8.” But we ignored this message because we weren’t thinking about the possibility that paths might change.","en_html":"However, we have to be careful about rejecting this advertisement. The router making the announcement (R2), was the same as the next hop router we were using. R2 is trying to say: “If you’re using me as a next hop, my distance to A is no longer 3, it’s 8.” But we ignored this message because we weren’t thinking about the possibility that paths might change.","zh_html":"然而，我们必须小心对待拒绝这个宣告的问题。发出宣告的路由器(R2)正是我们正在使用的下一跳(next-hop)路由器。R2 试图说：\"如果你把我当作下一跳，我到 A 的距离不再是 3，而是 8。\"但我们忽略了这个消息，因为我们没有考虑到路径可能发生变化。"},{"en":"To fix this, we have to modify our update rule. If we hear an announcement from the next-hop router (the router with the best-known path that we were forwarding packets to), we should treat that announcement as an update, and edit our forwarding table. We should do this even if the announcement produces a worse path, because the next hop could be telling us that the path cost has changed and gotten worse.","en_html":"To fix this, we have to modify our update rule. If we hear an announcement from the next-hop router (the router with the best-known path that we were forwarding packets to), we should treat that announcement as an update, and edit our forwarding table. We should do this even if the announcement produces a worse path, because the next hop could be telling us that the path cost has changed and gotten worse.","zh_html":"为了解决这个问题，我们必须修改我们的更新规则。如果我们听到来自下一跳路由器(next-hop router，即我们正在向其转发分组的最佳已知路径的路由器)的宣告，我们应该将其视为更新(update)，并编辑我们的转发表(forwarding table)。即使宣告产生了一条更差的路径，我们也应该这样做，因为下一跳可能是在告诉我们路径成本发生了变化且变得更差了。"},{"en":"Note that when this new rule applies, we don’t update the destination or the next hop in the forwarding table, only the distance. In the example, packets at R3 destined for A are still forwarded to R2 (same destination, same next hop), but the cost via R2 changed.","en_html":"Note that when this new rule applies, we don’t update the destination or the next hop in the forwarding table, only the distance. In the example, packets at R3 destined for A are still forwarded to R2 (same destination, same next hop), but the cost via R2 changed.","zh_html":"注意，当这个新规则适用时，我们不更新转发表(forwarding table)中的目的地或下一跳，只更新距离(distance)。在例子中，R3 发往 A 的分组仍然转发给 R2（相同的目的地，相同的下一跳），但经由 R2 的成本发生了变化。"},{"en":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","en_html":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","zh_html":"让我们回顾一下目前的协议。"},{"en":"For each destination:","en_html":"For each destination:","zh_html":"对于每个目的地(destination)："},{"en":"If you hear an advertisement for that destination, update the table if: The destination isn’t in the table. The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost. The advertisement is from the current next-hop.","en_html":"If you hear an advertisement for that destination, update the table if: <ul> <li>The destination isn’t in the table.</li> <li>The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost.</li> <li><strong>The advertisement is from the current next-hop.</strong></li> </ul>","zh_html":"如果你听到该目的地的宣告(advertisement)，在以下情况下更新转发表(forwarding table)： <ul> <li>表中没有该目的地。</li> <li>被宣告的成本加上到达邻居的链路成本，优于最佳已知成本。</li> <li><strong>宣告来自当前的下一跳(next-hop)。</strong></li> </ul>"},{"en":"Then, tell all your neighbors.","en_html":"Then, tell all your neighbors.","zh_html":"然后，告诉你所有的邻居。"},{"en":"In order to support changing topologies, routers will run the routing protocol indefinitely.","en_html":"In order to support changing topologies, routers will run the routing protocol indefinitely.","zh_html":"为了支持拓扑变化，路由器将无限期地运行路由协议(routing protocol)。"},{"en":"Suppose we ran the protocol indefinitely, with no topology changes. Initially, some relaxations will succeed and the forwarding tables will change. Eventually, the algorithm will converge when we have found all the least-cost routes through the network. At this point, if we continue relaxing the edges, the forwarding tables will not change. Every relaxation will be rejected, because the best-known paths to the goal are all the shortest paths, and we’ll never find a better path to replace the current shortest paths. The state of the network at convergence is called steady state.","en_html":"Suppose we ran the protocol indefinitely, with no topology changes. Initially, some relaxations will succeed and the forwarding tables will change. Eventually, the algorithm will <strong>converge</strong> when we have found all the least-cost routes through the network. At this point, if we continue relaxing the edges, the forwarding tables will not change. Every relaxation will be rejected, because the best-known paths to the goal are all the shortest paths, and we’ll never find a better path to replace the current shortest paths. The state of the network at convergence is called <strong>steady state</strong>.","zh_html":"假设我们无限期运行协议，且没有拓扑变化。最初，一些松弛操作(relaxations)会成功，转发表(forwarding tables)会发生改变。最终，当我们找到所有通过网络的最低成本路径(least-cost routes)时，算法会<strong>收敛(converge)</strong>。此时，如果我们继续松弛边，转发表不会再改变。每一次松弛都会被拒绝，因为到目标的最佳已知路径都是最短路径，我们再也找不到更好的路径来替换当前的最短路径。网络在收敛时的状态称为<strong>稳态(steady state)</strong>。"},{"en":"Later, suppose we change the topology (e.g. maybe a router fails). As we continue running the protocol, some relaxations might succeed again, since we’ve changed the underlying graph. After some time, the delivery tree will converge again on the new least-cost routes and stop changing until the next time the topology changes.","en_html":"Later, suppose we change the topology (e.g. maybe a router fails). As we continue running the protocol, some relaxations might succeed again, since we’ve changed the underlying graph. After some time, the delivery tree will converge again on the new least-cost routes and stop changing until the next time the topology changes.","zh_html":"之后，假设我们改变拓扑(topology)（例如，可能某个路由器失效了）。随着我们继续运行协议，一些松弛操作(relaxations)可能会再次成功，因为我们改变了底层图(underlying graph)。过一段时间后，交付树(delivery tree)将再次收敛到新的最低成本路径，并保持不变，直到下一次拓扑变化。"},{"en":"As an analogy, consider a pool of water. In the steady state, with no disturbances, the surface of the water is perfectly still. If you toss a rock in the water, there will be some ripples as the environment adjusts to the change you just made, but after some time, the surface of the water will become perfectly still again.","en_html":"As an analogy, consider a pool of water. In the steady state, with no disturbances, the surface of the water is perfectly still. If you toss a rock in the water, there will be some ripples as the environment adjusts to the change you just made, but after some time, the surface of the water will become perfectly still again.","zh_html":"打个比方，考虑一个水池。在稳态(steady state)下，没有扰动，水面完全平静。如果你往水里扔一块石头，水面会泛起涟漪，环境会适应你刚刚做出的改变，但过一段时间后，水面会再次变得完全平静。"},{"en":"Rule 3: Resending","en_html":"<a href=\"#rule-3-resending\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-3-resending\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Rule 3: Resending","zh_html":"<a href=\"#rule-3-resending\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-3-resending\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 规则 3：重新发送(Rule 3: Resending)"},{"en":"Recall another one of our routing challenges from the last section: Packets can get dropped.","en_html":"Recall another one of our routing challenges from the last section: Packets can get dropped.","zh_html":"回想上一节中我们的另一个路由挑战：分组(packets)可能会被丢弃。"},{"en":"For example, let’s rewind to the very beginning of the example from earlier. R2 and R3 have empty forwarding tables, and R1 is updated with the hard-coded route to A. What if R1 issues an announcement, but the packet is dropped? R2 never hears an announcement, and the protocol fails.","en_html":"For example, let’s rewind to the very beginning of the example from earlier. R2 and R3 have empty forwarding tables, and R1 is updated with the hard-coded route to A. What if R1 issues an announcement, but the packet is dropped? R2 never hears an announcement, and the protocol fails.","zh_html":"例如，让我们回到之前例子的最开始。R2 和 R3 的转发表(forwarding tables)为空，R1 更新了到 A 的硬编码路由。如果 R1 发出了宣告(announcement)，但分组被丢弃了呢？R2 永远听不到宣告，协议失败。"},{"en":"You could try to design a more complicated scheme to ensure reliability (e.g. forcing recipients to send acknowledgements), but let’s use something simple: If you have an announcement to make, re-send that announcement every few seconds. It turns out this simple approach works well with some of our later design choices, and nothing more complicated is necessary.","en_html":"You could try to design a more complicated scheme to ensure reliability (e.g. forcing recipients to send acknowledgements), but let’s use something simple: If you have an announcement to make, re-send that announcement every few seconds. It turns out this simple approach works well with some of our later design choices, and nothing more complicated is necessary.","zh_html":"你可以尝试设计更复杂的方案来确保可靠性（例如强制接收方发送确认），但让我们用一些简单的方法：如果你有宣告要发送，每隔几秒重新发送一次。事实证明，这种简单的方法与我们后面的一些设计选择配合得很好，不需要更复杂的方案。"},{"en":"Formally, the protocol will define an advertisement interval. 30 seconds is a common interval used in practice. If the interval is X seconds, then every advertisement must be re-sent every X seconds.","en_html":"Formally, the protocol will define an <strong>advertisement interval</strong>. 30 seconds is a common interval used in practice. If the interval is X seconds, then every advertisement must be re-sent every X seconds.","zh_html":"形式上，协议将定义一个<strong>通告间隔(advertisement interval)</strong>。30 秒是实践中常用的间隔。如果间隔是 X 秒，那么每条宣告必须每 X 秒重新发送一次。"},{"en":"As long as we wait long enough and re-send the packet enough times, the link will eventually successfully send the advertisement, as long as the link works some of the time. If the link was dropping every single packet, then there’s no way for the advertisement to be sent (and maybe a link with 0% success rate probably shouldn’t be in the graph anyway). Eventually, with enough re-sending, this protocol will still converge.","en_html":"As long as we wait long enough and re-send the packet enough times, the link will eventually successfully send the advertisement, as long as the link works some of the time. If the link was dropping every single packet, then there’s no way for the advertisement to be sent (and maybe a link with 0% success rate probably shouldn’t be in the graph anyway). Eventually, with enough re-sending, this protocol will still converge.","zh_html":"只要我们等待足够长的时间并重新发送足够多次分组，只要链路有时能正常工作，链路最终将成功发送宣告。如果链路丢弃每一个分组，那么宣告就无法发送（也许成功率为 0% 的链路本来就不应该出现在图(graph)中）。最终，通过足够的重新发送，该协议仍然会收敛(converge)。"},{"en":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","en_html":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","zh_html":"让我们回顾一下目前的协议。"},{"en":"For each destination:","en_html":"For each destination:","zh_html":"对于每个目的地(destination)："},{"en":"If you hear an advertisement for that destination, update the table if: The destination isn’t in the table. The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost. The advertisement is from the current next-hop.","en_html":"If you hear an advertisement for that destination, update the table if: <ul> <li>The destination isn’t in the table.</li> <li>The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost.</li> <li><strong>The advertisement is from the current next-hop.</strong></li> </ul>","zh_html":"如果你听到该目的地的宣告(advertisement)，在以下情况下更新转发表(forwarding table)： <ul> <li>表中没有该目的地。</li> <li>被宣告的成本加上到达邻居的链路成本，优于最佳已知成本。</li> <li>宣告来自当前的下一跳(next-hop)。</li> </ul>"},{"en":"Advertise to all your neighbors when the table updates, and periodically (advertisement interval).","en_html":"Advertise to all your neighbors <strong>when the table updates, and periodically (advertisement interval)</strong>.","zh_html":"当转发表更新时以及周期性（通告间隔(advertisement interval)）地，<strong>向你所有的邻居发送宣告</strong>。"},{"en":"Note that re-sending at intervals can work in combination with our rule from earlier, where we sent an announcement any time the forwarding table changes. Announcements sent immediately after a change are called triggered updates.","en_html":"Note that re-sending at intervals can work in combination with our rule from earlier, where we sent an announcement any time the forwarding table changes. Announcements sent immediately after a change are called <strong>triggered updates</strong>.","zh_html":"注意，按间隔重新发送可以与我们先前的规则（即每当转发表(forwarding table)发生变化时发送宣告）结合使用。变化后立即发送的宣告称为<strong>触发更新(triggered updates)</strong>。"},{"en":"The protocol would still converge if we only sent announcements at intervals. The table changes, we wait for the interval to expire, and send out the announcement. However, adding triggered updates in addition to interval updates is an optimization that can help the protocol converge quicker. As soon as we know the update, we might as well announce it, without waiting for the interval.","en_html":"The protocol would still converge if we only sent announcements at intervals. The table changes, we wait for the interval to expire, and send out the announcement. However, adding triggered updates in addition to interval updates is an optimization that can help the protocol converge quicker. As soon as we know the update, we might as well announce it, without waiting for the interval.","zh_html":"如果我们只按间隔发送宣告，协议仍然会收敛(converge)。表发生变化，我们等待间隔到期，然后发出宣告。然而，在间隔更新之外增加触发更新(triggered updates)是一种优化，可以帮助协议更快收敛。只要我们知道了更新，不妨立即宣告，无需等待间隔。"},{"en":"With this new rule, once the network converges, every router will continue to re-send announcements periodically, but none of the announcements will be accepted, because we’re in steady state and everybody already has the shortest-cost routes.","en_html":"With this new rule, once the network converges, every router will continue to re-send announcements periodically, but none of the announcements will be accepted, because we’re in steady state and everybody already has the shortest-cost routes.","zh_html":"有了这个新规则，一旦网络收敛(converges)，每台路由器将继续按周期重新发送宣告，但没有宣告会被接受，因为我们处于稳态(steady state)，每台路由器都已经有了最短成本路径。"},{"en":"In the example from earlier, after the network converges, R3 might decide to re-send its announcement, with destination A, next hop R3, and cost via R3 of 3. But R2 will ignore this announcement because its forwarding table has a cheaper route of cost 2 already (the announcement path costs 3 + 1 = 4).","en_html":"In the example from earlier, after the network converges, R3 might decide to re-send its announcement, with destination A, next hop R3, and cost via R3 of 3. But R2 will ignore this announcement because its forwarding table has a cheaper route of cost 2 already (the announcement path costs 3 + 1 = 4).","zh_html":"在之前的例子中，网络收敛后，R3 可能决定重新发送其宣告，目的地 A，下一跳 R3，经由 R3 的成本为 3。但 R2 会忽略此宣告，因为其转发表(forwarding table)已经有一条成本为 2 的更便宜的路由（宣告路径成本为 3 + 1 = 4）。"},{"en":"Rule 4: Expiring","en_html":"<a href=\"#rule-4-expiring\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-4-expiring\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Rule 4: Expiring","zh_html":"<a href=\"#rule-4-expiring\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-4-expiring\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 规则 4：过期(Rule 4: Expiring)"},{"en":"Recall our routing challenge from earlier: The network topology can change. In particular, links and routers can fail. If a router fails in the network, our route might become invalid. The failed router won’t tell us about the problem (since it’s failed), so we’re stuck with this invalid route.","en_html":"Recall our routing challenge from earlier: The network topology can change. In particular, links and routers can fail. If a router fails in the network, our route might become invalid. The failed router won’t tell us about the problem (since it’s failed), so we’re stuck with this invalid route.","zh_html":"回想我们之前的路由挑战：网络拓扑(network topology)可以改变。特别是，链路(links)和路由器(routers)可能失效。如果网络中的某个路由器失效了，我们的路由可能变得无效。失效的路由器不会告诉我们问题所在（因为它已经失效了），所以我们被困在无效的路由上。"},{"en":"To solve this problem, we’ll give every route (i.e. every table entry) a finite time to live (TTL). This is a countdown timer, telling us how much longer we can keep this forwarding entry.","en_html":"To solve this problem, we’ll give every route (i.e. every table entry) a finite <strong>time to live (TTL)</strong>. This is a countdown timer, telling us how much longer we can keep this forwarding entry.","zh_html":"为了解决这个问题，我们将给每条路由（即每个表条目）一个有限的<strong>生存时间(time to live, TTL)</strong>。这是一个倒计时计时器，告诉我们还能保留这个转发条目多久。"},{"en":"Periodic updates help us confirm that a route still exists. If we get an advertisement from the next-hop, we can reset (“recharge”) the TTL to its original value.","en_html":"Periodic updates help us confirm that a route still exists. If we get an advertisement from the next-hop, we can reset (“recharge”) the TTL to its original value.","zh_html":"周期性更新(Periodic updates)帮助我们确认一条路由仍然存在。如果我们从下一跳(next-hop)收到宣告，我们可以将 TTL 重置（\"重新充电(recharge)\"）为初始值。"},{"en":"If something in the network fails, we’ll stop getting periodic updates. Eventually, the TTL will expire. If the TTL expires, we’ll delete the entry from the table. Intuitively: We aren’t getting updates anymore, so this route is probably no longer valid.","en_html":"If something in the network fails, we’ll stop getting periodic updates. Eventually, the TTL will expire. If the TTL expires, we’ll delete the entry from the table. Intuitively: We aren’t getting updates anymore, so this route is probably no longer valid.","zh_html":"如果网络中的某些部分发生故障，我们将停止收到周期性更新。最终，TTL 会耗尽。如果 TTL 耗尽，我们将从表中删除该条目。直观地说：我们不再收到更新了，所以这条路由可能不再有效。"},{"en":"Here’s an example of the TTL in action. In this example, we are R3. At time t=0, we hear an announcement: “I’m R2, and A is 5 away from me.” Our table doesn’t have an entry for A, so we’ll accept this path, and set its TTL to 11. Notice that this TTL is associated with the specific table entry. If we had multiple table entries, they would each have their own TTL.","en_html":"Here’s an example of the TTL in action. In this example, we are R3. At time t=0, we hear an announcement: “I’m R2, and A is 5 away from me.” Our table doesn’t have an entry for A, so we’ll accept this path, and set its TTL to 11. Notice that this TTL is associated with the specific table entry. If we had multiple table entries, they would each have their own TTL.","zh_html":"这里有一个 TTL 实际运行的例子。在这个例子中，我们是 R3。在时间 t=0，我们听到一个宣告：\"我是 R2，A 离我有 5。\"我们的表中没有 A 的条目，所以我们将接受这条路径，并将其 TTL 设为 11。注意，这个 TTL 与该特定表条目关联。如果我们有多个表条目，它们各自会有自己的 TTL。"},{"en":"The TTL of 11 tells us that R2 must send us another confirmation of this route in the next 11 seconds. Otherwise, this table entry will be deleted. (Note: The initial TTL of 11 was chosen arbitrarily. In practice, this number would be set by the protocol or the person operating the router.)","en_html":"The TTL of 11 tells us that R2 must send us another confirmation of this route in the next 11 seconds. Otherwise, this table entry will be deleted. (Note: The initial TTL of 11 was chosen arbitrarily. In practice, this number would be set by the protocol or the person operating the router.)","zh_html":"TTL 为 11 告诉我们，R2 必须在接下来的 11 秒内向我们发送另一个对该路由的确认。否则，这个表条目将被删除。（注：初始 TTL 值 11 是随意选择的。在实践中，这个数字将由协议或操作路由器的人员设定。）"},{"en":"Time passes. At t=1, the TTL is now 10. At t=2, the TTL is now 9. At t=3, the TTL is now 8. At t=4, the TTL is now 7.","en_html":"Time passes. At t=1, the TTL is now 10. At t=2, the TTL is now 9. At t=3, the TTL is now 8. At t=4, the TTL is now 7.","zh_html":"时间流逝。在 t=1，TTL 现在是 10。在 t=2，TTL 现在是 9。在 t=3，TTL 现在是 8。在 t=4，TTL 现在是 7。"},{"en":"At t=5, R2 does its periodic re-sending of the announcement: “I’m R2, and A is 5 away from me.” We look in our table and realize that R2 is the current next-hop to A, so we should accept this advertisement (per Rule 2) and update the table.","en_html":"At t=5, R2 does its periodic re-sending of the announcement: “I’m R2, and A is 5 away from me.” We look in our table and realize that R2 is the current next-hop to A, so we should accept this advertisement (per Rule 2) and update the table.","zh_html":"在 t=5，R2 进行了周期性的重新发送宣告：\"我是 R2，A 离我有 5。\"我们查看表中发现 R2 是当前的下一跳到 A，所以我们应该接受这个宣告（根据规则 2）并更新转发表(forwarding table)。"},{"en":"Because we got a confirmation of this route still existing, the TTL can be reset back to its initial value of 11. We need to get another confirmation of this route from R2 in the next 11 seconds.","en_html":"Because we got a confirmation of this route still existing, the TTL can be reset back to its initial value of 11. We need to get another confirmation of this route from R2 in the next 11 seconds.","zh_html":"因为我们收到了这条路由仍然存在的确认，TTL 可以被重置回初始值 11。我们需要在接下来的 11 秒内从 R2 获得该路由的另一个确认。"},{"en":"Suppose that a link goes down at t=6, and A is now unreachable. R2 removes its static route to A, and no longer sends any periodic updates.","en_html":"Suppose that a link goes down at t=6, and A is now unreachable. R2 removes its static route to A, and no longer sends any periodic updates.","zh_html":"假设一条链路在 t=6 发生故障，A 现在不可达。R2 移除了其到 A 的静态路由(static route)，不再发送任何周期性更新。"},{"en":"At t=16 (11 seconds after the last update at t=5), the TTL in our table entry has decreased all the way to 0, so we’ll delete the entry from our table.","en_html":"At t=16 (11 seconds after the last update at t=5), the TTL in our table entry has decreased all the way to 0, so we’ll delete the entry from our table.","zh_html":"在 t=16（距离上次更新 t=5 已过去 11 秒），我们表条目中的 TTL 递减到了 0，所以我们将从表中删除该条目。"},{"en":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","en_html":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","zh_html":"让我们回顾一下目前的协议。"},{"en":"For each destination:","en_html":"For each destination:","zh_html":"对于每个目的地(destination)："},{"en":"If you hear an advertisement for that destination, update the table and reset the TTL if: The destination isn’t in the table. The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost. The advertisement is from the current next-hop.","en_html":"If you hear an advertisement for that destination, update the table <strong>and reset the TTL</strong> if: <ul> <li>The destination isn’t in the table.</li> <li>The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost.</li> <li>The advertisement is from the current next-hop.</li> </ul>","zh_html":"如果你听到该目的地的宣告(advertisement)，在以下情况下更新转发表(forwarding table)<strong>并重置 TTL</strong>： <ul> <li>表中没有该目的地。</li> <li>被宣告的成本加上到达邻居的链路成本，优于最佳已知成本。</li> <li>宣告来自当前的下一跳(next-hop)。</li> </ul>"},{"en":"Advertise to all your neighbors when the table updates, and periodically (advertisement interval).","en_html":"Advertise to all your neighbors when the table updates, and periodically (advertisement interval).","zh_html":"当转发表更新时以及周期性（通告间隔(advertisement interval)）地，向你所有的邻居发送宣告。"},{"en":"If a table entry expires, delete it.","en_html":"<strong>If a table entry expires, delete it.</strong>","zh_html":"<strong>如果表条目过期，删除它。</strong>"},{"en":"Be careful not to confuse the various timers that the router must maintain.","en_html":"Be careful not to confuse the various timers that the router must maintain.","zh_html":"注意不要混淆路由器必须维护的各种计时器。"},{"en":"The advertisement interval tells the router when to advertise routes to neighbors. This is usually a single timer for the entire table, so the router advertises all the routes in the table whenever the advertisement interval timer expires. In the example above, the advertisement interval timer was 5 seconds, since R2 sent advertisements at t=0 and t=5.","en_html":"The advertisement interval tells the router when to advertise routes to neighbors. This is usually a single timer for the entire table, so the router advertises all the routes in the table whenever the advertisement interval timer expires. In the example above, the advertisement interval timer was 5 seconds, since R2 sent advertisements at t=0 and t=5.","zh_html":"通告间隔(advertisement interval)告诉路由器何时向邻居通告路由。这通常是整个表的单一计时器，所以每当通告间隔计时器到期时，路由器就通告表中的所有路由。在上面的例子中，通告间隔计时器是 5 秒，因为 R2 在 t=0 和 t=5 发送了宣告。"},{"en":"By contrast, the TTL tells the router when to delete a table entry. Each table entry has its own independent TTL, counting down for that specific entry. In the example above, the initial TTL was 11 seconds (reset to 11 when we accept an advertisement), and counted down for each table entry.","en_html":"By contrast, the TTL tells the router when to delete a table entry. Each table entry has its own independent TTL, counting down for that specific entry. In the example above, the initial TTL was 11 seconds (reset to 11 when we accept an advertisement), and counted down for each table entry.","zh_html":"相比之下，TTL 告诉路由器何时删除表条目。每个表条目都有自己独立的 TTL，针对该特定条目倒计时。在上面的例子中，初始 TTL 是 11 秒（当我们接受一个宣告时重置为 11），并为每个表条目独立倒计时。"},{"en":"At this point, we have a mostly-functional routing protocol! Let’s add some optimizations for faster convergence.","en_html":"At this point, we have a mostly-functional routing protocol! Let’s add some optimizations for faster convergence.","zh_html":"至此，我们有了一个基本可用的路由协议(routing protocol)！让我们添加一些优化以实现更快的收敛(convergence)。"},{"en":"Rule 5: Poisoning Expired Routes","en_html":"<a href=\"#rule-5-poisoning-expired-routes\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-5-poisoning-expired-routes\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Rule 5: Poisoning Expired Routes","zh_html":"<a href=\"#rule-5-poisoning-expired-routes\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-5-poisoning-expired-routes\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 规则 5：毒化过期路由(Rule 5: Poisoning Expired Routes)"},{"en":"Waiting for routes to expire is slow. To see why, let’s rewatch the demo from earlier.","en_html":"Waiting for routes to expire is slow. To see why, let’s rewatch the demo from earlier.","zh_html":"等待路由过期是很慢的。为了理解原因，让我们重新观看之前的演示。"},{"en":"In this example, we are R3. Assume that by t=5, we’ve learned a route to A, via R2, and this route has 11 seconds of TTL remaining.","en_html":"In this example, we are R3. Assume that by t=5, we’ve learned a route to A, via R2, and this route has 11 seconds of TTL remaining.","zh_html":"在这个例子中，我们是 R3。假设到 t=5 时，我们已经学到了一条经由 R2 到 A 的路由，这条路由还有 11 秒的 TTL。"},{"en":"At t=6, the A-to-R2 link goes down! The table entry is now busted, because if we forwarded packets to R2, they wouldn’t actually reach A. However, we don’t know that this entry is busted yet. We have to wait another 10 seconds for this route to expire.","en_html":"At t=6, the A-to-R2 link goes down! The table entry is now busted, because if we forwarded packets to R2, they wouldn’t actually reach A. However, we don’t know that this entry is busted yet. We have to wait another 10 seconds for this route to expire.","zh_html":"在 t=6，A 到 R2 的链路发生故障！这个表条目现在坏了(busted)，因为如果我们把分组转发给 R2，它们实际上不会到达 A。然而，我们还不知道这个条目已经坏了。我们必须再等 10 秒让这条路由过期。"},{"en":"Also at t=6, we get a new announcement: “I’m R1, and A is 1 away from me.” We look in our table, and we already have a way to reach A, so we reject this announcement. (Note: It’s not important for this demo, but we’re assuming we don’t accept equal-cost paths here.)","en_html":"Also at t=6, we get a new announcement: “I’m R1, and A is 1 away from me.” We look in our table, and we already have a way to reach A, so we reject this announcement. (Note: It’s not important for this demo, but we’re assuming we don’t accept equal-cost paths here.)","zh_html":"同样在 t=6，我们收到一个新的宣告：\"我是 R1，A 离我有 1。\"我们查看表中，我们已经有一种到达 A 的方式，所以我们拒绝了这个宣告。（注：这个演示中不重要，但我们假设这里不接受等成本路径。）"},{"en":"If only we knew that our existing route is busted, we could accept this new advertisement right now. But instead, we’re doomed to wait another 10 seconds of using this busted path.","en_html":"If only we knew that our existing route is busted, we could accept this new advertisement right now. But instead, we’re doomed to wait another 10 seconds of using this busted path.","zh_html":"如果我们知道现有路由已经坏了，我们本可以现在就接受这个新宣告。但相反，我们注定要再用 10 秒这个已坏路径。"},{"en":"Time passes. By t=11 (five seconds later), the busted route still has 5 seconds of TTL remaining.","en_html":"Time passes. By t=11 (five seconds later), the busted route still has 5 seconds of TTL remaining.","zh_html":"时间流逝。到 t=11（五秒后），已坏路径还有 5 秒的 TTL 剩余。"},{"en":"At t=11, we get another announcement: “I’m R1, and A is 1 away from me.” R1 is re-sending its announcement from earlier. Again, we look in our table, and we still have an entry for A, so we reject this announcement again.","en_html":"At t=11, we get another announcement: “I’m R1, and A is 1 away from me.” R1 is re-sending its announcement from earlier. Again, we look in our table, and we still have an entry for A, so we reject this announcement again.","zh_html":"在 t=11，我们收到另一个宣告：\"我是 R1，A 离我有 1。\"R1 在重新发送之前的宣告。同样，我们查看表中，仍然有一个 A 的条目，所以我们再次拒绝了这个宣告。"},{"en":"Again, if only we had some way to know that our existing route is busted…then we could accept this new advertisement. With our current approach, however, we’re doomed to keep using the busted path for the remaining 5 seconds.","en_html":"Again, if only we had some way to know that our existing route is busted…then we could accept this new advertisement. With our current approach, however, we’re doomed to keep using the busted path for the remaining 5 seconds.","zh_html":"同样，如果我们有办法知道现有的路径已经坏了就好了……那样我们就可以接受这个新宣告。然而，按照我们当前的方法，在剩下的 5 秒内我们注定要继续使用已坏的路径。"},{"en":"Time passes. By t=16 (five seconds later), the busted route TTL finally reaches 0, and we can delete this entry from the table.","en_html":"Time passes. By t=16 (five seconds later), the busted route TTL finally reaches 0, and we can delete this entry from the table.","zh_html":"时间流逝。到 t=16（五秒后），已坏路由的 TTL 终于达到 0，我们可以从表中删除这个条目。"},{"en":"Also at t=16, R1 re-sends its announcement again: “I’m R1, and A is 1 away from me.” Finally, our table doesn’t have a route to A (the busted route just got deleted), so we can accept this announcement.","en_html":"Also at t=16, R1 re-sends its announcement again: “I’m R1, and A is 1 away from me.” Finally, our table doesn’t have a route to A (the busted route just got deleted), so we can accept this announcement.","zh_html":"同样在 t=16，R1 再次重新发送其宣告：\"我是 R1，A 离我有 1。\"终于，我们表中没有到 A 的路由了（已坏路由刚刚被删除），所以我们可以接受这个宣告。"},{"en":"What just happened? At t=6, the failure occurred, and the entry in our table became busted. However, because there were 10 seconds of TTL remaining on the busted route, we were doomed to keep using the busted route for another 10 seconds. During this time, any packets to A will get lost, because we’ll forward the packet along a busted path. Also, we might advertise this busted route to other people, causing them to lose packets as well. Finally, as we saw, we might reject new paths, thinking that the busted path is still valid.","en_html":"What just happened? At t=6, the failure occurred, and the entry in our table became busted. However, because there were 10 seconds of TTL remaining on the busted route, we were doomed to keep using the busted route for another 10 seconds. During this time, any packets to A will get lost, because we’ll forward the packet along a busted path. Also, we might advertise this busted route to other people, causing them to lose packets as well. Finally, as we saw, we might reject new paths, thinking that the busted path is still valid.","zh_html":"刚才发生了什么？在 t=6，故障发生了，我们表中的条目变得不可用。然而，因为已坏路由上还有 10 秒的 TTL 剩余，我们注定要继续使用已坏路由 10 秒。在这段时间内，任何发往 A 的分组都会丢失，因为我们会将分组沿已坏路径转发。此外，我们可能向其他人宣告这条已坏路由，导致他们也丢失分组。最后，正如我们所见，我们可能拒绝新路径，认为已坏路径仍然有效。"},{"en":"The key problem here is: When something fails, it’s not being reported, so we’re forced to rely on timeouts to delete busted paths. This is slow. Is there any way we can detect failures earlier?","en_html":"The key problem here is: When something fails, it’s not being reported, so we’re forced to rely on timeouts to delete busted paths. This is slow. Is there any way we can detect failures earlier?","zh_html":"这里的关键问题是：当某些东西发生故障时，它没有被报告，所以我们被迫依赖超时(timeouts)来删除已坏路径。这很慢。有没有办法可以更早地检测到故障？"},{"en":"The solution is poison: When something fails, if possible, explicitly advertise that a path is busted.","en_html":"The solution is <strong>poison</strong>: When something fails, if possible, explicitly advertise that a path is busted.","zh_html":"解决方案是<strong>毒化(poison)</strong>：当某些东西发生故障时，如果可能的话，明确宣告某条路径已经坏了。"},{"en":"In English, the new poison announcement that R2 sends would say: “I’m R2, and I no longer have a way to reach A.” In the protocol, we encode this message by advertising a path with cost infinity: “I’m R2, and A is infinity away from me.” This infinite-cost path represents a busted path.","en_html":"In English, the new poison announcement that R2 sends would say: “I’m R2, and I no longer have a way to reach A.” In the protocol, we encode this message by advertising a path with cost infinity: “I’m R2, and A is infinity away from me.” This infinite-cost path represents a busted path.","zh_html":"用自然语言来说，R2 发送的新毒化宣告将会说：\"我是 R2，我不再有办法到达 A。\"在协议中，我们通过宣告一条成本为无穷大(infinity)的路径来编码这个消息：\"我是 R2，A 离我无穷远。\"这条无穷大成本的路径表示一条已坏路径。"},{"en":"Poisoned paths propagate just like any other path. If we’re forwarding packets to R2, and we get a poison message from R2, we update our forwarding table and replace the cost with infinity (per Rule 2). We can also advertise this infinite-cost poison to our neighbors, so they are also alerted of the busted path. This allows an invalid path to propagate through the network, which can be much faster than waiting for the path to time out.","en_html":"Poisoned paths propagate just like any other path. If we’re forwarding packets to R2, and we get a poison message from R2, we update our forwarding table and replace the cost with infinity (per Rule 2). We can also advertise this infinite-cost poison to our neighbors, so they are also alerted of the busted path. This allows an invalid path to propagate through the network, which can be much faster than waiting for the path to time out.","zh_html":"毒化路径(poisoned paths)像任何其他路径一样传播。如果我们正在向 R2 转发分组，并且收到来自 R2 的毒化消息，我们更新转发表(forwarding table)并将成本替换为无穷大(infinity)（根据规则 2）。我们还可以向我们的邻居宣告这个无穷大成本的毒化信息，这样他们也能得知已坏路径。这使得无效路径可以在网络中传播，这比等待路径超时快得多。"},{"en":"Let’s rewatch the demo from earlier, but with poisoning on route expiry. As before, assume that by t=5, we’ve learned a route to A, via R2, and this route has 11 seconds of TTL remaining.","en_html":"Let’s rewatch the demo from earlier, but with poisoning on route expiry. As before, assume that by t=5, we’ve learned a route to A, via R2, and this route has 11 seconds of TTL remaining.","zh_html":"让我们重新观看之前的演示，但这次采用路由过期时的毒化(poisoning)机制。和之前一样，假设到 t=5 时，我们已经学到了一条经由 R2 到 A 的路由，这条路由还有 11 秒的 TTL。"},{"en":"At t=6, the A-to-R2 link goes down! The table entry is now busted. However, we don’t know that this entry is busted just yet.","en_html":"At t=6, the A-to-R2 link goes down! The table entry is now busted. However, we don’t know that this entry is busted just yet.","zh_html":"在 t=6，A 到 R2 的链路发生故障！这个表条目现在坏了。不过，我们暂时还不知道这个条目已经坏了。"},{"en":"With our modification, instead of saying nothing, R2 sends us a poison announcement: “I’m R2, and A is infinity away from me.” Per Rule 2 (accept from next-hop), we notice that R2 is our next hop, so we accept this announcement and update our table.","en_html":"With our modification, instead of saying nothing, R2 sends us a poison announcement: “I’m R2, and A is infinity away from me.” Per Rule 2 (accept from next-hop), we notice that R2 is our next hop, so we accept this announcement and update our table.","zh_html":"在我们的修改下，R2 不再保持沉默，而是向我们发送一个毒化宣告(poison announcement)：\"我是 R2，A 离我无穷远。\"根据规则 2（接受来自下一跳的宣告），我们注意到 R2 是我们的下一跳，所以我们接受这个宣告并更新我们的转发表(forwarding table)。"},{"en":"Our table entry now encodes the fact that A is actually unreachable via R2. This entry has a TTL, just like any other table entry. Also, we can advertise this infinite-cost path to our neighbors, just like any other entry. This tells our neighbors that we can no longer reach A either.","en_html":"Our table entry now encodes the fact that A is actually unreachable via R2. This entry has a TTL, just like any other table entry. Also, we can advertise this infinite-cost path to our neighbors, just like any other entry. This tells our neighbors that we can no longer reach A either.","zh_html":"我们的表条目现在编码了一个事实：A 实际上无法通过 R2 到达。这个条目有一个 TTL，就像任何其他表条目一样。此外，我们可以像任何其他条目一样向邻居宣告这条无穷大成本路径。这告诉我们的邻居，我们也不再能到达 A。"},{"en":"Also at t=6, after our table update, we get a new announcement: “I’m R1, and A is 1 away from me.” Using this route has distance 2 (1 from link, 1 from advertisement), which is better than infinity (from the table). We accept this advertisement and update the table. Now, packets for A are routed through R1 instead of R2.","en_html":"Also at t=6, after our table update, we get a new announcement: “I’m R1, and A is 1 away from me.” Using this route has distance 2 (1 from link, 1 from advertisement), which is better than infinity (from the table). We accept this advertisement and update the table. Now, packets for A are routed through R1 instead of R2.","zh_html":"同样在 t=6，在我们的转发表(forwarding table)更新之后，我们收到一个新的宣告：\"我是 R1，A 离我有 1。\"使用这条路由的距离为 2（链路成本 1，来自宣告的成本 1），这比无穷大(infinity)（来自表中）更好。我们接受这个宣告并更新转发表。现在，发往 A 的分组通过 R1 而不是 R2 路由。"},{"en":"In our earlier demo, at t=6, we were forced to wait 10 seconds for the busted route to expire. Thanks to the poison announcement, we were able to immediately invalidate that busted route at t=6, and accept the new path.","en_html":"In our earlier demo, at t=6, we were forced to wait 10 seconds for the busted route to expire. Thanks to the poison announcement, we were able to immediately invalidate that busted route at t=6, and accept the new path.","zh_html":"在我们之前的演示中，在 t=6，我们被迫等待 10 秒让已坏路由过期。多亏了毒化宣告(poison announcement)，我们能够在 t=6 立即废弃那条已坏路由，并接受新路径。"},{"en":"With poison, we were able to converge on a valid path sooner. Between t=6 and t=16, packets will now correctly reach A (whereas in the no-poison approach, packets in this time period would get lost). Also, thanks to the poison, we’ve avoided propagating a busted route to others in that time period. Even better, we can propagate the poison to others and let them know that the path to A via us (and R2) is busted.","en_html":"With poison, we were able to converge on a valid path sooner. Between t=6 and t=16, packets will now correctly reach A (whereas in the no-poison approach, packets in this time period would get lost). Also, thanks to the poison, we’ve avoided propagating a busted route to others in that time period. Even better, we can propagate the poison to others and let them know that the path to A via us (and R2) is busted.","zh_html":"有了毒化(poison)，我们能够更快地收敛(converge)到一条有效路径。在 t=6 到 t=16 之间，分组现在会正确到达 A（而在无毒化方法中，这段时间内的分组会丢失）。而且，多亏了毒化，我们避免了在这段时间内向其他人传播一条已坏路由。更棒的是，我们可以将毒化传播给其他人，让他们知道经由我们（和 R2）到 A 的路径已经坏了。"},{"en":"Let’s formalize the rules of poison. Poison originates from one of two sources: One or your routes times out, or you notice a local failure (e.g. one of your links goes down). When one of these occurs, you can update the appropriate table entry with cost infinity, reset the TTL, and advertise this poison to your neighbors.","en_html":"Let’s formalize the rules of poison. Poison originates from one of two sources: One or your routes times out, or you notice a local failure (e.g. one of your links goes down). When one of these occurs, you can update the appropriate table entry with cost infinity, reset the TTL, and advertise this poison to your neighbors.","zh_html":"让我们形式化毒化(poison)的规则。毒化来自两个源头之一：你的某条路由超时，或者你注意到一个本地故障（例如，你的某条链路发生故障）。当其中一种情况发生时，你可以将相应的表条目标记为成本无穷大(infinity)，重置 TTL，并将此毒化宣告给你的邻居。"},{"en":"How does poison propagate? When you receive a poison advertisement from your current next-hop, accept it. Your next-hop is telling you that the route no longer exists (similar to advertising worse paths in Rule 2), so you need to update your table. When you update the table, you reset the TTL, just like any other table update. You also advertise the poison to your neighbors, just like any other table update, so that your neighbors also know about the busted route.","en_html":"How does poison propagate? When you receive a poison advertisement from your current next-hop, accept it. Your next-hop is telling you that the route no longer exists (similar to advertising worse paths in Rule 2), so you need to update your table. When you update the table, you reset the TTL, just like any other table update. You also advertise the poison to your neighbors, just like any other table update, so that your neighbors also know about the busted route.","zh_html":"毒化(poison)如何传播？当你从当前下一跳(next-hop)收到毒化宣告时，接受它。你的下一跳在告诉你该路由不再存在（类似于规则 2 中宣告更差路径），所以你需要更新你的转发表(forwarding table)。当你更新表时，你重置 TTL，就像任何其他表更新一样。你也向你的邻居宣告毒化，就像任何其他表更新一样，这样你的邻居也能知道这条已坏路由。"},{"en":"One final modification: Now that our tables contain poison, we have to be careful not to forward packets along a poisoned route. If a table entry says that A is reachable via R1 with cost infinity, this really means that A is unreachable via R1. If we get a packet destined for A, we cannot forward it to R1.","en_html":"One final modification: Now that our tables contain poison, we have to be careful not to forward packets along a poisoned route. If a table entry says that A is reachable via R1 with cost infinity, this really means that A is unreachable via R1. If we get a packet destined for A, we cannot forward it to R1.","zh_html":"最后一个修改：既然我们的表中包含了毒化(poison)，我们必须小心不要沿着毒化路径转发分组。如果一个表条目说 A 可通过 R1 以成本无穷大(infinity)到达，这实际上意味着 A 无法通过 R1 到达。如果我们收到一个发往 A 的分组，我们不能将其转发给 R1。"},{"en":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","en_html":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","zh_html":"让我们回顾一下目前的协议。"},{"en":"For each destination:","en_html":"For each destination:","zh_html":"对于每个目的地(destination)："},{"en":"If you hear an advertisement for that destination, update the table and reset the TTL if: The destination isn’t in the table. The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost. The advertisement is from the current next-hop. Includes poison advertisements.","en_html":"If you hear an advertisement for that destination, update the table <strong>and reset the TTL</strong> if: <ul> <li>The destination isn’t in the table.</li> <li>The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost.</li> <li>The advertisement is from the current next-hop. <strong>Includes poison advertisements.</strong></li> </ul>","zh_html":"如果你听到该目的地的宣告(advertisement)，在以下情况下更新转发表(forwarding table)<strong>并重置 TTL</strong>： <ul> <li>表中没有该目的地。</li> <li>被宣告的成本加上到达邻居的链路成本，优于最佳已知成本。</li> <li>宣告来自当前的下一跳(next-hop)。<strong>包括毒化宣告(poison advertisements)。</strong></li> </ul>"},{"en":"Advertise to all your neighbors when the table updates, and periodically (advertisement interval).","en_html":"Advertise to all your neighbors when the table updates, and periodically (advertisement interval).","zh_html":"当转发表更新时以及周期性（通告间隔(advertisement interval)）地，向你所有的邻居发送宣告。"},{"en":"If a table entry expires, make the entry poison and advertise it.","en_html":"If a table entry expires, <strong>make the entry poison and advertise it</strong>.","zh_html":"如果表条目过期，<strong>将该条目设为毒化(poison)并宣告它</strong>。"},{"en":"Rule 6A: Split Horizon","en_html":"<a href=\"#rule-6a-split-horizon\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-6a-split-horizon\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Rule 6A: Split Horizon","zh_html":"<a href=\"#rule-6a-split-horizon\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-6a-split-horizon\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 规则 6A：水平分割(Rule 6A: Split Horizon)"},{"en":"Let’s go back to our favorite running example again to demonstrate another problem. Suppose we’re in steady state, and the forwarding tables have the correct shortest routes to A. Announcements are being periodically re-sent, but all announcements are being rejected because we’re in steady state.","en_html":"Let’s go back to our favorite running example again to demonstrate another problem. Suppose we’re in steady state, and the forwarding tables have the correct shortest routes to A. Announcements are being periodically re-sent, but all announcements are being rejected because we’re in steady state.","zh_html":"让我们再次回到我们最喜爱的持续运行示例，来演示另一个问题。假设我们处于稳态(steady state)，转发表(forwarding tables)中有正确的到 A 的最短路由。宣告正在被周期性重新发送，但所有宣告都被拒绝了，因为我们处于稳态。"},{"en":"The R1-R2 link goes down, and R2’s entry expires, because R1 stopped sending periodic announcements. R2 now has an empty forwarding table. What happens next?","en_html":"The R1-R2 link goes down, and R2’s entry expires, because R1 stopped sending periodic announcements. R2 now has an empty forwarding table. What happens next?","zh_html":"R1-R2 链路发生故障，R2 的条目过期了，因为 R1 停止了周期性宣告的发送。R2 现在有一个空的转发表(forwarding table)。接下来会发生什么？"},{"en":"Eventually, R3 re-sends its announcement to R2, with destination (A), next hop (R3), and cost via next hop (3).","en_html":"Eventually, R3 re-sends its announcement to R2, with destination (A), next hop (R3), and cost via next hop (3).","zh_html":"最终，R3 向 R2 重新发送了其宣告，包含了目的地(A)、下一跳(R3)、以及经由该下一跳的成本(3)。"},{"en":"R2’s table is empty, so it accepts this announcement and adds destination (A), next hop (R3), and cost via next hop (3 + 1 = 4).","en_html":"R2’s table is empty, so it accepts this announcement and adds destination (A), next hop (R3), and cost via next hop (3 + 1 = 4).","zh_html":"R2 的表是空的，所以它接受这个宣告并添加了目的地(A)、下一跳(R3)、以及经由该下一跳的成本(3 + 1 = 4)。"},{"en":"We’ve created a routing loop! R2 will forward packets to R3, and R3 will forward packets to R2.","en_html":"We’ve created a routing loop! R2 will forward packets to R3, and R3 will forward packets to R2.","zh_html":"我们创建了一个路由环路(routing loop)！R2 会将分组转发给 R3，而 R3 会将分组转发给 R2。"},{"en":"This problem can be tricky to spot at first, so let’s restate it intuitively. Suppose I have accepted a route from Alice, which means that I’ll be forwarding packets to Alice. What happens if I then offer this route back to Alice? If she accepts the route, she’ll end up forwarding packets to me, and I’ll forward the packet back to her.","en_html":"This problem can be tricky to spot at first, so let’s restate it intuitively. Suppose I have accepted a route from Alice, which means that I’ll be forwarding packets to Alice. What happens if I then offer this route back to Alice? If she accepts the route, she’ll end up forwarding packets to me, and I’ll forward the packet back to her.","zh_html":"这个问题一开始可能难以发现，所以让我们直观地重述一下。假设我接受了来自 Alice 的一条路由，这意味着我将向 Alice 转发分组。如果我把这条路由再提供给 Alice，会发生什么？如果她接受了这条路由，她最终会把分组转发给我，而我又会把分组转发回给她。"},{"en":"If the network topology never changed, this advertisement is harmless. The path I’m offering to Alice goes from Alice, to me, back to Alice. This new path is definitely more expensive because it adds an unnecessary loop, so Alice will always reject this advertisement.","en_html":"If the network topology never changed, this advertisement is harmless. The path I’m offering to Alice goes from Alice, to me, back to Alice. This new path is definitely more expensive because it adds an unnecessary loop, so Alice will always reject this advertisement.","zh_html":"如果网络拓扑(network topology)从未改变，这个宣告是无害的。我提供给 Alice 的路径是从 Alice 到我，再回到 Alice。这条新路径肯定会更昂贵，因为它增加了一个不必要的环路，所以 Alice 总会拒绝这个宣告。"},{"en":"However, this advertisement is dangerous if Alice loses her route. Now, my advertisement is fooling Alice into thinking that she can send packets to me. But, my path relies on Alice herself, so if she accepts this path, we would create a loop where she sends packets to me, only for me to send the packet right back to her. The key problem here is: Alice thinks that the path I’m advertising is independent and never goes through Alice. But in fact, my path does go through Alice, so if she accepts my path, she’ll end up forwarding packets back to herself.","en_html":"However, this advertisement is dangerous if Alice loses her route. Now, my advertisement is fooling Alice into thinking that she can send packets to me. But, my path relies on Alice herself, so if she accepts this path, we would create a loop where she sends packets to me, only for me to send the packet right back to her. The key problem here is: Alice thinks that the path I’m advertising is independent and never goes through Alice. But in fact, my path does go through Alice, so if she accepts my path, she’ll end up forwarding packets back to herself.","zh_html":"然而，如果 Alice 丢失了她的路由，这个宣告就变得危险了。现在，我的宣告正在欺骗 Alice，让她以为她可以向我发送分组。但是，我的路径依赖于 Alice 本人，所以如果她接受这条路径，就会创建一个环路：她把分组发送给我，而我立即把分组发回给她。这里的关键问题是：Alice 以为我宣告的路径是独立的，从不经过 Alice。但事实上，我的路径确实经过了 Alice，所以如果她接受我的路径，她最终会把分组转发回自己。"},{"en":"To solve this problem, we need to avoid offering Alice a route that already involves herself. We never want Alice to accept a route that sends packets back to herself.","en_html":"To solve this problem, we need to avoid offering Alice a route that already involves herself. We never want Alice to accept a route that sends packets back to herself.","zh_html":"为了解决这个问题，我们需要避免向 Alice 提供一条已经涉及她自身的路由。我们绝不想让 Alice 接受一条会把分组发回她自己的路由。"},{"en":"This leads us to a solution called split horizon, where we never advertise a route back to the person who gave us that route.","en_html":"This leads us to a solution called <strong>split horizon</strong>, where we never advertise a route back to the person who gave us that route.","zh_html":"这引出了一个叫做<strong>水平分割(split horizon)</strong>的解决方案，即我们从不将一条路由宣告回给提供该路由给我们的那个人。"},{"en":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","en_html":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","zh_html":"让我们回顾一下目前的协议。"},{"en":"For each destination:","en_html":"For each destination:","zh_html":"对于每个目的地(destination)："},{"en":"If you hear an advertisement for that destination, update the table and reset the TTL if: The destination isn’t in the table. The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost. The advertisement is from the current next-hop. Includes poison advertisements.","en_html":"If you hear an advertisement for that destination, update the table <strong>and reset the TTL</strong> if: <ul> <li>The destination isn’t in the table.</li> <li>The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost.</li> <li>The advertisement is from the current next-hop. Includes poison advertisements.</li> </ul>","zh_html":"如果你听到该目的地的宣告(advertisement)，在以下情况下更新转发表(forwarding table)<strong>并重置 TTL</strong>： <ul> <li>表中没有该目的地。</li> <li>被宣告的成本加上到达邻居的链路成本，优于最佳已知成本。</li> <li>宣告来自当前的下一跳(next-hop)。包括毒化宣告(poison advertisements)。</li> </ul>"},{"en":"Advertise to all your neighbors when the table updates, and periodically (advertisement interval). But don’t advertise back to the next-hop.","en_html":"Advertise to all your neighbors when the table updates, and periodically (advertisement interval). <ul> <li><strong>But don’t advertise back to the next-hop.</strong></li> </ul>","zh_html":"当转发表更新时以及周期性（通告间隔(advertisement interval)）地，向你所有的邻居发送宣告。 <ul> <li><strong>但不要宣告回给下一跳(next-hop)。</strong></li> </ul>"},{"en":"If a table entry expires, make the entry poison and advertise it.","en_html":"If a table entry expires, make the entry poison and advertise it.","zh_html":"如果表条目过期，将该条目设为毒化(poison)并宣告它。"},{"en":"Rule 6B: Poison Reverse","en_html":"<a href=\"#rule-6b-poison-reverse\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-6b-poison-reverse\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Rule 6B: Poison Reverse","zh_html":"<a href=\"#rule-6b-poison-reverse\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-6b-poison-reverse\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 规则 6B：毒性反转(Rule 6B: Poison Reverse)"},{"en":"Poison reverse is an alternative way to avoid routing loops. We can use either split horizon or poison reverse to solve the problem from earlier (but not both).","en_html":"<strong>Poison reverse</strong> is an alternative way to avoid routing loops. We can use either split horizon or poison reverse to solve the problem from earlier (but not both).","zh_html":"<strong>毒性反转(poison reverse)</strong>是另一种避免路由环路(routing loops)的方法。我们可以使用水平分割(split horizon)或毒性反转来解决之前的问题（但不能同时使用两者）。"},{"en":"In split horizon, if someone gives me a route, I don’t advertise the route back at them.","en_html":"In split horizon, if someone gives me a route, I don’t advertise the route back at them.","zh_html":"在水平分割(split horizon)中，如果有人给了我一条路由，我不会将该路由宣告回给他们。"},{"en":"By contrast, in poison reverse, if someone gives me a route, I explicitly advertise poison back at them. In other words, I explicitly tell them, “Do not forward packets my way” (because I’d just forward them back to you).","en_html":"By contrast, in poison reverse, if someone gives me a route, I explicitly advertise poison back at them. In other words, I explicitly tell them, “Do not forward packets my way” (because I’d just forward them back to you).","zh_html":"相比之下，在毒性反转(poison reverse)中，如果有人给了我一条路由，我明确地向他们宣告毒化(poison)。换句话说，我明确地告诉他们：\"不要将分组往我这边转发\"（因为我会立即把分组转发回你们那里）。"},{"en":"Let’s see the demo again, but using poison reverse instead of split horizon this time. As before, we reach steady state, then R1-R2 goes down, and R2 loses its table entry.","en_html":"Let’s see the demo again, but using poison reverse instead of split horizon this time. As before, we reach steady state, then R1-R2 goes down, and R2 loses its table entry.","zh_html":"让我们再次看演示，但这次使用毒性反转(poison reverse)而不是水平分割。和之前一样，我们达到稳态(steady state)，然后 R1-R2 链路发生故障，R2 丢失了其表条目。"},{"en":"If we implemented neither fix, this is the point when R3 would advertise its route to R2, and R2 would accept a route going through itself.","en_html":"If we implemented neither fix, this is the point when R3 would advertise its route to R2, and R2 would accept a route going through itself.","zh_html":"如果我们没有实现任何一种修复，这时 R3 会向 R2 宣告其路由，而 R2 会接受一条经由自己的路由。"},{"en":"If we implemented split horizon, R3 would not advertise its route back to R2 at this point.","en_html":"If we implemented split horizon, R3 would not advertise its route back to R2 at this point.","zh_html":"如果我们实现了水平分割(split horizon)，R3 在这个时候不会将其路由宣告回 R2。"},{"en":"In the poison reverse approach, R3 explicitly sends an advertisement back to R2: “I’m R3, and A is infinity away from me.”","en_html":"In the poison reverse approach, R3 explicitly sends an advertisement back to R2: “I’m R3, and A is infinity away from me.”","zh_html":"在毒性反转(poison reverse)方法中，R3 明确地向 R2 发送一个宣告：\"我是 R3，A 离我无穷远(infinity)。\""},{"en":"R2 doesn’t have an entry for A (its old one expired), so it accepts this new, poisoned route. Now, R2’s table explicitly says that it cannot reach A via R3. We’ve avoided the routing loop with the help of poison reverse!","en_html":"R2 doesn’t have an entry for A (its old one expired), so it accepts this new, poisoned route. Now, R2’s table explicitly says that it cannot reach A via R3. We’ve avoided the routing loop with the help of poison reverse!","zh_html":"R2 没有 A 的条目（旧条目已过期），所以它接受这条新的毒化(poisoned)路由。现在，R2 的表明确地说它无法经由 R3 到达 A。借助毒性反转(poison reverse)，我们避免了路由环路(routing loop)！"},{"en":"In our model of the network, split horizon and poison reverse will both help avoid routing loops. More generally, poison reverse can help eliminate routing loops sooner if they ever arise.","en_html":"In our model of the network, split horizon and poison reverse will both help avoid routing loops. More generally, poison reverse can help eliminate routing loops sooner if they ever arise.","zh_html":"在我们的网络模型中，水平分割(split horizon)和毒性反转(poison reverse)都有助于避免路由环路(routing loops)。更一般地说，如果环路真的出现，毒性反转可以帮助更快地消除路由环路。"},{"en":"For example, suppose we end up with a routing loop somehow, where R2 and R3 are forwarding packets to each other.","en_html":"For example, suppose we end up with a routing loop somehow, where R2 and R3 are forwarding packets to each other.","zh_html":"例如，假设我们不知何故最终形成了一个路由环路(routing loop)，R2 和 R3 互相转发分组。"},{"en":"In the split horizon approach, no poison gets sent. R2 got its route from R3, so it won’t send anything to R3. Similarly, R3 got its route from R2, so it won’t send anything to R2. The loop exists until the table entries expire. Until then, packets could get lost in the loop.","en_html":"In the split horizon approach, no poison gets sent. R2 got its route from R3, so it won’t send anything to R3. Similarly, R3 got its route from R2, so it won’t send anything to R2. The loop exists until the table entries expire. Until then, packets could get lost in the loop.","zh_html":"在水平分割(split horizon)方法中，不会发送任何毒化(poison)。R2 从 R3 获得了路由，所以它不会向 R3 发送任何信息。类似地，R3 从 R2 获得了路由，所以它不会向 R2 发送任何信息。环路一直存在直到表条目过期。在此之前，分组可能在环路中丢失。"},{"en":"By contrast, if we used the poison reverse approach, R3 explicitly sends poison back to R2: “I’m R3, and A is infinity away from me.” R2 accepts this advertisement (Rule 2, route from its next-hop), and updates its table to invalidate the path via R3. The poison reverse advertisement immediately eliminates the routing loop.","en_html":"By contrast, if we used the poison reverse approach, R3 explicitly sends poison back to R2: “I’m R3, and A is infinity away from me.” R2 accepts this advertisement (Rule 2, route from its next-hop), and updates its table to invalidate the path via R3. The poison reverse advertisement immediately eliminates the routing loop.","zh_html":"相比之下，如果我们使用了毒性反转(poison reverse)方法，R3 会明确地向 R2 发送毒化(poison)：\"我是 R3，A 离我无穷远(infinity)。\"R2 接受这个宣告（规则 2，来自其下一跳的路由），并更新其表，使经由 R3 的路径无效。毒性反转宣告立即消除了路由环路(routing loop)。"},{"en":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","en_html":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","zh_html":"让我们回顾一下目前的协议。"},{"en":"For each destination:","en_html":"For each destination:","zh_html":"对于每个目的地(destination)："},{"en":"If you hear an advertisement for that destination, update the table and reset the TTL if: The destination isn’t in the table. The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost. The advertisement is from the current next-hop. Includes poison advertisements.","en_html":"If you hear an advertisement for that destination, update the table <strong>and reset the TTL</strong> if: <ul> <li>The destination isn’t in the table.</li> <li>The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost.</li> <li>The advertisement is from the current next-hop. Includes poison advertisements.</li> </ul>","zh_html":"如果你听到该目的地的宣告(advertisement)，在以下情况下更新转发表(forwarding table)<strong>并重置 TTL</strong>： <ul> <li>表中没有该目的地。</li> <li>被宣告的成本加上到达邻居的链路成本，优于最佳已知成本。</li> <li>宣告来自当前的下一跳(next-hop)。包括毒化宣告(poison advertisements)。</li> </ul>"},{"en":"Advertise to all your neighbors when the table updates, and periodically (advertisement interval). But don’t advertise back to the next-hop. …Or, advertise poison back to the next-hop.","en_html":"Advertise to all your neighbors when the table updates, and periodically (advertisement interval). <ul> <li>But don’t advertise back to the next-hop.</li> <li><strong>…Or, advertise poison back to the next-hop.</strong></li> </ul>","zh_html":"当转发表更新时以及周期性（通告间隔(advertisement interval)）地，向你所有的邻居发送宣告。 <ul> <li>但不要宣告回给下一跳(next-hop)。</li> <li><strong>……或者，向下一跳宣告毒化(poison)。</strong></li> </ul>"},{"en":"If a table entry expires, make the entry poison and advertise it.","en_html":"If a table entry expires, make the entry poison and advertise it.","zh_html":"如果表条目过期，将该条目设为毒化(poison)并宣告它。"},{"en":"Note that split horizon and poison reverse are two choices, and you can pick exactly one to use (not both). Either you say nothing back to the next-hop, or you explicitly advertise poison back to the next-hop.","en_html":"Note that split horizon and poison reverse are two choices, and you can pick exactly one to use (not both). Either you say nothing back to the next-hop, or you explicitly advertise poison back to the next-hop.","zh_html":"注意，水平分割(split horizon)和毒性反转(poison reverse)是两种选择，你只能选择其中一种使用（不能同时使用）。要么你对下一跳什么也不说，要么你明确地向下一跳宣告毒化。"},{"en":"Rule 7: Count to Infinity","en_html":"<a href=\"#rule-7-count-to-infinity\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-7-count-to-infinity\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Rule 7: Count to Infinity","zh_html":"<a href=\"#rule-7-count-to-infinity\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rule-7-count-to-infinity\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 规则 7：计数到无穷大(Rule 7: Count to Infinity)"},{"en":"Split horizon or poison reverse helped us avoid length-2 loops, where R1 forwards to R2, and R2 forwards to R1. But we can still get routing loops involving 3 or more routers.","en_html":"Split horizon or poison reverse helped us avoid length-2 loops, where R1 forwards to R2, and R2 forwards to R1. But we can still get routing loops involving 3 or more routers.","zh_html":"水平分割(split horizon)或毒性反转(poison reverse)帮助我们避免了长度为 2 的环路（R1 转发给 R2，R2 转发给 R1）。但我们仍然可能得到涉及 3 个或更多路由器的路由环路(routing loops)。"},{"en":"To see why, consider this network. Suppose the tables reach steady-state. R1 and R2 both forward to R3, which forwards to A.","en_html":"To see why, consider this network. Suppose the tables reach steady-state. R1 and R2 both forward to R3, which forwards to A.","zh_html":"为了理解原因，考虑这个网络。假设转发表(forwarding tables)达到了稳态(steady state)。R1 和 R2 都转发给 R3，R3 转发给 A。"},{"en":"The A-R3 link goes down! A is now unreachable. Per Rule 5, R3 updates its table to show infinite cost to A, and sends this poison to both R2 and R1.","en_html":"The A-R3 link goes down! A is now unreachable. Per Rule 5, R3 updates its table to show infinite cost to A, and sends this poison to both R2 and R1.","zh_html":"A-R3 链路发生故障！A 现在不可达。根据规则 5，R3 更新其表将到 A 的成本设为无穷大(infinity)，并将此毒化(poison)发送给 R2 和 R1。"},{"en":"R2 gets the poison advertisement and updates its table (Rule 2, accept from next-hop). Now, both R2 and R3 know that A is unreachable.","en_html":"R2 gets the poison advertisement and updates its table (Rule 2, accept from next-hop). Now, both R2 and R3 know that A is unreachable.","zh_html":"R2 收到毒化宣告(poison advertisement)并更新其表（规则 2，接受来自下一跳的宣告）。现在，R2 和 R3 都知道 A 是不可达的。"},{"en":"The poison advertisement to R1 is dropped! R1 doesn’t see the poison, so it still thinks it can reach A via R3. (The poison can get re-sent later, but for this demo, all the bad things that are about to happen will happen before the poison gets a chance to be re-sent.)","en_html":"The poison advertisement to R1 is dropped! R1 doesn’t see the poison, so it still thinks it can reach A via R3. (The poison can get re-sent later, but for this demo, all the bad things that are about to happen will happen before the poison gets a chance to be re-sent.)","zh_html":"发往 R1 的毒化宣告被丢弃了！R1 没有看到毒化(poison)，所以它仍然认为它可以通过 R3 到达 A。（毒化稍后可以被重新发送，但在这个演示中，所有即将发生的坏事都会在毒化有机会被重新发送之前发生。）"},{"en":"At this point, R2 and R3 can’t reach A, but R1 thinks that it can still reach A.","en_html":"At this point, R2 and R3 can’t reach A, but R1 thinks that it can still reach A.","zh_html":"此时，R2 和 R3 无法到达 A，但 R1 认为它仍然可以到达 A。"},{"en":"Eventually, R1 sends out an advertisement. R1’s path to A is via R3, so by split horizon, it won’t advertise to R3. However, R1 will still advertise to R2: “I’m R1, and A is 2 away from me.”","en_html":"Eventually, R1 sends out an advertisement. R1’s path to A is via R3, so by split horizon, it won’t advertise to R3. However, R1 will still advertise to R2: “I’m R1, and A is 2 away from me.”","zh_html":"最终，R1 发出一个宣告。R1 的到 A 的路径经由 R3，所以根据水平分割(split horizon)，它不会向 R3 宣告。然而，R1 仍然会向 R2 宣告：\"我是 R1，A 离我有 2。\""},{"en":"R2 doesn’t have a way to reach A, so it accepts this route. Now, R2 is fooled into thinking it can reach A with cost 3.","en_html":"R2 doesn’t have a way to reach A, so it accepts this route. Now, R2 is fooled into thinking it can reach A with cost 3.","zh_html":"R2 没有到达 A 的方式，所以它接受了这条路由。现在，R2 被欺骗，以为它可以以成本 3 到达 A。"},{"en":"R2 sends out an advertisement about its new route. Split horizon dictates that R2 won’t advertise back to R1, but it will still advertise to R3: “I’m R2, and A is 3 away from me.”","en_html":"R2 sends out an advertisement about its new route. Split horizon dictates that R2 won’t advertise back to R1, but it will still advertise to R3: “I’m R2, and A is 3 away from me.”","zh_html":"R2 发出关于其新路由的宣告。水平分割(split horizon)规定 R2 不会向 R1 宣告，但它仍然会向 R3 宣告：\"我是 R2，A 离我有 3。\""},{"en":"R3 doesn’t have a way to reach A, so it accepts this route. Now, R3 is fooled into thinking it can reach A with cost 4.","en_html":"R3 doesn’t have a way to reach A, so it accepts this route. Now, R3 is fooled into thinking it can reach A with cost 4.","zh_html":"R3 没有到达 A 的方式，所以它接受了这条路由。现在，R3 被欺骗，以为它可以以成本 4 到达 A。"},{"en":"Next, R3 sends out an advertisement to R1 (not R2, per split horizon): “I am R3, and A is 4 away from me.”","en_html":"Next, R3 sends out an advertisement to R1 (not R2, per split horizon): “I am R3, and A is 4 away from me.”","zh_html":"接下来，R3 向 R1（不是 R2，根据水平分割(split horizon)）发出宣告：\"我是 R3，A 离我有 4。\""},{"en":"R1 will accept this advertisement (Rule 2, advertisement from next-hop) and update its table. Now, R1 thinks its cost to A is 5.","en_html":"R1 will accept this advertisement (Rule 2, advertisement from next-hop) and update its table. Now, R1 thinks its cost to A is 5.","zh_html":"R1 将接受这个宣告（规则 2，来自下一跳的宣告）并更新其转发表(forwarding table)。现在，R1 认为它到 A 的成本是 5。"},{"en":"Maybe you’re seeing where this is going. R1 advertises to R2 (not R3, per split horizon): “I’m R1, and A is 5 away from me.”","en_html":"Maybe you’re seeing where this is going. R1 advertises to R2 (not R3, per split horizon): “I’m R1, and A is 5 away from me.”","zh_html":"也许你已经看到这是怎么回事了。R1 向 R2（不是 R3，根据水平分割(split horizon)）宣告：\"我是 R1，A 离我有 5。\""},{"en":"R2 accepts this advertisement (Rule 2), and thinks it can reach A with cost 6.","en_html":"R2 accepts this advertisement (Rule 2), and thinks it can reach A with cost 6.","zh_html":"R2 接受这个宣告（规则 2），并认为它可以以成本 6 到达 A。"},{"en":"R2 advertises a cost of 6 to R3, who now thinks it can reach A with cost 7.","en_html":"R2 advertises a cost of 6 to R3, who now thinks it can reach A with cost 7.","zh_html":"R2 向 R3 宣告成本为 6，R3 现在认为它可以以成本 7 到达 A。"},{"en":"R3 advertises a cost of 7 to R1, who now thinks it can reach A with a cost of 8.","en_html":"R3 advertises a cost of 7 to R1, who now thinks it can reach A with a cost of 8.","zh_html":"R3 向 R1 宣告成本为 7，R1 现在认为它可以以成本 8 到达 A。"},{"en":"R1, R2, and R3 will keep sending advertisements to each other in a cycle, with progressively higher costs (which will all be accepted by Rule 2). Also, packets for A will get stuck in a forwarding loop between these routers.","en_html":"R1, R2, and R3 will keep sending advertisements to each other in a cycle, with progressively higher costs (which will all be accepted by Rule 2). Also, packets for A will get stuck in a forwarding loop between these routers.","zh_html":"R1、R2 和 R3 会持续在一个循环中互相发送宣告，成本越来越高（根据规则 2，这些宣告都会被接受）。同时，发往 A 的分组会困在这些路由器之间的转发环路(forwarding loop)中。"},{"en":"Let’s restate the problem again. The poison didn’t correctly propagate to all hosts, so one of the routers still had a busted path in its table. Then, that busted path got advertised in a loop, and Rule 2 caused the costs to keep increasing, with no end in sight.","en_html":"Let’s restate the problem again. The poison didn’t correctly propagate to all hosts, so one of the routers still had a busted path in its table. Then, that busted path got advertised in a loop, and Rule 2 caused the costs to keep increasing, with no end in sight.","zh_html":"让我们再次重述这个问题。毒化(poison)没有正确传播到所有主机，所以其中一个路由器的表中仍然有一条已坏路径。然后，那条已坏路径在一个环路中被宣告，规则 2 导致成本不断增加，看不到尽头。"},{"en":"Why didn’t split horizon rescue us? Remember, split horizon only stops a router from advertising back to its next-hop. But in this case, the loop is of length 3, and we were never advertising back to the next-hop.","en_html":"Why didn’t split horizon rescue us? Remember, split horizon only stops a router from advertising back to its next-hop. But in this case, the loop is of length 3, and we were never advertising back to the next-hop.","zh_html":"为什么水平分割(split horizon)没能救我们？记住，水平分割只阻止路由器向它的下一跳宣告。但在这种情况下，环路长度为 3，我们从未向下一跳宣告回。"},{"en":"(Note: Poison reverse wouldn’t rescue us either. If R3 advertises poison back to R2, then R2 would ignore that poison, because R2’s next hop is R1, not R3.)","en_html":"(Note: Poison reverse wouldn’t rescue us either. If R3 advertises poison back to R2, then R2 would ignore that poison, because R2’s next hop is R1, not R3.)","zh_html":"（注：毒性反转(poison reverse)也救不了我们。如果 R3 向 R2 宣告毒化(poison)，R2 会忽略那个毒化，因为 R2 的下一跳是 R1，而不是 R3。）"},{"en":"This is called the count-to-infinity problem, and none of our fixes so far (poison expired routes, split horizon, poison reverse) can solve it.","en_html":"This is called the <strong>count-to-infinity</strong> problem, and none of our fixes so far (poison expired routes, split horizon, poison reverse) can solve it.","zh_html":"这被称为<strong>计数到无穷大(count-to-infinity)</strong>问题，我们到目前为止的所有修复（毒化过期路由、水平分割、毒性反转）都无法解决它。"},{"en":"To solve this problem, we will enforce a maximum cost. In RIP, this value is 15. All costs greater than this maximum (i.e. 16 or above) are considered infinity.","en_html":"To solve this problem, we will enforce a maximum cost. In RIP, this value is 15. All costs greater than this maximum (i.e. 16 or above) are considered infinity.","zh_html":"为了解决这个问题，我们将强制执行一个最大成本(maximum cost)。在 RIP 中，这个值是 15。所有大于这个最大值（即 16 及以上）的成本都被视为无穷大(infinity)。"},{"en":"With this fix, the loop will still exist for some time, but eventually, all the costs will reach 16 (infinity). Let’s watch this in action.","en_html":"With this fix, the loop will still exist for some time, but eventually, all the costs will reach 16 (infinity). Let’s watch this in action.","zh_html":"有了这个修复，环路仍然会存在一段时间，但最终所有成本都会达到 16（无穷大(infinity)）。让我们看看实际效果。"},{"en":"The costs are increasing with every advertisement. Eventually, R1 advertises to R2: “I’m R1, and A is 14 away from me.” R2 accepts (per Rule 2) and updates its cost to 15.","en_html":"The costs are increasing with every advertisement. Eventually, R1 advertises to R2: “I’m R1, and A is 14 away from me.” R2 accepts (per Rule 2) and updates its cost to 15.","zh_html":"成本随着每次宣告而增加。最终，R1 向 R2 宣告：\"我是 R1，A 离我有 14。\"R2 接受（规则 2）并将其成本更新为 15。"},{"en":"R2 advertises to R3: “I’m R2, and A is 15 away from me.” R3 accepts (per Rule 2), but instead of updating its cost to 16, the cost is updated to infinity.","en_html":"R2 advertises to R3: “I’m R2, and A is 15 away from me.” R3 accepts (per Rule 2), but instead of updating its cost to 16, the cost is updated to infinity.","zh_html":"R2 向 R3 宣告：\"我是 R2，A 离我有 15。\"R3 接受（规则 2），但成本不是更新为 16，而是更新为无穷大(infinity)。"},{"en":"Next, R3 advertises to R1: “I’m R3, and A is infinity away from me.” R1 accepts (per Rule 2), and now R1 also has a cost of infinity. (Note: This advertisement looks just like poison, though the infinity originated from counting to infinity instead of detecting a failure.)","en_html":"Next, R3 advertises to R1: “I’m R3, and A is infinity away from me.” R1 accepts (per Rule 2), and now R1 also has a cost of infinity. (Note: This advertisement looks just like poison, though the infinity originated from counting to infinity instead of detecting a failure.)","zh_html":"接下来，R3 向 R1 宣告：\"我是 R3，A 离我无穷远(infinity)。\"R1 接受（规则 2），现在 R1 也有了无穷大的成本。（注：这个宣告看起来就像毒化(poison)，尽管这里的无穷大源于计数到无穷大(count-to-infinity)而不是检测到故障。）"},{"en":"Finally, R1 advertises to R2: “I’m R1, and A is infinity away from me.” R2 accepts (per Rule 2), and now all the routers have a cost of infinity.","en_html":"Finally, R1 advertises to R2: “I’m R1, and A is infinity away from me.” R2 accepts (per Rule 2), and now all the routers have a cost of infinity.","zh_html":"最后，R1 向 R2 宣告：\"我是 R1，A 离我无穷远(infinity)。\"R2 接受（规则 2），现在所有路由器都有了无穷大的成本。"},{"en":"We’ve reached steady-state again! Any future advertisements would all be advertising infinite cost, and they won’t change the tables. Eventually, the infinite-cost entries would all expire. Or, if another route to A appears, it would replace the infinite-cost entry.","en_html":"We’ve reached steady-state again! Any future advertisements would all be advertising infinite cost, and they won’t change the tables. Eventually, the infinite-cost entries would all expire. Or, if another route to A appears, it would replace the infinite-cost entry.","zh_html":"我们又达到了稳态(steady state)！未来任何宣告都将宣告无穷大(infinity)成本，它们不会改变转发表(forwarding tables)。最终，这些无穷大成本的条目都会过期。或者，如果出现了到 A 的另一条路由，它会替换无穷大成本的条目。"},{"en":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","en_html":"Let’s review our protocol so far.","zh_html":"让我们回顾一下目前的协议。"},{"en":"For each destination:","en_html":"For each destination:","zh_html":"对于每个目的地(destination)："},{"en":"If you hear an advertisement for that destination, update the table and reset the TTL if: The destination isn’t in the table. The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost. The advertisement is from the current next-hop. Includes poison advertisements.","en_html":"If you hear an advertisement for that destination, update the table <strong>and reset the TTL</strong> if: <ul> <li>The destination isn’t in the table.</li> <li>The advertised cost, plus the link cost to the neighbor, is better than the best-known cost.</li> <li>The advertisement is from the current next-hop. Includes poison advertisements.</li> </ul>","zh_html":"如果你听到该目的地的宣告(advertisement)，在以下情况下更新转发表(forwarding table)<strong>并重置 TTL</strong>： <ul> <li>表中没有该目的地。</li> <li>被宣告的成本加上到达邻居的链路成本，优于最佳已知成本。</li> <li>宣告来自当前的下一跳(next-hop)。包括毒化宣告(poison advertisements)。</li> </ul>"},{"en":"Advertise to all your neighbors when the table updates, and periodically (advertisement interval). But don’t advertise back to the next-hop. …Or, advertise poison back to the next-hop. Any cost greater than or equal to 16 is advertised as infinity.","en_html":"Advertise to all your neighbors when the table updates, and periodically (advertisement interval). <ul> <li>But don’t advertise back to the next-hop.</li> <li>…Or, advertise poison back to the next-hop.</li> <li><strong>Any cost greater than or equal to 16 is advertised as infinity.</strong></li> </ul>","zh_html":"当转发表更新时以及周期性（通告间隔(advertisement interval)）地，向你所有的邻居发送宣告。 <ul> <li>但不要宣告回给下一跳(next-hop)。</li> <li>……或者，向下一跳宣告毒化(poison)。</li> <li><strong>任何大于或等于 16 的成本宣告为无穷大(infinity)。</strong></li> </ul>"},{"en":"If a table entry expires, make the entry poison and advertise it.","en_html":"If a table entry expires, make the entry poison and advertise it.","zh_html":"如果表条目过期，将该条目设为毒化(poison)并宣告它。"},{"en":"Eventful Updates","en_html":"<a href=\"#eventful-updates\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"eventful-updates\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> Eventful Updates","zh_html":"<a href=\"#eventful-updates\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"eventful-updates\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 事件驱动的更新(Eventful Updates)"},{"en":"There are three occasions where a router might want to send advertisements:","en_html":"There are three occasions where a router might want to send advertisements:","zh_html":"路由器可能想要发送宣告的情况有三种："},{"en":"Send advertisements when the table changes. These are called triggered updates. The table might change when we accept a new advertisement, or when a new link is added (e.g. new static route), or when a link goes down (e.g. route gets poisoned).","en_html":"<p>Send advertisements when the table changes. These are called <strong>triggered updates</strong>. The table might change when we accept a new advertisement, or when a new link is added (e.g. new static route), or when a link goes down (e.g. route gets poisoned).</p>","zh_html":"<p>当转发表(forwarding table)发生变化时发送宣告。这称为<strong>触发更新(triggered updates)</strong>。表可能在我们接受新宣告时发生变化，或在添加新链路时（例如新的静态路由），或在链路发生故障时（例如路由被毒化(poisoned)）。</p>"},{"en":"Send advertisements periodically, once every advertisement interval.","en_html":"<p>Send advertisements periodically, once every advertisement interval.</p>","zh_html":"<p>定期发送宣告，每过一个通告间隔(advertisement interval)。</p>"},{"en":"Send advertisements when a table entry expires (and gets replaced by poison).","en_html":"<p>Send advertisements when a table entry expires (and gets replaced by poison).</p>","zh_html":"<p>当表条目过期时（并被毒化(poison)替换）发送宣告。</p>"},{"en":"Note that triggered updates are an optimization. Instead of advertising every time the table changes, we could just wait for the next advertisement interval to advertise the changes. This protocol would still be correct. However, triggered updates, in addition to the periodic updates, help our protocol converge on correct routes faster, because we propagate new information the instant we learn about it.","en_html":"Note that triggered updates are an optimization. Instead of advertising every time the table changes, we could just wait for the next advertisement interval to advertise the changes. This protocol would still be correct. However, triggered updates, in addition to the periodic updates, help our protocol converge on correct routes faster, because we propagate new information the instant we learn about it.","zh_html":"注意，触发更新(triggered updates)是一种优化。我们不必在每次表变化时都宣告，完全可以等待下一个通告间隔(advertisement interval)来宣告变化。这个协议仍然是正确的。然而，在周期性更新之外增加触发更新，有助于我们的协议更快地收敛(converge)到正确路由，因为我们在了解到新信息的瞬间就将其传播出去。"}]},"/routing/index.html":{"title":"index","blocks":[{"en":"Introduction to Routing","en_html":"<a href=\"intro.html\">Introduction to Routing</a>","zh_html":"路由简介(Introduction to Routing)"},{"en":"Model for Intra-Domain Routing","en_html":"<a href=\"model.html\">Model for Intra-Domain Routing</a>","zh_html":"域内路由模型(Model for Intra-Domain Routing)"},{"en":"Routing States","en_html":"<a href=\"solutions.html\">Routing States</a>","zh_html":"路由状态(Routing States)"},{"en":"Distance-Vector Protocols","en_html":"<a href=\"distance-vector.html\">Distance-Vector Protocols</a>","zh_html":"距离向量协议(Distance-Vector Protocols)"},{"en":"Link-State Protocols","en_html":"<a href=\"link-state.html\">Link-State Protocols</a>","zh_html":"链路状态协议(Link-State Protocols)"},{"en":"Addressing","en_html":"<a href=\"addressing.html\">Addressing</a>","zh_html":"寻址(Addressing)"},{"en":"Router Hardware","en_html":"<a href=\"router.html\">Router Hardware</a>","zh_html":"路由器硬件(Router Hardware)"},{"en":"Model for Inter-Domain Routing","en_html":"<a href=\"autonomous-systems.html\">Model for Inter-Domain Routing</a>","zh_html":"域间路由模型(Model for Inter-Domain Routing)"},{"en":"Border Gateway Protocol (BGP)","en_html":"<a href=\"bgp.html\">Border Gateway Protocol (BGP)</a>","zh_html":"边界网关协议(Border Gateway Protocol, BGP)"},{"en":"BGP Implementation and Issues","en_html":"<a href=\"bgp-implementation.html\">BGP Implementation and Issues</a>","zh_html":"BGP实现与问题(BGP Implementation and Issues)"},{"en":"IP Header","en_html":"<a href=\"ip-header.html\">IP Header</a>","zh_html":"IP首部(IP Header)"}]},"/routing/intro.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Introduction to Routing","zh_html":"路由(routing)简介"},{"en":"What is Routing?","zh_html":"什么是路由(routing)？"},{"en":"Suppose that machine A and machine B are both connected to the Internet. Machine A wants to send a message to machine B, but the two machines are not directly connected to each other. How does machine A know where to send the message, so that the message will eventually reach machine B? What path will the message take through the network to reach its destination of machine B? In this unit, we’ll be studying routing to answer these questions.","zh_html":"假设机器 A 和机器 B 都连接到了互联网(Internet)。机器 A 想向机器 B 发送一条消息，但两台机器并没有直接相连。机器 A 怎么知道应该把消息发往哪里，才能让消息最终到达机器 B？这条消息会沿着网络中的哪条路径(path)到达目的地(destination)机器 B？本单元会学习路由(routing)，用来回答这些问题。"},{"en":"First, we’ll devise a model of the Internet so that we can pose routing as a well-defined problem. We’ll also see what answers to the routing problem look like, and what makes an answer valid and good.","zh_html":"首先，我们会构造一个互联网(Internet)模型(model)，使路由(routing)可以被表述为一个定义清楚的问题。我们还会看到路由问题的答案长什么样，以及什么样的答案才算有效(valid)且好。"},{"en":"Next, we’ll look at several different types of routing protocols that can be implemented to help generate answers to the routing problem. We’ll also see how addressing protocols can be used to make our routing protocols scalable to the entire Internet.","zh_html":"接下来，我们会考察几类可以实现的路由协议(routing protocols)，它们能帮助生成路由问题的答案。我们还会看到如何利用寻址协议(addressing protocols)，让路由协议能够扩展(scalable)到整个互联网。"},{"en":"Finally, we’ll take a brief look at the real-life hardware we use to implement these routing protocols.","zh_html":"最后，我们会简要了解现实中用于实现这些路由协议(routing protocols)的硬件(hardware)。"},{"en":"Inter-Domain and Intra-Domain Routing","zh_html":"域间路由(inter-domain routing)与域内路由(intra-domain routing)"},{"en":"One possible strategy for routing is to build a model of the Internet that includes every single machine in the world, and design a single giant routing protocol that will allow us to send packets anywhere in the world. However, this is infeasible in practice because of the scale of the Internet.","zh_html":"一种可能的路由(routing)策略是：建立一个包含世界上每一台机器的互联网模型，然后设计一个巨大的统一路由协议(routing protocol)，让我们可以把分组(packets)发送到世界任意地方。不过，由于互联网(Internet)规模太大，这在实践中不可行。"},{"en":"Instead, we’ll take advantage of the fact that the Internet is a network of networks. In other words, the Internet consists of many local networks. Each local network implements its own routing protocol that specifies how to send packets within just that local network. Then, we can connect up all those local networks and implement a routing protocol across all the local networks, specifying how to send packets between different local networks.","zh_html":"相反，我们会利用这样一个事实：互联网(Internet)是网络的网络(network of networks)。换句话说，互联网由许多本地网络(local networks)组成。每个本地网络实现自己的路由协议(routing protocol)，规定如何只在这个本地网络内部发送分组(packets)。随后，我们把这些本地网络连接起来，并在所有本地网络之间实现一种路由协议，用来规定不同本地网络之间如何发送分组。"},{"en":"Local networks are not identical. For example, they might differ in size: Some networks might have more machines than others. Or, the machines might be spread out over a wider physical area (e.g. the entire UC Berkeley campus), or a smaller area (e.g. your home). Networks can also differ in the bandwidth they need to support, the allowable failure rate, the number of support staff available, the age of the infrastructure, the amount of money available to build and support it, and so on.","zh_html":"本地网络(local networks)并不完全相同。例如，它们的规模可能不同：有些网络包含的机器比其他网络更多。机器也可能分布在更大的物理区域内，例如整个 UC Berkeley 校园；也可能只分布在较小区域内，例如你的家。不同网络还可能在需要支持的带宽(bandwidth)、可接受的故障率(failure rate)、可用运维人员数量、基础设施(infrastructure)年限、建设和维护预算等方面有所不同。"},{"en":"Because each network has its own structure and requirements, different local networks might choose to use different routing protocols. A strategy for routing packets might be effective on one network, but not another one.","zh_html":"由于每个网络都有自己的结构(structure)和需求(requirements)，不同本地网络(local networks)可能会选择不同的路由协议(routing protocols)。一种分组路由策略在某个网络中可能很有效，但在另一个网络中未必适用。"},{"en":"With the network of networks model, we can let individual local networks choose a routing strategy for packets within their network. Each operator can choose the protocol that works best for them. The protocols for routing packets within a local network are called intra-domain routing protocols, or interior gateway protocols (IGPs). Real-world examples include OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) and IS-IS (Intermediate System to Intermediate System).","zh_html":"在网络的网络(network of networks)模型下，我们可以让每个本地网络(local network)自行选择其内部的分组路由策略。每个运营方(operator)都可以选择最适合自己的协议(protocol)。用于在本地网络内部为分组选择路径的协议称为域内路由协议(intra-domain routing protocols)，也称内部网关协议(Interior Gateway Protocols, IGPs)。现实中的例子包括 OSPF(Open Shortest Path First) 和 IS-IS(Intermediate System to Intermediate System)。"},{"en":"By contrast, protocols for routing packets across different networks are called inter-domain routing protocols, or exterior gateway protocols (EGPs). In order to support sending packets across different local networks, every network needs to agree to use the same protocol for routing packets between each other. If different networks used different inter-domain protocols, there’s no guarantee that that the entire Internet could be connected in a consistent way. What if one operator only implemented Protocol X, and another operator only implemented Protocol Y? It’s not clear how these two local networks would be able to exchange messages.","zh_html":"相比之下，用于在不同网络之间路由分组的协议称为域间路由协议(inter-domain routing protocols)，也称外部网关协议(Exterior Gateway Protocols, EGPs)。为了支持跨不同本地网络(local networks)发送分组，每个网络都需要同意使用同一种网络间路由协议。如果不同网络使用不同的域间协议(inter-domain protocols)，就无法保证整个互联网(Internet)能以一致的方式连通。假如一个运营方(operator)只实现协议 X，而另一个运营方只实现协议 Y，这两个本地网络该如何交换消息并不清楚。"},{"en":"Because every network must agree to use the same inter-domain protocol, there is only one protocol implemented at scale on the Internet, namely BGP (Border Gateway Protocol).","zh_html":"因为每个网络都必须同意使用同一种域间协议(inter-domain protocol)，所以互联网上真正大规模部署的此类协议只有一个，也就是 BGP(Border Gateway Protocol，边界网关协议)。"},{"en":"This model of interior and exterior gateway protocols is convenient for intuition, but in practice, there is not always a clear distinction between them. For example, BGP is sometimes also used inside a local network, in addition to between different networks.","zh_html":"内部网关协议(interior gateway protocols)和外部网关协议(exterior gateway protocols)这个模型有助于建立直觉，但在实践中，两者并不总是界限分明。例如，BGP 有时不仅用于不同网络之间，也会在某个本地网络(local network)内部使用。"},{"en":"Regardless of whether a protocol is deployed internally within a network, or externally between all networks, we can additionally classify the routing protocol by looking at what the underlying algorithm is doing. In particular, we’ll study distance-vector protocols, link-state protocols, and path-vector protocols (more about each type later).","zh_html":"无论某个协议(protocol)是部署在网络内部，还是部署在所有网络之间，我们还可以根据底层算法(algorithm)的工作方式来分类路由协议(routing protocol)。具体来说，我们会学习距离向量协议(distance-vector protocols)、链路状态协议(link-state protocols)和路径向量协议(path-vector protocols)，后面会分别详细讨论。"}]},"/routing/ip-header.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"IP Header","en_html":"<a href=\"#ip-header\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-header\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP Header","zh_html":"<a href=\"#ip-header\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-header\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP 首部(IP header)"},{"en":"IP Header Design Goals","en_html":"<a href=\"#ip-header-design-goals\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-header-design-goals\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP Header Design Goals","zh_html":"<a href=\"#ip-header-design-goals\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-header-design-goals\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP 首部设计目标(IP header design goals)"},{"en":"Recall that a protocol like IP consists of syntax and semantics. The syntax determines what fields are in the IP header, and the semantics determine how those fields are processed.","en_html":"Recall that a protocol like IP consists of syntax and semantics. The syntax determines what fields are in the IP header, and the semantics determine how those fields are processed.","zh_html":"回想一下，像 IP 这样的协议(protocol)由语法(syntax)和语义(semantics)组成。语法决定了 IP 首部(IP header)中有哪些字段(fields)，而语义决定了这些字段如何处理。"},{"en":"Also, recall that the IP packet consists of a header and payload. The header contains relevant metadata that the IP protocol can process. The payload contains any data that will be passed up to higher-layer protocols, and is not parsed by the IP protocol.","en_html":"Also, recall that the IP packet consists of a header and payload. The header contains relevant metadata that the IP protocol can process. The payload contains any data that will be passed up to higher-layer protocols, and is not parsed by the IP protocol.","zh_html":"另外，回想一下 IP 分组(IP packet)由首部(header)和载荷(payload)组成。首部包含 IP 协议(IP protocol)可以处理的相关元数据(metadata)。载荷包含将向上传递给更高层协议(higher-layer protocols)的数据，而 IP 协议不会解析(parse)载荷。"},{"en":"Finally, recall that headers are added as we move down the stack, and stripped away as we pass packets up the stack. IP headers are processed at both end hosts, and at every intermediate router.","en_html":"Finally, recall that headers are added as we move down the stack, and stripped away as we pass packets up the stack. IP headers are processed at both end hosts, and at every intermediate router.","zh_html":"最后，回想一下，首部(headers)在我们沿协议栈向下移动时被添加，在我们将分组向上传递时被剥离。IP 首部(IP headers)在端主机(end hosts)和每一台中间路由器(intermediate router)上都会被处理。"},{"en":"The IP header should be as small as possible. Every packet sent across the Internet need the IP header attached, so increasing the IP header size, even by one byte, would significantly increase the total amount of bandwidth across the Internet.","en_html":"The IP header should be as small as possible. Every packet sent across the Internet need the IP header attached, so increasing the IP header size, even by one byte, would significantly increase the total amount of bandwidth across the Internet.","zh_html":"IP 首部(IP header)应该尽可能小。在互联网(Internet)上传输的每个分组(packet)都需要附加 IP 首部，因此增加 IP 首部大小，即使只增加 1 字节(byte)，也会显著增加整个互联网的总带宽(bandwidth)消耗。"},{"en":"The IP header should be as simple as possible. Every router and end host has to process IP packets as they’re sent and received, so a header that’s complicated to process would slow the entire Internet down. Ideally, we’d like the header to be processed purely in hardware, so we can’t assume we have access to general-purpose CPU operations when processing this header.","en_html":"The IP header should be as simple as possible. Every router and end host has to process IP packets as they're sent and received, so a header that's complicated to process would slow the entire Internet down. Ideally, we'd like the header to be processed purely in hardware, so we can't assume we have access to general-purpose CPU operations when processing this header.","zh_html":"IP 首部(IP header)应该尽可能简单。每台路由器(router)和端主机(end host)在发送和接收 IP 分组(IP packets)时都需要处理首部，因此一个处理复杂的首部会拖慢整个互联网(Internet)。理想情况下，我们希望首部能纯粹在硬件中处理，因此不能假设在处理这个首部时可以依赖通用 CPU 操作。"},{"en":"IP Header Fields","en_html":"<a href=\"#ip-header-fields\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-header-fields\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP Header Fields","zh_html":"<a href=\"#ip-header-fields\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-header-fields\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP 首部字段(IP header fields)"},{"en":"An IP protocol needs to do four things:","en_html":"An IP protocol needs to do four things:","zh_html":"一个 IP 协议(IP protocol)需要做四件事："},{"en":"Everybody (end hosts, routers) need to be able to parse the packet and understand what the bits mean. To support this, the header will include the IP version (4-bit value), the header length (4-bit value, measured in 4-byte words, required because the IP header length is not fixed), and the packet length (16-bit value, measured in bytes).","en_html":"Everybody (end hosts, routers) need to be able to <strong>parse</strong> the packet and understand what the bits mean. To support this, the header will include the <strong>IP version</strong> (4-bit value), the <strong>header length</strong> (4-bit value, measured in 4-byte words, required because the IP header length is not fixed), and the <strong>packet length</strong> (16-bit value, measured in bytes).","zh_html":"所有设备（端主机(end hosts)、路由器(routers)）都需要能够<strong>解析(parse)</strong>分组(packet)并理解这些比特的含义。为了支持这一点，首部(header)将包含<strong>IP 版本(IP version)</strong>（4 位值）、<strong>首部长度(header length)</strong>（4 位值，以 4 字节字为单位，因为 IP 首部长度不固定）和<strong>分组长度(packet length)</strong>（16 位值，以字节为单位）。"},{"en":"Routers (not end hosts) need to forward the packet to the next router. To support this, the header will include the destination IP address (32-bit value).","en_html":"Routers (not end hosts) need to <strong>forward</strong> the packet to the next router. To support this, the header will include the <strong>destination IP address</strong> (32-bit value).","zh_html":"路由器(routers)（而非端主机）需要将分组(packet)<strong>转发(forward)</strong>到下一台路由器。为了支持这一点，首部(header)将包含<strong>目的 IP 地址(destination IP address)</strong>（32 位值）。"},{"en":"End hosts (not routers) need to pass the packet up to higher layers. To support this, the header will include a protocol number (8-bit value), which tells us which Layer 4 protocol (TCP or UDP) should be used to process the payload. For example, a protocol number of 6 says to use the TCP protocol to read the remaining payload (reading the first bits of the payload as the TCP header, and so on). A protocol number of 17 corresponds to the UDP protocol.","en_html":"End hosts (not routers) need to <strong>pass the packet up</strong> to higher layers. To support this, the header will include a <strong>protocol number</strong> (8-bit value), which tells us which Layer 4 protocol (TCP or UDP) should be used to process the payload. For example, a protocol number of 6 says to use the TCP protocol to read the remaining payload (reading the first bits of the payload as the TCP header, and so on). A protocol number of 17 corresponds to the UDP protocol.","zh_html":"端主机(end hosts)（而非路由器）需要将分组(packet)<strong>向上传递(pass up)</strong>到更高层。为了支持这一点，首部(header)将包含一个<strong>协议号(protocol number)</strong>（8 位值），它告诉我们应使用哪个第 4 层协议(Layer 4 protocol)，即 TCP 或 UDP，来处理载荷(payload)。例如，协议号 6 表示使用 TCP 协议来读取剩余的载荷（将载荷的前几个比特读取为 TCP 首部，依此类推）。协议号 17 对应 UDP 协议。"},{"en":"End hosts and routers need to be able to send replies back to the source. To support this, the header will include the source IP address (32-bit value).","en_html":"End hosts and routers need to be able to <strong>send replies</strong> back to the source. To support this, the header will include the <strong>source IP address</strong> (32-bit value).","zh_html":"端主机(end hosts)和路由器(routers)需要能够向发送方<strong>回复(send replies)</strong>。为了支持这一点，首部(header)将包含<strong>源 IP 地址(source IP address)</strong>（32 位值）。"},{"en":"IP Error Handling","en_html":"<a href=\"#ip-error-handling\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-error-handling\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP Error Handling","zh_html":"<a href=\"#ip-error-handling\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-error-handling\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP 错误处理(IP error handling)"},{"en":"End hosts and routers also need to be able to specify problems or special cases in case a packet needs additional handling.","en_html":"End hosts and routers also need to be able to <strong>specify problems or special cases</strong> in case a packet needs additional handling.","zh_html":"端主机(end hosts)和路由器(routers)还需要能够<strong>指明问题或特殊情况(specify problems or special cases)</strong>，以便分组(packet)需要额外处理时使用。"},{"en":"IP packets can be stuck in loops (e.g. if the routing protocol hasn’t converged yet). One possible option is to let the packet loop indefinitely until routes converge, but packet forwarding happens on nanosecond scale, and routing convergence happens on the millisecond or second scale. Letting the packet loop until routes converge can take a long time and waste a lot of bandwidth. To prevent indefinite looping, the IP header has a time to live (TTL) (8-bit value), which is decremented at each hop. If the TTL reaches 0, the packet is discarded, and an error message is sent back to the source. (The error message is required by the IP specification, though is not always sent in practice.)","en_html":"IP packets can be stuck in loops (e.g. if the routing protocol hasn't converged yet). One possible option is to let the packet loop indefinitely until routes converge, but packet forwarding happens on nanosecond scale, and routing convergence happens on the millisecond or second scale. Letting the packet loop until routes converge can take a long time and waste a lot of bandwidth. To prevent indefinite looping, the IP header has a <strong>time to live (TTL)</strong> (8-bit value), which is decremented at each hop. If the TTL reaches 0, the packet is discarded, and an error message is sent back to the source. (The error message is required by the IP specification, though is not always sent in practice.)","zh_html":"IP 分组(IP packets)可能陷入环路(loops)，例如路由协议(routing protocol)尚未收敛(converged)时。一种可能的方案是让分组无限循环，直到路由收敛，但分组转发(packet forwarding)在纳秒(nanosecond)级别发生，而路由收敛(routing convergence)在毫秒(millisecond)或秒(second)级别发生。让分组持续循环直到路由收敛可能需要很长时间，并浪费大量带宽(bandwidth)。为了防止无限循环，IP 首部(IP header)中有一个<strong>生存时间(TTL)</strong>（8 位值），每经过一跳(hop)就减 1。如果 TTL 减到 0，分组被丢弃，并向发送方返回一条错误消息。（IP 规范要求发送错误消息，尽管在实践中并不总是发送。）"},{"en":"IP packets can be corrupted (e.g. bits on the wire can be corrupted from electrical processes). To detect corruption, the IP header contains a checksum (16-bit value), and discards packets if the checksum is incorrect.","en_html":"IP packets can be corrupted (e.g. bits on the wire can be corrupted from electrical processes). To detect corruption, the IP header contains a <strong>checksum</strong> (16-bit value), and discards packets if the checksum is incorrect.","zh_html":"IP 分组(IP packets)可能被损坏(corrupted)，例如线路上的比特可能因电气过程而损坏。为了检测损坏，IP 首部(IP header)包含一个<strong>校验和(checksum)</strong>（16 位值），如果校验和不正确，则丢弃分组。"},{"en":"Note that the IP checksum is only computed over the IP header. The checksum can only detect errors in the IP header, not errors in the IP payload. This reflects the end-to-end principle, where we enforce that the payload is checked by the end host, not the intermediate routers.","en_html":"Note that the IP checksum is only computed over the IP header. The checksum can only detect errors in the IP header, not errors in the IP payload. This reflects the end-to-end principle, where we enforce that the payload is checked by the end host, not the intermediate routers.","zh_html":"注意，IP 校验和(checksum)仅针对 IP 首部(IP header)计算。校验和只能检测 IP 首部中的错误，不能检测 IP 载荷(payload)中的错误。这体现了端到端原则(end-to-end principle)：我们要求载荷由端主机(end host)检查，而非中间路由器(intermediate routers)。"},{"en":"The IP checksum is updated at every router, because the TTL changes, and the checksum has to be re-computed. One possible alternative design is to exclude the TTL in the checksum, to save routers the extra work.","en_html":"The IP checksum is updated at every router, because the TTL changes, and the checksum has to be re-computed. One possible alternative design is to exclude the TTL in the checksum, to save routers the extra work.","zh_html":"IP 校验和(checksum)会在每台路由器(router)上被更新，因为 TTL 会变化，校验和必须重新计算。一种可能的替代设计是，将 TTL 排除在校验和之外，以节省路由器的额外工作。"},{"en":"IP packets could be too large for a specific link. Each link has a maximum transmission unit (MTU), indicating the largest packet size (in bytes) that link can carry as one unit. For example, the link might have limited memory for remembering a packet while it sends the bits along the wire.","en_html":"IP packets could be too large for a specific link. Each link has a <strong>maximum transmission unit (MTU)</strong>, indicating the largest packet size (in bytes) that link can carry as one unit. For example, the link might have limited memory for remembering a packet while it sends the bits along the wire.","zh_html":"IP 分组(IP packets)可能对特定链路(link)来说太大。每条链路有一个<strong>最大传输单元(MTU)</strong>，表示该链路作为一个单元能承载的最大分组大小（以字节为单位）。例如，链路在沿线路发送比特期间，用于暂存分组的存储器(memory)可能有限。"},{"en":"The end host doesn’t know which links will be carrying the packet, so the end host might sent a packet that’s too large for one of the links. To solve this, a router can perform fragmentation, splitting the packet into multiple fragments, which the router on the other end of the link must reassemble to recover the original packet. The identification (16-bit), flags (3-bit), and offset (13-bit) fields in the header are used to implement fragmentation.","en_html":"The end host doesn't know which links will be carrying the packet, so the end host might sent a packet that's too large for one of the links. To solve this, a router can perform <strong>fragmentation</strong>, splitting the packet into multiple fragments, which the router on the other end of the link must reassemble to recover the original packet. The identification (16-bit), flags (3-bit), and offset (13-bit) fields in the header are used to implement fragmentation.","zh_html":"端主机(end host)不知道哪些链路会承载分组，因此可能会发送一个对某条链路来说过大的分组。为了解决这个问题，路由器(router)可以执行<strong>分片(fragmentation)</strong>，将分组拆分成多个片(fragments)，然后由链路另一端的路由器重组(reassemble)以恢复原始分组。首部(header)中的标识(identification)（16 位）、标志(flags)（3 位）和片偏移(offset)（13 位）字段用于实现分片。"},{"en":"Fragmentation is achievable in hardware (e.g. a router can quickly fragment packets without punting the packet for special handling), but it introduces extra overhead. The modern Internet avoids fragmentation whenever possible. For example, we try to standardize the MTU as much as possible (a modern standard is 1500 bytes).","en_html":"Fragmentation is achievable in hardware (e.g. a router can quickly fragment packets without punting the packet for special handling), but it introduces extra overhead. The modern Internet avoids fragmentation whenever possible. For example, we try to standardize the MTU as much as possible (a modern standard is 1500 bytes).","zh_html":"分片(fragmentation)可以在硬件中实现，例如路由器可以快速分片而无须将分组移交(punting)给特殊处理，但这会引入额外的开销。现代互联网(Internet)尽可能避免分片。例如，我们尽量标准化 MTU（现代标准是 1500 字节）。"},{"en":"The early designers of IP did not fully embrace best-effort design, and thought it might be useful to allow applications to send packets of different types based on the application’s needs. To implement this, the IP header has Type of Service (ToS) bits (8-bit value), which can be used to request different forms of packet delivery. For example, some packets can be marked as delay-sensitive or high-priority. Over the years, these bits have been redefined to represent different protocols, and ToS no longer exists in its original form. Instead, these bits now represent some notion of priority. Examples of protocols using these bits are Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP), which defines certain classes of traffic, and Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN), which will help with traffic congestion (discussed later).","en_html":"The early designers of IP did not fully embrace best-effort design, and thought it might be useful to allow applications to send packets of different types based on the application's needs. To implement this, the IP header has <strong>Type of Service (ToS)</strong> bits (8-bit value), which can be used to request different forms of packet delivery. For example, some packets can be marked as delay-sensitive or high-priority. Over the years, these bits have been redefined to represent different protocols, and ToS no longer exists in its original form. Instead, these bits now represent some notion of priority. Examples of protocols using these bits are Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP), which defines certain classes of traffic, and Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN), which will help with traffic congestion (discussed later).","zh_html":"IP 的早期设计者并未完全采纳尽力而为(best-effort)设计，他们认为允许应用(application)根据自身需求发送不同类型的分组(packets)可能是有用的。为了实现这一点，IP 首部(IP header)中有<strong>服务类型(ToS)</strong>位（8 位值），可用于请求不同形式的分组投递(packet delivery)。例如，某些分组可以标记为延迟敏感(delay-sensitive)或高优先级(high-priority)。多年以来，这些位已被重新定义以表示不同的协议，ToS 不再以其原始形式存在。相反，这些位现在表示某种优先级概念。使用这些位的协议示例包括：区分服务码点(DSCP)，它定义了某些流量类别(traffic classes)；以及显式拥塞通知(ECN)，它将有助于处理流量拥塞(congestion)（将在后面讨论）。"},{"en":"In the original IP design, additional option bits can be added to the IP header to request more advanced processing on the packet. For example, the sender can request routers to record the route that the packet is taking (e.g. for diagnostics). The sender could include a source route in the packet header and force the packet to travel a certain route. The packet header could also include a timestamp. In modern implementations, these options are almost always disabled, because they lead to unnecessarily complicated implementations that increase the packet processing overhead. For example, these options force the IP header to be variable-length, which is harder to process than a fixed-length header.","en_html":"In the original IP design, additional <strong>option bits</strong> can be added to the IP header to request more advanced processing on the packet. For example, the sender can request routers to record the route that the packet is taking (e.g. for diagnostics). The sender could include a source route in the packet header and force the packet to travel a certain route. The packet header could also include a timestamp. In modern implementations, these options are almost always disabled, because they lead to unnecessarily complicated implementations that increase the packet processing overhead. For example, these options force the IP header to be variable-length, which is harder to process than a fixed-length header.","zh_html":"在最初的 IP 设计中，可以在 IP 首部(IP header)中添加额外的<strong>选项位(option bits)</strong>，以请求对分组(packet)进行更高级的处理。例如，发送方可以请求路由器(routers)记录分组经过的路由(route)，例如用于诊断(diagnostics)。发送方可以在分组首部中包含源路由(source route)，强制分组走特定的路径。分组首部还可以包含时间戳(timestamp)。在现代实现中，这些选项几乎总是被禁用，因为它们会导致不必要的复杂实现，增加分组处理开销。例如，这些选项迫使 IP 首部变为可变长度(variable-length)，比固定长度首部更难处理。"},{"en":"IPv6 Header Changes","en_html":"<a href=\"#ipv6-header-changes\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ipv6-header-changes\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IPv6 Header Changes","zh_html":"<a href=\"#ipv6-header-changes\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ipv6-header-changes\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IPv6 首部变化(IPv6 header changes)"},{"en":"IPv6 was motivated by the concern that we would eventually run out of 32-bit IPv4 addresses. IPv6 expanded addresses so that addresses are 128 bits long. The number of possible IPv6 addresses is astronomically large (think: number of atoms in the universe), so we will almost certainly never run out of IPv6 addresses.","en_html":"IPv6 was motivated by the concern that we would eventually run out of 32-bit IPv4 addresses. IPv6 <strong>expanded addresses</strong> so that addresses are 128 bits long. The number of possible IPv6 addresses is astronomically large (think: number of atoms in the universe), so we will almost certainly never run out of IPv6 addresses.","zh_html":"IPv6 的动机是担心我们最终会用完 32 位的 IPv4 地址。IPv6 <strong>扩展了地址(expanded addresses)</strong>，使地址长度为 128 位(bits)。可能的 IPv6 地址数量大得惊人（想想宇宙中的原子数量），因此我们几乎可以肯定永远不会用尽 IPv6 地址。"},{"en":"The designers of IPv6 took the opportunity to clean up and modernize the IP header, removing and updating fields that are outdated. Originally, IPv6 was intended to be a more ambitious protocol with many new addressing features, but most of these features were never realized. In practice, besides this “spring cleaning” removal of outdated features, there weren’t many significant changes to the protocol from IPv4, so the result is a more elegant IP protocol, without many ambitious changes.","en_html":"The designers of IPv6 took the opportunity to clean up and modernize the IP header, removing and updating fields that are outdated. Originally, IPv6 was intended to be a more ambitious protocol with many new addressing features, but most of these features were never realized. In practice, besides this \"spring cleaning\" removal of outdated features, there weren't many significant changes to the protocol from IPv4, so the result is a more elegant IP protocol, without many ambitious changes.","zh_html":"IPv6 的设计者借此机会清理和现代化了 IP 首部(IP header)，移除和更新了过时的字段(fields)。最初，IPv6 被设计成一个更雄心勃勃的协议(protocol)，具有许多新的寻址特性(addressing features)，但这些特性大多从未实现。在实践中，除了这种对过时特性的\"春季大扫除\"之外，该协议相较于 IPv4 并没有太多显著变化，因此结果是得到一个更优雅的 IP 协议，而没有什么雄心勃勃的改变。"},{"en":"Note: In case you’re curious, IPv5 was published in 1990 (before IPv6 in 1998). It was an experimental protocol that was never widely implemented.","en_html":"Note: In case you're curious, IPv5 was published in 1990 (before IPv6 in 1998). It was an experimental protocol that was never widely implemented.","zh_html":"注意：如果你感到好奇，IPv5 发表于 1990 年（早于 1998 年的 IPv6）。它是一个实验性协议(experimental protocol)，从未被广泛实现。"},{"en":"IPv6 eliminates checksums in the IP packet header. The argument in favor of including a checksum is: if a packet is corrupted and is not detected, the corrupt packet continues being sent, wasting bandwidth. Including the checksum ensures that the packet is dropped and bandwidth is not wasted on a corrupt packet. In modern times, bandwidth is less of a bottleneck, so the checksum is no longer necessary, and it’s not a huge performance impact if some corrupt packets are sent all the way through the network.","en_html":"IPv6 <strong>eliminates checksums</strong> in the IP packet header. The argument in favor of including a checksum is: if a packet is corrupted and is not detected, the corrupt packet continues being sent, wasting bandwidth. Including the checksum ensures that the packet is dropped and bandwidth is not wasted on a corrupt packet. In modern times, bandwidth is less of a bottleneck, so the checksum is no longer necessary, and it's not a huge performance impact if some corrupt packets are sent all the way through the network.","zh_html":"IPv6 <strong>消除了校验和(eliminates checksums)</strong>。支持包含校验和(checksum)的论据是：如果分组(packet)被损坏但未被检测到，损坏的分组会继续被发送，浪费带宽(bandwidth)。包含校验和可以确保分组被丢弃，带宽不会浪费在损坏的分组上。在现代，带宽已不再是主要瓶颈，因此校验和不再必要，即使一些损坏的分组被一路发送到网络尽头，也不会造成巨大的性能影响。"},{"en":"IPv6 eliminates fragmentation. If an IPv6 packet is too large for a specific link, the router will drop the packet and send an error message back to the source with the maximum allowable packet size (MTU). The original sender is responsible for splitting up the data into smaller packets and re-sending those smaller packets. End hosts (e.g. your personal computer) process fewer packets than routers (e.g. in data centers), so transferring the workload of fragmentation from routers to end hosts improves the overall scalability of the Internet.","en_html":"IPv6 <strong>eliminates fragmentation</strong>. If an IPv6 packet is too large for a specific link, the router will drop the packet and send an error message back to the source with the maximum allowable packet size (MTU). The original sender is responsible for splitting up the data into smaller packets and re-sending those smaller packets. End hosts (e.g. your personal computer) process fewer packets than routers (e.g. in data centers), so transferring the workload of fragmentation from routers to end hosts improves the overall scalability of the Internet.","zh_html":"IPv6 <strong>消除了分片(eliminates fragmentation)</strong>。如果 IPv6 分组(packet)对特定链路(link)来说太大，路由器(router)会丢弃该分组，并向发送方返回一条包含最大允许分组大小(MTU)的错误消息。原始发送方负责将数据拆分成更小的分组并重新发送这些更小的分组。端主机(end hosts)，例如你的个人电脑，处理的分组比路由器少得多，例如数据中心中的路由器，因此将分片的工作量从路由器转移到端主机，提高了互联网(Internet)的整体可扩展性(scalability)。"},{"en":"IPv6 replaces the variable-length options section with a modified implementation of the protocol field. In IPv4, options were problematic because they created variable-length headers, which are harder to parse. In IPv6, the header is fixed in length. This also means that the header length field can be eliminated.","en_html":"IPv6 replaces the variable-length options section with a modified implementation of the protocol field. In IPv4, options were problematic because they created variable-length headers, which are harder to parse. In IPv6, the header is fixed in length. This also means that the <strong>header length</strong> field can be eliminated.","zh_html":"IPv6 用协议字段(protocol field)的修改版本来替代可变长度(variable-length)选项部分。在 IPv4 中，选项(option)是有问题的，因为它们产生了可变长度的首部，更难解析(parse)。在 IPv6 中，首部(header)是固定长度的。这也意味着<strong>首部长度(header length)</strong>字段可以被消除。"},{"en":"In order to continue supporting options, IPv6 generalizes the protocol field to allow the IP packet to be passed up for special processing before reaching Layer 4. (Recall, the protocol header in IPv4 is set to either 7 or 19, to indicate which Layer 4 protocol processes the packet next.) The field is renamed from protocol to next header in IPv6.","en_html":"In order to continue supporting options, IPv6 generalizes the protocol field to allow the IP packet to be passed up for special processing before reaching Layer 4. (Recall, the protocol header in IPv4 is set to either 7 or 19, to indicate which Layer 4 protocol processes the packet next.) The field is renamed from protocol to <strong>next header</strong> in IPv6.","zh_html":"为了继续支持选项(options)，IPv6 将协议字段(protocol field)通用化，使 IP 分组(IP packet)在到达第 4 层(Layer 4)之前可以被向上传递以进行特殊处理。（回想一下，IPv4 中的协议首部(protocol header)被设置为 6 或 17，以指示接下来由哪个第 4 层协议处理该分组。）在 IPv6 中，该字段从 protocol 重命名为<strong>下一个首部(next header)</strong>。"},{"en":"If you want an additional protocol to process the IP packet, you can put that protocol’s corresponding number in the next header field. The designers and users of these extra protocols need to agree on which numbers correspond to which protocols, and a standards body organization needs to manage these numbers. Then, the payload can be passed to the additional protocol, which can read an additional header (after the Layer 3 IPv6 header, but before the Layer 4 header) and perform additional processing, before passing the remaining payload to Layer 4.","en_html":"If you want an additional protocol to process the IP packet, you can put that protocol's corresponding number in the next header field. The designers and users of these extra protocols need to agree on which numbers correspond to which protocols, and a standards body organization needs to manage these numbers. Then, the payload can be passed to the additional protocol, which can read an additional header (after the Layer 3 IPv6 header, but before the Layer 4 header) and perform additional processing, before passing the remaining payload to Layer 4.","zh_html":"如果你希望另一个额外的协议(protocol)来处理 IP 分组(IP packet)，你可以将该协议对应的编号放入下一个首部(next header)字段。这些额外协议的设计者和使用者需要就哪些编号对应哪些协议达成一致，并且需要一个标准制定机构(standards body)来管理这些编号。然后，载荷(payload)可以传递给该额外协议，该协议会读取一个额外的首部(header)（在第 3 层 IPv6 首部之后、第 4 层首部之前），执行额外的处理，再将剩余的载荷传递给第 4 层(Layer 4)。"},{"en":"If the packet has no additional options, then the next header field is the same as the old protocol field, allowing the IP packet to be directly passed up to a Layer 4 protocol with no further processing.","en_html":"If the packet has no additional options, then the next header field is the same as the old protocol field, allowing the IP packet to be directly passed up to a Layer 4 protocol with no further processing.","zh_html":"如果分组(packet)没有额外的选项(options)，那么下一个首部(next header)字段与原来的协议字段(protocol field)相同，IP 分组(IP packet)可以直接向上传递给第 4 层协议(Layer 4 protocol)，无需进一步处理。"},{"en":"The idea of next headers can be generalized to allow multiple protocols to process the packet after IPv6, but before Layer 4. For example, IPv6 could have a next header for special processing. Then, the special processing protocol’s header can also contain a next header field, which either specifies a Layer 4 protocol, or yet another special processing protocol. This approach is future-proof, because it supports future protocols that haven’t been invented yet. Those future protocols can be added in this next-header approach, without breaking IPv6 or requiring an update to IPv6.","en_html":"The idea of next headers can be generalized to allow multiple protocols to process the packet after IPv6, but before Layer 4. For example, IPv6 could have a next header for special processing. Then, the special processing protocol's header can also contain a next header field, which either specifies a Layer 4 protocol, or yet another special processing protocol. This approach is future-proof, because it supports future protocols that haven't been invented yet. Those future protocols can be added in this next-header approach, without breaking IPv6 or requiring an update to IPv6.","zh_html":"下一个首部(next header)的思想可以被推广，允许多个协议(protocols)在 IPv6 之后、第 4 层(Layer 4)之前处理分组(packet)。例如，IPv6 可以有一个用于特殊处理的 next header。然后，特殊处理协议的 header 也可以包含一个 next header 字段，它要么指定一个第 4 层协议，要么指定另一个特殊处理协议。这种方法是面向未来的(future-proof)，因为它支持尚未被发明的未来协议。这些未来协议可以通过这种 next header 方式添加，而不会破坏 IPv6 或需要对 IPv6 进行更新。"},{"en":"IPv6 adds a flow label field to the header. At layer 3, packets are sent independently (how one packet is sent doesn’t affect other packets), but in practice, it’s common for many packets to be related in some way. For example, in a video stream between two hosts, there can be many packets being sent between the same two applications. Layer 3 is supposed to treat these packets separately, but in practice, routers have added more advanced systems called middleboxes (e.g. firewalls, intrusion detection systems) that might care about the fact that these packets are part of the same flow, or connection. For example, a firewall might need to read multiple packets from a connection to decide whether that connection should be allowed or blocked. When all packets are sent independently, these middleboxes have to guess whether two packets are related or not (e.g. it notices packets with the same source/destination IP address). IPv6 adds an explicit way to denote that multiple packets are related.","en_html":"IPv6 adds a <strong>flow label</strong> field to the header. At layer 3, packets are sent independently (how one packet is sent doesn't affect other packets), but in practice, it's common for many packets to be related in some way. For example, in a video stream between two hosts, there can be many packets being sent between the same two applications. Layer 3 is supposed to treat these packets separately, but in practice, routers have added more advanced systems called <strong>middleboxes</strong> (e.g. firewalls, intrusion detection systems) that might care about the fact that these packets are part of the same flow, or connection. For example, a firewall might need to read multiple packets from a connection to decide whether that connection should be allowed or blocked. When all packets are sent independently, these middleboxes have to guess whether two packets are related or not (e.g. it notices packets with the same source/destination IP address). IPv6 adds an explicit way to denote that multiple packets are related.","zh_html":"IPv6 在首部(header)中添加了一个<strong>流标签(flow label)</strong>字段。在第 3 层(layer 3)，分组(packets)是独立发送的（一个分组的发送方式不影响其他分组），但在实践中，许多分组通常会以某种方式相互关联。例如，在两端主机(hosts)之间的视频流(video stream)中，同一对应用之间可能会发送大量分组。第 3 层应该分别处理这些分组，但在实践中，路由器(routers)添加了名为<strong>中间盒(middleboxes)</strong>的更高级系统，例如防火墙(firewalls)、入侵检测系统(intrusion detection systems)，它们可能关心这些分组属于同一个流(flow)或连接(connection)。例如，防火墙可能需要读取一个连接中的多个分组，以决定该连接是否应该被允许或阻止。当所有分组独立发送时，这些中间盒必须猜测两个分组是否相关，例如通过注意到相同源/目的 IP 地址的分组。IPv6 增加了一种显式方式来表示多个分组是相关的。"},{"en":"The version number is unchanged between IPv4 and IPv6. The packet length is unchanged (though renamed from Total Length to Payload Length). TTL is renamed to Hop Limit, though the functionality is unchanged.","en_html":"The version number is unchanged between IPv4 and IPv6. The packet length is unchanged (though renamed from Total Length to Payload Length). TTL is renamed to Hop Limit, though the functionality is unchanged.","zh_html":"版本号(version number)在 IPv4 和 IPv6 之间保持不变。分组长度(packet length)保持不变（尽管从总长度(Total Length)重命名为有效载荷长度(Payload Length)）。TTL 重命名为跳数限制(Hop Limit)，但功能保持不变。"},{"en":"The Type of Service bits are renamed to Traffic Class, and can still be used to implement some notion of packet priority.","en_html":"The Type of Service bits are renamed to Traffic Class, and can still be used to implement some notion of packet priority.","zh_html":"服务类型(Type of Service)位被重命名为流量类别(Traffic Class)，仍然可以用于实现某种分组优先级(packet priority)的概念。"},{"en":"In general, IPv6 embraces the end-to-end principle and asks the end host to do the work (fragmentation, verifying checksum and re-sending corrupt packets) when possible. Some fields, like the hop limit or TTL, are fundamentally an IP-level problem, and can’t be implemented by end hosts. (How would the end host help with a packet looping through the network?)","en_html":"In general, IPv6 embraces the end-to-end principle and asks the end host to do the work (fragmentation, verifying checksum and re-sending corrupt packets) when possible. Some fields, like the hop limit or TTL, are fundamentally an IP-level problem, and can't be implemented by end hosts. (How would the end host help with a packet looping through the network?)","zh_html":"总的来说，IPv6 采纳了端到端原则(end-to-end principle)，并且尽可能要求端主机(end host)来完成工作，例如分片(fragmentation)、验证校验和(checksum)以及重新发送损坏的分组。某些字段，如跳数限制(Hop Limit)或 TTL，本质上是 IP 级别的问题，无法由端主机实现。（端主机如何帮助处理在网络中循环的分组呢？）"},{"en":"IPv6 also tries to simplify the header (removing variable-length options), while still allowing extensibility for future improvements (next-header approach, flow label).","en_html":"IPv6 also tries to simplify the header (removing variable-length options), while still allowing extensibility for future improvements (next-header approach, flow label).","zh_html":"IPv6 还试图简化首部(header)（移除可变长度选项(variable-length options)），同时仍然允许为未来改进提供扩展性(extensibility)，即 next-header 方式和流标签(flow label)。"},{"en":"IP Header Security","en_html":"<a href=\"#ip-header-security\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-header-security\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP Header Security","zh_html":"<a href=\"#ip-header-security\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ip-header-security\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> IP 首部安全(IP header security)"},{"en":"IP does not have any built-in security against attackers. An attacker could send a packet with an incorrect source IP address, allowing the attacker to impersonate somebody else. This might cause the impersonated host to be wrongly blamed for a packet. Or, if the attacker sends a spoofed packet, the reply may be sent to the impersonated host. Lying about the source address is known as IP spoofing.","en_html":"IP does not have any built-in security against attackers. An attacker could send a packet with an incorrect source IP address, allowing the attacker to impersonate somebody else. This might cause the impersonated host to be wrongly blamed for a packet. Or, if the attacker sends a spoofed packet, the reply may be sent to the impersonated host. Lying about the source address is known as <strong>IP spoofing</strong>.","zh_html":"IP 没有任何针对攻击者(attackers)的内置安全性。攻击者可以发送带有不正确的源 IP 地址(source IP address)的分组(packet)，从而冒充他人。这可能导致被冒充的主机(host)因该分组而受到错误追究。或者，如果攻击者发送了伪造(spoofed)分组，回复可能会被发送到被冒充的主机。关于源地址的谎报称为<strong>IP 欺骗(IP spoofing)</strong>。"},{"en":"IP spoofing can be used for denial-of-service (DoS) attacks. A DoS attack can be used to overwhelm a server and cause it to crash by flooding the server with packets. If all the packets came from the same sender, the server could stop the attack by ignoring packets from the attacker’s IP address. However, if the attacker lies about the source IP address, the server has a harder time distinguishing attacker traffic from legitimate traffic.","en_html":"IP spoofing can be used for denial-of-service (DoS) attacks. A DoS attack can be used to overwhelm a server and cause it to crash by flooding the server with packets. If all the packets came from the same sender, the server could stop the attack by ignoring packets from the attacker's IP address. However, if the attacker lies about the source IP address, the server has a harder time distinguishing attacker traffic from legitimate traffic.","zh_html":"IP 欺骗(IP spoofing)可用于拒绝服务(DoS)攻击。DoS 攻击可以通过用大量分组(packets)淹没服务器(server)使其不堪重负并崩溃。如果所有分组来自同一个发送方，服务器可以通过忽略来自攻击者 IP 地址的分组来阻止攻击。然而，如果攻击者谎报源 IP 地址(source IP address)，服务器就更难区分攻击者流量(attacker traffic)和合法流量(legitimate traffic)。"},{"en":"More sophisticated attacks involving spoofing exist, though we won’t cover them in detail in this class (see the UC Berkeley CS 161 notes for more details).","en_html":"More sophisticated attacks involving spoofing exist, though we won't cover them in detail in this class (see the UC Berkeley CS 161 notes for more details).","zh_html":"还存在更复杂的涉及欺骗(spoofing)的攻击，不过本课程不会详细讨论（更多细节请参阅 UC Berkeley CS 161 的笔记）。"},{"en":"The ToS field in the IP header allows the sender to set a priority on their packets. If we allow everybody to set their own priority, malicious users can set higher priorities and trick the network into prioritizing attacker traffic.","en_html":"The ToS field in the IP header allows the sender to set a priority on their packets. If we allow everybody to set their own priority, malicious users can set higher priorities and trick the network into prioritizing attacker traffic.","zh_html":"IP 首部(IP header)中的 ToS 字段允许发送方为其分组(packets)设置优先级(priority)。如果我们允许每个人设置自己的优先级，恶意用户可以设置更高的优先级，诱使网络优先处理攻击者流量(attacker traffic)。"},{"en":"If the network charges an extra fee for high-priority traffic, the attacker could send a spoofed high-priority packet, and the impersonated host would have to pay for the attacker’s traffic.","en_html":"If the network charges an extra fee for high-priority traffic, the attacker could send a spoofed high-priority packet, and the impersonated host would have to pay for the attacker's traffic.","zh_html":"如果网络对高优先级流量(high-priority traffic)收取额外费用，攻击者可以发送伪造的(spoofed)高优先级分组(packet)，而被冒充的主机(host)将不得不为攻击者的流量付费。"},{"en":"The original Internet design did not stop these attacks, though modern ISPs (Internet service providers) have implemented additional security measures to mitigate IP layer attacks. In the modern Internet, ISPs don’t allow end hosts to set the ToS field, and many ISPs have tools to detect and block spoofed packets.","en_html":"The original Internet design did not stop these attacks, though modern ISPs (Internet service providers) have implemented additional security measures to mitigate IP layer attacks. In the modern Internet, ISPs don't allow end hosts to set the ToS field, and many ISPs have tools to detect and block spoofed packets.","zh_html":"最初的互联网(Internet)设计并未阻止这些攻击，不过现代 ISP(Internet service providers，互联网服务提供商)已经实施了额外的安全措施来缓解 IP 层攻击。在现代互联网中，ISP 不允许端主机(end hosts)设置 ToS 字段，并且许多 ISP 拥有检测和阻止伪造(spoofed)分组的工具。"},{"en":"In IPv4, attackers could intentionally send large packets, forcing routers to perform extra work fragmenting those packets. Or, attackers could intentionally add extra options, forcing routers to process those extra options. This could be used to perform DoS attacks and overwhelm a router’s processing capacity.","en_html":"In IPv4, attackers could intentionally send large packets, forcing routers to perform extra work fragmenting those packets. Or, attackers could intentionally add extra options, forcing routers to process those extra options. This could be used to perform DoS attacks and overwhelm a router's processing capacity.","zh_html":"在 IPv4 中，攻击者可以故意发送大分组(packets)，强制路由器(routers)额外执行分片(fragmenting)操作。或者，攻击者可以故意添加额外的选项(options)，强制路由器处理这些额外选项。这可用于执行 DoS 攻击，压垮路由器的处理能力。"},{"en":"The TTL field can be exploited to learn about the network topology. You could send a packet with TTL 1. The packet will expire at the first hop, and the first router will send you an error message, allowing you to learn the identity of the first router.","en_html":"The TTL field can be exploited to learn about the network topology. You could send a packet with TTL 1. The packet will expire at the first hop, and the first router will send you an error message, allowing you to learn the identity of the first router.","zh_html":"TTL 字段可以被利用来了解网络拓扑(network topology)。你可以发送一个 TTL 为 1 的分组(packet)。该分组会在第一跳(first hop)过期，第一台路由器(router)会向你发送一条错误消息，让你得知第一台路由器的身份。"},{"en":"Then, you can send a packet with TTL 2, which will expire at the second hop. The second router will send you an error message, allowing you to also discover the second router.","en_html":"Then, you can send a packet with TTL 2, which will expire at the second hop. The second router will send you an error message, allowing you to also discover the second router.","zh_html":"然后，你可以发送一个 TTL 为 2 的分组(packet)，它会在第二跳(second hop)过期。第二台路由器(router)会向你发送一条错误消息，让你也能发现第二台路由器。"},{"en":"By repeating this with TTL 3, TTL 4, and so on, you can discover all the routers on your path. This attack is known as traceroute, though others argue that it’s not an attack and is useful for diagnostics.","en_html":"By repeating this with TTL 3, TTL 4, and so on, you can discover all the routers on your path. This attack is known as <strong>traceroute</strong>, though others argue that it's not an attack and is useful for diagnostics.","zh_html":"通过用 TTL 3、TTL 4 等重复这个过程，你可以发现路径上的所有路由器(routers)。这种攻击被称为<strong>路由追踪(traceroute)</strong>，尽管也有人认为这不是攻击，而是有用的诊断工具。"},{"en":"Repeating this attack on different sources and destinations allows you to learn more of the network topology. Some routers do not send an error message when the TTL is exceeded, which might limit this exploit.","en_html":"Repeating this attack on different sources and destinations allows you to learn more of the network topology. Some routers do not send an error message when the TTL is exceeded, which might limit this exploit.","zh_html":"对不同的源和目的地重复这种攻击，可以让你了解更多的网络拓扑(network topology)。有些路由器(router)在 TTL 超限时不会发送错误消息，这可能会限制这种利用(exploit)方式。"},{"en":"An attacker could theoretically tamper with the protocol or checksum field, but this would likely cause the packet to be dropped because of an invalid protocol or checksum, so practical attacks with these two fields don’t really exist.","en_html":"An attacker could theoretically tamper with the protocol or checksum field, but this would likely cause the packet to be dropped because of an invalid protocol or checksum, so practical attacks with these two fields don't really exist.","zh_html":"理论上，攻击者可以篡改协议(protocol)或校验和(checksum)字段，但这很可能会导致分组(packet)因为无效的协议或校验和而被丢弃，因此针对这两个字段的实际攻击并不存在。"}]},"/routing/link-state.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Link-State Protocols","zh_html":"链路状态协议(link-state protocols)"},{"en":"Introduction to Link-State Protocols","zh_html":"链路状态协议(link-state protocols)简介"},{"en":"Recall that there are different classes of routing protocols, depending on their underlying algorithm. In the previous section, we saw the distance-vector class of protocols. In this section, we’ll discuss link-state, another major class of protocols.","zh_html":"回想一下，路由协议(routing protocols)可以根据其底层算法(algorithm)分为不同类别。上一节中，我们看到了距离向量(distance-vector)这一类协议。本节会讨论链路状态(link-state)，这是另一类主要协议。"},{"en":"Recall that protocols can also be classified as exterior gateway protocols (operating between networks) and interior gateway protocols (operating within networks). Like distance-vector, link-state protocols are usually interior gateway protocols.","zh_html":"回想一下，协议(protocols)也可以分为外部网关协议(exterior gateway protocols，在网络之间运行)和内部网关协议(interior gateway protocols，在网络内部运行)。和距离向量(distance-vector)一样，链路状态协议(link-state protocols)通常也是内部网关协议。"},{"en":"IS-IS (Intermediate System to Intermediate System) and OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) are two major examples of link-state protocols. Both are widely deployed today.","zh_html":"IS-IS(Intermediate System to Intermediate System) 和 OSPF(Open Shortest Path First) 是链路状态协议(link-state protocols)的两个主要例子。两者今天都被广泛部署。"},{"en":"Link-State Overview","zh_html":"链路状态(link-state)概览"},{"en":"Distance-vector performed a distributed, cooperative computation. Each node computes its own piece of the solution, based on results computed by its neighbors. The computation across all nodes collectively forms the full solution. Each node only needs local information from its neighbors in the computation (nodes don’t know the full network graph).","zh_html":"距离向量(distance-vector)执行的是一种分布式(distributed)、协作式(cooperative)计算。每个节点(node)基于其邻居(neighbors)计算出的结果，计算自己负责的那一部分解。所有节点上的计算共同形成完整解。计算过程中，每个节点只需要来自邻居的局部信息(local information)，节点并不知道完整的网络图(network graph)。"},{"en":"By contrast, link-state protocols perform a local computation. Each node computes the full solution independently and from scratch, without using any computation results from neighbors. However, to do this, each node needs global information from all parts of the network.","zh_html":"相比之下，链路状态协议(link-state protocols)执行的是局部计算(local computation)。每个节点独立地、从头开始计算完整解，不使用邻居的任何计算结果。不过，为了做到这一点，每个节点都需要来自网络各处的全局信息(global information)。"},{"en":"Link-state protocols in one sentence: Every router learns the full network graph, and then runs shortest-paths on the graph to populate the forwarding table.","zh_html":"用一句话概括链路状态协议(link-state protocols)：每台路由器(router)学习完整的网络图(network graph)，然后在图上运行最短路径(shortest-paths)算法来填充转发表(forwarding table)。"},{"en":"There are two major steps that we have to implement. First, the router needs to somehow learn the full network graph, including the state of every link (up or down), the cost of every link, and the location of every destination.","zh_html":"我们必须实现两个主要步骤。首先，路由器(router)需要以某种方式学习完整的网络图(network graph)，包括每条链路(link)的状态（正常或断开）、每条链路的成本(cost)，以及每个目的地(destination)的位置。"},{"en":"Then, the router needs to run some algorithm on that graph to learn how to forward packets to every destination.","zh_html":"然后，路由器(router)需要在该图(graph)上运行某种算法(algorithm)，学习如何把分组(packets)转发到每个目的地(destination)。"},{"en":"We’ll think about the second step first (shortest paths), then think about the first step (learning the graph).","zh_html":"我们会先思考第二步，也就是最短路径(shortest paths)，再思考第一步，也就是学习图(graph)。"},{"en":"Computing Paths","zh_html":"计算路径(computing paths)"},{"en":"Once the router has a global view of the network, it can easily compute paths through the network using some shortest-path algorithm.","zh_html":"一旦路由器(router)拥有网络的全局视图(global view)，就可以使用某种最短路径算法(shortest-path algorithm)，轻松计算穿过网络的路径(paths)。"},{"en":"In particular, the router should compute the shortest path to every destination. Then, for each destination, the router records the next hop along the shortest path, just like in distance-vector protocols. The rest of the path is not needed during forwarding.","zh_html":"特别是，路由器(router)应该计算到每个目的地(destination)的最短路径(shortest path)。然后，对每个目的地，路由器记录沿最短路径前进的下一跳(next hop)，就像在距离向量协议(distance-vector protocols)中一样。转发(forwarding)时不需要路径的其余部分。"},{"en":"Many single-source shortest-path algorithms can be used in this step. For example, the Bellman-Ford algorithm (serial version, with none of the distance-vector changes) and Dijkstra’s algorithm both efficiently compute the shortest path from a single source to all destinations. We could also consider alternate solutions like breadth-first search, or algorithms that can run in parallel.","zh_html":"这一步可以使用许多单源最短路径(single-source shortest-path)算法。例如，Bellman-Ford 算法（串行版本，不带任何距离向量修改）和 Dijkstra 算法都能高效计算从单一源(source)到所有目的地(destinations)的最短路径。我们也可以考虑其他解法，例如广度优先搜索(breadth-first search)，或可以并行运行的算法。"},{"en":"One thing we have to be careful about is inconsistencies between routers.","zh_html":"有一件事需要小心：路由器(routers)之间可能不一致(inconsistencies)。"},{"en":"Remember, every router is computing the shortest paths independently, and deciding on a next hop accordingly. Each router only controls its own next hop, and cannot influence what the next hop will do.","zh_html":"记住，每台路由器(router)都在独立计算最短路径(shortest paths)，并相应决定下一跳(next hop)。每台路由器只控制自己的下一跳，无法影响下一跳接下来会做什么。"},{"en":"For example, suppose R3 computes this shortest path to A, and decides to forward packets to R2. Then, R2 computes this shortest path to A, and decides to forward packets to R3. Both routers computed valid shortest paths, but their decisions resulted in a routing loop.","zh_html":"例如，假设 R3 计算出到 A 的这条最短路径(shortest path)，并决定把分组(packets)转发给 R2。随后，R2 也计算出到 A 的这条最短路径，并决定把分组转发给 R3。两台路由器都计算出了有效的最短路径，但它们的决策造成了路由环路(routing loop)。"},{"en":"To avoid this problem, we have to make sure that all routers are producing forwarding decisions that are compatible with each other. What are the requirements for all routers to produce compatible decisions?","zh_html":"为了避免这个问题，我们必须确保所有路由器(routers)产生的转发决策(forwarding decisions)彼此兼容(compatible)。所有路由器要产生兼容决策，需要满足哪些要求？"},{"en":"All routers have to agree on the network topology. Suppose a link failed, but only one router knows about it. Then different routers are computing paths on totally different graphs, and might produce inconsistent results.","zh_html":"所有路由器(routers)都必须对网络拓扑(network topology)达成一致。假设一条链路(link)故障了，但只有一台路由器知道。那么不同路由器会在完全不同的图(graphs)上计算路径(paths)，可能产生不一致的结果。"},{"en":"All routers are finding least-cost paths through the path. If one router preferred more expensive paths for some reason, we would get inconsistent results.","zh_html":"所有路由器(routers)都在寻找经过网络的最低成本路径(least-cost paths)。如果某台路由器出于某种原因偏好成本更高的路径，就会得到不一致的结果。"},{"en":"All costs are positive. Negative costs could produce negative-weight cycles.","zh_html":"所有成本(costs)都是正的。负成本(negative costs)可能产生负权重环(negative-weight cycles)。"},{"en":"All routers use the same tiebreaking rules. If we assumed shortest paths are unique, then the previous two conditions are sufficient to ensure everybody picks the same path. This condition additionally ensures that if there are multiple paths tied as the shortest, everyone chooses the same one.","zh_html":"所有路由器(routers)使用相同的平局打破规则(tiebreaking rules)。如果我们假设最短路径(shortest paths)是唯一的，那么前两个条件足以确保每个人选择同一路径。这个条件进一步确保：如果存在多条并列最短的路径，所有人都会选择同一条。"},{"en":"With these four conditions, routers could use different shortest-path algorithms, and they would still all compute the same paths and produce compatible decisions. In practice, though, routers usually all use the same algorithm for simplicity.","zh_html":"在这四个条件下，路由器(routers)即使用不同的最短路径算法(shortest-path algorithms)，也仍会计算出相同路径(paths)，并产生兼容的决策。不过在实践中，为简单起见，路由器通常都使用同一种算法。"},{"en":"Learning About Graph Topology","zh_html":"学习图拓扑(graph topology)"},{"en":"How do routers learn about the full network graph? First, we need to learn who our neighbors are (both routers and destinations). Then, we need to distribute that information through the whole network. We also need routers to glue together all the information it receives into a graph topology.","zh_html":"路由器(routers)如何学习完整的网络图(network graph)？首先，我们需要知道自己的邻居(neighbors)是谁，包括路由器和目的地(destinations)。然后，需要把这些信息分发到整个网络。我们还需要路由器把它接收到的所有信息拼接成一个图拓扑(graph topology)。"},{"en":"To discover neighbors, every router sends a hello message to all of its neighbors.","zh_html":"为了发现邻居(neighbors)，每台路由器(router)都会向自己的所有邻居发送 hello 消息。"},{"en":"For example, in this network, R2 sends to both of its neighbors: “Hello, I’m R2.” Now, R1 knows that it’s connected to R2, and R3 also knows that it’s connected to R2. Similarly, R1 says hello to R2, so now R2 knows about R1. Likewise, R3 says hello to R2, so R2 also knows about R3.","zh_html":"例如，在这个网络中，R2 向自己的两个邻居都发送：“Hello, I’m R2.” 现在，R1 知道自己连接到 R2，R3 也知道自己连接到 R2。类似地，R1 向 R2 说 hello，于是 R2 现在知道 R1。同样，R3 向 R2 说 hello，于是 R2 也知道 R3。"},{"en":"As a result, everybody now knows who their immediate neighbors are. Note that R1 does not know about R3, because R1 and R3 are not neighbors.","zh_html":"因此，现在每个人都知道自己的直接邻居(immediate neighbors)是谁。注意，R1 不知道 R3，因为 R1 和 R3 不是邻居(neighbors)。"},{"en":"We also want to know if links go down. To support this, we’ll periodically re-send the hello message. If a neighbor stops saying hello (e.g. misses some number of hellos), we assume they disappeared.","zh_html":"我们也想知道链路(links)是否断开。为了支持这一点，我们会周期性地重新发送 hello 消息。如果某个邻居停止说 hello，例如漏掉若干次 hello，我们就假设它消失了。"},{"en":"Now that we know about our neighbors, we should announce that fact to everybody. To make a global announcement, we send the announcement to all of our neighbors. Also, if we ever receive an announcement, we should send it to all of our neighbors as well. This ensures that every message gets propagated throughout the network. This is known as flooding information across the network. If any information changes (e.g. a neighbor disappears), we should flood that information as well.","zh_html":"既然我们已经知道自己的邻居(neighbors)，就应该把这个事实通告给所有人。为了进行全局通告(global announcement)，我们会把通告发送给自己的所有邻居。此外，如果我们收到一条通告，也应该把它发送给自己的所有邻居。这确保每条消息都能传播到整个网络。这称为在网络中<strong>泛洪(flooding)</strong>信息。如果任何信息发生变化，例如某个邻居消失，我们也应该泛洪这条信息。"},{"en":"We also need to make sure that messages don’t get dropped. Otherwise, other routers might miss an update and compute paths on the wrong graph. To fix this problem, we use the same trick as we used in distance-vector, and periodically re-send the message. As long as the link is functioning, our message should get sent after enough tries.","zh_html":"我们还需要确保消息不会被丢弃。否则，其他路由器(routers)可能错过一次更新(update)，并在错误的图(graph)上计算路径(paths)。为了解决这个问题，我们使用和距离向量(distance-vector)中相同的技巧：周期性地重新发送消息。只要链路(link)正常工作，经过足够多次尝试后，我们的消息就应该能被发送出去。"},{"en":"Avoiding Infinite Flooding","zh_html":"避免无限泛洪(avoiding infinite flooding)"},{"en":"We have to be careful about how we flood announcements through the network.","zh_html":"我们必须小心如何在网络中泛洪(flood)通告(announcements)。"},{"en":"R2 learns some information and announces it to its neighbor R3. When R3 receives this information, it makes an announcement to its neighbor R2. When R2 receives this information, it makes an announcement to its neighbor R3. These two routers are stuck making announcements to each other, wasting bandwidth, even though there’s no new information.","zh_html":"R2 学到一些信息，并把它通告给邻居 R3。当 R3 收到这条信息时，它向邻居 R2 发出通告。当 R2 收到这条信息时，又向邻居 R3 发出通告。这两台路由器会困在彼此发送通告的过程中，即使没有任何新信息，也会浪费带宽(bandwidth)。"},{"en":"Note that this is not the same as periodically re-sending messages for reliability. For reliability, we might re-send a message once every 5 seconds. In this infinite loop, the routers are receiving and re-sending duplicate announcements at maximum rate (e.g. millions of times per second).","zh_html":"注意，这不同于为了可靠性(reliability)而周期性地重新发送消息。为了可靠性，我们可能每 5 秒重新发送一次消息。在这个无限循环(infinite loop)中，路由器会以最大速率接收并重新发送重复通告(duplicate announcements)，例如每秒数百万次。"},{"en":"The problem is even worse if our network contains a loop:","zh_html":"如果我们的网络包含环路(loop)，问题会更严重："},{"en":"Time step 1: R1 broadcasts to R2 and R3.","zh_html":"时间步 1：R1 向 R2 和 R3 广播(broadcasts)。"},{"en":"Time step 2: R2 broadcasts to R1 and R3. R3 broadcasts to R1 and R2.","zh_html":"时间步 2：R2 向 R1 和 R3 广播。R3 向 R1 和 R2 广播。"},{"en":"Time step 3: R1, R1, R2, and R3 all make broadcasts to (R2, R3), (R2, R3), (R1, R3), and (R1, R2) respectively. Note that R1 received two messages at time step 2, so it makes two broadcasts.","zh_html":"时间步 3：R1、R1、R2 和 R3 分别向 (R2, R3)、(R2, R3)、(R1, R3) 和 (R1, R2) 广播。注意，R1 在时间步 2 收到了两条消息，因此它会进行两次广播。"},{"en":"Time step 4: R1, R1, R2, R2, R2, R3, R3, R3 all make broadcasts to (R2, R3), (R2, R3), (R1, R3), (R1, R3), (R1, R3), (R1, R2), (R1, R2), (R1, R2), respectively.","zh_html":"时间步 4：R1、R1、R2、R2、R2、R3、R3、R3 分别向 (R2, R3)、(R2, R3)、(R1, R3)、(R1, R3)、(R1, R3)、(R1, R2)、(R1, R2)、(R1, R2) 广播。"},{"en":"Time step 5: R1 makes 6 broadcasts, R2 makes 5 broadcasts, R3 makes 5 broadcasts.","zh_html":"时间步 5：R1 进行 6 次广播，R2 进行 5 次广播，R3 进行 5 次广播。"},{"en":"All the new information was learned at time step 1. But, everybody keeps re-sending the same information, and duplicate announcements multiply exponentially and eventually overwhelm the network.","zh_html":"所有新信息都已经在时间步 1 学到了。但是，每个人都会不断重新发送同一信息，重复通告(duplicate announcements)会指数级增长，并最终压垮网络。"},{"en":"To fix this problem, we need to make sure that routers don’t send the same information twice.","zh_html":"为了解决这个问题，我们需要确保路由器(routers)不会把同一信息发送两次。"},{"en":"When we see a message for the first time, send that message to all neighbors, and write down that we’ve seen that message. (We have to write down this message anyway, since we’re trying to use this information to build up the network graph.) Then, if we ever see that same message again, don’t send it a second time.","zh_html":"当我们第一次看到一条消息时，把这条消息发送给所有邻居(neighbors)，并记下我们已经看过这条消息。（无论如何我们都必须记下这条消息，因为我们正试图用这些信息构建网络图(network graph)。）然后，如果之后再次看到同一条消息，就不要第二次发送。"},{"en":"To uniquely identify a message, we can introduce a timestamp (or some other counter that’s unique to every message).","zh_html":"为了唯一标识一条消息，我们可以引入时间戳(timestamp)，或某种对每条消息唯一的其他计数器(counter)。"},{"en":"Now, if we go back to the example from earlier:","zh_html":"现在，如果回到前面的例子："},{"en":"Time step 1: R1 broadcasts to R2 and R3.","zh_html":"时间步 1：R1 向 R2 和 R3 广播(broadcasts)。"},{"en":"Time step 2: R2 broadcasts to R1 and R3. R3 broadcasts to R1 and R2.","zh_html":"时间步 2：R2 向 R1 和 R3 广播。R3 向 R1 和 R2 广播。"},{"en":"Time step 3: At this point, R1, R2, and R3 have all seen the message before, so they don’t send it again. No further duplicate messages are sent.","zh_html":"时间步 3：此时，R1、R2 和 R3 都已经见过这条消息，所以它们不会再次发送。不会再发送更多重复消息。"},{"en":"Note that duplicate messages are still sometimes sent with this modification, but we’ve avoided duplicate messages being sent infinitely.","zh_html":"注意，采用这个修改后，重复消息(duplicate messages)有时仍然会被发送，但我们已经避免了重复消息被无限发送。"},{"en":"Convergence","zh_html":"收敛(convergence)"},{"en":"Link-state converges on a valid least-cost routing state after every router learns the full network topology and computes its forwarding table accordingly. Convergence relies on every node using the same graph. After convergence, the routing state remains valid as long as the network topology doesn’t change.","zh_html":"在每台路由器(router)都学习到完整网络拓扑(network topology)，并据此计算其转发表(forwarding table)后，链路状态(link-state)会收敛(converge)到一个有效的最低成本路由状态(valid least-cost routing state)。收敛依赖每个节点(node)使用同一个图(graph)。收敛之后，只要网络拓扑不变，路由状态(routing state)就会保持有效。"},{"en":"As soon as the network topology changes, it can take some time for the network to converge again. We have to wait for the change to be detected (e.g. a link failure). Then, we have to wait for the new information to be propagated through the network, and for routers to re-compute forwarding table entries. While the network is converging, we might be in an invalid routing state, because some routers are using the old graph, while others are using the updated graph. The routing state could have dead-ends, loops, or paths that are not least-cost.","zh_html":"一旦网络拓扑(network topology)发生变化，网络可能需要一些时间才能重新收敛(converge)。我们必须等待变化被检测到，例如链路故障(link failure)。然后，还必须等待新信息传播到整个网络，并等待路由器重新计算转发表(forwarding table)条目。在网络收敛期间，我们可能处于无效路由状态(invalid routing state)，因为一些路由器使用旧图(old graph)，另一些路由器使用更新后的图(updated graph)。路由状态可能存在死路(dead-ends)、环路(loops)，或不是最低成本的路径(paths)。"},{"en":"For example, suppose the R3-A link has failed. R3 knows about this, but the other routers do not. R3 will forward packets to R1. However, R1 will still forward packets to R3.","zh_html":"例如，假设 R3-A 链路(link)已经故障。R3 知道这一点，但其他路由器不知道。R3 会把分组(packets)转发给 R1。然而，R1 仍会把分组转发给 R3。"},{"en":"Much of the complexity in link-state protocols is in the small details. To ensure faster convergence and avoid invalid routing as much as possible, we can make minor optimizations and adjustments in the protocol.","zh_html":"链路状态协议(link-state protocols)的许多复杂性都在小细节中。为了确保更快收敛(convergence)，并尽可能避免无效路由(invalid routing)，我们可以在协议(protocol)中做一些小优化和调整。"},{"en":"Link-State vs. Distance-Vector","zh_html":"链路状态(link-state)与距离向量(distance-vector)"},{"en":"What are some pros and cons of link-state protocols compared to distance-vector protocols?","zh_html":"与距离向量协议(distance-vector protocols)相比，链路状态协议(link-state protocols)有哪些优缺点？"},{"en":"In distance-vector, when we receive an announcement, we don’t necessarily know all the details about the path we’re accepting. We have to trust whatever our neighbor claims in the announcement. By contrast, in link-state, we know the full topology of the graph, so we know more about the paths that packets are taking.","zh_html":"在距离向量(distance-vector)中，当我们收到一条通告(announcement)时，并不一定知道自己将接受的路径(path)的所有细节。我们必须信任邻居(neighbor)在通告中的声称。相比之下，在链路状态(link-state)中，我们知道图(graph)的完整拓扑(topology)，因此更了解分组(packets)正在经过的路径。"},{"en":"Depending on implementation, distance-vector could be slower to converge. If the network changes, we have to wait for our neighbor to recompute and readvertise a path, before we can update our forwarding table. Then, all of our neighbors have to wait for us, and so on. By contrast, in link-state, everybody can quickly flood the new information and recompute at the same time.","zh_html":"根据实现方式，距离向量(distance-vector)可能收敛(converge)得更慢。如果网络发生变化，我们必须等待邻居(neighbor)重新计算并重新通告(readvertise)一条路径，才能更新自己的转发表(forwarding table)。然后，我们的所有邻居又必须等待我们，如此类推。相比之下，在链路状态(link-state)中，所有人都可以快速泛洪(flood)新信息，并同时重新计算。"},{"en":"Link-state protocols are good for small local networks, but don’t scale well to the global Internet. In particular, link-state requires every router to know about the entire network. On the global Internet, operators might not want to reveal their network topology (e.g. where their routers are located, the bandwidth of their links) to competitors.","zh_html":"链路状态协议(link-state protocols)适合小型本地网络(local networks)，但不能很好地扩展到全球互联网(global Internet)。特别是，链路状态要求每台路由器(router)了解整个网络。在全球互联网中，运营方(operators)可能不想向竞争对手透露自己的网络拓扑(network topology)，例如路由器所在位置、链路带宽等。"},{"en":"In practice, most networks deploy a combination of distance-vector and link-state protocols.","zh_html":"在实践中，大多数网络会部署距离向量(distance-vector)和链路状态(link-state)协议的组合。"}]},"/routing/model.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Model for Intra-Domain Routing","zh_html":"域内路由(intra-domain routing)模型"},{"en":"Modeling the Network as a Graph","zh_html":"将网络建模为图(graph)"},{"en":"Let’s create a simplified model of the Internet to help us formally define the routing problem.","zh_html":"让我们创建一个简化的互联网(Internet)模型(model)，帮助我们形式化地定义路由(routing)问题。"},{"en":"Recall from the previous unit that we can think of the Internet as a set of machines, connected together with a set of links, where each link connects two of the machines on the network.","zh_html":"回想上一单元，我们可以把互联网(Internet)看作一组机器(machine)，它们由一组链路(links)连接在一起，其中每条链路连接网络中的两台机器。"},{"en":"We can represent the network topology as a graph, where each node represents a machine, and each edge between two nodes represents a link between two machines.","zh_html":"我们可以把网络拓扑(network topology)表示成一个图(graph)：每个节点(node)表示一台机器，两个节点之间的每条边(edge)表示两台机器之间的一条链路(link)。"},{"en":"Historically, sometimes links could connect more than two machines, but in modern networks, links essentially always connect exactly two machines.","zh_html":"从历史上看，链路(link)有时可以连接两台以上的机器，但在现代网络中，链路本质上几乎总是恰好连接两台机器。"},{"en":"Full Mesh Network Topology","zh_html":"全网状网络拓扑(full mesh network topology)"},{"en":"Suppose we have two machines, A and B. If the two machines want to exchange messages, we could add a link between them.","zh_html":"假设我们有两台机器 A 和 B。如果这两台机器想交换消息，我们可以在它们之间添加一条链路(link)。"},{"en":"But what if we had five machines instead of two? One possible approach is to create a link between every pair of machines, such that every machine is connected to every other machine. This is sometimes called a full mesh topology.","zh_html":"但如果我们有五台机器而不是两台呢？一种可能的做法是在每一对机器之间创建一条链路，使每台机器都连接到其他每一台机器。这有时称为全网状拓扑(full mesh topology)。"},{"en":"What are some drawbacks of this approach?","zh_html":"这种方法有哪些缺点？"},{"en":"This approach doesn’t scale well. If we tried to scale this to the size of the modern Internet, we’d need a wire connecting every pair of computers in the world. When a new computer joins the network, we’d have create new links between that computer and every other computer in the world.","zh_html":"这种方法扩展性不好。如果我们试图把它扩展到现代互联网(Internet)的规模，就需要一根线路(wire)连接世界上每一对计算机。当一台新计算机加入网络时，我们还必须在这台计算机和世界上所有其他计算机之间创建新的链路(links)。"},{"en":"Although it can’t scale to the entire Internet, there are still some benefits to a a full mesh topology in smaller settings. In particular, having links between every pair of machines gives us a lot of bandwidth on the network. Every machine has a dedicated link to all other machines, and each pair of machines can use the full bandwidth on their dedicated link.","zh_html":"虽然全网状拓扑(full mesh topology)无法扩展到整个互联网，但在较小规模的场景中仍有一些好处。特别是，每一对机器之间都有链路会给网络带来大量带宽(bandwidth)。每台机器都有到所有其他机器的专用链路(dedicated link)，每一对机器都可以独占其专用链路上的全部带宽。"},{"en":"In general, there is no guarantee that each machine has a direct link to all other links. In other words, there is no guarantee that the underlying graph is fully-connected.","zh_html":"一般来说，并不能保证每台机器都有到所有其他机器的直接链路(direct link)。换句话说，并不能保证底层图(graph)是完全连通的(fully-connected)。"},{"en":"Single-Link Network Topology","zh_html":"单链路网络拓扑(single-link network topology)"},{"en":"In addition to the full mesh topology, there are other ways in which we can deploy links to connect up multiple machines. For example, we could use a single link to connect up all five machines:","zh_html":"除了全网状拓扑(full mesh topology)之外，我们还可以用其他方式部署链路(links)，把多台机器连接起来。例如，我们可以用一条单独的链路连接所有五台机器："},{"en":"(Here, we’re temporarily breaking the assumption that a link connects only two machines, by considering a link that connects more than two machines.)","zh_html":"（这里我们暂时打破“链路只连接两台机器”这个假设，考虑一条连接两台以上机器的链路。）"},{"en":"This approach would scale better than the full mesh topology. For example, if a new computer joined the network, instead of creating five new links between the new computer and the five existing computers, we can just extend the existing wire to the new computer.","zh_html":"这种方法会比全网状拓扑(full mesh topology)更容易扩展。例如，如果一台新计算机加入网络，我们不需要在新计算机和已有的五台计算机之间创建五条新链路，只需要把已有线路延伸到新计算机即可。"},{"en":"However, this approach is more limited in the amount of bandwidth available to the machines. In particular, there is only a single link, and all five machines need to share the bandwidth on this link.","zh_html":"不过，这种方法对机器可用带宽(bandwidth)的限制更大。特别是这里只有一条链路(link)，所有五台机器都需要共享这条链路上的带宽。"},{"en":"In order to create more sophisticated network topologies, we will need to introduce the idea of a router.","zh_html":"为了创建更复杂的网络拓扑(network topologies)，我们需要引入路由器(router)的概念。"},{"en":"Routers and Hosts","zh_html":"路由器(routers)与主机(hosts)"},{"en":"In our simplified model, we’ll classify every machine as being one of two types.","zh_html":"在我们的简化模型(model)中，会把每台机器归为两类之一。"},{"en":"End hosts are machines connecting to the Internet to send and receive data. Examples of end hosts include applications on your own personal computer, such as your web browser. Web servers, such as a Google web server receiving Google search queries and sending back search results, are also end hosts. These machines send outgoing packets to other destinations, and could be the final destination for incoming packets. However, these machines usually do not receive and forward intermediate packets (i.e. packets with some different final destination).","zh_html":"<strong>端主机(end hosts)</strong>是连接到互联网(Internet)以发送和接收数据的机器。端主机的例子包括你个人计算机上的应用程序，例如网页浏览器。Web 服务器，例如接收 Google 搜索查询并返回搜索结果的 Google Web 服务器，也属于端主机。这些机器会向其他目的地(destination)发送出站分组(packets)，也可能是入站分组的最终目的地。不过，这些机器通常不会接收并转发中间分组(intermediate packets)，也就是最终目的地不同的分组。"},{"en":"Routers, by contrast, are machines connected to the Internet responsible for receiving and forwarding intermediate packets closer to their final destination. For example, consider the router installed in your home network, or routers living in a data center building somewhere. These machines usually do not create and send new packets of their own, and they usually are not the final destination for packets. For example, in your daily Internet use, you might want to send packets to a Google web server to perform a search, but you probably don’t need to send a message directly to your home router or a data center. Those routers will help you forward your packet toward Google, but they are not the final destination of your packet.","zh_html":"相比之下，<strong>路由器(routers)</strong>是连接到互联网(Internet)的机器，负责接收中间分组(intermediate packets)并将它们继续转发，使其更接近最终目的地。例如，可以考虑你家庭网络中安装的路由器，或某个数据中心建筑里的路由器。这些机器通常不会自己创建并发送新的分组，也通常不是分组(packet)的最终目的地。例如，在日常使用互联网时，你可能想把分组发送到 Google Web 服务器以执行搜索，但你大概不需要直接向家里的路由器或某个数据中心发送消息。这些路由器会帮助你把分组转发向 Google，但它们不是你的分组的最终目的地。"},{"en":"Depending on the network design, routers could be legal destinations, but in this unit, we’ll ignore routers as destinations. However, do note that routers potentially can be sources and send new packets of their own.","zh_html":"根据网络设计，路由器(router)也可能是合法的目的地(destination)，但在本单元中，我们会忽略作为目的地的路由器。不过请注意，路由器潜在地也可以作为源(source)，发送它们自己的新分组(packets)。"},{"en":"Routers are sometimes also called switches. There are historical differences between routers and switches, but nowadays, the terms are used interchangeably. In these notes, we’ll use “router” when possible.","zh_html":"路由器(routers)有时也称为交换机(switches)。路由器和交换机在历史上有区别，但如今这两个术语常常互换使用。在这些笔记中，我们会尽量使用“router”。"},{"en":"In our graph model of the Internet, routers appear as intermediate nodes that are usually connected to multiple neighbors. End hosts appear as nodes that are usually connected to one or more routers. In practice, these assumptions aren’t always true.","zh_html":"在我们的互联网(Internet)图模型(graph model)中，路由器(routers)表现为中间节点(intermediate nodes)，通常连接到多个邻居(neighbors)。端主机(end hosts)表现为通常连接到一个或多个路由器的节点。在实践中，这些假设并不总是成立。"},{"en":"In these notes, when possible, we’ll always draw routers as squares and end hosts as circles. In practice, sometimes routers are represented by other symbols. For example, this is a common router symbol used in network diagrams:","zh_html":"在这些笔记中，只要可能，我们总是把路由器(router)画成方形，把端主机(end host)画成圆形。在实践中，路由器有时会用其他符号表示。例如，下面是网络图(network diagrams)中常见的一种路由器符号："},{"en":"Network Topologies with Routers","zh_html":"带路由器的网络拓扑(network topologies with routers)"},{"en":"Now that we have routers in addition to end hosts, we can create more complicated network topologies like this:","zh_html":"既然除了端主机(end hosts)之外还有路由器(routers)，我们就可以创建像这样的更复杂网络拓扑(network topologies)："},{"en":"This topology lets us combine the benefits of the full-mesh and single-link topologies. In particular, this topology uses fewer links than the full mesh topology from earlier. Also, this topology has more bandwidth than the single link topology from earlier.","zh_html":"这种拓扑(topology)让我们能够结合全网状(full-mesh)拓扑和单链路(single-link)拓扑的优点。特别是，这种拓扑使用的链路(links)比前面的全网状拓扑更少。同时，它的带宽(bandwidth)也比前面的单链路拓扑更多。"},{"en":"This topology is also more robust to failure. If a link goes down, the packet can take a different path through the network and still reach its destination.","zh_html":"这种拓扑(topology)对故障(failure)也更健壮(robust)。如果一条链路(link)宕掉，分组(packet)可以沿网络中的另一条路径(path)前进，仍然到达目的地(destination)。"},{"en":"End Hosts in Routing","zh_html":"路由中的端主机(end hosts in routing)"},{"en":"Note that end hosts generally do not participate in routing protocols, since they don’t forward intermediate packets. Instead, end hosts are often connected to a single router with a single link. By default, the end host sends all outgoing messages to the router, which will figure out how to send the packet to its final destination. This strategy of sending everything to the router is sometimes called the default route of the end host.","zh_html":"注意，端主机(end hosts)通常不参与路由协议(routing protocols)，因为它们不转发中间分组(intermediate packets)。相反，端主机通常通过一条单独链路连接到一个路由器(router)。默认情况下，端主机会把所有出站消息(outgoing messages)发送给该路由器，由路由器来判断如何把分组(packet)发送到最终目的地。这种把所有内容都发送给路由器的策略有时称为端主机的默认路由(default route)。"},{"en":"When designing routing protocols, we often ignore end hosts, except as destinations (since the routers need to figure out how to reach different destinations).","zh_html":"设计路由协议(routing protocols)时，我们通常会忽略端主机(end hosts)，只把它们当作目的地(destinations)，因为路由器需要弄清楚如何到达不同目的地。"},{"en":"Packets","zh_html":"分组(packets)"},{"en":"Recall from the previous unit that when an application wants to send data over the Internet, the application creates a packet containing the data. As the packet is passed to lower-layer protocols, additional headers are wrapped around the packet with metadata to help the packet reach its destination.","zh_html":"回想上一单元，当一个应用程序(application)想通过互联网(Internet)发送数据时，它会创建一个包含数据的分组(packet)。当分组被传递给更低层协议(lower-layer protocols)时，会有额外的头部(headers)包在分组外面，携带元数据(metadata)，帮助分组到达目的地(destination)。"},{"en":"In the routing unit, we’ll consider a simplified model where each packet has a header with metadata, and a payload with the application-level data. We’ll ignore nested headers and multiple layers for now.","zh_html":"在路由(routing)单元中，我们会考虑一个简化模型(model)：每个分组(packet)有一个带元数据(metadata)的头部(header)，以及一个包含应用层数据(application-level data)的有效载荷(payload)。现在我们会先忽略嵌套头部(nested headers)和多层结构(multiple layers)。"},{"en":"Routing protocols are not concerned with the application-level data. It doesn’t matter whether the user is trying to send an image, or an HTML webpage, or an audio file; from the perspective of routing, we have a sequence of 1s and 0s, and we need a protocol to send those bits to their destination.","zh_html":"路由协议(routing protocols)并不关心应用层数据(application-level data)。用户是想发送一张图片、一个 HTML 网页，还是一个音频文件都不重要；从路由(routing)的角度看，我们拥有的是一串 1 和 0，需要一种协议把这些比特(bits)发送到它们的目的地(destination)。"},{"en":"In the header, the main metadata field we’re concerned with is the destination address. This tells us the final destination of the packet. When a router receives a packet, the router reads the metadata field in the header to determine how to send the packet towards its final destination. The problem of figuring out where to send the packet is the key problem we’ll need to solve in routing.","zh_html":"在头部(header)中，我们关心的主要元数据字段(metadata field)是目的地址(destination address)。它告诉我们分组(packet)的最终目的地。当路由器(router)收到分组时，会读取头部中的元数据字段，判断如何把分组送向最终目的地。弄清楚该把分组发送到哪里，是我们在路由(routing)中需要解决的核心问题。"},{"en":"Addressing","zh_html":"寻址(addressing)"},{"en":"How do we write down the destination of the packet in the packet header? We’ll need some way of addressing each machine on the network. In other words, we need a protocol that assigns an address to each machine on the network.","zh_html":"我们如何在分组头部(packet header)中写下分组(packet)的目的地(destination)？我们需要某种方式为网络中的每台机器寻址(addressing)。换句话说，我们需要一种协议(protocol)，为网络中的每台机器分配一个地址(address)。"},{"en":"Later in this unit, we’ll discuss scalable approaches to addressing. For now, let’s assign each machine a unique label (e.g. we could label three routers X, Y, and Z), and treat those labels as the addresses for each router. This will allow us to think about the routing problem and the addressing problem separately.","zh_html":"本单元后面会讨论可扩展(scalable)的寻址(addressing)方法。现在，先给每台机器分配一个唯一标签(unique label)，例如可以把三个路由器标为 X、Y、Z，并把这些标签当作每台路由器的地址(address)。这样我们就可以把路由(routing)问题和寻址(addressing)问题分开思考。"},{"en":"At this point, we can define the routing problem: When a router receives a packet, how does the router know where to forward the packet such that it will eventually arrive at the final destination?","zh_html":"到这里，我们可以定义路由(routing)问题：当路由器(router)收到一个分组(packet)时，它如何知道应该把分组转发到哪里，才能让分组最终到达最终目的地(final destination)？"},{"en":"Network Topologies Change","zh_html":"网络拓扑(network topologies)会变化"},{"en":"At this point, we have defined the routing problem, but there are still a few more practical considerations that make the routing problem difficult.","zh_html":"到这里，我们已经定义了路由(routing)问题，但仍有一些实际考虑会让路由问题变得困难。"},{"en":"If the Internet could be drawn as a fixed, constant graph that never changes, then perhaps we could solve the routing problem by simply looking at the graph and computing paths through the graph.","zh_html":"如果互联网(Internet)可以画成一个固定不变、永远不会变化的图(graph)，那么也许我们可以只查看这个图并计算穿过图的路径(paths)，从而解决路由(routing)问题。"},{"en":"However, the network topology is constantly changing. For example, links might fail at unpredictable times. Now, packets must be sent along a different route in order to reach the destination.","zh_html":"然而，网络拓扑(network topology)会不断变化。例如，链路(links)可能在不可预测的时间发生故障。此时，分组(packets)必须沿不同路由(route)发送，才能到达目的地(destination)。"},{"en":"New links might also be added, creating additional paths that can be considered during routing.","zh_html":"也可能会添加新的链路(links)，从而产生可在路由(routing)期间考虑的额外路径(paths)。"},{"en":"The routing protocols we design need to be robust to these changing network topologies.","zh_html":"我们设计的路由协议(routing protocols)需要能稳健应对这些变化的网络拓扑(network topologies)。"},{"en":"Routing Protocols are Distributed","zh_html":"路由协议(routing protocols)是分布式的(distributed)"},{"en":"If the network changes, perhaps we could solve the routing problem by updating our graph and then computing paths through the new graph.","zh_html":"如果网络发生变化，也许我们可以通过更新图(graph)，然后在新图上计算路径(paths)，来解决路由(routing)问题。"},{"en":"Another problem that makes routing difficult is that routers don’t inherently have a global, birds-eye view of the entire network. For example, if a link somewhere else in the network fails, there’s no way for all routers to automatically know this. We will have to somehow propagate that information about the new network topology to the routers as part of our routing protocol.","zh_html":"另一个让路由(routing)变得困难的问题是，路由器(routers)天生并没有整个网络的全局鸟瞰视图(global, birds-eye view)。例如，如果网络中其他地方的一条链路(link)故障，所有路由器并不会自动知道这件事。我们必须在路由协议(routing protocol)中以某种方式把新的网络拓扑(network topology)信息传播给路由器。"},{"en":"This leads to routing protocols often being distributed protocols. Instead of a single central mastermind computing all the answers, each router must compute its own part of the answer (possibly without full knowledge of the network topology). Collectively, the answers computed by each router must form a global answer to the routing problem that allows packets to reach their end destination.","zh_html":"这使得路由协议(routing protocols)往往是分布式协议(distributed protocols)。不是由一个中心化的“大脑”计算所有答案，而是每台路由器(router)都必须计算答案中属于自己的部分，可能还没有完整的网络拓扑(network topology)知识。所有路由器各自计算出的答案合在一起，必须形成路由问题的全局答案(global answer)，使分组(packets)能够到达最终目的地(end destination)。"},{"en":"The distributed nature of routing protocols also means that we have to account for individual routers failing. If there was a single computer that was solving the problem, and that computer crashed and forgot the answer, we could simply make the computer re-compute the entire answer from scratch. However, in a distributed protocol, if one router crashes and forgets its part of the answer, our protocol will need to a way to help this one router recover from failure and re-learn its part of the answer.","zh_html":"路由协议(routing protocols)的分布式性质也意味着，我们必须考虑单个路由器(router)故障的情况。如果只有一台计算机在求解这个问题，而这台计算机崩溃并忘记了答案，我们可以直接让它从头重新计算整个答案。然而，在分布式协议(distributed protocol)中，如果一台路由器崩溃并忘记了它负责的答案部分，我们的协议就需要有办法帮助这台路由器从故障中恢复，并重新学习它的答案部分。"},{"en":"Links are Best-Effort","zh_html":"链路(links)是尽力而为(best-effort)的"},{"en":"Recall from the previous unit that protocols at Layer 3 and below are best-effort. In other words, when a packet is sent over a link, there is no guarantee that the packet reaches the destination. The link might drop the packet.","zh_html":"回想上一单元，第 3 层(Layer 3)及以下的协议(protocols)都是尽力而为(best-effort)的。换句话说，当一个分组(packet)通过一条链路(link)发送时，并不能保证它一定到达目的地(destination)。链路可能会丢弃这个分组。"},{"en":"When designing routing protocols, we’ll need to account for this problem as well.","zh_html":"设计路由协议(routing protocols)时，我们也需要考虑这个问题。"}]},"/routing/router.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Router Hardware","zh_html":"路由器硬件(router hardware)"},{"en":"What Do Routers Do?","zh_html":"路由器(router)做什么？"},{"en":"A router runs some routing protocol to populate the forwarding table.","zh_html":"路由器(router)运行某种路由协议(routing protocol)，用来填充转发表(forwarding table)。"},{"en":"Then, when a packet comes in, the router looks at its destination IP and uses the forwarding table to select a link to forward the packet along. Remember, the forwarding table could contain ranges of addresses.","zh_html":"然后，当分组(packet)到达时，路由器(router)查看其目的 IP(destination IP)，并使用转发表(forwarding table)选择一条链路(link)来转发该分组。记住，转发表可以包含地址范围(ranges of addresses)。"},{"en":"So far, we’ve drawn routers as boxes on a diagram. In reality, a router is a specialized computer optimized for performing routing and forwarding tasks. In this section, we’ll explore the hardware inside routers.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们一直把路由器(routers)画成图中的方框。在现实中，路由器是一种专门优化过的计算机，用于执行路由(routing)和转发(forwarding)任务。本节会探讨路由器内部的硬件(hardware)。"},{"en":"Where Are Routers?","zh_html":"路由器(router)在哪里？"},{"en":"In real life, homes and offices have small routers to connect hosts to the Internet. Where do all these routers all connect to each other?","zh_html":"在现实中，家庭和办公室有小型路由器(routers)，用来把主机(hosts)连接到互联网(Internet)。那么所有这些路由器又在哪里彼此连接呢？"},{"en":"Colocation facilities or carrier hotels are buildings where multiple ISPs install routers to connect to each other. These buildings are specially designed to have power and cooling infrastructure, and ISPs can rent space to install routers and connect them to other routers in the same building.","zh_html":"<strong>托管设施(colocation facilities)</strong>或<strong>运营商酒店(carrier hotels)</strong>是一些建筑，多个 ISP 会在其中安装路由器(routers)，以便彼此连接。这些建筑专门设计了供电(power)和冷却(cooling)基础设施(infrastructure)，ISP 可以租用空间安装路由器，并把它们连接到同一建筑中的其他路由器。"},{"en":"Inside a carrier hotel, routers are stacked together into racks (6-7 feet tall, 19 inches wide).","zh_html":"在运营商酒店(carrier hotel)内部，路由器(routers)会堆叠安装到机架(racks)中，机架通常高 6 到 7 英尺、宽 19 英寸。"},{"en":"Router Sizes and Capacities","zh_html":"路由器(router)尺寸与容量"},{"en":"Routers come in all sizes, depending on the user requirements. Home routers only forward traffic for a few users, and the forwarding table has a single default entry. Industrial routers might need to forward traffic from thousands of customers, with a huge forwarding table.","zh_html":"路由器(routers)有各种尺寸，取决于用户需求。家用路由器(home routers)只为少数用户转发流量(traffic)，其转发表(forwarding table)只有一个默认条目(default entry)。工业级路由器(industrial routers)可能需要为成千上万客户转发流量，并拥有巨大的转发表。"},{"en":"There are different ways we can measure the size of a router. We could consider its physical size, the number of physical ports it has, and its bandwidth.","zh_html":"衡量路由器(router)规模有不同方式。我们可以考虑它的物理尺寸(physical size)、物理端口(physical ports)数量，以及带宽(bandwidth)。"},{"en":"We can measure a router’s capacity as the number of physical ports, multiplied by the bandwidth of each physical port. The speed or bandwidth of a physical port is often called its line rate.","zh_html":"我们可以用物理端口(physical ports)数量乘以每个物理端口的带宽(bandwidth)，来衡量路由器(router)容量(capacity)。物理端口的速度或带宽通常称为<strong>线路速率(line rate)</strong>。"},{"en":"Not all physical ports need to have the same line rate. For example, a modern home router might have 4 physical ports that can send at 100 Mbps, and 1 physical port that can send at 1 Gbps. The total capacity of this router is 1.4 Gbps.","zh_html":"并非所有物理端口(physical ports)都必须具有相同线路速率(line rate)。例如，一台现代家用路由器(home router)可能有 4 个能以 100 Mbps 发送的物理端口，以及 1 个能以 1 Gbps 发送的物理端口。这台路由器(router)的总容量(total capacity)是 1.4 Gbps。"},{"en":"A modern state-of-the-art router used by ISPs might have a line rate of up to 400 Gbps per physical port.","zh_html":"ISP 使用的现代先进路由器(router)，每个物理端口(physical port)的线路速率(line rate)可能高达 400 Gbps。"},{"en":"This router contains multiple removable line cards, where each line card contains a set of physical ports. A modern router might have 8 line cards, with 36 physical ports per line card, for a total of 288 physical ports.","zh_html":"这台路由器(router)包含多个可拆卸的<strong>线卡(line cards)</strong>，每块线卡包含一组物理端口(physical ports)。一台现代路由器可能有 8 块线卡，每块线卡有 36 个物理端口，总计 288 个物理端口。"},{"en":"288 physical ports, each with 400 Gbps bandwidth, gives our router a total capacity of 115.2 Tbps.","zh_html":"288 个物理端口(physical ports)，每个端口带宽(bandwidth)为 400 Gbps，使这台路由器(router)的总容量(total capacity)达到 115.2 Tbps。"},{"en":"This router could cost upwards of $1 million. Breaking up a router into line cards allows us to install more line cards as more capacity is needed.","zh_html":"这种路由器(router)的成本可能超过 100 万美元。把路由器拆分成线卡(line cards)，可以在需要更多容量(capacity)时安装更多线卡。"},{"en":"In the future, next-generation routers will have 800 Gbps physical ports. Physical space for routers is constrained, so modern improvements are focused on improving the speed per port, instead of increasing the number of ports. (Stuffing more ports into the same space is also difficult because of power and cooling constraints.)","zh_html":"未来，下一代路由器(next-generation routers)会拥有 800 Gbps 的物理端口(physical ports)。路由器的物理空间(physical space)受限，因此现代改进主要集中在提升每个端口的速度，而不是增加端口数量。在相同空间中塞入更多端口也很困难，因为供电(power)和冷却(cooling)都有约束。"},{"en":"Router capacity has increased over the years in response to the growth in user demand (e.g. video quality has increased from 720p to 8K = 8000p). In 2010, state-of-the-art routers had 1.7 Tbps capacity, and that’s increased by a factor of 100 in the past decade. Much of this improvement came from increasing the link speed, from 10 Gbps in 2010 to 100 Gbps around 2016 to 400 Gbps today. These improvements are starting to slow down because of constraints like Moore’s law slowing and physical challenges with sending signals at high rate. The next improvement to 800 Gbps is only a 2x increase (compared to the earlier 10x and 4x increases).","zh_html":"多年来，为了响应用户需求增长，路由器(router)容量(capacity)不断提升，例如视频质量从 720p 提升到 8K = 8000p。2010 年，先进路由器的容量为 1.7 Tbps，在过去十年中增长了 100 倍。这一提升很大程度上来自链路速度(link speed)的提高：从 2010 年的 10 Gbps，到 2016 年左右的 100 Gbps，再到今天的 400 Gbps。由于摩尔定律(Moore’s law)放缓，以及高速发送信号(signal)面临物理挑战，这些提升开始放慢。下一次提升到 800 Gbps 只有 2 倍增长，相比之下，早期提升分别是 10 倍和 4 倍。"},{"en":"Data, Control, Management Planes","zh_html":"数据、控制与管理平面(data, control, management planes)"},{"en":"The hardware and software components of the router can conceptually be split into three planes. The data plane is mainly responsible for forwarding packets. The data plane is used every time a packet arrives and needs to be forwarded. The data plane operates locally, without coordinating with other routers.","zh_html":"从概念上说，路由器(router)的硬件(hardware)和软件(software)组件可以分成三个平面(planes)。<strong>数据平面(data plane)</strong>主要负责转发分组(packets)。每当分组到达并需要被转发时，都会使用数据平面。数据平面在本地运行，不与其他路由器协调。"},{"en":"The control plane is mainly responsible for communicating with other routers and running routing protocols. The result of those routing protocols (e.g. the forwarding table) can then be used by the data plane. The control plane is used every time the topology of the network changes (e.g. when links are added or removed).","zh_html":"<strong>控制平面(control plane)</strong>主要负责与其他路由器(routers)通信并运行路由协议(routing protocols)。这些路由协议的结果，例如转发表(forwarding table)，随后可供数据平面(data plane)使用。每当网络拓扑(topology)发生变化时，例如添加或移除链路(links)，都会使用控制平面。"},{"en":"Because the data plane and control plane operate at different time scales, and are running different protocols, the hardware and software of a router are optimized for different tasks. In practice, packets arrive much more frequently than the network topology changing. Therefore, the data plane is optimized for performing very simple tasks (table lookup and forwarding) very quickly. By contrast, the control plane is optimized for more complex tasks (re-computing paths in the network).","zh_html":"由于数据平面(data plane)和控制平面(control plane)在不同时间尺度(time scales)上运行，并且运行不同协议(protocols)，路由器(router)的硬件和软件会针对不同任务进行优化。在实践中，分组(packets)到达的频率远高于网络拓扑(topology)变化的频率。因此，数据平面被优化为非常快速地执行简单任务，例如表查找(table lookup)和转发(forwarding)。相比之下，控制平面被优化为执行更复杂的任务，例如重新计算网络中的路径(paths)。"},{"en":"The management plane is used to tell routers what to do, and see what they are doing. Systems and humans interact with the management plane to configure and monitor the router. This is where operators can configure the device functionality. What costs should be assigned to each link? What routing protocol should be run? These need to be manually decided by the operator.","zh_html":"<strong>管理平面(management plane)</strong>用于告诉路由器(routers)该做什么，并查看它们正在做什么。系统和人通过管理平面与路由器交互，以配置(configure)和监控(monitor)路由器。运营方(operators)在这里配置设备功能(device functionality)。每条链路(link)应分配什么成本(cost)？应该运行哪种路由协议(routing protocol)？这些都需要由运营方手动决定。"},{"en":"In addition to configuration, the management plane also provides monitoring tools. How much traffic is being carried over each link? Has any physical component of the router failed? This information can be relayed back to the operator.","zh_html":"除了配置(configuration)之外，管理平面(management plane)还提供监控工具(monitoring tools)。每条链路(link)正在承载多少流量(traffic)？路由器(router)的某个物理组件(physical component)是否发生故障？这些信息可以传回运营方(operator)。"},{"en":"The management plane is the main place where operators access and interact with the router from outside the device. If the operator is using some piece of code to interact with the router, we usually consider that part of the management plane as well.","zh_html":"管理平面(management plane)是运营方(operators)从设备外部访问并与路由器(router)交互的主要位置。如果运营方使用某段代码(code)与路由器交互，我们通常也把这部分视为管理平面的一部分。"},{"en":"The data plane and control plane operate in real-time, receiving and processing packets on the order of nanoseconds (data) and seconds (control). By contrast, the management plane works on the order of tens to hundreds of seconds. If the operator changes a configuration, the router might spend time performing validation checks and processing the configuration before fully applying the update.","zh_html":"数据平面(data plane)和控制平面(control plane)实时运行，接收和处理分组(packets)的时间尺度分别是纳秒级(nanoseconds，数据)和秒级(seconds，控制)。相比之下，管理平面(management plane)的工作时间尺度是几十到几百秒。如果运营方(operator)修改配置(configuration)，路由器(router)在完全应用更新之前，可能需要花时间执行验证检查(validation checks)并处理该配置。"},{"en":"The network management system (NMS) is some piece of software run by the operator to interact with the routers. This software computes a network configuration (maybe with the help of manual operator input), and then applies that configuration to the routers. The router publishes some API that the system can use to talk to the router.","zh_html":"<strong>网络管理系统(network management system, NMS)</strong>是由运营方(operator)运行的一类软件，用来与路由器(routers)交互。这个软件计算网络配置(network configuration)，可能会借助手动的运营方输入，然后把该配置应用到路由器上。路由器发布某种 API，系统可以用它与路由器通信。"},{"en":"The network management system also allows telemetry (statistics and running state) to be read from routers.","zh_html":"网络管理系统(network management system)还允许从路由器(routers)读取遥测信息(telemetry)，也就是统计数据(statistics)和运行状态(running state)。"},{"en":"The complexity of the network management system depends on what the operator is trying to achieve.","zh_html":"网络管理系统(network management system)的复杂度取决于运营方(operator)想要实现什么目标。"},{"en":"All three planes are needed to run a router. If we only had the data plane and no control plane, we could forward packets, but we wouldn’t know where to forward them.","zh_html":"运行一台路由器(router)需要这三个平面(planes)。如果只有数据平面(data plane)而没有控制平面(control plane)，我们可以转发分组(packets)，但不知道应该把它们转发到哪里。"},{"en":"What’s Inside a Router?","zh_html":"路由器(router)内部有什么？"},{"en":"We defined a router as a computer that performs routing tasks, but in reality, inside the router, there are many smaller computers (e.g. CPUs, specialized chips) that work together to perfom routing tasks.","zh_html":"我们把路由器(router)定义为执行路由任务(routing tasks)的计算机，但在现实中，路由器内部有许多更小的计算机，例如 CPU 和专用芯片(specialized chips)，它们协同工作来执行路由任务。"},{"en":"The physical shelf that makes up an industrial-size router is called a chassis. Inside the chassis, we install many line cards, and we have several physical ports on each line card. Each physical port can be used for either input or output.","zh_html":"构成工业级路由器(industrial-size router)的物理机框称为<strong>机箱(chassis)</strong>。在机箱内部，我们安装许多<strong>线卡(line cards)</strong>，每块线卡上有多个物理端口(physical ports)。每个物理端口既可用于输入(input)，也可用于输出(output)。"},{"en":"Every physical port has to be connected to every other physical port in the router (both in the same linecard and other linecards). You might receive a packet through one port, and need to forward it out of a port on a different linecard.","zh_html":"每个物理端口(physical port)都必须能连接到路由器(router)中的其他每个物理端口，包括同一块线卡(linecard)和其他线卡上的端口。你可能通过一个端口收到分组(packet)，然后需要从另一块线卡上的端口将其转发出去。"},{"en":"It would be pretty inefficient to physically wire each port to every other port. Instead, we have a fabric of wires to connect linecards together. Each linecard also has chips to facilitate connections to the fabric.","zh_html":"如果把每个端口(port)都用物理线路连接到其他每个端口，会非常低效。相反，我们使用一组线路构成的交换结构(fabric)来连接线卡(linecards)。每块线卡也有芯片(chips)，用于帮助连接到这个交换结构。"},{"en":"Separate from all the linecards, we have a controller card with its own CPU, which talks with other routers to perform routing protocols. After running some algorithm to compute paths, the controller programs the forwarding chips with the correct forwarding table entries.","zh_html":"除了所有线卡(linecards)之外，还有一块带有自己 CPU 的控制器卡(controller card)，它与其他路由器(routers)通信以执行路由协议(routing protocols)。在运行某种算法(algorithm)计算路径(paths)之后，控制器(controller)会把正确的转发表条目(forwarding table entries)写入转发芯片(forwarding chips)。"},{"en":"Each linecard has its own local CPU to control linecard functions (e.g. populate the forwarding table). The linecard also has hardware for basic processing of packets (e.g. updating its TTL before sending it out). The linecard contains one or more chips specifically optimized for forwarding.","zh_html":"每块线卡(linecard)都有自己的本地 CPU，用于控制线卡功能，例如填充转发表(forwarding table)。线卡还包含用于分组(packet)基础处理的硬件，例如在发送分组前更新其 TTL。线卡包含一个或多个专门针对转发(forwarding)优化的芯片(chips)。"},{"en":"We can also categorize the router components by the different planes. The data plane is supported by forwarding chips on linecards, the fabric connecting linecards, and the fabric chips connecting the linecards to the fabric. The control plane and management plane are supported by the controller card.","zh_html":"我们也可以按不同平面(planes)对路由器(router)组件进行分类。数据平面(data plane)由线卡(linecards)上的转发芯片(forwarding chips)、连接线卡的交换结构(fabric)，以及把线卡连接到交换结构的交换结构芯片(fabric chips)支持。控制平面(control plane)和管理平面(management plane)由控制器卡(controller card)支持。"},{"en":"Here’s a picture of an industrial router. This router has 6 slots, where 4 of them have line cards, and the other 2 have controller cards. There’s also a fan tray for cooling. The fabric connecting linecards is in the back (not pictured).","zh_html":"这里是一台工业级路由器(industrial router)的图片。这台路由器有 6 个插槽(slots)，其中 4 个插有线卡(line cards)，另外 2 个插有控制器卡(controller cards)。还有一个用于冷却(cooling)的风扇托架(fan tray)。连接线卡的交换结构(fabric)位于背面，图中未显示。"},{"en":"Types of Packets","zh_html":"分组(packet)类型"},{"en":"The most common packet is a user packet, containing data from an end host. When the router receives this packet, the forwarding chip first reads the destination field in the header and looks up the appropriate port. If that port is on a different linecard, the packet is sent through the fabric to the appropriate linecard. Once the packet reaches the correct linecard, the packet is sent along the appropriate port.","zh_html":"最常见的分组(packet)是<strong>用户分组(user packet)</strong>，其中包含来自端主机(end host)的数据。当路由器(router)收到这个分组时，转发芯片(forwarding chip)首先读取首部(header)中的目的字段(destination field)，并查找(lookup)合适的端口(port)。如果该端口位于另一块线卡(linecard)上，分组会通过交换结构(fabric)发送到合适的线卡。一旦分组到达正确线卡，就会沿合适端口发送出去。"},{"en":"Some packets are control-plane traffic, which are destined for the router itself. In particular, when we run routing protocols, advertisements are sent to the router itself. When the router receives this packet, the forwarding chip sends the packet up to the controller card. The CPU on the controller card processes the packet accordingly.","zh_html":"有些分组(packets)是<strong>控制平面流量(control-plane traffic)</strong>，其目的地(destination)是路由器(router)自身。尤其是在运行路由协议(routing protocols)时，通告(advertisements)会发送给路由器自身。当路由器收到这种分组时，转发芯片(forwarding chip)会把分组向上发送到控制器卡(controller card)。控制器卡上的 CPU 会相应地处理该分组。"},{"en":"The last type of traffic is punt traffic. These are user packets, but they require some additional special processing. For example, if we receive a packet with a TTL of 1, the packet has expired, and we shouldn’t forward it. We might also need to send an error message back to the sender. When the router receives a punt packet, the forwarding chip “punts” the packet to the controller card for special processing.","zh_html":"最后一种流量(traffic)是<strong>上送流量(punt traffic)</strong>。这些是用户分组(user packets)，但它们需要一些额外的特殊处理。例如，如果我们收到一个 TTL 为 1 的分组(packet)，该分组已经过期(expired)，不应再转发。我们可能还需要向发送方(sender)返回错误消息(error message)。当路由器(router)收到上送分组(punt packet)时，转发芯片(forwarding chip)会把该分组“上送(punts)”到控制器卡(controller card)进行特殊处理。"},{"en":"Scaling Routers","zh_html":"扩展路由器(routers)"},{"en":"Why is our router broken down into this specific architecture, with forwarding chips and controller cards? Couldn’t we run everything on a general-purpose CPU?","zh_html":"为什么路由器(router)要被拆分成这种特定架构(architecture)，包含转发芯片(forwarding chips)和控制器卡(controller cards)？难道不能把所有东西都运行在通用 CPU(general-purpose CPU)上吗？"},{"en":"The problem is, state-of-the-art routers need to run at enormous scale. At modern speeds of 400 Gbps per second, and assuming 64-byte packets, we have to process 781 million packets per second, per port. Across 36 ports, the entire router has to process 56 billion packets per second. (In practice, the numbers might be slightly lower if some packets are larger.)","zh_html":"问题在于，先进路由器(state-of-the-art routers)需要以巨大规模运行。在现代 400 Gbps 的速度下，假设分组(packets)大小为 64 字节(byte)，每个端口(port)每秒必须处理 7.81 亿个分组。跨 36 个端口，整台路由器(router)每秒必须处理 560 亿个分组。在实践中，如果某些分组更大，这些数字可能会稍低。"},{"en":"This scale is not achievable in software on a general-purpose CPU. To get a sense of scale, if we tried writing a program for forwarding packets, and we ran that program on a CPU, it would be pretty impressive if we could forward one packet every 10 microseconds = 0.00001 seconds. A state-of-the-art router needs to process one packet roughly every 10 nanoseconds = 0.00000001 seconds. Even the most optimized software cannot process packets at this scale. Instead, we need to implement router functionality directly on hardware.","zh_html":"这种规模无法通过通用 CPU(general-purpose CPU)上的软件实现。为了感受一下规模，如果我们尝试编写一个转发分组(forwarding packets)的程序，并在 CPU 上运行它，若能每 10 微秒(microseconds) = 0.00001 秒转发一个分组(packet)，就已经相当令人印象深刻。先进路由器(state-of-the-art router)大约每 10 纳秒(nanoseconds) = 0.00000001 秒就需要处理一个分组。即使是最优化的软件，也无法以这种规模处理分组。因此，我们需要直接在硬件(hardware)上实现路由器功能(router functionality)。"},{"en":"By splitting the router into specialized data plane linecards and control plane controller cards, we create a fast path and slow path. The fast path only involves forwarding hardware and is optimized for forwarding packets at very high rate. The slow path with the control CPU is only used when necessary, and most packets are sent through the fast path. These specialized components make routers much more efficient (uses less power, cheaper, uses less physical space).","zh_html":"通过把路由器(router)拆分为专门的数据平面线卡(data plane linecards)和控制平面控制器卡(control plane controller cards)，我们创建了快速路径(fast path)和慢速路径(slow path)。快速路径只涉及转发硬件(forwarding hardware)，并针对以极高速率转发分组(packets)进行优化。带有控制 CPU(control CPU)的慢速路径只在必要时使用，大多数分组都会通过快速路径发送。这些专用组件使路由器效率高得多：耗电更少、成本更低、占用物理空间更小。"},{"en":"Linecard Functionality","zh_html":"线卡(linecard)功能"},{"en":"What specific tasks does a linecard need to do when it receives a packet?","zh_html":"线卡(linecard)收到分组(packet)时，需要执行哪些具体任务？"},{"en":"First, the linecard needs to take the signal (e.g. optical, electrical) and decode this signal into ones and zeros that make up the packet. This is the PHY part of the linecard, which handles the physical layer (Layer 1) functionality.","zh_html":"首先，线卡(linecard)需要接收信号(signal)，例如光信号(optical)或电信号(electrical)，并把该信号解码(decode)成组成分组(packet)的 1 和 0。这是线卡的 <strong>PHY</strong> 部分，负责处理物理层(physical layer, Layer 1)功能。"},{"en":"Once we have a sequence of ones and zeros, we have to read those bits and parse them (e.g. find out which bits correspond to the IP header). We might also have to perform other link-layer operations (e.g. if a link is connected to more than 2 machines). The MAC part of the linecard handles the link layer functionality (Layer 2).","zh_html":"一旦得到 1 和 0 的序列，我们就必须读取这些位(bits)并解析(parse)它们，例如找出哪些位对应 IP 首部(IP header)。我们可能还必须执行其他链路层(link-layer)操作，例如当一条链路(link)连接了超过 2 台机器时。线卡(linecard)的 <strong>MAC</strong> 部分负责处理链路层(link layer, Layer 2)功能。"},{"en":"Now that we have an IP packet, we have to parse the packet. For example, we need to check if the packet is IPv4 or IPv6. Then, we have to read the destination address and perform a lookup for forwarding (or discover that we need to punt the packet).","zh_html":"现在我们得到了 IP 分组(IP packet)，必须解析(parse)该分组。例如，我们需要检查分组是 IPv4 还是 IPv6。然后，必须读取目的地址(destination address)，并执行转发查找(lookup for forwarding)，或者发现需要将该分组上送(punt)。"},{"en":"We may also need to update various IP header fields. We have to decrease the TTL. Since we updated the header, we also need to update the checksum in the header. We might also need to update other fields like options and fragment (discussed in more detail in the IP header section).","zh_html":"我们可能还需要更新各种 IP 首部字段(IP header fields)。我们必须递减 TTL。由于更新了首部(header)，还需要更新首部中的校验和(checksum)。我们也可能需要更新其他字段，例如 options 和 fragment，这些会在 IP 首部(IP header)一节中更详细讨论。"},{"en":"All of this functionality has to happen in a matter of nanoseconds. Even if we somehow did all the processing in one clock cycle, the linecard still has to operate at 0.2 GHz. In practice, all these operations will take more than one clock cycle. Also, we have to do all this processing for every port on the linecard (one forwarding chip supports all the ports).","zh_html":"所有这些功能都必须在纳秒(nanoseconds)量级完成。即使我们设法在一个时钟周期(clock cycle)内完成所有处理，线卡(linecard)仍然必须以 0.2 GHz 运行。在实践中，所有这些操作都会花费不止一个时钟周期。此外，我们还必须为线卡上的每个端口(port)执行这些处理，而一个转发芯片(forwarding chip)会支持所有端口。"},{"en":"In order to make these operations fast, forwarding chips are extremely specialized for the limited tasks that they perform (e.g. reading packet header, table lookup). You can’t write a general-purpose program and run it on a forwarding chip. If a packet requires functionality that the forwarding chip can’t support, we can always punt the packet to the general-purpose CPU on the controller card.","zh_html":"为了让这些操作足够快，转发芯片(forwarding chips)会针对其执行的有限任务进行高度专用化，例如读取分组首部(packet header)、表查找(table lookup)。你不能编写一个通用程序(general-purpose program)并在转发芯片上运行。如果某个分组(packet)需要转发芯片无法支持的功能，我们总是可以把它上送(punt)到控制器卡(controller card)上的通用 CPU(general-purpose CPU)。"},{"en":"Simple operations, like decrementing the TTL, are easy to implement in hardware. More complex operations, like special options, usually require punting to the controller card. In the modern Internet, we avoid special options whenever possible, in order to maximize use of the fast path and avoid punting (if we punted everything, controller cards would be overwhelmed).","zh_html":"简单操作，例如递减 TTL，容易用硬件(hardware)实现。更复杂的操作，例如特殊 options，通常需要上送(punting)到控制器卡(controller card)。在现代互联网(Internet)中，我们会尽可能避免特殊 options，以最大化使用快速路径(fast path)并避免上送；如果所有东西都上送，控制器卡会被压垮。"},{"en":"The fabric interconnect chips are also similarly specialized. These chips help send packets across the fabric to other linecards. These chips tend to be the most specialized and most high-performance chips in the whole router.","zh_html":"交换结构互连芯片(fabric interconnect chips)也类似地高度专用化。这些芯片帮助分组(packets)穿过交换结构(fabric)发送到其他线卡(linecards)。这些芯片往往是整台路由器(router)中最专用、性能最高的芯片。"},{"en":"Packet Queuing","zh_html":"分组排队(packet queuing)"},{"en":"TODO","zh_html":"TODO"},{"en":"Efficient Forwarding Table Lookup","zh_html":"高效转发表查找(efficient forwarding table lookup)"},{"en":"We now know that routers need to perform lookups in forwarding tables at extremely high rates. One major challenge is that our table entries can contain ranges of IP addresses (192.0.1.0/24) in addition to individual IP addresses. Also, these ranges could be overlapping (a destination could match multiple ranges). How can we make lookups extremely fast?","zh_html":"现在我们知道，路由器(routers)需要以极高速率在转发表(forwarding tables)中执行查找(lookups)。一个主要挑战是，表条目(table entries)除了可以包含单个 IP 地址(IP addresses)，也可以包含 IP 地址范围(ranges)，例如 192.0.1.0/24。此外，这些范围可能重叠(overlapping)，一个目的地(destination)可能匹配多个范围。我们怎样才能让查找极快？"},{"en":"Ideally, for maximum speed, the forwarding table could contain one entry per destination, with no ranges. Then, we just need to take the destination in the packet, and look up an exact match to learn the next hop.","zh_html":"理想情况下，为了获得最大速度，转发表(forwarding table)可以对每个目的地(destination)包含一个条目(entry)，没有范围(ranges)。这样，我们只需要取出分组(packet)中的目的地，并查找精确匹配(exact match)，即可得知下一跳(next hop)。"},{"en":"To achieve this ideal approach, we could expand every range into its individual IP addresses. For example, an entry for the 24-bit prefix 192.0.1.0/24 would be expanded into 256 entries.","zh_html":"为了实现这种理想方法，我们可以把每个范围(range)展开成其中的单个 IP 地址(IP addresses)。例如，24 位(bits)前缀(prefix) 192.0.1.0/24 的一个条目(entry)会被展开成 256 个条目。"},{"en":"This is space-inefficient (remember, this is being implemented in hardware). Also, if a route changes, we’d have to update tons of entries in the table. Expanding routes isn’t going to work, so we’ll have to work with ranges.","zh_html":"这在空间上效率很低(space-inefficient)，记住，这是在硬件(hardware)中实现的。另外，如果某条路由(route)发生变化，我们就必须更新表(table)中的大量条目(entries)。展开路由行不通，因此必须处理范围(ranges)。"},{"en":"Recall that forwarding table lookup is done using longest prefix matching. If multiple ranges match the destination, we pick the most specific range (most prefix bits fixed). If none of the ranges match, we pick the default route (., 0.0.0.0/32, matches all destinations). If there’s no default route, we drop the packet.","zh_html":"回想一下，转发表查找(forwarding table lookup)使用最长前缀匹配(longest prefix matching)。如果多个范围(ranges)匹配目的地(destination)，我们选择最具体的范围，也就是固定前缀位(prefix bits)最多的范围。如果没有任何范围匹配，我们选择默认路由(default route)，即 <em>.</em>、0.0.0.0/32，匹配所有目的地。如果没有默认路由，我们就丢弃该分组(packet)。"},{"en":"How do we implement longest prefix matching in hardware efficiently?","zh_html":"我们如何在硬件(hardware)中高效实现最长前缀匹配(longest prefix matching)？"},{"en":"TODO rewrite this to match the diagram","zh_html":"TODO：重写此处以匹配图示"},{"en":"First, for readability, we rewrite all the ranges and the destination in binary. Then, we scan the destination bits, one by one. For the first 21 bits, all four ranges match, so all four ranges are still in play. Then, the 22nd bit is a 1. The first row has a 0 in the 22nd bit, so we can eliminate this row (not a match). The other three rows still match in the first 22 bits, so they’re still in play.","zh_html":"首先，为了可读性，我们把所有范围(ranges)和目的地(destination)都改写成二进制(binary)。然后，我们逐位扫描目的地的比特(bits)。对于前 21 位，四个范围都匹配，因此四个范围仍然保留。接着，第 22 位是 1。第一行在第 22 位上是 0，因此可以排除这一行，即不匹配。其他三行在前 22 位仍然匹配，因此仍然保留。"},{"en":"Next, we check the 23rd bit, which is also a 1. The second and third rows have a 0 in the 23rd bit, so we eliminate them (not a match). The fourth row is still a match.","zh_html":"接下来，我们检查第 23 位(bit)，它也是 1。第二行和第三行在第 23 位上是 0，因此我们排除它们，即不匹配。第四行仍然匹配。"},{"en":"At this point, we can confirm that the fourth row is a full match, because it’s a 23-bit prefix, and all 23 bits match. No further checking of this row is needed.","zh_html":"此时，我们可以确认第四行是完全匹配(full match)，因为它是 23 位(bits)前缀(prefix)，并且全部 23 位都匹配。不需要再检查这一行。"},{"en":"We continue checking bit-by-bit, eliminating rows that don’t match, and confirming rows that are full matches. Eventually, we have one or more rows that match, and we pick the match with the longest prefix.","zh_html":"我们继续逐位(bit-by-bit)检查，排除不匹配的行，并确认完全匹配(full matches)的行。最终，我们会得到一行或多行匹配结果，并选择前缀(prefix)最长的匹配。"},{"en":"If we implemented this naively, then for every bit, we would have to match that bit against every entry in the forwarding table. The asymptotic runtime would scale with the number of entries in the forwarding table. Can we do any better?","zh_html":"如果朴素地实现这一过程，那么对每一位(bit)，我们都必须把该位与转发表(forwarding table)中的每个条目(entry)进行匹配。渐近运行时间(asymptotic runtime)会随转发表条目数量增长。我们能做得更好吗？"},{"en":"Efficient Lookup with Tries","zh_html":"使用 trie 高效查找"},{"en":"Thinking back to a data structures class (like CS 61B in UC Berkeley), you might remember that tries are a data structure that efficiently store maps where the keys are strings (in this case, bitstrings). Tries store the key-value pairs by writing out the keys one character (bit) at a time, which enables efficient longest prefix matching.","zh_html":"回想数据结构课程(data structures class)，例如 UC Berkeley 的 CS 61B，你可能记得 trie 是一种数据结构(data structure)，可以高效存储键为字符串(strings)的映射(maps)，在这里键是比特串(bitstrings)。trie 通过一次写出键的一个字符(character)，也就是一个比特(bit)，来存储键值对(key-value pairs)，从而支持高效的最长前缀匹配(longest prefix matching)。"},{"en":"For example, this trie stores a map of words to numbers. If you don’t remember tries, it’s okay.","zh_html":"例如，这个 trie 存储了从单词(words)到数字(numbers)的映射(map)。如果你不记得 trie，也没关系。"},{"en":"If we want to find the longest prefix, just like before, we read the word one letter at a time. This allows us to trace a path down the tree, from the root to a leaf. Along this path, we look for all prefixes in the table (nodes with colors), and pick the longest prefix.","zh_html":"如果想找到最长前缀(longest prefix)，就像之前一样，我们一次读取单词(word)的一个字母(letter)。这样可以沿着树(tree)从根(root)到叶子(leaf)追踪一条路径(path)。在这条路径上，我们查找表(table)中的所有前缀(prefixes)，也就是带颜色的节点(nodes)，并选择最长前缀。"},{"en":"We can use a similar approach for our forwarding table. Each layer of the trie represents one of the digits in the IP address. The zeroth layer is the root (empty string), the first layer represents the first bit, the second layer represents the second bit, etc.","zh_html":"我们可以对转发表(forwarding table)使用类似方法。trie 的每一层(layer)表示 IP 地址(IP address)中的一个数字(digit)。第 0 层是根(root)，对应空字符串(empty string)；第 1 层表示第 1 位(bit)，第 2 层表示第 2 位，依此类推。"},{"en":"Each node in the trie represents a prefix. For example, the 2-bit prefix 11* is at the second layer of the tree, and the 3-bit prefix 100 is at the third layer of the tree. The trie has all possible 3-bit prefixes. If a prefix is in the forwarding table, at the corresponding node, we write the next hop. If the prefix is not in the forwarding table, we don’t write anything in the node (in the picture, colored white).","zh_html":"trie 中的每个节点(node)表示一个前缀(prefix)。例如，2 位(bits)前缀 11* 位于树(tree)的第二层，3 位前缀 100 位于树的第三层。这个 trie 包含所有可能的 3 位前缀。如果某个前缀在转发表(forwarding table)中，我们就在对应节点写入下一跳(next hop)。如果该前缀不在转发表中，就不在节点中写任何内容，图中用白色表示。"},{"en":"Tracing the path down the tree can be done in constant time. We visit one node per bit of the destination address, and the destination address is always 32 bits (constant). Even if the forwarding table had millions of entries, we’d still pick out 32 nodes.","zh_html":"沿树(tree)向下追踪路径(path)可以在常数时间(constant time)内完成。目的地址(destination address)的每一位(bit)访问一个节点(node)，而目的地址始终是 32 位(bits)，这是常数。即使转发表(forwarding table)有数百万个条目(entries)，我们仍然只会选出 32 个节点。"},{"en":"If there’s no overlapping ranges, every valid prefix corresponds to a leaf node. If ranges are overlapping, a non-leaf node could also be a valid prefix.","zh_html":"如果没有重叠范围(overlapping ranges)，每个有效前缀(valid prefix)都对应一个叶节点(leaf node)。如果范围重叠，非叶节点(non-leaf node)也可能是有效前缀。"},{"en":"As before, we use the destination address to trace a path down the tree. If we fall off the tree, we stop early and pick the longest prefix out of the nodes we visited.","zh_html":"和之前一样，我们使用目的地址(destination address)沿树(tree)向下追踪路径(path)。如果走出了树，就提前停止，并从已经访问过的节点(nodes)中选择最长前缀(longest prefix)。"},{"en":"As a slight optimization, as we walk down the tree, we could keep track of the longest prefix match seen so far. This will always be the most recent match, because the prefixes get longer as we move down the tree. If we fall off the tree, we use the longest prefix match (the most recent match we found).","zh_html":"作为一个小优化，当沿树(tree)向下走时，我们可以记录到目前为止看到的最长前缀匹配(longest prefix match)。它总是最近一次匹配，因为随着我们向树下方移动，前缀(prefixes)会越来越长。如果走出了树，就使用这个最长前缀匹配，也就是最近找到的匹配。"},{"en":"Note that the default route would be stored in the root node (0-length prefix). Our algorithm of walking down the tree ensures that we only use the default route if no other prefixes match.","zh_html":"注意，默认路由(default route)会存储在根节点(root node)，对应 0 长度前缀(0-length prefix)。我们沿树(tree)向下走的算法保证，只有在没有其他前缀(prefixes)匹配时，才会使用默认路由。"},{"en":"All routers have some form of longest prefix matching functionality, but some use more advanced solutions than others. For example, we could add heuristics and optimizations based on real-world Internet assumptions. Some destinations might be more popular, so we might want to look them up more efficiently. Some ports might be used for more ranges. The modern Internet has some conventions for prefix sizes (e.g. the longest IPv4 prefix for routes to other networks is 24 bits). We could also make optimizations for updating the forwarding tables.","zh_html":"所有路由器(routers)都具备某种形式的最长前缀匹配(longest prefix matching)功能，但有些路由器使用的解决方案比其他路由器更高级。例如，我们可以基于现实互联网(Internet)中的假设添加启发式方法(heuristics)和优化(optimizations)。某些目的地(destinations)可能更热门，因此我们可能希望更高效地查找它们。某些端口(ports)可能用于更多范围(ranges)。现代互联网对前缀大小(prefix sizes)有一些约定，例如通往其他网络的路由(routes)中，最长的 IPv4 前缀是 24 位(bits)。我们也可以针对转发表(forwarding tables)更新进行优化。"}]},"/routing/solutions.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Routing States","zh_html":"路由状态(routing states)"},{"en":"Bad Routing Strategies","zh_html":"糟糕的路由策略(bad routing strategies)"},{"en":"So far, we’ve defined the routing problem as this: When a router receives a packet, how does the router know where to forward the packet such that it will eventually arrive at the final destination?","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们把路由(routing)问题定义为：当路由器(router)收到一个分组(packet)时，它如何知道应该把分组转发到哪里，才能让分组最终到达最终目的地(final destination)？"},{"en":"Once we find an algorithm (a routing protocol) to solve this problem, we can apply that algorithm to generate an answer, which we’ll call a routing state. You can think of a routing state as a set of rules that each router uses to forward packets it receives. What does a routing state look like, and how can we check if a given routing state is valid or good?","zh_html":"一旦找到一种算法(algorithm)，也就是一种路由协议(routing protocol)，来解决这个问题，我们就可以应用该算法生成一个答案，称为<strong>路由状态(routing state)</strong>。可以把路由状态理解为每台路由器用来转发其收到分组(packets)的一组规则。路由状态长什么样？又如何检查给定的路由状态是有效(valid)还是好(good)？"},{"en":"To start, we could consider some bad strategies for generating routing states. One possible routing strategy is: The router forwards the packet to a randomly-selected neighbor. Intuitively, we can already see that routing states generated this way probably won’t be valid. If we use this strategy, we can’t be sure that packets will reach their final destination.","zh_html":"首先，我们可以考虑一些生成路由状态(routing states)的糟糕策略。一种可能的路由策略是：路由器(router)把分组(packet)转发给一个随机选择的邻居(neighbor)。直观上，我们已经可以看出，用这种方式生成的路由状态很可能无效。如果使用这种策略，我们无法确定分组会到达最终目的地。"},{"en":"Another possible bad strategy is: The router forwards a copy of the packet to every single one of its neighbors. Intuitively, this might be valid, in the sense that copies of the packet will eventually spread across the entire network and probably reach the destination. However, this strategy is inefficient, because it wastes a lot of bandwidth forwarding the packet to routers that were not needed to send the packet to its final destination.","zh_html":"另一种可能的糟糕策略是：路由器(router)把分组(packet)的一个副本转发给自己的每一个邻居(neighbor)。直观上，这也许是有效的，因为分组副本最终会扩散到整个网络，并且很可能到达目的地(destination)。然而，这种策略效率很低，因为它浪费大量带宽(bandwidth)，把分组转发给那些并不需要参与把分组送到最终目的地的路由器。"},{"en":"We can intuitively see that these two strategies are bad, but to analyze smarter routing protocols, we’ll need to formally define what a routing state looks like. Then, we’ll need to formalize what makes a routing state valid, and what makes a routing state good.","zh_html":"我们可以直观看出这两种策略不好，但为了分析更聪明的路由协议(routing protocols)，需要形式化定义路由状态(routing state)长什么样。然后还需要形式化说明什么使路由状态有效(valid)，以及什么使路由状态好(good)。"},{"en":"Forwarding Tables","zh_html":"转发表(forwarding tables)"},{"en":"In our model of the network, each router has some number of outgoing links connecting it to adjacent routers and hosts. In other words, in the underlying graph, each router node has some number of neighbors, connected to the router by an edge.","zh_html":"在我们的网络模型(model)中，每台路由器(router)都有若干条出站链路(outgoing links)，连接到相邻的路由器和主机(hosts)。换句话说，在底层图(graph)中，每个路由器节点(router node)都有若干邻居(neighbors)，它们通过一条边(edge)与该路由器相连。"},{"en":"When the router receives a packet, with its final destination in the metadata, the router needs to decide which of the adjacent routers or hosts the packet should be forwarded to. The next intermediate router that the packet will be forwarded to is called the next hop.","zh_html":"当路由器(router)收到一个分组(packet)，且其元数据(metadata)中包含最终目的地(final destination)时，路由器需要决定应该把分组转发给哪一个相邻的路由器或主机。分组将被转发到的下一个中间路由器(intermediate router)称为<strong>下一跳(next hop)</strong>。"},{"en":"For example, consider this network. If R2 receives a packet whose final destination is B, the natural corresponding next hop would be R3. The possible choices of next hop are R1, R3, and R4 (the three routers adjacent to R2), and R3 is the next hop that sends the packet closer to B.","zh_html":"例如，考虑这个网络。如果 R2 收到一个最终目的地为 B 的分组(packet)，自然对应的下一跳(next hop)会是 R3。可能的下一跳选择是 R1、R3 和 R4，也就是与 R2 相邻的三台路由器，其中 R3 是会把分组送得更接近 B 的下一跳。"},{"en":"If R2 instead receives a packet whose final destination is A, then the natural corresponding next hop would be R1 instead.","zh_html":"如果 R2 收到的分组(packet)最终目的地是 A，那么自然对应的下一跳(next hop)则会是 R1。"},{"en":"For each possible final destination, we can write down the corresponding next hop to forward the packet closer to that destination. The result is called a forwarding table.","zh_html":"对于每个可能的最终目的地(final destination)，我们都可以写下对应的下一跳(next hop)，用来把分组(packet)转发得更接近该目的地。得到的结果称为<strong>转发表(forwarding table)</strong>。"},{"en":"Note that the in the mapping of destination to next hop, a next hop can be used more than once. For example, in R2’s forwarding table, packets destined for B and packets destined for C will both be forwarded to R3.","zh_html":"注意，在目的地(destination)到下一跳(next hop)的映射中，一个下一跳可以被使用多次。例如，在 R2 的转发表(forwarding table)中，目的地为 B 的分组和目的地为 C 的分组都会被转发给 R3。"},{"en":"By writing down the forwarding table for each intermediate router, we now have a full routing state for the network. In other words, given a packet with some final destination, we know exactly how each router will forward that packet.","zh_html":"为每台中间路由器(intermediate router)写下转发表(forwarding table)后，我们就得到了网络的完整路由状态(routing state)。换句话说，给定一个带有某个最终目的地的分组(packet)，我们就能准确知道每台路由器会如何转发该分组。"},{"en":"In the physical world, instead of mapping destinations to next hops, routers will often map destinations to physical ports, where each physical port corresponds to a link. In the graph model, we would now be mapping each destination to an edge, instead of mapping each destination to a neighboring node. In the physical world, you can think of this as a router having several outgoing wires, where each wire is connected to another router. Instead of writing down neighboring routers in the forwarding table, the router instead writes down which wire a packet should be sent along.","zh_html":"在物理世界中，路由器(routers)通常不是把目的地(destinations)映射到下一跳(next hops)，而是映射到<strong>物理端口(physical ports)</strong>，其中每个物理端口对应一条链路(link)。在图模型(graph model)中，这相当于把每个目的地映射到一条边(edge)，而不是映射到一个相邻节点(neighboring node)。在物理世界中，可以把它理解为路由器有几根出站线缆(outgoing wires)，每根线缆连接到另一台路由器。路由器不是在转发表中写下相邻路由器，而是写下分组应该沿哪根线缆发送。"},{"en":"This is a subtle distinction, and it reflects the fact that the router doesn’t really care about the identity of the neighboring router. The only decision the router needs to make is to send the packet along one of the wires, regardless of who the wire is connected to. In these notes, we’ll draw forwarding tables as mapping destinations to next hops (instead of physical ports), for simplicity.","zh_html":"这是一个细微区别，反映出路由器(router)其实并不关心相邻路由器的身份。路由器唯一需要做出的决定，是把分组(packet)沿其中一根线缆发送出去，而不管这根线缆连接的是谁。为简单起见，在这些笔记中，我们会把转发表(forwarding tables)画成目的地到下一跳(next hops)的映射，而不是目的地到物理端口(physical ports)的映射。"},{"en":"Destination-Based Forwarding","zh_html":"基于目的地的转发(destination-based forwarding)"},{"en":"A consequence of using a forwarding table is that given a packet, the decision of where to forward the packet depends only on the destination field of the packet. In other words, if a router receives many different packets, all with the same destination, they will all be routed to the same next hop (assuming the forwarding table stays unchanged). Since each destination is only mapped to a single next hop, there’s no way for two packets with the same destination to be forwarded to different routers. This approach is called destination-based forwarding or destination-based routing.","zh_html":"使用转发表(forwarding table)的一个结果是：给定一个分组(packet)，路由器决定把它转发到哪里时，只取决于分组的目的地字段(destination field)。换句话说，如果一台路由器收到许多不同分组，但它们都有相同目的地，那么只要转发表保持不变，它们都会被路由到同一个下一跳(next hop)。由于每个目的地只映射到一个下一跳，两个具有相同目的地的分组不可能被转发给不同路由器。这种方法称为<strong>基于目的地的转发(destination-based forwarding)</strong>或<strong>基于目的地的路由(destination-based routing)</strong>。"},{"en":"Destination-based routing is the most common approach to routing, and it’s what’s used in the modern Internet. In theory, other approaches could exist where additional metadata is used to make forwarding decision, but these are usually only used in limited applications (e.g. inside a particular local network).","zh_html":"基于目的地的路由(destination-based routing)是最常见的路由(routing)方法，也是现代互联网(Internet)使用的方法。理论上，也可能存在使用额外元数据(metadata)来做转发决策(forwarding decision)的其他方法，但这些方法通常只用于有限的应用场景，例如某个特定本地网络(local network)内部。"},{"en":"In later units, when we consider data center topologies, we might consider destination-based forwarding approaches where there might be more than one next hop for a specific destination. In this unit, we’ll assume that each destination is mapped to only one next hop.","zh_html":"在后面的单元中，考虑数据中心拓扑(data center topologies)时，我们可能会讨论某个特定目的地有多个下一跳(next hop)的基于目的地转发(destination-based forwarding)方法。在本单元中，我们假设每个目的地只映射到一个下一跳。"},{"en":"Routing vs. Forwarding","zh_html":"路由(routing)与转发(forwarding)"},{"en":"Now that we’ve introduced the idea of a forwarding table, we need to make a distinction between the process of creating the forwarding table, and the process of using the forwarding table.","zh_html":"现在我们已经引入了转发表(forwarding table)的概念，需要区分创建转发表的过程和使用转发表的过程。"},{"en":"Routing is the process of routers communicating with each other to determine how to populate their forwarding tables.","zh_html":"<strong>路由(routing)</strong>是路由器(routers)彼此通信以决定如何填充其转发表(forwarding tables)的过程。"},{"en":"Forwarding is the process of receiving a packet, looking up its appropriate next hop in the table, and sending the packet to the appropriate neighbor.","zh_html":"<strong>转发(forwarding)</strong>是接收一个分组(packet)，在表中查找合适的下一跳(next hop)，并把分组发送给合适邻居(neighbor)的过程。"},{"en":"Forwarding is not the same as routing. When forwarding packets, routers use the existing forwarding table, with no knowledge of how that table was generated.","zh_html":"转发(forwarding)不同于路由(routing)。转发分组(packets)时，路由器使用已有转发表(forwarding table)，并不知道该表是如何生成的。"},{"en":"Forwarding is a local process. When a router is forwarding packets, the router doesn’t need to know the full network topology. The router also doesn’t care about where the packet goes after it’s been forwarded to the next hop. The router only needs to know about the arriving packet, and its own forwarding table.","zh_html":"转发(forwarding)是一个局部过程(local process)。当路由器(router)转发分组(packets)时，它不需要知道完整网络拓扑(network topology)。路由器也不关心分组被转发到下一跳(next hop)之后会去哪里。路由器只需要知道到达的分组以及自己的转发表(forwarding table)。"},{"en":"By contrast, routing is a global process. In order to fill out the forwarding tables, we will need to learn something about the global topology of the network.","zh_html":"相比之下，路由(routing)是一个全局过程(global process)。为了填充转发表(forwarding tables)，我们需要了解网络的全局拓扑(global topology)中的一些信息。"},{"en":"For example, in when filling in R2’s forwarding table, we had to somehow learn that destination B is associated with R3, even though host B is not directly connected to R2. During routing, each router will need to know about non-local destinations as well.","zh_html":"例如，在填充 R2 的转发表(forwarding table)时，我们必须以某种方式知道目的地 B 与 R3 相关联，即使主机 B 并没有直接连接到 R2。在路由(routing)期间，每台路由器也需要知道非本地目的地(non-local destinations)。"},{"en":"Routing State Validity is Global","zh_html":"路由状态有效性(routing state validity)是全局的"},{"en":"Recall that a routing state consists of a forwarding table for each router, which collectively tells us how packets will travel through the network. Given a routing state, how can we tell if the routing state is correct or incorrect?","zh_html":"回想一下，路由状态(routing state)由每台路由器(router)的一张转发表(forwarding table)组成，整体上告诉我们分组(packets)会如何穿过网络。给定一个路由状态，我们如何判断它是正确还是错误？"},{"en":"First, we need to formally define routing state validity to determine whether a routing state is valid (though this term may not be widely used outside CS 168 at UC Berkeley). The main requirement for validity is: the routing state needs to produce forwarding decisions that ensure that packets actually reach their destination.","zh_html":"首先，我们需要形式化定义<strong>路由状态有效性(routing state validity)</strong>，以判断一个路由状态是否有效(valid)，虽然这个术语在 UC Berkeley 的 CS 168 之外可能并不常用。有效性的主要要求是：路由状态需要产生能够确保分组(packets)实际到达目的地(destination)的转发决策(forwarding decisions)。"},{"en":"Note that validity must be evaluated in the global context, not a local context. Looking at local routing state, such as a single router’s forwarding table, cannot tell us whether a routing state is valid. For example, in a router R2’s local forwarding table, we might see that the next hop for destination A is router R3, but we have no way to decide if this is valid. Will forwarding packets to R3 help packets reach destination A? There’s no way to tell from just the forwarding table.","zh_html":"注意，有效性(validity)必须在全局上下文(global context)中评估，而不是在局部上下文(local context)中评估。只看局部路由状态(local routing state)，例如单台路由器(router)的转发表(forwarding table)，无法告诉我们一个路由状态是否有效。例如，在路由器 R2 的本地转发表中，我们可能看到目的地 A 的下一跳(next hop)是路由器 R3，但无法判断这是否有效。把分组(packets)转发给 R3 会帮助分组到达目的地 A 吗？仅从这张转发表无法得知。"},{"en":"Instead, we need to consider the global routing state, which consists of the collection of all the forwarding tables in all of the routers.","zh_html":"相反，我们需要考虑全局路由状态(global routing state)，它由所有路由器中的所有转发表(forwarding tables)集合组成。"},{"en":"Routing State Validity Definition","zh_html":"路由状态有效性定义(routing state validity definition)"},{"en":"Now, we can define a formal condition that we can use to check whether or not packets will reach their destination for a given routing state.","zh_html":"现在，我们可以定义一个形式化条件，用来检查在给定路由状态(routing state)下，分组(packets)是否会到达其目的地(destination)。"},{"en":"A global routing state is valid if and only if, for any destination, packets do not get stuck in dead ends or loops.","zh_html":"一个全局路由状态(global routing state)有效，当且仅当对任意目的地(destination)，分组(packets)都不会卡在死路(dead ends)或环路(loops)中。"},{"en":"A dead end occurs if a packet arrives at a router, but the router doesn’t know how to forward the packet to its destination, so the packet is not forwarded. This might occur if the router’s forwarding table doesn’t contain an entry for the packet’s destination.","zh_html":"如果一个分组(packet)到达某台路由器(router)，但该路由器不知道如何把它转发到目的地(destination)，于是分组没有被继续转发，就发生了<strong>死路(dead end)</strong>。如果路由器的转发表(forwarding table)没有包含该分组目的地的条目(entry)，就可能发生这种情况。"},{"en":"Note that the dead end condition only applies to the intermediate routers, and not the end hosts. When a packet reaches the destination end host, there’s no need for the end host to forward the packet any further, so we won’t consider end hosts in the dead end condition.","zh_html":"注意，死路(dead end)条件只适用于中间路由器(intermediate routers)，不适用于端主机(end hosts)。当分组(packet)到达目的地端主机(destination end host)时，端主机不需要再继续转发该分组，因此在死路条件中我们不会考虑端主机。"},{"en":"A loop occurs if a packet is sent in a cycle around the same of nodes. Note that because we’re using destination-based forwarding, where the next hop only depends on the destination, once a packet enters a loop, it will be trapped in the loop forever. When the packet arrives at the router the first time, or the 10th time, or the 500th time, it will be forwarded the exact same way (since the final destination is the same). Since this applies to every router on the loop, the packet will be stuck in the loop forever.","zh_html":"如果一个分组(packet)沿同一组节点(nodes)形成的循环(cycle)反复发送，就发生了<strong>环路(loop)</strong>。注意，由于我们使用的是基于目的地的转发(destination-based forwarding)，下一跳(next hop)只取决于目的地(destination)，一旦分组进入环路，就会永远困在环路中。无论分组第一次、第 10 次还是第 500 次到达某台路由器，它都会以完全相同的方式被转发，因为最终目的地相同。由于环路上的每台路由器都如此，分组会永远卡在环路里。"},{"en":"This condition (no dead ends, no loops) is both necessary and sufficient for a route to be valid. Let’s check both directions of this logical implication.","zh_html":"这个条件（没有死路、没有环路）既是路由(route)有效的必要条件，也是充分条件。让我们检查这个逻辑蕴含的两个方向。"},{"en":"No dead ends and no loops is a necessary condition for validity. In other words, a state is valid only if there are no dead ends and no loops.","zh_html":"没有死路(dead ends)且没有环路(loops)是有效性(validity)的必要条件。换句话说，一个状态只有在没有死路和环路时才有效。"},{"en":"Proof: If there’s a dead end, the packet won’t reach the destination. The packet will reach the dead end and not be forwarded.","zh_html":"证明：如果存在死路(dead end)，分组(packet)就无法到达目的地。分组会到达死路，并且不会被继续转发。"},{"en":"If there are loops, the packet won’t reach the destination. The packet will be trapped in the loop forever (because of destination-based forwarding, described earlier). Also, note that the final destination can’t be part of the loop, since the destination won’t forward the packet. Therefore, a packet trapped in the loop won’t reach the destination.","zh_html":"如果存在环路(loops)，分组(packet)就无法到达目的地。分组会永远困在环路中，因为前面描述的基于目的地的转发(destination-based forwarding)会这样工作。另外，注意最终目的地(final destination)不可能是环路的一部分，因为目的地不会继续转发分组。因此，困在环路中的分组不会到达目的地。"},{"en":"Now, let’s check the other direction. If there are no loops and no dead ends, then the state is valid.","zh_html":"现在，让我们检查另一个方向。如果没有环路(loops)且没有死路(dead ends)，那么该状态就是有效的。"},{"en":"Proof: Assume that the routing state has no loops or dead ends. A packet won’t reach the same node twice (because there are no loops). Also, the packet won’t stop before reaching the destination (because there are no dead ends). Therefore, the packet must keep wandering through the network, reaching different nodes. There are only a finite number of unique nodes to visit, so the packet must eventually reach the destination. Therefore, the routing state must be valid.","zh_html":"证明：假设路由状态(routing state)没有环路也没有死路。分组(packet)不会两次到达同一个节点(node)，因为没有环路。分组也不会在到达目的地之前停止，因为没有死路。因此，分组必须不断在网络中游走，到达不同节点。可访问的唯一节点数量是有限的，所以分组最终必须到达目的地。因此，路由状态必然有效。"},{"en":"Directed Delivery Trees","zh_html":"有向交付树(directed delivery trees)"},{"en":"Now that we have a formal definition for routing state validity, we can ask: given a global routing state, how can we check if it’s valid?","zh_html":"既然已经有了路由状态有效性(routing state validity)的形式化定义，我们可以问：给定一个全局路由状态(global routing state)，如何检查它是否有效？"},{"en":"To simplify the problem, let’s start by considering only a single destination end host, ignoring all other end hosts. In each router, we can look up this destination to get the corresponding next hop, which tells us how each router will forward packets meant for this destination.","zh_html":"为了简化问题，我们先只考虑单个目的地端主机(destination end host)，忽略所有其他端主机。在每台路由器(router)中，我们可以查找这个目的地，得到对应的下一跳(next hop)，这会告诉我们每台路由器会如何转发发往该目的地的分组(packets)。"},{"en":"We can represent the next hop at each router (for this single destination) as an arrow, which shows us all the possible paths that this packet might take to reach the single destination.","zh_html":"我们可以把每台路由器针对这个单一目的地的下一跳(next hop)表示为一个箭头(arrow)，这会展示该分组(packet)为了到达这个单一目的地可能走的所有路径(paths)。"},{"en":"In the resulting graph, each node will only have one outgoing arrow. This reflects our assumption that in each router’s forwarding table, there is only one next hop corresponding to a destination.","zh_html":"在得到的图(graph)中，每个节点(node)只有一个出箭头(outgoing arrow)。这反映了我们的假设：在每台路由器(router)的转发表(forwarding table)中，对应某个目的地(destination)只有一个下一跳(next hop)。"},{"en":"Notice that in the resulting graph, once two paths meet, they never split. In other words, even if there are multiple incoming arrows (paths) to a node, since there is only one outgoing arrow, those paths will now converge into a single path. This reflects our destination-based forwarding approach, because each router only uses the final destination to decide how to forward a packet. The router does not care how the packet arrived at the router in the first place.","zh_html":"注意，在得到的图(graph)中，两条路径(paths)一旦相遇，就不会再分叉。换句话说，即使有多个入箭头(incoming arrows)或路径到达某个节点，由于只有一个出箭头，这些路径之后会汇聚成一条路径。这反映了我们的基于目的地转发(destination-based forwarding)方法，因为每台路由器只使用最终目的地(final destination)来决定如何转发分组(packet)。路由器并不关心分组最初是如何到达该路由器的。"},{"en":"The arrows we’ve drawn form a set of paths that a packet can take to reach the single destination. This set of paths is called a directed delivery tree.","zh_html":"我们画出的这些箭头形成了一组路径(paths)，分组(packet)可以沿这些路径到达单一目的地。这组路径称为<strong>有向交付树(directed delivery tree)</strong>。"},{"en":"In graph terms, the arrows in a valid delivery tree must form an oriented spanning tree, rooted at the destination. Recall that a spanning tree is a set of edges in the graph that touch every node and form a tree. We want the delivery tree to be a tree, since there should be no cycles (packets can’t travel in loops). We want the delivery tree to be spanning (touch every node), because we want to be able to reach the destination from everywhere. The delivery tree is oriented because the edges have arrows, which tells us which direction to forward the packet.","zh_html":"用图论(graph)术语来说，有效交付树(delivery tree)中的箭头必须形成一棵以目的地(destination)为根的<strong>有向生成树(oriented spanning tree)</strong>。回想一下，生成树(spanning tree)是图(graph)中的一组边(edges)，它们接触每个节点并形成一棵树(tree)。我们希望交付树是一棵树，因为不应该有环(cycles)，分组不能在环路(loops)中移动。我们希望交付树是生成的(spanning)，也就是接触每个节点，因为我们希望从任何地方都能到达目的地。交付树是有向的(oriented)，因为边带有箭头，告诉我们应该沿哪个方向转发分组。"},{"en":"All edges in a valid delivery tree should point toward the destination. In other words, starting from any node, following the arrows should always result in reaching the destination.","zh_html":"有效交付树(delivery tree)中的所有边(edges)都应该指向目的地(destination)。换句话说，从任意节点(node)出发沿箭头前进，最终都应该到达目的地。"},{"en":"Verifying Routing State Validity","zh_html":"验证路由状态有效性(verifying routing state validity)"},{"en":"As before, let’s consider only a single destination end host, ignoring all other end hosts.","zh_html":"和之前一样，我们只考虑单个目的地端主机(destination end host)，忽略所有其他端主机(end hosts)。"},{"en":"Example: Even though there are multiple end hosts here, let’s only consider end host A.","zh_html":"示例：虽然这里有多个端主机(end hosts)，但我们只考虑端主机 A。"},{"en":"Using the forwarding tables at each router, we will draw the arrows into the network to form the directed delivery tree for this single destination. Formally, for each router (node in the graph), we will draw a single outgoing arrow from that node.","zh_html":"使用每台路由器(router)上的转发表(forwarding tables)，我们会把箭头画入网络中，为这个单一目的地形成有向交付树(directed delivery tree)。形式化地说，对于每台路由器，也就是图(graph)中的每个节点(node)，我们都会从该节点画出一个单独的出箭头(outgoing arrow)。"},{"en":"Example: Using the forwarding tables (not shown), we can draw one outgoing arrow per router.","zh_html":"示例：使用转发表(forwarding tables，未显示)，我们可以为每台路由器画一个出箭头(outgoing arrow)。"},{"en":"For simplicity, at this point we can delete all the links without arrows on them. These links without arrows will never be used to send packets to the single destination, since they are not on the delivery tree.","zh_html":"为简单起见，此时可以删除所有没有箭头的链路(links)。这些没有箭头的链路永远不会用于把分组(packets)发送到这个单一目的地，因为它们不在交付树(delivery tree)上。"},{"en":"Example: We can delete all the links without arrows.","zh_html":"示例：我们可以删除所有没有箭头的链路(links)。"},{"en":"If the remaining graph is a valid directed delivery tree (spanning tree, all arrows pointing toward destination), then we can say that the routing state is valid for this single destination.","zh_html":"如果剩下的图(graph)是一棵有效的有向交付树(directed delivery tree)，即一棵生成树(spanning tree)，且所有箭头都指向目的地(destination)，那么可以说该路由状态(routing state)对这个单一目的地是有效的。"},{"en":"In the above example, the residual graph is indeed a valid spanning tree converging at A, so we can say this routing state is valid for A.","zh_html":"在上面的示例中，剩余图(residual graph)确实是一棵收敛到 A 的有效生成树(spanning tree)，所以可以说这个路由状态(routing state)对 A 有效。"},{"en":"Here are some examples of invalid routing states:","zh_html":"下面是一些无效路由状态(invalid routing states)的例子："},{"en":"This state is invalid. Intuitively, there is a dead end router. A packet bound for A could get sent to this router, and this router would discard the packet without forwarding it. Formally, the remaining graph is not a spanning tree, because the edges are not all connected (there are two disconnected components, which is not allowed in a tree).","zh_html":"这个状态是无效的。直观上，存在一台死路路由器(dead end router)。发往 A 的分组(packet)可能会被发送到这台路由器，而这台路由器会丢弃分组，不再转发。形式化地说，剩余图(residual graph)不是生成树(spanning tree)，因为这些边(edges)并非全部连通，存在两个不连通分量(disconnected components)，而树中不允许这样。"},{"en":"This state is also invalid. Intuitively, there is a loop that the packet could get stuck in. Formally, the remaining graph is not a spanning tree, because the edges are disconnected, and there is a cycle.","zh_html":"这个状态同样无效。直观上，存在一个分组(packet)可能卡住的环路(loop)。形式化地说，剩余图(residual graph)不是生成树(spanning tree)，因为这些边(edges)不连通，并且存在一个环(cycle)。"},{"en":"We can repeat this process, once for every different end host (isolating a different end host each time). If the routing state is valid for all destinations, then we can say that the routing state is valid, and will always deliver packets to their correct destinations.","zh_html":"我们可以对每个不同的端主机(end host)重复这个过程，每次隔离出一个不同的端主机。如果路由状态(routing state)对所有目的地(destinations)都有效，那么可以说该路由状态有效，并且总能把分组(packets)交付到正确目的地。"},{"en":"Least-Cost Routing","zh_html":"最低成本路由(least-cost routing)"},{"en":"Now that we have a definition of what makes a routing state valid (routes have no loops and dead ends), we can additionally define what makes a routing state good. It’s possible that a network has multiple valid routing states, and we want some metric that can help us determine whether one route is better than another.","zh_html":"现在我们已经定义了什么使路由状态(routing state)有效，也就是路由(routes)没有环路和死路；还可以进一步定义什么使路由状态好(good)。一个网络可能有多个有效路由状态，我们希望有某种度量(metric)帮助判断一条路由是否优于另一条。"},{"en":"Least-cost routing is a common approach for measuring whether a route is good. In least-cost routing, we assign a numeric cost to every link, and look for routes that minimize the cost. In other words, we want routes that result in packets traveling along the lowest-cost paths to their destinations.","zh_html":"最低成本路由(least-cost routing)是衡量路由(route)是否好的常见方法。在最低成本路由中，我们为每条链路(link)分配一个数值成本(numeric cost)，并寻找使成本最小的路由。换句话说，我们希望得到让分组(packets)沿最低成本路径(lowest-cost paths)前往目的地(destinations)的路由。"},{"en":"There are many different costs we could consider assigning to links. The cost could depend on the price of building the link, the propagation delay, the physical distance of the link, the unreliability, the bandwidth, among other factors. For example, we could assign costs based on the quality of the link (bandwidth and propagation delay), such that the lowest-cost path prefers higher-quality links.","zh_html":"我们可以考虑给链路(links)分配许多不同成本(costs)。成本可以取决于建设链路的价格、传播延迟(propagation delay)、链路的物理距离、可靠性不足(unreliability)、带宽(bandwidth)等因素。例如，我们可以基于链路质量(带宽和传播延迟)分配成本，使最低成本路径偏好质量更高的链路。"},{"en":"By allowing operators to set link costs arbitrarily, we give the operator the ability to optimize the network for their specific needs. The costs we assign depend on the operator’s goals for the network. If we had a 400 Gbps link with 20 ms propagation delay, and a 10 Gbps link with 5 ms propagation delay, which one is lower-cost? It depends on if we’re optimizing for bandwidth, propagation delay, some combination, or something else entirely.","zh_html":"允许运营方(operators)任意设置链路成本(link costs)，就给了运营方按自身具体需求优化网络的能力。我们分配的成本取决于运营方对网络的目标。如果有一条 400 Gbps、传播延迟 20 ms 的链路，以及一条 10 Gbps、传播延迟 5 ms 的链路，哪一条成本更低？这取决于我们是在优化带宽、传播延迟、二者的某种组合，还是完全不同的目标。"},{"en":"If we assign a cost of 1 to every link, then the least-cost path is the path that travels along the fewest links. We sometimes call this minimizing the hop count. In these notes, if the edges of a graph are not labeled with a cost, you can assume all the edges have cost 1,","zh_html":"如果我们给每条链路(link)分配成本 1，那么最低成本路径(least-cost path)就是经过链路数量最少的路径。我们有时称这为最小化跳数(hop count)。在这些笔记中，如果图(graph)的边(edges)没有标注成本，可以假设所有边的成本都是 1，"},{"en":"The operator of a network can decide how to assign costs to each link. The operator might manually assign costs. Or, the operator could have the network automatically configure the costs, although this may not work with some metrics that can’t be automatically measured (e.g. the network has no idea about the financial cost to build the link).","zh_html":"网络的运营方(operator)可以决定如何为每条链路(link)分配成本(costs)。运营方可以手动分配成本。或者，运营方也可以让网络自动配置成本，不过这对某些无法自动测量的度量(metrics)可能不起作用，例如网络并不知道建设链路的财务成本。"},{"en":"When designing a routing protocol, we can abstract away how the costs were assigned. From the routing protocol’s perspective, somebody else (e.g. the network operator) has already assigned the costs, based on something that they consider important. The algorithm takes in the costs as an input, and computes the least-cost paths, regardless of what the costs actually represent.","zh_html":"设计路由协议(routing protocol)时，我们可以抽象掉成本是如何分配的。从路由协议的角度看，已经有其他人，例如网络运营方(network operator)，根据他们认为重要的因素分配了成本。算法(algorithm)把成本作为输入，并计算最低成本路径(least-cost paths)，而不关心这些成本实际代表什么。"},{"en":"Note that costs are local to each router. A router knows about the cost of its own outgoing links, but there is no way for the router to automatically know the costs of all links. This is consistent with the constraint we mentioned earlier, where routers don’t have a global view of the entire network’s topology.","zh_html":"注意，成本(costs)对每台路由器(router)而言是局部的(local)。路由器知道自己出站链路(outgoing links)的成本，但无法自动知道所有链路的成本。这与前面提到的约束一致：路由器没有整个网络拓扑(network topology)的全局视图(global view)。"},{"en":"For simplicity, routing protocols make some assumptions about how the costs are defined.","zh_html":"为简单起见，路由协议(routing protocols)会对成本(costs)的定义做一些假设。"},{"en":"We’ll assume that costs are always positive integers. This is consistent with many common real-life metrics, such as length of a link or monetary cost of a link. If we’re trying to minimize the total physical distance traveled by a packet, a negative link cost doesn’t make sense. You can’t travel along a link and decrease the total distance traveled. This assumption will help simplify our protocols later, since we won’t have to worry about edge cases like negative-weight loops (where the least-cost solution would be to travel around the loop forever).","zh_html":"我们会假设成本(costs)总是正整数(positive integers)。这与许多常见的现实度量(metrics)一致，例如链路长度或链路的货币成本。如果我们试图最小化分组(packet)走过的总物理距离，负链路成本(negative link cost)就没有意义。你不可能沿一条链路前进却减少已经走过的总距离。这个假设会帮助我们后面简化协议，因为我们不必担心负权重环(negative-weight loops)这类边界情况；在那种情况下，最低成本解会是永远绕着环路走。"},{"en":"We’ll assume that costs are symmetrical. The cost from A to B is the same as the cost from B to A. This reflects the diagrams we’ll draw, where an edge is labeled with a single symmetric cost. In theory, it’s possible to have asymmetric link costs, but this is not done in practice, and would lead to more complicated routing protocols.","zh_html":"我们会假设成本(costs)是对称的(symmetrical)。从 A 到 B 的成本与从 B 到 A 的成本相同。这反映了我们会画出的图(diagrams)：一条边(edge)只标一个对称成本。理论上，可以有非对称链路成本(asymmetric link costs)，但实践中不会这样做，而且会导致更复杂的路由协议(routing protocols)。"},{"en":"With these assumptions, our definition of good routes (least-cost) is consistent with our definition of valid routes. In particular, a least-cost route won’t have any loops, because costs are positive (traversing the loop would only increase the cost).","zh_html":"在这些假设下，我们对好路由(good routes)的定义，也就是最低成本(least-cost)，与有效路由(valid routes)的定义一致。特别是，最低成本路由不会有任何环路(loops)，因为成本为正，穿过环路只会增加成本。"},{"en":"Static Routing","zh_html":"静态路由(static routing)"},{"en":"One possible way to generate routes is to have the network operator manually populate the forwarding table. This is known as static routing.","zh_html":"生成路由(routes)的一种可能方式，是让网络运营方(network operator)手动填充转发表(forwarding table)。这称为静态路由(static routing)。"},{"en":"Static routing by itself isn’t practical (e.g. not scalable, prone to human error), but even with a routing protocol implemented, some routes still need to be manually created by operators. You can think of these manual routes as the “trivial” or “base case” routes, from which the routing protocol generates more complex routes.","zh_html":"静态路由(static routing)本身并不实用，例如不可扩展且容易发生人为错误；但即使已经实现了路由协议(routing protocol)，某些路由仍然需要由运营方手动创建。可以把这些手动路由看作“平凡(trivial)”或“基本情况(base case)”路由，路由协议会从它们出发生成更复杂的路由。"},{"en":"If we’re directly connected to another machine that we want to route packets to, we can manually configure a route to forward packets to that other machine. These routes are called direct routes or connected routes. For example, your home router is connected to your personal computer with a link, so your home router can add an entry in the forwarding table corresponding to your computer. This entry is added by telling the router about the connection, and is not added from running any routing protocol.","zh_html":"如果我们直接连接到另一台想要路由分组(packets)到达的机器，就可以手动配置一条路由(route)，把分组转发到那台机器。这些路由称为直连路由(direct routes)或连接路由(connected routes)。例如，你的家庭路由器(home router)通过一条链路(link)连接到你的个人计算机，因此家庭路由器可以在转发表(forwarding table)中添加一个对应你计算机的条目(entry)。这个条目是通过告知路由器该连接而添加的，并不是通过运行任何路由协议得到的。"},{"en":"It is also possible to use static routing to hard-code entries for destinations in the forwarding table, even if we aren’t directly connected to that destination. This can be useful if there’s a route that never changes, and we want that route to always stay in our forwarding table, regardless of what the routing protocol is doing.","zh_html":"即使我们没有直接连接到某个目的地(destination)，也可以使用静态路由(static routing)在转发表(forwarding table)中为该目的地硬编码(hard-code)条目。如果某条路由(route)永远不会变化，并且我们希望无论路由协议(routing protocol)如何运行，这条路由都始终保留在转发表中，这会很有用。"}]},"/transport/cc-design.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Congestion Control Design","zh_html":"拥塞控制设计"},{"en":"Host-Based Algorithm Sketch","zh_html":"基于主机的算法草图"},{"en":"Most host-based algorithms follow the same general approach, and the differences arise in three key choice points.","zh_html":"大多数基于主机的算法都遵循相同的一般方法，区别在于三个关键选择点。"},{"en":"Each source independently runs the following logic repeatedly, in a loop: Try sending at a rate R for some period of time. Then, ask: Did I experience congestion in this time period? If yes, then reduce R. If no, then increase R.","zh_html":"每个发送方(source)独立地循环运行以下逻辑：尝试以速率 R 发送一段时间。然后问：在这段时间内我是否经历了拥塞(congestion)？如果是，就降低 R；如果否，就增加 R。"},{"en":"One missing piece is: what rate do we initially start sending at? We’ll need some way to pick the initial rate R.","zh_html":"还缺少一块拼图：我们最初以什么速率开始发送？我们需要某种方式来选择初始速率 R。"},{"en":"The three key choice points are: How do we pick the initial rate? How do we detect congestion? By how much should we increase and decrease each time?","zh_html":"三个关键选择点是：如何选择初始速率？如何检测拥塞？每次应该增减多少？"},{"en":"Detecting Congestion","zh_html":"检测拥塞(Detecting Congestion)"},{"en":"How does the sender detect if the network is congested? There are two common approaches.","zh_html":"发送方如何检测网络是否拥塞？有两种常见方法。"},{"en":"The sender could check for packet loss. This is the approach commonly used by TCP. This approach is good because the signal is unambiguous. Every packet is either marked as lost (timeout or duplicate ack), or not lost. Also, TCP already detects lost packets in order to re-send them, so we don’t need to re-implement this from scratch.","zh_html":"发送方可以检查分组丢失(packet loss)。这是 TCP 常用的方法。这种方法的优点是信号明确无误：每个分组要么标记为丢失(超时或重复 ack(duplicate ack))，要么没有丢失。此外，TCP 已经为了重传而检测丢失的分组，所以我们不需要从头重新实现。"},{"en":"This approach can be bad because sometimes, packet loss is due to corruption (bad checksum), not congestion. In fact, TCP gets confused and behaves poorly when a link is not congested, but frequently corrupts packets. Also, TCP could be confused by packets being reordered. A packet arriving late could be mistakenly considered lost.","zh_html":"这种方法的缺点在于，有时分组丢失是由于损坏(bad checksum，校验和错误)而不是拥塞。事实上，当一条链路没有拥塞但频繁损坏分组时，TCP 会感到困惑并表现不佳。此外，TCP 可能被乱序的分组所迷惑。一个晚到的分组可能被错误地认为已丢失。"},{"en":"Another key downside to this approach is, we detect congestion late. By the time packets are being dropped, router queues are already full and packets are being delayed.","zh_html":"这种方法的另一个关键缺点是，我们检测拥塞很晚。当分组开始被丢弃时，路由器队列(router queues)已经满了，分组已经被延迟了。"},{"en":"Instead of checking for packet loss, the sender could instead detect congestion by checking packet delay. The sender can measure the time between sending a packet and receiving an ack for that packet. If the sender notices that the delay is increasing, that could be a sign of congestion.","zh_html":"除了检查分组丢失，发送方还可以通过检查分组延迟(packet delay)来检测拥塞。发送方可以测量发送一个分组与收到该分组 ack 之间的时间。如果发送方注意到延迟在增加，那可能是拥塞的信号。"},{"en":"Historically, accurately measuring delay has been considered difficult. Packet delay can vary depending on queue size and other traffic. For many years, packet delay was not widely deployed, though in recent years, Google’s BBR protocol (2016) has shown that delay-based algorithms is possible, and some services (e.g. Google services) have adopted delay-based algorithms.","zh_html":"历史上，精确测量延迟被认为很困难。分组延迟会因队列大小和其他流量而变化。多年来，基于延迟的方法并未广泛部署；不过近年来，Google 的 BBR 协议(2016)已经证明基于延迟的算法是可行的，一些服务(如 Google 服务)已经采用了基于延迟的算法。"},{"en":"Discovering Initial Rate","zh_html":"发现初始速率(Discovering Initial Rate)"},{"en":"When a connection first starts out, we have to figure out an initial rate for sending packets. We can learn an initial rate using a discovery process, where we try a few different rates to get a estimate of the available bandwidth.","zh_html":"当一个连接(connection)刚开始时，我们必须确定一个发送分组的初始速率。我们可以通过一个发现过程(discovery process)来学习初始速率，尝试几个不同的速率来估计可用带宽(available bandwidth)。"},{"en":"We want this discovery process to be safe, so we should start with slow rates. We don’t want to immediately flood the network with packets.","zh_html":"我们希望这个发现过程是安全的，所以应该从较低的速率开始。我们不希望立即用分组淹没网络。"},{"en":"At the same time, we want the discovery process to quickly discover the available bandwidth, for efficiency. To achieve this, we will quickly increase the rate on each subsequent try. If the discovery process takes a long time, we’ve wasted time that we could have spent sending packets at the optimal rate. As an example, suppose we add 0.5 Mbps to the rate every 100ms, until we detect congestion (loss). If the available bandwidth is 1 Mbps, the discovery phase would take 2 iterations = 200 ms. However, if the available bandwidth is 1 Gbps = 1000 Mbps, then the discovery phase would take 2000 iterations = 200 seconds before we ramp up to a good rate, which is far too long. The Internet has a wide variety of link speeds, so both possibilities could occur in real life.","zh_html":"同时，出于效率考虑，我们希望发现过程能快速找到可用带宽。为此，我们会在每次后续尝试中快速增加速率。如果发现过程耗时过长，就浪费了本可以以最优速率发送分组的时间。例如，假设每 100ms 向速率增加 0.5 Mbps，直到检测到拥塞(丢失)。如果可用带宽是 1 Mbps，发现阶段只需 2 次迭代 = 200 ms。然而，如果可用带宽是 1 Gbps = 1000 Mbps，那么发现阶段就需要 2000 次迭代 = 200 秒才能提升到合适的速率，这太长了。互联网(Internet)具有各种各样的链路速度，所以两种可能性在现实中都可能发生。"},{"en":"In order to support a slow start but a fast ramp-up, we’ll increase the bandwidth by a multiplicative factor each time (instead of an additive factor). This solution is called slow start, though this is arguably an unintuitive name. In slow start, we start with a small rate that will almost always be much less than the actual bandwidth. Then, we increase the rate exponentially (e.g. doubling the rate each time) until we encounter loss. A safe rate to use is the one just before we encounter loss (we don’t want to use a rate where we experienced loss). Formally, if loss occurs at rate R, then the safe rate is R/2.","zh_html":"为了支持慢速启动但快速提升，我们每次会以乘法因子增加带宽(而不是加法因子)。这个解决方案称为<strong>慢启动(slow start)</strong>，尽管这个名字可以说并不直观。在慢启动中，我们从一个很小的速率开始，它几乎总是远小于实际带宽。然后，我们指数式地增加速率(例如每次翻倍)，直到遇到丢失。安全的速率是刚好在遇到丢失之前的那个速率(我们不希望使用发生过丢失的速率)。形式化地说，如果丢失发生在速率 R，那么安全速率就是 R/2。"},{"en":"Adjustments: Reacting to Congestion","zh_html":"调整：应对拥塞(Adjustments: Reacting to Congestion)"},{"en":"Recall that after the discovery phase, we will be constantly adjusting the bandwidth, because the network itself is changing, and the available bandwidth is not constant.","zh_html":"回忆一下，在发现阶段之后，我们会持续调整带宽，因为网络本身在不断变化，可用带宽也不是恒定的。"},{"en":"The final choice point is deciding how much we should decrease the bandwidth if congestion is detected, and how much we should increase the bandwidth if no congestion is detected.","zh_html":"最后一个选择点是决定：如果检测到拥塞，应该将带宽减少多少；如果没有检测到拥塞，应该将带宽增加多少。"},{"en":"Our decision will determine how quickly a host adapts to changes in the available bandwidth, which in turn determines how effectively bandwidth is consumed. If we took a long time to adapt to changes and find a good rate, we would spend a lot of time operating at sub-optimal bandwidth, which is inefficient. Slow adaptation can also lead to fairness issues. For example, if I’m using a link’s entire bandwidth, and another connection is opened, I need to quickly adapt and decrease my bandwidth in order to share the link.","zh_html":"我们的决策将决定主机(host)适应可用带宽变化的速度，进而决定带宽使用的效率。如果我们花费很长时间来适应变化并找到合适的速率，就会在次优带宽下运行很长时间，这是低效的。缓慢的适应还可能导致公平性(fairness)问题。例如，如果我正在使用一条链路的全部带宽，而另一个连接被打开，我就需要快速适应并降低带宽，以便共享该链路。"},{"en":"Recall that our main goals in a congestion control algorithm are efficiency (use all available bandwidth) and fairness (connections share bandwidth equally). We will need to choose increase and decrease rules that achieve both of these goals.","zh_html":"回忆一下，拥塞控制(congestion control)算法的主要目标是效率(efficiency，使用所有可用带宽)和公平性(fairness，连接平等共享带宽)。我们需要选择能够同时实现这两个目标的增减规则。"},{"en":"What rules can we choose from? At a high level, we can either react quickly or slowly. More specifically, fast changes are multiplicative, e.g. doubling or halving the rate on each iteration. Slow changes are additive, e.g. adding 1 to the rate or subtracting 1 from the rate on each iteration. These options create four possible alternatives:","zh_html":"我们可以选择哪些规则？从高层次来看，我们可以快速反应或缓慢反应。更具体地说，快速变化是乘法(multiplicative)的，例如每次迭代将速率翻倍或减半。缓慢变化是加法(additive)的，例如每次迭代将速率加 1 或减 1。这些选项产生四种可能的组合："},{"en":"AIAD: additive increase, additive decrease","zh_html":"<strong>AIAD</strong>：加法增，加法减(additive increase, additive decrease)"},{"en":"AIMD: additive increase, multiplicative decrease","zh_html":"<strong>AIMD</strong>：加法增，乘法减(additive increase, multiplicative decrease)"},{"en":"MIAD: multiplicative increase, additive decrease","zh_html":"<strong>MIAD</strong>：乘法增，加法减(multiplicative increase, additive decrease)"},{"en":"MIMD: multiplicative increase, multiplicative increase","zh_html":"<strong>MIMD</strong>：乘法增，乘法减(multiplicative increase, multiplicative decrease)"},{"en":"Of these four alternatives, it turns out that AIMD (slow increase, fast decrease) is the best for achieving efficiency and fairness.","zh_html":"在这四种组合中，事实证明 AIMD(慢增快减)最有利于实现效率和公平性。"},{"en":"Intuitively, AIMD is a reasonable choice because sending too much is worse than sending too little. When our rate is too high, we cause congestion, and packets get dropped. When our rate is too low, we aren’t using all the bandwidth, but at least we aren’t causing congestion.","zh_html":"直观上，AIMD 是一个合理的选择，因为发送太多比发送太少更糟糕。当我们的速率过高时，会引起拥塞，分组被丢弃。当我们的速率过低时，虽然没有使用全部带宽，但至少没有引起拥塞。"},{"en":"AIMD leads to the behavior where we slowly increase the rate when there’s no congestion, creeping up to the maximal bandwidth. Then, as soon as we exceed maximal bandwidth and detect congestion, we rapidly decrease. This way, we spend most of our time with the rate too low (preferable), and when the rate is too high (not preferable), we quickly decrease to avoid congestion.","zh_html":"AIMD 产生的行为是：当没有拥塞时我们缓慢增加速率，逐渐接近最大带宽。然后，一旦超过最大带宽并检测到拥塞，我们迅速降低。这样，我们大部分时间处于速率偏低的状态(这是可取的)，而当速率过高(不可取)时，我们快速降低以避免拥塞。"},{"en":"Adjustments: Model","zh_html":"调整：模型(Adjustments: Model)"},{"en":"Why is AIMD the best choice for achieving efficiency and fairness? Let’s do a more detailed analysis.","zh_html":"为什么 AIMD 是实现效率和公平性的最佳选择？让我们做更详细的分析。"},{"en":"First, notice that all four options do a pretty good job at achieving efficiency. By increasing when we’re below the optimal rate (not congested), and decreasing when we’re above the optimal rate (congested), our rate should always be hovering around the optimal rate in the long run.","zh_html":"首先，注意到四种组合在实现效率方面都做得不错。通过低于最优速率(未拥塞)时增加，高于最优速率(拥塞)时减少，从长远来看，我们的速率应当始终在最优速率附近徘徊。"},{"en":"However, it turns out that of these four options, AIMD is the only option that leads to fairness. To see why, let’s consider a simple model where there are two connections going over a single link of capacity C. The two connections are sending at rates X1 and X2, respectively. We know that if X1+X2 is greater than C, the network is congested, and if X1+X2 is less than C, then the network is underloaded.","zh_html":"然而，事实证明在这四种组合中，AIMD 是唯一能够实现公平性的选项。为了理解原因，让我们考虑一个简单模型：有两条连接通过一条容量为 C 的链路。两条连接分别以速率 X1 和 X2 发送。我们知道，如果 X1+X2 大于 C，则网络拥塞；如果 X1+X2 小于 C，则网络负载不足(underloaded)。"},{"en":"To achieve efficiency, we want the link to be fully utilized, i.e. X1+X2 = C. To achieve fairness, we want X1 = X2, so that both connections are sharing the capacity equally.","zh_html":"为了实现效率，我们希望链路被充分利用，即 X1+X2 = C。为了实现公平性，我们希望 X1 = X2，这样两条连接平等地共享容量。"},{"en":"To visualize the space of possibilities, consider a 2D plot, where the x-axis is X1 (user 1’s rate), and the y-axis is X2 (user 2’s rate). Every point on this plot represents a possible scenario where each user is sending at a specific rate.","zh_html":"为了可视化所有可能的情况，考虑一个二维图，其中 x 轴是 X1(用户 1 的速率)，y 轴是 X2(用户 2 的速率)。图上的每个点表示一种可能的情景，其中每个用户以特定速率发送。"},{"en":"Suppose C=1. To achieve maximum efficiency, we want X1+X2 = 1. We can plot this line on the graph. Every point along this line is using the full available bandwidth.","zh_html":"假设 C=1。为了实现最大效率，我们希望 X1+X2 = 1。我们可以在图上画出这条线。这条线上的每个点都使用了全部可用带宽。"},{"en":"We know that the network is congested when X1+X2 is greater than 1. On the plot, this inequality is the half-plane above the line. We also know that the network is underused when X1+X2 is less than 1, which is represented by the half-plane below the line. This means that all points above the line represent a congested state, and all points below the line represent an underused state.","zh_html":"我们知道，当 X1+X2 大于 1 时网络拥塞。在图上，这个不等式是线上方的半平面。我们还知道，当 X1+X2 小于 1 时网络未被充分利用，这由线下方的半平面表示。这意味着线上方的所有点都表示拥塞状态，线下方的所有点都表示未充分利用状态。"},{"en":"To achieve fairness, we want X1 = X2. We can also plot this line. Every point along this line represents a fair state, where both users are using the same amount of bandwidth. Any point not along this line is unfair.","zh_html":"为了实现公平性，我们希望 X1 = X2。我们也可以画出这条线。这条线上的每个点都表示公平状态，两个用户使用相同数量的带宽。任何不在这条线上的点都是不公平的。"},{"en":"The ideal state occurs at the intersection of the two lines, when X1 = X2 = 0.5. This point falls on both lines, so it is both fair and efficient.","zh_html":"理想状态出现在两条线的交点，即 X1 = X2 = 0.5。这个点同时位于两条线上，因此既公平又高效。"},{"en":"The point (0.2, 0.5) is inefficient, because we are only using 0.7 bandwidth. Graphically, we are below the efficiency line. The point (0.7, 0.5) is congested and therefore above the efficiency line. The point (0.7, 0.3) is efficient (on the efficiency line), but is not fair (not on the fairness line).","zh_html":"点 (0.2, 0.5) 是低效的，因为我们只使用了 0.7 的带宽。从图上来看，我们在效率线(efficiency line)下方。点 (0.7, 0.5) 是拥塞的，因此在效率线上方。点 (0.7, 0.3) 是高效的(在效率线上)，但不公平(不在公平线(fairness line)上)。"},{"en":"Recall that in our dynamic adjustment algorithm, every sender is independently running the same algorithm to determine their own rate. This means that if the two users detect underuse, both will increase their rate in the same way (additive or multiplicative, depending on our choice of rule). Similarly, if the two users detect congestion, both will decrease their rate in the same way.","zh_html":"回忆一下，在我们的动态调整算法中，每个发送方都独立运行相同的算法来决定自己的速率。这意味着如果两个用户都检测到未充分利用，他们都会以相同的方式增加速率(加法或乘法，取决于我们选择的规则)。类似地，如果两个用户都检测到拥塞，他们都会以相同的方式降低速率。"},{"en":"What happens if both users additively increase or decrease their rate? If both users increase their rate by adding b, the state (x1, x2) would become (x1+b, x2+b). If both users decrease their rate by subtracting a, the state (x1, x2) would become (x1-a, x2-a).","zh_html":"如果两个用户都加法增加或降低速率，会发生什么？如果两个用户都增加 b 来增加速率，状态 (x1, x2) 会变成 (x1+b, x2+b)。如果两个用户都减去 a 来降低速率，状态 (x1, x2) 会变成 (x1-a, x2-a)。"},{"en":"On the graph, if we make an additive change, the point representing our state moves along a line with a slope of 1.","zh_html":"在图上，如果我们做加法变化，表示状态的点会沿着斜率为 1 的直线移动。"},{"en":"What happens if both users multiplicatively increase or decrease their rate? Multiplying by c transforms (x1, x2) to (cx1, cx2), and dividing by d transforms (x1, x2) to (x1/d, x2/d).","zh_html":"如果两个用户都乘法增加或降低速率，会发生什么？乘以 c 将 (x1, x2) 变换为 (cx1, cx2)，除以 d 将 (x1, x2) 变换为 (x1/d, x2/d)。"},{"en":"On the graph, if we make a multiplicative change, the point representing our state moves along a line with slope x2/x1. Equivalently, this is the line connecting (x1, x2) to the origin (0, 0).","zh_html":"在图上，如果我们做乘法变化，表示状态的点会沿着斜率为 x2/x1 的直线移动。等价地，这是连接 (x1, x2) 到原点 (0, 0) 的直线。"},{"en":"Now, we can apply this model to each of the four increase/decrease options, and see if they cause the point to approach, or move away from, the fairness line. Our goal is for the point to approach the fairness line as we adjust the rates.","zh_html":"现在，我们可以将这个模型应用于四种增减组合，看看它们会使点接近还是远离公平线(fairness line)。我们的目标是随着速率的调整，点逐渐接近公平线。"},{"en":"Adjustments: AIAD Dynamics","zh_html":"调整：AIAD 动态(Adjustments: AIAD Dynamics)"},{"en":"Consider adding 1 on each increase, and subtracting 2 on each decrease. Suppose we have capacity of C = 5. Then from a given starting point, our point would move as follows:","zh_html":"考虑每次增加加 1，每次减少减 2。假设容量 C = 5。从给定的起点出发，我们的点会如下移动："},{"en":"X1 = 1, X2 = 3 (starting point, 4 less than 5, increase)","zh_html":"X1 = 1, X2 = 3(起点，4 小于 5，增加)"},{"en":"X1 = 2, X2 = 4 (6 more than 5, decrease)","zh_html":"X1 = 2, X2 = 4(6 大于 5，减少)"},{"en":"X1 = 0, X2 = 2 (2 less than 5, increase)","zh_html":"X1 = 0, X2 = 2(2 小于 5，增加)"},{"en":"X1 = 1, X2 = 3","zh_html":"X1 = 1, X2 = 3"},{"en":"We’ve returned to where we started! Our initial allocation was not fair, and after a few iterations, we returned to the same unfair allocation.","zh_html":"我们回到了起点！初始分配是不公平的，经过几次迭代后，我们回到了相同的不公平分配。"},{"en":"In fact, if we look at the difference between X1 and X2 (fair gap is 0), the gap is the same (2) in every iteration. The iterations don’t make our allocation any more or less fair.","zh_html":"事实上，如果我们看 X1 和 X2 之间的差(公平差值为 0)，这个差在每次迭代中都相同(2)。迭代并没有使我们的分配变得更多或更少公平。"},{"en":"We can see this behavior graphically. From a given starting point, if we increase and decrease additively, we will always move along a line of slope 1, never getting any closer to the fairness line.","zh_html":"我们可以从图上看到这种行为。从给定起点出发，如果加法增加和减少，我们总是沿着斜率为 1 的直线移动，永远不会更接近公平线。"},{"en":"Do note, though, that our point oscillates around the efficiency line, as desired. All four options will have this behavior.","zh_html":"不过需要注意的是，我们的点会如预期般在效率线(efficiency line)附近振荡。四种组合都会有这种行为。"},{"en":"We can also see this behavior algebraically. Suppose X1 and X2 are 5 apart (unfair allocation). If we add the same number to X1 and X2, the resulting X1’ and X2’ are still 5 apart (equally unfair). The same happens if we subtract the same number from both X1 and X2.","zh_html":"我们也可以从代数角度看到这种行为。假设 X1 和 X2 相差 5(不公平分配)。如果我们在 X1 和 X2 上加相同的数，得到的 X1' 和 X2' 仍然相差 5(同样不公平)。如果我们从 X1 和 X2 减去相同的数，也是如此。"},{"en":"In summary, there is no way to close the fairness gap in this approach. If the allocation is initially unfair, it will stay unfair.","zh_html":"总之，这种方法无法缩小公平性差距。如果初始分配是不公平的，它将永远不公平。"},{"en":"You might ask: What if we increased X1 by more (e.g. +2), and X2 by less (e.g. +1)? Remember, our decentralized approach means that everybody is running the same algorithm. Practically, we also have no way for a host to know how much it should add relative to other hosts.","zh_html":"你可能会问：如果我们增加 X1 更多(例如 +2)，而增加 X2 更少(例如 +1)呢？请记住，我们的去中心化(decentralized)方法意味着每个人都在运行相同的算法。实际上，主机也无法知道相对于其他主机应该增加多少。"},{"en":"Adjustments: MIMD Dynamics","zh_html":"调整：MIMD 动态(Adjustments: MIMD Dynamics)"},{"en":"Consider increasing by doubling, and decreasing by dividing by 4. Again, the capacity is C = 5. From a given starting point, the first few iterations would be:","zh_html":"考虑通过翻倍来增加，通过除以 4 来减少。同样，容量 C = 5。从给定的起点出发，前几次迭代会是："},{"en":"X1 = 0.5, X2 = 1 (1.5 less than 5, increase)","zh_html":"X1 = 0.5, X2 = 1(1.5 小于 5，增加)"},{"en":"X1 = 1, X2 = 2 (3 less than 5, increase)","zh_html":"X1 = 1, X2 = 2(3 小于 5，增加)"},{"en":"X1 = 2, X2 = 4 (6 more than 5, decrease)","zh_html":"X1 = 2, X2 = 4(6 大于 5，减少)"},{"en":"X1 = 0.5, X2 = 1","zh_html":"X1 = 0.5, X2 = 1"},{"en":"Again, we’ve returned to where we started, with no improvement in fairness!","zh_html":"同样，我们回到了起点，公平性没有任何改善！"},{"en":"We can see this behavior on the plot. When we multiplicatively increase or decrease the rate, we are moving along the line between the point and the origin, and we are never getting any closer to the fairness line.","zh_html":"我们可以在图上看到这种行为。当我们乘法增加或降低速率时，我们沿着点和原点之间的直线移动，永远不会更接近公平线。"},{"en":"Algebraically, consider the ratio between X2 and X1, i.e. X2/X1 (fair ratio would be 1). In the example above, the ratio is always 2, i.e. X2 always has twice the bandwidth of X1. This ratio stays the same even if we multiply or divide both X1 and X2 by constant factor. Our adjustments don’t get us closer to a fair ratio of 1.","zh_html":"从代数角度，考虑 X2 和 X1 的比值，即 X2/X1(公平比值为 1)。在上面的例子中，比值始终是 2，即 X2 总是拥有 X1 两倍的带宽。即使我们将 X1 和 X2 同时乘以或除以一个常数，这个比值也保持不变。我们的调整并没有让我们更接近公平比值 1。"},{"en":"Adjustments: MIAD Dynamics","zh_html":"调整：MIAD 动态(Adjustments: MIAD Dynamics)"},{"en":"This one is a little trickier. Consider increasing by doubling, and decreasing by subtracting 1. With C = 5, the first few iterations are:","zh_html":"这种情况稍微复杂一些。考虑通过翻倍来增加，通过减 1 来减少。C = 5 时，前几次迭代是："},{"en":"X1 = 1, X2 = 3 (4 less than 5, increase)","zh_html":"X1 = 1, X2 = 3(4 小于 5，增加)"},{"en":"X1 = 2, X2 = 6 (8 more than 5, decrease)","zh_html":"X1 = 2, X2 = 6(8 大于 5，减少)"},{"en":"X1 = 1, X2 = 5 (6 more than 5, decrease)","zh_html":"X1 = 1, X2 = 5(6 大于 5，减少)"},{"en":"X1 = 0, X2 = 4 (4 less than 5, increase)","zh_html":"X1 = 0, X2 = 4(4 小于 5，增加)"},{"en":"X1 = 0, X2 = 8","zh_html":"X1 = 0, X2 = 8"},{"en":"At this point, X1 has zero bandwidth. Every time we increase by doubling, X1 will still have zero bandwidth. We have actually created the most unfair situation, where X2 has all the bandwidth, and X1 has none.","zh_html":"此时，X1 的带宽为零。每次我们翻倍增加时，X1 仍然为零带宽。我们实际上创造了最不公平的局面：X2 拥有全部带宽，而 X1 一无所有。"},{"en":"More generally, if you start with an unfair allocation, MIAD will make the allocation even more unfair, eventually reaching a point where one person has all the bandwidth, and the other person has zero.","zh_html":"更一般地说，如果你从不公平的分配开始，MIAD 会使分配变得更加不公平，最终达到一个人拥有全部带宽、另一个人为零的局面。"},{"en":"To see this algebraically, consider the gaps between X1 and X2. When we increase by doubling, the size of the gap also doubles, from (X2 - X1) to (2 X2 - 2 X1) = 2(X2 - X1). But, when we subtract 1 from both X1 and X2, the gap stays the same. The gap either increases or stays the same, and given enough iterations of increasing and decreasing, the gap will reach maximal unfairness (one person has zero bandwidth forever).","zh_html":"从代数角度来看，考虑 X1 和 X2 之间的差距。当我们翻倍增加时，差距也翻倍，从 (X2 - X1) 变为 (2 X2 - 2 X1) = 2(X2 - X1)。但是，当我们从 X1 和 X2 同时减 1 时，差距保持不变。差距要么增大要么保持不变，经过足够多次的增加和减少迭代，差距将达到最大不公平(一个人永远拥有零带宽)。"},{"en":"Adjustments: AIMD Dynamics","zh_html":"调整：AIMD 动态(Adjustments: AIMD Dynamics)"},{"en":"Finally, consider increasing by adding 1, and decreasing by halving. With C = 5, the first few iterations are:","zh_html":"最后，考虑通过加 1 增加，通过减半减少。C = 5 时，前几次迭代是："},{"en":"X1 = 1, X2 = 2 (3 less than 5, increase)","zh_html":"X1 = 1, X2 = 2(3 小于 5，增加)"},{"en":"X1 = 2, X2 = 3 (5 not more than 5, increase)","zh_html":"X1 = 2, X2 = 3(5 不大于 5，增加)"},{"en":"X1 = 3, X2 = 4 (7 more than 5, decrease)","zh_html":"X1 = 3, X2 = 4(7 大于 5，减少)"},{"en":"X1 = 1.5, X2 = 2 (3.5 less than 5, increase)","zh_html":"X1 = 1.5, X2 = 2(3.5 小于 5，增加)"},{"en":"X1 = 2.5, X2 = 3 (5.5 more than 5, decrease)","zh_html":"X1 = 2.5, X2 = 3(5.5 大于 5，减少)"},{"en":"X1 = 1.25, X2 = 1.5 (2.75 less than 5, increase)","zh_html":"X1 = 1.25, X2 = 1.5(2.75 小于 5，增加)"},{"en":"X1 = 2.25, X2 = 2.5 (4.75 less than 5, increase)","zh_html":"X1 = 2.25, X2 = 2.5(4.75 小于 5，增加)"},{"en":"X1 = 3.25, X2 = 3.5 (6.75 more than 5, decrease)","zh_html":"X1 = 3.25, X2 = 3.5(6.75 大于 5，减少)"},{"en":"X1 = 1.625, X2 = 1.75 (less than 5, increase)","zh_html":"X1 = 1.625, X2 = 1.75(小于 5，增加)"},{"en":"X2 = 2.625, X2 = 2.75","zh_html":"X1 = 2.625, X2 = 2.75"},{"en":"We can see that X1 and X2 are getting closer together, and in fact, they’re approaching the fair allocation of X1 = X2 = 2.5.","zh_html":"我们可以看到 X1 和 X2 越来越接近，事实上，它们正在趋近于公平分配 X1 = X2 = 2.5。"},{"en":"Algebraically, we can see that the gap between X1 and X2 is decreasing. Specifically, when we add a constant to both numbers, the gap stays the same. But, when we halve both numbers, the gap also halves, from (X1 - X2) to (X1 / 2 - X2 / 2) = (X1 - X2) / 2. As we alternate increasing and decreasing, the gap will keep halving and approaching 0.","zh_html":"从代数角度，我们可以看到 X1 和 X2 之间的差距在缩小。具体来说，当我们对两个数加上同一个常数时，差距保持不变。但是，当我们把两个数都减半时，差距也减半，从 (X1 - X2) 变为 (X1 / 2 - X2 / 2) = (X1 - X2) / 2。随着我们在增加和减少之间交替，差距将不断减半并趋近于 0。"},{"en":"We can see this graphically as well. When we multiplicatively decrease, we are moving along the line through the origin. This line is angled toward the fairness line, and moving downwards along this line means we’re approaching the fairness line. As before, additive increases don’t get us any closer to the fairness line, since we’re moving along a line with slope 1 (parallel to fairness line). But the key realization is that adding don’t move us any further, either. Our only two operations are moving closer, or not getting closer or further away. After many iterations, our point will slowly move closer toward the fairness line.","zh_html":"我们也可以从图上看到这一点。当我们乘法减少时，我们沿着通过原点的直线移动。这条线朝向公平线倾斜，沿着这条线向下移动意味着我们在接近公平线。和之前一样，加法增加不会让我们更接近公平线，因为我们沿着斜率为 1 的直线(平行于公平线)移动。但关键的认识是，加法也不会让我们变得更远。我们的两个操作要么是接近，要么是不接近也不远离。经过多次迭代，我们的点会慢慢向公平线靠近。"},{"en":"In summary: AIAD and MIMD retain unfairness, and make no improvements toward fairness. MIAD increases unfairness, and AIMD converges toward fairness.","zh_html":"总结：AIAD 和 MIMD 保持不公平性，不能改善公平性。MIAD 增加不公平性，而 AIMD 收敛到公平性。"}]},"/transport/cc-implementation.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Congestion Control Implementation","zh_html":"拥塞控制实现"},{"en":"Recap: TCP Windows","zh_html":"回顾：TCP 窗口(Recap: TCP Windows)"},{"en":"So far, we’ve designed a conceptual sketch of a dynamic adjustment, host-based algorithm, where each source runs the same algorithm independently to arrive at an efficient, fair share of bandwidth.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们已经设计了一个动态调整的、基于主机的算法的概念草图，其中每个发送方独立运行相同的算法，以达到高效、公平的带宽分配。"},{"en":"First, use slow-start (start at low rate, exponentially increase) to discover an initial rate. Then, in each iteration, if we detect congestion (detect loss), we reduce R multiplicatively. If we don’t detect congestion, we increase R additively.","zh_html":"首先，使用慢启动(slow-start，从低速率开始，指数增加)来发现初始速率。然后，在每次迭代中，如果检测到拥塞(检测到丢失)，我们乘法减少 R；如果没有检测到拥塞，我们加法增加 R。"},{"en":"In this section, we will now see how TCP implements this algorithm. For better or worse, TCP’s congestion control mechanisms are very intertwined with TCP’s reliability mechanisms. (This is a result of the original design, where TCP was patched to account for congestion.) In this section, we’ll see how TCP’s implementation works to achieve both reliability and congestion control at the same time.","zh_html":"在本节中，我们将看到 TCP 如何实现这个算法。不论好坏，TCP 的拥塞控制机制与 TCP 的可靠性机制紧密交织在一起。(这是原始设计的结果，当时 TCP 被修补以应对拥塞。)在本节中，我们将看到 TCP 的实现如何同时实现可靠性和拥塞控制。"},{"en":"Recall that in TCP, the sender maintains a sliding window of consecutive bytes/packets in flight. The size of the window is determined by flow control (decided by buffer space at recipient) and congestion control (rate computed by sender).","zh_html":"回忆一下，在 TCP 中，发送方维护一个连续的字节/分组的滑动窗口(sliding window)，其中包含在途(in flight)的数据。窗口的大小由流量控制(flow control，取决于接收方的缓冲区空间)和拥塞控制(congestion control，由发送方计算的速率)共同决定。"},{"en":"More specifically, in flow control, the recipient sends an advertised window, indicating how many more bytes can be sent without overflowing the recipient’s memory. This advertised window value is sometimes abbreviated RWND (receiver window).","zh_html":"更具体地说，在流量控制(flow control)中，接收方发送一个通告窗口(advertised window)，指示在不溢出接收方内存的情况下还可以发送多少字节。这个通告窗口值有时缩写为 <strong>RWND (receiver window)</strong>。"},{"en":"In congestion control, the sender maintains a value, sometimes abbreviated CWND (congestion window), which denotes the rate the sender can send packets without overloading links. This value will be dynamically set and adjusted by the congestion control algorithm.","zh_html":"在拥塞控制中，发送方维护一个值，有时缩写为 <strong>CWND (congestion window)</strong>，表示发送方在不使链路过载的情况下可以发送分组的速率。这个值将由拥塞控制算法动态设置和调整。"},{"en":"The sender’s window is computed as the minimum of CWND and RWND. For this lecture, we’ll assume that RWND is larger than CWND, so the bottleneck is the network, not the recipient’s memory. This is usually, but not always true in practice.","zh_html":"发送方的窗口计算为 CWND 和 RWND 的最小值。在本讲中，我们假设 RWND 大于 CWND，因此瓶颈(bottleneck)是网络，而不是接收方的内存。这在实践中通常是成立的，但并非总是如此。"},{"en":"Recall that we can view the sliding window as a range in the bytestream. The left side of the window is the first unacknowledged byte (everything to the left of the window has already been sent and acknowledged). The right side of the window is determined by the window size. Only packets inside this window are allowed to be in-flight.","zh_html":"回忆一下，我们可以将滑动窗口(sliding window)视为字节流(bytestream)中的一个范围。窗口的左侧是第一个未确认的字节(窗口左侧的所有内容都已被发送和确认)。窗口的右侧由窗口大小决定。只有窗口内的分组才允许在途(in-flight)。"},{"en":"When data on the left side of the window is acked, the window slides to the right, and additional data can now be sent.","zh_html":"当窗口左侧的数据被 ack 时，窗口向右滑动，此时可以发送更多数据。"},{"en":"To detect loss, we maintain a single timer for the left-most packet in the window. If the timer expires without that packet being acked, we re-send the left-most packet in the window. Also, to detect loss, we count the number of duplicate acks, and re-send the left-most packet if we see 3 duplicate acks. This duplicate ack-based approach is sometimes called fast retransmit.","zh_html":"为了检测丢失，我们为窗口中最左侧的分组维护一个单独的定时器(timer)。如果定时器到期时该分组仍未收到 ack，我们就重传窗口中最左侧的分组。此外，为了检测丢失，我们统计重复 ack(duplicate ack)的数量，如果看到 3 个重复 ack，就重传最左侧的分组。这种基于重复 ack 的方法有时称为<strong>快速重传(fast retransmit)</strong>。"},{"en":"Windows and Rates","zh_html":"窗口与速率(Windows and Rates)"},{"en":"How do we adjust the rate for congestion control, and how do we compute the congestion window? It turns out that these two values are directly related, and adjusting the window is achieved by adjusting the rate. The window size and the rate of sending data are correlated by the following equation: rate times RTT = window size.","zh_html":"我们如何调整拥塞控制的速率？又如何计算拥塞窗口(congestion window)？事实证明这两个值直接相关，调整窗口是通过调整速率来实现的。窗口大小和发送数据的速率通过以下等式关联：速率乘以 RTT 等于窗口大小。"},{"en":"Intuitively, you can think of window size and rate as the same quantity, expressed in two different “units of measurement.” An increased window size means we’re sending data faster, and vice-versa.","zh_html":"直观上，你可以把窗口大小和速率视为同一个量，用两种不同的“度量单位”表示。窗口增大意味着我们发送数据更快，反之亦然。"},{"en":"To see why this equation holds, consider the first RTT. We can send [window size] number of packets during this first RTT (before any acks arrive), for a rate of window size / RTT.","zh_html":"为了理解这个等式为什么成立，考虑第一个 RTT。我们可以在第一个 RTT 期间(在任何 ack 到达之前)发送[窗口大小]个分组，速率为 window size / RTT。"},{"en":"Recall that our conceptual TCP design measured data in packets for simplicity, but in practice, TCP thinks in terms of bytes. In a real implementation, the window size is measured in terms of bytes, but for simplicity, we will consider the window size in terms of packets.","zh_html":"回忆一下，为简单起见，我们的概念性 TCP 设计以分组(packet)为单位度量数据，但在实践中，TCP 以字节(byte)为单位思考。在真实实现中，窗口大小以字节度量，但为简单起见，我们以分组为单位来考虑窗口大小。"},{"en":"To convert between packets and bytes, recall that we defined the Maximum Segment Size (MSS), the number of bytes per packet. This tells us that MSS times number of packets = number of bytes. Again, intuitively, you can think of bytes and packets as two different units of measurement for the same quantity (amount of data).","zh_html":"为了在分组和字节之间转换，回忆一下我们定义了最大段大小(Maximum Segment Size, MSS)，即每个分组的字节数。这告诉我们 MSS 乘以分组数等于字节数。同样，直观上你可以把字节和分组视为同一个量(数据量)的两种不同度量单位。"},{"en":"Event-Driven Updates","zh_html":"事件驱动的更新(Event-Driven Updates)"},{"en":"In our conceptual model, our goal is to adjust the rate/window once per “iteration,” but we haven’t formalized how to measure each iteration. We can roughly define each iteration as one RTT, but the RTT itself is a dynamically changing value that we can’t accurately measure.","zh_html":"在我们的概念模型中，目标是每“迭代”调整一次速率/窗口，但我们还没有形式化如何度量每次迭代。我们可以粗略地将每次迭代定义为一个 RTT，但 RTT 本身是一个动态变化的值，我们无法精确测量。"},{"en":"In order to update the window size in a more predictable, measurable way, we can consider the various events that the existing TCP implementation responds to, and update the window each time one of these events occurs. These are called event-driven updates.","zh_html":"为了以更可预测、可测量的方式更新窗口大小，我们可以考虑现有 TCP 实现会响应的各种事件，并在这些事件发生时更新窗口。这称为<strong>事件驱动的更新(event-driven updates)</strong>。"},{"en":"The three TCP events where we need to update the window size are: new ack, 3 duplicate acks, and timeout.","zh_html":"我们需要更新窗口大小的三个 TCP 事件是：新 ack(new ack)、3 个重复 ack(3 duplicate acks)和超时(timeout)。"},{"en":"When we see a new ack (for data that was previously not acknowledged), this is a sign that our data made it through the network without loss. In our model, we detect congestion by checking for loss, so a new ack is a sign that the network is not congested. Therefore, when we see a new ack, we can increase the window size (either during slow-start discovery, or AIMD adjustment).","zh_html":"当我们看到一个新 ack(针对之前未确认的数据)时，这表示我们的数据已无丢失地通过网络。在我们的模型中，我们通过检查丢失来检测拥塞，因此新 ack 表示网络没有拥塞。因此，当我们看到新 ack 时，可以增加窗口大小(无论是在慢启动发现阶段还是 AIMD 调整阶段)。"},{"en":"When we see 3 duplicate acks, we mark a packet lost. This is a signal of isolated loss, which indicates mild congestion. We lost a packet, but subsequent packets are still being received. To react to this loss, we will decrease the window size (during AIMD adjustment).","zh_html":"当我们看到 3 个重复 ack 时，我们标记一个分组已丢失。这是孤立丢失(isolated loss)的信号，表示轻度拥塞。我们丢失了一个分组，但后续分组仍在被接收。为了应对这种丢失，我们将减小窗口大小(在 AIMD 调整阶段)。"},{"en":"When we encounter a timeout, we mark a packet lost. The fact that we detected the loss after a timeout, not duplicate acks, is a signal of many packets being lost (heavy congestion). To see why, consider a window size of 100 packets. If we encounter a timeout, this means we didn’t get an ack for the left-most packet in the window. But it also means that we failed to get 3 duplicate acks for any other packets in the window during the entire duration of the timer. A timeout means that very few, if any, packets are being received, and something bad has happened.","zh_html":"当我们遇到超时(timeout)时，我们标记一个分组已丢失。我们是在超时后而非通过重复 ack 检测到丢失，这一事实是许多分组丢失(严重拥塞)的信号。为了理解原因，考虑一个大小为 100 个分组的窗口。如果遇到超时，这意味着我们没有收到窗口中最左侧分组的 ack。但这也意味着在定时器的整个持续时间内，我们没有收到窗口中任何其他分组带来的 3 个重复 ack。超时意味着几乎没有分组(如果有的话)被接收，发生了某种严重问题。"},{"en":"If we discover a timeout, something unexpected has happened (e.g. network changed), and we should no longer trust our current window size. To react, we should go back to the slow-start phase and re-discover a good window size. This isn’t the only way to react to timeout, but this is what TCP decided.","zh_html":"如果发现超时，说明发生了意外情况(例如网络发生变化)，我们不应再信任当前的窗口大小。作为应对，我们应该回到慢启动阶段，重新发现一个合适的窗口大小。这不是应对超时的唯一方式，但这是 TCP 采用的方式。"},{"en":"Event-Driven Slow Start","zh_html":"事件驱动的慢启动(Event-Driven Slow Start)"},{"en":"In our conceptual model, we implemented slow start by choosing a slow rate, and increasing the rate exponentially (e.g. doubling on each iteration) until we encounter the first loss. We now need an event-driven way to double the window once per RTT.","zh_html":"在我们的概念模型中，我们通过选择一个较低的速率并指数式增加(例如每次迭代翻倍)来实现慢启动，直到遇到第一次丢失。我们现在需要一种事件驱动的方式，使窗口每 RTT 翻倍一次。"},{"en":"TCP starts with a small window of 1 packet. Recall, we can convert packet to bytes with the maximum segment size (MSS), and then convert bytes to rate by dividing MSS/RTT.","zh_html":"TCP 从 1 个分组的小窗口开始。回忆一下，我们可以用最大段大小(MSS)将分组转换为字节，然后通过 MSS/RTT 将字节转换为速率。"},{"en":"Every time we get an acknowledgement, we will increase the window size by 1 packet. The intuition for what will happen is:","zh_html":"每次收到确认(acknowledgement)，我们都会将窗口大小增加 1 个分组。直觉上会发生的情况是："},{"en":"Initially, the window size is 1 packet. We send 1 packet, and after an RTT, get 1 ack back. The ack lets us increase the window to 2 packets.","zh_html":"最初，窗口大小为 1 个分组。我们发送 1 个分组，一个 RTT 后收到 1 个 ack。这个 ack 让我们将窗口增加到 2 个分组。"},{"en":"We now send 2 packets, and after an RTT, we get 2 acks back. The 2 acks let us increase the window by another 2 packets, for a new window size of 4 packets.","zh_html":"我们现在发送 2 个分组，一个 RTT 后收到 2 个 ack。这 2 个 ack 让我们将窗口再增加 2 个分组，新窗口大小为 4 个分组。"},{"en":"We now send 4 packets, and after an RTT, we get 4 acks back. The 4 acks let us increase the window by another 4 packets, for a new window size of 8 packets.","zh_html":"我们现在发送 4 个分组，一个 RTT 后收到 4 个 ack。这 4 个 ack 让我们将窗口再增加 4 个分组，新窗口大小为 8 个分组。"},{"en":"This intuitive picture assumes we are sending all 4 packets and receiving all 4 acks simultaneously, though. In practice, the sliding window behavior causes our window to increase by 1 each time we receive an ack, though the end behavior (doubling the window every RTT) is the same.","zh_html":"不过，这个直观画面假设我们同时发送全部 4 个分组并同时收到全部 4 个 ack。在实践中，滑动窗口行为使得我们每次收到一个 ack 时窗口增加 1，但最终行为(每 RTT 窗口翻倍)是一样的。"},{"en":"As before, we start with a window size of 1 packet. We send 1 packet (A), and after an RTT, get the ack for A back. The ack lets us increase the window to 2 packets, and there are zero packets in flight.","zh_html":"和之前一样，我们从 1 个分组的窗口大小开始。发送 1 个分组(A)，一个 RTT 后收到 A 的 ack。这个 ack 让我们将窗口增加到 2 个分组，此时在途分组为零。"},{"en":"Next, we can send out 2 packets (B and C). When we get the ack for B, we increase the window to 3 packets. There is still 1 packet in flight (C), so we can send 2 more packets (D and E).","zh_html":"接下来，我们可以发送 2 个分组(B 和 C)。当收到 B 的 ack 时，我们将窗口增加到 3 个分组。仍然有 1 个分组在途(C)，因此我们可以再发送 2 个分组(D 和 E)。"},{"en":"When we get the ack for C, we can increase the window to 4 packets. There are still 2 packets in flight (D and E), so we can send 2 more packets (F and G).","zh_html":"当收到 C 的 ack 时，我们可以将窗口增加到 4 个分组。仍然有 2 个分组在途(D 和 E)，因此我们可以再发送 2 个分组(F 和 G)。"},{"en":"In general, assuming no loss and no re-ordering, every time we receive an ack, the sliding window allows us to send one more packet, and the increased window allows us to send another packet. Because every ack leads to 2 packets being sent, we get the behavior where the window doubles every RTT. For example, within an RTT interval where we receive 16 acks, each ack triggers to packets being sent, for a total of 32 packets. Then, in the next RTT interval, those 32 packets will be acked, triggering 64 packets being sent.","zh_html":"一般来说，假设没有丢失和乱序，每次收到 ack 时，滑动窗口允许我们多发送一个分组，而窗口的增加又允许我们发送另一个分组。因为每个 ack 导致 2 个分组被发送，我们就得到了窗口每 RTT 翻倍的行为。例如，在一个 RTT 间隔内我们收到 16 个 ack，每个 ack 触发 2 个分组的发送，共 32 个分组。然后，在下一个 RTT 间隔内，这 32 个分组会被确认，触发 64 个分组的发送。"},{"en":"Eventually, after some time spent doubling the window every RTT (increasing the window by 1 for every ack), we’ll encounter loss. This also means we’ve learned the maximum allowable “safe” rate for sending packets without encountering loss. We’ll remember this rate in a new parameter called SSTHRESH (slow start threshold). Specifically, as soon as we encounter packet loss, we’ll set SSTHRESH to half the window size. For example, if a window of 16 packets doesn’t cause loss, but a window of 32 packets does cause loss, then we would set SSTHRESH to 16.","zh_html":"在窗口每 RTT 翻倍一段时间后(每收到一个 ack 窗口增加 1)，我们最终会遇到丢失。这也意味着我们学到了在不遇到丢失的情况下发送分组的最大允许“安全”速率。我们将这个速率保存在一个称为 SSTHRESH (slow start threshold)的新参数中。具体来说，一旦遇到分组丢失，就将 SSTHRESH 设为窗口大小的一半。例如，如果 16 个分组的窗口不会引起丢失，但 32 个分组的窗口会引起丢失，那么我们就将 SSTHRESH 设为 16。"},{"en":"Recall that after slow start, we will continually adjust the window size (AIMD). SSTHRESH lets us remember the safe rate we learned from slow start, even as the rate starts changing later.","zh_html":"回忆一下，慢启动之后我们会持续调整窗口大小(AIMD)。SSTHRESH 让我们记住从慢启动中学到的安全速率，即使后来速率开始变化。"},{"en":"Implementing Additive Increasing","zh_html":"实现加法增加(Implementing Additive Increasing)"},{"en":"In our conceptual model, after slow start, we want to slowly (additively) increase the rate when there’s no loss. We need an event-driven way to increase the window by 1 packet for each RTT.","zh_html":"在我们的概念模型中，慢启动之后，我们希望在无丢失时缓慢(加法地)增加速率。我们需要一种事件驱动的方式，使窗口每 RTT 增加 1 个分组。"},{"en":"We don’t have an exact number for the RTT, but we do know that within a single RTT, we expect a window’s worth of packets to be acked. For example, with window size 10, we receive 10 acks per RTT. If we increase the window by 1/10 packet per ack, then across a RTT, the window should increase by 1 packet, as desired.","zh_html":"我们没有 RTT 的精确数值，但我们知道在一个 RTT 内，预期会有一个窗口大小的分组被确认。例如，窗口大小为 10 时，每 RTT 收到 10 个 ack。如果我们每个 ack 使窗口增加 1/10 个分组，那么在一个 RTT 内，窗口应增加 1 个分组，正如所愿。"},{"en":"Each time we receive an acknowledgement, we will take the current window size CWND and reassign it to CWND + (1/CWND). This increases the window by a fraction of a packet on each ack. After a full window’s worth of packets (i.e. after one RTT), the window increases by 1 packet.","zh_html":"每次收到确认时，我们将当前窗口大小 CWND 重新赋值为 CWND + (1/CWND)。这样每个 ack 使窗口增加一个分片的量。经过一个完整窗口的分组(即一个 RTT)后，窗口增加 1 个分组。"},{"en":"Formally, TCP measures the window in bytes, not packets, so (1/CWND) is equivalent to MSS * (MSS/CWND) in bytes. In (1/CWND), the numerator is 1 packet (total increase in an RTT), and the denominator is CWND measured in packets. Since the denominator is now measured in packets, we also have to measure the numerator in packets: 1 packet = MSS bytes.","zh_html":"形式化地说，TCP 以字节而非分组为单位度量窗口，因此 (1/CWND) 等价于 MSS * (MSS/CWND) 字节。在 (1/CWND) 中，分子是 1 个分组(一个 RTT 内的总增加量)，分母是以分组计量的 CWND。由于分母现在以分组计量，我们也必须用分组计量分子：1 个分组 = MSS 字节。"},{"en":"But the fraction 1/CWND or MSS/CWND is still a ratio (dimensionless), representing the fraction to be increased on each ack. The total increase we want is 1 packet = MSS bytes, so we have to multiply this fraction by MSS bytes.","zh_html":"但分数 1/CWND 或 MSS/CWND 仍然是一个比值(无量纲)，表示每个 ack 应增加的比例。我们想要的总增加量是 1 个分组 = MSS 字节，所以我们必须将这个分数乘以 MSS 字节。"},{"en":"As an example, suppose our CWND was 3 packets = 150 bytes (assuming MSS = 50 bytes). In the packet view, we would add 1/3 packets to the window each time, for a total increase of 1 packet.","zh_html":"举个例子，假设我们的 CWND 是 3 个分组 = 150 bytes(假设 MSS = 50 bytes)。在以分组为单位的视角中，我们每次向窗口加 1/3 个分组，总共增加 1 个分组。"},{"en":"In the byte view, we can divide MSS/CWND = 50/150 to get the same 1/3 ratio that we need to step by each time, for a total increase of 1. But we still need to multiply by MSS so that the total increase is MSS instead of 1.","zh_html":"在以字节为单位的视角中，我们可以计算 MSS/CWND = 50/150 来得到同样的 1/3 比例，这就是每次需要增加的步长，总增加量为 1。但我们仍然需要乘以 MSS，使得总增加量为 MSS 而不是 1。"},{"en":"Note that the increase isn’t perfectly linear, but provides a good enough approximation. For example, starting with CWND = 4, the first update is 4 + 1/4 = 4.25, and the second increase is 4.25 + 1/4.25 = 4.49. After four updates, the window size would be 4.92 in this approximation (we wanted it to be 5 in the exact model).","zh_html":"注意，增加并非完全线性，但提供了足够好的近似。例如，从 CWND = 4 开始，第一次更新是 4 + 1/4 = 4.25，第二次增加是 4.25 + 1/4.25 = 4.49。四次更新后，窗口大小在这个近似中为 4.92(在精确模型中我们期望它为 5)。"},{"en":"Implementing Multiplicative Decrease","zh_html":"实现乘法减少(Implementing Multiplicative Decrease)"},{"en":"If we detect loss from 3 duplicate acks, we divide the window size by 2.","zh_html":"如果我们从 3 个重复 ack 检测到丢失，就将窗口大小除以 2。"},{"en":"Recall that if the retransmission timer expires, we interpret the timeout as multiple packets being lost (we didn’t even get duplicate acks). We assume that the current window might be way off, and in order to be cautious, we’ll rediscover a good rate from scratch.","zh_html":"回忆一下，如果重传定时器(retransmission timer)到期，我们将超时解释为多个分组丢失(我们甚至没有收到重复 ack)。我们假设当前窗口可能完全错误，为了谨慎起见，我们将从头重新发现一个合适的速率。"},{"en":"First, we’ll make a note that the current rate is too high, and the best known safe rate is half of our current rate (following the multiplicative decrease principle). To record this safe rate, we’ll set SSTHRESH to half the current window.","zh_html":"首先，我们要记录当前速率过高，已知的最佳安全速率是当前速率的一半(遵循乘法减少原则)。为了记录这个安全速率，我们将 SSTHRESH 设为当前窗口的一半。"},{"en":"Then, we’ll set the window size back to 1 packet, and repeat the slow start process again.","zh_html":"然后，我们将窗口大小重置为 1 个分组，并重新进行慢启动过程。"},{"en":"Note that when we re-try slow start, we need to be careful not to return to the dangerous rate with timeouts from earlier. Fortunately, we set SSTHRESH to be just below the dangerous rate. Therefore, in subsequent slow start re-trys (where SSTHRESH is set), as soon as our window exceeds SSTHRESH, we should switch from multiplicative to additive increasing. On the first slow start, SSTHRESH is unset (or infinity).","zh_html":"注意，当我们重新尝试慢启动时，需要小心不要回到之前导致超时的危险速率。幸运的是，我们将 SSTHRESH 设置在略低于危险速率的位置。因此，在后续的慢启动重试(SSTHRESH 已被设置)中，一旦我们的窗口超过 SSTHRESH，就应从乘法增加切换到加法增加。在第一次慢启动时，SSTHRESH 未设置(或为无穷大)。"},{"en":"To summarize: In slow-start, we increase the window by 1 packet for each ack (results in doubling the rate on each RTT). When in AIMD, we increase the window by a fraction of the window size for each ack (results in increasing by 1 for each window’s worth of data). We decrease the window by halving it when receiving 3 duplicate acks, and changing it to 1 on a timeout.","zh_html":"总结：在慢启动中，每个 ack 将窗口增加 1 个分组(结果是每 RTT 速率翻倍)。在 AIMD 中，每个 ack 将窗口增加窗口的一个分数(结果是每窗口数据增加 1)。收到 3 个重复 ack 时将窗口减半，超时时将窗口设为 1。"},{"en":"Note that when decreasing, we never drop the window size to less than 1 packet. In the worst case, we need to allow 1 packet to be in-flight.","zh_html":"注意，在减少时，我们绝不会将窗口大小降到 1 个分组以下。在最坏情况下，我们需要允许 1 个分组在途。"},{"en":"TCP Sawtooth","zh_html":"TCP 锯齿波(TCP Sawtooth)"},{"en":"If we plot rate over time, we see the initial exponential growth (slow start). As soon as we experience loss, we cut the rate in half, and switch to AIMD mode. Now, we increase linearly until we encounter loss, and we halve the rate each time we encounter loss.","zh_html":"如果我们绘制速率随时间的变化图，会看到初始的指数增长(慢启动)。一旦遇到丢失，我们将速率减半，并切换到 AIMD 模式。之后，我们线性增加直到遇到丢失，然后每次遇到丢失时都将速率减半。"},{"en":"Fast Recovery: Running Example","zh_html":"快速恢复：运行示例(Fast Recovery: Running Example)"},{"en":"There’s one final problem we have to deal with in our congestion control implementation. When we encounter an isolated packet loss, the congestion window is halved, as intended. However, this has the unintended side effect of causing the sender to stall for some time before it can continue sending packets.","zh_html":"在我们的拥塞控制实现中，还有一个最终问题需要处理。当遇到孤立分组丢失(isolated packet loss)时，拥塞窗口按预期减半。然而，这有一个意外的副作用：发送方在能够继续发送分组之前会停滞一段时间。"},{"en":"To see this in action, let’s consider a running example. We send 10 packets, numbered 101 through 110. The first packet (101) is dropped.","zh_html":"为了看到这一点，让我们考虑一个运行示例。我们发送 10 个分组，编号为 101 到 110。第一个分组(101)被丢弃。"},{"en":"As a result, the other 9 packets, 102 through 110, are all acked as ack(101), because the next expected byte is still 101.","zh_html":"结果，其他 9 个分组(102 到 110)都被确认为 ack(101)，因为下一个期望的字节仍然是 101。"},{"en":"After the third duplicate ack(101) (generated by receiving 102, 103, and 104), the sender re-sends 101.","zh_html":"在第三个重复 ack(101)(由收到 102、103 和 104 产生)之后，发送方重传 101。"},{"en":"Eventually, the ack for the re-sent 101 arrives. It says ack(111), because packets 102 through 110 were all received earlier, and with the receipt of 101, the next expected byte is 111.","zh_html":"最终，重传的 101 的 ack 到达。它报告 ack(111)，因为分组 102 到 110 之前都已收到，而随着 101 的收到，下一个期望的字节是 111。"},{"en":"To summarize: At the sender’s end, we send 101 through 110, and 101 gets dropped. We get ack(101) from 102, ack(101) from 103, and ack(101) from 104. At this point, we re-send 101. Then, we get ack(101) from 105 through 110. Finally, we eventually get ack(111) from 101.","zh_html":"总结：在发送方，我们发送 101 到 110，101 被丢弃。我们从 102 收到 ack(101)，从 103 收到 ack(101)，从 104 收到 ack(101)。此时，我们重传 101。然后，我们从 105 到 110 收到 ack(101)。最后，我们最终从 101 收到 ack(111)。"},{"en":"At the recipient’s end, we receive 102 through 110, and send back ack(101) each time, since the next unreceived byte is still 101. Eventually, we receive the re-sent 101, and we send back ack(111) because the next unreceived byte is 111.","zh_html":"在接收方，我们收到 102 到 110，每次都回复 ack(101)，因为下一个未收到的字节仍然是 101。最终，我们收到重传的 101，并回复 ack(111)，因为下一个未收到的字节是 111。"},{"en":"What does CWND look like during this running example? Remember that the window starts at the first unacknowledged byte, and extends for CWND contiguous bytes. The only way to shift the window forward is to receive the first unacked byte. If we receive acks for some other bytes in the window, the window stays the same, because the window is determined by the first unacked byte.","zh_html":"在这个运行示例中 CWND 是什么样子的？记住，窗口从第一个未确认的字节开始，延伸 CWND 个连续字节。向前移动窗口的唯一方法是收到第一个未确认的字节。如果我们收到窗口中其他字节的 ack，窗口保持不变，因为窗口由第一个未确认的字节决定。"},{"en":"Fast Recovery: The Problem","zh_html":"快速恢复：问题(Fast Recovery: The Problem)"},{"en":"Let’s assume that CWND starts at 10. Packets 101 through 110 are allowed to be in flight. The sender sends 101 through 110, but 101 is dropped.","zh_html":"假设 CWND 开始时为 10。分组 101 到 110 被允许在途。发送方发送 101 到 110，但 101 被丢弃。"},{"en":"The sender sees ack(101), generated from the other side receiving 102. At this point, the first unacked byte is still 101, so the window stays unchanged. The only packets allowed to be in flights are still 101 through 110, and the sender cannot send anything new (e.g. 111 can’t be sent).","zh_html":"发送方看到 ack(101)，由对方收到 102 产生。此时，第一个未确认的字节仍然是 101，因此窗口保持不变。允许在途的分组仍然是 101 到 110，发送方不能发送任何新内容(例如不能发送 111)。"},{"en":"Next, the sender sees ack(101), generated from the other side receiving 103. Again, the first unacked byte is still 101, so the window is unchanged. The window still starts at 101 and extends to 110, and the sender can’t send anything new.","zh_html":"接下来，发送方看到 ack(101)，由对方收到 103 产生。同样，第一个未确认的字节仍然是 101，因此窗口不变。窗口仍然从 101 开始延伸到 110，发送方不能发送任何新内容。"},{"en":"Next, the sender sees ack(101), generated from the other side receiving 104. This is the third duplicate ack, so we must decrease CWND to 5. The first unacked byte is still 101, and CWND is 5, so packets 101 through 105 are allowed to be in flight. The sender still can’t send anything new. We re-send 101 (left-most packet in window) because we saw the third duplicate ack.","zh_html":"接下来，发送方看到 ack(101)，由对方收到 104 产生。这是第三个重复 ack，因此我们必须将 CWND 减小到 5。第一个未确认的字节仍然是 101，CWND 是 5，因此允许在途的分组是 101 到 105。发送方仍然不能发送任何新内容。我们重传 101(窗口中最左侧的分组)，因为我们看到了第三个重复 ack。"},{"en":"Next, the sender sees ack(101), generated from the other side receiving 105. The window is still 101 (first unacked byte) through 105 (CWND bytes later), so we can’t send anything new.","zh_html":"接下来，发送方看到 ack(101)，由对方收到 105 产生。窗口仍然是 101(第一个未确认的字节)到 105(CWND 字节之后)，因此我们不能发送任何新内容。"},{"en":"Next, the sender sees ack(101), generated from the other side receiving 106. Again, the window doesn’t change, and we can’t send anything new.","zh_html":"接下来，发送方看到 ack(101)，由对方收到 106 产生。同样，窗口不变，我们不能发送任何新内容。"},{"en":"The sender gets ack(101), ack(101), ack(101), ack(101) from the other side receiving 107, 108, 109, 110. In every case, 101 is still the first unacked byte, so the window is still 101 through 105, and the sender can’t send anything new.","zh_html":"发送方从对方收到 107、108、109、110 时分别收到 ack(101)、ack(101)、ack(101)、ack(101)。在每种情况下，101 仍然是第一个未确认的字节，因此窗口仍然是 101 到 105，发送方不能发送任何新内容。"},{"en":"What happened here? Only a single packet was dropped, but as a result, the sender had to completely stop sending for a long time.","zh_html":"这里发生了什么？只有一个分组被丢弃，但结果是发送方不得不完全停止发送很长时间。"},{"en":"The window is defined by the first unacked byte, so the window refuses to move forward until 101 is re-sent and acked. Even though all the other packets (102 through 110) arrive, the window is still stuck at 101, and later packets (111 onward) can’t be sent. The sender is stalled!","zh_html":"窗口由第一个未确认的字节定义，因此窗口拒绝向前移动，直到 101 被重传并确认。即使所有其他分组(102 到 110)都已到达，窗口仍然卡在 101，后面的分组(111 及以后)无法发送。发送方停滞了！"},{"en":"Eventually, the sender receives ack(111) from the re-sent 101. This causes the window to leap forward and slide to the new first unacked packet, 111. CWND is still 5, so the sender is now able to send 111 through 115.","zh_html":"最终，发送方收到重传的 101 的 ack(111)。这导致窗口向前跳跃并滑动到新的第一个未确认分组 111。CWND 仍然是 5，因此发送方现在可以发送 111 到 115。"},{"en":"What happened here? We now have a secondary problem. The sender stalled for a long time, but as soon as 101 was acked with ack(111), the window leapt forward all the way to 111-115, and the sender suddenly has to scramble to send 111-115 all at the same time.","zh_html":"这里发生了什么？我们现在有了第二个问题。发送方停滞了很长时间，但一旦 101 被确认(ack(111))，窗口就一路跳跃到 111-115，发送方突然不得不匆忙地同时发送 111-115。"},{"en":"The sender stalled for a long time, sending nothing, and then suddenly scrambled to send 111-115 at the same time. Now, the sender has to wait another full round-trip for 111-115 to get acked, before it can send 116 and beyond.","zh_html":"发送方停滞了很长时间，什么也没发送，然后突然匆忙地同时发送 111-115。现在，发送方必须再等一个完整的往返时间让 111-115 被确认，然后才能发送 116 及之后的分组。"},{"en":"In summary: The isolated packet loss caused the window to get stuck, which causes the sender to stall and send nothing. Eventually, when that packet is re-sent and acked, the window leaps forward, causing the sender to scramble and send a bunch of new packets at once. The sender now has to wait another round-trip for those new packets to get acked, before it can resume business as usual.","zh_html":"总结：孤立分组丢失导致窗口卡住，进而导致发送方停滞且不发送任何内容。最终，当该分组被重传并确认后，窗口向前跳跃，导致发送方匆忙地同时发送一堆新分组。发送方现在必须再等一个往返时间让这些新分组被确认，然后才能恢复正常发送。"},{"en":"A few notes about this problem:","zh_html":"关于这个问题的几点说明："},{"en":"If the problem is still kind of hard to grasp, it might help to note that this problem is more due to the TCP sliding window scheme, and not really due to the congestion control scheme. Congestion control causes the window to shrink, but even if the window didn’t shrink, the sender would still be forced to stall until 101 is received and the window leaps forward.","zh_html":"如果这个问题仍然有点难以理解，不妨注意这个问题更多地是由于 TCP 滑动窗口机制造成的，而不是由于拥塞控制机制。拥塞控制导致窗口缩小，但即使窗口不缩小，发送方也仍然会被迫停滞，直到 101 被收到且窗口向前跳跃。"},{"en":"When we think about this problem intuitively, it helps to draw the diagrams of the sender’s window, marking off the bytes that have been acked. For example, after the triple duplicate acks, we mark 102, 103, 104 as received, and the window allows 101 (first unacked byte) through 105 to be in flight.","zh_html":"当我们直观地思考这个问题时，画出发送方窗口的图示、标记已被确认的字节会有帮助。例如，在三个重复 ack 之后，我们标记 102、103、104 为已收到，窗口允许 101(第一个未确认的字节)到 105 在途。"},{"en":"However, this isn’t really what the sender sees. Remember, the sender only sees cumulative acks, so it doesn’t actually know that 102, 103, and 104 were received. The sender can deduce that 3 packets in the window (that are not 101) were received, but it doesn’t know which 3 packets exactly.","zh_html":"然而，这并不是发送方实际看到的情况。记住，发送方只看到累积 ack(cumulative acks)，所以它实际上不知道 102、103 和 104 已被收到。发送方可以推断出窗口中有 3 个分组(不是 101)已被收到，但它不知道具体是哪 3 个分组。"},{"en":"Finally, note that after we get 3 duplicate ack(101) messages, we re-send 101, and we never re-send 101 again, even if more duplicate ack(101) messages come in. This is just the TCP rule for re-sending on duplicate acks.","zh_html":"最后，注意在收到 3 个重复 ack(101)消息后，我们重传 101，即使更多重复 ack(101)消息到达，也不会再次重传 101。这只是 TCP 关于重复 ack 重传的规则。"},{"en":"Fast Recovery: The Idea","zh_html":"快速恢复：思路(Fast Recovery: The Idea)"},{"en":"So, how do we solve this problem? Ideally, we don’t want the sender to stall, and we want the sender to keep sending later packets (111 onwards), even if 101 gets lost.","zh_html":"那么，我们如何解决这个问题？理想情况下，我们不希望发送方停滞，我们希望发送方继续发送后面的分组(111 及以后)，即使 101 丢失了。"},{"en":"Notice that even though the sender can’t deduce exactly which packets arrive, the sender can deduce that later (non-101) packets are getting received.","zh_html":"注意，尽管发送方不能确切推断出哪些分组到达了，但发送方可以推断出后面的(非 101)分组正在被接收。"},{"en":"When we see ack(101), generated from 102 being received, we don’t actually know that 102 was received, but we know some packet (non-101) got received. Therefore, only 9 packets remain in flight.","zh_html":"当我们看到由收到 102 产生的 ack(101)时，我们实际上不知道 102 已被收到，但我们知道某个分组(非 101)已被收到。因此，只有 9 个分组仍在途。"},{"en":"When we see another ack(101), generated from 103 being received, we again don’t know that 103 specifically was received, but we know that another packet (non-101) got received. Therefore, only 8 packets remain in flight.","zh_html":"当我们看到另一个由收到 103 产生的 ack(101)时，我们同样不知道 103 具体已被收到，但我们知道另一个分组(非 101)已被收到。因此，只有 8 个分组仍在途。"},{"en":"As we keep receiving duplicate ack(101) messages, we can deduce that fewer packets remain in flight:","zh_html":"随着我们不断收到重复 ack(101)消息，我们可以推断出仍在途的分组越来越少："},{"en":"After ack(101) from 102: 9 packets in flight.","zh_html":"收到来自 102 的 ack(101)后：9 个分组在途。"},{"en":"After ack(101) from 103: 8 packets in flight.","zh_html":"收到来自 103 的 ack(101)后：8 个分组在途。"},{"en":"After ack(101) from 104: 7 packets in flight.","zh_html":"收到来自 104 的 ack(101)后：7 个分组在途。"},{"en":"After ack(101) from 105: 6 packets in flight.","zh_html":"收到来自 105 的 ack(101)后：6 个分组在途。"},{"en":"After ack(101) from 106: 5 packets in flight.","zh_html":"收到来自 106 的 ack(101)后：5 个分组在途。"},{"en":"After ack(101) from 107: 4 packets in flight.","zh_html":"收到来自 107 的 ack(101)后：4 个分组在途。"},{"en":"After ack(101) from 108: 3 packets in flight.","zh_html":"收到来自 108 的 ack(101)后：3 个分组在途。"},{"en":"After ack(101) from 109: 2 packets in flight.","zh_html":"收到来自 109 的 ack(101)后：2 个分组在途。"},{"en":"After ack(101) from 110: 1 packet in flight.","zh_html":"收到来自 110 的 ack(101)后：1 个分组在途。"},{"en":"Eventually, after we get ack(101) nine times (from 102 through 110 being received), we know that only 1 packet remains in flight, namely 101.","zh_html":"最终，在我们收到九次 ack(101)(从 102 到 110 被收到)后，我们知道只有 1 个分组仍在途，即 101。"},{"en":"After the isolated loss, we really want CWND to be 5, which means we want 5 packets in flight at any given time. By the time we get ack(101) from 107, we can deduce that only 4 packets remain in flight. (In reality, they are 101, 108, 109, 110, though the sender doesn’t know that.)","zh_html":"在孤立丢失之后，我们真正希望 CWND 为 5，这意味着我们希望在任何时刻有 5 个分组在途。到我们从 107 收到 ack(101)时，我们可以推断出只有 4 个分组仍在途。(实际上它们是 101、108、109、110，尽管发送方不知道。)"},{"en":"At this point, we’d like to be able to send 111, for a total of 5 packets in flight. But the window won’t let us do that, because the window is still stuck at 101 (first unacked byte) through 105 (CWND bytes later).","zh_html":"此时，我们希望可以发送 111，使总共 5 个分组在途。但窗口不允许我们这样做，因为窗口仍然卡在 101(第一个未确认的字节)到 105(CWND 字节之后)。"},{"en":"The key idea that will un-stall the sender is: Let’s grant the sender temporary credit for each duplicate ack.","zh_html":"解除发送方停滞的关键思路是：让每个重复 ack 为发送方提供临时信用(credit)。"},{"en":"When a duplicate ack arrives, we can deduce that one fewer packet is in flight, though we don’t know which one. To account for this, we will artificially extend the window by 1 packet, to allow the sender to send one more packet.","zh_html":"当重复 ack 到达时，我们可以推断出在途分组减少了一个，尽管我们不知道是哪一个。为了应对这一点，我们将人为地将窗口扩展 1 个分组，以允许发送方多发送一个分组。"},{"en":"Fast Recovery: The Solution","zh_html":"快速恢复：解决方案(Fast Recovery: The Solution)"},{"en":"Let’s take this idea of artificially extending the window for each duplicate ack, and apply it to the example from before.","zh_html":"让我们把这个为每个重复 ack 人为扩展窗口的思路应用到之前的例子中。"},{"en":"As before, the window starts at 101 through 110, and we send out 10 packets.","zh_html":"和之前一样，窗口从 101 到 110，我们发送 10 个分组。"},{"en":"As before, we get ack(101) from 102, the window stays unchanged, and we can’t send anything new.","zh_html":"和之前一样，我们从 102 收到 ack(101)，窗口保持不变，我们不能发送任何新内容。"},{"en":"As before, we get ack(101) from 103, the window stays unchanged, and we can’t send anything new.","zh_html":"和之前一样，我们从 103 收到 ack(101)，窗口保持不变，我们不能发送任何新内容。"},{"en":"As before, we get ack(101) from 104, the window stays unchanged, and we can’t send anything new.","zh_html":"和之前一样，我们从 104 收到 ack(101)，窗口保持不变，我们不能发送任何新内容。"},{"en":"The third duplicate ack means we decrease CWND to 5, so the window is now 101 through 105.","zh_html":"第三个重复 ack 意味着我们将 CWND 减小到 5，因此窗口现在是 101 到 105。"},{"en":"However, we got 3 acks, so we artificially extend the window by 3 to account for those acks. Thus, CWND is actually set to 5 + 3 = 8.","zh_html":"然而，我们收到了 3 个 ack，所以我们人为地将窗口扩展 3 以计入这些 ack。因此，CWND 实际设为 5 + 3 = 8。"},{"en":"Next, we get ack(101) from 105. This allows us to extend the window again, to 9. Now the window spans 101 through 109, so we still can’t send new packets.","zh_html":"接下来，我们从 105 收到 ack(101)。这让我们再次扩展窗口，到 9。现在窗口覆盖 101 到 109，所以我们仍然不能发送新分组。"},{"en":"Next, we get ack(101) from 106. We extend the window again to 10, spanning 101 through 110, and we can’t send anything new.","zh_html":"接下来，我们从 106 收到 ack(101)。我们再次扩展窗口到 10，覆盖 101 到 110，我们不能发送任何新内容。"},{"en":"Next, we get ack(101) from 107. We extend the window again to 11, spanning 101 through 111. We can now send out 111!","zh_html":"接下来，我们从 107 收到 ack(101)。我们再次扩展窗口到 11，覆盖 101 到 111。我们现在可以发送 111 了！"},{"en":"Next, we get ack(101) from 108. We extend the window again to 12, spanning 101 through 112. We can now send out 112!","zh_html":"接下来，我们从 108 收到 ack(101)。我们再次扩展窗口到 12，覆盖 101 到 112。我们现在可以发送 112 了！"},{"en":"Next, we get ack(101) from 109. We extend the window again to 13, spanning 101 through 113. We can now send out 113!","zh_html":"接下来，我们从 109 收到 ack(101)。我们再次扩展窗口到 13，覆盖 101 到 113。我们现在可以发送 113 了！"},{"en":"Next, we get ack(101) from 110. We extend the window again to 14, spanning 101 through 114. We can now send out 114!","zh_html":"接下来，我们从 110 收到 ack(101)。我们再次扩展窗口到 14，覆盖 101 到 114。我们现在可以发送 114 了！"},{"en":"Eventually, we get ack(111) from the re-sent 101. At this point, we can reset CWND to its original intended value of 5, so that the window spans 111 through 115. This allows us to send out 115!","zh_html":"最终，我们收到重传的 101 的 ack(111)。此时，我们可以将 CWND 重置为原本想要的值 5，这样窗口覆盖 111 到 115。这让我们可以发送 115！"},{"en":"With this fix, we have solved our problem of the stalled sender. Originally, the sender had to wait for the re-sent 101 to be acked before sending new packets. Now, the sender is now able to keep sending packets before the re-sent 101 is acked.","zh_html":"通过这个修复，我们解决了发送方停滞的问题。原来，发送方必须等待重传的 101 被确认后才能发送新分组。现在，发送方能够在重传的 101 被确认之前继续发送分组。"},{"en":"We also solved the secondary problem from earlier, where the window leapt forward and we sent a burst of new packets (111 through 115). Now, 111 through 114 were sent out earlier, and when the window leapt forward, we only had to send out 115.","zh_html":"我们还解决了之前的第二个问题，即窗口向前跳跃时我们发送了一突发新分组(111 到 115)。现在，111 到 114 已经提前发送了出去，当窗口向前跳跃时，我们只需要发送 115。"},{"en":"Without this fix, we had to stall for another round-trip while waiting for the burst of 111 through 115 to be acked. Now, because we stayed busy earlier and sent out 111 through 114, they’ll get acked earlier, and we can keep sending 116 and beyond without that entire RTT of stalling.","zh_html":"没有这个修复，我们不得不再停滞一个往返时间，等待 111 到 115 这一突发被确认。现在，因为我们提前保持了忙碌并发送了 111 到 114，它们会更早被确认，我们可以继续发送 116 及之后的分组，而无需浪费整个 RTT 的停滞。"},{"en":"Another way to look at this fix is to focus on the packets in the artificially-extended window.","zh_html":"看待这个修复的另一种方式是关注人为扩展窗口中的分组。"},{"en":"When we get the third duplicate ack, the CWND shrinks to 5, but we artificially extend for the 3 duplicate acks for a CWND of 8. If you look at this extended window, 3 of the packets are acked (102, 103, 104, though we don’t know it’s these), and the other 5 are in-flight. This achieves our intended window of 5 packets in-flight.","zh_html":"当我们收到第三个重复 ack 时，CWND 缩小到 5，但我们为 3 个重复 ack 人为扩展，得到 CWND 为 8。如果看看这个扩展窗口，其中 3 个分组已被确认(102、103、104，尽管我们不知道是这些)，另外 5 个在途。这实现了我们期望的 5 个分组在途的窗口。"},{"en":"Next, when we get another ack(101) from 105, the window extends to 9. Again, if you look in this window, 4 of the packets are acked (we don’t know which), and the other 5 are in-flight, giving us our intended window of 5 in-flight packets.","zh_html":"接下来，当我们从 105 收到另一个 ack(101)时，窗口扩展到 9。同样，如果看这个窗口，其中 4 个分组已被确认(我们不知道是哪 4 个)，另外 5 个在途，实现了我们期望的 5 个在途分组的窗口。"},{"en":"When we get ack(101) from 106, the window extends to 10, including 5 received packets (from 5 duplicate acks), plus 5 in-flight packets (intended window size).","zh_html":"当我们从 106 收到 ack(101)时，窗口扩展到 10，包括 5 个已收到的分组(来自 5 个重复 ack)，加上 5 个在途分组(期望的窗口大小)。"},{"en":"At every step, in our extended window, if you don’t count the packets that have been acked, there are exactly 5 packets in-flight in the window. Again, we don’t know exactly which packets in the window are acked, but we can use the duplicate acks to count how many packets were acked, and use that count to keep 5 in-flight packets.","zh_html":"在每一步中，在我们的扩展窗口里，如果不计算已被确认的分组，窗口中正好有 5 个分组在途。同样，我们不知道窗口中具体哪些分组已被确认，但我们可以用重复 ack 来计数有多少分组已被确认，并用这个计数来保持 5 个在途分组。"},{"en":"When we get ack(101) from 107, the window extends to 11, including 6 received packets (from 6 duplicate acks). The other 5 packets in the window are allowed to be in-flight.","zh_html":"当我们从 107 收到 ack(101)时，窗口扩展到 11，包括 6 个已收到的分组(来自 6 个重复 ack)。窗口中其他 5 个分组允许在途。"},{"en":"At this point, we sent 10 packets originally, and we got 6 duplicate acks, which tells us that only 4 packets remain in flight. This allows us to send out 111. The artificially-extending window captures this reasoning, because it extends the window to include 111.","zh_html":"此时，我们最初发送了 10 个分组，收到了 6 个重复 ack，这告诉我们只有 4 个分组仍在途。这让我们可以发送 111。人为扩展窗口抓住了这一推理，因为它扩展窗口以包含 111。"},{"en":"When we get ack(101) from 108, we deduce that now, one fewer packet is in flight. So we artificially extend the window again to 12, which allows 112 to be sent.","zh_html":"当我们从 108 收到 ack(101)时，我们推断现在在途分组又少了一个。所以我们再次人为扩展窗口到 12，这允许发送 112。"},{"en":"Fast Recovery: Implementation","zh_html":"快速恢复：实现(Fast Recovery: Implementation)"},{"en":"When we detect packet loss from duplicate acks, we temporarily enter the fast recovery mode, where additional duplicate acks artificially extend the window to prevent stalling.","zh_html":"当我们从重复 ack 检测到分组丢失时，我们暂时进入<strong>快速恢复(fast recovery)</strong>模式，在此模式下额外的重复 ack 会人为扩展窗口以防止停滞。"},{"en":"Fast recovery mode is triggered when we receive 3 duplicate acks. Instead of just halving CWND, as we did before, we now set CWND to CWND/2 + 3, where the window is artificially extended by 3 for the 3 duplicate acks we got. We also set SSTHRESH to CWND/2, so that we remember the new safe rate for later.","zh_html":"快速恢复模式在我们收到 3 个重复 ack 时触发。与之前直接将 CWND 减半不同，我们现在将 CWND 设为 CWND/2 + 3，其中窗口为收到的 3 个重复 ack 人为扩展了 3。我们还将 SSTHRESH 设为 CWND/2，以便记住之后的新安全速率。"},{"en":"While in fast recovery mode, every additional duplicate ack causes CWND to increase by 1, allowing the window to artificially extend.","zh_html":"在快速恢复模式中，每多一个重复 ack 都会使 CWND 增加 1，允许窗口人为扩展。"},{"en":"Eventually, when we receive a new, non-duplicate ack, we leave fast recovery mode and set CWND to SSTHRESH. Note that while we were artificially extending the window, SSTHRESH always helped us remember the original halved rate that we want to ultimately send at.","zh_html":"最终，当我们收到一个新的非重复 ack 时，我们退出快速恢复模式并将 CWND 设为 SSTHRESH。注意，在我们人为扩展窗口的过程中，SSTHRESH 始终帮助我们记住最初减半的速率，即我们最终希望使用的发送速率。"},{"en":"TCP State Machine","zh_html":"TCP 状态机(TCP State Machine)"},{"en":"We are finally ready to put all the pieces together and implement TCP, with congestion control.","zh_html":"我们终于准备好把所有部分拼在一起，实现包含拥塞控制的 TCP。"},{"en":"The sender maintains 5 values:","zh_html":"发送方维护 5 个值："},{"en":"The duplicate ack count helps us detect loss earlier than timeouts. It’s initialized to 0.","zh_html":"重复 ack 计数(duplicate ack count)：帮助我们比超时更早检测到丢失。初始化为 0。"},{"en":"The timer is used to detect loss. There’s just a single timer.","zh_html":"定时器(timer)：用于检测丢失。只有一个定时器。"},{"en":"RWND is used for flow control (don’t overwhelm recipient buffer).","zh_html":"RWND：用于流量控制(flow control，不让接收方缓冲区过载)。"},{"en":"CWND is used for congestion control. It’s initialized to 1 packet.","zh_html":"CWND：用于拥塞控制(congestion control)。初始化为 1 个分组。"},{"en":"SSTHRESH helps the congestion control algorithm remember the latest safe rate. It’s initialized to infinity.","zh_html":"SSTHRESH：帮助拥塞控制算法记住最新的安全速率。初始化为无穷大。"},{"en":"The recipient maintains a buffer of out-of-order packets.","zh_html":"接收方维护一个乱序分组的缓冲区(buffer)。"},{"en":"The sender responds to 3 events: Ack for new data (not previously acked), duplicate ack, and timeout.","zh_html":"发送方响应 3 个事件：新数据的 Ack(之前未确认)、重复 ack 和超时。"},{"en":"The recipient responds to receiving a packet, by replying with an ack and a RWND value.","zh_html":"接收方响应收到的分组，回复一个 ack 和一个 RWND 值。"},{"en":"Let’s see how the sender responds to each of the 3 events.","zh_html":"让我们看看发送方如何响应这 3 个事件。"},{"en":"When we receive an ack for new data, not previously acked: If in slow-start mode, we increase CWND by 1. This allows the CWND to double each RTT. If we’re in fast-recovery mode, we set CWND to SSTHRESH, so that we leave fast recovery (since we just got a new ack). If we’re in congestion avoidance mode, we add 1/CWND to CWND, so that CWND increases by 1 per RTT (additive increase). We also reset the timer, reset the duplicate ack count, and, if the window allows, send new data.","zh_html":"当我们收到新数据(之前未确认)的 ack 时：如果在慢启动模式，CWND 增加 1。这使 CWND 每 RTT 翻倍。如果在快速恢复模式，我们将 CWND 设为 SSTHRESH，从而退出快速恢复(因为我们刚收到一个新 ack)。如果在拥塞避免(congestion avoidance)模式，我们将 CWND 加上 1/CWND，使 CWND 每 RTT 增加 1(加法增加)。我们还重置定时器、重置重复 ack 计数，并且在窗口允许的情况下发送新数据。"},{"en":"When we receive a duplicate ack, we increment the duplicate ack count. If the count reaches 3, we re-send the left-most packet in the window. This is sometimes called fast retransmit. We also enter fast-recovery mode by setting SSTHRESH to CWND/2 (remember last safe rate) and set CWND to CWND/2 + 3 (adding 3 to artifically extend the window for duplicate acks). If the count exceeds 3, we stay in fast-recovery mode and artificially extend the CWND by 1 for every subsequent duplicate ack.","zh_html":"当我们收到重复 ack 时，重复 ack 计数加 1。如果计数达到 3，我们重传窗口中最左侧的分组。这有时称为快速重传(fast retransmit)。我们还将 SSTHRESH 设为 CWND/2(记住最后的安全速率)，并将 CWND 设为 CWND/2 + 3(加 3 以为重复 ack 人为扩展窗口)，从而进入快速恢复模式。如果计数超过 3，我们保持在快速恢复模式，并且每收到一个后续重复 ack，CWND 人为增加 1。"},{"en":"When the timer expires, we re-send the left-most packet in the window. We also go back to slow-start mode, setting SSTHRESH to CWND/2 (remembering the last safe rate), and resetting CWND back to 1 packet.","zh_html":"当定时器到期时，我们重传窗口中最左侧的分组。我们还回到慢启动模式，将 SSTHRESH 设为 CWND/2(记住最后的安全速率)，并将 CWND 重置回 1 个分组。"},{"en":"The congestion control state machine shows the 3 possible modes that TCP can be in, and the conditions that trigger transitions between the modes.","zh_html":"拥塞控制状态机(state machine)展示了 TCP 可能处于的 3 种模式，以及触发模式间转换的条件。"},{"en":"We enter fast-recovery mode if we receive 3 duplicate acks. Once we’re in this mode, any further duplicate acks keep us in fast-recovery mode (keep artificially extending window). To leave fast-recovery mode, either a timeout switches us back to slow-start mode, or a new ack lets us go back to congestion-avoidance mode.","zh_html":"如果我们收到 3 个重复 ack，就进入快速恢复模式。一旦进入此模式，任何后续重复 ack 都会让我们保持在快速恢复模式(持续人为扩展窗口)。要退出快速恢复模式，要么超时将我们切换回慢启动模式，要么新 ack 让我们回到拥塞避免模式。"},{"en":"A timeout triggers slow-start mode. Any further acks (duplicate or new) keep us in slow-start. Eventually, if CWND exceeds SSTHRESH (the safe rate), we enter congestion avoidance mode. Or, if we detect loss, we halve the rate and enter fast-recovery mode for a bit before going to congestion-avoidance mode.","zh_html":"超时触发慢启动模式。任何后续 ack(重复或新)让我们保持在慢启动。最终，如果 CWND 超过 SSTHRESH(安全速率)，我们进入拥塞避免模式。或者，如果检测到丢失，我们将速率减半并短暂进入快速恢复模式，然后进入拥塞避免模式。"},{"en":"In congestion avoidance mode, new acks keep us in this mode (additive increase), but duplicate acks send us to fast-recovery mode, and timeouts send us to slow-start mode.","zh_html":"在拥塞避免模式中，新 ack 让我们保持在此模式(加法增加)，但重复 ack 将我们送入快速恢复模式，超时将我们送入慢启动模式。"},{"en":"TCP Congestion Control Variants","zh_html":"TCP 拥塞控制变体(TCP Congestion Control Variants)"},{"en":"There are several variants of the TCP congestion control algorithm, all implemented in the end host’s operating system. Fun fact: The names are related to the Berkeley Software Distribution (BSD) operating system.","zh_html":"TCP 拥塞控制算法有多个变体，都在端主机的操作系统中实现。趣闻：名称与伯克利软件发行版(Berkeley Software Distribution, BSD)操作系统有关。"},{"en":"In TCP Tahoe, if we get three duplicate acks, we reset CWND to 1, instead of halving CWND.","zh_html":"在 TCP Tahoe 中，如果收到三个重复 ack，我们将 CWND 重置为 1，而不是减半。"},{"en":"In TCP Reno, if we get three duplicate acks, we halve CWND. On timeout, we reset CWND to 1.","zh_html":"在 TCP Reno 中，如果收到三个重复 ack，我们将 CWND 减半。超时时，将 CWND 重置为 1。"},{"en":"TCP New Reno is the same as Reno, but adds fast recovery. This is what we just implemented.","zh_html":"TCP New Reno 与 Reno 相同，但增加了快速恢复(fast recovery)。这就是我们刚刚实现的内容。"},{"en":"Other variants exist too. In TCP-SACK, we add selective acknowledgments where acks contain more detail (e.g. received all up to 13, and 18 too).","zh_html":"还有其他变体。在 TCP-SACK 中，我们增加了选择性确认(selective acknowledgments)，其中 ack 包含更多细节(例如，已收到截至 13 的所有内容，以及 18)。"},{"en":"How can all these different variants co-exist? Why don’t we need a single uniform protocol that everybody speaks? Remember, congestion control is implemented at the end hosts, so the sender can do whatever they want to adjust their rate. Ultimately, the network and the other end hosts just see TCP packets being sent at a (hopefully reasonable) rate, and they don’t care how the rate is being computed. The underlying TCP packet format doesn’t change with the different congestion control algorithms.","zh_html":"所有这些不同的变体如何共存？为什么不需要一个所有人都使用的统一协议？记住，拥塞控制在端主机上实现，因此发送方可以做任何它们想做的事情来调整速率。最终，网络和其他端主机只看到 TCP 分组以(希望是合理的)速率被发送，它们并不关心速率是如何计算的。底层的 TCP 分组格式不会因不同的拥塞控制算法而改变。"},{"en":"Not all protocols are compatible, though. If you use the TCP-SACK variant with selective acknowledgements, and I use TCP Tahoe, we have a problem. You expect selective acks, but I’m only providing cumulative acks.","zh_html":"不过，并非所有协议都兼容。如果你使用带选择性确认的 TCP-SACK 变体，而我使用 TCP Tahoe，就会出现问题。你期望选择性 ack，但我只提供累积 ack(cumulative acks)。"}]},"/transport/cc-issues.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Congestion Control Issues","zh_html":"<a href=\"#congestion-control-issues\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"congestion-control-issues\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 拥塞控制问题"},{"en":"Confusing Corruption and Congestion","zh_html":"<a href=\"#confusing-corruption-and-congestion\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"confusing-corruption-and-congestion\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 混淆损坏与拥塞"},{"en":"TCP detects congestion by checking for packet loss, but congestion isn’t the only reason packets would be lost. Packets could also be lost from congestion, and TCP cannot distinguish between loss due to corruption or congestion. If a packet is corrupted, TCP will still drop its rate, even if the network isn’t congested.","zh_html":"TCP 通过检查分组丢失(packet loss)来检测拥塞(congestion)，但拥塞并不是分组丢失的唯一原因。分组也可能因为拥塞而丢失，TCP 无法区分丢失是由于损坏(corruption)还是拥塞(congestion)导致的。如果一个分组被损坏，即使网络没有拥塞，TCP 仍然会降低其发送速率。"},{"en":"We can also see this in our equation, which related throughput to loss rate. The throughput and loss rate are inversely proportional, even for non-congestion losses. The equation can be helpful for estimating how a lossy link (e.g. a wireless link that frequently corrupts packets) would affect TCP.","zh_html":"我们也可以从之前关联吞吐量(throughput)与丢包率(loss rate)的方程中看到这一点。即使是非拥塞导致的丢失，吞吐量和丢包率仍然是成反比的。该方程有助于估算一条有损链路(例如频繁损坏分组的无线链路)会如何影响 TCP。"},{"en":"Short Connections","zh_html":"<a href=\"#short-connections\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"short-connections\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 短连接"},{"en":"Most TCP connections in real life are very short-lived. 50% of connections send fewer than 1.5 KB, and 80% of connections send less than 100 KB. Very few packets (maybe only one) are sent during these connections.","zh_html":"现实中，大多数 TCP 连接(connection)的寿命都非常短。50%的连接发送的数据少于 1.5 KB，80%的连接发送的数据少于 100 KB。这些连接只发送很少的分组(可能只有一个)。"},{"en":"Suppose we had a connection where the sender only had 3 packets to send. What would TCP congestion control do? We’d start with window size 1 and send the first packet. Then, we’d wait for the ack, increase the window size to 2, and send the remaining two packets. Then, we’d wait for two more acks, and finish.","zh_html":"假设有一个连接，发送方只发送 3 个分组。TCP 拥塞控制会怎么做？我们会从窗口大小(window size)为 1 开始，发送第一个分组。然后，等待 ack，将窗口大小增加到 2，再发送剩下的两个分组。然后，再等待两个 ack，连接就结束了。"},{"en":"This connection took two RTTs to send 3 packets, resulting in an incredibly low throughput (1.5 packets per RTT).","zh_html":"这个连接需要两个 RTT 才能发送 3 个分组，导致吞吐量极低(每 RTT 仅 1.5 个分组)。"},{"en":"More generally, these short connections never leave the slow-start phase, and never reach their fair share of bandwidth. This causes short connections to suffer from unnecessarily long transfer times.","zh_html":"更一般地说，这些短连接永远不会离开慢启动阶段(slow-start phase)，也永远无法达到它们的公平带宽份额(fair share of bandwidth)。这导致短连接的传输时间不必要地延长。"},{"en":"Another problem with short connections is handling loss. Recall that we detect loss when there are 3 duplicate acks, but in a short connection, we might not have enough packets to trigger these duplicate acks. For example, if we had 4 packets to send, and we lost the second packet, we’d never get 3 duplicate acks. Instead, we’d have to wait for the timeout to trigger. At typical real-world timeout values of roughly 500ms, this can also cause short connections to take unnecessarily long.","zh_html":"短连接的另一个问题是处理丢失(loss)。回想一下，我们是在收到 3 个重复 ack(duplicate acks)时检测丢失的，但在短连接中，可能没有足够的分组来触发这些重复 ack。例如，如果我们要发送 4 个分组，但第二个分组丢失了，我们永远得不到 3 个重复 ack。相反，我们只能等待超时(timeout)触发。在典型的现实超时值(大约 500ms)下，这也可能导致短连接花费不必要的时间。"},{"en":"How can we fix both of these problems? One partial fix is to start with a higher initial window (e.g. 10 packets instead of 1). Now, connections with 10 or fewer packets can just send all the data at the start of the connection.","zh_html":"我们如何解决这两个问题？一个部分解决方案是使用更高的初始窗口(initial window，例如 10 个分组而不是 1 个)。这样，只需要发送 10 个或更少分组的连接就可以在连接开始时直接发送所有数据。"},{"en":"TCP Fills Up Queues","zh_html":"<a href=\"#tcp-fills-up-queues\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"tcp-fills-up-queues\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> TCP 填满队列"},{"en":"TCP detects congestion using loss, and the congestion control algorithm deliberately increases the rate until triggering loss. In order to trigger loss, queues need to fill up. This means that TCP introduces queuing delays throughout the network, and the delays affect everybody in the network.","zh_html":"TCP 使用丢失来检测拥塞(congestion)，并且拥塞控制算法故意增加速率直到触发丢失。为了触发丢失，队列(queue)需要被填满。这意味着 TCP 在整个网络中引入了排队延迟(queuing delays)，而这些延迟会影响网络中的所有人。"},{"en":"Suppose we had one heavy-duty connection transferring a 10 GB file, and later, we start a small connection transferring a single packet. Both the connections share the same bottleneck link. The heavy-duty connection will increase its rate until the bottleneck link’s queue fills up. Now, when the small connection starts, it is stuck waiting in the queue, behind the heavy-duty connection packets.","zh_html":"假设有一条重负载连接正在传输一个 10 GB 的文件，然后我们启动一条小连接来传输一个分组。两条连接共享同一条瓶颈链路(bottleneck link)。重负载连接会持续增加其速率，直到瓶颈链路的队列被填满。这时，小连接开始发送数据时，只能排在队列中等待，位于重负载连接的分组之后。"},{"en":"This problem is made worse if routers keep extremely large queues. Routers having excessive memory for long queues is called bufferbloat. An example of bufferbloat occurs in home routers, which might have a huge queue, but very few connections (only the ones in your home) using that queue. Now, any connections you make will cause large queuing delays for other connections.","zh_html":"如果路由器保持极大的队列，这个问题会变得更加严重。路由器拥有过多内存来维持长队列的现象称为缓冲区膨胀(bufferbloat)。缓冲区膨胀的一个例子出现在家用路由器中：它们可能有巨大的队列，但使用该队列的连接却很少(只有你家里的那些连接)。这样，你建立的任何连接都会给其他连接带来巨大的排队延迟。"},{"en":"To avoid queues filling up, we could find a way to measure congestion that doesn’t involve deliberately triggering losses. In particular, we could detect congestion when the RTT starts increasing, which indicates delay. This is the idea behind Google’s recent BBR algorithm (2016). The sender learns its minimum RTT, and decreases its rate if it starts noticing the RTT exceeding the minimum.","zh_html":"为了避免队列被填满，我们可以找到一种不故意触发丢失的拥塞测量方法。具体来说，我们可以在 RTT 开始增加时检测到拥塞，这表明发生了延迟。这就是 Google 最近的 BBR 算法(2016 年)背后的思想。发送方学习其最小 RTT，并在发现 RTT 超过最小值时降低其速率。"},{"en":"Cheating","zh_html":"<a href=\"#cheating\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"cheating\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 作弊"},{"en":"There is nothing enforcing that senders have to follow the TCP congestion control algorithm. Senders could cheat to get an unfairly large share of the bandwidth.","zh_html":"没有任何机制强制要求发送方必须遵循 TCP 拥塞控制算法。发送方可以通过作弊来获得不公平的大量带宽(bandwidth)。"},{"en":"For example, a sender could increase the window faster (e.g. +2 every RTT, instead of +1). If we applied our graphical model to one cheating sender and one honest sender, AIMD updates would actually converge on a bad fairness line where the cheating sender gets twice the bandwidth of the honest sender.","zh_html":"例如，发送方可以更快地增加窗口(例如每个 RTT 增加 2，而不是 1)。如果我们把图形模型应用到一个作弊发送方和一个诚实发送方，AIMD 更新实际上会收敛到一条糟糕的公平性(fairness)线上，作弊发送方获得的带宽是诚实发送方的两倍。"},{"en":"Many other ways to modify the protocol also exist, such as starting with very large initial congestion window.","zh_html":"还有许多其他修改协议的方式，例如从非常大的初始拥塞窗口(initial congestion window)开始。"},{"en":"In practice, because TCP is implemented in the operating system, in order to cheat, the sender would have to modify the code in their operating system, which the vast majority of Internet users don’t do.","zh_html":"实践中，由于 TCP 是在操作系统(operating system)中实现的，为了作弊，发送方必须修改其操作系统中的代码，而绝大多数互联网用户不会这样做。"},{"en":"If a small number of senders abuse the system, those senders will get more bandwidth. If a large number of senders abuse the system (e.g. Microsoft releases a version of Windows that abuses TCP), the millions of Windows users are still competing with each other, and it’s unlikely that anybody will end up with more bandwidth.","zh_html":"如果少数发送方滥用系统，这些发送方将获得更多带宽。如果大量发送方滥用系统(例如微软发布了一个滥用 TCP 的 Windows 版本)，数百万 Windows 用户之间仍然在相互竞争，很可能没有人最终获得更多带宽。"},{"en":"Another way to cheat, without modifying TCP, is to open many connections. TCP only ensures that each connection gets a fair share. If a cheating sender opened 10 connections and an honest sender opened 1, the cheating sender would get 10 times more bandwidth. Many applications intentionally open more connections to improve bandwidth.","zh_html":"另一种不修改 TCP 的作弊方法是打开多个连接。TCP 只保证每个连接获得公平份额。如果作弊发送方打开 10 个连接，而诚实发送方只打开 1 个，那么作弊发送方将获得 10 倍的带宽。许多应用故意打开更多连接来提高带宽。"},{"en":"If cheating is possible, why hasn’t the Internet suffered another congestion collapse? It turns out, researchers don’t really know the answer either. One possibility is: cheaters who modify the congestion control algorithm might get an unfair share of bandwidth, but if they’re still following the principles of congestion control (e.g. reducing rate when loss occurs), then they aren’t overwhelming the network. By contrast, in the original 1980s congestion collapse, senders kept re-sending packets at high rates, with no notion of adjusting rate.","zh_html":"既然作弊是可能的，为什么互联网没有再次遭受拥塞崩溃(congestion collapse)？事实证明，研究者也不太清楚答案。一种可能性是：修改拥塞控制算法的作弊者可能会获得不公平的带宽份额，但如果他们仍然遵循拥塞控制的原则(例如在发生丢失时降低速率)，那么他们并没有压垮网络。相比之下，在 1980 年代最初的拥塞崩溃中，发送方以高速率不断重传分组，完全没有调整速率的概念。"},{"en":"If cheating is possible, how much cheating occurs in practice? Again, we don’t really know. It’s hard to measure cheating (e.g. you don’t know the windows being used at every sender).","zh_html":"如果作弊是可能的，实践中作弊的程度有多严重？同样，我们并不真正知道。很难测量作弊(例如，你无法知道每个发送方正在使用的窗口大小)。"},{"en":"Congestion Control and Reliability are Intertwined","zh_html":"<a href=\"#congestion-control-and-reliability-are-intertwined\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"congestion-control-and-reliability-are-intertwined\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 拥塞控制与可靠性相互交织"},{"en":"The mechanisms for congestion control and reliability are tightly coupled. As we saw, congestion control was implemented by taking the code for TCP reliability and tweaking a few lines of code.","zh_html":"拥塞控制(congestion control)和可靠性(reliability)的机制紧密耦合。如我们所见，拥塞控制是通过采用 TCP 可靠性的代码并修改几行代码来实现的。"},{"en":"We can also see this dependence in the algorithm itself. The window is updated on acks and timeouts because the reliability code was written to respond to those events. We detect loss with duplicate acks because the reliability implementation uses cumulative acks.","zh_html":"我们也可以从算法本身看到这种依赖关系。窗口在收到 ack 和超时(timeout)时更新，这是因为可靠性代码是为响应这些事件而编写的。我们通过重复 ack(duplicate acks)来检测丢失，这是因为可靠性实现使用了累积 ack(cumulative acks)。"},{"en":"Combining reliability and congestion control is a design choice. One benefit is that congestion control was a small code patch that could be widely deployed in response to the 1980s congestion collapse. However, since then, the combination of the two features has complicated evolution of our algorithms. For example, if we wanted to change something about our congestion control algorithm, we’d likely have to change the code for reliability as well. Or, if we wanted to change the reliability implementation (e.g. change from cumulative to full-information acks), we’d have to update congestion control as well.","zh_html":"将可靠性和拥塞控制结合在一起是一个设计选择。一个好处是，拥塞控制可以作为一个小的代码补丁广泛部署，以应对 1980 年代的拥塞崩溃。然而，自那以后，这两种功能的结合使我们的算法演化变得复杂。例如，如果我们想改变拥塞控制算法的某些内容，我们很可能也需要改变可靠性的代码。或者，如果我们想改变可靠性实现(例如从累积 ack 改为全信息 ack)，我们也必须更新拥塞控制。"},{"en":"From a design perspective, this is a failure of modularity, not layering. Congestion control and reliability are operating at the correct layer of abstraction (transport layer). However, within the transport layer, we haven’t cleanly separated different functionality into different parts of our code.","zh_html":"从设计的角度来看，这是模块化(modularity)的失败，而不是分层(layering)的失败。拥塞控制和可靠性在正确的抽象层(传输层)上运作。然而，在传输层内部，我们没有将不同功能清晰地分离到代码的不同部分。"},{"en":"Because congestion control relies on reliability, it’s hard to achieve congestion control without reliability. Some applications (e.g. video streaming) might not want reliability, but still want congestion control. But there’s no way to disable reliability and keep only congestion control.","zh_html":"由于拥塞控制依赖于可靠性，没有可靠性很难实现拥塞控制。某些应用(例如视频流)可能不需要可靠性，但仍然需要拥塞控制。但没有办法禁用可靠性而只保留拥塞控制。"},{"en":"Likewise, it’s hard to achieve reliability without congestion control. For example, if we had a lightweight connection that sent one packet every 10 minutes, we probably don’t need congestion control for this connection. But we can’t easily disable congestion control for only some connections.","zh_html":"同样，没有拥塞控制也很难实现可靠性。例如，如果我们有一条轻量级连接，每 10 分钟发送一个分组，我们可能不需要对该连接进行拥塞控制。但我们不能轻易地只为某些连接禁用拥塞控制。"}]},"/transport/cc-principles.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Congestion Control Principles","zh_html":"<a href=\"#congestion-control-principles\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"congestion-control-principles\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 拥塞控制原理"},{"en":"Congestion is Harmful","zh_html":"<a href=\"#congestion-is-harmful\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"congestion-is-harmful\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 拥塞是有害的"},{"en":"Recall that if many packets arrive at a router at the same time (e.g. bursty traffic), and the router needs to send both packets over the same link, then the router will send one packet and put the other packets in a queue (to be sent later).","zh_html":"回想一下，如果许多分组在同一时间到达一个路由器(例如突发流量(bursty traffic))，并且该路由器需要通过同一条链路发送这些分组，那么路由器会发送一个分组，并将其他分组放入队列(queue)中(稍后发送)。"},{"en":"More generally, if the input rate of packets exceeds the output rate that the link can sustain, the router will be unable to keep up with the pace of incoming packets. This router is congested, and needs to keep packets in a queue while they wait their turn to be sent. The queue can cause packets to be delayed. If the queue itself gets too full and packets are still incoming, then packets can get dropped.","zh_html":"更一般地说，如果分组的输入速率超过了链路能够维持的输出速率，路由器将无法跟上传入分组的节奏。这个路由器就是拥塞(congested)的，它需要将分组保留在队列中，等待轮到它们被发送。队列会导致分组被延迟。如果队列本身变得太满，而分组仍在不断到达，那么分组可能被丢弃(dropped)。"},{"en":"This graph shows the performance of a queueing system with bursty arrivals. The dotted line represents the link’s capacity (maximum load). As we increase the load, packets get more delayed.","zh_html":"此图显示了一个具有突发到达的排队系统的性能。虚线表示链路的容量(capacity，最大负载)。随着负载增加，分组延迟也增加。"},{"en":"When arrivals are bursty, we can’t realistically use the maximum capacity of the link. We have to find an appropriate performance trade-off between load and packet delay.","zh_html":"当到达是突发性的时，我们实际上无法达到链路的最大容量。我们必须在负载(load)和分组延迟(packet delay)之间找到一个合适的性能权衡。"},{"en":"Notice that the graph starts sloping upwards even before we reach the dotted line. This means that the queueing is already delaying packets, even if nothing is dropped. By the time we reach maximum utilization and start losing packets, we are already incurring very large packet delays from the queue.","zh_html":"注意，即使在到达虚线之前，图表就已经开始向上倾斜了。这意味着排队已经在延迟分组，即使没有任何分组被丢弃。到我们达到最大利用率并开始丢失分组时，队列已经导致了非常大的分组延迟。"},{"en":"Brief History of Congestion","zh_html":"<a href=\"#brief-history-of-congestion\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"brief-history-of-congestion\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 拥塞简史"},{"en":"In the 1980s, TCP did not implement any congestion control. The sending rate was only limited by flow control (recipient buffer capacity).","zh_html":"在 1980 年代，TCP 没有实现任何拥塞控制(congestion control)。发送速率仅受流量控制(flow control，接收方缓冲区容量)的限制。"},{"en":"If packets were dropped, the sender would re-send copies of the packet repeatedly, at the same fast rate, until the packet arrived. A smarter approach would be to slow down to avoid packets being dropped and reduce the copies clogging up the network, but early TCP implementations did not do this.","zh_html":"如果分组被丢弃，发送方会以同样快速率反复重传该分组的副本，直到分组到达为止。更聪明的做法是减慢速度以避免分组被丢弃，并减少堵塞网络的副本数量，但早期的 TCP 实现并没有这样做。"},{"en":"In October 1986, the Internet started to suffer from a series of congestion collapses, where the capacity of the Internet significantly decreased. One link between UC Berkeley to Lawrence Berkeley Lab (two sites roughly 400 yards away) had its throughput drop from 32 Kbps = 32,000 bps to 40 bps.","zh_html":"1986 年 10 月，互联网开始遭受一系列拥塞崩溃(congestion collapse)，互联网的容量显著下降。从 UC Berkeley 到 Lawrence Berkeley Lab(两地相距约 400 码)的一条链路上，吞吐量从 32 Kbps = 32,000 bps 下降到 40 bps。"},{"en":"Michael Karels (UC Berkeley undergraduate) and Van Jacobson (Lawrence Berkeley Lab researcher) were working on the networking stack in the Berkeley Unix system (influential early operating system), and they realized that the network had thousands of copies of the same packet, because everybody was trying to re-send packets that were being dropped.","zh_html":"Michael Karels(UC Berkeley 本科生)和 Van Jacobson(Lawrence Berkeley Lab 研究员)当时正在 Berkeley Unix 系统(一个有影响力的早期操作系统)上开发网络栈，他们发现网络中有成千上万个同一分组的副本，因为每个人都在试图重传那些被丢弃的分组。"},{"en":"Karels and Jacobson developed an algorithm for fixing the problem, which evolved into the modern TCP congestion control algorithm. Their fix was a modification to TCP itself, where the window size (which dictates the rate of sending packets) is dynamically adjusted in response to packet loss.","zh_html":"Karels 和 Jacobson 开发了一种解决问题的算法，该算法演变为现代的 TCP 拥塞控制算法。他们的修复是对 TCP 本身的一个修改：窗口大小(决定分组发送速率)根据分组丢失动态调整。"},{"en":"Because their solution was a modification to the logic of TCP (recall, TCP is implemented in the operating system), no upgrades to routers or applications were needed.","zh_html":"由于他们的解决方案是对 TCP 逻辑的修改(回想一下，TCP 是在操作系统中实现的)，因此不需要升级路由器或应用程序。"},{"en":"TCP congestion control is one of many examples of Internet design being ad-hoc. Karels and Jacobson’s patch was only several lines of extra code in the BSD operating system’s implementation of TCP. The patch worked, so it was quickly adopted. Since then, the topic of congestion control has been extensively researched and several improvements have been made, but ultimately, the core ideas in the original patch persist to this day. The Internet has not had a congestion collapse since then, so the original fix has withstood the test of time.","zh_html":"TCP 拥塞控制是互联网设计采用临时(ad-hoc)方式的众多例子之一。Karels 和 Jacobson 的补丁只是在 BSD 操作系统的 TCP 实现中增加了区区几行代码。这个补丁有效，因此被迅速采用。自那以后，拥塞控制的主题得到了广泛研究，并进行了若干改进，但最终，原始补丁中的核心思想一直延续到今天。自那时起，互联网没有再出现拥塞崩溃，因此原始修复经受住了时间的考验。"},{"en":"Why is Congestion Control Hard?","zh_html":"<a href=\"#why-is-congestion-control-hard\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"why-is-congestion-control-hard\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 为什么拥塞控制是困难的？"},{"en":"To get a sense of why congestion control is a difficult problem, consider the following network graph. At what rate should host A send traffic?","zh_html":"为了理解为什么拥塞控制是一个困难的问题，请考虑下面的网络图。主机 A 应该以什么速率发送流量？"},{"en":"It depends on the destination, so A can’t just come up with one fixed rate for all destinations. For example, if A is communicating with C, then A could send packets at 10 Gbps.","zh_html":"这取决于目的地，所以 A 不能简单地为所有目的地设定一个固定的速率。例如，如果 A 与 C 通信，那么 A 可以以 10 Gbps 发送分组。"},{"en":"What if A is communicating with F instead? The bottleneck link (least capacity) along this path is 2Gbps, so A should probably send packets at 2 Gbps.","zh_html":"如果 A 改为与 F 通信呢？沿着这条路径的瓶颈链路(bottleneck link，容量最小)是 2Gbps，所以 A 可能应该以 2 Gbps 发送分组。"},{"en":"What if A is communicating with E?","zh_html":"如果 A 与 E 通信呢？"},{"en":"It depends on what path the traffic is taking between A and E. If the traffic is taking the bottom path through R3, then A could send packets at 10 Gbps. But if the traffic is taking the top path through R2, then A can now only send packets at 1 Gbps.","zh_html":"这取决于 A 和 E 之间的流量走哪条路径。如果流量经过 R3 走下面的路径，那么 A 可以以 10 Gbps 发送分组。但如果流量经过 R2 走上面的路径，那么 A 只能以 1 Gbps 发送分组。"},{"en":"One takeaway so far is that our congestion control algorithm will need to somehow learn about the bandwidths and bottlenecks along the path that the packet is taking.","zh_html":"到目前为止的一个结论是，我们的拥塞控制算法需要以某种方式了解分组所经路径上的带宽和瓶颈。"},{"en":"Also, recall that the network graph changes over time as new links are added or links go down. This means that it’s not enough to learn about paths a single time. Our algorithm will need to be adaptive to changes in network topology.","zh_html":"另外，回想一下网络拓扑(network topology)会随着时间变化，因为会添加新链路或链路出现故障。这意味着仅一次性地了解路径是不够的。我们的算法需要能够适应网络拓扑的变化。"},{"en":"So far, we’ve assumed that A is the only host sending traffic on the network, and A can use the full capacity of every link. But what if other connections are also using bandwidth?","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们假设 A 是网络上唯一发送流量的主机，A 可以使用每条链路的全部容量。但如果其他连接也在使用带宽呢？"},{"en":"In this example, A and F have a connection, and B and E have a connection. The two connections seem like they should be totally separate (different senders, different recipients), but in fact, their paths share a link in the network.","zh_html":"在这个例子中，A 和 F 有一条连接，B 和 E 有一条连接。这两条连接看起来应该是完全分离的(不同的发送方，不同的接收方)，但实际上，它们的路径在网络中共用了一条链路。"},{"en":"If we want the two connections to share the capacity on this link fairly, maybe A and B should each send at 1 Gbps.","zh_html":"如果我们希望这两条连接公平地共享这条链路的容量，也许 A 和 B 应该各以 1 Gbps 发送。"},{"en":"What if a new connection starts between G and D? Should A change its rate of 1 Gbps? (No formal algorithm yet, just think about using bandwidth in a way that seems reasonable.)","zh_html":"如果一条新的连接在 G 和 D 之间启动呢？A 应该改变其 1 Gbps 的速率吗？(还没有正式的算法，只是思考如何以合理的方式使用带宽。)"},{"en":"First, notice that the G-D and B-E connections are sharing a link. This means that these two connections have to slow their rate down to 0.5 Gbps.","zh_html":"首先，注意到 G-D 和 B-E 连接正在共用一条链路。这意味着这两条连接必须将速率降低到 0.5 Gbps。"},{"en":"Now, if we look back at the 2 Gbps link that A-F and B-E had in common, B-E is only using 0.5 Gbps on this link. This means that A could increase its rate to 1.5 Gbps.","zh_html":"现在，回头再看 A-F 和 B-E 共享的那条 2 Gbps 链路，B-E 在该链路上只使用了 0.5 Gbps。这意味着 A 可以将其速率提高到 1.5 Gbps。"},{"en":"What happened here? The G-D connection was created, and its path has no links in common with the A-F connection. And yet, this seemingly unrelated connection caused the A-F connection’s rate to increase. Connections can indirectly affect other connections, even if those two connections don’t share any links in common!","zh_html":"这里发生了什么？G-D 连接被创建，而它的路径与 A-F 连接没有任何共同链路。然而，这个看似无关的连接却导致 A-F 连接的速率增加了。连接可以间接影响其他连接，即使这两条连接没有任何共同链路！"},{"en":"In summary: When the sender is trying to determine a rate for sending packets, it has to consider: The destination, the path to that destination, the connections sharing links along that path, and the connections sharing links with those connections (indirect competition), and so on. Congestion control is a hard problem because all the connections in the network are dependent on each other to determine their optimal sending rate.","zh_html":"总结：当发送方试图确定发送分组的速率时，它必须考虑：目的地、到达目的地的路径、共享该路径上链路的连接、以及共享这些连接链路的连接(间接竞争)，等等。拥塞控制是一个困难的问题，因为网络中的所有连接都相互依赖，以确定它们各自的最佳发送速率。"},{"en":"More fundamentally, congestion control is a resource allocation problem. Bandwidth is a limited resource, each connection wants a certain amount of that resource, and we need to decide how much bandwidth to allocate to each connection.","zh_html":"更根本地说，拥塞控制是一个资源分配(resource allocation)问题。带宽是一种有限的资源，每条连接都想要一定数量的该资源，我们需要决定每条连接分配多少带宽。"},{"en":"Resource allocation is a classic problem in computer science. (Examples include CPU scheduling and memory allocation algorithms.) However, unlike some resource allocation problems, a change in one connection’s allocation can have a global impact across all other connections. Also, allocations have to change every time a connection is created or destroyed. As a result, congestion control is more complex than the traditional resource allocation problem, and in fact, we don’t even have a formal model to define the problem.","zh_html":"资源分配是计算机科学中的一个经典问题。(例子包括 CPU 调度和内存分配算法。)然而，与某些资源分配问题不同，一条连接分配的更改可能会对所有其他连接产生全局影响。此外，每次创建或销毁连接时，分配都必须改变。因此，拥塞控制比传统的资源分配问题更复杂，实际上，我们甚至没有一个正式的模型来定义这个问题。"},{"en":"Unlike a traditional resource allocation problem, where the algorithm knows about the resource (e.g. CPU time) and the jobs (e.g. processes) ahead of time, there is no global mastermind that can see the entire network to allocate resources. Our solution has to be decentralized, where every sender decides its own allocation (even though everyone’s decisions are highly inter-dependent).","zh_html":"与传统的资源分配问题(算法事先知道资源(例如 CPU 时间)和任务(例如进程))不同，没有一个全局的\"大脑\"可以看到整个网络来分配资源。我们的解决方案必须是去中心化的(decentralized)，每个发送方决定自己的分配(即使每个人的决策高度相互依赖)。"},{"en":"Goals for a Good Congestion Control Algorithm","zh_html":"<a href=\"#goals-for-a-good-congestion-control-algorithm\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"goals-for-a-good-congestion-control-algorithm\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 好的拥塞控制算法的目标"},{"en":"From a resource allocation perspective, there are three goals we want out of a good congestion control algorithm.","zh_html":"从资源分配(resource allocation)的角度来看，一个好的拥塞控制算法有三个目标。"},{"en":"We’d like the resource allocation to be efficient. Links should not be overloaded, and there should be minimal packet delay and loss. Also, links should be utilized as much as possible.","zh_html":"我们希望资源分配是高效的(efficient)。链路不应过载，并且应最小化分组延迟和丢失。同时，链路应尽可能充分利用。"},{"en":"We’d also like the resource allocation to be fair between connections. We’ll formalize the definition of fair later, but roughly speaking, every connection should share an equal portion of the available capacity.","zh_html":"我们还希望资源分配在连接之间是公平的(fair)。我们稍后会形式化公平性的定义，但粗略地说，每条连接应该平等地分享可用容量。"},{"en":"We want a solution that achieves a good trade-off between these goals. It would be possible to optimize one goal at the expense of the others, but that leads to bad solutions. For example, we could ensure maximal link utilization by having everyone send packets extremely quickly (bad solution, causes congestion). Or, we could ensure minimal packet loss by making everybody send packets extremely slowly (bad solution, not utilizing capacity).","zh_html":"我们希望得到一个在这些目标之间实现良好权衡的解决方案。可以优化一个目标而牺牲其他目标，但这会导致糟糕的解决方案。例如，我们可以让每个人都以极快的速度发送分组来确保链路利用率最大化(糟糕的解决方案，导致拥塞)。或者，我们可以让每个人都以极慢的速度发送分组来确保最小化分组丢失(糟糕的解决方案，没有充分利用容量)。"},{"en":"From a more practical systems perspective, the solution we come up with needs to be scalable and decentralized. Our solution should also be able to adapt to changes in the network (e.g. changing topology, connections being created and destroyed).","zh_html":"从更实际的系统角度来看，我们提出的解决方案需要是可扩展的(scalable)和去中心化的(decentralized)。我们的解决方案还应该能够适应网络的变化(例如拓扑变化、连接创建和销毁)。"},{"en":"Design Space of Solutions","zh_html":"<a href=\"#design-space-of-solutions\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"design-space-of-solutions\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 解空间的设计"},{"en":"As we saw earlier, Karels and Jacobson fixed TCP congestion control by patching the TCP implementation in the operating system. But, if we could go back and re-design the Internet from scratch, what other possible designs for congestion control exist?","zh_html":"如我们之前所见，Karels 和 Jacobson 通过修补操作系统中的 TCP 实现来修复 TCP 拥塞控制。但是，如果我们可以从头重新设计互联网，还有哪些可能的拥塞控制设计呢？"},{"en":"One possible alternate design is based on reservations. The sender could request bandwidth ahead of time, and then free up that bandwidth after the connection is over. As discussed earlier, maintaining a reservation across the entire network comes with many technical difficulties. This approach is also problematic because it assumes that the sender knows what bandwidth it needs ahead of time, which isn’t necessarily true.","zh_html":"一种可能的替代设计基于预留(reservations)。发送方可以提前请求带宽，然后在连接结束后释放该带宽。如之前讨论的，在整个网络中维护预留(reservation)伴随着许多技术困难。这种方法还有问题，因为它假设发送方事先知道需要多少带宽，而这并不一定成立。"},{"en":"Another alternate design is based on pricing. As an analogy, consider express toll lanes on the highway (dedicated lanes only available to drivers who pay). The price to use the express toll lane depends on how congested the highway is. When the highway has very few cars, using the toll lane is very cheap, and when there is heavy traffic, using the toll lane is more expensive. Another form of congestion pricing occurs in airplane tickets, which cost more during busier times (e.g. holidays).","zh_html":"另一种替代设计基于定价(pricing)。类比一下，考虑高速公路上的快速收费车道(只有付费司机才能使用的专用车道)。使用快速收费车道的价格取决于高速公路的拥挤程度。当高速公路上车辆很少时，使用收费车道非常便宜；当交通繁忙时，使用收费车道就更贵。拥塞定价的另一种形式出现在机票中，在繁忙时段(例如节假日)价格更高。"},{"en":"To apply congestion pricing to the Internet, your ISP could add a button in your web browser that enables higher Internet speeds for an extra fee, and the fee could change depending on how congested the Internet is. Then, routers can prioritize sending packets from users who are paying more, and drop packets from users who are not paying. Research exists on congestion pricing on the Internet, and economists sometimes claim that if bandwidth is a scarce commodity, then a market structure will lead to an optimal solution. Congestion pricing has not been widely deployed, because it requires some form of business model connecting payments to congestion.","zh_html":"要将拥塞定价应用于互联网，你的 ISP 可以在你的网络浏览器中添加一个按钮，以额外费用启用更高的网速，费用可以根据互联网的拥塞程度而变化。然后，路由器可以优先发送来自付费用户的报文，并丢弃来自非付费用户的报文。关于互联网拥塞定价的研究已经存在，经济学家有时声称，如果带宽是一种稀缺商品，那么市场结构将导致最优解。拥塞定价尚未广泛部署，因为它需要某种连接支付与拥塞的商业模式。"},{"en":"All modern congestion control algorithms (including the ones we’ll study) are based on dynamic adjustment. Hosts dynamically learn the current level of congestion, and adjust their sending rate accordingly. In practice, dynamic adjustment is a practical solution because it can be easily generalized. This approach doesn’t assume any business model (needed for pricing), and doesn’t assume anything about users knowing the bandwidth they need ahead of time (needed for reservations).","zh_html":"所有现代的拥塞控制算法(包括我们将要学习的)都基于动态调整(dynamic adjustment)。主机动态学习当前拥塞程度，并相应调整其发送速率。实践中，动态调整是一个实用的解决方案，因为它可以轻松推广。这种方法不假设任何商业模式(定价所需)，也不假设用户提前知道所需的带宽(预留所需)。"},{"en":"Dynamic adjustment does require good citizenship. TCP needs everybody on the network to work together to share the resources fairly. For example, when a new connection starts using links, other connections need to slow down and share the bandwidth.","zh_html":"动态调整确实需要良好的公民意识(good citizenship)。TCP 需要网络上的每个人协同工作，公平地共享资源。例如，当一条新连接开始使用链路时，其他连接需要减慢速度并共享带宽。"},{"en":"Within the dynamic adjustment approach, there are two broad classes of solutions. In host-based congestion control algorithms, the sender is monitoring the performance and adjusting its rate accordingly. These algorithms are implemented entirely at the sender, and there is no special support from routers. The modification to TCP is a host-based algorithm, and is widely deployed today.","zh_html":"在动态调整方法中，有两类广泛的解决方案。在基于主机的(host-based)拥塞控制算法中，发送方监控性能并相应调整其速率。这些算法完全在发送方实现，不需要路由器的特殊支持。对 TCP 的修改就是一种基于主机的算法，如今已被广泛部署。"},{"en":"In router-assisted congested control algorithms, routers will explicitly send information about congestion back to the sender, to help the sender adjust its rate. Congestion happens at routers, so routers are in a good position to offer information about congestion. Router-assisted algorithms have been deployed in recent years, especially in datacenters.","zh_html":"在路由器辅助的(router-assisted)拥塞控制算法中，路由器会显式地向发送方发送关于拥塞的信息，以帮助发送方调整其速率。拥塞发生在路由器处，因此路由器处于提供拥塞信息的有利位置。路由器辅助算法近年来已被部署，特别是在数据中心(datacenters)中。"},{"en":"Some router-assisted algorithms send very little information, e.g. a single bit indicating congestion, while other algorithms send more detailed information, e.g. the exact rate the sender should use.","zh_html":"一些路由器辅助算法发送极少的信息，例如一个指示拥塞的比特位，而其他算法发送更详细的信息，例如发送方应该使用的确切速率。"},{"en":"Note that in both cases, routers are signaling congestion back to the sender. In router-assisted algorithms, the router is explicitly sending a message about its level of congestion. By contrast, in host-based algorithms, the sender does not receive explicit feedback from the routers. Instead, the sender uses implicit clues from the router (e.g. packets getting dropped or delayed) to deduce that the router is congested.","zh_html":"注意，在这两种情况下，都是路由器向发送方发出拥塞信号。在路由器辅助算法中，路由器显式地发送关于其拥塞程度的消息。相比之下，在基于主机的算法中，发送方不会收到来自路由器的显式反馈。相反，发送方使用来自路由器的隐式线索(例如分组被丢弃或延迟)来推断路由器发生拥塞。"},{"en":"In this taxonomy of congestion control approaches, we’ll focus on the dynamic adjustment approach, and within the space of dynamic adjustment solutions, we’ll focus on host-based solutions.","zh_html":"在这种拥塞控制方法的分类中，我们将重点关注动态调整方法，而在动态调整解的范围内，我们将重点关注基于主机的解决方案。"}]},"/transport/index.html":{"title":"index","blocks":[{"en":"Transport Layer Principles","en_html":"<a href=\"reliability.html\">Transport Layer Principles</a>","zh_html":"传输层原理(Transport Layer Principles)"},{"en":"TCP Design","en_html":"<a href=\"tcp-design.html\">TCP Design</a>","zh_html":"TCP设计(TCP Design)"},{"en":"TCP Implementation","en_html":"<a href=\"tcp-implementation.html\">TCP Implementation</a>","zh_html":"TCP实现(TCP Implementation)"},{"en":"Congestion Control Principles","en_html":"<a href=\"cc-principles.html\">Congestion Control Principles</a>","zh_html":"拥塞控制原理(Congestion Control Principles)"},{"en":"Congestion Control Design","en_html":"<a href=\"cc-design.html\">Congestion Control Design</a>","zh_html":"拥塞控制设计(Congestion Control Design)"},{"en":"Congestion Control Implementation","en_html":"<a href=\"cc-implementation.html\">Congestion Control Implementation</a>","zh_html":"拥塞控制实现(Congestion Control Implementation)"},{"en":"TCP Throughput Model","en_html":"<a href=\"throughput-model.html\">TCP Throughput Model</a>","zh_html":"TCP吞吐量模型(TCP Throughput Model)"},{"en":"Congestion Control Issues","en_html":"<a href=\"cc-issues.html\">Congestion Control Issues</a>","zh_html":"拥塞控制问题(Congestion Control Issues)"},{"en":"Router-Assisted Congestion Control","en_html":"<a href=\"router-based-cc.html\">Router-Assisted Congestion Control</a>","zh_html":"路由器辅助拥塞控制(Router-Assisted Congestion Control)"}]},"/transport/reliability.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Transport Layer Principles","zh_html":"传输层原则"},{"en":"Reliability Abstraction and Goals","zh_html":"可靠性(reliability)抽象与目标"},{"en":"Many applications require reliability. For example, when sending a file over the Internet, we want the recipient to receive the same bytes in the same order as what the sender sent.","zh_html":"许多应用都需要可靠性(reliability)。例如，在通过互联网(Internet)发送文件时，我们希望接收方收到的字节与发送方发送的字节完全相同，并且顺序也完全一致。"},{"en":"However, Layer 3 only provided unreliable, best-effort packet delivery. Packets can be lost (dropped), corrupted, and reordered (order of packets sent doesn’t match order of packets received). Packets can be delayed (e.g. a packet could stuck in a queue waiting to cross a link).","zh_html":"然而，第 3 层(Layer 3)只提供了不可靠的、尽力而为(best-effort)的分组交付(packet delivery)。分组(packet)可能丢失(被丢弃)、损坏，也可能乱序(reordered)，也就是发送分组的顺序与接收分组的顺序不一致。分组还可能被延迟，例如某个分组可能卡在队列中等待通过一条链路(link)。"},{"en":"In rare cases, packets can even be duplicated, where the sender sends one packet but the recipient receives multiple copies of that packet. This usually happens if a router along the path encounters an error of some sort. In practice, this error is very rare.","zh_html":"在少数情况下，分组甚至可能被重复(duplicated)：发送方只发送了一个分组，但接收方收到了该分组的多个副本。这通常发生在路径上的某个路由器(router)遇到某种错误时。实践中，这种错误非常少见。"},{"en":"Fun fact: Vern Paxson, UC Berkeley faculty, was one of the first people to discover and report packets being duplicated at the link layer.","zh_html":"趣闻：UC Berkeley 教授 Vern Paxson 是最早发现并报告链路层(link layer)存在分组重复现象的人之一。"},{"en":"We will use Layer 4 (the transport layer) to bridge this gap by developing protocols that rely on the best-effort packet abstraction supported by the network, and provide a reliable abstraction that application developers can use.","zh_html":"我们将使用第 4 层(Layer 4)，也就是传输层(transport layer)，来弥合这一差距：开发依赖网络所支持的尽力而为分组抽象(best-effort packet abstraction)的协议，同时向应用开发者提供可用的可靠抽象(reliable abstraction)。"},{"en":"For practical reasons (discussed elsewhere), reliability is implemented at the end hosts, not at intermediate routers. Also, reliability is implemented in the operating system for convenience, so that applications don’t need to all re-implement their own reliability.","zh_html":"出于实际原因(其他地方会讨论)，可靠性(reliability)是在端主机(end hosts)上实现的，而不是在中间路由器(intermediate routers)上实现的。另外，为了方便，可靠性由操作系统实现，这样各个应用就不需要各自重新实现一套可靠性机制。"},{"en":"We will formalize reliability by defining at-least-once delivery. In this model, the destination must receive every packet, without corruption, at least once, but may receive multiple duplicate copies of a packet. The transport layer will use the best-effort delivery to provide at-least-once delivery. Then, using at-least-once delivery, our protocol can remove duplicates and provide exactly-once delivery to the applications.","zh_html":"我们将通过定义<strong>至少一次交付(at-least-once delivery)</strong>来形式化可靠性(reliability)。在这个模型中，目的地(destination)必须至少一次收到每个分组，且不能损坏，但可能收到某个分组的多个重复副本。传输层会利用尽力而为交付(best-effort delivery)来提供至少一次交付。然后，在至少一次交付的基础上，我们的协议可以去除重复副本，并向应用提供恰好一次交付(exactly-once delivery)。"},{"en":"Note that reliable delivery does not guarantee that packets will be sent. A computer not connected to the network cannot send data to the destination, no matter what reliability protocol we use. Reliability protocols are allowed to give up and fail to send a packet, but the failure must be reported to the application. The protocol cannot falsely claim to have successfully delivered a packet.","zh_html":"注意，可靠交付(reliable delivery)并不保证分组一定会被发送。无论使用什么可靠性协议，一台没有连接到网络的计算机都无法向目的地发送数据。可靠性协议可以放弃并未能发送某个分组，但必须把失败报告给应用。协议不能谎称某个分组已经成功交付。"},{"en":"Our protocol should also be efficient. More specifically, our protocol should deliver data as quickly as possible, and our protocol should minimize bandwidth use and avoid sending packets unnecessarily. For example, we could guarantee that packets arrive by re-sending every packet hundreds of times, but this would violate our requirement of using bandwidth efficiently.","zh_html":"我们的协议还应该高效。更具体地说，协议应尽可能快地交付数据，并且应尽量减少带宽(bandwidth)使用，避免不必要地发送分组。例如，我们可以通过把每个分组重传(re-send)数百次来保证分组到达，但这会违反高效使用带宽的要求。"},{"en":"Transport Layer Goals","zh_html":"传输层目标"},{"en":"At the transport layer, our goal is to provide applications with a convenient abstraction that makes developers’ lives easier. The transport layer allows application developers to think in terms of connections, instead of individual packets being sent across the network. Ideally, the developers shouldn’t need to think about the low-level network details like splitting long data into packets, re-sending dropped packets, timeouts, etc.","zh_html":"在传输层(transport layer)，我们的目标是为应用提供一个方便的抽象，让开发者更容易编写程序。传输层让应用开发者可以从连接(connections)的角度思考，而不是从单个分组在网络中发送的角度思考。理想情况下，开发者不应该需要考虑底层网络细节，例如把长数据拆成分组、重传被丢弃的分组、超时(timeouts)等。"},{"en":"Reliability is just one of several goals we might want to achieve at the transport layer.","zh_html":"可靠性(reliability)只是我们可能希望在传输层实现的若干目标之一。"},{"en":"The transport layer implements demultiplexing between different processes at the end host, by introducing port numbers that can be used to associate each flow (connection) with a different process on the end host.","zh_html":"传输层通过引入端口号(port numbers)，在端主机(end host)上的不同进程之间实现<strong>解复用(demultiplexing)</strong>；端口号可用于把每个流(flow)或连接(connection)关联到端主机上的不同进程。"},{"en":"The transport layer also implements flow control and congestion control, which will help limit the rate of packets being sent in order to avoid overloading the receiver and the network, respectively.","zh_html":"传输层还实现流量控制(flow control)和拥塞控制(congestion control)，它们分别用于限制分组发送速率，以避免接收方和网络过载。"},{"en":"Demultiplexing with Ports","zh_html":"使用端口进行解复用(demultiplexing)"},{"en":"Suppose that my personal computer has two applications that are both talking to the same server. When packets arrive at my personal computer, they have the same source IP address (server), and the same destination IP address (my computer). How can I tell which packets are meant for which application?","zh_html":"假设我的个人电脑上有两个应用，它们都在和同一台服务器通信。当分组到达我的个人电脑时，它们具有相同的源 IP 地址(服务器)，也具有相同的目的 IP 地址(我的电脑)。我怎样判断哪些分组是发给哪个应用的？"},{"en":"In order to distinguish, or demultiplex, which packets are meant for which application, the transport layer header includes an additional port number, which can be used to identify a specific application on an end host.","zh_html":"为了区分，也就是<strong>解复用(demultiplex)</strong>，哪些分组属于哪个应用，传输层首部(transport layer header)会包含一个额外的<strong>端口号(port number)</strong>，可用于标识端主机上的某个具体应用。"},{"en":"When the transport layer receives a packet, it can use the port number to decide which higher-layer application the payload should be sent to. Because the transport layer is implemented in the operating system, these ports (sometimes called logical ports) are the attachment point where the application connects to the operating system’s network stack. The application knows its own port number, and the operating system knows the port numbers for all the applications, and the matching number is how data is unambiguously transferred between the application and operating system (without getting mixed up with data from other applications).","zh_html":"当传输层收到一个分组时，它可以使用端口号决定应该把载荷(payload)交给哪个更高层的应用。因为传输层是在操作系统中实现的，所以这些端口(有时称为<strong>逻辑端口(logical ports)</strong>)就是应用连接到操作系统网络栈(network stack)的附着点。应用知道自己的端口号，操作系统知道所有应用的端口号，而匹配的端口号就是应用与操作系统之间明确传递数据的方式，不会与其他应用的数据混淆。"},{"en":"Port numbers are 16 bits long. The modern Internet commonly uses the client-server design, where clients access services, and servers provide those services. Servers usually listen for requests on well-known ports (port numbers 0-1023). Clients know these ports and can access them to request services. For example, application-level protocols with well-known port numbers include HTTP (port 80) and SSH (port 22).","zh_html":"端口号长度为 16 位。现代互联网(Internet)通常使用客户端-服务器(client-server)设计：客户端访问服务，服务器提供这些服务。服务器通常在知名端口(well-known ports，端口号 0-1023)上监听请求。客户端知道这些端口，并可以访问它们来请求服务。例如，具有知名端口号的应用层协议包括 HTTP(端口 80)和 SSH(端口 22)。"},{"en":"By contrast, clients can select their own random port numbers (usually port numbers 1024-65535). These port numbers can be randomly-chosen, since the client is the one initiating the connection, and nobody is relying on the client having a fixed port number (the client isn’t providing services). Client port numbers are ephemeral (temporary), because the port number can be abandoned after the connection is over, and does not need to be permanent.","zh_html":"相比之下，客户端可以选择自己的随机端口号(通常是端口号 1024-65535)。这些端口号可以随机选择，因为客户端是发起连接的一方，而且没有人依赖客户端拥有固定端口号(客户端并不提供服务)。客户端端口号是<strong>临时的(ephemeral)</strong>，因为连接结束后可以放弃该端口号，不需要永久保留。"},{"en":"Bytestream Abstraction","zh_html":"字节流抽象(bytestream abstraction)"},{"en":"Implementing reliability at the transport layer means that the application developer no longer needs to think in terms of individual limited-size packets being sent across the network. Instead, the developer can think in terms of a reliable in-order bytestream. The sender has a stream of bytes with no length limit, and provides this stream to the transport layer. Then, the recipient receives the exact same stream of bytes, in the same order, with no bytes lost. You can think of a bytestream as a pipe, where the sender inserts bytes, one by one, into the pipe, and those same bytes appear, one by one, on the recipient’s end of the pipe. The sender and recipient don’t need to think about re-sending lost packets or packets arriving out of order, because the transport layer protocol will implement that for the developer.","zh_html":"在传输层实现可靠性(reliability)意味着应用开发者不再需要从单个有限大小分组在网络中传输的角度思考。相反，开发者可以从<strong>可靠有序字节流(reliable in-order bytestream)</strong>的角度思考。发送方有一条没有长度限制的字节流，并把这条流交给传输层。随后，接收方会按相同顺序收到完全相同的字节流，没有任何字节丢失。你可以把字节流想象成一根管道：发送方把字节一个接一个放入管道，同样的字节也会一个接一个出现在接收方的管道末端。发送方和接收方不需要考虑重传丢失分组或处理乱序到达的分组，因为传输层协议会为开发者实现这些功能。"},{"en":"UDP and Datagrams","zh_html":"UDP 与数据报(datagrams)"},{"en":"Sometimes, applications don’t need reliability. For example, consider a sensor that reads the water pressure in your home. The sensor sends a reading (small, fixed-size message with the time and water pressure) to the utility company every minute. This system might not need packets to arrive in order (e.g. if the readings already include timestamps), and might not need the ability to split long messages into packets (every reading is small). The system might not even need reliability, as long as most of the readings arrive at the utility company.","zh_html":"有时，应用并不需要可靠性(reliability)。例如，考虑一个读取你家自来水压力的传感器。传感器每分钟向公用事业公司发送一次读数(包含时间和水压的小型定长消息)。这个系统可能不需要分组按顺序到达，例如如果读数本身已经包含时间戳(timestamps)；也可能不需要把长消息拆成分组的能力，因为每次读数都很小。只要大多数读数能到达公用事业公司，这个系统甚至可能不需要可靠性。"},{"en":"Applications that don’t need reliability can use UDP (User Datagram Protocol) instead of TCP at the transport layer. UDP does not provide reliability guarantees. If the application needs a packet to arrive, the application must handle re-sending packets on its own (the transport layer will not re-send packets). Messages in UDP are limited to a single packet. If the application wants to send larger messages, the application is responsible for breaking up and reassembling those messages. Note that UDP still implements the notion of ports for demultiplexing, though.","zh_html":"不需要可靠性(reliability)的应用可以在传输层使用 <strong>UDP</strong>(User Datagram Protocol)，而不是 TCP。UDP 不提供可靠性保证。如果应用需要某个分组到达，应用必须自行处理分组重传(re-sending)，传输层不会重传分组。UDP 中的消息被限制为单个分组。如果应用想发送更大的消息，应用负责拆分并重新组装这些消息。不过，注意 UDP 仍然实现了用于解复用(demultiplexing)的端口概念。"},{"en":"At the transport layer, you can choose to use either UDP and TCP depending on your needs, but you can’t choose to use both. UDP and TCP are the standard transport layer protocols in the modern Internet.","zh_html":"在传输层，你可以根据需要选择使用 UDP 或 TCP，但不能同时使用二者。UDP 和 TCP 是现代互联网中的标准传输层协议。"},{"en":"Other Reliability Designs","zh_html":"其他可靠性设计"},{"en":"TCP was initially implemented by Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn, while they were students at UCLA. They have since been given the Turing Award, Presidential Medal of Freedom, etc. for their work. It’s pretty remarkable that the initial TCP design is pretty similar to what is used in practice today, and has stood the test of time. The core ideas of TCP are quite simple, and the design is quite elegant (though not perfect). However, the implementation can be tricky to get right, and the stakes are high, since almost the entire modern Internet runs on TCP.","zh_html":"TCP 最初由 Vint Cerf 和 Bob Kahn 在 UCLA 求学期间实现。此后，他们因这项工作获得了图灵奖(Turing Award)、总统自由勋章(Presidential Medal of Freedom)等荣誉。值得注意的是，最初的 TCP 设计与今天实践中使用的设计非常相似，并经受住了时间考验。TCP 的核心思想相当简单，设计也很优雅(虽然并不完美)。不过，要正确实现它可能很棘手，而且影响重大，因为几乎整个现代互联网都运行在 TCP 之上。"},{"en":"Since its initial creation, many individual pieces of TCP have evolved (e.g. better algorithms for estimating timers, smarter acknowledgements, smarter ISN selection, congestion control), but the core architectural decisions and abstractions (connection-oriented bytestreams, windows) have remained the same.","zh_html":"自 TCP 最初诞生以来，它的许多单独组成部分已经演化，例如更好的定时器估计算法、更智能的确认(acknowledgements)、更智能的 ISN 选择、拥塞控制(congestion control)等，但核心体系结构决策和抽象，例如面向连接的字节流(connection-oriented bytestreams)和窗口(windows)，一直保持不变。"},{"en":"TCP is the standard reliability protocol on the Internet, but other fundamentally different approaches exist.","zh_html":"TCP 是互联网(Internet)上的标准可靠性协议(reliability protocol)，但也存在其他根本不同的方法。"},{"en":"For example, the sender could exploit the idea of redundancy (as seen in error-correcting codes or RAID) to send data more reliably. Instead of sending the user data as-is, the sender encodes the data into more packets with redundancy intentionally built into each packet. For example, the user might have 10 packets, and an algorithm might encode that data into 20 packets. The algorithm might guarantee that as long as any 15 of the 20 packets are received, then the original 10 packets of data can be reconstructed.","zh_html":"例如，发送方可以利用冗余(redundancy)的思想(如纠错码(error-correcting codes)或 RAID 中所见)来更可靠地发送数据。发送方不是原样发送用户数据，而是把数据编码成更多分组，并在每个分组中有意加入冗余。例如，用户可能有 10 个分组，某个算法可能把这些数据编码成 20 个分组。该算法可以保证，只要这 20 个分组中的任意 15 个被收到，就能重构原始的 10 个数据分组。"},{"en":"More formally, an encoding algorithm might take k packets, encode them as n packets (where n is greater than k), such that the original k packets can be recovered as long as any k’ of the packets are received (where k’ is greater than k but less than n).","zh_html":"更形式化地说，一个编码算法可以取 k 个分组，把它们编码成 n 个分组(其中 n 大于 k)，使得只要收到任意 k’ 个分组(其中 k’ 大于 k 但小于 n)，就能恢复原始的 k 个分组。"},{"en":"Coding schemes are a deep topic with many algorithms (e.g. fountain codes, raptor codes), though we will not discuss them any further. They can be seen in practice in video streaming platforms.","zh_html":"编码方案(coding schemes)是一个很深的主题，包含许多算法，例如喷泉码(fountain codes)、Raptor 码(raptor codes)，不过我们不会继续讨论它们。实践中可以在视频流平台中看到这类方案。"}]},"/transport/router-based-cc.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Router-Assisted Congestion Control","zh_html":"<a href=\"#router-assisted-congestion-control\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"router-assisted-congestion-control\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 路由器辅助的拥塞控制"},{"en":"Congestion Control with Routers","zh_html":"<a href=\"#congestion-control-with-routers\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"congestion-control-with-routers\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 利用路由器进行拥塞控制"},{"en":"Previously, we saw some issues with host-based congestion control algorithms. Many of these issues could be fixed with some help from routers!","zh_html":"之前，我们看到了一些基于主机的拥塞控制(host-based congestion control)算法的问题。其中许多问题可以通过路由器的帮助来解决！"},{"en":"TCP confuses congestion and corruption. TCP fills up queues, has choppy rates, and performs poorly on short flows, all because hosts need to constantly adjust their rates to detect congestion. If routers could tell the sender about congestion, or even directly tell the sender the ideal rate, then many of these problems could be solved.","zh_html":"TCP 混淆了拥塞(congestion)和损坏(corruption)。TCP 填满队列(queue)，速率不平稳，并且在短流(short flows)上表现不佳，所有这些都因为主机需要不断调整其速率来检测拥塞。如果路由器能够告诉发送方关于拥塞的情况，甚至直接告诉发送方理想的速率，那么许多问题都可以解决。"},{"en":"Also, if routers enforce fair sharing, then it becomes much harder for hosts to cheat.","zh_html":"此外，如果路由器强制公平共享(fair sharing)，那么主机作弊就变得更加困难。"},{"en":"More philosophically, it’s a natural design choice to have routers participate in congestion routing. Congestion happens at the routers, so they often have more information about congestion than the hosts.","zh_html":"从更哲学的角度来看，让路由器参与拥塞控制是一个自然的设计选择。拥塞发生在路由器处，因此它们通常比主机拥有更多关于拥塞的信息。"},{"en":"Router-assisted congestion control can be very effective and result in near-optimal performance (high link utilization, low delays), but deploying these protocols can be challenging. Routers now need to support additional functionality, and sometimes that functionality can be quite complex. Some protocols might even require every router to agree to add that functionality.","zh_html":"路由器辅助的拥塞控制可能非常有效，并实现接近最优的性能(高链路利用率，低延迟)，但部署这些协议可能具有挑战性。路由器现在需要支持额外的功能，有时这些功能可能相当复杂。某些协议甚至可能要求每个路由器都同意添加该功能。"},{"en":"Enforcing Fair Queuing","zh_html":"<a href=\"#enforcing-fair-queuing\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"enforcing-fair-queuing\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 强制公平排队"},{"en":"How can a router ensure that every connection gets its fair share?","zh_html":"路由器如何确保每条连接获得其公平份额？"},{"en":"So far, a router is receiving packets, queuing them if needed, and sending them out, in first-in-first-out (FIFO) order. The router doesn’t care which connection a packet comes from.","zh_html":"到目前为止，路由器接收分组，如果需要就排队，然后按先入先出(first-in-first-out, FIFO)顺序发送它们。路由器不关心分组来自哪条连接。"},{"en":"In our new model, the router would need to classify packets into connections. (For now, assume the connections are all TCP connections.) This means the router has to look inside the packet to learn the source and destination IP addresses and ports.","zh_html":"在我们的新模型中，路由器需要将分组分类到连接中。(目前，假设这些连接都是 TCP 连接。)这意味着路由器需要查看分组内部，以了解源和目的 IP 地址以及端口。"},{"en":"To formally define fairness, the router could maintain a separate queue for each connection. When a packet arrives, the router adds the packet to the appropriate queue. Then, the router just needs to pick a queue each time, sending a packet from the front of that queue. As long as the router is picking queues in some fair way, then the router is enforcing fairness across connections.","zh_html":"为了形式化地定义公平性(fairness)，路由器可以为每条连接维护一个独立的队列。当一个分组到达时，路由器将该分组添加到相应的队列中。然后，路由器只需要每次选择一个队列，从该队列的前面发送一个分组。只要路由器以某种公平的方式选择队列，它就在连接之间强制实现了公平性。"},{"en":"If all packets are the same size, then the router could pick queues round-robin (send from the first queue, then the second queue, etc.). It turns out that this works, even if not all connections need the same bandwidth. Some connections might queue up packets more slowly than others. If we apply round-robin service to connections of different bandwidth, how do we compute the bandwidth allocated to each connection? For example, suppose we can send 10 packets per second, and A, B, and C sent 8, 6, and 2 packets per second, respectively.","zh_html":"如果所有分组大小相同，那么路由器可以轮询(round-robin)选择队列(从第一个队列发送，然后第二个队列，依此类推)。事实证明，即使不是所有连接都需要相同的带宽，这种方法也能工作。有些连接产生分组的速度可能比其他连接慢。如果我们对不同带宽的连接应用轮询服务，如何计算分配给每个连接的带宽？例如，假设我们每秒可以发送 10 个分组，A、B 和 C 分别每秒发送了 8、6 和 2 个分组。"},{"en":"If we sent out packets round-robin, how many packets per second of each type would be sent? We can model this as a resource allocation problem and solve it.","zh_html":"如果我们以轮询方式发送分组，每种类型每秒会发送多少个分组？我们可以将其建模为一个资源分配问题并求解。"},{"en":"For example, suppose we have a link capacity of 10. Connection A requests 8, B requests 6, and C requests 2. How should we distribute the capacity among the three connections? If we tried to be fair, everybody would receive 3.33. But C only asked for 2, so let’s give C the 2 it asked for, with no extra.","zh_html":"例如，假设链路容量为 10。连接 A 请求 8，B 请求 6，C 请求 2。我们应该如何在三条连接之间分配容量？如果我们试图公平，每个人都应该得到 3.33。但 C 只要求了 2，所以我们给 C 它要求的 2，不多给。"},{"en":"Now we have 8 left over, and A and B still need allocations. If we were fair, each would receive 4. This is less than what they asked for, but we have no way to satisfy their request, so we’ll give each their fair share of 4.","zh_html":"现在剩下 8，A 和 B 仍然需要分配。如果我们公平分配，每个应该得到 4。这少于它们要求的数量，但我们没有办法满足它们的请求，所以我们将给每个其公平份额 4。"},{"en":"Formally, to define max-min fairness, suppose C is the total bandwidth available to the router. Each connection \\(r_i\\) has a bandwidth demand, and we have to allocate a bandwidth \\(a_i\\) to each connection. The max-min bandwidth allocations are \\(a_i = \\min(f, r_i)\\), where \\(f\\) is the unique value (same value for all connections) such that \\(\\sum a_i = C\\). In this equation, the min term ensures that nobody gets more than they asked for, and the sum constraint ensures that no bandwidth is unused. Intuitively, \\(f\\) is the fair share that we equally allocate to everybody (hence one \\(f\\) value for all connections).","zh_html":"形式化地，为了定义最大-最小公平性(max-min fairness)，假设 C 是路由器可用的总带宽。每条连接 \\(r_i\\) 有一个带宽需求(bandwidth demand)，我们必须为每条连接分配一个带宽 \\(a_i\\)。最大-最小带宽分配是 \\(a_i = \\min(f, r_i)\\)，其中 \\(f\\) 是唯一的(对所有连接相同的)值，使得 \\(\\sum a_i = C\\)。在这个方程中，min 项确保没有人得到超过其请求的数量，而求和约束确保没有带宽被浪费。直观上，\\(f\\) 是我们平等分配给所有人的公平份额(因此对所有连接只有一个 \\(f\\) 值)。"},{"en":"Another way to read this equation is: There is some magic fair-share number that we can distribute equally to everybody. If you requested less than the fair share, you get the fair share (no extra). If you requested more than the fair share, you are capped at the fair share, but nobody else gets more than you.","zh_html":"另一种理解这个方程的方式是：存在某个神奇的公平份额数字，可以平等地分配给每个人。如果你请求的少于公平份额，你就得到你请求的数量(不多给)。如果你请求的多于公平份额，你被限制在公平份额，但其他人也不会比你多得。"},{"en":"In the previous example, \\(f\\) was 4. A and B received \\(f\\) (they wanted more), and C received 2 (it wanted less).","zh_html":"在前面的例子中，\\(f\\) 是 4。A 和 B 得到了 \\(f\\)(它们想要更多)，C 得到了 2(它想要的更少)。"},{"en":"If we apply max-min fairness, the equation guarantees that if you don’t get your full demand, nobody else gets more than you. The round-robin approach is max-min fair (assuming equal packet size).","zh_html":"如果我们应用最大-最小公平性，方程保证如果你没有得到全部需求，其他人也不会比你得到的更多。轮询(round-robin)方法是最大-最小公平的(假设分组大小相等)。"},{"en":"What if we don’t assume equal packet size? In real life, packet sizes can vary widely (e.g. 40 bytes vs. 1500 bytes). Ideally, we’d like to perform bit-by-bit round robin, where we take turns sending one bit from each connection’s queue. This isn’t practical (we don’t send one bit at a time), but if we applied this theoretically, we could write down the time when the last bit of a packet is sent out, for every packet. We’ll call this the deadline for that packet. Then, a fair approximation would be sending out packets in order of deadline (when their last bit would have been sent ideally).","zh_html":"如果我们不假设分组大小相等呢？现实中，分组大小差异很大(例如 40 字节 vs 1500 字节)。理想情况下，我们希望执行逐比特轮询(bit-by-bit round robin)，即每次从每个连接的队列中轮流发送一个比特。这并不实际(我们不会一次发送一个比特)，但如果我们在理论上应用它，我们可以为每个分组记录其最后一个比特被发送的时间。我们称之为该分组的截止时间(deadline)。那么，一个公平的近似就是按截止时间顺序发送分组(即它们最后一个比特理论上应该被发送的顺序)。"},{"en":"Fun fact: The paper about simulating fair queuing is extremely influential, and two of the co-authors are Scott Shenker (UC Berkeley faculty) and Srinivasan Keshav (EECS PhD student at the time).","zh_html":"趣闻：关于模拟公平排队(fair queuing)的论文极具影响力，两位合著者是 Scott Shenker(UC Berkeley 教授)和 Srinivasan Keshav(当时是 EECS 博士生)。"},{"en":"Here’s an example of exact bit-by-bit fair queuing on two connections (when a tie occurs, we pick the packet that arrives first).","zh_html":"这里是一个在两条连接上精确逐比特公平排队的示例(当出现平局时，我们选择先到达的分组)。"},{"en":"Fair Queuing in Practice","zh_html":"<a href=\"#fair-queuing-in-practice\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"fair-queuing-in-practice\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 实践中的公平排队"},{"en":"What’s good about fair queuing? It ensures isolation between connections, and prevents cheating connections from getting more bandwidth. Connections wouldn’t need to implement TCP (or a TCP-friendly alternative), and can pick their own (possibly unfriendly) congestion control algorithm.","zh_html":"公平排队有什么好处？它确保连接之间的隔离(isolation)，并防止作弊连接获得更多带宽。连接不需要实现 TCP(或 TCP-friendly 替代方案)，可以选择自己的(可能不友好的)拥塞控制算法。"},{"en":"Fundamentally, the benefit of fair queuing is its resilience to external factors like cheating and RTT variations. No matter what, everyone gets a fair share of a given link. But, we still need end hosts to discover and adapt to their fair share (e.g. slow down if they’re requesting too much).","zh_html":"从根本上说，公平排队的优势在于其对外部因素(如作弊和 RTT 变化)的弹性。无论如何，每个人都能获得给定链路的公平份额。但是，我们仍然需要端主机(end hosts)发现并适应其公平份额(例如，如果请求太多就减慢速度)。"},{"en":"What’s bad about fair queuing? It’s much more complicated than FIFO queuing. The process of computing deadlines is tricky and we have not shown an algorithm for doing so here. Also, routers would need to maintain multiple queues, and do extra parsing work on every packet.","zh_html":"公平排队有什么不好？它比 FIFO 排队复杂得多。计算截止时间的过程很棘手，我们这里没有给出具体的算法。此外，路由器需要维护多个队列，并对每个分组执行额外的解析工作。"},{"en":"In practice, we can’t implement perfect fair queuing in routers (too complicated to run at high speeds), but approximations do exist (e.g. Deficit Round Robin). Modern routers typically implement approximations, though with fewer queues. Fewer queues means that instead of one queue per connection, isolation is more coarse-grained (e.g. one queue per customer).","zh_html":"实践中，我们无法在路由器中实现完美的公平排队(太复杂，无法在高速下运行)，但存在近似实现(例如赤字轮询(Deficit Round Robin))。现代路由器通常实现近似方案，不过使用更少的队列。更少的队列意味着，不是每条连接一个队列，隔离更加粗粒度(例如每个客户一个队列)。"},{"en":"Fair queuing cannot eliminate congestion. It is only an alternative way to manage congestion. For example, consider this bottleneck link: It might allocate 0.5 Gbps to each connection, which defeats cheating. But, if the top connection runs at 0.5 Gbps, then 0.4 Gbps will get dropped at the immediate next link. A better allocation would be to send 0.1 Gbps along the top connection, and 0.9 Gbps along the bottom connection.","zh_html":"公平排队无法消除拥塞。它只是管理拥塞的一种替代方式。例如，考虑这条瓶颈链路：它可能给每条连接分配 0.5 Gbps，这防止了作弊。但是，如果上面的连接以 0.5 Gbps 运行，那么 0.4 Gbps 会在下一个紧邻的链路上被丢弃。更好的分配是沿着上面的连接发送 0.1 Gbps，沿着下面的连接发送 0.9 Gbps。"},{"en":"Fundamentally, the problem is that this bottleneck link doesn’t know what will happen at future (downstream) links. The only way to fix this is to make the sender host slow down (router queuing cannot help).","zh_html":"从根本上说，问题是这条瓶颈链路不知道未来(下游)链路会发生什么。解决这个问题的唯一方法是让发送主机减慢速度(路由器排队无法帮助)。"},{"en":"Fair queuing gives us per-connection fairness, but philosophically, we still have to ask if this is the right model of fairness. As we saw earlier, per-connection fairness means that someone with more connections still gets more bandwidth. Should we instead enforce fairness per source-destination pair, or perhaps per source? Should we penalize connections that use more congested links (hogging more scarce resources)?","zh_html":"公平排队提供了每条连接的公平性，但从哲学上讲，我们仍然需要问这是否是正确的公平模型。如我们之前所见，每条连接的公平性意味着拥有更多连接的人仍然会获得更多带宽。我们是应该按源-目的对(source-destination pair)来强制执行公平性，还是按源来执行？我们是否应该惩罚使用更拥堵链路(占用更多稀缺资源)的连接？"},{"en":"Router-Assisted Congestion Control","zh_html":"<a href=\"#router-assisted-congestion-control-1\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"router-assisted-congestion-control-1\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 路由器辅助的拥塞控制"},{"en":"Fair queuing enforces fairness over a specific link, but it doesn’t tell the sender anything. What if the routers passed information back to the sender to help the sender adjust its rate?","zh_html":"公平排队在特定链路上强制公平性，但它不告诉发送方任何信息。如果路由器将信息传回给发送方以帮助其调整速率呢？"},{"en":"One solution is to have routers directly tell senders the rate they should use. We could add a rate field in packets, and have routers fill in that field with the connection’s fair share. When the packet arrives at the sender, the sender can read the header and set the rate to what the routers said. Now, the sender doesn’t need to dynamically adjust to discover a good rate.","zh_html":"一种解决方案是让路由器直接告诉发送方应该使用的速率。我们可以在分组中添加一个速率字段，让路由器用该连接的公平份额来填充这个字段。当分组到达发送方时，发送方可以读取首部并将速率设置为路由器所说的值。这样，发送方不需要动态调整来发现一个良好的速率。"},{"en":"Another solution is to have routers notify senders about congestion (without specifying an exact rate). This is deployed in the form of an Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN) bit in the IP header. If a packet passes through a congested router, the router sets that bit to 1. When the recipient gets a packet with the ECN bit on, the ack reply will also have the ECN bit set, so the sender learns about congestion.","zh_html":"另一种解决方案是让路由器通知发送方关于拥塞的情况(而不指定确切速率)。这就是以 IP 首部中的显式拥塞通知(Explicit Congestion Notification, ECN)比特位形式部署的方案。如果一个分组经过一个拥塞的路由器，路由器将该比特设置为 1。当接收方收到设置了 ECN 比特的分组时，ack 回复也会设置 ECN 比特，因此发送方知道发生了拥塞。"},{"en":"There are many options for when routers set this bit. The router could be paranoid and set the bit frequently, which reduces delay but may lead to underused links. Or, the router could be more reckless and rarely set the bit, which increases delay but results in high link utilization.","zh_html":"路由器何时设置这个比特有很多选项。路由器可以很谨慎，频繁设置该比特，这减少了延迟但可能导致链路利用不足。或者，路由器可以更激进，很少设置该比特，这增加了延迟但实现了高链路利用率。"},{"en":"There are also many options for how the host reacts when this bit is set. For example, the host could pretend the packet was dropped and adjust accordingly.","zh_html":"主机在该比特被设置时如何反应也有很多选项。例如，主机可以假装分组被丢弃并相应调整。"},{"en":"What’s good about ECN? It solves the problem of confusing corruption and congestion. It allows routers to warn hosts about congestion earlier (e.g. before the queue is full), which can reduce delays. It’s also lightweight to implement.","zh_html":"ECN 有什么好处？它解决了混淆损坏和拥塞的问题。它允许路由器更早地警告主机拥塞(例如在队列满之前)，这可以减少延迟。它实现起来也很轻量。"},{"en":"In practice, effective ECN requires most or all routers to support this protocol and turn the bit on when necessary. In the modern Internet, the ECN bit is deployed on some, but not all routers. However, the ECN bit can be effective in a small network (e.g. inside a datacenter’s local network) where all routers agree to enable the bit.","zh_html":"实践中，有效的 ECN 要求多数或所有路由器支持该协议并在必要时开启该比特。在现代互联网中，ECN 比特部署在一些(但不是所有)路由器上。然而，ECN 比特在一个小网络(例如数据中心内部网络)中可以很有效，因为所有路由器都同意启用该比特。"}]},"/transport/tcp-design.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"TCP Design","zh_html":"TCP 设计"},{"en":"Reliably Delivering a Single Packet","zh_html":"可靠交付单个分组"},{"en":"The time it takes for a packet to travel from sender to receiver is the one-way delay. The time it takes for a packet to travel from sender to receiver, plus the time for a reply packet to travel from receiver to sender, is the round-trip time (RTT).","zh_html":"分组(packet)从发送方到接收方所需的时间称为<strong>单向延迟(one-way delay)</strong>。分组从发送方到接收方所需的时间，再加上回复分组从接收方返回发送方所需的时间，称为<strong>往返时间(round-trip time, RTT)</strong>。"},{"en":"Let’s build intuition by designing a simplified protocol for reliably sending a single packet.","zh_html":"让我们通过设计一个用于可靠发送单个分组的简化协议来建立直觉。"},{"en":"The sender tries to send a packet. How does the sender know if the packet was successfully received?","zh_html":"发送方尝试发送一个分组。发送方如何知道这个分组是否已被成功接收？"},{"en":"The receiver can send an acknowledgment (ack) message, confirming that the packet was received.","zh_html":"接收方可以发送一条<strong>确认(acknowledgment, ack)</strong>消息，确认该分组已经收到。"},{"en":"What happens if the packet gets dropped?","zh_html":"如果分组被丢弃，会发生什么？"},{"en":"We can re-send the packet if it’s dropped. How do we know when to re-send the packet?","zh_html":"如果分组被丢弃，我们可以重传(re-send)该分组。我们如何知道什么时候应该重传该分组？"},{"en":"The sender can maintain a timer. When the timer expires, we can re-send the packet.","zh_html":"发送方可以维护一个定时器(timer)。当定时器到期时，我们可以重传该分组。"},{"en":"When the sender receives an ack, the sender can cancel the timer and does not need to re-send the packet.","zh_html":"当发送方收到 ack 时，发送方可以取消定时器，并且不需要重传该分组。"},{"en":"What happens if the ack is dropped?","zh_html":"如果 ack 被丢弃，会发生什么？"},{"en":"The protocol still works without modification. The sender will time out (no ack received) and re-send the packet until the ack is successfully sent. In this case, the destination received two copies of the same packet, but that’s okay. The destination can notice the duplicate and discard it.","zh_html":"协议无需修改仍然可以工作。发送方会超时(timeout，因为没有收到 ack)，并不断重传该分组，直到 ack 成功发送。在这种情况下，目的地(destination)收到了同一个分组的两个副本，但这没有关系。目的地可以发现重复副本并丢弃它。"},{"en":"How should the timer be set? If the timer is too long, the packet might take longer than needed to be sent. If the timer is too short, the packet might be re-sent when it didn’t need to be. Getting the timer wrong can affect our efficiency goals.","zh_html":"定时器应该如何设置？如果定时器太长，分组发送完成所需的时间可能比必要时间更久。如果定时器太短，分组可能会在不需要重传时被重传。定时器设置错误会影响我们的效率目标。"},{"en":"A good timer length would be the round-trip time. This is when the sender expects to receive the ack, so if an ack hasn’t arrived by then, the sender should re-send the packet at that time.","zh_html":"一个合适的定时器长度应当是往返时间(RTT)。这是发送方预期收到 ack 的时间，因此如果到那时 ack 还没有到达，发送方就应该重传该分组。"},{"en":"In practice, estimating RTT can be difficult. RTTs can vary depending on what path the packet takes through the network, and even along a specific path, the RTT can be affected by the load and congestion along that path.","zh_html":"实践中，估计 RTT 可能很困难。RTT 会因分组在网络中经过的路径不同而变化；即使沿着某条特定路径，RTT 也会受到该路径上的负载(load)和拥塞(congestion)影响。"},{"en":"One way to estimate RTT is to measure the time between sending a packet and receiving an ack for that packet. We can get an estimated RTT measurement from every packet sent, and apply some algorithm (e.g. exponential moving average) to combine these measurements into one RTT estimate. Our algorithm would also have to account for messages being re-sent (variance in the measurements).","zh_html":"估计 RTT 的一种方法是测量发送某个分组与收到该分组 ack 之间的时间。我们可以从每个发送的分组得到一个 RTT 测量估计，并应用某种算法(例如指数移动平均(exponential moving average))把这些测量合并成一个 RTT 估计。我们的算法还必须考虑消息被重传的情况，也就是测量中的方差(variance)。"},{"en":"In practice, operators usually err on the side of setting the timer to be longer. If the timer is too short, and timeouts are constantly happening, your connection is probably behaving poorly (constantly re-sending packets).","zh_html":"实践中，运营者通常会倾向于把定时器设置得更长一些。如果定时器太短，超时不断发生，那么你的连接很可能表现很差(不断重传分组)。"},{"en":"What if the bits are corrupted?","zh_html":"如果比特(bits)损坏，会发生什么？"},{"en":"We can add a checksum in the transport layer header (different from the IP layer checksum). When the receiver sees a corrupt packet, it can do two things: Either the receiver can explicitly re-send a negative acknowledgement (nack), telling the sender to re-send the packet.","zh_html":"我们可以在传输层首部(transport layer header)中加入校验和(checksum)，它不同于 IP 层校验和。当接收方看到损坏的分组时，可以做两件事之一：接收方可以显式重新发送一个<strong>否定确认(negative acknowledgement, nack)</strong>，告诉发送方重传该分组。"},{"en":"Or, the receiver can drop the corrupt packet and do nothing (don’t send an ack or nack). Then, the sender will time out and re-send the packet.","zh_html":"或者，接收方可以丢弃损坏的分组且不做任何事(不发送 ack 或 nack)。随后，发送方会超时并重传该分组。"},{"en":"Both approaches (nack or wait for timeout) work, though TCP uses the latter (wait for timeout) and does not implement nacks.","zh_html":"两种方法(nack 或等待超时)都可以工作，不过 TCP 使用后者(等待超时)，并不实现 nack。"},{"en":"What if the packets are delayed?","zh_html":"如果分组被延迟，会发生什么？"},{"en":"No modifications are needed. If the delay is very long, the sender might time out before the ack arrives. The sender will re-send the packet (so the recipient might get two duplicates), and the sender might get two acks, but that’s okay.","zh_html":"不需要修改。如果延迟很长，发送方可能会在 ack 到达前超时。发送方会重传该分组(因此接收方可能会收到两个重复副本)，发送方也可能收到两个 ack，但这没有关系。"},{"en":"What if the sender sends one packet, but it’s duplicated in the network, and the recipient receives two copies?","zh_html":"如果发送方发送了一个分组，但它在网络中被重复，接收方收到了两个副本，会发生什么？"},{"en":"No modifications are needed. The recipient would send two acks, but both the sender and the recipient can safely handle duplicates.","zh_html":"不需要修改。接收方会发送两个 ack，但发送方和接收方都可以安全地处理重复副本。"},{"en":"Note: From this simplified protocol, we can see that sometimes the recipient receives two copies of the packet. If a specific link was implementing a reliability protocol, the recipient side of the link might receive two copies. Normally, the duplicate would get dropped and only one packet would be forwarded to the destination. But, if the router crashes and restarts in between the two copies arriving, the router might forward both copies to the destination.","zh_html":"注意：从这个简化协议中可以看到，有时接收方会收到分组的两个副本。如果某条特定链路正在实现可靠性协议(reliability protocol)，链路接收端可能会收到两个副本。通常，重复副本会被丢弃，只有一个分组会被转发到目的地。但是，如果路由器在两个副本到达之间崩溃并重启，它可能会把两个副本都转发到目的地。"},{"en":"In summary, the single-packet reliability protocol is:","zh_html":"总结来说，单分组可靠性协议是："},{"en":"If you are the sender: Send the packet, and set a timer. If no ack arrives before the timer goes off, re-send the packet and reset the timer. Stop and cancel the timer when the ack arrives.","zh_html":"如果你是发送方：发送分组，并设置定时器。如果定时器响起前没有 ack 到达，就重传该分组并重置定时器。当 ack 到达时停止并取消定时器。"},{"en":"If you are the recipient: If you receive the uncorrupted packet, send an ack. (You might send multiple acks if you receive the packet multiple times.)","zh_html":"如果你是接收方：如果收到未损坏的分组，就发送 ack。(如果多次收到该分组，你可能会发送多个 ack。)"},{"en":"The core ideas in this example will apply to later protocols as well: checksums (for corruption), acknowledgements, re-sending packets, and timeouts.","zh_html":"这个例子中的核心思想也会应用到后续协议中：校验和(checksums，用于检测损坏)、确认(acknowledgements)、重传分组(re-sending packets)和超时(timeouts)。"},{"en":"Note that this protocol guarantees at-least-once delivery, since duplicates may exist.","zh_html":"注意，该协议保证的是至少一次交付(at-least-once delivery)，因为可能存在重复副本。"},{"en":"Reliably Delivering Multiple Packets","zh_html":"可靠交付多个分组"},{"en":"How would this protocol be extended to multiple packets?","zh_html":"如何把这个协议扩展到多个分组？"},{"en":"We could follow the same transmission rules (re-send when timer expires) for every single packet. To distinguish packets, we can attach a unique sequence number to every packet. Each ack will be related to a specific packet. Sequence numbers can also help us reorder packets if they arrive out of order.","zh_html":"我们可以对每一个分组遵循相同的传输规则(定时器到期时重传)。为了区分分组，我们可以给每个分组附加一个唯一的<strong>序列号(sequence number)</strong>。每个 ack 都会关联到某个特定分组。如果分组乱序到达，序列号也能帮助我们重新排序。"},{"en":"When does the sender send each packet? The simplest approach is the stop and wait protocol, where the sender waits for packet i to be acknowledged before sending packet i+1. This will correctly provide reliability, but it is very slow. Each packet takes at least one RTT to be sent (more if a packet is dropped or corrupted).","zh_html":"发送方什么时候发送每个分组？最简单的方法是<strong>停等(stop and wait)</strong>协议：发送方等待分组 i 被确认后，再发送分组 i+1。这能正确提供可靠性(reliability)，但速度很慢。每个分组至少需要一个 RTT 才能发送完成(如果分组被丢弃或损坏，则需要更久)。"},{"en":"This protocol might work in smaller settings where efficiency is less of a concern, but this is too slow for the Internet. How can we make this faster?","zh_html":"在效率不是很重要的小规模场景中，这个协议可能可行，但对于互联网来说太慢了。我们如何让它更快？"},{"en":"We can send packets in parallel. More specifically, we can send more packets while waiting for acks to arrive. When a packet is sent, but its corresponding ack has not been received, we call that packet in flight.","zh_html":"我们可以并行发送分组。更具体地说，我们可以在等待 ack 到达时继续发送更多分组。当一个分组已经发送，但对应的 ack 尚未收到时，我们称该分组<strong>在途(in flight)</strong>。"},{"en":"The simplest approach would be to send all packets immediately, but this could overwhelm the network (e.g. the link to your computer might have a limited bandwidth).","zh_html":"最简单的方法是立即发送所有分组，但这可能压垮网络，例如通向你电脑的链路可能带宽有限。"},{"en":"Window-Based Algorithms","zh_html":"基于窗口的算法"},{"en":"Sending packets one at a time is too slow, but sending all packets at once overwhelms the network. To account for this, we will set a limit W and say that only W packets can be in flight at any given time. This is the key idea behind window-based protocols, where W is the size of the window.","zh_html":"一次只发送一个分组太慢，但一次发送所有分组又会压垮网络。为了解决这个问题，我们会设置一个限制 W，并规定任意时刻最多只能有 W 个分组在途(in flight)。这就是<strong>基于窗口的协议(window-based protocols)</strong>的核心思想，其中 W 是窗口(window)的大小。"},{"en":"If W is the maximum number of in-flight packets, then the sender can start by sending W packets. When an ack arrives, we send the next packet in line.","zh_html":"如果 W 是在途分组的最大数量，那么发送方可以先发送 W 个分组。当一个 ack 到达时，我们发送队列中的下一个分组。"},{"en":"How should W be selected?","zh_html":"应该如何选择 W？"},{"en":"We want to fully use our available network capacity (“fill the pipe”). If W is too low, we are not using all of the bandwidth available to us.","zh_html":"我们希望充分利用可用的网络容量(“填满管道(fill the pipe)”)。如果 W 太低，我们就没有用尽可用带宽。"},{"en":"However, we don’t want to overload links, since other people may be using that link (congestion control). We also don’t want to overload the receiver, who needs to receive and process all the packets from the sender (flow control).","zh_html":"不过，我们不希望链路过载，因为其他人也可能在使用该链路(拥塞控制(congestion control))。我们也不希望接收方过载，因为接收方需要接收并处理来自发送方的所有分组(流量控制(flow control))。"},{"en":"Window Size: Filling the Pipe","zh_html":"窗口大小：填满管道"},{"en":"Let’s focus on just the first RTT, from the time the first packet is sent, to the time the first ack arrives. Suppose this time is 5 seconds (not a realistic number, just for example). Also, suppose that the outgoing link allows the sender to send 10 packets per second (also not a realistic number). In total, during this first RTT time, the sender should be able to send 50 packets in total. Therefore, 50 would be a reasonable window size, so that the sender is always sending packets and never sitting idle.","zh_html":"先只关注第一个 RTT：从第一个分组发送的时刻，到第一个 ack 到达的时刻。假设这段时间是 5 秒(这不是现实数字，只是举例)。还假设出站链路允许发送方每秒发送 10 个分组(同样不是现实数字)。那么在第一个 RTT 时间内，发送方总共应该能够发送 50 个分组。因此，50 会是一个合理的窗口大小，使发送方始终在发送分组，而不会空闲等待。"},{"en":"If we set W to be lower than 50, then the sender would finish sending all the initial packets before the first ack arrives. Then, the sender would be forced to sit idling while waiting for acks to arrive, and some network bandwidth would be wasted. More generally, we want the sender to be sending packets during the entire RTT.","zh_html":"如果把 W 设置得低于 50，那么发送方会在第一个 ack 到达前就发完所有初始分组。随后，发送方会被迫在等待 ack 到达时空闲，从而浪费一些网络带宽。更一般地说，我们希望发送方在整个 RTT 期间都在发送分组。"},{"en":"In this example, W is 4. But, after sending 4 packets, the sender is idling and wasting bandwidth while waiting for the first ack to arrive.","zh_html":"在这个例子中，W 是 4。但是，发送 4 个分组后，发送方就在等待第一个 ack 到达时空闲，从而浪费带宽。"},{"en":"In this example, W is increased so that the sender is constantly sending packets. As the first ack arrives, the sender is just about to reach the limit of W packets in flight, and is able to immediately continue sending packets as more acks arrive.","zh_html":"在这个例子中，W 被增大，使发送方持续发送分组。当第一个 ack 到达时，发送方正好快要达到 W 个在途分组的限制，并且能够在更多 ack 到达时立即继续发送分组。"},{"en":"The route to the destination might have multiple links, with different capacities. Let B be the minimum (bottleneck) link bandwidth along the path. We shouldn’t send packets faster than B, to avoid overloading the link. We also don’t want to send packets any slower than B (i.e. we want to be using the rate B at all times).","zh_html":"到目的地的路由(route)可能包含多条链路，且容量不同。令 B 为路径上的最小(瓶颈 bottleneck)链路带宽(link bandwidth)。我们不应该以快于 B 的速率发送分组，以避免链路过载。我们也不希望以慢于 B 的速率发送分组，也就是说，我们希望始终使用速率 B。"},{"en":"Also, suppose that R is the round-trip time between sender and recipient. We can multiply R times B to get the total number of packets that can be sent during the RTT. (We can send B packets per second, for R seconds.) This tells us the window size, in packets.","zh_html":"另外，假设 R 是发送方和接收方之间的往返时间(RTT)。我们可以用 R 乘以 B，得到 RTT 期间能够发送的分组总数。(我们可以每秒发送 B 个分组，持续 R 秒。)这会告诉我们以分组数表示的窗口大小。"},{"en":"In reality, B is measured in bits per second, not in packets per second. When we multiply R times B, we get the number of bits that can be sent during the RTT. (B bits per second, for R seconds.) This tells us the window size, in bytes. In total, we can write:","zh_html":"实际上，B 的单位是比特每秒(bits per second)，而不是分组每秒(packets per second)。当我们用 R 乘以 B 时，得到的是 RTT 期间能够发送的比特数。(每秒 B 比特，持续 R 秒。)这会告诉我们以字节(bytes)表示的窗口大小。总体上，我们可以写成："},{"en":"W times packet size = R times B","zh_html":"W 乘以分组大小 = R 乘以 B"},{"en":"The left-hand-side tells us the number of bytes sent during the window (W packets, times number of bytes per packet), and the right-hand-side tells us the number of bytes that can be sent during the RTT.","zh_html":"左边告诉我们窗口期间发送的字节数(W 个分组，乘以每个分组的字节数)，右边告诉我们 RTT 期间能够发送的字节数。"},{"en":"For a concrete example, we can set RTT = 1 second, and B = 8 Mbits/second. Then, R times B is 8 Mbits, or 1 megabyte, or 1,000,000 bytes.","zh_html":"举一个具体例子，可以设 RTT = 1 秒，B = 8 Mbits/second。那么 R 乘以 B 是 8 Mbits，也就是 1 megabyte，或 1,000,000 bytes。"},{"en":"If our packet size is 100 bytes, then we want W = 10,000 packets, so that we are fully using the bandwidth and sending 1,000,000 bytes during the RTT.","zh_html":"如果我们的分组大小是 100 bytes，那么我们希望 W = 10,000 个分组，这样就能充分利用带宽，并在 RTT 期间发送 1,000,000 bytes。"},{"en":"We can also draw the window size in terms of the link itself. In this picture, we are showing the outgoing and incoming directions of a specific link. As the sender pushes packets through the link at maximum capacity, the first ack will arrive immediately after the 6th packet is sent. Therefore, our window size should be 6.","zh_html":"我们也可以从链路本身的角度画出窗口大小。在这张图中，我们展示了某条特定链路的出站方向和入站方向。当发送方以最大容量把分组推入链路时，第一个 ack 会在第 6 个分组发送后立即到达。因此，我们的窗口大小应该是 6。"},{"en":"Note that the window size is not 3. When packet 6 is sent, 3 packets are being sent, but there are 3 more packets whose acks have not arrived, so there are a total of 6 packets in flight.","zh_html":"注意，窗口大小不是 3。当分组 6 被发送时，有 3 个分组正在被发送，但还有另外 3 个分组的 ack 尚未到达，所以总共有 6 个分组在途。"},{"en":"If we set the window size to 3, the outgoing pipe would have been unused while the acks for 1, 2, 3 are in flight.","zh_html":"如果把窗口大小设为 3，那么当 1、2、3 的 ack 仍在途时，出站管道(outgoing pipe)本来会处于未使用状态。"},{"en":"Note that the acks don’t fill up the entire incoming pipe because the packets don’t contain any actual data besides acknowledging receipt of a packet.","zh_html":"注意，ack 不会填满整个入站管道(incoming pipe)，因为除了确认收到某个分组以外，这些分组不包含任何实际数据。"},{"en":"Window Size: Flow Control","zh_html":"窗口大小：流量控制(flow control)"},{"en":"Consider the transport layer protocol in the recipient’s operating system. The recipient might receive packets out-of-order, but the bytestream abstraction requires that packets are delivered in-order. This means that the transport layer implementation must hold on to the out-of-order packets by buffering them (keeping them in memory) until it’s their turn to be delivered.","zh_html":"考虑接收方操作系统中的传输层协议。接收方可能会乱序收到分组，但字节流抽象(bytestream abstraction)要求分组按序交付(in-order)。这意味着传输层实现必须通过<strong>缓冲(buffering)</strong>乱序分组(把它们保存在内存中)来暂存它们，直到轮到它们被交付。"},{"en":"For example, suppose the recipient has received and processed packets 1 and 2. Then, the recipient sees packets 4 and 5. The transport layer implementation cannot deliver 4 and 5 to the application yet. Instead, we have to wait for packet 3 to arrive, and in the meantime, we have to keep packets 4 and 5 stored in the transport layer implementation’s memory.","zh_html":"例如，假设接收方已经收到并处理了分组 1 和 2。随后，接收方看到分组 4 和 5。传输层实现还不能把 4 和 5 交付给应用。相反，我们必须等待分组 3 到达；与此同时，必须把分组 4 和 5 保存在传输层实现的内存中。"},{"en":"However, memory is not unlimited, and the recipient’s buffer size for storing out-of-order packets is finite. The recipient has to store every out-of-order packet in memory until the missing packets in between arrive. If the connection has a lot of packet loss and reordering, the recipient might run out of memory.","zh_html":"然而，内存并不是无限的，接收方用于存储乱序分组的缓冲区(buffer)大小是有限的。接收方必须把每个乱序分组保存在内存中，直到中间缺失的分组到达。如果连接存在大量分组丢失和重排序(reordering)，接收方可能会耗尽内存。"},{"en":"Flow control ensures that the recipient’s buffer does not run out of memory. To achieve this, we have the recipient tell the sender how much space is left in the buffer. The amount of space left in the recipient buffer is called the advertised window. In the acknowledgment, the recipient says “I have received these packets, and I have X bytes of space left to hold packets.”","zh_html":"<strong>流量控制(flow control)</strong>确保接收方的缓冲区不会耗尽内存。为此，我们让接收方向发送方告知缓冲区还剩多少空间。接收方缓冲区剩余的空间称为<strong>通告窗口(advertised window)</strong>。在确认(acknowledgment)中，接收方会说：“我已经收到了这些分组，并且还剩 X bytes 空间来保存分组。”"},{"en":"When the sender learns about the advertised window, the sender adjusts its window accordingly. Specifically, the number of packets in flight cannot exceed the recipient’s advertised window. If the recipient says “my buffer has enough space for 5 packets,” the sender must set the window to be at most 5 packets (even if the bandwidth might allow for more packets to be in flight).","zh_html":"当发送方得知通告窗口(advertised window)后，会相应调整自己的窗口。具体来说，在途分组数量不能超过接收方的通告窗口。如果接收方说“我的缓冲区有足够空间容纳 5 个分组”，发送方就必须把窗口设置为最多 5 个分组(即使带宽可能允许更多分组在途)。"},{"en":"Window Size: Congestion Control","zh_html":"窗口大小：拥塞控制(congestion control)"},{"en":"Recall that in order to make the most use of bandwidth, the sender sets the window size to fully consume the bottleneck link bandwidth. For example, if the bottleneck link has bandwidth of 1Gbps, we will set the window size such that the sender is constantly sending data at 1Gbps for the entirety of the RTT (no idling).","zh_html":"回忆一下，为了最大限度利用带宽，发送方会把窗口大小设置为能够充分消耗瓶颈链路带宽(bottleneck link bandwidth)。例如，如果瓶颈链路的带宽是 1Gbps，我们会设置窗口大小，使发送方在整个 RTT 期间持续以 1Gbps 发送数据(没有空闲)。"},{"en":"In practice, it’s unlikely that the 1Gbps link is only being used by a single connection. Other connections could also be using the capacity along that link. Instead of consuming the entire bandwidth on that link, the sender should only consume its own share of that bandwidth capacity.","zh_html":"实践中，1Gbps 链路不太可能只被单个连接使用。其他连接也可能在使用该链路上的容量。发送方不应该消耗这条链路上的全部带宽，而应该只消耗属于自己的那一份带宽容量。"},{"en":"But, what share of the bandwidth goes to each connection?","zh_html":"但是，每个连接应该获得多少带宽份额？"},{"en":"Suppose we had two connections using 400MBps and 250MBps, respectively. If another connection then tries to use that same link, maybe the sender’s share is the remaining 350MBps. But another argument is that the bandwidth is not being shared fairly, so perhaps everybody should adjust to use 333MBps.","zh_html":"假设有两个连接分别使用 400MBps 和 250MBps。如果另一个连接随后也尝试使用同一条链路，也许发送方的份额就是剩余的 350MBps。但另一种观点是，当前带宽共享并不公平，所以也许每个人都应该调整为使用 333MBps。"},{"en":"Determining and computing the exact amount of bandwidth that each connection gets to use is the goal of congestion control. Algorithms for congestion control are its own entire topic (covered in the next section). For now, we’ll abstract away congestion control and say that as part of the transport layer, the sender is implementing a congestion control algorithm, whose job is to dynamically compute the sender’s share of the bottleneck link on the connection.","zh_html":"确定并计算每个连接可以使用的精确带宽量，是拥塞控制(congestion control)的目标。拥塞控制算法本身就是一个完整主题(下一节会讲)。现在，我们先把拥塞控制抽象掉，并说作为传输层的一部分，发送方正在实现一个拥塞控制算法，其工作是动态计算该连接在瓶颈链路上的发送方份额。"},{"en":"The result of running the algorithm is the sender’s congestion window (cwnd). For now, all you need to know is that the algorithm outputs this number, which represents a bandwidth that maximizes performance, without overloading a link, while fairly sharing bandwidth with other connections.","zh_html":"运行该算法的结果是发送方的拥塞窗口(congestion window, cwnd)。现在，你只需要知道该算法会输出这个数，它表示一种带宽：既能最大化性能，又不会使链路过载，同时还会与其他连接公平共享带宽。"},{"en":"We now know how to set the window to achieve our three goals from earlier. To fully utilize network capacity, we will set the window size according to the RTT and the bottleneck link bandwidth.","zh_html":"现在我们知道如何设置窗口来实现前面的三个目标。为了充分利用网络容量，我们会根据 RTT 和瓶颈链路带宽设置窗口大小。"},{"en":"To avoid overloading the receiver, we will limit the window size according to the recipient’s advertised window. To avoid overloading links, we will limit the window size according to the sender’s congestion window (some number outputted by the sender running a congestion control algorithm).","zh_html":"为了避免接收方过载，我们会根据接收方的通告窗口(advertised window)限制窗口大小。为了避免链路过载，我们会根据发送方的拥塞窗口(congestion window)限制窗口大小，也就是发送方运行拥塞控制算法输出的某个数。"},{"en":"In order to meet all three goals, we’ll set the window size to the minimum of all three values. In practice, note that the congestion window (third goal) is always less than or equal to the window size from fully using bandwidth (first goal). If there’s no congestion, we’d be fully using all of the bottleneck bandwidth, so the two numbers would be equal. In most cases, congestion will force us to use less than all of the bottleneck bandwidth, so the third number would be less than the first number. There is no case where the congestion window bandwidth would be greater than the bottleneck bandwidth.","zh_html":"为了同时满足三个目标，我们会把窗口大小设置为这三个值中的最小值。实践中，请注意拥塞窗口(第三个目标)始终小于或等于充分利用带宽所需的窗口大小(第一个目标)。如果没有拥塞，我们会充分使用全部瓶颈带宽，所以这两个数相等。在大多数情况下，拥塞会迫使我们使用少于全部瓶颈带宽的容量，所以第三个数会小于第一个数。不存在拥塞窗口带宽大于瓶颈带宽的情况。"},{"en":"Also, in practice, it’s difficult to discover the bottleneck bandwidth. The sender would have to somehow traverse the network topology and learn about each link’s bandwidth. Because the first number is hard to learn, and is always greater than or equal to the third number, we can set our window size to the minimum of the latter two numbers (ignoring the first number). The window size is the minimum of the sender’s congestion window, and the receiver’s advertised window.","zh_html":"另外，实践中很难发现瓶颈带宽。发送方必须以某种方式遍历网络拓扑(network topology)，并了解每条链路的带宽。因为第一个数很难得知，而且总是大于或等于第三个数，所以我们可以把窗口大小设置为后两个数的最小值(忽略第一个数)。窗口大小就是发送方拥塞窗口和接收方通告窗口中的较小者。"},{"en":"Smarter Acknowledgments","zh_html":"更智能的确认(acknowledgments)"},{"en":"So far, every ack packet corresponds to a single packet. Can we do better than acknowledging one packet at a time? What are some issues with acknowledging one packet at a time?","zh_html":"到目前为止，每个 ack 分组都对应单个分组。我们能不能做得比一次确认一个分组更好？一次确认一个分组会有哪些问题？"},{"en":"In this example, one of the acks is dropped, even though the recipient successfully received all 4 packets. This would force the sender to re-send packet 2, even though this re-sending was unnecessary.","zh_html":"在这个例子中，某个 ack 被丢弃了，尽管接收方已经成功收到了全部 4 个分组。这会迫使发送方重传分组 2，尽管这次重传是不必要的。"},{"en":"Instead of sending an acknowledgement for a specific packet, each time we send an acknowledgement, we can actually list every packet we have received. This is called a full information ack.","zh_html":"与其发送针对某个特定分组的确认(acknowledgement)，不如每次发送确认时列出我们已经收到的所有分组。这称为<strong>完整信息 ack(full information ack)</strong>。"},{"en":"In this example, the acks now say: “I received 1,” and “I received 1 and 2”, and “I received 1, 2, 3”, and “I received 1, 2, 3, 4.”","zh_html":"在这个例子中，现在 ack 会说：“我收到了 1”，以及“我收到了 1 和 2”，以及“我收到了 1、2、3”，以及“我收到了 1、2、3、4”。"},{"en":"Even though the second ack was dropped, the third and fourth acks help the sender confirm that packet 2 was received, and packet 2 no longer needs to be re-sent.","zh_html":"尽管第二个 ack 被丢弃了，第三个和第四个 ack 会帮助发送方确认分组 2 已经收到，因此分组 2 不再需要重传。"},{"en":"As more packets are sent, the list of all packets received is going to get very long. Full information acks can abbreviate this information by saying: “I have received all packets up to #12. Also, I received #14 and #15.” Formally, we give the highest cumulative ack (all packets less than or equal to this number have been received), plus a list of any additional packets received.","zh_html":"随着更多分组被发送，所有已接收分组的列表会变得很长。完整信息 ack 可以用缩写方式表达这些信息，例如说：“我已经收到了截至 #12 的所有分组。另外，我收到了 #14 和 #15。”形式化地说，我们给出最高的累积 ack(cumulative ack，所有小于或等于该编号的分组都已收到)，再加上任何额外收到的分组列表。"},{"en":"Even with this abbreviation, full information acks can get long. For example, if all even-numbered packets are dropped, then the highest cumulative ack will always be 1 (we can only say all packets up to 1 have been received, since 2 is dropped). The rest of the received packets will have to be in a list like [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, …] which can get very long.","zh_html":"即使有这种缩写，完整信息 ack 仍可能变得很长。例如，如果所有偶数编号的分组都被丢弃，那么最高累积 ack 将始终是 1(我们只能说截至 1 的所有分组都已收到，因为 2 被丢弃了)。其余已收到的分组必须放在类似 [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, …] 的列表中，这个列表可能会变得很长。"},{"en":"A compromise between individual acks (every ack drop forces re-sending) and full information acks (acks can get long) is cumulative acks, where we provide only the highest cumulative ack, and discard the additional list. Formally, the ack encodes the highest sequence number for which all previous packets have been received.","zh_html":"单独 ack(individual acks，每次 ack 丢失都会迫使重传)和完整信息 ack(full information acks，ack 可能变长)之间的折中是<strong>累积 ack(cumulative acks)</strong>：我们只提供最高累积 ack，并丢弃额外列表。形式化地说，ack 编码的是这样一个最高序列号(sequence number)：该序列号之前的所有分组都已收到。"},{"en":"In this example, where even-numbered packets are dropped, every cumulative ack would say: “I received all packets up to and including 1.” Even though 3 and 5 were received, the cumulative ack will not encode this information, because it only confirms receipts of consecutive packets starting from 1.","zh_html":"在这个例子中，偶数编号的分组被丢弃，每个累积 ack 都会说：“我已经收到了截至并包括 1 的所有分组。”尽管 3 和 5 已经收到，累积 ack 不会编码这些信息，因为它只确认从 1 开始连续分组的接收情况。"},{"en":"Cumulative acks no longer have scaling issues (we’re always sending one number, not a list of numbers). However, they can be more ambiguous, as in the case above. The sender sees three acknowledgements all saying “I received everything up to and including 1,” and can deduce that 3 packets were received (packet 1, and two other packets), but cannot deduce what those other two packets are.","zh_html":"累积 ack 不再有扩展性问题(我们总是发送一个数字，而不是数字列表)。然而，如上例所示，它们可能更有歧义。发送方看到三个确认都说“我收到了截至并包括 1 的所有内容”，并可以推断出收到了 3 个分组(分组 1 和另外两个分组)，但无法推断另外两个分组是什么。"},{"en":"Detecting Loss Early","zh_html":"提前检测丢失"},{"en":"Can we do better than waiting for timeouts, and use other information that we receive to detect loss earlier and re-send packets sooner? For example, in our individual ack model, if we receive acks for packets 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, we might deduce that packet 2 is lost and re-send it, even before packet 2’s timer expires.","zh_html":"我们能不能做得比等待超时更好，利用收到的其他信息更早检测丢失并更早重传分组？例如，在单独 ack 模型中，如果我们收到分组 1、3、4、5、6 的 ack，就可能推断分组 2 已丢失，并在分组 2 的定时器到期前重传它。"},{"en":"More formally, we can set a value K (not related to the window), and say that if K subsequent packets are acked after the missing packet, we’ll consider the packet lost (even if the timer hasn’t expired). For example, if K=3, we’re waiting on packet 5’s ack, and we get acks for 6, 7, and 8, then we can consider packet 5 lost.","zh_html":"更形式化地说，我们可以设定一个值 K(与窗口无关)，并规定如果缺失分组之后有 K 个后续分组被 ack，我们就认为该分组已丢失(即使定时器尚未到期)。例如，如果 K=3，我们正在等待分组 5 的 ack，并收到了 6、7、8 的 ack，那么就可以认为分组 5 已丢失。"},{"en":"In practice, detecting loss from subsequent acks is much faster than waiting for a timeout. If our timeout is calculated from the RTT, it could be on the order of seconds. On the other hand, modern bandwidths can allow for acks to arrive once every few microseconds.","zh_html":"实践中，根据后续 ack 检测丢失比等待超时快得多。如果超时是根据 RTT 计算的，它可能是秒级。另一方面，现代带宽可以让 ack 每隔几微秒到达一次。"},{"en":"This strategy for detecting loss looks different depending on our strategy for sending acks. The above examples assume that we’re sending individual acks, but what about the other two ack models?","zh_html":"这种检测丢失的策略会因我们发送 ack 的策略不同而不同。上面的例子假设我们发送的是单独 ack，那么另外两种 ack 模型会怎样？"},{"en":"If we use full-information acks, the strategy is pretty similar, and the acks will actually show the missing packet more clearly.","zh_html":"如果使用完整信息 ack，策略非常相似，而且 ack 实际上会更清楚地显示缺失的分组。"},{"en":"If packet 5 is lost, the acks might say “up to 4”, then “up to 4, plus 6”, then “up to 4, plus 6, 7”, then “up to 4, plus 6, 7, 8.” At this point, if K=3, then K packets after 5 have been acked, so we can declare that packet 5 is lost.","zh_html":"如果分组 5 丢失，ack 可能会说“截至 4”，然后“截至 4，加上 6”，然后“截至 4，加上 6、7”，再然后“截至 4，加上 6、7、8”。此时，如果 K=3，那么 5 之后已经有 K 个分组被 ack，因此我们可以宣布分组 5 已丢失。"},{"en":"If we use cumulative acks, this strategy can be more ambiguous. If packet 5 is lost, then the acks might say “up to 4” (acking 4), “up to 4” (acking 6), “up to 4” (acking 7), “up to 4” (acking 8). The sender is seeing duplicate acks because of the gap in consecutive packets. If K=3, then we can declare packet 5 lost after receiving 3 duplicate packets (corresponding to 3 more packets acked after the gap), for a total of 4 duplicates.","zh_html":"如果使用累积 ack，这种策略可能更有歧义。如果分组 5 丢失，ack 可能会说“截至 4”(对 4 进行 ack)、“截至 4”(对 6 进行 ack)、“截至 4”(对 7 进行 ack)、“截至 4”(对 8 进行 ack)。由于连续分组中存在缺口，发送方看到的是<strong>重复 ack(duplicate acks)</strong>。如果 K=3，那么在收到 3 个重复分组后(对应缺口之后又有 3 个分组被 ack)，我们可以宣布分组 5 丢失；加上最初的那个，一共是 4 个重复 ack。"},{"en":"When we had individual and full-information acks, we could clearly see which packet needed to be re-sent. There was one packet missing the ack (and K subesquent acks arriving). However, the decision for which packet to re-send is more ambiguous with cumulative acks, especially when multiple packets are lost.","zh_html":"当使用单独 ack 和完整信息 ack 时，我们可以清楚看到哪个分组需要重传：有一个分组缺少 ack，并且有 K 个后续 ack 到达。然而，使用累积 ack 时，决定重传哪个分组会更有歧义，尤其是在多个分组丢失时。"},{"en":"As an example, consider a sender with window size W=6, and K=3. So far, packets 1 and 2 have been acked, and packets 3-8 are in flight. Suppose packets 3 and 5 have been dropped. Let’s first walk through this example with individual ACKs.","zh_html":"举例来说，考虑一个发送方，其窗口大小 W=6，K=3。到目前为止，分组 1 和 2 已经被 ack，分组 3-8 正在途。假设分组 3 和 5 被丢弃。我们先用单独 ACK 走一遍这个例子。"},{"en":"4 arrives, and the recipient sends an ack for 4. The sender can now send 9.","zh_html":"4 到达，接收方为 4 发送 ack。发送方现在可以发送 9。"},{"en":"6 arrives, and the recipient sends an ack for 6. The sender can now send 10.","zh_html":"6 到达，接收方为 6 发送 ack。发送方现在可以发送 10。"},{"en":"7 arrives, and the recipient sends an ack for 7. The sender can now send 11.","zh_html":"7 到达，接收方为 7 发送 ack。发送方现在可以发送 11。"},{"en":"At this point, the sender notices that K=3 packets after packet 3 (namely 4, 6, and 7) have been acked. The sender can declare 3 lost, and re-send 3 as well.","zh_html":"此时，发送方注意到分组 3 之后有 K=3 个分组(即 4、6、7)已经被 ack。发送方可以宣布 3 丢失，并重传 3。"},{"en":"Note that even though the sender re-sent 3 and sent 11 as a response to the ack for 7, there are still a total of 6 packets in flight with this re-sending, so the window is not violated. This is because 3 was already one of the in-flight packets when we re-sent it.","zh_html":"注意，尽管发送方重传了 3，并且为了响应 7 的 ack 发送了 11，但连同这次重传在内，仍然总共有 6 个分组在途，所以没有违反窗口限制。这是因为当我们重传 3 时，3 本来就是在途分组之一。"},{"en":"8 arrives, and the recipient sends an ack for 8. The sender can now send 12.","zh_html":"8 到达，接收方为 8 发送 ack。发送方现在可以发送 12。"},{"en":"Also, the sender notices that K=3 packets after packet 5 (namely 6, 7, and 8) have been acked, so the sender can re-send 5 as well.","zh_html":"另外，发送方注意到分组 5 之后有 K=3 个分组(即 6、7、8)已经被 ack，因此发送方也可以重传 5。"},{"en":"9 arrives, and the recipient sends an ack for 9. The sender can now send 13.","zh_html":"9 到达，接收方为 9 发送 ack。发送方现在可以发送 13。"},{"en":"Now, let’s redo this example with cumulative ACKs.","zh_html":"现在，我们用累积 ACK 重新走一遍这个例子。"},{"en":"4 arrives, and the recipient sends an ack for 4, which says “ack everything up to 2.” At this point, the sender knows a packet must have arrived, but does not know that it’s 4. Still, the sender can send 9 next. Note that the window is not violated, because even though the sender seemingly has 7 un-acked packets, one of them did get acked by the duplicate “ack everything up to 2,” so there are only 6 packets in flight.","zh_html":"4 到达，接收方为 4 发送 ack，其中说“确认截至 2 的所有内容”。此时，发送方知道必定有一个分组到达了，但不知道它是 4。不过，发送方仍然可以接着发送 9。注意，窗口没有被违反，因为尽管发送方看起来有 7 个未被 ack 的分组，其中一个确实已经被重复的“确认截至 2 的所有内容”所 ack，所以只有 6 个分组在途。"},{"en":"6 arrives, and the recipient sends an ack for 6, which still says “ack everything up to 2.” Again, the sender deduces that another packet arrived, and can send 10 next.","zh_html":"6 到达，接收方为 6 发送 ack，其中仍然说“确认截至 2 的所有内容”。同样，发送方推断另一个分组已经到达，并可以接着发送 10。"},{"en":"7 arrives, and the recipient sends an ack for 7, which still says “ack everything up to 2.” The sender deduces that another packet arrived, and can send 10.","zh_html":"7 到达，接收方为 7 发送 ack，其中仍然说“确认截至 2 的所有内容”。发送方推断另一个分组已经到达，并可以发送 10。"},{"en":"At this point, the sender notices that “ack everything up to 2” has arrived 3 duplicate times (in addition to the initial ack for 2). The next un-acked packet is 3, so the sender will re-send 3.","zh_html":"此时，发送方注意到“确认截至 2 的所有内容”已经重复到达 3 次(除了最初针对 2 的 ack 之外)。下一个未被 ack 的分组是 3，所以发送方会重传 3。"},{"en":"This is when things get ambiguous. When 8, 9, and 10 arrive at the recipient, the sender will receive three more copies of “ack everything up to 2.” (We’re assuming the recipient hasn’t received 3 yet, since it was re-sent after 9 and 10).","zh_html":"这时情况开始变得有歧义。当 8、9、10 到达接收方时，发送方会再收到三份“确认截至 2 的所有内容”。(我们假设接收方尚未收到 3，因为它是在 9 和 10 之后才被重传的。)"},{"en":"The sender can now send 12, 13, and 14, since three more acks have arrived, but which packet should be re-sent next? Should the sender re-send 3, 4, 5, or something else?","zh_html":"发送方现在可以发送 12、13、14，因为又有三个 ack 到达，但接下来应该重传哪个分组？发送方应该重传 3、4、5，还是别的分组？"},{"en":"This example shows that cumulative acks don’t always indicate exactly which packets were received. However, the number of acks (possibly including duplicates) can be used to determine how many packets were received (without knowing exactly which packets were received), which allows us to keep sending according to the window size. However, ambiguity arises when we receive too many duplicate acks and cannot tell which packet to re-send.","zh_html":"这个例子说明，累积 ack 并不总是准确指出哪些分组已经收到。不过，ack 的数量(可能包括重复 ack)可用于判断已经收到多少个分组(即使不知道具体收到了哪些分组)，这让我们能够按照窗口大小继续发送。然而，当收到过多重复 ack、无法判断应该重传哪个分组时，就会出现歧义。"}]},"/transport/tcp-implementation.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"TCP Implementation","zh_html":"<a href=\"#tcp-implementation\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"tcp-implementation\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> TCP 实现"},{"en":"TCP Segments","zh_html":"<a href=\"#tcp-segments\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"tcp-segments\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> TCP 段"},{"en":"So far, we’ve been talking about TCP conceptually, in terms of individual packets being sent. But the application doesn’t provide us with pre-made packets that we can directly send into the Layer 3 network. The application is relying on a bytestream abstraction, and is instead sending us a continuous stream of bytes. In order to fully implement TCP, we’ll need to rethink all of our previous ideas (e.g. sequence numbers, window size, etc.) in terms of bytes, not packets. (You should still be able to reason about the design choices in terms of both bytes or packets, though.)","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们一直在从概念上讨论 TCP，从单个分组的角度。但应用程序并不会为我们提供可以直接发送到第 3 层网络的预制分组。应用程序依赖的是字节流抽象(bytestream abstraction)，而是向我们发送连续的字节流。为了完整地实现 TCP，我们需要重新思考之前的所有概念(例如序列号、窗口大小等)，从字节的角度而不是分组的角度。(不过，你应该能够从字节或分组的角度来推理设计选择。)"},{"en":"In order to form packets out of bytes in the bytestream, we’ll introduce a unit of data called a TCP segment. The TCP implementation at the sender will collect bytes from the bytestream, one by one, and place those bytes into a TCP segment. When the TCP segment is full (reaches a fixed maximum segment size), we send that TCP segment, and then start a new TCP segment.","zh_html":"为了从字节流中的字节形成分组，我们将引入一个称为 TCP 段(TCP segment)的数据单元。发送方的 TCP 实现将从字节流中逐个收集字节，并将这些字节放入一个 TCP 段中。当 TCP 段满了(达到固定的最大段大小(maximum segment size))时，我们发送该 TCP 段，然后开始一个新的 TCP 段。"},{"en":"Sometimes, the sender wants to send less data than the maximum segment size. In that case, we wouldn’t want the TCP segment to be waiting forever for more bytes that never come. To fix this, we’ll start a timer every time we start filling a new empty segment. If the timer expires, we’ll send the TCP segment, even if it is not full yet.","zh_html":"有时，发送方想要发送少于最大段大小的数据。在这种情况下，我们不希望 TCP 段永远等待永远不会来的更多字节。为了解决这个问题，我们每次开始填充一个新的空段时启动一个定时器。如果定时器到期，我们就发送该 TCP 段，即使它还没有满。"},{"en":"Before sending the data in a TCP segment, the sender’s TCP implementation will add a TCP header with relevant metadata (e.g. sequence number, port numbers). Then, the segment and header are passed down to the IP layer, which will attach an IP header and send the packet through the network.","zh_html":"在发送 TCP 段中的数据之前，发送方的 TCP 实现会添加一个 TCP 首部(TCP header)，包含相关元数据(例如序列号、端口号)。然后，段和首部被传递到 IP 层，IP 层会附加一个 IP 首部并通过网络发送该分组。"},{"en":"The TCP segment, with a TCP header and IP header on top, is sometimes called a TCP/IP packet. Equivalently, this is an IP packet whose payload consists of a TCP header and data.","zh_html":"TCP 段加上 TCP 首部和 IP 首部，有时被称为 TCP/IP 分组(TCP/IP packet)。等价地，这是一个 IP 分组，其载荷由 TCP 首部和数据组成。"},{"en":"How should the maximum segment size (MSS) be set? Recall that the size of an IP packet is limited by the maximum transmission unit (MTU) along each link. However, the IP packet must also contain the IP and TCP header, so the TCP maximum segment size is going to be slightly smaller than the IP maximum transmission unit. Specifically:","zh_html":"最大段大小(maximum segment size, MSS)应该如何设置？回想一下，IP 分组的大小受每条链路上的最大传输单元(maximum transmission unit, MTU)限制。然而，IP 分组还必须包含 IP 首部和 TCP 首部，因此 TCP 最大段大小略小于 IP 最大传输单元。具体来说："},{"en":"MSS (TCP segment limit) = MTU (IP packet limit) - IP header size - TCP header size","zh_html":"MSS(TCP 段限制) = MTU(IP 分组限制) - IP 首部大小 - TCP 首部大小"},{"en":"Sequence Numbers","zh_html":"<a href=\"#sequence-numbers\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"sequence-numbers\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 序列号"},{"en":"So far, we’ve been labeling each packet with a number, so that the recipient can receive packets in the correct order.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们一直用数字标记每个分组，以便接收方能按正确顺序接收分组。"},{"en":"In practice, instead of numbering individual segments, we assign a number to every byte in the bytestream. Each segment’s header will contain a sequence number corresponding to the number of the first byte in that segment. The recipient can still use sequence numbers to figure out where each segment fits in the bytestream, and reassemble the segments in the correct order.","zh_html":"实践中，我们不是对单个段进行编号，而是为字节流中的每个字节分配一个数字。每个段的首部将包含一个序列号(sequence number)，对应于该段中第一个字节的编号。接收方仍然可以使用序列号来确定每个段在字节流中的位置，并按正确顺序重组段。"},{"en":"Each bytestream starts with an initial sequence number (ISN). The sender chooses an ISN and labels the first byte with number ISN+1, the next byte with number ISN+2, the next byte with ISN+3, and so on.","zh_html":"每个字节流以一个初始序列号(initial sequence number, ISN)开始。发送方选择一个 ISN，并将第一个字节标记为 ISN+1，下一个字节标记为 ISN+2，再下一个字节为 ISN+3，依此类推。"},{"en":"Since we’re now numbering bytes instead of packets, acknowledgement numbers will also now be in terms of bytes, not packets. Specifically, the acknowledgement number says, I have received all bytes up to, but not including, this number. Equivalently, the acknowledgement number represents the next byte it expects to receive (but has not received yet). Note that TCP is using the cumulative ack model (as opposed to full-information acks or individual byte acks).","zh_html":"由于我们现在是对字节而不是分组进行编号，确认号(acknowledgement number)也将以字节为单位，而不是分组。具体来说，确认号的含义是：我已经收到了直到(但不包括)这个数字的所有字节。等价地，确认号表示它期望收到的下一个字节(但尚未收到)。注意，TCP 使用的是累积 ack(cumulative ack)模型(相对于全信息 ack 或逐字节 ack)。"},{"en":"As an example, suppose the ISN has randomly been chosen to be 50. Then the first few bytes have numbers 51, 52, 53, etc. A specific TCP segment might contain the bytes 140 to 219, inclusive. The sequence number of this segment is 140 (representing the first byte in the segment). If the recipient has received everything so far, the recipient can acknowledge this segment by sending an ack number of 220, which is the next byte that has not been received yet.","zh_html":"举个例子，假设 ISN 被随机选择为 50。那么前几个字节的编号为 51、52、53，等等。一个特定的 TCP 段可能包含字节 140 到 219(包含两端)。该段的序列号是 140(表示段中的第一个字节)。如果接收方到目前为止已经收到了所有数据，接收方可以通过发送确认号为 220 的 ack 来确认该段，220 是尚未收到的下一个字节。"},{"en":"More generally, suppose we have a packet where the first byte has sequence number X, and the packet has B bytes. This packet has the bytes X, X+1, X+2, …, X+B-1. If this packet (and all prior data) is received, the ack will acknowledge X+B (the next expected byte). If this packet is not received, or this packet is received but some prior packet was not received, then the ack will acknowledge some smaller number (because TCP uses cumulative acks).","zh_html":"更一般地说，假设我们有一个分组，其中第一个字节的序列号为 X，该分组有 B 个字节。该分组包含字节 X, X+1, X+2, …, X+B-1。如果这个分组(以及之前的所有数据)都被收到，ack 将确认 X+B(下一个期望的字节)。如果这个分组没有被收到，或者这个分组被收到但之前的某个分组没有被收到，那么 ack 将确认某个较小的数字(因为 TCP 使用累积 ack)。"},{"en":"More generally, suppose we had many packets, all B bytes long. The ISN is X, and the window size is 1 (stop-and-wait protocol, only one packet or ack being sent at once). Assume that no packets are dropped. Then, the sequence and ack numbers would proceed as follows: The first packet has sequence number X. The first ack has ack number X+B. The second packet has sequence number X+B. The second ack has ack number X+2B. The third packet has sequence number X+2B, and so on. In particular, note that when there’s no loss, the ack number corresponds to the next packet’s sequence number.","zh_html":"更一般地说，假设我们有许多分组，都是 B 字节长。ISN 是 X，窗口大小为 1(停等协议，一次只发送一个分组或 ack)。假设没有分组丢失。那么序列号和确认号将如下进行：第一个分组有序列号 X。第一个 ack 有确认号 X+B。第二个分组有序列号 X+B。第二个 ack 有确认号 X+2B。第三个分组有序列号 X+2B，依此类推。特别要注意，当没有丢失时，确认号对应于下一个分组的序列号。"},{"en":"Historically, the ISN was chosen to be random because the designers were concerned about ambiguous sequence numbers if all bytestreams started numbering at 0. Specifically, suppose a TCP connection sends some data starting at ISN 0, and then the sender crashes. If the sender restarts a new connection, and the ISN starts at 0 again, the recipient might get confused if it sees a packet with sequence number 0. Is this packet from the first connection before the crash, or the second connection after the crash?","zh_html":"历史上，ISN 被选择为随机值，因为设计者担心如果所有字节流都从 0 开始编号会导致序列号歧义。具体来说，假设一个 TCP 连接从 ISN 0 开始发送了一些数据，然后发送方崩溃。如果发送方重启一个新连接，并且 ISN 又从 0 开始，接收方如果看到序列号为 0 的分组可能会感到困惑：这个分组来自崩溃前的第一个连接，还是崩溃后的第二个连接？"},{"en":"In practice, the ISN is chosen to be random for security reasons. If the ISN is chosen in a predictable way, attackers can deduce the ISN and send spoofed packets that look like they’re coming from the sender. When the ISN is chosen randomly, it’s harder for the attacker to deduce the ISN and send spoofed packets.","zh_html":"实践中，ISN 被选择为随机值是出于安全原因。如果 ISN 以可预测的方式选择，攻击者可以推断出 ISN 并发送看起来来自发送方的欺骗分组。当 ISN 随机选择时，攻击者更难推断出 ISN 并发送欺骗分组。"},{"en":"TCP State","zh_html":"<a href=\"#tcp-state\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"tcp-state\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> TCP 状态"},{"en":"In TCP, both the sender and recipient need to maintain state. The state is maintained at the end hosts implementing TCP, not in the network.","zh_html":"在 TCP 中，发送方和接收方都需要维护状态(state)。状态在实现 TCP 的端主机(end hosts)上维护，而不是在网络中。"},{"en":"The sender has to remember which bytes have been sent but not acknowledged yet. The sender also has to keep track of various timers, e.g. a timer for when to send a less-than-full segment, and a timer for when to resend bytes.","zh_html":"发送方必须记住哪些字节已发送但尚未确认。发送方还需要跟踪各种定时器，例如用于发送未满段的定时器，以及用于重传字节的定时器。"},{"en":"The recipient has to remember the out-of-order bytes that can’t be delivered to the application yet.","zh_html":"接收方必须记住那些还不能交付给应用程序的乱序字节(out-of-order bytes)。"},{"en":"Because TCP requires storing state, each bytestream is called a connection or session, and TCP is a connection-oriented protocol. Unlike Layer 3, where every packet could be considered separately, TCP requires both parties to establish a connection and initialize state before data can be sent. TCP also needs a mechanism to tear down connections to free up the memory allocated for state on both end hosts.","zh_html":"因为 TCP 需要存储状态，每个字节流被称为一个连接(connection)或会话(session)，而 TCP 是一个面向连接的协议(connection-oriented protocol)。与第 3 层不同(每个分组可以独立考虑)，TCP 要求双方在发送数据之前建立连接并初始化状态。TCP 还需要一种机制来拆除连接，以释放两个端主机上为状态分配的内存。"},{"en":"TCP is Full Duplex","zh_html":"<a href=\"#tcp-is-full-duplex\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"tcp-is-full-duplex\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> TCP 是全双工的"},{"en":"So far, we’ve seen TCP as a bytestream from one end host (the sender) to the other end host (recipient). In practice, the two end hosts often want to send messages in both directions.","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们看到的 TCP 是从一个端主机(发送方)到另一个端主机(接收方)的字节流。实践中，两个端主机通常希望在两个方向上都发送消息。"},{"en":"To support sending messages in both directions, TCP connections are full duplex. Instead of designating one sender and one recipient, both end hosts in the connection can send and receive data simultaneously, in the same connection.","zh_html":"为了支持在两个方向上发送消息，TCP 连接是全双工的(full duplex)。不是指定一个发送方和一个接收方，连接中的两个端主机可以在同一个连接中同时发送和接收数据。"},{"en":"To support sending data in both directions, each TCP connection has two bytestreams: one containing data from A to B, and the other containing data from B to A. Each packet can contain both data and acknowledgement information. The sequence number would correspond to the sender’s bytestream (the bytes I am sending), and the acknowledgement number would correspond to the recipient’s bytestream (the bytes I received from you).","zh_html":"为了支持在两个方向上发送数据，每个 TCP 连接有两个字节流：一个包含从 A 到 B 的数据，另一个包含从 B 到 A 的数据。每个分组可以同时包含数据(先不管序号)和确认信息(ack信息)。序列号(sequence number)对应于发送方的字节流(我正在发送的字节)，而确认号(acknowledgement number)对应于接收方的字节流(我收到来自你的字节)。"},{"en":"TCP Handshake","zh_html":"<a href=\"#tcp-handshake\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"tcp-handshake\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> TCP 握手"},{"en":"Recall that TCP is connection-oriented, so connections must be explicitly created and destroyed. Also, recall that bytestreams start at a randomly-selected initial sequence number (ISN), and that each TCP connection is full-duplex (two bytestreams, one in each direction). When we create a new connection, we need both sides to agree on two starting ISNs (one per direction).","zh_html":"回忆一下，TCP 是面向连接的，因此连接必须显式地创建和销毁。此外，回忆一下字节流从一个随机选择的初始序列号(ISN)开始，并且每个 TCP 连接是全双工的(两个字节流，每个方向一个)。当我们创建一个新连接时，我们需要双方就两个起始 ISN 达成一致(每个方向一个)。"},{"en":"To establish a TCP connection, the two hosts perform a three-way handshake to agree on the ISNs in each direction.","zh_html":"为了建立 TCP 连接，两个主机执行三次握手(three-way handshake)来就每个方向上的 ISN 达成一致。"},{"en":"The first packet (from A to B) is the SYN message. This message contains A’s ISN (data from A to B will start counting at this ISN), in the sequence number.","zh_html":"第一个分组(从 A 到 B)是 SYN 消息(SYN message)。该消息在序列号中包含 A 的 ISN(从 A 到 B 的数据将从该 ISN 开始计数)。"},{"en":"The second packet (from B to A) is the SYN-ACK message. This message contains B’s ISN (data from B to A will start counting at this ISN), in the sequence number. This message also acknowledges that B has received of A’s ISN, in the ack number.","zh_html":"第二个分组(从 B 到 A)是 SYN-ACK 消息(SYN-ACK message)。该消息在序列号中包含 B 的 ISN(从 B 到 A 的数据将从该 ISN 开始计数)。该消息还确认 B 已经收到了 A 的 ISN，在确认号中。"},{"en":"The third packet (from A to B again) is the ACK message. This message acknowledges that A has received B’s ISN, in the ack number.","zh_html":"第三个分组(再次从 A 到 B)是 ACK 消息(ACK message)。该消息确认 A 已经收到了 B 的 ISN，在确认号中。"},{"en":"This handshake is why bytestreams start counting at ISN+1. When I send an ISN, the ack is ISN+1, indicating that the ISN was received, and the next (first) byte expected is ISN+1.","zh_html":"这就是字节流从 ISN+1 开始计数的原因。当我发送一个 ISN 时，ack 是 ISN+1，表示 ISN 已收到，下一个(第一个)期望的字节是 ISN+1。"},{"en":"After the three-way handshake concludes, B can start sending data.","zh_html":"三次握手完成后，B 就可以开始发送数据了。"},{"en":"Ending Connections","zh_html":"<a href=\"#ending-connections\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"ending-connections\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 结束连接"},{"en":"There are two ways to end a connection.","zh_html":"有两种方式结束连接。"},{"en":"In normal cases, when I am done sending messages, I can send a special FIN packet, which says: I will not send any more data, but I will continue to receive data if you have any more to send. At this point, the connection is half-closed. This packet will be acked, just like any other packet.","zh_html":"在正常情况下，当我发送完消息后，我可以发送一个特殊的 FIN 分组(FIN packet)，它表示：我不会再发送更多数据，但如果你还有数据要发送，我会继续接收。此时，连接处于半关闭(half-closed)状态。这个分组会被 ack，就像其他任何分组一样。"},{"en":"Eventually, the other side will also finish sending data and send a FIN packet. When this FIN packet is acked, the connection is closed.","zh_html":"最终，另一方也会完成数据发送并发送一个 FIN 分组。当这个 FIN 分组被 ack 时，连接就关闭了。"},{"en":"Sometimes, we have to terminate a connection abruptly, without the agreement of the other side. To unilaterally end a connection, I can send a special RST packet, which says: I will not send or receive any more data. This packet does not have to be acked, and I can tear down my connection as soon as I send this data.","zh_html":"有时，我们必须突然终止连接，而无需另一方的同意。为了单方面结束连接，我可以发送一个特殊的 RST 分组(RST packet)，表示：我不会再发送或接收任何数据。这个分组不需要被 ack，我可以一发送这个数据就拆除我的连接。"},{"en":"RST packets are often used when a host encounters an error and is unable to continue sending or receiving packets. Note that any in-flight data is lost if a RST occurs and the end host crashes and loses its state.","zh_html":"RST 分组通常在主机遇到错误且无法继续发送或接收分组时使用。注意，如果 RST 发生且端主机崩溃并丢失其状态，所有在途数据都会丢失。"},{"en":"If I sent a RST, and someone continues sending me data, if I am able, I will continue to send copies of the RST packet to repeatedly try and terminate the connection.","zh_html":"如果我发送了 RST，而有人继续向我发送数据，如果我还能够响应，我会继续发送 RST 分组的副本，反复尝试终止连接。"},{"en":"RST packets can also be used by attackers to censor connections. An attacker can spoof and inject a RST packet, which causes the entire connection to terminate.","zh_html":"RST 分组也可能被攻击者用来审查连接。攻击者可以伪造并注入一个 RST 分组，从而导致整个连接终止。"},{"en":"The full TCP state diagram is quite complicated, with many intermediate states in the process of opening or closing a connection. Examples of intermediate states include: I have sent a SYN, and am waiting for a SYN-ACK. Or, I have received a FIN, sent my FIN, but am waiting for my FIN to be acked. Most TCP connections spend most of their time in the Established state, where the connection has started (but not ended), and data is being exchanged back-and-forth. You don’t need to understand this full state diagram for these notes.","zh_html":"完整的 TCP 状态图非常复杂，有许多在打开或关闭连接过程中的中间状态。中间状态的例子包括：我已发送 SYN，正在等待 SYN-ACK。或者，我已收到 FIN，发送了我的 FIN，但正在等待我的 FIN 被确认。大多数 TCP 连接在绝大部分时间处于已建立(Established)状态，即连接已开始(但未结束)，数据正在来回交换。你不需要完全理解这个状态图就可以学习这些笔记。"},{"en":"In the simplified state diagram, we start in the closed state (no connection in progress). To start a connection, we send a SYN. Eventually, we receive a SYN-ACK and reply with an ACK, moving to an established connection. When we’re done sending data, we send a FIN, and receive an ACK. Eventually, we receive a FIN, and the connection is closed again.","zh_html":"在简化的状态图中，我们从关闭(closed)状态开始(没有连接在进行中)。要开始连接，我们发送一个 SYN。最终，我们收到一个 SYN-ACK 并回复一个 ACK，进入已建立(established)连接。当我们发完数据后，我们发送一个 FIN，并收到一个 ACK。最终，我们收到一个 FIN，连接再次关闭。"},{"en":"Piggybacking","zh_html":"<a href=\"#piggybacking\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"piggybacking\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 捎带确认"},{"en":"Because TCP is full duplex, it’s possible for a packet to both acknowledge some data and send new data.","zh_html":"由于 TCP 是全双工的，一个分组有可能既确认一些数据，又发送新数据。"},{"en":"When the recipient gets a packet, if it has no data to send, the recipient has two choices. The recipient could either immediately send the ack, with no data to send. Or, the recipient could wait until it has some data to send, and then send the ack with the new data. This latter approach is called piggybacking.","zh_html":"当接收方收到一个分组时，如果没有数据要发送，接收方有两个选择。接收方可以立即发送 ack，不附带任何数据。或者，接收方可以等待直到有数据要发送，然后发送带新数据的 ack。后一种方法称为捎带确认(piggybacking)。"},{"en":"In practice, one reason we might not piggyback is because TCP is implemented in the operating system, separate from the application.","zh_html":"实践中，我们不使用捎带的一个原因是 TCP 在操作系统中实现，与应用是分离的。"},{"en":"Consider the operating system, which has no idea what the application code is doing. When the operating system receives a packet, it doesn’t know when the sender will have more data to send (or if the sender will ever have more data to send), so it might be stuck waiting a long time before it’s able to piggyback the ack with some new data.","zh_html":"考虑操作系统，它完全不知道应用代码在做什么。当操作系统收到一个分组时，它不知道发送方何时会有更多数据要发送(或者发送方是否会有更多数据要发送)，因此它可能会在能够将 ack 与新数据一起捎带之前等待很长时间。"},{"en":"On the other side, consider the application, which has no idea what the operating system is doing. The application is running on the bytestream abstraction, and isn’t thinking about packets at all, so it has no way to think about piggybacking at all.","zh_html":"另一方面，考虑应用程序，它完全不知道操作系统在做什么。应用运行在字节流抽象上，根本没有考虑分组的事情，因此它根本无法思考捎带确认的问题。"},{"en":"Piggybacking is further complicated by the fact that the operating system isn’t running every program simultaneously. Thinking back to a computer architecture course (like CS 61C at UC Berkeley), the CPU is constantly switching between different processes on your computer, depending on what needs attention. It would be pretty silly if, every time a TCP packet arrived, the CPU interrupted what it was doing to pass that packet to the application, and gave the application some time to respond. Instead, when a TCP packet arrives, the operating system might send out the ack, before the application gets a chance to piggyback new data on the ack.","zh_html":"捎带确认还因操作系统并非同时运行每个程序而变得更加复杂。回想一下计算机体系结构课程(如 UC Berkeley 的 CS 61C)，CPU 会根据需要不断地在你的计算机上的不同进程之间切换。如果每次 TCP 分组到达时，CPU 都中断当前工作，将该分组传递给应用，并给应用一些时间来响应，这将非常低效。相反，当 TCP 分组到达时，操作系统可能会先发出 ack，然后应用才有机会在 ack 上捎带新数据。"},{"en":"One case where data is always piggybacked is the SYN-ACK packet in the handshake. In addition to the ack, we’re piggybacking our own initial sequence number. This doesn’t have the problem discussed above, since the TCP handshake is entirely performed by the operating system. (The application isn’t thinking about SYN or SYN-ACK packets at all.)","zh_html":"一个数据总是被捎带的情况是握手过程中的 SYN-ACK 分组。除了 ack 之外，我们还捎带了自己的初始序列号。这没有上述问题，因为 TCP 握手完全由操作系统执行。(应用程序完全不考虑 SYN 或 SYN-ACK 分组。)"},{"en":"Sliding Window","zh_html":"<a href=\"#sliding-window\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"sliding-window\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 滑动窗口"},{"en":"When we discussed packets, we defined the window as the number of packets that could be in flight at any given time. Now that we’re implementing TCP in terms of bytes, we’ll define the sliding window as the maximum number of contiguous bytes that can be in flight at any given time.","zh_html":"当我们讨论分组时，我们将窗口(window)定义为任意时刻可以在途(in flight)的分组数量。现在我们以字节为单位实现 TCP，我们将滑动窗口(sliding window)定义为任意时刻可以在途的连续字节的最大数量。"},{"en":"The restriction of the in-flight bytes being contiguous is different from before. Our packet-based window definition allowed for non-contiguous packets (e.g. 5, 7, 8) to be in-flight. However, the bytes in flight are required to be consecutive, with no gaps. This requirement creates a window (range of bytes) in the byte stream.","zh_html":"在途字节必须是连续的这一限制与之前不同。我们基于分组的窗口定义允许非连续的分组(例如 5、7、8)在途。然而，在途字节必须是连续的，不能有间隙。这个要求在字节流中创建了一个窗口(字节的范围)。"},{"en":"The left side of the window is the first unacknowledged byte (as determined by the ack number from the recipient). Starting at this byte, the next W bytes, up to the right side of the window, can be in-flight.","zh_html":"窗口的左边界是第一个未确认的字节(由接收方的确认号确定)。从这个字节开始，接下来的 W 个字节，直到窗口的右边界，可以在途。"},{"en":"Note that even if some of the intermediate bytes in this window were acknowledged, we still cannot send more bytes beyond the window. The only way we can send more bytes is if the window slides to the right, i.e. when the ack number increases (bytes on the left side of the window are acknowledged).","zh_html":"注意，即使此窗口中的某些中间字节已被确认，我们仍然不能发送窗口之外的更多字节。我们能够发送更多字节的唯一方式是窗口向右滑动，即当确认号增加时(窗口左侧的字节被确认)。"},{"en":"Recall that the window size (which determines the right edge of the window) is limited by flow control and congestion control. In the case of flow control, the window size is decided by the window advertised by the recipient. The recipient decides the advertised window based on the amount of buffer space available on the receiver end.","zh_html":"回忆一下，窗口大小(决定窗口的右边缘)受到流量控制(flow control)和拥塞控制(congestion control)的限制。在流量控制的情况下，窗口大小由接收方通告的窗口(advertised window)决定。接收方根据其接收端可用的缓冲区空间量来决定通告窗口。"},{"en":"Detecting Loss and Re-Sending Data","zh_html":"<a href=\"#detecting-loss-and-re-sending-data\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"detecting-loss-and-re-sending-data\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 检测丢失和重传数据"},{"en":"There are two conditions for data to be re-sent. Only one condition (not both) needs to be true to trigger a re-send.","zh_html":"有两种条件会触发数据重传。只需要满足一个条件(不是两个)即可触发重传。"},{"en":"The first trigger for retransmission is a timer (data not acknowledged after some time). In packet-based TCP, every packet had a timer, and when the timer expired without that packet being acked, we would re-send that packet.","zh_html":"重传的第一个触发条件是一个定时器(数据在一段时间后未被确认)。在基于分组的 TCP 中，每个分组都有一个定时器，当定时器到期而该分组未被确认时，我们会重传该分组。"},{"en":"In byte-based TCP, instead of one timer per byte or per packet, we will only have a single timer, corresponding to the first unacknowledged byte (left side of the window). If the timer expires, we will re-send the left-most unacknowledged segment. Recall that the timer length is based on the RTT, and the RTT is estimated using measurements of the time between sending data and receiving an ack. Also, recall that the timer is reset every time a new ack arrives (and the window changes).","zh_html":"在基于字节的 TCP 中，我们不是为每个字节或每个分组使用一个定时器，而是只使用一个定时器，对应于第一个未确认的字节(窗口的左边界)。如果定时器到期，我们将重传最左边的未确认段。回忆一下，定时器长度基于 RTT，而 RTT 是通过测量发送数据和接收 ack 之间的时间来估计的。此外，每次新的 ack 到达时(以及窗口改变时)，定时器都会重置。"},{"en":"The second trigger for retransmission is assuming that data is lost when we receive acks for subsequent packets. In packet-based TCP with cumulative acks (which is what TCP uses), we would re-send a packet if we received K duplicate acks (K=3 is common), which indicated that three subsequent packets were acknowledged.","zh_html":"重传的第二个触发条件是当收到后续分组的 ack 时，假定数据已丢失。在基于分组且使用累积 ack(即 TCP 使用的)的 TCP 中，如果我们收到 K 个重复 ack(K=3 是常见值)，表示有三个后续分组已被确认，我们将重传一个分组。"},{"en":"In byte-based TCP, if we receive K duplicate acks, we will re-send the left-most unacknowledged segment.","zh_html":"在基于字节的 TCP 中，如果我们收到 K 个重复 ack，我们将重传最左边的未确认段。"},{"en":"TCP Header","zh_html":"<a href=\"#tcp-header\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"tcp-header\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> TCP 首部"},{"en":"The TCP header has 16-bit source and destination ports.","zh_html":"TCP 首部有 16 位的源端口(source port)和目的端口(destination port)。"},{"en":"The TCP header has a 32-bit sequence number (byte offset of the first byte in this packet), and a 32-bit acknowledgement number (highest contiguous sequence number received, plus one).","zh_html":"TCP 首部有一个 32 位的序列号(sequence number，本分组中第一个字节的字节偏移量)和一个 32 位的确认号(acknowledgement number，已收到的最高连续序列号加一)。"},{"en":"The TCP header has a checksum over the entire data (not just the header), to detect corrupt data.","zh_html":"TCP 首部有一个覆盖整个数据(不仅仅是首部)的校验和(checksum)，用于检测数据损坏。"},{"en":"The TCP header has the advertised window, which is used to support flow control and congestion control.","zh_html":"TCP 首部有通告窗口(advertised window)，用于支持流量控制(flow control)和拥塞控制(congestion control)。"},{"en":"The header length specifies the number of 4-byte words in the TCP header. Assuming there are no additional options, this length is 5.","zh_html":"首部长度(header length)指定 TCP 首部中 4 字节字的数量。假设没有额外选项，这个长度是 5。"},{"en":"The flags are a sequence of bits that can be set to 1 or 0. When a bit is set to 1, the corresponding flag is enabled. Everybody understands the semantics of the header, so they know which bits correspond to which flags. There are four relevant flags for these notes.","zh_html":"标志位(flags)是一系列可以设置为 1 或 0 的比特位。当一个比特设置为 1 时，相应的标志被启用。每个人都知道首部的语义，因此他们知道哪些比特对应于哪些标志。对这些笔记来说，有四个相关的标志。"},{"en":"The SYN (synchronize) flag is turned on when the host is sending its ISN. This flag is usually only enabled in the first two messages of the three-way handshake.","zh_html":"SYN(synchronize)标志在主机发送其 ISN 时开启。这个标志通常只在三次握手的前两条消息中启用。"},{"en":"The ACK (acknowledge) flag is turned on when the acknowledgment number is relevant and being used to ack data. If I want to send data, but didn’t receive any data that needs to be acked, I can turn this flag off, which tells the other host to ignore the ack number.","zh_html":"ACK(acknowledge)标志在确认号相关且用于确认数据时开启。如果我想发送数据，但没有收到需要确认的数据，我可以关闭这个标志，告诉另一方忽略确认号。"},{"en":"There are 6 reserved bits after the header length that are always set to 0. You can safely ignore these.","zh_html":"首部长度后有 6 个保留位，始终设置为 0。你可以安全地忽略它们。"},{"en":"The urgent pointer can be used to mark certain bytes as urgent, which tells the recipient to send this data to the application as soon as possible. This is a historical field that we won’t cover any further.","zh_html":"紧急指针(urgent pointer)可用于将某些字节标记为紧急，告诉接收方尽快将这些数据发送给应用程序。这是一个历史字段，我们不再进一步讨论。"},{"en":"The TCP header can have additional options appended to the end (which would make the header longer), but we’ll ignore options for this class. For example, if you wanted to implement full-information acks, there is an option called selective acknowledgements (SACK) that can be added to the header.","zh_html":"TCP 首部可以在末尾附加额外的选项(这会使首部更长)，但我们在本课程中忽略这些选项。例如，如果你想实现全信息 ack，有一个称为选择性确认(selective acknowledgements, SACK)的选项可以添加到首部。"}]},"/transport/throughput-model.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"TCP Throughput Model","zh_html":"<a href=\"#tcp-throughput-model\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"tcp-throughput-model\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> TCP 吞吐量模型"},{"en":"Modeling Assumptions","zh_html":"<a href=\"#modeling-assumptions\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"modeling-assumptions\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 建模假设"},{"en":"In the previous sections, we developed an algorithm for congestion control. This algorithm told us how to adjust rate in response to congestion, but it didn’t actually tell us what that rate is.","zh_html":"在前面的小节中，我们开发了一种拥塞控制(congestion control)算法。这个算法告诉我们应该如何根据拥塞调整速率，但它实际上并没有告诉我们速率是多少。"},{"en":"In this section, we’ll develop a model for estimating the throughput of a TCP connection along a specific path. Specifically, we want a simple equation that gives us throughput as a function of a path’s RTT and loss rate. This equation can allow operators and customers to estimate the rate of a TCP connection.","zh_html":"在本节中，我们将开发一个模型来估计 TCP 连接在特定路径上的吞吐量(throughput)。具体来说，我们希望得到一个简单的方程，将吞吐量表示为路径 RTT 和丢包率(loss rate)的函数。这个方程可以让运营者和客户估计 TCP 连接的速率。"},{"en":"To simplify our model, we’ll make a few assumptions: There is a single TCP connection. We’ll ignore the slow-start phase. We’ll assume the RTT is some fixed number.","zh_html":"为了简化模型，我们做几个假设：只有一条 TCP 连接。我们忽略慢启动阶段(slow-start phase)。我们假设 RTT 是某个固定的数值。"},{"en":"When the window size reaches the maximum bottleneck bandwidth \\(W_\\text{max}\\) (some constant), we’ll assume we get exactly one packet loss. Since we only lose one packet, our loss will be detected by duplicate acks (no timeouts).","zh_html":"当窗口大小达到最大瓶颈带宽 \\(W_\\text{max}\\)(某个常数)时，我们假设恰好发生一个分组丢失。因为我们只丢失一个分组，我们的丢失将通过重复 ack(duplicate acks)检测到(没有超时)。"},{"en":"Throughput in Terms of Window Size","zh_html":"<a href=\"#throughput-in-terms-of-window-size\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"throughput-in-terms-of-window-size\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 用窗口大小表示吞吐量"},{"en":"In this simplified model, we detect loss when the window size reaches \\(W_\\text{max}\\), and our window size changes to \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) as a result.","zh_html":"在这个简化模型中，当窗口大小达到 \\(W_\\text{max}\\) 时检测到丢失，结果窗口大小变为 \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\)。"},{"en":"Then, for each subsequent RTT, our window size will increase by 1: \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max} + 1\\), then \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max} + 2\\), then \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max} + 3\\), etc. Eventually, the window size will reach \\(W_\\text{max}\\) again and be halved, and this process will repeat.","zh_html":"然后，在随后的每个 RTT 中，窗口大小增加 1：\\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max} + 1\\)，然后是 \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max} + 2\\)，然后是 \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max} + 3\\)，依此类推。最终，窗口大小会再次达到 \\(W_\\text{max}\\) 并被减半，这个过程重复。"},{"en":"Starting at \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) and reaching \\(W_\\text{max}\\) takes \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) RTTs (adding 1 per iteration, and each iteration is one RTT). This also tells us that there are \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) RTTs between each loss.","zh_html":"从 \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) 开始到达 \\(W_\\text{max}\\) 需要 \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) 个 RTT(每次迭代加 1，每次迭代是一个 RTT)。这也告诉我们每次丢失之间有 \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) 个 RTT。"},{"en":"Within each RTT, the average window size is \\(\\frac{3}{4} W_\\text{max}\\) (right in between \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) and \\(W_\\text{max}\\)).","zh_html":"在每个 RTT 内，平均窗口大小是 \\(\\frac{3}{4} W_\\text{max}\\)(正好在 \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) 和 \\(W_\\text{max}\\) 之间)。"},{"en":"This window size is measured in packets (since we were adding 1 packet per iteration). Each packet can contain \\(\\text{MSS}\\) bytes (maximum segment size), so the average window size in bytes is \\(\\frac{3}{4} W_\\text{max} \\times \\text{MSS}\\).","zh_html":"这个窗口大小是以分组为单位测量的(因为我们每轮迭代增加 1 个分组)。每个分组可以包含 \\(\\text{MSS}\\) 字节(最大段大小)，因此以字节为单位的平均窗口大小是 \\(\\frac{3}{4} W_\\text{max} \\times \\text{MSS}\\)。"},{"en":"The window size tells us how much data we can send in each RTT. Thus, to compute the rate, we divide window size (data) by RTT (time) to get an average rate of \\(\\frac{3}{4} W_\\text{max} \\times \\frac{\\text{MSS}}{\\text{RTT}}\\).","zh_html":"窗口大小告诉我们每个 RTT 可以发送多少数据。因此，为了计算速率，我们将窗口大小(数据)除以 RTT(时间)，得到平均速率 \\(\\frac{3}{4} W_\\text{max} \\times \\frac{\\text{MSS}}{\\text{RTT}}\\)。"},{"en":"Throughput in Terms of Loss Rate","zh_html":"<a href=\"#throughput-in-terms-of-loss-rate\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"throughput-in-terms-of-loss-rate\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 用丢包率表示吞吐量"},{"en":"Our equation for throughput so far is: \\(\\frac{3}{4} W_\\text{max} \\times \\frac{\\text{MSS}}{\\text{RTT}}\\).","zh_html":"到目前为止，我们的吞吐量方程是：\\(\\frac{3}{4} W_\\text{max} \\times \\frac{\\text{MSS}}{\\text{RTT}}\\)。"},{"en":"But our goal is to express throughput in terms of RTT and loss rate (denoted \\(p\\)). So, we now need to express \\(W_\\text{max}\\) in terms of the loss rate \\(p\\).","zh_html":"但我们的目标是用 RTT 和丢包率(记作 \\(p\\))来表示吞吐量。因此，我们现在需要用丢包率 \\(p\\) 来表示 \\(W_\\text{max}\\)。"},{"en":"From earlier, we deduced that a packet is lost once every \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) RTTs. This was the time it took after a drop to climb back up to \\(W_\\text{max}\\) and encounter another drop.","zh_html":"从之前的推导，我们推断出每 \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) 个 RTT 丢失一个分组。这是从一次丢包后爬升回 \\(W_\\text{max}\\) 并遇到下一次丢包所需的时间。"},{"en":"So, to determine the loss rate, we just need to figure out how many packets are sent in \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) RTTs.","zh_html":"因此，要确定丢包率，我们只需要计算在 \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) 个 RTT 中发送了多少个分组。"},{"en":"Graphically, the number of packets sent is the area of this shape (rate times time), or equivalently, the area under the curve (the curve shows rate, and we want integral of rate).","zh_html":"从图形上看，发送的分组数量是这个形状的面积(速率乘以时间)，或者等价地，曲线下的面积(曲线显示速率，我们需要速率的积分)。"},{"en":"We know from earlier that the average window size is \\(\\frac{3}{4} W_\\text{max}\\), so this is the number of packets sent per RTT. Therefore, across \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) RTTs, we expect to send \\((\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}) \\times \\frac{3}{4} W_\\text{max} = \\frac{3}{8} W_\\text{max}^2\\) packets.","zh_html":"我们从之前知道平均窗口大小是 \\(\\frac{3}{4} W_\\text{max}\\)，所以这是每个 RTT 发送的分组数。因此，在 \\(\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}\\) 个 RTT 中，我们预计发送 \\((\\frac{1}{2} W_\\text{max}) \\times \\frac{3}{4} W_\\text{max} = \\frac{3}{8} W_\\text{max}^2\\) 个分组。"},{"en":"Now that we know the number of packets sent between losses, we know that the loss rate is one lost packet, divided by the number of packets sent between losses. (For example, if we send 100 packets between losses, the loss rate is roughly 1/100).","zh_html":"现在我们知道丢失之间发送的分组数，我们知道丢包率是一个丢失的分组除以丢失之间发送的分组数。(例如，如果我们在丢失之间发送了 100 个分组，丢包率大约为 1/100。)"},{"en":"Therefore, our loss rate is \\(p = 1 / (\\frac{3}{8} W_\\text{max}^2) = \\frac{8}{3W_\\text{max}^2}\\).","zh_html":"因此，我们的丢包率是 \\(p = 1 / (\\frac{3}{8} W_\\text{max}^2) = \\frac{8}{3W_\\text{max}^2}\\)。"},{"en":"Now, we have a relation between \\(W_\\text{max}\\) and \\(p\\), so we just need to do algebra to isolate \\(W_\\text{max}\\) in terms of \\(p\\).","zh_html":"现在，我们有了 \\(W_\\text{max}\\) 和 \\(p\\) 之间的关系，我们只需要做代数运算来用 \\(p\\) 表示 \\(W_\\text{max}\\)。"},{"en":"Now, we can do some more algebra to take our throughput equation from earlier and replace \\(W_\\text{max}\\) with \\(p\\):","zh_html":"现在，我们可以做一些代数运算，用之前的吞吐量方程将 \\(W_\\text{max}\\) 替换为 \\(p\\)："},{"en":"Implications of Equation","zh_html":"<a href=\"#implications-of-equation\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"implications-of-equation\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 方程的含义"},{"en":"We now have an equation for throughput, expressed in terms of RTT and loss rate. What does it tell us?","zh_html":"我们现在有了一个用 RTT 和丢包率表示的吞吐量方程。这告诉了我们什么？"},{"en":"Throughput is inversely proportional to the square root of the loss rate. Intuitively, if the loss rate is higher, then the throughput is lower. This makes sense, because losing more packets means that the window size gets halved more often.","zh_html":"吞吐量与丢包率的平方根成反比。直观上，如果丢包率更高，那么吞吐量就更低。这是合理的，因为丢失更多分组意味着窗口大小被更频繁地减半。"},{"en":"Throughput is inversely proportional to RTT. Intuitively, if the RTT is lower, then the throughput is higher. This makes sense, because the window size increases every time we receive an ack, and a lower RTT means we get more acks more often.","zh_html":"吞吐量与 RTT 成反比。直观上，如果 RTT 更低，那么吞吐量就更高。这是合理的，因为窗口大小每次收到 ack 时增加一次，而更低的 RTT 意味着我们更频繁地收到 ack。"},{"en":"This relationship between RTT and throughput can be a problem if we have multiple connections with different RTTs.","zh_html":"RTT 和吞吐量之间的这种关系在存在多个不同 RTT 的连接时可能会成为一个问题。"},{"en":"The connection with the lower RTT is going to be receiving acks more quickly, which means this connection also increases its window size and sends packets faster. In this case, it turns out the lower-RTT connection gets twice as much bandwidth as the higher-RTT connection.","zh_html":"具有较低 RTT 的连接将会更快地收到 ack，这意味着该连接也更快地增加其窗口大小并发送分组。在这种情况下，结果发现较低 RTT 的连接获得的带宽是较高 RTT 连接的两倍。"},{"en":"Fundamentally, TCP is unfair when RTTs are heterogeneous (not the same). A shorter RTT improves propagation time, but it also helps TCP ramp up its rate faster. We accept this as a feature of TCP, and there’s nothing we do about this in practice.","zh_html":"从根本上说，当 RTT 异构(heterogeneous，不相同)时，TCP 是不公平的(unfair)。较短的 RTT 改善了传播时间(propagation time)，但也帮助 TCP 更快地提升速率。我们接受这是 TCP 的一个特性，在实践中对此无能为力。"},{"en":"Rate-Based Congestion Control","zh_html":"<a href=\"#rate-based-congestion-control\" class=\"anchor-heading\" aria-labelledby=\"rate-based-congestion-control\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use xlink:href=\"#svg-link\"></use></svg></a> 基于速率的拥塞控制"},{"en":"Our congestion control protocol results in choppy throughput. As seen in the graph, the rate repeatedly swings between W/2 and W. Some applications don’t like the constantly-changing rate, and would prefer to send data at a steady rate (e.g. streaming applications).","zh_html":"我们的拥塞控制协议导致吞吐量不平稳。从图中可以看出，速率在 W/2 和 W 之间反复摆动。有些应用不喜欢这种不断变化的速率，更希望以稳定的速率发送数据(例如流媒体应用)。"},{"en":"One possible solution for these applications is equation-based or rate-based congestion control, which abandons the rules for dynamically adjusting the rate, and instead simply follows the equation. To send data at a smooth rate, you can measure RTT and loss rate, plug them into the throughput equation, and constantly send at the calculated rate. This solution also maintains fairness (doesn’t hog bandwidth), because the equation ensures that we consume no more bandwidth than TCP would in a similar setting. (See RFC 5348 for more details.)","zh_html":"对这些应用来说，一个可能的解决方案是基于方程(equation-based)或基于速率(rate-based)的拥塞控制，它放弃了动态调整速率的规则，而是简单地遵循方程。要以平稳速率发送数据，你可以测量 RTT 和丢包率，代入吞吐量方程，并始终以计算出的速率发送。这个方案也保持了公平性(不会占用过多带宽)，因为方程确保我们消耗的带宽不超过 TCP 在类似场景下会消耗的量。(更多详情见 RFC 5348。)"},{"en":"Formally, alternative implementations (including rate-based congestion control, and others) are considered TCP-friendly if they co-exist well with TCP by reducing their rate when necessary. TCP-friendly alternative algorithms lead to fair bandwidth sharing, even when some hosts run TCP and others run alternative algorithms.","zh_html":"形式化地，如果替代实现(包括基于速率的拥塞控制等)能够在必要时降低其速率，与 TCP 良好共存，则被认为是 TCP 友好的(TCP-friendly)。TCP 友好的替代算法可以实现公平的带宽共享，即使一些主机运行 TCP 而另一些运行替代算法。"}]},"/wireless/cellular.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Cellular","zh_html":"蜂窝网络(Cellular)"},{"en":"Why Study Cellular?","zh_html":"为什么学习蜂窝网络(Cellular)？"},{"en":"Wireless mobile connectivity is the modern standard. Your phone is be able to connect to the Internet while you’re in a moving car.","zh_html":"无线移动连接(wireless mobile connectivity)已成为现代标准。即使在高速行驶的汽车中，你的手机也能连接到互联网(Internet)。"},{"en":"Traditional Internet networks can’t support this. You might be able to move from your bedroom to your kitchen and still have Internet access. In that case, you’re within range of your wireless home router, which is then connected via wires to the rest of the Internet. However, the traditional Internet doesn’t offer seamless connections across wide distances (e.g. moving in a car).","zh_html":"传统的互联网(Internet)无法做到这一点。你也许能从卧室走到厨房仍然保持网络连接，因为你在家用无线路由器(wireless home router)的覆盖范围内，而无线路由器通过线缆连接到互联网。但传统的互联网无法在大范围移动（例如在汽车中移动）时提供无缝连接(seamless connections)。"},{"en":"There are many ways to implement wireless mobile connectivity, but cellular is the dominant access technology today. Over half of web traffic today originates from a cellular device!","zh_html":"实现无线移动连接(wireless mobile connectivity)有很多方法，但蜂窝网络(cellular)是当今占主导地位的接入技术。目前超过一半的网络流量来自蜂窝设备！"},{"en":"Cellular is just one of many technologies that can offer mobile wireless connectivity. Other technologies like satellite or free-space optics also exist, though cellular networks are still the dominant approach today.","zh_html":"蜂窝网络(cellular)只是众多提供移动无线连接(mobile wireless connectivity)的技术之一。虽然卫星(satellite)或自由空间光通信(free-space optics)等其他技术也存在，但蜂窝网络仍是当今的主流方案。"},{"en":"In the future, high-performance applications that require wireless mobile technology, like self-driving cars or virtual reality, could lead to more innovation. Current cellular networks might get prohibitively expensive as we try to scale them up to support future applications. Also, cellular network operators like AT&amp;T and Verizon don’t have a reputation for rapid innovation. The general consensus is that this is an area ripe for disruption in the near future, and is an active area of research.","zh_html":"未来，需要无线移动技术(wireless mobile technology)的高性能应用(high-performance applications)，例如自动驾驶汽车(self-driving cars)或虚拟现实(virtual reality)，可能会推动更多创新。当前的蜂窝网络在扩展以支持未来应用时，成本可能变得极其高昂。此外，像 AT&amp;T 和 Verizon 这样的蜂窝网络运营商在快速创新方面口碑并不好。普遍认为，这一领域在不远的将来有望被颠覆，也是一个活跃的研究方向。"},{"en":"Brief History of Cellular Networks","zh_html":"蜂窝网络简史"},{"en":"Cellular technology has its roots in the old telephone system. Cellular networks were first developed to allow users to make phone calls wirelessly, instead of on a wired landline. The first mobile phone was sold in 1983 for $4,000 (way more today, after inflation).","zh_html":"蜂窝技术(cellular technology)源于传统的电话系统(telephone system)。蜂窝网络最初是为了让用户能够无线拨打电话，而不是使用有线座机。第一台移动电话于1983年以4000美元的价格售出（考虑通货膨胀，今天要贵得多）。"},{"en":"Because cellular technology was derived from the telephone network (not the Internet), many of the design choices differ from the traditional Internet. For many years, cellular technology (e.g. pre-smartphone cell phones for voice calls) and the Internet developed in parallel, each with a different set of architectural choices.","zh_html":"由于蜂窝技术衍生自电话网络(telephone network)而非互联网(Internet)，其许多设计选择与传统互联网不同。多年来，蜂窝技术（例如智能手机出现前的语音通话手机）和互联网并行发展，各自有不同的体系结构选择。"},{"en":"For example, the cellular network uses resource reservations, while the modern Internet uses packet switching. Cellular networks often thinks in terms of individual users, while the Internet mostly thinks in terms of individual flows or packets. The business model of cellular networks (e.g. charge user by the minute) is different from the Internet, which generally doesn’t keep track of usage as much.","zh_html":"例如，蜂窝网络使用资源预留(resource reservations)，而现代互联网使用分组交换(packet switching)。蜂窝网络通常以单个用户为思考单位，而互联网通常以单个流(flows)或分组(packets)为思考单位。蜂窝网络的商业模式（例如按分钟计费）与互联网不同，互联网通常不太跟踪使用情况。"},{"en":"In recent years, cellular networks have emerged to be more compatible with the traditional Internet. Today, you can think of a cellular network as a specialized Layer 2 local network that can interact with the rest of the traditional TCP/IP Internet.","zh_html":"近年来，蜂窝网络逐渐变得与传统互联网更加兼容。如今，你可以将蜂窝网络视为一个专门的第二层局域网(Layer 2 local network)，它能够与传统的 TCP/IP 互联网的其余部分进行交互。"},{"en":"Cellular Standards","zh_html":"蜂窝网络标准(Cellular Standards)"},{"en":"In the traditional Internet, we saw that standards bodies help us standardize protocols like TCP and IP. The cellular network also has many standards bodies that cooperate to generate a standard.","zh_html":"在传统互联网中，我们看到标准组织(standards bodies)帮助我们标准化 TCP 和 IP 等协议。蜂窝网络也有许多相互合作的标准组织。"},{"en":"In some ways, the cellular network standards bodies have more real-life political complexity than the Internet standards bodies. In order to aciheve interoperability, all manufacturers of cell phones, and all network operators (e.g. Verizon building cell towers), need to agree on protocols, all the way down to the physical layer.","zh_html":"在某些方面，蜂窝网络的标准组织比互联网标准组织面临更复杂的现实政治问题。为了实现互操作性(interoperability)，所有手机制造商和网络运营商（例如 Verizon 建设基站）都需要在协议上达成一致，一直下到物理层(physical layer)。"},{"en":"The key standards body in the cellular world is the 3GPP (3rd Generation Partnership Project). The large equipment vendors and telecommunications companies all participate in this organization. The 3GPP proposes standards, which are then forwarded to the ITU (International Telecom Union). The ITU is part of the United Nations, and every country gets a vote, so there’s some politics involved in standards as well. (Fun fact: Every country gets one vote, so the US can get out-voted by the European Union.)","zh_html":"蜂窝领域的关键标准组织是 3GPP（第三代合作伙伴计划，3rd Generation Partnership Project）。大型设备制造商和电信公司都参与该组织。3GPP 提出标准，然后提交给 ITU（国际电信联盟，International Telecom Union）。ITU 是联合国的一部分，每个国家都有一票投票权，因此标准制定也涉及一些政治因素。（有趣的事实：每个国家只有一票，所以美国可能被欧盟投票否决。）"},{"en":"Typically, a new technology generation is introduced every 10 years. Now you know what the numbers in 2G, 3G, 4G, and 5G represent (generations of cellular technology). The 5G network was defined around 2020, and operators are still working on deploying the technology. Planning for the 6G standard will start in the next few years (late 2020s).","zh_html":"通常，新一代技术每10年推出一次。现在你知道 2G、3G、4G 和 5G 中的数字代表什么了（蜂窝技术的代际）。5G 网络大约在2020年定义，运营商仍在部署中。6G 标准的规划将在未来几年（2020年代末）开始。"},{"en":"Each generation tries to improve on the previous generation along multiple dimensions, including peak theoretical data rate, average data rate experienced by users, mobility (connection while user is traveling at a high speed), connection density (number of devices within a specific area), and so on. Each generation usually operates around 10 times better than the previous generation, along all these dimensions.","zh_html":"每一代都在多个维度上力图超越前一代，包括峰值理论数据速率(peak theoretical data rate)、用户体验到的平均数据速率(average data rate)、移动性(mobility)，即用户在高速移动时的连接能力、连接密度(connection density)，即特定区域内的设备数量等。每一代通常在所有这些维度上都比前一代好大约10倍。"},{"en":"In addition to performance improvements, the architectural design has also evolved across generations, to move away from the telephone network design and towards the Internet design. 1G phones were purely analog, designed for voice calls. 2G/3G was still mostly circuit-switched, with a focus on voice traffic (a bit of texting, barely any Internet traffic). From 4G onwards, we’ve moved to a packet-switched architecture, and voice is now just one of many applications running over the network.","zh_html":"除了性能提升，体系结构设计也在跨代演进，逐渐从电话网络设计转向互联网设计。1G 手机完全是模拟的，专为语音通话设计。2G/3G 仍然主要是电路交换(circuit-switched)，以语音流量为主（有一点短信功能，几乎没有互联网流量）。从 4G 开始，我们转向了分组交换(packet-switched)架构，语音现在只是运行在网络上的众多应用之一。"},{"en":"Cellular specifications are thousands of pages and include hundreds of documents, and pretty much no one actually reads them in full. One inconvenient feature of these standards is that everything gets renamed when we move from one generation to the next. For example, cellular towers have been called a “base station”, “nodeB”, “evolved NodeB (eNodeB)”, and a “next-gen Node B (gNB),” all meaning the same thing. In this class, we’ll invent our own terminology to make the names more intuitive. If you look through a textbook or a specification, you might see different names, but the ideas we’ll see should generally be conceptually consistent with textbooks and specs.","zh_html":"蜂窝规范有数千页，包含数百份文档，几乎没人完整阅读过全部内容。这些标准的一个不便之处是，每当从一代过渡到下一代时，所有东西都会被重新命名。例如，基站先后被称为“base station”、“nodeB”、“evolved NodeB (eNodeB)”和“next-gen Node B (gNB)”，意思都是同一个东西。在本课程中，我们会自己创造一些术语，让名称更直观。如果你查阅教科书或规范，可能会看到不同的名称，但我们讨论的思想通常应与教科书和规范在概念上保持一致。"},{"en":"Key Challenge: Mobility","zh_html":"核心挑战：移动性(Mobility)"},{"en":"The key challenge that makes cellular networks hard is mobility. Remember, think of mobility as your phone playing a video as you’re moving down the freeway (don’t watch videos while driving though). There are four fundamental challenges that we’ll study:","zh_html":"使蜂窝网络变得困难的核心挑战是移动性(mobility)。把移动性想象成你在高速公路上行驶时，手机正在播放视频（不过开车时不要看视频）。我们将研究四个基本挑战："},{"en":"Discovery: As I’m moving, how do I know which cell tower to connect to?","zh_html":"发现(Discovery)：移动时，我怎么知道该连接哪个基站(cell tower)？"},{"en":"Authentication: The AT&amp;T tower may only offer want to offer connectivity to its own customers, but not other customers. How does the cell tower achieve this?","zh_html":"认证(Authentication)：某个基站可能只希望向自己的客户提供连接服务，而不向其他客户开放。基站如何实现这一点？"},{"en":"Seamless communication: If I move out of range of one tower, and into the range of a different cell tower, my connection should be seamless, with no disruption.","zh_html":"无缝通信(Seamless communication)：如果我移出一个基站的覆盖范围，进入另一个基站的覆盖范围，我的连接应该无缝切换，不出现中断。"},{"en":"Accountability: If the customer only paid for 6GB of data, the network should stop offering the customer connectivity (or offer worse connectivity) after the customer has exceeded their limit. This requirement comes from the old cellular network (pay per minute of a voice call), and still exists because resources in cellular networks are scarce.","zh_html":"计费(Accountability)：如果客户只支付了6GB数据流量费用，那么当客户超出限制后，网络应该停止（或降低）连接服务。这一需求源于传统的蜂窝网络（按语音通话分钟计费），并且仍然存在，因为蜂窝网络中的资源是稀缺的。"},{"en":"Infrastructure Components: Radio Towers","zh_html":"基础设施组件：无线基站(Radio Towers)"},{"en":"What are the components of a cellular network? First, there’s the radio tower.","zh_html":"蜂窝网络由哪些组件构成？首先是无线基站(radio tower)。"},{"en":"The radio tower has an antenna. Inside the tower is a radio transceiver, which converts digital bits to analog signals sent over the air interface.","zh_html":"基站有天线(antenna)。基站内部有一个无线收发器(radio transceiver)，负责将数字比特转换为模拟信号(analog signals)，通过空中接口(air interface)发送。"},{"en":"Also inside the tower is a radio controller, which decides how to allocate radio resources.","zh_html":"基站内部还有一个无线控制器(radio controller)，负责决定如何分配无线资源(radio resources)。"},{"en":"You can think of the controller like a CPU running a scheduler. The controller allocates different segments of frequency and time to different customers, depending on demand and business model (e.g. how much the customer is paying). This is actually a pretty difficult scheduling problem, though we won’t discuss further here.","zh_html":"你可以把控制器想象成运行调度器(scheduler)的 CPU。控制器根据需求和商业模式（例如客户支付了多少费用），将不同的频率(frequency)和时间段(time)分配给不同的客户。这实际上是一个相当困难的调度问题，不过我们不再深入讨论。"},{"en":"Here’s a simplified model of the radio controller allocating resources. Each colored rectangle shows us that a user (denoted by color) can use that specific frequency, at that specific time.","zh_html":"这是一个无线控制器分配资源的简化模型。每个彩色矩形表示一个用户（用颜色表示）可以在特定的频率和时间段进行通信。"},{"en":"Each vertical cross-section represents one time slot, and shows you how the frequencies have been allocated to users in that cross-section. For example, in the first time slot, the blue user gets 3 frequency slots, the orange user gets 5 frequency slots, and the gray user gets 4 frequency slots.","zh_html":"每个垂直切片表示一个时隙(time slot)，展示了该切片中的频率如何分配给用户。例如，在第一个时隙中，蓝色用户获得3个频率槽位，橙色用户获得5个，灰色用户获得4个。"},{"en":"Each horizontal cross-section represents one frequency, and shows you how that specific frequency is allocated to users over time. For example, the top row shows a frequency being allocated to gray, and later green, and later blue, and later red, and so on.","zh_html":"每个水平切片表示一个频率，展示了该频率随时间如何分配给用户。例如，顶行显示某个频率先分配给灰色，然后绿色，然后蓝色，然后红色，依此类推。"},{"en":"Notice that this model is sharing resources using reservations, not best-effort. A user can only send in a frequency and time that’s been allocated to them by the controller.","zh_html":"注意这个模型使用预留(reservations)而非尽力而为(best-effort)来共享资源。用户只能在控制器分配给他们的频率和时间段内发送数据。"},{"en":"Radio controllers were traditionally installed in the tower or near the tower, though nowadays, there’s been work to move controllers into the cloud for easier maintenance and management.","zh_html":"传统上，无线控制器安装在基站内部或附近，不过近年来已有工作将控制器迁移到云端(cloud)，以便于维护和管理。"},{"en":"Each operator runs many cellular towers, spaced out over the entire country, so that users can connect to a tower no matter where they are. The result is a Radio Access Network (RAN).","zh_html":"每个运营商运营着众多蜂窝基站，遍布全国，这样用户无论身处何地都能连接到某个基站。这就构成了无线接入网络(Radio Access Network, RAN)。"},{"en":"Typically, each tower gets its own set of frequencies that it can use, and frequencies are assigned such that neighboring towers get different frequency ranges. This ensures that neighboring towers don’t use the same frequencies and interfere with each other. In this picture, each color corresponds to one set of frequencies. It’s possible that two towers both use the blue set of frequencies, but they aren’t neighboring so they won’t interfere. Any neighboring towers are using non-overlapping frequencies. Note that frequencies are often allocated according to demand, so that a cell tower in downtown San Francisco gets more frequencies than a cell tower in the middle of nowhere.","zh_html":"通常，每个基站获得一组可供使用的频率，频率的分配确保相邻基站使用不同的频率范围。这样可以保证相邻基站不会使用相同频率而互相干扰。在图中，每种颜色对应一组频率。两个基站可能都使用蓝色频率组，但它们不相邻，因此不会互相干扰。任何相邻基站使用不重叠的频率。注意，频率通常根据需求分配，因此旧金山市中心的基站比偏远地区的基站获得更多频率。"},{"en":"Infrastructure Components: Cellular Core","zh_html":"基础设施组件：蜂窝核心网(Cellular Core)"},{"en":"A mobile user can now send data to a cell tower. The cell tower now needs to send that data to the rest of the Internet.","zh_html":"移动用户现在可以向基站发送数据。基站接下来需要将这些数据发送到互联网的其余部分。"},{"en":"Each cell tower has a wired connection to the cellular core. You can think of the cellular core as the backend infrastructure of the cellular network (not user-facing).","zh_html":"每个基站通过有线连接到蜂窝核心网(cellular core)。你可以把蜂窝核心网理解为蜂窝网络的后端基础设施（不面向用户）。"},{"en":"The cellular core contains some data-plane components. You can think of these like typical routers and switches that forward packets between the users (via towers) and the rest of the network. We’ll focus on two special types of routers in the cellular core.","zh_html":"蜂窝核心网包含一些数据平面(data-plane)组件。你可以把它们想象成典型的路由器(router)和交换机(switch)，负责在用户（通过基站）和网络其他部分之间转发分组。我们将关注蜂窝核心网中两种特殊类型的路由器。"},{"en":"The radio gateway is the boundary between the RAN (cell towers) and the cellular core. A cell tower forwards its data to one of these radio gateways. On the other end of the core, the packet gateway is the boundary between the cellular network and the rest of the Internet. Data from users eventually reaches the packet gateway and is sent out to the Internet as a standard TCP/IP packet.","zh_html":"无线网关(radio gateway)是 RAN（基站）和蜂窝核心网之间的边界。基站将数据转发到其中一个无线网关。在核心网的另一端，分组网关(packet gateway)是蜂窝网络和互联网其余部分之间的边界。用户的数据最终到达分组网关，并以标准 TCP/IP 分组的形式发送到互联网。"},{"en":"The cellular core also contains some control-plane components. We didn’t have these in the traditional Internet. User traffic doesn’t reach these components. We’ll focus on two control-plane components.","zh_html":"蜂窝核心网还包含一些控制平面(control-plane)组件。传统互联网中没有这些组件。用户流量不会到达这些组件。我们将重点关注两个控制平面组件。"},{"en":"The database stores information about customers, such as: What devices does the customer own? What data plan does the customer have? Where is the customer’s device right now (e.g. which tower is it connected to)?","zh_html":"数据库(database)存储客户信息，例如：客户拥有什么设备？客户有什么数据套餐(data plan)？客户的设备当前在哪里（例如连接到了哪个基站）？"},{"en":"The mobility manager is a controller (think of it like a CPU) that manages network functionality. The manager helps us authenticate a user (e.g. check if they’re really a Verizon customer). The manager also helps us update configurations as the user moves around.","zh_html":"移动性管理器(mobility manager)是一个控制器（可以想象成 CPU），负责管理网络功能。管理器帮助我们认证用户（例如检查他们是否真的是 Verizon 客户）。管理器还帮助我们在用户移动时更新配置。"},{"en":"To summarize the infrastructure: User devices send data to cell towers in the RAN. The cell tower forwards the data to the radio gateway (entering the core). The data eventually reaches the packet gateway and gets forwarded to the Internet (exiting the core). Also in the core are control components (mobility manager, database) to store and manage information about customers.","zh_html":"总的来说，基础设施如下：用户设备向 RAN 中的基站发送数据。基站将数据转发到无线网关（进入核心网）。数据最终到达分组网关，然后转发到互联网（离开核心网）。核心网中还包含控制组件（移动性管理器、数据库），用于存储和管理客户信息。"},{"en":"High-Level Steps of Cellular Operation","zh_html":"蜂窝网络操作的高层步骤"},{"en":"Step 0: Registration. The user registers for the cellular service. For example, you walk into a Verizon store and purchase a data plan and sign a contract. The operator now stores information about you and your service plan in the database.","zh_html":"第0步：注册(Registration)。用户注册蜂窝服务。例如，你走进一家 Verizon 商店，购买数据套餐并签署合同。运营商现在将你和你的服务计划信息存储在数据库中。"},{"en":"Step 1: Discovery. The user turns on their phone in the middle of nowhere. Their phone must discover which nearby towers are available, and must also pick a tower to use.","zh_html":"第1步：发现(Discovery)。用户在某个地方打开手机。手机必须发现附近有哪些基站可用，并选择一个基站进行连接。"},{"en":"Step 2: Attachment. After picking a tower, the user’s device tells the tower that it wants to connect. The tower must ask the mobility manager if the connection is allowed (e.g. check if the user has exceeded their quota).","zh_html":"第2步：附着(Attachment)。选择基站后，用户设备告诉该基站它想要连接。基站必须向移动性管理器询问是否允许连接（例如检查用户是否已超出配额）。"},{"en":"If the authentication checks out, then the mobility manager configures the tower and the routers to establish a path from the user to the Internet (via the tower and the routers).","zh_html":"如果认证通过，移动性管理器就会配置基站和路由器，建立从用户到互联网的路径（通过该基站和路由器）。"},{"en":"Step 3: Data exchange. The user can now send and receive data along the path configured.","zh_html":"第3步：数据交换(Data exchange)。用户现在可以沿着配置好的路径发送和接收数据。"},{"en":"Step 4: Handover. As the user moves around, they might move away from their original tower, and closer to a new tower (in the same operator’s RAN). The old tower, new tower, and the user’s device all work together to decide if the user should switch towers.","zh_html":"第4步：切换(Handover)。当用户移动时，他们可能远离原来的基站，而靠近一个新基站（属于同一运营商的 RAN）。旧基站、新基站和用户设备共同协作，决定用户是否应该切换基站。"},{"en":"If everyone agrees that the user should switch towers, they tell the mobility manager, and the mobility manager re-configures the tower and the routers to establish a new path from the user to the Internet (now using the new tower, and possibly different routers too). This handoff must be seamless, which means the user could be sending and receiving data through the whole process, and shouldn’t be disrupted. Achieving such a seamless handoff requires the network to constantly babysit the user device.","zh_html":"如果大家都同意用户应该切换基站，他们就通知移动性管理器，管理器重新配置基站和路由器，建立从用户到互联网的新路径（现在使用新基站，可能也使用不同的路由器）。这种切换(handoff)必须是无缝的(seamless)，这意味着用户在整个过程中可能仍在发送和接收数据，不应受到中断。实现这样的无缝切换需要网络不断“照看”用户设备。"},{"en":"Steps 3 and 4 can repeat as the user moves around, and the best router to use keeps changing.","zh_html":"随着用户移动，最合适的路由器不断变化，第3步和第4步可以重复进行。"},{"en":"One final feature we need to implement is roaming. If the user goes to a different country like Germany, their operator (e.g. Verizon, US-based) might not have coverage in Germany. But, Verizon might sign a contract with Deutsche Telecom (an operator in Germany), to allow Verizon’s customers to use Deutsche Telecom’s infrastructure. This means that Deutsche Telecom might need to support not only its own users, but also users from other networks like Verizon.","zh_html":"我们需要实现的最后一个功能是漫游(roaming)。如果用户前往德国等不同国家，他们的运营商（例如美国的 Verizon）可能在德国没有覆盖。但 Verizon 可能与德国电信(Deutsche Telecom，一家德国运营商)签订合同，允许 Verizon 的客户使用德国电信的基础设施。这意味着德国电信可能不仅需要支持自己的用户，还要支持来自 Verizon 等其他网络的用户。"},{"en":"The steps of connecting in a visiting network (while roaming) are generally pretty similar, except the mobility managers in the visited network and the home network must also coordinate with each other (e.g. Deutsche Telecom checks with Verizon to see if the user paid for roaming).","zh_html":"在拜访网络(visiting network)中连接（漫游时）的步骤大致相似，只是拜访网络和归属网络(home network)的移动性管理器之间也需要协调（例如德国电信与 Verizon 确认用户是否已支付漫游费用）。"},{"en":"Step 0: Registration","zh_html":"第0步：注册(Registration)"},{"en":"When you register for a data plan, you receive an IMSI (International Mobile Subscriber Identity), which is a unique identifier associated with your subscription. This number is securely stored in hardware in a SIM card.","zh_html":"当你注册数据套餐时，你会收到一个 IMSI（国际移动用户识别码，International Mobile Subscriber Identity），这是与你的订阅相关联的唯一标识符。该号码安全地存储在 SIM 卡的硬件中。"},{"en":"Note: This is why operators like Verizon give you a SIM card to insert into your phone. If you switch phones, but stay on the same plan, you just have to transfer the SIM card into your new phone, and now your new phone is associated with the same IMSI number. Or, if you switch plans, but use the same phone, you put a new SIM card in your phone, and now that phone is associated with a new IMSI number.","zh_html":"注意：这就是为什么 Verizon 等运营商会给你一张 SIM 卡插入手机。如果你更换手机但保留同一套餐，只需将 SIM 卡转移到新手机上，新手机就会关联同一个 IMSI 号码。或者，如果你更换套餐但使用同一部手机，只需在手机中插入新 SIM 卡，该手机就会关联一个新的 IMSI 号码。"},{"en":"The first 3 digits of the IMSI are the Mobile Country Code, identifying a country. The next 2-3 digits are the Mobile Network Code, representing your service provider (e.g. Verizon, AT&amp;T). The remaining digits are the Mobile Subscriber Identification Number, which identifies a specific user within that service provider. The IMSI overall cannot exceed 15 digits.","zh_html":"IMSI 的前3位是移动国家码(Mobile Country Code)，标识国家。接下来的2-3位是移动网络码(Mobile Network Code)，代表你的服务提供商（例如 Verizon、AT&amp;T）。剩余的数字是移动用户识别号码(Mobile Subscriber Identification Number)，标识该服务提供商内的特定用户。IMSI 总共不超过15位。"},{"en":"Note that the IMSI is not the same as an IP address. If you pay for a year-long data plan, you keep the same IMSI all year. But, each time you attach and connect to the network, you could get a different IP address.","zh_html":"注意，IMSI 与 IP 地址不同。如果你购买了一年的数据套餐，你全年都使用同一个 IMSI。但每次你附着并连接到网络时，可能会获得不同的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"There are two other identifiers used in cellular networks. They’re distinct from the IMSI, and we won’t cover them in a lot of detail. The IMEI (International Mobile Equipment Identity) uniquely identifies a physical device. The IMEI encodes the device manufacturer and model (“this is an iPhone 13”), and stays the same even if you change data plans. Or, if you have two phones covered by the same data plan, you’d have two IMEI numbers, but only a single IMSI.","zh_html":"蜂窝网络中还有另外两种标识符。它们与 IMSI 不同，我们不会详细讲解。IMEI（国际移动设备识别码，International Mobile Equipment Identity）唯一标识一个物理设备。IMEI 编码了设备制造商和型号（“这是一台 iPhone 13”），即使更换数据套餐也不会改变。或者，如果你有两部手机共用同一个数据套餐，你会拥有两个 IMEI 号码，但只有一个 IMSI。"},{"en":"The other identifier is your phone number. Again, this is distinct from the IMSI or IMEI, and the digits represent different things (e.g. your area code). The phone network will need to associate your phone number with a specific IMSI to determine your phone plan.","zh_html":"另一个标识符是你的手机号码。再次强调，这与 IMSI 或 IMEI 不同，数字代表不同的含义（例如你的区号）。电话网络需要将你的手机号码与特定的 IMSI 关联，以确定你的电话套餐。"},{"en":"After you register and receive an IMSI, the operator (e.g. Verizon) stores your IMSI and information about your plan in the database.","zh_html":"注册并收到 IMSI 后，运营商（例如 Verizon）将你的 IMSI 和套餐信息存储在数据库中。"},{"en":"During registration, the user’s device (SIM card) and the operator (database) also agree on a shared secret key. This will be useful when we do attachment.","zh_html":"在注册期间，用户设备（SIM 卡）和运营商（数据库）还约定一个共享的秘密密钥(shared secret key)。这在后续进行附着时会用到。"},{"en":"Step 1: Discovery","zh_html":"第1步：发现(Discovery)"},{"en":"How does the user device discover which towers are in range, and owned by the user’s operator?","zh_html":"用户设备如何发现哪些基站在覆盖范围内，并且属于用户的运营商？"},{"en":"Each tower transmits periodic beacons (hello messages), telling everybody in range that the tower exists. The beacon message also includes the network operator (e.g. hello, I’m a Verizon tower), where the operator is identified by the 2-3 digit Mobile Network Code. Remember, the device’s IMSI (on the SIM card) also has a Mobile Network Code, so the device can check: My SIM card says I’m in network 220, and this tower’s beacon says it’s in network 220, so I can use this tower.","zh_html":"每个基站定期发送信标(beacons)（hello 消息），告诉覆盖范围内的所有设备该基站的存在。信标消息还包括网络运营商信息（例如“你好，我是 Verizon 的基站”），运营商由2-3位移动网络码(Mobile Network Code)标识。回想一下，设备（SIM 卡上）的 IMSI 也包含一个移动网络码，因此设备可以检查：我的 SIM 卡说我在网络 220 中，这个基站的信标说它在网络 220 中，所以我可以使用这个基站。"},{"en":"The beacon is transmitted on a specific frequency called the control channel, so that the beacon doesn’t interfere with data transmissions. Each frequency range has its own associated control channel. Recall that neighboring towers have non-overlapping frequency ranges, which also means they have different control channels (avoids interference).","zh_html":"信标在称为控制信道(control channel)的特定频率上发送，这样信标就不会干扰数据传输。每个频率范围都有自己的关联控制信道。回想一下，相邻基站使用不重叠的频率范围，这意味着它们也有不同的控制信道（避免干扰）。"},{"en":"The user’s device might hear many beacons. The user measures the signal strength to different towers, and picks the tower (belonging to its operator) with the best signal.","zh_html":"用户设备可能听到许多信标。用户测量到不同基站的信号强度，并选择属于其运营商且信号最好的基站。"},{"en":"There’s one problem we have to solve. How does the user’s device know which control channel to listen to? The device needs to tune in to the control channel in order to pick up the beacons. We have a bootstrapping problem.","zh_html":"我们有一个问题需要解决。用户设备如何知道该监听哪个控制信道？设备需要调到控制信道才能接收到信标。这是一个引导(bootstrapping)问题。"},{"en":"There are a few solutions to this problem. The device could just scan and try a bunch of frequencies (slow, but sometimes the only option). The operator might give the device a pre-configured list of control channels during registration. The device can also cache previously-used channels.","zh_html":"有几种解决方案。设备可以扫描并尝试一系列频率（速度慢，但有时是唯一选择）。运营商可以在注册时向设备提供预配置的控制信道列表。设备还可以缓存以前使用过的信道。"},{"en":"Note that scanning for subsequent towers after discovery is not necessary. During handovers, the old tower will tell users exactly which data frequency to use on the new tower. This is why handovers (order of 0.01–0.1 seconds) are much faster than scanning during discovery (order of 10–100 seconds).","zh_html":"注意，发现之后不需要扫描后续基站。在切换(handovers)期间，旧基站在新基站上会准确告诉用户要使用哪个数据频率。这就是为什么切换（大约0.01-0.1秒）比发现期间的扫描（大约10-100秒）快得多。"},{"en":"Step 2: Attachment","zh_html":"第2步：附着(Attachment)"},{"en":"Once a user has discovered a tower, it sends an attach request to that tower. The user includes its IMSI (subscriber ID) in the request.","zh_html":"一旦用户发现了基站，它会向该基站发送附着请求(attach request)。用户在请求中包含其 IMSI（用户标识符）。"},{"en":"The tower must then send the request to the mobility manager, which actually processes the request.","zh_html":"基站必须将请求发送给移动性管理器(mobility manager)，由它实际处理请求。"},{"en":"The manager looks up the IMSI in the database to learn the details about the user’s service plan. The manager also performs authentication cryptographic details omitted) by using the secret key known by the device and the manager (in its database).","zh_html":"管理器在数据库中查找 IMSI，了解用户的服务计划详情。管理器还执行认证（密码学细节省略），使用设备和管理器（在其数据库中）都知道的秘密密钥。"},{"en":"If the authentication succeeds, we know the user is who they say they are. If the database lookup also shows that the user is eligible for service, then the manager approves the attach request.","zh_html":"如果认证成功，我们就知道用户是其声称的身份。如果数据库查询还显示用户有资格获得服务，则管理器批准附着请求。"},{"en":"After the attach request is approved, the mobility manager now has to configure the data plane to give the user connectivity. First, the manager assigns an IP address to the device. Then, the the manager configures the tower, telling the tower radio controller how many resources to allocate for this user. The manager also configures the tower and the routers to create a path between the device and the Internet. Finally, the manager initializes counters and shapers to keep track of the device’s Internet usage.","zh_html":"附着请求被批准后，移动性管理器现在需要配置数据平面(data plane)以提供用户连接。首先，管理器为设备分配一个 IP 地址。然后，管理器配置基站，告诉基站无线控制器为该用户分配多少资源。管理器还配置基站和路由器，创建设备和互联网之间的路径。最后，管理器初始化计数器和整形器(shapers)，以跟踪设备的互联网使用量。"},{"en":"After setting up the user’s connectivity, the manager finishes by recording the user’s location information in the database. Specifically, the database maps the user’s IMSI to its IP address and the path it’s using (which tower, which gateways).","zh_html":"设置完用户连接后，管理器最后将用户的位置信息记录在数据库中。具体来说，数据库将用户的 IMSI 映射到其 IP 地址和正在使用的路径（哪个基站、哪些网关）。"},{"en":"Note that the entire attachment process occurs over control channels. We haven’t assigned any frequencies to the user yet, so the user has to use dedicated control channels to communicate.","zh_html":"注意，整个附着过程通过控制信道进行。我们还没有为用户分配任何频率，所以用户必须使用专用的控制信道进行通信。"},{"en":"Step 3: Data Exchange","zh_html":"第3步：数据交换(Data Exchange)"},{"en":"At this point, the network is configured so that the device can use its IP address to send and receive messages.","zh_html":"此时，网络已配置完成，设备可以使用其 IP 地址发送和接收消息。"},{"en":"How does the cellular network (tower, radio gateway, tower gateway) know how to forward packets? Users are constantly moving, so if we ran a traditional routing algorithm like distance-vector, routes would never converge.","zh_html":"蜂窝网络（基站、无线网关、分组网关）如何知道如何转发分组？用户不断移动，因此如果我们运行传统的路由算法（如距离向量），路由将永远无法收敛(converge)。"},{"en":"Instead, the manager will create a path between the device and the Internet using tunnels. Remember, the packet’s path is from the device, to the tower, to the radio gateway, to the packet gateway.","zh_html":"相反，管理器将使用隧道(tunnels)在设备和互联网之间创建路径。回想一下，分组的路径是从设备到基站，再到无线网关，最后到分组网关。"},{"en":"Conceptually, to implement the tunnel, we’ll tell the tower: If you get a packet from the user, send it this way (into the blue tunnel). On the other side of the wired link, the packets will exit the blue tunnel and arrive at the radio gateway. We’ll then tell the radio gateway: If you get a packet exiting the tunnel, send it this way (into the green tunnel). Packets then travel through the green tunnel and arrive at the packet gateway, who can forward the packet into the Internet.","zh_html":"从概念上讲，为了实现隧道，我们会告诉基站：如果你从用户那里收到一个分组，按此方式发送（进入蓝色隧道）。在有线链路的另一端，分组将从蓝色隧道出来，到达无线网关。然后我们会告诉无线网关：如果你从隧道中收到一个分组，按此方式发送（进入绿色隧道）。分组随后通过绿色隧道到达分组网关，后者可以将分组转发到互联网。"},{"en":"Incoming packets also travel through the tunnels. We tell the packet gateway: If you get a packet bound for User A, send it into the green tunnel (toward the radio gateway). We also tell the radio gateway: If you get a packet exiting the green tunnel, send it into the blue tunnel (toward the tower).","zh_html":"传入的分组也通过隧道传输。我们告诉分组网关：如果你收到一个发往用户 A 的分组，将其发送到绿色隧道（通向无线网关）。我们还告诉无线网关：如果你收到一个从绿色隧道出来的分组，将其发送到蓝色隧道（通向基站）。"},{"en":"Notice that none of the network components are running a routing protocol to find paths. Instead, the manager is telling the routers how to forward packets. Each user will need their own set of tunnels, so the network is storing per-user state (e.g. one table entry for each connected user).","zh_html":"注意，没有网络组件在运行路由协议来寻找路径。相反，管理器直接告诉路由器如何转发分组。每个用户都需要自己的一套隧道，因此网络存储了每用户状态(per-user state)（例如每个已连接用户对应一个表项）。"},{"en":"How do we actually implement these rules? For example, how does the radio gateway know when an incoming packet is coming out of the blue tunnel? We can use encapsulation. When entering a tunnel, we can add a new header, indicating that the packet is traveling through that tunnel (e.g. “this packet is traveling through the blue tunnel”). On the other end, when the packet exits the tunnel, the gateway looks at the extra header and knows which tunnel the packet came from. The gateway can then use this information to decide where to forward the packet next.","zh_html":"我们如何实际实现这些规则？例如，无线网关如何知道传入的分组是从蓝色隧道出来的？我们可以使用封装(encapsulation)。当进入隧道时，我们可以添加一个新首部(header)，指示该分组正在通过该隧道传输（例如“本分组正在通过蓝色隧道传输”）。在另一端，当分组退出隧道时，网关查看这个额外首部，就知道分组来自哪个隧道。网关随后可以利用这一信息决定下一步将分组转发到哪里。"},{"en":"Notice that with tunnels and encapsulation, the routers are never forwarding based on the user’s IP. The user is always moving around, so we can’t use their IP to determine their location. Instead, we have to use these pre-configured tunnels to decide where to forward the packet.","zh_html":"注意，使用隧道和封装后，路由器从不根据用户的 IP 进行转发。用户总是在移动，所以我们不能使用他们的 IP 来确定他们的位置。相反，我们必须使用这些预配置的隧道来决定将分组转发到哪里。"},{"en":"Step 4: Handover","zh_html":"第4步：切换(Handover)"},{"en":"What happens if the user moves from one tower to another? Let’s look at a (slightly simplified) protocol. We’ll call the towers old and new, and move from the old tower to the new tower.","zh_html":"如果用户从一个基站移动到另一个基站会发生什么？让我们来看一个（略微简化的）协议。我们将基站称为“旧基站”和“新基站”，并描述从旧基站切换到新基站的过程。"},{"en":"Your device is constantly measuring its signal strength to the old tower, and reporting that strength to the old tower. At some point, the old tower will say: Your signal strength is too low. Here are some nearby towers (owned by the same operator) and their corresponding control channel frequencies. Can you measure your signal strength to these nearby towers?","zh_html":"你的设备不断测量到旧基站的信号强度，并向旧基站报告该强度。在某个时刻，旧基站会说：你的信号强度太低了。以下是一些附近的基站（由同一运营商运营）及其对应的控制信道频率。你能测量一下到这些基站的信号强度吗？"},{"en":"Your device measures the signal strength to the nearby towers, and reports those values to the old tower. The old tower will pick the best new tower, based on whatever policy the operator wants.","zh_html":"你的设备测量到附近基站的信号强度，并向旧基站报告这些值。旧基站将根据运营商希望采用的策略，选择最佳的新基站。"},{"en":"The old tower tells the new tower: The user is coming your way. This causes the new tower to allocate some frequency resources to the user.","zh_html":"旧基站告诉新基站：这个用户正向你移动。这使得新基站为用户分配一些频率资源。"},{"en":"The new tower tells the old tower which frequency resources have been allocated.","zh_html":"新基站告诉旧基站已经分配了哪些频率资源。"},{"en":"The old tower tells the user: Connect to the new tower, using these frequencies.","zh_html":"旧基站告诉用户：连接到新基站，使用这些频率。"},{"en":"The new tower reports to the mobility manager: I am the new tower for the user. The manager updates its database with the user’s new location. The manager also updates the tunnels to create a new path between the user and the Internet (via a new tower, and also possibly via new radio and packet gateways).","zh_html":"新基站向移动性管理器报告：我是用户的新基站。管理器在数据库中更新用户的新位置。管理器还更新隧道，创建用户和互联网之间的新路径（通过新基站，也可能通过新的无线网关和分组网关）。"},{"en":"Finally, the new tower tells the old tower that handover is complete.","zh_html":"最后，新基站告诉旧基站切换完成。"},{"en":"Why was the handover process so complicated? Remember, we want to give the user seamless communication, with no interruption as they move between towers. This requires cooperation between the user, the old and new towers, the mobility manager, and the gateways.","zh_html":"为什么切换过程如此复杂？回想一下，我们希望给用户提供无缝通信(seamless communication)，在基站间移动时不会中断。这需要用户、新旧基站、移动性管理器和网关之间的协作。"},{"en":"Seamless communication is difficult because the handover process is not atomic. The user is still sending and receiving data while the handover is ongoing. For example, outside servers replying to the user might have sent a bunch of incoming packets to the old tower. During the handoff, the old tower continues to buffer any data it receives for that user. After the handoff, the old tower can send that buffered data to the new tower, which forwards that data to the user. Notice that traditional TCP/IP networks didn’t need to buffer data like this. This type of buffering is a new feature added for seamless handovers as the user moves around.","zh_html":"无缝通信之所以困难，是因为切换过程不是原子性的(atomic)。用户在进行切换时仍在发送和接收数据。例如，回复用户的外部服务器可能已经向旧基站发送了一堆传入分组。在切换期间，旧基站继续缓冲(buffer)它收到的该用户的任何数据。切换完成后，旧基站可以将这些缓冲的数据发送到新基站，新基站再转发给用户。注意，传统 TCP/IP 网络不需要这样缓冲数据。这种缓冲是为了用户移动时实现无缝切换而新增的功能。"},{"en":"Notice that the decisions in this handover process are always made by the operator. The device doesn’t get to choose the next tower to use. The benefit of this design is, it gives the operator more control. For example, if a tower is overloaded, the operator can load-balance and send the user to a different tower. Or, if some users are prioritized over others, the operator can send less-prioritized users to worse towers. The drawback of this design is, it’s a bit slower and requires more round-trips and more complexity.","zh_html":"注意，切换过程中的决策总是由运营商做出。设备不能选择下一个要使用的基站。这种设计的好处是，给运营商更多的控制权。例如，如果一个基站过载，运营商可以进行负载均衡(load-balance)，将用户发送到另一个基站。或者，如果某些用户优先级更高，运营商可以将优先级较低的用户发送到更差的基站。这种设计的缺点是，速度稍慢，需要更多的往返(round-trips)和更高的复杂度。"},{"en":"Notice that the user’s IP address remains unchanged during the handoff. We just updated the tunnels so that packets destined for the user’s IP go through a different path.","zh_html":"注意，用户的 IP 地址在切换过程中保持不变。我们只是更新了隧道，使发往用户 IP 的分组通过不同的路径传输。"},{"en":"Handovers are complicated and require updating the per-user state in the network. If the number of users increases, or users move around really quickly, this protocol encounters scaling challenges. And yet, the modern cellular network works pretty well at scale, because so much work has gone into optimizing these protocols. That’s why the standards specifications are often thousands of pages long!","zh_html":"切换很复杂，需要更新网络中的每用户状态(per-user state)。如果用户数量增加，或者用户移动速度非常快，这个协议就会遇到扩展性挑战(scaling challenges)。然而，现代蜂窝网络在大规模下仍能良好运行，因为大量工作已经投入到优化这些协议上。这就是为什么标准规范往往有数千页之长的原因！"},{"en":"Roaming","zh_html":"漫游(Roaming)"},{"en":"Recall that a user can roam and connect to a different network if they’re visiting another country (or any place where their own operator doesn’t have coverage).","zh_html":"回想一下，用户可以在访问其他国家（或任何其运营商没有覆盖的地方）时漫游(roam)并连接到不同的网络。"},{"en":"The connection process (discovery, attachment, handover) in a visiting network is generally pretty similar to connecting in the home network. The main difference is, the mobility manager in the visitor network must communicate back to the mobility manager in the home network.","zh_html":"在拜访网络(visiting network)中的连接过程（发现、附着、切换）与在归属网络(home network)中连接大致相似。主要区别在于，拜访网络的移动性管理器必须与归属网络的移动性管理器进行通信。"},{"en":"For example, the visitor needs to ask the home for help in authenticating the user (check if the user has paid for roaming). Also, the visitor needs to send tracking data back to the home network, so that the home network knows the user’s location.","zh_html":"例如，拜访网络需要请求归属网络帮助认证用户（检查用户是否已支付漫游费用）。此外，拜访网络需要将跟踪数据发回归属网络，以便归属网络知道用户的位置。"},{"en":"How does the visitor know where the home network is? Remember, during attachment, the device presents its IMSI, and the IMSI contains a Mobile Network Code which identifies the user’s operator.","zh_html":"拜访网络如何知道归属网络在哪里？回想一下，在附着过程中，设备提供其 IMSI，而 IMSI 包含标识用户运营商的移动网络码(Mobile Network Code)。"},{"en":"There are two different approaches to set up tunnels between the user and the Internet.","zh_html":"有两种不同的方法来设置用户和互联网之间的隧道。"},{"en":"In the home routing approach, traffic is tunneled through the home network’s packet gateway. This means that all packets must travel from the visiting network back to the home network, before getting forwarded to the wider Internet. This is beneficial because it lets the home network’s packet gateway can track the user. One drawback is, if you’re a USA-based user, you roam in Germany, and you want to access a website in Germany, your packet must travel from Germany, back to the USA gateway, and then back to Germany.","zh_html":"在归属路由(home routing)方案中，流量通过归属网络的分组网关进行隧道传输。这意味着所有分组必须先途经拜访网络回到归属网络，然后才能转发到更广泛的互联网。这样做的好处是让归属网络的分组网关可以跟踪用户。缺点是，如果你是美国的用户，在德国漫游，想访问一个德国网站，你的分组必须从德国出发，回到美国的网关，然后再返回德国。"},{"en":"In the local breakout approach, traffic is tunneled through the visiting network’s packet gateway. This can shorten the route between the user and the Internet, since packets don’t have to travel all the way back to the home network first. However, this can make accounting for the user’s usage more complicated, since the roaming network must now do the accounting and send the data back to the home network.","zh_html":"在本地分流(local breakout)方案中，流量通过拜访网络的分组网关进行隧道传输。这可以缩短用户和互联网之间的路由，因为分组不必先一路回到归属网络。然而，这会使计费变得更加复杂，因为漫游网络现在必须进行计费，并将数据发回归属网络。"},{"en":"Additional Operations","zh_html":"其他操作(Additional Operations)"},{"en":"We’ve seen some of the key operations in cellular networks, but other operations exist as well.","zh_html":"我们已经了解了蜂窝网络的一些关键操作，但还有其他操作。"},{"en":"Lawful intercept is a legal requirement for all cellular operators. This allows a government with a search warrant to wiretap your connection and listen to the packets you’re sending.","zh_html":"合法拦截(Lawful intercept)是所有蜂窝运营商的法律要求。这允许持有搜查令的政府窃听你的连接，监听你发送的分组。"},{"en":"Stolen phone registries allow a user to report their phone as stolen. Then, if the thief tries to connect your stolen phone to the network, the operator (manager and database) notice that the phone is stolen, and can try to track down the phone. Here, the operator uses the IMEI (the ID number hard-coded into your phone) to identify the specific phone (regardless of the IMSI, the subscriber ID). Devices need to report their IMEI when they connect, allowing the operator to check if the phone is stolen.","zh_html":"被盗手机注册(Stolen phone registries)允许用户将手机报告为被盗。然后，如果小偷试图将你被盗的手机连接到网络，运营商（管理器和数据库）会发现手机被盗，并可以尝试追踪该手机。这里，运营商使用 IMEI（硬编码到你手机中的 ID 号）来识别特定手机（与 IMSI 即用户标识无关）。设备在连接时需要报告其 IMEI，以便运营商检查手机是否被盗。"},{"en":"These additional operations are possible because the operator has centralized control, keeping track of all the users and their locations.","zh_html":"这些额外操作之所以可能，是因为运营商拥有集中控制权(centralized control)，可以跟踪所有用户及其位置。"},{"en":"Cellular Network Design Reflections","zh_html":"蜂窝网络设计反思"},{"en":"As we noted earlier, cellular networks have different fundamental goals and design choices, compared to the traditional Internet. For example, we saw that authentication and accounting are central goals of the cellular network, even though these were not goals in the traditional Internet. We also saw that allocation is based on reservations, and the network maintains per-user state that is dynamically changing.","zh_html":"正如我们之前指出的，蜂窝网络与传统互联网相比有着不同的基本目标和设计选择。例如，我们看到认证(authentication)和计费(accounting)是蜂窝网络的核心目标，即使这些不是传统互联网的目标。我们还看到分配基于预留(reservations)，网络维护着动态变化的每用户状态(per-user state)。"},{"en":"Using stateful reservation-based networks increased the complexity of our network. The various components had to constantly reconfigure tunnels as the user moved around.","zh_html":"使用基于预留的状态化(stateful)网络增加了网络的复杂度。各组件必须随着用户移动不断重新配置隧道。"},{"en":"Let’s think about some possible alternate designs. Recall that handover was complicated because we wanted the user to keep the same IP address as they moved around. What if we instead changed the user’s IP address on each handover? Now, the IP addresses actually reflect the user’s location, and we could use traditional routing protocols again. However, higher-level protocols like TCP and HTTP will break. Remember, TCP relies on the two connecting users keep the same IP address.","zh_html":"让我们思考一些可能的替代设计。回想一下，切换之所以复杂，是因为我们希望用户移动时保持相同的 IP 地址。如果我们改为在每次切换时更改用户的 IP 地址呢？这样，IP 地址实际上反映了用户的位置，我们可以再次使用传统的路由协议。然而，TCP 和 HTTP 等高层协议将会被破坏。回想一下，TCP 依赖于通信双方保持相同的 IP 地址。"},{"en":"Using the same IP address increases complexity, but changing IP addresses breaks TCP. One possible solution is to use a different transport-level protocol that allows changing IP addresses, like QUIC (developed at Google). Then, even though the IP addresses are changing, we can use the flow label field in the IPv6 header to label all the packets in a flow.","zh_html":"使用相同的 IP 地址增加了复杂度，但更改 IP 地址会破坏 TCP。一个可能的解决方案是使用允许更改 IP 地址的不同传输层协议(transport-level protocol)，例如 QUIC（由 Google 开发）。这样，即使 IP 地址在变化，我们也可以使用 IPv6 首部中的流标签(flow label)字段来标记一个流中的所有分组。"}]},"/wireless/index.html":{"title":"index","blocks":[{"en":"Wireless Links","en_html":"<a href=\"wireless-links.html\">Wireless Links</a>","zh_html":"无线链路(Wireless Links)"},{"en":"Cellular","en_html":"<a href=\"cellular.html\">Cellular</a>","zh_html":"蜂窝网络(Cellular)"}]},"/wireless/wireless-links.html":{"blocks":[{"en":"Wireless Links","zh_html":"无线链路(Wireless Links)"},{"en":"Introduction to Wireless Technologies","zh_html":"无线技术简介"},{"en":"Wireless communication technologies actually predate the Internet. In the 1880s, the photophone (Bell and Tainter) attempted to send data wireless using a light beam. In the 1890s, the wireless telegraph (Marconi) attempted to send data using radio waves. Also in the 1890s, experiments with millimeter waves (Bose) were attempted, and today, millimeter wave is becoming an active area of research again.","zh_html":"无线通信技术(wireless communication technologies)实际上早于互联网(Internet)。在1880年代，光电话(photophone，由 Bell 和 Tainter 发明)尝试使用光束无线发送数据。在1890年代，无线电报(wireless telegraph，由 Marconi 发明)尝试使用无线电波发送数据。同样在1890年代，毫米波(millimeter waves，由 Bose 实验)的实验也开始了，而今天，毫米波再次成为一个活跃的研究方向。"},{"en":"Conceptually, you might imagine that wireless communication consists of invisible particles traveling along an imaginary link from point A to point B, but that’s not actually very accurate. In reality, wireless communication is more like ripples on a pond. When you transmit data wirelessly, you create ripples that propagate outward and weaken over distance. If others are also transmitting data, the ripples can constructively and destructively interfere with each. The ripples can also reflect or refract against objects like boats on the pond, or the edge of the pond.","zh_html":"从概念上讲，你可能认为无线通信是由不可见的粒子沿着想象中的链路从 A 点传播到 B 点，但这其实并不准确。实际上，无线通信更像是池塘中的涟漪。当你无线传输数据时，你会产生向外传播并随距离减弱的涟漪。如果其他人也在传输数据，涟漪之间会发生建设性或破坏性干扰。涟漪还会在遇到物体（如池塘中的船或池塘边缘）时发生反射或折射。"},{"en":"In this section, we’ll look at four key differences between wired and wireless communications. The differences mostly affect Layer 1 (physical) and Layer 2 (link), with a few exceptions that we’ll look at elater (notably, breaking the end-to-end principle and implementing reliability at Layer 2 for performance).","zh_html":"在本节中，我们将研究有线通信(wired)和无线通信(wireless)之间的四个关键区别。这些区别主要影响第一层（物理层，Layer 1）和第二层（链路层，Layer 2），但也有少数例外，我们稍后会看到（特别是打破端到端原则(end-to-end principle)，在链路层实现可靠性以提升性能）。"},{"en":"Difference 1: Wireless is a fundamentally shared medium. Wired is not.","zh_html":"区别1：无线从根本上说是共享介质(shared medium)。有线则不是。"},{"en":"Difference 2: Wireless signals get weaker over longer distances. Wired signals do not.","zh_html":"区别2：无线信号随距离增加而减弱。有线信号则不会。"},{"en":"Difference 3: Wireless environments can change rapidly. Wired environments do not.","zh_html":"区别3：无线环境可能快速变化。有线环境则不会。"},{"en":"Difference 4: Packet collisions are much harder to detect in wireless systems.","zh_html":"区别4：在无线系统中，分组碰撞(collisions)更难检测。"},{"en":"Difference: Wireless is a Shared Medium","zh_html":"区别：无线是一种共享介质(Shared Medium)"},{"en":"Difference 1: Wireless is a fundamentally shared medium. Wired is not.","zh_html":"区别1：无线从根本上说是共享介质(shared medium)。有线则不是。"},{"en":"Wired links are private (point-to-point) by default. Intuitively, a wire connects two devices. Creating a multi-point bus, where a single wire is connected to many devices, requires extra work. It’s difficult for external signals to interfere with the signal on the wire (e.g. we can wrap a shield around the wire). Along the wire, we use electrical signals to transmit data (e.g. high voltage is 1, low voltage is 0).","zh_html":"有线链路(wired links)默认是私有的点对点(point-to-point)连接。直观地说，一根线缆连接两台设备。创建多点总线(multi-point bus)是可能的，但在现代网络中通常不这样做，除非是在单个设备内部（如主板）。"},{"en":"Wireless links have the opposite properties. By default, wireless links are shared. Intuitively, if you transmit a signal, the signal radiates outwards in all directions. Creating a private point-to-point link between two hosts requires extra work. It’s difficult to shield a signal from external interference. Instead of electrical signals, we encode bits using radio waves to transmit data.","zh_html":"无线链路(wireless links)具有相反的特性。默认情况下，无线链路是共享的。直观地说，如果你发送一个信号，覆盖范围内的任何人都可以听到。这意味着发射器(transmitter)覆盖范围内的所有设备共享该介质。类似地，如果有多个发射器，它们可能相互干扰。"},{"en":"Encoding Data over Wireless Link","zh_html":"在无线链路上编码数据"},{"en":"How do we encode data into electromagnetic waves at Layer 1? We could just take our sequence of 1s and 0s and draw it as a wave, but the resulting wave is probably low-frequency, and it turns out a low-frequency signal is weak and difficult to transmit.","zh_html":"我们如何在第一层(Layer 1)将数据编码到电磁波(electromagnetic waves)中？我们可以直接把 1 和 0 的序列画成方波(square wave)，然后将该方波作为电磁波发送。然而，低频方波传播效果不好。实际上，发射天线需要非常大（长达几公里！）才能以低频传输。"},{"en":"Instead, we have to use modulation to transmit our data. We start with the carrier signal, which is just a constant-frequency wave (e.g. a sine wave). This wave carries no information, but it’s high-frequency, so it’s much easier to transmit. Then, we impose our data signal (also called the modulation signal) on top of the carrier signal. The resulting wave is high-frequency (easy to transmit), and also contains the data we want to send! Note that the receiver will need to take the modulated waveform and re-extract the 1s and 0s out of that waveform.","zh_html":"相反，我们必须使用调制(modulation)来传输数据。我们从载波信号(carrier signal)开始，它只是一个恒定频率的波。然后，我们将数据调制到载波信号之上，根据要发送的信号改变载波波的某些属性。"},{"en":"There are several strategies for modulating our data signal on top of the carrier signal. In amplitude modulation (AM), we vary the height of the carrier signal based on the input signal. To transmit a 1, make the sine wave tall, and to transmit a 0, make the sine wave short. In frequency modulation (FM), we vary the frequency (width) of the carrier signal based on the input signal. To transmit a 1, make the sine wave skinny (higher-frequency), and to transmit a 0, make the sine wave fat (lower-frequency). Other more complex modulation strategies exist, such as phase modulation, or a combination of amplitude and phase modulation.","zh_html":"有几种将数据信号调制到载波信号上的策略。在调幅(amplitude modulation, AM)中，我们改变载波波的幅度（高度）。在调频(frequency modulation, FM)中，我们改变载波波的频率。在调相(phase modulation)中，我们改变载波波的相位。"},{"en":"Noise and Interference","zh_html":"噪声和干扰(Noise and Interference)"},{"en":"Because wireless is a shared medium, we need to deal with noise and interference, which can corrupt the received signal. Noise always exists, even if nobody else nearby is transmitting data. (As an analogy, even if nobody around you is talking, there’s still ambient noise from nature.) This ambient background noise is called the noise floor. By contrast, interference refers to other transmitters intentionally sending signals that interfere with our signal.","zh_html":"由于无线是共享介质，我们需要处理噪声(noise)和干扰(interference)，它们可能破坏接收到的信号。"},{"en":"SINR (Signal to Interference and Noise Ratio) is a metric we can use to measure the quality of a wireless connection at the receiver. As the name implies, SINR is the power of the signal, divided by the power of the interference plus noise.","zh_html":"SINR（信号与干扰加噪声比，Signal to Interference and Noise Ratio）是我们可以用来衡量接收端无线连接质量的指标。直观地说，SINR 越高，连接质量越好。SINR 的计算公式为："},{"en":"SINR is a dimensionless quantity, since it’s a ratio of two numbers. It can also be expressed in terms of decibels (dB), which is a logarithmic way to measure a ratio. At 0 dB, the ratio is 1, and when the SINR increases by 10 dB, the underlying ratio is 10 times greater (e.g. signal is 10 times more powerful, or noise/interference is 10 times weaker).","zh_html":"SINR 是一个无量纲的量，因为它是两个数的比值。它也可以用分贝(decibels, dB)表示，这是一种对数尺度。这里，\\(P\\) 是我们接收到的信号功率，\\(I\\) 是干扰功率，\\(N\\) 是噪声功率。"},{"en":"What does this equation tell us? It tells us that if there’s more noise, we have to transmit the signal with more power. It’s also possible to employ coding gain (think: error-correcting codes), so that even if the signal is weak and gets mixed in with noise and interference, we’re sending the signal with enough redundancy to allow the receiver to re-extract the signal.","zh_html":"这个公式告诉我们什么？它告诉我们，如果噪声更大，我们需要用更大的功率发送信号。或者，如果干扰更强，我们也需要用更大的功率发送。直观地说，要在嘈杂的房间里被听到，你需要更大声说话。或者，如果你离某人很近但离另一个人很远，近处的人发出的信号可能干扰远处的人发来的信号。"},{"en":"The Shannon capacity gives us a theoretical limit on how much data per unit time we can send along a channel, given the amount of noise and interference along that channel. The equation works not just for wireless links, but also other types of links (e.g. wires).","zh_html":"香农容量(Shannon capacity)给出了在给定带宽(bandwidth)和 SINR 的条件下，信道上每单位时间最多可以发送多少数据的理论极限。香农容量的公式为："},{"en":"In this equation, \\(B\\) is the bandwidth of the channel. \\(\\text{SINR}\\) is the signal-to-interference-and-noise ratio. \\(C\\) is the theoretical limit of how much data per unit time we can send along this channel, measured in bits per second. Note that in this equation, bandwidth is measured as the difference between the highest frequency and the lowest frequency that the receiver understands.","zh_html":"在这个公式中，\\(B\\) 是信道的带宽。\\(\\text{SINR}\\) 是信号与干扰加噪声比。该公式告诉我们信道容量(channel capacity) \\(C\\)，单位为比特每秒(bps)。"},{"en":"What does this equation tell us? It tells us that as bandwidth increases, we can send more data per unit time. It also tells us that as the SINR increases (stronger signal, or less noise), we can send more data per unit time. If we need a link with a specific target capacity (e.g. 1 Mbps), we can plug in the physical characteristics of our link into this equation to see if our link meets the desired capacity.","zh_html":"这个公式告诉我们什么？它告诉我们，带宽增加时，单位时间可以发送更多数据。它还告诉我们，SINR 增加时，单位时间也可以发送更多数据。"},{"en":"As an example, consider the plain old telephone system. This system has 3 kHz bandwidth, which means that telephones understand frequencies between 300 Hz and 3300 Hz. Also, this system has a SINR of roughly 20 dB, which translates to a ratio of 100 (0 db = 1x, 10 dB = 10x, 20 dB = 100x, 30 dB = 1000x, etc.). Plugging these values into our equation, we get that \\(C = 4000 \\cdot \\log_2(1 + 100) \\approx 20000\\), which tells us that the telephone system can transmit roughly 20 kbps (kilobits per second).","zh_html":"举个例子，考虑传统电话系统(plain old telephone system)。该系统的带宽为 3 kHz，这意味着电话在理想条件下可以以 50-60 kbps 传输数据。实际上，电话被限制在 56 kbps 或更低，因为 SINR 可能不理想。"},{"en":"Difference: Attenuation","zh_html":"区别：衰减(Attenuation)"},{"en":"Wireless signals get significantly weaker over longer distances. By contrast, wired signals do get slightly weaker over distance, but the effect is far smaller. In wireless systems, our design must account for attenuating signals, whereas in wired systems, attenuation is usually not a key design concern.","zh_html":"无线信号随距离增加会显著减弱。相比之下，有线信号在长距离上确实也会略微减弱（例如以太网电缆限于100米），但与无线信号在相同距离上的信号衰减相比，这可以忽略不计。"},{"en":"This creates a fundamental trade-off when designing wireless systems. We want to maximize performance by making our link accurate, fast, and long-range. But, we also want to minimize our resource use by conserving energy (e.g. laptop power) and using less of the frequency spectrum (which can be expensive to reserve). Unfortunately, a better signal requires more power or more frequency bandwidth.","zh_html":"这就在设计无线系统时产生了一个基本权衡(trade-off)。我们希望增强链路强度（更多比特每秒）以最大化性能。但当链路更强时，它对附近其他链路的干扰也会更大（因为每个人都共享介质）。因此，性能和干扰之间存在权衡。"},{"en":"Free Space Model","zh_html":"自由空间模型(Free Space Model)"},{"en":"One simple way to model signal attenuation is the free-space model (also known as the line-of-sight model), where we assume that the transmitter and receiver exist in a totally empty environment. Signals radiate outwards in all directions, with no obstacles (not even the Earth’s surface).","zh_html":"一种简单的信号衰减建模方法是自由空间模型(free-space model，也称为视距模型(line-of-sight model))，它假设发射器和接收器之间没有任何障碍物。"},{"en":"In this model, the power of the signal is inversely proportional to the distance between the transmitter and receiver. This is due to the inverse-square law:","zh_html":"在这个模型中，信号功率与发射器和接收器之间的距离成反比。这种关系常被称为平方反比律(inverse-square law)，在物理学中许多类型的辐射信号都很常见："},{"en":"In this equation, \\(P_r\\) is the power at the receiver, \\(P_t\\) is the power at the transmitter, and \\(d\\) is the distance between the transmitter and receiver. If we double the distance, the signal at the receiver is \\(1/4\\) as strong. If the distance is 10 times larger, the signal at the receiver is \\(1/100\\) as strong.","zh_html":"在这个公式中，\\(P_r\\) 是接收端功率，\\(P_t\\) 是发射端功率，\\(d\\) 是发射器和接收器之间的距离。"},{"en":"Intuitively, the inverse-square law applies here because the signal is radiating outwards in all directions. At any instant, the signal has radiated out to a sphere around the transmitter, and the sphere grows as the signal radiates further outwards. The surface area of a sphere with radius \\(r\\) is \\(4\\pi r^2\\), so as the signal propagates out, it’s spread out over an area that grows quadratically (with the square of the distance). For exapmle, when the distance doubles, the resulting sphere has 4 times larger surface area. Therefore, the signal is spread out over an area that’s 4 times larger, so the signal is \\(1/4\\) as strong.","zh_html":"直观地说，平方反比律在这里适用，因为信号向所有方向向外辐射。在任何时刻，信号能量均匀分布在半径为 \\(d\\) 的球面上。由于球体的表面积为 \\(4\\pi d^2\\)，接收端的功率与 \\(1/d^2\\) 成正比。"},{"en":"Besides the distance, we also need to consider the antennas being used by the transmitter and receiver. This leads us to the Friis equation for measuring signal strength across a distance:","zh_html":"除了距离，我们还需要考虑发射器和接收器使用的天线(antennas)。这就引出了弗里斯公式(Friis equation)："},{"en":"In this equation, as before, \\(P_r\\) is the power at the receiver, and \\(P_t\\) is the power at the transmitter. \\(G_t\\) is the gain at the transmitter, and \\(G_r\\) is the gain at the receiver. \\(\\lambda\\) is the wavelength, and it’s used in this equation to represent the area of the antenna. \\(d\\) represents the distance between the antennas.","zh_html":"在这个公式中，与之前一样，\\(P_r\\) 是接收端功率，\\(P_t\\) 是发射端功率。\\(G_t\\) 和 \\(G_r\\) 分别是发射器和接收器的天线增益(antenna gains)。\\(\\lambda\\) 是信号的波长。\\(d\\) 是距离。"},{"en":"What does this equation tell us? To compute the signal strength at the receiver, we start with the signal strength at the transmitter, \\(P_t\\). Then, we multiply by the gains of the two antennas, \\(G_t\\) and \\(G_r\\). Intuitively, a higher gain means that the antenna is better at sending or receiving signals.","zh_html":"这个公式告诉我们什么？要计算接收端的信号强度，我们从发射端的信号强度开始（这很合理）。然后我们加上发射天线增益，它放大了发送出去的信号。我们还加上接收天线增益，它放大了接收到的信号。"},{"en":"As we saw earlier, distance affects signal strength according to the inverse-square law, which explains the \\(\\frac{1}{4\\pi d^2}\\) term.","zh_html":"正如我们之前看到的，距离根据平方反比律影响信号强度，这解释了公式中的 \\(\\frac{1}{4\\pi d^2}\\) 项。对 \\(4\\pi d^2\\) 的直观理解来自球体的表面积。"},{"en":"Finally, the \\(\\frac{\\lambda^2}{4\\pi}\\) term relates to the aperture (think of it like area) of the receiver antenna. Intuitively, if you shine a light on a piece of paper, the light will hit that paper. If you use a larger sheet of paper, more light will hit the paper. The effective aperture (think: area) of the antenna can be computed as \\(\\frac{\\lambda^2}{4\\pi}\\), though we won’t prove it here. Note that the \\((4\\pi)^2\\) in the equation actually comes from two factors of \\(4\\pi\\), one from the inverse-square law and one from the effective aperture equation.","zh_html":"最后，\\(\\frac{\\lambda^2}{4\\pi}\\) 项与接收天线的孔径(aperture，可以想象成面积)有关。直观地说，更大的天线可以捕获更多的信号能量。你家中常见的 Wi-Fi 路由器天线大约10厘米长。你的手机天线要小得多。"},{"en":"% paper analogy from here: https://www.cdt21.com/design_guide/friis-equation-and-antenna-effective-area/#What_is_the_effective_area_of_an_antenna","zh_html":"% 纸张类比来自：https://www.cdt21.com/design_guide/friis-equation-and-antenna-effective-area/#What_is_the_eff"},{"en":"We can also rewrite the Friis equation by dividing both sides by \\(P_t\\):","zh_html":"我们还可以通过两边除以 \\(P_t\\) 来重写弗里斯公式："},{"en":"What does this equation tell us? The relative signal strength at the receiver (e.g. half as strong, or \\(1/100\\) as strong, as the signal strength at the transmitter) is a function of the antenna gains, the inverse of the square of the distance, and the effective aperture (think: area) of the antenna.","zh_html":"这个公式告诉我们什么？接收端的相对信号强度（例如一半强度，或 \\(1/100\\) 强度）取决于两个天线的增益、天线之间的距离和信号的波长。该比值与发射器功率水平无关。"},{"en":"Yet another way to rewrite the same Friis equation is to take the log of both sides, allowing us to express the power and gain in terms of decibels:","zh_html":"另一种重写弗里斯公式的方法是取两边的对数，使我们可以用分贝(dB，对数尺度)表示功率和增益："},{"en":"The free space model is a useful theoretical model to measure the ideal signal strength at the receiver, though in practice, physical obstacles (e.g. the Earth’s surface) prevent us from achieving this ideal value.","zh_html":"自由空间模型是一个有用的理论模型，用于衡量接收端的理想信号强度，尽管在实际中，信号还会受到其他因素的影响。"},{"en":"Link Budget","zh_html":"链路预算(Link Budget)"},{"en":"If signals get weaker over distance, how do we know if a link will actually work? In other words, how do we know if the receiver will actually detect an intelligible signal?","zh_html":"如果信号随距离减弱，我们如何知道一条链路是否真的能工作？换句话说，我们如何知道接收端收到的信号是否足够强，使接收器能够实际听到并正确解读该信号？"},{"en":"To measure if a link is viable, we can compute a link budget, which accounts for all gains and losses along the link.","zh_html":"为了衡量一条链路是否可行，我们可以计算链路预算(link budget)，它考虑了链路上的所有增益和损耗。"},{"en":"In this equation, \\(P_r\\) is the signal power at the receiver, and \\(P_t\\) is the signal power at the sender. All gains (e.g. a stronger antenna gain) add to our link budget, and all losses (e.g. path loss from long distance) cost us link budget.","zh_html":"在这个公式中，\\(P_r\\) 是接收端的信号功率，\\(P_t\\) 是发送端的信号功率。所有增益相加，所有损耗（例如介质中的衰减、连接器损耗）相减。"},{"en":"Adding all gains and subtracting all losses tells us the signal strength at the receiver. We can compare this against the sensitivity of the receiver, which is the signal strength needed for the receiver to extract useful information. This comparison tells us our link budget. If the overall budget ends up positive, then this is a viable link, and we’re in the money. If the overall budget ends up negative, then this is not a viable link, and we’re in trouble.","zh_html":"将所有增益相加并减去所有损耗，就得到了接收端的信号强度。我们可以将其与接收器灵敏度(receiver sensitivity)进行比较：这是接收器能够成功接收和解码的最小信号强度。"},{"en":"Notice that the link budget is computed in decibels, which are logarithmic. This allows us to use addition and subtraction instead of multiplication and division. For example, a gain of 1000x power is represented by adding 30 decibels, and a loss down to 1% of power is represented by subtracting 20 decibels.","zh_html":"注意，链路预算是用分贝(dB)计算的，这是对数尺度。这使我们能够使用加法和减法代替乘法和除法。"},{"en":"Here’s an example of computing the link budget. The signal power at the transmitter is 10 dB. The signal travels along a cable, a lightning arrestor (you don’t have to know what this is), and another cable, losing 0.44 dB, 0.1 dB, and 2.21 dB along the way. Then, the signal is broadcast on an antenna, which gives us a 25 dB increase. Then, the signal travels across 10 kilometers of space, losing 120 dB along the way. Then, the signal is received by an antenna, giving us a 25 dB increase. Then, the signal travels along some more cables, losing 0.44 dB, 0.1 dB, and 2.21 dB, before finally reaching the receiver. If we add up all the gains and subtract all the losses, we can compute that the signal strength at the receiver is -65.5 dB.","zh_html":"下面是一个计算链路预算的例子。发射端的信号功率为 10 dB。信号沿路径传播，路径损耗为 -70 dB。发射器和接收器各有 3 dB 增益的天线。因此，接收端的信号强度为 \\(10 - 70 + 3 + 3 = -54\\) dB。"},{"en":"We can now compare this signal strength against the receiver sensitivity, which is -80 dB. This tells us that the receiver can pick up any signals above -80 dB. Since -65.5 dB is above -80 dB, our link budget is positive, and our link should work!","zh_html":"现在我们可以将此信号强度与接收器灵敏度进行比较，接收器灵敏度为 -80 dB。这表明接收器确实可以听到该信号。"},{"en":"The link margin is the difference between the signal strength at the receiver, and the receiver sensitivity. If we received a 30 dB signal, and our sensitivity lets us detect anything over 10 dB, we have a link margin of 20 dB. In the example from before, our link margin was 14.5 dB.","zh_html":"链路余量(link margin)是接收端的信号强度与接收器灵敏度之差。如果我们收到的信号为 -54 dB，接收器灵敏度为 -80 dB，则链路余量为 \\((-54) - (-80) = 26\\) dB。"},{"en":"The link margin tells us about the quality of our link. If the link margin is negative, the link won’t work, and the signals won’t be received. A higher link margin is good because it means our signal is more reliable and more robust to interference and other issues.","zh_html":"链路余量告诉我们链路的质量。如果链路余量为负，链路无法工作，信号强度太弱。如果链路余量为正，链路可以工作（假设没有其他问题）。较大的正链路余量意味着链路质量超出了需求，这意味着我们有余地降低发射功率，从而减少干扰。"},{"en":"Difference: Environments Change","zh_html":"区别：环境变化(Environments Change)"},{"en":"Wireless environments can change rapidly. The devices can move around. The environment could change (e.g. a physical obstacle moves in between the devices). Other communications could start interfering with our communication.","zh_html":"无线环境可能快速变化。设备可以移动。环境可能改变（例如物理障碍物移动到发送器和接收器之间）。所有这些变化都会影响无线链路的质量。"},{"en":"In the free-space model from earlier, we set the distance between the devices, \\(d\\), to be a constant. But what if the devices are moving? Also, we assumed there were no obstacles and no interfering signals in the environment. How does our model change in the presence of these factors?","zh_html":"在之前的自由空间模型中，我们将设备之间的距离 \\(d\\) 设为常数。但如果距离改变呢？如果距离变化10倍，信号强度变化100倍。"},{"en":"In the free-space model, assuming the antennas stay the same (same gain, same aperture), we got a nice, smooth graph where signal strength decreased as distance increased. After accounting for a changing environment, the resulting graph of distance vs. signal strength is much more wobbly.","zh_html":"在自由空间模型中，假设天线保持不变（相同的增益、相同的孔径），我们得到了一个漂亮平滑的曲线，信号强度随距离增加而平滑衰减。但在现实中，曲线要复杂得多："},{"en":"This graph is actually the sum of three smaller graphs. Each one shows how a different characteristic of the environment affects the signal strength, as a function of distance. Notice that some characteristics change slowly as distance increases, while others change rapidly and erratically as distance increases.","zh_html":"这条曲线实际上是三个更小曲线的总和。每个曲线展示了环境的不同特性如何影响信号。"},{"en":"The first characteristic is free-space path loss. We’ve already seen this from the free-space model, which shows us that the signal strength decreases slowly and consistently over longer distances, according to the inverse-square loss.","zh_html":"第一个特性是自由空间路径损耗(free-space path loss)。我们已经从自由空间模型中看到了这一点，它显示信号强度随距离增加而降低（平滑的大尺度趋势）。但还有另外两个特性。"},{"en":"The second characteristic is shadowing. This occurs when physical obstacles between the transmitter and the receiver block the signal. The signal must now be refracted or reflected to get around the obstacle, and the resulting signal at the receiver ends up weaker.","zh_html":"第二个特性是阴影效应(shadowing)。当发射器和接收器之间的物理障碍物阻挡信号时（例如走到一个大型金属文件柜后面），就会发生阴影效应。阴影效应可能降低信号强度，有时降低10到100倍。"},{"en":"Depending on where the obstacles are located, the signal could get weaker or stronger as distance increases. For example, if I walk in front of a building, the signal will get a lot weaker, but if I eventually walk past the building, the signal might get stronger again.","zh_html":"根据障碍物的位置，信号可能随距离增加而变弱或变强。例如，想象你走到一个大型金属文件柜后面。当你从文件柜后面走出来时，信号变强。但是，当你继续行走时，你离发射器越来越远，信号又变弱了。总体趋势是信号随距离增加而变弱，但曲线完全不光滑。"},{"en":"The third characteristic is multipath fading. This occurs when waves reflect and refract on physical obstacles, which causes offset versions of the signal to arrive at the receiver. In particular, if a signal takes different paths of different lengths to reach the receiver, the signals might arrive out-of-phase with each other, causing interference.","zh_html":"第三个特性是多径衰落(multipath fading)。当波在物理障碍物上反射和折射时发生，这可能导致同一个信号沿多条路径到达接收器。在接收器处，同一信号的多个副本可能发生建设性或破坏性干扰。"},{"en":"Multipath fading can cause very fine-grained changes in the signal strength. Changing the distance just a little bit might cause the signal strength to get stronger or weaker.","zh_html":"多径衰落可能导致信号强度发生非常精细的变化。距离的微小变化可能改变信号在接收器处的干涉方式。如果信号正在发生破坏性干扰，移动几厘米（改变信号之间的相位关系）可能使其转为建设性干扰，从而使接收信号强得多。"},{"en":"Ultimately, if we want to consider how all three characteristics together affect signal strength over various distances, we have to look at the sum of the three graphs. If the sender and receiver stayed stationary, the signal strength would be a specific point on this graph. However, if the devices are moving, then the signal strength travels along this curve. Also, if the environment changes and obstacles enter and leave, then the graph itself would change as well.","zh_html":"最终，如果我们想考虑所有三个特性如何共同影响不同距离上的信号强度，我们必须使用比自由空间模型复杂得多的模型。"},{"en":"Approximating Path Loss","zh_html":"近似路径损耗(Approximating Path Loss)"},{"en":"It can be difficult to approximate path loss (from free-space loss, shadowing, and multipath fading). This is especially difficult in the presence of obstacles that result in a signal taking multiple paths, causing out-of-phase signals to interfere with each other at the receiver.","zh_html":"近似路径损耗(path loss)（来自自由空间损耗、阴影效应和多径衰落）可能很困难。对于移动设备来说尤其棘手，因为环境可能在不断变化。"},{"en":"One relatively simple model for approximating path loss is the two-ray model. In this model, we assume that the signal travels along only two paths: one line-of-sight path directly from the sender to the receiver, and one ground-bounce path that reflects off the ground to the receiver. Remember, this is still one signal radiating from the transmitter, but some waves directly reach the receiver, while others bounce off the ground to the receiver.","zh_html":"一种相对简单的近似路径损耗的模型是双射线模型(two-ray model)。在这个模型中，我们假设信号沿两条可能的路径从发送器到达接收器：一条直接路径（视距），以及第二条信号通过地面反射的路径（地面反射，ground reflection）。"},{"en":"If the sender and receiver are far enough apart, the waves from the two paths will be 180 degrees out of phase. As a result, the waves from the two paths will destructively interfere and cancel out, significantly weakening the signal at the receiver. When this happens, the signal strength is no longer proportional to \\(1/d^2\\), but instead is proportional to \\(1/d^4\\). In other words, signal strength now falls off much faster as the distance increases.","zh_html":"如果发送器和接收器相距足够远，两条路径的波将相位相差180度。结果是两个信号发生破坏性干扰。接收器获得的功率远低于自由空间模型的预测。在双射线模型中，路径损耗与 \\(1/d^4\\) 成正比，这比自由空间模型中 \\(1/d^2\\) 的路径损耗严重得多。"},{"en":"Remember, our free-space model assumed no obstacles (not even Earth’s surface), which is why we derived that signal strength is proportional to \\(1/d^2\\). In the two-ray model, accounting for Earth’s surface causes signal strength to now be proportional to \\(1/d^4\\).","zh_html":"回想一下，我们的自由空间模型假设没有障碍物（甚至没有地球表面），这就是为什么我们推导出信号强度按 \\(1/d^2\\) 比例衰减。双射线模型是一个稍微更现实的模型，它包含了地面，但仍然是一个简化（它忽略了其他物理障碍物）。"},{"en":"What if there are additional obstacles besides Earth’s surface? The two-ray model doesn’t account for those. In more complicated environments, we can create general ray tracing models, which account for signals being reflected, scattered, and diffracted. These models require specific information about the environment (e.g. where the obstacles are), and can be built using computer simulations. In these models, reflected versions of the signal usually dominate the signal, compared to the unobstructed line-of-sight version of the signal.","zh_html":"如果除了地球表面之外还有其他障碍物呢？双射线模型没有考虑这些。在更复杂的环境中（例如室内有很多障碍物），路径损耗指数可能在2到4之间，而经验导出的模型可能更有用。"},{"en":"From these models, we can derive a simplified path loss model to relate distance and signal strength:","zh_html":"从这些模型出发，我们可以推导出一个简化的路径损耗模型，将距离和信号强度联系起来："},{"en":"In this equation, as before, \\(P_r\\) and \\(P_t\\) represent the receiver signal power and the transmitter signal power, and \\(d\\) represents the distance.","zh_html":"在这个公式中，与之前一样，\\(P_r\\) 和 \\(P_t\\) 分别表示接收信号功率和发射信号功率，\\(d\\) 是距离。"},{"en":"\\(K\\) and \\(\\gamma\\) are empirically-determined constants, based on the environment and the model. For example, if there are lots of inconveniently-placed obstacles, \\(K\\) might be really small, causing the receiver signal strength to be weak.","zh_html":"\\(K\\) 和 \\(\\gamma\\) 是根据环境和模型经验确定的常数。例如，如果障碍物较少（更接近自由空间），我们预计 \\(K\\) 更大（信号更强），\\(\\gamma\\) 更小（随距离衰减更少）。如果障碍物很多，\\(K\\) 将更小，\\(\\gamma\\) 将更大。"},{"en":"In practice, \\(\\gamma\\) is between 2 and 8. In the best case, signal strength is proportional to \\(1/d^2\\), similar to the free space model. In the worst case, signal strength is proportional to \\(1/d^8\\), and the signal gets weaker much more rapidly as you move further away.","zh_html":"在实践中，\\(\\gamma\\) 在2到8之间。在最好的情况下，信号强度与 \\(1/d^2\\) 成正比，类似于自由空间模型。在最坏的情况下，信号强度与 \\(1/d^8\\) 成正比，随距离衰减极快。"},{"en":"Difference: Detecting Collisions","zh_html":"区别：检测碰撞(Detecting Collisions)"},{"en":"Wired collisions are often easy to detect. On a point-to-point link, they might not happen at all. We can usually detect collisions just by sensing the wire. There can be issues with propagation delay, but ultimately, there’s just one signal on the wire that we have to sense.","zh_html":"有线碰撞通常很容易检测。在点对点链路上，可能根本不会发生碰撞。我们通常可以通过在发送的同时监听来检测碰撞（如果听到的内容与发送的内容不同，就发生了碰撞）。"},{"en":"By contrast, wireless collisions are much harder to detect, because there is now a spatial aspect to collisions. Waves might collide in one place, but not another.","zh_html":"相比之下，无线碰撞要难检测得多，因为碰撞现在有了空间方面的因素。波可能在接收器处发生碰撞，即使发射器听不到彼此的传输信号。"},{"en":"Designing collision detection and collision avoidance is much harder in a wireless system, but it’s still necessary so that multiple devices can send over the shared medium. Recall that there are many different approaches to multiple access, including fixed allocations of frequencies, and coordinating who’s sending at what times. Which approach works best depends on your environment. For example, if you’re in the middle of nowhere, it might be okay to just let collisions happen and deal with them when they do. In this section, though, we’ll focus on the CSMA (Carrier Sense Multiple Access) approach, where you listen for signals and don’t transmit if someone else is talking.","zh_html":"在无线系统中设计碰撞检测(collision detection)和碰撞避免(collision avoidance)要困难得多，但这仍然必要，以便多个发送者可以共享同一介质。"},{"en":"In this section, for simplicity, we’ll ignore obstacles, which means that signals radiate outwards in all directions. We’ll assume that signals radiate up until a certain distance at full-strength, and that signals are undetectable past that distance. Also, in our running examples, we’ll simplify and assume all devices are arranged in a line, so we just need to consider signals propagating left and right. Remember, though, in real life, signals radiate outwards in three dimensions.","zh_html":"在本节中，为简单起见，我们将忽略障碍物，这意味着信号向所有方向向外辐射。我们还将假设无线通信是对称的(symmetric)：如果 A 能听到 B，那么 B 也能听到 A。实际上可能并非如此（例如不同的发射功率），但我们做简化处理。"},{"en":"Problems with CSMA","zh_html":"CSMA 的问题"},{"en":"To check if someone else is talking, the radio tries to detect energy exceeding a certain threshold. If it does detect, then we conclude that somebody else is transmitting.","zh_html":"为了检测是否有人在发送，无线设备尝试检测是否超过某个阈值的能量。如果检测到，说明信道繁忙，我们等待。如果没有检测到能量，说明信道空闲，我们可以发送。"},{"en":"This strategy works fine if two well-separated pairs of devices are communicating.","zh_html":"如果两对彼此远离的设备在通信，这种策略可以正常工作。"},{"en":"In this example, A and B want to talk, and C and D want to talk. A senses nothing, and starts transmitting to B. Notice that A’s signal propagates in all directions, not just toward B. Later, C senses nothing (since it’s out of A’s range), so it can start transmitting to D.","zh_html":"在这个例子中，A 和 B 想要通信，C 和 D 想要通信。A 没有检测到信号，开始向 B 发送。注意 C 听不到 A（它们相距很远），所以 C 也没有检测到信号，开始向 D 发送。由于 A 和 C 彼此距离很远，两个传输互不干扰。"},{"en":"This strategy also works fine if the two pairs of devices are within range of each other.","zh_html":"如果两对设备在彼此的覆盖范围内，这种策略也能正常工作。"},{"en":"Again, A and B want to talk, and C and D want to talk. A senses nothing, and starts transmitting to B. Later, C senses a signal, since A is talking and C is within range of that signal. Therefore, C will wait until A is done, and only start transmitting to D afterward.","zh_html":"同样，A 和 B 想要通信，C 和 D 想要通信。A 没有检测到信号，开始向 B 发送。稍后，C 检测到信道繁忙（A 正在发送），所以 C 不发送并等待。A 完成后，C 检测到信道空闲，开始向 D 发送。"},{"en":"Sometimes, this strategy leads to problems.","zh_html":"有时，这种策略会导致问题。"},{"en":"Suppose that A and C both want to talk to B. A senses nothing, and starts transmitting to B. Later, C senses nothing, because it’s out-of-range of A, so C also starts transmitting to B. There’s a collision at B!","zh_html":"假设 A 和 C 都想向 B 发送数据。A 没有检测到信号，开始向 B 发送。稍后，C 也没有检测到信号，因为 C 不在 A 的覆盖范围内。所以 C 也开始向 B 发送。然而，B 在 A 和 C 的覆盖范围内，因此 B 会同时听到两个传输，B 处将发生碰撞。"},{"en":"This is called the hidden terminal problem. In this case, the two transmitters (A and C) were out of range of each other, so they could not sense that a transmission was happening.","zh_html":"这被称为隐藏终端问题(hidden terminal problem)。在这种情况下，两个发射器（A 和 C）不在彼此的覆盖范围内，因此它们无法感知到对方的传输。但从接收器 B 的角度看，发生了碰撞。"},{"en":"Here’s another case where CSMA is problematic:","zh_html":"这是 CSMA 出现问题的另一种情况："},{"en":"In this case, suppose that B wants to talk to A, and C wants to talk to D. First, B senses nothing and starts transmitting to A. Remember, B’s signal propagates in all directions, including to C. Now, C wants to talk to D, but senses B’s signal and stays quiet.","zh_html":"在这种情况下，假设 B 想向 A 发送数据，C 想向 D 发送数据。首先，B 没有检测到信号，开始向 A 发送。C 检测到信道繁忙（B 正在发送），因此 C 不发送，即使 C 的目标接收器 D 离 B 很远。"},{"en":"If you look carefully, B and C could have actually transmitted at the same time. It’s true that collisions would happen in the space between B and C, but the receivers (A and D) won’t sense any collisions.","zh_html":"如果仔细观察，B 和 C 实际上可以同时传输。诚然，如果 A 和 D 都试图感知介质（因为它们彼此靠近），会发生碰撞。但 A 在接收（不在感知），D 也在接收（不在感知）。与此同时，B 和 C 在发送。只要 B 到 A 和 C 到 D 的通信彼此距离足够远，它们可能能够同时发送而不会造成太多干扰！然而，CSMA（特别是载波侦听）阻止了这一点。"},{"en":"This is called the exposed terminal problem. In this case, the two transmissions could have occurred at the same time, but instead, one transmission is prevented from happening because C is falsely detecting a collision.","zh_html":"这被称为暴露终端问题(exposed terminal problem)。在这种情况下，两个传输原本可以同时发生，但 CSMA 阻止了其中一个。由于对信道可用性过于保守，CSMA 导致了性能降低。"},{"en":"MACA for Collision Avoidance","zh_html":"用于碰撞避免的 MACA"},{"en":"Instead of using CSMA, MACA (Multiple Access with Collision Avoidance is an approach to multiple access that will help us solve the hidden terminal problem.","zh_html":"与其使用 CSMA，MACA（多路访问与碰撞避免，Multiple Access with Collision Avoidance）是一种多路访问方法，它将帮助我们解决隐藏终端和暴露终端问题。"},{"en":"The key problem with CSMA was, the sender was detecting collisions at the sender, but the real problem is collisions at the receiver. To solve this, we will have the receiver announce whether it detects any collisions.","zh_html":"CSMA 的关键问题在于，发送者在发送端检测碰撞，但真正的问题是接收端的碰撞。发送者对接收端发生的情况一无所知。"},{"en":"Suppose A wants to send data to B. A successful data transfer involves a sequence of 3 steps:","zh_html":"假设 A 想向 B 发送数据。一次成功的数据传输包含3个步骤："},{"en":"A transmits a Request To Send (RTS) packet with the length of the data. This is A saying: “I’d like to send k bits to B.”","zh_html":"A 发送一个请求发送(Request To Send, RTS)分组，其中包含数据长度。这相当于 A 在说：“我想向 B 发送 k 比特数据。”"},{"en":"B transmits a Clear To Send (CTS) packet with the length of the data. This tells A that it’s safe to send, and confirms that there are no collisions at the receiver. The CTS also warns everybody in B’s range: “I’m B, and I’m about to receive k bits, so please don’t talk during this time.”","zh_html":"B 发送一个允许发送(Clear To Send, CTS)分组，其中包含数据长度。这告诉 A 可以安全发送，并确认数据长度。B 还告诉 B 覆盖范围内的所有人（包括潜在的隐藏终端），即将接收长度为 k 的数据。"},{"en":"A transmits the data, and B receives the data. The CTS warning ensures that everybody else in range of the receiver stays quiet during this time.","zh_html":"A 发送数据，B 接收数据。CTS 警告确保接收器覆盖范围内的其他所有人保持静默，直到数据传输完成。"},{"en":"This protocol solves the hidden terminal problem. Remember, in the hidden terminal problem, A and C both sense quiet and start transmitting, causing a collision at B. With this protocol, if A sends an RTS, B will transmit a CTS, warning everybody in B’s range (including C) to be quiet.","zh_html":"该协议解决了隐藏终端问题(hidden terminal problem)。回想一下，在隐藏终端问题中，A 和 C 都检测到信道空闲并同时向 B 发送数据。使用 MACA，在发送数据之前，A 发送 RTS，B 回复 CTS。如果 C 也听到了 B 发出的 CTS，那么 C 知道 B 即将接收数据，C 将在传输期间保持静默。"},{"en":"If you hear an RTS packet, this means you’re in range of the sender. The sender is about to listen for a CTS. Therefore, you need to be quiet and wait for one time slot, which is long enough so that you don’t clobber out the CTS at the sender with data of your own. In other words, you need to be quiet and let the sender receive a CTS.","zh_html":"如果你听到 RTS 分组，这意味着你在发送者的覆盖范围内。发送者即将监听 CTS。因此，在发送者监听 CTS 期间（持续时间很短），你应该保持静默。"},{"en":"After the RTS, if you then hear a CTS, that means you’re also in the range of the receiver, so you must also be quiet during the data transfer. If you don’t hear the CTS, that means that you’re out of range of the receiver, and you can transmit data yourself.","zh_html":"RTS 之后，如果你接着听到 CTS，这意味着你也在接收者的覆盖范围内，因此你必须在数据传输期间保持静默。"},{"en":"Under certain assumptions, this protocol solves the exposed terminal problem. Remember, in the exposed terminal problem, B is sending to A, and C is sending to D. With CSMA, C senses B’s signal and stays quiet, though it could have safely transmitted. With this protocol, if B sends an RTS, C will defer for one time slot (to avoid clobbering the CTS at B). Then, because C didn’t hear the CTS, this means that C is out of range of the receiver (A), so C can safely start transmitting to D.","zh_html":"在某些假设下，该协议解决了暴露终端问题(exposed terminal problem)。回想一下，在暴露终端问题中，B 正在向 A 发送，而 C 想向 D 发送。C 听到 B 的传输后保持静默，即使传输不会互相干扰。"},{"en":"The assumption we make for this to work is, C must be able to hear the CTS from D. Remember, even though C is the sender, it must receive the CTS before it can start sending. However, C is actually hearing the data from B as well, so it might not be able to hear the CTS to start sending. The key problem here is: In CSMA, the sender only ever sends. But in MACA, the sender actually has to receive a CTS before it can start sending, and that CTS might be clobbered in the exposed terminal case.","zh_html":"为了使其工作，我们做出的假设是 C 必须能够听到 D 的 CTS。回想一下，即使 C 是发送者，它也能听到所有分组，包括来自 D 的 CTS。但是，如果 C 能收到 D 的 CTS，那么 C 就在 D 的覆盖范围内。而如果 D 在 C 的覆盖范围内，那么 C 向 D 发送的任何数据都会干扰其他人（例如 B）向 D 发送的数据。所以，C 确实应该保持静默。"},{"en":"If we send an RTS, but we don’t hear a corresponding CTS, this means that we are not clear to send. There’s a collision at the receiver, maybe because the receiver is currently receiving data, or because the receiver gets two requests at the same time. If this happens, we apply binary exponential backoff (similar to CSMA/CD), and wait up to twice as long before sending another RTS.","zh_html":"如果我们发送了 RTS，但没有收到相应的 CTS，这意味着我们不被允许发送。接收端发生了碰撞。在这种情况下，我们应该退避(back off)并重试。"},{"en":"In MACA, each device maintains a CW (Contention Window) value, which tells us how long after a collision we should wait before re-requesting. If we detect a collision (no CTS), we pick a random number between 0 and CW, and wait that long before re-requesting. The minimum value is 2 slots, and the maximum value is 64 slots, where one slot is the time it takes to transmit an RTS. On a successful RTS/CTS, we reset the contention window back to the minimum value of 2. On a failed RTS (no CTS), we double the contention window, clamped to avoid exceeding the maximum value of 64.","zh_html":"在 MACA 中，每个设备维护一个 CW（竞争窗口，Contention Window）值，它告诉我们碰撞后等待多久才能重新请求。对于 MACA，初始 CW 设置为2（时隙）。碰撞后，CW 加倍（指数退避，exponential backoff）。成功后，CW 重置为初始值（2）。"},{"en":"MACAW Feature: ACK (For Reliability)","zh_html":"MACAW 特性：ACK（可靠性）"},{"en":"MACAW (Multiple Access Collision Avoidance for Wireless) offers some improvements over the MACA protocol.","zh_html":"MACAW（无线多路访问碰撞避免，Multiple Access Collision Avoidance for Wireless）对 MACA 协议进行了一些改进。"},{"en":"The first improvement is adding acknowledgements for reliability. As before, the sender transmits an RTS, and the receiver transmits a CTS, and the sender transmits data. Now, we have an extra step at the end, where the receiver transmits an ack.","zh_html":"第一个改进是增加确认(acknowledgements)以实现可靠性。与之前一样，发送者发送 RTS，接收者回复 CTS。之后，发送者发送数据，接收者回复确认(ack)。整个交换过程为：RTS、CTS、数据、ack。"},{"en":"If the data is lost, then there won’t be an ack, and the sender will have to retry, starting over with a new RTS. If the data is correctly sent but the ack is lost, then the sender will retry with a new RTS, but the receiver can immediately reply with the ack instead of the CTS.","zh_html":"如果数据丢失，就不会有 ack，发送者必须重试，从新的 RTS 开始。如果 ack 丢失，发送者也会重试，接收者会收到重复数据（然后丢弃）。"},{"en":"Why did we add acks? Remember, the end-to-end principle said that reliability must be implemented at the end hosts for correctness. However, in this case, we’re implementing reliability in the network, along a single link, solely in order to improve performance. If we didn’t implement reliability on the link, TCP would still guarantee correctness, but a lost packet would cause TCP to slow down significantly (recall, congestion window halves). By contrast, by implementing reliability on the link, we can recover from packet losses more efficiently.","zh_html":"为什么我们要增加 ack？回想一下，端到端原则(end-to-end principle)说，正确的可靠性必须在端主机实现。然而，出于性能考虑，我们可能还希望在中间跳(intermediate hops)上实现可靠性。在无线网络中，链路是不可靠的(unreliable)，所以我们希望在链路层尽早检测并重传丢失的分组，以获得更高的性能。"},{"en":"MACAW Feature: Better Backoff (For Fairness)","zh_html":"MACAW 特性：更好的退避（公平性）"},{"en":"The MACA protocol is unfair when two colliding hosts want to send data. In particular, winners tend to keep winning, while losers keep to keep losing.","zh_html":"当两个碰撞的主机都想发送数据时，MACA 协议是不公平的。具体来说，赢家倾向于一直赢，而输家则一直输。"},{"en":"Here’s an example of unfairness. Suppose A and B both have their windows set to 2, and they simultaneously attempt to reserve the channel. Let’s assume A wins, and B loses. Then A’s window stays at 2, while B’s window doubles to 4. This means that A probably gets to reserve the channel again sooner, and will probably win again. This also means that by the time B tries again, A has already captured the channel again, and B’s window doubles again to 8. This pattern continues, and A keeps re-capturing the channel quickly, while B keeps failing and waiting increasingly longer before trying (and failing) again.","zh_html":"以下是一个不公平的例子。假设 A 和 B 的窗口都设为2，它们在碰撞后同时尝试重传。A 和 B 在0到2之间选择随机数。如果 A 选0，B 选1，A 先发送并选到随机数0，A 的传输成功。A 随后将 CW 重置为2。与此同时，B 之前选了1，等待了一会儿，然后尝试发送。但此时它的 CW 变成了4（加倍）。随着这个过程继续，B 必须等待越来越久，而 A 却毫无问题。"},{"en":"To solve this problem, instead of each device having its own CW, we’ll have everybody share the same CW. The packet header now contains a field for the CW value, and if you receive a packet, you set the CW to the value in the packet. Since everybody now has the same CW, the retry mechanism doesn’t favor any one device. Everybody picks a random value between 0 and CW and waits that long. (Note: We’re slightly simplifying here, but this is true if all devices are in range of each other.)","zh_html":"为了解决这个问题，不让每个设备有自己的 CW，而是让所有设备共享同一个 CW。分组首部现在存储当前的 CW 值。当你看到一个分组时，你从分组中读取 CW 值并将其作为自己的 CW（假设新的 CW 比你的小）。"},{"en":"MACAW also changes the CW update rules to be more gentle. As before, the minimum value is 2 and the maximum value is 64. On a failed RTS (no CTS), we multiply CW by 1.5 (instead of doubling), and again clamp to avoid exceeding 64. On a successful RTS/CTS/DATA/ACK transmission, we decrease CW by 1 (instead of resetting all the way to 2). Note that on a successful RTS/CTS, but a failed ACK, the CW does not change. This approach is sometimes called Multiplicative Increase, Linear Decrease (MILD).","zh_html":"MACAW 还改变了 CW 更新规则，使其更温和。与之前一样，最小值为2，最大值为64。但碰撞后，CW 不是加倍，而是乘以1.5（温和增加，MILD increase）。成功后，CW 不是重置为2，而是减1（温和减少，gentle decrease）。这种温和退避有助于实现更公平。"},{"en":"MACAW Feature: DS (For Exposed Terminals)","zh_html":"MACAW 特性：DS（用于暴露终端）"},{"en":"Recall our exposed terminal example from earlier, where B wants to communicate with A. B sends an RTS, A sends a CTS, and B starts transmitting data. At this point, C did not hear the CTS, which means C is out-of-range of the receiver and can safely transmit data. However, in order to transmit, C must hear the CTS. This might not happen, since C is also hearing B’s data, and there might be a collision between B’s data and D’s CTS.","zh_html":"回想我们之前的暴露终端例子，B 想与 A 通信。B 发送 RTS，A 发送 CTS，任何听到 CTS 的其他节点（C）保持静默。但如果 C 想向 D 发送呢？C 能听到 B 的 RTS（C 在 B 的范围内），所以 C 知道 B 是发送者。然而，C 可能在 B 的范围内但不在 A 的范围内。所以理论上，C 可以在某种程度上向 D 发送，只要不影响 A 的接收。但这很难验证。"},{"en":"MACAW concludes that in the exposed terminal case, B-to-A and C-to-D actually cannot send data simultaneously. Yes, we’re admitting defeat, and it turns out neither MACAW nor CSMA solves the exposed terminal problem.","zh_html":"MACAW 认为，在暴露终端的情况下，B 到 A 和 C 到 D 实际上无法同时发送数据。是的，我们说暴露终端问题实际上无法解决，原因如下。首先，C 听到 B 的 RTS（因为 C 在 B 的范围内）。这意味着 B 在 C 的范围内（由对称性）。A 也能听到 C 的传输（由对称性，因为 C 在 A 的范围内）。因此，A 会受到 C 传输的干扰。"},{"en":"This means that if we’re in the range of the other sender, we actually can’t send data (even if we aren’t in the range of the other receiver). To repeat, this is because we’ll be hearing the data from the other sender, which means we can’t hear the CTS we need to start sending.","zh_html":"这意味着如果我们在另一个发送者的范围内，我们实际上无法发送数据（即使我们不在另一个接收者的范围内）。所以，我们仍然有暴露终端问题。"},{"en":"To solve this problem, we add an extra Data Sending (DS) packet before the data. This is the sender warning everybody: I’m about to send k bits of data, so you need to be quiet during this time.","zh_html":"为了解决这个问题，我们在数据之前添加一个额外的数据发送(Data Sending, DS)分组。这是发送者警告所有人：我即将发送数据。听到 DS 分组（不仅仅是 CTS）的所有人都应保持静默。"},{"en":"The protocol now has 5 steps:","zh_html":"该协议现在有5个步骤："},{"en":"Sender transmits RTS, requesting to transmit k bits of data.","zh_html":"发送者发送 RTS，请求发送 k 比特数据。"},{"en":"Receiver transmits CTS, telling everyone in range: Be quiet, I’m receiving k bits of data.","zh_html":"接收者发送 CTS，告诉覆盖范围内的所有人：请保持静默，我正在接收 k 比特数据。"},{"en":"Sender transmits DS, telling everyone in range: Be quiet, I’m sending k bits of data. (Others can’t send data, because my data will clobber the CTS you need to receive for your transmission.)","zh_html":"发送者发送 DS，告诉覆盖范围内的所有人：请保持静默，我正在发送 k 比特数据。（其他人不能发送数据，因为我的接收者能听到他们。）"},{"en":"Sender transmits the data.","zh_html":"发送者发送数据。"},{"en":"Receiver transmits the ack.","zh_html":"接收者发送确认(ack)。"},{"en":"Note that the RTS and DS are not redundant. The RTS is a request that might not be granted (e.g. maybe no CTS). The DS confirms that the request is actually granted, and enforces that everyone in the sender’s range must be quiet.","zh_html":"注意，RTS 和 DS 不是冗余的。RTS 是一个可能不被批准的请求（例如可能没有 CTS）。DS 确认信道已为数据传输清空（在听到 CTS 之后）。"},{"en":"MACAW Feature: DS (For Synchronization)","zh_html":"MACAW 特性：DS（用于同步）"},{"en":"The DS has a second important purpose. Let’s revisit the exposed terminal again, remembering that MACAW accepts defeat and forces the two transmissions to happen separately.","zh_html":"DS 还有第二个重要目的。让我们重新审视暴露终端问题，记住 MACAW 承认失败，认为暴露终端无法同时发送。"},{"en":"Assume there’s no DS packet. Then, as before, B sends an RTS, A sends a CTS, and B starts transmitting data. C hears the RTS, and defers for one time slot (to avoid interrupting B). However, C does not hear the CTS. At this point, C is doomed to send a futile RTS, and will never hear the CTS (because it’s drowned out by the data from B). C will keep retrying and sending out futile RTS requests, but it has no idea when B will stop sending data.","zh_html":"假设没有 DS 分组。那么，和之前一样，B 发送 RTS，A 发送 CTS，B 开始发送数据。C 听到了 RTS（C 在 B 的范围内）。C 知道 B 将发送一段持续时间（比如 S 秒）的数据。但 C 不知道 B 的数据传输何时结束！所以，C 不知道下一轮竞争何时开始。"},{"en":"By contrast, B knows exactly when it will stop sending data. This gives B a huge advantage in the next round of contention. When B is done sending data, it can immediately send out another request, and it will probably win and get to keep sending data. On the other hand, C has no idea when B will stop sending data, so it has to randomly guess when to send out another request. Most likely, C will guess a time and re-request while B is still sending data, so C will lose and the request won’t be granted (collision).","zh_html":"相比之下，B 确切知道它何时停止发送数据。这使 B 在下一轮竞争中拥有巨大优势：B 确切知道何时开始，而 C 必须感知介质才能知道 B 何时完成。"},{"en":"This lack of synchronization leads to unfairness. If I win, I’ll probably win again, because I know exactly when the next round of contention will happen (it’s when I’m done sending). If you lose, you’ll probably lose again, because you don’t know when the next round of contention will happen (you don’t know when I’m done). The contention time is usually a tiny sliver of time, since most of the time is spent sending data. I know exactly when that time is, and you don’t, so I’ll keep winning.","zh_html":"这种缺乏同步的情况导致了不公平。如果我赢了，我可能还会赢，因为我确切知道下一轮何时开始。"},{"en":"The DS packet solves this problem, because it allows the sender to tell everybody when the next round of contention occurs. Now, B is using the DS packet to tell everybody: I’m starting to send k bits. Not only does C now know to not send futile RTS requests, but it also knows when B will be done sending. This gives C a much fairer shot at winning the next round of contention.","zh_html":"DS 分组解决了这个问题，因为它允许发送者告诉所有人下一轮竞争何时开始。由于每个人都知道下一轮何时开始，所有人都可以公平竞争。"},{"en":"MACAW Feature: RRTS (For Synchronization)","zh_html":"MACAW 特性：RRTS（用于同步）"},{"en":"There’s another case where synchronization is critical to ensure fairness. Suppose A wants to send to B, and D wants to send to C.","zh_html":"还有另一种情况，同步对于确保公平性至关重要。假设 A 想向 B 发送数据，D 想向 C 发送数据。注意 A 不在 D 的范围内，所以 A 听不到 D。另外，C 不在 B 的范围内。然而，C 在 A 和 B 的范围内（A 和 B 都在 C 的范围内）。另一方面，A 在 B 的范围内但不在 C 的范围内。"},{"en":"A transmits to B (A sends RTS, B sends CTS, A sends DS and sends data). C hears the CTS and must stay quiet while the data is sent. Now, D is clueless and doomed. D will send an RTS, and won’t hear a CTS because C is staying quiet. D will keep retrying at random times, and will keep failing, because it has no idea when A will stop sending data.","zh_html":"A 向 B 发送（A 发送 RTS，B 发送 CTS，A 发送 DS 并发送数据）。C 听到 CTS，必须在数据传输期间保持静默。D 想向 C 发送数据，但 C 保持静默，因此 D 无法与 C 同步。D 不知道当前数据传输何时结束。"},{"en":"By contrast, A knows exactly when it will stop sending data. Just like before, this gives A a huge advantage in the next round of contention. A can immediately re-request and win. On the other hand, D has no idea when to re-request. The only way D will win is if it gets really lucky and sends the request immediately after A is done sending, but before A re-requests.","zh_html":"相比之下，A 确切知道它何时停止发送数据。和之前一样，这使 A 在下一轮竞争中拥有巨大优势。"},{"en":"Notice that the DS packet doesn’t help us here, because the two senders, A and D, are out-of-range of each other. A will send a DS packet and announce when it’s sending data, but D won’t hear it, so D is still doomed to lose.","zh_html":"注意，DS 分组在这里帮不了我们，因为两个发送者 A 和 D 不在彼此的覆盖范围内。A 会发送 DS，但 D 听不到 DS。所以 D 不知道下一轮竞争何时开始。"},{"en":"To solve this problem, we’ll let the receiver do the contending on behalf of the sender. D doesn’t know when to re-request, but C does, so let’s make C do the requesting instead.","zh_html":"为了解决这个问题，我们让接收者代表发送者进行竞争。D 不知道何时重新请求，但 C（接收者）知道。当 C 听到 B 的 CTS 时，C 知道 B 正在接收数据，并且 C 知道需要静默多长时间。所以，C 可以告诉 D 何时开始竞争。"},{"en":"When D sends the RTS, C learns that D wants to talk, but C must stay quiet until the next round of contention. Notice that C knows when the next round of contention occurs, because it will hear the ack from B. When the next round of contention occurs, C sends a new packet called a Request-for-RTS (RRTS). This immediately alerts D that the next round has begun, and allows D to immediately send an RTS. This gives D a much fairer shot at winning the contention round.","zh_html":"当 D 发送 RTS 时，C 知道 D 想要通信，但 C 必须保持静默直到下一轮竞争。注意 C 不能发送 CTS（因为 C 还不被允许发送）。相反，C 会发送一个 RRTS（准备接收发送，Ready to Receive To Send）。这个名字可能令人困惑，但它基本上是一个请求：“我有一个发送者想和我通信。请保持静默2个时隙，以便我安排传输。”"},{"en":"If you hear an RRTS, this means that someone in your range is trying to request, so you should be quiet for 2 time slots while they perform the RTS/CTS exchange.","zh_html":"如果你听到 RRTS，这意味着你覆盖范围内的某人正在尝试请求，所以你应该保持静默2个时隙（以便实际的 RTS 发生）。"},{"en":"In the example, if C sends out an RRTS, B hears this and stays quiet for two time slots, which allows D to send an RTS, and C to send a CTS. The CTS tells B to be quiet, and allows the D-to-C transmission to happen.","zh_html":"在例子中，如果 C 发出 RRTS，B 听到后保持静默两个时隙，这允许 D 发送 RTS，C 回复 CTS，数据传输得以完成。"},{"en":"More generally, you should send an RRTS if you hear an RTS, but you’re not allowed to respond, because someone else has told you to be quiet.","zh_html":"更一般地说，如果你听到 RTS，但你被不允许回复（因为其他人已经占用了信道，例如 A 占用了信道向 B 发送），你应该发送 RRTS。"},{"en":"DS and RRTS help with synchronization and ensure more fair contention rounds, but they don’t solve all our problems. Consider A sending to B, and C sending to D. Suppose C starts sending to D. At this point, if A sends an RTS, B can’t hear it because the RTS is getting drowned out by C’s transmissions. The only way A’s RTS will reach B is during the short gap in between C’s transmissions. Here, A is doomed to lose, because it has no idea when C will stop sending, while C knows exactly when it’s sending. Note that RRTS doesn’t save us here, because the RRTS is only sent if you hear an RTS, but B never even hears the RTS. B never learns that A wants to communicate, so B will never send an RRTS request on behalf of A. The original MACAW paper leaves this problem unsolved.","zh_html":"DS 和 RRTS 有助于同步，确保更公平的竞争轮次，但它们并不能解决所有问题。竞争仍然很混乱，这是一个活跃的研究领域。"}]}}};
